ks by Mangi.\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: The president's going to continue to do everything that we can to get him through.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): Democrats against Mangi worry about his ties to controversial organizations including the Alliance of Families for Justice. The New Jersey State Patrolmen's Benevolent Association says the group calls for, "Cop killers to be freed from prison."\par \par \uc2 That is a concern of mine, he is not only a former prosecutor but somebody who's married to law enforcement.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): That sparked Democrats attacking Democrats.\par \par \uc2 SEN. CORY BOOKER (D-NJ): Mr. Mangi has faced accusations that tried to smear his character to whip up fear against him, to turn him into something he is not, that is a lie.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): Republican cited Mangi's links to the Center for Security, race and rights at Rutgers, where he served on the board of advisors. Mangi claimed ignorance when asked about a speaker to the center who pleaded guilty to helping the terrorist group Islamic Jihad.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You sit on an organization that would invite these terrorists, convicted terrorists counselor.\par \par \uc2 ADEEL MANGI, NOMINATED TO SERVE AS A UNITED STATES CIRCUIT JUDGE OF THE COURT OF APPEALS FOR THE THIRD CIRCUIT: Senator, it is an advisory --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You didn't resign.\par \par \uc2 MANGI: Senator, I never heard of that event until today.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: But you're on the board.\par \par \uc2 MANGI: Senator Klobuchar advisory.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): Mangi's advocates claim Republicans targeted him because he's Muslim.\par \par \uc2 BOOKER: What American has to defend their condemnation for the 9/11 attacks. But sadly, they smack of bigotry. They intend to exploit people's fears.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (on camera): President Biden is struggling with Muslim voters upset about his Middle East policy, withdrawing the nomination could provoke that group even more. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Chad, where does the House Speaker Mike Johnson stand on extending aid to Ukraine? Does he have a plan to do that?\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Well, Johnson says Ukraine is a priority when Congress returns from recess but \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  is threatening to oust Johnson if he puts an aid bill on the floor.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE JOHNSON (R-LA): We don't need any dissension right now. She's frustrated. She and I exchanged text messages even today, we're going to talk early next week. Marjorie is a friend, she's very frustrated about, for example, the last appropriations bills.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Johnson is floating loans for Ukraine, also a way to turn repossessed Russian resources against the country, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Chad Pergram live on the Hill. Chad, thanks.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: When it comes to the supplemental, we've been working to build that consensus. When we return after this work period, we'll be moving a product. We're not just giving for an aid, we're setting up in a relationship where they can provide it back to us when the -- when the time is right.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MARJORIE TAYLOR GREENE\uc2  (R-GA): He should not bring their funding for Ukraine, that would also go against what the American people want.\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: Ukraine can't afford any more delays. We have seen what's been happening the past several weeks of past several months in Ukraine, they are losing ground on the battlefield.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, negotiations on Capitol Hill as you heard they're over aid to Ukraine. Israel could potentially put speaker Johnson's job in jeopardy. Let's bring in Fox News Chief Political Analyst Brit Hume and start there.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Brit. Your thoughts on Johnson kind of tiptoeing through the tulips here. He's got a number of -- number of possibilities to do this. But he has said before that it's important.\par \par \uc2 BRIT HUME, FOX NEWS SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST: Yes, that's right, Bret. I mean, it's been thought in some quarters of Johnson was not in supportive aid for Ukraine, which is not the case.\par \par \uc2 He said early on in this -- in this discussion that we can't let Russia win in Ukraine. And, of course, he's got a gun to his head because it only takes a single member to do what \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  has done, which is to make a motion to declare the speakership vacant, and oust him from his job.\par \par \uc2 She hasn't completely pulled the trigger on that as yet. And obviously, he has to worry about that. But he's doing I think what a smart politician tries to do in a situation like this, especially as a leader which is to try to formulate something that a wide majority can support, and can pass muster even with the backbenchers in his caucus, \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  included who might try to ask him for it.\par \par \uc2 You noticed he's talking about making this an effect alone. That is an idea that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has also spoken favorably about. So, if Trump would have supported, it might allow Johnson to get it passed.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So, sticky politics there for Republicans. On the flip side, sticky politics for Democrats, and this judgeship nominee Adeel Mangi, already, there are three senators, Democratic senators who've said they are going to vote against him. Senator Manchin, \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2  and Jackie Rosen from Nevada.\par \par \uc2 This seems like the administration sticking with him Brit, because the politics this whole thing. Meantime, you have the fallout over the transvestite recognition day so called on Easter. What do you make --\par \par \uc2 HUME: Transgender.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Transgender, rather.\par \par \uc2 HUME: