 chip in. We have to wait for that investigation to happen, but the \uc2 NTSB\uc2 , as you say, is beginning that effort. I talked to the chair of the \uc2 NTSB\uc2  this morning.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Senator, we appreciate the time on a tough day there in Baltimore. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 VAN HOLLEN\uc2 : Thanks, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Up next, the panel on former RNC Chairwoman \uc2 Ronna McDaniel\uc2 's ouster from NBC, plus the presidential race, Robert F. Kennedy Junior's veep- stakes, a special edition of Candidate Casino.\par \par \uc2 And later, you ask, I answered, "Tuesday Xtras."\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , FORMER WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: Our democracy is in danger because of the lies that people like \uc2 Ronna McDaniel\uc2  have pushed on this country.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE O'DONNELL, MSNBC HOST: There is an easy way to avoid the controversy NBC News has stumbled into. Don't hire anyone close to the crimes.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL MADDOW, MSNBC HOST: I find the decision to put her on the payroll inexplicable.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The inmates are running the asylum at NBC.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, \uc2 Ronna McDaniel\uc2  is out at NBC. "Axios" writes this, "NBC has dropped \uc2 Ronna McDaniel\uc2  as a paid contributor. NBCU News Group Chair Cesar Conde confirmed in a memo to staff following backlash from journalists. Conde apologized in the memo to NBC News staff for hiring the ex-RNC chair, saying it has become clear this appointment undermines the goal of a cohesive and aligned newsroom."\par \par \uc2 We're going it start there with the panel, get a lot of things in, FOX News senior political analyst Juan Williams, syndicated radio host \uc2 Hugh Hewitt\uc2 , and Katie Pavlich, news editor at Townhall.com.\par \par \uc2 All right, Hugh, you used to be over there. How about this thing?\par \par \uc2 HUGH HEWITT, SYNDICATED RADIO HOST: Well, Ronna is a friend, and I did work for NBC. And in November I worked with Cesar and Rebecca and the whole team. I have never seen anything this brutal since I got started in the media in 1990. Ronna is going to sue everyone who defamed her for breach of contract, for intentional infliction of mental distress. They are going to sue for the destruction of her business opportunity that come from being on TV. I think they made a terrible decision and they allowed the MSNBC bleed to take over their network, and the cult has taken over the news division. And it's going to hurt. The 74 million people who voted for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  are not going to watch NBC News.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Juan, what that? All of this talent rising up and saying that she, specifically, because she said what she said about the 2020 election, even though she addressed it on "Meet the Press," shouldn't be heard on NBC.\par \par \uc2 JUAN WILLIAMS, FOX NEWS POLITICAL ANALYST: I'm puzzled by, in the sense that why don't you just have her on and ask her the tough questions? Right? Ask her, what about the 2020 election? What about January 6th? What about being forced out by Trump as head of the party or paying legal bills, having the party pay -- ask the tough question.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And it would be a unique perspective or a prism by which maybe their viewers will see.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: That's what I think. Now, let me just say, I understand the concern about the brand, that she doesn't fit with the brand. But I'm sitting here on FOX News. I don't necessarily fit with the brand. But you guys have people like me.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You fit on our brand, Juan.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Thank you, thank you. But, to my mind, I just think that what Hugh said is right. I have never seen a situation where -- the management knew who Ronna McDaniels is. She was not a secret. It's not her opinions and her statements are not secret. The people on the staff do not have the right to hire and fire who comes on. They don't design the entire deal. If you feel that way, leave. But it's unbelievable to me that you can sit there and curse out the bosses and have no consequence.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And the apology came from the bosses.\par \par \uc2 KATIE PAVLICH, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, look, they claim that this is about a backlash from journalists at NBC and MSNBC. This is a backlash from people, journalists who have a lot of conflicts when it comes to their relationships with Democratic politicians. Also, it's coming from people who were quite literally Democratic operatives who worked in the Biden White House as early as last year. And they can't handle \uc2 Ronna Romney McDaniel\uc2  coming to the network to maybe explain why these things are happening?\par \par \uc2 I went back to Super Tuesday coverage and remembered when the MSNBC panel was laughing about how could it be that illegal immigration was a top issue in Virginia? Well, someone like \uc2 Ronna McDaniel\uc2  could actually explain why that's an issue. And when it comes to November, if Trump wins again these same people are going to wonder what happened. And it's quite rich coming from a network that breathlessly pushed the Russia hoax for years and claimed that President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was an illegitimate president, which is election denial.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But prior to that, the morning show had \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  on pretty much every other day.\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: When it was beneficial, they had him on a lot.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, we're going to turn topics. \uc2 Robert Kennedy Jr.\uc2  Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT F. KENNEDY JR\uc2 ., (D) PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: When Nicole and I both left the Democratic Party, our values didn't change, but the Democratic Party did.\par \par \uc2 PAUL BEGALA, FORMER CLINTON CAMPAIGN STRATEGIST: Third parties are like cockroaches in the kitchen, OK. It's not what they carry off that upsets you. It's what they fall into and foul up. \uc2 Bobby Kennedy\uc2  can fall into every swing state and foul it up for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 LARA TRUMP, RNC CO-CHAIR: The fact that you have the Biden campaign worried about a third-party candidate, the alarm bells are certainly going off for them, and they are hemorrhaging all these voters.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, RFK Jr. choosing Nicole Shanahan as his vice-presidential running mate. She is 38 years old. She is a tech lawyer. She is the ex-wife of the founder, one of the founders of \uc2 Google\uc2 . "New Republic" writes this, Hugh. "The running mate is young and very rich. That worries Dems. This might seem like a strange choice. Shanahan has no political experience or national name recognition. Her image as a wealthy dilettantish sort has no obvious appeal to the disaffected voters that Kennedy hopes to lure from President Biden and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . But, if you want to understand this decision and why Democrats believe it poses a potential problem for them, follow the money." Thoughts?\par \par \uc2 HEWITT: Follow the money makes sense to me, Bret, but also follow the demographic. Young, female, under the age of 40, well-heeled, active in some of the same causes. The Democrats are very worried. They brought in Luca Brasi of the Democrats, Liz Smith, to take on the third party candidate. They are very worried. And Maria Shriver was in the first lady's box for a reason. They're sending the message, every other Kennedy is with us. Don't pay attention to this fellow.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Usually these third party runs maybe don't make a big splash. Remember back \uc2 Ross Perot\uc2  got 19 percent of that vote in George H.W. Bush and \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 . If you look at the map, RFK Junior's ballot access right now, he's only officially on U,tah appealing to be on other states. But potentially with the running mate could open the door to a number of states.\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: Right. One of the reasons why they wanted to get their vice presidential candidate named is so they have access to these ballots. But the Democrats are concerned for two big reasons. First, Biden is having a very hard time just holding on to his base, which is why they have been so far to the left so far with the campaign. The second thing is that RFK Jr. is actually much closer to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  on policy than he is with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . The Trump PAC came out today attacking RFK and looking at his record, saying he is bad on the Second Amendment. He wants the Green New Deal. He wants high tax rates. Those are not things that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  wants. Those are things that maybe disenfranchised Democrats are looking at and saying I don't want \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , but RFK is a good option as someone who is on the left.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: His third party threat, though, if you look at the Real Clear Politics of polls, is real from RFK Jr. You look at these polls, this is just as of today, there is a lead for the former president. But he's getting 12.3 percent, that doesn't even include a Cornel West and a Jill Stein and maybe they get one, two, three points, it's significant in some of these swing states if they get on that ballot.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: The history, as you pointed out, \uc2 Ross Perot\uc2  19 points, but he gets no electoral votes. But you go back to 16, 2016, and then come forward to 2020. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  actually gets a higher percentage of votes in 2020 in states like Pennsylvania, Wisconsin, Michigan, than he got in 2016. But you have Jill stein in 2016, you have \uc2 Gary Johnson\uc2  in 2016, and they are taking a chunk of votes away from \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 . And then \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is victorious. He doesn't have that in 2020, and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  wins. The Biden campaign is very concerned about this.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, speaking about the former president, here he is describing what he is looking for in a vice presidential pick.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: The first quality has to be somebody that you think will be a good president, because if something should happen, you have to have somebody that's going to be a great president. A lot of people are talking about that gentleman right over there.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Tim Scott, he has been much better for me than he was for himself.\par \par \uc2 The one thing that always surprises me is that the V.P. choice has absolutely no impact. It's whoever the president is.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So that brings us to a place that we haven't been in a while, Candidate Casino. It is back. But this is the vice presidential pick Candidate Casino. All right, the rules, again, $100 in chips. You have to bet the chips. Who will be \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's vice presidential pick? Hugh, $100?\par \par \uc2 HEWITT: I'm spreading it across four. In alphabetical order, Senator Tom Cotton, Senator \uc2 Joni Ernst\uc2 , former Secretary of State \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2 , and Senator J.D. Vance. They are all veterans, and they all contrast wonderfully well with \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  on everything.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It's $25 each. All right, Katie, $100.\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: All right, also breaking up into fours, but in a different way with the math -- $50 on Senator \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2 , $20 on Governor \uc2 Kristi Noem\uc2 , $20 on \uc2 Elise Stefanik\uc2  from New York, and the former Secretary of State, $10 for \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, Juan, round it out, $100.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Big bet on \uc2 Elise Stefanik\uc2 . I think that she is the leader of the pack here. But I'm going to put $20 on \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  to help consolidate the non-MAGA vote, $15 on \uc2 Kristi Noem\uc2 , $15 on \uc2 Joni Ernst\uc2 , and $5 on Tim Scott and Vivek to round out the field.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Charles used to have wine, women, and song, an extra $10.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But I get it. You guys are playing by the rules. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: Thanks, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, it's time for "Tuesday Xtras." First up, Tracey Williams asks, "Just over seven months until the presidential election, how significant is current polling?" It's a great question. A lot of overwrought about polls. Right now, it's just a snapshot. Really look at the polls after September and that's where it starts to get into likely voters.\par \par \uc2 Up next, T.E. Hrdbl wonders, "Do you have upset in the Men's Sweet 16?" I do. I have Clemson going all the way. I do have a personal tie. Brad Brownell, the coach, is my fraternity brother, so there's that.\par \par \uc2 Finally, Not Now asks, "Who do you like to win The Masters?" A very sports- centric Tuesday. I've got two. I think this could be Rory's year. Rory McIlroy could do it this year. And I would say dark horse may be Dustin Johnson for a repeat at the Masters. He is playing well. OK, watch those.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT, new FOX polls, speaking of polls, on abortion, the economy, and TikTok.\par \par \uc2 Remember, if you can't catch us live, set your DVR, 6:00 p.m. in the east, 3:00 p.m. on the west coast.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for this SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and unafraid. Here's Laura.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020240327ek3q00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Workers Raced To Stop Traffic After Ship MayDay; Six People Missing After Baltimore Bridge Collapse; Now: Introduction By RFK Junior's Wife Cheryl Hines; Now: RFK Junior Speaks Ahead Of Announcing Vice President Pick; Now: RFK Junior Names Nicole Shanahan As Running Mate\b0\par\par\uc2 Martha MacCallum, Kevin Corke, Jeff Paul, Bill McGurn, Shannon  Bream \par \uc2 9,607 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 March 2024\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Thank you both. Good afternoon, everybody. I'm Martha MacCallum, and this is THE STORY.\par \par \uc2 We have two big breaking stories at this hour. On the screen, you are looking at right now, this will be by \uc2 Robert F. Kennedy Jr.\uc2  who is an Independent candidate for president, he will announce his pick for his running mate, his vice presidential candidate who will join the ticket with him as he makes an independent run for the White House which has a lot of folks in the Biden camp very concerned and folks on both sides wondering how much he would take from either of the two main candidates.\par \par \uc2 We are also watching, as you've just seen, we heard a rather lengthy statement from the \uc2 NTSB\uc2  just moments ago as they have updated on the bridge collapse in Baltimore. A lot of questions are still circulating this tragic situation and the search is going on for six construction workers who are believed to have gone into the water when this bridge collapsed. This is the moment of the collapse, just stunning, horrible video as we look back at the middle of the night in Baltimore as it struck and then took down all of the span of that bridge. It is just an astonishing and really tragic sight there as they are trying to contact the families and update them on what's going on as they search for the missing.\par \par \uc2 Investigators say that the cargo ship lost power. It did send a mayday call around 1:30 in the morning. Thankfully, they were able to hold off some cars from ever crossing the bridge. A construction crew, as I said, and a few related vehicles believed to have plunged into the cold water there in the middle of the night. The \uc2 FBI\uc2  says there is no specific credible information to suggest that there were any ties to terrorism involved in this. Senior national correspondent, Kevin Corke, is on the scene in Dundalk, Maryland right now with the very latest for us. Hi, Kevin.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN CORKE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Martha, quite a day here, as you can well imagine. This is a multi-agency circumstance where they collaborate and try collectively to solve this puzzle, this devastating puzzle here that is played out at the Key Bridge. We can give you an update on a hospitalized individual. According to the \uc2 University of Maryland Medical Center\uc2 , that individual has been released from the hospital.\par \par \uc2 Still, the search for answers continues at this hour, trying to figure out, first of all, what happened to that vessel, and second of all, given the span, how did it end up hitting the part of the bridge that it did, which ultimately brought it down. So, the search continues as we try to find out what happened in this overnight crash that destroyed the bridge and sent untold numbers of individuals plunging into the cold, dark waters. As you just pointed out, we just heard from the \uc2 NTSB\uc2  chair.\par \par \uc2 She said there is not a ton of information she can share at this hour, although she did give us some bit of an update in letting us know that they will continue to work with the Coast Guard and other agencies to try to find the answers here. Meantime, it's also important to point out, and I think you mentioned this, Martha, and I want to make sure the folks at home know, that authorities did attempt to clear the bridge before the cargo ship crashed into it. Still, the search and rescue efforts continue at this hour.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHRIS VAN HOLLEN\uc2  (D-MA): We have, we believe, six people who are unaccounted for right now, but that mission is going on. I reached out early to the governor because I wanted to let him know that the federal government is a full partner in this effort. I've been in touch with Secretary \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2  twice, the head of the \uc2 NTSB\uc2 , \uc2 National Transportation Safety Board\uc2 , and others.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I tried to recover the data voice recorder --\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CORKE: Now, given the nature and scope of this disaster, it is also not surprising that the president was informed soon afterwards. Tonight, he is pledging the full weight of government in response to this tragedy.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I spoke with Governor Moore this morning as well as the mayor of Baltimore, the county executive, and my secretary of transportation is on the scene. I told them we're going to send all the federal resources they need as we respond to this emergency. I mean all the federal resources.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CORKE: All the federal resources says the president. Back out live now, we do expect to hear more from officials, including the governor. We're expecting to hear from the governor here around 3:30. Of course, we'll bring you that as soon as we get it. But for now, Martha, back to you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. Kevin Corke, thank you very much. We'll continue to keep a close eye on the search there as we bring in Charley Pereira, former \uc2 NTSB\uc2  Investigator, now President of Transportation Safety and Security Consulting. Charley, thank you very much for being here. We just heard from the current head of the \uc2 NTSB\uc2 . What did you think about what she had to say? And did it raise other questions for you as you listened to that?\par \par \uc2 CHARLEY PEREIRA, PRESIDENT, TRANSPORTATION SAFETY AND SECURITY CONSULTING: I think a standard operating procedure for them, they're going to work with the Coast Guard, collect all the data that they can, and hopefully report to us as soon as possible on the facts. Once they interview the crew and get the data recorder information, then hopefully we'll have more factual answers.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, you know, some of the questions obviously that surround this first and foremost whether or not there are survivors and the rescue mission is ongoing, as she made clear and others have made clear. And then you have the questions about why anchors weren't dropped if they weren't dropped. I think the initial take is that they weren't. Also, early on, there were questions about whether or not there was a tugboat in the area and whether or not a tug boat could have been effective at pushing this off course a little bit so that it didn't hit the bridge. What do you think about those?\par \par \uc2 PEREIRA: All of that is going to be explored during the investigation. It would be inappropriate to try to opine on the effectiveness of anchors or tugboats at this point. I think we really just need to focus on collecting the data, especially any perishable information, as soon as possible. Also, I think they should be taking a close look at things that they know we can do in the near future to prevent these kinds of accidents. Automated warning and protection systems for bridges, these are things they shouldn't wait a year or two to conclude their report to issue types of things. That's a year or two that they could be using what we already know and what's already obvious to try to prevent this from happening again.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Good point. Charley Pereira, thank you very much. Good to have you with us today, sir.\par \par \uc2 PEREIRA: You're welcome. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Now, we bring in Bob Pizzini, retired U.S. Navy Diving Officer and Bomb Tech. Sir, good to have you with us. Thank you very much for your service. Your thoughts on this, what we're told is an ongoing search and rescue mission at this point.\par \par \uc2 BOB PIZZINI (RET.), U.S. NAVY DIVING OFFICER AND BOMB TECH: Well, from my search and rescue or search and recovery perspective, this is very complex given the hazardous nature of the bridge, the concrete, all the material that's sitting in the water, 51 feet of water roughly. 51 feet of water is not terribly complex, but the debris is very dangerous and very complex, very similar to the Minnesota Bridge collapse and the recovery that took place there. So, this will take a long time, obviously, just to complete the search, rescue, and recovery of the missing people, and then to clear the waterway beyond that will be an enduring operation.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Is there anything that surprises you about the way that they're approaching it? I've seen some other boats in the water that look like they're not related to the search and we've seen choppers above. What do you think about the way it's being carried out as far as you can tell?\par \par \uc2 PIZZINI: I'm sure that all departments of the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2 , the state of Maryland, the \uc2 U.S. Coast Guard\uc2 , I'm very confident in their abilities and their ability to coordinate all their resources and use the resources wisely. But, you know, just an aerial search could reveal a piece of floating debris that could be a key piece of information, for example.\par \par \uc2 So, surveillance at this time, at this critical time, is important if there is going to be any type of recovery of any potential survivors. The current is very strong. Somebody could have been washed down the river, so to speak, and so a wide area search for human survivors, I think, is critical at this point.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And how long would you expect if someone were able, if they were swept down and maybe were able to grab onto something, how long would you expect them to be able to survive out there?\par \par \uc2 PIZZINI: My understanding is the water temperature there is roughly 48 degrees Fahrenheit. And hypothermia, I think, would set in very quickly, probably within two to three hours. And survivability against the water temperature definitely is the key challenge. And then, the scale of debris and the size and the mass of the debris could easily pin somebody, again, in the case of the Minnesota Bridge, there were vehicles that were pinned underwater by the debris. So, it's extremely challenging, and I think that survivability at this point is very limited.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, tell me what the procedure would be, you know, for someone with your experience when you first get on the scene. I mean, it's such a large area. How do you know where to begin?\par \par \uc2 PIZZINI: So, the first thing really is to make sure that we don't create any additional damage, or again, disturb the wreckage. There could be wreckage from above that's not secure, much like a building collapse, for example, where firefighters will first make sure that it's safe to enter or there may be debris that has to be made safe in some capacity.\par \par \uc2 So, really, the first thing to do is to assess the immediate dangers, the immediate hazards, and then determine the point of entry, whether it's from the remaining structure of the bridge or from the water or bringing in surface craft, which will certainly happen if it hasn't already, to start making penetrations into the water.\par \par \uc2 The water depth is 51 feet, not terribly challenging, but the debris will range from above the water to anywhere between the surface and 51 feet. So, there's a lot of investigation to do just to really figure out where to start.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Indeed, this is from the earlier news conference earlier today and this is about the crew and what they were doing on that bridge. Let's take a listen to this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The crew that was out there working was basically repairing potholes. Just so you understand that had nothing to do with a structural issue at all in the facility.\par \par \uc2 GOV. WES MOORE (D-MA): The bridge was actually fully up to code.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I should mention that we're going to hear from Governor \uc2 Wes Moore\uc2  at, excuse me, at 3:30 this afternoon. Just in terms of what you would want to understand about how this came apart when you begin a search mission or when you're entering a mission that's going on, it does appear that this wasn't a structural issue for the bridge, that it really was caused by this tanker that hit this pillar square on?\par \par \uc2 PIZZINI: Yes, well having seen similar bridge spans be disassembled, if you will, through the use of explosives, it's amazing to see this video, how this bridge came down very similar to the way I've seen bridges come down using explosives. So, clearly a key support structure was impacted, and again, the ship, a thousand feet long, you know a U.S. aircraft carrier, the \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2 , for example is roughly 1,100 feet long. So, so, the impact of that ship was significant on that particular support structure.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Bob, thank you very much for joining us today with your background on this. We appreciate it. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 PIZZINI: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, now, we're going to go across the country to Oakland, California. Independent presidential candidate, \uc2 Robert F. Kennedy Jr.\uc2 , is about to take the stage. He will name his running mate. Not since \uc2 Ross Perot\uc2  in 1992 have we seen an independent candidate get so much traction in the polls. He's anywhere from nine to 18 percent in some of the polls that we've looked at. They're playing a campaign video right now and there are a lot of reports that Team Biden is very concerned about the impact of this on the Biden candidacy and that they are "building a machine" to attack him and what will that look like?\par \par \uc2 I think we're going to find out fairly soon. Bill McGurn, Clay Travis, \uc2 Mark Penn\uc2  all here as we wait for this announcement. Cheryl Hines, the wife of the candidate who everybody knows from "Curb Your Enthusiasm" is going to come out and introduce him. We're going to go to that as soon as that gets underway, because that could be entertaining. So, we're going to check that out. So first, the correspondent Jeff Paul, who joins us live in Oakland. Hi Jeff.\par \par \uc2 JEFF PAUL, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Martha, we are expecting RFK Jr. to take the stage at any moment to not only announce that he's got a V.P. candidate, but really to signal that he's not going away anytime soon. We have a pretty good idea of who that person could be, but regardless of who it is, it's really going to help his campaign not only build on the enthusiasm of the hundreds of supporters who are here and who are all around the greater California area and beyond, but also, it's really going to help with fundraising.\par \par \uc2 Now, the other big challenge his campaign faces is getting on as many ballots as possible come November. By naming a running mate, it'll help him meet a requirement set by two dozen states currently. So, formally announcing a V.P. is a huge step forward, can lead to voter excitement, the ability to raise more money, and further challenge both President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Kennedy was recently asked about his intentions to run during a \uc2 New York Times\uc2  podcast and had this to say.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You aren't concerned at all about whether you will pull more votes from Biden or Trump.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT F. KENNEDY JR\uc2 ., INDEPENDENT PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: My purpose it to win the election.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: If there is any evidence that you would help one candidate or another win, would that cause you to drop out?\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY: No.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You don't have any personal fears about the possibility of playing a spoiler alert to either Biden or Trump?\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY: I have -- I have -- I have a fear both of them winning the election.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PAUL: Now, the name at the top of the list for his potential vice- presidential running mate is a woman named Nicole Shanahan. She is a 38- year-old tech lawyer --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. Jeff, thank you very much. We're going to jump right in here. Cheryl Hines is introducing her husband. Let's watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CHERYL HINES, WIFE OF ROBERT F. KENNEDY JUNIOR: The presidency. Yes.\par \par \uc2 And I just want to take a moment to say, as we move through our day, let's think about the people in Baltimore who are experiencing their tragedy, and we want to keep them in our thoughts, in our hearts, in our prayers today. Yes.\par \par \uc2 So, you're probably here to hear what Bobby has to say today.\par \par \uc2 It's a very exciting day, and you will not be disappointed. One of the things that I love about Bobby, there are a lot. I won't spend the day naming them. But one of the things that I truly respect about him, I have watched him and continue to watch him inspire people of all different parties to come together for the greater good of this country.\par \par \uc2 Yes.\par \par \uc2 So, when you look around you, you're probably going to see Republicans, Democrats, Independents, all gathered together, to see what we have in common and how we can work together. And that's what Bobby's all about. And that's who he is. And that's what he's doing. And America is listening and is inspired.\par \par \uc2 All right. I think we should just do it. Are you ready to hear from Bobby?\par \par \uc2 Are you at all interested to hear from the new V.P. pick?\par \par \uc2 OK. OK! Well, let's make a lot of noise. I want to really hear how enthusiastic you are about this. Please, ladies and gentlemen, put your hands together for my love -- for the next president of the United States, \uc2 Robert Kennedy Jr.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT F. KENNEDY, JR. (I), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Thank you all very much. Thank you all very much.\par \par \uc2 AMERICAN CROWD: Bobby! Bobby! Bobby!\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY: Thank you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 I want to start out, first of all, to thanking all of you. And thank you, Oakland, and thank all of you for being here today. I also want to thank the tribal chiefdoms, (INAUDIBLE) Ohlone tribe, and the chairwoman of the tribal council for endorsing me today, for putting their faith in me.\par \par \uc2 And they know, the Muwekma Ohlone Tribe. Oh, and they know very much they - - that the struggle for indigenous rights has consumed a lot of my life, my personal and my professional life, and that this work is going to continue when we are in the White House.\par \par \uc2 The Muwekma Ohlones are one of the many tribes that were -- that were decertified during the 1950s, 1960s, and these tribes need to be re- legitimized, and that's something that I will do as soon as I get a White House.\par \par \uc2 I want to say something about Oakland. My father spent a lot of time doing hearings in Oakland on the poverty issues or the poverty committee in 1967 when he was a United States senator, then, he came back here in 1968, on the presidential campaign.\par \par \uc2 And during that presidential run, he made an unscheduled visit to Oakland's Taylor Memorial Church. And he went there with Willie Brown, who as you know, later became mayor of San Francisco, but at that time, he was assemblyman.\par \par \uc2 And he came to me with a lot of local activists here in Oakland in a very, very violent, turbulent time. He met with the \uc2 NAACP\uc2  and the Black Panther Party. And it was a very rancorous meeting, he was advised by the local police chief not to go. He attended with two of his companions John Glenn, the astronaut, one of his best friends, Rafer Johnson, who had been the decathlon champion in 1960, and it was one of my father's closest friends.\par \par \uc2 And the meeting was so rancorous and vitriolic. At -- and at one point, Rafer and John Glenn advised my father to leave. People were insulting him. People were threatening him. And my dad refused. And he said, this is between me and them, I need to hear them out. I need to hear what they are going to say.\par \par \uc2 The night -- and he waited, he stayed throughout that meeting. And the next day, all of the people who were at that meeting signed up to join his campaign.\par \par \uc2 And the Black Panther Party provided his security detail here in Oakland, and continued to provide security for him later on in the campaign during in his convoys. And it was a lesson that I just wanted to share it with you. Because it's a lesson all of us need to learn at this point in our history. And we need to start listening to each other even when it's difficult.\par \par \uc2 We need to sit through the anger. We need to sit with each other and listen to the feelings and not walk away, and not see each other as enemies. But learn to love each other even through that anger and vitriol. We need to start coming back to each other as Americans again.\par \par \uc2 The last time I was in Oakland was when I served on the trial team in the \uc2 Monsanto\uc2  cases and we -- oh, we tried two have three cases in this city. We won -- we won an -- we won a 289 million in the first, and then, the third one which we tried here, we won -- we asked the jury for a billion dollars, and an Oakland jury gave us $2.2 billion.\par \par \uc2 That brought \uc2 Monsanto\uc2  to the negotiating table. We settled that all 40,000 cases. I lived here for several months during that trial and I got to really love this city. A \uc2 Monsanto\uc2  case was the latest in a lifetime of battles for me to get poisons out of our food and out of our farms and to restore our soils.\par \par \uc2 The effort -- that effort has consumed a lot of my life, and I wanted a vice president who shared my passion for wholesome healthy foods, chemical free for regenerative agriculture for good soils.\par \par \uc2 I found exactly the right person. And among other things, she has used over the past several years cutting edge technology, including A.I. to calculate the catastrophic health consequences of toxins in our soil, our air, our water, and our food. Technology has been a lifelong passion for my future vice president.\par \par \uc2 It is important because I also wanted a vice president who shares my indignation about the participation of big tech as a partner in the censorship, and their surveillance, and the information warfare that our government is currently waging against the American people.\par \par \uc2 And that's why I'm bringing on someone with a deep inside knowledge of out how big tech uses A.I. to manipulate the public. I want to partner with strong ideas about how to reverse those dire threats to democracy and our freedoms. I managed to find a technologist at the forefront of A.I. She has spent the last decade relying on neural networks, artificial intelligence and cutting-edge science to identify abuses in our government.\par \par \uc2 She understands that the health of every American is a national security issue and a national security risk. Her work has proven time and again that health drives our economy, and it is the foundation of our mental health, our national happiness, our ability to lead the world and innovation and prosperity and in peace.\par \par \uc2 I also wanted someone who was an athlete who could help me inspire Americans to heal to get them back in shape.\par \par \uc2 And I'm happy to report that my vice president is an avid surfer, who attend -- who attended school on a softball scholarship. I want it right here in Oakland. I wanted someone who is battle tested, able to withstand criticism and the controversy and all the defamations and slanders and perjuries that are thrown against anyone who embarks on a presidential campaign.\par \par \uc2 I wanted an advocate who has seen corruption of our regulatory agencies firsthand, who shares my indignation about the way it allows regulated industries, to commoditize our food, our wildlife, and our children. I wanted someone who would honor the traditions our nation, as a nation of immigrants. But who also understand that to be a nation, we need to secure borders.\par \par \uc2 I wanted a partner who is a gifted administrator, but also possesses the gift of curiosity, an open inquiring mind and the confidence to change even her strongest opinions. In the face of contrary evidence.\par \par \uc2 I wanted someone with a spiritual dimension, and compassion, and idealism, and above all, a deep love of the United States of America.\par \par \uc2 And I found all of those qualities in a woman who grew up right here in Oakland. The daughter of immigrants, who overcame every daunting obstacle and went on to achieve the highest levels of the American Dream.\par \par \uc2 So, that is why I'm so proud to introduce to you the next vice president of the United States, my fellow lawyer, a brilliant scientist, technologists, a fierce warrior mom, Nicole Shanahan.\par \par \uc2 I'm going to tell you a little bit about Nicole before we bring her out here. With that, we're going to see a video.\par \par \uc2 And Nicole's personal story began in Oakland. She was the daughter of impoverished immigrants. She grew up on food stamps and welfare in the city, beset by many, many other unique challenges, all of which she overcame. A very, very American journey took her to a career as a patent attorney, and as a Silicon Valley tech entrepreneur, and as a \uc2 Stanford University\uc2  fellow.\par \par \uc2 Like many of us, Nicole assumed our government was working for our people that our defense and intelligence agencies wanted peace, that public health agencies wanted us to be healthy, that the \uc2 USDA\uc2  supported wholesome foods and family farms, that the \uc2 EPA\uc2  would stand up for clean air and clean water, that the fed wanted prosperous America, that the Democratic Party was on the side of the middle class, the working poor and Main Street small businesses, that scientists were incorruptible and that science was an exalted search for the truth. And that the president of the United States could always be counted on to defend free speech.\par \par \uc2 I too used to believe those things. Do you remember those days? And she'll tell you that she now understands that the defense agencies work for the military industrial complex, that health agencies work for big pharma, that \uc2 USDA\uc2  works for big ag and the processed food cartels, that the \uc2 EPA\uc2  is in cahoots with the polluters. Bad scientists can be mercenaries, that government officials sometimes act as censors, and that the Fed works for Wall Street and allows millionaire banks --\par \par \uc2 (END LIVE COVERAGE)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. We can keep watching this but I want to bring in Bill McGurn, Wall Street Journal Editorial Board member and Fox News contributor, Clay Travis, OutKick founder and \uc2 Mark Penn\uc2 , \uc2 Stagwell\uc2  CEO and former Clinton advisor and pollster. Great to have all of you with us.\par \par \uc2 I want to get just a quick take from each of you on this announcement and what you think it means. Bill McGurn, let's start with you.\par \par \uc2 BILL MCGURN, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, there's a lot of excitement. As you mentioned, he polls between 9 percent and 18 percent. And the big question is who we take that from. I think in the beginning, when he announced there was a feeling he was taking Trump votes, and I think today the polls sort of show he's more likely to take Biden votes. I don't find this extraordinary because he's basically a liberal progressive agenda.\par \par \uc2 Few notes that maybe conservatives like against big tech and so forth, but his big issues is the environment and so forth. And I think he's a liberal progressive and picked someone like that to be his running mate.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Clay, let me bring. You know, he talks about the agencies, governmental agencies not being what we all thought they were supposed to be or doing what they're supposed to do and wanting to take that power back from the government in that sense. What do you think about that and what do you think about this decision?\par \par \uc2 CLAY TRAVIS, OUTKICK FOUNDER: I actually think he takes Trump voters away, and that may be a counterintuitive take. And by the way, it may be the case that in California he takes voters that are different than Michigan or Wisconsin, or Georgia, or Arizona where the swing states are, which is really where he could be impactful.\par \par \uc2 You mentioned earlier \uc2 Ross Perot\uc2  in 1992, and I remember very well watching \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  wanting \uc2 Ross Perot\uc2  on every debate stage because the data reflected that helped Clinton and hurt George H. W. Bush.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Right.\par \par \uc2 TRAVIS: I think we'll start to get a little bit of clarity on how RFK Jr. is polling based on whether Trump or Biden. And right now, I agree that Biden seems more reticent about it, wants him involved in the overall political process. And I also wonder whether RFK Jr. is open to the idea of being maybe a part of a Trump cabinet. I don't think Biden would put him in the cabinet. I think Trump might.\par \par \uc2 What is his real goal here? He's not going to win the election. At some point that becomes a reality. And then how does he play with all the other third party candidates? Cornel West, whether there's a No Labels candidate, Jill Stein, how do all of those interact if people have five, six, seven choices on their ballot when they go to vote in November?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Well, there's no doubt. \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  said that this is a huge issue that the Democrat Party needs to be very concerned about, \uc2 Mark Penn\uc2 . If you talk to Bobby Kennedy Jr., he says, look, all I need is 34 percent in his polling. He says he's at 18-20 in that range. He's like, I only have to grow 15 percent in order to really be a player in the swing states according to their polling. And we've seen some strength that he has in some of these swing states. So what do you say, Mark?\par \par \uc2 MARK PENN\uc2 , FORMER CLINTON POLLSTER AND ADVISER: Well, look, we did a poll just two days ago and, look, Jill Stein or Cornel West, they get 1 percent. And they do take that 1 percent from Democrats. Kennedy got like 14 percent, that means he could be a real factor here. Look, these things announce and they could, you know, a month from now flop down to 3 percent. We've seen that before, or soar, as \uc2 Ross Perot\uc2  did, to 39 percent in June.\par \par \uc2 So there is an opening here, it's a little bit of an unconventional speech but he's anti-big tech, he's anti-big government, he's anti-censorship, he's anti-big corporation, he's for pure environment. He had a message, he was delivering it, and now he has potential funding. So you're going to have to wait and see, and stop this parlor game of who it hurts and who it helps.\par \par \uc2 He's taken roughly equally, a little bit more from Biden because it widens the horse race. Trump's winning by two. He goes to three or four. But that's not necessarily how this is going to play itself out. You got to wait and see.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Well, you got a bunch of jets fans breathing a sigh of relief and hoping that Aaron Rodgers is back in the fall and not on the VP campaign trail with RFK Jr. Gentlemen, thank you very much. Good to have you with us today. We're going to keep an eye on this and thanks for weighing in. We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 So, in the most consequential abortion case to hit the Supreme Court since the overturning of Roe v. Wade, justices are weighing in on whether to roll back the access to pills that terminate pregnancies. A doctor connected to this case who says that she has treated women with severe complications from this drug next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Breaking right now, Maryland Governor \uc2 Wes Moore\uc2  providing a brand new update on the collapse of the Francis Scott Key Bridge in Baltimore. Let's listen in.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN LIVE COVERAGE)\par \par \uc2 WES MOORE (R), MARYLAND GOVERNOR: -- spent time with a lot of our first responders who we mentioned before, the work that they have done since early in the morning, and they have not stopped since, is truly remarkable. These are individuals who in the middle of the night jumped into some very challenging environments, some very challenging territories, and have not stopped in their quest to making sure that each and every person is seen and supported, and lifted up.\par \par \uc2 And so to our first responders, today and always, we just want to say from the bottom of our hearts, thank you.\par \par \uc2 We've also had the chance to speak with both the President and the Vice President who have been full-throated in their support of everything that we're doing here in Maryland. And we just want to say how grateful we are to them, how grateful we are to the secretary of Transportation and Department of Transportation who, within hours of this happening, I was on the phone with the secretary of Transportation, I think Secretary Buttigieg and called me at 330 in the morning. And now he and his entire team have been down here this afternoon spending time looking at the efforts, looking at the damage and also identifying what they can do to support and help.\par \par \uc2 I can't tell you enough how much we appreciate that, Mr. Secretary. And also, please let the President and the Vice President know from the bottom of our heart how much we appreciate it as well.\par \par \uc2 We've also had a chance to be here with our remarkable congressional delegation. From the earliest parts of the morning, our congressional delegation has been there throughout. And I always say I'm grateful that I've got such a remarkable congressional delegation. And today I just have another example as to why I am a very lucky governor to have the congressional delegation that I have.\par \par \uc2 And also our state -- our local elected officials, Mayor Scott, who I literally got a call from the very first thing in the morning, I know you have had no rest in a very long time, sir. And so, I want to say how much we appreciate you.\par \par \uc2 But also, it's to the members of the philanthropic community who have been reaching out and offering support. It's the members of the private sector who've been reaching out and offering support. It's the sandwich companies who have said, we're going to shut down because we just want to make sure that the first responders are getting meals.\par \par \uc2 Everybody has stepped up, everybody has raised their hands to serve. And I can tell you, it is so deeply appreciated, it's so deeply felt. And for everyone who is offering prayers and supports, I can tell you those prayers are working and we are grateful.\par \par \uc2 And the thing that I would ask for people to remember is this. First, this is very much still a search and rescue mission. We are still actively looking for survivors. We know, and that's the pledge we've made to these families, and this is still very much an active search and rescue mission.\par \par \uc2 And there is not a single resource that we will hold off on deploying. I have already authorized the deployment of everything from air, land, and sea resources to make sure that this search and rescue operation is carried out to its fullest intent. The second thing I want to remind people is that this will not be short. There's going to be a long road.\par \par \uc2 There's going to be a long road not just as we go from search and rescue, there'll be a long road as we talk about what does the future of this region, the future of the area look like. And we're going to need each and every one of you.\par \par \uc2 We're thankful to have partners like what we have in the Biden-Harris administration. We're thankful for the partners that we have in our federal delegation. We're thankful for the partners we have in our state and local leadership. We're thankful for the partners that we have in the private sector and the philanthropic community. We're thankful for the partners that we have within the Moore-Miller administration.\par \par \uc2 We're thankful for each and every one of you, both Marylanders and non- Marylanders, who have reached out and offered support. We feel it, we need it, and we are truly grateful for it.\par \par \uc2 And I think just in this time, this state has been able to show what it means to be Maryland tough and Baltimore strong. And this state and this city will continue to show exactly that. And so with that, I want to turn it over to our secretary of Transportation, \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2 , with a deep sense of thanks and appreciation.\par \par \uc2 Oh, I apologize. Before I turn it over to Secretary Buttigieg, I want to turn it over to the dean of our delegation and someone who has been leading from the front this entire time. And we are deeply grateful for the leadership of Senator Ben Cardin.\par \par \uc2 SEN. BEN CARDIN (D-MD): Governor Moore, first of all, thank you very much for getting our whole team together to meet this challenge. And our prayers are with the families of those that are lost at sea at this moment. As the governor said, it's still a search and rescue. So we are hopeful and we are with the families.\par \par \uc2 I also want to underscore our thanks to our first responders. They did an extraordinary job, acting very quickly and saved lives. So we thank our first responders for everything that they have done.\par \par \uc2 I just really want to underscore a couple points. Our first priority is the search and rescue for those that were on the bridge. We then need to make sure that the channel is reopened. It's critically important to our economy, it affects many jobs, it affects not only jobs here in Maryland but around the country and world. So our next priority is make sure we get that channel opened.\par \par \uc2 And then we also need to fix and replace the bridge for the surface transportation. We're going to work together as a team. I am very impressed by all the partners that are with us today. We heard from them, at the state level, the local level, at the federal level. I particularly want to acknowledge our federal partners. The secretary of Transportation is with us. We have the Coast Guard. We have the \uc2 Army Corps of Engineers\uc2 . We have the investigators. We have the small business administration.\par \par \uc2 They're all here because of the commitment of the partners to work together. A special thanks to President Biden, who's made it very, very clear that he'll do everything in his power to make sure that we get the help we need to deal with this challenge. But as Secretary Buttigieg told us in our briefings, he's going to need the help of Congress in order to get things done.\par \par \uc2 So I want to acknowledge our team. Senator \uc2 Van Hollen\uc2 , my partner in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  on the Appropriations Committee, the work that he's doing. Congressman Mfume is here. Congressman Trone is here. Team Maryland, our federal delegation is committed to working together.\par \par \uc2 Senator \uc2 Van Hollen\uc2  and I got calls from our leadership that said they're prepared. Secretary Buttigieg, we do everything that we need to do in Congress to make sure you had the resources and the federal partners had the resources in order to get the job done. So I want to thank Senator Schumer for his call and his comments, Senator Murray, Senator Carper, chair of the Environmental Public Works Committee have all been in touch with us.\par \par \uc2 It is a team effort, and we're going to make sure that we do everything we can to protect our economy and protect the people of our state.\par \par \uc2 With that, let me just turn it back to the governor or Secretary Buttigieg. This guy woke all of us up early this morning, so thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 PETE BUTTIGIEG, SECRETARY OF TRANSPORTATION: Thank you. And I want to thank Senator Cardin and the entire delegation for their leadership. They have been on this from the first moments. And as the senator mentioned, and I'll say more about this in a moment, we'll be needing to work together to make sure that we render all of the support that is needed at the federal level.\par \par \uc2 I want to recognize the leadership of Governor Moore, who was already wide awake and hard at work when I reached him in the middle of the night. We just had a very informative briefing with his extraordinary leadership team. And I was moved to see the partnership between state as well as county and city personnel, led by the county executive and the mayor, working hand in glove with us and with our sister federal agencies.\par \par \uc2 And if there's one thing I have to say today, it is a note of gratitude for the extraordinary and courageous work of the first responders, some of whom are in that cold water right now, some of whom are from right here, some of whom have traveled in to render mutual aid, all of whom are responding with extraordinary professionalism and whose work has already saved lives. And to those state, county and local responders, I would add the extraordinary work of the \uc2 United States Coast Guard\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 We should also recognize that this is an excruciating day for several families who went to bed last night, having it be a normal night, and woke up today to news that no one wants to receive. They are hoping and praying, and we are hoping and praying with them. We are all putting our arms around the community of Baltimore, and that is true for all of this country. I've even heard from counterparts as far away as the United Kingdom reaching out to express their support to the people of Baltimore.\par \par \uc2 As been mentioned, I've been in close contact with the governor, the mayor, county leadership, and the congressional delegation. And as President Biden has made clear, the federal government will provide all of the support that they need for as long as it takes.\par \par \uc2 This is no ordinary bridge. This is one of the cathedrals of American infrastructure. It has been part of the skyline of this region for longer than many of us have been alive. So the path to normalcy will not be easy, it will not be quick, it will not be inexpensive. But we will rebuild together.\par \par \uc2 In order to make sure that happens, the President's plan is to work with everyone here in order to rebuild this bridge and reopen this port, including our readiness as a department to approve emergency funding as soon as we receive that request. Meanwhile, our maritime administration will help with port, harbor and supply chain operations. Our \uc2 Federal Highway Administration\uc2  will assist when it comes to the bridge itself and any ways that we can help ease roadway congestion for residents and commuters who can no longer use this major thoroughfare.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Federal Aviation Administration\uc2  is even involved, working to keep the airspace above the bridge clear for emergency personnel. \uc2 PHMSA\uc2 , our \uc2 Pipeline and Hazardous Materials Safety Administration\uc2  is on the scene to help with any hazmat issues, and our newly stood up freight office is already at work preparing for supply chain impacts that we know are coming because of the importance of this port, not just to the Baltimore region, but really to the entire United States economy.\par \par \uc2 We're going to be working closely with the \uc2 National Transportation Safety Board\uc2  as they lead their independent investigation, and with the Coast Guard as they continue operations in the water. I've also spoken with Secretary Mayorkas who's working to ensure that all DHS assets are integrated.\par \par \uc2 So in many ways, our work is just beginning to rebuild this bridge and deal with impacts in the meantime, to reopen this port and deal with supply chain impacts in the meantime. But today we are most acutely focused on the emergency operations underway and on the families that have been impacted. I have no doubt that we will rebuild together and that Baltimore will come back stronger than ever before.\par \par \uc2 And with that, I will turn it back over to the governor to lead media questions.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Governor?\par \par \uc2 MOORE: There's no new information about the search efforts that we know that we still have the six individuals who are missing.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Governor, when you talk about the investigative standpoint, how concerned were you if there were any safety violations on this ship or the track record of the owner operator? And, Mr. Secretary, if you could speak to that as well, do you have any concerns about that?\par \par \uc2 Mayor Scott, we also didn't hear from you. What are your thoughts on this tragedy today?\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Well, I'll take it first and I can pass off to Mr. Mayor.\par \par \uc2 I know there's a thorough investigation that's going to be going on about everything that took place last night, the things that led up to it, and also the aftermath. And so, I don't have any further comments about any concerns that we have about the companies that are involved because there's still a thorough investigation that's going to take place.\par \par \uc2 BRANDON SCOTT\uc2  (D), BALTIMORE MAYOR: Thank you, Mr. Governor. I think we all know this is an unspeakable tragedy. And while, as the governor just said the investigation is still underway. All of that can wait. Right now, this is about the lives of those individuals that we are searching for and nothing else. Everything else, this team of folks will work together to make sure that we rebuild and do everything we need to do for our port. But right now we need everyone to understand this is about the lives and those families who are deeply impacted.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Mr. Secretary, this is -- we've seen now several bridge collapses happened over the past couple of years. Obviously, this was a catastrophic event that that bridge fell very quickly. How concerned should Americans be about the bridges that they're traversing every single day in this country?\par \par \uc2 BUTTIGIEG: Well, this is a unique circumstance. I do not know of a bridge that has been constructed to withstand a direct impact from a vessel of this size. What I will say is anytime anything happens to any bridge, we as a country take that and learn from that. Learning from incidences diverse as what happened to I-95 in Philadelphia, what happened to I-10 in Los Angeles, or another case that we're learning a lot from here, which is the 2007 collapse of I-35 West in Minnesota.\par \par \uc2 Let me emphasize that was a very different circumstance with very different causes. And the \uc2 NTSB\uc2 , by design, is independently leading the investigation into what those causes are. No question that we will take all of that information and apply it in our future work.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Mr. Secretary, how long are you preparing for the shipping channel to be closed? And you mentioned supply chain., what else are you doing to make sure other ports are prepared to maybe take on some of that?\par \par \uc2 BUTTIGIEG: Well, the port here in Baltimore does the most vehicle handling of any port at all, and that's just talking about the vehicle side. You also have container traffic, you have bulk traffic. There is no question that this will be a major and protracted impact to supply chains. It's too soon to offer estimates on what it will take to clear the.\par \par \uc2 Channel and reopen the port. There is one part of the facilities here, Trade Point, which is located at Sparrow Point, which is outside of the part that's blocked. But the main part of the port of Baltimore is, of course, inside the channel that has been blocked.\par \par \uc2 This is part of why we're fortunate to have that newly stood up freight office, which along with the Maritime Administration and all of our interagency and intergovernmental partners here, is we're going to make sure we coordinate. There is no central authority that directs maritime traffic the way you have with air traffic, so it's going to be important to have a number of dialogues established with ocean shippers, beneficial cargo owners, port operators, and everybody else who plays a role here.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I'm sorry, governor, just for clarity, a question from this morning. It was unclear if there were any vehicles on the bridge when the vessel hit it. Do you have an update now whether there were actually vehicles?\par \par \uc2 MOORE: So there's still an investigation going on about which vehicles that we had -- that was on the bridge. The thing that we know that we also can verify, though, is the quick work of public officials and law enforcement who kept more vehicles from coming onto the bridge, they undoubtedly saved innumerable amount of lives last night.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You're still looking for six people and that's it as far as you know?\par \par \uc2 MOORE: That's -- our investigation has not changed on that.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Thank you all. Thank you all.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: (Inaudible).\par \par \uc2 MOORE: We'll come back to that.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Thank you all.\par \par \uc2 (END LIVE COVERAGE)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. Governor Westmore in Maryland wrapping up a news conference with Transportation Secretary \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2 , who just took a question about bridges and infrastructure. As you remember a couple of years ago, $850 billion was earmarked for infrastructure projects, which is an interesting thing to look back at and see where all that money went. But in this case, it appears he is correct in the early going to say that no bridge likely could have withstood that a cargo ship of this size just heading directly into the structural pylon that took down this bridge tragically.\par \par \uc2 And also, \uc2 Brandon Scott\uc2 , the mayor of Baltimore, rightly reminding everyone that there are six missing individuals whose families are grieving and deeply concerned at this hour as this rescue mission goes on. It is a difficult one to be sure. And we have spoken to a few Navy SEAL divers today who are not overly optimistic about those chances. But yet, obviously, the search continues and the hope continues that those six individuals who are working on potholes on the bridge overnight in the dark, on a cold evening in the middle of Baltimore can still be found. So we'll continue to stay on that story for you. We'll give you all the latest developments as we get them.\par \par \uc2 We also want to squeeze in here in the last couple of minutes that we have the Supreme Court hearing its first abortion case since overturning Roe v. Wade almost two years ago. It could restrict access to Mifepristone, a medication used in almost 60 percent of the abortions in the United States last year.\par \par \uc2 Think about that, not happening in clinics, 60 percent are happening with this drug, which can be mailed to someone's house. And you can see the numbers that have risen in the use of this drug since 2020. That was the first year of the pandemic. And that data comes to us from the Guttmacher Institute.\par \par \uc2 Fox News Sunday anchor and chief legal correspondent Shannon Bream reporting live at the Supreme Court on the latest on what the judges had to say in the back and forth here. Very interesting earlier today, Shannon, good to have you.\par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR AND CHIEF LEGAL CORRESPONDENT: It certainly was. Yes, Martha, good to see you.\par \par \uc2 So they spent so much time talking about whether or not the doctors, the pro-life group who brought this lawsuit actually have standing that I'm not sure they're going to get to the merits. But that question was about whether or not those doctors can show they had an injury and that this court is the place for them to get a remedy. There was a strong turnout by people on both sides of this argument in full force at the court this morning.\par \par \uc2 This isn't, though, about taking Mifepristone, that drug, off the market altogether. It's about whether the \uc2 FDA\uc2  properly took action when it rolled back some of the safety restrictions in 2016 and 2021. Things like having - - have those in person doctor visits, get a prescription and the fact that the prescribers no longer have to report adverse incidents, things like infections and severe bleeding unless they're fatal. Here's Justice Alito.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SAMUEL ALITO\uc2 , ASSOCIATE JUSTICE OF THE SUPREME COURT: You don't want to sell a product that causes very serious harm to the people who take your product, relying on your tests and the \uc2 FDA\uc2 's tests. Wouldn't you want that, that data?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But Justice Sotomayor was among those who weighed in, who said, listen, the \uc2 FDA\uc2  is the expert or agency, that's what they do. They take that into context along with a lot of other evidence, then they are the ones who make the decision. So Justice Jackson weighed in with a similar theme.\par \par \uc2 End of the day, they'll take a secret vote this Friday. We probably won't know until the end of June. And, Martha, just an interesting nugget, Senator \uc2 Josh Hawley\uc2  was here today with his two young sons because his wife was one of the lawyers arguing the case today. Back to you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Interesting. Thank you very much, Shannon Bream, at the Supreme Court this afternoon. And we'll keep an eye on that decision expected in June.\par \par \uc2 So this for you, breaking moments ago, former President Trump now under a gag order in his criminal case here in New York City. This is the one about the hush money payment to porn star Stormy Daniels says he did nothing wrong. The judge says the former president cannot make public statements about lawyers or court staffers and their families, also witnesses and jurors or potential jurors. He can talk about the judge and Manhattan DA \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2 , which I would expect he probably would given the history on all this.\par \par \uc2 That trial gets started April 15th. They're going to start jury selection in the next trial on the docket in the Trump cases. So we'll stay on top of that as well.\par \par \uc2 So thank you very much for joining us this afternoon. A lot of breaking news on "The Story" today on this Tuesday. But as always, "The Story" goes on. Thanks so much for joining us. We'll be back here tomorrow at 3:00. Dow is a bit in negative territory as we get ready to take you to Your World. We'll get the complete recap of the market action as all of this continues today.\par \par \uc2 And we had an RFK junior VP announcement for you today as well. So stick around for more on that. Thanks for joining us. We'll see you back here tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020240327ek3q00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Presidential Son A No-Show At Congressional Hearing; \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  Texas Trip Does...\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Nate Foy, Aishah Hasnie, Peter Doocy, David Spunt,  Brit Hume, Garrett Tenney, Griff Jenkins, Charles Hurt \par \uc2 7,766 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 March 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Presidential Son A No-Show At Congressional Hearing; \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  Texas Trip Does Not Include Border Visit; Senator Threatened Over Potential Vote On Ban Of TikTok; Texas Law Changes Deportation And Arrest Process; Sen. Tim Kaine (D-VA), Is Being Interviewed About The Importance Of The Stop Tranq Act; Sen. Ted Cruz (R-TX), Teaming Up With Sen. Tim Kaine Fighting The Growing Drug Abuse of Tranq; Hearing On Alleged Influence Peddling; Former Associates Testify At House Hearing; Necrotic Skin Wounds And Overdose Deaths; Sen. Ted Cruz (R-TX) And Sen.\par \par \uc2  Tim Kaine (D-VA) Interviewed On Bipartisan Efforts To Reduce Deaths Related To Tranq And Fentanyl And House Bill Potentially Banning TikTok; State Of Indiana Taking Measures Against Land Investments From Foreign Adversaries; Researchers Developing Drug That Provides Benefits Of Exercise Without Physical Effort; President Biden Attempting To Appeal To Latino Voters In Recent Campaign Events; Former Business Of \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  Testifies Before Congress\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL LEGAL ANALYST: She makes bets with men that she would be able to lift them and has made so much money doing so she calls it a great side hustle.\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Oh, my. Wow.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: There she goes.\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Imagine if a guy did that.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Greg, don't get any ideas.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: All right. Have a great night.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Just imagine. Okay, Greg. Thanks.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Good evening. I'm Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, the latest effort by House Republicans to press their case for impeaching President Biden. Today, lawmakers heard from a key \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  associate about influence peddling allegations. But the president's son was a no show. Democrats insist this is much ado about nothing.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent David Spunt starts us off tonight with the latest. Good evening, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Bret, good evening. This hearing just ended literally minutes ago, almost eight hours, with both Democrats and Republicans expressing anger and frustration, even some laughter. Moments after it began, the hearing turned into a proxy fight with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  squaring off.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TONY BOBULINSKI, \uc2 HUNTER BIDEN\uc2 'S FORMER BUSINESS PARTNER: Should I allow Hunter to give his opening statement first?\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 JAMES COMER\uc2  (R-KY): Well, doesn't appear Mr. Biden showed up for his public hearing. So, we'll recognize you Mr. Bobulinski.\par \par \uc2 BOBULINSKI: Thank you, Mr. Chairman.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (voice over): Tony Bobulinski, a former \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  business associate defiant, sitting next to an empty chair reserved for the president's son. The president himself, the focus of today's hearing on alleged influence peddling.\par \par \uc2 BOBULINSKI: \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was more than a participant in an a beneficiary of his family's business. He was an active aware enabler.\par \par \uc2 COMER\uc2 : This investigation is not about \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 . It's about \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and the lies he continues to tell the American people.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (voice over): Democrats also focusing on former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his family businesses, including dealings with Saudi Arabian interests by son-in-law and former White House aide \uc2 Jared Kushner\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 REP. CORI BUSH (D-MO): We all know the truth. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is the quintessential influence peddler-in-chief.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (voice over): Democrats witness \uc2 Lev Parnas\uc2 , a former \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  associate who spent months looking into an alleged Biden corruption scandal in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 LEV PARNAS, FORMER RUDY GIULIANI ASSOCIATE: In nearly a year traveling the world and interviewing officials in different countries, I found precisely zero evidence of the Biden's corruption in Ukraine. No credible sources ever provided proof of criminal activity.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (voice over): Parnas recently spent several months behind bars for campaign finance violations and wire fraud. Testifying remotely, Jason Galanis, a former Hunter business associate who is serving a 14-year federal sentence for securities fraud.\par \par \uc2 REP. STEPHEN LYNCH (D-MA): He's sitting in prison for scamming workers' pensions. I mean, how low can you get?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (on camera): The hearing is part of the overall impeachment probe into the president. Though sources tell Fox News there may be a growing GOP reluctance privately to take an actual impeachment vote on the House floor. Publicly Republicans plan to move forward with criminal referrals to the Justice Department, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: David, thanks. President Biden is on the second day of his Western campaign swing today in Arizona. He came bearing gifts in the form of grants and loans for semiconductor production. While back in Washington, the \uc2 EPA\uc2  unveiled a gift for environmentalists.\par \par \uc2 White House Correspondent Peter Doocy has details tonight live from the North Lawn. Good evening, Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret. This is going to be President Biden's second night in a row in a border state and White House officials tried to get a lot of local news coverage when they go on trips like this. I just checked the websites for multiple local outlets in Dallas. They've all got stories on the homepage about the border but President Biden does not plan to go.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: A clean energy boom.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): And today, an olive branch to labor unions whose members build cars and environmentalists, a new requirement for more than half of new vehicle sales to be electric by model year 2032.\par \par \uc2 The president says, three years ago, I set an ambitious target that half of all new cars and trucks sold in 2030 would be zero emission and we'll meet my goal for 2030 and race forward in the years ahead. You have my word.\par \par \uc2 That is being panned as a, "reckless and ill-informed rule" by Senator Joe Manchin, a Democrat and many Republicans.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We're now telling the consumer what to buy.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): The president's influence over allies may be waning because this week, President Biden asked Israel to stay out of Rafah, where the White House says more than a million refugees are stuck until plans to move them are finished. But Israel may not want to wait.\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2 , ISRAELI PRIME MINISTER (through translator): I have already approved the \uc2 IDF\uc2 's operational plan.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): Republicans argue President Biden's priority in Israel is reversing uncommitted votes in swing states.\par \par \uc2 REP. STEVE SCALISE (R-LA): And when \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  talks about a two state solution, seems like the only thing that he's really interested in are Michigan and Nevada.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): Foreign policy almost entirely missing from the president's remarks today. He stuck to traditional election issues, at one point comparing prescription drug prices in the U.S. to Iraq.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Jump on Air Force One without fly to any country. Any major city in the world from Toronto, to Berlin, to Baghdad, anywhere around the world. You'll be able to buy it for 40 to 60 percent less. That's wrong.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (on camera): President Biden got a big endorsement tonight, the 850,000 members strong united steelworkers union, they say they were thinking about this for months. But it comes just a week after President Biden said he did not want a Japanese company to buy \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2 . President Biden said he wanted to show the U.S. steel workers that he had their backs, now they have his back and this union's headquarters is in Pennsylvania, which is the jewel of President Biden's blue wall electoral strategy, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Peter Doocy live in the North Lawn. Peter, thanks.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  keeps its key interest rate unchanged but says three cuts are coming this year. All three major Wall Street markets had record closings today. They liked that news. The Dow surging 401, the S&P 500 was up 46, the NASDAQ jumped 203.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, we're getting a look, a close look at just how seriously some fans of the social media app TikTok are taking the congressional effort that could result in its banishment. Congressional Correspondent Aishah Hasnie is on Capitol Hill this evening with this story. Good evening, Aishah.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Good evening to you, Bret. You know, we've been hearing a lot of stories from Senate offices about some TikTok users calling in threatening these senators.\par \par \uc2 Well, tonight, Senator Tom Tillis is actually releasing audio of a voicemail he received from what appears to be a young woman threatening to kill him if he bans TikTok, listen to this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: OK, listen. If you ban TikTok, I will find you and shoot you. That's people's jobs. And that's my only entertainment. Anyways, I'll shoot you and find you and cut you into pieces. Bye.\par \par \uc2 SEN. THOM TILLIS (R-NC): Does it actually help their case? It hurts their case. Shame on TikTok for not discouraging it.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice over): So, TikTok is responding to this tonight, posting on X, threats like this are unacceptable and we condemn this in the strongest possible terms, adding that users who do want to speak out should do it in a respectful manner.\par \par \uc2 Right now, the Senate is moving cautiously on that House bill which requires the app divorce its Chinese owner ByteDance or face a ban.\par \par \uc2 Today, senators got a classified briefing on TikTok and we could soon see CEO \uc2 Shou Chew\uc2  on the Hill again, as Chairwoman \uc2 Maria Cantwell\uc2  of the Commerce Committee says she wants a TikTok hearing too. Her concern is whether the bill can hold up against lawsuits.\par \par \uc2 MARIA CANTWELL\uc2 , CHAIRWOMAN, SENATE COMMERCE COMMITTEE: The whole issue is constitutionality making sure it upholds in court. We want our government to have a very strong tool to stop nefarious actions. So, the question is how do we get that? The House has one proposal, we're going to look at all of these.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (on camera): But Bret, nobody, not even Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  is giving anyone any clues about when we could finally see some action on the floor. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Aishah Hasnie live on the Hill. Aishah, thanks.\par \par \uc2 The Texas law allowing state officials to arrest suspected illegal immigrants is back on hold tonight. A federal appeals court is preventing enforcement following a ruling by the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  that ended a temporary stay. We're following all the back and forth, the legal wranglings here. Correspondent Nate Foy is in Eagle Pass, Texas tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 NATE FOY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Today, Texas made its case in court to enforce Senate Bill 4 now. The law allows Texas to arrest and deport migrants without referring them to Border Patrol.\par \par \uc2 AARON NIELSON, TEXAS SOLICITOR GENERAL: Texas has a right to defend itself at least some applications of S.B. 4 constitutional.\par \par \uc2 FOY (voice over): A district court initially blocked the law from taking effect, a decision the federal government agrees with.\par \par \uc2 DANIEL TENNY, U.S. DOJ ATTORNEY: All the district court did was preserved the federal system that's been in effect for 150 years.\par \par \uc2 FOY (voice over): The Biden administration argues the law interferes with its federal authority over our borders as the Supreme Court found in several provisions of a 2012 Arizona law.\par \par \uc2 Texas argues its law is different because S.B. 4 mirrors rather than contradicts federal law. Congressman Tony Gonzalez says this legal back and forth battle is forcing Americans to suffer immediate consequences.\par \par \uc2 REP. TONY GONZALES (R-TX): If the Biden administration spent as much time trying to fix the border as they are trying to block things from happening. Americans will be a whole lot safer for that.\par \par \uc2 FOY (voice over): Texas Governor Greg Abbott calls it an invasion.\par \par \uc2 GOV. GREG ABBOTT (R-TX): How many terrorists are roaming the United States of America as we speak? The answer is, no one knows.\par \par \uc2 FOY (voice over): Right now, illegal crossings in Texas are down as numbers skyrocket in America's west with Arizona's Tucson sector leading the way. And several large migrant groups crossing into Yucaipa (ph), California today.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 FOY (on camera): Those illegal crossings out west expected to skyrocket even more if S.B. 4 goes into effect.\par \par \uc2 Right now, we are awaiting a decision from the three judge panel on if Texas can enforce the law over the next two weeks before the federal appeals court hears arguments about the constitutionality of the law coming up on April 3rd, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Nate Foy in Eagle Pass. Nate, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, Brit Hume as analysis of today's big stories and yesterday's primary results.\par \par \uc2 First, Beyond our Borders tonight. Irish Prime Minister \uc2 Leo Varadkar\uc2  says he is stepping down for reasons he described as both personal and political. Varadkar made history as this country's first gay and first biracial leader. He will be replaced as prime minister in April after a party leadership contest.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine's emergency service as one person was killed and five wounded in a Russian strike in Kharkiv city. An industrial building caught fire as a result of that strike. Rescue workers were searching for survivors under the debris.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at London, one of the big stories there tonight. Britain's Foreign Secretary \uc2 David Cameron\uc2  and his Thai counterparts signed a strategic partnership agreement in Bangkok. The two countries will promote comprehensive cooperation in eight key areas including trade and investment, defense and security and a green economy.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories Beyond our Borders tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: President Biden and former President Trump easily won their presidential preference elections in Arizona yesterday. Notably, on the Republican side, former candidate \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  received about 18-1/2 percent of the vote. She dropped out of the race two weeks ago.\par \par \uc2 And Cleveland businessman Bernie Moreno won the Republican Senate primary in Ohio. He was endorsed by former President Trump. Moreno will take on Democratic incumbent Senator \uc2 Sherrod Brown\uc2 , considered by many as vulnerable.\par \par \uc2 The judge overseeing the Georgia election interference case against former President Trump has cleared the way for an appeal of his ruling, allowing the prosecutor to remain on the team. The judge granted the request by defense attorneys seeking permission to ask the Georgia Court of Appeals to review that ruling.\par \par \uc2 Last week the judge denied the defense's request to disqualify Fulton County District Attorney \uc2 Fani Willis\uc2  or dismiss the indictment over her romantic relationship with special prosecutor Nathan Wade. The judge saying, Willis can remain on the case as long as Wade resigns, which he did.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. ALEXANDRIA OCASIO-CORTEZ (D-NY): Did you witness the president commits a crime? Is it your testimony today?\par \par \uc2 TONY BOBULINSKI, FORMER ASSOCIATE OF \uc2 HUNTER BIDEN\uc2 : Yes.\par \par \uc2 OCASIO-CORTEZ: And what crime?\par \par \uc2 BOBULINSKI: Corruption statutes, RICO and (INAUDIBLE)\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 OCASIO-CORTEZ: What is it? What is -- what is the crime?\par \par \uc2 BOBULINSKI: I answered the question.\par \par \uc2 OCASIO-CORTEZ: No --\par \par \uc2 BOBULINSKI: RICO, you're obviously not familiar with.\par \par \uc2 OCASIO-CORTEZ: RICO is not a crime. It is a category. What is the crime?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Oh, no.\par \par \uc2 BOBULINSKI: If category of crimes, then you then charged --\par \par \uc2 OCASIO-CORTEZ: You have --\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That was fiery at times today in that House hearing. Tony Bobulinski, and there you see, \uc2 Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez\uc2 , the congresswoman from New York.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in Fox News chief political analyst, Britt Hume.\par \par \uc2 Brit, I know you saw the hearing. What was your take on that exchange and others?\par \par \uc2 BRIT HUME, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, not long after the part, we just heard there. \uc2 Alexandria Ocasio Cortez\uc2  was reclaiming her time, which is what you see happen on Capitol Hill sometimes when a witness is testifying and being questioned and the questioner, the member of Congress doesn't particularly like the answer, or wanted to -- or want to hear the witness explain his or her answer, so that you reclaim your time, which is a funny way to think to do when your time is supposed to be for you to ask questions and a witness to give answers.\par \par \uc2 But that happens all the time. I think this testimony and this hearing today by Tony Bobulinski gives an example of the fact that he, Bobulinski, more than almost any other witness has established that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  certainly knew what his son's business dealings were and knew many of his business associates.\par \par \uc2 And the idea, as Biden used to say, that he never discussed his business dealings with his son. I think that's been pretty well established to be not true.\par \par \uc2 The larger question, of course, Bret is we know is that, you know, you have to establish not just the \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's family benefited from influence peddling by his family, which was certainly ongoing, but that he personally did. That link has not yet been connected. But there is always been this larger question to, of how do \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  or a senator and later vice president's salary got to be so rich that he had this fabulous place up this spread of his compound really up in Wilmington, Delaware.\par \par \uc2 And of the house at Rehoboth Beach -- he had a very nice house at Rehoboth Beach. And he clearly lives the life of a rich man. He'd made some money speaking certainly after he's out of office for a while.\par \par \uc2 But the question remains and the committee investigating him hasn't completely answered it in a way that proves that he took money improperly.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. Chairman Comer is inviting President Biden to testify as well. I doubt that's going anywhere. But that's coming out apparently, tonight.\par \par \uc2 Turning topics now to the issue of abortion on the general election, Brit.\par \par \uc2 WABC Radio\uc2 , former President Trump suggests that he supports a national ban at 15 weeks. "The number of weeks now, people are agreeing on 15. And I'm thinking in terms of that. And it will come down to something that's very reasonable. But people are really, even hardliners are agreeing seems to be 15 weeks seems to be a number that people are agreeing at."\par \par \uc2 The Biden campaign put out a statement, saying after overturning Roe, Trump is making a national abortion ban, a hallmark of his campaign for the presidency. Your thoughts on this, the issue and how it plays.\par \par \uc2 HUME: Well, it was certainly an issue that Republicans have struggled with since the reversal of Roe versus Wade. And in some places, you know, you have six-week bans. The country is not with the Republicans any kind of a complete ban and may not be with him with the Republicans on a six-week ban.\par \par \uc2 But 15 weeks is a different story. And Republicans are grappling and groping for a way to speak to this issue, because Democrats, are great -- many of them, although they talk about what Republicans are trying to do themselves in many cases are for no restrictions whatsoever. That is not a majority position either.\par \par \uc2 So, I think as you see Republicans moving in this direction, you can see \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  doing the same thing. And I think he recognizes this as an issue that has hurt Republicans.\par \par \uc2 It is not particularly high on the current list of priorities for most people in the polling. But it is one that Republicans need to find a way to talk to because it's hurt him in the past and was 15-weeks they may be closer to the mark if that's where it ends up being full Republican position of closer to the mark than anywhere they have been before, where they have been struggling to find something to say.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. 15 weeks matches a lot of European countries as well. Brit, as always, Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, one Democrat, one Republican, we tried to find "COMMON GROUND" on bipartisan efforts to get rid of fentanyl and the new drug, tranq.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It is time for our "COMMON GROUND" segment.\par \par \uc2 Joining us this evening, Virginia Democratic Senator Tim Kaine and Texas Republican Senator Ted Cruz. They're working together on bipartisan legislation to combat illicit drugs such as tranq and fentanyl. Senators, thanks for being here.\par \par \uc2 Senator Kaine, first to you. The Stop Tranq Act. It is among other things to add this drug, xylazine to global tracking of fentanyl, cocaine, and heroin. "State Department threat assessments of international drug trade trends and resources to hold countries accountable." Why is this important?\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM KAINE (D-VA): Bret, we do a report every year that the state department does that really shines the light on what nations around the world are doing to stop drug trafficking.\par \par \uc2 Right now, the focus has really been on fentanyl and some other important drugs that get trafficked. But this uptick in the use of xylazine, a tranquillizer that was developed for veterinary uses that has serious health consequences for people. That's being trafficked as well, and Ted and I -- and we both worked on tranq-related legislation on the past.\par \par \uc2 We want to add, tranq, xylazine to this International Narcotics Control Strategy Report, so that we shine a spotlight on the bad actors, and then, the Congress can hopefully take efforts against them.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Senator Cruz, how does this happen? Like in the Senate? Do you just go to each other and say, this is a good idea? How does it physically the process happen of getting together on this bipartisan deal?\par \par \uc2 SEN. TED CRUZ (R-TX): Well, listen, the underlying substance, tranq is a real problem. It's a -- it's a growing drug abuse. You know, we had last year over 100,000 Americans die of drug overdoses. And tranq is rising. It's called a zombie drug.\par \par \uc2 And then, so, Tim and I have teamed up together to say, let's get ahead of this, let's stop young people from taking this drug and losing their lives. But in terms of how it happens, I'll tell you a dirty little secret, which is that Tim and I actually really like each other.\par \par \uc2 KAINE: Yes, we do.\par \par \uc2 CRUZ: And we do and we work together a lot. We were both elected on the same day in 2012. And it's -- Tim, I'm sorry for saying that I may hurt you in the Democrat primary in Virginia.\par \par \uc2 But Tim and I have teamed up on a -- on a lot of things over the last 12 years. I'll tell you, one of my favorite things that Tim and I did together was when there were comments in the House of Representatives that were really anti-Semitic, and troublesome, and the House try to pass a resolution condemning antisemitism, and it couldn't. Tim and I sat down, and we said look, can the Senate do better? And we drafted together a resolution that was a clear and unequivocal condemnation of antisemitism, and it ended up passing unanimously on the floor of the Senate. I think that was really important to show that not everything is partisan and we can come together on issues that are really important.\par \par \uc2 KAINE: We do a lot more together than people know. Frankly, Bret, cooperation isn't sexy. So, when we do stuff together, it's not likely to get the attention as when we have disagreements. But this is one. Look, the effect that this tranq epidemic is having on Texas and Virginia, you could have any senator stand here and they'd tell you the same thing. They are really worried about it.\par \par \uc2 And one thing in particular, tranq get cut into fentanyl. You often help somebody out of a fentanyl overdose with Narcan. But Narcan doesn't help mitigate the consequences of tranq. So you see somebody and they are in an overdose and you think Narcan is going to save their life. Narcan doesn't hurt people that have taken tranq, but it also doesn't help them, doesn't save them. So this is a really serious problem.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I want to play this soundbite from another Virginia Senator, Mark Warner, about TikTok. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARK WARNER, (D-VA): This is not about trying to get rid of the creativity. This is not about trying to eliminate you're deleting money if you are a social influencer. It's about the ability to make sure that this creativity that's happening on this platform can't be manipulated by the \uc2 Communist Party of China\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Senator Kaine, I have read that you've said the devil is in the details on this bill and you haven't really fully committed to it. Where are you on the TikTok divesture from where it is and away from the CCP? And do you feel the pressure that other senators have talked about publicly?\par \par \uc2 KAINE: I'm going to look at the House bill as soon as we get this headache- inducing appropriations bill off our shoulders, hopefully by the end of the week. I will take a look at that House bill. Ted is on the Commerce Committee. That bill would go through Commerce, and I suspect there may even be some effort to take a look at the House bill, the Senate bill, take the best of both, come up with the right balance.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Senator Cruz, you were in that briefing, the national security briefing.\par \par \uc2 CRUZ: Yes. Look, I'm very concerned about TikTok, really on two different levels. Number one, the threat it poses of espionage and surveillance of the Chinese Communist Party monitoring -- 170 million Americans use TikTok, and they have the ability to monitor what they are saying, what they are doing on their phones, where they are. I think it's a real privacy risk to Americans who may not realize that the Chinese government has that ability.\par \par \uc2 Secondly, I'm deeply concerned about the Chinese Communist Party using TikTok to push propaganda, to push propaganda that it's supporting. We have seen lots of instances, number one, of their pushing propaganda in support of the Chinese Communist Party, suppressing information about Tiananmen Square, about Hong Kong, about Tibet. And on top of that, they are pushing all sorts of harmful garbage to our kids, self-harm, cutting. It's amazing.\par \par \uc2 TikTok actually has a comparable product that they market in China. And in China they limit the hours their kids can use it, and they push videos on math and science and education. They are trying to have their students study and be really effective. Here they are pushing our kids to chew Tide pods.\par \par \uc2 What I think will happen is the Commerce Committee will set it for a markup. We'll consider it on the merits. I hope we will have a full and open amendment process. I'm not convinced the language the House passed is exactly what it will be in law. But I'm glad the House is acting to address this very serious threat.\par \par \uc2 And one thing to understand is this bill is not about banning TikTok. For the people who use TikTok and enjoy it, this is not about getting rid of that. What it is about is forcing China to divest, getting TikTok out of control of the Chinese Communist Party, getting it in the hands of an American company, getting it in the hands of anyone other than China or an enemy of America I think is very important and will benefit the users of TikTok and everyone else, too.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: This is not common ground here. But this is March Madness.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Senator Kaine, you've got Virginia's James Madison Dukes, it looks like, in the tournament, and you have a lot more competitors from Texas. You've got the Texas Longhorns, number one Houston Cougars, Texas Tech Red Raiders, Texas A&M Aggies, TCU Horned Frogs, and Baylor Bears. Is there some betting going on there?\par \par \uc2 KAINE: UVA lost even in the play in game last night. We have James Madison and Longwood. I would not bet much money with my Virginia teams against Houston or any of the Texas teams.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That's pretty good for you Senator Cruz.\par \par \uc2 CRUZ: So I will say, Tim and I have a long history of sports betting. And the world series when the Astros played the Nats, we had a wager. Tragically we lost that series, and so I had to show up with Texas barbecue and Shiner Bock beer. And even worse I had to wear a Nats jersey. I won two of those bets over the last few years, but that one I didn't win. This time I feel confident Texas going to do better than Virginia in the tournament. I will tell you my Final Four. I have \uc2 UConn\uc2  and Baylor against \uc2 University of Houston\uc2  and Texas. It's kind of a homer bracket.\par \par \uc2 KAINE: Wow.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I think so.\par \par \uc2 CRUZ: And my finals is Houston against Baylor. And I'm predicting U of H, go cougs, to win it all.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, well, Senators, thanks so much. We appreciate you coming on.\par \par \uc2 KAINE: Bret, thanks for doing this.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Pass the word about "Common Ground." Thank you.\par \par \uc2 You can see all of the segments, "Common Ground" segments on my podcast "Common Ground." Find that and the "All-Star Panel" podcast under the Bret Baier podcasts at FOXNewspodcast.com, \uc2 Spotify\uc2 , or wherever you download podcasts. You can see the discussion, the full discussion on the FOX News \uc2 YouTube\uc2  page.\par \par \uc2 Up next, one Midwestern state is saying thanks but no thanks to investment from China. Plus, all the benefits of exercise without actually doing it -- sounds impossible. Well, maybe not. But would you take this pill?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Is that a trick question? Of course. Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: No, I need to sweat every morning.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: No. I enjoy working out.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: State Department officials say they have evacuated more than 15 U.S. citizens from Haiti via helicopter today. The State Department saying they were taken to the Dominican Republic. The department estimates 30 U.S. citizens will be able to depart this way each day during this effort.\par \par \uc2 Charges have been dropped against the father of an American serviceman killed during the withdrawal of the U.S. troops from Afghanistan. Steve Nikoui yelled "Abbey Gate, Abbey Gate!" during President Biden's State of the Union address. That was a reference to where his son and 12 other troops were killed. \uc2 Capitol police\uc2  removed him from the chamber and then arrested him. The D.C. attorney general dropped the charges after they were challenged by various Republican lawmakers.\par \par \uc2 The state of Indiana is pushing back on investments from foreign adversaries. It's prohibiting countries such as China and Russia from buying or leasing farmland. Correspondent Garrett Tenney in is in Valparaiso, Indiana, tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 REP. DAVID SCOTT, (D-GA): We have got to protect our farmland and not get it in the hands of foreign interests.\par \par \uc2 GOV. KRISTI NOEM, (R) SOUTH DAKOTA: When America can't feed itself and we rely on other member of another country to feed us, it becomes a national security issue.\par \par \uc2 GARRETT TENNEY, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Bipartisan warnings on Capitol Hill today over the threat to the U.S. food supply as China tries to buy up large amounts of American farmland. While Washington mulls its own legislation, a growing number of states are taking action, including Indiana, which just became the 24th state to restrict the sale of farmland to America's adversaries, namely Russia, North Korea, Iran, Venezuela, Cuba, and most importantly, they say, China.\par \par \uc2 JEAN LEISING, (R) INDIANA STATE SENATOR: Especially the Chinese pick very strategic locations.\par \par \uc2 TENNEY: While crafting the bill, Indiana State Senator Jean Leising says lawmakers learned that companies with ties to the Chinese Communist Party were in active negotiations on at least 11 deals for farmland in the state, and that some sites could have posed a security threat, including one where two major railroads intersect and another over a huge aquifer that's a key source of water supply.\par \par \uc2 LEISING: To me that is a big warning that what are we doing in Indiana.\par \par \uc2 TENNEY: One of the Chinese companies trying to buy up strategic farmland in Indiana, the Fufeng Group, made headlines last year when it bought hundreds of acres of farmland in North Dakota just 12 miles from Grand Forks Air Force Base, a move that Air Force and national security officials said posed a significant national security concern.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 TENNEY (on camera): The latest figures from the \uc2 USDA\uc2  show Chinese now owns close to 400,000 acres of American farmland that we know of. That is less than a tenth of a percent of all farmland in the U.S. But lawmakers believe China likely owns a lot more than that. That is why they are pushing for federal regulation to both track and limit these kinds of sales. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Garrett Tenney in Indiana. Garrett, thanks.\par \par \uc2 So how would you like to have the benefits of exercising without the effort? Sounds too good to be true. Scientists are actually working on a pill that might let you do that, apparently. Correspondent Griff Jenkins has details tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I need to sweat every morning or my day is not off to a good start.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes, I will take the pill.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 GRIFF JENKINS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Researchers at the Washington University School of Medicine in St. Louis say an exercise pill may soon offer the same benefits as a workout, using drug compounds that mimic muscle building and physical endurance.\par \par \uc2 BAHAA ELGENDY, WASHINGTON UNIVERSITY IN ST. LOUIS: It boosts natural metabolic pathways that typical respond to exercise. So in effect, the drugs makes the body act like it's training while it's not training.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: Lead professor Bahaa Elgendy says his team has been working for a decade to find a solution to help those who have difficulty being active.\par \par \uc2 ELGENDY: People with physical limitations who cannot move, who cannot exercise, people who are very old age.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: But it may also appeal to those who don't have time to work out or simply want to skip the sweat.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: If somebody convinced me that it was healthy, not going to hurt my body, and not going to cost me a small fortune.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: Others say they depend on exercise for their well-being.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: For me it's primarily the mental health benefits and de-stressing from work and school.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The endorphins that are released when you run, I guess it's just like the feel good emotion.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: Even researchers agree it's not time to put away the sneakers or give up your gym membership.\par \par \uc2 ELGENDY: Exercise has so many benefits on body and mind. I don't think it will replace the fitness industry or anything.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS (on camera): And the professor is hopeful that this future exercise drug could replace weight loss pills, which he says causes muscle loss.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The fun is the challenge of it, so pushing yourself mentally. I don't think you could do that with a pill.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 JENKINS (on camera): However, the wait is going to be a little longer. Researchers say it will be several more years before they can begin clinical trials or human testing, so no skipping that work out today. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, Griff, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel on the presidential race and the latest effort on impeachment up on the Hill.\par \par \uc2 First, here's what some of our FOX affiliates around this country are covering tonight. FOX 5 in New York as a U.S. marine veteran pleads not guilty to charges stemming from the death of a man on the subway last year. Daniel Penny is charged with second degree manslaughter and criminally negligent homicide. His lawyers say Penny acted in self-defense after Jordan Neely began harassing passengers.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Raleigh, North Carolina. One of the big stories there from WRAL, our affiliate, the American Kennel Club names French bulldogs the most popular breed for U.S. dog owners. The dogs remain the most commonly registered purebreds last year. After Frenchies, the most common breeds were labs, golden retrievers, German shepherds, and poodles.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look outside the beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Wages are up more than prices. Inflation is down dramatically. Right here in Arizona, we have created 300,000 new jobs.\par \par \uc2 MONET FLORES BACS, ARIZONA VOTER: Despite what President Biden might want us to believe, the Latino community in Arizona is really hurting due to inflation.\par \par \uc2 REP. VERONICA ESCOBAR, (D-TX) BIDEN CAMPAIGN CO-CHAIR: It is still early. And what is going to be important is our direct engagement.\par \par \uc2 KARL ROVE, FORMER \uc2 GEORGE W. BUSH\uc2  SENIOR ADVISER: The Democrats have got problems with this emerging political power of the Latino communities, who are entrepreneurial, pro-faith, pro-family, and pro-military.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, President Biden on the second day of his western swing, just landed in Dallas moments ago. He is making an appeal directly to Latino voters. "The Wall Street Journal" writes it this way, "Why Democrats are losing their grip on Latino voters. The problem \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and his party face this year is growing number of Hispanics and other nonwhite working class voters view Democrats as out of touch. White voters without a college degree have been quitting the Democratic Party in earnest since the \uc2 Obama\uc2  presidency. Democrats didn't expect to see Hispanics follow. Few predict that the GOP will win a majority of Latino vote this November, but these inroads have smart Democrats terrified."\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in our panel, former \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2  communications director Douglas Heye, Olivia Beavers, congressional reporter for "Politico," and \uc2 Charles Hurt\uc2 , opinion editor for "The Washington Times". Charlie, it is really interesting to listen to the president on this trip, how much focus he's putting on Latino voters. It almost seems like there is a red alert, an alarm that has gone off to every speech focus that way.\par \par \uc2 CHARLES HURT\uc2 , FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Well, certainly I think President Biden is one of those Democrats who is rightly terrified about what they are seeing. And, you know, it's kind of interesting to look at it from the Republican standpoint. For decades we heard from Republicans that the only way to make inroads with the Hispanic community was with amnesty and other bills that were not -- other efforts that were not popular among a broader audience of Republicans.\par \par \uc2 But, in truth, what actually brought -- is bringing Hispanic voters to the Republican side are all the things that matter to everybody else, which is a good economy, enforce laws, and a secure border. And what's really interesting when you get into these polls is when you look at the support for having secure borders among Hispanic voters, it's in some cases higher than it is for the general population of voters. So, I think it's really kind of a fascinating turn of events here, and President Biden is wise to be worried about it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, here he is on \uc2 Univision Radio\uc2 , very specific about the former president.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: He separated kids and parents at the border, then he caged the children. He's planning mass deportations of literally several million people who are here in the country, several million people. He wants to end birthright citizenship. This guy despises Latinos. And I understand Latino values.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Doug, "this guy despises Latinos," that's pretty bold.\par \par \uc2 DOUGLAS HEYE, FORMER REPUBLICAN NATIONAL COMMITTEE COMMUNICATIONS DIRECTOR: We hear so often about unfortunate language that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  makes. We don't hear as much as we should have some of the things that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  says that really go over that line as well.\par \par \uc2 And it demonstrates, I think, the fear that we see within the Biden campaign. Look, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  should be in these areas. He should have been at the border much earlier. Obviously, we could talk about policy as well. He is acting right now out of very real political fear, which makes sense. This also should be an opportunity for Republicans, not just the Trump campaign, but House and Senate and gubernatorial candidates to target Hispanic media to really have a communications dialogue with Hispanic voters. And on those issues that they care about, yes, immigration is one of them, the border is one of them. But jobs and the economy, what things cost, they go grocery stores, too, the same ones that we do. And they are just as expensive. And that's why these numbers for Biden, I think, have been so bad.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: A lot of focus on the Hill today, Olivia, on this hearing with Tony Bobulinski. Take another listen to the House panel in this Biden impeachment hearing.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TONY BOBULINSKI: It is clear to me that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was the brand being sold by the Biden family.\par \par \uc2 REP. JIM COMER, (R-KY) HOUSE OVERSIGHT COMMITTEE CHAIRMAN: There's no other explanation. Either \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is complicit, or \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is incompetent.\par \par \uc2 REP. ALEXANDRIA OCASIO-CORTEZ, (D-NY): Name the crime. Did you watch him steal something?\par \par \uc2 BOBULINSKI: Corruption statutes, RICO.\par \par \uc2 OCASIO-CORTEZ: RICO is not a crime. It is a category. What is the --\par \par \uc2 BOBULINSKI: Oh, no. It's a category of crimes that you are then charged under.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So a lot about there is nothing to this from the Democratic side. You know, if you look at the polls, Olivia, the Reuters-Ipsos poll out about the president, should he be impeached from benefiting from \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's business dealings, agree 35 percent, disagree 42 percent. I don't think there's a lot of people who, besides watching FOX's coverage and a few other news organizations, who have a complete sense of where this is going or the complete testimony of somebody like Tony Bobulinski.\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA BEAVERS, "POLITICO": Talking to Republicans, we were at the Greenbrier for a GOP retreat recently. We were asking GOP leaders what about impeachment of the president? And they didn't really seem to give direct answers, just saying we're going to go where the investigation leads us. But if you talk to Republicans more privately, they basically seem to be acknowledging that they will not be pursuing an impeachment.\par \par \uc2 This hearing was particularly fiery. But you do have contradictory sort of testimony from different former business associates saying that the president was or was not involved with \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's business dealings, which is why you saw chairman comer decide to invite President Biden himself to come testify so that they could clear up, quote, discrepancies. And you might not be too surprised to know that the president's spokesperson has basically declined that offer.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, didn't expect that to come to pass. Charlie, what do you make of all of this?\par \par \uc2 HURT: Whether it's incompetence on the part of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  or corruption, the fact is none of this is good for him. It certainly smells like an influencing peddling scheme that has gone on under his watch while he was vice president, and that's a terrible thing for going into an election, having to defend against that.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. And Doug, quickly, do Republicans have to deliver? Do they have to get to the check that clears the bank account, or is this working at some level?\par \par \uc2 HEYE: For impeachment, yes. But the reality is any day that \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  is in the news, it's a bad day for the Biden campaign. It really muddies the waters of what they have tried to do for years in talking about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's business dealings. There's a lot of smoke here. We don't know there's fire, but there's so much smoke.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK. Panel, thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 HEYE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And finally tonight, a SPECIAL REPORT salute.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It was a great airplane right there to fly. That's the best we had, I always thought.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Paul Crawford is a World War II veteran who celebrated his 100th birthday yesterday by flying a P-51 Mustang fighter jet over his neighborhood in Georgia. How cool is that? Crawford piloted the Mustang more than 70 years ago in China. His family and friends say he flew almost 30 missions before he was shot down, celebrated 100 by flying it again this week. Congratulations.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT, Democratic Senator Chris Coons on federal government spending priorities and foreign policy hot spots for President Biden. We'll talk about the campaign well.\par \par \uc2 Remember, if you can't catch us live, set your DVR, 6:00 p.m. in the east, 3:00 p.m. on the west coast.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight and every night. That's it for this SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and still unafraid. THE INGRAHAM ANGLE with Laura starts now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020240321ek3k00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The Left Loses It Over Trump's "Bloodbath" Comment\b0\par\par\uc2 Sean Hannity, Charlie Hurt, Tomi Lahren, Kayleigh McEnany, Tammy  Bruce, Jesse Watters \par \uc2 8,065 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 March 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Hannity\par \uc2 SHAN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SEAN HANNITY\uc2 , FOX NEWS HOST: And welcome to "Hannity" this Monday night.\par \par \uc2 Wow, a lot of news tonight. Tonight, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  reportedly anxious and angry over his re-election prospects. Why? And poll after poll after poll -- well, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is beating the current president. Demographically, Joe is losing huge parts of his base. He's losing huge numbers with African- Americans, Hispanic Americans, young people, suburban moms, they're leaving him.\par \par \uc2 And now, Democrats, the Biden campaign, the mob and the media, they are all desperate and I mean desperate. Now some would even say that -- well, the left is bracing for a political bloodbath which is why they are desperately now praying that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  spends the next 231 days in courtrooms all across America in the hopes that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's weaponized Department of Justice can help Joey win the day.\par \par \uc2 But whatever you do, just don't call it a bloodbath, or Democrats, they will accuse you of fomenting violence, the same people that said those 574 riots in the summer of 2020 that killed dozens of Americans, injured thousands of cops, pelted with bricks, rocks, bottles, Molotov cocktails and caused billions of property damage, they claimed they were mostly peaceful.\par \par \uc2 Now, more desperation on their part -- over the weekend, Trump used the phrase to describe the potential economic fallout of a second \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  term. But according to the left and their allies in the mob and the media, Trump committed a mortal sin with a threatening call to arms, and what is now even a deleted post our old friend liberal Joe Scarborough breathlessly stating following, quote, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's America and he is proud of it promised, another bloodbath if he loses again.\par \par \uc2 You mean like the riots in 2020 that liberals said were mostly peaceful?\par \par \uc2 Anyway, now, this is just the tip of the iceberg. But before we get to all the insane hysteria and we've got great montages tonight and the blatant hypocrisy, let's just listen to former President Trump's full remarks. Let's actually play them in context in his own words, no editing. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT & 2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Let me tell you something to China, if you're listening President Xi and you and I are friends, but he understands the way I deal, those big monster car manufacturing plants that you're building in Mexico right now and you think you're going to get that, you're going to not hire Americans and you're going to sell the cars to us, no, we're going to put a 100 percent tariff on every single car that comes across the line. And you're not going to be able to sell those cars, if I get elected.\par \par \uc2 Now if I don't get elected, it's going to be a blood bath for the whole -- that's going to be the least of it. It's going to be a blood bath for the country. That'll be the least of it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: And he talks about cars again. He was talking about the cars and the auto industry. You just heard the speech, the president was discussing the automobile industry. If you have a brain -- and brain and any intellectual honesty, you will admit that and acknowledge that.\par \par \uc2 But let's see how the Democrats and the lying corrupt media mob, their best friends and their chief donors characterize these remarks. This should not shock you, but take a look anyway.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: One specific remark from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's rally on Saturday has set off a political firestorm but is it possible the firestorm is missing what should be of real concern?\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2  (D-CA): We have -- we just have to win this election because he's even predicting a bloodbath. What does that mean? He's going to exact a bloodbath? There's something wrong here.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: When the former president who's already incited violence among his followers says that there's going to be a bloodbath, what, after the election if he does not win, he is telling us what he is going to do.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: A major party candidate is saying you elect me, there's going to be dictatorship, bloodbath, violence, retribution against my political enemies, that equals what we saw in Italy and Germany and other places.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: If you look at the way he said this, it's going to be a bloodbath for the whole -- then he stops that's going to be the least of it, that's going to be the least of it. It's going to be a bloodbath for the country.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Obviously he's talking about a bloodbath for America. It's laid out in the terms of it and these idiots on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , these idiots on cable news, these idiots on Sunday show -- going where president, you know, he was talking only about the auto industry and this is one more -- it's just bull (EXPLETIVE DELETED), let me say that at 6:15 a.m., it's just bull (EXPLETIVE DELETED)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I mean, you got to laugh. Just, you know, full-blown lying, dishonesty hysteria from NBC, \uc2 CBS\uc2 , fake news CNN, MSDNC, "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 ", pretty much everyone in the Democratic Party, every single, quote, mainstream media outlet there's flat out lying to your face.\par \par \uc2 Now, Trump's full remarks are on tape. He's clearly not calling for political violence at all. In fact, he is using, well, let's say a pretty normal analogy. As a matter of fact, this phrase pay attention, liberal Joe, is very common on the left. Let's remind you.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Republicans understand that they're headed for another potential electoral bloodbath.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Quickly, it's still not clear that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  will in fact testify because I think that would be a bit of a bloodbath.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Major is bracing for a bleak financial situation why one expert is predicting a bloodbath as classes resume this fall.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: But as politico.com reports tonight on the, quote, bloodbath at the \uc2 RNC\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Coming out of Iowa, they were promising that this was effectively going to be a bloodbath, not only is it going to be a bloodbath, but after they leave New Hampshire, it's a bloodbath on her home turf. That's really tough.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And Trump has left a lot of corpses in his wake.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The thing I fear most is if he doesn't run, there's going to be a bloodbath of the Democratic Party.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Because the battle is already on, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is going to win this nomination. And we need to be ready for that. Look, it is a long fight ahead I'm not going to sugarcoat it for anybody, people who don't think this election is going to be a bloodbath are mistaken.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: As the administration braces itself for a bloodbath in 2018 and a wave of staff departures.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We're going to begin right off the bat with some breaking news in our money lead and what's being described as a market bloodbath.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: That it's like we're doing just great in Georgia and, you know, Ohio, God, was that a bloodbath the other night.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Florida is going to be a political bloodbath for \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: You can't make it up, can you? Even \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  himself, he used the term in 2020, telling donors that the \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  campaign might cause a primary bloodbath.\par \par \uc2 And here's where it gets the most interesting, Democrats, the mob, the media, they have done far worse than use the term bloodbath in a political analogy in recent years, some actually even calling for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's assassination, others including your Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , remember the bail fund she tweeted out after the precinct and Minneapolis was burned to the ground and they're not going to stop, we're not going to stop, encouraging the rioting in that summer of love, and promoting the bail fund for dangerous criminals.\par \par \uc2 Many have stated publicly they wanted a punch Trump in the face or as \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  said I wish we were in high school so I could take him behind the gym. Well, if there's a windstorm, Joe may be blown over.\par \par \uc2 The Senate Majority Leader Chucky Schumer even threatening the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2  during a violent speech on the very steps of the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  and that's not all. Let's take a trip down memory lane. This is probably my favorite montage of the night. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , THEN-VICE PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Everyone, beware, because they're not going to stop, it is going to -- they're not going to stop before election day in November, and they're not going to stop after election day.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: There needs to be unrest in the streets for as long as there is unrest in our lives. And unfortunately, there's plenty to go around.\par \par \uc2 PELOSI: I just don't even know why there aren't uprisings all over the country and maybe there will.\par \par \uc2 MADONNA, SINGER: Yes, I have thought an awful lot about blowing up the White House.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: When was the last time an actor assassinated a president? I want to clarify, I'm not an actor. I lie for a living. However, it's been awhile, and maybe it's a time.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: How dare he say the things he does? Of course, I want to punch him in the face.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: If you see anybody from that cabinet in the restaurant, in a department store, at a gasoline station, you get up and you create a crowd, any you push back on them and you tell them they are not welcome!\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: When someone who says, you know, when they go low, we go high. No, no, they go low, we kick them.\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: I put Mr. Burgess up against \uc2 Sean Hannity\uc2 . He'll tear him up.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , THEN-U.S. VICE PRESIDENT: The press always ask me, don't I wish I were debating him? No, I wish we were in high's clinic and I could take him behind the gym. That's what I wish.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY): I want to tell you, Gorsuch, I want to tell you, Kavanaugh, you have released a whirlwind and he will pay the price!\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Is that Obama wanting somebody to beat me up? I'll take him on, whoever Mr. Burgess is, if he wants to, you know, bring it on.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, keep in mind, there was no media hysteria after the violent rhetoric, none. But they take the words of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  completely out of context, they pretend that we are facing an existential crisis and this is what they do every day. The top donors to the Biden campaign this year, like in 2020, like \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  in 2016, will be the mob and the media.\par \par \uc2 Not to be outdone, the Biden campaign actually released this new disgusting, dishonest ad. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Now, if I don't get elected, it's going to be a bloodbath. It's going to be a bloodbath for the country.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Jews will not replace us!\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : But you also have people that were very fine people, on both sides.\par \par \uc2 It's going to be a bloodbath.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: This comes just a few days after a previous ad comparing again Trump to the KKK.\par \par \uc2 Here's the truth though -- well, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , your president, is the only candidate in this race who praised the former Klansman Robert KKK Byrd, called him a friend and a mentor. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , your president, he is the only candidate who worked with the Klansman Robert Byrd, and other segregationists. Why? They wanted to stop the integration of schools and school busing because he didn't want kids going to schools and growing up in a --