 childhood never knew his father, was teased and ended up -- it creates kind of a cycle of emotional abuse with these people.\par \par \uc2 But at the end of the day, what you really want for people is to get their lives together, so we can have a functioning society. Because, Sean, you look around, you've seen things that you'd never think you'd see 10 years ago. You have transsexual people saying that you need to call them certain things, you have people saying that they're oppressed even though they have every opportunity in the world, we are about this close to a tipping point where a majority of Americans feel like they're owed something, like that you and I are the problem, not them.\par \par \uc2 And so, if we can just kind of everybody buy my book, get it together, we can learn about where we came from and how to become a better society.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. You got to remind everybody. Who -- Jesse, whose world is it?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: It's my world, Sean, even though I've been hands-tied, it is still my world.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Oh, okay. Honestly, the book is fascinating. You spent hours with all these people. It takes you through kind of a range of emotions.\par \par \uc2 I urge people to get it. It's on amazon.com, bookstores around the country. Congrats on all your success. We're really proud of you. Jesse Watters, thank you.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. Coming up why I want Iron Mike Tyson to win and we'll show you his message for Jake Paul, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. Iron Mike Tyson as a message for YouTuber-turned-boxer Jake Paul ahead of their fight this summer. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MIKE TYSON, BOXER: Day three, you still want to (EXPLETIVE DELETED) with me.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Mike, I hope you kick his ass.\par \par \uc2 That's all the time we have left. In the meantime, let not your heart be troubled. Why? Greg Gutfeld is standing by to put a smile on your face. Have a great night.\par \par \uc2 END \par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document SHAN000020240319ek3i00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 Netanyahu Approves Rafah Ground Invasion Despite Biden Opposition; Senate Majority Leader Schumer Calls For New Elections in Israel; Biden Calls an Israeli Invasion of Rafah a "Red Line"; Biden's Foreign Aid Package Stalls on Capitol Hill; Violent Gang Uprising Brings Haiti to Brink of Collapse; House Foreign Affairs Committee to Hold Hearing on 2021 Afghanistan Withdrawal; Dems Make Abortion Rights a Top 2024 Issue; State Department Announces Flight For Americans In Haiti; White House Was Aware of Schumer's Speech Calling For New Israeli Leadership; Interview With White House National Security Communications Adviser John Kirby; \uc2 Fani Willis\uc2  Stays in Charge of Trump Georgia Case; House TikTok Bill Moves to the Senate; Trump Pushes Back on House TikTok Bill; Mitch Albom is Interviewed about His Evacuation from Haiti.\b0\par\par\uc2 Shannon Bream, Trey Yingst, Emily Compagno, Bryan Llenas, Lucas  Tomlinson \par \uc2 10,096 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 March 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Fox News Sunday\par \uc2 FOXN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: I'm Shannon Bream.\par \par \uc2 A growing divide on Capitol Hill and inside the White House as the Israel- \uc2 Hamas\uc2  War grinds on without a clear path to peace and escalating rhetoric.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY): Nobody expects Prime Minister Netanyahu to do the things that must be done to break the cycle of violence.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY): The Democratic Party doesn't have an anti- Bibi problem. It has an anti-Israel problem.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice-over): As the U.S. prepares to build a temporary port to supply critical aid to Palestinian civilians in Gaza, Israel plans a significant offensive on the last major \uc2 Hamas\uc2  stronghold in Rafah.\par \par \uc2 Plus, hundreds of Americans, maybe more, potentially trapped in Haiti as a violent rebellion rises, taking the country to the brink of collapse.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: The urgency of the support for Haitians has increased.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: The turmoil sparking fears of another mass migration into the U.S.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The United States of America, we've got our own border crisis going on right now and I think this would only make it worse.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: From Haiti to the Middle East to Ukraine, we'll discuss America's interests on the world stage with the president's top national security spokesman, John Kirby.\par \par \uc2 Plus, an exclusive interview with the chairman of the House Foreign Affairs Committee, Texas Republican Michael McCaul.\par \par \uc2 Then --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The yeas are 352, the nays are 65.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: The House passes a high-stakes bill calling on the owners of TikTok to break their ties with China, as outrage boils from legions of American users of the social media app.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I want President Biden and all the politicians to know this will heavily influence on how I vote.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And --\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT & 2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: We have these two low lives trying to take down a president of the United States.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: President Trump back out on the campaign trail as the lead prosecutor in the former President's Georgia election interference case \uc2 Fani Willis\uc2  stays in place after the special prosecutor she was having a romantic relationship with steps down..\par \par \uc2 Our Sunday panel joins us to break down the legal battles in the middle of the race for 2024.\par \par \uc2 All right now on "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM (on camera): Hello from FOX News in Washington. Happy St. Patrick's Day.\par \par \uc2 We begin with some headlines out of Haiti where the Caribbean country has been rocked by gang violence and the prime minister has resigned, leaving no clear leader in place. The \uc2 U.S. State Department\uc2  has announced a charter flight for Americans with valid passports who've been stranded since the unrest began, but warns it could be a very dangerous time getting to that flight area.\par \par \uc2 Over a million Haitians are now on the verge of famine as food supplies are running out. Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  says most of the nine people who will sit on Haiti's transitional council have been named, hoping that that will help bring an end to the chaos.\par \par \uc2 In a moment, we'll talk live with White House National Security Communications Adviser John Kirby, but first, Bryan Llenas is on the Dominican Republic border there with Haiti.\par \par \uc2 Hello, Bryan.\par \par \uc2 BRYAN LLENAS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Shannon, after initially saying that they -- they would not evacuate Americans that were trapped in Haiti, the \uc2 U.S. State Department\uc2  on Saturday said it had secured a charter flight for a limited number of Americans from Cap-Haitien, a five-and-a-half-hour drive through gang control territory from the capital of Port-au-Prince. The U.S. warns Americans to travel at their own risk while they work to secure other evacuation options from the capital.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Our families are scared. They just want us home.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS (voice-over): Inside Haiti, trapped Americans feel abandoned by the U.S.\par \par \uc2 LYNN JOSEPH, MISSION OF GRACE FOUNDER: We believe in our government they can do better, than leaving a struggling Haiti right now. This is not America.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: Nine Americans for the organization Mission of Grace are stuck in southwest Haiti after evacuating 300 orphan Haitian children from a gang- controlled area.\par \par \uc2 Gangs in Haiti threaten civil war and genocide. An estimated 80 percent of Haiti's capital Port-au-Prince is controlled by gangs who have burned down hundreds of buildings and openly shoot and kill people in the streets.\par \par \uc2 BRYAN STERN, PROJECT DYNAMO FOUNDER: A lot of the gang members are just kids that are excited to be shooting at stuff. They're trigger happy. They're crazy.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: Bryan Stern says his nonprofit Project Dynamo is working to rescue three dozen Americans from Haiti before an international police force led by Kenya enters the nation potentially jump-starting more violence.\par \par \uc2 STERN: The gang side are going to need leverage. They're not able to fight them face to face and nose to nose. They're going to need leverage.\par \par \uc2 And when that happens, almost always that equates to kidnappings and hostages.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: Aid groups say 1.4 million Haitians are on the verge of famine.\par \par \uc2 When merchants go out into the streets looking for money to feed our children and wives, we find nothing. Thousands of Haitians weekly cross over the border into Dajabon, Dominican Republic, to buy necessities.\par \par \uc2 The mayor says the D.R. is doing all it can.\par \par \uc2 I feel like the international community has turned their backs on Haiti, but perhaps they've just grown tired because how many chances have we given the Haitian people?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 LLENAS (on camera): The D.R., Texas and Florida are preparing for potential mass exodus of Haitian migrants. Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  deploying more than 250 additional officers and soldiers and more than a dozen air and sea craft to Florida's southern coast and the Keys -- Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Bryan Llenas on the Dominican Republic border with Haiti, thank you very much, Bryan.\par \par \uc2 We turn now to Lucas Tomlinson over at the White House.\par \par \uc2 Okay, Lucas, let's start with campaign 2024. Both President Biden and former President Trump making the case now officially for a second term.\par \par \uc2 LUCAS TOMLINSON, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: That's right, Shannon. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 President Biden speaking at the annual Gridiron Dinner here in Washington, joking about his age and stamina and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  holding a rally in Ohio focusing on the economy and taking shots at Biden. But it was a floor speech by the Senate majority leader about Israel that still reverberates.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SCHUMER: A new election is the only way to allow for a healthy and open decision-making process about the future of Israel.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (voice-over): President Biden appeared to endorse the speech and says he was given a heads-up.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Mr. President, do you agree with \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  that Netanyahu should go?\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: Biden on a campaign stop in Michigan where over a 100,000 people voted uncommitted in the primary. It's a state he narrowly won in 2020, and where \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  won four years earlier.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: The Democrats are very bad for Israel. Israel sticks with them. Biden is so bad for Israel. They should have never been attacked.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: \uc2 Benny Gantz\uc2 , a rival of Israel's prime minister, responded to Schumer saying: Israel is a robust democracy and only its citizens will decide its future and leadership.\par \par \uc2 Israel's war in \uc2 Hamas\uc2  not the only foreign policy challenge Biden faces in the region. Iran's proxy forces in Yemen continue its attacks on commercial shipping, sinking one recently despite two months of near-daily strikes by the \uc2 U.S. Navy\uc2 . \uc2 G7\uc2  leaders warning Iran not to transfer ballistic missiles to Russia, which would be used against Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 This weekend, Russia is holding presidential elections. The result is not in doubt, despite voter protests including setting fire to polling places and tampering with ballot boxes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (on camera): \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  first rose to power nearly a quarter century ago when \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  was here as president. Only \uc2 Joseph Stalin\uc2  has ruled Russia longer -- Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Lucas Tomlinson at the White House -- thank you very much, Lucas.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, White House National Security Communications Adviser John Kirby.\par \par \uc2 Admiral, welcome back to "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, WHITE HOUSE NATIONAL SECURITY ADVISER: Thanks. Good to be with you, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay. So that speech that \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  gave on Thursday, he lumped Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  in with \uc2 Hamas\uc2  as one of the obstacles to peace. He called for new elections there.\par \par \uc2 He said people within the government who oppose a Palestinian state are bigots, and I can only assume he means not only the prime minister, but \uc2 Benny Gantz\uc2  and others as well.\par \par \uc2 Then, our president said this about the speech.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2 , ISRAEL PRIME MINISTER (through translator): To our friends in the international community, I say, is your memory so short? So quickly, you forgot about October 7th, the worst massacre committed against Jews since the Holocaust? So quickly, you are ready to deny Israel's right to defend itself against the monsters of \uc2 Hamas\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Clearly not our president.\par \par \uc2 But what our president said is he thought that was a good speech and that he spoke for a lot of people.\par \par \uc2 That is Prime Minister Netanyahu's reaction this morning saying --\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Right.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: -- have you already forgotten?\par \par \uc2 Does the president think that \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  is a bigot, that he's an impediment to peace, that he should be lumped in with \uc2 Hamas\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: These are two men -- leaders that have known each other a long time, Shannon, and they don't agree on everything, haven't over 40 years. And there are certain aspects to the prosecution of operations in Gaza where we obviously don't agree with everything that Israel has done.\par \par \uc2 But they have a relationship where they can talk to one another. And they do and they will again.\par \par \uc2 He is the prime minister of Israel, and we respect the sovereignty of the Israeli people.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But does the president who called that speech a good speech agree there need to be new elections, that he shouldn't be the prime minister?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: The president believes it's up to the Israeli people and the Israeli government to determine if and when there's going to be new elections. But he does -- as he said shortly after leader Schumer's very passionate speech -- he recognizes that the leader was speaking for a lot of Americans who feel the same way about the way the war is going.\par \par \uc2 We also have concerns about some of the operations and how they're being conducted. We need to make sure that civilians are protected and secured, that civilian casualties come down that more trucks and assistants get in and, of course, we're still working on that temporary ceasefire to get all those hostages out.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay. So, the president also said this week that there would be a red line. He would consider Israel crossing if they went ahead with this invasion into Rafah, which they say they're going to do. I know there's been some quantifying of that saying if there is an escape route for civilians, that's part of the issue.\par \par \uc2 But if you consider that Israel is crossing a red line, what does that then mean? Do we pull aid? Do we pull weapons?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: What does the president mean on the other side of that red line?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: I don't want to get ahead of where we are right now, or engage in hypothetical, Shannon. What the president --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But he did say that.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Absolutely he did and he's -- what he's talking about is what we've been saying now for a while.\par \par \uc2 We understand the need to go after \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . We understand that there are \uc2 Hamas\uc2  brigades in Rafah, and certainly represent a viable threat to Israel and to the Israeli people.\par \par \uc2 But what we said is we will not support, cannot support an operation in Rafah that doesn't have an executable, verifiable, achievable plan to take care of the 1.5 million people that are trying to find refuge in Rafah, people by the way that were pushed into Rafah from Khan Younis a few weeks ago, and even further north from -- in Gaza City when the operations were there. They have to be accounted for.\par \par \uc2 Now, the Israelis will tell you they've got a plan for that. The evacuation, they call it humanitarian islands. And we welcome any credible plan to take care of them, but we haven't seen it yet.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Have you seen that?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: We haven't seen it yet.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Do you expect to get one before the offensive starts?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: We would certainly welcome the opportunity to get a look at what their plans are before they move into Rafah. And I think it's important for your viewers to understand they have not done that yet. They have not moved into Rafah.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Right. Correct. Okay, they say it could take a matter of weeks, that it could commence sooner rather than later. That's coming from the prime minister this morning.\par \par \uc2 There's also this issue of the pier that we're trying to build in Gaza --\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: -- to get aid in. Pentagon says about a thousand U.S. troops could be involved with that.\par \par \uc2 The question is, as we've said, no U.S. troops will go into Gaza. That's mighty close and so there are questions about their safety and also what happens to the aid once it gets unloaded.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Here's Senator \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  worried about that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARCO RUBIO (R-FL): The only people on the ground with any organization to distribute things are \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . So in essence, can they guarantee that we're going to go through all this trouble, put American lives on the line just so aid can be distributed to \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , who in turn will then control it like they've controlled everything?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So what are the guarantees that the U.S. troops don't get involved, and that \uc2 Hamas\uc2  doesn't take everything that unloads off that pier?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Well, in all fairness and with respect to the senator, \uc2 Hamas\uc2  isn't the only group distributing aid. There are many aid organizations on the ground that -- that are helping with the distribution of food, water, medicine.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But fair to say they get their hands on things.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: We have been watching this very closely and we haven't seen evidence that \uc2 Hamas\uc2  has pilfered off a lot of the humanitarian assistance in a nefarious way. Doesn't -- nobody's making excuses for them or what they've done to Gaza and the Gazan people and how they put the Gazan people at risk, or how they certainly put the Israeli people at risk. Not at all. But there are other organizations that are doing that.\par \par \uc2 I would tell you, we're still working out the details of exactly how the pier is going to operate, the modalities of it, and how the assistance is going to get from the temporary pier, this offshore pier, to the beach.\par \par \uc2 Obviously, we're still going to need ground transportation and we're working -- we're working our way through that right now.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I want to get to another hotspot internationally. The U.S. signed on to a warning to Iran this week about ballistic missiles and armaments flowing from Iran into Russia.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: That's right.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And its efforts against Ukraine as we get word this morning that Russians say that they've taken another key area in Ukraine, a small area in the Zaporizhzhia region.\par \par \uc2 So, it's a difficult stalemate at best there. Now, Iran playing into this.\par \par \uc2 Talk of more sanctions there, but there are a couple of authors with a new book out talking about how sanctions actually work in Iran. They say that rather than hurting the Iranian regime leaders, sanctions have strengthened the Iranian state and military, and ended up hurting the exact people they're supposed to be helping.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: We are working really hard to make sure that the sanctions don't hurt the Iranian people. And one of the things that we talked about earlier this week was yet another -- an update or a renewal of the sanctions relief package that allows Iraq for instance to get electricity from Iran because the Iraqi people are still dependent on Iran for energy and --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And plenty of critics of that, but we won't go down that --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: I understand that, but what the point I'm trying to make is that that the -- that sanctions relief doesn't go to the mullahs. It doesn't go to the regime. It does go to the Iranian people.\par \par \uc2 So we are working hard to make sure that as we employ economic measures against the regime, that we're not punishing the Iranian people. We don't have a beef with the Iranian people.\par \par \uc2 It is with the IRGC. It is with the supreme leader. It's with the mullahs who continue to fund and support and resource terrorist groups throughout the region.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, to give a little bit more information, a \uc2 Russian defense ministry\uc2  is claiming that they have captured the village of Mirne in the Zaporizhzhia region.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: This follows news just weeks ago another area that was reclaimed by Russia.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Avdiivka, yeah.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So where are we?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: So, you know, you called it a stalemate a little bit ago, and I think I'd push back on that. What we're seeing the Russians do, they got Avdiivka in the Donbas, and most of their effort has been in the east, the Donbas area. You talked about this town in Zaporizhzhia, which is clearly more to the south. But they are pushing west from Avdiivka.\par \par \uc2 The Ukrainians have about three lines of defense. The Russians are pushing up against that first one and trying to get to that second one. They're taking small towns and villages and farmland, not so much because it's strategically valuable, but because they want to show wins, particularly now with the election this weekend. They want to show that they're on the move. But they are physically on the move, slowly, but they are. Because the Ukrainians don't have the artillery ammunition and the other capabilities they need to push back on the Russians and to take that ground back. That's why it's so critical, Shannon, that we get this supplemental funding.\par \par \uc2 The Ukrainian commanders on the battlefield are literally making life and death decisions about whether they will fire back or won't, whether they'll move back because they're running out of ammunition. Not courage, not leadership, not skill. They're running out of ammunition.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So we're going to talk to Congressman McCaul up next about that and about what's potentially coming out of the House. But just very quickly, would the White House sign off on standalone measures, say, just aid for Ukraine, just aid for Israel?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: I don't want to get ahead of the President and his decision-making process, but we want that supplemental funding for Ukraine. They desperately need it. Time is not on their side. It's not on our side either.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Admiral, always good to see you. And Happy St. Patrick's Day.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: You too, ma'am.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: You too.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Up next, we're going to take you live to Israel to get the very latest on Prime Minister Netanyahu's plans for that ground invasion in that southern Gaza City of Rafah, as some Democrats say, they want to put conditions on further aid or weapons to our ally.\par \par \uc2 House Foreign Affairs Committee Chairman, Texas Republican Michael McCaul joins us exclusively. He's next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Israeli Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  has approved plans for a ground invasion into the city of Rafah in southern Gaza despite objections by the U.S. Netanyahu making the announcement Friday, saying the plans do include details for evacuating civilians from Rafah, although, as you heard Admiral Kirby say, we haven't seen them yet.\par \par \uc2 Fox News Foreign Correspondent Trey Yingst is live from Tel Aviv with the very latest from there. Hello, Trey.\par \par \uc2 TREY YINGST, FOX NEWS FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: Shannon, good morning. Amid political disagreements about next steps in the war, both the Israelis and the Americans agree more aid must make it into Gaza.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 YINGST: In the dark of night, a crane unloads pallets of food from a ship docked on the coast of Gaza.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You have that permission from the State of Israel.\par \par \uc2 YINGST: The supplies are the first sea delivery of aid to the enclave since the war began.\par \par \uc2 ALI ABU REESH, DISPLACED GAZAN: Look at the people, they are all over the food, and they can't have anything.\par \par \uc2 YINGST: This weekend also saw more support dropped from the air in a coordinated effort by the American and Jordanian militaries. And while there are clear agreements on the need for humanitarian aid, there is a growing rift between the Americans and Israelis about how to proceed with military operations.\par \par \uc2 What started as a spat between Israeli Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  and President Biden over plans to enter Gaza's southernmost city of Rafah has turned into much more with Senate Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  on Thursday calling for elections in Israel.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY), MAJORITY LEADER: Prime Minister Netanyahu has lost his way.\par \par \uc2 YINGST: Netanyahu has remained defiant, even in the face of anti-government protests in Tel Aviv Saturday and concerns from international organizations about the consequences of operating in Rafah.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANE DUJARRIC\uc2 , SPOKESMAN FOR UNITED NATIONS SECRETARY GENERAL: I think the consequences of a ground operation in Rafah in the current circumstances would be catastrophic for the people of Gaza.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 YINGST: With the Israeli Prime Minister juggling the politics of the war, Israel's Defense Minister Yoav Gallant held a special meeting overnight to discuss the return of hostages from Gaza. Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Trey Yingst, the priority remains there. Live in Israel, Trey, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, House Foreign Affairs Chair and Texas Congressman Michael McCaul. Good to see you. Welcome back.\par \par \uc2 REP. MICHAEL MCCAUL (R-TX): Thanks for having me, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Let me first start by getting your reaction to that \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  speech saying time for new elections, Bibi standing in the way of peace, slumping him in with \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . What do you make of that? You heard what Admiral Kirby had to say. The Biden and Bibi have differences, but they can talk to each other.\par \par \uc2 MCCAUL: But you don't talk about toppling a government in a democracy. That's -- you know, I was with the Ambassador Herzog from Israel at our retreat when this news came in and it was very inappropriate. I think it was embarrassing.\par \par \uc2 You know, there's a way to talk about your differences, not to topple a government in a democratic country. And I think, Shannon, it's indicative of how the split in the Democratic Party between the base, which is not in the traditional Israeli alliance anymore. And I think what you're seeing is a pro-Palestinian, anti-Israel faction in the Democratic Party.\par \par \uc2 I know they protest Schumer outside his home every morning. Perhaps that's why he's responding this way. But it's not a good sign because usually Israel's always been bipartisan.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, there are, you know, your Democratic colleagues, many of them raising questions about whether aid or weapons should be stopped. There should be conditions on what flows into Israel. And the President talked about this red line with regard to Rafah. We do have a lot of sway over Israel. So how much of that should we be using to make sure there is a concrete plan to protect civilians in Rafah?\par \par \uc2 MCCAUL: Well, the aid has been more complicated because \uc2 UNRWA\uc2 , the \uc2 U.N.\uc2  refugee, you know, they were actually involved in the invasion of Israel. So how can we have them deliver aid in Gaza? There's no trust there.\par \par \uc2 So Israel is now getting private contractors to move the aid. And we have the \uc2 World Food Program\uc2 , Catholic Relief Services, many NGOs that can deliver the aid if given the right security. And I think we need to look at that model moving forward.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But should we be continuing to warn Israel that there could be limits on what we send or give if they proceed with this Rafah invasion?\par \par \uc2 MCCAUL: My understanding, it's not going to be invasion. It's going to be a continuation of targeting high-valued targets in Rafah. They've taken out half the leadership of \uc2 Hamas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Once they take out, you know, the top leadership, including this \uc2 Yahya Sinwar\uc2  guy who's starting to bomb Jerusalem during Ramadan, that is when you'll see a turning point. I don't see anything wrong with doing that. High-valued targets in Rafah, not a full-fledged tanks rolling into Rafah.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. So let's talk about all of the funding that is sitting there in the supplemental, some of it for Israel, Ukraine, Taiwan, other priorities. You heard Admiral Kirby saying they want the full package.\par \par \uc2 But there are other things being floated, possible standalone measures, possible loans or lend-lease, the things that are potentially coming out of the House. The White House has signaled it really doesn't have any interest in. So what can the House pass that the President would actually sign?\par \par \uc2 MCCAUL: Well, the Speaker indicated he's committed to getting this done. You know, I've been working with the appropriate authorizers and appropriators. There are things we can do to make this bill better.\par \par \uc2 For instance, The REPO Act, which I passed out of my committee, allows us to attach to the Russian sovereign assets have been frozen to help pay for Russia's war crimes. I think most Americans agree with that concept.\par \par \uc2 Also, the loan program to provide for the direct government assistance to Ukraine. It's non -- you know, no interest, waivable, but it's symbolic that it is a loan program.\par \par \uc2 Finally, a strategy. And we've got to put these ATACMS in, the long-range artillery to hit the bridge between Russia and Crimea.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So do you think there's a chance still this comes through as a package? There is still a chance that what the Senate has put into the House's court could come back out as a package? Or do you think it is going to be potentially broken up into other measures?\par \par \uc2 MCCAUL: Well, I think we are moving through our normal appropriations process. Should be done by this Friday. After which the Speaker's indicated he's committed to putting this supplement on the floor. That would be Israel. And we should attach Iran sanctions to that. That would be Ukraine and also Indo-Pacific.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Do you think we get this all done by Friday? Because if not, it's government shutdown time.\par \par \uc2 MCCAUL: I do --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And there's real concern, especially about the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  portion of this and whether that gets passed. Do you think they all get done by Friday?\par \par \uc2 MCCAUL: I'm hopeful. I do think we will. The homeland piece, a little dicey. Last resort, maybe a CR.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Just for that?\par \par \uc2 MCCAUL: We could continue homeland on that basis. However, we will get that done. And we will move to the supplemental.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, I want to talk to you about Haiti because we are watching this violent, terrible situation, deadly there, unfolding. Democrats are pointing the finger at Republicans over some of this. The Democratic leader, \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2 , over in the House, wrote a letter to the Speaker this week.\par \par \uc2 By the way, he name-checked you in the letter. And he says this. The situation on the ground in Haiti has rapidly deteriorated while House Republicans have refused to deliver the resources necessary to carry out this mission. Now is the time to release the full $50 million in security support. Are Republicans responsible for what's happening in Haiti?\par \par \uc2 MCCAUL: No, I think the administration's failed policies have led to this, what is happening in Haiti, with no governance whatsoever, Shannon. The Prime Minister's in Puerto Rico, even Kenya won't put their peacekeeping forces because it's so chaotic.\par \par \uc2 Now, what they're asking me to do is release $50 million of cash and weapons to go into Haiti with no governance. And the worst thing we can do is arm the very warlords that threaten the people of Haiti. So this guy named Barbecue, I don't want this to happen.\par \par \uc2 We have a history in this country of throwing weapons and guns into countries and they backfire. Until I have assurances that the money can be accounted for and go to the right purpose, I cannot in good conscience release that funding for the American taxpayer, but most importantly, the Haitian people themselves.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, so that stays on hold for now. I want to make sure before you go that we talk about this hearing you're going to have this week. Trying to get some answers and some accountability about the withdrawal from Afghanistan. We all remember the horrible suicide bombing that took more than 100 lives, including 13 service members, American service members.\par \par \uc2 By the way, one of the fathers of the service member who was arrested last week at the State of the Union, I know you hosted, had a number of these families as guests. He says charges are still pending against him. Do you know if that's what's happening in that case?\par \par \uc2 MCCAUL: They are. I would recommend they'd be dropped. Look, this guy, Steve Nikoui, has been through so much. He lost his son. He's had a suicide death in his family. All these families, these Gold Star families, are grieving. They feel that this President has never apologized to them or said, I'm sorry that your son and one daughter was killed by a suicide bomber. We will hear from General McKenzie, the Chairman of Joint Chiefs. General McKenzie, the CENTCOM Commander, about the White House and State Department's failures that brought about the conditions that led to the suicide bomber.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Do you think we'll get information from this hearing that we didn't previously have? And bottom line, what the American people want to know is, is anyone then going to be held accountable?\par \par \uc2 MCCAUL: Well, the one thing I would like is, I want the DOD to provide the documents that the sniper testified to. He said, I saw the suicide bomber. It identified the BOLO. Be on the lookout. He sent the pictures over to the command center. They've yet to produce these a year later. And I'll be talking about that a lot at the hearing.\par \par \uc2 And by the way, General Milley and McKenzie agree with me that the DOD needs to turn over these documents.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, we'll be watching this week.\par \par \uc2 MCCAUL: Thanks, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Chairman, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 MCCAUL: Happy St. Patrick's Day.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And to you as well.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the general election is officially on, setting up that Trump-Biden rematch. We're going to tell you who's leading in two new brand-new battleground state Fox News polls.\par \par \uc2 As millions of primary voters head to the polls in Arizona, Florida, Illinois, Kansas, and Ohio on Tuesday. Our Sunday panel weighs in, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Let me tell you something to China. If you're listening, President Xi, and you and I are friends, but he understands the way I deal.\par \par \uc2 Those big monster car manufacturing plants that you're building in Mexico, we're going to put a 100 percent tariff on every single car that comes across the line. And you're not going to be able to sell those cars.\par \par \uc2 If I get elected, now, if I don't get elected, it's going to be a bloodbath for the whole -- that's going to be the least of it. It's going to be a bloodbath.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. So former president Trump taking heat last night, the headlines say bloodbath. But he was predicting an economic bloodbath. If he doesn't win this fall, so he can impose significant tariffs on cars that China wants to manufacture in Mexico and sell in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 We always think context is important. But Biden-Harris campaign spokesperson James Singer responded quickly, saying this, "The American people are going to give him another electoral defeat this November because they continue to reject his extremism, his affection for violence, and his thirst for revenge."\par \par \uc2 It is time now for our Sunday group, Emily Compagno, "Outnumbered" co-host. Bob Cusack, "The Hill" editor-in-chief. Morgan Ortagus, former State Department spokesperson and founder of "Polaris National Security." And \uc2 Kevin Walling\uc2 , former 2020 Biden campaign surrogate. Great to see you all of you this morning.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN WALLING\uc2 , FORMER 2020 BIDEN CAMPAIGN SURROGATE: Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BOB CUSACK, EDITOR-IN-CHIEF, THE HILL: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. So the general election is on in earnest. These two are going at each other. President Biden has just made a swing of some critical key battleground states and vice president out there as well.\par \par \uc2 But here's the headline from "Newsweek." "\uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's approval rating falls to an all-time low after the State of the Union."\par \par \uc2 Kevin, he didn't get that bounce they were expecting.\par \par \uc2 WALLING: Well, I think it's an important point at not just in the polling, but he got raised $10 million --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Did raise money.\par \par \uc2 WALLING: -- just in the hours after the State of the Union, mostly by small dollar donations.\par \par \uc2 So I think you're seeing enthusiasm among Democratic-based supporters. To your point, Shannon, he was on the ground in Michigan. The vice president was on the ground in Wisconsin. He was in Pennsylvania on Friday.\par \par \uc2 Again, hitting those key states, building out that team, right? So we've hired all of the key people in most of those battleground states.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, the \uc2 RNC\uc2  is being decimated now by its new leadership. So I think the president is in a good position come the next 232 days.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. Well, let's take a look at some polls that might not suggest that.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHS)\par \par \uc2 There's room for improvement, as my mom likes to say. OK. In Arizona, we've got our brand-new Fox Poll. If you're voting for president right now, this first one has everybody in the third-party contenders. Even with that, Trump's got a plus four there.\par \par \uc2 When we go head to head in Arizona without all of the extra candidates, still a plus four for President Trump. Much tighter in Pennsylvania, where head-to-head. Trump's got a two-point advantage, but that erases. When you throw in the third-party, it's then a tie.\par \par \uc2 So, Morgan, it looks good for President Trump right now, but these -- many of these are within the margin of error.\par \par \uc2 MORGAN ORTAGUS, FORMER STATE DEPARTMENT SPOKESPERSON: Yes, they are. I mean, listen, it does look good for President Trump because when you look at the things that still matter to most Americans, they're still filling the pinch of inflation.\par \par \uc2 I talk about this every time I go to the grocery store. The prices haven't come down every time you fill up your car with the tank of gas. It's still higher than it was four years ago. So there is an economic message to run on.\par \par \uc2 There's also for all the foreign policy dorks like me. Our issues finally matter in the presidential election. Unfortunately, it's for a really bad reason.\par \par \uc2 When you look in theater after theater after theater, there is new war, there is chaos. And there is a feeling that the adults are not only not in charge, but the people that are leading are making poor decisions that are leading to, again, wars in every theater.\par \par \uc2 I mean, we can't lightly skate over the fact that this is the worst war in Europe. Russia's invasion of Ukraine since World War II.\par \par \uc2 We can't lightly skid over the fact that more Jews were killed on October 7th than any day since the Holocaust.\par \par \uc2 Look at Venezuela, they let -- lessen (PH) sanctions on Maduro. Promised elections. They never happened.\par \par \uc2 I mean, I could go throughout the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 And, by the way, I'm not going to let this administration forget about Afghanistan. We had 13 of our best young men and women there. Twenty years of a CT fight that they flushed down the toilet through a chaotic and disastrous. Those are all that didn't need to happen.\par \par \uc2 No one in this national security team has ever been held accountable, has ever been punished, has ever been even reprimanded for failure after failure after failure. And that's what we're going to indict between now and Election Day.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and I'll be very interested to see as we talked about with Chairman McCaul, what comes from, if we get anything new, if there are any lines toward accountability on that Afghan withdrawal.\par \par \uc2 One of the problems that the Biden campaign is having to work with is Arab- American voters who think he's not managing the Gaza-Israel conflict well. And we see polling that says across the board by two-thirds, approximately Americans don't think he's handling foreign policy well.\par \par \uc2 "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " has this though specifically with the backlash from Arab- American voters. Saying any notion that those voters seething over America's role in Gaza will simply come home and vote for Biden in the general election need serious adjustment. For some voters, this isn't just a policy dispute. It's a moral mission and the mark of victory is a Biden defeat."\par \par \uc2 So the question now is, how large is that constituency? I mean, Emily, we've seen this show up in early states were uncommitted in Michigan was over 100,000 votes.\par \par \uc2 EMILY COMPAGNO, FOX NEWS HOST: That's right. And that was due to a strong lobbying effort.\par \par \uc2 And I want to dig into a little bit about the why. So the executive director of \uc2 CAIR\uc2  came out and he said, look, I cannot live under someone who pretends to be my friend. Because some argue, well, wasn't under President Trump's administration, wasn't there a clear as they say there was a travel ban on Muslim-Americans, there was rhetoric that seemed to show an antagonism to reflect that.\par \par \uc2 And for many Muslim-Americans, as he said, well, I knew how he stood. He didn't pretend to be my friend. And as he said, Trump was an actively arming and overseeing a genocide.\par \par \uc2 I think the betrayal that Arab-Americans feel under President Biden cannot be understated. And I have to say there's going to be overstated.\par \par \uc2 And I have to point out as well that the problem with President Biden's approach to foreign policy, which Morgan can articulate far greater than I can, is his inability and his to commit to something because of a fear of escalation.\par \par \uc2 And he is not winning any friends by doing so. His fear of escalation with Russia and his fear of escalation within Gaza has led to no friends. He is absolutely sort of projected out any type of Israeli support as well as any type of Arab-American support.\par \par \uc2 And by him not drawing a clear line, that is why voters are shying away and why the strong backlash is so strong and might be deeply damning.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes. It's something that he's got to work on in the polling and in policy. But something that Democrats know has been working for them is this issue of abortion.\par \par \uc2 We saw the sitting vice president, I think for the first time in history, go to visit an abortion clinic and talk about these rights that they, she says Democrats are fighting to defend. Here's a bit of what she said.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: These attacks against an individual's right to make decisions about their own body are outrageous. And in many instances, just plain old immoral.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And, Bob, that comes at the same time that we hear from the House GOP retreat, "Punchbowl" reports, the GOP leadership wants to go on attack. They say that Republicans aren't right to sit back on this issue. They need to put the onus on Democrats to explain what their position is, where their timelines are."\par \par \uc2 CUSACK: Yes, Shannon, that's good advice because we saw the Democrat in a special election talk about immigration. He won that race. You've got to basically talk about what people want to talk about.\par \par \uc2 Now, Democrats definitely have a huge advantage on abortion. And you're going to hear abortion talked about a lot.\par \par \uc2 Some Democrats want to talk more about abortion than the economy because that's where Trump has the advantage.\par \par \uc2 But those battleground states, major problem for Biden. And listen, I think there's going to be some big protests, the Democratic Convention in Chicago.\par \par \uc2 ORTAGUS (PH): Yes.\par \par \uc2 CUSACK: That could be a big embarrassment for the party.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, we'll all be there. Many of us.\par \par \uc2 WALLING (PH): Yes, we will.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So we will check it out first-hand.\par \par \uc2 OK. Panel, don't go far. Quick break here.\par \par \uc2 Up next, a special prosecutor in Trump's Georgia election interference case is out and the former president scores a temporary win in that hush-money case against him in New York.\par \par \uc2 Our panel breaks down the political implications of all the legal developments, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2  (R-SC): This is another example of a two-tiered justice system when it comes to president Trump and those around him. I think it's a sad day for Georgia and a bad day for the country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHANNON: That was South Carolina Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  -- apparently very humid where he was - reacting to the news out of Fulton County, Georgia, on Friday. That special prosecutor, Nathan Wade, is officially out, no longer part of the state's election interference case against former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , after a judge ruled District Attorney \uc2 Fani Willis\uc2  can stay on the case but that was on the condition that Wade left.\par \par \uc2 So, we are back now with the panel.\par \par \uc2 Here's what the judge said, by the way, here. He said, "The finding is by no means an indication that the court condones this tremendous lapse in judgment or the unprofessional manner of the district attorney's testimony during the evidentiary hearing."\par \par \uc2 So, Emily, it left both sides unhappy in some respects.\par \par \uc2 COMPAGNO: That's right. And I think what was surprising for a lot of people is that they felt, well, wait a minute, wasn't it obvious? Wasn't there enough testimony to prove that the relationship began well before Nathan Wade earned that position? And the judge essentially said it didn't. He said it doesn't rise to that legal standard, the legal sufficiency.\par \par \uc2 And so he - I think the - the issue with this is that many feel that the embarrassment that it reflects onto the district attorney, that there should be some type of accountability and it seems that a resignation by Nathan Wade doesn't fulfill people's expectation that there had to have been some remedy. But there remains to be seen whether the Georgia State Bar, the Georgia Senate, there's a lot of other bodies that might hold her to accounts, including, and most especially, the voters. But here, at least, people think that maybe this might light a new fire of vitriol under her for that prosecution.\par \par \uc2 I just have to point out that the defense was arguing, because for some people that are wondering, that it was prima facie, that on its face there was, in fact, an actual conflict of interest, and the judge simply said, look, we went back to case law, we went back to precedents, the standard is not prima facie, it's not on its face, and it didn't just rise here.\par \par \uc2 But as you pointed out, he said it left an odor of mendacity. So, clearly, there were a lot of expected lies that I think might come back to bite DA \uc2 Fani Willis\uc2  later.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, there are investigations ongoing. Now, over at "The LA Times," Anthony Michael Kreis, who is a Con Law professor, said this was actually a win for Willis. He said, "It's a total legal victory. Is the case preserved? Yes. Have things been derailed? No."\par \par \uc2 So, Bob, this could still end up being a real problem for President Trump and all these co-defendants down the line if she just picks up the ball and says, OK, we're proceeding from here.\par \par \uc2 CUSACK: Yes. And, I mean, but this is really bad. I mean this ruling was quite scathing. And, at the same time, as far as going forward, I mean the case is basically infected because of this poor judgment. It's been delayed because of this poor judgment. And that's exactly what Trump wants.\par \par \uc2 So, this -- I disagree, I think it's a -- it's a win for Trump.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and he's got the delay now on the \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2  hush money case in New York as well. All of these will eventually get to trial, with the exception of if the president wins and some of these federal things he could actually either pardon or potentially shut down as president.\par \par \uc2 But, Kevin, that essentially meaning he is running for his life in some ways to win this election.\par \par \uc2 WALLING: He absolutely is. And that's a very dangerous position to be running from in terms of what he is on the line for with these indictments.\par \par \uc2 You know, it's -- the process is going to move forward in - in Georgia. There's ready for co-defendants of the former president who have already pled guilty in that case that are cooperating with \uc2 Fani Willis\uc2 . So, that process, like I said, will take place.\par \par \uc2 Again, one of the key reasons why \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  was campaigning so hard on the campaign trail was the idea of this distraction for the former president. The time that he's going to spend in those courtrooms facing those charges, his inability to raise those funds to compete against the incumbent president is going to be a key issue for the former president.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and something he's also having to talk about is what appears to be a shift in position on TikTok, tried to get rid of it during his presidency. Seems a little bit more lukewarm on the issue that's coming out of the House. And the fact is, it's made it through the House, but here's the headline from "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 ." "After TikTok bill sails through the House, senators pump the brakes."\par \par \uc2 So, Morgan, there's now this conversation about where it goes from here. And there are concerns about the bill, some of the vagueness. There are some critics who say it actually could go a lot farther than it appears at first blush.\par \par \uc2 ORTAGUS: Well, this is not the first time in history that the House has passed something and the Senate decided to slow it down. In fact, that probably happens about every other week here in Washington.\par \par \uc2 I do think that there is clearly, by what - I mean what passes the House in such a bipartisan manner, really not many things.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Very few things.\par \par \uc2 ORTAGUS: Very few things. So, there are clearly some provisions, especially with like Ted Cruz and other consultation lawyers, \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 , you know, people in the Senate that are going to want to look at the legislation in detail and make sure there's nothing they could come back to bite lawmakers in the you know what as it relates to TikTok.\par \par \uc2 But fundamentally, the -- I think as - from the perspective of being a China hawk, the fact that we have Republicans and Democrats in Washington, D.C., that agree that the algorithm and the data behind this app is essentially the Chinese Communist Party controlling the - the minds and the public discourse in our country. The fact that we can get them all to agree on that is great. Now can the Senate, you know, go through and get - get the proper amendments in to get the legislation palatable to form President Trump? We'll see.\par \par \uc2 I don't think he was actually pushing back that much. He was bringing up, I think, a very key point that worries a lot of people, including like Senator \uc2 Marsha Blackburn\uc2  has put forward legislation, so has Elizabeth Warren, Lindsay Graham, on regulating social media writ large. And so I think there will be an appetite to look at social media, \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , \uc2 Instagram\uc2 , writ large after TikTok.\par \par \uc2 But, listen, again, from a China hawk perspective, the fact that we could get both sides of the aisle to Washington, D.C., to agree on anything, including the threat of authoritarian-owned social media, 5G, cars, you name it, this is what we have to be attacking holistically in Washington.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, let me play a little bit of President Trump's interview with our Howie Kurtz that you see in full on Media Buzz talking about TikTok and the - and the appearance that his position has shifted.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : What I would like to see is, if you're doing a do it to TikTok, do it to \uc2 Facebook\uc2 . And what you can do is, let them sell TikTok, let them sell it in the market, maybe get a good price, maybe not get a good price, I don't know, but take it away from China control.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, so "Politico" says this, "After he spent much of the last year of his presidency trying to ban TikTok, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's abrupt effort to defend the Chinese-owned app late last week caught many in Washington by surprise." They say it's "part of a pattern: changing course when an interested billionaire donor was in the mix."\par \par \uc2 So, Bob, do you think there is any appetite on The Hill for going after these other companies as well while they're looking at TikTok, as the former president suggests?\par \par \uc2 CUSACK: There's some appetite, but I think it's going to be very difficult. And I think this is a precarious decision by \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2 , if he allows the vote in the Senate, because it probably will pass. But at the same time, with the House and Senate and White House up for grabs, even those this got 352 votes in the House, it's actually not that popular and it could backfire with young voters.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and on that issue of young voters, I mean, Keven, the Biden- Harris campaign is on TikTok.\par \par \uc2 WALLING: Yes. No, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Even though apparently the president has said he would sign this.\par \par \uc2 WALLING: Well, and interestingly enough, Shannon, you played that clip earlier of the woman in front of the White House saying she will blame President Biden. I think to Bob's point, it's a good one, this is a key issue for young voters. Just like the war with Israel and \uc2 Hamas\uc2  in terms of animating younger voters. So, we - the administration has to tow a particular line I think with regards to this, especially now with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  coming out against this supposed ban.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I'm not sure if he's for or against after hearing that side (ph).\par \par \uc2 WALLING: I know, right?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I'm - I'm a little bit confused.\par \par \uc2 ORTAGUS: I think he's for a sale.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But - but watch the full interview with Howie and then you'll know more.\par \par \uc2 OK, panel, thank you very much. We'll see you next week.\par \par \uc2 Up next, bestselling author Mitch Albom on his harrowing escape from Haiti and growing concerned about those left behind, including at the orphanage he operates there in Haiti.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Chaos and violence has overrun Haiti, bringing the country to its knees. Haitians and Americans racing to escape the unrest have very few options. It was, in fact, a mission led by two members of Congress that successfully airlifted a group of volunteers from the island, including best-selling author Mitch Albom, who joins us now to share his story.\par \par \uc2 Mitch, welcome. It's great to have you with us.\par \par \uc2 I learned in interviewing you last year that you operate an orphanage there. Here is what the White House - or, excuse me, the administration, the State Department has said about Americans being there in Haiti.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW MILLER\uc2 , U.S. STATE DEPARTMENT SPOKESPERSON: For years we have been telling Americans, do not go to Haiti. Do not travel there. It's not safe to do so. And for those who are there, leave as soon as you can feasibly do so without putting yourself at risk.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, why has it been so important for you to be there?\par \par \uc2 MITCH ALBOM, BESTSELLING AUTHOR: Because it's easy to say don't go there because it's dangerous, but what about the people who are living there? What about the children who are there? I've operated an orphanage there for 14 years. I've been there every month for the last 14 years. And I can't stop going just because of danger. It's more dangerous to the children and the people there.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: What is the situation like on the ground there?\par \par \uc2 ALBOM: It's almost indescribable, Shannon. I mean every night there's gunfire. Our kids sing their prayers with the gunfire in the background. You hear about police stations being burned, banks being looted, bodies being left out of the middle of the street. There is no government to speak of. And the gangs basically control everything. Our kids haven't been outside of our orphanage doors in three years because it's too dangerous to go out in the streets. It's - it's just not a way to live. And I'm trying - been advocating to - for us to get a little bit more involved and try to stem this violence in Haiti.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, you do go back and forth on a regular basis, but this last time trickier, trying to get out with airport shut down, normal transit areas completely closed off. We have heard - we may have some footage of this that you were part of the group that was rescued in part by Congressman Cory Mills, who has done this in a number of places when he thinks it's important to get Americans out. Can you tell us anything about your journey out?\par \par \uc2 ALBOM: I didn't know Cory Mills before he started calling me, (INAUDIBLE), another congressperson, and said, we're going to get you out. It wasn't so much my wife and myself who go there every month and are kind of used to this danger, but we had a guest with us who had come down, some of whom had never been to Haiti before, one of whom had never been out of the country before. And we had no idea what we could get out again. The airports were closed. The ports were closed. The roads were closed. The borders were closed.\par \par \uc2 Cory Mills organized this trip. It was the middle of the night thing. You know, we came running out of a place where we were really held. Sixty-seven seconds, we were thrown into a helicopter and up and out.\par \par \uc2 But while we're happy that we were able to get out and heartbroken when we flew over Haiti airspace because my wife and I realized, you know, our kids were still there. And so are, by the way, hundreds if not thousands of other Americans, Canadians, and volunteer workers who can't get out right now and still are trying to.\par \par \uc2 You know, and I want to point out that these are not people who went to Haiti to go have a vacation. You know, people don't go there to go to Club Med. They're there to help children, they're there to do water projects or bring food or education or hospitals. They're doing good work. And if they need to get out now, I hope there will be efforts to bring them out.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Mitch, thank you so much for the work you're doing there. Blessings over you and your team in that. That's been something you've done quietly, selflessly and it's making a real difference. And I know your heart -- much of it is still left there in Haiti. We will pray for those who are left in your care from afar now.\par \par \uc2 ALBOM: Thank you for that. Please don't forget about them. Thank you, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We won't. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 By the way, Mitch has been on my podcast, and today we've got a new one. "Livin' the Bream" drops this morning. I sat down with Fox News correspondent Benjamin Hall, marking two years into his recovery. We did a deep dive on Israel, Gaza, Iran, Ukraine and more. Man, he's got a first- hand account of all those places. Get it anywhere you like to get your podcast, "Livin' the Bream."\par \par \uc2 That is it for today. Thank you for joining us. I'm Shannon Bream. Have a great week. Happy St. Patty's Day, and we'll see you next FOX NEWS SUNDAY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FOXN000020240317ek3h00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Why Does Biden Avoid Impromptu Questions?\b0\par\par\uc2 Sean Hannity, Kellyanne Conway, Tomi Lahren, Jason Chaffetz, Pete  Hegseth, Larry Kudlow \par \uc2 7,978 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 March 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Hannity\par \uc2 SHAN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SEAN HANNITY\uc2 , FOX NEWS HOST: And welcome to HANNITY.\par \par \uc2 And tonight in just moments we will bring you an explosive new report surrounding \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 , China and nuclear energy. John Solomon will join us with all these details. You're not going to get it from the media mob.\par \par \uc2 But, first, do you ever wonder why -- do you ever wonder why your President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is not allowed to answer impromptu questions? Hmm, a mystery, many people want to know. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Am I allowed to take any questions? Any -- anybody here has asked?\par \par \uc2 I better not start the questions, I'll get in trouble.\par \par \uc2 I'm sorry, I'm going to just the last question I'll take and I'm really going to be in trouble.\par \par \uc2 I'm sorry, I'm going to get in trouble with staff I don't do this the right way. I'm going to get in trouble with my staff. Yeah, go ahead, I pretend that I didn't answer.\par \par \uc2 You guys are bad. I'm not supposed to be answering all these questions. I'm supposed to leave.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, and I'm happy to take questions if that's I'm supposed to do now, whatever you want me to do.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: White House staffers, campaign officials and the first lady, they all try their best to keep Joey away from the press and anyone else with a question. Anyway, even the Easter Bunny might recall once shielded Joe from answering questions on the White House lawn.\par \par \uc2 Well, today in Michigan, more of the same. At one stop, the press pool, they were confined to a tent that was 20 feet away. No one could really hear what the president was even saying and, of course, reporters -- they were blocked from asking any questions. He might get in trouble. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Can I take a couple of questions?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We're going to take a few questions.\par \par \uc2 STAFFERS: Thank you, guys. Thank you so much. Head back to the cars. Thank you. Back to the cars. Thank you. We're happy to (INAUDIBLE). Thank you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Joe, you're the president of the United States. You're now running for reelection, but you have to ask your staff if you can take a question, seriously?\par \par \uc2 Now, unless the reporter's name as we know and picture and pre-planned topic is written on a giant no card ahead of time, he may not get his ice cream and everyone's going to be mad at him and everything. It's not acceptable.\par \par \uc2 Many in the press corps just don't really seem to care or even bother to ask why. Guess it's part for the course with Joe.\par \par \uc2 Now the answer is obvious. Joe's a cognitive wreck. Everybody knows it. So he's clearly not capable of performing the duties of his jobs.\par \par \uc2 You know, he's obviously not jacked up Joe like he was at the State of the Union, screaming his -- screaming his speech to an entire country and every day when he's not jacked up Joe, he gets worse and worse.\par \par \uc2 It's no secret that his vice president, by the way, could be called into action at any moment and that would be a huge problem because almost nobody actually likes or trusts the vice president. Now, she has even worse poll numbers than Joe. That's even possible. In fact, according to one NBC News survey, yep, Vice President Harris has the worst approval rating of any vice president ever. She has zero accomplishments in office and her role as borders czar, not exactly gone well to put it lightly.\par \par \uc2 But today, she did visit a high school softball team for some very awkward small talk. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I am so proud of you guys and I just wanted to come by to say hello. You guys are the reason I do what I do, and I just love your competitive spirit. I love how you guys are just working at it. You are ready to be a team and you're ready to compete, and I just that makes me so proud, so proud. How you guys do it?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Yes, we can.\par \par \uc2 Anyway while in Minnesota, while \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  also stopped by an abortion clinic to celebrate the right to choose. Now, there was once was a president named \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 , remember him. He once said about abortion it should be -- it should be safe and legal. He should have added rare. Whatever happened to that Democratic Party?\par \par \uc2 Now, by the way, now Democrats want abortion to be limitless and honored without any restrictions in the ninth month of pregnancy, and then in the case of, remember, Governor Northam from Virginia, even -- well, first, we'll deliver the baby, make the baby comfortable and then the mother and doctor will talk about whether the baby lives.\par \par \uc2 That's the exception for sure, but a lot of Democrats when you ask them, what restrictions at all would you put on abortion? They won't answer that question. Here's an example.\par \par \uc2 California Governor \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2 , our buddy.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Do you support any restrictions at all on abortion, especially in months seven, eight and nine, past viability?\par \par \uc2 GOV. GAVIN NEWSOM (D), CALIFORNIA: I'm going to answer that question but let's talk about the issue of abortion. Let's talk about the issue --\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Are you going to answer it?\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: I'm going to answer that question, I'll repeat that.\par \par \uc2 I trust and answer your question I trust the mother and her doctor to make that decision.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: So in other words, I want to be clear on this, if a woman and her doctor, for any reason --\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: Not for any reason. It's extremely rare.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Should there --\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: I know where you guys are going, Sean, you're even uncomfortable with this whole issue, and I understand it. This is where you guys have to cover for the extreme anti-abortion agenda of \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Should there be -- should there be -- would you support a ban on abortion in seventh, eighth, or ninth month, if the mother's life is not in jeopardy?\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: Extreme, extreme exception. People aren't going on and having abortion unless something devastating is happening.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Should it be up illegal then? Should it be illegal?\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: It should be to the mother and her doctor and her conscience, and it almost always --\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: So, the answer is no restriction.\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: I've already answered it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: No restrictions. There is no moderation in this radical left-wing Democratic party. Not on abortion, not on spending, not on taxes, not on illegal immigration, where it's really a bad thing to even say the word illegal, and by the way, not on COVID. We know that, God forbid if you held a different view on the emergency authorization vaccine, that's not been improperly studied scientifically, follow the science.\par \par \uc2 This is a party with radical and extreme positions on pretty much everything. So for many on the left, center left even, the Democratic party has totally abandoned them they're now looking for new options come to fall and I guess they will have other choices.\par \par \uc2 Looks like \uc2 Robert F. Kennedy Jr.\uc2  -- naturally the DNC is in panic mode over this. Is he going to bring back Jesse "The Body" Ventura? How great would that be? Anyway, don't expect them to moderate any of their policies.\par \par \uc2 Now, instead, Biden's 2024 campaign will continue to focus on four issues. They will demagogue abortion. They will obsess over January 6th and democracy in peril, the same people that would take -- allow one elected official to decide I'm going to take that person's name off the ballot because I'm going to declare them guilty of insurrection, even though they haven't been charged or let alone convicted.\par \par \uc2 And they'll keep key evidence out of January 6th. You know, like the fact that we now, know we played it last night, Trump offered the National Guard and in the days leading up to January 6th, why didn't \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  in the January 6th Committee which with a predetermined outcome, why didn't they let us know that?\par \par \uc2 Or exculpatory testimony that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  never tried to commandeer the presidential motorcade? We learned that from the driver of the presidential motorcade that they knew about that they interviewed that they didn't tell us about.\par \par \uc2 The left flat-out lie. They spread fear, hysteria, all things hate Trump, hate Trump 24/7, and, of course, Democrats will call Republicans racist, sexist, xenophobic, homophobic, Islamophobic, transphobic, that want dirty air and water. In other words, Democrats are using fear and division to mask what has been a terrible four years under Biden.\par \par \uc2 I repeat, they cannot run on are you better off than you are four years ago? This is all they have left. They don't really care about democracy or freedom or your rights as an American. No, they prefer one party rule, where Republicans are completely powerless and then if they want to, punish them, and use the Justice Department to go after them.\par \par \uc2 This is the party of censorship, and cancel culture, high taxation, record inflation, woke everything, and regulations galore. The party that defends supporters riots and crime, they ignore that, and lawlessness. They support open borders, sanctuary cities and states, this is the party that weaponized our Department of Justice and politicized it.\par \par \uc2 The party that wants to abolish the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  as we know it, and any other institution that dares get in their way or their agenda .So don't be fooled by the political smear tactics of the left in the next 235 days. However you deserve better.\par \par \uc2 Here now with reaction, FOX News contributor Kellyanne Conway, former White House chief of staff Reince Priebus, and the host of Outkick's "Tomi Lahren is Fearless", Tomi Lahren.\par \par \uc2 All right. Kellyanne, I don't think they have anything to run on. This is what we're going to be stuck with. It's predictable.\par \par \uc2 Now, if Republicans are not prepared for it I blame them, because we know what's coming.\par \par \uc2 KELLYANNE CONWAY, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: No question. So they have a flawed messenger but their messages are terrible. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's underwater in handling of every major issue. His personal attributes are also in the tank. People don't think he's got the energy, the acuity, the ability, the agility. They don't think. He cares about people like them, has a plan for the middle class, and has vision.\par \par \uc2 So what does he do? He's going to Michigan, Pennsylvania, Wisconsin. This is a smart political strategy because politics is about addition. You have to get more votes and the other person electoral votes.\par \par \uc2 All six swing states right now, Sean, Michigan, Wisconsin, Pennsylvania, Arizona, Georgia, Nevada are states that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  won five or six of them in 2016. If you add North Carolina, there he won on that too.\par \par \uc2 But in 2020, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  carried Michigan, Wisconsin, Pennsylvania, Arizona, Georgia by the slimmest of margins when you add it all together. The problem for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is people already know the way they feel about him. He lost independence in his approval rating in August of 2021, after the chaotic unnecessary withdrawal from Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 I looked at \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 's polling numbers, they're the lowest for any vice president in modern history, and they're particularly low among Hispanics, a group that they need to count on big time, a group that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is really reaching into right now, Hispanics, African-Americans, even young people, some women and independents.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  doesn't need to win a majority of any of the five groups I just mentioned. He can get a plurality of them. He can get a third of them. He can get 20 percent of African-Americans, and it's game over.\par \par \uc2 So, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  can fly to Michigan. He can go to Wisconsin, they can double their staffs. They're worried about those third party candidates so far, Robert Kennedy, Jr., Cornel West, Jill Stein all take votes from \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , and they know that Joe Trippi, a smart political strategist, today was quoted by saying what will hurt Biden, what will lose his election for him is, quote, not his age, not Trump's indictments, but these third party candidates chipping away from him.\par \par \uc2 Last point, I am personally offended and everybody should be that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  flew to Minnesota on Air Force Two on taxpayer dollars to go to an abortion clinic, when her boss, the president United States, wouldn't even dare say the word abortion in a State of the Union over an hour. He wouldn't say -- they call reproductive freedom, women's healthcare. If they care about women's health care, what about maternal mortality? What about heart disease? What about all the things that are -- cancer, all the things that are top of the list that are really truly women's health.\par \par \uc2 She went to an abortion clinic today. She's the abortion czar and on taxpayer dollars and they're calling it, Sean, last point, they're calling it -- she made history. No other vice president has ever visited an abortion clinic.\par \par \uc2 There's a reason for that, lady. It's a stupid idea.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Well, Tomi, let's get your take. I think all these issues would be front and center, but it's all going to be about attacking \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , talk about January 6, keep out the exculpatory evidence that either disappeared or they purposely did not give to the American people, they allowed outright lies and false narratives. \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  did that. Liz, where is your character by the way? Really, Liz, you kept all -- you didn't think it was important to bring out the driver of the limousine on January 6 after you allowed other testimony that said something to the contrary.\par \par \uc2 Or the fact that Chris Miller, I have it on tape, and \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and Kash Patel, and even General Milley, according to John Solomon, all on record saying \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  wanted to call up the national guard.\par \par \uc2 They will do and they will say anything, Tomi Lahren, to make sure that they slander, smear, besmirch, character assassinate anybody that gets in their way. They've been trying it, but still \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is ahead in the poll. So far, not working.\par \par \uc2 TOMI LAHREN, OUTKICK HOST: Right. Well, Kellyanne is exactly right. They have a bad message. They have a bad messenger, both as president and vice president.\par \par \uc2 But something that they have that unfortunately we don't is this -- they have unity and they have loyalty. You mentioned \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 , \uc2 Adam Kinzinger\uc2 , there's still plenty that are in office right now that are not unified with the Republican message, that are not unified with the America First agenda, that are not unified behind \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  as our candidate.\par \par \uc2 So they have what we don't have. They all fall in line. They all toe the line, because they also want something that I'm not convinced Republicans want, and that's that they want to win. They want victory.\par \par \uc2 And I'm not as worried about the third party candidates either for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's sake or \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's. I'm far more worried about them replacing \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  with \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2 , and I know everybody says they can't do that, they can't leap frog over Kamala. I say that's BS. Democrats can do whatever they want.\par \par \uc2 If they replace Kamala or Joe with \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2 , you're going to get social media outraged for a couple of days and that's it.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. Tomi, let me -- let me play along with you. Okay, they bring in Newsom, he's the head of sanctuary state, free health care for illegal immigrants, do you -- that has come up time and time again throughout the primary season is the number one issue on people's minds.\par \par \uc2 How's that one issue going to go over? How's his answer on no -- not even having restrictions in the seventh, eighth and ninth month of pregnancy, how's that going to go over with the American people?\par \par \uc2 LAHREN: I worry because right now, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is ahead in the polls. But he's ahead of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  who is a horrible messenger with a horrible record. What happens when you put a 40-something-year-old in that position? You've got a lot of people that they might look at California and think it's a mess, but if that many people are willing to vote for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  again, I'm really worried that they'll be more than happy to vote for \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So that Republicans need to unify, they unify now. We have to want to win.\par \par \uc2 And I'll say the last point on the abortion message, if Republicans have a hard-line abortion stance and they talk about a federal abortion ban, we will lose. That's not helpful and that's just the reality.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I agree 100 percent and as do I think a lot of people. That would -- we saw that in Pennsylvania with \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2 , no exceptions, rape, incest, mother's life, and I think if you want to know why we didn't see a red wave in '22, that was a big reason why.\par \par \uc2 Reince, probably you are one of the most successful \uc2 RNC\uc2  chairs of all time. Let's get your take on what the Republican Party behind the scenes needs to do. We have talked about Republicans, conservatives overcoming their reluctance, their resistance to voting early, voting by mail. We' have talked about Democratic efforts at legal ballot harvesting. Those efforts need to be matched, hopefully surpassed by Republicans.\par \par \uc2 It's now March. This election is in November. That is not a lot of time. They need to make sure every single precinct and every swing state and beyond has trained partisan observers watching the voting all day, the vote counting all night, and have some measure to watch at least mail-in ballots where they're not in the hands of God knows who for God knows how long.\par \par \uc2 REINCE PRIEBUS, FORMER WHITE HOUSE CHIEF OF STAFF: Well, you're right, Sean. So what I would be doing and it's pretty simple stuff, number one, at the committee, you got to raise about $40 million in the next 60 days, number one. Number two, you have to put out paid workers, about 5,000 people in Michigan and Pennsylvania and Wisconsin that are paid full-time on the ground, targeting early vote, absentee ballot voting, using the data.\par \par \uc2 And I put in an artificial deadline to get all of that preliminary data work done by the end of May. And that has to happen now. And they can figure out a lot of the other things as they go.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, following the these court cases going back to pre-COVID laws so that we don't have a gazillion mail in ballots going out to people that didn't or don't want them or didn't request them, and they need to lock down the voting procedures in these states.\par \par \uc2 You know, Tomi is right and so is Kellyanne, what the Democrats are doing by sending \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  in the Saginaw -- Saginaw is about 190,000 people. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  won Saginaw by 300 votes, and his national image is terrible as you've all pointed out. But what he's trying to do by going to Saginaw, on these little -- little events that he's doing, he's trying to get local coverage, local news.\par \par \uc2 And then Harris going into an abortion clinic, yeah, we find it to be ridiculous, but she now wants to become the bullhorn on the abortion issue. And so, all that micro targeting, all that all that boring stuff that I just laid out, they're going to use that micro target, they're going to send 10 people every 10 blocks with 10 names on a piece of paper. They'll get paid a certain amount of money for every person that they bring in. They'll go to Madison. They'll go to Lansing. They'll go to state college in Pennsylvania and that's what they're doing.\par \par \uc2 The Democrats are obsessed with process. They love it. They love the process. Their donors pay for process.\par \par \uc2 And unfortunately, a lot of our folks you know they're crazy about candidates. They like to talk about who's going to win. You know, Bret Baier calls it candidate casino. That's what a lot of our people love.\par \par \uc2 Democrats love process. Our party needs to get in love with the process, the boring stuff, and that's how you win and lose an election.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. Thank you. We appreciate it, Reince Priebus, Kellyanne, Tomi Lahren.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is South Carolina Senator Tim Scott is with us.\par \par \uc2 One of the more interesting things we keep seeing in every poll are -- is the Democratic party, the demographics that traditionally make up their coalition and their base, African-Americans, Hispanic Americans, Asian- Americans, women are now moving in massive numbers to the Republican Party. Now we have seen past movement, not as large as this, but in the end, it seems that -- well, Democrats tend to return home on election day.\par \par \uc2 Is this year different?\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT (R-SC): Well, Sean, 1,000 percent, this year is different. In football, it's not the X's and the O's. It's the Jimmies and the Joes.\par \par \uc2 We have a once in a-lifetime leader called \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  who's bringing a coalition toge