on for 18 months. And to me I think this is an emergency that DeSantis is on top of because he has to be, but the Biden Administration has a border czar. Where is she?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Right? And I thought she's a foreign policy expert. I don't know where all of this could have possibly gone wrong. And desperate people will leave. And believe me, these people are desperate. And if they were to get to the Southern Border, they wouldn't walk across with the neck pillow and iPhone and a new Louis Vuitton suitcase. They have nothing to eat. So, it's a terrible situation.\par \par \uc2 I know that that the Biden Administration got a lot of stuff on their plate all around the world, but now all of a sudden you say immediately, like, we're going to get the Americans out. It's been going on for months and Americans should have been gotten out before this.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: It's -- Dana makes a great contrast, Judge. We spent the last I don't know how long watching people gingerly walk up to the border with suitcases and their fresh clothes. They do not look like they're seeking asylum.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Now you have the real thing it makes -- and it makes the point even clearer.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes, it makes it very clear. But you know, just to add to what Dana was saying is that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  decides he's going to throw money at it and he's going to give $150 million to Haiti. Who's he giving it to, Barbecue? I mean, there's no government. It's just gangs there. And you know, the amazing thing --\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: So, what should he do? I don't -- I still don't understand what you want him to do.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Let me just finish.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: OK. Yes, Judge.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Greg, when you talked about, you know, Haiti is great, Haiti is wonderful when somebody made a comment that it wasn't such a great place. Well, if it's so wonderful and so great, that's not fair either because for years no one has built up Haiti, for years no one has said, look, these people are in a mess, we've got to help them out. Bodies are now decomposing, the prisons are open. Now they're going to -- they're going to come to the United States.\par \par \uc2 And they do have an asylum claim, Harold. My concern is the prisoners, they get out of jail and \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  now is in the same position as Greg Abbott. He's going to try to stop them, and then Biden is going to try to stop \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . And the amazing thing is that, you know, my understanding, Dana, is that State Department doesn't have a plan to evacuate Americans just like Afghanistan. They don't even know how many Americans are there.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: It could be thousands.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: But Dana -- but 18 months ago when this problem was happening, why didn't -- why didn't the Americans who were there leave? I mean, it's also responsible, if I were there --\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: What were they doing there?\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: So, that's a -- that's an even bigger question. But anyway, I don't mean to interrupt. I still haven't heard what President Biden should have done.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Didn't Clinton like invest a ton of money there?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: They not only invested $4.4 billion in aid and \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  oversaw it as Secretary of State. And now, Haiti is a failed state. Where did $4.4 billion go?\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: They wasted it. And you all did too you. All gave money to them, President Bush did. You guys gave -- we've given billions of dollars --\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: I didn't give anybody a dime.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes, I didn't either.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: Well, you did. You just didn't know it.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Guess what? You did.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: You didn't know it.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: I stand or sit corrected, Jesse. Are you going to get Barbecue on your show?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: OK. There you go.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: So, the assassination was very suspicious.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: There were multiple \uc2 DEA\uc2  informants and \uc2 FBI\uc2  informants indicted for being a part of the assassination plot.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Right. They pinned it on the wife. Maybe she did, maybe she didn't, I don't know, but it looks sketchy. And then -- so you have a failed state and the guy is now out of the country. They're going to have to come in with all this cash and there's going to be some really rough guys that are going to have to go in there and hand cash out and just calm things down, pay off the gangs, and then eventually you're going to have to have a free and fair election which no one is going to believe. So, we're going to have to prop up some guy that's going to probably take some orders from the United States, but it never needed to get this bad.\par \par \uc2 And worst possible scenario is this summer you see rafts and Haitian vessels pour into Florida, pour into Texas, Alabama, and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  -- you know the Haitians are like the one group of people he doesn't want coming here. Remember a couple years ago they were amassing under the bridge? He doesn't want them. He does not want them.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes, 12,000 under the Del Rio Bridge.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Right.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Well, maybe we can get a comedic actor in Haiti to become president.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: But the other thing -- I'll just say, when the Marines were sent to Liberia off the coast, and there weren't that many of them, they were sent off the coast of Liberia. Charles Taylor said, sorry, I'm out of here and he left. So, that was one thing that they could have done that they could even do now.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: All right, let's move on, shall we?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Enough out of you. Coming up, Kamala is sharing her deep thoughts again. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Who's your favorite king you've met?\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: You're all kings.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: The Veep revealing what she does all day.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: The hits just keep coming for the sinking ship that's \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's reelection bid. Charlamagne tha God giving his brutal assessment of how things are going for Joe when it comes to the Black vote.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHARLAMAGNE THA GOD, RADIO HOST: When you look at \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's approval ratings, he's getting his (BLEEP) kicked everywhere. It's not just Black people.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It's fair.\par \par \uc2 CHARLAMAGNE THA GOD: So, I think that you know, in order to really get that campaign on the --- on the right track, they got to start looking at the totality of what's happening with that campaign and how different groups of people feel about him.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: And \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is making the rounds in an effort to shore up support for the ticket. But try not to laugh as she struggles to explain what she does every day.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: How do you spend your time --\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: -- what you do?\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: In terms of the work, I'll tell you I have by now we've counted -- I've met over 150 world leaders. I have been a part of pushing forward an agenda that is about what we need to do around gun violence and safety. A lot of my work has been to get more money into community banks to extend access to capital for small businesses.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: OK. You know, Trump supporters, Jesse, among Black voters is up 500 percent in four years. Do you think Charlamagne Tha God is on to something?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: He is. And I don't know if you read Axios the other day, Dana, but they really laid it out very crisply, Judge. There was a great allegiance among Black Americans to the Democratic Party, but it was based generationally on the Civil Rights Movement. They've grown up in the shadow of the Civil Rights Movement. It's fresh in their mind. They identify Black America with the struggle for civil rights.\par \par \uc2 And as generations pass by, young Black Americans are saying, OK, well, what have you done for me lately? Why are we keeping voting for Democrat politicians when things aren't getting better, when they keep talking a lot about the past but we want to talk about the future?\par \par \uc2 Now, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , can you imagine a reporter asking \uc2 Dick Cheney\uc2  what do you do all day? They only ask Kamala what you do all day because she's always justifying her existence. And she's counted the world leaders she's met, Judge. I mean, that's what children do. I've count 150 world leaders. Like, no one cares the number. What have you actually accomplished? She couldn't name it.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: You know, the truth is that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has today visited an abortion center. How do you think that plays to Americans, Dana?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: I'm not exactly sure. I would imagine that this was given some consideration, careful consideration at the White House because she has been designated the person on the Democratic side that is going to lead the charge to fight back against Republican this election cycle on the issue of abortion.\par \par \uc2 And she can spend her time doing whatever she wants. I was floored when I read the headline today that said \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  will make history today by visiting an abortion clinic. And it's not that people who are visiting the clinics are bad people. I'm not at all saying that. But why go and make history to do that? Why not talk about the -- maybe visit a foster care family that bringing in children who need care? There's a -- the foster care system is so critical to our country and gets very little attention, especially public attention. It needs a lot more of it and a lot more support. So, I just thought that that was something today that I wouldn't necessarily have done.\par \par \uc2 It's interesting too, Greg mentioned it yesterday, and it is the issue of men and women separating on many issues and it's not -- and this is -- I actually think this is across all races. I don't necessarily think that that is just either in the Black community or White Community. I think it's across the board. And Black communities -- and Herold can correct me if I'm wrong -- but for a long time like, you know, the culture of life is strong there. So, I wouldn't have gone to the abortion clinic for sure.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes. First on that one. All right, Charlamagne Tha God is now being called a MAGA messenger in a negative way. Isn't he just reading the room?\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: Well, I think he has every right to say what he wants to say. He certainly has an enormous microphone now and he's given -- and he's given his thoughts as he should. I'd say a couple of things just to put everything -- put things in context for one moment. And I've been critical of the of the Biden Campaign. I've tried to be constructively critical giving him advice on how you should go about thinking how you campaign with all voters, particularly Black and Hispanic voters which is the same way you campaign with everyone else.\par \par \uc2 We want good schools. We want safe neighborhoods. We want our country to be safe and we want prices to go down. If you have a message around that, they're going to resonate I think with all voters, particularly Black and Brown voters, Black and Hispanic voters.\par \par \uc2 Two, some context. In '22 -- 2022, we thought that Republicans would really rack up a lot of big victories. The Democrats actually grew their advantage in the Senate by vote in the House. As we sit here today, because \uc2 Ken Buck\uc2  is leaving the House at the end of the month, the Colorado Republican Congressman, the Republicans only have a three-seat majority in the -- in the -- it'll be two-seat majority because Santos, the seat now is gone to \uc2 Tom Suozzi\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 You look at '23, Democrats reelected Beshear in Kentucky. They kept them -- they kept a majority in the State Senate in Virginia and won the State House in Virginia. And then obviously Ohio decided to protect a woman's right to an abortion and made it a constitutional protection there in the state. So, Democrats have something to fall back on.\par \par \uc2 I do think it's on somewhat unfortunate that we're going to have a another campaign around abortion and it's going to be -- it's going to be -- it's going to be messy, it's going to be divisive. But we're going to come down to -- it's going to come down to the border and abortion. And I think the thing that could tip it, if the economy does not get better for everyday Americans, and the numbers seem to be trending in the right direction, but if everyday Americans don't feel more economic -- more economically secure, it'll probably be something in favor of President Trump.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: All right, Greg, I saved this one for you. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is in charge of a Roundtable talking about marijuana reform which I think is kind of ironic given the fact that she's put over 1500 people in jail for marijuana possession, and then when asked about it, she laughed. Do you think she's going to make a lot friends here?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: I have no idea. I will say that I have to agree with Harold. Abortion is -- it's divisive literally and it is messy literally, so it will always remain that way for a lot of people. I think when you look at Kamala and Charlamagne or Lenard McKelvey -- is that his name, McKelvey? That's his name, right?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Oh, I don't know.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Who, Charlamagne's name?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes, yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: His name is Lenard?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes. That's why he changed it. But it's two side of the same story, right? This is a party about abortion and it's a party for women. And men of every color are seeing how they're being abandoned by being branded as the lowest on the totem pole of identity. This is what happens with identity politics, you create a hierarchy, right, of identities and one gets stacked on the other. And all this energy is placed on pigment and not people.\par \par \uc2 And that's a hell of an opportunity cost because as the DEI programs, you know, blossom nothing's being done about prices, crime, illegal immigration. However, you're seeing a lot of conflict between groups of people because that's what DEI does. It even has conflict within DEI.\par \par \uc2 So, I think that the problem with the Dems is that it attracts -- it now attracts a person who attaches herself to every current cause as long as it's politically correct and they're compassion or so-called compassion gives them the excuse to be toxic to everyone else especially other women they don't share their views with, whether it is Riley Gaines or Ashli Babbit, both different types of victims that Liberals don't care about.\par \par \uc2 And I think men at a certain point are like, we can only nod along for so long. Yes, we get it. That's good. That's good. But then it's like, we'll see our way out. And I think that's what -- if you got Charlamagne off to the sides and said what's really bothering you, he said like what is this party, what is this party? It's abortion, abortion, abortion, identity, identity, identity. Why aren't we talking about jobs? Why aren't we talking about prices, like real prices? Why aren't we talking about immigration? Why is it always about my skin color?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Thank you for standing up for men, Greg.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: This is my new crusade. And I'll stand up for you too, Jesse.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Yes, men and boys, thank you.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Coming up, THE FIVE will only be on four days a week if Socialist \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  gets his way.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: Just -- imagine working just four days a week. Senator \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  is serious about this idea. He's so serious that he's pushing a bill that would reduce the standard work week from 40 to 32 hours, but you still get the same pay even if you overtalk someone. The senator getting feisty with our own very -- with our very own Hillary Vaughn when she on it in the Senate hallways. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 BERNIE SANDERS\uc2  (I-VT): We work the longest hours of any people in the industrialized world. I think it's time for a shortened work week.\par \par \uc2 HILLARY VAUGHN, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK CORRESPONDENT: Can I ask you a question about that? It seems like Democrats want businesses to be taxed more, pay their --\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Really, that's not my assumption. I don't think so.\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN: -- pay their workers more, lower prices, pay people not to work.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: You know what Democrats -- you know what I would like to see? I can --\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN: How are businesses going to --\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: Dana, you seem like you might have coached. She was so good there. Whatever you call that. I thought that she was very calm.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: She was so calm. And he was like yelling at her like, get off my lawn, Hillary. Also, I think it would have been funny if she said you're talking about a 40-hour work week, you are a Democratic member of Congress. When is the last time Washington worked 40 hours in Washington?\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: He seems to be fired up about it. What do you think about the proposal?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Anyway, the proposal, here's the thing. I think that -- the reality is I think this is going to happen, maybe not in the next 10 years -- maybe within like 10 years, but for some people it is a reality already. It is happening for them and they're figuring out a way to do it.\par \par \uc2 I also think that having a good job that you love with people that you like working with is such a benefit so good for your mental health, it's great for your socialization, it's great for the economy, it's a great for you to be able to provide for your family and to put uh savings away so that you can have money for a rainy day or save up for your retirement and not think that the government is going to do this for you.\par \par \uc2 Now, remember when \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  said it's going to be so great when we have ObamaCare because then you can just like learn to play the flute and write poetry? Like, that doesn't help the economy. We need people to be able to work. And I just wonder if he thinks of all these people who are coming across to America because they want economic advancement, do you think they only want to work 32 hours? I better hope not.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: Exclamation Point, what are your thoughts here?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Well, first, Dana, not everyone gets to do the job that they love. My knee injury prevented me from my career in Chip and Dales, so here I am at THE FIVE. This proposal never goes away. And I think it will be hard on people like Jesse switching to a four-day work week because then I'll have to work two extra days. But I go by what I see.\par \par \uc2 Technology has allowed you to compress work but also expand and camouflage tedium. So, you get all the work done in a fraction of the time and then you spend the rest of the day on social media, texting, or rereading emails you sent that you were really proud of.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: I do that.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Who doesn't do that?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Wow, I nailed it.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: I nailed it. I wonder what they're thinking. In your face.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: Judge, what do you think about this\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: You know, I don't have a lot of thoughts about it. You know, you read -- one thing, 78 percent of Americans are living paycheck to paycheck. I mean, that's scary. But then you read things like France does it, Norway does it, Denmark works these lesser hours, and they say there's an increase in productivity. I worry about the businesses. I don't know what the businesses are going to do.\par \par \uc2 You know, if you want to reduce your stress level, put the phone down and go for a walk in the woods like Jesse does every morning or you know, just learn to breathe. I've never learned to breathe. It just -- it just makes me -- it gives me anxiety. Once I breathe in, I get anxious. I was like, OK, that's it, I'm done.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: In defense of yourself here.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: I'm just so glad you came to me, Harold, because in Get It Together I interviewed an anti-work activist. And her proposed policy is this, two months paid off, that is not including vacations and holidays. Holidays you don't even have to ascribe to the religion. I can take Kwanzaa, Greg can take Rosh Hashanah even if he's not Jewish.\par \par \uc2 And so, if -- also, if you have a baby, maternity leave is a year and paternity is a year. This is what she wants but she didn't think about how that would result in probably the economy of Spain. No offense of Spain. We love you but we don't want to be Spain.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: Today marks two years since the deadly attack in Ukraine that took the lives of two of our Fox News colleagues and changed Benjamin Hall's life forever. He joins us next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Welcome back. Today marks two years since the attack in Ukraine that devastated the Fox News Media family. A vehicle carrying three journalists was struck in Ukraine by Russian fire. Beloved Fox News Photographer Pierre Zakrzewski was killed. We also lost producer Sasha Kuvshynova. She was just 24 years old. Pierre and Sasha's heroism and hard work will never be forgotten.\par \par \uc2 And Fox News Correspondent Benjamin Hall, he was the sole survivor in the attack and suffered severe injuries which he has overcome. And he's overcome so much physically and has found strength and courage in his journey and he joins us now. And it's wonderful to see you and looking so well.\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN HALL, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Yes, it's nice to see you guys too. Yes.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: On a day like this, what does an anniversary bring up for you?\par \par \uc2 HALL: I think there are two parts to it. One hand is about my own journey, how I've recovered, how far I've come. And I feel immensely grateful for that. And many of soldiers who are injured like I've been injured call it their alive day. It's the day where you look forward and you realize you've been given a second chance at life. Don't waste that opportunity.\par \par \uc2 But on the other hand, I have to remember Pierre and Sasha. Like, I can heal. My injuries can heal. I can walk. I'm fine, but that's an injury that can't heal. And so, I think today is about remembering them, remembering their families, remembering the work they've done, and giving the day to them rather than to me.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: We'll take it around the table for any sorts of questions. You want to go?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Sure. I -- you know, I talk to Joey Jones a lot about this. And when he talks about what happens, it always amazes me the resiliency of the human body and the human mind. How do -- how did you feel about how you responded to it? I mean, were you surprised that you could handle it?\par \par \uc2 HALL: Yes, you know, I've covered a lot of conflicts and I've seen a lot of people injured the way I've been injured, and I always ask myself, how would it be if I was there? You know, would I cope with it? And to be honest, I'm very proud with the way I did cope with it. I knew what was needed. I knew I had to get home to my family. And if you got one goal in mind and you trust the people around you, you can get through it.\par \par \uc2 And I honestly think that that strength is inside everyone. You might not believe it but when your back is to the wall, when you got nowhere to go, you will find that extra strength. And I found that numerous times when I didn't think I could keep going. I told myself I had extra strength. I told myself I could get through pain, and you can. You can. It's inside you. So, people have to remember that.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Judge?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: What about your faith. Is your faith in God, is it -- is it something that you call upon as a part of your life now?\par \par \uc2 HALL: Yes, it's really interesting because when I was lying on the side of the road and my leg was gone, my foot, and I was badly injured, I went to two places. One of them was to my family and I thought of my children, the other one was to God. And you know, I'm a Catholic but I don't go to church that often. Nevertheless that's where I went.\par \par \uc2 When you everything else is taken away from you, I said a prayer. And so, that stays close to me. I still feel very spiritual. Though I'm not going to church as often as I should, but I certainly feel a connection now that I didn't before.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Harold?\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: I thought one of the most incredible parts of your story when you came two years ago, you talked about the vision of your kids and how at that very moment how that -- how that made you react. And two, you wanted to be away from them during part of the recovery because you didn't want them bogged down by this. Tell us just a little bit of that again. I'm -- I was still amazed in it really. It was remarkable to hear you share that part of the story.\par \par \uc2 HALL: Well, it was after the second bomb had landed and I blacked out and I had the injuries on my face. So, shrapnel in the eye and in the throat and my skull. I saw my daughter. I blacked out. And she came to me. And I saw her and she said, daddy, get out of the car. You got to get out of the car. And that was the second before the third bomb hit the car itself.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, she's been incredibly important throughout it, all my daughters have. And they continue to motivate me today.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Your paperback is now out. It's an amazing book. Jesse has a book out.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: I would suggest everybody get Saved.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Not my book, Get It Together. Saved.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: That is so hard you, Jesse.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: You know what? You could -- you could get them both.\par \par \uc2 HALL: There's space for two books on the shelf.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: If you say so.\par \par \uc2 HALL: OK.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: You know, you give so many other people strength when they say, why am I worried, you know. God bless you for what you've done for all of us.\par \par \uc2 HALL: When you get through anything that's difficult in the same way, whether it's losing limbs or whether it's having a really difficult day at work or your family, you think about it and you find a way through.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: All right, guys --\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: God bless you.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Thank, Benjamin.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PERINO: It's time for "ONE MORE THING." Oh, but I'm not in charge.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Oh, I'm not in charge.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: I'll take it from here, children. Oh, this is embarrassing. This is a popular show and nobody takes it seriously, not at least.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: This?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes. Tonight, 10:00 p.m., \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2 , Tyler Fischer, Kat Timpf, Tyrus. This is going to be a great one.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Tonight, "JESSE WATTERS PRIMETIME," the Aaron Rodgers VP threat has people going bonkers. Tune in to "JESSE WATTERS PRIMETIME" at 8:00 Eastern.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Is he on? Is he on?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: He's on after Barbecue.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Oh, OK.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: OK.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Barbecue.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: And Get It Together comes out on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Yes, on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: All right -- don't forget that. All right, there's nothing better than a sweet treat after dinner. One clever toddler named Callie should have made it to the Oscar. She delivers an amazing performance pretending she's eating her food in order to get ice cream afterwards. Take a look at this. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Very good.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Her mother was like, eat that.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: You know what? My sister, Angie, she had that years ago.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: All right, Dana, I'm going to toss. Is that all right?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: You got to toss?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Bye, everybody!\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FOXFIV0020240315ek3e00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Judge Weighs Trump Classified Documents Case Dismissal; VP Harris Visits Abortion Clinic In Minnesota; Officials Give Update On Missing College Student; Judge Weighs Trump Team Push To Toss Docs Case; Awaiting Decision On Fate Of D.A. Fani Willis; Investor Eye TikTok Sale After House Vote; Graham Responds To Schumer Comments On Israel; \uc2 RNC\uc2  Denies Closing Minority Community Centers; \uc2 NCAA\uc2  Facing Landmark Lawsuit Over Trans Policy\b0\par\par\uc2 Martha MacCallum Martha MacCallum, Shannon Bream \par \uc2 8,285 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 March 2024\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: All right, thank you both very much. Good afternoon, everybody. I'm Martha MacCallum. And this is a story that's breaking right now -- a hearing now over, now complete for the day in Florida. That is where former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has been all throughout the day, as his attorneys have attempted to convince Judge Aileen Cannon to toss the 32-count criminal case against him involving classified documents found at Mar-a-Lago.\par \par \uc2 Andy McCarthy joins us in moments, and also later we will speak with the new \uc2 RNC\uc2  co-chair, \uc2 Lara Trump\uc2 , who is here with us on the story as well.\par \par \uc2 And a big sign of where Democrats are putting their focus as November approaches. Moments ago, Vice President Harris in an unprecedented move for president or vice president, she has visited a \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  in Minnesota. As I said, this has not happened before for a sitting vice president at an abortion clinic. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: In this environment, these attacks against an individual's right to make decisions about their own body are outrageous and in many instances just plain old immoral. How dare these elected leaders believe they are in a better position to tell women what they need.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Reproductive rights, a winning issue for the Democrats in 2022 in the midterms right after the Dobbs decision came down from the Supreme Court. In new Fox News polling, it shows 10 percent of voters say that this is the most important issue that drives their vote. And you can see the economy and immigration above those on our list there in the most recent polling.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in Shannon Bream, Chief Legal Correspondent and Anchor of "FOX NEWS SUNDAY". Shannon, good to have you with us. As I mentioned, President \uc2 Obama\uc2  spoke before a \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  conference when he was president. But this is the first time that we have seen a president or vice president actually visit a \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  clinic. What does this tell us about the focus for 2024 here?\par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CHIEF LEGAL CORRESPONDENT: I think you hit it right on the head there, Martha, that this is definitely going to be something to continue on the campaign trail. It is what Democrats know has worked for them in many of the special elections, the midterms, they believe this was an issue that galvanized their base.\par \par \uc2 And remember when she showed up there today and the vice president was speaking about this, she said, I'm doing this as an example of this clinic being especially busy because the states around this one have put limits driving women to this clinic. She talked repeatedly about states that have limited abortion in different ways and said that basically Republicans are trying to take away your rights, your access to health care.\par \par \uc2 A number of states, pro-choice advocates are trying to get these measures on the ballot because they know what drives Dems to get out and make their voices heard on this particular issue.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Absolutely, and the First Lady speaking about it as well, here is Jill Biden with Christina Aguilera in an \uc2 Instagram\uc2  post which they put out yesterday. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JILL BIDEN, FIRST LADY: Extremists are working harder than ever to drag us back to a past we thought was long over.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA AGUILERA, SINGER: But we say our reproductive health care decisions should be made by us and our doctors, not politicians.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, Shannon, it's going to be interesting to see how long- lasting the impact of this is, right? Obviously, in 2022, Republicans felt like they should just ignore it and it would go away, I think, based on what we reported on back then. Now, you see this push from the NRCC that oversees congressional races and advice to them that they are encouraging their members who are running, obviously, every two years in the House to speak about it, to be articulate about it, and to try to meet people where they are a bit on this issue, right?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, and Martha, you and I remember when the conversation from President Clinton was, let's make this safe, rare, and legal. It is a very different conversation now where people are being told to shout your abortions, to go demand that you have access and to all reaches of a pregnancy. We do have states in this country that allow abortion up until the ninth month. We have foreign countries, Europe, it goes at 12 or 15 weeks and so Republicans got to do a better job with that conversation about where the line should be drawn.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, absolutely, and we do remember that they should be safe, legal and rare with \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 . Shannon, thank you very much. We want to go to this breaking news. Nashville police providing an update on missing \uc2 University of Missouri\uc2  student, Riley Strain, last seen with his friends on Friday night in downtown Nashville. Let's listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SGT. BOB NIELSEN, NASHVILLE POLICE: -- to rescue people today. We've also utilized my cold case detectives on foot, travel through the area. We've also used aviation as well as our police boat, which as he mentioned has sonar. In the past couple of days, we've also involved the Office of Emergency Management. They have a boat with sonar and they also have a drone.\par \par \uc2 We've had their drone operator come out here and we've also been utilizing our downtown partnership people and just trying to make any contacts of some of the citizens walking through here including people that live in nearby homeless camps and have been re-canvassing for video and reaching out to some of the bars and businesses down here which I'd like to point out that they've all been extremely cooperative with us. And I'm open to any questions you have.\par \par \uc2 So, the last thing we had on the phone information was all of the pings that they did were based on exigency, so we were able to get that. \uc2 Apple\uc2  has been very helpful with us. We're still waiting on some phone record information. We're still waiting on that data. However, the last ping that they had was off a cell tower near Topgolf, which is on the other side of the river. However, the radius is about two miles, so it doesn't give us a direction of travel or more detailed information. We just know that that's the tower that it hit off of. We're still waiting on the phone records.\par \par \uc2 We don't have the information quite back on the \uc2 Apple\uc2  Watch. I didn't know this. I'm not an \uc2 Apple\uc2  person, but apparently it has its own separate phone number. So, that's one of the things that we're also waiting on is data from that as well. It doesn't tell us exactly why they went down. There could be a variable number of reasons of why both of those went down from we don't know how long he had them charged to, you know, turn them off.\par \par \uc2 I mean, any number of things. But the data right now, the rest of it, his phone records and so forth, we're still waiting on. We know that the last ping off that tower was between 9:55 p.m. and 10:00 p.m. And I believe the last phone conversation or attempted conversation by one of his friends was also during that same time. That was the last anybody had any kind of interaction with him on the phone.\par \par \uc2 Thank you. With the camera footage that we have right now. The last known footage we have of Mr. Strain was coming in this direction, which would be towards us in the northerly direction him. It caught him between the James Robertson bridge and Woodland Street Bridge traveling this way. He was on foot. There was nobody else around him that we can see. We do have some cameras on the back of our birch building which is our courthouse here.\par \par \uc2 However, it doesn't catch him crossing the path of that and they would have caught anybody on either side of the gay street connector. So, at this point we're kind of back canvassing, if you will, going back possible route he may have taken from Broadway up third, down towards Church Street, down in this direction to look for any other cameras we may have missed. As well as look for any other potential employees of some of these businesses that we can talk to that may not have been here the past few days but might have been here Friday night. So, we're still going back and look for more cameras and more witnesses.\par \par \uc2 No, there's no -- absolutely no indication whatsoever there's any kind of foul play or other crime involved. This is strictly a missing person investigation at this point. Obviously, those things can always change. We just don't know. But right now, all of our resources are dedicated towards finding him. We're not conducting a criminal investigation of any type. So, we're utilizing all of our detectives and our other resources, as we mentioned, in just looking for him. Because, like I said, there's no indication he was in a fight or argument with anybody on the street, as far as we know right now or anybody in any establishment he may have visited. So, we're just solely looking at this as a missing person case.\par \par \uc2 So, we don't believe based on the information we have so far from financial institution that he utilized any kind of rideshare. However, I know that the, I believe the family or someone has reached out asking if they may have caught him on video because as we all know, a lot of your rideshare partners will have dash cams. And so, people have reached out to them, hey, if you see anything, can you send it to us and to the police department? But we have not seen any evidence that he would have used that.\par \par \uc2 All the footage that we have so far, he was walking. So, there's no indication that he got into a vehicle, whether a cab or a ride share. And then, of course, until we get his phone records back, we can't see if he utilized any one of those types of applications as well.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: What a terrifying situation for the family What a terrifying situation for the family of this 22-year-old senior at the \uc2 University of Missouri\uc2 . He was on spring break with his friends and campus friends, you know, his mates from the \uc2 University of Missouri\uc2 , and he left Luke Bryan's bar. Apparently, according to the reports, he was asked to leave the bar. They were all out, you know, drinking and having spring break fun that night in Nashville. So, he, according to the records, told his friends he was going to walk home to his hotel and they checked on him and said, yes, I'm going to go back to the hotel.\par \par \uc2 And that is the last that they have heard of him. That happened on Friday night. So, that is the update that we're just getting. We understand that he's very tall, north of 6'5", I believe, according to the reports, blonde hair, and you can see these pictures of him smiling and he's 22-year-old Senior at the \uc2 University of Missouri\uc2 . So, you know, our hearts just go out to his family; they must be absolutely frantic as the search for him goes on. And if we get any more information from this briefing or beyond, we will certainly bring it to you as they search for Riley Strain in Nashville, \uc2 University of Missouri\uc2 , 22-year-old.\par \par \uc2 All right, let's get back to some of the other breaking news that just has come through a short time ago. Here's the first images of the inside of the courtroom as Judge Aileen Cannon in Florida says that she's going to rule promptly on whether or not to toss out the case against former President Trump involving his handling of classified documents. Andy McCarthy is here and later we'll talk to \uc2 RNC\uc2  co-chair, \uc2 Lara Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Former President Trump and Special Counsel \uc2 Jack Smith\uc2  facing off in court today in Florida. The former president trying to get his classified documents case thrown out.\par \par \uc2 Andy McCarthy standing by, former assistant U.S. attorney. But first, to senior correspondent Steve Harrigan, who has been reporting on this throughout and he joins us now from the courthouse in Fort Pierce, Florida. Hi, Steve.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARRIGAN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Martha, former President Trump and \uc2 Jack Smith\uc2  were in the same courtroom for several hours today. No sign of any exchanges between the two or even if the two even looked at each other.\par \par \uc2 The fight today by Trump's lawyers was to try to get this documents case dismissed. They made the argument that every president going back to George Washington had done the same thing Trump had done with documents.\par \par \uc2 The prosecution said, on the contrary, Trump knew what he was doing was wrong and repeatedly failed to return highly classified documents.\par \par \uc2 Perhaps the key question by Judge Cannon, she said the defense, at one point, you're making a strong argument for trial, but I fail to see how this gets me to a dismissal, really giving a pretty big hint there that dismissal is going to be a very big swing, a very tall order that could get this case though delayed to July or August.\par \par \uc2 We are seeing Trump supporters now file out in their vehicle convoy. They have been here since the early morning hours honking away in support of the former president. Martha, back to you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. Steve Harrigan, thank you very much from Fort Pierce, Florida.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in Andy McCarthy, former assistant U.S. attorney and Fox News contributor. Great to have you with us today, Andy.\par \par \uc2 ANDREW MCCARTHY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Thanks, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, it's interesting. Judge Cannon made some interesting points that came out of the courtroom. One of them was she said is this motion premature? You know, are you bringing this before me before we hear anything about immunity from the Supreme Court? It really feels like the motivation for this was hot on the heels of whatever one just watched with Robert Hur, and how he made his decision, in a similar case against the current president.\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: Yes. It's a lot to sort out, Martha. But I think the thing is, the immunity issue before the Supreme Court involves official actions of the president during the time of his presidency.\par \par \uc2 The chargers, in Smith's -- \uc2 Jack Smith\uc2 's Florida indictment involve documents that President Trump was able to get because he was president, but the actions are in his post presidency, the illegal -- with the allegedly illegal retention and the obstruction of the grand jury investigation.\par \par \uc2 So there, there are different issues. But it sounds like what Judge Cannon is homing in on is that the Trump defense has been making claims all along about the former president's handling of these documents, including suggesting that he may have declassified a number of them. That's the kind of thing a judge really can't make a decision about without having taken some testimony.\par \par \uc2 And what this motion was about today was to try to get the case kicked out on the papers, on the legal arguments. And it sounds like she is leaning against that. She wants to hear some evidence.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, what I'm hearing from you is that it doesn't sound as if the immunity issue is something that she is leaning on or waiting for. It sounds from what she said as though, she will treat this case on the merits on its own. She doesn't feel the need to rush it based on anything.\par \par \uc2 And that one of the determinations that will be flushed out in the course of this trial, we assume and based on what you just said is whether or not he designated any of these documents as personal records, and his team says that they do have the authority to have done that.\par \par \uc2 The special counsels team says that he could have -- that he -- that Trump himself said, he could have declassified the documents while he was president, but he didn't. And now he can't, according to some of this testimony.\par \par \uc2 We heard a lot about personal records and Biden assuming that they were all his. How do you think that will hold up in this case for the former President Trump Andy?\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: Yes. Where Biden made that argument, Martha was mostly about the notes that he had taken about classified documents, the crux of the case against Trump or the actual agency records, which simply aren't covered by the Presidential Records Act.\par \par \uc2 Presidential Records Act is really meant to sort out the information that is generated by a White House so that you can figure out what is government property and what is the president's personal property.\par \par \uc2 Most of what's at issue in the case is actually agency documents specifically intelligence reporting from our intelligence agencies. which really aren't meant to be covered by the Presidential Records Act. The former president and his counsel have the theory of why that should be covered, and it sounds to me like, Judge Cannon is saying she wants to hear some evidence to support what their arguments are.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. I'm going to swing you over to the hush money case, Andy. And I want to read to you a headline that we just got a few moments ago. And just to remind everybody at home, this is the New York case that was brought in the matter around Stormy Daniels, and weather -- and the check that was given to her, which was seen as hush money payment that was funneled as a legal payment. And that is the -- that is the question here. Whether or not the hush money was disguised as a legal payment.\par \par \uc2 Now, what we're just learning is that the Manhattan prosecutors -- this is \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2 's case, is now seeking a 30-day delay of this trial, which was supposed to be the first one. It's supposed to start on March 25th here in New York. So, it would have been the very first one to go to trial. The prosecutors who brought the case asked for a 30-day delay, a startling development in this first prosecution based on some new material that they want time to deal with. Andy, your reaction to that as you hear it right now?\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: Yes. A very interesting. It sounds like they apparently have come upon some factual evidence, we presume. I thought that there would be some litigation over the fact that at the 11th hour, former President Trump is trying to extend his immunity argument to the Manhattan case, as well.\par \par \uc2 And he's asked for a delay on the basis of that after getting some discovery about some of what Bragg plan to do at the trial, which he thinks intersects with his -- with his authority while he was president.\par \par \uc2 But this doesn't sound like that. This sounds like they actually have some factual information that's come to their attention that they have to sort out and decide what effect that has on the case.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: Be interesting to see what Judge Merchan does.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Indeed, and all of these delays, I think it's fair to say, work for the former president. He wants to see these pushed as close to or after the election and every way possible.\par \par \uc2 It says, just to fill in you and our viewers, before we move on here, it says that this is so that the defense can review a new batch of records. It only recently obtained these records from federal prosecutors, who years ago invested the same -- investigated the same hush money payments at the center of the case.\par \par \uc2 So, it seems weird that they wouldn't have had all of the information related to that former case and why that would be suddenly discovered at this stage of the game is a question that we'll leave for another day as we get more information on it.\par \par \uc2 But Andy, thank you very much. Always good to have you with us.\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: Thanks, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thanks, Andy.\par \par \uc2 All right. So, in the other case, and that was hard to keep track of all of these, but any minute now, we could learn whether D.A. Fani Willis will be off of the case that she brought in Georgia against the former president in the election interference case there.\par \par \uc2 She is facing misconduct accusations because of her relationship with a -- with special prosecutor Nathan Wade and the vacations that they took together after she hired him, and he was paid state dollars for the job that she helped him get.\par \par \uc2 They both testified that the relationship began after he was hired and Willis testified that she paid for her share of the trips in cash.\par \par \uc2 The judge said that he hopes to announce his decision on whether or not she and he will be allowed to stay on this case by tomorrow, which is Friday, of course, and we expect it could come through as early as this afternoon.\par \par \uc2 Senior correspondent Jonathan Serrie, reporting live from the Fulton County courthouse in Atlanta. Hi, Jonathan.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN SERRIE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Hi there, Martha.\par \par \uc2 All waiting outside of the courthouse for this decision that could come down anytime today or perhaps as late as tomorrow evening.\par \par \uc2 That was the deadline that Judge Scott McAfee set for himself. He said that he intended to stick to that self-imposed deadline while giving an interview to local radio station WSB last week.\par \par \uc2 The judge also said that the fact that he faces a contested special election in May is going to have no bearing on his decision that it will be non-political. And that, in fact, he drafted an outline for this decision, a sort of a rough draft before he even knew that he was going to face a challenger.\par \par \uc2 This judge was appointed by the governor, Brian Kemp just over a year ago. And then, in order to keep his position, he has to go through with the special election in May. Another local attorney recently announced that he intends to challenge the judge in that election.\par \par \uc2 As far as disqualifying \uc2 Fani Willis\uc2  from serving as the chief prosecutor in this case the allegations from several defense attorneys are that she had a conflict of interest both legally and financially, because of her romantic involvement with Nathan Wade, the attorney that she hired as a special prosecutor. But again, Willis and Wade deny that there is any conflict and insist the romance began after she hired him.\par \par \uc2 Martha?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Jonathan Serrie, thank you very much from the Fulton County courthouse in Atlanta. We'll go straight back there if we get breaking news on Judge McAfee's decision in that case.\par \par \uc2 And we also have a lot to get to with Shark Tank investor \uc2 Kevin O'Leary\uc2  as progressive senator, Bernie Sanders makes the case Americans should be working less, 32 hours.\par \par \uc2 We're also going to talk to him about his idea that he wants to buy TikTok, when we come back.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. BERNIE SANDERS (D-VT): We held a hearing on a 32-hour workweek, because what we have seen is that over the last 50 years, despite a huge increase in worker productivity, almost all of the wealth has gone to the top one percent. While 60 percent of the people living paycheck to paycheck, many of our people are exhausted.\par \par \uc2 We work longest hours of any people in the industrialized world. I think it's time for a shortened workweek.\par \par \uc2 HILLARY VAUGHN, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK CORRESPONDENT: Can I ask you a question about that? It seems like Democrats want businesses to be taxed more, pay their --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Really not. That's not my assumption.\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN: Pay their workers more, lower prices.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: I don't think so. No. We think -- You know, what Democrats --\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN: And now, pay people not to work.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: You know, what I would like to see -- I can --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN: How are businesses going to survive that? That's the question. How can businesses survive all of those proposals?\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Well, I think that -- when this -- the Bezos pays an effective tax rate, lower than the average worker, I think we have a real problem on our tax system.\par \par \uc2 I think that billionaires have got to stop paying their fair share of taxes. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KEVIN O'LEARY\uc2 , CHAIRMAN, O'LEARY VENTURES: It's not going to get banned because I'm going to buy it. I can put a syndicate together of debt and equity as long as I can get the blessing of the House. Because nobody wants to fund this thing if they think it will buck politically. But if we make it all American, including the servers, I can get this deal done. I want to buy it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Mr. Wonderful wanting to buy TikTok as the Senate takes up a bill that could potentially ban it here in the United States, or force a sale of the app, which is owned by Chinese company ByteDance. But it looks like he's going to get some competition.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 STEVE MNUCHIN\uc2 , FORMER SECRETARY OF TREASURY: I understand that technology, it's a great business and I'm going to put together a group to buy TikTok.\par \par \uc2 AARON SORKIN, CNBC ANCHOR: You're trying to buy TikTok?\par \par \uc2 MNUCHIN: I am because it should be owned by US businesses. There's no way that the Chinese would ever let a US company on something like this in China.\par \par \uc2 SORKIN: You say, have you already put a group together?\par \par \uc2 MNUCHIN: Yes. Well, I'm working on it.\par \par \uc2 SORKIN: You're exploring a group?\par \par \uc2 MNUCHIN: I've spoken to a bunch of people.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. Let's bring in \uc2 Kevin O'Leary\uc2 , Chairman of O'Leary Ventures and Shark Tank star, and investor. Kevin, great to have you with us.\par \par \uc2 I think it's great to see people like you and the former Treasury secretary, who are both interested in taking this and making it an American company. How far have you gotten in this process? And what do you think about the fact that Mr. Mnuchin is also interested?\par \par \uc2 O'LEARY: I think there'll be lots of competition. I know of five different groups being formed but we all face the same challenge. And in the end, there'll probably be only two at the table.\par \par \uc2 Here's the problem. We don't yet know how to deal with the fact that the database, the Texas Project it's called, of American people that's sitting in American servers, \uc2 Oracle\uc2  servers in Texas. That's fine. But the algorithms that make this work, they're not here in America, and they're never going to get here. The Chinese are never going to sell that. They passed that law in 2017 and made it even harder in 2019. They do not allow companies like DanceByte to sell this technology anywhere. And so, it won't be coming United States.\par \par \uc2 So the challenge for all of us is putting syndicates together that will buy this, not knowing really what the cost is to recreate the algorithm that makes this platform work. And I remind everybody, in recent times, the last transfer of ownership of a significant social media platform was \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , syndicated at 42 billion, today trading it between 12 and 18 on a mark to market for those investors on the debt and equity side who paid for it. This is still private, but you took it public today, you probably be down 60%.\par \par \uc2 Because that -- they also tried to change the algorithms on that. So it's not clear how that part is going to work. But here's the real problem, you have to sell this new code, this new company, this new American version, to all of the users, the 5 million small businesses, all the advertisers, and that's where you have to choose your syndicate properly, who's going to doing that? I think I'm a pretty good steward of that process so let the competition begin.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I think you could, you know, market it in a way that is patriotic, and that promises people a seamless crossover from one platform to the other platform, which obviously, is a little bit tricky. But here's what \uc2 Rand Paul\uc2  said this morning. He said this is completely unAmerican to try to ban TikTok in the United States. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. RAND PAUL (R-KY): This company is owned not by the Chinese Communist, it's owned by 60% of them are international investors, 20% of it is -- two inventors of it, who are Chinese software engineers, and then 20% of the companies owned by their employees, including 7,000 Americans. I think all those people, if you accuse them of doing something like sharing data with a communist government, you have to prove that. You don't just get to accuse somebody of something and then take their company.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: What do you say to that, Kevin?\par \par \uc2 O'LEARY: It's a difficult challenge, and he's right. If you actually look at DanceByte, there's many American investors, there's also sovereign wealth funds like ICH of the United Arab Emirates, and G42 also out of the United Arab Emirates, they're all shareholders. However, the Chinese government passed a law on domestic Chinese companies, including this one, DanceByte. They simply can't transfer technology.\par \par \uc2 And what Congress is worried about is a concept called cognitive warfare. And this is why the Indian government, when looking at TikTok, said no. They weren't going to let it happen. There's no TikTok in India.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: That's right.\par \par \uc2 O'LEARY: So unless you resolve this concept of cognitive warfare, in other words, stop it from happening in the US, this thing is going to pass the Senate too, and then the games will begin. Yes, I'd like to buy it after that happens.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. \uc2 Kevin O'Leary\uc2 , thank you very much. We've got some breaking news, but we're going to stay on that story. We hope you'll come back soon. And \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 , the senator from South Carolina, speaking on the Senate floor after Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  said Israel needs to hold new elections. He said it should happen after the threat from \uc2 Hamas\uc2  has "radically reduced." Schumer calling Israeli Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  a major obstacle to peace. Let's listen in.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN LIVE COVERAGE)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2  (R-SC): -- Saudi Arabia to recognize the one and only Jewish state. That's so easy thing for the Crown Prince to do given this environment. We're trying to get Israel to take a leap of faith here that it doesn't have to be this way all the time, to do some things that would allow Palestinian community to reorganize.\par \par \uc2 75% of the Israeli people do not support a two state solution now. They've been terribly wounded. There is no support by any politician in Israel, Gantz, Lapid, anybody to unilaterally to declare a Palestinian state. Five presidents of United States have said that if there's ever a Palestinian state, it will come through direct negotiations without conditions between the parties.\par \par \uc2 And the Trump administration, \uc2 Jared Kushner\uc2  had a plan to establish a Palestinian state that Prime Minister Netanyahu actually agreed with. The point here is that, what should America be doing now? Americans should be helping Israel without qualification. We should be trying to find a way to ease the suffering of the Palestinian people. And the best way to do that is to destroy \uc2 Hamas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The reason so many Palestinians have been killed, is because \uc2 Hamas\uc2  uses them as human shields. We live in a world that's literally upside down. We're having prominent Democratic members, people I respect, calling on the Israeli people to take their government down, I can't believe it. I thought it was a joke about somebody was pranking me this morning.\par \par \uc2 This is a departure in a very serious way about how the United States interacts with this allies, I think has done enormous damage to very delicate negotiations. I hope that Senator Schumer will revisit this. I don't know he's trying to please by saying that, but they're not worth pleasing. Because I don't know who you're trying to please by saying that the Israeli government needs to cease to exist, as it is today, and Israeli people need to find somebody better in the eyes of Senator Schumer.\par \par \uc2 I'm not asking the Israeli people to elect somebody I like. I'm asking them whenever you have an election, elect somebody that you like. I'm not asking the people of Israel to bow to my view of how to settle this matter after the largest loss of Jewish life since the Holocaust. I want to give unconditional, unqualified support to the people of Israel to destroy \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . After World War II, Mr. President, if anybody had suggested to America that we need to take our foot off the gas when it come when it came to destroying the Nazis and the Japanese, you had been run out of town.\par \par \uc2 What won the Oscar, a film called "Oppenheimer" talking about how the atomic bomb was created and used by our country to destroy two cities in Japan to end the war. You have to understand, and you do, Mr. President, you have done your homework. You'd have to understand that October 7th to the Israeli people, is Pearl Harbor and 911 on steroids. It's not just a tip for tat with \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . It was an attempt by \uc2 Hamas\uc2  to break the back of the Jewish people, to brutally rape and murder in a fashion they want the world to see.\par \par \uc2 So the Israeli perspective on what to do is similar to what we thought we should do after World War II, total complete victory. Everybody mobilize, do what you have to do to end the war, to take the Nazis down and the Imperial Japanese Army, destroy it unequivocally.\par \par \uc2 Millions of people were killed in World War II. War is literally hell. But when you've been attacked the way we were on Pearl Harbor and 911, you have to respond forcefully, you have to make sure it never happens again. And the only way Israel can do this is to destroy the military capability of \uc2 Hamas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So why did this happen, Mr. President? I believe that the great Satan, which is Iran, wanted this to happen to prevent the reconciliation between Saudi Arabia and the State of Israel, ending the Arab-Israeli conflict, a nightmare for the ayatollah that the Arabs and the Jews make peace and economically integrate leaving them behind.\par \par \uc2 So, Israel has signed agreements with six of their Arab neighbors, under Bibi's leadership. When I go I meet with Lapid, I meet with Gantz, I meet with Bibi, I meet with everybody, because it's not about Bibi, it's not about Gantz, it's about our relationship. And if it's Gantz or Lapid next time, I'll meet with them. I know them well --\par \par \uc2 (END LIVE COVERAGE)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  from South Carolina as the battle heats up between Senate Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  of New York and Senate Minority Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , and now \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  as well after Schumer called for essentially the Israeli people to remove \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  from leadership in Israel and to replace him. He has had some very vociferous pushback on that from the other leaders in the Senate. So we'll continue to stay on that story as \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  continues his speech on the floor of the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And coming up, we will speak with \uc2 RNC\uc2  co-chair, newly installed, \uc2 Lara Trump\uc2  coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So the \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2 's denying report today that it plans to shut down community centers focused on reaching out to minority voters under the new chairman, Michael Whatley, just as polls show former President Trump gaining ground among minority voters. 23% of black voters say that they support him in a new recent New York Times Siena poll more than double the 8% who voted for him in 2020. Those are huge jumps among black voters. President Biden's support dropping with black voters, almost 30 points. This is a huge dynamic to watch in the 2024 election.\par \par \uc2 And the former President is leading among Hispanic voters by six points after losing that group by almost 30 points back in 2020. So let's bring in the \uc2 RNC\uc2  co-chair \uc2 Lara Trump\uc2 , the former president's daughter-in-law. Good to have you here, Lara. Thank you very much for joining us today.\par \par \uc2 LARA TRUMP, CO-CHAIR, \uc2 RNC\uc2 : Well, thank you, Martha, great to be with you. And those numbers are a very big deal. It's why you see, I think, the panic on the Democrat side right now. The truth is, you can lie to people only for so long until they wake up and they realize this isn't working. And people all across this country are feeling the negative impact of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  as president. 65% of the country in that same polling said, we are on the wrong track. They want change. And the amazing opportunity people have right now is that they can compare two presidents.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was president for four years, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has been president for over three years, and you see the disaster that has ensued in just three years time. People --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, let's talk about that.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : -- want change, they want \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  back.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So you have a new role as co-chair of the \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2 . There's been a lot of discussion about fundraising and how the DNC has brought in a lot more money than the \uc2 RNC\uc2 , and a report in the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  that Republicans closed several minority outreach centers in battleground states more than a year ago. You, of course, weren't there at the time and didn't retain the minority media outreach directors.\par \par \uc2 So, what do you say about those reports? How significant do you think these minority outreach centers are? Do you think you need them or you don't need them based on what you're seeing out there? What's the plan?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Yes. Well, actually, we are keeping those all open. I can't report that today, so that is incorrect. And I think it's very important. As I just said, it doesn't matter who you are in this country, everybody, understands what's at stake I think in this upcoming election, and it is minority communities that have been significantly impacted by the bad policies of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 As you just read, Martha, you had 8% black vote for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in 2016 and right now he's polling at around 23%. That is huge, and that is very detrimental to the Democrats. And it's something we're paying close attention to, I can assure you, at the \uc2 RNC\uc2  and at the Trump campaign.\par \par \uc2 This is a wide open tent, and the idea that this is a party closed off to anyone is false. We want everyone to come in. We want everyone to vote because we want everyone in this country to succeed. So those reports are not correct.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So -- OK. Thank you for clearing that up. So let's take a look at this from Jamie Harrison, who is the chair of the DNC. Here's what he had to say.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JAMIE HARRISON, CHAIR, DNC: As they say the \uc2 RNC\uc2  is down bad. They're taken out lines of credit, laying off staff and they can't even afford to spend money on their racist down ballot. But this is a good thing, they are paying \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's legal bill. So as I've said with you earlier, man, go Lara, go girl, you are doing it. You are knocking it out of the park. Keep doing it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: What's your response to that, Lara?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : That's very cute. We're not paying any legal bills from the \uc2 RNC\uc2 . And actually this weekend, I personally had $2.7 million pledge to me. It was our largest digital fundraising weekend since 2020, so we're getting to work. And while things may be changing at the \uc2 RNC\uc2 , they're changing rapidly. And one of the things that changing is going to be our fundraising. So they can sit back and watch, and I can promise you, we are paying attention not just to the top of the ballot, but all the down ballot races as well.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, I know the folks on those down ballot races are very interested in support from the \uc2 RNC\uc2 . We'll, hopefully, continue this conversation because I want to talk to you about voting and what you're doing in that area as well next time we have an opportunity to talk. So thank you very much, Lara. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Thanks, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So coming up, more than a dozen former and current female collegiate athletes are taking action against the \uc2 NCAA\uc2 . This is a huge move in this fight. They are claiming that the \uc2 NCAA\uc2  violated Title IX by allowing biological males to compete in college against women. A story exclusive with the two swimmers who have launched this suit today, Riley Gaines and Reka Gyorgy coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Breaking today, a first of its kind lawsuit to protect fairness and women's sports, 16 current and former female student athletes allege that the \uc2 NCAA\uc2  is transgender policy is a violation of Title Nine that reduces female competitive opportunities and deprives women of their constitutional right to bodily privacy in locker rooms. At the center of the lawsuit is trans-athlete Lia Thomas, who results in the 2022 \uc2 NCAA\uc2  swim championships directly impacted my two guests, Riley Gaines is a former University of protective swimmer and host of the "Gains For Girls" podcast on OutKick, and Reka Gyorgy, swam for \uc2 Virginia Tech\uc2  and is now the Director of Operations for the school's swim and dive team. Both are part of today's filing. Ladies, great to have both of you with us. Thank you for joining us today.\par \par \uc2 So, Riley, let me start with you. What is the goal of this lawsuit and if you win, who would be impacted?\par \par \uc2 RILEY GAINES, FORMER UNIVERSITY OF KENTUCKY SWIMMER: Well, thank you very much for having us on. The \uc2 NCAA\uc2 's most basic job is to protect the fairness and safety of competition in collegiate sport. But instead, what Reka and I faced, and what countless other female athletes continue to face at the hands of the \uc2 NCAA\uc2 , is discrimination against us on the basis of our sex, which is of course, everything that Title IX was passed to prevent from happening.\par \par \uc2 So the \uc2 NCAA\uc2  is failing on their most basic duties to provide fair, equal and safe competition, not just for women but really for everyone. And who this would impact, what we are hoping for out of this lawsuit is one, accountability, two, responsibility and, three, for the \uc2 NCAA\uc2  to implement Title IX by its original intent. So, it would impact every athlete who competed at the collegiate level and, of course, future athletes who hope to compete at the collegiate level.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So I don't know if you've seen, Reka, any response from the \uc2 NCAA\uc2  on this lawsuit yet, have you?\par \par \uc2 REKA GYORGY, FORMER SWIMMER FOR \uc2 VIRGINIA TECH\uc2 : No, I haven't.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. So to tell me a little bit about, you know, in some ways when I read the stipulations on all of these programs, about testosterone levels that need to be tested in three different ways over different time periods in order to allow some of these trans-athletes to participate in competition. I mean, I'm wondering if some of these programs might not welcome not having to go down that road anymore. It's very complex situation that they got themselves tied up in with all of these different regulations.\par \par \uc2 I wonder how you think this is going to go over with universities, whether they're going to fight this hard, or whether they might welcome it.\par \par \uc2 GYORGY: Yes. I don't know, personally, how it's going to go down, but I hope for the best that each university will have the opportunity to use their resources, everything to go down this path and how future athletes to have an equal opportunity when it comes to racing against somebody who's a biological male in the female sports.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, it's interesting, when you look at the transcript of the conversation that Charlie Baker, the head of the \uc2 NCAA\uc2  had with Senator Mike Lee. Lee asked him, has the \uc2 NCAA\uc2  assess the physical, emotional, psychological harm of its transgender inclusion policy on female athletes? And what are the findings? If not, why not? And Baker responded that they hadn't conducted any research related to their current policy in that regard. Riley, what do you think about that?\par \par \uc2 GAINES: Well, I'm not surprised. This is the same approach that former President \uc2 Mark Emmert\uc2  had now, of course, Charlie Baker. What we've seen across the board, as it pertains to the \uc2 NCAA\uc2 , is it's full of cowards, spineless, morally bankrupt cowards at that.\par \par \uc2 So, look, we're hoping they do the right thing. Honestly, I believe that people within the \uc2 NCAA\uc2  are hoping that this is a successful lawsuit. Because honestly, I don't think they want to be troubled with making this decision, because it puts them, it puts Charlie Baker in a difficult position. Same thing, the states that are implementing, you know, statutes that prevent males from competing in women's sports at the state level, I believe that the \uc2 NCAA\uc2  wants these states to get this legislation through. So it takes it off their plate because again, they want to avoid accountability and responsibility at all cost.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Reka, your final thought on that. Do you think that this is something that's going to be successful?\par \par \uc2 GYORGY: I really do believe that it's going to be successful. We have very great group of women standing up and speaking for others. And, you know, we gave the opportunity to people to support as they can visit this website called (inaudible).com, and they can get all the updates on the case going forward.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. Reka Gyorgy, thank you very much. Riley Gaines, thank you. You deserve a lot of credit, both of you, for pushing this forward and we'll be watching where it goes. Thank you very much, ladies.\par \par \uc2 GYORGY: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAINES: Thank you so much, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right, guys. So that is THE STORY on this Thursday, March 14th. THE STORY goes on. Thanks for being here today, everybody. I look forward to seeing you back here tomorrow and Friday. In the meantime, "Your World" starts right now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020240315ek3e00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 State Of The Union Address\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Martha MacCallum, Peter Doocy, Dana Perino, Harold  Ford, Jr., John Roberts, Brit Hume \par \uc2 9,264 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 March 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Live Event\par \uc2 FXLV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: The Supreme Court justices waiting as they just finished a big case moments ago.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Yeah, it was mentioned earlier that Speaker Johnson had asked people to be -- to have decorum. We'll see.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Let's take a pause right now, listening to the House floor.\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: A live look at the U.S. Capitol, a beautiful evening actually here in Washington as we are just moments away from President Biden and tonight's State of the Union address.\par \par \uc2 Good evening from Washington. I'm Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Good evening, everybody. I'm Martha MacCallum.\par \par \uc2 Any moment now, President Biden will be announced on the House floor with the familiar call of the sergeant at arms. He will make his way down the aisle, greeted by lawmakers along the way.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You can see \uc2 Mitt Romney\uc2  talking to Joe Manchin. It seems like they are pretty engaged in some conversation. A lot of that happens on the sidelines, as you have a lot of lawmakers waiting a long time in the chamber, a lot of folks expressing themselves, different ways, Republican some of them wearing buttons about the border, others dressed in white on the Democratic side in solidarity.\par \par \uc2 Let's set the stage now with White House correspondent Peter Doocy with a preview of the president's speech.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret.\par \par \uc2 Officials here at the White House think that most people that are going to watch it tonight have not been very politically active so far this cycle. So, they see it as a clean slate to tell what they described as an American comeback story where President Biden will talk of how many jobs have been created since 2021. He will blame Republicans for border problems and he will suggest his age shouldn't be an issue because \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is roughly the same age.\par \par \uc2 The president has also been practicing dealing with hecklers when he talks about Gaza potentially from progressives in his own a party -- Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK. We're waiting for President Biden to get into position.\par \par \uc2 Peter, thank you.\par \par \uc2 We have walked down and they have timed all of this out but we also have the walk out after the speech and we expect the speech to be about one hour, but with applause lines, you never know, the State of the Union Address.\par \par \uc2 I do -- I do know who knows, Dana Perino, who's been around the block, the co-anchor of "America's Newsroom", co-host of "The Five"; \uc2 Harold Ford\uc2 , Jr., former Tennessee congressman, co-host of "The Five"; John Roberts, not co- host of "The Five", co-anchor of "America Reports"; and FOX News chief political analyst Brit Hume joins us remotely.\par \par \uc2 Brit, you -- we have covered so many of these. What do you think about tonight?\par \par \uc2 BRIT HUME, FOX NEWS CHIEF POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, I think unlike a lot of these speeches where we are preoccupied with what the president will say about this issue are that issue or what programming they will announce or what steps they will announce they are taking to deal with for example the border, what the big focus tonight is on really is on his demeanor, how he seems. Does he seem energetic and authoritative and will he be smooth and polished?\par \par \uc2 You know, we've seen so many stumbles and so many awkward moments, falling down, just getting caught in the middle of sentences, losing his train of thought, and all of that. I would not discount the possibility at all that the president will come here tonight, be very well-rehearsed, very well- prepared, very well rested and will deliver a speech with a lot of energy and clarity after which, of course, we can watch while the Democrats and their allies in the media will ring down saying that that puts the senility and the age and acuity issue to rest. It doesn't but that is what I suspect we'll hear.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Brit, one of the things tonight is a contrast to the White House is saying between President Biden and his likely challenger, soon to rack that all up, former President Trump, who is taking to Truth Social already, saying, quote, the president is very substantially late, not a good start but let's give him the benefit of the doubt. I'm sure he had very important thanks to do, but now just getting into the car and will have to drive very quickly, you just don't want to be late to the State of the Union. They need Mario Andretti to be at the wheel of the limo.\par \par \uc2 I think it's going to be a play-by-play all night long on Truth Social.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I think that's true. Yeah, we're going to wait for the commentary.\par \par \uc2 Dana?\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, CO-HOST OF "THE FIVE": One of the things I'm watching for is that President Bush used to remind us that every speech, you have multiple audiences. One of the audiences for President Biden tonight is his own party, the disaffected members of his own party, those who voted not willing to commit, those who wanted to maybe see him do something else in Israel, though -- I mean, what more could he do in terms of bending over backwards for ten voters in Michigan. And they still protest him tonight and want to disrupt his speech.\par \par \uc2 But, you know, the speech might tell us if the concerns about the problems within his own party are a minor annoyance or a genuine crisis.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: John, it's always an interesting storyline. One of them is expelling GOP Congressman George Santos is on the floor. It's a privilege that former members have and he decided to take it. It's just a bizarre thing that's happening.\par \par \uc2 But your thoughts on the speech tonight.\par \par \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS, CO\uc2 -ANCHOR OF "AMERICA REPORTS": I think a bit of a minor story in this overall, I think we're going to see here tonight is opening salvo in the main general election campaign. And there's indications that the Democrats will be fighting this around abortion very hard.\par \par \uc2 We've got a couple of guests here in the first lady's box, Latorya Beasley. She was an IVF patient in Alabama who is just about to go to her embryo transfer and had to delay because they shut down IVF. Another is Kate Cox who had a baby with Trisomy 18, she could not get an abortion in Texas, she had to go out of the state.\par \par \uc2 And in his speech, this is from an excerpt so we are okay talking about this, this is what the president will say: Clearly those bragging about overturning Roe v. Wade and no clue about the power of women in America, this will be a five-minute applause line, but they found out when reproductive freedom was on the ballot and one in 2022, 2023, and they will find out in 2024.\par \par \uc2 So while the Republican's may be putting immigration front and center watch for the Democrats to put Roe v. Wade and the president pledged against the second term and again the Democratic Congress to instill federal protections for abortion.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Harold Ford Jr., you know, it's an issue he does not like to talk about a whole lot, President Biden, but basically wedged into having to speak about this tonight, he doesn't usually use the word abortion when he talks about it. He's Catholic, all those issues around this, but it is something that he needs to -- he needs to dig in on this issue.\par \par \uc2 HAROLD FORD, JR., CO-HOST OF "THE FIVE": It's one of the contrast that you speak up between the two parties. I think overarching in all of this, people around the country, Americans are fed up with the rancor, with the division .The most recent congressional election saw Democrat elected Tom Souzzi mentioned George Santos with his district that Souzzi was elected and he's come on shows all across this network saying people want us to work together.\par \par \uc2 I hope that President Biden tonight, we have said it all along, be cheerful, not contentious. Be optimistic, not ominous, the American, people want their president to talk about how they will get things done.\par \par \uc2 And finally to those in the chamber, somebody who had the blessing the privilege to serve in that chamber, I hope members of Congress and chamber guests show the respect that the presidency deserves, show the respect that the country deserves, that's not deal with the gimmicks and some of the outbursts but let the speech play out and let Katie Britt give her speech. And if President Trump wants to respond afterwards, we'll let him do that as well.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It's great point. I mean, you know, people do look to these speeches. They are opportunities to lift up the country, what we see in these numbers, 70 percent think the country is going the wrong direction, it's really astonishing how down people are, down about the economy, very down about what is going on at the border. They feel like there is a loss of control in the country. And so, you know, we talk about the low bar, just getting through the speech, but, Dana, there is an opportunity here, if he seizes it and is able to do it.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: I think that the low bar, it's interesting that you say that because the mood in the country is pretty dark and if you like in a way people kind of tuned out Washington. They don't think it's going to help them, they don't see or hear from Washington about how things are going to get better for them and also the chaos that President Biden talks about from the Trump years, I think that when you see people say, 72 percent of people say the country is going in the wrong direction, that's because they feel like their lives are chaotic now.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Let me bring in, former House Speaker Kevin McCarthy. He joins us.\par \par \uc2 Mr., Speaker, you've been in this position. You've been in that chair behind as the president delivers speeches, you have to control your facial remarks and how you react to things.\par \par \uc2 Your thoughts on this night? Do you miss this moment?\par \par \uc2 KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA), FORMER SPEAKER OF THE HOUSE: Yeah, I miss any opportunity to be there. It's a unique moment because for members themselves, it such a great privilege but very seldom do you get the House and Senate, you get the cabinet members, you get the joint chiefs, you got the Supreme Court. So, it's a time to interact with one another.\par \par \uc2 But the part that the listeners and viewers really don't know is, whatever somebody says in that chamber is you can hear, but you don't get a pickup on telephone. When you sit behind the president, it's actually hard to hear him, when he speaks there. And it is a moment in time for whoever is president to really have a bully pulpit, to lay out and speak to the American public.\par \par \uc2 Now, if I'm going to talk to my former members of the Republican conference, I would say don't take the bait if he wants to say something negative to get you to react. Be polite, listen to him and go through because it never plays well on television.\par \par \uc2 What is interesting is before this night starts, there will be a whole group of Republican's and Democrats who will go greet the president before hand from the chambers to the side and escort him in. And then as the speech begins, you'll see whether he's going to lay out, and this is an election year so I know this is going to be an election speech with election guests and for Republicans, listen. Be respectful. And get through it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You see Senator Mark Kelly, in the distance, the chief justice, and Justice Sotomayor. It's interesting that obviously, in the background of all of this, the legal cases, some of them at the Supreme Court including immunity, that will be decided soon.\par \par \uc2 I think we have the First Lady Jill Biden arriving.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Jill Biden arriving in the chamber she is joined as John pointed out before by two women who have had expenses with IVF and with abortion, also a cancer nurse is joining her this evening, several guests. I mean, this whole tradition used to be maybe one or two people. Now, the list from the White House is about 20 people long, there's \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , the former speaker of the House. You know, it's hard to say how much of an impact these guests have, but let's listen in.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: Mr. Speaker, the president's cabinet.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And so the cabinet walks in.\par \par \uc2 Speaker McCarthy, we Secretary Blinken behind him, Secretary Austin, the defense secretary, obviously, at all of the controversy about not telling the president when he was in the hospital. This cabinet has a lot -- around the block here with a number of different controversies, but they're also dealing with war and Ukraine and Israel.\par \par \uc2 How much of the speech do you think is going to be aimed at the goals of the election and laying out a vision for his next term?\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: I think he's going to try to be talking to his own base. He wants to motivate them to get them behind him because that is a place that President Biden currently has a -- has a real problem with. He wants to talk about successes, and it would be smart of him to talk about where they can work together.\par \par \uc2 This is one chance to have a very big audience in America, to control exactly what you're going to say without being asked a question and to lay out why you deserve to be reelected again in the process, and what have you achieved?\par \par \uc2 You know, this election is different. When you ran for the office, it was open. But now it's a report card on the job he has done, so he's going to try to check off and say, I kept this promise, this promise, and this promise. And if you elect me again, this -- this is further I will be able to go.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Speaker McCarthy, \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2  entering the room. We just had Dana Sullivan of Veterans Affairs, a difficult week over the famous World War II kiss photograph which some underling hoped to cancel from all of the V.A. hospitals and get rid of that photo. There is Mayorkas, obviously, the man who is the head of the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And here is the message from former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . There's nothing he can say tonight that can absolve him from letting 15 million people into our country legally. They will probably blame me but I have the safest border in the history of our country. So, that won't go very far he says. All kinds of different numbers over how many people have gotten into the country. That one is a very high number.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: One of the cabinet members always the designated survivor. It's not Kiefer Sutherland in the show.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It's not?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It is the Education Secretary Cardona tonight, who is not in the chamber in case, well, continuity of government, someplace else.\par \par \uc2 We're going to wait as the cabinet members come through and then we will get the announcement from William McFarland, House sergeant at arms.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in the panel.\par \par \uc2 Harold, your thoughts on this night as we sit at the table here, the former president is right, the president is behind schedule here. Usually these things are to the minute but this is a big moment for President Biden.\par \par \uc2 FORD: Look, obviously, President Biden may be saying that everyone thinks I'm old, they joked about how I go to bed early, well, I'll show up, I'll show up a little late, I'll speak a little longer, and I'll stay in the chamber a bit longer than some do.\par \par \uc2 I think we've all framed it right. He's got a couple of things that he can deal with. He can talk about the issues and he can talk about the things he wants to do. The one thing that he can't do is reverse his age.\par \par \uc2 So, he's got to show strength tonight. He's got to show he's in command tonight.\par \par \uc2 And he too can't be baited by some of the things that some in the chamber may say and how they may heckle him. I think he's going to be up to the task tonight.\par \par \uc2 The bar is a little low for him or I should say lower than it is for most presidents. So I think you will exceed it and I think what Speaker McCarthy was saying, he's got to not just reach out to the base, he's got to show he's seeking answers and not acrimony, and that someone on this panel says quite well, he's got to seek a little common ground on some of the important issues.\par \par \uc2 If he is able to express that, it will be a big success for him tonight. We'll see.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: One area where common ground would be very helpful is on the border, John Roberts, and we heard earlier today that we should not expect any executive order that he will pull the levers that are at his disposal as president of the United States to do remain in Mexico, and catch and release, he's not going there.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: No, he's standing by his proclamation that he has done all he can and that there's nothing else that he can do. Whereas, you know, anybody will tell you that President Trump got the lowest immigration numbers in recent history, pretty much totally through executive orders.\par \par \uc2 And, Bret, I think somebody is watching because we mentioned a second ago that the Democrats are going to fight this election on abortion, Republicans are going to try to fight on immigration. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  just put out on Truth Social, there is nothing he can say, Biden, that can absolve him from letting 15 million people into our country illegally. He'll probably blame me, but I had the safest border in history.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: If the former president wants to call and we are happy to put him, instead of just reading the Truth Social posts.\par \par \uc2 FORD: He just may.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yeah, he may just might.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: But it's an indication that, you know, it's going to be immigration against abortion I think as the main issues.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Brit, a lot of it is how this is delivered because there's so many stories about, you know, his age and what things look like. What about that thing that Harold talked about, the low expectations, that low bar that essentially he says?\par \par \uc2 HUME: I think there has been a low bar set, by what we have all witnessed, I don't think it will be hard for the president to clear it. And he will be greatly aided I think in terms of how the public reacts if \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2 , who has already said I think today to us here at Fox that she feels that decorum is not an issue because, you know, decorum was broken by what \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  did about this or that or the other thing. And, of course, she yelled at him last time he was in the chamber and she or someone else on the Republican side may do that again.\par \par \uc2 And I don't think that it will go down well with the audience if any Republican does that, or anybody does that, and it will probably be of help to him.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yeah. Last time, he felt that he parried quite well with \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2 . She was bringing up -- he basically accused Republicans of wanting to get rid of Social Security or Medicare, she called him a liar in that moment, and here we go.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM MCFARLAND, HOUSE SERGEANT AT ARMS: Mr. Speaker, the president of the United States!\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: This could take a while everybody knows President Biden, he likes to work the room. In fact, the last State of the Union, getting out of the chamber, it took more than 20 minutes. Usually, they gave him 10 minutes from speech to door and it took him a lot.\par \par \uc2 But, you know, all these lawmakers, they post up and try to get face time with President Biden or the president at hand, both parties try to get a word in and a moment, let see if we can listen for a second.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Behind him obviously, you can see the leadership as Speaker McCarthy was talking about, Senator Schumer and Senator McConnell who just recently announced that this is his last time representing Kentucky, being the leader of the Republicans in the Senate. And you have Steve Scalise and the other leadership in the House escorting body on both parties.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Republicans and Democrats welcoming him, there you have a glimpse of \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  she has a big red Make America Great hat on. And she is ready to roll.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The shirt says, "Say her name for Laken Riley" in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: She's trying to hand him a button. Let's see if he pays any attention to her, I think that's the button about the Biden's border crisis I believe is what those buttons say, a number of the Republicans are wearing and he'd -- unsurprisingly went right by MTG.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: He may hug the left side of the aisle during that part of the walk down.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You can see the "say her name" Laken Riley shirt, there is an empty chair in the chamber tonight to represent Laken Riley. Her family was invited by Congressman Michael Collins. Understandable, they are not ready to attend anything at this point. They are deeply grieving and this president has not reached out to her family at all. She no doubt is becoming a symbol of the so many individuals who have been violently attacked and in her case murdered by someone who is in the country illegally.\par \par \uc2 This is an outrage issue for many, many people across this country. The president's polling is dismal on this.\par \par \uc2 That's Tom Souzzi, the new congressman who took George Santos' seat in Long Island.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So, Brit, you know, we've covered so many of these and usually, \uc2 Sheila Jackson Lee\uc2  who was -- there she is, even as I called her name out.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Right on cue.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Right there, posted up. You've seen her there many times, Brit.\par \par \uc2 HUME: You know, it's an amazing moment because you see these politicians without any hesitation just reaching over and leaning over, leaning in, touching the president and looking for a moment on national television where they can be seen in proximity to the president, it may all look a bit craven or weak at least but they all -- look at them all, they all do this, this goes on for as long as I can remember.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Dana?\par \par \uc2 HUME: The president looks in good form tonight it seems.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Again, and that's why I say this is set up for the president to have success. I mean, your -- the speech is written for you buy a team of professionals who have a chance to practice for several days and everyone's, you know, excited to see you. So he's enjoying that moment.\par \par \uc2 The one thing I do want to mention is that as you see the cabinet file and I don't know if this is the least cohesive cabinet that I have seen in the time that I have been covering these things. I believe that they've only have like fewer than ten cabinet meetings during his administration. You think of all the problems they have on the border or education or even when it comes to natural disasters, why not call them together? They are ready- made for the media and they are all there for him and I'm sure they would take issue with what I am saying but you don't ever see them actually utilized.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Great point. I mean, I remember when President Trump was in office, he would often have those open events where every -- all the members of the cabinet were sitting at the table. They would take questions, he would take questions very freewheeling with the press in the room, we haven't seen that.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You know, it's five minutes since he entered the chamber so far. Usually, the walk in is not the long time with the selfies and the conversation but this one is taking a little while. He's got a speech that is going to be, we are told, roughly about an hour but maybe with applause, you know, you are talking about long time here.\par \par \uc2 There's Kirsten Gillibrand from New York. Now he's taking the picture with Chris Coons, and how's on the campaign. And Democrats obviously will be cheering. There will be other times where you're going to have Republicans react, and Democrats react. It's quite something. It is drama in Washington.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: He did not stop to talk to Joe Manchin, somebody who a lot of people thought he was flirting with a potential third-party run. In the last polling that we did, 18 percent are looking for another candidate and whether or not that will materialize, RFK Jr. obviously is already in there as an independent.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: He chatted with Manchin just a bit and he's sitting with \uc2 Mitt Romney\uc2  which is obviously Republican and Democrat, sending a signal there.\par \par \uc2 Speaker McCarthy still with us. You watched presidents walk down this aisle. Final thoughts before we get to the speech and what you think this is shaping up to be as he shakes the hands of the Supreme Court and the chairman of the joint chiefs there.\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: One thing I've noticed from all presidents is that President Biden takes more time walking down and walking out. He literally waits until everyone leaves. I've never seen anything quite like it.\par \par \uc2 But uniquely, he is going right around the Supreme Court, he will go to the joint chiefs and then he will walk up to the stage there and he will hand his speech to the speaker and to the vice president, and then he'll turn around and he'll have the teleprompters right there for a speech that he has practiced. There is an old story where one of the speeches that the State of the Union that \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  was giving, he had to turn back around and tell \uc2 Al Gore\uc2  that they had last year's speech in the teleprompter.\par \par \uc2 He turned around and started giving it from memory. I mean, it's a nerve- racking -- when you sit in that position and look out, the room is very small, even though it looks big on TV and it's -- it gets a little nerve- racking at that moment in time. But you do command a great deal of presence sitting -- standing there.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yeah, speaking of speeches, obviously, the moment when Speaker Nancy ripped up President Trump's speech after that, that was one moment that was remembered after one State of the Union.\par \par \uc2 You are seeing him there.\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: That will never happen again.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That will never happen again.\par \par \uc2 Bernie Sanders --\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: I thought I got to keep the speech.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And you didn't.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yeah, there's been a few pieces.\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: Yeah, they take it back from you. They give you a very nice folder and I thought I'd get that for history. I thought I'd get -- be able to keep the history the speech but it's all taken back.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yeah. Well, I don't think you -- they didn't take the ripped up speech, I don't think. I think they threw that one away.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yeah, that was a remarkable moment.\par \par \uc2 MACCARTHY: I think they put it back in the archives.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: As he makes his way to the podium there, he will hand as mentioned the speech to Speaker Johnson, a new relationship here between the two, and to Vice President Harris.\par \par \uc2 Let's listen in.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. Good evening.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 If I were smart, I'd go home now.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 Mr. Speaker, Madam Vice President, members of Congress, my fellow Americans, in January 1941, Franklin Roosevelt came to this chamber to speak to the nation, and he said, "I address you in a moment unprecedented in the history of the Union."\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Hitler was on the march. War was raging in Europe. President Roosevelt's purpose was to wake up Congress and alert the American people that this was no ordinary time. Freedom and democracy were under assault in the world.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, I come to this same chamber to address the nation. Now it's we who face an unprecedented moment in the history of the union. And, yes, my purpose tonight is to wake up the Congress and alert the American people that this is no ordinary moment either.\par \par \uc2 Not since President Lincoln and the Civil War have freedom and democracy been under assault at home as they are today. What makes our moment rare is that freedom and democracy are under attack at -- both at home and overseas at the very same time.\par \par \uc2 Overseas, Putin of Russia is on the march, invading Ukraine and sowing chaos throughout Europe and beyond. If anybody in this room thinks Putin will stop at Ukraine, I assure you he will not.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: But Ukraine -- Ukraine can stop Putin.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Ukraine can stop Putin if we stand with Ukraine and provide the weapons...\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: ... that it needs to defend itself.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: That is all -- that is all Ukraine is asking. They're not asking for American soldiers.\par \par \uc2 In fact, there are no American soldiers at war in Ukraine, and I'm determined to keep it that way.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: But now assistance to Ukraine is being blocked by those who want to walk away from our world leadership.\par \par \uc2 It wasn't long ago when a Republican president named \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  thundered, "Mr. Gorbachev, tear down this wall."\par \par \uc2 Now...\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Now my predecessor, a former Republican president, tells Putin -- quote -- "Do whatever the hell you want."\par \par \uc2 (BOOING)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: That's a quote.\par \par \uc2 A former president actually said that, bowing down to a Russian leader. I think it's outrageous, it's dangerous, and it's unacceptable.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: America is a founding member of \uc2 NATO\uc2 , the military alliance of democratic nations created after World War II prevent - to prevent war and keep the peace, and today we've made \uc2 NATO\uc2  stronger than ever. We welcomed Finland to the alliance last year.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 And just this morning, Sweden officially joined, and their minister is here tonight.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 Stand up.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 Welcome. Welcome, welcome, welcome.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 And they know how to fight.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Prime Minister, welcome to \uc2 NATO\uc2 , the strongest military alliance the world has ever seen.\par \par \uc2 I say this to Congress -- we have to stand up to Putin.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS)\par \par \uc2 Send me a bipartisan national security bill. History is literally watching. History is watching.\par \par \uc2 If the United States walks away, it will put Ukraine at risk. Europe is at risk. The free world will be at risk, emboldening others to do what they wish to do us harm.\par \par \uc2 My message to President Putin, who I've known for a long time, is simple: We will not walk away.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 We will not bow down!\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 I will not bow down!\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 In a literal sense, history is watching. History is watching.\par \par \uc2 Just like history watched three years ago on January 6th, when insurrectionists stormed this very Capitol and placed a dagger to the throat of American democracy.\par \par \uc2 Many of you are here on that darkest of days. We all saw with our own eyes the insurrectionists were not patriots. They'd come to stop the peaceful transfer of power to overturn the will of the people.\par \par \uc2 January 6th lies about the 2020 election and the plot to steal the election posed a great -- gravest threat to U.S. democracy since the Civil War.\par \par \uc2 But they failed!\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 America stood.\par \par \uc2 America stood strong, and democracy prevailed!\par \par \uc2 We must be honest. The threat to democracy must be defended. My predecessor and some of you here seek to bury the truth about January 6th.\par \par \uc2 I will not do that. This is the moment to speak the truth and to bury the lies.\par \par \uc2 Here's the simple truth: you can't love your country only when you win.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 As I've done ever since being elected to office, I ask all of you without regard to party, to join together and defend democracy. Remember your oath of office, of defending against all threats, foreign and domestic.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 Respect -- respect free and fair elections. Restore trust in our institutions, and make clear political violence has absolutely no place, no place in America. Zero place.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 Again, it's not -- it's not hyperbole to suggest history is watching you. They're watching you. Your children or grandchildren will read about this day and what we do.\par \par \uc2 History is watching another assault on freedom.\par \par \uc2 Joining us tonight (ph) is Latorya Beasley, a social worker from Birmingham, Alabama. Fourteen months ago, 14 months ago, she and her husband welcomed a baby girl, thanks to the miracle of IVF. She scheduled treatments to have that second child.\par \par \uc2 But the \uc2 Alabama Supreme Court\uc2  shut down IVF treatments across the state, unleashed by a Supreme Court decision overturning Roe v. Wade. She was told her dream would have to wait.\par \par \uc2 What her family had gone through should never have happened. Unless Congress acts, it could happen again.\par \par \uc2 So tonight, let's stand up for families like hers. To my friends across the aisle, don't keep this waiting any longer.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 Guarantee the right to IVF. Guarantee it nationwide.\par \par \uc2 Like most Americans, I believe Roe v. Wade got it right. I thank Vice President Harris for being an incredible leader defending reproductive freedom and so much more.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 Thank you.\par \par \uc2 My predecessor came to office determined to see Roe v. Wade overturned. He's the reason it was overturned. And he brags about it. Look at the chaos that has resulted. Joining us tonight is Kate Cox, the wife and mother from Dallas. She's become pregnant again and had a fetus with a fatal condition. Her doctor told Kate that her own life and her ability to have future -- children in the future were at risk if she didn't act. Because Texas law banned her ability to act, Kate and her husband had to leave the state to get what she needed. What her family had got through should have never happened as well. But it's happening to too many others.\par \par \uc2 There are state laws banning the freedom to choose, criminalizing doctors, forcing survivors of rape and incest to leave their states to get the treatment they need. Many of you in this chamber and my predecessor are promising to pass a national ban on reproductive freedom. My God, what freedom else would you take away?\par \par \uc2 Look, in it's decision to overturn Roe v. Wade, the Supreme Court majority wrote the following. And with all due respect, justices, women are not without electoral -- electoral power. Excuse me, electoral or political power. You're about to realize just how much you brought it up.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 Clearly.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 Clearly, those bragging about overturning Roe v. Wade have no clue about the power of women. But they found out when reproductive freedom was on the ballot. We won in 2022 and 2023, and we'll winning again in 2024.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 If you -- if you, the American people send me a Congress that supports the right to choose, I promise you, I will restore Roe v. Wade as the law of the land again.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 Folks, America cannot go back. I'm here to tonight to show what I believe is the way forward. Because I know how far we've come. Four years ago, next week, before I came to office, the country was hit by the worst pandemic and the worst economic crisis in a century.\par \par \uc2 Remember the fear? Record losses. Remember the spikes in crime and the murder rate? Raging virus that took more than 1 million American lives of loved ones? Millions left behind? A mental health crisis of isolation and loneliness. A president, my predecessor, failed the most basic presidential duty that he owes to American people. The duty to care. I think that's unforgivable.\par \par \uc2 I came to office determined to get us through one of the toughest periods in the nation's history. We have. It doesn't make new but -- news, in a thousand cities and towns, the American people are writing the greatest comeback story never told.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 So let's tell the story here, tell it here and now, America's comeback is building a future of American possibilities, building an economy from the middle out and the bottom up, not the top down. Investing in all America and all Americans to make sure everyone has a fair shot. And we leave no one, no one behind.\par \par \uc2 The pandemic no longer controls our lives. The vaccines that saved us from COVID are now being used to beat cancer, turning setback into comeback. That's what America does. That's what America does.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Folks, I inherited an economy that was on the brink, now our economy is literally the envy of the world. Fifteen million new jobs in just three years, a record, a record.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Unemployment at 50-year lows.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: A record 16 million Americans are starting small businesses. And each one is a literal act of hope. With historic job growth and small- business growth for blacks and Hispanics and Asian-Americans, 800,000 new manufacturing jobs in America, and counting.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Where is it written we can't be the manufacturing capital of the world?\par \par \uc2 We are, and we will.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: More people have health insurance today...\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: More people have health insurance today than ever before. The racial wealth gap is as small as it's been in 20 years. Wages keep going up. Inflation keeps coming down. Inflation has dropped from 9 percent to 3 percent, the lowest in the world, and tending lower.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: The landing is and will be soft.\par \par \uc2 And now, instead of importing -- importing foreign products and exporting American jobs, we're exporting American products and creating American jobs...\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: ... right here in America, where they belong.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: And it takes time, but the American people are beginning to feel it. Consumer studies show consumer confidence is soaring. "Buy America" has been the law of the land since the 1930s. Past administrations, including my predecessor, including some Democrats as well in the past, failed to buy American. Not any more.\par \par \uc2 On my watch, federal projects that you fund, like helping build American roads, bridges and highways, will be made with American products and built by American workers...\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: ... creating good-paying American jobs.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: And thanks to our CHIPS and Science Act, the United States is investing more in research and development than ever before. During the pandemic, a shortage of semiconductors, chips, that drove up the price of everything from cell phones to automobiles -- and by the way, we invented those chips right here in America.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 (UNKNOWN): We did!\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Well, instead of having to import them -- instead, private companies are now investing billions of dollars to build new chip factories here in America, creating tens of thousands of jobs...\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: ... many of those jobs paying $100,000 a year and don't require a college degree.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: In fact, my policies have attracted $650 billion in private-sector investment, in clean energy, advanced manufacturing, creating tens of thousands of jobs here in America.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: And thanks -- and thanks to our Bipartisan Infrastructure Law, 46,000 new projects have been announced all across your communities.\par \par \uc2 And by the way, I notice some of you who strongly voted against it are there cheering on that money coming in.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I like it.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I'm with you.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I'm with you.\par \par \uc2 And if any of you don't want that money in your district, just let me know.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 Modernizing our roads and bridges, ports and airports, public transit systems, removing poisonous lead pipes so every child can drink clean water without risking brain damage.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 Providing affordable -- affordable high-speed Internet for every American, no matter where you live, urban, suburban or rural communities, in red states and blue states.\par \par \uc2 Record investments in tribal communities. Because of my investment in family farms...\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 Because I invested in family farms led by my secretary of agriculture, who knows more about this than anybody I know, we're better able to stay in the family for those farms and their children and grandchildren won't have to leave, leave home to make a living. It's transformative.\par \par \uc2 The great comeback story is Belvidere, Illinois, home to an auto plant for nearly 60 years. Before I came to office, the plant was on its way to shutting down. Thousands of workers feared for their livelihoods. Hope was fading. Then I was elected to office and we raised the Belvidere repeatedly with auto companies, knowing unions would make all the difference.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 UAW\uc2  orked like hell to keep the plant open and get these jobs back, and together we succeeded. Instead of auto factories shutting down, auto factories reopened and the new state-of-the-art battery factories being built to power those cars there at the same --\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Folks -- for the folks at Belvidere, I say, instead of your town being left behind, your community is moving forward again because instead of watching auto jobs in the future go overseas, 4,000 union jobs with higher wages are building the future in Belvidere right here in America.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Here tonight is \uc2 UAW\uc2  president Shawn Fain, a great fan and a great labor leader.\par \par \uc2 Sean, where are you? Stand up.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: And Dawn. And Dawn Simms, a third-generation worker, \uc2 UAW\uc2  worker at Belvidere.\par \par \uc2 Shawn, I was proud to be the first president to stand in the picket line. And today, Dawn has a good job in her hometown, providing stability for her family and pride and dignity as well. Showing once again Wall Street didn't build America. They're not bad guys. They didn't build it, though. The middle class built the country and unions built the middle class.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I say to the American people, when America gets knocked down, we get back up. We keep going. That's America.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: That's you, the American people. It's because of you America is coming back. It's because of you our future is brighter. It's because of you that tonight we can proudly say the state of our union is strong and getting stronger.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 (CROWD CHANTS "FOUR MORE YEARS)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Tonight -- tonight I want to talk about the future of possibilities that we can build together. A future where the days of trickle-down economics are over. And the wealthy and the biggest corporations no longer get all the tax breaks. And by the way, I understand corporations. I come from a state that has more corporations invested than every one of your states in the United States combined. And I represented it for 36 years.\par \par \uc2 I'm not anti-corporation, but I grew up in a home where trickle-down economics didn't put much on my dad's kitchen table. That's why I determined to turn things around so middle class does well. When they do well, the poor have a way up and the wealthy still do very well. We all do well. And there's more to do to make sure you're feeling the benefits of all we're doing.\par \par \uc2 Americans pay more for prescription drugs than anywhere in the world. It's wrong, and I am ending it.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: With a law that I proposed and signed, not one of your Republican buddies voted for it, we finally beat big pharma. Instead of paying $400 a month or thereabouts for insulin with diabetes, it only cost 10 bucks to make, they only get paid $35 a month now and still make healthy profit.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: And I want -- what to do next? I want to cap the cost of insulin at $35 a month for every American who needs it. Everyone.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: For years, people have talked about it, but finally we got it done and gave Medicare the power to negotiate lower prices on prescription drugs. Just like the V.A. is able to do for veterans. That's not just saving seniors money, it's saving taxpayers' money. We cut the federal deficit by $160 billion.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Because Medicare will no longer have to pay those exorbitant prices to big pharma. This year, Medicare is negotiating lower prices for some of the costliest drugs in the market to treat everything from heart disease to arthritis.\par \par \uc2 It's now time to go further and give Medicare the power to negotiate lower prices for 500 different drugs over the next decade.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: They're making a lot of money, guys.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: And they will still be extremely profitable.\par \par \uc2 It will not only save lives; it will save taxpayers another $200 billion.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Starting next year, the same law caps total prescription drug costs for seniors on Medicare at $200 -- $2,000 a year, even for expensive cancer drugs that cost $10,000, $12,000, $15,000.\par \par \uc2 And I want to cap prescription drug costs at $2,000 a year for everyone.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Folks, I'm going to get in trouble for saying it, but any of you want to get in Air Force One, we can fly to Toronto, Berlin, Moscow -- I mean -- excuse me -- and -- well, even Moscow, probably.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: And bring your prescription with you, and I promise you I will get it for you for 40 percent the cost you're paying now, same company, same drug, same place.\par \par \uc2 Folks, the Affordable Care Act, the old Obamacare, it's still a very big deal.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Over 100 million of you can no longer be denied health insurance because of a preexisting condition. Well, my predecessor and many in this chamber want to take those -- that prescription drug away by repealing Affordable Care Act.\par \par \uc2 (BOOING)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I'm not going to let that happen.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: We stopped you 50 times before, and we will stop you again.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: In fact, I'm not only protecting it. I'm expanding it.\par \par \uc2 The -- the enacted tax credits of $800 per person per year reduced health care costs for millions of working families. That tax credit expires next year. I want to make that savings permanent.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: To state the obvious, women are more than half our population, but research on women's health has always been underfunded.\par \par \uc2 That's why we're launching the first ever White House Initiative on Women's Health Research led by Jill, doing an incredible job as first lady.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: We will pass my plan for $12 billion to transform women's health research and benefit millions of lives all across America.\par \par \uc2 I know the cost of housing is so important to you. If inflation keeps coming down, mortgage rates will come down as well, and the Fed acknowledges that.\par \par \uc2 But I'm not waiting. I want to provide an annual tax credit that will give Americans $400 a month for the next two years as mortgage rates come down to put toward their mortgages when they buy their first home or trade up for a little more space.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: That's for two years.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: And my administration is also eliminating title insurance on federally backed mortgages.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: When you refinance your home, you can save $1,000 or more as a consequence.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: For millions of renters, we're cracking down on big landlords who use antitrust laws -- using antitrust -- who break antitrust laws by price- fixing and driving up rents.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: We have cut red tape, so builders can get federally financing, which is already helping build a record 1.7 million new housing units nationwide.\par \par \uc2 Now pass...\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Now pass and build and renovate two million affordable homes and bring those rents down.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: To remain the strongest economy in the world, we need to have the best education system in the world.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: And I, like I suspect all of you, want to give a child, every child, a good start by providing access to preschool for 3 and 4 years old.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 You know, I think I pointed out last year...\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 I think I pointed out last year that children coming from broken homes, where there's not books, they're not read to, they're not spoken to very often, start school, kingergarten or first grade hearing - having heard a million fewer words spoken. Well, studies show that children who go to preschool are nearly 50% more likely to finish high school, go on to earn a two- and four-year degree no matter what their background is.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING & APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I met a year and a half ago with the leaders of the business roundtable, they were mad that I -- they were angry. I said, well, they were discussing why I wanted to spend money on education. I pointed out to them as Vice President, I met with over 8 -- I think was 182 those folks don't hold me the exact number and I asked them, what they need most, the CEOs and you've had the same experience of both sides now. They say a better educated workforce, right? So I looked at them, and I say I come from Delaware, \uc2 DuPont\uc2  used to be the eighth largest corporation in the world and every new enterprise they bought they educated the workforce to that enterprise, but none of you do that anymore. Why are you angry with me providing you the opportunity for the best educated workforce in the world? They all looked at me and said, I think you're right.\par \par \uc2 I want to expand high quality tutoring and summer learning to see that every child learns to read by third grade.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING & APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I'm also connecting local businesses in high schools so students get hands-on experience and a path to good paying job whether or not they go to college. And I want to make sure the college is more affordable.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING & APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Let's continue increasing the Pell Grants to working in middle-class families and increase record investments. In HBCU, the minority serving institution including Hispanic institution.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING & APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: And I was told I couldn't universally just change the way in which you did -- that was student loans. I fixed two student loan programs that already existed to reduce the burden of student debt for nearly four million Americans including nurses, firefighters, and others in public service. Like Keenan Jones, a public educator in Minnesota who's here with us tonight. Keenan, where are you?\par \par \uc2 Keenan, thank you. He's educated hundreds of students so they can go to college. Now, he's able to help after debt forgiveness get his own daughter to college.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING & APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: And folks, look, such relief is good for the economy because folks are now able to buy a home, start a business, start a family while we're at it. I want the public-school teachers a raise.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING & APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: And by the way, the first couple years, we cut the deficit. Now, let me speak to the question of fundamental fairness for all Americans. I've been delivering real results in fiscally responsible ways. We've already cut the federal deficit. We've already cut the federal deficit over a trillion dollars.\par \par \uc2 I signed the bipartisan deal to cut another $1 trillion in the next decade. It's my goal to cut the federal deficit another $3 trillion by making big corporations, the very wealthy, finally beginning to pay their fair share.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING & APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Look, I'm a capitalist, if you want to make or can make a million or millions of bucks, that's great. Just pay your fair share in taxes.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING & APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: A fair tax code is how we invest things to make this country great, health care, education, defense, and so much more. But here's the deal, the last administration enacted a $2 trillion tax cut, overwhelmingly benefit the topping 1 percent, the very wealthy and the biggest corporation and exploded the federal deficit.\par \par \uc2 They added more to the national debt than any presidential term in American history. Check the numbers. Folks at home, does anybody really think the tax code is fair? Do you really think the wealthy and big corporations need another $2 trillion tax break? I sure don't.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FXLV000020240308ek370002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  Exits Without Backing Presumptive GOP Nominee; \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  Endorses \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  Despite Past Acrimony; President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  Reaches Out To \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  Supporters; Renewed Push To Diminish Social Media TikTok Outlet Influence; Chinese Warships Clash With Philippine Coast Guard; Beijing Boosts Budget While Pentagon Remains Stagnant; Beijing Drops "Peaceful Reunification" Plan With Taiwan; Voters Dissatisfied With Likely Nominees; \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  Suspends Presidential Campaign After Former President Trump Wins Most Super Tuesday State Primaries; Pollsters Examine Divides In Republican And Democratic Party And Possible Effects On Presidential Election; MSNBC Host Decides Not To Show Former President Trump's Victory Speech On Super Tuesday; \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  Calls On President Biden To Debate Him For Presidential Election; President Biden Prepares To Give State Of The Union Address\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Peter Doocy, Chad Pergram, Bill Melugin, Bryan Llenas,  Jennifer Griffin, Howard Kurtz, Juan Williams, Ben Domenech \par \uc2 7,601 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 March 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Goodbye great white. Sorry about that.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, RFK Jr. and the guy who is going to win the nomination for the Democrats, Jason Palmer, the Samoan dragon, 8:00.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: I'm going to watch all of those shows. That's it for us, have a great night everyone. Hi, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: A lot about Samoans tonight. A lot about, OK.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: I mean, do you have one on your show?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I don't and I don't have any cookies either.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: OK.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, thanks, Dana.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, welcome to Washington. I'm Bret Baier. A rematch of the 2020 election between President Biden and former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  seems inevitable on this Wednesday after the Super Tuesday primaries. The current president was essentially unopposed and the former president has vanquished his lone remaining serious challenger.\par \par \uc2 Former South Carolina governor and former ambassador \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  suspended her campaign today after winning just one of the 15 state primaries on the Republican side last night.\par \par \uc2 She is not however throwing her support behind the presumptive nominee, at least not yet. We'll have the first Fox News power rankings for the presidential election shortly.\par \par \uc2 First up though, Correspondent Bill Melugin starts us off tonight live from Charleston, South Carolina. Good evening, Bill.\par \par \uc2 BILL MELUGIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Bret, good evening to you. \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  had been facing mounting questions about the viability of her campaign heading into Super Tuesday last night. Last night did not go the way she was hoping so. Today, right here in Charleston, she announced her exit from this race.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY\uc2 , FORMER U.S. AMBASSADOR TO \uc2 U.N.\uc2 : The time has now come to suspend my campaign. I said I wanted Americans to have their voices heard. I have done that. I have no regrets.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN (voice over): Former South Carolina Governor \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  calling it quits in a quick and concise speech this morning. After winning only the state of Vermont on Super Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 HALEY: In all likelihood, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  will be the Republican nominee when our party convention meets in July. I congratulate him and wish him well.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN (voice over): While Hayley was gracious to Trump, she did not endorse him. Instead, she urged him to earn the support of Republicans and independents who have splintered away from him.\par \par \uc2 HALEY: At its best, politics is about bringing people into your cause, not turning them away. And our conservative cause badly needs more people. This is now his time for choosing.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN (voice over): Haley began the race last year polling at one percent amongst a pool of over a dozen candidates. She ended up being the last one standing against Trump and the first woman to ever win Republican primary state.\par \par \uc2 And although I will no longer be a candidate, I will not stop using my voice for the things I believe in. In this campaign. I have seen our country's greatness.\par \par \uc2 From the bottom of my heart. Thank you, America. God bless you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN (on camera): But in the short term, \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  says she's looking forward to becoming a private citizen once again. But looking at the bigger picture here, several questions are now developing. Where are \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  supporters going to go, is the relationship between \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  going to be fixed and is \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  going to end up endorsing \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in the coming months? We'll have to wait and see. Bret, back to you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Bill Melugin live in Charleston, South Carolina. Bill, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Former President Trump predicted an avalanche of delegates on Super Tuesday and that is just what he received. Now, his focus is on the general election and another battle with now President Biden. That is eight months from yesterday.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Bryan Llenas has that part of the story tonight from West Palm Beach, Florida.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BRYAN LLENAS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  taking another victory lap tonight as \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  suspends her campaign. Trump riding on Truth Social, "\uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  got trounced last night in record setting fashion."\par \par \uc2 And even though Haley did not endorse him, Trump said he'd, "Like to invite all of the Haley supporters to join the greatest movement in the history of our nation."\par \par \uc2 It's possible Trump could reach the 1,215 delegates needed to officially secure the Republican nomination as soon as next week.\par \par \uc2 Last night, the presumptive Republican nominee attacked President Biden and called on the GOP to unite.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We have a great Republican Party with tremendous talent. And we want to have unity and we're going to have unity, and it's going to happen very quickly.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS (voice over): Today, Senate Minority Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  endorsed Trump despite years of animosity. Trump personally attacked McConnell's Asian American wife \uc2 Elaine Chao\uc2 , who he called Coco Chow and China loving. McConnell said Trump is practically and morally responsible for the January 6th Capitol riot. Yet today, McConnell said Trump has his vote.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY): I've said in February of 2021, shortly after the attack on the Capitol, that I would support President Trump if he were the nominee of our party, and he obviously is going to be the nominee of our party.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS (voice over): The Trump campaign now focused on Biden and winning over independent voters.\par \par \uc2 JASON MILLER\uc2 , DONALD TRUMP CAMPAIGN SENIOR ADVISER: Maybe they went with President Trump in 16. Maybe they went with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  in 2020. They're back with President Trump now and everyone says they were better off with Trump.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 LLENAS (on camera): The Trump campaign is so confident that former President Trump they believe that he can win Minnesota and Virginia, which haven't voted Republicans since 1972 and 2004 respectively, but if they plan on expanding that electoral map, they're going to need money and thus far, the Biden camp has about twice as much cash on hand as Trump's, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Bryan Llenas in West Palm Beach, Florida. Bryan, thanks.\par \par \uc2 A Georgia State Senate committee is investigating allegations of impropriety against the prosecutor in the election interference case involving former President Trump. Fulton County District Attorney \uc2 Fani Willis\uc2  is accused of financial irregularities and conflict of interest.\par \par \uc2 Today, Defense Attorney Ashley Merchant explained why she wants Willis removed from the case, this is in the state Senate. The judge in that case has also decided what to do. Merchant is representing one former president's co -- the former president's codefendants in the case.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, the California Senate race in November will feature Democratic Congressman Adam Schiff against Republican Steve Garvey. They finished first and second respectively in yesterday's primary. Garvey is seeking to become the state's first elected GOP senator since Pete Wilson in 1982 and 88. He's a former Major League Baseball star, first baseman for the \uc2 Los Angeles Dodgers\uc2  and \uc2 San Diego Padres\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, President Biden emerged from Super Tuesday relatively unscathed with one loss in American Samoa and some protest votes in various states. Now though, it is on to the general election.\par \par \uc2 White House Correspondent Peter Doocy reports tonight live from the North Lawn. Good evening, Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret. There might not be anything President Biden can do to win back progressives mad there isn't a ceasefire right now. So, President Biden is trying to put together a new coalition.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: How do you beat \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in Wisconsin Madam Vice President?\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: One vote at a time.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): That includes \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  voters. President Biden said today, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  made it clear he doesn't want \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2 's supporters. I want to be clear, there is a place for them in my campaign. Biden brass in Wilmington write in a memo that they assess Trump does not have the resources or the infrastructure needed to run a winning general election campaign.\par \par \uc2 That says Trump reportedly met with \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  about campaign cash, leading the billionaire to post