erein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FOXFIV0020240130ek1t00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Biden Projected To Win New Hampshire With Write-In Campaign; Trump Projected New Hampshire Winner, Haley Vows To Fight On\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Martha MacCallum, Kellyanne Conway, Kayleigh McEnany,  Jessica Tarlov, Dana Perino, Brit Hume, Trey Gowdy, Shannon Bream, Bill  Hemmer, Charlie Hurt, Katie Pavlich, Marc Thiessen, Juan Williams \par \uc2 8,998 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 January 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Live Event\par \uc2 FXLV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Grinding out a win in the Granite State, former President Trump emerging victorious tonight. \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2 , saying this is far from over.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, everybody. I'm Martha MacCallum.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: And I'm Bret Baier. Welcome to our snowy special coverage of the NEW HAMPSHIRE PRIMARY. I think it's a snow globe.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It is.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: In the Fox (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 Fox News projecting former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has won New Hampshire. It was a big win. We are just don't know how big. Right now, it stands at 11.5. It is in the double digits. It looked like undeclared voters were going to close the gap here.\par \par \uc2 But some of these late counting areas are coming in bigger for the former president. And that number is going up. 11.5 right now.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, on the Democratic side a writing campaign, propelling President Biden to victory. The President was not on the ballot after the DNC opted to skip the state in favor of South Carolina. Skipping Iowa and New Hampshire.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Meanwhile, former President Trump characterizing his win as a knockout blow. \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2 , saying not so fast. She said at first.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY\uc2  (R), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: New Hampshire is first in the nation. It is not the last in the nation.\par \par \uc2 The worst kept secret in politics is how badly the Democrats want to run against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 AMERICAN CROWD: Yes!\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: She did very poorly, actually. She had to win. The governor said, she is going to win -- she is going to win -- she is going to win. Then, she failed badly.\par \par \uc2 We'll see you soon. We'll see you on the trail.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So, the former president clearly came out and was not happy with the fact that \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  came out first, well before the former president speech and said essentially that she was moving on and fighting on.\par \par \uc2 In fact, the e-mail I just received, two e-mails in a row, team Haley, this fight is not over. And then, the next e-mail, President Trump, the race is over.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So, I think that you just see in those two e-mails exactly how this is going.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And it's a little ugly at this moment between these two, Kellyanne.\par \par \uc2 KELLYANNE CONWAY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: A little bit. But remember, a couple years ago, we all had a look at the dress, some said Laurel some said Yanni, this is (INAUDIBLE) happen sometimes.\par \par \uc2 But I think, President Trump also sees Nikki sometimes as ungrateful. He made her \uc2 U.N.\uc2  Ambassador. She never mentioned who put her there.\par \par \uc2 In the 16 years, I believe, of presidents \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  and President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 . That position was an ambassador position. It was not a Cabinet position, and President Trump exceeded and allowed that to be a Cabinet position.\par \par \uc2 And I think if people like \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  are so upset that Republicans didn't won in 2018, the question is very simple. Why did you leave in October and September of 2018, and then not go and campaign for all those Republican presidents.\par \par \uc2 She could have joined Vice President Pence on the trail and helped usher in a Republican majority here in New Hampshire, where she had the support of the sitting governor, \uc2 Chris Sununu\uc2 . He has presided over a record number losses of state legislative seats. They went from a 54-seat majority when he first got elected to just a three-seat majority now. That's a fact.\par \par \uc2 She endorsed General Bolduc. She thanked him on the station and he and his wife, I thank him for her service in the military. But she endorsed in here and campaigned for me, loss by nine points. So, there is a lot going on with Republican candidates who underperform.\par \par \uc2 I think her better messages would be if she talked about the America First agenda and promised the voters including the Trump voters, that she would do exactly what he's done, but do it differently. Her differences with him tonight were not about policy. Her differences were about personality or cognitive tests.\par \par \uc2 And I don't know, I think going to South Carolina far is high risk, low reward. She is losing in the Real Clear Politics average by 30 points in our home state. And last point, the statewide elected officials in South Carolina. The governor, the attorney general, lieutenant governor, the members of Congress, the two United States senators, they were all here this weekend campaigning for Trump.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: For the former president.\par \par \uc2 Kayleigh, you know, in the close, it seemed like the Trump campaign was pretty disciplined. They hit the immigration issue a lot at his rallies. And we saw his pitch at the town hall and the speech in Iowa. This was a different speech. You know, he is a fighter, he punches back. He obviously didn't like the speech you heard from \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But he's also, at some point, has to get into general election going after independence and maybe disaffected Democrats.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 KAYLEIGH MCENANY\uc2 , FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And this speech probably whatnot.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: No, the speech was a speech to dispose of the last remaining competitor. And if you could call it that, she is still significantly behind him. He's pulling a majority support. But to get her out of the race, I would suggest that adopting a general election tone is what you do at this point.\par \par \uc2 And here is why I say this. This was actually a fairly good night for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . When you look at our voter analysis, only 10 percent said, I would not vote for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , if he is the nominee.\par \par \uc2 He won a plurality of voters who said he was too old, he won a majority of voters who were upset about the Gaza war. So, the divides in the Democrat Party, and this is a small sample size, but perhaps aren't as stark, as one would think.\par \par \uc2 But when you look at the Republican Party, seven in 10, \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2 , voters said I would not vote for Trump. There is a Des Moines Register poll 43 percent said, no, I wouldn't vote for Trump.\par \par \uc2 If I'm Trump, I sit back and I exclusively focus on the general election, I take the posture of a presumptive nominee, I focused on number one, uniting the party, and number two, winning the independence, which \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  won 55 to 39 percent.\par \par \uc2 That's what I would do. \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2 . I mean, the closest margin is 30 percent in the states ahead. For all intents and purposes, he is the presumptive nominee.\par \par \uc2 You know, in the 2015 campaign, it was all about bringing out new voters about electrifying the base, which has stuck by President Trump throughout all of this really Jessica?\par \par \uc2 And but I wonder if they're going to pivot this time to try to get some of these voters that are voting for \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  that some of them have voted for \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  who maybe is more in his lane.\par \par \uc2 But the \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  voter, are they going to try to get them do you think?\par \par \uc2 JESSICA TARLOV, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Well, I'm certainly not an advisor to the Trump campaign. But, it is -- you know, we talk in the millions of votes when we're talking about the general election, but it actually comes down to the tens of thousands. Right?\par \par \uc2 The margins in Georgia and Arizona, in Michigan, and Pennsylvania, it's minut. Right? It can be the difference between whether you came out and you criticize your opponent's dress, which some could interpret as demeaning a woman. Again, which is obviously something that he suffers from, or just making a little offhanded. Comment that someone says, you know what, I'm going to sit home or actually, I want four more years of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So yes, it would be smart as it always was when he was president, if he would listen to Kayleigh about this.\par \par \uc2 But he has an uncontrollable narcissism and rage about him, when he feels insulted. And it's appealing to the base that we love it. He's a counter puncher. But you've now lost a general election.\par \par \uc2 You've presided over the loss of this -- the Republican Senate, the Republican House. The abortion, it's been on the ballot seven times, Democrats have swept it in all of that. And he has not moderated at all. And frankly, I don't think it's in him for -- him to be able to do it for more than 20 minutes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You know, Charles, he talked about the issues here and how immigration here is at the top, but it also is an economic issue.\par \par \uc2 CHARLES PAYNE, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK HOST: Right.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And it crosses all boundaries. And it is a weakness for President Biden and the Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Dean Phillips\uc2  talks about this on the trail, and he talks about how Democrats demean MAGA voters. He talked about it with me today, saying that, you know, it's just strange that they are talking about a group of voters like that.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes, and I don't think it's narcissism. I think it's -- if you've lived in New York long enough, you know, it's maybe a New York thing. Someone punches you, you punch it back harder.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: That's where I grew up in Harlem -- so --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Now, does the country is at stake. Right?\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: That he won -- he became president of the United States with that same personality. I don't think the -- you know, listen, I think it's worse with Biden calling MAGA.\par \par \uc2 Biden's anger and vitriol and hatred for MAGA is far more worse than President Trump's individual battles with someone who crosses him. That is something that is really detrimental to this country that the president of the United States despises half of the United States.\par \par \uc2 President Biden, I said earlier today, I just (INAUDIBLE) about a week ago. I had a segment coming up. So, I googled Biden hates MAGA. Nothing but articles, after articles -- after articles. He has express hatred for (INAUDIBLE) of the country.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: And whose writing those articles?\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: It doesn't matter.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: And it's not.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Because the bottom line is he says it. He has vitriol for them. It so does MSNBC. And so does CNN, and so does the \uc2 New York Times\uc2 , they have vitriol for half of the nation. They don't look at them, like fellow Americans. And it's unfortunate. They tried to paint them as racist that all the things that they do to their fellow Americans who simply want a safe home, a safe community for the children and prosperity. They want the same thing, but they demean them all the time.\par \par \uc2 That's why I think Phillips was so intriguing because he went to a rally to find out for himself. And guess what? Golly, these are some pretty cool people. They are just like us.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And that's exactly what \uc2 Jamie Dimon\uc2  said this week as well.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: \uc2 Jamie Dimon\uc2  said in Davos. Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And it's just -- it's smart to understand. And also, you see the shift that's happening, right?\par \par \uc2 You look at black voters, Hispanic voters, college women, Latinos who have moved towards Trump.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, it's no longer that people are in these boxes based on what their gender is or what they look like. They appear to want, you know, a safe country and they appear to want a good economy, regardless of what label they have.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: That's completely fine. And I know that the lines have shifted dramatically, and -- but \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is responsible for a lot of that. So that he's demeaned half the country first of all, it's 74 million people that voted for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and he has explicit. Every time he talks about the MAGA movement to say that this is a subsection of the Republican Party, which is borne out in elections when you see people voting against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And this idea that --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But what about the people voting for him?\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: The way that Democrats --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: What about the people voting for him?\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Yes. I'm getting --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: He used teleprompter speeches. I mean you have to understand, but it's --\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Yes, but he is talking about insurrectionists?\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: No.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Yes, he is.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: No, no, no.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Why is he talking of January 6th? And he calls \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: He says MAGA like it's a swear word, you know, instead of a slogan.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: If you get called regularly --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: He is the president of the United States.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: And what's Democrats do, thugs.\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: He is the commander-in-chief.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Pedophiles.\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: Why can't he solve the border crisis?\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Socialist, communist.\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: While he's denigrating and castigating Americans.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: That's what we're talking about, Kellyanne.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: No, I -- no, the voters are talking about that Jessica.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: No. But,\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: (INAUDIBLE) demeaning voter.\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: The president of the United States, the commander in chief went to Philadelphia a year and a half ago with a marine posted behind.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: I saw.\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: And gave a decided be political speech where he insulted a large part of the country. And I've said many times on this network, and I'll say it again tonight.\par \par \uc2 If people, a lot of people spend a fraction of the time that they spent obsessing over \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , the person, learning one thing about the Trump voter, things may be very different.\par \par \uc2 This is \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's version of Hillary's deplorable and irredeemable.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And here is one thing, we're going to be talking about this for many months. So, we have to move on.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: I'm exit (PH).\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK. Thank you all very much.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. So, Alexis McAdams is here in Bedford. She was at the Trump campaign headquarters earlier tonight.\par \par \uc2 Hi, Alexis.\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS MCADAMS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Martha and Bret. Yes, the party there ended. So, we came back here to the BVIs. We can give you the latest update. It wasn't electric atmosphere, though inside of the Trump watch. Party there, as the former president took the stage, Hammering \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2 , which is nothing new, right.\par \par \uc2 But also said, the primary is over and the general election starts night watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : We are going to win this. And we have no choice, if we don't win, I think our country is finished. I do.\par \par \uc2 I believe our country is finished.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: And throughout the day, the former president had told us in some interviews we had with him that he was very confident. He thought he had this in the bag. How confident, Martha and Bret, well, he was already talking about who is going to pick for V.P., right? We saw the \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2  in that room. And also, Senator Tim Scott. We talked to Senator Tim Scott asking him if he's going to want that V.P. pick, but nobody would say too much about that. But they all sure he stood.\par \par \uc2 Next, to the former president on that stage. I also asked some people in the room about, you know, what do you think about this margin? Do you think Trump won by enough here on the ground, and they said is a win is a win and they're looking forward to seeing what happens in South Carolina. And people here have a lot of, you know, cares about the southern border and the northern border.\par \par \uc2 So, that kind of is what it came down to you guys.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Alexis, thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thanks, Alexis.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: \uc2 RNC\uc2 . Chairwoman. \uc2 Ronna McDaniel\uc2  joins us now. Ronna, thanks for being here. Your thoughts tonight, we have obviously the former president celebrating this win, but \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  saying, she is going to fight on.\par \par \uc2 We have from the Haley campaign, a sense that, you know, there are 430,000 voters that have voted so far. And they want to hear more from the other half of the party that is not supporting the former president.\par \par \uc2 How do you look at tonight? And what's the \uc2 RNC\uc2 's take?\par \par \uc2 RONNA MCDANIEL\uc2 , CHAIRWOMAN, REPUBLICAN NATIONAL COMMITTEE: Well, one thing I will say about the whole field of candidates that have run for president on our side, I commend them, they've been great. This has been a great contest.\par \par \uc2 But I think there's some history that was made tonight. We have never had a nominee in our party that has won without winning both without -- and winning either Iowa or New Hampshire. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is the first ever to win both. I'm looking at the math and the path going forward. And I don't see it.\par \par \uc2 For \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2 , I think she's run a great campaign. But I do think there is a message that's coming out from the voters, which is very clear. We need to unite around our eventual nominee, which is going to be \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And we need to make sure we beat \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 It is 10 months away until the November election. And we can't wait any longer to put our foot on the gas to beat the worst president to beat a president that's kept our borders open, allowed fentanyl to pour through, allowed inflation to go rampant.\par \par \uc2 He is hurting the American people. And we need to do everything we can to unite so that we can defeat him.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, Ronna, let's talk about the turnout tonight and what your take is on the Republican vote for President Trump and the independent vote and how many there were, I think about 4,000 Democrats who registered to be part of this process, they changed their affiliation to registered an unaffiliated.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, how are you seeing all of that sorted out? Because there's a lot of talk about who made up the vote for each of these candidates.\par \par \uc2 You know, Iowa, New Hampshire have had these special roles. And you saw \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  really Park himself in Iowa and say, this is where I'm going to win. And if I don't win here, there really wasn't a path.\par \par \uc2 And you saw Nikki, really plant herself in New Hampshire with the support of Governor Sununu, with these undeclared voters, with Democrats crossing over, and I just think if she came in second here, I don't see the path.\par \par \uc2 President Trump won by double digits in both states. This is unprecedented. This isn't the \uc2 RNC\uc2  speaking, this isn't the establishment speaking, this is the voters speaking. And when you look at the path going forward, you know, we take personality out of it.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You're telling \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  that she needs to get out?\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: I'm just saying, look at the path and the math. And I think you have to reflect tonight, if you're on the Haley campaign and say, if I'm not on the ballot in Nevada, if I'm not going to maybe win my home state, what's the most important thing going forward?\par \par \uc2 And every Republican has to agree that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is a threat to our country. And if we do not unite, and if we don't come to coalesce to beat him, then we're not going to 