ght? So, let's go back to the map and see where we are coming up on midnight here in New Hampshire. Here's your mark. You have 83 percent. Trump is almost hitting the 30,000 raw vote total, percentage-wise 11 points, OK. So, 11 points is going to marinade overnight tonight. We're going to let that settle in and see.\par \par \uc2 You know, \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2 , based on all the polling prior to this primary, she's doing better. That's a fact. Some of the polling suggested that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  -- that daily tracking poll yesterday, Trump at 60 percent. And I think right now at the moment that might be outside the margin of error for what that daily tracking poll was doing.\par \par \uc2 Let's dive in here for a second here and check out what's happening in Hillsborough. There's going to be more votes coming in guys throughout the night. And the Secretary of State suggesting right now the number is going to be about 322, maybe 324,000. That's a big turnout. That's a big deal for Republicans when you compare it to - with two candidates compared to 2016 where you did around 280,000. So, do the math on that. Bring Charles Payne back. He can -- he can figure out what the percent ages from the jump from uh from eight years ago.\par \par \uc2 Pop on over here to Rockingham County. Let's just canvas this. 25 percent remaining. Trump handedly there almost 15 percent. Here's Merrimack County. You got about 16 percent out. OK, a little closer there with Trump and Haley and I'll just check the other one. Strafford is where most of the people live in New Hampshire, and Trump is up 11 points here.\par \par \uc2 This is Grafton County. We mentioned this a bit earlier today. That seems to be \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2 's best performance. It looks like going in the bed tonight. You know, \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  will hold up in Grafton County. This is where -- this is where John Kasich did his best eight years ago, so we were watching that.\par \par \uc2 I don't want to neglect this, OK, because the boss will kill me. Coos County, OK. Up in the north, I think we said it correctly once, then we went back to the traditional Coos which folks in the Granite State do not like to hear, so we just want to make sure we point that out. You've got -- these are the rurals now. So, more voters out there overnight. Trump is going to run up the score here in Coos County, but we'll see whether or not that changes this final tally of the 11-point separation between Trump and Haley.\par \par \uc2 So, that's where we are coming up on midnight in the Fox Box.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It is quite something to watch. But Bill, if you look back in the time machine to 2016, obviously he was a new candidate, but he's exponentially outperforming in each of those places tonight, right?\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: Well, I mean, look at the raw vote number is here. And that's 145,000 raw votes, OK. Let me show you a couple of things. There's Democrats --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Testing his steering ability at the big board.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: Yes, yes. You got it. So, they're well under. The projection for Democrats coming in tonight was 100,000. They're not there yet. They're about 2,000 shy, about 98,000 as we speak right now. But if you go back in time, Bernie Sanders -- there's a lot of competition, a lot of people in the bout, but he's with 76,000 in 2020.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, look, Trump's got a lot to brag about tonight. Now, he's done something that we have not seen since 1976. \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  has got a lot to brag about tonight. She outperformed. But how they go at it whether it's in Nevada which is really not going to be much of a contest, but certainly South Carolina which is 31 days away, I do believe.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes it is. And there'll be a board somewhere there for you to use.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: That works.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You were awesome tonight, man. Thank you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Final thoughts with our panel on a historic night in New Hampshire. Big win for the former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  says she's hanging in there. Our special coverage continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : This is an evening uh that I will not forget because it's the third time. But more importantly, I think it's going to be -- I think it's going to be the most important time.\par \par \uc2 HALEY: This race is far from over. There are dozens of states left to go.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  and former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  both looking ahead after a hard-fought battle in New Hampshire. Some final Thoughts with our final panel, Katie Pavlich, Charlie Hurt, Juan Williams, and Marc Thiessen.\par \par \uc2 Charlie, you know, this -- the President is winning a decisive win in this primary -- we don't know how decisive yet until all the numbers come in -- despite the fact that more Independents and Democrats voted in this primary than Republicans in the Republican primary. So, all of the numbers as we look at this, this is the best scenario for \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  as you look at the picture. And still \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is winning by 11, 12 or whatever it ends up being.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE HURT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: And she spent weeks here campaigning exclusively and she's not going to be able to do that going forward, you know, a week and a couple of days ago. The big question to me was, you know, are Republican voters ready to despite what they tell pollsters, are they ready to move on and as \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  says uh go past the chaos that follows \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And voters have I think been uh very clear that they are ready to saddle up and fight on, and fight on with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And the fact that she made that the centerpiece of her campaign I think only makes it the answer that much clear.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, Katie, when you look at that question of, you know, how he - - how he keeps moving forward and builds on this momentum, right? And it is -- it is true that -- it's an unusual situation because you have more Independent voters than either party, than Republicans or Democrats. So, the largest group of voters here are Independents, and a lot of them you know -- I mean, a lot of them went for \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2 , but Trump won more of them. So, I mean it's a pretty impressive -- does it help him to expand his base?\par \par \uc2 KATIE PAVLICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Well, first it's clear that Republican voters believe that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's campaign is one of unfinished business. When you look at the issues that they are most interested, immigration being the top one, they think that's something he can handle, that he should go back and clean up what the Biden Administration has done. And then the economy of course comes in as number two which is also affected by immigration.\par \par \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2 's campaign today put out a lengthy memo talking about her strategy moving forward. And it's actually not about Republican voters, it's about taking advantage of open and semi-open primaries in 11 out of 16 Super Tuesday state. So, there's been a lot of talk tonight about South Carolina, but she's looking to push all the way into March 5th for Super Tuesday. So, her plan is to do that.\par \par \uc2 At the end of the day, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's campaign and Republicans are going to need some kind of Independent voter to beat \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . And there will have to be some kind of reconciliation in the Republican Party. We're not there yet but that is certainly something in the general election if they want to beat \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  they're going to have to grapple with.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: There's about 10 or 11 open primaries on Super Tuesday that that they have their eyes on at this point.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But South Carolina, Mark, is winner take all. But before South Carolina is the Nevada caucus, and \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  is not even in that. She cannot get any delegates in that because she signed up for the primary, didn't pay the whatever it is, $55,000 to get into the caucus. So, you know, you're going to have a couple of losses here in a row, Iowa, New Hampshire, Nevada, and then you're going to defend in your home state.\par \par \uc2 MARC THIESSEN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: She will. And she essentially concede in those and trying to win -- do -- exceed expectations in South Carolina and then go on to Super Tuesday. We'll see if it works. I will tell you that the way \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  won the presidency was he won the Reagan Democrats. And the Reagan Democrats were decisive and the Reagan Democrats became Republicans. And we are going to need the Haley Democrats if the Republicans are going to beat \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Martha, you made the point earlier about how he's sort of a quasi- incumbent. In 1992, the biggest challenge to an incumbent president was in 199o when Pat Buchanan challenged George H.W. Bush. Buchanan got 38 percent here in New Hampshire, to Bush's 52 percent. Very similar numbers. Actually, Haley did a little bit better than that. \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  headline, Bush jarred in first primary. President scored a less than impressive victory over Patrick J. Buchanan.\par \par \uc2 Right now, Haley is doing better than Buchanan did against the de facto incumbent Republican president. Why isn't that jarring? That should be very worrying. And it wasn't that Buchanan had a shot at the nomination. He never did. But it was a sign of general election weakness. You've got a situation now where half of the -- almost half of the voters here in New Hampshire have voted for somebody else. And that's -- and that's should be worrisome going forward.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Juan, the Trump campaign pushes back and says they're not in the incumbent seat, they're an insurgent. They've been bombarded by political attacks, by -- this is how they talk about it. And that he is after all of this coming out on top. That's how they paint the picture.\par \par \uc2 JUAN WILLIAMS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Well, I think that's delusional. I mean, the fact is that he -- not only -- you know, in the game of politics, it's money, message, but it's also name ID. You can't beat \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's name ID. He dominates in terms of media. I think the big question now is \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  is going to push for debates.\par \par \uc2 And she's got ways, if you look at the polls, she's got a way -- she's got messages that she says I can go after \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  successfully, chaos, his lawsuits, his lame duck status, his age. I think that's --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But she's going to have to win a primary at some point.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: No, no, but I'm saying she's going to push and say if you want to win those Independence, those Reagan Democrats, you're going to have to make an effort to reach out to them. You need a debate.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, it's going to be -- it's going to be fascinating to watch. Panel, thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you, guys.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And thank you for joining us tonight for Democracy 24 coverage of the New Hampshire primary.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: The coverage continues next with "FOX NEWS @ NIGHT" with Trace Gallagher. Good night from Bedford. Great to have you all here. We'll see you back in New York and Washington in the coming days.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Get ready.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FXLV000020240124ek1n0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  Says Border Is Biggest Concern For Voters; \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  And \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  Host Reproductive Rights Rally; Examining Factors Driving Ballot Decisions; \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  And \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  Go Head-To-Head In New Hampshire Primary; Multiple Casualties In Gaza Fighting; Biden Administration Urged To Mediate Cease-Fire; Two-Month Truce Reportedly Proposed; New Hampshire Voters Discuss Their Preferences In Republican Presidential Primary; Rep. \uc2 Dean Phillips\uc2  (D-MN) Discusses His Presidential Campaign Against President Biden; Republican Presidential Candidate Ryan Binkley Interviewed On His Continuing Campaign; President Trump Hoping To Seal Up Republican Presidential Nomination With Win In New Hampshire Primary; Democratic Party Will Not Count Delegates From New Hampshire Primary\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Brit Hume, Kellyanne Conway, Alexis McAdams, Bryan  Llenas, Peter Doocy, Sandra Smith, Alex Hogan, Bill Hemmer, Trey Gowdy,  Juan Williams, Josh Kraushaar \par \uc2 8,363 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 January 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL LEGAL ANALYST: They'll talk about the UFC, WWE, Trump and the possibility of Rock running for president. I don't think so. But what do I know? And much more. Don't miss it.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Dana, you have 10 seconds.\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Watch before we go tomorrow because I have something funny about Johnny and the marching band.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: All right. That's it for us. Have a great night, everybody.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: All right, Jesse. Did you check out the spa, I didn't remember?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: No, you were booked.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. I'm Bret Baier. We are coming to you tonight live from Bedford, New Hampshire, where citizens here and throughout the state had been voting all day long in the first in the nation primary election of the 2024 presidential cycle.\par \par \uc2 Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  had coming into this day a commanding lead in the polls while on the Democratic side, President Biden is not even on the ballot here. We'll go over what we're learning from our Fox News voter analysis, the early version in just a few minutes.\par \par \uc2 But we have Fox team coverage, Bryan Llenas in Concord, New Hampshire is following the \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  campaign. Correspondent Alexis McAdams starts us off tonight from Nashua with Team Trump. Good evening, Alexis.\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS MCADAMS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Bret, good evening. Well, we did have a chance to catch up with the former president here on the ground in New Hampshire. He feels good going into primary night and feels like he has a lot of support here in the Granite State.\par \par \uc2 But I asked him when we caught up with him if he's worried about \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  gaining steam at all, this is what he had to say, watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP) MCADAMS: \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  wanted it to be a two person race, right? I mean, she got that. You think it's going to go in her favor at all?\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I think it hurts her actually. I think it's going to hurt her. Probably have a big loss today. But who knows?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: And Fox has been on the ground for days, Bret trying to talk with voters to figure out what issues they care about. And it simply comes down to two main things, immigration and also the economy.\par \par \uc2 Trump tells us that he believes his strong stance on the border is bringing people out to the polls.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : You know, the economy was big and inflation was big, it's all big. But this, I think the voter issue now is the biggest.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: And so how confident is \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  here in the Granite State, Bret? Well, he's already talking about potential V.P. picks. Some of those names that have been floated could be here in this room tonight in Nashua, New Hampshire. So we'll keep a close eye on that.\par \par \uc2 But how is \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  feeling tonight? Let's check in with my colleague Bryan Llenas who's been following her campaign. Hi, Bryan.\par \par \uc2 BRYAN LLENAS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Alexis. Well, look, \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  says regardless of the result tonight, she's not going anywhere. The former \uc2 U.N.\uc2  Ambassador under President Trump says she's staying in this race even if her former boss thinks she should drop out.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY\uc2 , FORMER U.S. AMBASSADOR TO \uc2 U.N.\uc2 : I don't do what he tells me to do. I've never done what he tells me to do.\par \par \uc2 Look, I mean, I think I don't strong -- I've always said, we'll know strong when the numbers come in. It's not like a certain number.\par \par \uc2 We're going to go and fight until the very last poll closes. And then we're headed to my sweet state of South Carolina. And we're going to make the case there as well.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS (voice over): Tonight, the Haley campaign says it's feeling good, no campaign has covered more ground in New Hampshire with more than 100 campaign stops or spent more money with $30 million in ads.\par \par \uc2 Haley says it's time for a new generation of Republican leadership, arguing Trump and President Biden are too old and too distracted by investigations.\par \par \uc2 Now, Haley's campaign is betting on high voter turnout today, particularly from independents, a group with which she fares well.\par \par \uc2 In a memo today, Haley's campaign urged everyone to take a deep breath. This campaign is going all the way through at least Super Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 LLENAS (on camera): Now, before losing badly to Trump in Iowa, Haley infamously said, Iowa starts it. New Hampshire corrects it. Tonight, we'll find out, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We will indeed. Bryan, Alexis, thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and President Biden in Virginia today to talk about abortion rights. While it is a core issue for progressives, doesn't resonate with a significant portion of voters across the country. White House Correspondent Peter Doocy takes a look tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I believe 2024 is going to be the most important election we've had since 1864.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): The 2024 debut of the Democratic ticket, a rally for reproductive rights.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The government should not be telling her what to do with her body.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): This event, 500 miles away from New Hampshire where President Biden isn't on the ballot and hopes to win as a write-in.\par \par \uc2 RAY BUCKLEY\uc2 , NEW HAMPSHIRE DEMOCRATIC PARTY: We're looking forward to a great turnout. There's going to be significantly more people voting in this primary than did in 2012 when President Obama was running for re-election.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): Immigration a top issue in nearly every poll as the administration wins a suit against Texas allowing Border Patrol agents to cut razor wire along the border.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: What Texas was doing, the governor was doing was actually ineffective.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): Congressman Chip Roy, the Texas Republican posts on X, consider that we are a nation so unserious and broken that we are paying Border Patrol to secure the border but we'll pay them instead to cut razor wire against most of their/slash personal desire to stop Texas from trying to do what BP's core mission is.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Why are you guys making it easier for people to enter the country illegally?\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, PRESS SECRETARY, PENTAGON: I don't believe we are.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (on camera): It took President Biden a lot longer than planned to deliver his remarks about abortion access tonight, he was interrupted by hecklers shouting about a ceasefire in Gaza 14 times.\par \par \uc2 Towards the end, the president appeared to grow frustrated and he conceded to the crowd that it was going to go on for a while because they, the protesters, had it all planned. And he was right, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Peter Doocy live on the North Lawn. Peter, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Let's find out what the voters here in New Hampshire are saying. "AMERICA REPORTS" anchors Sandra Smith is in New York with a look at the early Fox voter analysis. Good evening, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Hi, Bret. Super excited to bring our viewers this brand new data that we're getting from voters on the ground there. Our team has been investigating what is driving voters to the ballot box in New Hampshire. This is an early look at our Fox News voter analysis survey of Republican primary voters on the ground there.\par \par \uc2 So far, our top findings on the ground do show the difference in the landscapes from what we saw in Iowa, particularly when it comes to evangelical Christians. We know Trump won that category in large part thanks to their support in Iowa.\par \par \uc2 But in New Hampshire, this is a different story. Only one in five there I say that they are evangelical Christians.\par \par \uc2 Remember, this was a much different number in Iowa, it was 46 percent on the ground in Iowa, you're looking at a 25 point margin there. That is a key difference.\par \par \uc2 Another key difference is our findings when it comes to the share of voters that identify as MAGA supporters, OK, on the ground in New Hampshire, that number is 49 percent. You'll remember it was more like 62 percent on the ground in Iowa.\par \par \uc2 So, you're looking again at a pretty big margin, 23 percent difference there.\par \par \uc2 Now, here's a bit of a gut check question that we put to voters on the ground in New Hampshire. Who do you think's going to win the GOP nomination? Pretty simple question. Pretty simple answer.\par \par \uc2 Fewer than two and 10 GOP primary voters expect that person to be \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2 , but far more as you can see, nearly eight and 10 say \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  wins. That includes some Haley supporters.\par \par \uc2 But how satisfied would they be if it is actually \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ? That's the next question we put to New Hampshire voters. Most say they'd be happy enough to vote for him in November. But one third, 32 percent said that they would not.\par \par \uc2 Now, look at this. The overall satisfaction is the same when we asked that question for \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2 , taking a look at that. Here's the numbers. Would you vote for \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  if she was the nominee, one third said they would not support her at the top of the ticket. 64 percent said yes.\par \par \uc2 Now, on the other side, how satisfied are New Hampshire Democrats with President Biden? This is a really big question for voters on the ground there. Nearly all Democratic primary voters had said that they would back Biden in November.\par \par \uc2 Now, Bret, one more key finding for you before we go, was a question that we put in our survey about party identification. Our survey shows that 10 percent of GOP primary voters today say they generally think of themselves as Democrats. Really important status. I hand it back to you there, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Sandra, that's interesting. We'll have more of that throughout the night. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Let's talk about today's primary, the presidential election. Joining us now Fox News Chief Political Analyst Brit Hume and Kellyanne Conway, former senior counselor to President Trump. Thank you both for being here.\par \par \uc2 You know, that last stat, it tracks with the New Hampshire voter registration by party. Republicans that roughly 30 percent, Democrats 30 percent, undeclared here, Brit, big, almost 40 percent. And really that number is what we're looking at how it turns out for \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  tonight.\par \par \uc2 BRIT HUME, FOX NEWS SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST: Exactly, and we see the party identification of those who actually voted in the Republican, overwhelmingly Republicans. Much more overwhelming than the numbers might suggest if you have all these independents who are undeclared, as they call and vote here in New Hampshire.\par \par \uc2 So, that on at least looking at it at first blush would not be good news for \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2 , who, you know, she ran into the lane, very different from what \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  tried in Iowa. He failed, but he ran basically in the same lane as Trump and was bidding for the Trump voter who he believed I think, unfortunately for him, those Trump voters are ready to turn away from Trump. They turned out not to be.\par \par \uc2 Haley presumably had a more a different lane and a better chance perhaps here and this was a good state for her. If she doesn't do well tonight, will we begin to think, well, where do you go? You know, how can anybody beat this guy in this cycle?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Right. On the flip side of that, Kellyanne, is that if you have, let's say, 320,000, roughly voters today, you had 100,000, roughly in Iowa, that would be 420,000 overall who had voted and someone could say who was a pro-\uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  person will want to keep the fight going. Even if you lose by five, 10. It starts to get ugly at 15.\par \par \uc2 KELLYANNE CONWAY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Sure. Well, the short answer, Bret is that there probably isn't enough anti-Trump vote to beat \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . New Hampshire has a tradition of shock and awe. They like to say that they're the protest vote, but I believe the protest vote this year for New Hampshire, Republican primary participants is not \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  or \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  or \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2 . It's \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . He's still seen as the insurgent outsider. He's still seen as the disrupter. And that is an advantage he has that he can couple with a presidential record now.\par \par \uc2 Now, sure, less than half a million in Iowa, New Hampshire voting as you correctly point out, but these are the first two states and it looks like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is on track to perhaps get the majority of the vote again, if you look at everybody's polling. That is truly dominant.\par \par \uc2 Look, I think the DeSantis point is an important one. People kept making excuses for him giving him a mulligan, they never give on to the world's most famous political golfer, but it was, wait, he didn't announce it. Wait till the money comes in, wait until he starts campaigning. Everybody waited.\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  was chasing people whose hearts already belong to someone else. And we women know that's a bad thing strategy from the beginning.\par \par \uc2 But look, I think in the case of Nikki, I think, look, for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , his opponents were confident, his opponent's donors were certain. But the voters still seem unconvinced.\par \par \uc2 I think if she can turn it around to go to South Carolina, then she's talking but there's just doesn't tend to be enough as Republican nominee within the Republican constituency for her.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You look at the Fox News voter analysis, the number one issue here in New Hampshire is the number one issue we saw on Iowa immigration. I went to a couple of rallies for the former president.\par \par \uc2 You know, for all the talk about all the controversial stuff that he says the campaign is pretty disciplined on that immigration issue. And he hits it every time, a lot of time.\par \par \uc2 HUME: And he has a record on it. Just talk about needing more money policy changes and all that. He without any policy changes, without any new legislation, without a lot more money, he was able to get things basically under control of the border.\par \par \uc2 So, that normalcy in his favor because of the prevalence of the issue. And what's striking about that issue to me with for a long time, it didn't appear that it was really emerging as an issue.\par \par \uc2 So, the scenes down on the border and you're saying, my God, how can we tolerate that? It was -- it was a ticking time bomb. And I think now it's gone off and it redound to the benefit principally in this race of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And quickly, I mean, the President, President Biden has to be looking at that as a major weakness in his campaign. As you start to paint the general election, eventually we get there.\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: That's right, Bret. And actually, he's got Democratic governors and Senator \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  and others now raising the alarm bells and saying it is a crisis. We don't care if you call it that or not. But it is a true crisis. Eight million people is larger than the population of about 40 of our states.\par \par \uc2 So, people know what they see, not just what the Democrats say,\par \par \uc2 Look, when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  elevated immigration in 2015, nine years ago, it was about three percent in the polling, but he'd figured it's also an economic issue. It's a fairness issue. It's a sovereignty and security issue.\par \par \uc2 Now, it's a top issue because Biden has completely ignored it. He needs to go back down there, he needs to have \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  go back down there and stop pretending that abortion is going to be the number one issue in the general.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Some tells me the former president will probably be down on the border sometime soon as well. Kellyanne, Brit, thank you very much. See you later.\par \par \uc2 Another headlines tonight, the man referred to as \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's sugar brother told house impeachment investigators he voluntarily loaned money to help a struggling friend. A transcript of the interview with Kevin Morris reveals he met Hunter at a Biden Harris campaign event November of 2019. Started helping him covers bills just weeks later and paid the majority of the $1.4 million the younger Biden owed in federal back taxes.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, a federal appeals court in Washington has denied former President Trump's request to toss the gag order on his speech related to special counsel Jack Smith's election interference case. A judge first imposed the partial gag order in October, blocking the former president from making statements targeting Smith, his staff, witnesses and court personnel.\par \par \uc2 Wall Street Journal reporter \uc2 Evan Gershkovich\uc2  was visited by the U.S. Ambassador to Russia last week. Lynne Tracy says Gershkovich appeared in good spirits despite circumstances. She said he remains grateful for the support he continues to receive from family and friends.\par \par \uc2 Gershkovich was detained in March of last year on espionage charges he denies. He spent his 300th day in custody earlier this week.\par \par \uc2 Stocks were mixed today on week earnings. The Dow lost 96, the S&P 500 gained 14, the NASDAQ was up 66.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, the worst tragedy for Israeli troops since the war with \uc2 Hamas\uc2  began. We'll take you live to Tel Aviv.\par \par \uc2 And later, what voters here in New Hampshire are saying about the first in the nation primary? Before that, the history list.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It didn't really become the primary as we know it until 1952.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And do you think that since that time, it's evolved into kind of a pride?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes. And this is something that really defines the state's political culture. It means the world comes to us every four years. It makes our lives Interesting.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Tell me why 1952 is important.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Sure. In 1952, you had an open nomination for the Republican Party. One of the leading candidates was Robert Taft,\par \par \uc2 ROBERT TAFT, FORMER SENATOR OF OHIO: I'm Robert Taft.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Mr. Taft, because I think he is the best qualified man in the country for the job.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: He's very isolationist, in his view of the world. And the more moderate, more internationalist wing of the Republican Party wasn't really happy about that. They were trying to find somebody who could oppose him, and they still thought of Eisenhower.\par \par \uc2 Well, Eisenhower wasn't sure he wanted to run. So, they actually put his name on the ballot without his permission, and essentially told people, look, you'd like Ike, right, vote for Ike.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I'm (INAUDIBLE), but I'm going to vote for Ike.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And it worked.\par \par \uc2 DWIGHT D. EISENHOWER\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Ladies and gentlemen, I believe we can have peace with honor reasonable security with national solid, I believe in the future of the United States of America.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Turkish legislators have endorsed Sweden's membership in \uc2 NATO\uc2 . That lifts a major hurdle on the entry into the military alliance, for the previously non-aligned country.\par \par \uc2 Hungary is the lone remaining \uc2 NATO\uc2  ally that has not yet ratified Sweden's accession.\par \par \uc2 Russian missile -- missiles struck three Ukrainian cities today. At least seven people were killed there. Officials say this barrage, included more than 40 ballistic cruise and anti-aircraft and guided missiles, the United Nations is saying it appears that this is the heaviest bombardment since early January.\par \par \uc2 Israeli Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  is vowing what he calls absolute victory, following the death of 21 Israeli soldiers in the deadliest attack on \uc2 IDF\uc2  forces since the war with \uc2 Hamas\uc2  began.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Alex Hogan is in Tel Aviv with the latest tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ALEX HOGAN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): It's the deadliest attack on Israeli forces in Gaza since the start of the war.\par \par \uc2 LT. GEN. HERZI HALEVI, CHIEF OF THE GENERAL STAFF, \uc2 ISRAEL DEFENSE FORCES\uc2  (through translator): We share the sorrow of their families for the heavy loss and to know the pain is unbearable.\par \par \uc2 HOGAN (voice over): The \uc2 IDF\uc2  says 21 were killed by Palestinian militants when a grenade aimed at a tank took down two buildings flushing the troops inside. The military planes it's now encircled the city of Khan Younis. But the \uc2 U.N.\uc2  warns Israeli attacks hit a shelter for displaced civilians, killing, at least six people.\par \par \uc2 As the death toll soars, the \uc2 World Health Organization\uc2  reports that strikes on health care facilities are rising as well.\par \par \uc2 And in Jerusalem, families of the hostages are demanding action.\par \par \uc2 LIRAN BERMAN, BROTHER OF HOSTAGES: We saw that only through deals, the hostages came back alive.\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: Among the families are Liran, Elay, and Omri -- men fighting to bring home their little brothers, Ziv, Gali, Idan and Evyatar.\par \par \uc2 They are begging the Biden administration and governments of Qatar and Egypt to mediate. According to a senior Egyptian official, \uc2 Hamas\uc2  has rejected Israel's proposal of a two-month ceasefire in exchange for the Israeli hostages.\par \par \uc2 Know when a deal is on the table, the brothers say their role is clear.\par \par \uc2 OMRI SHITIVI, BROTHER OF HOSTAGE: Our efforts in Israel, it's to press on our government to accept a deal.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: No matter what the deal is.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: As tensions boil in the Middle East, New tonight, the USS it launched airstrikes on three facilities in Iraq used by a Iranian-backed militia. This follows repeated attacks on U.S. personnel in Iraq and Syria. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Alex Hogan, in Tel Aviv. Alex, thanks.\par \par \uc2 From immigration to inflation. We hear from New Hampshire voters as they decide who will get their vote this primary. Also, we talked to two of the lesser-known candidates on the ballot here.\par \par \uc2 But as we go to break, a trip to a very special political stop.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Robie's Country Store here on the banks of the Merrimack River is an historic landmark in between Concord, Manchester. If you want to talk politics, this is the place to do it. It's been happening for decades.\par \par \uc2 There is a plaque next to the counter, commemorating \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2 's stop here in 1975. Now, this place is owned again by