st voters in this state are registered independents. Many have voted for candidates from both parties.\par \par \uc2 So you have kind of evolved, and you think the Democratic Party has evolved.\par \par \uc2 EHL: The Democratic Party left the people. The people aren't leaving the party.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Alyssa Ehl voted for \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  in 2008 and later changed her affiliation to Republican.\par \par \uc2 EHL: They're going too far extreme to the left. And I just can't support their ideals at this time.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Alyssa says she likes several candidates this election cycle, including \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2 , \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . But with just the former president left out of her top three, she still has some hesitancy.\par \par \uc2 He's obviously up in the polls, so what is your concern about just being able to beat the president, President Biden?\par \par \uc2 EHL: He lost last time in 2020. So I'm kind of concerned that his reputation is going to be an issue.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Because they did mess with me, now they've got us and me for four more years.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : We will drive them crazy.\par \par \uc2 EHL: A lot of people are saturated with the whole Trump drama.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  is running on the fact that she points to polls that she is up over Biden. That's one of her closing pitches.\par \par \uc2 EHL: I just feel as though it's just not her time. She is just not firm enough on a lot of her issues.\par \par \uc2 VAILAS: New Hampshire voters are unique. They do their reading. They do their research, and they make the best vote.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Maryann Vailas is a bookkeeper and property manage from her Bedford.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: What other things are issues that you care about?\par \par \uc2 VAILAS: I have four sons that live in the area and a stepdaughter. And I worry that they can't afford to buy a house. And I worry about the border issue, huge.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: She says \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  impressed her that first debate.\par \par \uc2 VAILAS: I'm generally a pro-life person, but I think there needs to be some exceptions, and when she pointed out that we would never pass that throughout Congress, it just struck me.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Maryann says New Hampshire takes its job seriously.\par \par \uc2 What's amazing here is that there are so many people that are passionate about this choice. You all take it seriously, it seems to me.\par \par \uc2 VAILAS: New Hampshire has had a longstanding tradition. It's part of our constitution that we have in the first in the nation primary. And that's why the Democrats are missing the boat, basically.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Tim Kolojay believes former President Trump is the only one who can win.\par \par \uc2 KOLOJAY: I have never been to a Trump rally.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Thank you very much. This is a crowded place.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 KOLOJAY: But he draws them like it's unbelievable.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Tim has voted for President Trump in the past two election cycle and plans to support him once again.\par \par \uc2 If you're leaning towards Trump, you think he can win a general election. Are you pretty confident?\par \par \uc2 KOLOJAY: Well, I thought he was going to win the general election last time. I guess people are just upset with his actions and his attitudes. I think with all these criminal indictments thrown at him, you always vote for the underdog. I vote for the underdog. And to me, he has become an underdog. So we'll see.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So you think it actually kind of unites people.\par \par \uc2 KOLOJAY: I think it unites people. I really do.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Tim says he understands why the former president has faced criticism, but also believes Trump's policies were much better for the country.\par \par \uc2 What's the issue that most drives you?\par \par \uc2 KOLOJAY: You talk to a lot of people, and they say economy and immigration. I liked a lot of things that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  did back the four years he was here. He may have spoken too loudly and too much on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . He upset a lot of people. But as far as what he did for this country, I liked it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We first met Donna Morin eight years ago.\par \par \uc2 It's been a while. We came in 2016, 2020. In 2016 you supported --\par \par \uc2 MORIN: I kind of like \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And Jeb Bush.\par \par \uc2 Go Jeb.\par \par \uc2 So embarrassing, yes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And then 2020 we came back.\par \par \uc2 Are you settled on one?\par \par \uc2 MORIN: I am.\par \par \uc2 And?\par \par \uc2 MORIN: I'm settled on \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2 . I'm very excited about him.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And so this time, as you look at it, where are you?\par \par \uc2 MORIN: I am definitely going to be supporting \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Similar to candidates she supported in past elections, Donna likes \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2 's more moderate policies.\par \par \uc2 MORIN: She wants to stay strong by helping Ukraine. That's vital. I just feel like she has a lot more honesty to her.\par \par \uc2 HALEY: I've always spoken in hard truths, and I'm going to do that with you tonight.\par \par \uc2 MORIN: Whatever the result Tuesday night, Donna says it's a lesson for her students.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So you teach American government.\par \par \uc2 MORIN: I do. Love it. Yes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Do you?\par \par \uc2 MORIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And the kids, are they into this primary process?\par \par \uc2 MORIN: Yes, a lot of them are really into it. They better vote on Tuesday because a lot of them are 18.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER (on camera): Our thanks to the voters there.\par \par \uc2 As we told you earlier, President Biden is not on the ballot here in New Hampshire. The DNC chose South Carolina to start officially. But that leaves a bit of an opening here for someone who is on the Democratic side, Minnesota Congressman \uc2 Dean Phillips\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: What are you thinking about today? What needs to happen?\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 DEAN PHILLIPS\uc2 , (D-MN) PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Well, first of all, what I'm thinking about is what a beautiful practice of democracy here. It is so unique, so few Americans know how seriously they take it here. It's kind of almost the first interview for the entire country.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PHILLIPS: And I think we should treasure that.\par \par \uc2 My party has disenfranchised Democrats here. They have literally said this is meaningless. We won't seat your delegates.\par \par \uc2 I'm here to bring a little of excitement to this race, because gosh knows we need it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Could this be embarrassing for President Biden.\par \par \uc2 PHILLIPS: I think it already is. And it saddens me because I respect him. I think he made a terrible decision. And I think our parties right now are actually diminishing this practice rather than elevating it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I saw that you went to a \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  rally, and you talked about that and kind of the message it sent, the people you met.\par \par \uc2 PHILLIPS: I thought, I'm going to show them how I lead, which is to run to the fire. I didn't expect to be treated nicely, to be honest with you. But my goodness, I will never forget it. Fifty people I spoke to, hospitable, friendly, kind, a lot of high fives, a lot of statements of glad you're in this race, showing some courage. And I've got to tell you, a diverse crowd. Many of them there for the first time.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: What do you have to see?\par \par \uc2 PHILLIPS: I have been a longshot most of my life. So if we're in the 20s today from zero, that's a heck of a statement. Either way, I'm going to keep going. I have to introduce myself to the country, at least give voters a choice. Neither party should be pursuing a coronation. That's what mine is right now. I think that's dangerous. Our country was founded on competition.\par \par \uc2 I have spent so much money on this campaign I have nothing left in my pockets.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: If it doesn't work in the long run to get the nomination, what's the message you want to send to President Biden and the administration?\par \par \uc2 PHILLIPS: The message is that when you stay around a little too long, it poses great risks not just to your own legacy but to this country. Competition is good. And if the president would just come out and debate, face voters, answer questions, do a town hall, show up, at least show the Americans that are going to vote that you are ready for the task. That's what I'm doing. I think the contrast alone might be just what he and the country need.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER (on camera): One of the least known of the remaining Republican candidates on the ballot here in New Hampshire is Ryan Binkley. He's a businessman, church pastor from Texas, virtually no national profile. But he is on 30, yes, 30 state ballots. That is no small feat. And he is suing, actually, to be on another one. And he did outperform some better known politicians in Iowa.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 RYAN BINKLEY, (R) PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: They are saying, who in the blank is Ryan Binkley?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You are a businessman, but you are a pastor?\par \par \uc2 BINKLEY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And what happened? You just got a calling?\par \par \uc2 BINKLEY: Yes, I did. You know, my heart, I really felt it.\par \par \uc2 I know that many of you may not have met me yet.\par \par \uc2 If I would have told you nine months ago, Bret, that there would be a guy standing here today with you, and he made it past a vice president, a couple governors, a few billionaires, I don't know, congressmen, a senator, would have you taken that bet?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You are on a lot of ballots. It costs a lot of money. Sometimes it takes a lot of signatures. There's people wondering, so why are you going to this level of effort?\par \par \uc2 BINKLEY: You know, Bret, if you a child that's in a burning building, and this is what I see America today with the debt and the division with no plan out, and you've got a small percent chance of getting them out, you and them out alive, do you run in?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BINKLEY: Or do you walk away?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You have a lot of individual donors. Where is all the money coming from?\par \par \uc2 BINKLEY: Primarily a lot from me. I've self-funded quite a bit of it. But we do have 82,000 donors.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It's not a moneymaking operation, can't be. Money is going out the door, right?\par \par \uc2 BINKLEY: Oh, absolutely. But you know what? I just believe in it. I'm fighting for your kids, my kids.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You know what the Trump people are saying, he is trying to get on all these ballots to be the Cornel West, the RFK Jr. to the Republican side, so evangelicals who are disaffected from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  have another line to vote for.\par \par \uc2 BINKLEY: I'm not trying to disrupt anything. Listen, America can't wait four years. Our generation, yours and mine, will be known as the generation that prospered the most but sacrificed the least.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So you don't think \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is able to tap into those issues?\par \par \uc2 BINKLEY: Well, I don't know. He has been around for many years, and has he? So you look at our big cities. I'm from Texas. We lost every major city, Dallas, Houston, San Antonio, Austin, for the first time in 65 years, Fort Worth. This was like the alamo of conservatism in north Texas.\par \par \uc2 Did dear god help us.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You are going to keep going?\par \par \uc2 BINKLEY: Yes. You see the voices. Are we connecting to the multiculture communities that are growing, the changing electorate of our nation? We're not. You know it. I know it. And I'm telling America not every Republican is like that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER (on camera): We'll see what happens tonight.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, our all-star panel breaks down the first in the nation primary. Plus, Bill Hemmer is at the big board for a preview of tonight's returns, coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Let's find out what history and demographics tell us about what to expect when we start getting results from here in New Hampshire at the primary. My colleague Bill Hemmer at the touch screen.\par \par \uc2 BILL HEMMER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Hi, brother.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You'll be busy tonight.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: We left the monitor in Des Moines. It is still thawing out.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: We shipped a new one up here, so this is 2.0.\par \par \uc2 So come over here and I will paint a little broad strokes for you based on what we are seeing here in New Hampshire. We start on the Republican side, big story on the Democratic side, Brit mentioned it a moment ago. I'll come back in a minute here. But this is what is on the line, OK, 22 delegates at stake here in New Hampshire. We pop on in here. You are going to see the colors populate around 7:30, 8:00 tonight, all right? But you've got a little bit of orange on the screen already because \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  won six votes at midnight last night. Dixville Notch, a tiny little community up here in the northern part, the rural part of New Hampshire. It's a tradition in this state. She went six and zero against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  earlier today.\par \par \uc2 This going back eight years now, OK. This is the last contested Republican primary in New Hampshire. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  rolled over John Kasich by 20 points, won all 10 counties. And we'll see whether or not he still has that force tonight as well.\par \par \uc2 On the Democratic side, let me click on over here. See this? Delegates at stake -- zero. Now, there is a long explanation for this. But the DNC and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  didn't want it. They want to start in New Hampshire -- I'm sorry. They want to start in New Hampshire. New Hampshire said we are still going to do it. It's our state constitution. And that's the reason why you have 21 Democrats plus a write-in candidate tonight in New Hampshire. Now, we're going to be able to get an idea around 8:00 tonight how many wrote in \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's name. So, watch that throughout the night.\par \par \uc2 As I wrap this up, Bret, so far we are OK, so this is -- this is the ballgame now. Trump got 20 delegates in Iowa. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  dropped two days ago, picked up nine. \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  stands at eight. It's 1,215 needed for that nomination. That's the ballgame.\par \par \uc2 You mentioned history. If \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  wins in New Hampshire tonight, he will be the first Republican in a contested primary to go two for two in the first two contests, Iowa and New Hampshire, since Gerald Ford in 1976.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: He will also be the only person to win three New Hampshire primaries.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: And there is that too.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: History in the making. Bill, thanks. We'll see you a little bit later.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in our panel, Josh Kraushaar is FOX News Radio political analyst, \uc2 Trey Gowdy\uc2 , former Congressman from South Carolina, FOX News senior political analyst Juan Williams.\par \par \uc2 OK, Josh, you have seen some of the voter analysis going in. What strikes you? What strikes me, first of all, and we talked about this with Brit, is immigration for the second time in a row top issue.\par \par \uc2 JOSH KRAUSHAAR, FOX NEWS RADIO POLITICAL ANALYST: Yes, immigration, and you just have to look at the campaign ads here in New Hampshire, and one of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's most effective issues is hitting \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  for not being tough enough on illegal immigration. And I think he has gotten some traction in the final weeks of this campaign by showing he is the most hawkish candidate here on immigration.\par \par \uc2 And it's been a really interesting contrast between Trump, who has the base, he has the MAGA base, and that makes up about half the Republican vote here in New Hampshire. But he has also been able to make some inroads with more traditional Republican voters, independent voters who also consider immigration as a very significant issue for them.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, Trey, what are you looking at tonight? Obviously, it's a big pot of undeclared voters.\par \par \uc2 TREY GOWDY\uc2 , FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: It's a question to me of whether never- Trumpers really are never-Trumpers. People who vote for \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2 , will they come around in November, or conversely, if she is the nominee, will MAGA voters support her? It's also what I'm going to be listening for. The victory speech was conciliatory in Iowa. What kind of tone does the president -- I mean, there aren't enough Republicans to fight among themselves and still win in November. So, is there going to be a coalescing, or are they going to take this thing to my beloved home state?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Right. And the question is how far that go. Does, Juan, a second place within 10 points, does that give the momentum? Does the donor base say we are going to stick with you, \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2 ? We don't know the results, but if that pans out, 10 points, does that move her on?\par \par \uc2 JUAN WILLIAMS, FOX NEWS POLITICAL ANALYST: That's what the whole stake here in New Hampshire does the race go on towards Nevada and South Carolina in specific.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  in 92 was the comeback kid in second.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: That's right, but it was because he was in second, right? But I think that tonight, as people are watching, I think you should pay attention to whether or not she -- last night I said to you, can she get within five percentage points. I think the tone that we are hearing is more, maybe 10 points. But even if she does that, and I might add she is getting an infusion of money. She has done very well in the last few days in terms of fundraising. She had a big addition in terms of political action committee after Iowa. Her ads here have been strong. It looks like there's real argument that she has been making, foreign policy. There's a lane for her. There's a lane for her on fiscal restraint. There's a lane for her in terms of saying Trump is chaotic, he lies.\par \par \uc2 But how much, to get back to Trey's point, how many of the educated, more urban Republicans who both in Iowa and here say we are not sure about Trump, are they willing to come home once we get towards November?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. Secretary of State told Bryan Llenas that the turnout looks very strong. They made a prediction of anywhere between 322,000, 325,000. Doesn't know if that's going to hold up. If it does, it would be historic. And that would bode well at least for \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  tonight.\par \par \uc2 KRAUSHAAR: Yes. I mean, look, she needs to get a lot of independent voters. She is hoping that some Democrats even cross over, anti-Trump Republicans, Democrats cross over into the Republican primary. So she needs big turnout.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You are saying undeclared that are Democrat?\par \par \uc2 KRAUSHAAR: Correct, people who don't register as Democrats but tend to vote for them. So she needs to win over the lion's share of moderates, independents, less partisan voters. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has, you look at the polls, Bret, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has a sizeable advantage with the partisan, traditional rank-and-file Republicans. So \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  needs to expand that electorate tonight to win over the moderates and independents.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And Trey, on the Democratic side, we could have an interesting story, could have an interesting storyline with the write-in candidate. You have got \uc2 Dean Phillips\uc2  who is working the crowds. It could be embarrassing for the president, possibly.\par \par \uc2 GOWDY: It could. And then he will have to come South Carolina like he did once before, and \uc2 Jim Clyburn\uc2  will drag him over the finish line. I think he's going to wind up being the nominee. I don't want to spoil the ending, but I think he is going to wind up being the nominee.\par \par \uc2 But this is an unnecessary story that somebody with approval in the 30s probably doesn't need right now.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: You know what's interesting to me here is the tremendous sentiment that says we don't agree with your Middle East policy. Now, I know Republicans are going to say no, we think he has been trying there. But, boy, there are a lot of Democrats who don't buy into it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: There is a progressive movement to write in "Ceasefire" on the write-in instead of President Biden. Gentlemen, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we'll take you behind the scenes here at the FOX News headquarters in New Hampshire. Be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, welcome to the \uc2 Bedford Village Inn\uc2 . We have essentially taken over this whole property. Here's the makeup. Melissa (ph), can you say hi? Hello. And we have taken over this entire area and made it TV sets, a bunch of them, that we're going to show you behind the scenes very quickly.\par \par \uc2 As we go past here, this is the coffee area, we will go into this room here. This is the big set. This is where Hannity is. This is where they have FOX NEWS SUNDAY, the FOX & FRIENDS couch is put in here. Harris Faulkner does a show here, has done it. And then we have other sets as well.\par \par \uc2 So, follow me. We will go outside and show you around. OK, so you popped in between the buildings. This is obviously the set you see behind us in the big box. I'm going to take you over there now. A little fast-forward. Come on, follow me.\par \par \uc2 Guys, how are you? What's going on? I wanted to pop up here.\par \par \uc2 This is what we call the FOX box. Let's take you inside here. This is our studio -- you got the door there? Inside here is our studio for the election coverage for SPECIAL REPORT. You can see through the window, it's kind of like a snow globe here. And this is the billboard where Bill Hemmer will be getting all of the results in through the night.\par \par \uc2 I'm going to take you to one more spot. Follow me.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jesse Watters.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS HOST: Bret, I've got to be at work in about five minutes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You can handle the cold, man. Look at you.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: I know. I don't need to bundle up. I'm from New England.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I'm going to get you one of these SPECIAL REPORT jackets.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: I don't need any of your free stuff.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: See you.\par \par \uc2 OK. So this is the area that we have our dining for all the staff. Right in here. Dinner, behind the scenes, lovely spread. And then come on down this hall.\par \par \uc2 All right, and this is our newsroom, our home away from home as we finish up the New Hampshire primary. Thanks for watching. Oh, wait, Harris. No, no. We won't show you. We won't show you. Hold on, she has got curlers in. It's OK.\par \par \uc2 We will see you tonight for special coverage fair, balanced and unafraid.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020240124ek1n00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Interview With Fmr. Gov. \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2  (R-MD); President Biden Targets Abortion Rights; Interview With Pentagon Press Secretary Patrick Ryder; New Hampshire Holds Presidential Primary\b0\par\par\uc2 Alexis McAdams, Mark Penn, Bryan Llenas, Rich Edson, Neil Cavuto \par \uc2 8,589 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 January 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It's always fun for New Hampshire to have the first-in- the-nation primary.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY\uc2  (R), U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: We have worked New Hampshire hard, and we're excited to see what the day brings.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I like the ideas she has with China and Russia.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  made a lot more sense to me.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES (R) AND CURRENT U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE (R): We have had, I think, more enthusiasm than even the other two races.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: He did very well the last time he was president.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: He's the only one that's going to correct the madness that's going on in this country.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 CHRIS SUNUNU\uc2  (R-NH): This isn't the 56,000-vote coronation out in Iowa.\par \par \uc2 VIVEK RAMASWAMY\uc2  (R), FORMER U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: End this primary right here.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: It is the final countdown, the last polls set to close in New Hampshire in less than four hours, make-or-break time for one \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 She needs a strong finish, but \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  hoping after tonight she's just finished, period. And what about Joe? The president isn't even on the ballot. The latest on the frantic rush to write him in before people start to write him off. It's that weird, that big. And, again, that is New Hampshire and it's all going down now.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everybody. I'm Neil Cavuto.\par \par \uc2 So much to get to, so let's get to it with Bryan Llenas in Concord, New Hampshire, on how the voting site there is handling all of this and the folks coming in and out.\par \par \uc2 To you, Bryan.\par \par \uc2 BRYAN LLENAS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Neil, good afternoon.\par \par \uc2 Well, look, it's steady. That's the latest from New Hampshire's secretary of state in terms of turnout. Polls in New Hampshire close, a lot of them at 7:00 p.m., some at 8:00 p.m. And when I got off the phone with the secretary of state's office, they said, look, it looks like right now it could be, it could reach the estimated 322,000 voter turnout they were estimating, which would be a record high for the Republican primary.\par \par \uc2 But it's still a little bit too soon to know. Behind me is Ward 4 in Concord, New Hampshire. This is a live look inside of that ward. About 2,600 registered voters vote here. So far, they have had 820 or so voters that have cast their ballot. That's about a 30 percent showing, a little lower than what they expected.\par \par \uc2 The Haley campaign is hoping for high voter turnout, particularly because she does very well with the independent voters in this state. Now, former Governor \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  made three stops today at polling locations. She says she's staying in the race, regardless of tonight's result, and had this to say about former President Trump's suggestion that she'd drop out.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HALEY: I don't do what he tells me to do. I have never done what he tells me to do.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 HALEY: Look, I mean, I think -- I don't -- strong. I have always said, we will know strong when the numbers come in. It's not like a certain number. I don't go there and say, oh, I have to have this number, I have to have that number.\par \par \uc2 I'm a fighter. I work hard. And I do it because I love this country. And we're going to go and fight until the very last poll closes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: Trump stopped at a polling place in Londonderry, New Hampshire.\par \par \uc2 He said he was feeling very confident about tonight, had this to say about Haley staying in the race.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: ... staying in through Super Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I don't care.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Is she a threat to you?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: No, no, she -- and I don't care if she stays in. Let her do whatever she wants. It doesn't matter.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  wanted it to be a two-person race, right? I mean, she got that. You think it's going to go in her favor at all?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: I think it hurts her, actually. I think it's going to hurt her.\par \par \uc2 Probably have a big loss today, but who knows.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Once it's done and they drop out and they get behind you, do you forgive those people or are you kind of still mad?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I do. I'm a very forgiving person.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: Our teams have spoken to voters across the state today. This is what they had to say.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2 , absolutely. We need some fresh blood in our government.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Well, believe it or not, the first time Trump ran, I voted for him. But with all the shenanigans and out-of-office actions, I just don't think he's the right guy for the way America needs to go.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: He's a clear winner here. I mean, he's a clear winner there. They're engaging in everything they can to stop Trump. He's the best candidate and he's the only one that's going to correct the madness that's going on in this country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: Haley will speak tonight from right here in Concord, New Hampshire. Then she will head tomorrow to Charleston, South Carolina, where that primary, it's about a month away -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Wow, they just keep on rolling in.\par \par \uc2 All right, Bryan, thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 Well, not only are voters and the media watching this race closely. So are donors, particularly \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  donors, particularly billionaires like \uc2 Andy Sabin\uc2 , who was telling me earlier on FOX Business, which, if you don't get, you should demand -- Andy gets it and has demanded it and was on with me today to talk about the high bar he has set for \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2 . Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANDY SABIN\uc2 , NIKKI HALEY DONOR: You know what Custer's last stand is? She must win in New Hampshire, not come in second by two points or one point. In order to be a viable candidate, she must clearly show that she's a winner. She has to win, or I don't think she should go on.\par \par \uc2 I think it's political suicide to continue for her unless she's a clear winner in New Hampshire, which I think she could be. I wouldn't go by Dixville Notch, because the prediction rate of the six people or nine people has not been great. But this is her last stand. She's a great candidate, and we will have to see how it plays out.\par \par \uc2 But she -- it's a must-win situation.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK, you're referring to the six who've already voted right past midnight in Dixville, New Hampshire.\par \par \uc2 SABIN: Yes. Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And all six voted for her.\par \par \uc2 SABIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But you're quite right. That connection is simply not there in the last few elections, to put it mildly.\par \par \uc2 But having said that, so second isn't good enough, no matter how close? She's got to win outright? And then what do you do, Andy?\par \par \uc2 SABIN: Neil, do I have a choice? I hate to tell you this. I'm going to support Trump. But you never saw some of my comments in the press about I'm never going to give him another so what nickel.\par \par \uc2 And although I will support him, I will still not give him any money.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 SABIN: I'm not comfortable giving money for all his legal battles. And I will support him.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, just to be clear, you would support him as the party nominee in that event, but you wouldn't give him any money. That's not happening, right?\par \par \uc2 SABIN: I won't give him a nickel.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, but he would support \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  if he were eventually the party nominee.\par \par \uc2 To Frank Laukien right now, a New Hampshire billionaire who is backing \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2 , Independents Moving the Needle, the co-founder.\par \par \uc2 Frank, good to have you back.\par \par \uc2 You probably heard what Andy was saying. Do you agree with that, that \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  has to win outright before she gets another dime?\par \par \uc2 FRANK LAUKIEN, CO-FOUNDER, INDEPENDENTS MOVING THE NEEDLE: Yes, here's the purple team, the independents moving the needle.\par \par \uc2 And this is my button that I voted today here in New Hampshire. So I couldn't agree -- I couldn't disagree more. This is the first time we really have a two-person race. And the non-Trump vote for the first time won't be split in terms of attention or vote with Ron or Vivek or \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 This is a very good setup for Nikki. And I think the voters really have the opportunity to figure out who is the -- quite honestly, the higher energy, the mental acuity, the -- most importantly, the high-integrity candidate. And that is \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So I think the next month and the next few months until July will be that one-on-one comparison. And I think Nikki will come out looking like a star.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, what does she have to do today, though? I mean, he is going so far as to say you have to win it to be in it, and coming in second, even a close second, ain't going to cut it. How do you feel?\par \par \uc2 What would be OK for you?\par \par \uc2 LAUKIEN: No, I think Ron just dropped out, and he's been relentlessly negative. Now the MAGA and Trump low-integrity campaign is throwing out mischaracterizations and outright lies, hoping that they cannot be contradicted before people go to the primaries.\par \par \uc2 Hey, I hope that voters in New Hampshire will see right through that.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: No, Frank, I'm sorry. I wasn't clear with my question. What does it take?\par \par \uc2 LAUKIEN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: That if she has 10 points or more behind, do you still stick with her?\par \par \uc2 LAUKIEN: Oh, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK.\par \par \uc2 LAUKIEN: But I think she will -- we're looking for that strong silver medal, as I said before, Neil.\par \par \uc2 And I think she has an excellent chance for that. And we will all see. We will know more in a few hours. But for the first time, we have this one-on- one matchup where people really can compare. And I'm very optimistic that she will come out on top in the next few months.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, there are a lot of people who say she has nothing to lose staying in the race. She can be a last candidate standing if anything happens to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , if there any convictions on any of these indictments and charges and all.\par \par \uc2 And there's a benefit for her remaining in the race because no one else is in the race. What do you think of that?\par \par \uc2 LAUKIEN: Yes, I mean, I don't disagree with that. Yes, I think that makes sense.\par \par \uc2 But, really, I think -- I hope she has a path to victory, rather than sticking around and see whether something happens to Trump.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 LAUKIEN: That could happen. But I think she will -- the American people will get to know her that much better.\par \par \uc2 And she's not running for vice president. That's ridiculous. He wished, but, no, she's not. She's running to be president of the United States.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, could you support \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  if it doesn't work out for her?\par \par \uc2 LAUKIEN: No, we're the independents. As I said before, I don't need to be in politics.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 LAUKIEN: I certainly am not in Republican politics. We support next- generation, high-integrity candidates that can get the -- that can solve our problems, rather than just bicker and mischaracterize.\par \par \uc2 So, no, sir, I will not.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, we will watch it very closely.\par \par \uc2 Frank, good catching up with you again. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 LAUKIEN: Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, well, we do know there has been some voting, that it's official, tabulated all six individuals, all of whom went for \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  overnight just past midnight in Dixville Notch.\par \par \uc2 That's what gets a lot of attention. It's very iconic every four years. It's like the media world descends. This was what \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  was saying about that early victory.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HALEY: Right now, we will take whatever we can take. It was certainly a good start. It gave us some good energy and momentum. And I'm grateful to those six people.\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: We're not promising that it's going to be 350,000 to zero, but we're definitely on track for that.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, with us now is Tom Tillotson, the Dixville Notch, New Hampshire, town moderator.\par \par \uc2 Tom, very good to have you and thanks for taking the time.\par \par \uc2 This is always something I look forward to every four years. I know that it doesn't always translate into the final vote, but it's the first vote and it's the kickoff, the first primary vote in the country. And all six of these individuals went for \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2 . What did you make of that?\par \par \uc2 TOM TILLOTSON, DIXVILLE NOTCH, NEW HAMPSHIRE, TOWN MODERATOR: I was happy.\par \par \uc2 Obviously, six to zero, everybody knows who everybody voted for.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 TILLOTSON: And I voted for \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  and I'm glad all my other residents saw it the same way.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Now, there used to be a pretty good parallel between the final vote, I think prior to 2012, that was like a pretty good Super Bowl indicator, and then it kind of frizzled away a little bit. So there's no significance to it.\par \par \uc2 But what do you think of the reception she's getting in New Hampshire on a day a lot of polls seem to be saying \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  could rack up a big victory?\par \par \uc2 TILLOTSON: Well, I think the important point is what the independent voters will do.\par \par \uc2 I mean, we only had two independent voters, but they both went for \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  and chose a Republican ballot. With 40 percent of the electorate, the voters in New Hampshire being independents, that could swing one way or the other and make a big difference that the pollsters just haven't picked up.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Tom, it's a dumb question, but you have endured them, I guess, over time. Why are these vote results tabulated and announced, and not others, as the night and the day ensues, especially the next day?\par \par \uc2 TILLOTSON: Simply because it's the practical thing to do that New Hampshire law allows. Once 100 percent of your registered voters have voted, you can close the polls. And once you close the polls, you can do the counting, and then after you have counted, you can post it.\par \par \uc2 We didn't start that.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 TILLOTSON: That was started back in the '40s with railroad workers that basically had to go to work before the polls could open. So they started doing it at midnight.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 TILLOTSON: And once you have got everybody voted, why sit around until 7:00 p.m. the next day to finish?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: It makes perfectly good sense to me.\par \par \uc2 Tom, thank you very much. And good luck. We will see how things go.\par \par \uc2 TILLOTSON: All right.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, in the meantime, can you believe already after New Hampshire, they're talking about the next big -- the next big event, the next big hurdle, whatever you want to call it? And that is in South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 Of course, this is \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2 's home state, a two-term governor. A lot of the key players in that state have already come out for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . So, she would face enormous pressure there, no matter what happens in New Hampshire now. But that's only a month away, not that we're counting day by day. But it's a month away.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I think that Trump's the only one that can bring this country back.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I think she's the best candidate for everyone in the country and bringing the country together.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I voted for \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  because \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  really disgraced himself on January 6.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Don't get snowed by Sununu and tricky Nikki. Vote for Trump.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, extreme positions in a state where it's a friendly, feisty back-and-forth to see who will decide and ultimately win this battleground state.\par \par \uc2 Want to put it in perspective, though. If it gets up to 300,000 or more votes, of course, that's a heavy count, and, of course, participation, we're told, is very, very heavy. But, again, after Iowa, where you had 100,000 people voting, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  won about 50,000 of those votes.\par \par \uc2 So when all is said and done, depending on how it all sorts out, you will have about the returns of 400,000 Americans who voted in a country of more than 340 million people. So are we closing this too soon and saying that this race is locked up one way or the other?\par \par \uc2 Jim Merrill is joining us, the former \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  for president senior adviser, was the New Hampshire campaign manager for \uc2 Mitt Romney\uc2  as well, \uc2 Mark Penn\uc2  back with us, former Clinton adviser, Democratic pollster.\par \par \uc2 Jim, to you first.\par \par \uc2 For Republicans, to hear a lot of the pollsters tell it, it's the size of the victory for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , big enough that he scares off any potential donors for \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  as well. How likely is that?\par \par \uc2 JIM MERRILL, FORMER RUBIO CAMPAIGN ADVISER: Yes, I mean, look, that's -- what I'm hearing certainly is people are looking at that likely margin, and they're saying, the larger the margin, the more likely that Haley is going to be forced to drop out, and Trump can go on as the effective nominee.\par \par \uc2 What I'm hearing from people, though, is, this race, even though we're voting in New Hampshire today, it feels like the race is just getting under way...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Wow.\par \par \uc2 MERRILL: With DeSantis dropping out, and Haley is now drawing these much sharper contrasts, I think there are a lot of people that are kind of curious about where this goes.\par \par \uc2 Let's remember, in 2016, the primary fight went into May. We're in January right now, and so it kind of feels like things are really just warming up. So I think Haley's going to have a solid night tonight, but she's got her home state a month away.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MERRILL: And I think people are inclined, with people I'm talking to, let's see where this goes, and let's give her a shot to come out of New Hampshire and continue to make this case against President Trump.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, it's tough because the big difference between now and 2016, of course, we're down to one opponent for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , not like the half- a-dozen or more that we had back then.\par \par \uc2 Mark Penn\uc2 , let me get your thoughts on how important New Hampshire is. I'm thinking of \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 . He didn't win the state, but he came in a strong enough second after losing Iowa to earn the moniker of the comeback kid, went on to get the nomination, and ultimately the White House.\par \par \uc2 So, stranger things have happened. So, the expectation game is a little over the top right now. What do you think of that?\par \par \uc2 MARK PENN\uc2 , FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, that's right.\par \par \uc2 I think \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  lost and declared victory.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 PENN: And the press went along with it. His campaign rolled on.\par \par \uc2 So, I think what we have here is -- I mean, look, my personal estimation more or less, if she gets within five, she can declare victory. If it's 10, I think she has a good case for staying in; 10 to 15, I think it's going to depend on her donors. And she's the only non-Trump candidate standing.\par \par \uc2 So, she's the only place to put any non-Trump Republican money. So, she probably can keep going. And if it's more than 15, in the 20 range...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PENN: ... it's going to be hard to make a case that she's really going to be able to pull off this nomination.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Jim, I talked to a one billionaire donor who loves her to death, thinks she's a great candidate. But if she doesn't outright win tonight, he's done with her. He's not going to give her another penny. Now, not everyone shares that view, I know.\par \par \uc2 But, again, I'm going back thinking of the math here, looking at the numbers and how few have voted. And I know it would be very prohibitive in the next month or so for her to come back from something like that and get support. But a month is an eternity in politics. And look at all the changes we have seen in prior campaigns, where you get into the winter and then the spring and on and on.\par \par \uc2 One way or the other, too soon to wrap it up and put a cap on this?\par \par \uc2 MERRILL: I think it is.\par \par \uc2 I think that we need to take this into context. \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  started in New Hampshire at 2 percent 10 -- nine, 10 months ago.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MERRILL: And where she is today, within striking distance, is a reflection of the campaign she's run here.\par \par \uc2 She put time in, effort. She ran the most authentic grassroots campaign in New Hampshire. And so she's at least gotten herself to a position where she's close to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , and who came into this as a virtually incumbent with three years to build his case.\par \par \uc2 And so with two states having voted -- and, of course, ours is finishing voting in a few hours -- her home state a month away,and with questions around \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  about what's happening with his legal matters and everything else, I think it's good for the process, give the other 48 states a chance to start paying attention a little bit here.\par \par \uc2 Let's see where Haley can go with these sharpened attacks. Let's see where she can go with her home state. Let's let New Hampshire voters speak first and see where we are. But, from my standpoint, I'm looking forward to having this debate about the future of the party continue in the weeks ahead.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, I just can't fathom -- I'm a numbers guy -- just say, wrap it up now, and we're only like a couple of percentages to all the votes.\par \par \uc2 Mark, real quickly, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is not on the ballot here. If you want him, you have to write him in. It's a convoluted soap opera battle with the party because they have skipped New Hampshire, put the emphasis on South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 I wonder if that's going to come back to bite the president, who's never had a good track record in this state to begin with, but is making wounds even more sore now. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 PENN: Yes, I think this was sort of a tactical error. I think they would have been better leaving him on the ballot. And I think he would have been in a good position to win comfortably.\par \par \uc2 I think now it's sort of made a whole mess of it and gotten everybody in New Hampshire upset and given \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  more votes, and probably because it's not an official contest, and then same thing at Iowa. I'm not sure they handled it as well.\par \par \uc2 But, of course, look, there's no better way to get the nomination than not have any contest. And I think they have set it up the way they wanted to.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But what if it's an LBJ moment for him, the write-in vote is so pathetic, you can have a situation where Congressman Phillips does so well, \uc2 Marianne Williamson\uc2  does so well, they eclipse you. All bets are off in that case, right?\par \par \uc2 PENN: I don't know if bets are off. I just think that because they have already kind of decertified it, I don't think the whole -- you could have an LBJ moment here.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, we will watch that one closely.\par \par \uc2 Mark Penn\uc2 , final word. Jim Merrill, great talking to you as well. So, gentlemen, we will see how it all sorts out later on.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime here, we're getting more updates and details on that joint U.S.-British attack on Houthi rebel sites in Yemen. It was much more involved than we thought.\par \par \uc2 The read for Major General Patrick Ryder, the Pentagon press secretary -- coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You are looking live in Derry, New Hampshire, where the vote is on right now. They're expecting about 322,000 to 325,000 to vote this go- round. That would be 53 percent of eligible Republicans, which would beat the roughly 44 percent who did so in 2016.\par \par \uc2 We will see.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, we are learning a little bit more about these joint attacks conducted by the United States and Great Britain on the Houthi rebel sites in Yemen.\par \par \uc2 With us right now, Major General Patrick Ryder, of course, the Pentagon press secretary.\par \par \uc2 Secretary, General, very good to have you. What can you tell us about how involved these were? It seems a little bit more involved than we earlier thought.\par \par \uc2 MAJ. GEN. PATRICK RYDER, PENTAGON PRESS SECRETARY: Yes, thanks very much for having me, Neil.\par \par \uc2 So what we had last night were multinational strikes conducted by the United States and the United Kingdom, with support from Australia, Bahrain, Canada, and the Netherlands. And these strikes were very specific to target capabilities that the Houthis have been using to conduct attacks against international shipping and mariners in the Red Sea.\par \par \uc2 So far, our initial assessments are that we had good effects, but we will continue to assess going forward. Again, the goal here is to disrupt and degrade their ability to conduct these kinds of attacks going ahead in the future.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: No, I understand maybe from your briefing earlier, sir, that the U.S. has at this point destroyed and degraded more than 25 missile facilities since January 11.\par \par \uc2 They have had follow-up attacks. So the fear is, they have got other means or areas from which they can attack us. I know that's sort of like a pinprick strategy. You can't get to all pin sites, but what do you say about their ability to attack U.S. element shipping entrants in the Red Sea now? How much has that been degraded?\par \par \uc2 RYDER: Yes, so, as you highlight, since January 11, we assess that we have successfully destroyed or degraded over 20 missiles and 25 missile launch and deployment facilities, as well as a variety of other capabilities, to include coastal radars, unmanned aerial vehicles, aerial surveillance and weapon storage areas.\par \par \uc2 So all of that combines to degrade and disrupt their ability to conduct these kinds of attacks going forward. Now, we do know that the Houthis maintain capabilities, additional capabilities, and would not be surprised if we see other attacks in the future.\par \par \uc2 But what we're doing here is putting a cost on those attacks and essentially making it very clear that there will be consequences. So, going forward, we will continue to work with the international community to again put this pressure on the Houthis with the ultimate goal being that the Red Sea and the Gulf of Aden are safe and secure for international shipping.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Secretary, today was our first chance to see Lloyd Austin. He delivered virtual remarks in his first public appearance since his hospitalization.\par \par \uc2 Many have been scrutinized that and wonder, what is his health status? Is he permanently out of the hospital? What can you tell us?\par \par \uc2 RYDER: Yes, so thanks very much for asking.\par \par \uc2 Well, the secretary is recovering well. He continues to work from home and perform his duties from there as he recuperates, conducting physical therapy post-hospital. And so we, of course, wish him a speedy recovery and a return to the Pentagon as soon as he can.\par \par \uc2 But, as you highlight, today was the 18th Ukraine Defense Contact Group, which brought together more than 50 nations to focus on what we need to do to continue helping Ukraine defend themselves against Russia's illegal and unprovoked aggression.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Secretary, his looks notwithstanding, and some are concerned that he looked still under the weather -- it would be an understatement of someone who was in the hospital as long as he has -- but that he is still, according to you, conducting these operations, not only addressing the Ukraine matter you just alluded to, but these follow-up attacks, joint or otherwise, on Houthi rebels?\par \par \uc2 RYDER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Secretary Austin, of course, resumed his full duties January 5 and has been conducting and actively engaged in departmental operations, leading the department and, as evidenced by last night, continues to stay very actively engaged in implementing the national security policies of our nation.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, I'm sorry if I'm slow on the take, sir.\par \par \uc2 Is he back at his Defense Department offices or is he doing this from home?\par \par \uc2 RYDER: Yes, again, as I mentioned just a minute ago there, he's continuing to perform his duties from home. And we look forward to him returning to the Pentagon as soon as possible.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Has there been any discussion of his stepping down?\par \par \uc2 RYDER: No. Right now, again, he maintains his focus on carrying out his duties as secretary of defense and ensuring that our nation remains safe.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And the president still has full confidence in him and on these follow-up strikes and all, still confidence in the secretary leading those strikes, commanding those strikes?\par \par \uc2 RYDER: Yes, thanks, Neil. I'm not going to speak for the president.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 RYDER: But I think you have heard him and the White House communicate the president's confidence in Secretary Austin.\par \par \uc2 Again, there are lots of things happening in the world right now, as you have seen, and Secretary Austin remains squarely focused on executing his duties as the secretary of defense and protecting our nation.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, then, finally, sir, on the Houthi rebels and their resilience to a lot of attacks, not all, they are degraded with each and every attack, but not to the point where they're unable to attack.\par \par \uc2 And that's led many of these shipping lines and oil tanker parent companies who've been with me on my business show to say, pretty much to a man and company, sir, they don't want a risk going back in that area, for fear of this happening again. Even if there's a task force accompanying them, they just don't feel safe.\par \par \uc2 What do you tell them?\par \par \uc2 RYDER: Well, certainly, that is a decision that each company needs to decide on its own.\par \par \uc2 But what you saw here again was the Houthis began to conduct these attacks back in mid-November.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 RYDER: And despite multiple warnings from many nations, and despite the fact that you had 14 nations, plus the United States issue a statement in early January, warning of consequences, they have made the decision to continue conducting these attacks.\par \par \uc2 And so there is a cost to that. And, again, our goal here is to ensure that those mariners, that those companies are able to transit this international waterway safely and that the impacts on the global economy are removed.\par \par \uc2 And so, again, we will continue to stay focused on conducting that mission to turning those attacks should we need to, but one would certainly hope that the Houthis stop, and stop now.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: General, thank you very much, Major General Patrick Ryder, the Pentagon press secretary.\par \par \uc2 Secretary, thank you again.\par \par \uc2 RYDER: Thanks, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, in the meantime, it's all politics today, right, with the attention on New Hampshire and the issue that is gripping that state, which is the border, but it's not the same issue that the White House is emphasizing.\par \par \uc2 We will explore and explain -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I believe his policies are what we need, the border, wall.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I like what she wants to do for the country. I like her stance on immigration.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, it's a big issue for Republicans in the New Hampshire primary, immigration, the wall, national security there at the border, just as it was in Iowa.\par \par \uc2 Very odd these are not border states, but nothing bordering on a major issue for them. It is the issue for them. For Democrats, though, and the White House in particular, the president in Virginia today, the emphasis seems to be on an entirely different issue.\par \par \uc2 Rich Edson has more from Manassas, Virginia -- Rich.\par \par \uc2 RICH EDSON, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: And good evening, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Well, the Biden/Harris reelection campaign is going right at the abortion issue. We have got Vice President Harris speaking right now. She will introduce President Biden in just a quick bit here.\par \par \uc2 But this is a rally here in Northern Virginia that the campaign has billed as highlighting the stakes for the election for a woman's right to make her own health care decision. Harris has already been hitting this issue. She was out in Wisconsin yesterday to begin with the campaign is calling a multistate reproductive freedom tour, reminding voters that then-President Trump's three Supreme Court picks tipped the balance on the court in favor of overturning Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 Through rallies, TV, digital advertising and swing states, the Biden campaign says it will highlight abortion. It's an issue Democrats say helped galvanize voters and their candidates in the 2022 midterms and in local elections last November.\par \par \uc2 Now, here in Virginia, Governor \uc2 Glenn Youngkin\uc2  campaigned for Republicans to win control of both houses of the state legislature. They lost both. Democrats point out that Republicans had pushed a 15-week abortion ban in that campaign.\par \par \uc2 Biden and Harris are pushing Congress to pass a bill that would make Roe v. Wade federal law. Around the time the Supreme Court overturned that abortion decision, Democrats tried passing a measure to do so. Republicans rejected it because they say the Democrats bill went further than Roe with no explicit time limits on abortions.\par \par \uc2 Other Republicans say states should put limits on abortions or decide what to do. Others have said there should be a national standard stopping abortions after a certain number of weeks. Now, here in Manassas, we are about 30 miles from the White House. We are about 500 miles from New Hampshire and the New Hampshire primary.\par \par \uc2 Remember, Democrats and the Democratic Party downgraded the New Hampshire primary. So President Biden isn't even on the ballot there. They're conducting a write-in campaign there, his supporters, to try to get him at least to win a primary that he's not actively running in.\par \par \uc2 Remember, it was only four years ago that he finished in fifth place -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, he's always had a devil of a time in the Granite State.\par \par \uc2 Fascinating stuff, Rich. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Rich Edson in Manassas with the president and the vice president.\par \par \uc2 Bob Cusack right now, The Hill editor in chief.\par \par \uc2 It's interesting. They're going back to the abortion issue. It has worked for them in the last couple of elections, a presidential one. You could argue right now in the midterms that it did. But I don't know if it's a gift that keeps giving for them or this real issue that unites a good many Republicans, to say nothing of Democrats, and that is the border crisis.\par \par \uc2 I wonder if they're missing a chance to pound that before it pounds them.\par \par \uc2 BOB CUSACK, EDITOR IN CHIEF, THE HILL: Yes, I mean, listen Republicans in -- certainly in the primary -- and that's why \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  won the presidency. He went hard right on immigration and the border and that helped him certainly in the primary.\par \par \uc2 And Democrats, they did very well in the midterms better than expected. They lose the House, but just narrowly. So that's something Democrats are going to be talking a lot, but I think it's an interesting point you raised, Neil. Like, how long can that last? Obviously, it's an important issue.\par \par \uc2 It's so important for Democratic turnout. But how long will it be as -- will it turn out people like it did in 2020, especially 2022? It is their number one issue that they're going to be talking about, I think, more than the economy, because Trump has the advantage on the economy.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: On the economy and all that, it's a big issue in New Hampshire, which is a thriving economic state. If you talk to \uc2 Chris Sununu\uc2 , the Republican governor, it's about the Republican leadership in the state and the fact that it's high-tech and all that.\par \par \uc2 So it might not be a perfect microcosm of other states and how the country is going, but even there a lot of folks are sick of the price increases. They know that inflation has come down from the heights it was, but it's still pretty high and it's not sitting well.\par \par \uc2 CUSACK: Yes, and people aren't getting their money back. Inflation, as you correctly note, Neil, has gone down, but things are still expensive.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 CUSACK: And even when they go down, I think there's an inflation hangover that the Biden/Harris ticket has to deal with and make the claim that -- well, at first, I think they really downplayed it and so did the Fed chair.\par \par \uc2 Now it's more under control, but still the polls are not good in this department. Trump is beating Haley on the economy and beating Biden on the economy.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: What do you think of what the pressure to keep Haley is under to do well?\par \par \uc2 I mean, I talked to one big donor, who said, no, no, she can't just come in second. It can't be a strong second, and it can't be within 5 percentage points. She has to win outright. Now, not everyone agrees with that. And many of her strongest supporters are saying, keep running, keep running, because you're the only alternative out there right now.\par \par \uc2 What are you hearing? What do you think of that?\par \par \uc2 CUSACK: I agree with that donor.\par \par \uc2 I think she's got to win. If she narrowly loses, OK, she can certainly spin it maybe within five points.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CUSACK: But if she loses by double digits -- and, listen, Trump world is going to -- they're going to pick up the pressure on her to drop out.\par \par \uc2 And a lot of the Trump surrogates have already called on Haley to drop out. And if she loses by double digits tonight, those calls are going to continue. I do think she's going to continue on to South Carolina, but she's not doing well in South Carolina and could really lose that state by, again, double digits.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: It is still early, to your point, so we will watch it closely.\par \par \uc2 Bob, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 CUSACK: Thanks, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Bob Cusack, The Hill editor in chief.\par \par \uc2 We talk a lot about endorsements and the role that they play. Sometimes, the biggest and most noteworthy endorsements really don't do squat when it comes to the final vote. There are a couple of exceptions, though.\par \par \uc2 We will explore -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2 , absolutely. We need some fresh blood in our government.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: What I liked about President Trump is, he showed us what he could do the first time, and I think he will do it again.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2 , absolutely. We need some fresh blood in our government.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: What I liked about President Trump is, he showed us what he could do the first time, and I think he will do it again.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, well both candidates had a chance to make some last- minute pit stops today, to make one final claim for New Hampshire residents of putting their faith and trust and ultimately vote in them.\par \par \uc2 Alexis McAdams following both around, including the former president himself -- Alexis.\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS MCADAMS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Yes, we were able to catch up with former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to talk to him about how he's feeling on the ground. He says he's confident and has a lot of support here in the Granite State, but we have also been talking to voters right over the past week or so.\par \par \uc2 And they tell us it all comes down to the border and the economy when they head to the polls. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VANESSA THOMAS, TRUMP VOTER: The main issues are definitely closing the border up to keep the United States of America safe.\par \par \uc2 SYLVIA HORSLEY, TRUMP VOTER: I don't think she's strong enough on the border, for one. I don't think she's good for the economy, for another.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: So, let's take a live look right now, Neil, at these four polling locations here in New Hampshire.\par \par \uc2 This is kind of what we have been seeing throughout the day, but it has been a steady flow of voters going there throughout the afternoon hours. And voters here take the first-in-the-nation primary seriously.\par \par \uc2 As that field has narrowed down, Trump says he's winning voters over simply because of his stance on the border and says people really care about those issues here. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : You have to watch both borders and you have to watch fly-ins. You have to watch everything, but the southern border is like nobody's ever seen, but the northern border is bad too.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: And, Neil, we talked with the former president and asked him, hey, this is a two-person race now. This is what \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  wanted. Do you think that's going to help her? He simply said, no, she doesn't have a shot here.\par \par \uc2 So her team continues to try to gain support on the ground, but we're waiting to see the former president out here with lots of supporters later tonight -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Alexis, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well, \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2 , the former Maryland governor, a very popular Republican governor in a very blue state, had thrown his support to \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2 , but if that doesn't work out a lot of people are looking to the former governor to see what he's going to do.\par \par \uc2 We will talk to him next.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I have been to a couple of her rallies, and she's very organized, very meticulous about what she says.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: He's been pretty steadfast all along. He believes in what he believes, and the American people stand behind him.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: A lot of people are endorsing him, back him. I was going to vote Vivek in, but he decided to endorse Trump, so I'm going to stick with that.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I'm going to go for some change here and go for \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2 . I have long been a supporter of Governor Sununu, and he's been backing her pretty aggressively.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: All right, all eyes on \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  tonight. Can she pull it out? Can she do good enough in a second-place finish or maybe win outright?\par \par \uc2 The pressure's on \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2 , the former Maryland governor, an oddity, in that, a Republican who was very popular in a very blue state for two terms in a row, endorsed \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2 . And now -- well, now she could be in some trouble if early polls are right, but they could be wrong.\par \par \uc2 Governor, always good to have you.\par \par \uc2 FMR. GOV. \uc2 LARRY HOGAN\uc2  (R-MD): Great to be with you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: What do you do if \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  does poorly tonight?\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: Well, first of all, I'm hoping that she doesn't do poorly. I think she's got a real shot to surprise some people. I think she's going to outperform expectations. They're expecting a really big turnout.\par \par \uc2 About 40 percent of the state of New Hampshire are registered independents, and I think there's a lot of frustration. So it's about expectations, the margins, how well she does tonight. But I'm still -- for the next three hours, I'm focused on trying to see if we can't get \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  to do what happens a lot in New Hampshire, which is pull a major upset.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Does she have to win? I talked to one of her billionaire backers who said, it's not coming in second that matters. You got to win outright.\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: Well, I think there's a lot of people counting her out completely.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: And they're giving kind of false expectations that it's going to look like Iowa. She's not going to get blown out like Iowa. It's going to be -- I think, if she gets pretty close, and it's a second place, that still gives her some momentum.\par \par \uc2 But, obviously, if she pulled an upset, it gives her all the attention she needs to roll back into her home state of South Carolina. And we're going to know in about three hours or so, but I'm still hoping that she performs better than everybody's talking about today.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You were once attached to No Labels. You left that maybe with the endorsement of \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2 , but No Labels was looking and talking to you. They want a different type of a candidate if it's down to a repeat of what we had last time, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Would you be interested in that, if that were to materialize?\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: Well, Neil, I understand why they're so focused on it, because there are a little over 70 percent of the people in America who really do not want to vote for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  or \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And they're frustrated with both parties.\par \par \uc2 There's what I call the frustrated majority, about 40 percent of the people in America now registered independent, just like New Hampshire.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: And so I think right now we're focused on what happens in the primary. I'm hoping we can elect a Republican nominee that can beat \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But, yes, the group has certainly been talking about it. I'm flattered that they're mentioning me, but it's not something that I'm really going after or focused on.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Do you think it could be a moot point, though? Because in some of these polls out lately, Governor, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is beating \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , in one pretty comfortably by five points. Now, that could change, and you remind me of that. But what do you think of that?\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: Yes, well, I think all along they were saying, look, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  can't beat \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . And then now it looks as if it's a very close race.\par \par \uc2 I think Trump is the -- and Trump is the only one that Biden could beat. Any other Republican, I think, would beat \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , but I think it's going to be close. It could go either way. And while it looks good right now, the election is not taking place until November. So if he's the nominee, things might be different.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: If you ran something -- and other names that have come up, the conventional argument is that It would take and divide the Republican vote, and it would hand the White House back to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . How do you feel about that?\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: Yes, well, I think it depends on who the candidates are.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: If it's a credible ticket with a Republican at the top of the ticket and a Democratic running mate -- they're talking about a unity ticket.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: I think a lot of people could get behind that, and we don't know where it's going to come from.\par \par \uc2 Look, I -- the only place I have been on the same -- on the ballot together, I ran ahead of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . I ran 45 points ahead of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . So we have a good ability to win swing voters and crossover. And that's, I think, why they're expressing interest in talking to me.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: If \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  loses by a lot tonight, Governor, you think she should still stay in just as a fallback candidate to whatever could happen to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: I think it's really a tough personal decision for her, and it depends on how close the race is.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: If it's not close, she's going to have to think long and hard before going down to South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 But, otherwise, I think she might hang in there as long as she can.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK.\par \par \uc2 We will watch closely. \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2 , former Maryland governor, good seeing you.\par \par \uc2 Here comes "The Five."\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020240123ek1n0005l\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Siding With Leftism Only Brings Ruin To America; The Angle Was Right: Trump Will Be The Nominee; Mother Speaks On Daughter Killed By MS-13 Member; Georgia Election Fraud Case Against Trump Is Unraveling; \uc2 Fani Willis\uc2  Accused Of Misconduct In Trump Georgia Case; Media Lovefest With Fani Willis Is Coming To An End; Representative Barbara Lee's Mystery White Guy Update; Democrats Says Black Voters Don't Like Biden Due To Misinformation; Judge Judy Says Country Would Croak If Trump Wins; Biden Expands Chemical Abortion Via Executive Order\b0\par\par\uc2 Laura Ingraham, Raymond Arroyo \par \uc2 7,574 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 January 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: The Ingraham Angle\par \uc2 INGANG\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS HOST: Still unafraid. Why don't I release some time for a nice bump out shout to Ingraham Angle is next.\par \par \uc2 LAURA INGRAHAM, FOX NEWS HOST: Good evening, everyone. I'm Laura Ingraham. This is The Ingraham Angle back in Washington tonight. One step closer. That's the focus of tonight's Angle.\par \par \uc2 All right, right after the Iowa caucuses, the Angle gave advice to Governor DeSantis and Governor Haley, drop out.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: People forget how young he is. So with what three more years as Florida's governor, he can build an even greater track record of success in that very important state. So, a future in the Republican party for him could be really bright. But he's simply not going to be president this time around. So, it's time to step aside and endorse Trump, waiting for South Carolina, it's a colossal waste of time, it's a waste of money and frankly, it's a waste of your future political capital.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now for having the nerve to say that I was set upon by a bunch of paid flacks for both candidates who obviously preferred to stay right on that gravy train as long as possible. But yesterday, DeSantis made the right call by suspending his campaign and endorsing Trump.\par \par \uc2 Now, I've been telling you, there's a lot of good news lately. And tonight, I'm going to say it again, there is a lot of good news. And this is good news for the populist movement and for the country. Now, the Angle has always had great respect for how DeSantis has led Florida, thanks to his policies, it's now a solidly red state, and it's growing like gangbusters. We all know that narrative.\par \par \uc2 He's built a strong multicultural coalition there that I think represents the future of the Republican Party. It's fantastic. It's smart for both Trump and DeSantis now to put all the past slights aside and work together. But \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  thinks she's smarter than both of them. She thinks she's tougher and more resilient. She's not a quitter, and she's not a victim. Except when she plays one on TV.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY\uc2  (R) GOP PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: We were the only Indian family in our small southern town. I was teased every day for being brown. So, anyone that wants to question it, can go back and look at what I've said on how hard it was to grow up in the deep south as a brown girl. I was disqualified from a beauty pageant because I wasn't white or black, because they didn't know where to put me.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, none of us knows what went down there. I'm sure there were slights and insults, but it all kinds of sounds very convenient, and a little bit odd. And frankly, it sounds like something a liberal would say. But it's something that the so-called moderates might actually like to hear because they like the same -- remember, the hash tagging of BLM during the Floyd riots, the same I'm a fiscal, not a social conservative, the billionaire types who happily virtue signal about DEI and nod silently when others talk about structural racism, because they think it makes them seem less greedy and more groovy.\par \par \uc2 And how many of them are now lining up behind Haley, the answer a lot. For some reason, Haley thinks that the right way to attract working class Republicans and independents is to court Wall Street.\par \par \uc2 The Hill is reporting that billionaire \uc2 Stanley Druckenmiller\uc2 , \uc2 Henry Kravis\uc2 , \uc2 Ken Langone\uc2  and Cliff Asness are reportedly planning a fundraiser for Haley, a big one on January 30th. It's going to be in New York then. But that's not all. Because after that, she'll be hitting Miami and Palm Beach to meet with wealthy donors and also go to California and Texas before the South Carolina primary, where she hopes she's going to meet more donors according to a source.\par \par \uc2 Now, Haley's team is bragging about all this, bragging about their $24 million fourth quarter money haul, and that's a lot of money. But it also got a third-place finish in Iowa for her and the spending in all that adds, did it really work? It's hardly impressive to me. She's hoping an additional $4 million ad buy in South Carolina is going to ensure that she's no Jeb Bush, she's not the Bush of 2024. No way.\par \par \uc2 But in this climate, with a winning message is closer to power to the people, she's veering toward power to the plutocrats. It's not smart at all for either the billionaires, or Governor Haley. They might as well light their money on fire at this point, throw it off the top of the tallest building they can find or send it to their favorite cause. I don't know I'm trying to be creative here, Ukraine? Send it right to Zelenskyy.\par \par \uc2 Haley is finished and prolonging the obvious is just ensuring that she has no political future. It's not really going to hurt Trump at this point. Well, maybe she has a political future if you think \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  has a bright political future.\par \par \uc2 Now Trump didn't win because DeSantis ran a weak campaign, a lot of people are dumping on DeSantis. Trump won because the Republican base was never going to abandon him, once the Biden administration decided to use the Justice Department against him.\par \par \uc2 I mean, conservatives understand properly that we can never let the Democrats pick our nominee. Never. And we know that if they succeed in weaponizing the DOJ against President Trump, they're going to use the same tactics against anyone who ever really threatens their hold on power. That sort of tyranny cannot stand, and Republicans have wisely pulled together to make sure it doesn't.\par \par \uc2 Now, under these circumstances, most Republicans were not looking for an alternative in 2024. But let's not forget all those former Republicans who claim that they were appalled by the chaos of President Trump. For almost eight years, they told us that they weren't so concerned with President Trump's policies, oh, no, they were just upset that he didn't follow the norms that he was dangerous.\par \par \uc2 Now, Governor DeSantis's campaign proved that those people were actually lying. Because if they really had been looking for the principal conservative, they claim they wanted, they would have rallied to Governor DeSantis. But they didn't. Because they don't like DeSantis's policies either or his beliefs. They want open borders. They want the U.S. to be China's equal or even maybe junior partner. And they want social conservatives to stand down. And they want war, lots and lots of war. So, as long as it doesn't inconvenience a CCP, that is.\par \par \uc2 That's what they've always wanted. That's what they want now. Everything else they tell you is a lie. They aren't conservatives. And frankly, they're not really patriots, not least the way I think of patriots. They're not principled. And that's why they had little to no interest in supporting DeSantis, because they only support politicians they can control. But believe me, Governor Haley, you are -- Governor DeSantis, excuse me, you're better off without them, because when Ron and \uc2 Casey DeSantis\uc2  appear at the \uc2 RNC\uc2 , and they'll do so this summer, they're going to be greeted with cheers and joy, but not the self-proclaimed principled Republicans who work to help Biden and destroy Trump.\par \par \uc2 Now, does \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  want to be with the players on the field or does she want to be like, I don't know John Kasich or \uc2 Jeff Flake\uc2  or \uc2 Adam Kinzinger\uc2  and pouting from the stands. Now, last week, I warned that she was headed down a path that could effectively end her career inside the GOP.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Stop listening to your high price campaign consultants who are telling you what they think you want to hear. If she wants a future in the Republican Party, Haley needs to tune out the Never Trump-ers, not embrace them. And then she needs to go to her donors, and this will be a toughy and say to them this, look, the party has moved on. It's not 2003 anymore, and we have to move on with it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: With Governor DeSantis and \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2  now behind President Trump and others, she has no path forward to the nomination. None. The RealClearPolitics average gives Haley only 11.5 percent nationwide. Her home state Senator Tim Scott endorsed President Trump last week. So, whatever happens in New Hampshire tomorrow night, President Trump will be nominated this summer. Other than the billionaires who's really with Haley. Oh, sorry. \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2 , Maryland; Asa Hutchinson, Arkansas; \uc2 Chris Sununu\uc2 , governor of New Hampshire. Oh, sorry, the editorial page of The \uc2 Boston Globe\uc2 . Yes, that's for Haley.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: On Friday, we told you that America got a lot of good news last week. And yesterday, DeSantis gave us more good news with that endorsement of Trump. So, the Republican Party is indeed uniting. You might not have believed it, believe it now. The country has a clear choice in 2024. It's either Trump or Biden.\par \par \uc2 And going forward, we sincerely hope that Governor Haley will reject the support of those who just want to use her to help Biden and join the rest of us, we're a lot more fun, because we're backing President Trump. But whatever she does, President Trump and his movement are one step closer to the greatest political comeback of all time. And that's the Angle.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Congressman Byron Donalds. Congressman, it's great to see you tonight. They all got mad at me last week when I said you know, it's time to step aside, your future in politics is not over, they're both still pretty young and could both do a lot. But now we have DeSantis doing the right thing, but Haley has a series of big-ticket fundraisers scheduled. What does that tell you?\par \par \uc2 REP. BYRON DONALDS (R-FL): What it tells me is, Laura, you have a lot of donors who haven't seen the writing on the wall, but I'm up here in northern New Hampshire right now. They just got done with the national anthem, and I'm going to tell you, the fever for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is real. That's what Republican voters want all across our country. The line outside and 20-degree weather proves it. And the donors need to come face to facts and face the reality. That the thing that's going to save this nation is an America First agenda and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's going to deliver it.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, congressman, you're in New Hampshire, you feel the energy. But some of the so-called Never Trump-ers who used to work in the Trump administration and if they could, they would come crawling back now, but they can't because they're actually hosts of The View. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: There's some sexism we haven't talked about. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , actually, intellectually knows that \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  is more qualified and more fit to be the GOP nominee. He's a Harvard grad. He's a Yale undergrad. He served in the Navy. He knows \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is not better than her,\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Congressman, is sexism the reason \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  isn't winning?\par \par \uc2 DONALDS: No, it's not. It's about the agenda. And it's about what the American people have seen. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has done this job, Laura, we've talked about that many, many times. And \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  does represent the old guard and the old way in the Republican Party. That's how she came up. That's how she governed in South Carolina. The voters in our country, Republican voters in our country do not want that.\par \par \uc2 They are tired of business, as usual in Washington, DC, and frankly, in a lot of state capitals in our country, what they want is an agenda that speaks for them. That speaks to them that demonstrates that our government is serious about doing everything of, by and for the people, not for businesses, not for foreign nationals, not for international globalist communities before the American people first and foremost, that's what they want, and that's what they're going to get.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Congressman, I'm just glad that you have a really loud voice given where you're standing right now. Thank you very much for joining us and have fun out there, my friend. All right, if you thought 2020 was a mess, Well, buckle up because blue states have already been passing big overhauls of their voting laws. In New York, Governor Hochul signed into law, no excuse mail in voting. Michigan now grants voters an extra nine days for early voting. They've also made it easier to vote by mail and even added more drop boxes, of course.\par \par \uc2 One Illinois lawmaker has a bill making voting by mail the default option for the state. So, what are Republicans doing, if anything. Joining me now Ned Ryun, Founder and CEO of American Majority. Ned, you and I have talked about this before. I don't think I've seen the Republican Party more united than it is right now, contrary to what the corporate media will try to convey to the people, but even if Trump wins the nomination by a landslide, does it matter if these mail in ballot drop box issues are not countered by the Republicans?\par \par \uc2 NED RYUN, AMERICAN MAJORITY FOUNDER AND CEO: Well, I would agree, Laura, on some levels, we have to be on parall