2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES OF AMERICA: I said on that day that Roe was overturned, the health and lives of women in this nation would now be at risk, and that has unfortunately proven to be true.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Yeah. Well, especially for the little girls who are aborted, I guess. And now he's deploying \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . She's going state to state. She was in Wisconsin today. They're going together to Virginia, tomorrow, a big abortion rally. And they've created, Laura, they're also sending Rachel Levine out. I don't know why Rachel Levine is going out telling women about healthcare, but there you go.\par \par \uc2 She's going out to push the -- to apply the abortion line. And they've put ballot initiatives, abortion ballot initiatives, in key swing states during this presidential election. Wisconsin, Arizona, Georgia, Michigan, Nevada, North Carolina, they're hoping abortion will bring out the faithful to support \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . This is a really callous political scenario.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I'm trying to figure this -- I'm trying to figure this out, OK? So, you're someone in Ohio, right? Or no, you're someone in New York, and no new jobs come in New York, right? Terrible crime, all the mess of migrants. But you're supposed to vote for a Democrat, so what, there can be more abortions for women in Mississippi? Like, it doesn't make any sense to me.\par \par \uc2 No, no, they're not going to expand prosperity, but abortions for everyone? Raymond, I don't think it works. Raymond, I know we're going to hear from the Kelsey brother's fans, but that's good to see you, as always. All right, Jesse, next. See you tomorrow night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020240123ek1m00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Supreme Court Allows Border Patrol To Remove Razor Wire; \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  Supporters Rally Voters On Write-In Effort; \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  Faces Pressure From Democrats Over Border; United States Declares Two Missing Navy Seals Dead; Trump Congratulates DeSantis After Withdrawal; Trump And Haley Battle For Republican Nomination; \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  And \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  Campaign In New Hampshire For Upcoming Republican Presidential Primary; Immigration Top Of Recent New Hampshire Polls For Issues Voters Concerned About; Supreme Court Rules Five To Four For Temporary Restraining Order On Texas Preventing Access To Border Patrol To Some Border Areas; President Biden Will Not Appear On New Hampshire Democratic Presidential Primary Ballot\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Peter Doocy, Alexis McAdams, Danamarie McNicholl,  Madison Alworth, Jennifer Griffin, Brit Hume, Guy Benson, Juan Williams \par \uc2 8,106 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 January 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Why do we have that shot when he didn't make it?\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL LEGAL ANALYST: Yes, but where did the money go?\par \par \uc2 HAROLD FORD JR., FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: I didn't put that together.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Who do we blame? I don't know, I'm going to blame Greg just because it's fun.\par \par \uc2 All right. That's it for us. Everybody have a great night. We'll see you tomorrow. Hi, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Hi, Dana. So, if somebody in New Hampshire wanted to write in Gutfeld's name, do they add the exclamation point or they?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: You know if you do, I hear it counts for two votes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Two votes, all right. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. I'm Bret Baier. We are coming to you live from Bedford, New Hampshire for the second voting and first primary of the 2024 presidential election cycle. We will set the stage tonight for the now two candidate race in tomorrow's primary election in just a moment.\par \par \uc2 But first, breaking tonight, a closely divided \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  says federal agents can begin removing razor wire along the southern border in an area of federal land that Texas has taken over.\par \par \uc2 The five to four decision is seen as a big win for the Biden administration and comes as Texas officials are refusing to back down installing new barbed wire fencing in that area.\par \par \uc2 Immigration and Border Patrol officers, the border issues actually were top issues for voters during the Iowa caucuses. They are trending issues here for voters in New Hampshire.\par \par \uc2 Danamarie McNicholl is in Eagle Pass with the latest on the ground. Good evening.\par \par \uc2 DANAMARIE MCNICHOLL, FOX NEWS CHANNEL REPORTER: Bret, the decision that came down just hours ago really does affect what happens here in Shelby Park. I can tell you Border Patrol from what I can see has not tried to take any of the razor wire down but that could quickly change.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MCNICHOLL (voice over): Texas military engineers spent the whole weekend installing hundreds of yards of razor wire in Shelby Park. City owned property that the state of Texas recently seized control of and kicked the federal government out.\par \par \uc2 Texas state troopers have been arresting migrants who tamper with the razor wire while crossing illegally, charging them with criminal trespassing, but we don't know if that will continue or for how long.\par \par \uc2 The reason Texas DPS took on the role of arresting migrants was because Border Patrol was ordered out at the park.\par \par \uc2 Today, the justices by a five to four vote granted an emergency appeal from the Biden administration. Texas Governor Greg Abbott defended his strategy before the decision.\par \par \uc2 GOV. GREG ABBOTT (R-TX): This is an invasion. But people we don't know who they are, where they're coming from, or the danger they may pose. And so, it's right for Texas to have taken the steps that we have taken to crack down on the illegal crossings.\par \par \uc2 MCNICHOLL (voice over): It's notable that Chief Justice Roberts and justice Barrett sided with the courts, three left leaning justices to rule in favor of the Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MCNICHOLL (on camera): And we just heard from the Border Patrol union president who said, "Agents do support what Texas was trying to accomplish in the absence of border security policies from this administration." Another question is what will happen next? Will we see changes, if any, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Danamarie, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now, to the New Hampshire primary. And then there were two, former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his former United Nations Ambassador \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  are the last major candidates standing in the Republican presidential field after Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  suspended his campaign this weekend and endorsed the former president.\par \par \uc2 The withdrawal could shift the landscape of the primary season especially here. Correspondent Alexis McAdams takes a look tonight from Manchester.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS MCADAMS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Tonight, just hours away from the first in the nation primary, things have shifted to a two person race.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY\uc2  (R), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: We haven't even started with this election yet. We don't do coronations. A democracy is about giving people options.\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS (voice over): \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  officially dropping out and endorsing his former rival, former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  sounding confident that DeSantis voters will lean her way.\par \par \uc2 HALEY: The DeSantis voters, they love America and they wanted a conservative that was going to go in there and clean up government and that's what we're going to do.\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS (voice over): Trump firing back at her slides on his age and acuity.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I think I'm a lot sharper than her, I would do this. I would sit down right now and take an aptitude test and it would be my result against her result.\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS (voice over): So, what do voters think? Well, according to the latest Monmouth University Washington Post poll, 52 percent of potential voters say they plan to vote for Trump in Tuesday's Republican presidential primary. 34 percent picking Haley.\par \par \uc2 Haley says this race is nowhere close to over and has no interest in being vice president. Though Trump won't say if Haley would even have a spot.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : There's no reason to rule people out. But I think it's highly unlikely.\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS (voice over): Now that DeSantis has dropped out, polls could shift. Some voters say they're happy the Republican field is narrowing.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We're Republicans, we're elephants. So we've got long memories. We're not going to forget, we're not going to forgive you, Ron.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS (on camera): Now, it's that final push tonight here in the Granite State, Bret. We know both candidates are holding events here on the ground. \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  having one right now.\par \par \uc2 And former President Trump is on his way back after that was supposed to be a court appearance in New York. So, he'll be here in just a short time from now.\par \par \uc2 This as we're now learning, Bret, that Trump will be allowed on that ballot in Massachusetts, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Alexis, thank you.\par \par \uc2 President Biden will not be on the New Hampshire ballot tomorrow after the DNC and the state could not come to an agreement on the primary schedule. That is forcing grassroots campaigns to push for write in votes and causing concern in the Biden camp over the optics possibly of losing the Granite State.\par \par \uc2 Fox Business correspondent Madison Alworth explains from here in Manchester.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TERIE NORELLI, NEW HAMPSHIRE VOTER: The DNC made a mistake. Lots of people make mistakes, but we're going to set the record straight.\par \par \uc2 MADISON ALWORTH, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK CORRESPONDENT: Biden supporters want the president to win New Hampshire's Democratic primary. But for him to do that, he will need a majority of voters to write in his name. That's because the president is not on the ballot after he instructed the DNC to make South Carolina the first primary of 2024 and New Hampshire did not comply. Biden has not been in the state, but some of his friends have been.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE WU\uc2 , MAYOR OF BOSTON: We need a leader with the temperament, with the tenacity and with the team around them to move us forward.\par \par \uc2 ALWORTH (voice over): Boston Mayor \uc2 Michelle Wu\uc2  and California Congressman \uc2 Ro Khanna\uc2  were both here this weekend, lending their support to the write in Biden efforts.\par \par \uc2 And this morning, Senator \uc2 Maggie Hassan\uc2  stumped for the president, but also made her opinion known regarding the change in primary calendar and his absence.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 MAGGIE HASSAN\uc2  (D-NH): We have addressed our concerns with him directly. And it was a very bad decision. And we've been very clear that that's what we think.\par \par \uc2 ALWORTH (voice over): According to polls, the write in effort looks like it will handedly win. But not all Democrats are happy.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN, NEW HAMPSHIRE VOTER: I think probably the best answer right now is to vote for \uc2 Dean Phillips\uc2 . Just to send a message to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ALWORTH (voice over): Biden's two main challengers have also been openly critical of the decision. Williamson said the DNC wants to anoint the president and Phillips claimed the DNC is suppressing voters.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 DEAN PHILLIPS\uc2  (D), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: I don't think Americans are being told the truth about what is really happening. It's shameful. It's wrong. It's dangerous.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ALWORTH (on camera): Phillips is hosting a meet and greet with voters behind us. You know, he has said that he's going to go forward with his campaign no matter what happens tomorrow, but he has expressed an affinity for joining a No Label type third party, depending on the circumstances, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Madison, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Back to our top story, the Biden administration is working overtime to try to shift the focus off the southern border crisis and onto policies it sees as winning issues for Democrats. White House correspondent Peter Doocy has the story.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: A fundamental right ripped away important to so many Americans.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Abortion which the Biden White House believes will be a big motivator this election year.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER KLEIN, WHITE HOUSE GENDER POLICY COUNCIL: Every time Americans are given the opportunity to make their voices heard. They make their voices heard very loudly and clearly in favor of reproductive freedom.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): But what about the freedom to pursue the American dream?\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (D-PA): We want to provide the American dream for any migrant, but it seems very difficult when you have 300,000 people showing up encountered.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): Just because the White House is pivoting to abortion access, doesn't mean problems at the border have been solved.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: He needs resources to deal with the situation at the border.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): It's unclear what resources would have prevented this, a jailed illegal immigrant from Honduras accused of sexually assaulting a Virginia minor and producing sexual abuse material was released by Virginia authorities.\par \par \uc2 ICE later apprehended him nearby. And now ICE complaints in a statement, Fairfax County refused to honor the ICE detainer lodged against this Honduran non-citizen. When ICE detainers are ignored by local authorities, the public is put at risk.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Does not go to show that as record numbers of people appear at border. You guys have no idea what kind of people are coming into this country.\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: Let me just say, first of all, this is why the president is having negotiations with the Senate. Republicans in the Senate is working with us. Republicans in the House are trying to get in the way.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (on camera): Immigration policy not something that's White House is trying to draw attention to. Abortion policy is.\par \par \uc2 So, the day of the New Hampshire primary, officials here are hoping to draw some attention away from Republicans with a rare Biden and Harris side by side rally about abortion in Virginia, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Peter Doocy on the North Lawn. Peter, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Optimism over earnings reports lead the Dow and the S&P 500 to new record closes today. The Dow gained 138, the S&P rose 11. The NASDAQ added 49.\par \par \uc2 Up next, a new round of strikes on Iran backed targets and the latest on the exhaustive search for two missing Navy SEALs.\par \par \uc2 But first, growing optimism in the Haley camp that she can outperform expectations here against former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: A big crowd in Exeter, New Hampshire as \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  gets the endorsement of Judge Judy. This crowd believes that she is going to take it down to the wire and win on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Will she do it? That's the question. SPECIAL REPORT continues after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Dexter Scott King, the youngest son of \uc2 Martin Luther King\uc2 , Jr. and Coretta Scott King has died after battling prostate cancer. The King Center in Atlanta, which Dexter served as chairman says the 62-year-old son of the civil rights icon died peacefully in his sleep at his home in Malibu, California.\par \par \uc2 The former House Select Committee on the January 6th 2021, Capitol riot deleted more than 100 encrypted files from its probe just days before Republicans took over the majority in the House of Representatives.\par \par \uc2 According to Fox News Digital, a Republican-led panel was set to receive all documents from the select committee, but a forensic team determined 117 files were both deleted and encrypted.\par \par \uc2 Former President Trump's ability to testify in his civil defamation case is in doubt after the presiding judge postponed the trial because one juror and a parent of one of Trump's lawyers became ill.\par \par \uc2 Judge Lewis Kaplan is considering allowing the former president to testify Wednesday. Writer E. Jean Carroll is seeking millions of dollars for defamation. Last May a different jury ordered the former president to pay her $5 million after finding he sexually abused her in a department store dressing room in the 1990s.\par \par \uc2 The former president has denied all allegations.\par \par \uc2 A judge has unsealed a divorce case that has entangled the Atlanta district attorney prosecuting former President Trump in the Georgia election interference case against him and his allies.\par \par \uc2 However, the judge halted plans to force testimony from Fulton County District Attorney \uc2 Fani Willis\uc2 , who appointed lawyer Nathan Wade to manage that case. Willis is accused of having an improper relationship with Wade.\par \par \uc2 A Georgia Republican state senator has reportedly introduced legislation to establish a committee to look into the allegations of misconduct.\par \par \uc2 Dozens of family members have Israeli hostages held by \uc2 Hamas\uc2  stormed in Israeli parliament committee meeting today to demand a deal to win their loved ones' release.\par \par \uc2 Roughly 130 hostages are believed to remain in \uc2 Hamas\uc2  custody. Israeli Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  is facing growing demands from relatives of hostages to cut a deal with \uc2 Hamas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "BREAKING TONIGHT", the U.S. and the United Kingdom are carrying out new multiple strikes on Iran-backed Houthi-controlled areas of Yemen.\par \par \uc2 That news comes as U.S. Central Command ends a search and rescue operation for two Navy SEALs reported missing while on a mission to seize Iranian weapons headed to Yemen.\par \par \uc2 Chief national security correspondent Jennifer Griffin has the story tonight live from the Pentagon. Good evening, Jen.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRIFFIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret. American and British warships and warplanes carried out multiple strikes on Houthi targets in Yemen, the second time since January 11th. F-18 fighter jets from the USS Eisenhower and Tomahawk cruise missiles struck multiple targets at eight Houthi locations, including Al Dailami Air Base, and missile launch sites, and underground facilities where the Iranian proxy stores its ballistic missiles and drones being fired at Red Sea shipping lanes.\par \par \uc2 Separately, the \uc2 U.S. Navy\uc2  called off the search for two of its own.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN (voice over): After an exhaustive 10-day search off the northern coast of Somalia two U.S. Navy SEALs have been declared dead. Navy Special Warfare operator first class Christopher Chambers and Navy Special Warfare operator second class Nathan Gage Ingram, both of the West Coast-based SEAL Team Three.\par \par \uc2 REAR ADM. JOHN KIRBY (RET.), COORDINATOR FOR STRATEGIC COMMUNICATIONS, NATIONAL SECURITY COUNCIL: We are to -- just take a moment to remember that a couple of families are just got the worst news possible. And it's important that they know that the president and first lady will stay solidly behind them and their families make sure that they get all the support they need.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN (voice over): The SEALs were part of a team that boarded a ship smuggling Iranian ballistic and cruise missile parts to Yemen. Gage Ingram has slipped and fell into the water off the ladder as they were boarding the dhow for a flag verification. And Chris Chambers dove in to rescue his teammate.\par \par \uc2 The U.S., Japan, and Spain search more than 21,000 square miles to locate these missing Americans. The SEAL team was operating from the USS Lewis Puller.\par \par \uc2 This weekend, the U.S. ramped up its shadow war on Iranian proxy forces, hitting Houthi missile sites in a series of airstrikes as a group in Iraq launched 17 ballistic missiles and rockets at the Al Asad Air Base, where most of the 2,500 U.S. troops in Iraq are based.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. fired 15 Patriot surface to air missiles from Al Asad to intercept the missiles two manage to get through the air defense and hit the base.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN (on camera): Two U.S. service members suffered a suspected traumatic brain injury from the attack. We're told they have returned to duty. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jennifer Griffin, live at the Pentagon. Jennifer, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, my conversation is on the campaign trail with former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and former Governor \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2 . First, "BEYOND OUR BORDERS" tonight, a landslide buries at least 47 people in a remote village in southwestern China. Two survivors were rescued as crews continued their search in freezing temperatures and snow.\par \par \uc2 State media there says at least eight people have been killed.\par \par \uc2 Indian Prime Minister Modi opens a controversial Hindu temple that was built on the ruins of a raised historic mosque. The temple sits on a site where Hindu mobs tore down a mosque three decades ago. Some see the temple as an example of the rise of Hindu supremacy leaders under Modi's tenure.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Victoria, Australia. One of the big stories there tonight, a pro-Palestinian protester throws papers onto an Australian Open tennis court briefly disrupting a match between Alexander Zverev and Cameron Norrie, before being stopped by other spectators.\par \par \uc2 Olympic champion, Zverev, says he didn't feel unsafe. The question why it took several minutes for security to react, leaving it to tennis fans to intervene.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories "BEYOND OUR BORDERS" tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The reshaped Republican presidential field could have a major impact on the outcome here in the New Hampshire primary, and really all the way down the line.\par \par \uc2 I went out on the campaign trail here, caught up with the two major contenders about the shifting dynamics of this race.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I'd like to take time to congratulate \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . And, of course, a really terrific person who have gotten to know his wife Casey for having run a great campaign for president. He did. He ran a really good campaign.\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: It's clear to me that a majority of Republican primary voters want to give \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  another chance. He has my endorsement because we can't go back to the old Republican guard of yesteryear.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : He was very gracious and he endorsed me. So, I appreciated that. I appreciate that.\par \par \uc2 It's a very hard thing to do he left.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Somebody said what are you going to do with the name, DeSanctimonious? I said that name is officially retired as of this moment. We are retiring that name.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: How is it feel, obviously, you're getting ready for a big rally here?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : It feels fantastic. That's a tremendous group of people. This is the biggest arena in the state. And it's packed and they could -- if it was larger, that would be even better, because they have a lot of people outside and they're probably not going to be able to get in.\par \par \uc2 It's a great feeling. That's a poll, as you and I just said. That's a poll. It's the ultimate poll. But we feel very good. We feel very confident.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Down to two, how does this change the dynamic for you?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: Well, we came into New Hampshire knowing this was a two-person race. And so, that's why you've seen me totally focused on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Because that's the one fell I've got left to take on.\par \par \uc2 And I think that this is a very stark contrast is, do we want more of the same? Or do we want a new generational leader? 70 percent of Americans don't want to see a Biden-Trump rematch. The majority of Americans disapprove of both of them. Both \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  put us trillions of dollars in debt that our kids are never going to forgive them for.\par \par \uc2 And you look now, they are so busy focused on their own investigations, or who hurt their feelings that day that they're talking about the past, when we need someone who's going to focus on the solutions of the future. That's what I want to do.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I got to know Nikki very well. Nikki was not a great negotiator. She had certain things that she was good at. But she wouldn't be good for this job. I can tell you that right now.\par \par \uc2 Look, she is a globalist. I've always known that about her. And our country cannot be globalist. Wouldn't even have that choice anymore.\par \par \uc2 HALEY: You know, what he also said when I was \uc2 U.N.\uc2  ambassador? He said I was the best \uc2 U.N.\uc2  ambassador he ever had. He said I was a killer. He said I was tough. And he said everybody was afraid of me.\par \par \uc2 So, what I'll tell you now is I am the wife -- proud wife of a combat veteran.\par \par \uc2 My husband is deployed right now. Ask any military family. The last thing you ever want is to see your love one go to war.\par \par \uc2 What I will do is I will be tough with every country we deal with. I will tell them what America is for and what America is against. I don't care if they don't like me. But I want them to respect America. And this is about preventing wars.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Nikki comes from South Carolina. Tim Scott is from South Carolina. But if you look, the governor, great governor, another senator, Lindsey, we happen to like Lindsey. But \uc2 Henry McMaster\uc2  knows her very well. He endorsed me. It's very hard for a governor to endorse somebody when you have -- I mean, \uc2 Henry McMaster\uc2  was the lieutenant governor under her, and he endorsed me. And he is going to be here tonight. In 15 minutes you're going to be watching him speak. I'm going to introduce him. He is a great guy. He has done a great job. So everyone, almost everybody in South Carolina has endorsed me. What does that tell you?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Governor, you lieutenant governor, who is now governor endorsing Trump. The two senators, including one that you put in the job endorsing Trump is under Tim Scott, other South Carolina lawmakers. He says that tells a story. What do you say?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: It sure does tell a story, because the governor is also the same person that I defeated when I ran for governor, and he lost to me. So if course he is going to be supporting \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And then you go and you look at all these legislators, those are the same ones that I forced them to show their votes on the record because they were hiding things by voice votes. Those were the same ones that didn't want to pass ethics reform and we got it done. Those are the same ones that I made totally transparent and sat there and made them -- vetoed all of their pet projects so that they couldn't get those through. Of course they're not going to like me because they know what I did to them.\par \par \uc2 He can have all those legislative endorsements he wants. At the end of the day, I know when you get political endorsements, whether it's Congress or whether it's in South Carolina, you are trying to get the political elite. I don't ever want the political elite.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Here in New Hampshire, \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  has made an unholy alliance with RINOs, never-Trumpers.\par \par \uc2 (BOOS)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Americans for no prosperity, they're headed up by bad people. Globalists, radical left communists, to get liberals and Biden supporters to vote. For her, what kind of a governor do you have in this state where they allow Democrats --\par \par \uc2 (BOOS)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Democrats, they've already got 5,000 signed up. But that's peanuts compared to the numbers we get. No, but they've already got -- you had to be signed up by like October 7th. But think of it, Democrats to vote in the Republican primary, what kind of a governor would allow that?\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 CHRIS SUNUNU\uc2 , (R) NEW HAMPSHIRE: All the chips on the line, two candidates, and what we are predicting to be record turnout tomorrow. So it's a lot of wind in the sails.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So you feel good? You've been going to a lot of these events, almost every one. You see something different than you see in the polls?\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: Oh, sure. Look, the polls are always off. There is no question about that. Incredible energy for \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2 . The retail politics, it's all Nikki. Trump isn't even there to be seen. So she's connecting with voters as a strong conservative, incredible background as a conservative Tea Party candidate, governor of South Carolina. But she is still likeable, and that's important.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: She told me that last night, too, Tea Party candidate. Is that to counter the Democrat, independent push?\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: Yes, Democrats don't vote here, guys. Democrats cannot vote here. They can't.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But he's talking about that.\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: Of course he is, because he's panicked. Trump wouldn't be throwing all these lies out if he wasn't nervous about what he seeing in her own internal polls and his own internal numbers.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Just to be clear, Democrats could have in October switched parties and been --\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: In every state you can do that. You can always switch parties. But you have to do it months beforehand, just like every other state.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Undeclareds can --\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: Yes. Look, in Iowa you could be a Democrat and go caucus for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  if you wanted to.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That day.\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: Yes, yes. You can't do that here.\par \par \uc2 So no, we have the right process, it's the right system. Trump is trying to throw everything he can in terms of attacking Nikki. But she is on the ground. She's the one connecting with voters.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But being a former president, having the name recognition, a short window between Iowa and New Hampshire, that's still --\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: That definitely favors Trump. There is no question. Look, I know we're saying he's yesterday's news, and he is, but he is the incumbent. He's effectively the standard-bearer of the party for the last seven years. He has taken that for granted, I think, especially on the ground here.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : If you took the 10 worst presidents in the history of the United States and added them up, they would not have done near the destruction to our country as crooked \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and the Biden administration. There is nobody that has ever done damage to our country like this.\par \par \uc2 You know Vivek? Just came with us. And now Ron just came with us. They're all coming with us. They're all coming.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Judy Sheindlin.\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 JUDY SHEINDLIN, "JUDGE JUDY": The country needs somebody who is focused, poised, energetic, knowledgeable, has executive experience, international experience, doesn't have an axe to grind, doesn't create chaos wherever she goes. America needs her. Believe me, America needs her more than she needs this aggravation.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So, are you ready for the judge Judy riff from the former president?\par \par \uc2 SHEINDLIN: We both have our New York roots. I wouldn't like him to start up with me.\par \par \uc2 HALEY: I have been very consistent. I said I wanted to be strong in Iowa. We were. We started at two percent. We ended at 20 percent. I said I want to be stronger in New Hampshire. We will do that because now it's a two- person race, and everybody else had to get out. Then I will be even stronger than that in South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Just so you know, she is way behind. You are talking like, oh, gee, we are concerned with her. I'm not concerned with her at all. I think we are going to win very big here. We won extremely big in Iowa. She came in third place. She seems to be the only remainder, but I don't think she is going to do very well either. I don't see a path for her either.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER (on camera): Hours away from polls opening.\par \par \uc2 Up next, inside perspective on the New Hampshire primary with a pair of local journalists covering this story.\par \par \uc2 But first, a big prediction from former President Trump in front of a fired-up crowd.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Former president \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  here in New Hampshire at Southern New Hampshire University Arena, a packed crowd, very enthusiastic about the former president here. He says this is the beginning of the end of this race, and that a big win in New Hampshire means that he is going to win in Nevada and South Carolina and end everything quickly. We'll see if that's the case.\par \par \uc2 SPECIAL REPORT continues after this.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The border, I'd say, you know, inflation, the economy, everything.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Energy. Absolutely, we do need to continue with the growing.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The border.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The border? OK, what about you?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Definitely the border.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Our tax dollars can't keep going to these illegal immigrants when they're coming in illegally. They are called an illegal immigrant for a reason.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: This is matching what we saw in Iowa. If you take a look at one of the recent polls about top issues here in New Hampshire, you guessed it -- border, 29 percent. Protecting democracy, interestingly here, at 22 percent. And you look at the rest.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, we are going to discuss the presidential campaign with journalists covering New Hampshire. Joining me tonight "New Hampshire Union Leader" statehouse bureau chief Kevin Landrigan and New Hampshire radio host Chris Ryan. Gentlemen, thanks for coming in.\par \par \uc2 Kevin, let me start with you. Obviously, \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  is looking to close. She has to have a really, really good night with independents, undeclared here, to close the gap that we're seeing going in, at least right now.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN LANDRIGAN, "NEW HAMPSHIRE UNION LEADER": She really does. And what is unheard of about this election, we've never seen a frontrunner this popular with the base and this unpopular with independent swing voters. The problem is independents make the lion's share of the electorate but not the lion's share of the people who actually vote in each party primary. Trump has that conservative base. They are going to show up tomorrow. And she has to get this seismic turnout the likes of which we have never seen, and run the tables, say, two-and-a-half to one against Trump among those independents for her to get a clear surprise.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Right. Chris, you have been talking to all these candidates. If I talked to you six months ago, and I think we did, and I said to you it's going to be two left, right, before the primary, what would have you said, oh, yes?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS RYAN, TALK RADIO HOST: We talked about this. I thought that it would be Haley and it would be Trump. But if you did it a year ago it would have been \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  and Trump. The race changed when DeSantis was unable to perform and was unable to really be the challenger to Trump in this contest.\par \par \uc2 Haley has done really good work here in the granite state, but Kevin is spot on. She is going to have to pull a \uc2 John McCain\uc2  type of number with independents if she is going to be competitive with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  tomorrow night, and we haven't seen that. I'm talking about voters that don't normally vote, people that are looking at the times and saying we need a change. We need a candidate who is going to come and who is going to be different than Biden and Trump. Has she done enough in order to compel those individuals to not take the kids to practice and have somebody else to do that and to come to the polls to vote tomorrow night, and I don't think she has.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Meantime, on the other side, Democrats still a little smarting from South Carolina officially going first. And now you have a write-in campaign. Kevin, is that going to happen for the president over \uc2 Dean Phillips\uc2 , for example?\par \par \uc2 LANDRIGAN: Right, and \uc2 Marianne Williamson\uc2 , the other candidate, Bret. It's a tall order. I say that simply because, one, write-in is not an obvious thing that voters ordinarily do. And to do it for an incumbent president who doesn't want to be on the ballot in New Hampshire. So you are asking voters --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Who also came in fifth last time he was here.\par \par \uc2 LANDRIGAN: He's never done better than fifth in three times running in a Democratic primary in New Hampshire. So among the base he has always had this problem, which is some suspect that's why he dissed us and went with South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 But Democrats here in New Hampshire have faced obstacles for the last 20 years and they've still managed to win general elections in New Hampshire. They're organized. This write-in campaign, no TV ads, no radio ads, no polls, no consultants. But they think they are going to score a big victory for this president.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Meanwhile, they have a little trouble. A fake Biden call uses A.I. to urge Dems to stay home on Tuesday, the attorney general is warning. That's from you, Kevin.\par \par \uc2 LANDRIGAN: That's right. What was so bizarre about it, if you didn't want to get those calls anymore, call this number. And the number was the personal cell phone number of the former Democratic Party Chairman Kathy Sullivan who is running the write-in campaign. So she has filed the complaint with the attorney general. She is looking to get the federal law enforcement involved. It was reminiscent of 2002, Chris will remember, when phone jamming occurred of Democratic Party headquarters in that election, and Jeanne Shaheen lost a very close \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  race. A couple of Republican consultants ended up going to jail over it all. So it's a pretty wild pre-election thing to have happen again.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Last thing, Chris, what's one thing we need to look at tomorrow, heading into tomorrow?\par \par \uc2 RYAN: I think I'm watching the independent voters and looking to see what happens there, because I do feel that a lot of the polls we have seen have under-sampled independents. The question then, again, though is when you are counting on invisible voters, the people that don't normally vote, that's not really strategy. That's hope. And hope is not a strategy.\par \par \uc2 So, I think that -- the question is, has Haley touched enough of those voters down the stretch of the campaign, people that do not want to see \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  as president, don't want to see \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  as president, and realize that right here in the granite state tomorrow we have the last ditch hope to change that trajectory before we get to the general election.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Fascinating to watch. Great to talk to both of you. Kevin and Chris, I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the all-star panel on the two-person race for the Republican nomination, all of the talk about it. Biden off the ballot. We'll go into all of it.\par \par \uc2 Plus, you've got food, politics, and history coming together at a New Hampshire hotspot here.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Welcome to the Red Arrow Diner in Manchester. This is the flagship of four Red Arrow Diners. It's a political institution here. It's essentially center hub for politicians all the way back to 1992 when \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  became the comeback kid. He did it from one of these places here. A lot of politicians come in here. In fact, there is a burger called the Trump Tower. I'm going to get this accurate here. It is a seven-ounce patty well done, of course, the base for a mac and cheese ball, all slathered cheese sauce and top to bottom with a pair of grilled cheese sandwiches. So if you can get through this, you can probably get through the New Hampshire primary. We'l see if the former president can do that come Tuesday. Big vote here. Red Arrow Diner, a big part of political life in Manchester.\par \par \uc2 SPECIAL REPORT continues after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HALEY: DeSantis voters, they love America, and they wanted a new generational leader. And they wanted a conservative that was going to go in there and clean up government, and that's what we're going to do.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : They endorsed me in dropping out. I think we were headed to a very big victory on Tuesday. Maybe now it gets to be bigger, because most of those people are going to go with me.\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS, (R-FL): He has my endorsement because we can't go back to the old Republican guard of yesteryear, a repackaged form of warmed-over corporatism that \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  represents.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, it changed everything, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  dropping out of this race over the weekend. Now we're just hours away from the start of the primary. We had breaking news at the top of the show with the Supreme Court ruling five to four for a temporary restraining order on Texas along the border. Just moments ago, the Texas attorney general, this is about the wire, the barbed wire that they put up, Texas put up. "The Supreme Court's temporary order allows Biden to continue his illegal effort to aid foreign invasion of America," this is from the A.G. \uc2 Ken Paxton\uc2 , "The destruction of Texas's border barriers will not help enforce the law or keep Americans citizens safe. This fight is not over. I look forward to defending our state's sovereignty." That from the Texas attorney general.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in our panel, FOX News chief political analyst Brit Hume, Guy Benson, political editor at Townhall.com, host of "The Guy Benson Show" on FOX News Radio, and FOX News senior political analyst Juan Williams.\par \par \uc2 Brit, we showed earlier how border issues topped Iowa. Now they top here in New Hampshire. You have the Supreme Court stepping in here. It's a hot issue.\par \par \uc2 BRIT HUME, FOX NEWS SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST: Yes, and you keep it top of mind for voters turning out tomorrow, how much difference it will make. I don't know. We're kind of left to guess because there was no written opinion, nobody said anything. They just issued the order. But you have to think that this is about the supremacy of federal law over state and the supremacy of the federal government's handling of immigration policy. Five to four, though, shows you that there's another way to look at it. And of course, this is just pending a fuller hearing. So I suppose it could change.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Right. And in short, this is Texas Governor Abbott authorizing the razor wire along the border to step up the deterrent that they have seen of the onslaught of numbers coming across that border.\par \par \uc2 GUY BENSON, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Yes, it seems that the court has decided that the Biden administration has the sovereignty to destroy America's sovereignty if they choose to do so with their governance, which I would argue they are doing. It's notable in this fight that the Border Patrol union, which is in the federal side of this, has sided with the state of Texas because, believe it or not, border agents actually want to protect the border unlike some of their higher-ups, apparently, their bosses.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It's such a big issue, Juan, that it's big here in the northern states, it's big with Democrats and independents. You hear \uc2 Dean Phillips\uc2  on the trail saying that this is huge issue for Democrats, too. It is a vulnerability for the Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 JUAN WILLIAMS, FOX NEWS POLITICAL ANALYST: No question it's a vulnerability. I think it's a bigger issue among Republicans. I think it's become a big issue for New York City's mayor, Denver's mayor, Chicago's mayor, because they find their resources strained in terms accommodating these newcomers. But what you are seeing from \uc2 Ken Paxton\uc2  tonight, I think, I'm not sure that helps him or helps the cause, because it simply says that Republicans were made in antagonistic sort of fighting mode over this issue. They are not invested in trying to resolve the issue.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, let's turn to the primary now. The earth-shaking news, campaign news about \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  getting out, Brit, now you have three interesting endorsements of former President Trump, one of them happening today. South Carolina Congresswoman Nancy Mace endorsed the former president. You have Tim Scott, the senator from South Carolina, and then had you \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  at the beginning. All three of them were helped by \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2 , the South Carolina governor. In fact, Nancy Mace, she was endorsed by Haley while the opponent to Nancy Mace was endorsed by Former President Trump. So what about the implosion of DeSantis?\par \par \uc2 HUME: Well, I would say a couple things about it. One is there is a lot of talk now about he did this wrong and that wrong in the campaign and didn't get off to a good start and the media strategy. I think all of that had very little to do with the outcome. I think The outcome came down to one thing, that that is that there is a reservoir of voters in the Republican Party who were going to vote for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  if they had the chance. It was obscured by polling for about a year ago that suggested that he could be beaten and that \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  could beat him. But once he got indicted, a sketchy indictment in New York, the sentiment for him woke up. And I think -- I don't think that either he or really DeSantis or any other candidate ever really had a chance against that. These Republicans want this man and they're voting for him.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So Former President Trump should send a gift basket to \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 HUME: He should. He should. He could.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: What are your thoughts heading into tomorrow, Guy?\par \par \uc2 BENSON: I'm expecting a huge Trump win. I think that \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  has fought hard. She's certainly spent a lot more time on the ground here than President Trump has, and she is really going for those independents as the experts from New Hampshire were talking about in the previous segment. But all the wind is behind the back of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  at this point electorally. DeSantis not just dropping out but explicitly endorsing Trump and taking a twist of the knife to Haley at the very end. Look, she could shock everyone tomorrow night, but it's a tall order given all the dynamics that we are aware of, at least.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Juan, on the Republican side, where does she have to finish to continue the fight? Obviously, you've got Nevada where she is not in the caucus there for any delegates, and then her home state of South Carolina where she is trailing.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Trailing badly, and as you have seen, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has rolled out endorsements, not only the senators, Nancy Mace, the governor, everybody. She has got the party establishment behind \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . So the short answer to your question is I think if she can come within five percentage points, wow, then maybe things are shaken up. You get a new dynamic. But I don't see it at the moment. I just don't see it.\par \par \uc2 But this is -- New Hampshire will be the place where it decides whether or not this contest continues. New Hampshire is a place where surprises have come. \uc2 Gary Hart\uc2 , remember Pat Buchanan? That was a shocker. So it's possible, but I just don't see it at this moment. Trump had a solid win in Iowa. She is, by the way, making interesting arguments. It's just not resonating. It's as Brit was saying, Republican voters simply seem locked into the populist Trump sort of moment, this momentum.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Brit, finally, listening to those local reporters, the Democratic side could be an interesting --\par \par \uc2 HUME: Who would have thought it could be something more interesting happening on the Democratic side than on the Republican. It's a very strange thing. It seems to me that this write-in strategy could end up embarrassing Biden if it doesn't work, right, because, after all, he wasn't going to come here. He tried to write it out of the calendar. And now even though it's not official, it's on the calendar and we will all be watching it tomorrow night. So --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It will be fascinating. Polls open pretty soon, just hours away, 12 hours, I guess. Thanks, guys. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow, on SPECIAL REPORT, complete coverage from here in New Hampshire. We will talk to voters around the state about the issues they care about.\par \par \uc2 And then please join Martha MacCallum and me tomorrow night at 10:00 p.m. eastern time as we bring you special coverage of live primary results with a cast of thousands, including these guys.\par \par \uc2 Remember, if you can't catch us live, set your DVR, 6:00 p.m. in the east, 3:00 p.m. on the west coast.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for this SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and still unafraid. I wanted to leave some time for a nice bump out shot. THE INGRAHAM ANGLE is next.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020240123ek1m00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Interview with Senator JD Vance (R-OH); Trump Widens NH Primary Lead in New CNN/\uc2 UNH\uc2  Poll; DeSantis Trails Trump by Wide Margin; Haley Aims to Finish Strong in NH; Biden Campaign Plans Abortion Rights Effort; Speaker Pushed Through Short-Term Funding Deal Despite Opposition from Hard-Liners; GOP Lawmakers at Odds Over Ukraine and Border Funding; Biden and Netanyahu Offer Clashing Visions on End to Israel-\uc2 Hamas\uc2  War; Biden Supporters Push Write-In Campaign in New Hampshire\b0\par\par\uc2 Shannon Bream, Alexis McAdams, Rich Edson, Bret Baier, Martha  MacCallum, Brit Hume, Harold Ford Jr. \par \uc2 10,087 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 January 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Fox News Sunday\par \uc2 FOXN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: "FOX News Sunday" is live in New Hampshire just two days before the first in the nation primary.\par \par \uc2 I'm Shannon Bream.\par \par \uc2 The GOP candidates are making their last pitches to voters.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT AND PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: She's not going to make it. She has no chance.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY (R), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: If he thinks I have no chance and I have no hope, then why is he running millions of dollars of ads against me?\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice-over): After a record-setting showing in the Iowa caucuses, the Trump campaign targets Nikki Haley and rolls out a big endorsement from her home state.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT (R-SC): We need \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 !\par \par \uc2 BREAM: While \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  shifts his focus away from his poor polling in New Hampshire.\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: There's a lot of real estate between now and the South Carolina primary in terms of political time.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Ahead of the vote, we'll bring in key surrogates for all three campaigns -- South Carolina Congressman Ralph Norman for Nikki Haley, Kentucky Congressman \uc2 Thomas Massie\uc2  for \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , and Ohio Senator JD Vance for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: There is no better place to see retail politics to look in their eyes and understand where they come from, why they're here.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Then, the House and Senate find a financial band-aid in a short- term spending bill to keep the government up and running for now. But the tug-of-war to secure a deal on the southern border and aid for Ukraine and Israel hangs in limbo.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2  (R-SC): There will be no money for Ukraine, no money for Israel unless you stop the abuse of parole.\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE JOHNSON (R-LA), SPEAKER OF THE HOUSE: We understand the necessity about Ukraine funding and we want to say that the status quo is unacceptable.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Our Sunday panel weighs in as the speaker faces growing pressure from the left and the right.\par \par \uc2 Plus, Biden not on the ballot in the Granite State. We'll go behind the effort to get him votes anyway.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Even though \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is not on the ballot in New Hampshire, that we're going to write him in.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All, right now, on "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM (on camera): And hello from Bedford for a special New Hampshire primary edition of "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 The Granite State has been where the Haley campaign has hoped to make their mark in this race with some recent polling showing her closing the gap with the front runner, former President Trump. But brand new numbers out this morning show he is widening his lead here in New Hampshire over Nikki Haley to 19 points with Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  trailing the pack at a distant third.\par \par \uc2 While Republican hopefuls vie for 22 delegates two days from now, zero delegates are up for grabs on the Democratic side, after a heated dispute between the DNC and New Hampshire over which state gets to cast primary votes first.\par \par \uc2 In a moment, we will be joined by a surrogate for the Trump campaign, Ohio Republican Senator JD Vance.\par \par \uc2 But, first, we have live team coverage on the ground. FOX News senior national correspondent Rich Edson in Myrtle Beach, South Carolina, where DeSantis has sent much of his staff already, and FOX correspondent Alexis McAdams in Manchester, New Hampshire.\par \par \uc2 We start with Alexis.\par \par \uc2 Good morning.\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS MCADAMS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Shannon. Good morning from Manchester, New Hampshire, where we can tell you, everywhere you look, you can tell it is about to be primary time here in the Granite State. You see those campaign attack ads on a local TV stations and fliers all over town. And voters are noticing as these candidates make their final push.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS (voice-over): The final countdown is on in the Granite State.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS)\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: And as those rallies rage on, former President Trump continues to dominate the race. But former South Carolina Governor Nikki Haley has been more competitive here, leaving Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  trailing behind.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: New Hampshire will never let us down.\par \par \uc2 HALEY: We've got to start getting this right, New Hampshire can be the solution.\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: We'll be back in different parts of New Hampshire and look forward to being able to do first in the nation.\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: New Hampshire has been first in nation primary state for more than a century and every four years, it sits in political spotlight, a role that New Hampshire Governor \uc2 Chris Sununu\uc2 , who has endorsed Nikki Haley, says voters take seriously.\par \par \uc2 As your dad has said, Iowa picks corn and New Hampshire picks presidents. Is that still true?\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 CHRIS SUNUNU\uc2  (R), NEW HAMPSHIRE: That's what he said. I think our main role is to narrow the field.\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: But Trump is watching those polls closely. The former president working to maintain momentum, adding another endorsement, this one from South Carolina Senator Tim Scott and still slamming his competition.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Nikki Haley will never secure the border or stop the fentanyl that is killing thousands and thousands of New Hampshire citizens.\par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS HOST: What do you think is going on?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: I mean, it's what he does. This is what he does when he feels insecure. This is what he does when he feels threatened. I worked with him, I know exactly how he is.\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: The ongoing fight for those undeclared voters has been a major focus. Registered Democrats cannot vote in the Republican primary and vice versa, but undeclared voters can. At nearly 350,000, they make up the largest voting bloc in the state.\par \par \uc2 But no matter what their party, they care about the same issues.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Immigration, illegals coming across the border.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I think the economy right now with the interest rates that went up.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Gun rights, immigration and foreign policy.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS (on camera): And officials say Shannon they expect record voter turnout in New Hampshire on Tuesday -- Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Alexis McAdams live here in New Hampshire, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 So, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is currently more than 800 miles away in South Carolina trying to boost primary chances in Nikki Haley's home state.\par \par \uc2 FOX correspondent Rich Edson joins us live from Myrtle Beach, South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 Hello, Rich.\par \par \uc2 RICH EDSON, FOX NEWS SENIOR NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Hello, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 Well, the primary here is nearly five weeks away. Governor DeSantis says he is returning today to New Hampshire and with that travel, the campaign says he's cancelled a handful of television interviews. They say he'll reschedule them. The Trump campaign maintains those cancelled appearances show that the end is near for the DeSantis campaign.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: When I got off the plane, I didn't know whether I was in South Carolina or New Hampshire because it was 30 degrees.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 's campaign says they are wasting no time taking the fight to former Governor Nikki Haley's home state. DeSantis opened his swing through South Carolina fielding questions from voters in Myrtle Beach, promising to declare emergency at the border and slash the size of the federal government.\par \par \uc2 A campaign aide says the pressure is on Haley to win her home state and if not to get out of the race.\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: I think pretty clearly there's just too many conservative voters here for someone like Nikki Haley to win the state. I think that that's -- that that's obvious.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Haley has maintained this is already a two-candidate race, between her and the front-runner, former President Trump. To this trip, a Haley spokesperson says that South Carolina is a great state and they hope DeSantis's team enjoys their vacation time here.\par \par \uc2 HALEY: He's been invisible in New Hampshire. He's been invisible in South Carolina. He's closer to zero than he is to me in either one of those states. But that's his choice if that's what he wants to do.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: The DeSantis campaign denied it's skipping New Hampshire, pointing the governor was there Friday, and says he'll return ahead of this week's primary after investing millions and months in Iowa and finishing a distant second and just ahead of Haley, and aide tells FOX the DeSantis campaign is moving the majority of its staff here to South Carolina, a state where Trump has polled well, as he's done across the country.\par \par \uc2 In endorsing Trump, Senator Tim Scott joins fellow senator from South Carolina, \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 , and the state's governor, \uc2 Henry McMaster\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 EDSON (on camera): DeSantis has no events listed today for South Carolina. He has a campaign event tonight in Manchester, New Hampshire -- Shannon.\par \par \uc2 SHANNON: All right. Rich Edson reporting in South Carolina, thank you, Rich.\par \par \uc2 So, here in New Hampshire, I've been talking to voters as they get ready to head to the polls on Tuesday. These are savvy consumers. They've heard from the contenders. Many of them multiple times. You're going to hear from these voters throughout the show today.\par \par \uc2 Here's one of our first chats.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, Lois, what I find interesting is that you went to the Trump event, you waited outside in the freezing cold, in the snow. You're here at DeSantis event but you're voting for someone else?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Yes, I'm voting for Nikki.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: What was the difference with her that she won you over?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: No baggage.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Joining us now is the surrogate for the Trump campaign, Ohio Senator JD Vance.\par \par \uc2 Senator, welcome back to "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 You heard what Lois (ph) told me there. She said there's no baggage with Nikki Haley. That's something I've heard again and again from people here who are opting not to choose President Trump as their primary choice in New Hampshire.\par \par \uc2 What's your argument to them about what Nikki Haley continues to say from the campaign trail is a choice for chaos?\par \par \uc2 SEN. JD VANCE (R-OH): Well, first of all, Shannon, I think Trump is way out ahead in New Hampshire, but also nationally. That's very important to remember.\par \par \uc2 But I guess my response to that voter would be, Nikki does have baggage. She just hasn't faced the onslaught of years of media attacks, of super PAC attacks that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has faced. And, frankly, the fact that Trump is still standing and is still leading in the polls against \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  suggests he has real political resilience.\par \par \uc2 I know a lot of people who are choosing Nikki Haley think she's the low baggage candidate. In reality, she's the candidate the Democrats haven't teed off on, but they will tee off on her if she's the nominee. She won't be, but if she is, she will face exactly what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has faced for the past six years.\par \par \uc2 And I don't think she'll survive, and I certainly don't think she'll win the presidency through it. That's why we need to elect the guy who's shown some resilience. That's why we need to nominate \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, we'll see what voters decide here. So, let's break this down a little bit more because others I talked to follow this argument about how Nikki Haley does head-to-head against President Biden.\par \par \uc2 Here she is making that point in one of her routine points now in campaign stump speeches.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY (R), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: It's going to be a nail-biter of an election. We're going to go end up holding our breath again. I'm in every one of those same general election polls and I beat Biden by 17 points.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: It's something that's resonating with people here. Here are a couple voters I talked to that made that point, as well.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Tom and Linda (ph), OK, have you made a decision? Are you here to get more information? Where are you guys at?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I think we are here to get more information, but I'm sort of leaning towards Nikki.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, why?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I think she's got the best chance to beat \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay. You've seen the polls, not all of them are the wide range that Nikki Haley cited there, 17 points. Most -- most of them are closer, but she is in all the head-to-head polls involving the GOP, the one who seems to do best against the president.\par \par \uc2 So, what do you tell those voters who say that's why they're choosing her?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Well, first of all, Shannon, I just saw a Rasmussen poll just yesterday that suggested that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  did better against \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  than Nikki Haley does. I don't think that's true of anything close to the majority of the polls.\par \par \uc2 But again, I go back to something I said a little bit earlier, Nikki Haley has not faced the Democrat and media machine for six years. And we have to ask ourselves, not what polls will say today, but what do polls say when Democrats run ads saying that Nikki Haley wants to slash your Social Security so that she can send more money to foreign countries?\par \par \uc2 That is exactly what they're going to do because that is, in fact, what Nikki Haley says she wants to do out on the campaign trail.\par \par \uc2 We have to remember negative ads --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, the --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 VANCE: -- and the messaging behind them, Shannon, if I can just finish --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: -- they really do make an impact. And we have to ask ourselves who's the most resilient candidate in the face of the Democratic machine? I don't know how anybody can look at the last six years and say that's anybody but \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, to be fair, I don't think on the campaign trail, she's telling people that she's going to cut Social Security to send money to other countries, but -- and we pressed her on this, she does talk about potentially raising retirement age for people now in their 20s entering the system. So, definitely talking about changes to entitlements and her foreign policy breaks with the foreign president, as well. We'll talk a little bit more about that.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, Governor DeSantis is out there saying there's a reason that people want President Trump to win and he thinks it's to benefit the other side. Here's his take.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: They are invested in a Trump nomination because they want the election to be about the legal issues, the criminal cases, all these things. The liberal media likes it because they know that will help the Democrats.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, polls do show that the president would lose some support from folks who say now that he would be their choice if he is convicted, innocent until proven guilty. We've got a long way to go on that road.\par \par \uc2 But with that in mind, do you tell GOP voters this is a gamble that you're going to have to take, how do you convince them it's worth it?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Well, Shannon, I just -- I'm sorry, I just don't think that Ron's point passes the smell test there. They're trying to do everything that they can to remove him from the ballot. Even moderate Democrats are coming out and saying some of the tricks that they're using against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  don't even pass constitutional muster.\par \par \uc2 So, the idea that the Democrats want him to be the nominee when they're throwing everything against the wall to throw him off the ballot, it's insane, nobody actually believes that. I think broadly speaking, Republican voters recognize not only was \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  a good president in the past, he'll be a good president in the future and he has survived the machine.\par \par \uc2 I've tasted a little bit of this. Most Republican politicians taste some small piece of this. Trump has faced an onslaught of character assassination, hundreds of millions of dollars, maybe billions of dollars of attack ads. Surviving in the face of that is maybe the most important signal of whether you are a winning candidate and I think Trump has survived it better than anybody I've seen in politics.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. We've got a couple of fresh polls out this morning. We've got the Suffolk poll that puts President Trump at 55 percent, Nikki Haley at 36, DeSantis at 6.\par \par \uc2 But we've just got this CNN poll that has it much closer. Trump at 50 percent, Haley at 39 percent, DeSantis again at 6 percent.\par \par \uc2 You know here the largest group in New Hampshire is undeclared. They can vote in the GOP primary. There are a couple of newspaper editorials today endorsing Nikki Haley, telling people if you're independent, get out, quote, save the country and vote in that GOP primary.\par \par \uc2 I mean, that's a substantial group of people here. How concerned are you about them showing up and not choosing President Trump on Tuesday?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Well, Shannon, we have to draw a distinction between independent minded and New Hampshire voters. I think a lot of them are going to vote for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , but also liberal -- many of them from Massachusetts who are moving to New Hampshire, they're never going to vote for a Republican nominee, even if it's Nikki Haley. They're just trying to play spoiler to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But I think Republicans really have to ask ourselves, do we want liberal suburbanites from Massachusetts to choose our nominee or do we want to choose our nominee as Republicans and independent-minded conservatives?\par \par \uc2 I think if we choose our nominee, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is going to win and he's going to win handily.\par \par \uc2 By the way, Shannon, this campaign strategy of getting liberals to vote for you in a Republican primary, it doesn't work nationally. It won't work in South Carolina. It won't work in Ohio. It won't work in most other states.\par \par \uc2 We actually need to select our nominee with voters who we can actually get, not with voters who are trying to play spoiler because they hate \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and they hate the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and Nikki Haley has said she does welcome the support of Democrats and independents, that we should have a big tent party, she argues, as Republicans. Does the President not agree?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: I think the President certainly agrees. In fact, I come from a party of sort of ancestral Democrats who migrated to the Republican Party in large part because of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's message. But it's one thing to get Democrats who are open-minded to vote for you. And it's another thing to get Democrats who are never going to vote for Republicans to vote for you. I think we've got to be a big tent party, but with people who have an open mind, not with Democrats who just hate \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and want to play spoiler.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, Ohio Senator JD Vance. Thank you for your time today. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Thanks Shannon, good to see you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Up next, we've got surrogates from both the Haley and DeSantis campaigns as one contender decides to skip the Sunday shows and another celebrates a birthday on the campaign trail.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I respect Nikki Haley, I respect DeSantis, but they don't have what it takes at this time in history to be able to move this country back in its rightful place of opportunities, security and promise.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: That was one of the Trump supporters I spoke to here in New Hampshire. He told me he does like the other candidates, but he doesn't think they're equipped to handle our current challenges in the same way the former president is.\par \par \uc2 We are joined now by surrogates for those two Republican campaigns, South Carolina Republican Congressman Ralph Norman representing the Haley campaign. And Kentucky Republican Congressman \uc2 Thomas Massie\uc2  representing the DeSantis campaign.\par \par \uc2 We begin with Congressman Norman. Welcome. Good to see you this morning.\par \par \uc2 REP. RALPH NORMAN (R-SC): I'm glad to be with you, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, let's start with the two polls fresh out this morning. The Suffolk poll has got President Trump at 55%, Nikki Haley back by 19 points. We've got the CNN poll which has this closer, but still Nikki Haley backed by double digits in eleven percentage points. How does she change the conversation with 48 hours left to go here in New Hampshire?\par \par \uc2 NORMAN: Well, you know, the polls are one thing, Shannon, but what Nikki has done when she announced this February is basically showcase her vision for the country. She showcased her talent. And she's the only candidate that's gone up in the polls. She has not gone down. And she's attracting the crowds because people like what they hear. And Shannon, she has got the 8 factors. She attracts voters from all different sides because she's -- they like her and they like what she's saying, and her optimism.\par \par \uc2 And so, you know, what she will do in the remaining days in New Hampshire and what she'll do in South Carolina is continue what she's been doing, which is telling American people what her vision is and she'll deliver on it.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Congressman, you heard what Senator Vance had to say, that she's trying to attract liberal voters and people who don't really care about GOP or conservative ideals. And it's not going to be a winning strategy in the general election were she to make it there. What do you make of his comments?\par \par \uc2 NORMAN: Well, I mean, Senator Vance knows you don't -- when you go in front of an audience, you don't ask, are you Democrat, are you Republican? No. You make the case for why they should vote for you. And that's what Nikki does and he knows that.\par \par \uc2 But Nikki gives people a reason to vote for her and that's what's so attractive about her and that's why she's -- that's why she's gaining. And, you know, people like what they see. And that's, again, the polls show that. And she'll do very well in New Hampshire. She'll do very well in South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So you were once a supporter of President Trump, so much so that you voted against certifying some of the electoral college votes on January 6. At what point and for what reason did you pivot away to now supporting one of his biggest rivals?\par \par \uc2 NORMAN: A couple of things. And I call President Trump. His policies were great. What he did was great for the country, particularly with what Biden is doing now. But it's time for a change, Shannon. I think the youth, Nikki just turned 52. She's 25 years younger than \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . I'm sorry, when you have a president like the pressures they're going to be facing, when you see what they're going to have to do to attract new talent into Congress and the Senate, you've got to go to work doing that. Nikki will do that.\par \par \uc2 And the other big thing that people need to know, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  can only give you four years. That's like a football team can only play half of the game. It's going to take eight years to get this country straight. But Nikki's youth and the fact that she can give eight years, and I would also make the argument it's really three years because the last year of a term is political like you're seeing now. And President Trump is going to be tied up in legal fights and we need somebody that can come in and work. And the criticism that he has of her, the only reason he's doing that is because she's gaining in the polls and he knows she is a serious threat and it's a two-person race now.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Let's talk about one of those critiques because he says if you elect her president, you're going to have eight years potentially of war. Here's what he said the other night about her foreign policy.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , (R) FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT AND PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Nikki Haley is backed by the deep state and the military industrial complex. She's never seen a war she doesn't like.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: He called her a globalist. What's your response?\par \par \uc2 NORMAN: Then why did he pick her for ambassador to the United Nations? That's one of the greatest picks he made. If she was that, then why did she -- he praise her for her toughness, for the way she dealt in Israel, with the capital? No, that's just political talk and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  knows that. And the same thing, you know, he got her confused yesterday with \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  on the January 6 with the National Guard. And I would say 25 years ago he wouldn't have made that mistake but he kept saying, Nikki Haley did this, you know, with the National Guard. He was talking about \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 . And that's another example of -- again, he wouldn't have made that mistake if he was younger. But, I just --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Congressman, let me ask you before you go, why are you one of the only elected officials in South Carolina who's backing her and why isn't she doing better in the polls there?\par \par \uc2 NORMAN: Well, I went with Nikki because I work with her. She and I came in the legislature in 2005. At the same time, I see what she can do, Shannon. She's a tireless worker and I know what she'll do for the country. And the fact that the polls -- she's the only one that's gone up and I think she'll continue to go up. That's why they're criticizing her like they are. And so, I don't make any -- any -- I don't have any problem with that. I like where we are. I like the fact that what she's doing. And I'll be with her in South Carolina as I was in Iowa and wherever she needs us.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, Congressman Ralph Norman, thank you for your time this morning.\par \par \uc2 NORMAN: My pleasure.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Joining us now, Congressman \uc2 Thomas Massie\uc2 . He is surrogate for the DeSantis campaign. Good to see you again, Congressman. Both the polls out this morning are pretty dire for your candidate. Both the Suffolk poll and CNN poll have Governor DeSantis at just 6%. There's a lot of talk this morning about why he canceled appearances on Sunday shows. Is it because he doesn't want to talk about these numbers?\par \par \uc2 REP. THOMAS MASSIE (R-KY): Well, you know, we never had high expectations for New Hampshire anyway. And Nikki's exploiting, you know, a quirk of their primary, which is that undeclared, i.e. Democrats, can vote in a Republican primary.\par \par \uc2 You know, we're -- I'll be going to New Hampshire later today, but the real fight I think is in South Carolina, and that's where Nikki Haley, I think, stumbles. She's going to miss expectations and not even win her own state.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So let's talk about Governor DeSantis and the fact that he has tried to make his pitch in Iowa, New Hampshire, South Carolina, everywhere he's been, in that he is a governor, he says, who gets things done. You asked me to do something. I have a success rate. Here's my legislative record. And he's contrasted that against both Governor Haley and also President Trump, saying there were a number of things that he promised that he didn't get done. Why do you think that isn't resonating better with voters? Is it a choice of policy versus personality? Why isn't that landing with voters?\par \par \uc2 MASSIE: Well, let me talk about that difference and talk about why it might not be resonating. First of all, you know, the main thing I hear from people back home is they're mad that nobody's held accountable in Washington, D.C. Well, I think we need to hold Trump accountable. He didn't build the wall. He didn't get Mexico to pay for it. He ran up $7.8 trillion of debt. He fumbled the repeal of Obamacare. There's so many mispromises.\par \par \uc2 He gave a commendation and award to Dr. Fauci after he shut down our country and hasn't held anybody at the FDA or the CDC responsible. So you know, let's hold that guy accountable. Contrast that with \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . He reduced the debt in Florida 25%. He fired the Soros DAs. He got all the wokeness out of their schools and their universities and he banned China from buying land there.\par \par \uc2 So he's got a great contrast. They don't want to talk about it. They won't debate him now. Neither Nikki Haley nor the president because his record is so stellar and they don't -- neither of them have much to run on. Why isn't it resonating?\par \par \uc2 I would say people have rallied to Trump's defense because he's been indicted four times. And, you know, I feel compelled to rally to his defense because that's an attack on all of us.\par \par \uc2 But here's the problem. You know, this summer we're going to be lit -- they're going to be litigating these cases in the court and these indictments are going to ripen into convictions. And we're going to be talking about the finer points of whether you can pay a porn star with your campaign money or whether you should write it off as a business expense.\par \par \uc2 I mean, the lawsuit itself is ridiculous, but the underlying facts aren't disputed. And I think people are going to kind of recoil from that when all that stuff starts coming out this summer. And the polls that show him beating Biden, those are going to switch very quickly.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, a lot of folks I talked to here who are Trump supporters saying he's their choice for Tuesday here in New Hampshire say all of those cases against him only made them double down in their support. We'll see if that continues as this do progress.\par \par \uc2 You are one of very few people in Congress who's actually campaigning for someone who is not President Trump. You have said, quote, "It's a political risk." How much do you think all of those congressional endorsements in the neighborhood of about 140 at this point are about a fear of President Trump versus love his policies, love him, thinks he's the best candidate? What kind of price do you think you might pay if he wins?\par \par \uc2 MASSIE: Well, I've got the Trump antibodies. He came at me for opposing the CARES Act. I was the only congressman who did. I said it was going to cause inflation, that it would cause shortages. And oh, by the way, it funded the mail-in ballots and spent $2 trillion.\par \par \uc2 Contrast that with \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . He called me up while everybody was hating on me, including that president, and said, I'm going to open our economy and make sure our kids go to school. Now, Trump came at me and I won my reelection, so I'm not worried about it, but I think there are, a lot of those endorsements aren't wholehearted endorsements.\par \par \uc2 They're either afraid that he will get involved in their primaries, or they're afraid that if he does win, he'll be vindictive. You know, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  can work with anybody. And so there's no vindication expected from him after he wins, so that's -- you know, that's just the way it is. It's sort of bullying tactics from the former president.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: If he finishes third here as he is expected to do in New Hampshire and also in South Carolina, what's the future path for him?\par \par \uc2 MASSIE: Yeah, that's a great question. He was second in Iowa and she was third. She'll be second and he'll be third in New Hampshire. She's not even on the ballot in Nevada and Ron is. Then we go to South Carolina where she's going to lose this.\par \par \uc2 Look, I think what we're seeing is that the only way you can beat Trump is if it's a head-to-head race between two people. And the question is, is that person going to be Nikki Haley or \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 Nikki Haley's ideology is somewhere in the zip code of \uc2 John McCain\uc2 , \uc2 Mitt Romney\uc2  and \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 , and she said that \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  was her inspiration. She has a ceiling in these primaries that are closed primaries where only Republicans can vote of about 35%. She can't beat \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  can. And as I said, as these indictments matriculate into convictions and we start learning about the details in this stuff, his poll numbers are going to go upside down against Biden. And we'll see that Ron is the only one that can win in November.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, Congressman Massie for the DeSantis team. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 MASSIE: Thanks, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Up next, we're going to bring in our Sunday group, including Bret Baier, who's got some scoop from President Trump.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Why this time when, you know, the court cases, all these things, or did that make you feel like I want to support him even more? Was there any part of you that questioned whether he would run again or whether you'd support him again?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It made me more passionate to support him as I felt that there was a lot of political motivation in trying to get him out of the Washington, D.C. political arena.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I like Trump, I think he's too brash. And Biden, he is too old. So there's nobody else coming up to the plate like Haley is.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: In Florida, we love our governor and we think that he'd be a great president. We just love his ideals. We love his conservatism. We love his faith. We love his family. Everything he has done for Florida. We think he's going to do for the nation.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And to be clear, Dennis is not voting here in New Hampshire.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: No.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But he happens to be here and wanted to come see his governor.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, so I've been here in New Hampshire speaking to voters ahead of the state's presidential primary on Tuesday two days to go. Poll show former President Trump is still the clear front runner, Nikki Haley in second, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  a distant third.\par \par \uc2 It's time to break it down with our Sunday group, former Tennessee Democratic Congressman \uc2 Harold Ford\uc2 , Jr. Fox News Chief Political Anchor and Anchor of special report on the Fox News Channel Bret Baier, Anchor and Executive Editor of the story on the Fox News Channel, Martha MacCallum, and Brit Hume, Fox News Chief Political Analyst. We got the A team in here. Big leagues. We're glad to see you guys this morning.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, to Bret, last night you were out and got to have a little chat with former President Trump. We want to play a little bit of what you talked about many issues, but this is about the Veepstakes.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, "SPECIAL REPORT" ANCHOR: You said in our town hall that you had an idea or you might have already decided about your VP pick. When do you think you're going to make that?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Well, it's never really had that much of an effect on an election, which is an amazing thing, both election and primary. It's never really had much of an effect. I may or may not release something over the next couple of months. There's no rush to that. It won't have any impact at all. The person that I think I like is a very good person, pretty standard. I think people won't be that surprised. But I would say there's probably a 25% chance it would be that person.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Is Senator Tim Scott on the list now?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: He's a great guy. He endorsed me. There's an example. Nikki comes from South Carolina. Tim Scott is from South Carolina. But if you look, the governor, great governor, another senator, Lindsey, we happened to like Lindsey, but \uc2 Henry McMaster\uc2  knows her very well. He endorsed me. It's very hard for a governor to endorse somebody when you have, you know, I mean, \uc2 Henry McMaster\uc2  was the lieutenant governor under her. And he endorsed me. Almost everybody in South Carolina has endorsed me. What does that tell you?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So you ask about the vice president and then we get into the, you know, the critique of one of the current rivals.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yeah, we went through a lot of things actually in this interview and we'll be putting out a lot today. But in that VP thing he did say in our town hall that he had come to a conclusion. He knew who it was and maybe at some later point he'd tell us who it was, that he said there's a 25% chance, but that's a good person who he's thinking about.\par \par \uc2 I think his thought process overall, Shannon, was that he feels like he's in a good place. This rally here was really well attended, was really packed. He likes that thinks it's a poll in and of itself. And at the end of that rally, he brought out all of these South Carolina figures to say the next stop is going to be even bigger.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So you got that. And I would agree the events I've gone to here that have been Trump focused events, even when he's not there, he's got a surrogate there, they're a packed house. So that is a measure of how people are feeling here on the ground.\par \par \uc2 You guys talked about cognitive ability, you talked about the legal fights, all kinds of things. And Martha, one of the things I asked these Trump voters about these legal fights, you heard the lady there who was like, oh no, it's made me double down even more on supporting him. They see it as -- well, his team has always seen it as a plus, not a minus?\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, "THE STORY" ANCHOR: Well, I think that soundbite's extraordinary and I think it encapsulates a lot of what we hear from these Trump voters. They feel that they have a need to be there for him, right? They're saying, he's being attacked, so more than ever we need to be there for him.\par \par \uc2 And I would also just point out that in early 2023, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  was polling in first place in New Hampshire, OK? And what have you had in the interim? You've had all of these successive indictments and court dates and he went back for court dates he didn't even need to be at over the course of the last week. So he clearly knows, he's a very astute politician, that these are helping him. And you hear it in the soundbite from that woman.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yeah. So Nikki Haley here has, as she tries to woo these undeclared, we know it's the biggest pool of voters here in New Hampshire. She's tried to woo, "Politico" says this way, both sides. "She's got to deliver two messages, one to moderates who largely dislike Trump and another to conservatives who seem quite comfortable with him. It's an exercise in needle-threading under the best of circumstances." But she's gained on him here, but can she close this any further?\par \par \uc2 BRIT HUME, FOX NEWS CHIEF POLITICAL ANALYST: It looks like it does it. And, you know, the premise here is that the attacks against her are that she's attracting Democrats and independents who might not be normally Republican. Oh, heaven forbid that our nominees should be somebody who would attract voters from outside the party.\par \par \uc2 But that's the message that's coming against her now, and I think at the moment, you know, given what we're seeing in the polling and so forth, and given the reduced expectations announced by people who are supporting her here, that she's looking for second place, and she hopes it'll be closed, but it doesn't look that way.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, Harold, on the other side of the ticket, President Biden's not even on the ballot here. There's been a lot of fighting. We're going to explain that a little bit later in the show about what will happen with the DNC. But he's got folks here saying, it could be very embarrassing to come out of this Tuesday with a headline that he lost the first Democratic contest though it is unsanctioned.\par \par \uc2 HAROLD FORD JR., FORMER TENNESSEE CONGRESSMAN: We'll see. Thanks for having me on. Happy Sunday. I think President Biden, the real takeaway, I think, right now for the Democrats are two things. First, the border security is the number one issue on Republican voters' minds, and I would even contend independent voters' minds, even if you're not a border state. The border states have been pushed north in this conversation, too.\par \par \uc2 The chaos around President Trump is not going to go away, not because it's not going to go away from the legal issues, but you have his two chief opponents, DeSantis and Haley raising this over and over again, and that will be used much like some of the things that \uc2 Dean Phillips\uc2  may be saying against President Biden will be used against him.\par \par \uc2 The Democrats are banking on two things, that women's health choice and women's health rights continue to be in the forefront, and two, that the economy continues to improve and consumer sentiment improves. If we find ourselves six months from now with a different kind of economic state and a mood on the part of the American voter, President Biden will be in a better position. But Biden has one big challenge.\par \par \uc2 A majority of Democrats wish there might be another person running on top of the ticket. I wish I was six, seven. I'm not. So I don't know if that will come true, and if that will cause them not to vote for Biden.\par \par \uc2 But I think what Brit said is what Democrats on the flip side are hoping for. We're going to be able to attract Independent and Republican voters who may be concerned about the chaos in the women's health issues. But if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  or whomever it is, is able to attract on the border, this is a jump ball come November.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, you mentioned the issue of abortion, so we're around the time of year, we mark the anniversary of Roe v. Wade. The Supreme Court has gotten rid of it, but "AP" reports this. "President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's reelection team is preparing a nationwide series of events and an advertising campaign focused on the battle over abortion rights. Highlighting what Democrats believe will be a potent political weapon against likely Republican nominee \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ." Brit, they say it's worked for them in all these post-Dobbs elections.\par \par \uc2 HUME: Yeah, it certainly did work for them in 2022, I think. There are counter-arguments to be made. The Republicans have not really done it very well. I mean, because a lot of what Democrats support in terms of abortion is a position of abortion with such availability. It's so long through pregnancy that a majority of the country does not support.\par \par \uc2 So Republicans have a case to make. They just haven't made it. And unless and until they do -- it'll be a good issue for the Democrats. But it's not unrebuttable.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, yeah, the President and Vice President both having events on that over the next few days.\par \par \uc2 OK, panel, do not go far. We will see you again in just a minute. But up next, senators are closing in, we think, on a bipartisan deal on the border. But it faces major opposition from the House GOP, and now former President Trump is threatening to blow up a bipartisan compromise. We get the panel's take on what comes next when we come back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Can you turn that microphone off, please?\par \par \uc2 RONALD REAGAN\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: You asked me if you - I am paying for this microphone, Mr. (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: That was \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  leading up to the 1980 New Hampshire primary. George H.W. Bush had accepted a challenge to debate Reagan, which was moderated by "The Nashua Telegraph" but paid for by Reagan's campaign. In an attempt to take control of the event, the debate moderator asked for Reagan's mic to be cut, leading the future president to utter a line that went down in history.\par \par \uc2 We turn back now to the panel.\par \par \uc2 So, along with what's happening on the campaign trail, there are a lot of shenanigans in Washington as well. They're fighting over this Senate border deal. We don't know the contours. We think we have some idea of it coming together. But we're told it's dead on arrival in the House. And now potentially that the speakership of Mike Johnson is almost done. House GOP is the headline from "Politico" today. Already considering a future without Johnson.\par \par \uc2 So, Martha, can Washington get its act together?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, good question. Mike Johnson finds himself in very much the same position that Kevin McCarthy was in. He gets in there. He sees the reality of it. He knows how slim the margin is in terms of what he can get across the - across the finish line.\par \par \uc2 But, you know, the issue that I think has become so compelling in this whole election environment and in Washington is immigration. I mean when you have people in urban centers across the country, heavily blue areas who are very upset about what's going on with immigration, they want more than just a deal that appears to basically throw some more money at the problem and continue to support Ukraine. So, I think it's become very troublesome. I mean I look at the morning show host, Charlamagne tha God, who is saying that working class people are very upset over this migrant crisis. The tenor on this issue has changed dramatically and I think it's - it's put Johnson in a very tough position.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, and - and you noted this has moved north, because I hear about it from voters all over the place here in New Hampshire. They are very worried about the border. Even though they're much closer to the north than the south, they are worried about what's happening down south.\par \par \uc2 You've got Congressman Dan Crenshaw talking about how there's this accusation that some Republicans are not going to let this go because they think it's a good issue for them and they don't want to give a win to President Biden. He's quoted as saying, how many hundreds of thousands of illegals would you allow in the country just because it might help your chance of the election. He says he ran on getting the border secure, Bret, and that if you're not working toward that end you're a traitor.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, and it's a tough, tough issue. I think there are a lot of people that want to solve it, but there aren't the votes to solve it. With that slim majority in the House, they want HR-2, which is the immigration bill that they passed. They would like that, not some watered-down - what they call watered-down Senate compromise. We haven't even seen the language of the Senate compromise as of yet.\par \par \uc2 It is a powerful issue. I thought it was a powerful issue prior to 2022. Abortion and threat to democracy turned out to be a bigger issue than we thought it would be. I think, to Martha's point, this has bubbled up to the top, not just for border states, but all over the country. And Democrats, to Harold's point earlier, are feeling the back end of some of these discussions. The White House does not have a good answer on these things.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes. And, Harold, you've got Democrats begging and calling out this administration publicly. And that's a very difficult optic for this White House. At the same time polls show, from ours to everybody else out there polling this, that the president is a disaster on the border.\par \par \uc2 HAROLD FORD JR. (D), FORMER TENNESSEE CONGRESSMAN, EMPOWERMENT AND INCLUSION CAPITAL CEO AND FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: If there is a deal to be had, I served in Congress it seems like 100 years ago now, but it was only 20 years ago, we - we used to like to try to get the country to higher ground. And if you have an opportunity to pass new policies that are going to put more dollars at the border, reform the asylum process, put more law enforcement there, why wouldn't you do it? And for President Trump, it confounds me a bit, he's out talking about border - border security and immigration, but is encouraging Republicans to blow up the compromise.\par \par \uc2 Dan Crenshaw had it just right. I don't agree with Dan Crenshaw on a whole bunch of stuff, but he's exactly right, how many more illegals do you want to come in if - if it - if you believe you're going to advantage the other party by passing policy? You're telling me we won't arrest criminals because it's going to help Democratic mayors? You're telling me we won't put people in jail because you're going to help a Democrat run for Congress in the Senate. That's nonsense. If they get a good deal, they should pass it.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Brit, will they? You've been watching for decades as it's not gotten fixed in Washington.\par \par \uc2 HUME: This controversy with the speaker's job on the line supposedly is a wonderful example of why the Republican Party is sometimes referred to as the stupid party, because what we have here is a tiny little majority, you can barely do anything, you only control one house, the other party has the Senate and the White House, more importantly. There's very severe limits on what you can ever hope to do. Here's a chance to do something, right?\par \par \uc2 And these guys who want the whole enchilada, everything they - they're - all their wish list on the border, which maybe if they had a full majority and the White House they could do, but you can't do it this way. And if you decapitate your leadership yet again after all this, I mean that really is the definition of politically stupid.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, I can't imagine watching them go through more candidates, more rounds of voting, weeks and weeks of not having a speaker. So, we'll see if the threats materialize.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, so the president is struggling on the border. He's also struggling on foreign policy. And one of the most recent poll on this has him way upside down. Quinnipiac on how he's doing, approve, 35 percent, disapprove, 59 percent.\par \par \uc2 And, Bret, this comes at the same time that people are now asking questions about how he's handling Israel, is there now daylight, more of it, between him and the prime minister?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I think that that's a really big question. I think how the White House has handled Israel has actually split Democrats and a number of different constituencies.\par \par \uc2 And if you look around the world, at the hot spots that are happening, it is only increasing. It is getting more chaotic. So, as the White House campaigns against former President Trump about chaos, you look around the world and the Trump campaign says, remember the world under President Trump. That's their pitch. It wasn't as chaotic. I think there's a number of fronts where the White House is on the back end of some tough decisions here.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, even Democrats will have to acknowledge the Abraham Accord, something that was really critical in the Middle East, so we know that this president, though, hadn't talked to Netanyahu we think for about a month. AP saying this, "The leaders' relationship has increasingly shown signs of strain as Netanyahu has repeatedly rebuffed Biden's calls for Palestinian sovereignty, gumming up what the U.S. president believes is the key to unlocking a durable peace in the Middle East. The oft-sided elusive two state solution, which, Martha, this week the prime minister said, not going to happen.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Not going to happen. He said the security of Israel wont' allow for a two state solution at this point. We know there have been some frosty conversations. The ones that have happened between the White House and Netanyahu. One of them ending with this conversation is over apparently on - on the part of President Biden.\par \par \uc2 I think, you know, the president has not - and it's not the first time this has happened -- articulated where he stands on this issue in a way that is helping Americans and his voters, Democrat voters, independent voters, understand where he stands on this. The other thing is, that, you know, for - for some kind of solution to happen, you need to have the support of the Arab countries and leadership from the Arab countries to help see that happen. And - and so far, you know, we're - we're not seeing President Biden push for that in a way that people can get behind and see him as a leader on this issue.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Panel, thank you very much. Good to see all of you. We'll see you next Sunday.\par \par \uc2 Up next, President Biden is in danger of losing his party's primary here on Tuesday. We're going not explain why the incumbent's in trouble and what his allies are doing to try to save him, up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, though most of the focus is on the GOP primary here in New Hampshire, there's a compelling story brewing on the other side of the aisle, that the incumbent, President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , may not win his party's primary Tuesday night. But here's the back story on why.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I'm writing in \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice over): That's right, the current president of the United States is not on his party's primary ballot here in New Hampshire. Democrats have been haggling for more than a year over their primary calendar, forcing early states, Iowa and New Hampshire, to fight for their key spots at the top.\par \par \uc2 New Hampshire state law mandates that it is the first in the nation primary. A position it refused to seed. But the DNC has officially put South Carolina at the front of the line, meaning, New Hampshire Democrats are essentially holding an unsanctioned primary that won't get to award any delegates. Much to the consternation of candidate \uc2 Marianne Williamson\uc2 , who is on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 MARIANNE WILLIAMSON\uc2  (D), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Shame on you. Shame on you. You don't protect democracy by suppressing democracy.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice over): The president stood with his party and stayed off the ballot. But now his allies are crisscrossing the granite state pushing a write-in campaign, hoping to help Biden avoid the awkward optics of headlines proclaiming him the loser of New Hampshire's primary.\par \par \uc2 REP. RO KHANNA (D-CA): I believe President Biden will win. And it's going to be a hard-fought victory as a write-in candidate. That's a big deal.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Though some volunteers told us they aren't thrilled about being put in this position.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I think he's making a big mistake and he didn't -- doesn't have the courtesy to put his name on the ballot so we don't have to stand here and say, please put his name on the ballot. It's very disappointing.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice over): There's also a sense of pride among locals fighting to help the president score a win come Tuesday, even though they're disappointed with the DNC's decision to bump New Hampshire.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We are trying to show the \uc2 Democratic National Committee\uc2  that our election is not meaningless, that we're going to show we still support Biden.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM (on camera): So, President Biden is almost certain to fare better than he did here in the 2020 primary where he finished fifth among the Democratic candidates. He went on, of course, to rebound in South Carolina with the help of Congressman \uc2 Jim Clyburn\uc2 . That state is now the first recognized Democrat contest of the cycle.\par \par \uc2 And Fox News will have complete coverage of the New Hampshire voting on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 That is it for us today. We will spread out, keep you informed on what voters here have to say as we had into this final day of getting ready for the first primary sanctioned by Republicans, not by Democrats this time around here in New Hampshire.\par \par \uc2 I'm Shannon Bream. Have a great week. We'll see you next FOX NEWS SUNDAY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 Cont