itted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TYRUS MURDOCH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Horsepower.\par \par \uc2 HURT: Horsepower!\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR: Here we go.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Very good.\par \par \uc2 HURT: Good old-fashioned horsepower.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: The guy against the police should have been arrested for speeding.\par \par \uc2 HURT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes, I agree.\par \par \uc2 MURDOCH: So, everyone (INAUDIBLE) a comedian.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: All right, everybody. That's it for us. Have a great night and enjoy the weekend.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR: All right, Judge. Amish horsepower. Have a good weekend. We'll see you.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Too. Bye.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Good evening. I'm Bret Baier. "BREAKING TONIGHT", two big stories breaking. Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  picking up a key endorsement just four days away from the New Hampshire primary. One of his most dedicated pursuers, meantime, in court is facing new allegations of impropriety. Controversial Georgia prosecutor, \uc2 Fani Willis\uc2 , is blaming the estranged wife of her alleged lover for accusations she was -- she is engaged in an improper relationship with him.\par \par \uc2 Willis is the prosecutor leading the election-related case against former President Trump. Now, she is facing conflict of interest allegations herself. Correspondent Steve Harrigan is following this, has details tonight from Atlanta. Good evening, Steve.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARRIGAN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Bret, things are really getting complicated, bitter, and personal, even ahead of a February 15th hearing, that will try to determine if there was an improper relationship between the D.A. and the prosecutor she hired, and also to determine whether the two misspent public money on vacations for themselves. Now, the prosecutor, Nathan Wade, is in the middle of a divorce fight, and some court documents have come to light that shows that he paid four trips, taken by himself and the D.A.\par \par \uc2 In October 2022, he bought tickets for the two of them to fly from Atlanta to Miami, and then later, purchases on \uc2 Royal Caribbean\uc2  cruises. And then, again, April 2023, he bought tickets for the two of them to fly from Atlanta to San Francisco. And again, later purchases a \uc2 DoubleTree Hotel\uc2  in Napa Valley, California.\par \par \uc2 Now, Wade is a former municipal judge in the suburbs. He mainly dealt with family law and misdemeanors. This, before being tapped to build a complex RICO case against the former president and earning more than $650,000 in the process.\par \par \uc2 This hearing could delay things considerably. It could even cause the case against President Trump and his 14 co-defendants to be thrown out entirely.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PHILIP HOLLOWAY, CRIMINAL DEFENSE ATTORNEY: If these allegations are true, it could lead to a whole host of things that the D.A. doesn't want to happen.\par \par \uc2 She could be removed from the case, she could be held to have a conflict of interest, the case could potentially be dismissed, if it's so structurally unsound that it amounts to a violation of due process.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN: The D.A. has been subpoenaed to appear in a divorce case. She is fighting it. She says it's an organized attempt to interfere with her prosecution of President Trump. Bret, back to you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Steve Harrigan, live in Atlanta. Steve, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Now, to New Hampshire. Former President Trump says Nikki Haley has no chance to win the Republican nomination. And the person who was once Haley's political ally is now weighing in against her.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Bryan Llenas is in Manchester, New Hampshire tonight with the latest. Good evening, Bryan.\par \par \uc2 BRYAN LLENAS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Bret, good evening.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, sources tell Fox South Carolina Senator Tim Scott will endorse former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  tonight in New Hampshire. We're told both Trump and former Governor Nikki Haley called Scott to try to secure his endorsement, but in the end, he chose the front-runner.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY (R), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Imagine America with no drama, with no vengeance, with no chaos.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS (voice over): Tonight, former Governor Nikki Haley, ramping up attacks against former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 HALEY: He knows I know him very well. He knows I know what his lies are.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS (voice over): The gloves coming off four days before the New Hampshire primary.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: I know Nikki very well. She worked for me for a long time. She would not be able to handle that position, she would not be able to handle the onslaught.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS (voice over): The attacks coming as South Carolina senator and former presidential candidate Tim Scott is set to endorse Trump at a rally tonight in Concord, despite the fact that Haley appointed Scott to the Senate when she was governor of South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 Haley's supporter, New Hampshire Governor \uc2 Chris Sununu\uc2 , downplayed the endorsement.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 CHRIS SUNUNU\uc2  (R-NH): First, look, Tim Scott wouldn't have a job without Nikki Haley. Nobody cares what Tim Scott's thinks.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS (voice over): Scott's endorsement comes as Trump's lead in the Granite State appears to be widening. A new poll out today shows Trump with the 17-point lead over Haley. Yet, Haley insists that's not the poll that ultimately matters.\par \par \uc2 HALEY: Poll after poll, after poll shows that I defeat Biden by a good margin, and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  does not.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS (voice over): Florida governor, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , who refocused his presidential campaign to South Carolina, says Trump will win New Hampshire.\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: I think her problem is, is Trump will run away with it in New Hampshire. I think that that's pretty clear at this point. He's got the momentum from Iowa. And I don't think anyone thinks that she can beat Trump in her home state of South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 LLENAS (on camera): The South Carolina primary is more than a month away. Now, Haley was born in Bamberg, South Carolina. She is the daughter of immigrants from India.\par \par \uc2 Today, on True Social, Trump called Haley, Nimbra, mocking her given first name Nimarata. She goes by Nikki, which is her middle name. Haley says this is a sign that Trump is feeling threatened and insecure. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We're heading out to New Hampshire tomorrow. See you out there, Bryan. Thanks.\par \par \uc2 Huge day on Wall Street today, driven by optimism over artificial intelligence. The Dow and the S&P 500 hit new record closes, gaining 395 and 59, respectively.\par \par \uc2 The Nasdaq surged 255 to its highest close in a year. For the week, the Dow was up three quarters of a percentage point. The S&P 500 gained about one and a fifth. The NASDAQ jumped two and a quarter.\par \par \uc2 "BREAKING TONIGHT", the Biden administration holds Cabinet-level talks with Mexico about the border crisis. Immigration, also a major topic, of course, for the nation's mayors, meeting with the president a short time ago at the White House.\par \par \uc2 They also helped the president mark the signing of a measure to keep the government open.\par \par \uc2 White House correspondent Jacqui Heinrich has the latest tonight live from the North Lawn. Good evening, Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret.\par \par \uc2 Speaking to mayors from across the country, President Biden celebrated the short-term funding bill that he just signed to keep the government open with a subtle jab at Congress that something as basic as that counts as progress these days.\par \par \uc2 And he expressed hope the bipartisan border policy reform bill that's coming out of the Senate will be unveiled next week. But he said, whatever happens next is up to House Republicans.\par \par \uc2 The questions for the Speaker and the House Republicans, are they ready to act as well?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: They have to choose whether they want to solve a problem or keep weaponizing issues to score political points against the president. I'm ready to solve the problem, I really am. A massive changes, and I mean it sincerely.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): House Republicans have reiterated demands that this deal the Senate produces also includes several Trump-era policies, including rebuilding the wall, restoring Remain in Mexico, and ending so- called catch-and-release.\par \par \uc2 Despite hosting a Mexican delegation today to talk about migration challenges, White House officials would not say whether they're consulting with Mexico on this bill.\par \par \uc2 And in the meantime, House Republicans are pressing forward with their efforts to impeach DHS Secretary Alejandro Mayorkas, something President Biden says he doesn't get, even though he admits the border is not secure.\par \par \uc2 What do you say to House Republicans who were trying to impeach, Mayorkas, sir?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I don't get it.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Do you think it's unconstitutional?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Are you on the same page with Prime Minister Netanyahu?\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: And do you believe that the border is secure, sir?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: No, it's not. I haven't believed that for the last 10 years. I've said it for the last 10 years. Give me the money.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Do you believe your policies have enabled any of this crisis at the border (INAUDIBLE)?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: No. I've asked for thousands more -- everything from judges anyway.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Do you think you can convince --\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Will you fight Mayorkas' impeachment in court?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: That's a decision for --\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (on camera): The White House Counsel's Office indicated there may be efforts coming to fight Mayorkas's impeachment in court. A spokesman telling me, "it's a two by four" across the face of the Constitution.\par \par \uc2 But they are also here facing down opposition to new tranches student loan forgiveness programs, benefiting service workers announced today, some Republicans calling it an effort to buy votes before the election, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jacqui Henrich on a snowy North Lawn. Jacqui, thanks.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Texas officials are installing additional razor wire and fencing along the southern border in an area of federal land the state has now taken over, and they're making arrests there.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Matt Finn is in Eagle Pass Texas again tonight.\par \par \uc2 MATT FINN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Fox cameras today captured Texas state troopers arresting migrants who illegally crossed the razor wire barrier in Shelby Park, a major change in policy.\par \par \uc2 Texas DPS tells Fox News that the migrants it arrests will be charged with criminal trespassing and wait in jail until sentencing.\par \par \uc2 However, DPS points out after that, Texas will turn the migrants over to ICE, which will decide whether to deport.\par \par \uc2 FINN: Could you see how some people would say that's a waste of your resources to arrest them, to book in the process, and then turn them back over to the Biden administration who releases them?\par \par \uc2 SGT. RENE CORDOVA, \uc2 TEXAS DEPARTMENT OF PUBLIC SAFETY\uc2 : Yes, that's kind of like a double standard there. You know, we're trying to do one thing, and then, they do a different thing.\par \par \uc2 Over the past week, Fox crews have not seen any large migrant groups attempting to cross Texas's barbed wire.\par \par \uc2 A few weeks ago, Texas DPS says up to 4,000 migrants a day, freely crossed in and around the park. That number has dipped down to 700 yesterday.\par \par \uc2 Smaller groups of about five people approach, but Texas National Guard ordered them back to Mexico.\par \par \uc2 This man and woman holding babies tried to get through the wire, a reminder of the humanitarian crisis.\par \par \uc2 Today, Texas installed more razor wire fencing on top of cargo containers. Democratic candidate for Maverick County Commission thanks Governor Abbott for fortifying this park.\par \par \uc2 YOLANDA RAMON, DEMOCRATIC CANDIDATE, MAVERICK COUNTY, TEXAS COMMISSION: He is helping a lot more than anybody has here, you know. Again, I know that's a controversial issue here, because I know that a lot of people don't want to hear that.\par \par \uc2 FINN: And Bret, today, Texas DPS told me that it is getting considerable cooperation from the Mexican state of Coahuila, if they catch migrants on trains looking to illegally cross into the United States. They are sending them on buses back south. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Matt Finn along the border. Matt, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, it appears Alec Baldwin is not out of the woods yet over that fatal shooting on a New Mexico movie set. We'll bring you the latest. Plus, pro-life activists around the country converge on Washington, D.C.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Breaking tonight, there's a new charge against actor and producer Alec Baldwin over the shooting of two people on a New Mexico movie set in 2021. Baldwin is accused of involuntary manslaughter. He insists he did not pull the trigger.\par \par \uc2 Chief Correspondent Jonathan Hunt is in Los Angeles tonight with the latest on this. Good evening, Jonathan.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN HUNT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CHIEF CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret. The roller coaster for everyone touched by this tragedy continues. More than two years after cinematographer Halyna Hutchins was killed on the set of the low budget movie "Rust" when she was hit by a live bullet fired from a gun that was being held by Alec Baldwin as he rehearsed a scene.\par \par \uc2 Involuntary manslaughter charges were brought against the actor last January, and then dropped just three months later amid a flurry of legal objections from his legal team.\par \par \uc2 But now a grand jury has once again indicted the actor who has repeatedly claimed he did not pull the trigger of the gun and therefore, is not responsible for Hutchins's death.\par \par \uc2 But a new analysis by a company called forensic science services claims that gun being used by Baldwin during these rehearsals could not have been fired without the trigger being pulled, setting the stage for a trial that will likely include competing experts arguing opposite views of that claim, as well as arguments about Baldwin's broader responsibility or lack thereof for checking whether there was actually a live bullet loaded in the weapon.\par \par \uc2 Attorneys for Halyna Hutchins's family tonight welcomed the prospect of a trial saying they want to know the truth about Hutchins's death. Baldwin's lawyers said simply, "We look forward to our day in court". Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We'll follow this. Jonathan, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Big weather alert now, the next surge of arctic air is moving south, it's reaching the southern plains in Mississippi Valley today. More than 70 million Americans are under some type of wind chill alert this morning. The cold air will sweep into the Eastern U.S. tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 Snow is already falling from the Great Lakes through the Ohio Valley into southern New England. At least 10 deaths have been attributed to the weather in recent days.\par \par \uc2 A small plane made an emergency landing on a Virginia highway today moments after taking off from Dulles International Airport in the Washington area. No injuries reported, no details about what prompted that landing.\par \par \uc2 In Washington, pro-life activists were on the march, the annual March For Life, coincides with the anniversary of the original Roe vs. Wade ruling.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Mark Meredith takes a look tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MARK MEREDITH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Braving both snow and bitter cold temperatures. Thousands of pro-life activists gathered on the National Mall urging lawmakers to restrict abortions.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We need to understand that life is everything.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (voice over): Organizers of today's 51st annual March For Life rally say they're eager to keep the issue front and center nationally.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We've come here every year. That's a pilgrimage.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (voice over): Even after the Supreme Court ruled individual states can decide abortion policies.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We are always seeking to change hearts and minds.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (voice over): House Speaker Mike Johnson who once called abortion an American Holocaust told the crowd Congress should do more to help women who feel abandoned.\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE JOHNSON (R-LA): This is a critical time to help all moms who are facing unplanned pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (voice over): On the campaign trail, former President Trump takes credit for the Supreme Court's ruling ending Roe v. Wade. He appointed three justices. Still Trump says he supports some exceptions for abortion.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Go with your heart, your mind, go with it. But you do also have to put in there a little bit, you have to win elections.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (voice over): Democrats are also eager to make abortion a top issue this fall.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 PATTY MURRAY\uc2  (D-WA): And since Roe was overturned, we have seen that every time abortion rights was on the ballot. Abortion rights have won.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (voice over): President Biden who supports access to abortion held back on criticizing those marching in his backyard.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What's your message to those attending?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: March.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (voice over): And March they did. Ending at the Supreme Court where justices will soon decide whether abortion medications can be restricted and if doctors can be forced to perform the procedure and emergencies.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (on camera): Today's crowd was a bit smaller compared to last year. We spoke with people who are out here last year, the winter weather could be a factor in all of this.\par \par \uc2 Next week, the abortion debate is going to shift from here in D.C. over to Virginia where President Biden is going to be holding a campaign rally on this topic. They'll also be rolling out a new ad campaign in battleground states trying to make abortion a bigger issue before the fall, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Mark Meredith just off the National Mall in D.C. Mark, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the latest on the war in Ukraine. Plus, General Jack Keane with the ongoing global challenges for the U.S. military.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: \uc2 NATO\uc2  forces are about to participate in the most extensive war games in decades. They come amid what many consider a growing Russia threat exemplified in Moscow's invasion of Ukraine. Senior Foreign Affairs Correspondent Greg Palkot has the latest tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GREG PALKOT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR FOREIGN AFFAIRS CORRESPONDENT (voice over): The war between Russia and Ukraine rages on. An oil depot in Russia hit by Ukrainian drones overnight. Increasingly though, the war is becoming a grinding stalemate with Russia dug in and blasting away.\par \par \uc2 This threat from Moscow prompting \uc2 NATO\uc2  to announce its biggest military exercise in Europe since the Cold War dubbed Steadfast Defender 24.\par \par \uc2 Starting next week, 90,000 troops, 1,100 tanks and combat vehicles, 130 jets and ships in action. The drill aimed at getting U.S. forces to Europe and rehearsing at least \uc2 NATO\uc2  deterring Russia from targeting a member country.\par \par \uc2 GEN. CHRISTOPHER CAVOLI, SUPREME ALLIED COMMANDER EUROPE: Steadfast Defender 24 will be a clear demonstration of our unity, our strength and our determination to protect each other.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT (voice over): The Kremlin defiant about \uc2 NATO\uc2 's challenge.\par \par \uc2 MARIA ZAKHAROVA\uc2 , \uc2 RUSSIAN FOREIGN MINISTRY\uc2  (through translator): To increase the activity and military potential of \uc2 NATO\uc2  near the border of Russia is provocative and will not remain without an appropriate reaction from Russia.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT (voice over): As Russian saber rattling grows, Moscow officials rejecting a Biden administration offer to resume nuclear arms control talks as long as the U.S. supports Ukraine. Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  says this is big.\par \par \uc2 VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY, UKRAINIAN PRESIDENT: If anyone thinks this is only about us. This is only about Ukraine. You are fundamentally mistaken.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT (voice over): As billions of dollars of U.S. military aid is held up on Capitol Hill. \uc2 European Union\uc2  help also blocked, more ammunition for Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  to strengthen his fight with the West.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT KAGAN, AMERICAN ENTERPRISE INSTITUTE: It's fueling Putin. It's encouraging him to think more broadly about U.S. willingness to resist him at all.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PALKOT (on camera): And other top \uc2 NATO\uc2  officials saying they are facing the most dangerous world in decades, calling for a war making transformation of the alliance. Tough talk, high stakes, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Greg, thank you.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, PRESS SECRETARY, PENTAGON: You know, if you look at the levers of national power, you know, there's an acronym for a dime, right, diplomacy, information, military, economic. We're using all of those levers of national power and, frankly, international power to try to convince the Houthis to stop these attacks. And if they don't, and they clearly haven't, to make sure that we're holding them accountable for that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, spanning the globe from Central Europe to the Middle East to the Korean peninsula beyond there's a long list that has Pentagon leader's full attention. Let's talk about Ukraine, some of the other hotspots globally with Fox News senior strategic analyst retired General Jack Keane.\par \par \uc2 General, thanks for joining us. I want to start where Jack Kirby -- John Kirby left off there about the Houthis and the effort to get them to stop firing on U.S. troops.\par \par \uc2 If you look at the map, there have been 35 attacks since November on U.S. forces in the Red Sea and in around U.S. forces and also U.S. ships.\par \par \uc2 Just Wednesday, there was an attack on Genco Picardy U.S. on bulk carriers ship hit by Houthi drones and you can see the side of the ship attacked there. What about this effort to take out their capabilities and to get them to stop and they're not stopping?\par \par \uc2 GEN. JACK KEANE (RET.), FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR STRATEGIC ANALYST: Yes, well, certainly the Houthis have had their impact for sure. And they've stopped about 50 percent of the shipping through the Suez Canal and the Red Sea, which is obviously impacting the global supply chain.\par \par \uc2 I mean, the reality is, is they have thousands of rockets and missiles, and hundreds of launchers, radar systems, command and control. They've been at war for years with the Saudi Arabia and the UAE. So, they're obviously skilled at what they're doing.\par \par \uc2 And the administration finally took a long time to get going, but started a comprehensive campaign that take down their capability. That's the first thing we have got to do. And what am I talking about? Rockets, missiles, command and control, launcher systems. We have to take all of that down using the capabilities that we have and the good intelligence that we have. We estimate about 25 percent of that has been destroyed or is ineffective. So a lot of work ahead here, but it requires a sustained campaign.\par \par \uc2 The second thing is, we have got to stop the trans shipments that go into Yemen from Iran. And we appear to be getting after that now. And that certainly is something we have to sustain.\par \par \uc2 And then I have said this before. The center of gravity is Iran. You have to hold them accountable. All the proxies are responding to Iran, and that's why they have all upped their game in the Middle East. We have to go after them. I believe the IRGC is the most likely target. Cent Com has certainly a plethora of targets they can select from, but I would go after their training center, their bases, their headquarters, and their leaders. Hold them accountable because they are really pulling the strings here.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: how concerned are you about U.S. capabilities and stockpiles? Here's a quote from "politico," "Lawmakers push for more money and missiles amid Red Sea clash. The U.S. raided it's inventories by donating missiles and artillery shells to Ukraine. And the effort to suppress Houthi attacks using multimillion dollars systems to shoot down cheap drones and missiles is the latest fight that lawmakers worry could divert resources from deterring a potential future conflict with China over Taiwan." Are you concerned about that?\par \par \uc2 KEANE: Yes, certainly. We do have serious defense industrial base challenges. Wargames have presented that to us as a major issue if we fight a war with China, and certainly some of the things that are taking place here in the Middle East adding on to what's taking place in Ukraine have identified all of that. The Congress in the United States is trying to get after some of this in the defense budget. I don't think it's sufficient in terms of what we need to do. And we have got to accelerate the development of these weapon systems and munitions if we expect to be able to sustain ourselves.\par \par \uc2 Look at the problems we have. There's two wars going on in the world now, both are in geographical areas of vital interest to the United States, Europe and the Middle East. And President Xi is threatening more in the Indo-Pacific region. These are major, major problems the United States hasn't had for decades. These are significant challenges, and we really have to wake up to the seriousness of these threats and get after fixing the deficiencies we have.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: There is a real pushback in the Republican caucus, specifically about Ukraine money, and I know we have talked about how you look at it and how you see that U.S. troops not fighting the battle on the ground in Ukraine against Russia. I want to put up these side-by-side maps of where Ukraine is and what's happening. This is November '23 on the left and today on the right. There is not much change at all from what we can see on the ground anecdotally and what we're being told. Where do you see it? And how concerned are you about what you see politically about Ukraine funding?\par \par \uc2 KEANE: Yes, well, certainly what is happening, Ukraine is very much aware of the political deadlock taking place in the United States over funding for their war that they're involved in. There's other issues with Israel and certainly with Taiwan as well tied up in that funding dispute. But what are they doing as a result of that? They have gone on the defensive. There's a significant amount of anxiety. I mean, they are hoarding the weapon systems and the munitions they have. Their adversaries are Russians, so they are attacking offensively along the front. They are not making a lot of progress because of the ineptness that some of those formations have. They have conducted a massive air campaign over a number of weeks here that we've seen.\par \par \uc2 And what's really happening is Putin and the thugs around him are triumphant because they believe that the United States and the Europeans are showing signs of taking a knee, so to speak. They're not as determined in commitment. Not only that, he is absolutely making plans and beginning to set the conditions in Finland and Latavia and Estonia for what he did in 2014 that invited him into Crimea and also into eastern Ukraine. That is setting the conditions for an invasion into the those countries, force \uc2 NATO\uc2  to respond to that and not respond effectively.\par \par \uc2 This is where Putin's head is. And we have got to understand that this is much more than Ukraine, as the president of Ukraine, President Zelenskyy said. Putin does have desires to break the trans-Atlantic NATO treaty. And he has plans for that -- the Germans just revealed secret plans about the very thing that I'm discussing right now in a public forum. That's the reality of what we're facing here. If we walk away from Ukraine, Russia wins.\par \par \uc2 And what do they do with that victory? They can generate about 300,000 new conscripts and mobilized forces a year. We believe in 18 months to two years they will make a move on eastern Europe, which would put us into that war because it's a \uc2 NATO\uc2  violation. And the money we would spend in doing something like that would absolutely exceed the commitment we're making right now to Ukraine on an annual basis. We've got understand the implications of our action in terms of what a loss in Ukraine would do for Russia and blowing up Europe as a result of it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: General, we always appreciate your analysis. Thanks a lot. Have a great weekend.\par \par \uc2 KEANE: Yes, you, too, Bret. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Up next, our "Whatever Happened To?" segment on the Keystone XL pipeline cancelation and the green energy jobs pipeline workers were promised back then. Remember that?\par \par \uc2 As we go to break, the oldest living Master's champion has died. Jack Burke Jr. staged the greatest comeback ever at Augusta National in 1956, coming from eight shots back in the final round. He also won the PGA championship, won 16 times in the PGA tour, captained the winning U.S. Ryder Cup team in 1973. He died this morning in Houston. Jack Burke was 100 years old.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: This weekend marks three years since President Biden took office and three years since he canceled construction of the Keystone XL pipeline. So how are the pipeline industry and its workers doing? Senior correspondent Alicia Acuna has tonight's "Whatever Happened To?" report.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PATRICK STRICKLAND, WARRIOR FIELD SERVICES: I have friends that have just sold their trucks, welding machines, all of their equipment.\par \par \uc2 BUGSY ALLEN, FORMER KEYSTONE XL PIPELINE WORKER: They are tired of fighting it. They don't see any future in it.\par \par \uc2 ALICIA ACUNA, FOX NEWS SENIOR CORRESPONDENT: Pipeline welder Bugsy Allen was working at a Keystone XL pump station in Corrigan, Texas, when President Biden signed an executive order revoking the pipeline's key federal permit.\par \par \uc2 ALLEN: We was laid off that day instantly with no questions asked.\par \par \uc2 ACUNA: U.S. pipeline employment was already in decline in January, 2021, but according to the Energy Department, the Keystone cancellation cost the industry up to 59,000 jobs.\par \par \uc2 STRICKLAND: We felt like we were outright attacked. We rely on these jobs to be able to support our families.\par \par \uc2 ACUNA: The instability is pushing many in the industry to take early retirements. While some will accept work on projects that don't require federal permits, they won't take career advice from outgoing Biden climate envoy \uc2 John Kerry\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 JOHN KERRY\uc2 , SPECIAL PRESIDENTIAL CLIMATE ENVOY: They could be the people who go to work to make the solar panels.\par \par \uc2 ALLEN: My job, I make double over what a green energy boy does putting up solar panels.\par \par \uc2 ACUNA: When pushed by FOX News on that very idea back in 2021, the Biden administration balked.\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , FORMER WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: I'd certainly welcome to you present your data of all the thousands and thousands of people who won't be getting a green job.\par \par \uc2 ACUNA: And 22,000 clean powered generation jobs were added between January of 2021 and March 2023. Now, Keystone construction sites sit abandoned. FOX News visited this one in Dorchester, Nebraska, in January, 2021. Today, it looks the same. As some workers moved on, those entering the workforce choose other industries. Republican North Dakota Senator \uc2 Kevin Cramer\uc2  says his state now faces a shortage of need talent.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 KEVIN CRAMER\uc2 , (R-ND): We're back up to almost 1.2 million barrels of oil production a day in my state. We have a lot more that we can produce if we just had the workers.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 ACUNA (on camera): Three years into his presidency, Biden has yet to nominate an administrator for the agency in charge of U.S. pipeline safety and oversight. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Alicia, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel on the presidential race and next week's New Hampshire primary.\par \par \uc2 First, here's what some of our FOX affiliates around the country are covering tonight. WSVN in Miami with dramatic video of a cargo plane on fire. It happened as the jet was taking off from \uc2 Miami International Airport\uc2 . That plane landed safely and there were no injuries. Hard to believe.\par \par \uc2 FOX 5 in Atlanta as a new study suggests almost 1,000 Georgians are falsely arrested each year because of unreliable roadside drug tests. The report says substances such as cotton candy, vitamins, and breath mints have tested positive for drugs.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at very foggy San Francisco. We can't really see anything from FOX 2. Maybe make it out in the distance. One of the big stories there tonight, sports bars in the Bay Area prepare for tomorrow night's \uc2 NFL\uc2  playoff game before the 49ers and the \uc2 Green Bay Packers\uc2 . It's a big football weekend. Establishments are expecting huge crowds. You can see the game on the FOX broadcast network, big FOX, Saturday at 8:00 p.m. eastern time. Cannot wait for football this weekend.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look, foggy look, outside the beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: Nikki Haley will never secure the border. She actually opposed my border wall.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY, (R) PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: He said that I didn't want a border wall, and that's not what I said. I said I don't just want a border wall. We have got to do a whole lot more than that.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : She is not going to make it. She has no chance. She has no way. MAGA is not going to be with her.\par \par \uc2 HALEY: It's not personal with me and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . This and how do we save our country.\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS, (R-FL) PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Trump will run away with it in New Hampshire. I don't think anyone thinks that she can beat Trump in her home state of South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, New Hampshire primary closing in. I head out to New Hampshire tomorrow. If you look at the new Suffolk University-"Boston Globe" tracking poll in New Hampshire, it has the former president at 51.8 percent, Nikki Haley at 35.4. And you see Governor DeSantis at 6.4.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, the news today, Senator Tim Scott to endorse the former president. "His decision to back Trump is a blow to Nikki Haley," says "Politico", "a fellow South Carolina Republican who is aiming to have a strong showing in New Hampshire's upcoming primary. It was Haley who appointed Scott to the Senate in 2012 while serving as governor of the palmetto state."\par \par \uc2 With that, let's bring in our panel, Kimberley Strassel, a member of the editorial board at "The Wall Street Journal," Byron York, chief political correspondent of "The Washington Examiner," and "THE FIVE" co-host Jessica Tarlov. Byron, what about the Senator Scott endorsement? Does it move the needle in a place called New Hampshire?\par \par \uc2 BYRON YORK, CHIEF POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT, "THE WASHINGTON EXAMINER": Well, the endorsement of Governor Sununu has been pretty valuable for Nikki Haley. It seems unlikely at this last minute that Senator Scott's endorsement is going to have a lot of impact in New Hampshire. It has a lot of impact in South Carolina where have you Haley, former governor of South Carolina running, and the senior senator, \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 , has endorsed \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . The junior senator, Tim Scott, is going to endorse \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , and the Governor \uc2 Henry McMaster\uc2  have all endorsed \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . That's really not sort of favorite son, favorite daughter status for Nikki Haley.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. Kimberley, you wrote this piece, the "Them versus Us Election," in part saying, "The challenge for Mr. Trump's GOP opponents as they move past Iowa is to recognize the sense of alienation. That doesn't mean calling to burn everything down. \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2  tried that and freaked people out. But it does require a campaign that offers more than vague promises to strengthen the cause of freedom or run on your issues." I get the gist of that. The former president is in the poll position. What's your conclusion?\par \par \uc2 KIMBERLEY STRASSEL, "WALL STREET JOURNAL": That they are going to have to do a lot more. She is going to have to do a lot more if she actually wants to actually catch him. You have seen her, since moving on to Iowa she has sharpened her attack. She's got a new ad out putting Trump and Biden in the same category of people that are obsessed with past grievances, chaotic, their rein will be chaotic. And she is going to turn the corner.\par \par \uc2 I think what is then lacking is a real distinction between how she would do that versus How Trump would do that. There are some notable areas of difference, in particular on foreign policy. She gets into them in some ways. But these vague promises that you will restore the cause of freedom or that you are going to fix the economy, a lot of voters think that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  will do that, too.\par \par \uc2 So I think that she is going to have to, and \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , are going to have to be a lot more specific about how their time in office would materially be different than \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  if they want to make a race of this.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jessica, she is also saying that definitively VP is off the table. She wouldn't take it, not going to do it. That's something that she wouldn't say a few weeks ago. She is saying it now. Meantime, the former president is calling her by her given name, not Nikki, in a Truth Social post. So it's getting interesting in the final days here.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA TARLOV, CO-HOST, "THE FIVE": Yes, well, he is not really calling her by her given name. He is calling her by a name that he concocted.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: A nickname of the given name.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Of the given name, calling her "Nimbra." People pointing to that he has brought back the birtherism, those attacks that her parents weren't citizens when she was born here, that she is ineligible to be able to be running. And then of course he is saying that Democrats are just showing up and voting for her when we know that anyone who had to switch parties had to do that back at the end of October of 2023.\par \par \uc2 So, he is back to his old playbook with the racism and birtherism. And I don't know, though, if that means that whenever \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  claims the nominee status, and that is what I believe is going to happen, Nikki Haley won't change her mind and say I'm open to being his VP. And that's what \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  was caught talking about on that, quote, hot mic moment. I'm not sure how hot it was rather than he just wanted people to hear him. But people have alleged that she is quite slippery that way, and that she will say what she needs to say. Like, she admitted in Iowa, I'm a different person in Iowa than I am in New Hampshire, and then I'm going to be different in South Carolina. And I think that's something that has hurt her, that kind of consistency to it. Whether you like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  or not, he is who he is, and that never changes. He can't make it more than, like, 12 minutes right. That's what we saw on Iowa caucus winning night.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Right. All right, Byron, March for Life, snowy, wintry weather, still a significant turnout. And obviously people passionate about this issue from the other side that we usually hear about in the media. Byron?\par \par \uc2 BYRON YORK, CHIEF POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT, "THE WASHINGTON EXAMINER": Oh, sorry. This is in an entirely, this march is in an entirely different context than we have seen before, except the last year, after the Dobbs decision. And the anti-abortion movement, the pro-life movement has had this incredible success, and you had these decades and decades of Marches for Life all directed toward one goal, which was the repeal of Roe v. Wade. Now you have what the pro-lifers did want, which is political battles in all 50 states about this. And it's a much, much murkier situation.\par \par \uc2 Plus, it was a true factor in the 2022 midterms and could well be a big factor in the presidential election this year. It's just a much different event than it used to be.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Definitely.\par \par \uc2 OK, let's talk Winners and Losers. Kimberley, first to you.\par \par \uc2 STRASSEL: So, my winner is Congress. Thank you very much for not putting us through another shutdown. Hopefully they will make some progress over the next couple of weeks on a spending deal.\par \par \uc2 And my loser are all those poor individuals who own EVs in cold states who are only now being told about physics, namely that batteries do not like frigid temperatures. But it's a little bit too late and there's probably some buyer's remorse.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: By the way, Ford is cutting production of the F150 lightning pickup trucks after slow EV sales growth. That was an alert today to your losing point.\par \par \uc2 OK, Jessica, winner and loser?\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: My winner was the Biden campaign for their huge fundraising haul, $97 million in the fourth quarter, $117 million on hand, outpacing even the Obama campaign at the end of 2011. So in the same place.\par \par \uc2 And my loser, I connect it to Kimberley, is my loser was the American people who can't get a Congress that can do anything but continue to pass short CRs. So get a real spending bill done, guys.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, Byron, winner and loser?\par \par \uc2 YORK: My winner is \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . He won the Iowa caucuses. That's enough said about that. And my loser is Trump's -- one of Trump's main pursuers which is \uc2 Fani Willis\uc2 , the D.A. of Fulton County, Georgia. We are getting more and more details about a pretty sorted story of what appears to be her relationship with the prosecutor she chose to lead the Trump prosecution and all sorts of money and hotel bills and plane tickets and all sorts of stuff. Continuing story.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, we're going to follow that story. Panel thanks. Have a great weekend.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You know that sound. That means it's Friday. Time for "Notable Quotables."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: Even if you vote and then pass away, it's worth it.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: There are no snow days on caucus day.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: This guy, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , my mom approves of him.\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS, (R-FL) PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: We've got our ticket punched out of Iowa.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY, (R) PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Iowa made this Republican primary a two-person race.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Between \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: I want to congratulate Ron and Nikki for having a good time together.\par \par \uc2 VIVEK RAMASWAMY\uc2 , (R) PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: We are going to suspend this presidential campaign.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : These people are dropping like rocks.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , (D) VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I am scared as heck.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: My predecessor likes to say America is a failing nation.\par \par \uc2 MERRICK GARLAND\uc2 , U.S. ATTORNEY GENERAL: The law enforcement response at Robb Elementary school was a failure.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I hope that the failures end today.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  had a Hunter Biden problem.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Instead, he's talking about vanilla and chocolate ice cream and sniffing children.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : He looked great in that bathing suit. Show that body and they're going to vote for you in droves.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  put immigrants in cages. He separated families.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: There are more kids in custody under the Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Democrats need to own the facts that immigration is at a crisis level.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The secretary of state announced the designation of the Houthis as a specially designated global terrorists.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We don't control the Houthis.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I'm not retiring, folks.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Congratulations. Nobody cares about that, do you?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: One week.\par \par \uc2 Monday on SPECIAL REPORT, we'll be live in New Hampshire with a preview of Tuesday's primary. I'm heading out there tomorrow. Please watch Shannon Bream live from New Hampshire on FOX NEWS SUNDAY. Her guests are Ohio Senator J.D. Vance along with Congressman Ralph Norman and \uc2 Thomas Massie\uc2 . I'll be on the panel as well.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for this SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and still unafraid. "THE INGRAHAM ANGLE" is now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \