, firing his pistol through a door while she was crouching in a bathroom.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: There is no reason or no explanation.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT (voice over): In the trial he claimed he thought there was an intruder in his home. The prosecution said the shots were fired in anger following an argument.\par \par \uc2 In the end, Pistorius was convicted of murder, sentenced to nearly 13-and- a-half years in jail. After serving more than half that time, he is now out on parole. Reeva Steenkamp's mother June said in a statement today, "There can never be justice if your loved one is never coming back, and no amount of time served will bring Reeva back."\par \par \uc2 The killing raised issues in South Africa about women's safety. Today's release raised them again.\par \par \uc2 BULELWA ADONIS, WOMEN FOR CHANGE: South Africa is the place to be as a predator, because it's definitely not the place to be as a victim or survivor.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT: On the street, people sounded more favorable.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I believe in second chances and it's OK that he got out.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I hope to see him one day back on the road running again.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT: It's reported Pistorius is now at his uncle's house in a suburb of Pretoria where he is expected to serve out a nearly six year long parole.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 PALKOT (on camera): Among the requirements of that parole, Pistorius must perform community service, stay away from the media, and attend anger management classes. Gillian?\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Greg Palkot in London tonight for us, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well, in tonight's "Common Ground" segment, we got a pair of congressmen, one Democrat, one Republican, who are working together now to help veterans here in America in crisis. Here's my colleague, Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Thanks, Gillian.\par \par \uc2 It is time for our "Common Ground" segment. Joining us tonight are Illinois Republican Congressman \uc2 Mike Bost\uc2  and California Democratic Congressman \uc2 Mark Takano\uc2 . Congressmen, thanks for being here. You both are concerned and active on veterans issues, being on the Veterans Affairs Committee. I want to put up this chart of suicides from veterans from 2001 to 2021. And you can see the increase there, just in 2021, 6,392 veteran suicide deaths, up 114 from the year before. Congressman Bost, you're working on legislation. You have worked together in a bipartisan way --\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE BOST, (R-IL): Yes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: -- to try to help on this. Talk about that.\par \par \uc2 BOST: No matter what, one veteran suicide is too many. We have been working to make sure that, one, our veterans we implemented the new three- digit 988 number so that we can make sure that veterans can have access to the hotline whenever they need it. We're also expanding and look into each program and the successfulness of each program, and also making sure that we're providing and working with our community providers that can provide mental health care early on to meet those needs of our veterans no matter where they are, whether they are close to V.A. or they're not close to a V.A. We cannot afford to keep that number where it's at. We have got to do everything in our power to bring it down.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Congressman Takano, you all have this Compact Act under the Veterans Comprehensive Prevention Access to Care Treatment, the Compact Act. Veterans with acute suicidal crisis can go immediately to any V.A. or non-V.A. healthcare facility for emergency healthcare at no cost to them. And that seems like a big deal.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MARK TAKANO\uc2 , (D-CA): It is a big deal. I am pleased to say that I authored the bill and got it signed into law by President Trump in 2020, and I give the Biden administration a lot of credit for also implementing the law and making sure that the 988 number is funded. As Chairman Bost said, if you are a veteran having an emergency or an emergence of mental health episode, you are going through suicidal thoughts, call 988 and dial 1. You can be connected to mental health care, whether it's inpatient or outpatient, and you won't have to worry about a bill. So if you have served our country and you are a veteran, whether you are enrolled in V.A. or not, don't worry about the bill. Just call that number and get help.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And I was saying you all worked on it together, a common ground element of the Veterans Affairs that is now law and active, as you point out. I want to put up this graphic about recruits. And that is a concern for a lot of folks. The \uc2 Department of Defense\uc2  fiscal year 2023, military services collectively missed recruiting goals by about 41,000. That's 15 percent down. How big a concern is that for readiness, Mr. Chairman?\par \par \uc2 BOST: That's a concern. I come from an area where we have a lot of service people, and they are encouraged by their parents to go into our military. Unfortunately, some of those parents are not encouraging. I don't know whether it's because of the things with the COVID vaccine, the problem that was there being forced, and then we brought that back and changed it. I don't know whether it is -- and not to go political, but the woke positions in the military that these different parents are telling their family not to be involved.\par \par \uc2 We need to have our recruits there and serving this country. I'm a marine veteran. My grandfather, my uncle, me, my son, and my grandson -- my grandson is now serving. My son is now serving. So we've got to work to encourage people. Our nation can only be secure if we have those people who are willing to, in a volunteer force, step forward to protect and secure this nation.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And Congressman Takano, just to leave it here, for people who say look up to Capitol Hill and say there's just nothing getting done and it's a partisan mess, and Congress doesn't work, what do you say to them?\par \par \uc2 TAKANO: Well, I would say look to the Veterans Affairs committee as a ray of hope. I have worked well with Chairman Bost. And let me just say that I believe that these recruitment numbers are going to improve. I believe this generation of Americans is every bit as patriotic as previous generations. We need to make sure that they know about the tremendous V.A. benefits, that they can own a home at a younger age than many of their peers because of the V.A. home loan program. They can finance their education through the G.I. bill. And I would say that the Biden economy has been improving so much that we have -- we have to compete. The military has to compete with all the higher salaries and the low unemployment out there.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Congressmen, we appreciate you coming on common ground, and we encourage others on both sides of the aisle to do the same. Thanks a lot.\par \par \uc2 You can see all of the "Common Ground" segments on my podcast "Common Ground." Find that and the "All-Star Panel" podcasts under the Bret Baier podcasts at FOXNewspodcast.com, \uc2 Spotify\uc2 , or wherever you download podcasts. You can also see the whole extended discussion on the FOX News \uc2 YouTube\uc2  page.\par \par \uc2 Gillian, we'll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Great segment, as always, Bret.\par \par \uc2 Coming up next, the Friday Lightning Round on the 2024 election landscape, the likely Biden versus Trump matchup, and the southern border surge. Plus, we have got Candidate Casino.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Trump's assault on democracy isn't just part of his past. It's what he is promising for the future.\par \par \uc2 We must be clear -- democracy is on the ballot. Your freedom is on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 REINCE PRIEBUS, FORMER WHITE HOUSE CHIEF OF STAFF: I don't buy this threat to democracy case that the Democrats are making. There is no chance in the world that if they actually believe that that it would -- that they would be sitting with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Time to bring in the panel. We've got Spencer Brown, managing editor of "Townhall," FOX News senior political analyst Juan Williams, and Olivia Beavers, congressional reporter for "Politico." Thanks, all, for being here. Great to see you.\par \par \uc2 Olivia, what this Biden speech today coupled with the release of the first reelection campaign ad sort of illustrated for us is that he is not, right now, anyway, going to run on the core issues for the base -- abortion, the economy. He is instead kind of circling back from his theme 2020, the battle for the soul of democracy, the battle for the soul of the nation.\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA BEAVERS, "POLITICO": Long gone are the days where he wasn't even naming \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in some of his speeches. He is really going after him now. But he has to be careful about also emphasizing what his administration has done and is going to do. Otherwise, it risks going too negative too early, and he's clearly trying to push into the democracy angle where polls have indicated he is doing better than \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . That's not so much in other top tier issues like the economy and inflation. He's hoping the economy numbers get better for him before the election day. But in democracy, that's why you see him hitting January 6th. That's why you see him hitting Charlottesville. And he is trying to paint Trump as a threat. And \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is, of course, responding that Biden is a threat.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: So, Juan, to that point, critics say that Biden should not look backwards, he should not focus on the past. But the reality is that he is in this unique position where he's running against an opponent, Trump, who already served as president. He has got a history. He has got a very long record for folks to consider.\par \par \uc2 JUAN WILLIAMS, FOX NEWS POLITICAL ANALYST: Absolutely. And this is an interesting moment. I think we are seeing today with this speech the start of the campaign. It really kicks off. And of course, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is in Iowa, and so we're seeing it on both sides. But what you have is a moment where the Republicans still have a contest going on. The Democrats don't. So this is an opportunity for President Biden to get his message out. And the key to his message at this moment is to remind voters why they threw out \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . He was the president, as you point out, Gillian. So they threw him out. And he is going to say remember all the chaos. Remember what happened January 6th. Tomorrow is January 6th. It's an anniversary of that day. Is he going to absolutely exploit that for political advantage.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: And President Biden was slated to give the speech tomorrow, but they had to move it forward a day because of weather, Spencer. University of Maryland poll out, take a look at this. Americans are divided along party lines whether or not the former president is to blame for January 6th, whether he deserves responsibility. They're really split, clearly along party lines. Look at that, 86 percent of Democrats think it's his responsibility, his fault, only 14 percent of Republicans.\par \par \uc2 SPENCER BROWN, "TOWNHALL" MANAGING EDITOR: I think it's interesting to see Biden launch his campaign the same way that he kind of campaigned in the midterms. He made that case in front of Independence Hall in Pennsylvania. He did the same thing at Union Station right before the election. And the whole idea he is trying to sell here is because he doesn't have those domestic issues that Olivia was mentioning to campaign on, he has got to campaign essentially on this fear of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  getting into the White House again and having that power.\par \par \uc2 And so I think you see in the very partisan polling on that as far as who is responsible for January 6th, you see \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  leaning into that, because he needs to solidify his base because he's losing among so many key people groups that he won in 2020 with.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Juan, good news on the economy today for the president -- 216,000 jobs added, something not a lot of folks were predicting or really expecting. Do you think they should now circle back and pick up the Bidenomics thread again?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: It's not a matter of picking up. I think it's a matter of consistency. When you look at polls, you tend not to want to look at any one moment. You want to look at trend lines. And the trend lines obviously are that we had good holiday spending, so people were out spending. People were taking trips, going to restaurants, buying gifts. And the second thing, and I think this underlines it, is consumer sentiment going up. We saw the stock market, 2023, no recession. That could be a bold headline because all of us sitting here started last year, we were saying, boy, this economy is kind of wobbly and we don't know if we're going to go into recession. The recession didn't take place. The economy looks steady. And now the question is, can consumers change what has been a kind of grumpy, anxious attitude towards that economy, and will they change in time for the Biden camp going towards November?\par \par \uc2 TURNER: So maybe one chip in the president's column, something that's shaping up to be a liability for him, Olivia, is the crisis along the southern border. Take a look at this, another record-breaking month. It seems like we say that every month, but this one, we saw more than 300,000 illegal migrant crossings for the first time in history. Compare that to about the size of the city of Pittsburgh. Is there anything to be done between now and the year from now?\par \par \uc2 BEAVERS: I mean, the Biden administration knows this is a political liability. And they -- that's why I think they are actively involved in the Senate negotiations. They are hoping to have some border policy that can be tacked on to foreign aid to Israel, Ukraine, Taiwan. They keep saying that they are making progress. That's where we have heard a lot. We might see something next week. But until we see a deal and until we have an idea if Speaker Mike Johnson is OK with it, it's got a tough path.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Border security is shaping up to be a pretty major election issue, at least among Republicans, Spencer. Take a look at this polling out from "The Wall Street Journal", 54 percent of voters think Trump is better able -- excuse me, is best able to handle this crisis compared to just 24 percent President Biden.\par \par \uc2 BROWN: That's pretty brutal numbers for Biden on that one. And I think it's easy to understand why the American people feel that way. If you look at what the border was during the years Trump was in office and you look at it since Biden took office, it's pretty easy to just compare and say, well, we didn't have all of this going on when Trump was in office, so Biden probably isn't the one to handle it. And it doesn't help that at every turn, whether it's Secretary Mayorkas or other people, they are making excuses, they're refusing to call it a crisis. They're just kind of trying to pretend this is normal. You see the White House all the time saying there is nothing extraordinary about this, this isn't unusual, when in fact December we just saw again the largest number of illegal immigrant encounters at the border. So clearly \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is not convincing the American people that he can handle it.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: And Secretary Mayorkas on with Biden last night again declining to call it a crisis.\par \par \uc2 All right, it's time for Candidate Casino. All right, the stakes are very high. I need each of you to tell me who is going to be the Republican candidate for 2024, followed by your pick for who is going to be the Democratic candidate. Juan, you get to go first.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: So we have $100 on the Candidate Casino table. I'll put $100 on \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . I think that's a sure bet. But on the Republican side, $80 on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , $15 on Nikki Haley. And I'm not ready to write off DeSantis, $5 on DeSantis.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: All right, $5 ain't nothing. Olivia?\par \par \uc2 BEAVERS: I did something similar. I had $85 on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , I had $12 on Nikki Haley, and I had $8 on \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 TURNER: All right, Spencer?\par \par \uc2 BROWN: On the Republican side, I put $90 on Former President Trump based on the polls and everything we have going on. But I'm still not willing to count out DeSantis or Haley, so I gave them each $5. We will see what happens in Iowa and New Hampshire.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: We will. And Iowa is just exactly 10 days away from today. A lot of the eyeballs going to be on what going on down there. We've got three separate townhalls with the candidates coming up leading into the first Iowa caucuses.\par \par \uc2 Coming up next, we're going to take a look back at 15 years of Bret Baier right here at this anchor desk in a special version of "Notable Quotables."\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Welcome back. Finally tonight, we're doing something a little different, because it's 15 years ago today that Bret Baier hosted his first ever SPECIAL REPORT as the show's permanent anchor. We are going to take a look back now at some of Bret's most memorable moments in a very special edition, an anniversary edition of "Notable Quotables."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Finally tonight, you may have noticed, after 2,851 shows at SPECIAL REPORT with Brit Hume, this show has a new name. I'm honored, extremely honored to have been given this opportunity to follow my mentor's career path.\par \par \uc2 This.\par \par \uc2 This.\par \par \uc2 This is SPECIAL REPORT\par \par \uc2 A lot of people just ask me why are you doing.\par \par \uc2 More than 80 bodies have been pulled out of what's left of the southside of the Pentagon.\par \par \uc2 In the mountains heading toward the border.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Twelfth time.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: This is the DMZ.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Please let me know the answer. It would be the first time in four years.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Large cheese, please.\par \par \uc2 Would letting one of the big three automakers go to bankruptcy be a big mark on your legacy?\par \par \uc2 GEORGE W. BUSH\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: I'm looking at all options.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: This week?\par \par \uc2 BUSH: I'm thinking it through.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: No date, though?\par \par \uc2 BUSH: You're trying to make news. I'm not going to let you make news.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: So -- so the notion.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You guarantee they are not going -- they are going to be able to keep their doctor?\par \par \uc2 OBAMA: Bret, you've got to let me finish.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: At times you thought it would be a model for the nation.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITT ROMNEY, (R-UT): You're wrong, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: No, no, I mean --\par \par \uc2 ROMNEY: Bret, Bret. This is an unusual interview. All right, let's do it again.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Delegates are very fired up to officially kick off this convention. Here's the stage, and the seating here on the floor very important.\par \par \uc2 You will see all the Romneys sitting in these chairs watching the speech, maybe not all the Romneys. You couldn't fit all the Romneys.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT, LATE NIGHT TALK SHOW HOST: Here he is pumping mustard into his bottle. Why is Bret Baier hoarding mustard?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I have heard others say that neither you nor your lawyers have been apprised that you are a target of the investigation. Is that true?\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Absolutely true.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Your current or former staff?\par \par \uc2 CLINTON: Absolutely not.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Your staff deleted nearly 32,000 emails.\par \par \uc2 CLINTON: Let me clarify this.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You don't remember?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Dude, this is like two years ago. We are still talking about the most mundane process.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It is the thing that everyone is talking about.\par \par \uc2 You were asked yesterday if you have ever spoken to \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: I have no comment on that. No comment. I was in Russia.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: One of the things people like about you is to answer any question.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Let's assume I did. Perhaps it was personal.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, so we can say --\par \par \uc2 You've mocked Mr. Trump's hand, you've made fun of his spelling. You called him a con artist. So what happened?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: There were places where the wall would not be realistic. He has evolved in the way he has looked at things.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And Mexico is not going to pay?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We could get the revenue from Mexico but not directly from their government.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I knew it was funded first by Republicans.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But that's not true.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I'm sorry?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That's not true, that the dossier that \uc2 Christopher Steele\uc2  was funded by the Republicans?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We don't tell social media companies to censor anything.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, the \uc2 Twitter\uc2  files suggest something different.\par \par \uc2 Is there criminal implications in what you were investigating when it comes to Hunter Biden?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Oh, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I really wish that people.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Do you think that was helpful for the American people?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Instead of quoting one sentence from it, if people actually read maybe an entire paragraph, it shows that we don't know if it's Russian.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You don't think it affected anything?\par \par \uc2 Why not just hand them over then.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Because I had boxes. I wanted to go through the boxes and get all my personal things out.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Do you ever get sick of hearing Ron De-sanctimonious?\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS, (R) FLORIDA: Not really. At the end of the day it is what it is.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You spoke today about how most of the country is kind of in the middle one way or the other.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Damn right, I believe that.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: If you were to characterize it, are you close?\par \par \uc2 MOHAMMED BIN SALMAN\uc2 , SAUDI CROWN PRINCE: Every day we get closer. For us the Palestinian issue is very important. We need to solve that part.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: How do you solve that part?\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2 , ISRAELI PRIME MINISTER: I think the Palestinians should be part of the process, but I don't think the Palestinians should have a veto over the process.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: What are you willing to agree to to get a peace deal?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What matters most is to save as many lives of our people as people.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: A top virologist warning you that the virus pointed to gain of function research and a lab leak. It does not sound like you are openminded to it.\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 ANTHONY FAUCI\uc2 , FORMER WHITE HOUSE CHIEF MEDICAL ADVISER: Bret, I know you are a good person. I have known you a long time.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Fair.\par \par \uc2 Fair.\par \par \uc2 Fair.\par \par \uc2 Balanced\par \par \uc2 And unafraid.\par \par \uc2 Have you ever seen the movie "Caddie Shack"?\par \par \uc2 DALAI LAMA: What?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And that's what it takes to get here.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Happy anniversary to Bret and the entire team here at SPECIAL REPORT, best team in the business.\par \par \uc2 Coming up Monday, Bret and Martha MacCallum will be live from Des Moines, Iowa, for a town hall with Republican presidential candidate Nikki Haley. Please join Shannon Bream this weekend for FOX NEWS SUNDAY with Texas Governor Greg Abbott, Denver Mayor Mike Johnson, and Oklahoma Senator \uc2 James Lankford\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Thanks for joining us on SPECIAL REPORT.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020240106ek1500001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The Upside Down World Of The Democratic Party\b0\par\par\uc2 Sean Hannity, Nat Foy, Jonathan Turley, Charlie Hurt, Kayleigh  McEnany, Lisa Boothe \par \uc2 7,658 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 January 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Hannity\par \uc2 SHAN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SEAN HANNITY\uc2 , FOX NEWS HOST: And welcome to HANNITY.\par \par \uc2 And also welcome to the upside down world of your liberal Democratic socialist party, where right is wrong, up is down, and pushing out Harvard's president for plagiarism and antisemitism is racist. This is a Democratic Party that unilaterally now removes certain candidates from ballots, and they call it democracy, a party obsessed with January 6th but still referring to the 574 riots in the summer of 2020 as mostly peaceful and a summer of love. A party that says your southern border is secure while allowing and enabling a non-stop wave, an unprecedented wave of illegal immigration. As a matter of fact, the most in history.\par \par \uc2 A party that blames the border crisis they claim doesn't exist, let's say, climate change, Texas Governor Abbott, President Trump, who himself secured the border. This is now a party that claims to care so much about disadvantage youth, but what, some associated with \uc2 Jeffrey Epstein\uc2  after he was convicted of abusing young girls from poor neighborhoods in Florida?\par \par \uc2 Now, in a moment, we're going to have much more on the upside down world of this liberal Democratic socialist party. But first, well, we get to the newest release from the Epstein case just earlier, not that long ago tonight. A second batch of court documents were unsealed.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now with all the details, our very own -- very own Nate Foy is with us.\par \par \uc2 Nate, what's the latest?\par \par \uc2 NATE FOY, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Sean, in this new batch of documents, lawyers for Virginia Giuffre and Ghislaine Maxwell discuss pictures of Maxwell at Chelsea Clinton's wedding as possible evidence in the 2015 civil case. \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  himself is again mentioned by Giuffre in an email, she wrote about a publisher labeled VF, presumably "Vanity Fair". It reads quote the email it does concern me what they want to write about me considering that B. Clinton walked into VF and threatened them not to write sex trafficking article about his good friend J.E. Clinton denies this claim through a spokesperson.\par \par \uc2 Attorney \uc2 Alan Dershowitz\uc2  is again mentioned in the documents, accused of having sex with an underage girl, a charge that Dershowitz strongly denies and tonight, he's coming out saying that there are smoking guns that have not been released yet.\par \par \uc2 With tonight's second batch, 59 documents are now unsealed. Giuffre's lawyer tells FOX News, 240 documents are coming in total. So we're about 25 percent of the way through. It could take until Monday before they're all released. We'll send it back to you, Sean.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. Nate Foy, thank you tonight.\par \par \uc2 Now, let's turn back if we can to the upside world of the Democratic socialist party. This very strange relationship that they have with democracy. Now, it is a word that that almost everyone on the left they love to repeat over and over and over again. They claim to cherish and revere democracy, both here at home and around the world. But, well, in reality, Democrats seem to love democracy in name only.\par \par \uc2 As Democratic professor Jonathan Turley points out, they're now moving to bar \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and other Republican candidates from ballots all over the country. In some cases, they're using activist judges who are unelected. In Maine, you have a far-left Biden loving secretary of state unilaterally removing \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  from the Republican primary ballot. Well, so much for letting the voters decide.\par \par \uc2 Now, the longtime Democrats said it was, quote, her duty to block Republicans from voting for Trump. Oh, one person decides.\par \par \uc2 And then in the great commonwealth of Pennsylvania, Democrats now there, they're attempting to remove Congressman Scott Perry from the ballot. And according to Jonathan Turley, the guy that filed the challenge is a former congressional candidate who made headlines burning MAGA flags with swastikas outside of court houses. Great resume.\par \par \uc2 In every case, a common theme, the word insurrection. It's a phrase used by Democrats, Trump haters over and over and over and over again, talking about January 6th. They always forget the words peacefully patriotically march to the Capitol so your voices will be heard. That always gets ignored. Now they're using insurrection as an excuse to ban \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , other Republicans from all future elections.\par \par \uc2 Now, none of these candidates, including former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  were ever convicted of insurrection. As a matter of fact, they were never charged and up to now have never been charged with insurrection. In other words, Democrats are trying to completely upend what is a real democratic process, one they claim to revere solely on the basis of their own political smear.\par \par \uc2 Now, this is what you would expect from the, what, communist party and the old Soviet Union. Not really from people that claim to revere democracy. Now, of course, many Democrats don't actually care about democracy. They just want power and perpetuity, one party, no checks, no balances and no dissent.\par \par \uc2 They believe deeply in their hearts that we the American people, we simply cannot be trusted and they -- we should not have any say in deciding the vote. Take a look at the 2024 Democratic primary, look at North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 With Biden's popularity at an all-time low, state officials there accepted a request from the Democratic Party to actually remove every candidate from the primary ballot except for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . What a great system. I applaud North Carolina. It's just a little too risky to actually have voters have another option. Does that seem like real democracy to you?\par \par \uc2 But this is just the beginning. Ultimately, Democrats would like to expand the Supreme Court. We know about that. They want to pack it with Democratic activists that would legislate from the bench. They would love to grant statehood to Washington, D.C. and Puerto Rico, they believe to secure four more Democratic U.S. senators and, of course, they would absolutely love to grant amnesty to 8.5 million new reliable voters who they aided and abetted in lawbreaking.\par \par \uc2 And now, they would grant the great gift of citizenship. But, of course, I'm sure they wouldn't expect votes and return for that great gift, would they? Not the people that dared to insist, you know, you come into the country legally. You're not going to vote for those people. The people that said follow the law, respect our borders, respect our sovereignty, respect our laws.\par \par \uc2 This is all about power in perpetuity, not democracy. So when you have a Democrat on fake news CNN or MSDNC proclaiming their love for democracy, just know they're nothing more than wolves and sheep's clothing.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, here with reaction, FOX News contributor and law professor Jonathan Turley is with us.\par \par \uc2 You know, you wrote a great piece on this earlier today, and you said it's only the latest effort targeting congressional candidates as Democrats seek to bar opponents as insurrectionists. Now, correct me if I'm wrong, has anybody been charged with insurrection that they're citing this word to get them off the ballot? Because I'm not unaware of anybody.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN TURLEY, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: No, they haven't, and insurrectionist is now the new sort of term. In McCarthy's period, you would have follow travelers of communists. Now, you have supporters of insurrection. And that is in fact the allegation against Representative Perry. It's in -- not that he was an insurrectionist, but that he supported it by challenging the election of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and supporting those allegations.\par \par \uc2 And the point about my raising that effort is that there is no limiting principle here that is people keep on talking about the effort to bar Trump. What they need to keep in mind is that years ago, dozens of Democrats went on the record saying that they wanted to bar many of their Republican colleagues from ever holding office again. There was a legislation that was put forward signed by dozens of Democrats, saying that they're all fellow travelers of insurrectionists. That's the same underlying theory under the 14th Amendment that is being used in the Trump case.\par \par \uc2 So this can be replicated throughout the country, and that's what makes this not just so untenable and unfounded, but so dangerous for our democracy.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. So let me ask you this. Explain why the 14th Amendment doesn't apply. I mean, just -- I don't know, 14th Amendment for dummies, if you will, for people that, you know, can't read the very plain language in it because it seems very obvious to me and I'm not the constitutional scholar that you are.\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: Well, many -- many of the appeals in states like Colorado and Maine have raised the fact that this amendment refers to a different oath that is taken not by the president by but other officeholders, but also refers to federal officials which they insist do not reference the president, but generally reference people appointed by the president, lower ranked officials.\par \par \uc2 My biggest objection is that the key of Section Three is -- are the words insurrection and rebellion. We have neither in January 6. January 6 was many things, none of them good, but I don't believe that that it was an insurrection, and neither do most the members of the public have been polled. They view this as what it was, which was a protest that became a riot.\par \par \uc2 And that's the reason you haven't had people charged with insurrection. You've had a relatively small number of people charged with seditious conspiracy, but that's interfering with official proceedings, you know? So there is this disconnect there and it -- but the other aspect about this which is troubling is that the advocates are saying that it is self- enforcing, that there's no act of Congress that is required.\par \par \uc2 So you can go to someone like the secretary of state in Maine, Bellows, who said right after January 6, that it was an insurrection and say, do you think this is an insurrection? And she said, yeah, and she then barred Trump from the ballot.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Yeah. Can you explain this to me? In the summer of 2020, we were told that all the riots we were witnessing before our very eyes, day after day, night after night, that killed a couple of dozen Americans injured thousands of police officers, they were being hit with bricks and rocks and bottles and Molotov cocktails, thousands of cops injured, billions of property damaged. We have a lot of videotape evidence.\par \par \uc2 Those were riots. I condemned in real time January 6th on my radio show that night on this very TV show. I said that can't happen in our country, but nor can what happen in the summer of 2020.\par \par \uc2 Why has there never been any interest at all whatsoever with all the evidence available, video evidence of people committing crimes and rioting, why was there never any interest in that?\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: Well, that's the danger of this theory, is that it is so subjective for people like Bellows. You know, keep in mind, Democrat members used this very same law to oppose the certification of Republican presidents. There were opposition to certification in Congress when there were riots in Washington, violent riots.\par \par \uc2 Now, were all of those people rebels and insurrectionists? No, those were protests. They were not well-founded. I didn't think that the objections to the Biden certification were well-founded, I said at the time. But that doesn't mean that it's a rebellion, and that that's the danger that the Supreme Court's now going to have to look at.\par \par \uc2 This is -- this could metastasize throughout the country. You know, Sean, this country is the most successful democracy in history. It is the most stable democracy in history, and what these advocates have done through this blind rage, is to introduce an instability that hasn't existed before. It is to put us on a slippery slope, to political chaos, if we adopt this theory.\par \par \uc2 That's why the Supreme Court needs to act with finality and I hope unanimity.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Yeah, I hope so, too. I would -- I would anticipate it, but you know what? You can never predict those things as you know better than anybody. Jonathan Turley, great to have you. Thank you. Happy New Year.\par \par \uc2 Now as we predicted on Tuesday, all right, Biden's first ad for the 2024 campaign, it's not about all his great accomplishments, oh, that's right, he doesn't have any. Instead, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is dusting off -- let's see -- \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , January 6, January 6, January 6. They'll get to abortion, but this is the first ad. It has nothing to do with Biden or success because he doesn't have any. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I've made the preservation of American democracy the central issue of my presidency. I believe in free and fair elections and the right to vote fairly and have your vote counted. There's something dangerous happened in America. There's an extremist movement who does not share the basic beliefs in our democracy.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Could they get a \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  moment where maybe he doesn't sound like he's mumbling and mumbling?\par \par \uc2 Anyway, the ad was released after a Democratic activist posing as historians met with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  at the White House this week, reportedly imploring him to be more hostile against Republicans. Oh, he's not hostile enough?\par \par \uc2 Now, they can't tout a single Bide