ccused of any wrongdoing.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, here with reaction, Tennessee Senator \uc2 Marsha Blackburn\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Well, according to one of the victims who writing a book and an excerpt showing up in "The Daily Mail", it suggests that \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  was there. I guess time will prove that.\par \par \uc2 I'm hearing last minute that there might have been a delay. One of the John Doe's, quote, claiming that the mention of their name may bring them physical harm. Have you heard that, Senator?\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARSHA BLACKBURN (R-TN): We have not heard that but, Sean, I would expect that that is what is happening and thank you for continuing to bring attention to this issue. You know, this is not a list that anyone wants their name to be on, and there are going to be people there, on this list that are accused of wrongdoing. There are going to be those on the list who are accusing Epstein and Maxwell of wrongdoing and there be people that were witnesses to some of this activity but did not report that activity.\par \par \uc2 So I'm certain that \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  and other people are very nervous. They don't want to be on this list. There are people that are going to be ashamed that they are and he's probably one of them.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: The only one I could think of that might be on the list is \uc2 Alan Dershowitz\uc2  who was an attorney. Everybody has a right to counsel. He did have charges brought up against him and he's been adamant in his denial of any wrongdoing or any knowledge of even the things that have happened there.\par \par \uc2 Why is this important to get out to the public in your view?\par \par \uc2 BLACKBURN: You know, it is so important that we figure out what happened with this sex trafficking ring, this human trafficking ring. You've been talking about the border and the cartels and the amount of human trafficking.\par \par \uc2 And, Sean, we know that human trafficking and sex trafficking has gone from being about a $500 million a year business a few years ago, to upwards of $150 billion a year. This is modern-day slavery. There are women and children that need this protection. This is not about celebrity. It's about criminal activity it's getting to the bottom of what is going on and seeing who was affiliated and associated with \uc2 Jeffrey Epstein\uc2 , so we break these sex trafficking rings.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Yeah. All right. Senator, we'll stay on it. I promise.\par \par \uc2 All right. More "Hannity" from the -- our new home, the free state of Florida as we continue.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: And, unfortunately, that's all the time we have left. Thank you for joining us from our new permanent home, the free state of Florida. Please set your DVR, time never changes, 9:00 Eastern and join us. Don't ever miss an episode. Set your DVR.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, let not your heart be troubled, Greg Gutfeld standing by to put a smile on your face. Have a great night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document SHAN000020240103ek1200001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Hamas\uc2  Rejects Peace Proposal From Egypt; Congressional Border Talks To Resume Wednesday; \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  Claims Immunity In Election Interference Case; Backlash Grows Against Chicago's Sanctuary City Status; Fraternal Order Of Police Calls It A Slap In The Face; E.V. Owners Receive More Than $48K Over A Decade; Subsidy Supporters Claim Damage Reduction Is A Benefit; China Halts Rare Earth Technology Exports; U.S. Based Companies Making Efforts To Develop \uc2 Rare Earth Minerals\uc2  In Competition With China; U.S. Senate Election Map Favors Republicans In 2024; Democrats Likely To Press Abortion Issue In Upcoming Elections; President Biden Has Conversation With Israeli Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  Regarding Ongoing Israeli-\uc2 Hamas\uc2  War\b0\par\par\uc2 Rich Edson, Trey Yingst, Chad Pergram, David Spunt, Garrett  Tenney, Alicia Acuna, William La Jeunesse, Bret Baier, Aishah Hasnie, Jason  Riley \par \uc2 7,409 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 December 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: A lot of work.\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Yes, all right, there we go.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Merry Christmas, Greg.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: That's it for us. Thanks for joining us, Merry Christmas everyone.\par \par \uc2 RICH EDSON, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Good evening. Welcome to New York. I'm Rich Edson in for Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 Former President Trump's legal team is asking a federal appeals court to drop the election interference case, arguing that he is immune to prosecution. We'll have the latest on that.\par \par \uc2 And the fight for the Senate will be one of the biggest stories of 2024. We'll take a closer look.\par \par \uc2 Plus, whatever happened to those nationwide protests over the death of \uc2 George Floyd\uc2  in 2020? Some protesters got a major payday.\par \par \uc2 But first, breaking tonight, \uc2 Hamas\uc2  has reportedly rejected an Egyptian led peace proposal that would have required the terrorist organization to cede power in its place, a Palestinian Parliament formed after free elections. Negotiations are at an impasse again, \uc2 Hamas\uc2  says it will release more hostages if Israel stops its attacks. Israeli Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  vowed Sunday to keep fighting.\par \par \uc2 All this as \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2  delivered his Christmas message, calling for peace in the world and an end to the military operations in Gaza. Correspondent Trey Yingst is in Tel Aviv tonight. Hi, Trey.\par \par \uc2 TREY YINGST, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Rich, good evening, it's day 80 of the war between Israel and \uc2 Hamas\uc2  as the Middle East marks the Christmas holiday.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 YINGST (voice over): It was a dark and dreary Christmas Eve in Bethlehem, no festive music or decorations. Normally, there are thousands of visitors to the traditional birthplace of Jesus. But not this year amid the backdrop of an ongoing war.\par \par \uc2 Rana Bishara, I came to Bethlehem tonight to deliver a message of peace in times of war, and bombing and killing. My message here to stop the war. First of all, stop the war now.\par \par \uc2 YINGST (voice over): An Egyptian proposal this weekend to stop the fighting in Gaza was swiftly rejected by \uc2 Hamas\uc2  and Islamic Jihad, who have ruled out any deal that includes a change of leadership in Gaza.\par \par \uc2 With more than 100 hostages still being held in the Strip, Israel has vowed to continue their offensive despite heavy losses on the battlefield. Israeli forces say 17 of their soldiers were killed this weekend alone.\par \par \uc2 Israel's Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  visited troops today in northern Gaza. Netanyahu calling on his forces to finish the job of killing or capturing \uc2 Hamas\uc2  leadership.\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2 , ISRAELI PRIME MINISTER (through translator): We are not stopping. Whoever talks about stopping, there is no such thing. We are not stopping, the war will continue until the end, until we finish them, no less. I know that your left your families and that your families support you. That is amazing. That is the power, the key to victory.\par \par \uc2 YINGST (voice over): The Israeli air campaign against Gaza left hundreds of Palestinians dead in new strikes over the weekend according to the \uc2 Hamas\uc2  run health ministry.\par \par \uc2 Still, a small group of Christian Palestinians gathered on Christmas Eve in the southern city of Rafah.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 YINGST (on camera): One Palestinian in Gaza today telling Fox News that more evacuation orders have been given and residents in the central part of the Strip are now moving further south, Rich.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Trey, there's more tension along the northern border tonight?\par \par \uc2 YINGST: Absolutely, Israeli forces in the north remain on high alert. Earlier today, there was an airstrike outside of Damascus, Syria and Sayyed Razi Mousavi, a senior IRGC commander was killed, and the Iranians say there will be a response. We don't know what that response will be. But there are concerns in the Israeli military have renewed rocket fire from Lebanon and Syria, Rich.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Trey Yingst live for us in Tel Aviv late tonight. Trey, thank you.\par \par \uc2 As we mentioned, \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2  focused his Christmas Day message on World Peace specifically targeting the weapons industry and its, "instruments of death" that he said fuels wars.\par \par \uc2 The Pope lamented \uc2 Hamas\uc2 's attack in southern Israel on October 7th, but also called on Israel to end its war in Gaza in what he called an appalling harvest of innocent civilians.\par \par \uc2 President Biden called members from each branch of the U.S. military to wish them a Merry Christmas today. Meantime, some protesters are choosing to spend the holiday outside the home of the White House National Security Adviser Jake Sullivan, as the President faces more blowback over his support for Israel.\par \par \uc2 Lawmakers are now home for the holidays. But talks on a border security and immigration deal that are also key to unlocking more aid for Ukraine are set to resume Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Senior Congressional Correspondent Chad Pergram has the latest from the nation's capital.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Both parties now fixated with fixing the border.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2  (R-SC): I am trying to secure a border that's broken.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The borders a mess.\par \par \uc2 SEN. RON JOHNSON (R-WI): We're getting numb to the numbers.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): But all talk and no action before Christmas left negotiators without a deal.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY): Border Security Policy is complex.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): Fights over parole, migration caps and asylum vexed bipartisan Senate negotiators before the holiday. Talks resumed Wednesday after a Christmas pause.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY): We will not abandon our principles on border. But we will work to find common ground wherever we can.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): Democrats resisted addressing border security for years.\par \par \uc2 REP. DARRELL ISSA (R-CA): They're now beginning to feel the heat from their own party.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): Democrats now paying attention.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (D-PA): To look at the numbers. It's almost 270,000 encounters. And that's a lot. It's not xenophobic to have a conversation about border security.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): But Republicans view the democratic conversion about the border as political.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: The only thing they see as a threat to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's reelection.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): No border security also means no money for Israel and Ukraine as the issues are intertwined. President Biden is trying to thread the needle on all three issues while facing an uproar about the Middle East crisis.\par \par \uc2 There is now a democratic chasm between those who support Israel and liberals who say Israel has gone too far. That's why the left demands limits on aid destined for Israel. Border talks could collide with a rush to avoid a government shutdown in mid-January, plus a House GOP push to impeach the president.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (on camera): A government shutdown likely sideswipes, border security efforts, it could also overwhelm the impeachment inquiry, Rich.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Chad, talked a little bit more about that government funding fight because you know, we're all talking about this supplemental, which would be aid to Israel, aid to Ukraine in exchange for border security measures. But you know, you've got this major government funding fight just to keep the lights on.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: We're told that they probably would not connect the supplemental to any effort to keep the government open. We are looking at 25 days from now until the government runs out of funding. That is the first deadline. The second one is February 2nd. Ironically, that is Groundhog Day. Rich?\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Isn't it always. Chad Pergram. Chad, thank you.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Presidential election year is just days away and what will likely impact voters the most might not happen on the campaign trail but in the courts. Correspondent David Spunt is in Washington with the latest.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  making a late Saturday pitch to a federal appeals court, hoping the judges will throw out charges relating to attempts to overturn the 2020 presidential election.\par \par \uc2 In the filing, Trump's team calls his criminal indictment unlawful and unconstitutional. Writing, President Trump has absolute immunity from prosecution for his official acts as President. The indictment alleges only official acts so it must be dismissed.\par \par \uc2 The filing comes a day after the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  declined to fast track the case, a temporary blow for special counsel Jack Smith, who wants to take Trump to trial on March 4th, one day before Super Tuesday. That date is expected to slide.\par \par \uc2 BILL BARR, FORMER U.S. ATTORNEY GENERAL: His strategy is to run out the clock.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (voice over): Trump's former Attorney General Bill Barr tells Fox News that absolute immunity may not extend to the former president.\par \par \uc2 BARR: What was the function he was performing when he was doing these things after the election? And the government's position is going to be that he was a candidate, he was acting as a candidate, not really as president.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (voice over): It's one of four state and federal prosecutions Trump faces with possible trials in the middle of the election season. His successor also taking a swipe over the weekend.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Mr. President, can you think of a president, any president is absolutely immune from criminal prosecution? Any president at all?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I can t thik of one.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (voice over): But legal challenges also plagued the Biden family as the president's son prepares for next month's arraignment on federal tax charges, including three felonies. \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  also faces felony gun charges in Delaware.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (on camera): The president faces a separate special counsel investigation into his handling of classified documents while vice president, Rich.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: David Spunt in D.C. David, thank you.\par \par \uc2 The state of Illinois is creating more room for migrants opening up more than 200 hotel rooms to asylum seekers in the Windy City. Correspondent Garrett Tenney is in Chicago tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 RICHARD IRVIN\uc2 , MAYOR OF AURORA, ILLINOIS: I hope Chicago would make good on their promises to take care of the migrants. But again, Chicago made this promise, that can't be responsibility put on the city of \uc2 Aurora\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GARRETT TENNEY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Officials in Chicago suburbs are growing frustrated over the fallout they're getting from Chicago's sanctuary city status after Mayor Brandon Johnson tried to slow the flow of migrants coming to his city by creating tougher penalties for what he calls rogue operators, buses that dropped migrants off without coordinating with the city.\par \par \uc2 Texas has responded by busing migrants to the suburbs which are in turn sending them to the Windy City, saying, we don't want to deal with Chicago's problem.\par \par \uc2 IRVIN: Based on them opening their arms and saying that they're a welcoming city. We are going to forward folks because that's their final destination, to Chicago.\par \par \uc2 TENNEY (voice over): But a growing number of suburbs are also passing their own ordinances to try to keep migrants from being dropped off or housed in their towns.\par \par \uc2 Chicago has struggled to meet the demand for housing as it continues to welcome migrants and is now under fire for the kind of living conditions it's putting them in.\par \par \uc2 This past week, a 5-year-old boy and several other children and adults were hospitalized from one of the shelters. But when the mayor was asked about it, he laid the blame for the migrant crisis squarely on Texas Governor Greg Abbott.\par \par \uc2 BRANDON JOHNSON, MAYOR OF CHICAGO: We have a governor, a governor, an elected official in the state of Texas that is placing families on buses without shoes, cold, wet, tired, hungry, afraid traumatize.\par \par \uc2 The governor of Texas needs to take a look in the mirror of the chaos that he is causing for this country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TENNEY (on camera): Mayor Johnson has lots of criticism for how Governor Abbott is dealing with the record number of migrants who are crossing into Texas, but very little for the White House and the president's policies that are allowing it to happen, Rich.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Garrett Tenney in Chicago. Garrett, thank you. Markets are closed today in observation of Christmas.\par \par \uc2 Up next hour, Whatever Happened To segment on protesters getting major payouts after the death of \uc2 George Floyd\uc2  in 2020.\par \par \uc2 First, here's what some of our Fox affiliates around the country are covering tonight. Fox 31 in Denver as a Christmas Eve shooting resulted in the death of one man and three people injured at a mall in Colorado Springs. Police say they detained multiple people during the incident.\par \par \uc2 Fox 35 in Orlando in a story straight out of "Home Alone 2" as criticism mounts for \uc2 Spirit Airlines\uc2 . A 6- year-old boy ended up at the wrong airport. His grandmother said she drove 3-1/2 hours to pick them up at the airport in Orlando when he was supposed to arrive in Fort Myers. The grandmother says she has not heard from the airline except for an apology via text.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Detroit from WJBK where Christmas came early for the Motor City as their \uc2 NFL\uc2  team the Lions are headed to the playoffs after clinching the NFC North Division title Sunday. It'll be the Lion's first home playoff game in decades and the first ever at Ford Field.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look outside the Beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 EDSON: "FOX WEATHER ALERT", people traveling in the central U.S. may be contending with blizzard conditions caused by a low-pressure system that's already caused problems in the Southern California area last week.\par \par \uc2 This comes as residents in Chicago are experiencing one of the 10th warmest Christmas days in history, and also one of the wettest.\par \par \uc2 In tonight's "WHATEVER HAPPENED TO" segment, some protesters who demonstrated after the death of \uc2 George Floyd\uc2  in 2020 are thousands of dollars richer.\par \par \uc2 The head of the nation's largest police union says cops were the ones who faced brutality. Sorting it all out tonight, senior correspondent Alicia Acuna.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ALICIA ACUNA, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR CORRESPONDENT (voice over): From California, to Colorado, to New York.\par \par \uc2 ELENA COHEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT, \uc2 NATIONAL LAWYERS GUILD\uc2  COUNCIL: This is, we believe the largest settlement in terms of amount of money to people impacted for protesters ever in this country.\par \par \uc2 ACUNA: More than three years after police protests following \uc2 George Floyd\uc2 's death in 2020, legal battles are now being settled and protesters are getting big payouts.\par \par \uc2 DARA PLUCHINO, PLAINTIFF: I am still triggered seeing police.\par \par \uc2 ACUNA: New York City is set to pay, at least $35 million for settlements citing police misconduct, with suing protesters, each receiving between 10 and 20 grand. The Fraternal Order of Police calls it a slap in the face to officers.\par \par \uc2 JOE GAMALDI, NATIONAL VICE PRESIDENT, FRATERNAL ORDER OF POLICE: During those riots, we were getting smashed in the head with bricks. We were having feces, urine, Molotov cocktails thrown on us. I mean, they burned our cities down.\par \par \uc2 ACUNA: Nearly 20 cities have settled class action or lawsuits. For example, Philadelphia at $9.25 million; Austin, $14 million; Denver, more than $20 million. San Jose, more than $3 million so far.\par \par \uc2 Legal experts say cities could have paid even more if they went to trial.\par \par \uc2 AYA GRUBER, PROFESSOR OF LAW, USC GOULD SCHOOL OF LAW: If you get in front of a jury. The jury is going to award a bigger award that if you were to come to an agreement before going to trial.\par \par \uc2 ACUNA: Another reason, legal experts say, is publicity. Cities would rather deal with paying settlements than months of bad headlines.\par \par \uc2 And while many municipalities did settle, they did not admit fault.\par \par \uc2 GAMALDI: You've set a date dangerous precedent in this country that if you're willing to sue cities and departments for police officers, who have done nothing wrong, the city is likely just going to pay out, just to make you go away.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Go home.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: (INAUDIBLE)\par \par \uc2 GRUBER: That he's going to come out and say and maybe even the higher up police brass are going to say, we're going to change a lot of our tactics, and then, they'll come the next protest. And it goes to the same tactics because things didn't really change.\par \par \uc2 ACUNA (voice over): Places like New York City agreed to change how police respond to protests, but it remains to be seen if change will happen.\par \par \uc2 In Denver, Alicia Acuna, Fox News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Up next, how you may be funding the electric car revolution even without owning one.\par \par \uc2 First, "BEYOND OUR BORDERS" tonight. Ukrainian troops say they've shot down two Russian fighter jets on Christmas Eve. A move, President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  says sets the right mood for the entire year ahead.\par \par \uc2 In a break with Russian tradition. Today is also the first time Ukraine has observed the Christmas holiday, December 25th, instead of January 7th.\par \par \uc2 Russian opposition leader \uc2 Alexei Navalny\uc2  is now at a prison colony above the Arctic Circle. That word comes after almost three weeks of no contact. Navalny is serving a 19-year sentence on charges of extremism.\par \par \uc2 Nearly 300 Indian citizens traveling to Central America have returned to India, after a four-day ordeal that left them stranded at a French airport over suspicions of human trafficking.\par \par \uc2 Prosecutors would not reveal their final destination. The incident comes amid a surge of Indians crossing the Mexico-U.S. border.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Earth from the International Space Station. One of the big stories here tonight, \uc2 NASA\uc2  releases a new image revealing a cluster of young stars that look like a Christmas tree decorated with ornaments.\par \par \uc2 The stars are 1-1/2 million years old, but that's still pretty young compared to the sun's 5 billion years.\par \par \uc2 Just some of our other stories, "BEYOND OUR BORDERS" tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 EDSON: A live look at Fox Square there, and a pretty popular Christmas tree. Sixth Avenue, New York City. It is crowded and it has been crowded all day. And certainly, that is the case here on Christmas night.\par \par \uc2 Well, many cannot afford an electric car but that doesn't mean you aren't helped pay for it. The car, the charging network, power lines, electricity, all funded by tax and utility ratepayers. A new report says Americans will collectively pay almost $50,000 over the next decade in hidden subsidies, and credits that most of us will never see.\par \par \uc2 Senior national correspondent William La Jeunesse reports from Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM LA JEUNESSE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Most drivers don't own an E.V., but a new study shows Americans subsidize, everyone that's built, almost $50,000 over a decade.\par \par \uc2 JASON ISAAC, DIRECTOR, TEXAS PUBLIC POLICY FOUNDATION: Those costs are being borne by ratepayers and taxpayers and people that buy internal combustion engines are heavily subsidizing the cost of an electric vehicle.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: How? State and federal subsidies cost almost $9,000 per vehicle. Charging stations power plants transmission lines to handle the added capacity? 15,000. Add in \uc2 EPA\uc2  credits and incentives for zero emission vehicles.\par \par \uc2 So, unlike those who own gas powered cars, E.V. owners receive in total subsidies and benefits worth more than $48,000 over a decade.\par \par \uc2 ISAAC: If you buy a gasoline or diesel engine, part of the cost of that vehicle includes these credits to help lower the cost of an electric vehicle.\par \par \uc2 ANTONIO BENTO, PROFESSOR, UNIVERSITY OF SOUTHERN CALIFORNIA: Subsidizing electric vehicles at a minimum, allows us to reduce all the damage that we do on a daily basis through the tailpipe emissions.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE (voice over): Critics call the study misleading because it doesn't measure the benefits of grid upgrades nor the drawbacks of pollution and human health and the environment.\par \par \uc2 BENTO: Our purchases of gasoline certainly do not reflect the cost that we are generating for society.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE (on camera): So, the bottom line is, E.V.s are the future. But the transition will not be cheap. As taxpayers and ratepayers, collectively, you're going to pay for the revolution, whether you buy an E.V. or not. Rich?\par \par \uc2 EDSON: William La Jeunesse for us in Los Angeles. William, Thank you.\par \par \uc2 China is halting exports of rare earth processing technologies. Beijing announced the move, claiming it was protecting its national security and public interest.\par \par \uc2 It comes as US companies are working to increase mining, refining and manufacturing capabilities.\par \par \uc2 My colleague, Bret Baier, takes a look at efforts to develop those technologies domestically.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Thanks, Rich.\par \par \uc2 In our past installments looking at rare earth elements, we explained how China has grown to dominate the industry. Now, the U.S. is working on new ways to build back some of its domestic supply chain.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, we learn about the innovative ways the U.S. companies are working to increased production, while maintaining clean energy standards.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT DUNN, NOVEON MAGNETICS CEO: Foreign dependency is real. It's actually not getting better. A lot of years and a lot of dollars have to really be invested in this space.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Noveon Magnetics is helping increase domestic rare earth magnet production with a sustainable shortcut.\par \par \uc2 DUNN: We sort of just bypass that entire chain. We take waste magnetic material directly into magnet manufacturing and deliver a finished product, which is really what the market needs.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Less than one percent of the world's rare earth elements are recycled, with most ending up in landfills.\par \par \uc2 DUNN: They're a little bit tough to handle. They exist in lower weight percentage compared to other metals that may be more available for recovery.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Noveon CEO Scott Dunn says his company is working to reverse the supply chain on China by using old magnets to manufacture new ones to be used in cars, medical equipment, and renewable power generation.\par \par \uc2 DUNN: We actually take apart motors and generators and things like that. Old hybrid vehicles can become magnetic material for new next generation hybrid vehicles.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Recycling rare earth magnets helps bypass several steps in a process that faces strict environmental regulations, including mining, separation, and alloy manufacturing.\par \par \uc2 GOV. GREG ABBOTT, (R) TEXAS: With the help of businesses like Noveon, Texas is number one in the United States for the most new jobs.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Earlier this year, Texas Governor Greg Abbott gave his State of the State address from Noveon's facility in San Marcos. Dunn says while Texas has been good for manufacturing, he believes more should be done to help U.S. companies increase production as demand for magnets increases.\par \par \uc2 DUNN: Once upon a time rare earth magnets were really just an application that made sense for the hard disk drive. As we move basically further and forward towards electric, high tech, low carbon technology, the magnet is playing an increasing role in all of those technologies pretty much across the board.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Rare earth mining company, U.S. Critical Materials and the \uc2 Idaho National Laboratory\uc2  are focusing on filling the gap whether it comes to another part of the rare earth supply chain.\par \par \uc2 EDWARD COWLE, U.S. CRITICAL MATERIALS: What we are going to use is cutting edge technology.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The \uc2 Idaho National Laboratory\uc2  is one of 17 Energy Department labs. It focuses on nuclear research, renewable energy, and security solutions.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT FOX, \uc2 IDAHO NATIONAL LABORATORY\uc2 : China has a significant amount of not only production capacity but separation refining capacity. It's not that we have to surpass them. We simply need to be able to fairly compete against the global monopoly.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Scientists will spend eight months working to develop new environmentally friendly ways to separate and process rare earth materials.\par \par \uc2 FOX: Environmental laws have become more stringent over the years to maintain a clean and healthy and diverse environment. How do we develop a technology that allows for resource recovery or manufacturing to be environmentally compliant?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: They will use ore from U.S. Critical Materials Sheep Creek mine to develop the new refining capabilities.\par \par \uc2 COWLE: We sent about 70 pounds of our ore to Idaho National Lab six months ago so that they could determine if this was a project that they could do.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Sheep Creek is currently operating under exploration permit. U.S. Critical Materials says its area has high amounts of rare earth elements. The company hopes to request a drilling permit next year and says they expect to be able to fast track the permitting process.\par \par \uc2 COWLE: Rather than drilling, we actually have a bird's eye view.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: With domestic innovation and the help of government funding, U.S. companies hope to put pressure on China's dominance.\par \par \uc2 DUNN: I think America is capable of more and more of that as long as some of the right political and economic incentive is addressed.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER (on camera): Coal often contains a variety of rare earth elements. The Energy Department has invested millions into projects that will establish manufacturing to separate those elements from more than 250 billion tons of coal reserves.\par \par \uc2 We'll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Thanks, Bret.\par \par \uc2 Up next, why there is optimism in the Republican Party over flipping the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And, later, the panel on the prospects of a peace deal in the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Much of the focus in 2024 will be on the presidential election and a possible rematch between Presidents Biden and Trump. But no matter who wins the White House, they will need control of the Congress if they want to get much done. Tonight, we preview some of the key races that might dictate the balance of power in the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 . Here is congressional correspondent Aishah Hasnie.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JON TESTER\uc2 , (D-MT): I know the Montana that's worth protecting because I have lived it every day.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: The race for control of the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  is heating up, and the 2024 map is shaping up to be a favorable one for Republicans. Thirty-three seats are up for grabs, 20 Democrats, three independents who caucus with them, and 10 Republicans. The GOP needs to flip just two to take back control without winning the White House.\par \par \uc2 One of those is likely West Virginia, expected to turn red after Democrat Senator \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  announced his retirement. Then any of these seven competitive races could tip the scales. That includes Montana, t toss up state where the three-term Democrat \uc2 Jon Tester\uc2  is casting himself as a Washington outsider. Ohio is also considered a toss-up where incumbent Democrat \uc2 Sherrod Brown\uc2  is within single digits of all three Republican challengers. There's also battleground states where former President Trump won like Pennsylvania, where Democrat Senator Bob Casey likely faces former hedge fund CEO \uc2 Dave McCormick\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 If the GOP's disastrous performance in 2022 is a roadmap, the best chance for vulnerable Dems is facing controversial Republican candidates. That's why "Axios" reports the new chair of the Senate Republican's campaign arm is seeking conservative candidates who are acceptable to former President Trump but not so far right that GOP moderates rejects them.\par \par \uc2 How involved is the former president in helping to pick those candidates?\par \par \uc2 JASON THIELMAN, NATIONAL REPUBLICAN SENATORIAL COMMITTEE: He is very engaged. I tell Democrats be careful what you wish for, because I think pro-Trump candidates are candidates that can win in a general election.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: But some on the left warning Democrats to focus on their own issues.\par \par \uc2 SEN. PETER WELCH, (D-VT): We have got to really connect with working class folks. The economy is coming back for a lot, but for a lot of other people it isn't.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: On Capitol Hill, I'm Aishah Hasnie, FOX News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 EDSON: And that's where we are going to start our panel. Daniel Lippman, "Politico" White House and Washington reporter, Julia Manchester, the national politics reporter for "The Hill," and Jason Riley, "Wall Street Journal" columnist and senior fellow at the Manhattan Institute.\par \par \uc2 Daniel, first to you. Aishah began that piece talking about Senator Tester, just one of the many Democrats running in a state that former President Trump won a couple of times, won in 16 and won in 2020. Is this map navigable by Democrats, and how do they do it?\par \par \uc2 DANIEL LIPPMAN, "POLITICO": I think Democrats are hoping that their saving grace would be that in a presidential year turnout is higher, and they can find -- get some of those younger voters who are disaffected by Gaza to turn out and say that if they do keep the Democratic Senate blue, then that could serve as a potential check on a second Trump administration, because the second Trump administration is going to want to pull out of \uc2 NATO\uc2 , make the civil service political. And so that can be kind of a break in the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: You know, Julia, we just came off a campaign season in Virginia where you heard a number of times, keep the House, flip the Senate. That's what Republicans were trying to do with Governor \uc2 Glenn Youngkin\uc2 . They did neither. So what does that election and that race say about national race or series of national races?\par \par \uc2 JULIA MANCHESTER, NATIONAL POLITICS REPORTER, "THE HILL": Yes, so in terms for the series of national races that we're going to see at the House and Senate level going into 2024, I think Republicans need to be cognizant of the fact that Democrats are going to be campaigning on the issue of abortion and abortion access. That's what they did in Virginia, and they were able to do it successfully. We saw that Republicans in Virginia sort of experimented with this idea of a 15-week limit whereas Democrats would say a 15-week ban on the procedure. That didn't work out for Republicans in Virginia.\par \par \uc2 So I think we can expect to see Democrats continue to replicate that strategy across the country, but Republicans need to rework on abortion. If the economy, if it continues to be on the uptick, that's going to be good for Democrats, good for President Biden. But if we going to continue to see inflation, if there's more pain at the grocery store and people's wallets, that's going to be a good sign for Republicans that they can sort of latch on to. But if Democrats are talking about abortion, that's going to be difficult for them.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Jason, in the 2022 midterms, Republicans were disappointed. They were disappointed in Virginia just last month. How do they break the streak here? When you look at the Senate map, it looks really good for them. There's not a really high bar when you look at some of the competitive races that you have there. Can they clear that hurdle, can they reclaim the Senate?\par \par \uc2 JASON RILEY, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, they were disappointed, true. But they still took control of the House of Representatives.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Very true.\par \par \uc2 RILEY: Which is one reason we have an impeachment inquiry going on right now. I would think that would be one of the more immediate concerns if Republicans also took control of the Senate, that the impeachment inquiry in the House wouldn't just be a formality. If Republicans can control the Senate, then we are talking about a serious issue here, because the Senate is where the trial would be held.\par \par \uc2 So it's a very, very big deal whether or not Republicans are able to take advantage of this map. And this map does really advantage them. I'm particularly looking at that race in Ohio where you have an incumbent, \uc2 Sherrod Brown\uc2 , seeking a fourth term. And he -- this is expected to be a very close race. Ohio is a state that has been trending Republican. \uc2 Obama\uc2  won it twice, then Trump won it twice. And you see in how Brown is campaign that he is concerned. For instance, he is running ads about securing the border. No, Ohio is not a southern border state, but clearly this is an issue where Democrats are vulnerable. And so, it's one of the races I'm keeping an eye on, and it's one of the issues, immigration, that I'm keeping an eye on. Yes, abortion will be out there. And we have seen Democrats take advantage of that. But there are other issues going forward with an unpopular president at the top of the ticket. And some of these Democrats in vulnerable states will have to pay attention to that.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Jason mentioned that he is watching Ohio, a third of the Senate is up. Daniel, what are you watching?\par \par \uc2 LIPPMAN: The race that I think is the most interesting is the three-way race potential in Arizona where you have \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2  who became an independent, left the Democratic Party recently. And so she is kind of on an island of her own. She hasn't declared for reelection, and she is facing Kari Lake on the Republican side who hasn't really moderated since her gubernatorial run last year, and also \uc2 Ruben Gallego\uc2  on the Democratic side who is raising a lot of money, is a Marine combat veteran. And so he feels -- they feel pretty confident in the Gallego camp that they can beat Sinema who may want to kind of, you know, feels like she didn't have any allies right now.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: All right, Julia, what are you watching?\par \par \uc2 MANCHESTER: I'm watching Michigan. This is a state that has said to be -- leans towards Democrats, at least when we are looking at the Senate race. It's the race to replace outgoing Senator \uc2 Debbie Stabenow\uc2 . But I think it's interesting from the Republican and Democratic perspective. When we look at the Republican primary that's forming in Michigan it's fascinating, because you have more pro-Trump candidates like former Detroit police chief James Craig running. And then you have former Congressman Pete Meijer who voted to impeach former President Trump and essentially lost his job in a primary in Michigan because of that decision to impeach Trump.\par \par \uc2 And then, of course, once we go to the general election, it's likely that Congresswoman \uc2 Elissa Slotkin\uc2  will be at the top of the ticket on the Senate race for Democrats, but the presidential race is really going to impact this Senate race. And to go to a point that Daniel made at the beginning of the segment in terms of Gaza and how that impacts -- the war in Gaza and how that impacts these races, Michigan has a very big Arab American population. So, whatever happens overseas in this war could impact Michigan races up and down the ballot.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Yes, really great point there. We will get into that and a little bit more.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel with the latest on the war in Israel and President Biden's to-do list. Plus, volunteers spreading Christmas cheer.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Did you tell him not to strike \uc2 Hamas\uc2  in a preemptive strike?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I -- I had a long talk with Netanyahu today, and it was a private conversation.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Mr. President, did you ask for a ceasefire today?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I had a private conversation. I did not ask for a ceasefire.\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2 , ISRAELI PRIME MINISTER (through translator): We are not stopping. Whoever talks about stopping, there is no such thing. We are not stopping. The war will continue until the end.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EDSON: And we are back with our panel on this very issue. And the readout from the White House that we got from the president's conversation with Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2 , quote, "The president emphasized the critical need to protect the civilian population, including those supporting the humanitarian aid operation and the importance of allowing civilians to move safely away from areas of ongoing fighting. The leaders discussed the importance of securing the release of all remaining hostages."\par \par \uc2 Jason, to you. How much pressure is the White House getting not only domestically from his own party but internationally as well to push Israel to ramp down this assault on Gaza?\par \par \uc2 RILEY: Well, the first thing that's remarkable about the clip you showed is how out of sorts \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  looks. This does not look like a president who is in control, exerting authority in this situation. He looks like someone very much on his heels. And that sends a statement to the world. And it gets to the situation going on in Israel.\par \par \uc2 Israel needs to be able to defend itself. \uc2 Hamas\uc2  has made it clear that its ultimately objective is the elimination of Israel. And so any sort of ceasefire short of stripping \uc2 Hamas\uc2  of any control of Gaza I think is unacceptable on its face. That is not something -- we are not looking for a ceasefire just for a ceasefire. We are looking for a ceasefire that's not going to leave us in a situation we'll have to face two, three, four years from now. And that's the goal here, and the U.S. should support Israel in its objective to eliminate \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . And that should be what we are supporting and what America should allow Israel to do.\par \par \uc2 And our allies all over the world are watching what our reaction will be, whether it's Taiwan with respect to China, whether it's Ukraine with respect to Russia. The world is watching. So this is very, very important.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Daniel, to next steps here, is the U.S., or how involved is the U.S. in figuring out who is going to govern Gaza after this, and how involved should the U.S. publicly be on that issue?\par \par \uc2 LIPPMAN: Well, this has been a huge focus of the U.S. talks with the Israeli government, which have previously said, hey, no Palestinian authority in Gaza. But if you poll Israelis, the majority of Israelis do not want to occupy Gaza for a long period of time. They have seen this movie before. They saw how the U.S. got involved in quagmires in Afghanistan and Iraq. And you have seen the Israeli government kind of shift publicly on this. You had the national security adviser of Israel writing in a Saudi newspaper recently that Israel would accept a strongly reformed \uc2 Palestinian Authority\uc2  exerting control in Gaza. And so I think the U.S. is looking at that and saying, hey, our pressure is making a difference.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Julia, part of this equation is that you have got Congress now negotiating more money for Israel. That's tied to more money for Ukraine, which is also tied to border security and the negotiations ongoing there with Republicans. Where is Congress on that? And do you think they are successful in creating a grand package putting that all together, and what would that even look like?\par \par \uc2 MANCHESTER: Yes. It's a major sticking point for Republicans and Democrats in Congress. Look, I think, as we talked about in the last segment, there is some bipartisan agreement but there needs to be something done at the border. But there is division in the Democratic Party on that issue. On Ukraine there is divisions in the Republican party on that issue. In Israel, divisions -- or funding for Israel, divisions in the Democratic Party on to how to work on that issue.\par \par \uc2 So you have these divisions in these three key issues, but you also have divisions among Republicans and Democrats as to whether aid to Israel should have something attached to it, conditions, if you will, or if there should be -- if it should be tied to border security.\par \par \uc2 So I think with the state of where the parties are on these issues and the current state of division, it's hard to see what that will look like, what a future package will look like. I think it's going to get messy next year.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Jason, what do you think? Do they get a deal?\par \par \uc2 RILEY: This is up to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and the Democrats. I think the Republicans have been very clear as to what they want, what their demands are. And this is a question of whether \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  will stand up to his progressive leftwing or not. I think it's that simple.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: And, Daniel, after that, we're going to have a government funding conversation. Shut down in the future, or how acrimonious do you think that gets?\par \par \uc2 LIPPMAN: I don't think we're going to see a shutdown. Republicans never benefit from shutdowns, and I don't think Mike Johnson, the House speaker, would think that he has a longer shelf life if people can't go to the national parks or access other critical government services.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: And Julia, what happens here with that right flank in the Republican Party? How much of a leash does Johnson have.\par \par \uc2 MANCHESTER: I don't think he has a very loose leash, but I think Mike Johnson has been trying to figure out how to govern all of the families within the Republican conference. Like Daniel said, he can't get to a shutdown, or he doesn't want to end up there. So I think he is going to start to see how other, maybe his predecessor, former Speaker McCarthy sort of was able to turn away from that part of the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Something to watch for the new year. Panel, thanks so much for a great discussion, and merry Christmas.\par \par \uc2 LIPPMAN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MANCHESTER: Merry Christmas.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Finally tonight, a special delivery just in time for Christmas. The Salvation Army is continuing its tradition of delivering meals to thousands. One employee estimates around 500 volunteers are