r \par \uc2 BAIER: White House press secretary talking about the disconnect. Let's talk about the U.S. economy with Austan Goolsbee, president of the \uc2 Federal Reserve Bank of Chicago\uc2 . Austan, good evening. Thanks for coming.\par \par \uc2 AUSTAN GOOLSBEE, PRESIDENT, FEDERAL RESERVE BANK OF CHICAGO: Yes, it's kind of you to have me back, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You know, we just heard some of this, the White House trying to deal with this disconnect. Take a look at this, the economic gains under President Biden, the Dow is up almost 20 percent, jobs added 9.9 percent, hourly wage average 14 percent, the new median home prices up 9.7 percent.\par \par \uc2 But then, look at this poll, Biden economic policy impact has it helped you? 14 percent. Has it hurt you? 46 percent. We've got this Fox poll that has these numbers. Have you been helped? I mean, that's a major, major difference, Austan, how do you explain that?\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: I don't know that I can explain it well. And I try to avoid the political context of that in the -- in the case of the Fed. As you know, the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  Act gives the fed a job to maximize employment stabilize prices.\par \par \uc2 So, if you look at the employment side of the numbers that you described, they've been extremely strong. If you look at the inflation side while we've saw improvement in 2023, they haven't been good. We had inflation, we've been dealing with an inflation crisis, similar to the crisis facing a lot of the advanced economies.\par \par \uc2 Normally, the Fed, that since it's by law has to care about the actual numbers. That's where our main focus has been, rather than on how do people feel it. And the only thing I will say is we always cared about consumer sentiment, because it was a good leading indicator of actual consumer spending.\par \par \uc2 But over the last 10 years or so, its role as an indicator of consumer spending is kind of broken down.\par \par \uc2 So, for whatever is the reason that's driving that divergence, which has never been bigger between the actual numbers and how people feel, it's less on the front radar screen for the Fed. Because our job is to look at the -- at what the actual numbers are.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, well, let's talk from Fed perspective about is it a concern that wages have not kept up with inflation? And maybe this explains some of this, the feeling part of it.\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: Yes, look, I think there's an element of the cost of living, with -- that always becomes an issue when there's inflation and that for sure, over the last two, three years, has been the biggest negative in the economy, has been that we missed so badly on inflation.\par \par \uc2 And like I say, that's a -- that's a dilemma that's facing all the central banks and all the advanced economies of the world.\par \par \uc2 I think the other things that in the past have been correlated with people not feeling it are very public prices, like, price of gasoline, price of groceries, they tend to have an outsized impact on how people feel, tends to lag a bit, actual economic conditions. It takes a while for that to work its way through.\par \par \uc2 And there is always a partisan component that has risen up. When \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's the president, Democrats reporting they don't feel it on the economy. When \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is a president, the Republicans saying they don't feel it on the economy, so that there are a lot of factors that are floating around in there.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: If the Fed is still serious about meeting that two percent goal for core inflation. Is it helpful for the Fed and the chairman to be talking about as many as three rate cuts in the next year in 2024?\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: I love that you've identified the Fed has an explicit target for inflation. We're trying to get the inflation rate down to two percent. It got up as high the headline inflation as nine percent. We made a lot of progress in 23. Hopefully, we'll continue to make progress in 24 to get down to target.\par \par \uc2 We've had this euphoria that that broke out in the stock market when they perceived that the Fed was deciding that it was going to be looser. I think maybe they got a little ahead of themselves. Because what the Feds decisions are about meeting by meeting, they're not -- we don't decide things in the future. We're going to see what the conditions are.\par \par \uc2 If inflation continues to come down to target, then, the Fed can reconsider how restrictive it wants to be. But if inflation turns around and starts going back up, it just feels like --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: People might it got a little too ahead of themself.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: While, and clearly, it was perceived that you thought that, because after the Fed chairman, \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2 , talked and said, it may become appropriate to begin dialing back and this is a topic of discussion, Reuters had a write up, says, "Goolsbee confused by market reaction to fed chief's rate cut remarks."\par \par \uc2 Saying, "We don't debate specific policies speculatively about the future," which I guess is what you're saying right here.\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: Right. Yes, that's what I'm saying here. It's not a contradiction to what -- I saw \uc2 Jay Powell\uc2 's press conference. He didn't say anything that I disagreed with. The --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Reaction to it.\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: What the market -- the market's reaction seemed to not be rooted in how the FOMC actually works. That is --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, listen --\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: The Open Market Committee decides on today's policy. They don't decide on what are we going to do and commit ourselves six months from now. They -- that's not how it works.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: This is not -- this is on the edge of politics, but it still falls under the Fed. The markets clearly getting this boost from all the talk about possible rate cuts and 24. And critics would say that you're putting your finger on the scale in an election year to affect how the economy looks.\par \par \uc2 Kevin Hassett\uc2  held your previous job when you were at the Obama administration. Here is what he said on \uc2 Larry Kudlow\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT\uc2 , FORMER CHAIRMAN, WHITE HOUSE COUNCIL OF ECONOMIC ADVISERS: Inflation is not under control. But what is under control is the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  and it's under control by the Democrats. You have to understand that.\par \par \uc2 And so, what's going to happen is that they're going to juice the economy in an election year by giving us rate cuts for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Is he wrong?\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: I don't -- that's not you can see the minutes of the Federal Open Market Committee. And you can see, it's not about politics. So, I think he is wrong, alleging that that's a political moves.\par \par \uc2 What drives the Feds decision at this point is we have met the employment side of our mandate, and we have failed on the inflation side of the mandate.\par \par \uc2 Like I say, we've been making progress. And the thing that will determine whether we can loosen or be less restrictive, and consider that is whether we're getting inflation down.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: If not, you will not see, in my opinion, I don't think there will be justified any rate cuts, unless you see progress on the inflation mandate.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Right. Last thing. Are you concerned the price of gold's going up to 2050 that may signal this uptick in inflation.\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: From the market? I don't think that the market going up, that they are being happy. We got to be careful not to get in a circle.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: The markets going up because they think they're going to cut. It doesn't work the other way. That we should not be bullied at the Fed, in my view, by what the market wants. That's not in the mandate.\par \par \uc2 The mandate is get the maximum employment and get the inflation rate down to two percent. Doesn't say anything about make the stock market happy.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Austan Goolsbee, we appreciate your job. Different than a previous job that I've talked to you about in this -- in this vein. But thank you for talking about the U.S. economy.\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: It's great to see again, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: "BREAKING TONIGHT" at this hour, the Colorado Supreme Court has disqualified former President Trump from the state's 2024 election ballot, over his involvement in the capital riot of January 6th.\par \par \uc2 Again, just getting this in the justices have stayed the decision until January 4th to allow for the inevitable appeals. But they have stepped in and disqualified him from the Colorado ballot. It's just breaking this hour. We'll continue to follow it, and what this means big picture, and appeal, obviously coming.\par \par \uc2 Up next, some high-profile Friends of the late sex abuser, \uc2 Jeffrey Epstein\uc2  may soon have their activities revealed to the public. It's a big story on Capitol Hill and elsewhere.\par \par \uc2 And later, Washington says goodbye to a trailblazing justice.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Also "BREAKING TONIGHT", a busy night. We may soon learn just who participated in the sexual escapades and abuses of the late financier \uc2 Jeffrey Epstein\uc2 . Some of the names may be familiar to you.\par \par \uc2 Chief Washington correspondent Mike Emanuel has details tonight. It's a big story, Mike.\par \par \uc2 MIKE EMANUEL, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CHIEF WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT: Bret, good evening to you.\par \par \uc2 This is likely an anxious time for high-profile friends and associates of the late \uc2 Jeffrey Epstein\uc2 , after a federal judge in New York rule to unseal court documents that would name names. Judge Loretta Preska is allowing 14 days for any of the 177 people to appeal her ruling.\par \par \uc2 In addition to high profile associates, the list of Jane Doe's and John Doe's also includes ex-employees of Epstein and sexual abuse victims. Those victims are likely to remain anonymous.\par \par \uc2 The unsealed court documents are related to a defamation case brought by Virginia Giuffre Roberts in New York against Ghislaine Maxwell.\par \par \uc2 Roberts claims Maxwell defamed her by claiming that she was lying about being sex trafficked by Epstein when she was a teenager. Roberts accused Prince Andrew of raping her when she was a teenage victim of his friend, Epstein.\par \par \uc2 Andrew paid a settlement to Roberts last year.\par \par \uc2 Tennessee Republican Senator \uc2 Marsha Blackburn\uc2  has been pushing for making the names of Epstein associates public.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARSHA BLACKBURN (R-TN): We need to know who was on that plane, who was participating in this? They redacted those records in the Ghislaine Maxwell trial. But getting to the bottom of this is important.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL (voice over): Congressman Tim Burchett, also a Tennessee Republican has suggested the flight logs are not being publicly released, because too many of his colleagues could be compromised.\par \par \uc2 Epstein killed himself in 2019 while behind bars, awaiting trial on sex abuse charges. Presidential candidate \uc2 Robert F. Kennedy Jr.\uc2  recently told Jesse Watters about his two flights on Epstein's plane and is calling for records to be released to the public.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT F. KENNEDY, JR. (D), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: I was on it in 1993. And I was in -- and I went to Florida, with my wife and two children that visit my mom over Easter.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL (on camera): Just after the first of the year, we should know plenty of other names who flew on the so-called Lolita Express. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: May not be a happy New Year for some.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Not all.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right. Mike, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we go inside the poll numbers with two experts, both sides fair and balanced.\par \par \uc2 First, here is what some of our Fox affiliates around the country are covering tonight.\par \par \uc2 WSVN in Miami, as a massive explosion rocks a once quiet Westpark neighborhood, leaving a family of four injured there. Emergency calls flooded in just before midnight, reporting a loud boom heard throughout the area.\par \par \uc2 The source of the commotion was quickly identified as a house suspected to have exploded due to a gas leak.\par \par \uc2 Fox 31 in Denver as five gray wolves are released in a remote part of Colorado's Rocky Mountains. It begins a voter approved reintroduction program, embraced in the state's mostly democratic urban corridor by staunchly opposed and conservative rural areas where ranchers worry about attacks on livestock.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Dallas from our affiliate, FOX4, the sun sets there. One of the big stories there tonight.\par \par \uc2 Jeff Bezos\uc2 's \uc2 Blue Origin\uc2  rocket company launches for the first time since the crash in 2022.\par \par \uc2 The New Shepard rocket soared from West Texas, lifting a capsule full of experiments -- no people. The vessel made it to the fringes of space, exposing the cargo to a few minutes of weightlessness before parachuting back down to the desert.\par \par \uc2 That's a nice live look "OUTSIDE THE BELTWAY" from SPECIAL REPORT, we'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Just getting that breaking news about the Colorado State Supreme Court saying, based on the 14th Amendment and former President Trump's actions during January 6th, that he is not allowed to be on the ballot in Colorado.\par \par \uc2 We're just receiving word from the Trump campaign, a reaction to this ruling, quote, "Unsurprisingly, the all Democrat appointed Colorado Supreme Court has ruled against President Trump, supporting a Soros funded leftwing group's scheme to interfere in an election on behalf of crooked \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  by removing President Trump's name from the ballot and eliminating the rights of Colorado voters to vote for the candidate of their choice. Democrat Party leaders are in a state of paranoia over the growing dominant lead President Trump has amassed in the polls. They have lost faith in the failed Biden presidency and are now doing everything they can to stop the American voters from throwing them out of office next November. The Colorado Supreme Court issued a completely flawed decision tonight. We will swiftly file an appeal to the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2  and a concurrent request for a stay of this deeply undemocratic decision. We have full confidence that the Supreme Court will quickly rule in our favor and finally put an end to these un-American lawsuits." Again, that's from a Trump campaign spokesperson. And, obviously, we will follow this as it heads up.\par \par \uc2 We are talking about the polls tonight as this breaking news comes down. Let's discuss the polls and this breaking news with pollsters \uc2 Lee Carter\uc2  and Douglas Schoen. Good evening. Lee, let me just get your reaction to what we just heard, the decision, and the reaction to it.\par \par \uc2 LEE CARTER\uc2 , POLLSTER: Well, I think we're going to see a lot of backlash as a result of this. I think \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  supporters are going to be up in arms about this. And we know that as much as the Democrats are talking about democracy being on the ballot, 78 percent of Americans believe that democracy is on the ballot, and it's not just Democrats. Republicans are concerned that they are not going to be able to be represented, that they're not going to be able to be heard, that they're going to be silenced. And so this is going to be something I think that is going to really energize Republicans to get more involved here.\par \par \uc2 Now, on the other hand, I think Democrats are going to look at this as a victory. So, again, we're going to see that Republicans are from Mars, Democrats are from Venus reaction that's going to be coming out of this.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, there is a lot of controversy around how it's based, Doug, and that is on the 14th Amendment, Section 3, which says "No person shall be a senator, representative in Congress or elector of president and shall have engaged in insurrection or rebellion against the same." There are specific words here that are in question. But your reaction and whether that helps or hurts Democrats broadly?\par \par \uc2 DOUG SCHOEN, POLLSTER: Well, this is what one Democrat who believes this is going to hurt Democrats and certainly hurt democracy. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has not been convicted of anything. And to kick him off the ballot preemptively is, to me, anti-democratic and against the interests of the Democratic Party. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has a lot of negatives but to throw him off the ballot based on allegations which have not been fully vetted in a court of law with appeals is, to me, contrary to everything our country stands for. And I'm a Biden supporter. I would vote for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  tomorrow, but I would tell \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  that he should press the Supreme Court to get \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  back on the Colorado ballot, indeed on every ballot, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Doug, let me stay with you. You say you are a Biden supporter. As you look at the Biden job performance on specific issues, national security, Ukraine, the economy, China.\par \par \uc2 SCHOEN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: He's really, really underwater.\par \par \uc2 SCHOEN: He is.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Especially on inflation and border security.\par \par \uc2 SCHOEN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: As you look at those numbers, is there a chance your party turns this and somehow there is a change at the top, or do you think that's very farfetched?\par \par \uc2 SCHOEN: A month ago, I would have said, Bret, very farfetched. Given that every poll I have seen and that has been published shows that he is behind in swing states and nationally, I think there is a chance, particularly with the impeachment inquiry having been opened and questions about his cognitive ability having been raised, I think there's a chance that he will decide over the holidays that maybe, just maybe, it's best for him not to run. I still think that's unlikely. But it's a lot more possible today than it was a month or two ago.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Lee, Vice President Harris announced that she is starting going out on a tour to protect reproductive rights starting in Wisconsin. It seems like the playbook is laid out there. A lot of people didn't think it was as powerful as it was in 2022. Looking at these numbers, can that turf it around for Democrats in the eyes of a Republican pollster?\par \par \uc2 CARTER: I think the abortion issue is one that the Republicans are really going to struggle with, and they're going to struggle with in some really important states where abortion is in fact on the ballot. The question is, is \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  the best spokesperson for it. She's only got a 35 percent approval rating, which is only one point better than \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . She is not necessarily rallying people.\par \par \uc2 But I do know that there are a huge number of independent women voters. Seven in 10 of them say that one of the primary drivers for them is going to be the decision on abortion. And so when it comes to the general election, I think this is going to be really important. And it's one of the only issues that the Democrats have. So it's not surprising that they have a playbook that they're going to be running. It's not going to be surprising to be hearing about this a lot. And Republicans are really going to have to come up with an answer, not just on abortion, because they are not going to change their position on abortion, but the question here is really what is their position on women? What is their position on childcare? What is their position on supporting women who do choose to have a baby in situations that are really, really difficult? So they've got up with a full surround, pro-family, pro-woman story that they don't have right now. And I think it's going to be a losing issue for them.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Lee, Doug, thanks for rolling with the breaking news.\par \par \uc2 SCHOEN: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 CARTER: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Up next, the funeral for the first female justice on the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: \uc2 Google\uc2  will pay $700 million and revamp its play app store to allow for greater competition. It's part of an antitrust settlement with states and consumers. \uc2 Google\uc2  was accused of overcharging consumers through unlawful restrictions on the distribution of apps and on android devices, and unnecessary fees for in-app transactions.\par \par \uc2 The funeral for a trailblazing jurist was held today here in the nation's capital. \uc2 Sandra Day O'Connor\uc2  died earlier this month at the age of 93. Correspondent David Spunt looks at today's service from Washington.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: America's first female Supreme Court Justice, \uc2 Sandra Day O'Connor\uc2 , remembered at the National Cathedral in Washington. Hundreds poured into the pews, including family, friends, former colleagues, and the president of the United States.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: \uc2 Sandra Day O'Connor\uc2 , the daughter of the American west, was a pioneer in her own right, breaking down the barriers of legal and political worlds and the nation's consciousness.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: Born in El Paso, Texas in 1930, she sat on the Supreme Court for 24 years, thanks to a nomination by President \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  in 1981. She retired in January 2006. During her time on the bench, the moderate conservative built a power base as the swing or deciding justice in key cases.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE W. BUSH\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: So help me God.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: That included the 2000 ruling in the famous Bush v. Gore case that settled the election. O'Connor's bench colleague recalled one secret to her success, often speaking first at oral arguments, thereby setting the tone.\par \par \uc2 CHIEF JUSTICE JOHN ROBERTS, U.S. SUPREME COURT: Justice O'Connor was different. Her approach was let's get what's most important to me on the table at the outset. Get it done.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: Her youngest son sharing a private message from mother to her children.\par \par \uc2 JOY O'CONNOR, SON: Our purpose in life is to help others along the way. What a beautiful, powerful, and totally Sandra day O'Connor sentiment.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: At the National Cathedral in Washington, David Spunt, FOX News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Up next, the panel on the breaking news about the Colorado Supreme Court ruling four to three that President Trump cannot be on the state ballot. We will look at the polls for President Biden and the investigations into his son.\par \par \uc2 First, Beyond our Borders tonight. A strong overnight earthquake rattles the mountainous region of northwestern China. Authorities say it destroyed homes, leaving residents out in the below freezing winter night. At least 127 people died there in the nation's deadliest quake in nine years.\par \par \uc2 A volcano in southwestern Iceland continues to spew lava into the air. We brought you that last night. Monday night's eruption occurred after weeks of strong seismic activity. Iceland sits above volcanic hotspot in the north Atlantic. It averages an eruption every four to five years.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at London. One of the big stories there tonight, animals at London's Sealife Aquarium got a little Christmas treat this week. The display supervisor donned a Santa costume, brought a little yuletide cheer to the fish and other creatures in the ocean tank. The penguins and terrapins also received little gourmet tweets from the aquarium staff. There you go. Sometimes it's just fun news.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories Beyond our Borders tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Breaking tonight, something that could affect the race for the White House that's underway in about a month, and that is out of the state of Colorado. The Colorado State Supreme Court ruling that President Trump, former President Trump, cannot be on the ballot in Colorado based on the 14th Amendment, Section Three. Here's the quote that they write, this is a vote four to three in the Colorado State Supreme Court, "We conclude that because President Trump is disqualified from holding the office of president under Section Three, it would be a wrongful act under the election code for the secretary to list President Trump as a candidate on the presidential primary ballot. But we stay our ruling until January 4th, 2024, the day before the secretary's deadline to certify the content of the presidential primary ballot. If review is sought in the Supreme Court before the stay expires, it shall remain." Again, this is for the primary ballot. Significant, nonetheless, and obviously we will deal with it the nominee, the state as well.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in our panel, Byron York, chief political correspondent of "The Washington Examiner," Josh Holmes, co-host of "The Ruthless" podcast, and Olivia Beavers, congressional reporter for "Politico." Byron, it kind of came out of left field. A lot of legal experts were saying there is no chance any of these happen, but it happened and now is on appeal.\par \par \uc2 BYRON YORK, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Kind of stunning, and you're right about left field. This was an idea that's been kicking around on the left for a couple of years since January 6th. Mostly just talked about on some cable news show until a couple of law professors wrote an encyclopedic article about this, came out over the summer. And it basically argued that this was a done deal, that the 14th Amendment was, quote, self-enacting. And all the state secretary of state had to do was just take Trump's name off the ballot.\par \par \uc2 That led to a lawsuit in Colorado, an anti-Trump group here in Washington filed a lawsuit in Colorado. And it is absolutely stunning that it has gone from talk with the left to actually disqualifying Trump from the ballot in such a short time.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: This isn't any small thing, Olivia. You have states where this action is pending. You've got Wyoming, West Virginia, South Carolina, Alaska, Texas, Wisconsin, Nevada, Virginia, New Mexico, New Jersey, Oregon, New York, and Vermont. Those are lawsuits that are pending. And there are appeals after Arizona and Michigan said he was allowed to be on the ballot. But that's just being appealed. So this is really an active thing.\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA BEAVERS, "POLITICO": It is. And I think we are going to see very quickly the argument about whether this court was politicized. That's what Republicans are going to be arguing. And then it's going to be going to a court where you have six Supreme Court justices who are conservative, on the conservative leaning side, and then three who are leaning towards the liberal side. And we're going to see what they decide to do.\par \par \uc2 But it would be a big blow if they actually ruled that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  can't be on the ballot. And it comes right before he is expected to be entering the early primary states. This was considered one of the more potent fast- tracked legal cases in terms of kicking him off the ballot. And \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's team has basically argued that they have taken information from the January 6th select committee, which they thought was unfair, and they have been trying to argue against the information that's the basis of this.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Throughout this, Josh, in this ruling, the agreeing justices, four of them, say, "We are mindful of the magnitude and the weight of the questions now before us. We're mindful of the solemn duty to apply the law without fear or favor." It goes on. The politics of this, I touched on this with Doug Schoen and \uc2 Lee Carter\uc2 , what do you think it does?\par \par \uc2 JOSH HOLMES, "RUTHLESS" PODCAST CO-HOST: I think the people appreciate it the least are \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's primary opponents, because we've seen through all of the 91 indictments previously, every time there is court action or any sort of DOJ action against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , it only makes him stronger. When you have got courts of law that are saying you can't vote for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , it's only reaffirmed more voters' opinion that they do want to vote for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . It's made him stronger throughout. I expect this will do the same. But I also expect the Supreme Court to throw this all the way back to Colorado.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Mark Levin has written extensively on this. Among these pieces, "The Democrat Party's fetish for the 14th Amendment is a vile attack on our elections directed at one man, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ." And he has got extensive defense of why this cannot apply, in Mark Levin's legal analysis of it. But to Josh's point, it does almost supercharge that argument that they are all coming after me.\par \par \uc2 YORK: It does. It does. And it's true, they are all coming after him. And just on the substance of this, to reach this decision, the court had to decide that, one, the January 6th Capitol riot was, in fact, an insurrection. And that, two, Trump engaged in insurrection on January 6th. And third, that it actually applies to the president. And these are actually very controversial issues that, as Olivia suggested, the courts out there relied on the January 6th committee's one-sided report quite a bit.\par \par \uc2 So there is going to be a lot for the Supreme Court to decide. It's amazing how many Trump issues seem to be headed to the Supreme Court very, very quickly.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Here is the 14th amendment, Section Three, "No person shall be a senator or representative in Congress or elector of president and vice president or hold any office civil or military under the United States or under any state who having previously taken an oath as a member of Congress or as an officer of the United States or as a member of any state legislature as an executive or judicial officer of any state to support the Constitution of the United States shall have engaged in insurrection or rebellion against the same or given aid or comfort to enemies thereof. But Congress may by a vote of two-thirds of each House remove such disability."\par \par \uc2 Obviously, the word "president" is not if there specifically in the text, but they exclude the word "president" from the text, but every other form of officeholder is listed specifically. So there's all kinds of interpretations of this as you get in the weeds of it. The bottom line is that this is going to appeal, and likely the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 BEAVERS: Certainly. This is even a different opinion than the last judge, the district court judge, Judge Wallace, who was saying she felt that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was guilty but he still should be on the ballot. And so I think we're going to be seeing a lot of legal arguments.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Josh, if you are the opponent to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in the primary, what are you saying about this?\par \par \uc2 HOLMES: I think it's just more of the same. You will see all of them come out and attack this. There is just no room in the Republican primary at this point for this sort of extrajudicial action against a primary opponent, namely \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, guys, thanks for rolling with the punches.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT, we will talk to journalists from Iowa, New Hampshire, and South Carolina, people on the ground, about how the presidential race is developing in those early voting states. A month away.\par \par \uc2 Remember, if you can't catch us live, set your DVR, 6:00 p.m. in the east, 3:00 p.m. on the west coast. We had a busy breaking news show today.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for this SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and still unafraid. THE INGRAHAM ANGLE, which probably will touch on this as well, is coming up now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020231220ejcj00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Senate Negotiators And White House Race To Break Stalemate On Border And Ukraine Funding; Interview With Sen. \uc2 John Cornyn\uc2  (R-TX); Hispanic Lawmakers Question Why They Are Not Included in Border Talks; House Republicans Authorize Biden Impeachment Inquiry; Candidates Blitz Iowa, New Hampshire Ahead of First Republican Contests Next Month; Story Behind Wreaths Across America\b0\par\par\uc2 Shannon Bream, Lucas Tomlinson, Jonathan Turley, Mark Meredith \par \uc2 9,844 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 December 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Fox News Sunday\par \uc2 FOXN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: I'm Shannon Bream.\par \par \uc2 With the clock ticking down, Republicans are refusing to back down on major changes in border policy and a package tied for Ukraine and Israel.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY), MAJORITY LEADER: As I've said, if we believe something is important and urgent, we should stay and get the job done.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice-over): The House heads home for the holidays, but the Senate sticks around to try and reach a deal on the president's supplemental spending request. Republicans dug in on border security as Customs and Border Protection officers are warned to look out for explosive devices.\par \par \uc2 The president sending his homeland security secretary to the Hill.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN CORNYN\uc2  (R-TX): I think it's going to take the president himself telling his cabinet and telling his staff, get this done.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We'll discuss the state of negotiations with Republican Senator \uc2 John Cornyn\uc2  of Texas and California Democratic Congressman \uc2 Ro Khanna\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Then, \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  makes a brief appearance at the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 HUNTER BIDEN\uc2 , SON OF PRESIDENT BIDEN: For six years, I've been the target of the unrelenting Trump attack machine shouting, where's Hunter? Well, here's my answer: I am here.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But skips out on his subpoena.\par \par \uc2 We'll ask our legal panel what options Republicans are weighing as the president's son faces another indictment.\par \par \uc2 And, one month until the first vote.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT: The polls came out just recently and we're way, way, way up.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: The battle for Iowa, up next. We'll debut brand-new FOX News polls and reaction from our Sunday panel.\par \par \uc2 Then, the long running tradition that is this week's Sunday special.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: A wreath, you know, is just a bunch of balsam fir placed in a ring. It's a catalyst. It has a big meaning.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All, right now, on "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM (on camera): Hello, from FOX News in Washington.\par \par \uc2 After days of tough negotiations from senators on both sides of the aisle and the White House, there's no deal yet on border security.\par \par \uc2 And nationwide, tough new poll numbers for the president on the issue, only adding to the pressure. Brand-new FOX News polling shows 35 percent of Republicans named the border as the most important issue the country is facing. While just 5 percent of Democrats feel the same way.\par \par \uc2 In a moment, we will speak with Republican Senator \uc2 John Cornyn\uc2  of Texas about the state of play in these negotiations.\par \par \uc2 But first, we turn to FOX News correspondent Lucas Tomlinson with the very latest from the White House on a foggy day.\par \par \uc2 Hello, Lucas.\par \par \uc2 LUCAS TOMLINSON, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hello, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine's president came here to the White House hoping to secure more funding. Many House Republicans want to see more of the aid tied to increase funding to secure the southern border.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We want to see Ukraine win the war.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (voice-over): With the war at a stalemate for more than a year and after a failed counteroffensive, House Speaker Mike Johnson wants to see a new plan.\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE JOHNSON (R-LA), SPEAKER OF THE HOUSE: We need a clear articulation of the strategy to allow Ukraine to win.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: What's also not clear, White House Israel policy. The president blasting Israel over what he called indiscriminate bombing which he claimed zapped support for the Jewish state.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I want them to be focused on how to save civilian lives, not stop going after \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , but be more careful.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: Congress remains deadlocked over increased aid to Israel and Ukraine unless more money is spent to secure the southern border. The House left for Christmas recess without a deal, while the Senate has remained in Washington as negotiations continue. The White House even sent Homeland Security Secretary \uc2 Alejandro Mayorkas\uc2  to meet with negotiators from both parties in hopes of ironing out an agreement.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JOHN HICKENLOOPER (D-CO): You know, spending a few extra days before the holidays, it seems a pretty good investment if we can actually make progress.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: One day earlier this month, there are more than 12,000 migrant encounters on the southern border, the highest in American history. Capitol aides say they hope to have a deal reached on the southern border in the Senate as early as day -- Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Lucas Tomlinson from the White House -- Lucas, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now from Austin, Texas, Republican Senator \uc2 John Cornyn\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Senator, good to see you today.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN CORNYN\uc2  (R-TX): Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. We got brand new FOX News polls and immigration is among what we polled.\par \par \uc2 So, let me show you this -- FOX News polling shows that 79 percent of Americans when you combine these top two criteria there think that the border is either an emergency or a major problem.\par \par \uc2 So, based on what you know about the negotiations so far, what can you tell Americans out there about the possibility, the likelihood there is actually a change in border policy?\par \par \uc2 CORNYN: Well, I talked to a couple of key negotiators yesterday and they feel like they're making some progress, but I know Senator Schumer thinks there's going to be some deal cut behind closed doors and then jam it through the Senate and then jam the House. That's not going to happen.\par \par \uc2 But we are making some progress, but this isn't just about money. This is about policies that will actually stem the flow of mass migration across the border, along with the drugs and everything that goes with it.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: You know there's pressure on the White House from the left, that they think that these are very extreme proposals, at least what they've heard floated so far.\par \par \uc2 I want to play something from Congresswoman Jayapal who says that this is going to take us back to a very difficult dark time. Here's what she says.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. PRAMILA JAYAPAL (D-WA): It is imperative my Senate colleagues and the White House understand what is on the table are policies so extreme that, if enacted, it would literally be the most exclusionary, restrictive immigration legislation since the racial quota laws of the 1920s -- literally turning the clock back 100 years.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Now, progressives sent a letter to the White House saying that they object to things like near mandatory incarceration for anyone who shows up here seeking asylum. They say Republicans want to undermine a multi-racial democracy.\par \par \uc2 How do you explain what Republicans are asking for?\par \par \uc2 CORNYN: Shannon, all we're asking for the president to do is to actually enforce the law. And now that we've got the purse strings of Congress that Congress controls, we're hoping to use that as leverage to change those policies.\par \par \uc2 What we're asking for is humane, orderly and legal immigration. Right now, the only people benefitting from the status quo are the human smugglers, the criminal organizations that are getting richer by the day along with the drug cartels.\par \par \uc2 And for the last three years, President Biden has demonstrated he simply didn't care. Well, apparently, now, he cares. We've got his attention and we intend to do something about it.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. To that point, you know what the White House says, that on day one when he became president, he offered up a migration proposal and that you all have said "no, thank you" to everything he has put on the table.\par \par \uc2 Here's the White House press secretary speaking to that this week.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: They said no to border agents. They said no to new technology to fight fentanyl. They said no to additional troops. And they voted on eliminating 2,000 Customs and Border Patrol agents. That is what Republicans have done.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Now, on top of that, one House Democrat said this week that you're actually trying to shut down legal immigration. Here's his take.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. GREG CASAR (D-TX): What is so wrong is that we actually have Republican lawmakers trying to make legal immigration more difficult. And that is a gift, frankly, to folks like Mexican cartels who will actually benefit from these Republican policies.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay, so you mentioned this administration is benefitting the cartels. He says it's the GOP who's doing it. How do you respond?\par \par \uc2 CORNYN: Well, you know, we finally have seen according to the polls that you cited earlier that the American people are not fooled by the propaganda coming out of the White House and, you know, including the secretary of Homeland Security who's repeatedly lied to Congress and the American people by saying the border is secure. It is not secure.\par \par \uc2 And we have a historic inflection point here in negotiations, for the last -- well, the years I've been in the Senate. For quite a while now, all we've done is talk about it. But now, we intend to use this leverage to do something about it.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay. So, another poll I want to cite that came in this week, brand-new out this morning, asking about the president's job approval on the border. And it is 2-1, people disapproving, thinking he is not doing well.\par \par \uc2 So, there are cynics out there who say Republicans don't actually want to help solve this issue, especially going into an election year because it is such a bad topic, a bad issue for the president.\par \par \uc2 Is Washington that broken?\par \par \uc2 CORNYN: I think that that those considerations are purely secondary. I live and work in Texas and obviously, we've borne the brunt of the Biden open border policies for years now, and the governor and legislature have done everything they can using the tools they have to do something about it.\par \par \uc2 But we want to stop the flow of humanity coming across the border, the drugs. The fact that 300,000 children have been placed with sponsors in the United States and the Biden administration has lost track of them.\par \par \uc2 This is a catastrophe, and it's a result of the Biden open border policies. We're going to use this opportunity to change that.\par \par \uc2 The president has failed to faithfully execute the laws as required of him by the Constitution. And we intend to use every tool in the toolbox to try to make him do his job.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: You have said no reason to rush something that can't get passed in the House. So while they're working out what you will get to vote on in the Senate over the House, they want something much closer to HR2, much tougher.\par \par \uc2 Do you think there is something that you guys could agree to, the Senate Democrats, the White House, the House GOP that would actually get passed?\par \par \uc2 CORNYN: Well, I know that Speaker Johnson is being briefed by some of our negotiators, and you know, what he can pass in the House is going to be frankly up to him. But we do need to be aware of the fact that this is not just an exercise in the Senate. It's not just the Senate and the president agreeing to something. It's something that can actually pass the House and be signed into law.\par \par \uc2 So this is a very delicate and difficult negotiation but we're not going to let this opportunity pass without doing everything we can to secure the border.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay, something that did get done this week was the National Defense Authorization Act, and part of that, though, included an extension of Section 702 of the Foreign Service -- Surveillance Intelligence -- Foreign Intelligence Surveillance Act, FISA. And it essentially -- it's meant to track people that are foreign threats, foreign citizens.\par \par \uc2 And yet, your colleague \uc2 Mike Lee\uc2  is among those who say it is involving American citizens and violating their Fourth Amendment right. He says this in an op-ed: The \uc2 FBI\uc2  has used Section 702 to conduct warrantless back door searches of the private electronic communications of American citizens. It is done so not just sporadically and by accident, but quite deliberately and on hundreds of thousands of occasions.\par \par \uc2 Now, like Senator Lee, you're a distinguished attorney. You are Texas Supreme Court justice. You served as the attorney general.\par \par \uc2 If this is, as he alleged, actually happening, blatantly unconstitutional - - why not say it is time to rein in 702, it is time for major reforms when these are American citizens' rights being impacted?\par \par \uc2 CORNYN: Well, I agree with Senator Lee that there have been abuses, and I'm certainly willing to enact reforms that will make those much less likely. Director Wray has changed the policies at the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . Compliance is up to roughly 98 percent. It's not good enough until it's 100 percent.\par \par \uc2 But we cannot intentionally blind ourselves to the threats from foreign adversaries. This is as you said, the Foreign Intelligence Surveillance Act. This is -- these are the crown jewels necessary to protect our country against unprecedented threats from all over the world.\par \par \uc2 And so, I hope we will fix it and not nix it, and I expect that will happen in the coming months.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay. While I've got your legal hat on, let me ask you about impeachment. You've said you support the inquiry, the House has now officially decided and voted to launch that.\par \par \uc2 But here's what the Democrat over in the House who led the impeachment against President Trump said this week, \uc2 Jamie Raskin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMIE RASKIN (D-MD): There are thousands and thousands of pages of evidence but all of the evidence demonstrates beyond any reasonable doubt that President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is not guilty of any impeachable offense that we can determine.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: He says there's no smoking gun.\par \par \uc2 You know, even if the House sends this to you, it goes nowhere in the Senate. So, do you worry that this is going to look political and be used against your party in 2024?\par \par \uc2 CORNYN: Well, Democrats have introduced impeachment into our politics by trying to impeach the former president, President Trump, two different times, unsuccessfully. The basic reason why I support the inquiry is because this investigation needs to continue. It seems like we are peeling back layers of an onion and the investigation is revealing new and very serious evidence along the way.\par \par \uc2 So this also will enhance the ability of the -- of the House to enforce those subpoenas in court, and something that Biden administration has been resisting along the way.\par \par \uc2 So I think it's a little premature for our Democratic friends to be declaring victory while the investigation is ongoing.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: It's one of the many things we'll be tracking into the New Year.\par \par \uc2 Senator Cornyn, we appreciate your time. If we don't see you before then, Merry Christmas, sir.\par \par \uc2 CORNYN: Merry Christmas to you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Up next, if the Senate gets through deals, we said there are going to be rough waters in the House. We're going to bring in Democratic Congressman \uc2 Ro Khanna\uc2  to discuss what it will take to get on the same page with House Republicans if that's possible, next.\par \par \uc2 And President Biden's past comments coming back to haunt him after his son, Hunter, brazenly defied a congressional subpoena. Our legal panel weighs in on the chances Hunter could wind up facing jail time.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: The White House on attack this week against House Republicans, saying they would rather go on vacation than address national security issues. Despite the administration's calls to delay its recess, the House is now on its holiday break as issues surrounding the southern border, aid for Ukraine, Israel and elsewhere remain in limbo.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, California Democratic Congressman \uc2 Ro Khanna\uc2 . Congressman, welcome back to Fox News Sunday.\par \par \uc2 REP. RO KHANNA (D-CA): Thank you for having me back.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, so why did the White House, the President himself, wait so long to get involved here? Has it been wasted time?\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: Well, Shannon, let me start out where I agree with Senator Cornyn, and that is that no backroom deal on the border without the involvement of the House, the House Hispanic Caucus, Latino senators is going to pass. And it's not going to pass in the House both because of Democrats and conservatives. The House has to be at the negotiating table. We want a safe border. We want an orderly and humane process. But I agree with Senator Cornyn that the negotiation hasn't involved the relevant people.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and what do you make of that? Because we've had a number of members come forward, of Latino congressmen and women and senators to say, how are we not part of this discussion? How are we not at the table?\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: I don't understand it. I mean, I think there's this view sometimes because a lot of senators become president, that you could just have the White House and the Senate come up with a deal and somehow jam the House. But the House is still the people's house and we're going to have our say. We need to be involved. You have to at least have someone like \uc2 Nanette Barragan\uc2 , the Chair of the Hispanic Caucus, at the table. You need to have some of the Latino senators at the table.\par \par \uc2 Now, look, we need a secure border. We need more funding for immigration agents for the border patrol, and we can have a conversation, but they're not even at the table.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, let's talk about this. Because "Vox" is among those that thinks a deal is going to come together. There is a lot of skepticism. I think, that the Senate's going to get this done in the next few days. But they think a deal is likely for a number of reasons. First, the horrible poll numbers across our polls and every other poll out there on how the President is handling the border, but also because he's facing pressure on so many different sides. They write this, "More and more democrats have grown uncomfortable with the border and asylum situation in the Biden years, which have featured a dramatic surge in border crossings and asylum requests."\par \par \uc2 On top of that, we have members within the Biden administration who are warning about the growing number of people on terror watch lists that are showing up at the border, the growing threat of people coming across and being motivated to use the open border for a terror attack. This week, you know, warning to border agents that there were IEDs found along the border.\par \par \uc2 With all of that and these increasing numbers, do you agree that the border is out of control at this point? Are you one of these Democrats as "Vox" says who's become uncomfortable about what's going on there?\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: I agree that we need a solution to the border and that we need to do more to secure the border. What I would do is something that the House Democrats have done, which is pass more funding for border patrol agents, pass more funding for immigration judges, and have a conversation about how we secure the border.\par \par \uc2 But what we can't do is just have certain ideas, in my view, like getting rid of parole, which allowed us to have Ukrainian refugees here, Afghan refugees here that fought with us in Afghanistan, just doing away with it, that I don't think actually makes the border more secure because it'll just have more people come to the border.\par \par \uc2 So we need a rational solution and we certainly need House members involved in the conversation.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, OK, House members getting involved. They want -- the House GOP wants something much closer to H.R.2, as we talked about with Senator Cornyn. It is much tougher than what we've heard being floated over in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 One of the things it would include is a measure that would require employers to use some kind of system to verify the legal status of people that they employ. One of our brand-new Fox News polls out this morning shows that 64% of people support punishing employers who hire people who are here in the U.S. illegally.\par \par \uc2 When you break it down by party, it's a majority of Democrats and Republicans who believe that employers should have to face some penalties. Would you support a measure like that, verification?\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: I would support holding employers accountable. The problem with verification is it often ends up punishing people who are undocumented and the families and not the employers. If we're holding the employers accountable for breaking the law and underpaying folks, I am for that.\par \par \uc2 I also think that it has to be part of a border agreement that does something for the DACA folks and does have some path to citizenship. And this is why we need to have a conversation with all parties on board.\par \par \uc2 But I just want to be clear, right now in the House, you're both going to have conservatives and Democrats vote against something that just is going to come from the Senate. So I'm not sure what they're trying to achieve.\par \par \uc2 And why not just have a clean bill on the Ukraine aid and the other aid? I mean, we would be able to pass that through the House.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, let's talk about that because one of the biggest Ukraine hawks, Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 , a Republican who wants to make sure there's no break in funding a military aid or other aid, he sounds very skeptical that something's going to come together on the border that would convince him to vote for that Ukraine aid. Here's what he said this week.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2  (R-SC): I look forward to voting no to a bad deal next week. I hope they bring up an immigration proposal, that doesn't do the job to see if I'll vote. No, I will gladly vote no to a bad deal next week. I am not going to be pressed to do something that doesn't make sense.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So listen, he is one of the loudest voices for getting more aid to Ukraine. How worried are you hearing that from him?\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: I'm concerned and I agree that we should just separate the issues. I mean, why are we trying to rush comprehensive immigration reform that we haven't been able to do for 20 to 30 years in a few weeks right before the Christmas holidays and link it to aid to Ukraine?\par \par \uc2 I mean, the whole Republican caucus, one of the reasons they got rid of Speaker McCarthy is they said have single issue votes, don't tie things that are unconnected. Let's have a clean, up or down vote on aid to Ukraine. If people are philosophically opposed, give them the right to oppose. I believe you'd have 300 House members who vote for it. And that's really, in my view, what we need.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Would you also separate a standalone issue for Israeli aid or do you think that should be tied to Ukraine as well?\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: I think that's fine to have Ukraine, Israel, Taiwan as a supplemental aid. I mean, we could do it separately, but that at least is all dealing with national security, foreign policy issues, and let people vote their conscience.\par \par \uc2 But to tie foreign policy issues and the President's request on foreign policy to a border policy, and then not to involve the Chair of the Hispanic Caucus, not to involve any Latino senators, not to involve any House members, and to just assume that the Senate is going to cut some deal and the House is just going to ratify it, is to forget Article 1 of the Constitution and the role of the House of Representatives.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, I want to talk to you about something the House did do this week, which was vote to move forward on an impeachment inquiry. We have some polling out this morning on this issue. When it comes to whether the President was involved in Hunter's business or did something related to it, 38% told us they think he did something illegal.\par \par \uc2 This is the president, 27% say something unethical. So that's 65% of Americans who think something shady went on there with regard to the President and Hunter's business. And now we have a flip on impeachment. There are now more Americans who tell us they believe it's a legitimate inquiry than a bogus inquiry. That has flipped since we last polled this in September. Do you have any curiosity about the changing story from the White House, the testimony from the \uc2 IRS\uc2  whistleblowers, anything that has come forward so far? Do you feel like you need answers?\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: I don't think the President did anything wrong as President of the United States. Look, when you impeach someone, it's because they took conduct as president that violated the law, and there's not a shred of evidence of that.\par \par \uc2 This whole thing with \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  was litigated extensively for five years in the 2020 campaign and if people want to litigate the President's past life and the details of finances and \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's finances in 2024, fine, litigated as an election issue.\par \par \uc2 I spoke out against the censorship of the story in the New York Post. But don't take up the time of the Congress when the President hasn't done anything wrong as President of the United States.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, that is where the two parties have quite a difference of opinion. Again, we'll follow that into the new year as that impeachment inquiry is now official and moving forward. Congressman Khanna, thank you very much always for your time. Great to see you today.\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: Thank you, Shannon. Merry Christmas and Happy Holidays to your viewers.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And to you as well. Thank you, sir.\par \par \uc2 So the Supreme Court agrees to hear a case over access to an abortion pill. That puts a decision due right in the middle of an election year. We're going to bring our legal panel to lay out the stakes and get their takes on breaking legal news for President Trump and \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: What's your message to people who defy congressional subpoenas on the January 6th committee?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) U.S. PRESIDENT: I hope that the committee goes after them and holds them accountable criminally.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Do you think they should be prosecuted by the Justice Department?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I do, yes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: President Biden in 2021 saying people who defied the January 6th Committee subpoenas should be held criminally accountable. That newly resurfaced video clip making the rounds this week after the President's son defied his own congressional subpoena, refusing to sit for House deposition.\par \par \uc2 Time now for our legal panel, George Washington University Law Professor Jonathan Turley. Tom Dupree, former Principal Deputy Assistant Attorney General, and Constitutional Accountability Center President, Elizabeth Wydra.\par \par \uc2 Good to see all of you. OK, gosh, we have a lot to try to get through, but let's start here. Do you think that Hunter will actually be held in contempt by the House?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN TURLEY, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: I think he should be. He's in flagrant contempt. This was a valid subpoena. And he went to the Capitol to essentially blow a raspberry at the House. He did it from the Senate side so the Sergeant of Arms couldn't pull him in.\par \par \uc2 But there was no question about it. The arguments being made that somehow this was not valid because there was a vote later on impeachment is nonsense. I mean, this was an Oversight Committee. It was issued under two committees and three different grounds for jurisdiction.\par \par \uc2 The really, to me, the question is not whether he could be charged with contempt. The answer is most certainly yes. The question is, what do you do with \uc2 Eric Swalwell\uc2 ? You've got a congressman who arrived with him, facilitated what may be a federal crime, and to defy his own house on a valid subpoena.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and if there is a contempt charge for either or both of them, then the question becomes whether the DOJ will actually prosecute it. "New York Post" Editorial Board says this is a serious hot potato for Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 : He can shield Hunter, and thus his dad from, the consequences of defying the law, but only at the cost of exposing himself as utterly partisan. Given the \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  case, the \uc2 Peter Navarro\uc2  case, what does DOJ do?\par \par \uc2 THOMAS DUPREE, FORMER PRINCIPAL DEPUTY ASSISTANT ATTORNEY GENERAL: My guess is that the DOJ under the control of President Biden and the Biden administration would not prosecute \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  for contempt. Should they? Yes. Is there a double standard? Yes. But I think at the end of the day, I would be pleasantly surprised if \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  got a contempt referral from \uc2 United States Congress\uc2  and said, we are going to prosecute \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  for contempt.\par \par \uc2 But look, I totally agree. It's going to put the Justice Department in a very tight box because we are going to be able to point to all sorts of examples where they have prosecuted these contempt citations. They take them seriously. They enforce the law. But in \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's case, they're going to make an exception.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, I noted this with Congressman Khanna. I want to bring this poll up again. A view on congressional Republicans pushing impeachment proceedings. Those numbers have now flipped, Elizabeth, to more people believe it's legitimate than bogus at this point. They've seen enough, they say, at least they have questions, the inquiry should proceed.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WYDRA, CONSTITUTIONAL ACCOUNTABILITY CENTER PRESIDENT: Yeah, you know, impeachment is an incredibly powerful tool in our Constitution, and it should be wielded with care and responsibly. I think that we've seen months and months and months of efforts by House Republicans to try to come up with something that they could tell the American people that would connect the President to his son's dealings, whatever you may think of them.\par \par \uc2 And there hasn't been a shred of direct evidence about President Biden presented to the American people. So I think that far from this being a carefully wielded, responsibly wielded impeachment inquiry, it seems to be careless and irresponsible, you know, particularly when there is so much more business that is pressing to the American people.\par \par \uc2 But I just want to step back for a minute and talk about the contempt charge, because I fully believe that \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  will follow the facts of the law where they lead. That is, has been his hallmark. There has been accountability for \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  in some criminal prosecutions. He has not stopped that. And I don't believe he ever would stop that. That's not the kind of person \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  is.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So I'll take a little vote here then. Who, show of hands, thinks the DOJ would actually criminally pursue \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  if he's held in contempt? Tom, I'm going to put you down for a no.\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: I actually think that they would have no choice.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, maybe you think they would.\par \par \uc2 WYDRA: I think it depends on the facts, because look, there is also like Abbe Lowell, \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's lawyer is saying, look House Oversight Committee Chairman Comer said you could choose whether to do a public testimony or private testimony. We'd choose public, then you changed your mind. So I think there are more facts here, but I think \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  would follow the facts in the law where they lead.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, let me play something from Ian Sam's over the White House. He tends to agree with you on this whole thing. Here's what he said.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 IAN SAMS, WH SPOKESPERSON FOR OVERSIGHT AND INVESTIGATIONS: These guys have made up and moved the goalposts every step of the way of this investigation. It's all baseless. They've been investigating the President all year long. For example, they've gotten 100,000 pages of documents. They've interviewed witnesses for 40 hours. And guess what? They've come up with not a single shred of evidence of any wrongdoing of any kind by President Biden.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: What do you say, Jonathan? Because, listen, folks will say there's no smoking gun, as Elizabeth said.\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: That's simply not true. I mean -- there's been an enormous amount of evidence put together by the House committees, millions of dollars that have gone through a labyrinth of different accounts and shell companies to Biden family members. There are Biden associates who said that what the President has said publicly is nonsense, that he did know about this influence peddling.\par \par \uc2 And most people agree that this is influence peddling. This is corruption. But I have to correct one notion that is being bantered about, and that is that you have to really show that money went directly to the President. That's not the standard. That in federal cases, evolving bribery and other crimes, giving money to a principal's family members is in fact a benefit under federal law. Otherwise, everyone would just give money to family members and say it's not a bribe, it's non-impeachable.\par \par \uc2 And by the way, it is impeachable. I was lead counsel in the last traditional impeachment trial. My client was impeached because benefits were given to a judge's family members. So many of these congressmen repeating this argument voted on that impeachment and said it is impeachable.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, I want to try to get to a quick lightning round if we can on the Supreme Court. So they've agreed to at least have a conversation about whether to expedite this case. The President Trump is claiming immunity. He's got -- his team's got to file something this week. How quickly could we hear from the Supreme Court?\par \par \uc2 DUPREE: I don't know I think we're going to hear very quickly from the Supreme Court. Look, it is extraordinary the time schedule that is being proposed. But as all sides have acknowledged, this is an absolutely extraordinary case.\par \par \uc2 What the Supreme Court has said is that they take this request seriously. They're going to give it due consideration. And if they agree to review it, I would expect they would wrap this up in about a month or two, which is absolute lightning speed by Supreme Court standards.\par \par \uc2 But look, everyone understands what's at stake here. It's a hugely important legal question. In all likelihood, it will be decided at some point by the Supreme Court. So I think the question on the table now is, are they going to decide it before a trial occurs or after?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Right, because what Special Counsel Jack Smith is trying to do is start a March trial on these charges. So Elizabeth, what do you think are the odds that whether it's the Appellate Court or the Supreme Court actually derails that March start date? Because the Trump team, I'm sure, would love to see this happen after the election, not before?\par \par \uc2 WYDRA: Right. I mean I think the March trial date given these appellate processes are you know that's -- that's a little bit precarious. But I do think that if there is delay it probably won't be very long because Tom's exactly right that the Supreme Court is taking this seriously. It's good to take these questions of immunity off the table, decide them at this early juncture and frankly the D.C. Circuit could move even while the Supreme Court is considering this expedited petition Fitzgerald (ph) before judgment.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: There's also this issue of the obstruction charge that a couple of other January 6th defendants, the Supreme Court's agreed to hear whether those charges, those federal charges should have been used against them, but they're part of the Trump case as well. How does that factor in?\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: Well, two of the four counts against Trump with the Special Counsel in D.C. involve obstruction claims. If the court changes the interpretation of those provisions, it could have a major blowback on that trial. It could literally cut those counts in half. But the problem for the Special Counsel is his sense of urgency that we need to try Trump before the election. And they sort of shoehorn to this March day just into the schedule just before Super Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 It's not clear to me that the Supreme Court is going to share that sense of urgency. Because if Trump is convicted, it doesn't mean he won't be eligible for the presidency. He could still take office. So the Supreme Court may be looking at this and saying, maybe we should wait for the D.C. Circuit in regular order to play out.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And quickly, Elizabeth, because I know you've been interested in this case, the Supreme Court has also agreed to hear a case on a use of an abortion medication, mifepristone. That's going to be on the schedule in the -- in the spring as well, probably decision in June, right in the middle of the election.\par \par \uc2 WYDRA: Absolutely right in the middle of presidential and congressional campaigns. You know, this case is not just about the liberty and freedom of American individuals because it has to do with a particular regulated -- \uc2 FDA\uc2  regulated drug mifepristone. It also has to do with the pharmaceutical industry and the availability of drugs generally to Americans. So this is a very key case on a number of issues before the court.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yeah, and throws abortion right back into the middle of the 2024 conversation.\par \par \uc2 OK, legal eagles, thank you very much. Great to see all of you.\par \par \uc2 All right, up next, can anyone in the GOP primary field catch President Trump? We've got brand new polls to unveil for you just a minute away. We're going to bring in our Sunday panel on the state of the 2024 race and more.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We're a month away now from the Iowa caucuses, and as voters get ready to actually begin casting ballots, we've got brand new poll numbers about what they're thinking. Mark Meredith is live in Hampton Falls, New Hampshire, where the key state is preparing to hold its first in the nation primary next month.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Mark.\par \par \uc2 MARK MEREDITH, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Shannon, hello to you. It is essentially crunch time for these Republican presidential candidates. Former President Trump, he was here in New Hampshire on Saturday and he offered scathing criticism for his Republican rivals who were trying to unseat him as the party's standard bearer.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Next month, New Hampshire is going to weed out the insincere rhinos.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: Former President Trump told voters in New Hampshire his victory in January's primary is all but guaranteed.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : This will be a historic victory by Christmas of next year.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: New Fox News polling shows Trump has a reason to celebrate. Among Republican voters, Trump remains the most popular of all the GOP presidential candidates, some of whom are seeing support drop.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY, (R) PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: That was rock solid of an endorsement as we could hope for.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: Trump's lead comes as notable Republicans, including New Hampshire Governor \uc2 Chris Sununu\uc2 , urge voters to look beyond Trump. Sununu supports Nikki Haley.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 CHRIS SUNUNU\uc2 , (R-NH): We're 110% behind Nikki. I think she's going to be the nominee. I think New Hampshire's going to be the reset point.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: Fox's new polling shows Haley beating President Biden by six points in a hypothetical matchup. Haley travels to Iowa tonight to shore up support. Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is already there telling crowds despite multiple campaign shakeups, he believes his heavy focus on Iowa will pay off.\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS, (R-FL) PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: I don't think pundits decide these things and so I think you guys should be determined to make your voice heard. Don't let the media pick the candidate.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2  remains just as busy out on the campaign trail. He's barnstorming Iowa, holding multiple events this week. And here in New Hampshire, \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2 , still betting big on the Granite State. His pack now launching its first TV ads of this cycle. Shannon?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, Mark Meredith reporting from New Hampshire. Thank you very much, Mark.\par \par \uc2 Time now for our Sunday group, "USA Today" White House Correspondent, Francesca Chambers. Mark Short, former Chief of Staff for Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 . Charlie Hurt from "The Washington Times." And Elizabeth Wydra, doing double duty for us today from the Constitutional Accountability Center where she is the president. Welcome back. It's good to see all of you.\par \par \uc2 Let's start here, though, because what is happening with President Trump out on the campaign trail last night, he's getting a lot of headlines over this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : You know when they let, I think the real number is 15, 16 million people into our country when they do that, we've got a lot of work to do. They're poisoning the blood of our country. That's what they've done.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, critics are pointing to that saying he used that language also in an interview with the National Pulse and they point to Mein Kampf from Hitler saying this, "All great cultures of the past perished only because the originally creative race died out from blood poisoning. Charlie, he has to know that people are going to be fired up about language?\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE HURT, THE WASHINGTON TIMES: Yeah, well, he always likes using language that gets people fired up.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, he does.\par \par \uc2 HURT: That's kind of not surprising. But I think it's kind of funny to listen to \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  say, don't let the media pick the next president. I don't think the media is picking the next president on this one. But he is tapping into an issue that is obviously very, very important to voters, both Democrats and Republicans, that a lot of people around here, and in particular the media, have pretended is not a problem, and that is illegal immigration.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, he's also handing material to the Biden-Harris campaign, who had this statement last night in part. Senator tonight, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  channeled his role models as he paraded Adolf Hitler, praised Kim Jong-un, and quoted \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  while running for president on a promise to rule as a dictator and threaten American democracy.\par \par \uc2 They went on to say, "He's betting he can win this election by scaring and dividing this country." Francesca?\par \par \uc2 FRANCESCA CHAMBERS, USA TODAY WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Giving them lots of tape there, they also have commented on the dictator on day one comments that he made in that town hall with \uc2 Sean Hannity\uc2 . And this is a way that they have determined that they can focus on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in the election without talking about his legal woes, which gets into dicey territory for them, because they say that of course, they are independent from the Department of Justice.\par \par \uc2 So they don't want to weigh into his legal battles, but when he makes comments like that on policy, then that gives them something that President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  can then go out there and campaign on aggressively.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Marc, unforced error or on purpose?\par \par \uc2 MARC SHORT, FORMER CHIEF OF STAFF TO VICE PRESIDENT MIKE PENCE: Look, I think it's highly unlikely that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's ever read Mein Kampf. I think the reality is what we have is that the left continues to attack him or something outrageous, he says. But it continues to drive the issue back to border security, which is what he wants to be talking about.\par \par \uc2 He has a decisive lead on border security over \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , just like he does on the economy, just like he does on their national affairs. And so, yeah, he says something outlandish, they attack what he said rhetorically, but if you come back to the root of the issue, it's where a lot of the American people agree with him on.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I want to skip ahead because we've got a lot of polls here today, but I want to look at the one that has GOP candidates head-to-head with President Biden. And when you put \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  and Nikki Haley, they all tie or beat President Biden.\par \par \uc2 Given what has gone on, what we're just talking about, Elizabeth, does that surprise you? Are you disappointed there's not better polling?\par \par \uc2 WYDRA: You know, I think that where we are right now, the polls can change, you know, obviously. I think if you look at what's important to people, you know, the economy, obviously, we've seen in state after state that when abortion is on the ballot, that Democrats win, or people who support abortion rights pretend to be Democrats win, although Nikki Haley's trying to thread the needle a little bit there.\par \par \uc2 And so I think once we get to the actual election and you have, you know, we talked about just a little bit ago, the abortion case at the Supreme Court likely being decided in late June 2024, right in the middle of these presidential campaigns, that that is going to change a lot of people's minds, particularly women who have seen instances like in Texas, where a woman that, you know, a lot of people see themselves in, a mother who desperately wanted this child, but something tragic happened, she wants to preserve her ability to have future children.\par \par \uc2 When people see people like that having to go into court and beg to make the decision that they and their families and their doctors made, and then having that rebuffed by elected leaders, that gets a lot of people scared appropriately, and voting for people who will make sure that they have freedom and liberty and choice and not go the other direction.\par \par \uc2 So I don't put too much stock in the polls right now, and also look, there are ways to talk about immigration, there are ways to talk about policy choices that don't use that chilling offensive language that should be offensive in our American democracy, which is a multiracial diverse democracy.\par \par \uc2 Well, interestingly enough, in the midst of this campaign, and the two choices it looks like, absent something changing that are going to end up on the ballot in the fall, when asked about favorables, head-to-head, President Biden and President Trump, President Trump now has a four-point advantage on favorables over President Biden. And when it comes to unfavorable, President Biden is now three points higher than President Trump. Charlie, does any of that surprise you?\par \par \uc2 HURT: Not really, and by the way, I sort of agree that I think all the polls are sort of kind of hard to suss out at this point. But I do think that, you know, when you boil down and look at the issues like the economy, you look at the issues like foreign policy, you look at the issues like border security, all of them with the possible exception of the issue of abortion, Trump has a -- and Republicans have a very strong hand over Biden on.\par \par \uc2 And that's a pretty good -- that's a pretty good situation to be going into the year of an election on, because especially -- because I don't know what variables change in \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's direction between now and the election.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: The economy, they feel like there are green shoots, there's improvement there, the numbers there. And Francesca, that's something they talk about quite a bit, that they hope that there will be a turnaround in time for November of 2024.\par \par \uc2 But you've also got -- there's so many other polls that are troublesome for him this week. "Few" has his job approval rating at 33%. "The Hill" reports on another pullout that has seven swing states that President Trump is beating President Biden in all of those swing states. And there's more public chatter from Democrats who are worried about this.\par \par \uc2 CHAMBERS: And when you look at the talks that are going on with the border on Capitol Hill right now, you know, previously the White House had said that this was a closed discussion. This is the aid package that he had put forward. They were not going to be negotiating border policy. Now you see the President saying that he is open to this. That's exactly what the conversations are.\par \par \uc2 And part of it you could say is because of the polling, part of it here, of course, because they want to get that Ukraine aid, they want to get that Israel aid, and they know that's the only way to get it done. But it doesn't change the fact that they said that they wouldn't do it, and here's where they are.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and you've got David Axelrod out there, again, publicly talking about his concerns. He says he's alarmed by numbers regarding voters' perceptions of Biden's and Trump's policies when president. Twenty- three percent of voters said Biden's policies have helped them. Forty-nine percent said that about Trump's policies. Fifty-three percent said Biden's policies have hurt them personally. And 37$ said the same about Trump. That's a tough batch of numbers to run for reelection against.\par \par \uc2 SHORT: I would think that most Democrats are in the same place as David Axelrod. I think the reality is the polling is very troublesome for Democrats and they've got to be scared at this point. I think when you go down the top issues of concern for most Americans on the border, the economy, to having war in Eastern Europe, war in the Middle East, and I think the reality is that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  certainly appears far more vigorous than \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  at this point, I think Democrats should be pretty frightened.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Is there any chance they change out the Biden-Harris ticket? Elizabeth?\par \par \uc2 WYDRA: You know, I don't think so. Everyone has said that President Biden is the nominee. You know, look, things can always change. But I think that, you know, as we go forward in the process, and if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is the nominee, I think we're going to see many echoes of the 2020 election, where people see that rhetoric, see the potential policies.\par \par \uc2 You know, the dictator on day one is an extremely concerning statement across the spectrum.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I would note, he's coming up, he was sort of chuckling when he said that no one wants to hear dictator but it seemed very tongue-in-cheek.\par \par \uc2 CHAMBERS: Yeah, like, you know, is that funny? I don't think so. Like, you know, it's not really what you want to hear from your president -- potential president, but I think that people are going to take things very differently as the election approaches, and we see which candidates are coming up to each other.\par \par \uc2 HURT: It does get all the right people upset though when he says things.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Really likes when the right people get upset. He likes to get them upset on this show too.\par \par \uc2 All right, panel, thank you very much. We'll see you next Sunday.\par \par \uc2 Up next, honoring American veterans, here from the man behind one of the most treasured Christmas traditions in our country.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We are back with a look at the thousands of wreaths laid at each grave site at Arlington National Cemetery. It's a holiday tradition here at Fox News Sunday to share the story of how one man from Maine came up with this idea to honor our fallen with some humble greenery.\par \par \uc2 Here again is the story behind wreaths across America. It's our Sunday Special.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MORRILL WORCESTER, WREATHS ACROSS AMERICA FOUNDER: We wouldn't have what we have today if it wasn't for them. They stepped up for us time and time again all down through the history of the country.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice-over): Morrill Worcester on how his reverence for service members inspired a beloved American holiday tradition. He's the founder of "Wreaths Across America," the nonprofit that organizes volunteers to lay Christmas wreaths at Arlington National Cemetery and its cemeteries across the U.S.\par \par \uc2 (On camera): Do you think you'll ever be able to get your mind around the impact of what this has turned into and what it means to thousands of people?\par \par \uc2 WORCESTER: Probably not. No, it but whether it's a hundred people or a hundred thousand people, it means the same, the families.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: It started with a childhood trip to Washington in 1962, when Worcester was moved by the graceful rows of iconic whitehead stones.\par \par \uc2 WORCESTER: I was here as a 12-year-old. I just never forgot it. I couldn't believe how big it was.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Three decades later in 1992, Worcester was in charge of a wreath company and found himself with extras around the holidays.\par \par \uc2 WORCESTER: When I had those wreaths left over years ago, I said, you know, if I can make it happen, I'm going to bring them here. The wreaths were just made, and it's too bad to waste them. And I thought it would be great to have them on the graves.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: He asked if he could donate them to the cemetery. Dozens of volunteers helped him drive down and lay each one on the headstones. Fox News Sunday first met Worcester back in 2006.\par \par \uc2 WORCESTER: They're not just tombstones. I mean, those are all people.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: By that time, a photo of his quiet annual tribute had gone viral.\par \par \uc2 WORCESTER: There was snow on the wreaths, and it was just a great shot. And that hit a chord with just millions and millions of people around the world. And it changed everything. I mean, you wouldn't believe the number of emails that we got, and phone calls.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: People were touched and thousands began volunteering to place wreaths at Arlington and in cemeteries in their hometowns. Fox News Sunday joined Worcester back then to see the wreaths arrive after a 750-mile trip. Worcester calls it the world's longest veterans parade.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The ceremony you are about to witness is an Army wreath laying ceremony to be conducted for the Worcester Wreath Company.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Did you ever imagine when you showed up that first year with 5,000 wreaths that it would turn into what it is now?\par \par \uc2 WORCESTER: Never. No, because it was going to be a one-time thing.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: What does it mean to you, to people who volunteer, who work all year long to get this ready?\par \par \uc2 WORCESTER: It's come over time. I mean, you know, something that just didn't happen one year. And it's evolving into learning more and more about people and what it means to them and about the families.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Worcester says after more than three decades, he still feels the reverence in each wreath.\par \par \uc2 (On camera): It feels like you're giving multiple gifts to those who are honored here in Arlington, but those who also get to take these wreaths and feel like they're giving an honor as well?\par \par \uc2 WORCESTER: It does. A wreath, you know, is just a bunch of Balsam fir placed in a ring. It's a catalyst. There's a big meaning, really.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So what's the future?\par \par \uc2 WORCESTER: The future of this, honestly, is to remember and honor every single veteran.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: What does it mean to you when you walk in here and look around?\par \par \uc2 WORCESTER: It's all in a lifetime, it really is. I've had the opportunity to do this, and the good Lord runs everything, runs all of us. And He's in charge of all of it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Mr. Worcester says he believes this whole thing has been a divine assignment. The tradition has continued every year since 1992. This year, volunteers placed more than three million wreaths on veterans' graves at more than 4,100 locations. And that includes the more than 250,000 wreaths right here at Arlington National Cemetery.\par \par \uc2 That's it for us today. Thank you so much for joining us. I'm Shannon Bream. Have a fantastic week. We'll see you right back here for next Fox News Sunday.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FOXN000020231217ejch00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Gavin's Grievances, CA Gov. Shifts The Blame To GOP For State's Decay; "DEI Must Die," Musk Wages War On Woke Ideological Takeover Of U.S.; Marxist Tap Dancers, Jill Biden Hires Anti-Police Group For Christmas Video; Polling Panic: Dems Sound Alarm As Biden's Approval Falls To Just 33 Percent; Gate Lice: Why Do People Line Up Before Boarding Group Is Called?; From Toilet To Tap: California Plants To Turn Sewage Into Drinking Water\b0\par\par\uc2 Shannon Bream, Kellyanne Conway, Greg Gutfeld, Jessica Tarlov,  Will Cain \par \uc2 8,445 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 December 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: The Five\par \uc2 FOXFIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR: All right \uc2 Sean O'Brien\uc2 , the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2 ' general president here. And so again, maybe they do that, you know, talk or something at a diner, but it doesn't look very, very eminent right now. But you never know. You never know.\par \par \uc2 That will do it here. Another record on the Dow. We'll explore that tomorrow at 10:00 a.m. In the meantime, here's THE FIVE.\par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR: Hello, everyone, I'm Shannon Bream along with Kellyanne Conway, Jessica Tarlov, Will Cain, and Greg Gutfeld. It is 5:00 in New York City and this is THE FIVE.\par \par \uc2 California Governor \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  taking a swing at Republicans while discussing his state's problems. Right now, growing concerns that California is reverting back to its old ways after spending millions to clean up rampant homelessness and open-air drug markets for the APEC Summit. The Golden State Governor taking too liberal late-night T.V. and blaming the GOP for amplifying the quality-of-life issues.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. GAVIN NEWSOM (D-CA): They've been on a doom loop about San Francisco ever since \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  became speaker. California has outperformed the American economy. It's the tentpole in American recovery.\par \par \uc2 And its values are stark. Contrast differences in daylight and darkness between the Republican Party. They have to focus on our failures in order for them to be right.\par \par \uc2 San Francisco is a special place. It is a unique and special place. Incredibly resilient and vibrant.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And sounds like someone's looking for a rematch after the big red versus blue state debate. Newsom taking some shots at Florida's \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: I'm not running for president of the United States. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  apparently still is. I have developed a lot of animus towards him because of his animus towards all of you.\par \par \uc2 You know, this guy is out there scapegoating vulnerable communities. He's attacking minorities. Attacking women.\par \par \uc2 For me, it's not anti-woke. What he really means is anti-Black. He's out there censoring historic facts.\par \par \uc2 He's rewriting history. He was out there -- you know, he eliminated AP African American Studies. He said slavery was some awesome workforce development program.\par \par \uc2 And he doubled down on that. So, for me, it's a very serious moment where I just felt it was important to blowback against that -- against that narrative.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: A lot of finger-pointing coming from Newsom as California grapples with major issues of its own. This year, San Francisco recording the highest number of overdoses in the city's history. Michael Shellenberger calling out California for exacerbating the homeless crisis with what he says is bad policy.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL SHELLENBERGER, ENVIRONMENTAL PROGRESS PRESIDENT: Usually, we pay the highest taxes in the entire country in California. They have plenty of resources. In fact, all of the spending is a reason that we have these problems subsidizing addiction.\par \par \uc2 They literally pay people to be homeless in San Francisco. And that's the underlying problem to it, and that there's no discipline, there's no law enforcement, and there's no mandate of the treatment that people with mental illness and drug addiction need in order to get into recovery.\par \par \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. Kellyanne, let's start here. He says that -- the governor does, that California is the temple of the American Recovery. But you know, the hundreds of thousands of people who've left in the last couple of years don't seem to agree.\par \par \uc2 KELLYANNE CONWAY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: No. 750,000 people have left California in the last years. 352 businesses in the last four years. That is not a coincidence. That's causation.\par \par \uc2 Bad policy, high taxes, homelessness, you are tripping over people and their natural discharge as you try to make your way to school or to work or places of worship. And other man-made problems for \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2 , Shannon. The light rail that he said he was going to abandon, he turned -- changed his mind on that. Now, he's pumping billions more into it. It's not going to work.\par \par \uc2 And you mentioned San Francisco. The fact is Los Angeles also now has fentanyl surpassing meth as the number one crisis in drug overdose death. Oakland is completely destroyed. So, this isn't a fantasy or a concoction of the "right-wing."\par \par \uc2 We know that the left is failing in our major cities, including all across \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2 's California because we have two eyes and two ears. It's very simple. One thing I want to say.\par \par \uc2 You can't blame Republicans when the eight top officeholders in the state of California are all elected Democrats. When Democrats control, if you added up the Senate and the assembly together, 96 seats to about 26 seats, Democrat and Republican. He owns all of this.\par \par \uc2 And all of the great things we love about California, the weather, the ocean, the beaches, the mountain, the -- has nothing to do with \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  and the Democrats. All the things we don't like about California, they have put in our footsteps.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well --\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: I think \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  should say mirror, mirror on the wall, who's got the most homeless of them all? And narcissists, the governor, will find the answer very plain and clear.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, I thought it was interesting that he said -- with right coming after him, he says they have to focus on our failures in order for them to be right. But, Greg, that seems to admit they do have failures.\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: First, I think it's really unfair of Kellyanne to wear that outfit. Because there are like 15 jokes I want to say, but I'm not going --\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: Don't.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: I can't.\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: Don't made me call -- (INAUDIBLE)\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Because I don't know her well enough. I'm afraid -- I'm as -- I'm afraid of Kellyanne Conway.\par \par \uc2 WILL CAIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Do one. Just one.\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: You will be.\par \par \uc2 CAIN: It will be wonderfully awkward.\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: Super --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Wait, am I going to have to call HR?\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: Superimpose Gavin's head on -- (INAUDIBLE)\par \par \uc2 CAIN: Ridiculous.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: I would say Beetlejuice is looking for his jacket. But anyway, I'm joking. Look, he -- a couple of things -- or a lot -- a lot of things.\par \par \uc2 First of all, he called San Francisco resilient. You don't describe a place that's go -- doing really well as resilient. A very -- you describe a person who's undergoing serious health problems as they're really resilient.\par \par \uc2 So, this place is going through hell, hence, he calls it resilient. He brought up race, so you -- that opens the door to his policies, which are anti-black, anti-female, anti-Asian because all three of them are victims of his policies. You know, at least DeSantis's policies are about lifting all boats, not sinking all of them.\par \par \uc2 However, what is -- his point is irrelevant. Politicians point to each other's failures all the time, left and right. But that doesn't negate the failures.\par \par \uc2 We're going to talk about it later in the show. California is now going to be drinking water made out of feces, OK? That's not -- that's not a successful state.\par \par \uc2 Newsom's reasoning is that it's like blaming the guy who calls the fire department to put out a fire for the actual fire itself. His reasoning would blame anyone for making the 911 call to report a crime for simply calling out of concern and saying something. He would prefer that you do not say something, remain silent, lie back, and enjoy it.\par \par \uc2 You know, I was born and raised in California. It's not a political thing for me. It's a tragedy to see what's happening to the country -- to the state, and in effect to the country.\par \par \uc2 I would like to help. I'm not there just to point fingers. I saw firsthand experience here in NYC at the crime wave. We pointed this out on the show, I don't know, three years ago. We were the canaries in the coal mine here on THE FIVE.\par \par \uc2 We were mocked by people on the -- on CNN who are no longer employed. Was that our fault for calling this out? We were called hysterics for pleading with Democrats to do something. If they listened years ago, they would have been heroes, and we would have been happy for them. And that's another point here.\par \par \uc2 You know, Republicans actually do want to help. They're not just pointing fingers, right? The weird thing about conservatives is they'll pull over and help fix your car or change a flat even if you got a Biden-Harris sticker on it.\par \par \uc2 It's part of being for small government, you know, relying on your -- on your neighbors and individuals. The model -- motto of people on the right is we're here to help not the government, even if you hate us. But for some reason, it's never reciprocated because they see through the lens of politics.\par \par \uc2 Oh, they're only coming after us because it's political, that we actually do care about the fentanyl poisonings because we've been talking about it for years. We've been talking about the drug addiction, the homelessness, and the crime wave because we care. It's not a political thing for us, even if it is for you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well -- and listen, if you do or don't like his policies, you talk about smaller government, he's going to have it whether he wants it or not because now the governor is talking about having to make major cuts because there's -- this huge deficit of people leaving, of businesses leaving, so you're going to have a tax base. That's different. But, Will, he says Republicans are making their failures, sort of these Democrats that he's an avatar for what's wrong in the world.\par \par \uc2 It reminds me of the Chicago Mayor who said something recently too. Like it's the right wing that's making us look bad.\par \par \uc2 CAIN: First, I want to say, Greg, I think you can sleep soundly and safely. I think that the amount of incoming that has come the way of Kellyanne Conway, a little bit of ribbing over her jacket probably even dent the surface most lefts leaving a scratch.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: That is real. She's thick-skinned.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAIN: If I were to find out you are sensitive about her jacket, I would request you my total understanding of reality, Kellyanne Conway.\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: Thank you for supporting the \uc2 Philadelphia Eagles\uc2  tonight, Will.\par \par \uc2 CAIN: That is not what's happening. And now I am sensitive --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Kellyanne, I'm not part of that.\par \par \uc2 CAIN: As a Cowboy fan, America's team.\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: Cowgirl.\par \par \uc2 CAIN: What I would say about \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  is it is incredible that he has become the face of the Democrat Party because he is a self-evident failure. Everything about that state is on its face, a failed state. And I could go through.\par \par \uc2 And I don't need to because you really only need the one condemnation. The one observation that Kellyanne and you, Shannon, point out is that people are fleeing the state. In Thomas Jefferson's little laboratories of democracy, that's the biggest indictment that you can receive.\par \par \uc2 I'm out. All he has left -- the only thing he has to recommend, and my guess is that he's a face jock. And secondarily, that he is -- he's once again pulled out the knife of racism, homophobia, vulnerable communities. He says \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is an anti-woke, he's anti-black.\par \par \uc2 The last time I was on THE FIVE was right after the DeSantis-Newsom debate. And I said we shouldn't be worried about Newsom because he is a very effective liar. But if he wants to lie about the nature of America and \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 's moral soul, I think he's pulled out a knife that's dull.\par \par \uc2 I think America is done with everyone who disagrees with you as a racist. That knife has no edge. And he's going to have to come up with some better lies if he's going to be the face of Democrats.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, Jessica, a lot of people think he is going to be the face of Democrats like he's in maybe on the ticket for you guys next year.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA TARLOV, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: You mean in 2028? Next year is the - -\par \par \uc2 BREAM: No. I mean, 2024. He says he's not running. But it -- a lot of folks think it looks an awful lot like a shadow campaign.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: I think that there was a lot more merit to that argument, like six months ago, remember when he went to the White House on a day when \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was out, etcetera.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Right.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: And now, he really has become one of the strongest Biden-Harris surrogates out there. And I think the fact that he took on that debate with \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  and stood up there and said, you should be talking to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , right? You're standing here with me, someone who's not on the ticket, and he'll be on in 2028. I'm sure that he'll get into the primary then.\par \par \uc2 But I don't think that he has an aspiration to do it right now. Frankly, I think that having just come out of COVID and the scenes that we see that California is not going to be ready. It's not the best laboratory to go out and run a presidential campaign on at this particular moment.\par \par \uc2 It doesn't mean that there haven't been strides in the right direction. We see pictures of homeless people all the time. The homeless rates are down in California, though.\par \par \uc2 10 percent of the jobs that were gained just last month in the U.S. came from California. And he's making a very valid point. Republicans -- and, Greg, you might be right that the average Republican is desperate to change my tire. And I would welcome it if I got a flat because I don't know what to do under those circumstances.\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , stood up there and held a big poop map right, and didn't want to talk about the fact that Jacksonville has a 67 percent higher murder rate than San Francisco, right? He doesn't want to talk about the homelessness problem in Tampa. You talk about having to step over people, you got to do that in Tampa, too.\par \par \uc2 I have seen no politician of either party that stands up there and wants to olive branch with the other side and say, you know what you're doing? This right. Especially in a debate or when you have a friendly interview, you're going to take every opportunity to pound the other guy.\par \par \uc2 And they're clearly targeting DeSantis because if it's not Trump, he's quite f