ark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: I just want to point that out to everybody at home in case --\par \par \uc2 JESSICA TARLOV, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: And it is her birthday.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: It is her birthday, she's 47.\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL LEGAL ANALYST: She's 34.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: She's 47.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: She's 34.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: She's had a lot of work done.\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Did you get invited to the party?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: I turned it down. I have plans. You know, we dated back in the '90s.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: I know.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Oh, that's right.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: That's what that songs about?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Oh my God. All right, that's it for us, everyone.\par \par \uc2 MIKE EMANUEL, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CHIEF CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, welcome to Washington. I'm Mike Emanuel in for Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, we are following two major stories. The president's son defies a congressional subpoena to appear privately for a deposition about his business dealings.\par \par \uc2 And leading off, the House has voted to formally initiate an impeachment investigation into President Biden. Senior Congressional Correspondent Chad Pergram is on Capitol Hill with what exactly this means. Good evening, Chad.\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Mike, good evening. First, the House wanting information from the son, then the House turning to an investigation of dad. The House just voted to formalize an impeachment inquiry for President Biden, the vote 221 to 212.\par \par \uc2 The House could have been on firmer legal ground enforcing its subpoena for \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  to appear for a deposition today had the impeachment inquiry already been in place. Instead, \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  skipped out.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 REP. TOM COLE (R-OK): All we're trying to do is get the needed people who have been blocked or refused to cooperate to come in and testify under oath before Congress.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): The House lacked the votes to initiate the investigation for months. But moderates from swing districts finally climbed on board, Democrats accused their colleagues of a fishing expedition.\par \par \uc2 REP. ERIC SWALWELL (D-CA): I want to give James Comer some credit, because after 50,000 pages of depositions and secret hearings and closed hearings, I think if we give them enough time, he's going to prove that \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  is \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's son.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Republicans say it would be malpractice not to investigate further.\par \par \uc2 REP. TOM MCCLINTOCK (R-CA): The Democrats would have a simply turn a blind eye to mounting evidence of a family influence peddling scheme that implicates the president.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): Democrats contend the GOP wants to foster a counter narrative for 2024 as former President Trump goes to trial, Democrats also allege the GOP is out for revenge, since they impeach the former president twice.\par \par \uc2 REP. KATHERINE CLARK (D-MA): This has never been about the truth. This is about avenging \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . This is about undermining our democracy and influencing the 2024 election.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (on camera): A vote to bless the investigation is not the same as impeaching the president.\par \par \uc2 Also, the House just voted to condemn anti-Semitism on campus. The vote 303 to 126 with three voting present, Mike.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Chad Pergram live on Capitol Hill with the breaking news. Chad, thanks very much.\par \par \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  could face a contempt of Congress charge after refusing to give a transcribed interview before House lawmakers today. The president's son has insisted on testifying only in public.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent David Spunt starts us off on Capitol Hill. Good evening, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Mike. good evening to you. \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  skipped today's plan deposition, instead setting his own terms for a public statement. That move did not sit well with angry Republicans and \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  now may be facing more trouble down the pipeline.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HUNTER BIDEN\uc2 , \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 'S SON: I have been the target of the unrelenting Trump attack machine, shouting, where's Hunter? Well, here's my answer. I am here.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (voice over): \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  defiant as he arrived outside the U.S. Capitol Building moments after he was due in this room for a closed-door deposition under oath. His nametag visible, the chair empty.\par \par \uc2 Outside the president's son maintained, he will only testify in a public hearing.\par \par \uc2 H. BIDEN: Republicans do not want an open process where Americans can see their tactics, expose their baseless inquiry or hear what I have to say.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): House Republicans planned for the younger Biden to arrive. Instead they found themselves watching his comments on T.V.\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMES COMER (R-KY): This has been a 10 month investigation, a credible, transparent investigation that has produced a lot of troubling evidence.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (voice over): He's also facing to criminal indictments in federal court, speaking to Congress on the record could jeopardize those legal battles, as any comments on Capitol Hill could be used in court. House Democrats defending \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  from Republicans.\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMIE RASKIN (D-MD): Because they wanted to conduct the deposition in a closed door interview so the public couldn't see it. And so, they could continue to cherry pick little pieces of evidence and distort and misrepresent.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (on camera): Mike, House Republicans said they're going to move forward with contempt proceedings if it eventually passes the House to hold \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  in contempt of Congress, it may land on the desk of U.S. attorney Matthew Graves here in Washington D.C. who was appointed by Hunter's father, the president. Mike.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: David, there's also a word from the Supreme Court on the January 6 riots and former President Trump.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: Mike, that's exactly right. The Supreme Court agreed to hear an appeal from a former police officer who was charged with obstruction. He's one of more than 300 defendants who have been charged with trying to disrupt the congressional certification of the 2020 election. That includes the former president who faces a march trial March 4th, and other inter -- election interference claims.\par \par \uc2 But the Supreme Court review of this all could actually put \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's March 4th trial date in jeopardy, Mike.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: We will follow it. David Spunt live on the Hill. David, thanks a lot.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Former President Trump's 2020 election interference case in Washington is on hold tonight. The judge ruled today the proceedings will wait until Mr. Trump pursues his claims that he's immune from prosecution, the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  will decide whether to hear arguments on the matter.\par \par \uc2 President Biden's team is backing up a bit from yesterday's comments about Israel's tactics against \uc2 Hamas\uc2  terrorists. This comes amid a major shift in the president's handling of the legal problems involving his son.\par \par \uc2 White House Correspondent Peter Doocy has the story. Good evening, Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: -- we're being told President Biden and \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  coordinated on a public statement, the wall that has been up between them to protect President Biden has been breached, as officials are telling us that President Biden was familiar with what Hunter was going to say before he popped up on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: If I called my dad and said, I am about to violate a congressional subpoena. You'd probably say, son, you shouldn't do that. Was there any attempt by President Biden to talk Hunter out of it today?\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: You're going to call your dad Steve?\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: I call him Dad, usually.\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: Look, I don't have anything else to add. The president was familiar with what Hunter was going to say today.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): White House officials argue Congress has better things to do than scrutinize a private citizen.\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: He and the first lady are proud of his son, how he's rebuilding his life back up. He's going to focus on what is needed on the American people.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): President Biden spent part of the day talking infrastructure.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Pretty good, huh?\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): As officials cleaned up his comments to donors about international support for Israel, "They're starting to lose that support by the indiscriminate bombing that takes place".\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, PRESS SECRETARY, PENTAGON: The president was expressing concerns, again, as I said, about the civilian casualties that we've seen.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): And about \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2 , "He's a good friend, but I think he has to change and with this government, this government in Israel is making it very difficult for him to move."\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: President realizes that Israel is a powerful, vibrant democracy and any change in the government is going to have to be determined by the Israeli people.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (on camera): So, even though President Biden said that Israel was indiscriminately bombing Gaza, which would be a war crime, his team says he was not meaning to accuse them of committing war crimes and that despite this recent criticism, the president remains a friend of Israel, Mike.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Peter Doocy live on the North Lawn. Peter, many thanks.\par \par \uc2 Stocks jumped after the Federal Reserve kept its key interest rate unchanged, the Dow surged 512 to close at a new record high. The S&P 500 was up 63, NASDAQ jumped 201.\par \par \uc2 The Israeli military says Palestinian militants ambushed troops in a dense Gaza City neighborhood Tuesday killing at least nine of them. The fighting comes as \uc2 Hamas\uc2  puts up stiff resistance in areas that Israel has isolated and padded with airstrikes for more than two months.\par \par \uc2 Senior Foreign Affairs Correspondent Greg Palkot is in Tel Aviv tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GREG PALKOT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR FOREIGN AFFAIRS CORRESPONDENT (voice over): A pledge from \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  that Israel will fight until victory. After one of the deadliest days for the \uc2 IDF\uc2  and its war against \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , nine soldiers killed, some top officers along with several injured Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Hamas\uc2  militants ambush troops on a mission in the Gaza City neighborhood. Another soldier killed further north.\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2 , ISRAELI PRIME MINISTER (through translator): The true heroes of Israel, my heart and our hearts go out both to the fallen and the families we know the lives have changed.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT (voice over): \uc2 Hamas\uc2  putting up a fight in other ways, sending rockets against nearby Israeli towns. Its leader declaring any future government in Gaza without \uc2 Hamas\uc2  would be a delusion.\par \par \uc2 Some viral video knows of Israeli soldiers acting inappropriately in Gaza got a tamp down from the military. A spokesman saying disciplinary steps will be taken as the lives of civilians in Gaza continue to be disrupted, heavy rains turning 10 cities into a muddy chaos. The \uc2 U.N.\uc2  warns of a major health crisis.\par \par \uc2 LYNN HASTINGS\uc2 , UNITED NATIONS: The shelters have long ago exceeded their full capacity with people lining up for hours just to get to a toilet.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT (voice over): And anger grows here among families of hostages still held in Gaza. A protest in front of the prime minister's office and emotional rally tonight in Tel Aviv. 28-year-old Idan Tel Aviv. 28-year-old Adan Shtivi remains a captive. His father wants him home.\par \par \uc2 ELI SHTIVI, FATHER: I want my son. 67 day. No phone. I don't know what you feel.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT: You want your son?\par \par \uc2 SHTIVI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PALKOT (on camera): I want my son, he says and the hostages keeps getting worse. An announcement late this day of the death of another \uc2 Hamas\uc2  captive, thought to be still alive. His name is Tal Haimi, the 41-year-old was described as a pillar of his community. He leaves behind a wife and three young children. The death of branded a murder, Mike.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Greg Palkot live in Tel Aviv. Greg, thanks very much.\par \par \uc2 A barrage of Russian missiles targeted Kyiv today wounding at least 53 people. A series of loud explosions rocked Kyiv as the city's air defenses were activated.\par \par \uc2 For the second time this week, Ukraine's Air Force said Russia launched 10 ballistic missiles toward the capitol, and all were innocent intercepted by air defenses.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, we get more, Bret Baier's conversation with Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  during his trip to Washington yesterday.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: The narrative, the storyline is that the counter offensive has stalled and that there hasn't been much movement. What do you say when you hear that to someone who a critic who says it's not going anywhere?\par \par \uc2 VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY, UKRAINIAN PRESIDENT (through translator): So, this narratives of the Russian Federation. And it has spread in this narrative. So the United States of America, unfortunately, have this in Europe, we are advancing and we have had a successful operation in the Black Sea. And today, 7.5 million tons, independently of the Russian Federation, is transported to the countries of Asia and Africa into some countries in Europe and we are fighting this hunger.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So, you would definitively say it is not a stalemate?\par \par \uc2 ZELENSKYY (through translator): Yes, that's real, they're fact. So, we wanted that in our directions, in our counter offensive. And the cells of our country to achieve a tangible result and including our partners, including the United States of America, political partners, they wanted in the south of our country to move as quick as possible.\par \par \uc2 If really in the south, we need to break this total control in the sky of the Russian Federation before moving forward. Then, frankly speaking, we haven't managed to do that, we have not had enough of air defense system and the long range artillery and today we talked to with President Biden about that and we need to strengthen to ramp up our air defense systems and we still need to strike the final decision on our long range artillery.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Up next, two of Washington's major sports teams may be headed out of the nation's capital.\par \par \uc2 And later, San Francisco's cleanup for a major international conference is a thing of the past. We will show you.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: The \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  has agreed to hear a bid by the Biden administration to preserve broad access to the so-called abortion pill. The justices took up the appeal of an August decision by the New Orleans-based Fifth U.S. Circuit Court of Appeals that would curb how the Mifepristone is delivered and distributed, borrowing telemedicine prescriptions and shipments by mail of the drug.\par \par \uc2 And yet, another sign of a city in decline. Washington, D.C. could be losing two of its major sports teams to Northern Virginia.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 NBA\uc2 's Wizards and the NHL's Capitals. Senior national correspondent Rich Edson outside Capital One Arena, where the teams currently play. Good evening, Rich.\par \par \uc2 RICH EDSON, FOX NEWS CHANNEL WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT: Yes. Good evening, Mike.\par \par \uc2 When about 30 minutes, the \uc2 Washington Wizards\uc2  tip off here against the \uc2 New Orleans Pelicans\uc2  in about six years. That very matchup could be six-mile south of here in Alexandria, Virginia. The latest blow to D.C.'s image, the Virginia Governor \uc2 Glenn Youngkin\uc2  made the announcement this morning that he's lured away the Wizards and the NHL is \uc2 Washington Capitals\uc2  from D.C. to what he calls an amazing spectacular $2 billion sports and entertainment project.\par \par \uc2 D.C. is already struggling with a significant increase in crime, businesses leaving, and government employees abandoning the district for their home offices.\par \par \uc2 Monumental sports owns the Wizards and Capitals, the company says it plans to move the WNBA's Washington Mystics to the older arena.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JORDAN SILBERMAN, GENERAL MANAGER, CAPITAL ONE ARENA: We will continue to be able to put content and inside Capital One Arena that we'll make sure that it's a busy venue and complimentary to the revitalization of downtown D.C.\par \par \uc2 EDSON (voice over): A monumental had reportedly asked for $600 million in D.C. government money to renovate the 26-year-old downtown D.C. Arena. The city came up with a late $500 million offer. Mayor Muriel Bowser says D.C.'s offers still on the table and maintains her administration will work to improve downtown.\par \par \uc2 MURIEL BOWSER, MAYOR OF WASHINGTON, DISTRICT OF COLUMBIA: Our experience with crime is kind of a blip, is a phenomenon, and we can look back over the last several years and see a lot of contributing factors. But we will drive it down.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 EDSON: The Virginia Legislature still has to approve this plan. Thanks to November's elections, Democrats will control both chambers in the new year.\par \par \uc2 The president pro-tem of the Virginia Senate tweeted "While some people want sports stadiums, I want tolls to disappear from Hampton Roads, and I want recreational sale of marijuana. Guess we will have to find compromise this season.\par \par \uc2 The Alexandria City Council also has to sign off on this proposal to make it official. Mike.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Rich Edson, reporting live. Rich, thanks very much.\par \par \uc2 Up next Bret Baier talks with families of Americans taken hostage by \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . First, here is what some of our Fox affiliates around the country are covering tonight.\par \par \uc2 FOX5 in New York, as a federal appeals court rules former President Trump cannot assert presidential immunity from a defamation lawsuit by writer E. Jean Carroll. The second U.S. Circuit Court of Appeals in Manhattan upheld a federal judge's decision to reject Mr. Trump's claim of immunity, finding he had waited too long to raise it as a defense.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Milwaukee from FOX6. One of the big stories there tonight. The final nights of the Geminid meteor shower as Earth passes through the particles and pieces of an asteroid.\par \par \uc2 NASA\uc2  says the Geminids are one of the few meteor showers associated with an asteroid rather than a comet. The peak of the event will be this week with up to 150 meteors per hour in ideal conditions.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look "OUTSIDE THE BELTWAY", from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Today, President Biden met with families of Americans who were taken hostage by \uc2 Hamas\uc2  terrorists in October. Bret Baier talked with some of them yesterday. Here is that conversation. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Mike, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, we speak with some relatives of American citizens still believed to be held hostage by \uc2 Hamas\uc2  terrorists inside Gaza.\par \par \uc2 They're in town to meet with President Biden. Joining us tonight, Ronen and Orna Neutra, parents of Omer Neutra. Adi and Yael Alexander, parents of Idan Alexander. Jonathan Dekel-Chen, father of Sagui. Ruby Chen, father of Itay Chen, and Itay's brother Roy.\par \par \uc2 Also with us, Liz Naftali. Great aunt to Avigail Mor-Idan, who was released November 26th.\par \par \uc2 Thank you all for being here. I know this is a tough time and I know you have a specific message that you want to deliver to President Biden.\par \par \uc2 I want to talk about each one of your family members, who's believed to be held hostage, Jonathan Sagui last seen where? And tell me something about him?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN DEKEL-CHEN, FATHER OF SAGUI DEKEL-CHEN: Well, Sagui is 35 years old. He is the father of three little girls, a newborn, a 2-year-old, and a 6-year-old. He was last seen on our kibbutz, Kibbutz Nir Oz on October 7th at 930 in the morning. And from the testimony of hostages that were released a couple of weeks ago, we do know from within from inside the tunnels that he has been -- that he is alive.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Adi, you're the father of Idan. Tell me about him.\par \par \uc2 ADI ALEXANDER, FATHER OF IDAN ALEXANDER: Idan grew up in Maryland, actually, moved up to New Jersey, graduated last year from Tenafly High School, and he choose to volunteer to IDF. He was kidnapped October 7th from his post, from the \uc2 IDF\uc2  post. And since then, we have no additional information about this abduction.\par \par \uc2 YAEL ALEXANDER, MOM OF IDAN ALEXANDER: It's tough. It's tough. It's Hanukkah now. Every night we are lighting the candles with his sister, Mika (PH) and his brother Roy. And I'm just feeling sad but full of hope that something is going to happen. Something good and he will return home.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Ronen, Orna, tell me about Omer.\par \par \uc2 ORNA NEUTRA, MOTHER OF OMER NEUTRA: So Omer was born in New York one month after 9/11. I remember that day, and I remember that I couldn't believe that this was happening as I can't believe that this is happening to us now, again. He's like an all-American kid. He loves sports. He was accepted to Binghamton University but decided to defer his school, and he went to Israel on a gap year. And he connected deeply with the country, with his peers, and he decided to volunteer to the \uc2 IDF\uc2 . And he was taken from his post.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Ronan, when he volunteered for the \uc2 IDF\uc2 , what were you thinking?\par \par \uc2 RONAN NEUTRA, FATHER OF OMER NEUTRA: It wasn't easy for us. But as Israelis, Americans, we served in the army as well. It's a reality in Israel that we all live with. It's hard for us to think about him doing the right thing for the country and being on the wrong place on the wrong time.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Ruby, tell me about Itay.\par \par \uc2 RUBY CHEN, FATHER OF ITAY CHEN: Etie also went to the \uc2 IDF\uc2 , protecting values of the western society, liberty, democracy. Happened to be on the board of Gaza. The vision that I keep is that he knows how to do card tricks. Hopefully he has a deck of cards with him with the other people that are held in custody somewhere, hopefully passing time.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And you've got something, passing time.\par \par \uc2 CHEN: They idea that for 67 days, we don't have time. They don't have time. Each day that passes by the probability of getting U.S. citizens back home is not good. And any meeting that I have with officials, I put this on the table to emphasize the fact that you're going back to your family, going to have a nice dinner. We have an empty chair.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Liz, there is obviously a very bright story. You are the great aunt of Abigail, a four-year-old who was released. But there is a horrible part to that story as well.\par \par \uc2 LIZ NAFTALI, GREAT AUNT OF ABIGAIL EDAN: Abigail is a miracle because she survived both of her parents being murdered on October 7th. And she is a miracle because she crawled out from under her father's body and made her way to a neighbor's home. And they took her in. But she is also a miracle because then this mother and her three children and Abigail were taken hostage by \uc2 Hamas\uc2  terrorists. She came back to Israel after 50 days.\par \par \uc2 And today we're celebrating Hanukkah, and Hanukkah is a celebration of light and miracle. And we have Christmas coming up, which is another celebration of miracles. And I look at all of these people here whose children have not come back, and I pray and I ask people who are joining us and watching to pray for the Michelle of Christmas that we should have these young people back home with their families.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jonathan, there's a lot of fear. There's a lot of prayer. There has to be anger. There has to be some feeling of is enough getting done. What is the message to President Biden that you bring?\par \par \uc2 DEKEL-CHEN: President Biden and the administration as a whole have been amazing partners in this struggle to get all of our loved ones back. Great efforts have been made. We ask that even greater ones be invested now. These are critical moments.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Adi, when you hear of investigations about ceasefires and all of the back and forth day-to-day, what do you think?\par \par \uc2 ADI ALEXANDER: I think the hostages have to be returned. This is the real humanitarian crisis. And that needs to happen first. And after that, I think the ceasefire can happen.\par \par \uc2 ORNA NEUTRA: I feel like we are reaching this boiling point we heard people came back there from there that the situation is dire, that they are kept under the ground in the dark, no food.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Edan, strong, can withstand this stuff?\par \par \uc2 YAEL ALEXANDER: I know my son, and I know he is strong. He's a professional swimmer. He is a cool guy, like Edan is the coolest, you know. Loves extreme sports. Like, he is doing everything.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Do you get a lot of outpouring of support from your community? Have people reached out?\par \par \uc2 CHEN: It's been enormous the amount of support that we are getting. I would actually like to move that question to the international community and where the U.S. is getting its support. And, we feel that the United States as the leader of the free world, there are different players in the ecosystem that can be doing more and the U.S. maybe can pressure a bit more, specifically Egypt, Qatar, other players that have influence in order to get a deal done. Sign the dotted line, get the hostages out, which is a humanitarian crisis, as well as what is happening in Gaza. Let's not forget, the Palestinians are also hostages of the \uc2 Hamas\uc2  terrorist organization.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You put the blame on \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , obviously?\par \par \uc2 CHEN: For sure. The question is how to influence \uc2 Hamas\uc2  to get to the negotiation table, as well as the state of Israel.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We should point out that there are other American hostages not represented here, and I'm sure you all speak for them as well. Liz, you mentioned Hanukkah and this festival of lights, a joyful celebration.\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The prayer of the first lighting of menorah. English translation, includes "Blessed are you Lord our God, king of the universe, who performed miracles for our forefathers in those days at this time." And I'm sure you all are hoping for a miracle now.\par \par \uc2 ORNA NEUTRA: We say this every night of Hanukkah. We bless the candles every night and we pray for our children to come home for all 137 of them that are held hostage. And we pray for our leaders. And we ask everyone to pray for our children and to pray for the leaders.\par \par \uc2 CHEN: I would like to also remember in our prayers the 1,500 people that were massacred, burned alive, beheaded by savages. I would like also to mention over 100 Israeli soldiers that have gone into Gaza to find our kids, find our loved ones, and have been killed on that task.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Adi, Hanukkah is going to come to an end. As I read that prayer, what do you think about?\par \par \uc2 ADI ALEXANDER: I have nothing to say right now. So maybe cut it off. It's too -- it's too personal.\par \par \uc2 DEKEL-CHEN: I think every one of us who still has a loved one who was a hostage, has a personal vision or a personal dream that in a way manifest in that prayer that point of light. And for me, it would be that is Sagui, I hope, walks on his own two feet into a room or down a hallway tomorrow, today, and his little girls go leaping into his arms. That, for me, is the miracle that I'm waiting to see.\par \par \uc2 There is a moral imperative to get them out. If there ever is to be hope for any kind of lasting peace in and around Israel, they have to come home alive. And that means now.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Very well said.\par \par \uc2 We appreciate you sharing your pain, your hope, your message with us. This is not similar, but my son started with a lot of health problems when he was a baby. We were in the hospital a lot. And we used to pray we're one day closer to getting home. So we pray that you're one day closer. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 ORNA NEUTRA: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CHEN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 DEKEL-CHEN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Mike, we'll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Bret, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, another surge of migrants preparing to come to the U.S. We will have a live report from the border.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Breaking tonight, we have some amazing new exclusive video from the southern border showing thousands of migrants waiting to get trains in Mexico bound for the U.S. Correspondent Griff Jenkins is in Eagle Pass, Texas, tonight. Hello, Griff.\par \par \uc2 GRIFF JENKINS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Mike. And the Border Patrol agents here in Eagle Pass have encountered more than 2,700 migrants in the last 24 hours and can't take much more. But they are going to get it anyway. FOX sources captures this exclusive video showing thousands of migrants waiting to board trains bound for the U.S. about three hours south of here. Officials expect they will be arriving at our southern border in the coming days. Nowhere in sight do you see here, Mike, Mexican officials attempting to prevent migrants from getting on those trains.\par \par \uc2 Now, this has consistently been the method of how the migrants are getting to this location. This comes as agents here are overwhelmed and reaching a breaking point yesterday. We watched as multiple groups of hundreds of migrants you see here crossing multiple locations simultaneously in this area exhausting manpower and resources. One agent telling me, Mike, that the transnational criminal organizations, or the cartels, know what they're doing.\par \par \uc2 FOX also getting an exclusive insight into what is happening on the Mexican side of the border. Independent Mexican journalist Auden Cabello who has been documenting the migrants -- the migrant crisis, says he has a name for what is driving them in such large numbers. Listen here.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 AUDEN CABELLO, INDEPENDENT MEXICAN JOURNALIST: I call it the selfie factor. So as soon as they cross, they take a selfie. They are communicating with their relatives back home, saying I'm already here in the U.S. So that's encouraging more of them to come. Regardless of what see are seeing in the news that migrants are in the streets like in Chicago or New York.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: And CBP officials confirming that there have been more than 10,000 migrant encounters across the entire southwest border. That means, Mike, that at least half of those selfies may have been turned loose and have a temporary stay into the U.S. Mike, we will send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Griff Jenkins live in Eagle Pass. Griff, thanks a lot.\par \par \uc2 Tesla is recalling more than 2 million vehicles across its model lineup to fix a defective system designed to ensure drivers are paying attention when they use autopilot. Safety regulators say the company will send out a software update to fix the problems. The government says its investigation found autopilot's method of ensuring that drivers are paying attention can be inadequate and can lead to foreseeable misuse of the system.\par \par \uc2 San Francisco's cleanup for the recent Asian Pacific Economic Cooperation Summit did not last long. Homeless people and drug dealers who were relocated have returned. Senior correspondent Claudia Cowan shows us tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 CLAUDIA COWAN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: It was a chance for the city by the bay to shine, and during last month's APEC Summit, San Francisco put on its Sunday best for the hundreds of dignitaries and media visiting from around the world. Homeless encampments near the convention sites were swept away along with needles, trash, and human waste. Locals were hopeful the massive effort to clean things up for this one week would finally lead to permanent change.\par \par \uc2 MARIA, SAN FRANCISCO RESIDENT: Hopefully it stays better. We want a better San Francisco.\par \par \uc2 COWAN: But when the summit ended, the fences came down. The extra law enforcement left, and the homeless, many of whom had just shifted to other neighborhoods, moved back. Video shows the dramatic difference during and after the convention in areas where tents and dealers have returned, along with people who use the sidewalk as their personal bathroom.\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS, (R) FLORIDA: Human feces is now a fact life, except when a communist dictator comes to town. Then they cleaned up the streets.\par \par \uc2 COWAN: Mayor \uc2 London Breed\uc2  says moving forward a recent court order will make it easier to clear sidewalk encampments. Facing a tough reelection fight next year, Breed says she is working hard to build on the momentum of \uc2 APEC\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MAYOR LONDON BREED, SAN FRANCISCO: Are we where we need to be? No. But our goal is to continue it as long as we have the resources that continue from the state and federal partners, which we have a commitment that those are going to be a part of our continual effort.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 COWAN (on camera): But many residents and workers wonder why the city, which earmarks more than $600 million a year to helping the homeless, can't keep the streets safe and clean every week instead of just one. But it was nice while it lasted. Mike?\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Claudia Cowan, thanks very much.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel on \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's defiance and his father's possible impeachment.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. TIM COLE, (R-OK) HOUSE RULES COMMITTEE CHAIRMAN: The procedures we are adopting today closely parallel those the Democrats created in 2019.\par \par \uc2 REP. JIM MCGOVERN, (D-MA) HOUSE RULES COMMITTEE: These Republicans, they don't work for you, the American people. They work for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . This whole thing is an extreme political stunt.\par \par \uc2 REP. JIM JORDAN, (R-OH) HOUSE JUDICIARY COMMITTEE CHAIRMAN: -- we've heard is \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  had no involvement. Now his son does a press conference when he is supposed to be being deposed and says he wasn't financially involved.\par \par \uc2 JARED MOSKOWITZ, (D-FL) HOUSE OVERSIGHT COMMITTEE: The only thing they have uncovered is that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is the father of \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 . That's it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: And late today, of course the House passed an impeachment inquiry vote with Republicans staying unified with that. Let's bring in our panel, Byron York, chief political correspondent of "The Washington Examiner," Michael Shear is a White House correspondent for "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 ", and Morgan Ortagus, former State Department spokesperson. Panelists, welcome. Morgan, what does it all mean?\par \par \uc2 MORGAN ORTAGUS, FORMER STATE DEPARTMENT SPOKESPERSON: It means that we're going to spend a lot of 2024 investigating President Trump and now President Biden as well. And so I think you're going to see duel and competing headlines. I can tell you, as someone who was in the State Department and in the administration during the first Trump impeachment, it is a distraction for the staff whenever this happens. So, President Biden's team is going to have the challenge of trying to run a reelection campaign, trying to deal with an impeachment inquiry. We're also having to deal with an unprecedented amount of war and chaos around the war and Israel and Ukraine. God forbid, hopefully not over China and Taiwan, but that also is the big prize in all of this, so to speak, as it relates to foreign affairs.\par \par \uc2 So he has a full plate for 2024, his staff does. And so the headlines are investigating all of the presidents.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: All right, let's put on the screen reaction from the Biden team to the impeachment inquiry vote. They say, quote, "Instead of doing anything to help make Americans' lives better, they are focused on attacking with me with lies instead of doing their job on the urgent work that needs to be done. They are choosing to waste time on this baseless stunt that even Republicans in Congress admit is not supported by facts." Michael, like it or not, it is a headache for the president and his team, no doubt. Right?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL SHEAR, NEW YORK TIMES: Right. No, I agree with Morgan completely. I think it is a headache. I think the way they are going to try it make it advantage or at least turn it into an advantage if they can, assuming that President Biden is running against former President Trump in the fall of next year, they will use the specter of the impeachment to tie the former president to members of Congress on the Hill to cast it as a big stunt, to sort of remind voters of the kind of chaos and overreaching that the Republicans under President Trump did when he was in office. And I think they are going to try to turn that into electoral advantage.\par \par \uc2 But I agree, and I covered both impeachment the last time around, and it's a big headache for the White House.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Byron, is this a win for Speaker Johnson keeping all Republicans in line for this vote?\par \par \uc2 BYRON YORK, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Yes. He could only afford to lose three, so it was actually a win. But previous impeachments are virtually completely partisan as well. I think that Republicans have accomplished a lot. They have shown, I think, without any doubt that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  did not tell the truth about not knowing his son's business dealings and then not having taken part in his son's business dealings. I also think they showed beyond any doubt that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was the product that \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  was selling in his influence peddling operation.\par \par \uc2 What they have not shown is this direct link in which \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  actually benefits, gets paid for this, which is, of course, the whole enchilada as far as the impeachment is concerned. So while they have, I think, a great reason to continue this investigation, the only way you can ever get any bipartisan support at all is if they actually show those payments.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: While we were coming on air there was also a vote in the House to condemn antisemitism on college campuses. It passed overwhelmingly, but what caught our eyes was the Democratic votes, 84 voting yes, 125 voting no, three voting present. Obviously, they have different reasons why they may have voted that way, but are you surprised by that, Morgan?\par \par \uc2 ORTAGUS: No, I think most of the Democrat who voted against it will say this is because of the legislation also called for the university presidents to step down. What I think we should focus on is \uc2 Elise Stefanik\uc2  had a major win in getting this legislation through. It was passed, as you said, by a large majority of both parties of the Republicans and then 85, I think it's 85 Democrats. And considering how fearful so many Jewish Americans feel right now, they worry about their kids going on campus, they are worried about wearing openly Jewish symbols like yarmulkas or Stars of David or whatever it may be, because they are fearful in this country, sometimes symbolic statements don't mean a lot in Congress. This one does. To Jewish Americans around this country, to know that the \uc2 United States Congress\uc2  has their back and is condemning antisemitism is really important to Jewish families around the country.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Does this open the door for campaign ads for the Democrats who voted no on this, Michael?\par \par \uc2 SHEAR: Sure, I think just about anything these days are open for campaign ads.\par \par \uc2 I think, look, the Democratic Party that we have right now is very split on some of these issues, I think probably more so than the Republican party in terms of -- in terms of support for the Palestinian cause and the situation that's going on in Gaza right now versus support for Israel. And the party is really going through a lot of painful division, and I think the vote reflected that.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Byron, your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 YORK: It shows you how politicized this has become. I think you saw in the Harvard Corporations deliberations over what to do with President Claudine Gay coming in unanimous support, so many on the faculty coming out in support, I think there was this feeling that if Republicans want this, we don't want this. And I think here you have seen Republicans almost unanimously in favor of this, and Democrats badly split on it. So, it has become another political fight.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Panelists, thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 Finally tonight, a special delivery.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Our Austin community full of friends, people we know, and strangers all across the world have helped us with that, and we are very grateful for it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: With the holiday season in full swing, one Florida teen is doing everything he can to get toys to hospitalized kits. Monday Teddy Pentz and his mom Krista donated nearly 300 toys to Arnold Palmer Hospital for Children. The donations are part of the foundation started by the two called Teddy's Giant Heart Project. How awesome is that? Great job, Teddy, and your mom.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT, despite previous warnings of doom, the federal government is getting deeper into the artificial intelligence realm.\par \par \uc2 Remember, if you can't catch us live, set your DVR, 6:00 eastern, 3:00 on the west coast. Thanks for watching SPECIAL REPORT. THE INGRAHAM ANGLE is next. Goodnight.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020231214ejcd00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Supreme Court Asked To Quickly Decide \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's Immunity; White House Mum...\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Peter Doocy, Rich Edson, David Spunt, Brit Hume, Nate  Foy, Bryan Llenas, Griff Jenkins, Tyrus, Juan Williams, Mollie Hemingway \par \uc2 7,843 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 December 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Supreme Court Asked To Quickly Decide \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's Immunity; White House Mum On Latest Hunter Biden Developments; Joe Manchin Mulls Third- Party Presidential Run; \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  Mocks Prosecution Attempts By Biden "Thugs"; Israel Accused Of Using White Phosphorus Munitions; Military Leader Vows To Push \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  Back; Hamas Rocket Barrage As Humanitarian Crisis Worsens; United Nations Expected To Vote On Gaza Cease-Fire; University Of Pennsylvania President Resigns After Testimony To Congress Regarding Antisemitism On Campus; Arizona Democratic Governor Sends Biden Administration Bill For Reimbursement Of State's Costs In Handling Border Crossings; FOX News Contributor Tyrus Discusses His New Book; Former President Trump Has Big Lead In Republican Primary In Iowa Caucuses According To Latest Poll; Special Counsel Jack Smith Asks Supreme Court To Rule If Former President Trump Can Be Prosecuted On Charges Relating To Overturning 2020 Election; New Visitor's Center Opened At Netherlands \par \par \uc2 American Cemetery For Fallen U.S. Soldiers\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Harold?\par \par \uc2 HAROLD FORD JR., FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: They play a basketball game. The kids don't cheer until they score their 10th point. And then they all storm the court.\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL LEGAL ANALYST: Wow.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: They're dressed in costume. It's a great, great tradition.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Judge, you have eight seconds.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: I can't do it in eight.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: She's not going to do it in eight but she's going to do it tomorrow. We're going to give our double time. All right. That's it for us. Have a great night, everybody. Hi, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Double time for the Judge tomorrow. OK, thanks.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Good evening. Welcome to Washington, I'm Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, the Supreme Court has agreed to fast track consideration of a case testing whether former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has immunity from some of the criminal charges against him. The man in charge of prosecuting the former president on charges of election interference asked the nation's top court today to step in. And now the highest court in the land has responded.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent David Spunt has details tonight. Good evening, David\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Bret, good evening. They certainly did just moments ago, the justices instructed the former president's attorneys to weigh in by next Wednesday, December 20th.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court has a body of nine that tries to remain apolitical. But those justices are now being asked to enter the nerve center of the 2024 presidential election.\par \par \uc2 Special Counsel Jack Smith has made an emergency plea to the justices asking them to rule on whether \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is immune from prosecution. Smith petitioned the court earlier this afternoon asking for a quick decision. The federal judge overseeing the case ruled the criminal trial involving alleged 2020 election interference can proceed and that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  does not deserve a lifetime "get out of jail free pass" just because he was president.\par \par \uc2 Trump's attorneys say even a former president is immune from criminal prosecution for actions that fell within his White House duties while in office. They were expected to ask an appeals court to review the issue.\par \par \uc2 However, Bret, Smith has gone above the appeals court and now wants the Supreme Court involved. The special counsel writing it involves issues of exceptional national importance, "whether a former president is absolutely immune from federal prosecution for crimes committed while in office or is constitutionally protected from federal prosecution when he has been impeached but not convicted before the criminal proceedings begin".\par \par \uc2 As of now, the trial is scheduled to begin March 4th with jury selection, that is just one day before Super Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Smith wants this decision from the high court fast so he can begin the trial and finish the trial before the election next November, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. David, also, next year, President Biden's son may face two trials in the heat of this election. What's the latest on that tonight?\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: Well, \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's legal team filed to have the federal gun charges in Delaware dropped late this afternoon. His attorney Abbe Lowell went a step further and said David Weiss was illegally appointed special counsel. He says special counsel is supposed to be from outside the government when appointed. Weiss also a U.S. Attorney in Delaware.\par \par \uc2 What was argument don't may not hold up because it's happened before, Bret, former Attorney General Bill Barr appointed Connecticut U.S. Attorney \uc2 John Durham\uc2  to become special counsel. Durham held both jobs for a period of time. Now we wait to see when \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  will be arraigned on those tax charges in California, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We'll follow it all. David, thank you. We're not hearing anything from the White House about the most recent developments in the Hunter Biden case. White House Correspondent Peter Doocy is on the North Lawn tonight with the latest. Good evening, Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Bret, House Republicans believe the White House is holding out on them. And when President Biden says he never profited from his son's overseas business deals, these lawmakers want him to prove it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): President Biden hasn't said a word about his son Hunter's latest indictment and his team's responses to Republicans looking for records have been unsatisfactory to the House Speaker.\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE JOHNSON (R-LA): The impeachment inquiry is the next necessary step because the White House is now stonewalling our investigation.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): White House officials maintain there is no there there, connecting the president to his son's overseas business dealings.\par \par \uc2 ANDREW BALES, DEPUTY PRESS SECRETARY: He's focused on what matters to American families, not \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2 's conspiracy theories.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): It's unclear if Hunter will comply with a congressional subpoena to testify Wednesday as his lead attorney accuses the Justice Department of overcharging.\par \par \uc2 ABBE LOWELL, \uc2 HUNTER BIDEN\uc2 'S ATTORNEY: What you can say is Hunter was late in filing and paying taxes in certain years. That is a given, as are millions and millions of Americans who do the same thing.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): President Biden raised $15 million for his campaign during the west coast swing telling donors about Trump. The other day he said, he'd be a dictator only on the first day. Thank God, only one day.\par \par \uc2 The political focus lately has been more on Trump than on the economy as the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  calculates that most new jobs created under Biden are either in health care or government. President Biden and his team are hoping it's enough to keep unions in key swing states with him. Appealing today to Pennsylvania firefighters.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: All my friends either became cops, firefighters, or priests and here I am\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (on camera): Yes, here he is hosting \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  tomorrow, trying to make the case to Congress that they need to fund Ukraine's military if they want to save democracy.\par \par \uc2 But because the White House is not playing ball on border funding, that is unlikely to happen before the end of the year, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We'll be speaking with President Zelenskyy tomorrow here on SPECIAL REPORT. Peter, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Former President Trump is beating President Biden in another head-to-head national poll. The ages of the two presumptive major party presidential nominees once again though becoming an issue.\par \par \uc2 West Virginia Democratic Senator Joe Manchin made a comment about a possible third party run over the weekend, citing those ages. Here's Senior National Correspondent Rich Edson.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 RICH EDSON, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Facing two federal indictments, the former president of the United States has a warning for the current one.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: They've opened up a Pandora's box and I only can say to Joe is be very careful what you wish for.\par \par \uc2 EDSON (voice over): Playing off Trump's own words, Democrats have warned a second Trump term would threaten American democracy.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I said I want to be a dictator for one day because I want a wall. Right? I want a wall and I want to drill, drill, drill.\par \par \uc2 EDSON (voice over): While Trump spoke in New York, his GOP rivals campaigned in Iowa where the former president has a commanding lead in the polls.\par \par \uc2 The Des Moines Register says 51 percent of likely Republican caucus goers choose Trump as their first choice. Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is at 19. Former \uc2 U.N.\uc2  Ambassador Nikki Haley is at 16. Haley touted a different poll.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY, FORMER U.S. AMBASSADOR TO \uc2 U.N.\uc2 : How do you get Congress to do things? Well, if you win by 17 points, that's a pretty good mandate.\par \par \uc2 EDSON (voice over): That referring to a Wall Street Journal national poll showing Haley over President Biden by 17.\par \par \uc2 Trump leads Biden in that poll by four. Haley is also securing some major financial support. Ken Langone the billionaire co-founder of \uc2 Home Depot\uc2  says he's on board.\par \par \uc2 Two weeks ago, the Koch affiliated \uc2 Americans for Prosperity\uc2  action pledged its support. Former Secretary of State Hillary Clinton is expected to pitch voters on a second Biden term. She's already forcefully defending the president's Israel policy.\par \par \uc2 NBC reports Clinton hosted a Biden fundraiser last month that raised nearly a million dollars. President maintains Trump would be an authoritarian in his second term. Saying at a fundraiser last week, "Trump's not even hiding the ball anymore".\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 EDSON (on camera): Then there are the potential third party candidates this weekend. Retiring Democratic Senator Joe Manchin noted that Biden and Trump are 81 and 77 years old and that voters are ready for them to pass the torch to a new generation. Someone closer to 76. Big reveal here, that's Manchin's age, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I just -- 76 is how old Manchin is and he's saying 77 is too old?\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Little tongue in cheek I think, yes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Wow. OK, Rich, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Today, lawyers for two Georgia election workers played audio recordings of graphic and racist threats the women received after \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  falsely accused them of fraud. That trial is underway to determine how much the former Trump attorney might have to pay those two women. He's already been found liable in the defamation lawsuit.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in Fox News Chief Political Analyst, Brit Hume. Brit, good evening, you know, we've just been talking about the former president, his legal issues. And you know, you look at this calendar versus the calendar of voting, and it is going to come together in all of these different dates.\par \par \uc2 And now you have the Supreme Court possibly weighing in, or at least considering whether to take the case of fast tracking the appeals process.\par \par \uc2 BRIT HUME, FOX NEWS SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST: Yes, it's been quite a year already, Bret and next year is going to be even more intense, I think, that all this coming together, you got actually in the courts on a whole range of fronts.\par \par \uc2 You know, we've got the primaries finally starting. And I have to say this about the polling that you've been citing regarding Trump and his lead over his two chief rivals, who are only a fraction of what he commands in the polling, at least, and that is it, you know, something -- it always seemed possible that something could happen and his support would begin to erode. And that one of the other press, both of his top rivals would start to really gain ground on him.\par \par \uc2 What he's getting -- we're getting pretty close to the Iowa caucuses now. To be followed immediately by New Hampshire. And none of that has actually happened.\par \par \uc2 He seems in a stronger position than ever. So, I think we can look ahead to -- you know, it would take something quite remarkable and dramatic now to change the course of this -- of this situation.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. And there are polls, there are snapshots in time. But the Des Moines Register poll this weekend has some exponentially up. If you look at the RealClearPolitics average in Iowa. You take a look at that 50 to 19.7.\par \par \uc2 You look at New Hampshire, these are the early states again and it is exponential again over Nikki Haley, and then South Carolina, her home state. And he again has huge leads.\par \par \uc2 So, to your point, polls are not votes. But they do give you an indication of where things are trending five weeks from tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 HUME: Right. And, Bret, you know, in terms of Iowa, which is first up, and I've had people say to me, well, you know, if Haley and DeSantis, were to combine forces, you know, you add up what they both have are holding in the polls, we'd have a real race.\par \par \uc2 But the problem is that polling has indicated that a lot of DeSantis support, if he were to drop out of the race, would go to -- would go to Trump.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, it's not even clear that that scenario would unfold in the way that people who are opposed to Trump would hope.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Last thing, this is the president over the weekend about abuse of power. He's kind of taking on this charge by President Biden, his administration, head on, take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Every abuse of power, the fake news is ridiculously claiming. I will commit is in fact, being committed by crooked Joe and his thugs. They're trying to lock up their political opponents, they want to lock up their political opponents in particular me, thank you very much. It's an honor.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You know, he's doing what the former president has done on the stump. How big a deal do you think this is by the time you get to these cases ahead of a convention?\par \par \uc2 HUME: Well, we'll see. You know, the effort in to get the Supreme Court to rule quickly on Trump's immunity claim seems clearly to be an effort to speed up, or at least to make sure that the trial timetable doesn't move. And this would seem palpably to be an effort to get him convicted of something before the election.\par \par \uc2 If he were elected, anyway, it wouldn't matter, because he could pardon himself after the fact. But, you know, you look at these cases. And Trump does have a point in this sense, which is that he has been prosecuted by people who are answerable to the incumbent president, his opponent. And that has left a bad taste in a lot of people's minds. And I think it counts powerfully for the fact that Trump seem to have been undamaged, at least at the polling is any indication by the allegations against him, which, you know, in some cases are quite serious, and they're quite a lot of them.\par \par \uc2 But they look at this and they say, you know, the Jack Smith indictment. Jack Smith, which works for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . So, I think that actually ended up helping Trump.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We'll follow it all. Brit, as always, thank you.\par \par \uc2 HUME: You bet.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Up next, the latest word on how long the Israel-\uc2 Hamas\uc2  war may last from Tel Aviv's top military official, well bring you there.\par \par \uc2 First, here's what some of our Fox affiliates around this country are covering tonight. Fox 29 in Philadelphia as two police officers are injured during a shootout with a suspect. 40-year-old man accused of ramming his vehicle into a police car was seriously injured in the exchange of gunfire.\par \par \uc2 Fox 9 in the Twin Cities as a judge vacates the first degree murder conviction for a man who spent nearly two decades in prison for the death of a flower shop owner in 2004. The judge ruled the police lineup procedures violated Marvin Haynes' due process rights. His family has always insisted Haynes was sleeping at home when that murder occurred.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Los Angeles from Fox 11, our affiliated there. One of the big stories there tonight. Barbie tops the Golden Globe Awards nominations. The blockbuster received nine of them including Best Picture and acting nominations for Margot Robbie and Ryan Gosling. \uc2 Oppenheimer\uc2  was next with eight nominations.\par \par \uc2 So, that's tonight's live look outside the Beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Attorneys for a pregnant Texas woman who sought court permission for an abortion, says she's left the state to get the procedure. 31-year-old Kate Cox was awaiting a ruling from the Texas Supreme Court over her challenge to one of the country's most restrictive abortion bans.\par \par \uc2 A judge gave Cox permission last week but that decision was put on hold by the state's all Republican High Court. Her lawsuit says doctors told \uc2 Cox\uc2  her fetus has a high likelihood of miscarriage or stillbirth and that inducing labor or carrying the baby to term could jeopardize her ability to have another child.\par \par \uc2 The justice department has filed charges against 10 alleged members of separate carjacking rings here in the Washington, D.C. area. A 19-count indictment and a 33-count superseding indictment charged the suspects with holding victims, stealing vehicles, and then selling them. The defendants are believed responsible for at least a dozen incidents involving carjacking or attempted carjacking in the district or parts of Maryland.\par \par \uc2 Stocks sprinted to highs for the year ahead of inflation data and a Federal Reserve meeting. The Dow gained 157 today. The S&P 500 finished ahead 18. The NASDAQ was up 29.\par \par \uc2 Israel's defense minister is pushing back against international calls to wrap up the country's military offensive in the Gaza Strip. The defense minister saying the current phase of the operation against the \uc2 Hamas\uc2  terrorist group will take time.\par \par \uc2 Let's get an update on the fighting and the global reaction to it. Correspondent Nate Foys in northern Israel tonight with the latest. Good evening, Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE FOY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Bret. Good evening.\par \par \uc2 So, as Israel pushes farther and farther south in the Gaza Strip, tensions here on the Northern Front continue to escalate with \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  launching three anti-tank missiles today, destroying two homes in northern Israel, and this happened immediately after Israeli shelling killed the mayor of a village in southern Lebanon.\par \par \uc2 And this comes as Israel is answering new questions about its military tactics.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 FOY (voice over): Israel is facing more international pressure, accused of reportedly using U.S.-provided white phosphorus munitions in an attack that injured civilians last month in southern Lebanon.\par \par \uc2 YOAV GALLANT, MINISTER OF DEFENCE, ISRAEL (through translator): The \uc2 IDF\uc2  and the entire security establishment operate according to international law.\par \par \uc2 FOY (voice over): The \uc2 U.S. State Department\uc2  calls the report concerning and promises to learn more.\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW MILLER, SPOKESMAN, UNITED STATES STATE DEPARTMENT: There is legitimate military use for white phosphorus, but that does not include using them on civilians.\par \par \uc2 FOY (voice over): \uc2 IDF\uc2  Chief of Staff Herzi Halevi, said the country is ready for a full-scale war with \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  if needed.\par \par \uc2 FOY: Artillery units like this are used to fire shells across the border into Lebanon and strike \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  targets where attacks originate from.\par \par \uc2 FOY (voice over): This is a former \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  ammunition warehouse. The Israeli strike caused a chain reaction explosion. \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  operates immediately across Israel's border in direct violation of a 2006 \uc2 U.N.\uc2  resolution from the end of the Second Lebanon War.\par \par \uc2 Israel now promises to push \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  back.\par \par \uc2 LT. GEN. HERZI HALEVI, CHIEF OF THE GENERAL STAFF, \uc2 ISRAEL DEFENCE FORCES\uc2  (voice over): There is a military way to do this. The beginning of which is also what you are doing here to damage, to deter, to kill \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 FOY (voice over): In central Israel, \uc2 Hamas\uc2  rockets struck the city of Holon, outside Tel Aviv. In Gaza, \uc2 Hamas\uc2  terrorists fight Israeli soldiers from homes, schools, and places of worship.\par \par \uc2 Israel reports some \uc2 Hamas\uc2  fighters are surrendering, as the \uc2 IDF\uc2  cements full control of Palestine square in Gaza City. But the humanitarian crisis worsens.\par \par \uc2 PHILIPPE LAZZARINI\uc2 , COMMISSIONER-GENERAL, UNITED NATIONS PALESTINE REFUGEE AGENCY: The people not just sleeping on the concrete.\par \par \uc2 FOY (voice over): For every person in the Gaza Strip, nowhere is safe.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 FOY (on camera): Bret, tomorrow, the \uc2 U.N. General Assembly\uc2  is set to vote on a resolution demanding an immediate humanitarian ceasefire in Gaza. And this comes after last week, the U.S. vetoed a \uc2 U.N. Security Council\uc2  resolution demanding the same thing. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Nate Foy, live in northern Israel. Nate, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, one down two to go. That is how a Republican lawmaker reacted to the resignation of an Ivy League university president over an anti-Semitism protests. We'll bring you that story.\par \par \uc2 First "BEYOND OUR BORDERS" tonight. A Russian missile attack on Ukraine's capital destroyed several homes, leaves more than 100 households without electricity in that area. The pre-dawn attack on Kyiv came amid warnings Russia will step up its attacks on the country's energy infrastructure, as winter sets in.\par \par \uc2 Again, I will speak to Ukrainian president, \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  here on set tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 Countries at the \uc2 U.N.\uc2  climate talks in Dubai, move closer to reaching what critics called a watered down final deal. The proposal avoids calls from more than 100 nations to phase out fossil fuels.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Tokyo from EarthCam, one of the big stories there tonight. Japan's prime minister says he plans to take appropriate steps over his governing party's widening slush funds scandal.\par \par \uc2 There is speculation Fumio Kishida may purge implicated Cabinet members in a major shuffle this week.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories "BEYOND OUR BORDERS" tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: "FOX WEATHER ALERT" now, residents in Tennessee and Kentucky, picking up the pieces after more than a dozen tornadoes touched down Saturday. At least six people were killed. A preliminary report from the National Weather Service says the tornado that hit Clarksville, Tennessee was an F-3. Two Tennessee counties canceled school because of that damage.\par \par \uc2 Pressure is growing on the presidents of Harvard and MIT, following the resignation of the leader at the \uc2 University of Pennsylvania\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 It follows the uproar over their comments to Congress, during a hearing about campus anti-Semitism. Correspondent Bryan Llenas has the latest tonight from New York.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 BRYAN LLENAS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: There are growing calls for Harvard to fire its president, Claudine Gay, accused fostering an unsafe environment for Jewish students after failing last week to unequivocally say that calls for the genocide of Jews violate the school's code of conduct.\par \par \uc2 CLAUDINE GAY, PRESIDENT, \uc2 HARVARD UNIVERSITY\uc2 : It can be, depending on the context.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: Calls for Gay's removal come after the \uc2 University of Pennsylvania\uc2 's President Liz Magill resigned over the weekend following similar comments on Capitol Hill. In a report not independently corroborated by FOX News, gay is being accused of plagiarizing multiple sections of her 1997 PhD thesis. In a statement to "The Boston Globe" Gay says, "I stand by the integrity of my scholarship. Throughout my career I have worked to ensure my scholarship adheres to the highest academic standards."\par \par \uc2 About half of Harvard's faculty, some 700 members, are now urging Harvard's governing boards not to fire Gay, writing in a letter, quote, "The critical work of defending a culture of free inquiry in our diverse community cannot proceed if we let its shape be dictated by outside forces." Critics claim there is a double stashed at Harvard, curious how a school ranked dead last nationwide in a 2024 college free speech ranking has allowed antisemitic protests to continue unabated.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: There is a clear double standard for Jews. Let's be real. If any other group was facing calls for genocide against them, would the response be nothing? Would the response be muted?\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: Congress says universities are on notice amid a new report that \uc2 Stanford University\uc2 's antisemitism co-chair aligned with anti-Israel groups and concluded antisemitism was not a problem on campuses in a 2017 paper.\par \par \uc2 REP. JIM BANKS, (R-IN) HOUSE EDUCATION COMMITTEE: We're going to subpoena their records, their emails, any documents, anything that proves that these universities aren't creating a safe environment for their Jewish students.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 LLENAS (on camera): Tonight, \uc2 Stanford University\uc2  tells FOX News they have full confidence in Professor Ari Kelman's ability to lead the Antisemitism Committee. "The Harvard Crimson" newspaper reports Harvard's governing boards met today and discussed Gay's future. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: More on this later with the panel. Bryan, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Capitol police\uc2  say 49 people were arrested this morning for an illegal protest inside the Hart Senate Office Building. One person climbed a statue in the atrium. He was taken into custody separately for resisting arrest. One woman was also detained on that charge.\par \par \uc2 There is an expectation tonight that talks about the president's border policy will intensify this week. One of his key congressional allies says the White House will become more engaged, while rejecting the latest Republican proposal as unreasonable. A member of the president's own party has apparently had enough of the president's border policy. Arizona Democratic Governor \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2  has sent the administration a huge bill for reimbursement of the state's cost. Correspondent Griff Jenkins is in Eagle Pass, Texas, with the latest tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 GRIFF JENKINS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Outnumbered 200 to one, migrants from Senegal, Bangladesh, Egypt, and China pushing Border Patrol agents to a breaking point in Lukeville, Arizona, where they are seeing an average of 2,700 encounters a day.\par \par \uc2 KATIE HOBBS\uc2 , (D) ARIZONA GUBERNATORIAL NOMINEE: For far too long Arizona has borne the brunt of federal inaction on our southern border, and I'm tired of it.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: Arizona's Democratic governor sending a letter to President Biden along with a $512 million reimbursement bill for costs related to the border cris.\par \par \uc2 A thousand miles away, agents in Eagle Pass, Texas can't catch a break, alongside a local pecan farmer experiencing the same frustration.\par \par \uc2 MAGALI URBINA, TEXAS PECAN FARMER: We appreciate the Border Patrol. I would ask the federal government to do more for them and stop this insanity.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: CBP sources exclusively tell FOX the numbers are so unsustainable they have begun releasing as many as 5,000 migrants a day into the U.S. In a disturbing acknowledgment, 30 individuals have matched the \uc2 FBI\uc2 's terrorism watchlist since October 1st, putting border state lawmakers on high alert.\par \par \uc2 REP. TONY GONZALEZ, (R-TX): It's only a matter time of what happened in October 7th in Israel, something similar happens here. We've got to put Americans above everything else.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: Congressman Gonzales also says his Senate colleagues are close to a deal on the border as Texas Governor Greg Abbott says it better include more than just funding.\par \par \uc2 GOV. GREG ABBOTT, (R) TEXAS: We need policy changes, policy changes that will stop people from crossing the border illegally into the United States.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 JENKINS (on camera): And Bret, at this hour, senators on both sides of the aisle are urging action on a border bill, calling the current situation untenable. But one senator close to the negotiations, Republican \uc2 James Lankford\uc2 , tells FOX News that the talks have stalled because the White House is only talking to Democrats. Lankford not optimistic of a deal this week. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Griff Jenkins live along the border. Griff, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, we talk to former professional wrestler, current political commentator here on FOX, Tyrus. He is the author of the new book "Nuff Said." Hey, Tyrus, great to see you.\par \par \uc2 TYRUS, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Always a pleasure. Always a pleasure.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Congrats on the new book. I want to talk about it in a second. I have read some of it. It's awesome. I want to talk to you about his college professor, college president scuffle, and what we saw on Capitol Hill and how it's being covered. "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " covered it this way. "As fury erupts other campus antisemitism, conservatives seize the moment." The word seize comes up on some of these stories in how it's covered. And then you had "Saturday Night Live" big back and forth comedy skit, supposedly comedy, about that hearing. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Now, I'm going to start screaming questions at these women like I'm Billy Eichner. Antisemitism yea or nay?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I'm sorry, what?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Yes or no, is calling for the genocide of Jews against the code of conduct for Harvard?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Well, it depends on the context.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: What? That can't be your answer.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That's supposed to be Congresswoman \uc2 Elise Stefanik\uc2 . What do you make of this and the fallout from it?\par \par \uc2 TYRUS: You know, it's -- I watched it the first time and I was just shocked. And I'm the first one I don't think there -- I think there is a joke in every situation, but it's always timing. It kind of makes you just go, wow, what was the point? It wasn't funny. It wasn't classy. What that hearing was showed the true face of a lot of so-called progressives and people who supposedly care about everybody. We saw that they only care about their narrative and what is best for them.\par \par \uc2 There is no joke when it comes to antisemitism. Just like there is no joke when it comes to being racist against people of different colors and stuff. So there was no joke in this. And if anything, they were trying to make fun of the conservative asking the question -- and this has nothing to do, and great point. You said conservatives seized the day. No, no, no. This has nothing to do with politics. This is a human being thing. If you are a brother, a father, a husband, an uncle, anything you saw that happened October 7th should have nothing to do with political beliefs or how you vote. It is how you feel about fellow humans and seeing people, women and children slaughtered, babies being decapitated. That has nothing to do with politics. That should reach in the core of your stomach and your chest and should drive you insane, and you should want retribution for those families and those people that went through that that. That was a massacre it. It has nothing to do with the voting rights.\par \par \uc2 And the fact that they are trying to bring that into that, to make it about conservatism and liberalism is laughable. That's the only thing funny about the whole thing. And there is nothing to laugh about antisemitism.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. You mentioned families. And listen, I have been through some of this book. It's really great, just like your first one was. I just want to ask you about this one chapter. You talk about an abusive father and how your mother tried to protect you. You call it sea turtles. The chapter is called "Sea Turtles." And you write, "If you don't know about sea turtle mothers, they will travel the world until they find the perfect beach with the perfect sand, the perfect temperature, the perfect moisture, the perfect environment. Then she crawls out on to a difficult terrain she is not built to walk upon. But she does it. Then she digs a hole deep enough to keep predators away from precious eggs. Then she covers them up with sand. She has set them up for success. Exhausted, she drags herself back into the water. She will never see what happens to them, but she has given them their best chance at a good life. My mother did what she had to do to give us the best chance at a good life."\par \par \uc2 So well-written, Tyrus. And you kind of pour your heart out about your experience.\par \par \uc2 TYRUS: Well, one of the toughest things -- and you are so good at this. Let me take my glasses off.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 TYRUS: When I wrote that, it was about sometimes you just can't fix -- when families break up, sometimes the best thing to do is to let go. And with my particular family, we've tried reunions. We tried to put -- but there's some things that you just can't get past. One of the things that my mother struggled with is I look exactly like my father. And it was the face, and if you read both my books, you know what she went through trying to raise us. Sometimes the toughest thing to do is to let somebody go that you love because they are better off without you. You are better off without them.\par \par \uc2 So when I talk about sea turtles, she still prepared me. In the chapter I talk about there is a confrontation with my father where my mother chose to protect me, even though having your child say certain things to you in the moment you don't ever want to have to repeat or force them to say, but she was doing it to keep me safe. So I have a lot of respect for my mother, and I will always love her. Our lives are better apart. Sometimes we need to focus on everything that was good for your family with you, but when it's time to move on, you need to move on. But the quote is, if you actually read it, that's not what it means.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. It was really well-written, and you said don't be afraid to speak your mind. You have spoken your mind in two great books. Congratulations. Thanks for coming on.\par \par \uc2 TYRUS: Thank you for your time, boss. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Up next, the panel with the latest polls in Iowa, the activity in the presidential race, and all that's going on with the special counsel. That's next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: Starting on Inauguration Day, 2025, the Biden dictatorship will be over, and we are going to bring our country back from hell.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Trump just talks the talk. We walk the walk. He likes to say America is a failing nation. Frankly, he doesn't know what the hell he is talking about.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 DEAN PHILLIPS\uc2 , (D-MN) PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: When you are almost certainly to lose to the most dangerous person in the world and you continue to run, I think that's damaging.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Do you think \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  today in 2023 is fit to be president?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY, (R) PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Should he be president? I don't think he should be president.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Five weeks to the Iowa caucuses and we have a new "Des Moines Register" poll. They are well respected in Iowa, and it shows the former president with a big, big lead currently in Iowa over Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . And there you see Nikki Haley. The other question here is the view among Trump legal challenges. Among Iowa voters, 73 percent of Republican caucus goers think the former president can beat President Biden regardless of his legal challenges. This comes as the special counsel appealed to the Supreme Court to fast track a possible appeal there. And the Supreme Court saying we are going to hear whether we are going to fast track that and whether they are going to take the case. That came in right before the show tonight.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in our panel, FOX News senior political analyst Juan Williams, Franco Ordonez, he's a White House correspondent for National Public Radio, and Mollie Hemingway, editor in chief at "The Federalist."\par \par \uc2 First, Mollie, the polls are overwhelming. They're just polls, they're snapshots in time. It's not actual votes. But we have never seen heading in five weeks from the first voting something like this. Yes, it's very much a challenge for the anti-Trump forces in that both of the top two candidates are really seeming to be competing for third place in these caucuses and primaries.\par \par \uc2 It's been a pretty stable situation for Trump in that he has always had the lead. The way that it's not stable is that his lead is