 POLITICAL ANALYST: And pardon himself potentially. So Jack Smith pushed this to the Supreme Court. He wants the Supreme Court to rule here, and, obviously, thinks that he will win with the Supreme Court. And, of course, even if he doesn't win, well, then why go through the appellate process? Why go through the trial, because then nothing can be charged to a man who says that he is immune from those charges given that he was president of the United States? So, let's get it on. And what Jack Smith is saying, let's ring the bell. Let's just do this now.\par \par \uc2 MOLLIE HEMINGWAY, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Just because Jack Smith wants things done quickly doesn't necessarily mean the Supreme Court is going to want things done quickly. He just keeps asserting, we need to get this done quickly. It's obvious he wants to have a quick and easy conviction. That's the point of trying to put your political opponents in prison. But the Supreme Court doesn't share that viewpoint that Jack Smith has. They are probably less inclined to get involved.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: To your point, here is Jonathan Turley on that very question.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN TURLEY, GEORGE WASHINGTON UNIVERSITY LAW PROFESSOR: He decided that he wanted to leapfrog over the court of appeals and go directly to the Supreme Court. The only reason for that is that he is really focused entirely on trying Trump during this campaign and trying to convict him before the election. The question about the Supreme Court is going to feel that that is such an urgent priority that you bring actually this case before Super Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: He has asked for a Trump campaign, the Trump lawyers, by next Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 I want to turn to \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 , "Washington Examiner." She "has emerged as key champion of Biden's 2024 campaign. At a recent fundraiser in Washington hosted by Clinton at her Whitehaven estate, at her Whitehaven estate, the former secretary of state, Women's Leadership Forum, reportedly pulled in nearly $1 million for Biden's reelection. However, her fundraising prowess isn't the only support she's lending to Biden's reelection. Clinton is also stepping up as one of Biden's higher profile surrogates."\par \par \uc2 Franco, obviously, she doesn't have a super frac record in these national elections, but it seems like the Biden folks are leaning on the former secretary of state.\par \par \uc2 ORDONEZ: Apparently so. That certainly seems to be the reporting. I can say, it's only going to give more enthusiasm to many of Biden's detractors. This Hillary Clinton for Trump is kind of, in some ways it's red meat. It's a way to attack Biden, and that's what he does so often. I mean, there is a reason that Trump used to talk about crooked Hillary and switching the name to crooked \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . So I think this is very beneficial for the Trump campaign. I'm very curious to see how the Biden administration responds.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But maybe on the abortion issue she makes that case, like \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  makes it. And that's really the issue that Democrats feel like is their sweet spot.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Absolutely. And not only that, you've got to think about right now when we look at the polls, and the polls do not look good for Biden at this moment, a lot of that has to do with the Democratic base, young people, people under 40 and young women, and a lot of opposition on Israel and all that, inflation. Tomorrow he's going to get potentially good numbers on inflation. But right now, it's the Democratic base that's weak. And Hillary Clinton is good there.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Really quickly, it doesn't look like all of this funding, the supplemental, is going to get done.\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: It looks like they thought that if they claimed to pretend to care about border security that they might be able to get more funding for Ukraine. That doesn't seem to be going over as well.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Again, we will talk to President Zelenskyy tomorrow here on SPECIAL REPORT. We will see.\par \par \uc2 Up next, a new memorial to America's war dead in Europe. We'll bring you there.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: In tonight's SPECIAL REPORT Salute, we take a look at how American soldiers killed in War World II are being honored overseas. A new visitor's center opened today at the Netherlands American Cemetery. Last week I spoke with \uc2 American Battle Monuments Commission\uc2  Secretary Charles Djou jew about the grand opening as the commission celebrates its 100th anniversary.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 CHARLES DJOU, AMERICAN BATTLE MONUMENTS COMMITTEE SECRETARY: It's our duty, it's our mission to make sure that our country doesn't forget, that the world doesn't forget what America has done.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The \uc2 American Battle Monuments Commission\uc2  was established in 1923 following World War I.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: When American and French soldiers fought shoulder to shoulder through those terrible years of war.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The commission worked with family members to bury soldiers overseas in cemeteries constructed and maintained to honor those killed or missing in war. One-hundred years later the commission maintains 26 cemeteries and 32 memorials, honoring more than 200,000 soldiers.\par \par \uc2 You have visitors that come from around the world, right?\par \par \uc2 DJOU: Probably behind our site at Normandy at the D-Day landing fields and Manila is probably the most visited site.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Today a new visitor's center opens at the Netherlands American Cemetery.\par \par \uc2 What will that include?\par \par \uc2 DJOU: As we get a little bit further in time, we are opening visit centers, museums that can tell the stories because so many of our veterans and the widows and mothers have moved on.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It features original artifacts and personal narratives from battles that took place nearby.\par \par \uc2 DJOU: It is right next to the battlefields from Operation Market Garden and the Battle of the Bulge.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Some of this fighting kind of went back and forth between the Nazis and allied forces.\par \par \uc2 DJOU: This particular area changed hands five times, went from Allied to Axis to Allied to Axis, back to Allied.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The site honors nearly 10,000 American soldiers.\par \par \uc2 DJOU: Not only do we have Americans from all of our states, the Dutch people adopted every single one of the American service members who fell in the Second World War and still to this day continue to take care of the grave sites.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The Dutch people often visit that cemetery bringing flowers and researching the lives of service members.\par \par \uc2 DJOU: These communities, particularly in the Netherlands, remember what America did for freedom and for democracy. This is a story that sometimes, though, I think more Americans need to hear.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER (on camera): Very powerful.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow, Ukraine's president comes to Washington looking for funding. You will hear from him on SPECIAL REPORT exclusively. Plus, we talk to the new House Speaker Mike Johnson. Fair, balanced, and unafraid. Here's Laura.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020231212ejcb00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 College Leaders Under Fire After Excusing Anti-Semitism; Democrats Ask \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  To Help \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's Flailing Campaign; Soft On Crime Backlash: Houston's New Mayor Vows To Crack Down On Lawlessness; Law And May I Take Your Order? Woman Sentenced To Fast-Food Work After Chipotle Attack; Office Sugar Pushers Trying To Curry Favor\b0\par\par\uc2 Dana Perino, Jeanine Pirro, Harold Ford Jr., Jesse Watters, Greg  Gutfeld \par \uc2 8,129 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 December 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: The Five\par \uc2 FOXFIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Wish we had more time. Mark Regev, the senior adviser to the Israeli Prime Minister.\par \par \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  will keep you posted on these latest developments and the confusion over just the number of hostages still left and held by \uc2 Hamas\uc2  or \uc2 Hamas\uc2  operatives, there's just not a firm number on it. That'll do it here. Here comes THE FIVE.\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Hello, everyone, I'm Dana Perino along with Judge Jeanine Pirro, Harold Ford Jr., Jesse Watters and Greg Gutfeld. It's 5:00 in New York City and this is THE FIVE.\par \par \uc2 One down, will the two others follow? UPenn President Liz Magill resigning in disgrace after she was unable to condemn anti-Semitism raging on her campus during a congressional hearing last week. And the Harvard Board of Governors reportedly meeting behind closed doors to determine if their president Claudine Gay should be the next to go.\par \par \uc2 Remember, she insisted that we should consider the context on when students call for the genocide of Jews.\par \par \uc2 Protesters swarming Harvard's campus with billboard trucks that call for her firing, but she is getting some support. About 700 members of Harvard's faculty, urging the board to spare Claudine's career in the name of, "resisting political pressures".\par \par \uc2 And "Saturday Night Live" seems to think that the real villain here is the congresswoman who asked the question, the show going after \uc2 Elise Stefanik\uc2  in a sketch making light of her.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Now I'm going to start screaming questions at these women like I'm Billy Eichner. Anti-Semitism, yea or nay?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I'm sorry, what?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Yes or no, is calling for the genocide of Jews against the code of conduct for Harvard?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Well, it depends on the context.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: What? That can't be your answer Upenn, lady. Same question, yes or no?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Well, we are serious about stopping all forms of hatred, anti-Semitism, Islamophobia.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The second one. And keep in mind, if you don't say yes, you're going to make me look good. Am I winning this hearing?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERINO: So, imagine, Judge, that they actually do a dress rehearsal for "Saturday Night Live". And they still went with this. But we found out just before the show that Cecily Strong was initially going to be a part of the sketch and she backed out saying she was uncomfortable with it.\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL LEGAL ANALYST: Yes, she was uncomfortable with it. And I think it really speaks to her integrity, because she had actually -- had actually gone through the -- when they did it ahead of time.\par \par \uc2 But I think that the identification of \uc2 Elise Stefanik\uc2  as the -- as the antagonist here, as opposed to recognizing what is going on in the universities just shows how clueless "Saturday Night Live" was.\par \par \uc2 I mean, this is not funny. They couldn't make it funny. \uc2 Elise Stefanik\uc2  is not funny, the whole issue is not funny.\par \par \uc2 And you know what, these presidents instead of cloaking themselves in this free speech, you know, at the same time, they are willing to shut down conservative speech.\par \par \uc2 And it's interesting at Harvard, where Claudine Gay is still president. Apparently, there was a deanship held by a black law professor, who then joined the legal team of Harvey Weinstein, and they threw him off the -- out of the university, they took away his deemed ship, because they didn't like what he did. And yet they can't say that killing Jews and going for genocide is worth throwing off campus.\par \par \uc2 It's like they don't know what the conduct or the context is. What is the context other than calling for the death of Jews, and the fact that you've got Jewish students on campus who are afraid?\par \par \uc2 And the kudos goes to people like Bill Ackman and the rest of them, who are not going to tolerate it, because the President is not standing up for this. No one is standing up for the rights of the Jewish students who are being intimidated and threatened and you know, locking themselves up as far as Cooper Union is going in a library.\par \par \uc2 So, I think more than anything, what they've got to do is they've got to get rid of all of them. Magill is the first one. And by the way, she's not totally gone. She still has her -- she's still on the faculty. So that's kind of like a smoke and mirrors.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: I wanted to ask you about that, Jesse. The fact that so at the -- Magill, the president resigns, but it's not like she resigns as now is looking for work. She just resigns and goes back to being a tenured professor.\par \par \uc2 