hink it's -- you know, they're -- she's educating a spoiled brat that work, dealing with the public is hard work.\par \par \uc2 We -- and the fact is, we aren't punishing people anyway, so why not teach him a lesson, right? Make shoplifters, you know, resell the stores they rob. Make people who vandalize clean up their neighborhoods. I don't see that as a problem at all. And if they don't do it, then you lock them up. I'd be for that.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: And we're saving money because we don't have to house them and feed them in prison.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: We can just eat the burritos and go home at night.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: I love that the judge had a feeling that this person needed to be taught a lesson about empathy. And I think it shows that there's dignity in work. And like, I did -- I never worked in fast food but I waited tables for a long time, and I am hyper sensitive about how wait staff is treated. And I'm a s big tipper.\par \par \uc2 By the way, if you ever go out to breakfast, double the tip always because breakfast is the least expensive meal and those people work very hard to make sure that your eggs and your toast and your coffee are exactly the way you want them. People are the most picky at breakfast.\par \par \uc2 So, I like this. I thought that it was -- again, the judge is showing their discernment, like what do they think is best. And who are we to judge them?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: You know, Dana, it's usually the people that don't brag about being big tippers that are the big tippers.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Yes, like you.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Like me.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: I'm not bragging about it. I'm encouraging you to be a better tipper. So, if you go to breakfast, you better tip double.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: OK. Everybody, double the bill at breakfast. That could hurt you, Harold. I know the brunches you go to, Four Seasons, Ritz Carlton.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: So, I'm a big fast-food guy. I love -- matter of fact, for lunch today, I had \uc2 Chick-fil-A\uc2 . And I'm a Popeye's guy and I'm a Wendy's guy.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: You don't look it.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: I like \uc2 McDonald's\uc2  but I think --\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Yes, he does.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: The -- and I park cars. I understand the importance of it. But you said it well. You got -- you got to -- and Judge, you said it last week on the show, so I hope you -- I hope I remind you of this. You got to educate spoil brats. Part of punishment in the legal system is to do two things. To ensure that the person that there's a proportionate punishment for whether it's jail or whatever for the person that committed the crime. And two, part of that punishment is deterrence.\par \par \uc2 And that judge believes because the person threw a burrito in the face of an employee -- I mean, how do you throw a burrito in the face of any human being? And the judge believes I guess after listening to the testimony that maybe this defendant didn't respect that kind of -- respect the person that worked at Chipotle which I think is kind of quasi fast food.\par \par \uc2 So, I think this is a -- I think it's a creative and perhaps brilliant way to deter not only this person, but perhaps other people, and to make people think more highly of humans who service food in restaurants who you tip generously.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Thank you, Harold. I do. Judge Jeanine.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: A lot of people commit crimes for different reasons, greed, they're evil, they're angry. People like this are stupid, OK. And she needs to be put in the shoes of the person that she assaulted, that she abused, that she harassed. And you know, the judge gave her an option. And I don't know what everybody is complaining about. To me it's just -- it's just white noise. I could care less about it.\par \par \uc2 The truth is this woman will learn for the rest of her life what it's like to be disrespected by people who treat you like garbage. And it happens all the time. And whether it's education as it relates to, you know, a young kid who puts a swastika on a school who really knows nothing but doesn't even understand the history of what that swastika means and the -- and the tremors that people go through when they see that.\par \par \uc2 You need -- we need to educate a certain segment. This woman needs to be educated. The judge did absolutely the right thing. I did stuff like this all the time. You know, if you want to mess things up, you're going to clean up the block for the next two months at a time of day when everybody sees you including your friends, OK.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: And that's all I have to say about that.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: That's the end of it.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: Judge, I agree 100 percent with you.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: That's a good punishment.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: OK. "THE FASTEST" is up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: Welcome back. Time for "THE FASTEST." First up, we all have that one coworker who loves to tempt the office by bringing in some homemade cookies or sweets. But why? Why do they insist on torturing our waistline with such sugary delights.\par \par \uc2 Well, the Wall Street Journal has a theory on what those office sugar pushers really want basically, and it's a bribe. And they're using the food to disarm or curry favor.\par \par \uc2 Now, in fairness, someone gave us some cookies the other day from a -- from a party that she hosted that was -- seemed to be a smashing success. D.P., were you -- do you resemble this article or what were you thinking when you brought us this cookies.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Well, I mean, I do -- I think the biggest pusher in the building is Johnny, right? He brings us all those sugary treats. I think he likes it. Have you seen Johnny? He must have the metabolism of like a hummingbird. So, I can't -- I can't handle it but I admire it.\par \par \uc2 I don't think that -- and especially for people like Lily who bake, I don't think that they do it because they try to curry favor. I think they love to bake but they don't want to have all those cookies at home by themselves, so they bring them here and they like to make people happy.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: As someone that likes sweets, do you have a theory about this or a position on this?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: You can't buy me with cookies. Jewelry maybe, but not cookies.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: Cookies -- let me write that down. Jewelry but not cookies. When you wear a sweater like that, Mr. Gutfeld, you really are inviting people to give you sweets.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: This is The Melvin's Christmas sweater. See, it says Melvin's. OK, first, I got to point out before I make my point. The Wall Street Journal has been doing a lot of these stories lately about people that upset them. Like, it's about a coworker making noises or somebody bringing in food or there's a relative -- there is somebody at the Wall Street Journal who's very upset all the time about everything because every freaking day, the Wall Street Journal has a new story about so -- when so and so doesn't answer your email, this is what you should do. It's like there's some really angry person on that floor.\par \par \uc2 Look --\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: This is our in-house newspaper.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes. It's a -- a lot of people give you food to alleviate the guilt because they want to enlist you in their gluttony. But people do this to me all the time at work and it's because I'm in great shape. Probably in the best shape of anybody on floor 21. So, they're trying to fatten me up out of envy. They're trying to bring me down. I try so hard to be disciplined. I work out every morning. I watch what I eat and drink. And they just want -- they want to bring me down with their little cookies and their little snacks. It's not going to work.\par \par \uc2 Look at me. Does this guy look a day over 40?\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: My Colonizer, when you think about sweets and bribing people at work with your fantastic cookie skills, making skills, is that what you're thinking?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Well, when anybody ever gives me anything, the first thing that runs through my mind, what's their agenda.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: No. You have Johnny eat it. Test it.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: I say -- the second thing I say is, are they trying to kill me? And then I say, what do I owe them. Those are the types of selfish things that should be running through everybody's mind when anybody gets a sweet. Of course, this is stupid. And I know what's going on at the Journal, Greg.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: What?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Now, everyone's back to the office.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: And it's all annoying. Everything is annoying.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Everything is annoying them. Everything -- go back and look at all of the stories that we have. They're all from that same person.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Well, either somebody is upset or they have some really annoying people that work there.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: "ONE MORE THING" -- "ONE MORE THING" is up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Time now for "ONE MORE THING." Jesse. Sorry, I thought it was THE FIVE. All right.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: It's me. And we have big news.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: What?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: A new addition to THE FIVE family.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Who?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Our incredible stage manager, Allison, is going to be a mother.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: Hear, hear, hear.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Welcome to the world, Levi Jackson Conley. Great name, seven pounds, 14 ounces, great hair, just like Greg. Congratulations, Ali.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: What do you say?\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: Congrats. Congrats.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Take all of the maternity you need.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Beautiful. Yes, you do. Congratulations.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Tonight, "JESSE WATERS PRIMETIME," we have \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2  and Mike the Situation.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Oh, where's he been?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: He's been in prison.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: OK. Quickly, I was in Michigan for Kyle Jennings and Friends. It was a great night of country music songwriting. Lee Thomas Miller, Tony Arata, Bob DiPiero. Amazing. Kyle Jennings is also really great. We had a really good time. I don't even remember singing that song with Peter, but apparently we did. And a podcast just up with Matt Whitlock this week just posted. Greg.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Tonight, we got a great show, even with Brian Kilmeade. Emily Compagno, the great Walter Kirn, the wonderful Kat Timpf. That's tonight at 10:00. Watch it or die.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Harold.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: I love Kilmeade. Taylor University, big, big tradition they have. They play a basketball game. The kids don't cheer until they score their 10th point. And then they all storm the court.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Wow.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: They're dressed in costume. It's a great, great tradition.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Wow.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Judge, you have eight seconds.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: I can't do it in eight.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: She says she can't do it in eight but she's going to do it tomorrow. We're going to give our double time.\par \par \uc2 All right, that's it for us. Have a great night, everybody. Hey, Bret!\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FOXFIV0020231212ejcb00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Biden Struggles Mount As 2024 Heats Up\b0\par\par\uc2 Sean Hannity, Tomi Lahren, Brian Brenberg \par \uc2 8,044 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 December 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Hannity\par \uc2 SHAN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SEAN HANNITY\uc2 , FOX NEWS HOST: And welcome to "Hannity".\par \par \uc2 And tonight, we are less than 24 hours away from our exclusive town hall with former President \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  in Davenport, Iowa. By the way, if you'd like to join us or attend, you can sign up on hannity.com. It is for free, tickets are free.\par \par \uc2 Anyway in poll after poll, as of now, President Trump is now beating \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  in a hypothetical rematch by pretty significant margin at this point. Many polls are showing it. One key reason for the numbers, well, Trump's actually willing and able to participate in a real town hall and passionately an answer important questions of our time. Biden meanwhile, he can barely answer two or three pre-selected questions without imploding.\par \par \uc2 Now, sadly, the president's mental condition is likely going to get worse, not better. As it stands now, Joe is not doing well. Over the weekend, he looked really, really bad yet again. Despite a very light workload, the president still managed to struggle mightily. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The brothers bound made it all seem effortless and almost magical. They described it as, quote, we'll be walking down the street together and we'll simultaneously begin to sing the same song and the same key. God love you, Billy. I tell you. That's incredible.\par \par \uc2 Before I travel from the Bronx to change the culture forever before they did and by the way, with other movies and movies, she's earned a Golden Globe and a prime time enemy -- enemy. That's me. Prime time Emmy.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: And after his remarks, Joe had trouble yet again, attempting to step away from a podium. He looked dazed and confused shuffled his feet until his wife was able to coax him in the right direction yet again. Somebody needs to help him just like at the Tomb of the -- you know, Unknown Soldier. Here we go again.\par \par \uc2 Biden's only going to get worse from here. Unfortunately, yeah, it tends to get worse and worse when you have cognitive decline. It is degenerative.\par \par \uc2 Now, his reelection bid defies all logic. I cannot think of a public appearance that your president has made now in months where he's not mumbling, stumbling, bumbling in his speech and/or dazed and confused about where to go and what to do next. And tonight, whether Biden realizes it or not, he has a huge problem on the horizon, as a matter of fact many problems.\par \par \uc2 FOX News has confirmed, House Republican leaders, they will most likely push forward with the vote to initiate an impeachment inquiry next week. Now, this after Biden has once again been caught in a lie. You might recall. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Mr. Vice President, how many times have you ever spoken to your son about his overseas business dealings?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I have never spoken to my son about his overseas business dealings.\par \par \uc2 I have never discussed with my son or my brother or anyone else anything having to do with their businesses, period. And what I will do is the same thing we did in our administration. There will be an absolute wall between personal and private and the government.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Do you stand by your statement that you did not discuss any of your son's overseas business?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Yes, I stand by that statement.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Now, according to newly unearthed bank records subpoenaed by the House Oversight Committee Chairman \uc2 James Comer\uc2 , not only did \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  discuss his son's business ventures, but he also received direct payments from one of Hunter's corporations.\par \par \uc2 Now, \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's attorneys claim -- well, their client was merely paying his father back for a truck that Hunter financed. But keep in mind that these 2018 payments were not made from Hunter's personal account. Instead, one of Hunter's many LLCs deposited the money into Joe's account, an account used to conduct business in China.\par \par \uc2 So why did Hunter pay his father from a business account? And why did Joe lie about ever discussing business -- foreign business deals with his son, his brother or anybody for that matter?\par \par \uc2 Now, last month, Comer revealed two very unusual checks to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  from his brother also purporting to be loan repayments. Now, we've also long reported texts and emails from Hunter complaining about having to fork over half his income to pops. Another email from Hunter's business partner discussing setting aside 10 percent for the big guy, old Tony Bobulinski told us who the big guy was.\par \par \uc2 Another document revealing that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  used his son's \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2  account to pay bills and, of course, there are photographs and text messages and emails and eyewitness accounts detailing when Vice President Biden had his meetings, his phone calls, his letters and even dinners, Cafe Milano, with Hunter's foreign partners.\par \par \uc2 Now, these are not business leaders or Wall Street executives. Instead, Hunter and other Biden family members, they were raking in massive amounts of money, tens of millions of dollars from sketchy oligarchs, well- connected foreign nationals and some of our country's biggest geopolitical foes like Russia, oh, China, Ukraine, Romania, Kazakhstan, you name it and other countries.\par \par \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 , who admittedly at the time was struggling with addiction, had no relevant experience at all with finance or in the gas, coal and energy industry. Getting paid millions for what? Now, that's the 20 plus million dollar question, isn't it? As Hunter's former business partner Devon Archer stated, they were buying access to the Biden brand and possible influence over U.S. policy that could, well, could some maybe think about calling that potentially, maybe , needs to be investigated as bribery?\par \par \uc2 Let's look at Article 2, Section 4 of our Constitution. The president, the vice president and all civil officers of the United States shall be removed from office on impeachment for and conviction of treason, bribery and other high crimes and misdemeanors.\par \par \uc2 Now, here's just one example of what we're talking about. According to a U.S. Senate Committee on Homeland Security and Governmental Affairs report in 2014, when \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was vice president, \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  received a $3.5 million wire transfer from a woman, former first lady of Moscow, Elena Baturina. She's also a Russian oligarch. Her husband, yeah, was the mayor of Moscow.\par \par \uc2 Baturina was known as, you know, the first lady of Moscow. By the way, she wasn't sanctioned with many other Russian oligarchs after the invasion of Ukraine. I'm sure just a coincidence, and was one of the wealthiest people in Russia, deep ties to \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  himself. And yet, following Russia's invasion of Ukraine, the first lady of Moscow was one of the few oligarchs not targeted by \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's sanctions. This reeks of corruption.\par \par \uc2 Remember, Congress has a sworn duty to uphold the U.S. Constitution, and check the executive branch. And now, there is more than enough evidence for a trial. For once in his life, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , he's got to be held accountable. If Democrats truly believe that foreign interference is a serious threat like they've been lecturing us over and over again, that Russia collusion is a clear and present danger, then they will join with Republicans. They will hold Biden's feet to the fire, otherwise, all of their righteous indignation has been nothing but -- well, frankly, show.\par \par \uc2 Here now with more, House Oversight Committee Chairman \uc2 James Comer\uc2  himself.\par \par \uc2 All right. Let's explain this. I noticed the media is going after you hard, saying, that, well, that that doesn't prove anything. That's only a loan repayment from Joe. There's an awful lot of loan repayments that they talk about, I would imagine though, Congressman, correct me, if I'm wrong, if it's a loan repayment, wouldn't we have a record of the loan being made? Have you ever been able to get that that information?\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 JAMES COMER\uc2  (R-KY): Absolutely not. And, of course, if you made a loan to someone, you would have a check going out to that person, in addition to the check coming back for the so-called loan repayment. That's what that email was we talked about on your show last week, Sean. I mean, the bank questioned \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  about what was the reason for the $5 million wire from China. He apparently told the loan officer, it was a -- it was a loan.\par \par \uc2 So the loan officer said, well, we're going to need documentation to prove to the bank examiners it was a loan. Do you have loan documentation? He said no.\par \par \uc2 So we're supposed to believe that China gave Hunter Biden a loan for $5 million? If you look at the last four payments that we found with ties directly to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , they amount to a half a million dollars. And in every instance, they've said this was a loan repayment and that's just hard to believe, Sean, unless you've got documentation to prove that was a loan, which obviously according to the emails from the banks, they do not.\par \par \uc2 But whether it was a loan or not, it's not important, Sean. What matters is the way the money was paid to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was directly through influence peddling. And now, this is the second payment or series of payments to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  that came directly from China. There's no disputing that, even the White House isn't disputing the money to repay \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  if in fact it was a loan came from China.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Okay. You say you and your committee have identified all of these shell corporations. Now, a corporation at an LLC, if they are legitimately doing business, you would see business activity of some kind, would you not? Have you found associated with any of these shell corporations that you are describing them as? Have you seen any real business activity, legitimate business activity?\par \par \uc2 COMER: No legitimate activity. Most of the shell companies don't have a single asset. Again, that was mentioned in the bank email that we talked about on your show last week. The bank examiner said he's supposedly running in this shell company an investment firm but they have no investments. And the other shell companies, they don't have any assets. But we do see wires. We see lots of wires from bad people and bad countries around the world.\par \par \uc2 And one reason we wanted to depose \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 , we want to say, what did you do to receive this income? If it was in fact lobbying, if he was a lobbyist for China, then that's -- that's called a foreign agent, and he's an unregistered foreign agent, and that's a -- that's a penalty of up to years imprisonment and $250,000 fine.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  then was receiving direct benefit from his son being an unregistered foreign agent, so you could make a strong argument that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  violated the FARA laws as well.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Let me ask you about the subpoena because, obviously, our friends in the media mob don't quite understand what a subpoena means. But certainly, I would argue \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  understands it, and Peter Navarro understands it. A subpoena compels somebody to come into Congress to be deposed. That usually takes place behind closed doors. That is the normal procedure, correct?\par \par \uc2 COMER\uc2 : That's correct, 100 percent of the time.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Okay, and they were saying that you don't want to do -- we're willing to do it publicly but not behind closed doors. I would assume there probably are questions you could ask in private in a deposition that you would not be able to ask publicly. Is that true, sir?\par \par \uc2 COMER\uc2 : That's true, both quality and quantity of questions in a public hearing where you have 25 members, they each have five minutes each that includes the time to ask the question. I mean, you -- you might get 35, maybe 40 questions in, if everything works out perfectly.\par \par \uc2 In a deposition, we can ask 600, 700, 800 questions to \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 . And look, we have over 20 shell companies. We have 20-plus thousand pages of bank documents. We expect to get 82,000 emails in pseudonyms.\par \par \uc2 We have lots of specific questions that we need to ask the president's son and the president's brother. And you just don't have enough time in a public hearing. We will have a public hearing and these depositions will essentially be public because there will be transcripts and we will vote --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: So, you're willing -- to be clear, you're willing to have both. Now, let me go to the issue of energy and oil because they -- they seem to come into play big time in terms of this business enterprise.\par \p