dreary look in per -- you know, perspective of the world, that's concerning a lot of people. If that high of a percentage of people are saying, this is keeping them up at night.\par \par \uc2 RANTZ: Yeah, I mean, and think about it from this perspective. So you've got Democrat policies, Biden policies that are responsible for homes being so unaffordable, for the cost of living going up while at the exact same time the quality of life in your neighborhood is going down, and yet those same exact people are the ones who are coming to the rescue of all these folks, trying to play hero, saying hey, don't worry, if you have a lot of student loan, we're just going to erase it, which, of course, just makes the economic situation in this country even worse.\par \par \uc2 They keep creating a problem, then they come up with a solution that isn't actually a solution. It's just a PR campaign to bring some people over to their camp come election season, but ultimately, will hurt each and every one of the people that they pretend to care about.\par \par \uc2 I mean, when you look at either of the polls you mentioned tonight, "The Wall Street Journal" one is so incredibly devastating not just because people feel hopeless right now, but because when you have a population that feels hopeless or helpless, doesn't really think that the country is looking out for them, well guess what? They start to not be as loyal to the country not wanting to fight as hard as they ought to for this country.\par \par \uc2 While at the same time, you got all those activists out there on the far left who are trying to destroy institutions, who are trying to destroy our culture, who are trying to turn everyone against each other. And so, this is just a really bad recipe right now.\par \par \uc2 CHAFFETZ: Yeah, and you see a lot of that across the country, but certainly there in Seattle.\par \par \uc2 Jackie and Jason, thank you for joining us.\par \par \uc2 All right. Coming up, the Biden campaign spent Thanksgiving focusing on the important things, releasing a guide on how to deal with crazy MAGA nonsense at the dinner table. Kaylee McGhee White and Leo Terrell have reaction, coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CHAFFETZ: Welcome back to this special edition of HANNITY. While President Biden spent Thanksgiving in Nantucket, his campaign spent the holiday trashing about half the country, releasing a guide on X for responding to, quote, crazy MAGA nonsense, and, quote, from your relatives on Thanksgiving, which contain numerous attacks on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and, of course, a desperate defense of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's sinking poll numbers.\par \par \uc2 Here now with reaction, "Washington Examiner" opinion editor Kaylee McGhee White and FOX News contributor Leo Terrell.\par \par \uc2 All right. Leo, I heard it. I saw with my own eyes, he promised he was going to unite the country. He's going to bring the country together, the whole reason, the whole purpose of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was he was going to bring us all together. We were just going to come together -- like how in the world does this jive with what he put out?\par \par \uc2 LEO TERRELL, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Now, let me be very clear, he lied. He lied to the American people, and he continues to lie. And what he wanted Americans to do during Thanksgiving is to defend the indefensible, what is basically happening in this country the last two years, they want to try to throw Trump administration under the bus, the American people, Jason is voting for Trump in all these polls because they compared and contrast. The American people were better off under the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 You cannot lie about how we left Afghanistan. You cannot lie about the inflation. You cannot lie about the border, the crime, the attack on Catholics, all these things have occurred in less than two years and the American people not only are upset with the Biden administration, they look at the facts and they know for a fact undisputedly that they were better off under the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 CHAFFETZ: Yeah, Kaylee, I'm sure there's some staffer that put this thing together and thought they came up with a cute little thing. They were going to put out and it would get all this attention. And I guess, you know, here we are talking about it. But when it offends half the country and it's not defensible, how does that actually grow, how does that make it a creative to what he's trying to do and make \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's numbers go up?\par \par \uc2 KAYLEE MCGHEE WHITE, RESTORING AMERICA EDITOR FOR THE WASHINGTON EXAMINER: Well, I think that's the point, Jason. I mean, the guide is better titled, how to gaslight and lie to your relatives 101. Let's just look at the very first claim on this guide which is a response to the claim that the economy was better under \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Biden goes on to list a litany of policies claiming that he supposedly lowered inflation, that he's lowered gas prices, that the GDP is growing -- these are all lies.\par \par \uc2 You do not get claim credit for lowering inflation when you inherited a 1.4 percent inflation rate at the end of the Trump administration and raised it to 7 percent by the end of your first year in office. You do not get to -- claim credit for lowering gas prices when you inherited an average of $2 a gallon per gas at the end of the Trump administration and they were at $4 per gallon by the end of your first year in office.\par \par \uc2 These are outright lies and he's encouraging his own supporters to go and lie to their family members because he knows that it is the only way to justify supporting his campaign come 2024.\par \par \uc2 CHAFFETZ: And, Leo, to Kaylee's point, I mean, I -- they -- what did, they have to run, I recognize they're going to run on abortion. Okay, there's a difference, there's a debate that could be had there. But when it comes to the economy, the border, safety, security overseas, your own personal safety -- I mean, education. What are they going to point to?\par \par \uc2 TERRELL: They can't and here's the thing about it, he's talking about MAGA, how about the Democrats who are leaving in droves from the Democratic Party because they're fed up with the Biden administration? Blacks are leaving. Latinos are leaving, individuals under the age of 30 are leaving. It's not just MAGA.\par \par \uc2 It's Democrats who are leaving the Biden administration because they're fed up, because the press secretary could lie every day, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  can lie every day, but American families who are hurting at the dinner table, on the paycheck, they're not going to lie to each other, and that's what \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  wants. He's in Nantucket right now with power and control, rubbing elbows with the rich folks. The average American citizen realized they were better off under a Trump administration and those polls, all those national polls aren't lying. Trump is going to be the next president.\par \par \uc2 CHAFFETZ: Yeah, how do you actually go out and convince people that -- you know, to your point, you were proud up that we showed the graphic. Economically, oh, no, you were better under \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  -- no, you weren't. They -- I mean, they're trying to rewrite history. People know if they're better off or they're not better off.\par \par \uc2 MCGHEE WHITE: They are, and this campaign is struggling for one simple reason. Any of the justifications people had for voting for Biden back in simply no longer exist. We know Biden isn't a moderate. We know he's not a unifier. He discredited that claim the moment he got up on a stage and smeared half the country as semi-fascist for supporting \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 We know he's not an economics expert. Our wallets tell us otherwise. We know he's a foreign policy nightmare. The rest of the world can confirm that.\par \par \uc2 There is no justification anymore for reelecting Biden. Everything that he had to run on again in 2020, it no longer exists.\par \par \uc2 CHAFFETZ: Kaylee, Leo, happy Thanksgiving. Thanks for joining us.\par \par \uc2 More of this special edition of HANNITY right after the break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CHAFFETZ: Welcome back to this special edition of HANNITY. Unfortunately, it's all the time we have left this evening. This holiday season, if you need a gift for a friend or a loved one, I got a good one for you. Hope you can check out my book "The Puppeteers: The People Who Control the People Who Control America", "The Puppeteers". And I also hope you can catch my podcast, "Jason in the House", just type in "Jason in the House" wherever you listen to podcast, "Jason in the House", think you'll love it.\par \par \uc2 Thank you for turning in tuning in. Thursday on HANNITY, that's a night you don't want to miss. That's the big debate. Thursday, HANNITY.\par \par \uc2 Have a great holiday weekend.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document SHAN000020231125ejbo00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Special Edition of Hannity\b0\par\par\uc2 Kayleigh McEnany, Ari Fleischer, Newt Gingrich, Mike Pompeo, Jason  Chaffetz, Brian Brenberg \par \uc2 8,095 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 November 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Hannity\par \uc2 SHAN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 KAYLEIGH MCENANY\uc2 , FOX NEWS HOST: Welcome to this special edition of "Hannity". I'm \uc2 Kayleigh McEnany\uc2 , in for Sean.\par \par \uc2 And tonight, we want to wish a very happy birthday to President Biden.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: And by the way, I -- it's my birthday today and they can actually sing birthday to me. I just want you to know it's difficult turning 60, it's difficult.\par \par \uc2 Now, just to get here, \uc2 Liberty\uc2  and Bell had to beat some tough odds -- the competition. They had to work hard, show patience, and be willing to travel over a thousand miles.\par \par \uc2 You could say even it's harder than getting a -- a ticket to the Renaissance Tour or -- or -- Brit -- Britney's tour. She's down in -- it's kind of warm in Brazil right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Now I think he meant to say Taylor Swift and that was after he said the turkey saying happy birthday to him. Yes, that happened. That's the oldest president in American history and he keeps getting older.\par \par \uc2 Today, Biden turns 81 and he might not have liked the headlines that he woke up to. Check these out. "The Wall Street Journal" editorial board put it this way, quote, President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  turns 81 years old, running for reelection and his condition is an act of profound selfishness. Ouch.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, "Politico" spoke to a dozen Biden donors and fundraisers and they had a very clear takeaway. Biden campaign facing heat over plans to deal with his age. And in that one, their recommendation was for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  to joke about his age, to call himself Grandpa Joe. Not sure that's going to quell the fears of the American voter.\par \par \uc2 And then there was "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 ", this was based on 30 interviews. The Biden campaign works to ease Democratic anxiety over re-election chances. One fundraiser in the article had this brutal assessment. The bad news is that everybody is wetting the bed inside of Biden world. It's really an unhappy confluence of Biden world donor's cocktail party friends saying, can't you get him not to run, which is stupid and absurd if you know \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . That's selfish \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2 , she just tried to laugh it all away. She said this in September.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: How does the president plan to convince the American people over the next year that 80 is not too old for someone who's running for reelection?\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: Eighty is the new 40, didn't you hear?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: It's the new 40. The American people meanwhile appear to see it differently. Seventy-one percent of voters say Biden is too old to be an effective president, according to "The New York Times"/Sienna College poll in six swing states. That's a pretty big number and Trump, this is brutal for the president too, he beats Biden in the last eight -- eight national polls conducted.\par \par \uc2 Even the liberal lap dog media, they're in full panic mode. According to Obama's onetime senior adviser David Axelrod, quote, I think he has a 50-50 shot here, but no better than that and maybe a little worse. And Axelrod is not the only Democrat who sees the writing on the wall. Take a look at this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Happy 81st birthday to President Biden but he is looking at some not so happy poll numbers. A new NBC News poll puts his approval rating at 40 percent, the lowest of his presidency.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: NBC News polling on a hypothetical Trump-Biden rematch is not looking good for democracy.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I've said it before, do I think \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  can do the job? Absolutely. I don't think he can win the job, and that's what I care about. He's going to lose because the people think he's too old, and perception is reality, I'm sorry.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: If we are heading towards Trump-Biden, a rematch in 2024, how does that look in our polling right now? Here it is, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , we have at 46 percent, Biden, 44.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And this is significant because this is the first time in the history of our poll that former President Trump beats President Biden still within the margin of error, but still significant.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: So those are the lucid liberal lap dogs, but some on the left, they have opted for willful blindness. Biden isn't the problem, no, he couldn't be. You are the problem, your inability to be controlled.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON\uc2 , FORMER SECRETARY OF STATE: Sadly, so many of those extremists, those MAGA extremists take their marching orders from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  who has no credibility left by any measure. He's only in it for himself. He's now defending himself in civil actions and criminal actions, and when do they break with him? You know, because at some point, you know, maybe there needs to be a formal deprogramming of the cult members.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: You're the problem, remember it. Prepare for the most negative campaign in history because Democrats have nothing positive to run on.\par \par \uc2 Here now with reaction, FOX News contributor \uc2 Ari Fleischer\uc2 , the Harris Poll chairman \uc2 Mark Penn\uc2 , along with pollster \uc2 Lee Carter\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 You know, Ari, I began specifically with those pieces from "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " and politico. They have a lot of sources in Democrat circles. This is based on at least 42 Democrat sources. There's a lot of angst. You know, this were both former press secretaries, you don't want these pieces landing on your desk.\par \par \uc2 ARI FLEISCHER\uc2 , FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: No, and let me talk about one of my favorite things in that "\uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " article was Democrats at the White House saying they want them to walk less, so the public doesn't see him have difficulty walking. So what's the most concrete thing they've done? They have stopped him from walking up the big majestic steps to the top of Air Force One, and he goes in the baby steps on the bottom.\par \par \uc2 But, Kayleigh, as you know, once you enter the bottom level of Air Force One, there's no elevator, there's nothing that gets you to the higher levels making it easier there's an inner staircase. So the only thing they're doing is hiding \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . There is no reason to have him take those lower steps, nothing is easier for him. He still has to climb, but it's the only way they can hide him.\par \par \uc2 And that's unfortunately the reality of what you have to deal with when you're dealing with an 81-year-old. They are hiding the president so the country cannot see the difficulties he has. That's a shame. That is not what you do with a president.\par \par \uc2 It's -- it's basement campaign 2.0, except this time it's the Oval Office. You know, Mark, in one of these pieces, \uc2 Ron Klain\uc2  was asked at one of these swanky events that both parties have, he was asked, you know, what's the backup plan? To which he replied, there is no backup plan.\par \par \uc2 That seems inconceivable to me, given the polling, given the president's age. Do you think there's a backup plan?\par \par \uc2 MARK PENN\uc2 , THE HARRIS POLL CHAIRMAN: When your age is twice your approval rating, that is a problem, no question about it.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Well-said.\par \par \uc2 PENN: But I don't think that there is going to be a change here in the nominee. In that NBC poll, 77 percent of Democrats picked Biden for the nominee. He's got the full party machinery. He wants to run again he is running. So I don't think there's a plan B.\par \par \uc2 Biden has a year, I've worked with presidents before. So is Ari, a year was a long time in presidential history. And let's face it, if people thought the economy was going well, they wouldn't care about his age. It's really about the economy, immigration, crime and key issues he's got a year to fix.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Yeah, year's a long time, Lee. I mean, you look at the polls, eight national polls have Trump beating Biden, that's got to cause trepidation among Democrats, but among Republicans, polls haven't always translated to electoral victories. I think of the red wave for instance. So where are we?\par \par \uc2 LEE CARTER\uc2 , POLLSTER: When you look at what happened with the red wave, there was -- there was something else that was going on there though, because a lot of people were saying that the economy was the number one concern, but really there was also abortion on the ballot, and a lot of women's issues that hurt Democrats -- it hurt Republicans. In addition, there were a lot of weak candidates on the Republican ticket.\par \par \uc2 So this is a very different situation because you're looking at polling that says this is Trump versus Biden, not just generic ballot, just this is exactly what people are looking at. It does not look good for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  right now. And there's a couple of things that I would be most concerned about if I were him. Number one, he is losing significant numbers among younger voters. He carried young voters by a lot last time. It doesn't look like he's going to be able to do that.\par \par \uc2 The other thing that is really damning in some of these polls is his favorability among independents two and three are saying they look at him unfavorably, 55 percent, I'm sorry, 65 percent of even Democrats say they don't think he's fit, mentally fit for office. So these numbers are really, really staggering and he can't just get through them by denying it, and he's not going to be able to do it by hiding either.\par \par \uc2 I mean, to Ari's point, you can't stop him from walking, you can't stop him from talking. He's got to get out there and tell his story. And you know, you heard Yellen today say it's her job to tell his story. No, it's not. It's his job.\par \par \uc2 He's got to get out there and convince the American people he's up for the job and right now, it's not looking good.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Ari, you know to that point, you know, I worked on a campaign. You always know the Achilles heel of your boss. You know their strengths, you know their weaknesses. This is a huge weakness for Biden, his age.\par \par \uc2 How as a team do you unwind that? Do you reverse the conception, the perception rather of the American people?\par \par \uc2 FLEISCHER: Well, there's only one way and that's to turn him into Benjamin Button and watch him get younger, and that's not going to happen. And so, it really is a huge problem for the Democrats, and what a massive opportunity for Republicans, whether the nominee is President Trump or Nikki Haley or somebody else, as I've like to say and I've said it forever, don't compare \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to the Almighty, compare him to the alternative. And the alternative is \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 I'm just struck by how the Democrats are create committing political suicide. It is beyond belief that they are putting up the worst two candidates against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and the country. There is nobody who would lose easier to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  than \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , yet that is the path that Democrats are on. It's inexplicable.\par \par \uc2 And that's why I think you're going to see huge pressure built around \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  to get him to drop out right before the convention.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: I think that pressure is going to build.\par \par \uc2 And, Mark, you know, in the wake of the 2023 elections, not a good night for Republicans yet again, not a good night in 2022 for Republicans. But in the wake of that election, "Politico" said there are two camps of Democrats. There's the time to freak out Democrats because they see the weakness of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , and there's the time to chill out Democrats because they keep doing well in elections.\par \par \uc2 Which camp are you, chill out or freak out?\par \par \uc2 PENN: I've been in the freak out camp for a long time. I've been calling attention to these same numbers. I mean, Trump has been ahead in the Harris polls for months. Biden's been in this kind of doldrums, particularly I thought he had a very bad summer.\par \par \uc2 But I come back to, you know, Biden like Trump in many ways has managed to have nine lives time and time again. Everybody thought he was out, you know, last time in the Democratic primary and suddenly he was in. Everybody thought he was going to lose and he went -- and he won. So I do think you're going to see Democrats hold on to that.\par \par \uc2 But the reality is, we've got to see economic improvement. That's what this election is really about, not about age.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Lee, one thing that that Biden team has done well is mobilized an army of influencers to reach Gen Z, to reach millennials. You mention Trump is doing very well with this cohort, unusually well for any Republican. But the day before the midterms, Biden brought all the TikTok influencers into the Oval and the days before I should say with an aggregate following of 67 million. Obama met with them, they toured D.C.\par \par \uc2 How powerful and how difficult is that to combat, this army of influencers that has a huge megaphone?\par \par \uc2 CARTER: It would be really hard to combat the army of influencers because they can mobilize on key issues, because some of the polling that I've seen suggests that seven and independent women are going to be driven to the polls this election cycle because of abortion, and three of our key states, abortion is going to be on the ballot, seven in 10 independent women, nine in 10 Democratic women.\par \par \uc2 And so if influencers are really able to get out there and mobilize on that issue, I don't think they're going to be able to mobilize on \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , but if they're going to be able to mobilize on women's issues, they could have a lot of success and I think that Republicans must come up with an answer that is an all of the above answer. That isn't just to say they have to change their position on abortion, but what they do need to do is change perceptions on how they view women.\par \par \uc2 And so, they need to come up with a women's agenda that are going to mobilize them just as much, because these influencers are going to be hard to beat especially on that particular issue.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Great points.\par \par \uc2 Ari Fleischer\uc2 , \uc2 Mark Penn\uc2 , \uc2 Lee Carter\uc2 , thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well, it's no secret that Democrats are now in panic mode over Biden's terrible poll numbers. It's a game of how low can you go for Joe, but \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , she is not worried. The vice president told CNN that she and Biden will just have to earn their reelection, but how?\par \par \uc2 Joining us now with more FOX News contributor and former speaker of the House, Newt Gingrich.\par \par \uc2 You know, Speaker Gingrich, it's always a pleasure to hear your wisdom and at a moment in history where Republicans -- you know, we control the House, that's it. We don't have the Senate, we don't have the presidency and we know elections are about choice. How does the house under newly elected Speaker Mike Johnson who I sat across from was very impressed by, how does the House give the American people a choice, illustrate a contrasting agenda to Biden. What should they be doing?\par \par \uc2 NEWT GINGRICH, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, I think they're doing a lot of it. I think that if you look at the bills they've been passing, aid to Israel but offset by cutting money out of the \uc2 Internal Revenue Service\uc2 , a series of steps, a very strong parental rights bill that virtually every Democrat voted against, I think there are a lot of steps they can take.\par \par \uc2 But you also have to be realistic. I think what Speaker Johnson decided was that keeping the government open was the practical next step given where they were in the mess that they'd been in. And I suspect that was right. Now, they're going to have some big fights over appropriations.\par \par \uc2 But in the long run, there are a lot of other things the House Republicans are doing that are going to end up mattering. Now, the fact is that the Biden family increasingly looks like in the newsletter recently I called them the Delaware sopranos. We get more evidence every week from people like Chairman Comer and Chairman Jordan and Chairman Smith, every single week, we're getting more evidence that this is just a family of crooks.\par \par \uc2 And that's an important job which would not be happening if the House Republicans didn't have a majority.\par \par \uc2 So I think you're going to see both the process of legislation and the process of investigation moving forward. But let me also be clear, if Biden can't solve the challenge with Israel, the challenge with Ukraine, the challenge on the border, the challenge with fentanyl, the challenge with crime, the challenge with prices going up everywhere.\par \par \uc2 Callista and I were just out shopping yesterday, and -- I mean, the degree to which grocery prices keep going up affects every single American. It's not about polling. It's not about advertising. It's about pain.\