t? Are all bears bad?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: No, no, the mama -- the mama bears you got to watch out for. All right. Judge, thank you.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: So, the girl bears are bad. I got it. Thanks, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: They're protective. Good evening. I'm Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 We'll take you aboard an Israeli naval ship for a behind the scenes look at the \uc2 Hamas\uc2  war from the water.\par \par \uc2 Members of the Squad meantime are begging for help as their anti-Israel views inspire primary challengers in their home districts.\par \par \uc2 And Our Whatever Happened To segment deals with the transportation revolution stuck in neutral.\par \par \uc2 But first, with a little less than two months until the Iowa caucuses. That state is the center of the presidential campaign universe this weekend. Several Republican candidates are participating in a forum tonight organized by a Christian group. Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is not taking part but he will be in Iowa tomorrow for a rally.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Mark Meredith starts us off tonight live from Des Moines with the latest. Good evening, Mark.\par \par \uc2 MARK MEREDITH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Bret, good evening to you. Tonight, Republican presidential candidates are doing all they can to win over Iowa's evangelical voters, a group which can help a candidate go from obscurity to victory in this first contest state.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (voice over): Sitting together at one table. Tonight, three Republican presidential candidates are addressing The Family Leader foundation trying to win over Christian conservatives.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: So, this is more about you and bot about whoever your opponent is.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (voice over): Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is betting big on Iowa. Today, his campaign celebrating growing support among the state's faith leaders, some of whom supported Senator Tim Scott before he dropped out of the race earlier this week.\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL): We have over a hundred faith leaders that are on board and obviously that's instrumental in terms of bringing people out to the caucus.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (voice over): Tomorrow, DeSantis will campaign with Iowa's Governor, his most high profile state surrogate but the latest national Fox News polling shows DeSantis needs more help. Former President Trump is dominating among GOP primary voters, his support up three points since October.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY (R), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: How many of you are here listening to me to townhall for the first time?\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (voice over): Former \uc2 U.N.\uc2  Ambassador Nikki Haley support is rising too, she's now pleading with voters to keep the momentum going.\par \par \uc2 HALEY: I trust Iowans, I really do. I trust them to get this right.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (voice over): But Haley's campaign remains in damage control after she suggested all social media accounts should be verified. Her opponents seizing the moment, \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2  now adding dozens of events in Iowa just before Thanksgiving.\par \par \uc2 VIVEK RAMASWAMY\uc2  (R), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: My sole opponent in this race, she is not even the main obstacle we face in this, reviving our national soul in this country.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (voice over): Tonight, Democrats are embracing the Republican rivalries, launching new billboards in Des Moines, accusing the GOP of supporting a national abortion ban.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (on camera): Former President Trump was invited to this conservative forum here in Iowa, he declined to attend. He was in New York today for his sister's funeral but Trump he'll be here in Iowa about an hour north of where we are tomorrow. He's going to hold a rally.\par \par \uc2 And Bret, then he's off to Texas, where it looks pretty darn likely he's about to get the endorsement of Texas Governor Greg Abbott, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Mark Meredith in Des Moines. Mark, thanks. More on this with the panel.\par \par \uc2 Also breaking tonight, Fox News brings you behind the scenes to look at an Israeli naval vessel. We'll look at that part of the war with \uc2 Hamas\uc2  from the water.\par \par \uc2 Also tonight, Israeli troops continue to operate in that Gaza hotel -- I'm sorry, Gaza hospital complex that they say and U.S. intelligence agrees was a \uc2 Hamas\uc2  staging area and command and control center.\par \par \uc2 And in the north, \uc2 IDF\uc2  fighters took control of an Islamic Jihad stronghold. Correspondent Alex Hogan is in northern Israel tonight with the latest. Good evening, Alex.\par \par \uc2 ALEX HOGAN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret. In response to attacks here in northern Israel. The \uc2 IDF\uc2  says that it is using helicopters and fighter jets to strike \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  sites in Lebanon. And looking to the south in Gaza, the \uc2 IDF\uc2  says that its forces there are expanding.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOGAN (voice over): Israeli ground forces comb through the Al-Shifa Hospital searching for what they believe is a \uc2 Hamas\uc2  command center, more tunnels and more information on the hostages.\par \par \uc2 MAJ. GEN. YARON FINKELMAN, \uc2 ISRAEL DEFENSE FORCES\uc2  (through translator): We see the presence of \uc2 Hamas\uc2  in all the hospitals.\par \par \uc2 HOGAN (voice over): The \uc2 IDF\uc2  blew up a warehouse making new weapons and has taken control of the Gaza harbor. With its navy patrolling the maritime border, Israel is also reinforcing operations on the ground.\par \par \uc2 CAPT. ELROM, ISRAEL NAVY: We are ready, we are prepared for any scenario.\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: This is one of the Navy's missile ships currently being used in Gaza. It's here in Haifa just today to refuel and then it will head back to the battle.\par \par \uc2 In Gaza, ground forces are sending specific coordinates to the Navy so ships just like this can take out those targets.\par \par \uc2 HOGAN (voice over): Almost six weeks into the war, the \uc2 U.N.\uc2  says nearly 800,000 civilians are living in its shelters.\par \par \uc2 HANI ABU ZANAD, KHAN YOUNIS, GAZA RESIDENT (through translator): Every second of every minute there's a massacre. What humanitarian ceasefires?\par \par \uc2 HOGAN (voice over): At the request of U.S. officials, Israel has agreed to allow two fuel trucks into Gaza each day down from the roughly 33 trucks worth of fuel Gaza received every day before the war.\par \par \uc2 Food and water are also more difficult to find.\par \par \uc2 SAMER ABDELJABER, WORLD FOOD PROGRAM: In Gaza, people are surviving on just one meal a day if they are lucky.\par \par \uc2 HOGAN (voice over): Back in Israel, loved ones gathered for the funeral of an Israeli soldier taken hostage, her body was found this week. The family of the other hostage found dead Thursday says at least the painful turmoil of wondering is over.\par \par \uc2 OMER WEISS, SON (through translator): There's a slight relief that we finally know what happened.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOGAN (on camera): Now, a new body has been found that of a 22-year-old man from Tanzania who had been in Israel on an internship studying agriculture. The Israeli government had listed him as one of the hostages. And now, Tanzania's foreign ministry says that conversations are underway to bring his body home, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Alex, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Here in the U.S. the Education Department is investigating schools were incidents of anti-Semitism and Islamophobia have been reported. This comes as leaders of prominent Jewish organizations are urging President Biden to take an aggressive approach to such behavior.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Molly Line reports tonight from Wellesley, Massachusetts.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MOLLY LINE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Hundreds of students at \uc2 Columbia University\uc2  protesting the school suspension of two pro-Palestinian student groups for violating university policies.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I think it's completely unjustified and it's a blatant attack on free speech.\par \par \uc2 LINE (voice over): This as the Ivy League university lands on a new list of seven schools now under investigation for discrimination by the Education Department. Its office for civil liberties is investigating allegations of anti-Semitism and Islamophobia at some of America's top universities and one K-12 district.\par \par \uc2 MIGUEL CARDONA\uc2 , EDUCATION SECRETARY: There's a line children need to be safe. They need to be protected and we as educators need to do everything in our power to make sure that they feel safe in their own skin.\par \par \uc2 LINE (voice over): Cornell and Columbia are not commenting, a Lafayette College spokesperson says the school doesn't know why the college is being investigated.\par \par \uc2 The Wellesley incident involves an e-mail sent to residents by dorm residential assistance declaring that there should be no space, no consideration and no support for Zionism within the \uc2 Wellesley College\uc2  community. Wellesley claims the school responded quickly and decisively to the incident, while making clear anti-Semitism has no place at Wellesley.\par \par \uc2 Not on the list, Harvard, where more than a hundred faculty members are accusing university president, "Gay of bowing to pressure from wealthy donors". Gay announced the formation of an advisory board to combat anti- Semitism after pro-Palestinian students released a statement blaming Israel for the \uc2 Hamas\uc2  attacks.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 LINE (on camera): The U.S. Secretary of Education \uc2 Miguel Cardona\uc2  anticipates the number of schools on the list will grow, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Molly Line lives -- live in Wellesley. Molly, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Busy day for President Biden when it came to foreign policy. He hosted Mexico's president for a bilateral meeting this afternoon and a short time ago, he met with leaders of the Asian Pacific Economic Summit APEC in San Francisco. All this happens amid sinking poll numbers here at home and still multiple hotspots flaring up all over overseas.\par \par \uc2 Senior National Correspondent Rich Edson has the letters tonight from the White House.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 RICH EDSON, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): At the close of the annual APEC summit, President Biden and his Mexican counterpart pledged to address some of the top issues vexing his presidency.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We're working side by side to address the opioid epidemic.\par \par \uc2 ANDRES MANUEL LOPEZ OBRADOR\uc2 , MEXICAN PRESIDENT (through translator): He is the first president in the United States in recent times who has not built walls.\par \par \uc2 EDSON (voice over): Less than a year from the presidential election, a new Fox News national poll shows President Biden trailing all three leading Republican presidential candidates. Biden gets poor marks from voters on issues from national security, the Israel-\uc2 Hamas\uc2  war, immigration and the economy.\par \par \uc2 REP. DAN MEUSER (R-PA): Gasoline, groceries continue to be a very high inflationary rates. Very unaffordable for certainly my constituents and the overall bumbling of this administration.\par \par \uc2 EDSON (voice over): American consumers face elevated prices and the vastly higher interest rates designed to slow that inflation. Democrats point out inflation eased last month. And some are pushing Biden to make a better economic pitch to voters.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KOTT, FORMER JOE MANCHIN SENIOR ADVISER: I have no problem contrasting that message with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  of just tax cuts for millionaires and eight trillion more dollars in spending and deficits.\par \par \uc2 EDSON (voice over): On foreign policy, Biden faced delicate negotiations this week with Chinese President Xi Jinping. They ended with agreements on curbing fentanyl chemicals and resuming communications between the Chinese and American militaries.\par \par \uc2 Hours after their session, Biden referred to Xi as a dictator. Biden has also tried to convince a Republican controlled House to take up his request for billions in aid to Israel and Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 EDSON (on camera): There is also opposition to that from within the administration, several hundred top U.S. or some U.S. officials have signed on to a memo criticizing the president's support of Israel, they want an immediate ceasefire. Top administration officials have said that a full ceasefire only benefits \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And Rich, White House officials are now responding to subpoena requests from House Republicans?\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Yes, White House counselor -- a counselor to the president of the White House Richard Sauber has written a letter dated today to the Oversight Committee Chairman James Comer, judiciary chair Jim Jordan. In it he writes, "You appear so determined to impeach the president that you have misrepresented the facts, ignored the overwhelming evidence disproving your claims and repeatedly shifted the rationale for your inquiry", goes on to say, "You should reconsider your current course of action and withdraw these subpoenas and demands for interviews".\par \par \uc2 Republicans want to speak with several former and current White House officials about the classified documents found at the president's home and his former office. They also have subpoenaed the president's son Hunter and his brother James. We've now just gotten a response from \uc2 James Comey\uc2  the Oversight Committee Chairman. In part of it, he says, if President Biden has nothing to hide, then he should make his current and former staff available, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Rich Edson live in the North Lawn. Rich, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Ex-Chairman \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  has been criticized for statements, several lawmakers and activists are calling anti-Semitic. The most recent post has Musk endorsing a user's opinion that Jewish communities have expressed the kind of hatred against white people that they want people to stop using against them. Musk's reply, "You have said the actual truth."\par \par \uc2 That user's comment is said to be part of a conspiracy theory that inspired the man who killed 11 people at a Pittsburgh synagogue in 2018.\par \par \uc2 Today, the White House responded by saying, "It is unacceptable to repeat the hideous lie behind the most fatal act of anti-Semitism in American history at any time, let alone one month after the deadliest day for the Jewish people since the Holocaust".\par \par \uc2 Stocks were slightly up today, the Dow finished ahead two, the S&P 500 rose six, the NASDAQ gained 12.\par \par \uc2 For the week, a big week, the Dow was up almost two percentage points. The S&P 500 gained 2-1/4, the NASDAQ finished ahead, 2-1/3.\par \par \uc2 Up next, a Chinese businessman is accused of illegally making COVID-19 tests in a filthy lab. We'll have that story.\par \par \uc2 First, here's what some of our Fox affiliates around the country are covering tonight. Fox 5 in New York, as New York City Mayor Eric Adams administration announces the city is enacting about $4 billion in budget cuts over the next year and a half. Those cuts will cancel the hiring of new police officers, slashed the education budget by a billion dollars and closed libraries in New York on Sundays.\par \par \uc2 City officials say this is all due to the migrant crisis and without additional federal aid, these cuts will continue to grow.\par \par \uc2 Fox 13 in Tampa with the arrest of a 14-year-old in connection with the fatal Ybor City shooting in October that left two people dead, 16 injured.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Las Vegas from Fox 5. One of the big stories there tonight, a rough start for the Las Vegas Grand Prix, the first practice of the $500 million Formula One race was halted just nine minutes into the session when a driver ran over a water valve cover that badly damaged his Ferrari. Fans were told to leave before the end of the practice which lasted until 4:00 in the morning.\par \par \uc2 The race takes place Sunday at 1:00 a.m. Eastern 10:00 p.m. Saturday Pacific time. That's tonight's live look outside the Beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Former First Lady \uc2 Rosalynn Carter\uc2  is in hospice care at home in Plains, Georgia. \uc2 The Carter Center\uc2  is saying the 96-year-old spending time with former President \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2  who is 99 also in hospice care since February. The family announced earlier this year that the former first lady is suffering from dementia.\par \par \uc2 The judge in the civil fraud trial a former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his associates has rejected a bid for a mistrial. Justice \uc2 Arthur Engoron\uc2  of New York State court in Manhattan said he could not in good conscience let the former president pursue a mistrial with request that was in his words utterly without merit. The former president accused the judge and his law clerk of being politically biased.\par \par \uc2 House Speaker Mike Johnson says the House will release more than 40,000 hours of video taken at the Capitol on January 6, 2021 when protesters stormed the building. He says some video is already available to the public with the bulk of it to be released gradually over time.\par \par \uc2 While scary outgrowth of the coronavirus pandemic to tell you about tonight, a federal grand jury in California has indicted a Chinese national for allegedly manufacturing unauthorized COVID tests at a filthy and dangerous laboratory.\par \par \uc2 National Correspondent William La Jeunesse has details from Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM LA JEUNESSE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): An illicit underground Bio Lab uncovered in central California.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE ZIEBRA, REEDLEY, CALIFORNIA CITY MANAGER: This was a true house of horrors, nestled next to an elementary school, a supermarket, a city hall and a public water system.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE (voice over): Charged with selling fake COVID-19 test kits. Jia Bei Zhu an illegal Chinese immigrant is now behind bars.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MIKE GALLAGHER\uc2  (R-WI): It was owned by a PRC national with ties to the PRC government controlled entities and a $330 million judgment outstanding against him for stealing U.S. intellectual property.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE (voice over): Officials discovered the lab last year inside what they thought was a vacant warehouse finding freezers wired illegally, vials of blood, jars of urine, a thousand lab mice and refrigerators filled with potentially deadly viruses COVID, hepatitis, herpes, HIV, malaria, and Ebola.\par \par \uc2 REP. JIM COSTA (D-CA): If a country was threatening us, like China, what better way to create havoc than to be able to spread through private labs?\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE (voice over): Ultimately, officials removed more than a hundred tons of medical waste. Zhu, a wanted fugitive in Canada helped prompt a congressional inquiry.\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): The owner of the lab shouldn't even been in America. He was here illegally living under a false name.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE (voice over): The panel also faulted the CDC.\par \par \uc2 REP. RAJA KRISHNAMOORTHI (D-IL): There's massive problems with our biosafety regulations where anybody, literally anybody can purchase pathogens on the internet.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE (on camera): Zhu who face his eight years in prison pled not guilty yesterday for selling these fake COVID test kits and lying to federal officials. But what was he doing with malaria and Ebola? We still don't know, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: William, thank you. Up next, members of the progressive Squad beg for help to fend off primary challengers.\par \par \uc2 First, Beyond our Borders tonight, Ukraine's troops secure multiple bridge heads on the eastern bank of a river in the Kherson region. Ukrainian troops say the fighting has left nearly 35 Russians killed or wounded and dozens of ammunition depots, tanks, armored vehicles and other weaponry destroyed.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, officials in Iceland say a volcanic eruption could start anytime in the next few days. The Icelandic media Meteorological Office says around 2,000 earthquakes have been recorded in the last 24 hours in the southwest of that country.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Rome. One of the big stories there in Italy tonight, scuffles outside the Italian premier's office over lab grown meat ahead of a vote banning cultured meat in Italy.\par \par \uc2 The Italian Parliament approved the ban of the production cultivation, sale and consumption of lab grown meat in Italy in a 154 to 53 vote after hours of tension both inside and outside the parliament.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories Beyond our Borders tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Looking live there in midtown Manhattan, a pro-Palestinian demonstration. There are these pro-Palestinian demonstrations in several cities tonight. They're called Shut It Down rallies. They're aimed at stopping what organizers call Israel 75 years of ethnic cleansing apartheid and colonialism. Among the cities you see here in New York, there's also one tonight in Boston, Washington D.C. and Philadelphia.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Griff Jenkins is actually at Washington's Union Station tonight where a Shut It Down rally is underway there. Good evening, Griff.\par \par \uc2 GRIFF JENKINS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret. The Friday evening commute is a mess because hundreds of those pro-Palestinian protesters are trying to shut Union Station down.\par \par \uc2 Let me let you have a look here. You can see the sign stand with Palestine in the occupation. A few hundred of the protesters have turned out here a few hours ago. They're loud. They're chanting and we've got police setting up barricades and monitoring the entrance, keeping the protesters out.\par \par \uc2 Now, their message is demanding an immediate ceasefire, and it's a position, Bret, shared by progressive lawmakers that are now begging for help to ward off primary challenges.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 REP. RASHIDA TLAIB, (D-MI): Our movement for ceasefire is only growing stronger.\par \par \uc2 REP. ALEXANDRIA OCASIO-CORTEZ, (D-NY): A ceasefire is not just an option. It is our only way out of this.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: Progressive members of the so-called squad are starting to realize anti-Israel fervor could hurt them politically, caught in a divide with Democrat leaders supporting Israel.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2 , (D-NY) SENATE MAJORITY LEADER: The United States has always stood with Israel, and we will do everything to see that that never ever changes.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS)\par \par \uc2 REP. HAKEEM JEFFRIES, (D-NY) HOUSE MINORITY LEADER: Our commitment to Israel's security is ironclad. And let me be clear. Israel has an absolute right to defend itself against \uc2 Hamas\uc2  terror.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS)\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: "Politico" reports progressive leaders met behind closed doors at DNC headquarters with House Minority Leader Hakeem Jefferies and the DNC campaign arm, demanding protection from primary challenges from pro-Israel Democrats, And pressuring them to counter the largest pro-Israel lobby group \uc2 AIPAC\uc2 , which is spending a reported $100 million to unseat anti- Israel progressive members.\par \par \uc2 REP. BRAD SCHNEIDER, (D-IL): \uc2 AIPAC\uc2  has a prerogative to support or oppose whoever they want.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: This divide is growing wider, fueled by pro-Palestinian protesters angry with pro-Israel Democrats refusal to call for a ceasefire.\par \par \uc2 EVA BORGWARDT, IFNOTNOW POLITICAL DIRECTOR: We have tried to ask them to do what 80 percent of their base is asking for and call for a ceasefire. They are not listening.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: And there are signs, Bret, that the progressive lawmakers are listening to these protesters that are getting quite active out here because just yesterday Congresswoman \uc2 Ilhan Omar\uc2  introduced -- excuse me one second. Excuse me one second. Congresswoman \uc2 Ilhan Omar\uc2  introduced legislation to block a $300 million arm sale to Israel. It's the first of its kind, first attempt, since an October 7th attack. As you can see, things are under control here, but a little bit active. Passions are running high. That's the scene on Friday night here outside Union Station, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Nice job there, Griff. Maybe you should have gone without the tie, more incognito. I don't know.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Thank you very much for being out there.\par \par \uc2 Hundreds of Jewish Americans are traveling to Israel to fight in the war against \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . They are called lone soldiers and are often without family or friends in a dangerous environment. Senior correspondent Casey Stegall takes a look tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 TARYN DANIELS, MOTHER: We're very proud of her. She is very determined. She is very focused.\par \par \uc2 CASEY STEGALL, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Eddie and Taryn Daniels beamed when talking about their 19-year-old daughter Megan.\par \par \uc2 EDDY DANIELS, FATHER: She had the regular, all-American life growing up. She was a cheerleader in high school.\par \par \uc2 STEGALL: But once she graduated, Megan had dreams of trading in her pom- poms for a uniform. While on a trip to Israel in middle school she decided she wanted to join the \uc2 Israeli Defense Forces\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 EDDY DANIELS: Just a few weeks after she graduated high school, she moved to there.\par \par \uc2 STEGALL: Megan had just completed basic training. Then came the October 7th terror attack.\par \par \uc2 EDDY DANIELS: She told us don't worry. I have 90 seconds to get to my bomb shelter. And --\par \par \uc2 TARYN DANIELS: And it only takes me 45 seconds.\par \par \uc2 STEGALL: Megan is one of about 1,000 Americans from more than 20 different states volunteering their time for a program called Lone Soldiers.\par \par \uc2 STEVEN WEIL, FRIENDS OF \uc2 ISRAEL DEFENSE FORCES\uc2 : We support their housing. We fund their food and living expenses.\par \par \uc2 STEGALL: A U.S. based group called Friends of the \uc2 IDF\uc2  works closely with the Israeli government, providing everything from military personnel to medical and relief supplies. The whole operation is funded through donations and people like Megan who are willing to give their money and time for a greater cause.\par \par \uc2 WEIL: Their kids are standing up on the frontline defending just basic, basic human decency, basic human morality.\par \par \uc2 STEGALL: A sacrifice the Daniels family is proud to make.\par \par \uc2 TARYN DANIELS: She's very dedicated to Israel, her people, and, of course, helping the fight for the free world, as she sees it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 STEGALL: Megan's parents say they had intended to spend the Thanksgiving holiday together, but those plans are on hold. Their hope is to be reunited with their daughter soon. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Casey, thank you.\par \par \uc2 In tonight's "Whatever Happened To?" segment, it has been a decade since the world was first introduced to the hyperloop, a concept promising to transform transportation. Tech giants embraced the idea of tubes that could theoretically take passengers more than 700 miles per hour. But 10 years later, is the hyperloop all hype? Here is FOX Business correspondent Kelly O'Grady.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 KELLY O'GRADY, FOX BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT: Over a decade ago, the hyperloop was perceived as the future of transportation, faster than planes, immune to inclement weather, and zero emission. The hyperloop would connect major cities through underground vacuum-like tunnels. Pods would transport passengers and goods by magnetic propulsion, making a journey from Los Angeles to San Francisco take just 35 minutes.\par \par \uc2 But today, it has yet to move beyond concept. \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  was championing the effort, creating an open source design and even building a one mile prototype with \uc2 SpaceX.\uc2  The project was recently put on hold, though, and the model turned into a parking lot. A big barrier has been engineering the right technology. Maintaining an airtight tube for hundreds of miles is challenging, as is keeping passengers comfortable at near supersonic speeds. But cost is the biggest factor.\par \par \uc2 JONAH BLISS, CURBIVORE FOUNDER: Building a huge network of perfectly airtight tubes around the country, it turns out that's pretty expensive. So, if anything, it's been a real factor. It's just been the capital necessities. It would be hard to do that without billions of dollars.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 O'GRADY: The Biden administration's infrastructure bill allows for hyperloop companies to apply for loans, but a mass transportation overhaul would also require government execution. In \uc2 Walter Isaacson\uc2 's new biography on \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , he shares the billionaire blames the government.\par \par \uc2 WALTER ISAACSON, "ELON MUSK" AUTHOR: If you ask him what the biggest problem facing America these days is that we're too risk averse. We have too many referees and not enough doers, and that's why we don't build high speed trains.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 O'GRADY (on camera): Overseas, a number of hyperloop companies are pushing ahead, and the global market is expected to balloon by nearly seven times in the next five years. In the U.S., however, the hyperloop still remains a pipe dream. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Kelly, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel on Republicans in Iowa and President Biden's toxic roadmap to reelection.\par \par \uc2 Thank you to all the folks who bought "To Rescue the Constitution." Still selling a lot, getting a lot of great feedback from that book. But this week and next week I'm promoting a new kid's book, "History Club, Duel Across Time." I will be in Naples at the \uc2 Barnes and Noble\uc2  on Wednesday, 1:00. If you are around, say hi. It's a really fun way for kids seven to 14 to get some history.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The strength of our economy is driving growth and spurring investment throughout our region.\par \par \uc2 MARK PENN\uc2 , FORMER CLINTON POLLSTER AND ADVISER: If the election were held today, according to the polls, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  would win. The president has to fix the economy, do something about immigration, do something about crime, and show that he can have the international leadership to move to something in Ukraine and Israel. He has a huge job.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The president and the administration talking about the economy in positive ways, but the polls, how people feel about it, just not backing that up. Our latest FOX poll has 78 percent of those polled saying the economy is only fair or poor. And if you do the hypothetical matchup versus the former president and the current president, the gains since October 2020, independents, black voters, Hispanic voters, suburban voters, men, this all is going to the former president, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  at the current time.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in our panel, Mollie Hemingway, editor in chief of "The Federalist," syndicated radio host \uc2 Hugh Hewitt\uc2 , and Leslie Marshall, Democratic strategist. Mollie, the White House continues to talk about the substance, the numbers, how great they are. And yet, the disconnect continues to grow. Each poll continues to see this separation about what people are feeling at their kitchen table.\par \par \uc2 MOLLIE HEMINGWAY, EDITOR IN CHIEF, "THE FEDERALIST": Yes, the strategy from the White House seems to be to present a very happy-go-lucky attitude about the situation either with the economy or with the border, crime, or the global situation. And the problem that they face is really just the facts, what people are experiencing with their take-home pay, what they see happening in cities, what they are seeing happening throughout the world. And being told that things are better than they are is not likely to change things.\par \par \uc2 The Real Clear Politics polling average does this interesting thing where they show where you are right now in the race versus where we were four years ago. Four years ago, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  had like pretty consistently very big leads, like 10-point leads in an election that turned out to be very close. Right now he is, like \uc2 Mark Penn\uc2  was saying, he is behind President Trump. And that's a really dramatic shift from where we were four years ago of like 11 points or more. So the White House has got to be concerned.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. If you look, Hugh, at the individual issues in our latest poll, he is underwater on it. Just right down the list, Israel-\uc2 Hamas\uc2 , his handling of overseas, national security, guns, immigration, economy at 33 percent approval, and then inflation at 29 percent. Here is Joe Manchin last night.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. JOE MANCHIN, (D-WV): \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is not the person when he came there saying I know how it works, I can bring the country together. I'm a centrist. Well, he has pulled clear far to the left. The economy from the standpoint of jobs opportunity, inflation is horrible. I agree.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So you can say Bidenomics is not working?\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: Well, from food and fuel and everything in between, so the bottom line is, can we do better? Absolutely. We should do better.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Hugh, your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 HUGH HEWITT, SYNDICATED RADIO HOST: Well, Senator Manchin is right. We should do better. I also have to add, no matter what the issue set is, I keep looking at a president who is infirm. And I think we all have to begin to recognize the president's infirmity is something that you cannot hide and perhaps they will try to hide it as we go along by keeping him out of sight. But that factors into these numbers because you really can't see him getting better at this job than he has already been.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Leslie, "New Republic," "Why Biden shouldn't run for reelection according to Biden himself. When \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was deciding late in 2018 whether to run for president, he reached out to his network of would be supporters with a pithy, pragmatic ask. "If you can persuade me there is somebody better who can win, I'm happy not to do it," he said. While the strategic arguments for Biden's candidacy have all but collapsed, the pressing moral argument he made in 2018 remains as true now as ever." Thoughts about that?\par \par \uc2 LESLIE MARSHALL, DEMOCRATIC STRATEGIST: I would agree with that 100 percent. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has a lower approval rating than be the president. And even though my governor, \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2 , has a pretty high approval rating among Democrats, it's pretty late in the game for him to come to the table, and he has already said he is not going to do that unless \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  were like, we're out. And that's just not going to happen.\par \par \uc2 So \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , whether people like it or not, and regarding his mental health, which I'm not a doctor. I'm not going to go and speculate. I also find it so interesting, Republicans talk about this now, but \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2 , I remember the crickets back in the day. But when we look at \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , even though the numbers are bad, you've got to look at the outcome of elections. The last election, with the exception of the mayoral position in New Hampshire, which went to a Republican, very strong for Democrats. The issue of abortion in both this and the midterm election -- I'm not believing the polls.\par \par \uc2 And to Mollie's point, although I agree with some of what she said, to Mollie's point, it has been election after election, especially general election cycle, that it gets very, very close the closer we get to the Election Day because our country is so divided, almost 50-50.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Mollie, on the list of all of those issues, Leslie is right that abortion, he is not upside down because Democrats have figured out a way electorally in these recent elections to do very well with that issue. But besides that, he is underwater.\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: Well, is he underwater, but to what Leslie is saying, too, there is an issue here for the Republican Party. They keep thinking that the fact that people don't like Democrat policies will be sufficient for them to have electoral gains. That's not actually true. They need to present a vision. They need to get things done with whatever limited power they have in Washington, D.C. And they absolutely need to make the case on abortion, to explain that what the Dobbs ruling did was allow states to make their own policies on abortion, to return it to the people so they can decide. And that pointing out how Democrats, across the board, are very extreme on this, very outside of where the American public is, where they would like some protections for unborn children and their mothers, and that Democrats in Congress and in the Senate have voted for no protections of that kind. They need to say it. And if they don't say it, they can expect to have continued losses like they've had.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, quickly, Hugh, a lot of focus on Iowa this weekend and the possible endorsement Bob Vander Plaats and his group. But New Hampshire, "The Washington Post"-Monmouth poll just out, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  at 46 percent, Nikki Haley moving up to 18 percent, and \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  moves to third. \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2  in fourth, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  in fifth in that poll. What are your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 HEWITT: Three tickets out of Iowa, and two out of New Hampshire. The field has gotten smaller, but it hasn't consolidated yet against former President Trump. So I will wait and see what Mr. Vander Plaats does. He is probably the most influential activist in the United States who is not elected to anything. Not elected to the NRC, not elected to office, but, boy, he makes the weather in Iowa. So we will have to watch that space closely.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And is he going to be on our show Tuesday. We will have that.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the Friday Lightning Round, plus Winners and Losers. Later, "Notable Quotables."\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 JAMAAL BOWMAN\uc2 , (D-NY): I think the White House might be corrupted by violence, because how can we allow the siege that is taking place right now in Gaza?\par \par \uc2 REP. HAKEEM JEFFRIES, (D-NY) HOUSE MINORITY LEADER: You can't have a ceasefire if there's no legitimate negotiating partner on the other side of the conflict. And \uc2 Hamas\uc2  is not a legitimate negotiating partner.\par \par \uc2 REP. KAT CAMMACK, (R-FL): The Democrat Party is going to have to really come to a decision of who they are and what they want to be. I think that they are having an identity crisis.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The protesters, we have seen all kinds of images. Remember the red paint, the vandalism on the White House with the handprints on the walls there, getting cleaned up over days. Then you had the protest outside the DNC headquarters that got kind of chippy at times, back and forth with police officers. And then you've had vandalism and other offices, like Dan Goldman's office in New York, spraypainted "Free Palestine."\par \par \uc2 Here is the "Slate," that's clearly kind of a left leaning organization, "The Squad is about to fight for its political life. The Squad has been vocal in its criticism of Israel's retaliatory assault on Gaza following the October 7th massacre of Israelis by \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . Close watchers now expect \uc2 AIPAC\uc2  to spend at least $100 million in Democratic primaries largely trained on eliminating incumbent Squad members from their seats."\par \par \uc2 And we're back with the panel. Hugh, they're worried about it, and demanding protection.\par \par \uc2 HEWITT: I'm just a little bit stunned that "Slate" used the term "retaliatory attack," as opposed to they are fighting a war for survival over in Israel. And I believe the Squad is simply the canary in a coalmine of Democrats who are going to be turned out by their party because it indeed is a pro-Israel party, as is the Republican Party. And the minority within it that is anti-Israel, they are going to be rebuffed at the polls.\par \par \uc2 MARSHALL: OK. Leslie?\par \par \uc2 MARSHALL: I don't agree with you. We're seeing growing sentiment for ceasefire. We're seeing -- and not just within my party, but within the world. You have leaders like Macron in France who have called for a ceasefire, and there are other leaders that are going to come forward, I would imagine, in the coming weeks simply because of the children, because of the numbers of the children, because of the children that are dead, children in incubators, and the list goes on.\par \par \uc2 Regarding funding, you have funding both. There are going to be very, very pro-Palestine, pro ceasefire, pro two state solution that are going to help candidates that have, or incumbents that have that opinion. But at the end of the day, if Democrats are looking at a \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  or a \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , who going to be more in favor of, if not a ceasefire, a two state solution? That's going to be \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . So I'm not fearful this will cut my party for the long term.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, but here's we're talking about, Mollie, we are talking about primaries, and the progressives getting really worried that very powerful Democrats in the Jewish -- with support of the Jewish lobby, are going to win those primaries.\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: Well, I think what Leslie said though I think is interesting to look at here. A lot of people are trying to say that this progressive part of the party is some kind of fringe, that they don't represent a huge part of the party. I don't think that's true. I think that, in fact, the Democrat Party is very focused on identity politics. They do believe in this kind of grievance culture. They think America is the oppressor, that Israel is the oppressor, that white people are the oppressor. It's not just the Squad.\par \par \uc2 And when you look at where the energy and excitement in the Democrat Party is, it is with this identity politics that is, at heart, in conflict with much of what Americans want. But it's not going to be so easy as running a few primary opponents of the Squad. This is really deeply rooted throughout the Democrat Party.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. I wanted to get a little deeper on this, and I appreciate it, panel. Have a great weekend.\par \par \uc2 HEWITT: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, you know what that means. That sound, it's Friday. It is Friday. Time for "Notable Quotables."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2 , ISRAELI PRIME MINISTER: We don't want to make human shields an effective strategy for terrorists.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: When you look behind the machine, you see the weapons and ammunition.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: There's about 300 peaceful protesters in front of the DNC right now.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We stand with Israel!\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: What outcome is he looking for here in that talk?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: New pandas coming back to the D.C. Zoo.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It's not like these are two teenagers dating. China is our number one adversary in the world.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Planet earth is big enough for the two countries to succeed.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: He is a dictator in the sense that he is a guy who runs a country that is a communist country.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: We will root out the communists, Marxists, fascists, and the radical left thugs that live like vermin.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JOE MANCHIN, (D-WV): \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  being elected again would destroy democracy as we know it.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Like I said before, I'm staying in office.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I've been drinking from Niagara Falls for the last three weeks. It is a terrible way to run a railroad. I can't turn an aircraft carrier overnight.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: He has been given a soup sandwich. It's a soup sandwich.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Do you want to do it now?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I'd love to do it right now.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Stand your butt up then.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You stand your butt up.\par \par \uc2 BEN DOMENECH, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Hold it. Stop it.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I did not run and hit the guy. I did not kidney punch him.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: His voice got higher as the conversation went on. You can talk to people about what that means.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You all continue to -- you look like a Smurf here.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It's just another day in the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And that is one week of covering Washington and the world.\par \par \uc2 Monday on SPECIAL REPORT, we have the leaders of the House Foreign Affairs Committee, Michael McCaul and \uc2 Gregory Meeks\uc2 , for a "Common Ground" segment on supporting Israel in its fight against \uc2 Hamas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Please join Shannon Bream for FOX NEWS SUNDAY. Her guest, Democratic Senator Chris Coons and former Secretary of State \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for this SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and still unafraid. And as we look live at more protests happening in New York, we're monitoring all these things around the country. We have got you covered here on FOX. "THE INGRAHAM ANGLE" is next.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020231118ejbh00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Interview with \uc2 Ronna McDaniel\uc2 , Chair, \uc2 RNC\b0\par\par\uc2 John Roberts \par \uc2 1,266 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 November 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Live Event\par \uc2 FXLV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Republicans taking a hit following last week's election results, and now they are focused on finding a winning message for next November, with abortion being a key issue.\par \par \uc2 RNC\uc2  Chairwoman \uc2 Ronna McDaniel\uc2  says, Republicans need to push for restrictions nationwide. But many Republicans say that approach has not worked in the past.\par \par \uc2 RNC\uc2  Chairwoman \uc2 Ronna McDaniel\uc2  joins us now.\par \par \uc2 It's good to see you.\par \par \uc2 RONNA MCDANIEL\uc2 , CHAIR, REPUBLICAN NATIONAL COMMITTEE: Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: And thank you for the cupcake. That is...\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: Happy birthday.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Thank you for the cupcake. That is...\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: Best cupcakes in D.C. I won't say from where.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: ... very nice of you to do.\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: But they're really good.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: All right. I'm still not going to go eat...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: What happened November the 8th?\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: Well, I would disagree with that intro a little. I don't think I'm saying they need to pass restrictions nationwide.\par \par \uc2 What I'm saying is, you have got to put money behind your message. Democrats are only running on abortion. In Virginia, you saw them run nine times more ads on abortion -- spend nine times more ads on abortion.\par \par \uc2 And my point is, if somebody is lying about you, and you let it go on answer, the lie becomes the truth. So, our candidates need to take this head on. I have been saying this for two years as a suburban woman. But the \uc2 RNC\uc2  doesn't do the ads. That's what the candidates do.\par \par \uc2 I will give you one candidate in Virginia that did flip a seat, Danny Diggs. He took abortion head on. He put his daughter in an ad. She said: This is where my dad stands. He's strong with women. He believes in women.\par \par \uc2 I highly recommend you put that ad out, because he won a flipped seat, flipped a Dem seat, because he took it head on. And we have to do this if we're going to win in 2024.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: All right, well, I will show you a Republican who did not flip a seat. That was Daniel Cameron in Kentucky.\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: And one of the reasons why he didn't flip the seat was this ad here. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I was raped by my stepfather after years of sexual abuse. I was 12. Anyone who believes there should be no exceptions for rape and incest could never understand what it's like to stand in my shoes.\par \par \uc2 This is to you, Daniel Cameron.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: All right. So, he didn't have any answer for that.\par \par \uc2 And when Democrats say that Republicans are the party that's going to restrict your right to an abortion, the majority still thinks that abortion should be accessible, even with some restrictions. How do you change the messaging for next year?\par \par \uc2 Obviously, this is going to remain a big issue, because Democrats win on this. How do you adjust the messaging so that Republicans can win?\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: It's going to be the candidates individually taking this on.\par \par \uc2 Candidates have got to get comfortable going on TV and saying, listen, I'm proud to be pro-life, I'm proud to be the party of pro-life, but post-Roe, we need to come to consensus. We should have the exceptions for rape, life, and incest, but we should also have humane, commonsense limitations, that, when a baby feels pain, when a baby can actually feel its life being taken from them, that that should be a consensus we should have, and Democrats won't meet me there.\par \par \uc2 They're on the extreme on this. We're willing to come to a consensus.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: So, that was the 15-week law that Glenn Youngkin...\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: And Danny Diggs talked about that in his ad.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Right. But that was the 15-week law that Glenn Youngkin was trying to get the Senate flipped on and hold on to the House, and lost both.\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: I applaud Glenn Youngkin for doing that, but, if you're outspent 9-1, people aren't getting that message.\par \par \uc2 So you can't say, this is where we are, and not spend money behind it. So the candidates need to get on TV and say it. And I would say, this is where I stand. Don't let the Democrats lie about you. Don't say that I won't give lifesaving care for miscarriages or ectopic pregnancies or that Republicans don't support IVF.\par \par \uc2 What the Democrats are doing is, they're flooding the airwaves with lies about Republicans, and they're going unchallenged and unanswered. And Republican consultants, not the \uc2 RNC\uc2 , have got to tell their candidates, we have got to go on TV and inoculate this message.\par \par \uc2 And then we can talk about crime, the border, fentanyl, inflation, energy independence, all the things Democrats can't win on.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: So here's the part where I forget you gave me a cupcake.\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: Go for it.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Because some Republicans are pointing the finger at you, one of them, not surprisingly, \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Here's what he said.\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: Sure.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VIVEK RAMASWAMY\uc2  (R), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Let's speak the truth. I mean, since \uc2 Ronna McDaniel\uc2  took over as chairwoman of the \uc2 RNC\uc2  in 2017, we have lost 2018, 2020, 2022, no red wave that never came.\par \par \uc2 We got trounced last night in 2023. And I think that we have to have accountability in our party. For that matter, Ronna, if you want to come on stage tonight, you want to look the GOP voters in the eye and tell them you resign...\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: All right, so you -- that's expected, I think, to some degree, from Ramaswamy.\par \par \uc2 But now we're also hearing rumblings that maybe \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  may be going south too.\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: Oh, I'm not hearing that.\par \par \uc2 Listen, I would say this to Vivek. I understand that you want to take me on. I'm focused on taking on Joe Biden. Joe Biden's ruining this country. Joe Biden's destroying lives for millions of Americans. They're living paycheck to paycheck. We're going into Thanksgiving.\par \par \uc2 So, I'm focusing on taking on Joe Biden. And, by the way, Republicans are really sick of this infighting. We just saw it with the speaker's race. They are so sick of us hitting each other, and not focusing on the issues and taking on the Democrats, because that's a losing strategy, if we're hitting each other.\par \par \uc2 I'm going to take the high road and focus on beating Joe Biden.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Yes, no more infighting.\par \par \uc2 They should be more fighting like Markwayne Mullin and O'Brien from the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: Yes, right? I don't know if I could do that. I don't think I could have taken that guy on.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: I -- I watched that entire exchange and thought, wow.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Well, put it this way. O'Brien better not get in the ring with Mullin.\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: I know. I wouldn't do that either.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Five-time MMA champion.\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: I think I would bet on Mullin for that one.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: That -- that would not end well.\par \par \uc2 Ronna, great to see you. Thanks so much. And thank you...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: Thank you. Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 Happy birthday.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Thank you. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Document FXLV000020231115ejbf00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Republicans Lob Personal Insults At Third Debate; Radical \uc2 Rashida Tlaib\uc2  Calls Anti-Semitic Slogan "Coexistence"; Biden's EV Dreams Are Crashing Into Reality; Migrant Mayhem: Chicago Mayor Blocks Plan To End Sanctuary City Status; Swiping Left: College Students Fall Out Of Love With Dating Apps\b0\par\par\uc2 Dana Perino, Jeanine Pirro, Jessica Tarlov, Jesse Watters, Greg  Gutfeld \par \uc2 7,812 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 November 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: The Five\par \uc2 FOXFIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 BRAD RAFFENSPERGER\uc2 , GEORGIA SECRETARY OF STATE: What happens when these people vote and make these decisions?\par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Got it.\par \par \uc2 RAFFENSPERGER: We support, protecting the rights of every voter to have free, accurate, secure elections.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes. And it's not a sure thing until Election Day. \uc2 Brad Raffensperger\uc2 , Georgia Secretary of State. Thank you, Secretary. Good seeing you. Here comes THE FIVE.\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Hello, everyone. Good to see you. I'm Dana Perino along with Judge Jeanine Pirro, Jessica Tarlov, Jesse Watters and Greg Gutfeld. It's 5:00 in New York City and this is THE FIVE.\par \par \uc2 Republicans trying to seize the momentum after slugging it out at another contentious debate. The big talk of the night was a blow-up between Nikki Haley and \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2 , which devolved into a series of personal insults.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VIVEK RAMASWAMY\uc2  (R), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: I want to be careful to avoid making the mistakes from the neocon establishment of the past. Do you want a leader from a different generation that is going to put this country first? Or do you want \uc2 Dick Cheney\uc2  in three-inch heels? In which case, we've got two of them on stage tonight.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY (R), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: They're five-inch heels and I don't wear them unless you can run in them.\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: In the last debate, she made fun of me for actually joining TikTok while her own daughter was actually using the app for a long time. So, you might want to take care of your family first.\par \par \uc2 HALL: Leave my daughter out of your voice. You're just scum.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Wasn't just the taunts, the candidates digging into the issues about -- the issues that Americans care most about, watch here.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Everything involving Bidenomics, I'm going to rip it up and I'm going to throw it in the trashcan on day one.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE\uc2  (R), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: I have a son who's in the audience tonight who's 30 years old. If he can't adjust to a few-year increase in Social Security retirement age over the next 40 years, I got bigger problems with him than his Social Security payments.\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: Level with the American people here. Ukraine is not a paragon of democracy. To frame this as some kind of battle between good versus evil, don't buy it.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT (R), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: My foreign policy is simple, you cannot negotiate with evil, you have to destroy it.\par \par \uc2 HALEY: We'll put 25,000 more Border Patrol and ICE agents on the ground and let them do their job. We will defund sanctuary cities.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Of course, once again, the GOP's front-runner was not there. Instead, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  held his own rally about 10 miles away and mocked his Republican rivals.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Good thing and I was watching these guys, they're not watchable. Standing in front of tens of thousands of people right now and it's on television. That's a hell of a lot harder to do than a debate.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERINO: But we can't forget the biggest headline of the night. It was a short moment. You might have even missed it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: Do you think the Democrats would actually hire Greg Gutfeld to host a democratic debate? They wouldn't do it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERINO: I don't know, Greg, your name was invoked, you get 30 seconds to respond.\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Well, you know what, I can't tell if that was a compliment or an insult. You know, and says they wouldn't do that. But you know what, to me, it would be a demotion because I'm here saving the world.\par \par \uc2 And also, they could not handle me moderating a debate. I would outshine them all. That was why the last time Dana, I said, try Dana Perino because I said they needed Dana's lightness to balance out the intellectual depth of Univision.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, Nikki's response, calling him scum was a little harsh, it revealed the personal animosity. I don't think they're going to share a ticket. But I thought my favorite part was at the end, where both use their families as buffers, right? You know, it was like \uc2 Hamas\uc2  wasn't the only one using human shields. They were like, going like, nope, I got my kid here and got my kid there. I thought that was interesting.\par \par \uc2 I thought -- I thought Vivek though won the night because he was the most memorable and unapologetic, and his voice by far is the strongest. I mean, it's -- you know, DeSantis is a good leader, but he's not good at this and it doesn't shine and Tim Scott, I couldn't remember a thing.\par \par \uc2 But him going out -- Vivek going after the \uc2 RNC\uc2  for their inexplicable choices, with the boss in the room. That takes some serious word rhymes with talls (PH).\par \par \uc2 And it's true because you know, bias Democrats asking questions that usually guarantee they'll be loaded with antagonism means you have to untangle the question before you can actually talk about whether you're qualified or not.\par \par \uc2 I think that the TikTok thing, I think what, Vivek, he made a good point, that it's not about data collection, Republicans keep talking about data collection. It's about brain collection.\par \par \uc2 So, you got to look closer at home, you got to look at the kids. You can't talk about TikTok if your kids are on it. And TikTok is that rare phenomenon that can get healthy kids to mutilate themselves under the delusion of gender affirmation. It's warping young minds.\par \par \uc2 So, I don't think you can rail against TikTok unless you say I don't want my kids on there because it's not -- it isn't about data collection. It's about what's happening to kids.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: There were no complaints really, though about the questions that I saw.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Well, I have a confession. I didn't watch the entire debate. I was changing diapers.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: You got an important job.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: You're at the White House?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Very good, Greg. That's why you shouldn't co-hosted that event.\par \par \uc2 I -- but what I did, my impression was that Vivek dominated and whatever it is about the vague, he's able to capitalize on the format, because he's incredibly charismatic, incredibly articulate. And he creates these breakthrough moments that everybody talks about.\par \par \uc2 Now, is he the most charming person on the stage? He probably lacks a little charm. And that's his deficiency, but he's gotten to the sweet spot of populism, where you are thrashing around that the powers that be, you are a little isolationist. And you don't sound like a politician. And Vivek doesn't sound like a politician and he's connecting emotionally to people out there.\par \par \uc2 Nikki Haley, smart, earnest, liked what she said about abortion. Sometimes she doesn't really translate with the grassroots, if you know what I mean. And the attack against DeSantis for not drilling in the Everglades and may not know anybody would drill in the Everglades or offshore and the beautiful beaches off there.\par \par \uc2 DeSantis, I agree, Greg. I looked online after the debate, trying to find moments. What happened with DeSantis, I couldn't find anything. And he's -- and then I had to look and I looked at what he said, sounds good, makes the right points. But there's no -- there's no sizzle with DeSantis. And I just don't think this race changes at all after that.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Judge Jeanine, what did you think?\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL LEGAL ANALYST: Well, it's interesting. I think that the Nikki Haley calling Vivek scum was over the top. And I ran for office five times, I get it when they come after your family, believe me, I get it.\par \par \uc2 But I think Greg, and I agree with you on that. I think that her reaction was more about the Vivek saying, not just that you're \uc2 Dick Cheney\uc2  in three- inch heels. But then she's on the board of \uc2 Raytheon\uc2 . And she's on the board of \uc2 Lockheed\uc2 . And she's a neocon, and she wants to go in and bomb everybody. And I thought that that's what got under her skin for her to call him scum. I thought just saying your daughter is -- yes, I did.\par \par \uc2 But I think that the Vivek -- I think the Vivek is very much as Jesse said, he's a populace. He said a lot of things. And people wanted to hear. The biggest applause in the audience and the crowd went crazy. When the Vivek went after, is it Kristen Welker? And he said, you know, you basically did a Russia collusion hoax for four years. And you're up here, you know, moderating this debate, and the audience went nuts.\par \par \uc2 So, in my nonscientific, and certainly random \uc2 Twitter\uc2  poll of 20,000 people today, and I want to thank you for answering, 79 percent of those people thought that the Vivek one.\par \par \uc2 I think that -- I think that Nikki on abortion, and that whole issue, she is so well -- she's thought it out so well. And I think that is she is really on top of her game there.\par \par \uc2 I think that in the end, DeSantis, he can do the job, but he's not -- he can't get any excitement. I mean, you just kind of looked at him and I go, oh, it's him again. You know, does he have his cowboy boots on again. You know, there's really nothing to say.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: All right, Jessica.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA TARLOV, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Well as the target demographic for the debate last night, I'm pleased to share my opinion.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: OK, great.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Just kidding. Not voting in the GOP primary. But I did think that Vivek went too far. I thought it was about the daughter that she got so upset. I mean, she's had -- she's run for office for a very long time. She knows what it's like to be personally attacked. That was a visceral mom move. That didn't feel like a \uc2 Raytheon\uc2  thing to me, though. Of course, she doesn't like being called neocon establishment and all of that.\par \par \uc2 But frankly, I think it's benefiting her and she should lean into it as much as possible because there are two lanes, there's the populist lane and there's the establishment lane, and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  owns the populist lane.\par \par \uc2 Until \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is out of this race, for whatever reason, and I don't expect that he will be, certainly listening to how he's talking about it. That leaves one other spot and DeSantis, \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2 , and Nikki Haley and Tim Scott, who I think blew up his hold. I mean, it's been a slow roll blow up and then last night when he looked at the results from the night before and said we need a 15-week national ban. It's over.\par \par \uc2 Like, that is the most inelegant thing that you can say after people in Ohio told you -- conservatives in Ohio told you that they don't want to talk about abortion bans.\par \par \uc2 But Nikki Haley for me presented as the most feasible candidate that could win a general election. If it's Nikki Haley versus \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , I saw this popping up in liberals \uc2 Twitter\uc2  feeds last night that that's someone who they could -- it's not their choice, but they could go to bed on election night and not worry about what the next four years is going to look like if it's Nikki Haley.\par \par \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2  about Ronna McDonnell -- McDaniel, sorry. And what's going on with the GOP, I thought was really meaningful that he had considered the election results. And that's something if Republicans want to change this culture of losing that I think is important to do. I continue to like \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2 , I think the way that he talked about the prolife movement, also, including people are suffering from addiction was really meaningful. And I don't know why that doesn't happen more often that you consider everybody. It's not just about preserving life in the womb, but also taking care of people who are on the outside.\par \par \uc2 And I just want to note, you know, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was having his rally. And he was throwing out lines like Kim Jong-un role -- rules 1.4 billion people, and they have no doubt who the boss is, and they want me to say that he's not an intelligent man. That's the perfect juxtaposition for Nikki Haley, right, to talk about the fact that we don't praise dictators like that. We don't talk about other leaders.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: You just pay them.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: OK.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: All right, up next, far left Democrat \uc2 Rashida Tlaib\uc2 's anti-Semitism getting directly called out by Israel's Prime Minister.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: New anti-Israel protests breaking out in New York City. This as Republican candidates call out the disturbing rise of anti-Semitism on college campuses, and radical protesters who've been terrorizing Jewish students.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: And we need leadership at the top in the United States of America that restores our founding values and that has no place for this kind of anti-Semitic hate.\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: And what is Biden doing? Not only is he not helping the Jewish students who are being persecuted, he is launching an initiative to combat, so-called Islamophobia? No, it's the anti-Semitism that's spiraling out of control.\par \par \uc2 HALEY: We are about taking care of people, not going and making them live in fear because some other terrorist activity says they want to destroy them.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Compare that with the unhedged anti-Semitism spewing from the Democrat Party. Recently censured Congresswoman \uc2 Rashida Tlaib\uc2  got asked point blank to explain what she meant when she endorsed the pro-\uc2 Hamas\uc2  slogan, from the river to the sea, which calls for the eradication of Israel.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. RASHIDA TLAIB (D-MI): I'm asking my colleagues, don't distort the words of my residents. Many people in this movement for human rights for Palestinians have always centered around coexistence. You hear them calling for that and saying that, you know, no matter your faith, your ethnicity, your background, you should be able to live without fear, without discrimination, without this kind of inequality that, you know, Netanyahu is extremist party and his leadership has been pushing.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: And now, \uc2 Rashida Tlaib\uc2 's anti-Semitic comments are embarrassing America on the world stage. Bret Baier has an exclusive interview with \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  tonight at 6:00 p.m. The Israeli prime minister is slamming Tlaib's comments.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Were you surprised by all the pushback not just in the United States with these protests but around the world?\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2 , ISRAELI PRIME MINISTER: Well, the river to the sea -- from the river to the sea means there's no Israel, from the Jordan River to the Mediterranean, which is a tiny area by the way that encompasses Israel, there is no Israel.\par \par \uc2 And so, what this Congresswoman is calling for is palisade and genocide, the elimination of the Jewish state, the one and only Jewish state of the Jewish people.\par \par \uc2 So, that's absurd. And I salute the Congress for censoring her. But it's beyond that. I think the protests that you're seeing, I'm sure it includes some naive people, but there are a lot of people who know exactly what they're saying.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: All right, Jessica. Yesterday, we talked about what she thought -- what we thought she meant. But today she was asked specifically, and she says that from the river to the sea is actually about peaceful coexistence.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Yes, I don't think so. And if she really thought that, it wouldn't have taken her several days to prep that answer, right? That's something if you are part of the Palestinian community, and that is a phrase that you use so regularly that's being chanted in your community, that you know how to explain it.\par \par \uc2 So, I maintain my position from yesterday that I'm not buying it. I also think, you know, you run to a friendly interviewer about it, put out an op- ed, do interviews with everybody. Everyone who wants to talk to me about this, they want to talk to me about my past comments made on the intercept podcast a few years ago, talking about one-state solution. And I'm pretty sure that her one-state solution is not that Israel rules over Gaza and Palestine, it's the other way around. So, I'm not into it.\par \par \uc2 It's interesting to hear Netanyahu talk about what's going on here. I think this is the first time that we've heard comment from him on it. And it really speaks to the power and the importance of what's happening across America, and in huge metro poles, like in London and Paris, and how disturbing it is, especially for the leader of Israel, and I'm no fan of Netanyahu and his policies.\par \par \uc2 But it's very striking to me to hear that and that he called out that there are for sure some people who are naive, but that he doesn't buy it, that most people don't understand. And I think that maybe we have been giving too many hall passes to people who are absorbed in their TikTok feeds and that people do understand more than we think about this, certainly the faculties at these universities and people's positions of power. And I just -- I continue to be deeply disturbed by what's going on.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: You know, Dana, last night, the candidates talked about how they would handle the anti-Semitism that's going on the university campuses, did you have a chance to hear about what their plans were?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: I mean, I listened to the whole thing. I think that all of that, like part of it was about the visas, the student visas that was once something that will, you'll get into a big free speech deb