ill. The place got higher on abortion, initially. A place got higher church attendance than most states in the union. How could they have passed this constitutional amendment that's so obsessed with death. And the way they did it was by sneaking it through with the language of civil rights.\par \par \uc2 Republicans ought to be trying to get ahead of these things talking about the rights, talking about health, talking about life, and using these nice words essentially to get that agenda ahead before the left does. They're very good at it.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now, Sean, this is -- this is a conundrum for a lot of folks, because they see the media is always going to, of course, be on the side of the Democrats, always. It doesn't matter if it's Romney running in 2012, or McCain running in 2008, they were kind of acceptable Republicans, they were slaughtered by the media.\par \par \uc2 The media is always going to be against them. Money is always going to be raised more, and higher quantities by the Democrats. There's no question about that. So, that's just a constant. I don't think people are going to be able to bet on changing that any time soon.\par \par \uc2 DAVIS: That's true. What we really need from Republican candidates and from conservatives is a spine. We need some intestinal fortitude. And we need them to go out and boldly talk about the policies that they support that make America better, because I have no doubt that the policies on the right are far preferred by a majority of Americans than the policies that the left are pushing.\par \par \uc2 Even on abortion, for example, the left is pushing abortion up to, and through the moment of birth, funded by taxpayers. That is a heinous, awful, evil extreme position and, yet, Republicans have been cowed into just hoping they can just not talk about abortion and survive.\par \par \uc2 No, we should absolutely be talking about the issues in an emotional way just like Chris said, because when the people hear the 