t.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Good evening. I'm Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, the U.S. strikes back, hitting Iran back proxies carrying out a series of airstrikes today in eastern Syria. This as Houthi militants have shot down an American Reaper drone off the coast of Yemen, that follows dozens of recent attacks we've been telling you about against facilities housing U.S. troops. This is increasing another pushback by the U.S.\par \par \uc2 Chief National Security Correspondent Jennifer Griffin is at the Pentagon tonight with the latest. Good evening, Jennifer.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRIFFIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret. We broke the news moments ago that the U.S. military has carried out new airstrikes against Iranian targets in northeastern Syria. We now have a statement from Defense Secretary \uc2 Lloyd Austin\uc2 , "Today at President Biden's direction, U.S. military forces conducted a self-defense strike on a facility in eastern Syria used by Iran's Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps, the IRGC, and affiliated groups". This strike was conducted by two U.S. F-15s against a weapons storage facility. This precision self- defense strike is a response to a series of attacks against U.S. personnel in Iraq and Syria by IRGC Quds Force affiliates.\par \par \uc2 Defense Secretary Austin went on to say the United States is fully prepared to take further necessary measures to protect our people and our facilities. There have been more than 42 attacks by Iranian proxies on U.S. troops and bases in Iraq and Syria since October 17th.\par \par \uc2 Earlier we confirm that Houthi forces in Yemen have shot down and American MQ-9 Reaper drone earlier today as it was flying over the Red Sea. This as a $32 million unmanned surveillance aircraft, which uses sophisticated cameras, sensors and radars to collect intelligence. Its high powered cameras include ones that can detect heat and operate with little to no light, it can fly for up to 27 hours at over 25,000 feet.\par \par \uc2 The Iranian backed Houthis who in the past have used SA-6 surface to air missiles to down and MQ-9 Reaper in 2019 release this footage of the downing today.\par \par \uc2 On October 19th 2023, the USS Kearney, which was in the Red Sea at the time, shot down 15 drones, four cruise missiles that were fired during a nine hour timespan by the Hutus from Yemen toward Eilat, Israel. Iran has long back the Houthis in Yemen.\par \par \uc2 And it's not just Iranian proxies in the Middle East, Bret, that are carrying out attacks attempting to spread the conflict. Today, Brazilian authorities arrested two suspects linked to \uc2 Lebanese Hezbollah\uc2  who were planning they say attacks on Jewish synagogues and targets recruiting Brazilians to carry out the attacks.\par \par \uc2 Brazilian police raided 11 locations in two states to break up what they described as a terrorist plot, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jennifer, you spent so much time in Israel and no \uc2 Hamas\uc2  and \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 . We haven't seen those suicide bombers, those attacks that we've seen before. But that has to be on the mind of authorities not only in Israel, but around the world.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Absolutely. In fact, most likely those kinds of suicide bombings would take place in soft targets around the world where they're not expecting it. The reason you're not seeing that inside Israel is that you have basically a wall separating the West Bank and checkpoints stopping those kinds of suicide bombs. That's why the wall was built, frankly, back in the -- during the Second Intifada.\par \par \uc2 And so, but certainly the Israeli police, Israeli security forces on high alert for any kind of attacks right now given the number of proxy forces that are trying to attack.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, and other officials around the world Jennifer Griffin at the Pentagon. Jennifer, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Pressure, though, is increasing on Israel as it prosecutes this war against \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . \uc2 Hamas\uc2  leadership, several of its closest allies, including the U.S. now pressing for what are called humanitarian pauses to allow for the importation of necessary food, water, medicine, and fuel to Gaza.\par \par \uc2 Israeli officials say \uc2 IDF\uc2  troops have reached the heart of Gaza City. Senior Correspondent \uc2 Mike Tobin\uc2  is in Sderot, Israel tonight with the latest. Good evening, Mike.\par \par \uc2 MIKE TOBIN\uc2 , FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR CORRESPONDENT: And Bret, a source tells Fox the U.S. Israel and Qatar are talking about a pause in fighting up to three days in exchange for a hostage release. Absent an agreement, Israel presses on with the offensive.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOBIN (voice over): Amid destruction and churned up dirt, Israeli forces are in the early stages of destroying the vast tunnel network, nicknamed the Metro under the Gaza Strip. At least 130 tunnel entrances located and destroyed by combat engineers.\par \par \uc2 Hamas\uc2  released a video with their fighters in the rubble of Gaza using AK- 47s to take potshots at a mechanized army.\par \par \uc2 REAR. ADM. DANIEL HAGARI, \uc2 ISRAEL DEFENSE FORCES\uc2  (through translator): \uc2 Hamas\uc2  terrorists are telling themselves there will be a ceasefire. There is no ceasefire, we continue forward.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN (voice over): Israel only increases combat intensity from the air, by land and sea.\par \par \uc2 Israeli military leaders claim forces are now surrounding and in the heart of Gaza City. In droves, Palestinians who originally refused to evacuate, take advantage of the humanitarian corridor opening each day.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE (through translator): We were scared. We thought they would kill us last night, we moved south.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN (voice over): On foot pushing wheelchairs and carrying their belongings. They marched south to limited safety and overwhelmed temporary housing.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE (through translator): We didn't have food or drinking water. There wasn't drinking water for our children. They struck the bakeries. There is no life in Gaza.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN (voice over): G7 nation join the growing calls for a humanitarian pause in the war to get aid in and people out. The group also unequivocally condemning the October 7th terror attacks. This as the \uc2 U.N.\uc2  Secretary General says the laws of war are being violated all around, staggering civilian casualties, the use of human shields and hostages.\par \par \uc2 ANTONIO GUTERRES\uc2 , U.N. SECURITY-GENERAL: It's absolutely essential that all hostages are released immediately and unconditionally.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOBIN (on camera): Israeli forces are so deep in Gaza City. Witnesses say they're less than a kilometer from the Shifa Hospital. Israel Shin Bet says \uc2 Hamas\uc2  leaders, senior leaders are hiding out in hospitals especially Shifa. Hoping they won't be bombed, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Mike, thank you. The Biden administration is putting plenty of stock and last night's off year election wins in the results across the board and it is hoping its diplomatic efforts can come up with some long term changes in the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 White House Correspondent Jacqui Heinrich has that story tonight live from the North Lawn. Good evening, Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret. We have heard increasingly loud calls from the Biden administration for Israel to take action against what they call extremist settlers in the West Bank.\par \par \uc2 And now the administration is taking things a step further saying that after the war, Gaza and the West Bank should be unified under the \uc2 Palestinian Authority\uc2 , essentially a step toward a Palestinian state.\par \par \uc2 Netanyahu's hardline government has expanded Jewish settlements into the West Bank. So, this is