iden\uc2 's attorney Abbe Lowell said in a statement to Fox News that his client is eager to appear publicly at the right time. Adding this is yet another political stunt aimed at distracting from the glaring failure of representative Comer and his MAGA allies to prove a single one of their wild and now discredited conspiracies about the Biden family.\par \par \uc2 Lowell earlier today wrote a letter to New House Speaker Mike Johnson, asking him to call off probes into \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 . The spokesman for the speaker told Fox News in part, President Biden and the White House have repeatedly lied to the American people about his involvement and knowledge of the Biden family's business dealings, receiving millions through their shell companies from foreign adversaries.\par \par \uc2 Now, the top Democrat on the committee \uc2 Jamie Raskin\uc2  said after nine months, the committee did not produce any evidence of a crime, much less an impeachable crime. Bret, a White House spokesman says Chairman Comer is once again lying to the American people. Back to you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We'll stay on it all, David, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's daughter \uc2 Ivanka Trump\uc2  took the witness stand today in the civil fraud trial against her father and adult brothers. Correspondent Nate Foy once again outside the courthouse tonight in Manhattan. Good evening, Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE FOY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret. \uc2 Ivanka Trump\uc2  remain poised and collected on the witness stand today. She answered questions from the state and lawyers for her father, former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 She left the courthouse tonight just like she entered it this morning without speaking to reporters, a state court denied her request to avoid testifying in the trial. During her direct examination, Ivanka testified she never prepared any statements of financial condition for the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2 . She answered questions about helping secure loans to buy properties as far back as 2011, which is outside the statute of limitations. She also testified she didn't recall the specifics of several e-mails within the statute of limitations.\par \par \uc2 Ivanka is no longer a co-defendant after a state court dismissed her from the lawsuit because of the statute of limitations ran out. But after her testimony tonight, New York Attorney General Letitia James spoke about her as if she was still a defendant.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 LETITIA JAMES, NEW YORK ATTORNEY GENERAL: She clearly was involved in negotiating and securing loans, favorable loans for the benefit of the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2 , for Mr. Trump and her brothers and for herself.\par \par \uc2 FOY (voice over): Bret former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  laid out his defense on Truth Social today writing, "No victims, no defaults. Conservative financial statements, 100 percent Disclaimer clause, corrupt Attorney General, Trump hating judge equals no case". Trump's attorney Alina Habba said they will move for a mistrial tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 ALINA HABBA, LEGAL SPOKESPERSON FOR \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : It is very clear that they have failed to prove the essential elements of the case.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 FOY (on camera): The state rested its case today, Bret. So, if mistrial is not granted, Trump's lawyers will call their own witnesses to the stand to present his defense starting on Monday, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Nate Foy outside the courtroom. Nate, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, results from last night's elections, what they may mean going forward.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Stocks were mixed today. The Dow lost 40. The S&P 500 finished ahead four. The NASDAQ was up 11.\par \par \uc2 It was "FOX NEWS DEMOCRACY '23" because the third presidential debate takes place in Miami tonight comes just one day after Democrats did very well in off year '23 elections in a handful of states. 31 to be specific. Handful of big ones.\par \par \uc2 We have "FOX TEAM COVERAGE". Aishah Hasnie in Miami has a preview of tonight's debate. First up, senior national correspondent Rich Edson in Washington, with a look at what really happened yesterday.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Rich.\par \par \uc2 RICH EDSON, FOX NEWS CHANNEL WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT: Yes. Good evening, Bret. Democrats across the country are touting these results for governors, state legislatures, and ballot initiatives.\par \par \uc2 Party officials say these 2023 election show Democrats have the winning 2024 agenda.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GOV. GLENN YOUNGKIN (R-VA): I'm a little disappointed.\par \par \uc2 EDSON (voice over): Governor Glenn Youngkin, campaigned across Virginia to secure the state legislature for Republicans. Instead, Virginia voters handed Democrats control of both the State House and Senate.\par \par \uc2 Youngkin is facing questions over whether Republicans overreached, especially on abortion.\par \par \uc2 YOUNGKIN: Virginia is a purple state.\par \par \uc2 The one thing that we know is that abortions are really difficult topic, that there is a place to come together around a reasonable limit.\par \par \uc2 EDSON (voice over): Republicans proposed limits on abortion after 15 weeks. Youngkin says many topics went into these election results.\par \par \uc2 LAUREN BLAUVELT, EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR, PLANNED PARENTHOOD ADVOCATES, OHIO: Abortion access is the law of the land in Ohio.\par \par \uc2 EDSON (voice over): In Ohio, voters changed the state's constitution to protect abortion rights. Since the Supreme Court ended federal abortion protections last June, voters have supported abortion rights referendums in Kansas, Kentucky, Michigan, Vermont, and California.\par \par \uc2 Advocates are planning for more than 10 potential ballot questions next year.\par \par \uc2 MARJORIE DANNENFELSER, PRESIDENT, \uc2