re prepping for a variety of topics here, including national security and abortion, which as, Rich, just mentioned is still a huge issue for voters and was in last night's elections.\par \par \uc2 Fox News has learned that Nikki Haley plans to tackle it head on by focusing on the majority consensus. So, like banning late term abortions.\par \par \uc2 The issue, obviously, gave her a big bump in polling after past debates. She's hoping to take that opportunity up again tonight. Her camp expecting big punches from DeSantis, who returned to his home state tonight, carrying a big endorsement by popular Iowa Governor \uc2 Kim Reynolds\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But there is also news tonight, Bret that his biggest donor is thinking about ditching him. Entrepreneur Robert Bigelow tells the \uc2 Financial Times\uc2  that he's looking at the strongest and most experienced candidate here and that is former President Trump, according to him.\par \par \uc2 It comes as yet another poll now shows Trump beating the president by four points if the election was held today. That from CNN. Trump is holding a rally tonight, just 30 minutes away from Miami, in the heavy Hispanic community of Hialeah.\par \par \uc2 Most Miami voters that we've been talking with today, Bret, think that Trump will be the nominee.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TOMAS MOSCHINI, VOTER, FLORIDA: \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is my governor, but I prefer Trump.\par \par \uc2 JODIE FABIAN, VOTER, FLORIDA: I am an old Chicago Democrat turned Republican. I would support Trump or DeSantis.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: What about this big debate tonight?\par \par \uc2 RICH FERNANDO, VOTER, FLORIDA: I don't think they have a shot.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Now, we did not hear a ton of enthusiasm about President Biden amongst these Miami voters. But I can tell you that team Biden surrogates are here in town. They're going to try to contrast the president's policies with what they call the extreme MAGA policies. And we're also expecting Bret, a mobile billboard to encircle this venue here tonight once the debate kicks off. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right. Thank you, Aishah.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, actually within the past few minutes, the Minnesota Supreme Court has dismissed a lawsuit seeking to bar former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  from the state's 2024 primary ballot.\par \par \uc2 Critics sought to disqualify him under a constitutional provision that forbids those who engaged an insurrection from holding office.\par \par \uc2 Justices in Minnesota dodge the central question of the lawsuit on whether the former president's role in the January 6th, 2021 Capitol riot qualifies. They instead ruled state law allows parties to put whomever they want on the primary ballot. There are other state challenges to come.\par \par \uc2 Up next, political analysis from Democratic and Republican pollsters, fair and balanced.\par \par \uc2 And later, anti-Semitism and free speech on college campuses.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DANNENFELSER: There is no question that the other side understands the power of it and they're gaining the upper hand. They think it's the silver bullet. Our side needs to wake up, make sure that we have a message that makes sense. That it contrasted with the other side as a winning message, we have that message. And we are all about serving women. We must always say and do that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Leaders of the Susan B. Anthony Pro-Life America commenting on the elections last night. The headlines today, especially when it came to Virginia, not good for the Republican governor. If you look at "Politico," "Democrats romp, Youngkin flops," "Axios," "Glenn Youngkin handed presidential buzzkill." "Wall Street Journal," "Abortion rights supporters rack up victories putting GOP in bind for 2024."\par \par \uc2 Let's take a look at the political landscape tonight with a pair of veteran analysts. \uc2 Lee Carter\uc2  is a Republican pollster, president of Maslansky and Partners, and Doug Schoen is a Democratic pollster, former Clinton adviser. Lee, what do you take from last night?\par \par \uc2 LEE CARTER\uc2 , REPUBLICAN POLLSTER: I take just what we're talking about. I think abortion is a bigger issue than the Republicans are accounting for. And that was true in the midterms in 2022. We knew that abortion was a big issue and it turned red wave into a red ripple. And it was very much true last night where abortion was on the ballot. When you look at Virginia, you look at what happened in Ohio. You look at Kentucky. Over and over again women are coming out to the polls and they're saying this is what matters to us the most.\par \par \uc2 Seven in 10 independent women say it's the primary thing that's going to put them in the polls in 2024. Nine in 10 Democrats say it's the primary issue for them is what is going to drive them to the polls. And so Republicans really have to get a message that's going to draw women in here. It's a big, big problem for them.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, Doug, listen, Republicans are leading in every poll when it comes to the economy, when it comes to immigration, when it comes to crime, when it actually comes to overseas, when it comes to dealing with the government. But when it comes to abortion, they are under water.\par \par \uc2 DOUG SCHOEN, DEMOCRATIC POLLSTER: That's exactly right. We are sitting, Bret, with an agenda that favors the Republican party as much -- dominant issue in 2022 could well decide the presidential election in 2024 in the face of all --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We're going to try to work on that audio problem, but you are right. It is just a key issue for Democrats. And you would think, Lee, that the president and the vice president are going to be out now on the trail talking abortion, because, really, it's the best item they have. What do you think the prescription is for Republicans at this point?\par \par \uc2 CARTER: I think that Republicans really need to come up with a message that is pro-women, because abortion isn't just about abortion. It's really about women feeling that they have control over their bodies, control over their decision-making, and really feeling equal to men. So if Republicans want to win, they are going to have to have a message that says we're going to put women and babies first. And here's how we are going to do it. They're going to have to have some policies that are going to support women who make the difficult decision to keep their child if they can't afford that child. And there are some policies that are going to have to go along with it. It's just an unfortunate reality that it's not quite as simple as black and white on this issue.\par \par \uc2 So, the other thing that Republicans are going to have to do is they need to stop using that word "ban." That really killed Youngkin. He tried to change it just a few days ago. But Americans by and large, whether you are Republican or Democrat, vote against bans. We don't want bans on abortion. We don't want bans on books. We don't want bans on anything. We are a country who loves freedom, especially in the Republican Party. And so they better eliminate that from their vocabulary as soon as possible.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, and there are abortions measures in 2024 already planned to be on the ballot. You have got confirmed measures, Maryland and New York, pending in Arizona, Colorado, Florida, Iowa, Missouri, Nebraska, Nevada, Pennsylvania. You can see a theme there. And some of them, Lee, are key, key states in the presidential election. And they look at the results from Ohio, and they look at the results from Virginia, and they say that is perfect to get out the vote.\par \par \uc2 CARTER: There's no doubt about it. Especially when you look there at Pennsylvania, Arizona, Nevada, those states are so close and so important in the race. And 