mocrats perform better with him on the ballot or without?\par \par \uc2 SCHOEN: They perform better with him on the ballot because inside the party, Bret, what you are hearing in response to the polls, yes, it's a five alarm fire, but the other thing you're hearing is what you have got on the screen now, with Trump's favorability down at 42, and the weakness in the 2020 and in the 2022 election still being apparent, the campaign against ultra MAGA, four indictments, the criminal -- I'm sorry, the civil case in New York. The Democrats believe that ultimately the gap we saw in the "Times" poll that we've seen in the CNN poll can be made up with a campaign as it was in 2020 and 2022 against ultra MAGA policies and an ultra MAGA president with severe ethical and legal problems.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The problem is that President Biden's approval rating is very, very low, too, across the board.\par \par \uc2 SCHOEN: And he also has ethical issues which appear to me to be getting worse.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. Lee and Doug, we appreciate the time. Thanks.\par \par \uc2 SCHOEN: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Up next, free speech and antisemitism, the reaction to the Israeli- \uc2 Hamas\uc2  war on college campuses. It's continuing.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: -- in New York, Connecticut, Pennsylvania, Chicago, Houston, everywhere. Gaza is Palestine.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You're looking live in New York City just outside Grand Central Station. I think that's 42nd right there. Decent crowd, pro-Palestinian protest in New York, matching other protests we've seen around the country. And you see Cornel West there, presidential candidate, running as an independent, and he is standing there with a sign in this protest in New York.\par \par \uc2 The Israel-\uc2 Hamas\uc2  war has sparked, obviously, fierce dissent on college and universities campuses as well. Most of it, like that, in favor of Palestinians. Today, the House Judiciary Committee examined the issue of free speech at those schools and the rise of antisemitism. Here is correspondent Alexis McAdams.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: You will not silence us. We will not be silenced. Cease-fire now. Cease-fire now.\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS MCADAMS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: A tense hearing on Capitol Hill as pro-Palestinian protesters interrupt the House Judiciary Committee.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Palestinian students should not be censored.\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: The committee reviewing the state of the First Amendment at American colleges and universities. Jewish students and recent grads telling stories about harassment. One Cornell undergrad distraught, saying she is confronted daily by people screaming "from the river to the sea," a saying that critics say advocates for the elimination of Israel.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA SILBERSTEIN, \uc2 CORNELL UNIVERSITY\uc2  STUDENT: Professors and student organizations have been fueling Jew-hatred and spreading it across campus with disregard or potentially even with deliberate intent to incite.\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: This comes as protests and rallies at campuses across the country continue. We have seen large crowds of students marching on both sides, some protests leading to violence.\par \par \uc2 PAMELA NADELL, AMERICAN UNIVERSITY PROFESSOR: This antisemitism is part of antisemitism that is raging across the United States.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: From the river to the sea!\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: And it's not just on college campuses. Pro-Palestinian protests spilling out onto the streets of major cities. These two large rallies happening within just the past 24 hours in New York City. The Justice Department now investigating seven threats made against the Jewish community in just the last month, including this Arizona man arrested after police say he threatened to kill a local rabbi and every other Jew he could find.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS (on camera): And tonight the DOJ is also investigating a threat against the Arab-Muslim community. According to the \uc2 Anti-Defamation League\uc2 , Bret, since the October 17th terror attack on Israel there has been 400 percent spike in antisemitic incidents across the country just compared to last year. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Alexis, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Another tragedy tonight near the southern border. Texas authorities say eight people are dead, including two innocent Americans, after a human smuggler driving a vehicle full of illegal immigrants crashed head on into an SUV. It happened while the smuggler was trying to evade police in Batesville. Everyone in both vehicles died.\par \par \uc2 The president's homeland security chief took considerable fire today up on Capitol Hill about the border situation. Chief Washington correspondent Mike Emanuel shows us tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 MIKE EMANUEL, FOX NEWS CHIEF WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT: Homeland Security Secretary \uc2 Alejandro Mayorkas\uc2  was on Capitol Hill asking senators to support the White House request for $14 billion to address the border crisis. Some Republicans making the case it is not about money but about policy.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN KENNEDY\uc2 , (R-LA) SENATE APPROPRIATIONS COMMITTEE: Either you are not qualified to manage a \uc2 Costco\uc2  food court, or you believe in open borders. And I think it's the latter.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: South Carolina Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  went after Mayorkas on terrorism concerns with millions of illegally crossing the U.S. southern border.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2 , (R-SC) SENATE APPROPRIATIONS COMMITTEE: There's 280- something people on the terrorist watchlist that we know of. Where are they?\par \par \uc2 ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS\uc2 , HOMELAND SECURITY SECRETARY: They very well may be out of the country.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: But you don't know. There are people on the terrorist watchlist, and you can't tell me where they are at.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Our colleague Bill Melugin reporting along the Texas border shows us how state officials have responded to a mile-long island in the middle of the Rio Grande, a no man's land used by Mexican cartels as a base to smuggle drugs, weapons, and illegal immigrants.\par \par \uc2 BILL MELUGIN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: This is one of the cartel structures on the Mexican side of the river directly across from us. You can see cartel graffiti on it as well as it's riddled with bullet holes. Here on the U.S. side, you can see what the state of Texas has done with Fronton Island. They have completely cleared it out, deployed razor wire. And if any cartel gunmen want to cross illegally, they now have nowhere to hide.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Back on Capitol Hill Mayorkas insisted the funding package would make a difference to those on the front lines of the border cris.\par \par \uc2 MAYORKAS: We owe them the funding, resources, and support needed to do their dangerous and difficult jobs.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL (on camera): Senate Republicans have unveiled their own plan to focus on security by completing the border wall and tightening up asylum rules. Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  is calling it a nonstarter. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Mike, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel on that testimony up on Capitol Hill. Also, the latest on the Israel-\uc2 Hamas\uc2  war and U.S. action tonight against Iranian proxies.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. TOM COTTON, (R-AR) SENATE INTELLIGENCE COMMITTEE: Within days of taking office, the Biden administration removed the Houthis, another Iranian proxy, from the list of terrorist organizations. Iran's proxies have attacked American troops and positions in the Middle East over 110 times since \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  took office.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARK WARNER, (D-VA) SENATE INTELLIGENCE COMMITTEE CHAIRMAN: I think we have to be very careful and thoughtful. Who fires the full first bullet directly from Iranian forces or American forces at each other bears a huge amount of responsibility.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: New U.S. strikes inside Syria at facilities tied to Iranian proxies. The defense secretary putting out a statement, some of it read by Jen Griffin earlier. "The United States is fully prepared to take further necessary measures to protect our people and our facilities. We urge against any escalation. U.S. personnel will continue to conduct counter ISIS missions in Iraq and Syria." That's the defense secretary. Meantime, 42 attacks on bases housing U.S. troops in the Middle East since October 17th, two in the last 24 hours.\par \par \uc2 Let's start with our panel, FOX News chief political analyst Brit Hume, "Axios" political reporter Stef Kight, and Byron York, chief political correspondent of "The Washington Examiner." Brit, these attacks keep mounting and they are tied directly to Iranian proxies that are tied directly to Iran. What about this latest action by the Defense Department?\par \par \uc2 BRIT HUME, FOX NEWS SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, Bret, the thing to remember is that a number of these attacks have occurred since our last response, which was basically blow up some ammunition dump, or the equivalent, in Syria. And this strike looks similar, that we have made, looks similar. It's a weapons depot of some kind. And if the first time they tried that didn't deter Iran and its proxies, it's not clear to me why this same kind of response will do the job. So I think it's something to worry about. Contrast that with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , who went after Soleimani after he was involved in attacks on the U.S. So we will see where we go, but it's a little worrisome that it may not be enough.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Stef, this is after the Houthis in Yemen, which again, are tied to Iran, shot down a $32 million U.S. drone. Add that to the 42 attacks. The administration has been repeating what President Biden said, and that's one word "don't." But they are doing. They are not listening to "don't," it seems.\par \par \uc2 STEF KIGHT, POLITICAL REPORTER, "AXIOS": It's true. And it's just another example of additional pressure that President Biden is facing in the Middle East. It's just going to continue to get worse. He is already battling his own party's divide over what is happening in Israel with \uc2 Hamas\uc2 's terrorist attack and the fallout from that. And we are truly in an age of crisis right now, the world in crisis that the president is having to deal with on many fronts, not to mention the own domestic political situation he is facing as well. But we continue to see this escalating, with something to certainly keep an eye on moving forward.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. Israel and Brazil stopped a \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  attack, Byron, but the concern about attacks here in the U.S. continues to rise, specifically about the southern border. Here's Senator Graham and Secretary Mayorkas today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2 , (R-SC): There is 280-something people on the terrorist watchlist that we know of. Where are they?\par \par \uc2 ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS\uc2 , HOMELAND SECURITY SECRETARY: Senator, the terrorist screening data set.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Where are the people?\par \par \uc2 MAYORKAS: The individuals whom we encounter that are on the terrorist screening data set.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Are they out of the country?\par \par \uc2 MAYORKAS: Are screened.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Are they out of the country or are they in the country?\par \par \uc2 MAYORKAS: Senator, they very may be out of the country.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: But you don't know.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It was awkward at times, Byron, today.\par \par \uc2 BYRON YORK, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: That was a really pathetic defense from Secretary Mayorkas. For a long time, since the rush at the border began when \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  took office, we have known that thousands, hundreds of thousands, and now millions of illegal border crossers were being allowed to stay in the United States, often with very little vetting. We have also known that some untold millions of so-called got-aways are here, and of course they had no vetting at all.\par \par \uc2 So just the fact that we know that some number on the terrorist watchlist have come into the United States, that is instructive, but it's not definitive. We don't know that at all. We don't know where they are. And when you saw the secretary of Homeland Security come in and try to say well, maybe they are out of the country, it was clear that he didn't know. And that is not homeland security.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Brit, as the president continues to have trouble in the polls on a number of different fronts, including immigration, the former Democratic nominee, former secretary of state, \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  appeared on "The View" today to talk about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: I think it would be the end of our country as we know it.\par \par \uc2 Well, Hitler was duly elected.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: That's right.\par \par \uc2 CLINTON: Right. And so all of a sudden, somebody with those tendencies, dictatorial, authoritarian tendencies, would be like OK, we're going to shut this down.\par \par \uc2 Trump is telling us what he intends to do. Take him at his word. The man means to throw people in jail who disagree with him, shut down legitimate press outlets, do what he can to literally undermine the rule of law and our country's values.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Now, we haven't heard from the former president on this. But "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " had this big story about "Trump and allies plotting revenge, Justice Department control in a second term." It's very detailed. As you can imagine, it was anonymous sourced but had a lot of details about what the plans would be in a second Trump term. Your reaction to what the former secretary of state said?\par \par \uc2 HUME: Well, you don't have to be a big fan of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to recognize that this is a question of the source of this criticism. This, after all, is the woman he beat in an election to become president. And then he was president for four years. And the things that she is handwringing about didn't really happen. Yes, he used to say -- he says all kinds of things and suggests all kinds of actions he is going to take. But the thing the Democratic Party and its allies face now is the fact that for tens of millions of Americans, life was unmistakably better when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was president.\par \par \uc2 So they can scream and caterwaul all they want about how he is going to throw us all in jail and end democracy as we know it. But it didn't quite happen before, and there is reason to be skeptical it would under another term.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Stef, I only have a few seconds, but it seemed like it was a red alarm polls after the polls over the past few days, but last night, was it a lifesaver for Democrats or at least a breather for them?\par \par \uc2 KIGHT: Last night's results are certainly giving Democrats room to hope moving forward that they do have a pathway to victory next year. Of course, abortion continues to be clearly a very strong issue for Democrats up and down the ballot, and it'll be interesting to see whether we see Biden pivot to really focusing on that issue given these latest polling that we have seen.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I would bet yes. Panel, thanks a lot.\par \par \uc2 Finally tonight, a special delivery for China. A sad day for animal lovers in Washington. The Smithsonian's National Zoo had to say farewell to its three beloved pandas. The animals were on loan from the Chinese government. They're now headed back to China, first time in 23 years the panda exhibit at the National Zoo is empty. Sad.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT, we talk to Ron Dermer, Israel's minister of strategic affairs, member of the war cabinet.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for this SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and still unafraid. We crammed a lot in one hour. Laura Ingraham has THE INGRAHAM ANGLE next.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020231109ejb800001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Radical \uc2 Rashida Tlaib\uc2  Censured Over Her Anti-Semitism; \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  Takes Credit For Democrats' Election Night Wins; U.S. Carries Out Airstrikes In Eastern Syria; End DEI: Bari Weiss: Stop Diversity, Equity, And Inclusion Programs; No To Grizzlies: Residents Revolt Over Biden's Plan To Release Bears\b0\par\par\uc2 Greg Gutfeld, Jeanine Pirro, Jessica Tarlov, Jesse Watters, Dana  Perino, Jennifer Griffin \par \uc2 7,534 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 November 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: The Five\par \uc2 FOXFIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: We appreciate your understanding. Senator Mark Warner, the Senate Intelligence Committee chairman. So much we don't know, this much we do. The war that everyone doesn't want to widen keep stubbornly trying to widen, we'll stay on top of it. THE FIVE is now.\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Hi, I'm Greg Gutfeld along with Judge Jeanine Pirro, Jessica Tarlov, Jesse Watters and her biggest fear is getting stuck in a glue trap, Dana Perino. THE FIVE.\par \par \uc2 Radical \uc2 Rashida Tlaib\uc2  refusing to back down on her anti-Semitism after getting censured by Congress. The House voted to punish the Michigan Congresswoman after she blamed Israel and America for the October 7th terror attacks. And for pumping out slogans that you can find in the \uc2 Hamas\uc2  charter, like when she posted a video that included the phrase from the river to the sea.\par \par \uc2 In the end, 22 Democrats voted to discipline their fellow colleague. Tlaib bawling her eyes out over being held accountable and tried to paint herself as a free-speech warrior.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. RASHIDA TLAIB (D-MI): I will not be silenced. And I will not let you distort my words. The idea that criticizing the government of Israel is anti-Semitic sets a very dangerous precedent.\par \par \uc2 I can't believe I have to say this. But Palestinian people are not disposable. The cries of the Palestinian and -- Palestinian and Israeli children sound no different to me. Why? I don't understand is why the cries of Palestinians sound different to you all.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: The Squad having a screaming match while defending Tlaib.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. ILHAN OMAR (D-MN): Every single one of them has not acknowledged the fact that Palestinians are dying in the tens of thousands. But will continue to say it is us, we're not acknowledging humanity.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 JAMAAL BOWMAN\uc2  (D-NY): You lack the cognitive and emotional ability to recognize diverse opinions.\par \par \uc2 REP. CORI BUSH (D-MO): It's outrageous that my colleagues are blatantly attempting to silence the only Palestinian American representative right here.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Oh, boy, I got a headache. But if Rashida really cared about the Palestinian children, she could start by calling out the terrorists looting their future.\par \par \uc2 The top three \uc2 Hamas\uc2  leaders are worth a staggering $11 billion. And these photos revealing how they're living in luxury, playing ping pong, stretching after an intense treadmill workout and flying private while Gaza's people suffer.\par \par \uc2 All right, Jessica, you're a Democrat. But you're also Jewish. Congratulations.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA TARLOV, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: It happens.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes. Would you have voted for or against the sensor -- censure? Is it censure? Tough word for me.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: I wasn't going to say anything but yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: I'm sure.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: I think this is a tough one. And you saw that even in the 22 Democrats who did end up voting for it that they said that this wasn't a clear cut thing for them, that it was important, because of the way that this particular censure was phrased versus last week, there was one from \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2 , where this was really about making a statement from the House of Representatives that they condemn anti-Semitism. And that's why they went for it in the end.\par \par \uc2 She obviously has a First Amendment right to be saying whatever it is, and to be posting whatever it is. And that's where Jamie Raskin who's Jewish, and \uc2 Elissa Slotkin\uc2  who's Jewish and also from Michigan, came down on this.\par \par \uc2 But I think ultimately, I probably would have voted for it, because I don't think that she did the work to show that she believed the things that she said in her speech.\par \par \uc2 You know, we all make mistakes, right? We could say something that we don't know is offensive to a particular group. I think someone from Palestine knows how Israelis and Jews think about from the river to the sea.\par \par \uc2 But let's say that she didn't. And it gets explained to her every which way, right? From colleagues who she's close with and works with all over social media in every single newscast. Why wouldn't you then just say, I apologize, I should take that down. I didn't mean that. The annihilation of Israel is not my goal here or that the video falsely claiming that Israel blew up the hospital in Gaza is still up. And that's something that she continues to maintain.\par \par \uc2 And so ultimately, I think that's probably why I would have gone for it, but it felt like an alternative reality listening to them, because, you know, you're watching this and then simultaneously there's new footage of Israeli tanks protecting innocent Gazans, they're wearing the white flags, right. So they can be led out thousands getting out because the Israelis are protecting them because \uc2 Hamas\uc2  wants them to stay, right? They want them to continue living like that. Dying like that. So yes, that's where I am.\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL LEGAL ANALYST: Well, you know, when you think about it, I mean, what you're saying is so correct. I mean, you're obviously on top of what the facts are. The woman is a congresswoman. She has access to all kinds of classified information. All kinds of reports you can be briefed on anything. And yet she continues to leave up the fact that, oh, 500 Palestinians were killed, because Israel is bombing a hospital.\par \par \uc2 But you know, if she really cared about the Palestinians, what she's trying to convince everybody that she cares about, why wouldn't she go after \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , and the \uc2 Hamas\uc2  organization that is not allowing them to leave, or at least come to the southern part of Gaza, or those billionaires that you talked about in the open, the three of them were $3 billion each and more who are living a life of luxury, while the Gazans or the Palestinians are unable to access food, some of them.\par \par \uc2 No one doesn't feel sorry for the Palestinians. What the problem is, is \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . And when she says from the river to the sea, that is the \uc2 Hamas\uc2  mantra. It's been a mantra from the PLO, Ele Rozelle (PH) talked about it. Everybody knows what it means.\par \par \uc2 And for a woman to say she doesn't know what it means. Or she didn't mean it like, oh, you know, I'm really a great person and the cries of the Israeli children are the same to me as a Palestinian. No, they're not.\par \par \uc2 When Hillary Vaughn asked her about what do you think about the beheading of the Israeli children? She couldn't even answer but she cries when she talks about the Palestinians, which is fine.\par \par \uc2 But I mean, she's trying to pull the wool over everyone's eyes, and she should have been censured. And but I think at the end, I think what's going to happen is all the Squad apparently is being primaried now. And I think the voters will decide whether or not they even stay in Congress.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Dana, do you think those precious little protesters are aware that the folks behind the so-called freedom fighters are billionaires living it up in splendor while Palestinians are encouraged to kill and die for them?\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: I don't know if they know. But if anybody could tell them about that, it would be \uc2 Rashida Tlaib\uc2 , right? She's got their ear. She could do a lot of good on behalf of the Palestinian people, instead, she complains about every other rich person except for these guys.\par \par \uc2 And then, I mean, I do appreciate that 22 Democrats voted on the censure, but I do feel that the story actually maybe should be 188 Democrats voted with her, right, 188.\par \par \uc2 And I understand also that there were some people who were saying, we shouldn't get into the censure back and forth. It's a waste of time. Let's stop doing that.\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 Ilhan Omar\uc2  in defending \uc2 Rashida Tlaib\uc2  said, well, you are criticizing this free speech. She's free to say whatever she wants, but censure is free speech as well, it is making a point. So that is something there.\par \par \uc2 The last thing I would say is \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2 , when asked about Tlaib's call for genocide against Israel, basically, she said, it is important to be mindful about the language we use. And I'm pretty sure that's what Emma says to Jesse Jr. in the morning, right? She's a congresswoman.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Do we have that piece of tape, let's play that?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: I'm not going to speak to the actions that Congress takes when it comes to the phrase that was used from river to the sea. It is divisive, it is hurtful to -- many find it hurtful. And also, many find it anti-Semitic. And so obviously, we categorically reject applying the term to this conflict.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERINO: If you guys have a chance, if you listen to some of the briefing remarks from White House spokesperson John Kirby, super strong, in pushing back against some of the Arab media that is in the briefing room pushing him to basically disavow what he's been saying and what the administration's position is, and he pushes right back at them.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: You know, Jesse, when you see the continuing protests, and this kind of unhinged behavior, even in D.C., our politicians, I'm wondering if river to the sea means the Hudson River to the Pacific Ocean.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: I did not even know what river to the sea meant. And I don't even think the binder knew until 24 hours ago. I'm learning a lot over the last couple of days about the Israeli-Palestinian conflict. And I think everybody is.\par \par \uc2 But I have to say, this censure thing is a waste of time. Get to work Congress. They have like five more days before they have to pass a spending bill or we shut the government down again. And they're spending all day grandstanding about something this woman said. I think it's a waste and who cares what she said, OK.\par \par \uc2 They called Trump a racist, a traitor, a rapist, a Klansmen. Everything in the book. No one got censured. So, she says the president might be a genocidal maniac, OK. Like, OK, we'll give you that.\par \par \uc2 Here's what I think, Greg, I think if you're going to censure someone, you do it with someone like Schiff, who abused his power, abused the Intelligence Committee to perpetrate a hoax. You do it with someone like Charlie Randall who was censured because he was taking kickbacks, but if someone like \uc2 Paul Gosar\uc2  is putting out a meme, or if Tlaib is saying the president's a bad guy, I'm not going to jump on the bandwagon of censorship, but I will jump on this bandwagon.\par \par \uc2 Jessica, I hate to say I told you so. But I told you so. Two weeks ago, you said though the Squad is just marginal, the Squad does not speak for us. You have millions of people on the streets right now surrounding the White House, all over college campuses, sounding exactly like the Squad. And this is a huge problem. I didn't think it was this big. Apparently, it's bigger than anybody thought. And the Squad is just the tip of the spear. You have a huge problem with these people. And it's not just the Squad, millions, and now you're seeing it affect \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's base. And he's now in a little bit of trouble.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: We'll talk about last night's election and the next block. I'm pretty excited. What I was saying was in terms of the government that \uc2 Rashida Tlaib\uc2  could not sound more different from Democratic leadership from \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2 , \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2 , that was my point that they -- I think we have a huge ideological problem, especially with the Gen Z cohort. But my point stands.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: So, I was right. Got it.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: All right. We've got to move on. Well done.\par \par \uc2 Up next, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  taking credit after Democrats have a good election night even though his party ran away from him.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  trying to steal credit for the Democrats big election night, despite the president's dumpster fire polling. His party scoring some wins in Kentucky, Ohio and Virginia. Abortion rights mostly driving the Democrats to the polls, but never mind that, the braggadocious big guy thinking it's all about him. Joe spiking the football saying democracy won and MAGA last.\par \par \uc2 Politico reports how Biden basked in the moment making call after call to the night's big winners, and how his dorky staff was sharing dark Brandon memes in their group chats. Binder also praising her boss, listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: If you look at what Cameron did there, he spent $30 million -- $30 million -- $30 million trying to connect the governor of Kentucky Beshear to the president, right? And he called it a Beshear Biden agenda. Beshear ran on infrastructure. He ran on lowering costs. Those are -- those are the president's agenda.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The president didn't go out and campaign himself on that issue.\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: It is rare for a president -- it is rare for a president to go to a state that is been coined by all of you, a deeply red state.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: The White House boasting about the Beshear Biden agenda, but Joe barely lifted a finger to help \uc2 Andy Beshear\uc2  secure his win as Kentucky governor. In fact, the Democrat didn't want anything to do with Biden.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 ANDY BESHEAR\uc2  (D-KY): Governor's race has nothing to do with who's in the White House. It has to do with what's going on in your house. It has to do with bringing in good jobs. It's about expanding health care. And at the end of the day, this should be about the best candidate with the best plan and the best track record.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: All right, Dana Perino, what happened last night?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Lots of things. I do -- let's just take Beshear, Kentucky, for example. Young governor, pretty popular, had a pretty good approval rating. Abortion was an issue early on. And I think \uc2 Daniel Cameron\uc2 , who is a great candidate is the Attorney General of Kentucky. He had Trump's backing, that's a state that Trump won by like 35 points or something like that. But incumbents are hard to beat. Abortion is an issue that's hard to beat and get out the vote is difficult. You mentioned in the commercial break about fundraiser. I don't know the details in terms of how much was spent in Kentucky versus others.\par \par \uc2 But overall, yesterday, Republicans were outspent by a lot. One of the things that happening -- that's happening is that small-dollar donors are really strong in the Democratic Party, right? They -- basically they have this called act blue. And you just -- it's like throwing seeds into a field with lots of fertilizer that you saw out in California, and all of a sudden, like, oh, yes, here, everybody's sending their money.\par \par \uc2 And on the Republican side of things, the GOP primary has taken a lot of small-dollar donations, right? Also, President Trump's PACs take a lot. And some of these campaigns didn't have enough. And Republicans are g