view With Sen. Mark Warner (D-VA); Interview With Rep. Michael Waltz (R-FL); \uc2 Ivanka Trump\uc2  Testifies in New York; Republican Presidential Candidates Set For Next Debate; Abortion Major Issue in 2024?\b0\par\par\uc2 Jennifer Griffin, Mike Tobin, Nate Foy, Jack Keane, Jacqui  Heinrich, Aishah Hasnie, Neil Cavuto \par \uc2 8,318 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 November 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Whoa. Maybe it is not the economy, stupid.\par \par \uc2 The day after the last big election before the really big election, we can say another issue is really, really giving the economy a run for its money, or lack of money, or whatever. We're talking about abortion.\par \par \uc2 Consider abortion rights supporters winning a key Ohio ballot measure, a Democrat governor campaigning on reproductive rights holding on to win reelection in beet red Kentucky, a Democrat picking up an open seat on the Pennsylvania Supreme Court after pledging to uphold abortion rights, and the biggest of all of them, Democrats taking full control of the Virginia Statehouse, presumably blocking Republicans for passing -- stop me if you see a pattern here -- new abortion restrictions, in a blow to Governor Glenn Youngkin.\par \par \uc2 So you see what's going on? No wonder why many Democrats see hopeful signs for next year, and not because they will be focusing on the economy, but precisely because they will not. That is not a winning issue for them. Abortion is an.\par \par \uc2 We're all over it on "Your World" starting now.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everybody, I'm Neil Cavuto.\par \par \uc2 And, boy, one election changed everyone's mind about maybe what will be consequential and substantial in the next big election, of course, all of this as Republican presidential candidates, the five you see there, get ready to debate in what will be their third such debate of this go-round, and, obviously, the sobering lessons they will likely try to bring up from what happened last night and what it could mean a year from now.\par \par \uc2 Let's go to Aishah Hasnie with the reset going on in Miami -- Aishah.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey. Good afternoon to you, Neil.\par \par \uc2 You're right. Look, all of these candidates have to be ready to tackle abortion head on. And we are hearing that Ambassador Nikki Haley is ready to take it on. We know in previous debates that she has seen a bump in polling when she's talked about this. So this could be a really big opportunity for her later tonight.\par \par \uc2 Here is where they all kind of stand when it comes to abortion. Of course, Haley is focusing on a national consensus. We know Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , he supports a 15-week ban. Tim Scott is there at 15 weeks too. But I'm told that Scott is actually not fazed at all by what happened here last night.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Governor \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  says abortion should be left to the states, while \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2 , the GOP -- says that the GOP really needs to have a better messaging strategy. Listen to this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FMR. GOV. \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE\uc2  (R-NJ), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: I support voters making these choices and states making these choices. And whatever the results are, we're going to have great variation across the country in terms of what people want.\par \par \uc2 VIVEK RAMASWAMY\uc2  (R), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: I don't think this should be a sign that the Republican Party should abandon a pro-life position. I think it is a sign that the Republican Party needs to graduate in how not only we talk about this issue, but putting substance into what it means to be pro-life.\par \par \uc2 But I do think that if we frame this issue correctly, this need not be some sort of final sign that we take.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Now, Neil, when you talk to folks here in Miami, abortion is not at all what they are talking about. They're telling me they're worried about the economy, the border and national security, and many of them favor former President Trump.\par \par \uc2 This comes as new -- new CNN polling shows Trump is beating Biden by four points if the election was held today. But team DeSantis and Haley are dumping on Trump this morning for the disappointing GOP results last night, Haley calling Trump a loser for his endorsed candidate losing the Kentucky governor's race.\par \par \uc2 And team Biden is expected, of course, in Miami as well with some counterprogramming of their own. They're going to try to contrast what they call these extreme MAGA policies, Neil. The DNC just put up some Spanish- language billboards around the area around South Florida.\par \par \uc2 A mobile billboard will be circling the venue later tonight -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Aishah, thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 Aishah Hasnie.\par \par \uc2 Let's go to Jacqui Heinrich at the White House, how they're digesting a possible escape route for them from people bemoaning the economy to now maybe talking about abortion, a far more friendly issue to Democrats for the time being.\par \par \uc2 Jacqui, what are you hearing?\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, Neil, the White House definitely sees last night's results as a boost for Biden. In fact, they're trying to tie Democratic winners to the president, saying that they won Biden's values and his agenda.\par \par \uc2 And they're pointing, for example, to Kentucky \uc2 Andy Beshear\uc2 's win over the Republican Attorney General \uc2 Daniel Cameron\uc2  and Cameron's failed effort to tie Beshear to Biden and sink him with it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: If you look at what Cameron did there, he spent $30 million, $30 million, $30 million, trying to connect the governor of Kentucky, Beshear, to the president, right?\par \par \uc2 And he called it a Beshear-Biden agenda. And what did the people in Kentucky say? They rejected -- rejected that $30 million.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Beshear's messaging focused on Cameron's stance on abortion, another reason the White House can feel more comfortable about the president's poor polling, because, in their words, Americans have spoken loud and clear about the need to protect reproductive rights, and they view Republicans as a threat to that.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, today, no significant updates on Israel out of today's briefing. Officials would not confirm the president's ask for a three-day pause to get hostages out or Israel's resistance to that request. However, \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2  also danced around a question whether the White House thinks that it was appropriate that Congress censured Congresswoman \uc2 Rashida Tlaib\uc2  over her antisemitic remarks.\par \par \uc2 Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: I'm not going to speak to the actions that Congress takes. When it comes to the phrase that was used, "From river to the sea," it is divisive. It is hurtful to many -- many find it hurtful and also many find it antisemitic.\par \par \uc2 And so, obviously, we categorically reject applying the term to this conflict.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: \uc2 Rashida Tlaib\uc2 's use of that phrase, the White House denouncing her use of that phrase, but they would not go so far as to say that censure was appropriate.\par \par \uc2 But the State Department condemned that very phrase a couple of weeks ago. It reads: "Reveling in the murder of raping civilians, chanting 'From the river to the sea,' and calling for the destruction of Israel do not advance the security, prosperity, freedom, democracy, or dignity of the Palestinian people. Such hatred only makes peace harder to achieve."\par \par \uc2 That was from President Biden's special envoy to combat antisemitism, an official statement on the U.S. State Department Web site that was not quite fully mirrored today from the podium, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Jacqui, thank you for all of that.\par \par \uc2 Jacqui at the White House.\par \par \uc2 Want to go to Amie Parnes right now, The Messenger chief White House correspondent.\par \par \uc2 Amie, we're still trying to digest the significance of last night's vote across a smattering of states. Abortion was key and influential in almost all of them. I'm just wondering now whether that changes or could change the dynamic. It was an issue that tripped up Republicans last year in the midterms, tripped some of them up this go-round, not in Mississippi, where the governor, \uc2 Tate Reeves\uc2 , was reelected.\par \par \uc2 But I'm just wondering how you see this playing out as an issue in 2024.\par \par \uc2 AMIE PARNES, THE MESSENGER: Well, I'm hearing, Neil, from a lot of Democrats who feel -- the Biden campaign has been here doing a lot of counterprogramming here in Miami where I am.\par \par \uc2 And I'm hearing from a lot of Democrats that this should be the main issue that they talk about, not the economy, not anything else, that they should focus and hone in on this.\par \par \uc2 And they're getting -- I think the Biden campaign is hearing from a lot of those Democrats that they should learn from what has happened the last two cycles, that it is an issue that moves voters, especially Democratic voters, people who are considering not voting or staying on the sidelines, that they will get these people activated.\par \par \uc2 And so I think that you're going to be seeing much more of this, especially coming from the Democrats, and making this more of an issue and maybe hearing more from the president himself and not just \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . I think that they -- the White House was kind of leaving that to her for a while, but a lot of people want to hear from the president himself on this issue.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You mentioned the president. I was hearing one Democratic consultant, I believe, on one of the other news networks -- I watch them all so I can know what's going on here.\par \par \uc2 And one of them had said to the point, this gives the president a pass or a relief on that he's too old for the job and the revolt going on in the party. I don't know if that's a bit extreme, but it does reposition some things. Then the question becomes, can it last? What do you think?\par \par \uc2 PARNES: I don't know, because I'm hearing from a lot of Democrats who are a little worried about the polls. They feel like they are real. The Biden campaign is obviously saying -- the campaign manager yesterday, Julie Chavez Rodriguez, saying, this is true. This -- is it predictive of the next year? And we're feeling good about where we are.\par \par \uc2 But a lot of Democrats feel other -- feel completely differently. And I think that that is -- you're going to see them sort of try to do more, try to talk more about the economy. They haven't sold it well. They know they haven't sold it well. And you're going to hear more of this coming up from them, because they haven't done a good job selling the president's agenda and what he has been doing.\par \par \uc2 There are a lot of voters who still feel that they were better off four years ago under President Trump or former President Trump.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So things could change, I think you're telling me.\par \par \uc2 Amie Parnes, always good talking to you. Thanks.\par \par \uc2 PARNES: Thanks, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, \uc2 Ivanka Trump\uc2  on the list today, testifying in her father's fraud trial against the family business.\par \par \uc2 We're told that she comported herself fairly well, had to say a number of times "I don't recall" to some questions here. But there are some ironies in this testimony or why she had to testify at all.\par \par \uc2 Nate Foy has more right now from the courthouse -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE FOY, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Neil.\par \par \uc2 The direct examination of \uc2 Ivanka Trump\uc2  is now over. So lawyers for former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  are questioning her, so that she doesn't have to come back to New York from Florida later in this trial. During her direct examination today, Ivanka answered questions about her role at the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  as a former executive securing loans for various properties, some transactions dating as far back as 2011, which is outside the statute of limitations.\par \par \uc2 She testified she never had any role in preparing statements of financial condition. Ivanka was originally a co-defendant in the trial alongside her father and two brothers, but a state court removed her from the lawsuit because of the statute of limitations.\par \par \uc2 But while entering the courthouse today, New York Attorney General Letitia James talked about Ivanka as if she was still a defendant. Listen to this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LETITIA JAMES, NEW YORK ATTORNEY GENERAL: She will attempt today to distance herself from the company. But, unfortunately, the facts will reveal that, in fact, that she was very much involved. We uncovered this scheme, and she benefited from it personally.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FOY: Neil, the former president laid out his defense on TRUTH Social today, writing -- quote -- "No victims. No defaults, conservative financial statements, 100 percent disclaimer clause, corrupt attorney general, Trump- hating judge equals no case."\par \par \uc2 Now, Ivanka is the final witness that the state will call before resting its case, at which point lawyers for former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  will present their own witnesses in making his defense -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Nate Foy, thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 Want to switch on the other side of Manhattan right to get a peek at what's going on outside the United Nations, more protests being planned both on behalf of Israelis and behalf of the Palestinian. So far, they're peaceful, but the crowds are building we're keeping an eye on.\par \par \uc2 Of course, we saw a smattering of these not only in Manhattan, but the greater five boroughs outside, including Manhattan. So we will keep an eye on this.\par \par \uc2 Also keeping an eye on details in Israel itself and, from \uc2 Mike Tobin\uc2 , maybe a deal to get hostages out, as long as Israel is open to doing something else. And therein lies the potential rub -- \uc2 Mike Tobin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MIKE TOBIN\uc2 , FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: And inside the Gaza Strip, Neil, the Israeli forces are busy closing down the \uc2 Hamas\uc2  tunnel network.\par \par \uc2 I will have the details coming up.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, a pause for some hostages? Try a 48-hour one for some 15 hostages. That is the back-and-forth of this debate about getting a temporary pause in military activity on the part of the Israelis and Gaza.\par \par \uc2 Mike Tobin\uc2  has been looking at that and a lot more, joins us from Southern Israel -- Mike.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN: And, Neil, this is three-way talks between Israel, the U.S., and the Qataris, and they're talking about a three-day pause, which would involve a substantial return of the hostages.\par \par \uc2 And, if you remember, \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  has said -- said he's hoping to brief pauses, but nothing like a cease-fire, unless it involved return of hostages. Meantime, you have a substantial Israeli force inside of the Gaza fence.\par \par \uc2 The primary job right now is finding the openings to these tunnels. They say they're finding them in schools. They're finding them alongside hospitals. They even found one in a children's amusement park at the base of a ferris wheel.\par \par \uc2 And also the sad and very large exodus of Palestinian civilians from the north of the Gaza Strip to the south is taking place really every day as a humanitarian corridor opens up. People are leaving with whatever belongings they can take, pushing people in wheelchairs to try to get south and away from the heaviest of the fighting.\par \par \uc2 The problem they have is, when they get to the south, the aid camps are overflowing right now. There's no one for the -- nowhere for them to stay, and there's not enough food for them.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, I talked with a member of the unity government and war cabinet, one of the ministers there, \uc2 Benny Gantz\uc2 . And he talked about the day after the war in Gaza. And he said: "We will sit down, review an alternative mechanism for Gaza. I do not know what it will be, but I do know what cannot be there, an active presence of \uc2 Hamas\uc2  with governance and military capabilities."\par \par \uc2 Meantime, 106 aid trucks entered the Gaza Strip today through the Rafah Crossing, 756 since October 21st. Aid agencies say it is just a trickle compared to the need, and no fuel has been allowed in -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Mike, thank you. Be safe, Mike.\par \par \uc2 Mike Tobin\uc2  there in the middle of all of that, a brave young man.\par \par \uc2 Taking a look outside the United Nations going on, we have sort of competing rallies, pro-Palestinian, some pro-Israeli, others, so far very peaceful, but the crowds are building.\par \par \uc2 General Jack Keane is with us, the U.S. four-star general, FOX News contributor, much, much more.\par \par \uc2 But, General, first on these protests that happened, and they -- you pick a city in a major Western nation, they're all over the map, but they're all out there. Do you think these protests, especially the ones that are pro- Palestinian, because the -- it's lopsidedly in favor of Palestinian rallies, that that's a sign \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , not to conflate the two, is winning at least the P.R. rally on this?\par \par \uc2 GEN. JACK KEANE (RET.), FOX NEWS SENIOR STRATEGIC ANALYST: Oh, no, it's indisputable.\par \par \uc2 I mean, \uc2 Hamas\uc2 ' focus on killing civilians this time, versus \uc2 IDF\uc2 , which they did do, but it wasn't their focus, and the viciousness and savagery of that attack was designed to get a significant Israeli violent response. And they welcomed that response and they held people in the area of that response to drive these casualties up.\par \par \uc2 So what they're getting here is worldwide international condemnation of Israel, and check mark. That's exactly what they wanted. And they're getting it.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KEANE: They also want geopolitical isolation of Israel. And some of that is happening to a certain degree, but not widespread.\par \par \uc2 And I think they want to end the normalization that was taking place with the Arab states, two of them two other Muslim states. They want to undermine that and maybe, as a result of what is taking place with the Palestinians, that would actually dissolve. And they certainly want to stop Saudi Arabia from normalizing with Israel, because, if that happens, every Arab nation in the Middle East and Muslim nation will fall into play.\par \par \uc2 And that is absolutely what Iran is opposed to, and \uc2 Hamas\uc2  is their instrument here.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 And it has frozen all of this activity on the part of these Arab nations. So, at the very least, \uc2 Hamas\uc2  has succeeded in doing that. There was a deep dive into the strategy of \uc2 Hamas\uc2  in today's New York Times. I don't know if you saw it, but the premise was, why would they do something so crazy like this horrific butchering attack on 1,400 Israelis? To what end, when they knew full well in advance that the response from Israel would be tenfold, that there would be the world protest.\par \par \uc2 But, essentially, the premise came away that \uc2 Hamas\uc2  leaders believe -- quoting here -- 'The Palestinian cause was being lost and was slipping away and that only violence could revive it."\par \par \uc2 What did you make of that?\par \par \uc2 KEANE: No, I think that's accurate.\par \par \uc2 And, lookit, they're counting on the international condemnation. They have got that. And this is so diabolical and sinister on that part. I mean, what frame of reference do we have for people like this who are elected by a population, the Gazan people, been in power for 16 years? Principal aspect of governing a people is to protect them.\par \par \uc2 But they actually want them to be casualties and they make certain that they are. They have got 300 miles of tunnel complexes. They have known bunker tunnel complexes for the population, and they're there to protect. They serve the population up as human shields, wanting those women and children to be killed incidentally to the operations that the Israelis are conducted.\par \par \uc2 I mean, the...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Do they think then, General, that the shock effect of seeing something like this wouldn't register or, like we're seeing now, is being denied as never happening?\par \par \uc2 KEANE: Well, the shock effect certainly of women and children being killed in Gaza is widespread around the world. And Israelis are being held accountable for it.\par \par \uc2 And they recognize that, as soon as that started to take place, people's memories are short, they're going to forget the savage butchery of what took place.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 KEANE: And, after all, what is being played out on an international global network is Palestinians being killed every single day...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 KEANE: ... day in, day out, day in, day out.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But do a lot of people even understand the region, General?\par \par \uc2 KEANE: No.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: The reason why I ask is that we know that, for almost two decades now, Israel has had no control over Gaza, that that's been \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . It's their baby. You don't like how things are going over there, you can't blame Israel, that they ceded all that to \uc2 Hamas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And you could say they are the duly elected representatives. We can question how that all happened. But the fact is, that's the fact, and yet it's Israel getting blamed for the treatment of Palestinians or what they have been dealing with, particularly over this 18 -- or 20-year period, over which they had zero control of this region.\par \par \uc2 KEANE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 And, Neil, you put your finger on it. That is why the Arabs are fed up with this. One, they totally disagree with \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , because they're a terrorist organization and they displayed their savagery. And they are -- they can't make political progress when you have one aspect of this movement that wants to destroy the state of Israel.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the Arab nations want to recognize Israel...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right. Right.\par \par \uc2 KEANE: ... and have a positive relationship with them. We have got two Arab nations sitting right there in Jerusalem who came out against \uc2 Hamas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 This is the UAE and Bahrain.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 KEANE: And the kingdom of Saudi Arabia is moving in that direction.\par \par \uc2 Lookit, here's the sad thing the people in the streets don't even appreciate. If we get rid of \uc2 Hamas\uc2  -- and the hostage-taking is there to prevent that from ever happening -- these people are not stupid who took the hostages. They have never done that on this kind of a scale. They want to protect themselves using those hostages.\par \par \uc2 But if they're able to get rid of \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , I'm convinced that life for Palestinians will improve, civil society will improve, economic uplifting of their society. The oppression from \uc2 Hamas\uc2  will stop on top. And they talk about Israel. They repress these people. And they don't give them the basic benefits of a normal life, for crying out loud.\par \par \uc2 So, there's -- I don't want to be Pollyannish about it. This is complicated who's going to run it after.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: No, I hear you.\par \par \uc2 KEANE: But the Arab states privately are expressing interest here, let's fix this once and for all if \uc2 Hamas\uc2  goes away.\par \par \uc2 And if they really come into play here geopolitically, that will be helpful.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Thank you, General, very much.\par \par \uc2 We're going to be pursuing all of this with Congressman Mike Waltz on what role we should have or who it will ultimately have in deciding Gaza's fate.\par \par \uc2 Before we go to him in -- after this quick break, I do want to show you some tape that's come in of \uc2 Ivanka Trump\uc2  leaving the courthouse now in New York, of course, dealing with the civil trial of her father and the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 She was asked a number of questions for financial details, of which at least two dozen times she was not able to recall any specifics here. But the fact of the matter is, as far as interviewing family members, not quite sure they are totally done with her. I believe they are. But they're not totally done with the former president.\par \par \uc2 We'll have more after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: We have reported how the markets have been going their own way throughout this war. They're higher now than they were before.\par \par \uc2 And this is the kind of news that does it, a better-than-expected earnings report, in this case out of \uc2 Disney\uc2 . The stock is climbing 2 percent after hours. That is the story of these markets.\par \par \uc2 More after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, some updated news right now on some violent incidents certainly happening in the Middle East right now.\par \par \uc2 Jennifer Griffin can update you on that -- Jennifer.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRIFFIN, FOX NEWS NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT: Neil, we just received this breaking news, but FOX News can confirm that, off of the coast of Yemen, a us MQ-9 Reaper drone was shot down earlier today.\par \par \uc2 A senior U.S. defense official said in a statement to us moments ago -- quote -- "We can confirm that a U.S. military MQ-9 remotely piloted aircraft was shot down while in international airspace over international waters off the coast of Yemen."\par \par \uc2 U.S. Central Command officials are assessing the incident, we're told. But, according to multiple defense officials who I spoke to earlier today, the MQ-9 Reaper was conducting surveillance over Yemen when a Houthi militia likely shot down the $32 million U.S. surveillance drone. The Houthis put out a video today of the downing -- of what they said was the downing of the American Reaper, most likely with an SA-6 surface-to-air missile.\par \par \uc2 The Houthis last shot down an MQ-9 Reaper on June 6, 2019. The Reapers are flown generally out of a U.S. air base in Djibouti. On October 19, 2023, you will remember, Neil, that the USS Carney, which was in the Red Sea at the time, shot down 15 drones that were fired from Yemen, four cruise missiles that were heading towards Eilat in Israel.\par \par \uc2 If those drones had actually landed -- or cruise missiles had landed in Eilat, it could have escalated the conflict quite significantly. Iran has long backed the Houthis in Yemen. They're primarily -- these MQ-9 Reapers are primarily used to collect intelligence, but they are armed with -- can be armed with eight laser-guided Hellfire missiles, according to the Air Force.\par \par \uc2 They use sophisticated cameras, sensors, radars to collect intelligence. It's a high-powered camera and can detect heat and operate with very little to no light. They can loiter for 27 hours at over 25,000 feet -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And, of course, the Houthis are financed, not just in part, by Iran.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Absolutely. Iran has been backing the Houthis as part of that civil war in Yemen. The U.S. has been engaged in Yemen going after Al Qaeda, but now the surveillance drones that are in the air, the MQ-9s, they most likely are watching to see if the Houthis are planning any attacks or firing any of those missiles towards Israel, because that could significantly escalate the conflict.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Thank you for that, Jennifer Griffin.\par \par \uc2 To Congressman Mike Waltz of Florida, what he makes of this.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, if we had any doubt about direct or indirect role of this by Iran, of course, we have just got the latest proof. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 REP. MICHAEL WALTZ (R-FL): Well, there is no doubt, Neil. All roads lead back to Iran, period.\par \par \uc2 How do you think the Houthis got the sophisticated air defense system to be able to shoot down one of our top-of-the-line drones? They got it from Iran.\par \par \uc2 And with this administration, until they reverse on their Iran policy, until they go back to maximum pressure and dry up their resources, Iran is going to continue to fund these militias, whether it's in Syria, \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 , in Lebanon, \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , in Gaza, or the Houthis in Yemen, to enact their ring of fire strategy, which comes straight from the ayatollah, to dominate the region, exterminate Israel, control and -- and control Middle East energy.\par \par \uc2 And then finally, Neil, this goes broader than the Middle East, and this is what people, I think, need to fully understand. \uc2 Mossad\uc2  -- the \uc2 Mossad\uc2 , the Israeli intelligence agency, just announced that, in cooperation with Brazilian security services, they just stopped a terror plot in Brazil.\par \par \uc2 So not only are we seeing it in the Middle East. We're seeing it in Brazil. And, of course, our Southern border is wide open, and the \uc2 FBI\uc2  director is stating that we are at our highest state of threat and the potential terror attack in recent memory.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And, in the meantime, there have been 40 such attacks on U.S. soldiers over the last three weeks or so. This incident is another manifestation of that.\par \par \uc2 Do you think that Iran counts on the fact that we will not have a strong response? In other words, we usually go after intermediaries or shoot a tent in the desert, usually a desert in Syria, and we leave it at that, but the Iranians seemingly convinced that we won't dare escalate this to a strike, surgical or otherwise, on Iran, because that invites fears of World War III, and the U.S. doesn't want to do that, so we're going to keep doing what we do.\par \par \uc2 WALTZ: No, it's not what I think, Neil.\par \par \uc2 It's they're just going on what they have seen in the last two years. I mean, you talked about the number of strikes on our bases just in the last month, but the commander, the military -- our military commander of all Middle East forces testified earlier this year that there had been 70 in the last two years, all of which went unresponded to.\par \par \uc2 Look, you cannot take an appeasement approach with the Iranians. You cannot say, if we just give a little, they will come to the table and behave responsibly.\par \par \uc2 No, they see that weakness as opportunity. They see this administration's fear of escalation as an opportunity to be more aggressive. That actually invites more instability. Sometimes, you have to escalate, to de-escalate.\par \par \uc2 That's exactly what the Trump administration, President Trump, Secretary Pompeo did when they took out Soleimani, and all of this provocation stopped. Deterrence was restored. And until we impose costs on Iran itself, whether they're financial or otherwise, this type of aggression is going to continue.\par \par \uc2 They will trade proxy casualties -- they will trade proxy casualties for us all day long.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Congressman, I don't mean to interrupt you, but you just...\par \par \uc2 WALTZ: Sure.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You said that, and, sure enough, you predicted it. And it has happened again.\par \par \uc2 Now we're getting word that armed drone targets at another air base hosted U.S. forces in Northern Iraq have been hit. We don't know the degree. But Reuters is citing two security sources that say armed drone targets this Al-Harir Air Base.\par \par \uc2 This would be, I believe, sir, the 41st such attack in three weeks, and on we go. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 WALTZ: Until you impose costs on Iran, Neil, this is going to continue. They will trade proxy casualties for American casualties and Israeli casualties all day long.\par \par \uc2 It's a good deal for Iran right now. And that's the dynamic we have to change.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it. I'm sorry to jump on you there.\par \par \uc2 WALTZ: No worries.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But I did want to pass along that news development.\par \par \uc2 WALTZ: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Thank you, and for your incredible service to this country, Congressman.\par \par \uc2 We're going to update you on this.\par \par \uc2 Imagine being a family that lost a key member, killed on October 7, and then others who are still hostages, and you're hearing the ramp-up of this type of violence. We're going to talk to that family and the ones who want to get the rest of them out -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: We have been talking back and forth about October 7 and then the horrific events that launched this now-better-than-one month war.\par \par \uc2 There are a lot of people who don't believe that October 7 ever happened, despite the fact that there are tapes, in fact, tapes shot by \uc2 Hamas\uc2  itself.\par \par \uc2 But my next guests know a thing or two about it. They are Merav Raviv, Itay Raviv. They are looking for members of the Munder family. They are relatives up there. And, of course, this all started on October 7.\par \par \uc2 Both of you are here, and I appreciate you're taking the time. I know you have been making this a mission to get the word out.\par \par \uc2 You're talking about, I guess, your uncle...\par \par \uc2 MERAV RAVIV, FAMILY MEMBER OF \uc2 HAMAS\uc2  HOSTAGES: CAVUTO: ... Abraham Munder -- your uncle -- I apologize -- and your aunt Ruth and her status.\par \par \uc2 Their son was killed in this attack. And the rest...\par \par \uc2 M. RAVIV: Murdered.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And they were taken hostage.\par \par \uc2 Now, can you explain that a separate cousin has since died or what...\par \par \uc2 M. RAVIV: Yes, I will tell you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 M. RAVIV: My uncle and my wife -- I mean, his wife -- sorry -- they're both 78 years old. They live in kibbutz Nir Oz, which is only 1.5 miles from the Gaza border, for almost 60 years.\par \par \uc2 And they have a small family, a daughter and a son. Their son still lived in that kibbutz, but not on the same -- not in the same house. He was single. He was 50 years old.\par \par \uc2 And Keren, their daughter, left the kibbutz, like, a few years ago. And she lives with her partner in another town in the center of Israel with her only son, their only grandson, Ohad, which just turned 9 on the 23rd of October.\par \par \uc2 On that weekend, it was a holiday, the long weekend in Israel. Keren and Ohad just came to visit grandma and grandpa for the weekend.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 M. RAVIV: And they found themselves in that horrible Saturday morning.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: How did you find out about it?\par \par \uc2 M. RAVIV: We got used to it. As you said before, for almost 20 years, there are missiles all over that area.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But how did you find out about the attack October 7?\par \par \uc2 M. RAVIV: So -- yes.\par \par \uc2 So we got used to, sometimes, they had missiles.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 M. RAVIV: And then we text them, and they said everything is OK, and life goes back to normal.\par \par \uc2 But on that Saturday morning, we woke up with missiles all over Israel. So, at the beginning, we didn't ask them how are they, because it was unique that the missiles were all over Israel. So, only at about -- the whole family tried to contact.\par \par \uc2 And Keren text her partner that she doesn't want Ohad, her son, to be scared, so they're not watching the news on TV, and he's playing with his iPad. Ruth's sister, my aunt's sister, called her at 9:58, and she was whispering, "We can't talk," since the terrorist entered the kibbutz, arrived to the kibbutz.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So they broke in?\par \par \uc2 M. RAVIV: Yes. And that was it.\par \par \uc2 Later on, we know that their phones and Ohad's smartwatch was geolocated in Gaza.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Ohad was their son.\par \par \uc2 M. RAVIV: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And he was killed.\par \par \uc2 M. RAVIV: No, no, no, no, no, no.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Oh, he was not. OK.\par \par \uc2 M. RAVIV: They have -- in the picture, you can see now Roee, my cousin. He was single. He was...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: He was the one.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 M. RAVIV: Yes, he was the one who just...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I apologize. OK.\par \par \uc2 M. RAVIV: So, we didn't know anything during that Saturday.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Just that the others were eventually taken.\par \par \uc2 ITAY RAVIV, FAMILY MEMBER 