I understand.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But at -- whoever was on the 7th, and there's no word on the others since.\par \par \uc2 Now you know that there's reports of 240, maybe more...\par \par \uc2 M. RAVIV: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: ... hostages under \uc2 Hamas\uc2  control. And there's a move afoot to cool it on the bombing, if Prime Minister Netanyahu accepts hostages. That's the only time he would stop to get hostages.\par \par \uc2 What do you think of that?\par \par \uc2 I. RAVIV: We think that all hostages should be released unconditionally and immediately, because this is a crime against humanity.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Are you afraid that \uc2 Hamas\uc2  will just sort of space this out?\par \par \uc2 I. RAVIV: Yes, obviously, obviously. \uc2 Hamas\uc2  took that many hostages so they would have this time to maneuver and play those mind games and strategic plays.\par \par \uc2 But this is just a -- continuous of their atrocities. They're -- every day that they're hold -- that they're held there is another day of a crime against humanity. They took civilians.\par \par \uc2 M. RAVIV: There are 30 babies, 30 kids from the age of 10 months -- he was 9 months when he was taken -- until the age of 15, 30 kids.\par \par \uc2 Some of them were evidence of the murder of their parents. They shot the parents and they kidnapped the 4-year-old baby.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, how do you feel? We have been following all of these protests, guys, around the world.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Most are in support of the Palestinians. They're not so bold in a lot of cases to say, we support \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . But they -- the ones who are orchestrating a lot of those rallies don't believe this October 7 happened.\par \par \uc2 How do you explain that?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 I. RAVIV: They say it didn't happen, so where's our family?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 I. RAVIV: It's as simplest as possible. Like you said, \uc2 Hamas\uc2  filmed their atrocities by themselves.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Was Roee's killing filmed?\par \par \uc2 M. RAVIV: Yes. Oh, no, not...\par \par \uc2 I. RAVIV: No. No.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: It was not.\par \par \uc2 I. RAVIV: No, Roee's killing was not filmed.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So how do you know -- I'm sorry for the ignorance. How do you know he's dead?\par \par \uc2 M. RAVIV: Yes.\par \par \uc2 I. RAVIV: There's...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I'm sorry.\par \par \uc2 M. RAVIV: Someone saw -- yes, but before that, on Monday morning, one of the evidence of the kibbutz told us -- he had the courage only on Monday morning to tell us that he saw his body in front of his house.\par \par \uc2 And he's -- I send you the video. His house was burned to ashes. We even couldn't find the DNA to identify his body.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: How did they decide who lived and who died?\par \par \uc2 M. RAVIV: I don't know.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Who became a victim versus who became a hostage?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 I. RAVIV: This is just, I think, in random.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 I. RAVIV: Although they also had maps of every kibbutz. They had maps, and they knew where the security person lives and where there are more people, where there are kids, so they could go there and kill as many people as possible.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But did you guys see that the strategy that \uc2 Hamas\uc2  was using here - - there was a New York Times story on this -- that they thought they would get bloody here to make a statement and to put the fear of God out there?\par \par \uc2 They said that they believed the Palestinian cause was slipping away as an issue and that only violence could revive it. So the whole purpose of these attacks, if we're to believe this, was to jolt the world into action and attention to Palestinians.\par \par \uc2 M. RAVIV: They don't care about Palestinians. I know that for sure.\par \par \uc2 First of all, they shot even the Palestinians that were...\par \par \uc2 I. RAVIV: There were some Palestinians in Israel with the festival that happened, as you probably know, and they shot Palestinians as well. They shot Muslims. They shot Christians.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So what do you think is \uc2 Hamas\uc2 ' endgame?\par \par \uc2 I. RAVIV: They -- I don't think they have an endgame. I think they're...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: They just hate you.\par \par \uc2 I. RAVIV: They just want to exterminate all Jewish people, and they want to exterminate Israel, as they said bluntly and to the open as many times as possible.\par \par \uc2 And the world hasn't understood it enough, apparently. And now they -- the goal is to demolish \uc2 Hamas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 I. RAVIV: But I think that the number one goal should be first to release all the hostages, because this is just a cynical game that they're playing.\par \par \uc2 They're saying, we want cease-fire only if -- sorry -- we will release hostages only if you have a cease-fire.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 M. RAVIV: On October 6, there was a cease-fire.\par \par \uc2 I. RAVIV: There was a cease-fire.\par \par \uc2 M. RAVIV: A day before.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Very good point. Very good point. All right.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: We will watch it very closely, guys. I know this has been wrenching for you. I cannot imagine it, but you're on a mission. You're on a mission from God.\par \par \uc2 M. RAVIV: Yes, but one important thing.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Please, finish it.\par \par \uc2 M. RAVIV: There are hostages like my uncle, for example. He is not in a good condition. He hardly walks. He walks with a cane. He hardly sees. He has problems with his eyes. He wear glasses.\par \par \uc2 We don't know if he gets his medicine. And I told you the youngest is 10 months old, and the oldest is 85 years old.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Incredible.\par \par \uc2 M. RAVIV: And from that kibbutz, there are 78 hostages out of population of 400.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Just amazing. Keep us posted. We will be following it. We'd love to have you back, see how this story ends, hopefully, hopefully happily.\par \par \uc2 M. RAVIV: Yes.\par \par \uc2 I. RAVIV: Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 M. RAVIV: Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: We shall see.\par \par \uc2 We'll have more after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, I want to bring you up to date on two urgent developments in the Middle East right now.\par \par \uc2 One concerns an armed drone targeting Al-Harir Air Base hosting U.S. forces in Northern Iraq. There is still another development we're keeping on top of, an MQ-9 Reaper drone, about $32 million worth, shot down by Houthi rebel missiles. Of course, Houthi rebels of Yemen are part of the so-called proxies that Iran has and pays for. We don't know much more than that.\par \par \uc2 We do know that we have just the right guest for the moment, Senator Mark Warner, the Senate Intelligence Committee chairman, among others.\par \par \uc2 Senator, we don't know a lot about these details. We do know right now that they that they involve entities that are connected to Iran. What do you make of it?\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARK WARNER (D-VA): Well, I literally got told about the incident just as we were coming on. So I'm -- I know as little as you.\par \par \uc2 I do know this, that we have been worried for some time that Iranian proxies in the region, whether it be the Houthis or whether it be Shia militia groups that received backing from Iran, may try to go after more of our troops, and in the region. We have got literally tens of thousands of troops, not just in Syria, Iraq, Bahrain, all across the region.\par \par \uc2 It's one of the reasons why, while we all support Israel's efforts to eliminate the \uc2 Hamas\uc2  leadership, we do think they need to do this with some care, because the chance and possibility of this widening to a regional war is enormous. It's one of the reasons why I was proud -- or happy, finally, to see Prime Minister Netanyahu say he was going to try to rein in some of the settler violence that is taking place in the West Bank, where innocent Palestinians are getting killed.\par \par \uc2 Because the last thing Israel needs at this point is for the Palestinian security forces, for example, in the West Bank to all quit at once. And, suddenly, you have got to have the Israeli Defense fund -- Forces not only deal in Gaza, but also on the West Bank.\par \par \uc2 So this is a very dangerous time, Neil, as you know, and I hope most members appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I just wonder if we can appreciate the wrath of Iran here. If it is behind these now, what, 40-some attacks in the last three weeks, maybe the assumption, Senator, is that they don't think we will hit them. We might hit their proxies.\par \par \uc2 And you have heard the old story, we will hit a tent in the desert in Syria and leave it at that. And they know that because they know that we're anxious about targeting them directly because that would invite a wider conflict, some say even World War III. How do you feel about that? Do you think we should respond to these attacks going after Iran itself?\par \par \uc2 WARNER: I think we have to be very careful and thoughtful.\par \par \uc2 Who fires the first bullet directly from Iranian forces or American forces at each other bears a huge amount of responsibility. I think the Iranians are following very closely, not one, but the fact that we have got two carrier groups in the region, one that's actually more closely aligned, I believe, over on the Red Sea side, closer to Iran, and one in the Eastern Mediterranean.\par \par \uc2 I think they are aware of that show of force. That's basically -- we haven't put forces like that...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But do you think they're assuming -- we only have a little less than a minute left. I apologize, Senator.\par \par \uc2 WARNER: Sure.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Do you think they safely assume we won't go after them directly in this environment?\par \par \uc2 WARNER: I do not believe they...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And they take advantage of that?\par \par \uc2 WARNER: Neil, I do not believe they don't think we will safely -- or they -- we won't go after them.\par \par \uc2 I do tell you this, though, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Sure.\par \par \uc2 WARNER: If we walk away from Ukraine and don't honor our commitment there, then I think that gives more signals to Iran that America doesn't keep its word, it doesn't honor its alliances.\par \par \uc2 And anybody that cares about Iran or cares about President Xi's rise and the CCP's rise in China, if you don't understand the connection that has to Putin and Ukraine, you're flunking geopolitics 101.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 All right, Senator, I know we hit you with a lot of breaking news at the last second. We appreciate your understanding there.\par \par \uc2 WARNER: Thanks, Neil. That's all right.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Senator Mark Warner, the Senate Intelligence Committee chairman.\par \par \uc2 So much, we don't know. This much, we do. The war that everyone doesn't want to widen keeps stubbornly trying to widen. We will stay on top of it.\par \par \uc2 "The Five" is now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020231108ejb80005l\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Election Night In America\b0\par\par\uc2 Sean Hannity, Rich Edson, Bret Baier, Charlie Hurt, Kayleigh  McEnany, Chad Pergram \par \uc2 7,963 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 November 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Hannity\par \uc2 SHAN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SEAN HANNITY\uc2 , FOX NEWS HOST: And welcome to "Hannity".\par \par \uc2 We begin tonight with the FOX News alert. Election night 2023 is well underway. Polls are now closed in several key states where close races could serve as maybe a small preview of coming attractions for the 2024 election.\par \par \uc2 In a moment, we'll take you live on the ground in Virginia where Governor Glenn Youngkin's GOP turnout strategy is now being put to the test. Will state Republicans be able to flip the Senate and hold on to the House? Well, a heavy lift for sure, but we'll bring you the very latest results straight ahead tonight.\par \par \uc2 Plus, we'll check with Kentucky where the race has now been called for the incumbent Democrat \uc2 Andy Beshear\uc2 . We're also tracking another governor's race, this one in Mississippi, an abortion referendum in Ohio and a state Supreme Court race in Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 Also tonight, coming up, House Judiciary Committee Chairman Jim Jordan, he will join us. We have a lot of breaking news with details from today's closed door hearing with \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's DOJ guardian angel turned special counsel David Weiss.\par \par \uc2 And the former prime minister of Israel will be here as well. We'll have the very latest of the war in Gaza, and the brutality that is the terrorist group \uc2 Hamas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But, first, joining us now at the very latest out of Fredericksburg, Virginia, our very own Rich Edson is with us.\par \par \uc2 Rich, all eyes on the Commonwealth of Virginia. What's going on there?\par \par \uc2 RICH EDSON, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yeah, good evening, Sean, and it is a real test for Governor Glenn Youngkin, his turnout strategy and the Republican agenda here to see if they can control both houses of the legislature to push that agenda through the state government. What you've got here is Governor Glenn Youngkin. He has visited Fredericksburg, this district of Virginia 27, a number of different times over the past several weeks.\par \par \uc2 He was here campaigning yesterday. It was one of his six stops that he made to polling locations today, six total locations for the governor about 156 miles when you talk to his team there. On top of all that, you've got the governor down in Richmond watching right now, and has really been campaigning on the economy. Republicans have also been campaigning on a number of different issues like public safety and on education.\par \par \uc2 Democrats have hit abortion and they've also hit gun control. On abortion, the 15-week plan that Republicans have to try to curb abortions after 15 weeks, that's something that they have really, really focused on in this state. It's what they've brought a lot of outside help to do, a lot of outside money to do. Former President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  has recorded a robocall in this state and President Biden, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , the vice president, have made endorsements in this race.\par \par \uc2 So, a really, really national race here as all eyes are indeed on Virginia as the returns begin coming in here. Polls closed about a couple of hours ago -- Sean.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. Rich, by the way, Governor Youngkin's plan, I like the idea of voting early voting by mail, which have Republicans and conservatives have been reluctant and resistant to do, certainly a shifting paradigm I think for the long term is going to be a good plan.\par \par \uc2 I had basically given up on Virginia as ever turning red again until Governor Youngkin came on the scene. But certainly, put himself out there but still a very heavy lift especially when you get into northern Virginia where a lot of the D.C. swamp has moved into, especially in the last, you know, 15 years or so, right?\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Yeah, I mean, this is still a state that President Biden won in 2020 by more than 10 percentage points. It was a year later that Governor Youngkin won by about two percentage points. But this is by no means a solidly red state or the solidly red state that it once was.\par \par \uc2 And so, you know, Republicans have really had the challenge here, education was an enormous issue for Glenn Youngkin when he ran initially back in 2021. Republicans have made that an issue in the first two years of his -- of his term here. They have broadened that out really to deal with public safety and, of course, the abortion issue on top of that.\par \par \uc2 But that is why this state is so important going forward to what we're looking at next year. And I talked to Governor Youngkin about this earlier today. I said, are the issues that are important in this state and what is at stake in this election going to set the table for the national election that's going to start next year and it really has already started, and he says it absolutely will.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I think there's no doubt that this will have an impact regardless of how tonight might turn out in the commonwealth. Rich Edson, thank you.\par \par \uc2 And some breaking news, Ohio voters, they have passed a constitutional amendment protecting access to abortion, extending statewide wins for abortion rights. We'll give you more details on that as our breaking election night coverage continues.\par \par \uc2 Let's check in with the host of "Special Report", our friend Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 Bret, I don't know, this used to be the Hemmer big board. I'm not sure if Bill Hemmer's going to like this.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS HOST: No, no, I'm just sitting in, just sitting in.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. You're allowed to sit in.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Borrowing it, yeah.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: You're allowed to borrow. It used to be the Hannity big board way back in the day, but Bret's had the big board tonight. Yeah.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Listen, we got a couple races. We just, you know, the call in Kentucky, the call in Ohio, we just bounce around here. The two governors races, Kentucky and Mississippi, you know, in Mississippi, the incumbent \uc2 Tate Reeves\uc2  leading, but it's single digits coming in so far.\par \par \uc2 This is deep, deep Republican territory though and his Democratic challenger \uc2 Brandon Presley\uc2 , state utility, regulator cousin of the Elvis Presley, if he managed to pull off a win here, you know, deep red Mississippi would be all shook up.\par \par \uc2 All right. So if you've got a check now here in Kentucky because \uc2 Andy Beshear\uc2  wins this race called over \uc2 Daniel Cameron\uc2 , he really outperformed 2019. Back in 2019, you may remember, he ran against \uc2 Matt Bevin\uc2 , only won by 5,000 votes. Here, he outperformed in coal country, Beshear did, the incumbent Democrat and \uc2 Daniel Cameron\uc2  underperformed where Bevin was in some of those places, in some of those counties that he was supposed to do better in.\par \par \uc2 Five statewide referendums, you talked about Virginia. Now, this is really interesting because we don't have all of the data yet, because these are individual races in control of the House and the Senate. Governor Glenn Youngkin wants to take over control of all of the chambers in Virginia. In the House, there's a slight Republican lead, 48, 46, they're trying to hold on to that.\par \par \uc2 In the state Senate, they're trying to pick up seats against the Democrats, some early numbers in some of these races are looking good for Democrats. In state district 31, for example, Russet Perry is ahead. That's a really close race, one we're watching. You can't call this yet but it's looking good on the state Senate side for the Democrats at this point.\par \par \uc2 Obviously, Glenn Youngkin has a lot of different legislative things he wants to do with the House and Senate if he gets that in the commonwealth.\par \par \uc2 The other one we're talking about is what was just called in Ohio. There are actually two referendums there. One is to legalize marijuana, the other one is you just called a yes vote on the state Issue 1, that is individual reproductive rights, abortion rights in the state constitution. What this does with this win, the yes one, it does bring up whether this issue, the abortion issue is as strong as it was in 2022, and what it portends for 2024 in a lot of these states.\par \par \uc2 Democrats are going to look at this and this win and say, we need to put this on other ballots for 2024 to affect the turnout obviously in the upcoming election. So that's what we have so far. Waiting on Mississippi numbers, waiting on Virginia numbers, but a couple of big calls in Kentucky and Ohio.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right, Bret. We'll check in all throughout the night. Thank you, Bret. Appreciate it, and regardless of what happens tonight, Republicans and Democrats now agree \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  he's in deep trouble,\par \par \uc2 An NBC News poll put it this way, there is a five alarm fire among Democrats as \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's support is now bottomed out. In fact, if the presidential election were held tonight, Joe would lose in an electoral landslide. Now, that's according to the brand new "New York Times" poll.\par \par \uc2 Another poll, this one from "Reuters" shows that Biden is near his lowest approval ever, at only 36 percent. The sitting president frankly just can't win re-election with those kind of numbers.\par \par \uc2 Now, voters also strongly disapprove of Biden's handling of the economy, so much for the Bidenomics that they've been pushing. But you have real inflation, real difficulties at the highest mortgage rates we've had in decades. We don't have secure borders, national security concerns around the globe. American soldiers now have been attacked 40 times in just the past few days at bases in the Middle East, with almost no response from \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  at all, barely any comments.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, his own party is now deeply divided over the issue of Israel, as Biden's radical pro-\uc2 Hamas\uc2  base is threatening to stay home in 2024. And last but not least, Americans are very concerned with Biden's obvious cognitive decline. It appears to be getting worse, which will only seem to continue as we march towards 2024, and one year from now.\par \par \uc2 As Kamala would say, that's the significance of the passage of time. And according to a growing number of Democrats are now begging Biden, to now bow out of the race, including former Democratic congressman and Senate candidate \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2 . But, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is clinging on for dear life. As always, Biden values his own personal vanity above all else.\par \par \uc2 And unfortunately, for Joe and his fellow Democrats,, he is now so unpopular and incompetent that even his own weaponized Justice Department won't be able to push him across the finish line this time.\par \par \uc2 We're going to have more on this failed political strategy to get \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , that's coming up. But, first, here with reaction to all of tonight's top stories, we have FOX News contributor Charlie Hurt, and the co-host of "Outnumbered", \uc2 Kayleigh McEnany\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Kayleigh, I'm not sure what we could read into this. Kentucky may be a surprise to somebody -- to some people because they thought that was -- might have been a competitive race. However, on the issue of energy, the current Democratic governor is at great odds with his own party, isn't he?\par \par \uc2 KAYLEIGH MCENANY, CO-HOST OF "OUTNUMBERED": He is and he ran away from \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , and why would you not? I mean, you just went through the polling and it's a game of how low can you go for Joe?\par \par \uc2 But one thing I would note, you know, Kentucky's a red state. Ohio is a red state. Mississippi, we don't know what will happen this evening, we hope that Reeves pulls it off, but it is a red state.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, the midterm elections, the last few elections, we must recognize that as a party, good polling does not always translate into resounding victory. It must be operationalized with a mail and vote strategy, with a voting early strategy, with a get out the vote strategy, to put a tailwind behind those very poor numbers for Biden, and very good numbers for Trump.\par \par \uc2 One other point I would just make because on the issue of abortion, in Ohio tonight, we continue the losing streak in the pro-life movement. Every ballot initiative has been lost post Dobbs for the pro-life movement.\par \par \uc2 As a party, Sean, we must -- we must not just be a pro-baby party, that's a great thing. We must be a pro-mother party. We need a national strategy and I talked to Mike Johnson about this, we'll air that tomorrow on "Outnumbered", there's legislation we must put forward as a party to support women.\par \par \uc2 And it's out there. Senator Rubio's laid it out, but we've got to get Trump behind it, the speaker of the House behind it, and have a national strategy to help vulnerable women because the results of next year's election could be determined by that.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: If we're really going to be honest about this and I consider myself pro-life, but I understand that's not where the country is. I would say first trimester or Dobbs, 15 weeks seems to be where the country is, Kayleigh. I want to stay with you on this issue and these issues will be decided by the states.\par \par \uc2 You asked -- you talk to the speaker of the House, I talk to the speaker of the House, it is not going to be an issue in the House of Representatives. This is not going to be decided any longer Washington, D.C. The states will decide.\par \par \uc2 So if states say that, okay, 15 weeks is the legal time period where women can have an abortion, then the other side of the issue, there are many Democrats that want no restrictions on abortion at all whatsoever, that this should be a decision only between a woman and a doctor. I don't think that argument has been made enough by Republicans. Thoughts?\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: No, no. The language in Ohio tonight, Sean, this is what happens. It is so loose, the language. The \uc2 ACLU\uc2  will roll in and this is abortion until birth we're talking about. This is minors getting abortion without parental consent. We must as a party number one expose the extremity of the left, number two, pass legislation.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow, I want the House of Representatives passing a legislation for men to pay women child support from the moment of conception, legislation to make the child tax credit apply to the unborn, legislation to have women have access to the supplemental food and nutrition program up to two years after child birth, these are things that can be done today that will make a difference.\par \par \uc2 But until we own this issue as a party, we will lose again and again and again.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: You know, Charlie, let's get your thoughts on this. Do you agree with me that probably the national consensus on abortion is about 12 to 15 weeks, first trimester, 15 weeks?\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE HURT, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: I think -- no, I think you're exactly right about it, and obviously, Kayleigh is exactly right about this, you know? And this is what happens when you go for 50 years on an unelected group of Supreme Court justices take this vitally important issue out of voters hands and make the -- you know, rule by fiat in Washington, and then -- when we -- you know, thankfully get it turned to return to the states and return to voters, it's a difficult issue and we're working through it.\par \par \uc2 This is the third election. It's going to be difficult and it's going to be awkward and we're -- everybody's kind of got to try to find their voice on it. Democrats demagogue the issue to the -- to -- you know, very --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: They're trying to scare -- but Democrats are trying to scare women into thinking Republicans don't want abortion legal under any circumstances. And I go back to Pennsylvania 2022, the Republican candidate for governor, if I remember correctly, no exceptions for rape, incest, to the mother's life, and lost to a non-incumbent gubernatorial candidate, Democratic candidate by a margin that was not seen since the 1940s.\par \par \uc2 So I have to believe that is an indication that the women in America, suburban moms want it probably legal and rare, and probably earlier than, you know, at the point of viability.\par \par \uc2 HURT: And absolutely, and I -- but I think that what Kayleigh said is so important and that is that, you know, the Republican position on abortion is shared by like 80 percent of the population. The Democrat position which is abortion up to the point of birth and in the case, in Virginia, you're talking like abortion afterbirth where you put the baby on a table and keep the baby comfortable while people make decisions. I mean, it's crazy, it's nuts. That's the Democrats' decision.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Those were the words of then-Governor Northam. That was Governor Northam that said that.\par \par \uc2 HURT: Right, and this is -- and that stuff is so whacko crazy and Republicans have to learn as Kayleigh said, have to learn how to talk about this and explain how wacko extreme Democrats are on this issue, and that -- and while, you know, you know, strictly pro-life people are not going to be happy with the end result of what you get, they'll be happy to take -- to start with the 15 weeks. They'll be happy to bring some sort of reasonable sanity to this because when Democrats get their way, it is full 9 months and possibly beyond.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: By the way, this is just breaking, the House of Representatives will vote again tonight on the issue of censure for Congresswoman Tlaib. We're not sure exactly when, it may be in this hour. Stay with us. We'll cover that for you.\par \par \uc2 I want to go to the Commonwealth of Virginia. There's something that Governor Glenn Youngkin, regardless of what the results are tonight, before Glenn Youngkin won that race, you know, two years ago, I thought -- I thought Virginia was gone. I never thought it was even on the map for Republicans, I didn't think it would be worth investing a penny in Virginia, but he proved a lot of us wrong.\par \par \uc2 And he certainly put a couple of things on the map here regardless of what the outcome is going to end up being, and that is that Republicans and their reluctance and resistance, Kayleigh, to voting early, voting by mail has to stop. The second part of this, and I know that the \uc2 RNC\uc2  has adopted bank your vote, smart -- a smart strategy.\par \par \uc2 The second part of it has to be, is Democrats have mastered in states that have what's called legal ballot harvesting, they have mastered strategies. Republicans aren't even in the legal ballot harvesting game, and I have not seen any effort movement at all to get in that game and I think they're making a huge mistake if they don't.\par \par \uc2 Whatever the results are tonight, Glenn Youngkin was on the right track in terms of Republicans trying to stop the bleeding and going into election day down hundreds of thousands of votes. But I also think in those states where they have legal ballot harvesting, that's got to be adopted by the Republicans and they must equal the efforts of the Democrats or surpass them.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: No doubt about it. I mean, he has spot on, Glenn Youngkin, to say, vote early. We must vote early. We must embrace these laws.\par \par \uc2 Sean, 50 days of early voting in Pennsylvania, 50 days. When we don't vote early as a party, what, we all show up on election day and then rain happens, something happens with your kids, whatever happens in life, people don't show up. There are emergencies, you've got to vote early.\par \par \uc2 But one thing \uc2 Ronna McDaniel\uc2  actually pointed out to me because I do believe she's on top of this, Nevada -- okay, look at Nevada, they have codified, Sean, into law that every registered voter, you know what happens on those voter roles, dead people are in the voter roles, people -- they're not cleaned early and often, everyone gets a ballot, okay? Everyone gets a ballot, even people who aren't with us any longer.\par \par \uc2 We as a party have to vote because 11 points \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is up in Nevada, that doesn't matter until we vote, and have an early vote strategy and play the same game as Democrats.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Yeah, I agree with that.\par \par \uc2 Charlie, anything else that you think that we can glean from tonight regardless of whether or not Republicans win the House of Delegates and the Senate in Virginia, do you think that now, Virginia's is back to at least being purple?\par \par \uc2 HURT: Right. I mean, I think that Virginia is going to be a very difficult state for Republicans going forward, just because largely because of what you were pointing out earlier about the encroachment of the swamp into more of Virginia. But, you know, the bright spot of this is, that Glenn Youngkin won Virginia by drilling -- taking an issue that was historically Democrat, drilling down into it, and exposing the fraud that that Democrats were perpetuating on parents through education.\par \par \uc2 And he did a very good job of it, and -- but I would argue that there are a lot of issues the Democrats claim to you know credit for in Virginia and elsewhere that you can go after them on and you have to do it smart and, of course, obviously, Kayleigh is exactly right, it's not just enough to be right on the issues, and to expose how Democrats are bad on the economy, they're bad on education, they're bad on immigration, they're bad on, you know, all of these things.\par \par \uc2 You -- it's not enough to just win the argument, you have to win the battle which is -- and coming up with the plans to win on Election Day and win on early voting or whatever, you know, whatever the rules allow, you've got to play by those rules and you've got to win.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. Kayleigh, Charlie Hurt, thank you both.\par \par \uc2 We have another FOX News alert, breaking only moments ago, the House is now planning to hold a vote any minute maybe to censure antisemitic Congresswoman Tlaib. We'll take you there live in the House as it happens.\par \par \uc2 Also coming up, \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's special counsel David Weiss testified before the House Judiciary Committee today. Jim Jordan, he'll join us live next to tell us, well, what happens behind closed doors as we continue.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. A big day on Capitol Hill today where David Weiss, the special counsel investigating \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 , was grilled during a closed door interview with the House Judiciary Committee.\par \par \uc2 Now, here's committee chairman Jim Jordan after that interview. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. JIM JORDAN (R-OH): When he was specifically asked, did you ever request special attorney authority under section 515, Mr. Weiss's response was yes in the spring of 2022. So, that goes to the heart of the matter. He requested it, was not given that request, never had that authority throughout the time, and yet he pretends that somehow he did.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Well, Jordan also broke more bombshell news last night with shocking new evidence on the federal government censorship campaign ahead of the 2020 election. Now, we know that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  was meeting with big tech week after week after week in the months leading up to the 2020 election, warning big tech that they may be victims of a down -- victims of a campaign -- a disinformation campaign. We learned that, and then we learned that, in fact, the disinformation campaign had everything to -- may have everything to do with Hunter, may have everything to do with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And in fact, when they knew the truth, they never told the truth. Oh, and by the way, the government we learned was censoring information from a lot of other people, including me, yours truly, the -- Molly Hemingway, Governor \uc2 Mike Huckabee\uc2 , \uc2 Newt Gingrich\uc2 , Tom Fitton, and so many others. Really? Wow.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, here with the very latest is House Judiciary Committee Chairman Jim Jordan.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, sorry to ask the selfish question first, but I feel my privacy has already been invaded. It's why I've not had an email account for years and years and years because, you know --\par \par \uc2 JORDAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: -- it seems like, you know, the government's perfectly fine releasing my private text messages with Paul Manafort, my private text messages with people that work for the government, my private text messages on January 6th. So I don't really have privacy.\par \par \uc2 How were they trying to -- they were censoring me, our government was involved in this. Do I have any recourse for this, legal recourse?\par \par \uc2 JORDAN: We actually -- we've introduced legislation, Senator Paul and I, that would give you some recourse and would hold these people accountable. So, that -- that's a legislative remedy that we're pursuing. But here's -- here's the real takeaway, the censorship industrial complex was bigger than we thought.\par \par \uc2 It wasn't just big government working with big tech to limit your speech, and limit \uc2 Twitter\uc2  or tweets and posts on \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , it was big government, big universities and big tech all working together to limit speech. And it was disproportionately -- as you listed off all those conservatives, disproportionately targeting conservatives. Molly Hemingway, \uc2 Sean Hannity\uc2 , President Trump, members of Congress, you can go down the list. That's who they went after.\par \par \uc2 And here's the kicker, it was all true stuff, or some cases it was satire. It was a joke, but it was all true and yet they went after it uh in this organized fashion where they limited how -- the reach it would have, the visibility filtering they did, and they took things down, that's how egregious this was.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. Let me ask you about Weiss that you had an opportunity to grill today, because the -- we learned from the \uc2 IRS\uc2  whistleblowers and they took contemporaneous notes that Mr. Weiss said he had no power to bring any charges, investigative charges to \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  in jurisdictions outside of his own.\par \par \uc2 Now, correct me if I'm wrong, didn't Mr. Weiss and didn't \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  testify to just the opposite?\par \par \uc2 JORDAN: Yeah, \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  said he had ultimate authority. In fact, David Weiss this summer told us three different things in the 33-day time period, June 7th, he writes to me and says, I have ultimate authority to determine when where and whether to bring charges. June 30th, he writes me again and says, well, no, actually, I don't my authority is limited to my U.S. attorney's district, the district of Delaware.\par \par \uc2 And then July 10th, he writes to \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 , the ranking member on the \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2  and says, well, I've not sought special counsel authority but I've had discussions with the Department of Justice.\par \par \uc2 Now, today under oath, he said something different because he was directly asked, did you ever seek special attorney status and his response was yes, as I pointed out earlier today, yes, in the spring of 2022.\par \par \uc2 So he sought it but was never actually given that, and yet he maintained somehow that, oh, I still had the authority, even though I asked for it and even though they never gave it to me, somehow he still had it.\par \par \uc2 So his story doesn't stack up, but the guy's story who does hold true, that is never wavered is Gary Shapley and Mr. Ziegler, the whistleblowers. And everything we learn today further confirms what Mr. Shapley and Mr. Ziegler have come forward and told us.\par \par \uc2 And remember, Sean, they stood up to a couple hours of cross examination from Democrats in the oversight committee and again their story has checked out with every witness that we've deposed.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Okay, so that means that before Congress, one side lied and one side told the truth, both sides can't be telling the truth. Now, I tend to believe the whistleblowers, the ones that took contemporaneous notes, and I tend not to believe the people that we know that have been active and involved in protecting all things Biden family. So I thought if you lied before Congress, you can get arrested for that and be charged for that, am I wrong?\par \par \uc2 JORDAN: Yeah. You can, it's tough to prove, but it sure looks like \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  misled us. I think David Weiss certainly misled us with his initial response to me on June 7th this summer, because that changed as I said in a one month time period, and what he said today didn't back up what he told us in those letters this summer.\par \par \uc2 HANNIT