bility in the region, but he has not shown the Iranians any will to actually use that capability, and therefore, he is failing to restore deterrence.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, Richard, President Biden is asking Netanyahu for a brief three-day. They're not calling a ceasefire. They call it a pause to make themselves feel better so they could release more hostages. Netanyahu reportedly rejected the idea and said, he doesn't trust \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . And it sounds like to me that Biden is listening to the political conversation in the streets and the pressure being brought to bear against the White House over the weekend. Your thoughts on the pause suggestion.\par \par \uc2 GOLDBERG: Well, remember, this is the same White House that brokered a hostage deal paying $6 billion for five people. Who brokered that hostage deal? Qatar. Who's brokering this hostage deal? Qatar. Who harbors \uc2 Hamas\uc2  and is the sponsor of \uc2 Hamas\uc2 ? Qatar. What are we doing here? Of course, this is going to be a hostage negotiation in \uc2 Hamas\uc2 's favor.\par \par \uc2 And so, you know what, there's 240 hostages. Let's not talk about a ceasefire to let \uc2 Hamas\uc2  live to massacre another day for 12 people. Let's talk about unconditional release of 240 people, or we start putting maximum pressure on Qatar.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, apparently, they're not worried about Qatar. They're worried about politics back home. Richard, thank you. All right, we're going to have another live report from Virginia as election results are beginning to trickle in. Plus, the return of Jimmy Failla. We need that tonight, don't we? Stay there.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: The results are beginning to come in Virginia, where all 140 legislative seats are on the ballot tonight. So, let's go to Fredericksburg, Virginia, where Fox's senior national correspondent, Rich Edson, is standing by. Rich, where do things stand now?\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Good evening, Laura. Well, you know, the returns are just coming in here. Polls have been closed about 45 minutes or so, the beginning of the hours when that all happened. You've got the supporters who are now coming in. The candidates are coming into the watch party. This is Virginia District, Senate District 27, Tara Durant, the Republican, Joel Griffin, the Democrat here.\par \par \uc2 This is something also, in an area and a district that Governor Glenn Youngkin visited earlier today. He was here yesterday, part of six separate stops, according to his team, across the Commonwealth today only, alone, over the last several weeks. He's visited this area a number of times.\par \par \uc2 Some of those close districts that Republicans and Democrats have identified that are really going to be the difference in this race here. Republicans are trying to hold on to the House. They're trying to flip the State Senate. That would give them unified control of the state government here. And it's a way that really the governor has been invested in, as have national Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Governor Youngkin, according to his team is going to be watching these returns down in Richmond, about an hour or so, south of here, probably not going to hear from until we've really got a sense of where this thing is going, and whether Republicans have been successful.\par \par \uc2 Told us, in fact, in this district about five miles from here earlier today, that he's feeling pretty hopeful, but it's going to be tight, he says. It's probably going to come into a few hundred votes for each race, that he's expecting this question really to be an issue that's going to go down to the wire here as Republicans look to get that clean sweep, look to take control of the House and Senate and push their agenda through. Back to you, Laura.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: All right, Rich, thank you. And now it's time for WTF, What the Failla. Joining me now is Jimmy Failla, host of Fox Across America. All right, Jimmy, it's been too long. So, first of all, I missed you. So, thank you for being here tonight.\par \par \uc2 The pro-Palestine protesters at colleges across the country. You've seen, they've basically been just repeating the \uc2 Hamas\uc2  talking points, and it's gotten so absurd that even an Israeli TV show is mocking them in a little skit, watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: I wish I could just be there with you.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: You can. You can come to Gaza anytime and we will throw you from the roof, you homosexual dirt.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Do you hear? Roman, throw me a rooftop party.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: They are so welcoming and inclusive.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: So, Shukran. And you are also very welcome to come here to America.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: We will come. First, we finish with Israel, and America is next.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Jimmy, it does points out the hypocrisy of the left -- or sorry, the ignorance of the left.\par \par \uc2 JIMMY FAILLA, FOX ACROSS AMERICA HOST: Yes. Well, that's why some people on the left got mad at this sketch, because it's true, OK? Starting a group called Queers for Palestine is like starting a group called Wives for O.J. OK, not exactly the best record of handling your people. But this is what they don't get. That's why I like the sketch so much.\par \par \uc2 All of these protesters in America, a good majority of them are idiots. Yes, we have some raging anti-Semites, but a lot of these dopey guys showing up to these protests, Laura, think Gaza Strip is a gentleman's club. They have no idea what they're doing. So, good on the sketch group.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Don't you also sense that there are a lot of these pajama boy types, Jimmy, who are --\par \par \uc2 FAILLA: Oh, yeah.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: -- or just desperate for friends.\par \par \uc2 FAILLA: That's what it is.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Right. They show up with their, you know, it's like their costumes. They go to wardrobe and get their costumes, and then they make signs that, and we have to show you, they're so stupid, they don't know how to spell the word Israel in one of this. Is-real --\par \par \uc2 FAILLA: No.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: -- Israel.\par \par \uc2 FAILLA: Well, listen, OK, we know this woman truly supports \uc2 Hamas\uc2  because she's clearly not allowed in a library. This is a dumb person. At least she's committed to Sharia law. This is a woman who's dumb. But that's the point of this. I've never been prouder, Laura. I always make fun of myself for going to community college, but I've never been prouder to not gotten into Harvard than I am right now.\par \par \uc2 Like, I used to joke, like, oh, I majored in intro to Xbox. But the joke ain't on me, girlfriend. I'm not $800,000 in debt stumping for a terror group right now.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Oh, my God. It's just pathetic. All right, Jimmy, moving on to your favorite state of California, the Washington Free Beacon is reporting that the state has spent more than $4 million on taxpayer money on surgical sex changes and cosmetic gender-affirming enhancements for 157 inmates, including four on death row since 2017.\par \par \uc2 Now, some of the enhancements include laser hair removal, Jimmy, and facial feminization.\par \par \uc2 FAILLA: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Take it away.\par \par \uc2 FAILLA: I love this. So, the taxpayers are funding some weirdo death row inmate to get rid of his junk. What is this called? The Shawshank reduction? This is the most bizarre time there's ever been. Like, yo, when you send a death row inmate to the electric chair, it's not to get a perm.\par \par \uc2 But should we be surprised? No, because this is California, where they won't let you have a plastic straw. But if a female inmate wants to attach a straw to her body, we've got to foot the bill. Way to go, Newsome.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yeah, it gives new meaning to the words supermax. We were sent -- that's the enhancement, supermax. All right, Jimmy, finally this week, and the happy couple, Harry and Meghan. Some people asking questions about the relationship took a break from their environmental activism to fly on a G4 owned by an oil heir to a Katy Perry concert. It's a Gulf Stream, reportedly emits an estimated two tons of CO2 an hour, nearly a quarter of the 7.4 tons of the average Brit produces every year.\par \par \uc2 Jimmy, there's no -- there's absolutely no double standard or hypocrisy.\par \par \uc2 FAILLA: Yeah, they shouldn't have gone to Resorts World to see Katy Perry. They should have went to Circus-Circus because they're both clowns, OK. This is beyond hypocrisy, Laura, because it's a 40-minute trip.\par \par \uc2 So, it's not like -- you know how many flights there are from L.A. to Vegas? I've taken most of them. Look at me. I have a gambling problem. But the point is, they're not just being hypocritical, they're using a private jet like they're running errands in a Hyundai Sonata. Like --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Wait a second.\par \par \uc2 FAILLA: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Jimmy, you're so unfair because they're actually -- they live with the simple people, you know, the regular people in Montecito, in Santa Barbara. They do not live in L.A. They don't want to drive all that way. They got to fly all that way.\par \par \uc2 FAILLA: And they -- you're the best. They just wanted privacy in the front row of a Katy Perry concert. We've all been there.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Jimmy, you got 10 seconds, but I'm going to see you next week at the Patriot Awards in Nashville. I can't wait. All right, Jesse, next.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020231108ejb700001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Family Members Of Hostages Taken By \uc2 Hamas\uc2  Speaks On Capitol Hill; \uc2 Hamas\uc2  And Israel Trade Fire As \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  Vows Victory; \uc2 Rashida Tlaib\uc2  Faces Censure Resolutions; Special Counsel David Weiss Testifies In Private Before House Panel On The Hunter Biden Probe; Youngkin Highlights Top Issues For Virginia Voters; DeSantis Hopes To Build On Key Iowa Endorsement; \uc2 Israel Defense Forces\uc2  Expand Control Of Northern Gaza; Rep. \uc2 Mike Gallagher\uc2  (R-WI) And Rep. Ritchie Torres (D-NY) Interviewed On House Of Representatives Censured Representative \uc2 Rashida Tlaib\uc2  And TikTok's Influence On Young Americans Opinions Of Israel-\uc2 Hamas\uc2  War; Parts of Manifesto Of Shooter Who Killed Three Children And Three Adults At Nashville Christian School Leaked; Media Coverage Of Israel-\uc2 Hamas\uc2  War Examined; Questions Raised Over Possible Israeli Occupation Of Gaza After Destruction of \uc2 Hamas\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, David Spunt, Jacqui Heinrich, Mike Tobin, Chad  Pergram, Rich Edson, Bryan Llenas, Nate Foy, Howard Kurtz, Juan Williams,  Josh Kraushaar, Mollie Hemingway \par \uc2 7,851 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 November 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 HAROLD FORD JR\uc2 ., FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Never played baseball. Decides to play. Wins the Pitch Hit and Run tournament in Tampa and goes into regionals. And then he won the national.\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Wow, 12?\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL LEGAL ANALYST: Wow.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: And the kid never really done it before. 12 years old. Congrats to the kid.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Bravo.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: You go, boy.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: I'm looking at that birth certificate, I don't know, he doesn't look 12 to me.\par \par \uc2 That's it for us. Have a great night everybody.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: All right, 12 years old. That's good. I can applaud that.\par \par \uc2 All right. Thank you, Jesse. See you later.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Good evening. Welcome to Washington, I'm Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, it is election day in more than 30 states across the country today. A handful of those races are being closely watched tonight. Possible implications down the road as polls close. Polls close in an hour in Virginia and Kentucky. We'll have Fox team coverage on all of that a bit later.\par \par \uc2 But first, one month after the \uc2 Hamas\uc2  attack on his nation, Israel's prime minister is ruling out any ceasefire or pause in the fighting without the release of all hostages. President Biden asked \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  to consider what are being called tactical pauses in the fighting, perhaps a three-day stop to get humanitarian aid into Gaza and try to get hostages out.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, we're also hearing from some relatives of American hostages still believed to be held by \uc2 Hamas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And there is more controversy about where President Biden stands regarding all of the dissent in this country for Israel's actions.\par \par \uc2 White House correspondent Jacqui Heinrich has details tonight live from the North Lawn. Good evening, Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret. Today officials would not speak to reports that President Biden and top aides have discussed the likelihood that Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2 's political days are numbered and the U.S. they say is unequivocally and unwaveringly still behind Israel.\par \par \uc2 But we're seeing more distance between the U.S. and its ally than ever before on a few key issues.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): President Biden speaking to reports that he pushed Israeli Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  to embrace a three day pause in fighting in exchange for the release of 10 to 15 hostages.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Did you ask the Prime Minister of Israel to do a pause?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I didn't get a chance to talk to him today. I did ask him for a pause in the past -- yesterday. Still waiting to hear.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): Netanyahu today evidently rejecting Biden's ask, saying there'll be no ceasefire or even fuel to Gaza until the hostages are released.\par \par \uc2 The prime minister reportedly told Biden yesterday he doesn't trust \uc2 Hamas\uc2 's intentions or believe in negotiating in good faith. And there are more apparent disagreements over Israel's plans to keep forces in Gaza.\par \par \uc2 VEDANT PATEL, STATE DEPARTMENT DEPUTY SPOKESMAN: We do not support reoccupation of Gaza and neither does Israel.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): That's not what Netanyahu said.\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2 , ISRAELI PRIME MINISTER: I think Israel will for an indefinite period will have the overall security responsibility because we've seen what happens when we don't have it.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): The administration insists there's no daylight between the U.S. and its allies.\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, PRESS SECRETARY, PENTAGON: Friends don't have to agree on every single nuance and every single word.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): But officials are under pressure to deliver clear messaging.\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE WALTZ (R-FL): I think the entire world is getting mixed messages. \uc2 Anthony Blinken\uc2  standing next to foreign ministers around the region talking about a ceasefire, calling it a pause, as though we don't know the difference.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): After families of hostages pleaded for action on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: This is a wake-up call for all of you, all of you here, all of America, all of Europe. You are next.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): The press secretary initially refused to say whether it's wrong that pro-Palestinian protesters are tearing down hostage posters across the country.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: I'm just not going to go into specifics on that particular thing.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): Prompting this correction on X, "Tearing down pictures of their loved ones who are being held hostage by \uc2 Hamas\uc2  is wrong and hurtful".\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (on camera): And pro-Palestinian protesters are targeting major military and government contractors tomorrow in a national day of action according to a threat advisory that Fox News obtained.\par \par \uc2 Defense and national security personnel are being told to stay cognizant of their surroundings and avoid demonstrations but the list of places being targeted includes \uc2 Lockheed Martin\uc2 , \uc2 Raytheon\uc2 , \uc2 Boeing\uc2 , \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 , \uc2 Amazon\uc2  and a few others, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jacqui Heinrich live in the North Lawn. Jacqui, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Israel's Prime Minister says his country will take overall security responsibility for Gaza indefinitely after the war ends, raising question marks about what that looks like.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Israeli troops battle \uc2 Hamas\uc2  fighters deep inside Gaza's largest city. Senior Correspondent \uc2 Mike Tobin\uc2  is live again in southern Israel tonight. Good evening, Mike.\par \par \uc2 MIKE TOBIN\uc2 , FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret. A month since the massacre, Israel's Prime Minister promises to stay on the offensive until Gaza can no longer be a threat to Israel. \uc2 Hamas\uc2  defiantly fires rockets and Palestinian civilians pay the price.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOBIN (voice over): Waving the white flag, hundreds of Palestinian civilians emerge from their homes in the northern half of the Gaza Strip on foot for a march south away from Israeli forces.\par \par \uc2 In a wasteland of scorched earth and rubble, the \uc2 IDF\uc2  takes out buildings they call command and control structures. Some with heavy equipment.\par \par \uc2 Hamas\uc2  video shows fighters in civilian clothes peppering Israeli forces with mortars. So, the process for the invading force is slow as they locate tunnels used by \uc2 Hamas\uc2  one was found in a children's amusement park, then destroy the entrance.\par \par \uc2 MAJ. GEN. YARON FINKELMAN, \uc2 ISRAEL DEFENSE FORCES\uc2  (through translator): For the first time in decades, the \uc2 IDF\uc2  is fighting in the very heart of Gaza, in the very heart of terror. This is a complex and difficult war and unfortunately it comes at a price.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN (voice over): One month into the war, Israel's Prime Minister vows to keep the pressure on.\par \par \uc2 NETANYAHU (through translator): This enemy will be defeated, Gaza will not be a threat to Israel again. Together we will win.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN (voice over): The aggressive combat terrifies loved ones of the hostages held in Gaza. They gather at the Prime Minister's residence and demand their return.\par \par \uc2 ASHER FRIED, FRIEND: We must stop all cease-fire and the first thing we must do to bring them back.\par \par \uc2 NIVA WENKERT, MOTHER: To bring back to his family, to his life. To say give all the children and the old people again to be freedom.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN (voice over): \uc2 U.N\uc2 . agencies providing temporary shelter for Palestinians are overrun with more than a half million refugees. 2000 evacuated Sunday, another 5000 On Monday, many are left to sleep on the ground.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE (through translator): Look at our situation. Is this a life that we are living? We have no food, electricity or water. We sleep in the corridors without any blankets. It is very cold. This is not a life.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOBIN (on camera): The \uc2 IDF\uc2  says the weapons seized from the Gaza Strip include 427 explosive belts and mechanisms, reminiscent of the second Palestinian Intifada and the suicide bombers Israel hasn't been hit by a suicide bomb for seven years, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: \uc2 Mike Tobin\uc2  live in southern Israel. Mike, thank you.\par \par \uc2 One of the lawmakers who has been most critical of Israel and most supportive of the Palestinians is now facing two separate censure resolutions. Michigan Congresswoman \uc2 Rashida Tlaib\uc2  posted a message that is causing considerable tension. This is just part of the ongoing fissure in the Democratic Party over Israel and anti-Semitism.\par \par \uc2 Senior Congressional Correspondent Chad Pergram is on Capitol Hill tonight. Good evening, Chad. Why is the House seeking to punish to Tlaib?\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Well, \uc2 Rashida Tlaib\uc2  is on a collision course with censure. The House plans to punish to Tlaib, the daughter of Palestinian immigrants for anti-Semitic language she expressed support for the phrase from the river to the sea. Jews say that calls for destroying Israel.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 REP. RICH MCCORMICK (R-GA): This type of anti-Semitic hate is being promoted by a small group of members in this body, chiefly Representative Tlaib. We must hold her accountable.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): Tlaib defended herself on the House floor, she became emotional and was consoled by fellow members of the Squad.\par \par \uc2 REP. RASHIDA TLAIB (D-MI): Palestinian people are not disposable. We are human beings. Just like anyone else.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (on camera): Tlaib says no matter what happens to her, the House can't silence Palestinian voices. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And Chad, what are Democrats saying about Congresswoman Tlaib?\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Well, Democrats hope to kill the effort to rebuke Tlaib, they oppose her remarks but defended to Tlaib's right to say offensive things. Especially when she parrots \uc2 Hamas\uc2  talking points.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. TED LIEU (D-CA): They also call for the killing of all Jews. So, if someone repeats that phrase in America, it is highly inflammatory.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Why not have the Democratic caucus then take some action against it?\par \par \uc2 LIEU: As members of Congress, we have outsize platforms so we can individually criticize, command, say whatever we want about any issue we want to.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: There's another censure on the floor tonight for Tlaib. The House votes tomorrow to sanction Tlaib, she becomes the 26th member to receive this form of punishment, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Chad Pergram live on the Hill. Chad, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Police say an elderly Jewish man died from injuries after he was attacked at a pro-Palestinian rally Sunday in California. Paul Kessler was 69 years old. Authorities say it is being treated as a homicide and they add the alleged perpetrator has been cooperating.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JAMES FRYHOFF, VENTURA COUNTY, CALIFORNIA SHERIFF: The suspect was identified, willingly remained at the scene and was interviewed by deputies. The suspect was cooperative and indicated he was involved in an altercation with Mr. Kessler. Investigators have not ruled out the possibility of a hate crime. And this is being investigated as a homicide.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Many witnesses say the assailant crossed the street, hit Kessler over the head with a megaphone, knocking him to the ground. He died Monday of a skull fracture.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, the prosecutor who says he is in charge of the Hunter Biden investigation was on the hot seat himself today on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent David Spunt has that story. Good evening, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Bret, good evening. Special Counsel David Weiss spent about six hours behind closed doors. He made history today as the first special counsel in the middle of an active investigation to answer questions before Congress, not to mention an investigation involving the sitting president's son.\par \par \uc2 House Republicans say in the room that Weiss refused to answer many questions about the investigation. But the purpose of the interview today Bret was to discuss questions about his authority, not investigation details.\par \par \uc2 On this broadcast back in June, \uc2 IRS\uc2  whistleblower Gary Shapley testified and told you that Weiss complained he was denied Special Counsel status and could not charge the president's son in Washington D.C. or California because of politics. Weiss refuted that today. He said, "It is not what I said. Nor is it what I believed".\par \par \uc2 He also said in his opening statement today, "At no time was I blocked or otherwise prevented from pursuing charges or taking steps necessary in the investigation by other United States Attorneys, the Tax Division or anyone else at the Department of Justice". Republicans insist he was denied Special Counsel status in 2022.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 REP. JIM JORDAN (R-OH): He requested it, was not given that request and never had that authority throughout the time and yet, he pretends that somehow he did have that.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (voice over): Three sources tell Fox News House Republicans chose to interview Weiss behind closed doors, though DOJ officials and some Democrats wanted this to be a public hearing today.\par \par \uc2 REP. DAN GOLDMAN (D-NY): The reason the Department of Justice would agree and want this to the public is because they are well aware that there was no interference.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (on camera): In addition to the federal gun charges against \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 , Special Counsel Weiss indicated in the summer filing, he plans to charge the first son with tax crimes in either California or Washington D.C., Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: David, thank you. Stocks climbed on optimism over interest rates. The Dow gained 57, the S&P 500 finished ahead 12, the NASDAQ rose 121 today.\par \par \uc2 Up next, as we said at the beginning of the show, it is Election Day in 31 states today, a handful of them will be closely watched tonight as the polls close about 45 minutes in a couple of states. We'll look at some of them.\par \par \uc2 First, here's what some of our Fox affiliates around the country are covering tonight. Fox 11 in Los Angeles, as a fire rips through a hangar at the former Marine Corps Air Station, Tustin. A hangar once housed blimps used in World War II and has been featured in films and T.V. shows including "JAG", "The X Files" and "Pearl Harbor".\par \par \uc2 Fox 5 in Las Vegas where a Friday morning strike deadline has been set by the largest culinary union in the city. Negotiations are scheduled for this week between the three major resort companies and the union representing 35,000 hospitality workers on the strip.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Miami, one of the big stories there tonight from WSVN, our affiliate down there. Online sports betting has returned to Florida. The Seminole Tribe is offering limited access to existing Florida customers to test it's hard rock bet platform. That system relaunched the day after two years.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look outside the Beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: This is voting day in 31 states. Legislative elections underway for both Houses of the state legislature in Louisiana, Mississippi, New Jersey, as well as the Commonwealth of Virginia. We'll bring you there in a second.\par \par \uc2 There are governor's races in Kentucky and Mississippi. Six states are holding a statewide referendum: Oklahoma, Wisconsin, Texas, Maine, New York, and Ohio, a number of different issues there.\par \par \uc2 Voters, as we mentioned in Virginia are deciding whether to give Republicans full control of the state government for the first time in a decade. Part of the GOP agenda is the governor's 15-week abortion ban.\par \par \uc2 We have "FOX TEAM COVERAGE" tonight. Bryan Llenas in Miami, where Republicans are gathering ahead of tomorrow night's presidential debate.\par \par \uc2 We start though with senior national correspondent Rich Edson, reporting tonight from Stafford, Virginia. Rich.\par \par \uc2 RICH EDSON, FOX NEWS CHANNEL WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT: All right. Good evening, Bret.\par \par \uc2 Governor Glenn Youngkin is campaigning right up into well polls close, about 40 minutes from now.\par \par \uc2 The governor told us at a nearby polling location that he feels pretty good about a Republican sweep of the legislature. Though he acknowledges that these races are tight, that turnout is large, and it's going to probably come down to just a few 100 votes.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GOV. GLENN YOUNGKIN (R-VA): Did you voted yet?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 EDSON (voice over): It's just about a year until the presidential election, and voters across the country are heading to the polls to decide legislative elections and ballot initiatives.\par \par \uc2 In Virginia, a test for Governor Glenn Younkin and whether he can deliver his party full control of the state legislature. He is highlighting inflation, public safety, and education.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Do you see this and these issues setting up a national conversation ahead of the presidential election next year?\par \par \uc2 YOUNGKIN: Well, I expect it that it does. Going into next year, I expect exact same issues will, of course, be so important to Virginians and Americans, because this is what impacts everybody's daily life.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Also, part of this campaign, abortion, and whether voters here will support Republican plans to pass limits after 15 weeks.\par \par \uc2 YOUNGKIN: We will make sure that that people understand, that's when a baby feels pain.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 YOUNGKIN: And that's where we can come together as Virginians.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Democrats have made gun control, education, and also abortion a centerpiece of this campaign.\par \par \uc2 HEATHER WILLIAMS, EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR, DEMOCRATIC LEGISLATIVE CAMPAIGN COMMITTEE: At stake is whether or not Virginians on Wednesday expect an abortion ban in the state. To have their vote counted to make sure that they're safe in schools and don't have to fear being shot while they are there.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Abortion is on the ballot directly in Ohio, and in a referendum to add a constitutional amendment guaranteeing the right to abortion.\par \par \uc2 GOV. MIKE DEWINE (R-OH): It just goes much, much too far.\par \par \uc2 It would enshrine in our Constitution. The right to have an abortion up until birth.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Proponents point out the amendment allows lawmakers to restrict abortion until the fetus can survive outside the womb. A determination a treating physician would make.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 EDSON (on camera): A simple majority decides that issue. This summer, Ohio voters rejected a measure that would have raised the threshold to change the state constitution from 50 percent to 60. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Rich, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Florida Governor, Republican presidential candidate \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is in Iowa today, trying to capitalize on that endorsement from the state's popular governor, a move that has been criticized by former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And one of the other candidates, \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2 . is in South Florida hosting a rally tonight that he says is aimed at preventing World War III.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Bryan Llenas is in Miami tonight, site of the next presidential debate tomorrow night. Good evening, Bryan.\par \par \uc2 BRYAN LLENAS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Bret, good evening.\par \par \uc2 It's not just Republican presidential candidates that are here in Miami, so too, is the Biden-Harris campaign.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, they are using a familiar playbook. The abortion issue to attack Republicans after new polls show former President Trump handedly defeating Biden in a rematch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GOV. JAY ROBERT PRITZKER (D-IL): Every Republican running wants to ban abortion and forced victims of rape or incest to show proof to be able to get access to health care.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS (voice over): Trump remains the GOP front runner in the Iowa caucus with a 27-point lead according to the latest Des Moines register poll. Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is stagnant at second place. But the DeSantis campaign says Iowa Governor \uc2 Kim Reynolds\uc2  endorsement last night is a game changer.\par \par \uc2 Today, they campaign together in Davenport, Iowa. DeSantis is betting her popularity and vast statewide political operation will lead to a big win.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 KIM REYNOLDS\uc2  (R-IA): And I am ready to go to the math and do everything we can to get him elected the next president of the United States.\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL): Clearly, having the governor helping us out is a big boost. And we're just going to continue to ride that all the way to the January caucuses.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: In a new memo, the DeSantis campaign argues he's the only viable threat to Trump, urging all the candidates specifically former Ambassador Nikki Haley, to drop out of the race.\par \par \uc2 Haley's campaign responded with a new ad, attacking DeSantis's energy policy.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY (R), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is against fracking.\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: No, it's not true.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Do you also support a ban on fracking?\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: Yes, yes, yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: Now, DeSantis says he supports fracking outside of Florida. \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2  is holding a rally here in Miami tonight to stop World War III. Ramaswamy and DeSantis have been gone after Haley, expect that same exact kind of attack here at the -- tomorrow night's debate in Miami.\par \par \uc2 It's the third debate in which Trump will not be featured. He will instead be hosting a rally across town. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right. Bryan Llenas in Miami. Bryan, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, a look at the ground war between Israel and \uc2 Hamas\uc2  and the latest on the ground. Plus, the effect of social media on our perceptions of that war. How that's been talked about on Capitol Hill?\par \par \uc2 One Democrat one Republican find the "COMMON GROUND" next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Welcome back to SPECIAL REPORT. Let's take a look at what's happening in Israel in the last 24 hours. Israeli troops have targeted \uc2 Hamas\uc2  terrorists on the ground and in the air here in Gaza. And they responded to rocket fire from Lebanon, right up here in the northern section.\par \par \uc2 Now, in the West Bank, Israeli troops exchanged gunfire with terrorists in two different locations. They were searching for weapons in those places. There has been increased violence inside the West Bank.\par \par \uc2 As we move over to Gaza, take a closer look. We can tell you that \uc2 Israeli Defense Forces\uc2  expanded their control in the north. We have been talking about this effort. They are now inside Gaza City and fighting there. They took control of a \uc2 Hamas\uc2  military stronghold where they found some weapons and intelligence materials in northern Gaza.\par \par \uc2 Now, in the central region here, you had some Israeli jets strike a building near a hospital. That strike caused a secondary explosion from a \uc2 Hamas\uc2  weapons warehouse nearby. Troops also located a series of tunnels in northern Gaza in this area right here, and they are saying that these tunnels are very extensive in northern Gaza. We have talked about how it has more than 300 miles of tunnels. And remember, this entire Gaza Strip is only 25 miles long. But the tunnels extend underneath. These were found in a residential area. Also, paratroopers located tunnels near an amusement park. As you see these pictures, the discovery was part of a targeted operation north of the Gaza Strip.\par \par \uc2 Now, earlier today, Israeli forces reopened an evacuation route. Let's try to get out of people out of Gaza whose still in Gaza trying to get out of the way. It extends all the way down here, all the way through the Wadi Gaza which is an area south of that, a nature preserve just north of a refugee camp. They are trying to get people down here into southern Gaza and eventually across the way to Egypt if they can get through that pass in Rafah.\par \par \uc2 In the north, they have been -- there have been about 20 different launches into Israeli territory from Lebanon, and a couple of known attacks in these two areas right here along the border where the gunfire has been exchanged as the threat from Iran-backed groups really grows throughout the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 As far as attacks on U.S. forces, there have now been 40 attacks on U.S. bases in the Middle East since October 17th. The Pentagon confirming that just a couple of hours ago. A rocket attack on mission support site Euphrates in Syria just in the past hours, and there were two attempted attacks at the Erbil Air Base in northern Iraq overnight. The Iran backed Islamic Resistance in Iraq is claiming responsibility for that, for almost all of these attacks. And it comes as Iran's president plans to attend a summit alongside other Arab leaders in Saudi Arabia. Biden officials are warning, looking at this map now, Iran's attendance there is concerning.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, NATIONAL SECURITY COUNCIL: Our perspective is Iran is a destabilizing actor in the region. They support groups like \uc2 Hamas\uc2  and \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 . They are supporting these militia groups that continue to fire rockets at our bases in Iraq and Syria. And they are also supporting Putin's war in Ukraine. So it's difficult for us to see where Iran would have some sort of meaningful, constructive, productive discussion about conflict resolution at this point given that they are the arsonists here.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It is time for our "Common Ground" segment. Tonight, we talk about social media, its influence on public opinion about the Israel-\uc2 Hamas\uc2  war. Joining us are Wisconsin Republican Congressman \uc2 Mike Gallagher\uc2  and New York Democratic Congressman Ritchie Torres. Thank you both for being here. First of all, 30,000 feet. We have been watching what is happening in Israel. And there is this kind of back and forth on Capitol Hill about some members, how they feel, what they are saying.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. RASHIDA TLAIB, (D-MI): Mr. President, the American people are not with you on this one.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Where do you all stand on whether she should be censured or not?\par \par \uc2 REP. RITCHIE TORRES, (D-NY): I intend to vote for the censure. When you utter the phrase "from the river to the sea," you are not simply calling for the creation of a Palestinian state, which is fair game, but you are calling for the destruction of Israel as a Jewish state, which is a line that no member should ever cross. And so it's the right of Congress as an institution to vote for a censure that condemns hate speech, because it's incompatible with the values of Congress as an institution. And the U.S.- Israel relationship is one of our central values.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Folks who not for this say it's about the First Amendment, she can say whatever she wants to say.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TLAIB: My criticism has always been of the Israeli government and Netanyahu's actions.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MIKE GALLAGHER\uc2 , (R-WI): She can certainly say whatever she wants to say, and we can choose whether or not to censure that and stand up for what I hope is the bipartisan sentiment of the institution which is to support Israel, to push back on forms of antisemitism which we are seeing abhorrent displays of antisemitism here domestically, and particularly as Israel continues in this very complex task of destroying \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , completely eliminating its ability to threaten Israel militarily, and hopefully in the process restoring some deterrence in the north with respect to \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 . It's going to require the American president and the \uc2 American Congress\uc2  sticking steadfastly by our closest ally in the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Today we heard from some family members of some of the Americans being held hostage.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DORIS LIBER, MOTHER OF HOSTAGE GUY ILUZ: It's been 30 days. Evidence day is like eternity to me. And I can't wait any longer because I know that he was shot.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Ohad had a birthday two weeks ago on October 23rd. He was very lovable, and he shouldn't have spent his ninth birthday in captivity.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Congressman Torres, it's tough to listen to that. We don't really know exactly how many Americans are being held or how many Americans want to get out. And that's a real question mark.\par \par \uc2 TORRES: Look, for me, there should be a diplomatic operation to secure the release of the hostages. There should be humanitarian operation to deliver aid to Palestinians in the Gaza Strip. And there should be a military operation to bring \uc2 Hamas\uc2  to justice so that there is no repeat of October 7th. And none of those objectives are opposing. We should be able to achieve all of them. But the highest priority should be to secure the release of those hostages. And I find it troubling that more energy has been spent calling on Israel to enter into a ceasefire rather than calling on \uc2 Hamas\uc2  to surrender and release the hostages.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That is something, that the focus is not on releasing the hostages. It's on stop the attack on Palestinians is the phrasing.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes. And I think Ritchie brought up the point about ceasefire even as I think even \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  has recognized, as former Secretary Clinton has recognized, a ceasefire at this point would only stand to benefit \uc2 Hamas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Going forward, I also think we need to find ways to put pressure on \uc2 Hamas\uc2 's primary sponsor, Iran, without which \uc2 Hamas\uc2  would not have weapons, it would not have finances. And if we don't get that, which is our foremost threat in the region, if we don't get our approach to Iran right, then we're going to have a trouble making sense of other parts of the regions as well as building upon any tactical or operational success Israel has against \uc2 Hamas\uc2  in Gaza.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You wrote in "\uc2 Free Press\uc2 ," "Why do young people support \uc2 Hamas\uc2 ? Look at TikTok." You believe that TikTok is forming some opinion here?\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: There is the overall problem with social media, which I think political scientists like Jonathan Haidt have convincing argued is correlated with rising rates of mental health, anxiety, depression, and suicide. Then there's the specific problem with TikTok, which is that it is controlled by ByteDance, a Chinese company that is beholden to the Chinese Communist Party. So we have to ask ourselves whether we want the dominant media platform in America, because a lot of young kids in America get their news from TikTok. It's not just about dance videos.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It's not just some kids. It's a ton of kids, Congressman.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: A massive amount of kids get their news from TikTok. Whether we want that company to be a company to be controlled potentially by the Chinese Communist Party.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And where are you on that, Congressman?\par \par \uc2 TORRES: For me it's a clear national security risk. The fact that we have put the leading news source for the next generation of Americans, for the most impressionable minds of our society in the hands of our leading foreign adversary, historians will remember that catastrophic miscalculation. It's national self-sabotage.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And there is disinformation. A.P. has misinformation about Israel- \uc2 Hamas\uc2  war flooding social media. Among the fabrications users have shared false claims that a top Israeli commander had been kidnapped, a false video imitating a \uc2 BBC News\uc2  report and pushed old and unrelated clips of Russian \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  with inaccurate English captions. We're going to see more and more of this.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: I think we have to assume that we will. Given everything we know about the regime in Beijing, the Chinese Communist Party, no one actually on our committee has spoken more eloquently about the nature of the regime than, I would argue, Ritchie Torres has. You just have to assume that they would use this tool as a weapon of information warfare. And, therefore, we need to take steps in the form of either a ban or a forced sale to guard against that worst case scenario. And control of the algorithm is really the core of the issue. If we don't have an American company with control of the algorithm, then the only other option is a ban.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We often focus on the rifts on Capitol Hill. Congressman, there are things you all are working together on. China is one of them. But there is some common ground on Israel, \uc2 Hamas\uc2  up there as well, too.\par \par \uc2 TORRES: And not only Israel, but there are issues like artificial intelligence, the strategic challenge of China, that are so consequential that it transcends partisanship. And I truly believe the majority of Democrats and Republicans want to do right by their districts, want to do right by their country. It's a small subset who poison the well.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: There you go. Thank you both for being here.\par \par \uc2 TORRES: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You can see all of the "Common Ground" segments on my podcast "Common Ground." Find that and the "All-Star Panel" podcast under the Bret Baier podcasts at FOXNewspodcast.com, \uc2 Spotify\uc2 , or wherever you download podcasts. You can also see the extended discussion on the FOX News \uc2 YouTube\uc2  page.\par \par \uc2 Up next, answers about what the Nashville school shooter was thinking before the deadly attack. Plus, how the media are covering the Israel-\uc2 Hamas\uc2  war, analysis from Howie Kurtz.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Live look at Nashville there. We're getting details tonight on that breaking story we brought