eal until a decision is made in the courts. A group of Covenant School parents are working to prevent those writings from being made public. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Nate, thank you.\par \par \uc2 One month into the Israel-\uc2 Hamas\uc2  war seems like a good time to try to break down some of the media coverage of this story. Here is FOX News media analyst, host of FOX's "Media Buzz" Howard Kurtz. Good evening, Howie.\par \par \uc2 HOWARD KURTZ, FOX NEWS HOST: Bret, good evening. As the media's focus in the Israel-\uc2 Hamas\uc2  war increasingly turns to the rising death toll in Gaza, some journalists are showing signs of tilting toward the terrorist group. Consider this NBC News headline, "Man dies after hitting head during Israel and Palestinian rallies, officials say." Hitting head, as you reported earlier, Paul Kessler, who is Jewish, got into a physical battle with a pro-Palestinian protester in California, and the local medical examiner ruled it a homicide caused by a blunt force head injury. The headline has since been changed online. ABC chief White House correspondent Mary Bruce tweeted, "The White House gates this morning after passionate protest over the weekend." "Passionate" is an interesting word with the pro-Palestinian demonstrations since she included a photo of red painted graffiti on the gates. Some activists accusing President Biden of supporting genocide.\par \par \uc2 Then there's a question of credibility. A "\uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " headline yesterday said "Gaza Health Ministry, death toll in Gaza surpasses 10,000 after four weeks of war." The entire government is run by \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , which has a record of inflating casualty figures.\par \par \uc2 And Pulitzer winning journalist Glenn Greenwald reposted a quote of a senior U.S. official in an NBC Story, "If this goes really bad, we want to be able to point to our past statements." The user's comment, "U.S. officials are posting meaningless statements of concern now so they can point to them later to downplay their complicity."\par \par \uc2 There is a pattern of softening language when it comes to antisemitic violence when a bloodthirsty Russian mob was hunting down Jews who had landed from Tel Aviv. The A.P. headline read, "Crowd storms Russian airport to protest flight from Israel." Protest. That was changed after an uproar.\par \par \uc2 People who support the Palestinians or even the \uc2 Hamas\uc2  terrorists have every right to peaceful protest. But journalists have a responsibility to explicitly describe their action. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Howie, it's really great, and it's good to focus on that, and the way you look through that prism really matters what words are used.\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: Words are important, and their meaning carries a great deal of impact.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. Howie, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel with the latest on the war in Israel and what's at stake on this Election Day. Later, we will look at Election Day and what we have coming up tonight.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RITA LIFSHITZ, FAMILY MEMBERS HELD HOSTAGE: I have 76 family members that \uc2 Hamas\uc2  took to Gaza. Every day I feel their hearts. We need to bring them back home now.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: If the warzone is becoming a graveyard for children, can you still say that Israel is following the laws of war?\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, NATIONAL SECURITY COUNCIL COORDINATOR FOR STRATEGIC COMMUNICATIONS: \uc2 Hamas\uc2  is putting those children and their families in greater danger. We know that there's a portion of the 240-plus that they are holding as hostages are kids. They are kids.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: President Biden calling the Israeli prime minister, saying maybe there should be this humanitarian pause, possibly three days. The Israeli prime minister saying no go as of now. "The Wall Street Journal" writes "Netanyahu says Israel will control Gaza's security indefinitely," saying "Israel would seek to assume overall responsibility for security in Gaza in an early indication that Israel plans to occupy the strip after the war, raising more questions about its exit strategy."\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in our panel, FOX News senior political analyst Juan Williams, \uc2 Josh Kraushaar\uc2 , FOX News Radio political analyst, and Mollie Hemingway, editor in chief of "The Federalist." That comment about what post going after \uc2 Hamas\uc2  looks like has just raised a lot of eyebrows, caused a lot of concern, Mollie, and obviously has the administration pushing for more humanitarian pauses.\par \par \uc2 MOLLIE HEMINGWAY, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Yes, when you heard Kirby say that they didn't support necessarily Israel occupying Gaza after the conflict is over, first off, I'm not entirely sure the United States is in a position to lecture other people how to handle things after how they handled Afghanistan and winning that war. But there is also this issue that the Biden administration claims that \uc2 Hamas\uc2  is not the true representative of the Palestinian people. And if they believe that, then they certainly have to believe that there has to be a chance here for the Palestinians to get some sense of order absent \uc2 Hamas\uc2  or Islamic Jihad. And that is not going to happen unless you exert some control.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Juan, there is this focus on this pause and the humanitarian aid going in and effort to get hostages out, but not the demand that hostages come out first. I mean, it seems like the rhetoric is a little different.\par \par \uc2 JUAN WILLIAMS, FOX NEWS POLITICAL ANALYST: My attention is not on the rhetoric but on what is actually going on. I don't think there is any doubt the United States is heavily invested in trying to get those hostages released. And I think Secretary of State Blinken is not only invested in negotiations over the hostage release, trying to get Qatar, Jordan to act in such a way as to -- as intermediary with \uc2 Hamas\uc2  to get those hostages out. But I think it also then leads into this other conversation that you were having about what happens once this war stops. They might have disagreement about pause versus ceasefire and all of that, but I don't think anyone doubts that this has got to come to an end. And the question is then what comes next. And I think that what we have seen so far is that there is now an effort to get the \uc2 Palestinian Authority\uc2 , \uc2 Mahmoud Abbas\uc2 , and to get Egypt, Jordan, and others --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Saudi Arabia.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Yes, to support -- exactly -- to support the \uc2 Palestinian Authority\uc2  being a governing structure in Gaza, because you can't expect \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , and I think it's too much to ask of Israel.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Josh, I'm going to turn to politics, because it is Election Day, 31 states today. What do you look for tonight? There is a few states that we're really looking at, the two governor's races, Kentucky and Mississippi, and obviously what is happening in Virginia.\par \par \uc2 JOSH KRAUSHAAR\uc2 , FOX NEWS RADIO POLITICAL ANALYST: One of the big issues is the test over abortion and whether Republicans can overcome some of the challenges they've faced by taking a very staunch pro-life position. And in Virginia, Governor Glenn Youngkin is hoping that Republicans can run on a 15-week abortion ban that's pretty much been the party line in these Virginia state legislative elections. And if Republicans can hold the state house and maybe even win the state Senate, that would be the message. That would be sort of the middle ground that Republicans are looking for on the issue of abortion. Kentucky, the issue of abortion, also, an issue in a state where they have some of the tightest abortion regulations. And the governor running for reelection in that race, \uc2 Andy Beshear\uc2 , has been attacking his Republican opponent for supporting too strict measures even in a very, very conservative state.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, and obviously that had a big effect on the 22 election, that issue alone. Every media organization has their things that they look for. "Politico" said these five things. It's, "Abortion, is it still a big winner for Democrats? Is \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  an electoral drag for Democrats. Will black voters turn out? Are Republicans actually voting early? And are the suburbs still swinging?" Mollie, what are you looking at?\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: It's going to be hard to get good answers to these questions because we don't have that many races happening tonight. But those are what everybody is looking for. One issue on abortion that I think Republicans have to pay attention to is right now Democrats have invested so much in terms of how much they are willing to spend on issue that and how much they are willing to talk about it, and you are still seeing Republicans kind of reluctant to engage.\par \par \uc2 And it might be -- these races might be something of a toss-up tonight. But they should definitely learn the lesson that you have to engage. You have to make the case. It's actually politically popular to have some protections for unborn children. That is a majority position, but they have to say it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, 15 weeks, that's where a lot of European countries are, actually. Juan?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: I think it's no question abortion is the big issue. And it's kind of post the Dobbs decision Democrats have been overperforming at the polls. We saw this in 22. We have seen it in Kansas, Wisconsin, on referendum and State Supreme Court issues.\par \par \uc2 But the question is, does it continue? Because Democrats, as Mollie was saying, have invested heavily in this issue. In Virginia, what you have seen is an effort by Republicans led by the governor to really focus more on education, crime as a counter to the fact that abortion has become a dominant issue in our politics.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, we'll watch it. Panel, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Speaking of watching it, I will be on Jesse's show, 8:00. We will see some early results. Polls close at 7:00 in a couple of these states, and then I will be on later in the night to get more results. Tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT, we will have all of the results from the off-year elections.\par \par \uc2 And remember, if you can't catch us live, set your DVR, 6:00 p.m. in the east, 3:00 p.m. on the west coast.\par \par \uc2 Thank you for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for this SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and still unafraid. It's been a busy week so far. And what is did, Tuesday? OK. THE INGRAHAM ANGLE is coming up now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020231108ejb700001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Congresswoman \uc2 Rashida Tlaib\uc2  Under Fire; Jewish Man Dies At Pro- Palestinian Protest; America Votes; Can Any Republican Beat \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ?; Donor Backlash as Biden Slides in Polls\b0\par\par\uc2 Chad Pergram, Alexis McAdams, Mike Tobin, William La Jeunesse,  Rich Edson, Jacqui Heinrich, Charlie Gasparino, Neil Cavuto \par \uc2 8,751 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 November 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: On top of donors on edge, a president sliding, and now big backers bolting? What our own Charlie Gasparino is hearing.\par \par \uc2 Then, the big election before the election, at issue, whether once purple Virginia goes all red this day, whether a Republican governor can survive the fight of his life in Mississippi this day, whether a Democratic one in Kentucky can get, well, reelected in a usually ruby-red state this day, all this day, when the entire New Jersey state Senate and state assembly are up for grabs, and constitutional abortion measures in Ohio are on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 Big day, big issues, big show, and it all starts now.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everybody. I'm Neil Cavuto, and so much to get to on this "Your World."\par \par \uc2 They say that money talks, but if you are one \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , what if the money walks? Charlie Gasparino on the financial fallout from a polling wipeout in just a moment.\par \par \uc2 First to Jacqui Heinrich at the White House on whether anyone there is freaking out at the moment -- Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, maybe not freaking out. At least they're not letting on if they are.\par \par \uc2 But we do know that the president has apparently seen these polls. We will play that sound for you in just a second, but there was a batch over the weekend that obviously caused some concern within Democratic circles. You had the president losing in hypothetical matchups to former President Trump in five critical swing states. That was a New York Times poll.\par \par \uc2 Then you had an ABC poll showing that three-quarters of Americans think the country is going in the wrong direction. Officials here are not going to weight in on specifics, but they did suggest that the president has had his eyeballs on these. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: So President Biden has seen them?\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: Obviously, we have all here at the White House have seen the polling. I'm just saying that we have to take them -- as a general rule, we should take this polling with a grain of salt.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Some other news out of the briefing, the press secretary, \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2 , was asked about pro-Palestinian protesters tearing down posters of hostages that are being held by \uc2 Hamas\uc2  in Gaza and whether the White House thinks that's appropriate or will condemn it.\par \par \uc2 Her initial answer was pretty weak. She said, I'm not going to get into specifics on that kind of thing. And the reporter who asked said, well, can you take that question back in and see if you can get a better answer for us, because it's caused a lot of divide within the country?\par \par \uc2 And it sounds like Karine did get a different answer, because she just tweeted a clarification to her earlier answer. It says: "As a result of the \uc2 Hamas\uc2  terrorist attacks, communities and families are grieving. For the past month, the families of those who have been taken hostage have lived in agony. Tearing down pictures of their loved ones who are being held hostage by \uc2 Hamas\uc2  is wrong and hurtful."\par \par \uc2 This is at least the second instance where Karine has had to clarify an answer that she gave in a briefing that didn't adequately defend Israel or the Jewish people, the first being a couple of weeks ago. She was asked about rising antisemitism. She pivoted to an answer about Islamophobia and later issued a clarification.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, there have been 40 attacks on U.S. troops in Iraq and Syria since October 17, 46 service members now reporting injuries, traumatic brain injuries for some of them. We asked the White House whether the president's warning to the ayatollah that the Iranian-backed proxies need to stop hitting U.S. troops was sufficient.\par \par \uc2 They said that it was, but he might take another action. Take a look at what John Kirby told me just a few minutes ago.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Have there been any additional warnings from the president since the last time we responded that this needs to stop? Because, clearly, the attacks on our troops didn't stop.\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, NSC COORDINATOR FOR STRATEGIC COMMUNICATIONS: I will not get into the decision-making process or options before the commander in chief. I would just tell you that we will continue to take the action we believe is appropriate to protect our troops and our facilities.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: So, not ruling out the possibility of some future response from the U.S., but, in his answer, did say that the president felt that the U.S. taking out those facilities in an attack a couple of weeks ago, retaliatory attack, was sufficient and did have an impact because it took out some weapons storage facilities, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Jacqui Heinrich at the White House.\par \par \uc2 Thank you for that update, Jacqui. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, it's one thing for the president to be losing support among some of his probably most friendly backers, but when they include donors, and they have thick wallets, and they're not opening them for you or potentially could close them for you, that bears watching.\par \par \uc2 Charlie Gasparino with more on that side -- Charlie.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE GASPARINO, FOX NEWS SENIOR CORRESPONDENT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 And, Neil, these are executives that -- on Wall Street -- and full disclosure, those are my sources -- who basically have an open ear -- have the White House's ear. They have the ear of the Democratic -- the top people in the \uc2 Democratic National Committee\uc2 . I mean, these are very powerful people.\par \par \uc2 And, quite frankly, they are in freak-out mode, from what I understand. They are really worried that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  will not be able to beat \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , to them, is the second coming of Satan, if you ask them. This is the last thing they want, the last person they want as president, and they don't believe \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has to wherewithal to do it.\par \par \uc2 Now, if \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is the nominee, obviously, you will see them support him. I mean, I don't know how much money they will throw at him, but there will still be support from these same donors, but they are making it very clear to the Democratic Party leadership, to their sources in the White House that they don't think \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has the mental fitness -- and it's mostly the mental fitness.\par \par \uc2 They just don't think he's up to the job of beating Trump, and they also believe, just quite frankly, when they tell this to the -- well, from what I understand, when these conversations go on, they say Trump is an existential threat to -- they have a problem with him in terms of what he might pose, given what happened on January 6.\par \par \uc2 I'm not saying I agree with this -- with you on this, Neil, but this is the argument they make to the White House. There's got -- fall on your sword, Uncle Joe, get someone else in there that could beat \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , and they also see the polls that show that everybody else does kind of -- well, all -- if you -- if you start sort of stacking up some of the other potential candidates, they do better than Biden against Trump.\par \par \uc2 So that's where we are right now. There's a lot of media ink spilled on just the disarray in the Republican Party, the infighting that's going on.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: There's a lot of concern right now from the donor class, in particular. And that's my sources. It's not necessarily the White House, but the donor class.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But that would have more impact if it does turn out that they're withholding giving money to the campaign or as the months ensue following that up with still more money.\par \par \uc2 But then the issue becomes, you got a year to go here.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: True.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: That's not a lot of time, given deadlines in key states and filing, for another candidate to take advantage of that, unless it's \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . But many of them aren't too keen on her.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: Yes. I mean, she's not the answer, obviously. And they all know that. I mean, it's got to be...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Are they saying that to you, Charlie, that she's not the backup we want?\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: Oh, yes, yes, yes, that she's not the backup.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So who do they? If they had their druthers, who do they like?\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2 . I mean, the names that come up, \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2 , \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Anybody, they think -- believe -- anybody of that stature could probably beat. Trump, they believe. And they really do believe that Trump is the likely nominee. Now, a lot of them talk about Nikki Haley. It was interesting. Some of these sort of moderate Wall Street Dems actually wouldn't mind getting behind Nikki Haley.\par \par \uc2 At least, those are the conversations I hear. They're impressed by her foreign policy chops. But it's -- at this point, it's like anybody but Trump.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: And they don't think that Biden can beat Trump.\par \par \uc2 We -- you point out that it is a year from now.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: A lot could happen in a year. And \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  hasn't really started...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And that's a small pool of folks, right? We look at Wall Street generally as a Republican bastion. It isn't exclusively that at all, just looking at history.\par \par \uc2 But these are significant players who right now are tiring of playing with the concept of a \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  getting reelected, right?\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: Neil, look at it this way. It's a year from now.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You're right.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: A lot can happen in a year. And that's what the president's people are saying.\par \par \uc2 But do you think \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is going to be more mentally fit a year from now than he is today? I mean, that's what they are saying. That's the point. He's only going to get worse. And in the middle of a campaign, if he's really worse, that could all but doom his presidency.\par \par \uc2 I mean, that's it. He could lose the race. The American people are not going to vote for somebody that appears mentally unsound, that -- or senile. And I think that's what we're getting at here. And that's the points that they're making. I'm not saying he is. I'm not a shrink.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: I don't know. But I'm just telling you that, as crazy as \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  sounds at times, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's mental instability, his weakness, they feel, is even a bigger impairment and could really hurt them.\par \par \uc2 It's a vulnerability that they are really worried about.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I got you.\par \par \uc2 But, given some of their decisions in the past, I could question their mental soundness. And sometimes mental soundness, look at you and me, Charlie, that's in the eye of the beholder.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: I agree 100 percent. Pot calling the kettle black there.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I hear you, my friend.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Good stuff. Very interesting stuff too. We will see how that pans out, Charlie Gasparino.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, this is Election Day, the biggest one, of course, before the big one next year almost about the same time to the day, but a couple of key races we're focusing on, but probably most importantly what's going on as we speak in Virginia, where they hope to do something they have not seen in the better part of a decade.\par \par \uc2 Rich Edson has more from Stafford, Virginia.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Rich.\par \par \uc2 RICH EDSON, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey. Good afternoon, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Well, Governor Glenn Youngkin is campaigning across the state. He's still up to it up in the final hours of this campaign right here. We caught up with him just a couple of miles from here at another polling location. He told us that Virginia voters are going to the polls right now to determine the issues that are setting up a national conversation to go right into the presidential election in less than a year.\par \par \uc2 He's highlighting inflation, environmental regulation, stiff penalties for fentanyl dealers, and education. And he says he's pretty confident Republicans will take control of the full legislature here.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. GLENN YOUNGKIN (R-VA): These are tight. We feel good, but these races are going to be decided by hundreds of votes in large turnouts. So get out and vote.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EDSON: It's divided government here in Virginia for now. If the GOP flips the Senate, hangs onto the House, they're also planning to limit abortions after 15 weeks in the state.\par \par \uc2 That's an issue Democrats have highlighted, and they have enlisted some party heavyweights to make the point. Former President \uc2 Obama\uc2  has recorded a robo-call playing in Virginia. He also posted -- quote -- "Progress in Virginia is at stake in this election, and there is no time to waste. Every single seat in the Virginia General Assembly will be on the ballot."\par \par \uc2 And President Biden, Vice President Harris, they have endorsed nearly two dozen Democrats in the closest races in the state, saying -- quote -- "Whether it means protecting a woman's right to make her own health care decisions, defending voting rights, or standing up for commonsense gun safety laws, electing these candidates on November 7 will protect our fundamental freedoms and will keep Virginia and our country moving forward."\par \par \uc2 This is a state that Governor Glenn Youngkin, the Republican, won by less than 2 percentage points a couple of years ago. Two years before -- or a year before that, two years ago, in 2020, President Biden won by more than 10 percentage points -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Rich, thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 Rich Edson in Virginia.\par \par \uc2 Tiana Lowe Doescher joins us right now, The Washington Examiner.\par \par \uc2 Tiana, let's say they succeed at this, and Republicans have a full run of the table of the House of Delegates, the Senate there, of course, and the governor's mansion. What did they do with that? What message did they send to the rest of the country with that?\par \par \uc2 TIANA LOWE DOESCHER, THE WASHINGTON EXAMINER: So, primarily, Youngkin ran and won in 2021 by really focusing on the schools. And that is what -- he got that Northern Virginia margin that usually is incredibly blue, is usually 80 points blue.\par \par \uc2 And Youngkin was able to win 25 percent of the vote. He was able to build almost 10 points on top of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's margin from 2020 because of the schools. While Youngkin has been able to use that executive power to codify some different things related to how you teach race in school, do you teach Critical Race Theory, teacher mandates, codifying that with the legislature in order to make it last into a next administration, that needs to be done with both the House and the Senate.\par \par \uc2 And, yes, we have -- the GOP has not had the governor's mansion, the state House, and the state Senate in 10 years at this point. The key with Youngkin is that he isn't just aggressively campaigning for the general, but, during the primary, he hand-selected a lot of the candidates that go in these crucial purple districts.\par \par \uc2 He wanted to make sure that he could get people who could cross the finish line and win over independent voters, win over maybe Democratic voters who were not happy under the COVID regime and not happy when they saw what their children were learning during the school shutdowns.\par \par \uc2 So it will all come down to turnout, but also will this candidate quality be able to overcome that abortion message that really pushed back on the ostensible red wave that was supposed to materialize last year?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Even with the abortion message you alluded to, the fact of the matter is, Virginia is trying to find this middle ground that essentially wouldn't tick off either side. I think 15 weeks is the trigger here.\par \par \uc2 And that seems to be acceptable. He has criticized other states that have gone and cut it off at six weeks or what have you. I'm just wondering, besides the way he played that, the way he played and kept his distance, not judging or saying anything bad about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  when he was running for governor, a lot of people think, since he can't run for reelection as governor in Virginia -- in other words, you can't serve a second term concurrently -- that there might be other possibilities for him down the road.\par \par \uc2 I don't know if it's too late to enter the race now, but what are you hearing on that front?\par \par \uc2 LOWE DOESCHER: So, Youngkin is obviously passing the filing deadlines to get into any of these primaries.\par \par \uc2 I think the myth that there will be some sort of brokered convention to have Glenn Youngkin save all the Republicans is more of a fantasy, even though he's clearly done -- he's clearly laid out a good road map for Republicans in other states that they absolutely should follow. Virginia will have a national Senate election that he is eligible to sit for.\par \par \uc2 And, with that being said, when you compare what the national GOP has done over the last six years to what Governor Youngkin has been able to do in the last two, I could see someone like Governor Youngkin trying to run the \uc2 RNC\uc2  and doing quite a better job than what we have gotten.\par \par \uc2 Staying out of the Trump mania, the Trump twister is always a good strategy.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LOWE DOESCHER: And you can afford to do it on a state level.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, well, he's doing it. So we will watch very closely, see how things turn out tonight in so many of these contests that the world is watching, including in New Jersey, where Republicans think they have a good shot of taking over the overall House, that's to say nothing of the Senate there, so something we have not seen in that state in the better part of 20 years.\par \par \uc2 So we're following that. Also following this death of an elderly Jewish man at a pro-Palestinian rally in California. I say they're still looking into that death, because, to them, it's looking more and more like a homicide -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, you have heard a good deal about this 69-year-old Jewish man who died at a pro-Palestinian rally. Now authorities they are looking into the possibility it wasn't natural, it was murder.\par \par \uc2 William La Jeunesse with more from Los Angeles -- William.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM LA JEUNESSE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, Neil, it was Sunday afternoon.\par \par \uc2 You had pro- and anti-Israeli protesters squared off at an intersection near Los Angeles. Moments later, 69-year-old Paul Kessler, a man you will see here carrying an Israeli the flag, was dead.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JAMES FRYHOFF, VENTURA COUNTY, CALIFORNIA, SHERIFF: Investigators have not ruled out the possibility of a hate crime. And this is being investigated as a homicide.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: So the sheriff's department interviewed a 50-year-old man and searched his home, but have made no arrests as of yet.\par \par \uc2 Witnesses say the assailant cross the street and hit Kessler over the head with a megaphone, knocking him to the ground. Now, an autopsy shows that Kessler died Monday not from the megaphone strike, but blunt-force trauma to the head.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DR. CHRISTOPHER YOUNG, VENTURA COUNTY, CALIFORNIA, CHIEF MEDICAL EXAMINER: The injuries on the left side of the face were nonlethal. And the lethal injury was the impact of the back of the head from the -- from Mr. Kessler falling and striking his head off the ground.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN OSWAKS, WITNESS: When I saw the fracas start, I saw the bullhorn come across. It was very easy to see from where I was.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: So, this video shows a Palestinian supporter with a megaphone and a flag explaining to deputies what happened, where the incident occurred, and how Kessler was struck with the megaphone.\par \par \uc2 Now, some Jewish supporters wanted to immediately confront the larger Palestinian group, but elected not to.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 OSWAKS: They were like: "Let's go."\par \par \uc2 And I turned around and I said: "Don't you move. I said, if you go over there, I'm going over there, and it's not going to end well for anybody."\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: So, Monday area, Jews held a candlelight vigil.\par \par \uc2 The Los Angeles Jewish Federation released a statement, saying: "Violence against our people has no place in civilized society. We demand safety. We will not tolerate violence against our community. And we will do everything in our power to prevent it."\par \par \uc2 So, right now, Neil, police are asking for video, maybe to see what immediately preceded this attack. Was it provoked, unprovoked? The question now is, in addition to the homicide charge, Neil, what evidence did they find in the suspect's home that could support a hate crime? And when could we see an arrest? -- back to you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: William La Jeunesse.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, William.\par \par \uc2 All right, a month into the war, growing signs, at least from Israel, what it wants to see after the war -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Pro-Palestinian demonstrations again in New York City. We're seeing more of these. And more are planned. Not too many the other way around.\par \par \uc2 What to make of that -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Israeli troops are now in Gaza. We don't know how many. We don't know what the longer-term or even shorter-term plans are.\par \par \uc2 But it is a big development amid calls that \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  have these sort of targeted breaks in action or calculated momentary lapses here, so that help can get in and aid and others can get out.\par \par \uc2 Let's go to \uc2 Mike Tobin\uc2 . He's in Sderot, Israel, with the very latest -- Mike.\par \par \uc2 MIKE TOBIN\uc2 , FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: And, Neil, one month into this war, we're getting a unified message out of the Israeli leadership, and that is there won't be a humanitarian pause, there won't be a cease-fire unless it involves a return of the hostages.\par \par \uc2 In fact, something Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  said tonight is, there only going to turn up the pressure on \uc2 Hamas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2 , ISRAELI PRIME MINISTER: The fighting in the south is advancing at the might that \uc2 Hamas\uc2  hasn't known. Gaza is encircled. We are operating in it and deepening the pressure on \uc2 Hamas\uc2  within the hour, every day.\par \par \uc2 So far, we have killed thousands of terrorists above the ground and under the ground.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TOBIN: Now, one of the primary jobs of the soldiers inside of the fence in Gaza is to find those terrorists underground and to find the entrances to the tunnels.\par \par \uc2 They're finding a lot of them in residences. They're finding them in the landscape in the north of the Gaza Strip. They found one in a children's amusement park right over by the ferris wheel. When they find the entrances to these tunnels, they blow them up.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, you have Palestinians who were sticking it out in the northern end of the Gaza Strip, raising the white flag by the hundreds and beginning the slow, perilous march to the south. They march past Israeli tanks. They march past the dead alongside of the road.\par \par \uc2 And, ultimately, when they get to places like Khan Yunis, the camps there are overwhelmed and they're sleeping where they can. They're getting food, whatever they can get their hands on. Through it all, \uc2 Hamas\uc2  is still able to fire rockets even out of the northern end of the Gaza Strip. We watched about three barrages launched tonight.\par \par \uc2 One of the rockets made it all the way through. The defenses got -- it hit a house near Tel Aviv. The family is OK because they listened to the sirens and they got in a safe room -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Mike, when you talk to Israelis in general, and they see and hear things like a Palestinian protest that's going on right now in New York, countless of them around the region you're reporting from, do they get frustrated?\par \par \uc2 Do they not like this notion that, in some crazy way, \uc2 Hamas\uc2  somehow winning the P.R. war here with this global push for a cease-fire or cessation in activity?\par \par \uc2 TOBIN: They're very frustrated. And they feel like the world is out to get them, that antisemitism is on the rise, that \uc2 Hamas\uc2  indeed is playing the P.R. game and winning at it.\par \par \uc2 They understand that \uc2 Hamas\uc2  was never going to beat a tank. You can't have a paraglider versus a tank and expect to win. The P.R. game was always part of the strategy. And when you have people protesting at Berkeley or wherever they're having their student protest, they were always part of the \uc2 Hamas\uc2  strategy, and they're playing the role -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Amazing. All right, thank you very much. Be safe, my friend. I know you get tired of hearing it, but I do mean it.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN: You got it.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: \uc2 Mike Tobin\uc2  in Israel there.\par \par \uc2 I want to go to \uc2 Danny Danon\uc2  right now, the former Israeli ambassador to the United Nations, a member of the Israeli Knesset.\par \par \uc2 Ambassador, it's always good to have you.\par \par \uc2 And I think I'm going to pick up with you what I left off with \uc2 Mike Tobin\uc2 , this P.R. campaign that, obviously, \uc2 Hamas\uc2  is certainly gaining among Arab communities to universally push for a cease-fire. And now it's seeping into American politics, with more and more people urging the same, the White House at a crossroads, being pulled and pushed in both directions.\par \par \uc2 How do you feel about that?\par \par \uc2 DANNY DANON\uc2 , FORMER ISRAELI AMBASSADOR TO THE UNITED NATIONS: Neil, I completely agree with you.\par \par \uc2 We commemorated today a month for the massacre. And it seems that the international community has forgotten what happened, who started this vicious attack against us. And you see the demonstrations, the language they use, the violence against Jews in the U.S., in France, in the U.K. and I ask myself, haven't they seen the footage of the horrible massacre, the raping?\par \par \uc2 We have babies now in the \uc2 Hamas\uc2  hostage for a month. I will give you an example of Abigail Edan. She is a 3-year-old baby. She was kidnapped from a village which is called Kfar Aza. Her parents were massacred in front of her eyes, and she ran to the neighbors and was kidnapped from her home.\par \par \uc2 For one month, this pure baby, who knows what's happening to her? So we have 240 kidnapped Israelis. So I would advise those who are demonstrating maybe to look at the pictures of the Israelis who were kidnapped and ask themselves why they're demonstrating. For what?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: The issue of what happens after this war ends, as we're a month into it, \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  has made it very, very clear that they have got to plan for that and Israeli control of the region for a good while after that, an indefinite amount of time, an indefinite period, in his words.\par \par \uc2 What do you suspect?\par \par \uc2 DANON: So, first, we have to be realistic.\par \par \uc2 We have only started the war. We are now operating in Northern Gaza. We have to clean the area from \uc2 Hamas\uc2  terrorists. It will take a while, and we expect to get the support from our friends to realize that we need moral clarity when we fight evil.\par \par \uc2 Once we finish the job, which will take us some time, I think we have to think about a few ideas. Some can be about international mandate involving different countries in the region or from the world that will be there.\par \par \uc2 But, for us, the most important thing is that we will continue to make sure that we have the freedom to fight terrorism. We cannot sit outside of Gaza and watch \uc2 Hamas\uc2  coming back again. So we have no intention to stay there permanently, but we're going to have to have the ability to come in and out as much as we need to eliminate terror, very similar to what's happening today in Judea and Samaria, where you have the P.A.\par \par \uc2 But when we know about some kind of activity that is supporting a threat to us, we operate.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You and I had chat about this over the weekend, Ambassador. I was just curious whether your opinion has evolved a little bit here, because you obviously want to roll in, understandably, after the butchering of all of those Israelis, 1,400 of them, that you don't put Israelis in that danger ever again.\par \par \uc2 But this -- this Rafah peace way or passageway, whatever you want to call it, Egypt played a role in that, but so did Qatar. And a lot of American officials are looking at Qatar as playing a vital role as a mediator and a bridge, but it's also home to some of \uc2 Hamas\uc2 ' worst guerrillas and fighters, some of them living in swanky five-star hotels.\par \par \uc2 Would you entertain Qatar having a role in deciding Gaza's fate after this ends?\par \par \uc2 DANON: Well, I think it's a good question.\par \par \uc2 I think now we have no choice but to use the connection of Qatar to speak with the \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . But I think, in the future, we have to draw a very clear line. Either you support terrorists or you fight terrorists. And Qatar, for years, they are playing in between. They support terrorists and then, at the same time, they speak against them.\par \par \uc2 But look what's happening now, the entire leadership of \uc2 Hamas\uc2  sitting in fancy hotels in Doha, Qatar, and from there they are sending orders to \uc2 Hamas\uc2  in Gaza. I don't think the international community should allow it in the future. Qatar will have to choose whether they want to be a terror state or a state that is fighting terror.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I'd be remiss. I'm showing these Palestinian sympathetic marchers in New York City, Ambassador.\par \par \uc2 And a lot of them are talking about the provocation for this being Israel and how it treated those in Gaza, totally ignorant of the fact that Israel has had no control, no role in that region for close to two decades now. It's been under \uc2 Hamas\uc2 ' control. So if there are problems with how people and Palestinians were treated there, it's on \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . It's not on Israel.\par \par \uc2 But this is something that plays out again and again. And it seems like that never comes through on any level, with many people buying that worldwide that this is on you, that this was all seeded by Israel. How do you fight that?\par \par \uc2 DANON: So, we are fighting it with the tools, that, two days ago, I took a Prime Minister \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  from the U.K. and former Prime Minister Scott Morrison from Australia, and I took to show them what happened.\par \par \uc2 I wish I could take all those people who are demonstrating to show them what's happening. But let's stick to the facts. The facts are that we left Gaza in 2005 completely. The second fact, that Gaza has a border with Egypt, so they can come to Egypt and ask them, why you are not allowing the Palestinians to leave through Egypt, or to receive whatever they want from Egypt?\par \par \uc2 And the third fact the most important one, on October 7, they attacked us. They invaded our communities. There was no aggression coming from Israel, no reason for them to invade them to attack us in a barbaric way. So I think, when you put the facts on the face, they will have no answers.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Some people don't listen to facts, Ambassador. Some people refuse to acknowledge they exist.\par \par \uc2 DANON: Yes, those who are full of hate and incitement, we cannot convince them. But we should not be deterred.\par \par \uc2 We should continue with the tools. And we are determined to fight until we eliminate evil. It's either us or them.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Ambassador, thank you. Very good chatting with you again.\par \par \uc2 Danny Danon\uc2 , the former Israeli ambassador to the United Nations, Israeli Knesset, a key member here, a key player in whatever emerges in that region after this all ends, hopefully sooner, rather than later.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, more on these broadcasts that are popping up. They're already getting a long list of ones that are planned for this weekend yet again. Those are global. This is the Big \uc2 Apple\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, one month into this war, the protests go on and on.\par \par \uc2 We have had an inordinate number of them just in the New York metropolitan area. This looks like another big one, another pro-Palestinian March.\par \par \uc2 Alexis McAdams following it all -- Alexis.\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS MCADAMS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Neil.\par \par \uc2 So, in the past half-hour or so, we saw pretty big crowds here in Manhattan. They all met up at City Hall there, as you're kind of looking at. Some of it's kind of died down now. But it got pretty wild. One of my producers who's on the ground said they were kind of climbing up on different cars enchanting things like "Genocide Joe has got to go," saying the United States is paying for the genocide of the Palestinian people.\par \par \uc2 We want to see if we can hear some of this. We will take this full here, so people at home can kind of hear what's going on there on the city streets. You can see they're waving Palestinian flags there. And the group says the protest is against all elected officials who are actively complicit in the genocide of the Palestinian people.\par \par \uc2 This is the same group, Neil, that's had some other protests across the city too. So, meanwhile, the president of Israel calling on colleges and universities across the United States to address the rise of Jewish hate.\par \par \uc2 We want to pull up this letter right now, sending this letter. This is from President \uc2 Isaac Herzog\uc2 , who attended both Cornell and \uc2 NYU\uc2 . He's writing in part: "How can anyone endorsing, excusing or glorifying the \uc2 Hamas\uc2  atrocities have a place in any college or in this civilized world?"\par \par \uc2 This as student protests continue across the country. The Israeli ambassador to the \uc2 U.N\uc2 ., Neil, also saying the rise in antisemitism on college campuses cannot be ignored. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GILAD ERDAN, ISRAELI AMBASSADOR TO THE UNITED NATIONS: When university presidents facilitate terror-supporting rallies, we must call for their resignation loud and clear. When Jewish students are assaulted on campus, we must demand the antisemites be expelled.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: Adding to the calls for change, more than two dozen members of Congress wrote a letter to \uc2 UPenn\uc2 , slamming the school's response or lack of response to the \uc2 Hamas\uc2  terror attacks.\par \par \uc2 In that letter, they wrote, in part: "We implore you to act swiftly and decisively here. It's your responsibility to condemn this terrorist attack unequivocally and publicly."\par \par \uc2 So, back out here live in New York, Neil, we also saw another large protest that was at the United Nations earlier today. People were sitting in front of the \uc2 U.N\uc2 ., demanding that they do something, take some type of action to try and get those innocent hostages who were being held by \uc2 Hamas\uc2  home.\par \par \uc2 But as you saw and as you have said, Neil, these protests are happening in big cities and those campuses across the country.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, and getting quite the number of people attending them.\par \par \uc2 Alexis, thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 Alexis McAdams in New York City.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime here, you heard what John Kirby had to say about \uc2 Rashida Tlaib\uc2  and her comments regarding that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  had blood on his hands. Now it's Congress' turn to take up action, if any, against her.\par \par \uc2 Chad Pergram with more on that -- Chad.\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Neil, Michigan Democrat \uc2 Rashida Tlaib\uc2  is on a collision course tomorrow with censure.\par \par \uc2 That's because she supports what lawmakers believe are antisemitic comments. More on that coming up.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, there are some Democratic leaders who do blame Israel.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman \uc2 Rashida Tlaib\uc2 , of course, has accused the Biden administration of genocide and sort of turning the other way in the face of this. Now, she's been criticized by people like \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  and others, to be fair.\par \par \uc2 But are you concerned this is leading to a schism even within the Democratic Party and among those who would normally support what the president wants to do in a foreign affairs matter?\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, NSC COORDINATOR FOR STRATEGIC COMMUNICATIONS: Well, certainly, members of Congress have got to speak for themselves and for their beliefs about this.\par \par \uc2 We're not focused here at the National Security Council about the political -- domestic political situation here. We're really focused on -- we're...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, what she said, though, was that the administration was supporting genocide.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Well, of course we're not doing that.\par \par \uc2 And what is happening in Gaza, again, as terrible as all these civilian casualties are -- and we know there's many, many thousands of them, and we don't want there to be any. I don't want to minimize that at all.\par \par \uc2 But you can't look at what is happening in Gaza and say that it fits the definition of genocide. And, clearly, we don't agree with that -- with that description. We think that is an irresponsible way of describing this. We don't associate ourselves with that. And that is not -- in our view, that is not what is happening here.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, what is happening now is a move afoot by many in Congress to censure the Michigan Congresswoman \uc2 Rashida Tlaib\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Chad Pergram has more on that effort and how that is going.\par \par \uc2 What can you tell us, Chad?\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Neil, good afternoon.\par \par \uc2 The House intends to punish \uc2 Rashida Tlaib\uc2  tomorrow for antisemitic comments. The House voted against killing an effort to sanction Tlaib today. She retweeted an anti-Israeli trope, which calls for the destruction of Israel.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. RICH MCCORMICK (R-GA): It's a sad fact, but this type of antisemitic hate is being promoted by a small group of members in this body, chiefly, Representative Tlaib. We must hold her accountable.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Tlaib expressed support for the statement "From the river to the sea."\par \par \uc2 Israelis view that as offensive. Some Democrats oppose discipline for Tlaib on free speech grounds. Florida Democrat \uc2 Jared Moskowitz\uc2  is Jewish. He voted to table the resolution today, but Moskowitz will support censure for Tlaib tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. JARED MOSKOWITZ (D-FL): I do think Representative Tlaib's comments about eliminating a country -- because that's what from the river to the sea means. It means eliminating an entire country. That's unacceptable of any member of Congress to call on part of any of our allies.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Tlaib is the first Palestinian American in Congress. Her grandmother still lives in the region. The House has only censured 25 members in history.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. RASHIDA TLAIB (D-MI): I can't believe I have to say this, but Palestinian people are not disposable. Speaking up to save lives, Mr. Chair, no matter faith, no matter ethnicity, should not be controversial in this chamber.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Democrats are coming after GOP members. The House will also consider it discipline for GOP Florida Representative \uc2 Brian Mast\uc2 . He said that Palestinian civilians are -- quote -- "Nazis" -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Chad Pergram, thank you very much for that.\par \par \uc2 Want to take you right now to Richmond, Virginia. Governor Glenn Youngkin, of course, has been leading this effort to see if he can pull off something that would be a pretty huge political feat, at least in Virginia, where we haven't seen in the better part of a decade. He is working on trying to make the blue Senate in that state red, have the Republicans take it over.\par \par \uc2 They presently control the House of Delegates. So, essentially, this would mean all 140 seats of the General Assembly are now up for grabs. And with a Republican governor, they would run the table if they succeeded in doing that. The governor is very confident that can happen and will happen. It's being closely watched across the country.\par \par \uc2 And he is a rising star was doing the kind of things without any of the friction or divisiveness we have seen elsewhere, someone to consider. Whether it's too late for presidential run or to be considered as a potential vice president, anyone's guess, but this is being very, very closely followed in Virginia, the state for lovers and, if the governor has his way, for Republicans across the board as well.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, you have heard, in politics terms, a big move on the part of Iowa's \uc2 Kim Reynolds\uc2 , the governor of Iowa, breaking her silence and maybe announcing the best not so secret, that she's supporting \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  for president. She's already been quickly criticized by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , who calls her the worst governor in the nation.\par \par \uc2 But the fact of the matter is that is something that could be crucial to the Florida governor. She carries considerable weight, popularity and influence, at least in Iowa. Whether that helps him improve his poll standings there -- he's still a distant, distant second to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  -- anyone's guess.\par \par \uc2 But it got us thinking here, which is always dangerous on "Your World," whether one influential player in that state influences others to also get off the fence and announce supporting a candidate.\par \par \uc2 Bob Vander Plaats comes to mind, The Family Leader president and CEO. He's holding this big, I guess you would call it like a Thanksgiving event. Every Republican candidate wants to make it and just sort of have a chance to rub elbows with Bob and maybe get his endorsement.\par \par \uc2 Bob, very good to have you.\par \par \uc2 Have you committed to anyone, first off?\par \par \uc2 BOB VANDER PLAATS, CEO, THE FAMILY LEADER: I have not committed to anyone, Neil, but thanks for letting me on your show.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: No, I'm glad to have you.\par \par \uc2 You obviously know what \uc2 Kim Reynolds\uc2  did. I mean, that was not a big surprise, even though she had been standing back and avoiding that, but chose this moment to announce DeSantis is her guy.\par \par \uc2 What do you think that will mean to \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 VANDER PLAATS: Well, obviously in the past, three cycles at least, Iowans break very late. They broke late for Huckabee. They broke late for Santorum and for Cruz.\par \par \uc2 And so everybody's looking for a game-changer. And I'd say Governor \uc2 Kim Reynolds\uc2 , obviously, the most popular governor in my lifetime, and someone who has won overwhelmingly in this state, coming out to endorse you, that's a big thing. And I don't think \uc2 Kim Reynolds\uc2  endorses unless she sees that he may have a pathway to win the Iowa caucuses, but time's going to tell how impactful her endorsement is.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Do you like \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 VANDER PLAATS: Oh, I definitely like \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . Hard not to like him. I think every Republican or conservative would like him, and especially the results that he's achieved as governor of Florida.\par \par \uc2 The big thing, does that translate now to being president of the United States. Nikki Haley's obviously saying she has a better shot, Tim Scott, \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2 , and obviously the former president as well.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 VANDER PLAATS: So, it's still a very competitive race and a competitive field, but I think we're going to have a real Iowa caucus.\par \par \uc2 And Iowa is known to kind of upset the apple cart here and there.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: No, you're right. You're right about that.\par \par \uc2 VANDER PLAATS: And so I think we know that, and I think \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  knows that.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Are you going to commit to a candidate before the caucuses?\par \par \uc2 VANDER PLAATS: I may do it before the caucuses.\par \par \uc2 The Thanksgiving family forum that we're going to have that you just opened your show with, after we have that conversation with the candidates, our board, my team, and myself, I mean, we will just get a chance to discern if this is a time for us to weigh in or not. And if we do, we want to make sure that we can make our endorsement stick.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I look at the polls just like you do, and you could be quite right about this, that things could and likely will change right before the caucuses themselves, but it does seem like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's state to lose right now.\par \par \uc2 He has such a big lead that it would be hard to chip away at that. What do you think of that?\par \par \uc2 VANDER PLAATS: Well, there's no doubt he has a big lead right now.\par \par \uc2 And I think there's a poll out, I think, by CBS News that said 20 percent are locked into \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . They're never going to move; 31 percent are locked against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . They're never going to move. So it's that other 49 percent that are considering Trump and the other candidates.\par \par \uc2 And so if there is a candidate that can capture the imagination, maybe lock onto a \uc2 Kim Reynolds\uc2  endorsement or who knows what else, maybe get a candidate to emerge. But you can see that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  also knows that his lead in Iowa is fragile, at best. He's spending a lot of time here, spending a lot of money here.\par \par \uc2 And he's attacking those that he believes are his biggest threat.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: This forum that you talk about is only 10 days away. It's going to be at the Des Moines Marriott.\par \par \uc2 And among those who have been invited include all the obvious ones, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , Nikki Haley, \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2 , Tim Scott. Do you think \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  attends?\par \par \uc2 VANDER PLAATS: I don't believe he will attend. We invited him to our leadership summit as well, and he did not attend that. He doesn't like to do multicandidate events.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VANDER PLAATS: I think that's a mistake on his part. He still might be able to win Iowa. I just think it's a mistake on his part.\par \par \uc2 These are the people that carried a lot of water for him to win the state in 2016 and again in 2020.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: The argument against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  getting anyone's support is -- and some have expressed this in the party -- that he can win the nomination easily, but he can't win the election. And, then, lo and behold come these battle state polls that show him beating \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  rather handily in most of them, five of the six of them, and essentially tied, I think, in Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 Does that argument go away now? Is \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  very much electable and, as things stand now, very much in the running to be a president again?\par \par \uc2 VANDER PLAATS: Yes, I don't think that argument goes away yet, Neil.\par \par \uc2 And one other things, I think that poll shows more how weak \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is as a presidential candidate in 2024 than the strength of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . I would find it hard to believe that President Biden will be their nominee, will be their candidate for 2024. And I think what we want to do is rally around somebody that we believe can win, and not just win, but have the coattails for down-ballot offices, both federally and statewide.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But when I heard the booing that \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  received at this Florida event from Republicans in that audience who were very loyal to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , they don't seem to be in a mind-set to consider anyone else.\par \par \uc2 Now, that's Florida, I grant you. But we see this pop up at forums across the country when they happen. I'm not familiar with Iowa, and maybe you can educate me on that. But do you think that the party now is very much \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's party and hard for any of those other candidates to make headway?\par \par \uc2 VANDER PLAATS: I don't know if it's \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's party or \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's movement yet. I think he was a person that would help advance the movement, help advance the party. I think, in Iowa as well, Neil, people would get booed if they just openly criticized the former president, because there's a lot of things that he did that we really like and that we would really champion.\par \par \uc2 So this isn't being against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . This is about who can win in 2024. We really do believe this is the most important election of our lifetime. I think it's why \uc2 Kim Reynolds\uc2  is weighing in, saying, we want to get around somebody who can win. If you look at a DeSantis, he takes a tossup state and makes it deep red and wins in demographics that we haven't won in, in a long time.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Bob, there is -- we talk about the division among Democrats. There's ample division among Republicans too.\par \par \uc2 Obviously, with Mike Johnson now, maybe there's hope that in the House they have settled that and rallied around one guy for the time being. But when it comes to dealing with help for Israel and/or Ukraine, do you think the party is standoffish on foreign aid period now? Does that worry you, real quickly?\par \par \uc2 VANDER PLAATS: Oh, I -- yes, it does. Well, I don't know if it worries me.\par \par \uc2 I think what they're trying to do is achieve a level of common sense that we don't need to be involved in every foreign conflict that is out there.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 VANDER PLAATS: But I think, right now, there's unanimity in regards to how do we stand with Israel and how do we protect our Southern border as well.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right. Good luck at your event 10 days away. We will see what happens.\par \par \uc2 Bob Vander Plaats, who knows, maybe an announcement will come from you, The Family Leader president and CEO, a very influential player in Iowa. And we will see how that sorts out.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, that will do it for us.\par \par \uc2 Here comes "The Five."\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020231108ejb700001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Israeli Forces Claim Isolation Of Gaza City; \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  Faces Israel- \uc2 Hamas\uc2  War And Deficient Polling; Woman Thought She Was Driving Car Into Jewish School; Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  Testifies In New York Civil Fraud Trial; Trump Denounces New York Trial Prosecution; Biden Losing To Trump In Poll Of Key States; \uc2 Obama\uc2  Insider Questions Wisdom Of Biden Run; Beshear And Cameron In Close Race for Governor; Voters In Five States Consider Measures; Virginia State House And Senate Elections May Give Republicans Control Of Legislative Body And Governorship; Ohio To Vote On Whether Abortion Should Be Protected By State's Constitution; Federal Reserve Pauses Rate Hikes But Cautions Inflation Still Too High; New Poll Shows President Biden Trailing Former President Trump In Battleground States; Former President \uc2 Obama\uc2  Gives Speaks About Israeli-\uc2 Hamas\uc2  War And Suffering Of Palestinians\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Nate Foy, Jacqui Heinrich, Mike Tobin, Garrett Tenney,  Brit Hume, Rich Edson, Mark Meredith, Charles Hurt, Ben Domenech \par \uc2 7,898 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 November 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Do you have anything to promote Kayleigh?\par \par \uc2 KAYLEIGH MCENANY\uc2 , FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: "OUTNUMBERED" tomorrow at noon. I'm there every day. So, join me Harris and Emily who's is on your show tonight. We love Emily Compagno.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: And I will see you on "AMERICA'S NEWSROOM". Harold, are you going to be anywhere?\par \par \uc2 HAROLD FORD JR\uc2 ., FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Let's go Blue.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: All right.\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Just happy to be here.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Have a great night.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: You guys are running out of things to say, Dana, you didn't have anything else.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: It's not usually this way. I must have done something wrong or right.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Harold, do you have anything you're promoting? All right. OK. Dana, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Welcome to Washington. I'm Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  on the witness stand in his civil fraud trial, a day that saw some fireworks in and out of the courtroom. We'll take you there.\par \par \uc2 Plus, a major democratic officials sheds doubt on whether President Biden should even attempt reelection.\par \par \uc2 And an Indiana woman is in custody after trying to destroy what she thought was a building, housing an Israeli school filled with Jewish students and teachers. We'll tell you what she actually hid and what she's facing tonight.\par \par \uc2 But first, breaking tonight, Israeli officials say at one point earlier today, approximately 30 launches were identified from Lebanon toward Northern Israel. Troops are now responding with artillery fire aimed at the origin of those launches. Is this the beginning of that Northern front everyone has been talking about in earnest?\par \par \uc2 Meantime, Israeli forces in greater numbers are expected to enter Gaza City at any time. That will unleash as we've also talked about a street by street, building by building fight with \uc2 Hamas\uc2  terrorists using a vast network of tunnels.\par \par \uc2 Israel continues to reject U.S. calls for what the Secretary of State called humanitarian pauses in fighting to facilitate hostage release and humanitarian aid moving in.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, U.S. forces in the Middle East are coming under increasing fire. Senior Correspondent \uc2 Mike Tobin\uc2  is in southern Israel again tonight with the latest. Good evening, Mike.\par \par \uc2 MIKE TOBIN\uc2 , FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret. \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  continues to provoke Israel with rocket fire across the Lebanese border. There's fresh bloodshed in the West Bank as four \uc2 Hamas\uc2  gunmen were killed and Israel is intensifying the combat in Gaza.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOBIN (voice over): Explosions rocking Gaza night and day, with \uc2 Israeli Defense Forces\uc2  surrounding Gaza City ready to move in and start the next phase of the war.\par \par \uc2 As Israel rebuffs growing international pressure for a ceasefire, it says fighter jets hit 450 \uc2 Hamas\uc2  targets while troops on the ground seized a militant compound and killed high value targets.\par \par \uc2 New video shows a military convoy bringing in supplies to get Israeli troops ready for street by street urban warfare.\par \par \uc2 As Iran backed \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  supporting \uc2 Hamas\uc2  continues to shell Northern Israel, the prime minister called the groups the new axis of evil.\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2 , ISRAELI PRIME MINISTER: What we see is a broader battle between civilization and barbarism. This is not a local battle. This is a global battle. The Paramount need is to defeat this axis.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN (voice over): Following stops in Israel, Jordan, Cyprus and Iraq, the U.S. Secretary of State wrapped up his shuttle diplomacy trip in Turkey, where prior to his visit, pro-Palestinian crowds tried to storm an airbase that houses U.S. troops.\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN\uc2 , U.S. SECRETARY OF STATE: We've also had very important conversations throughout this trip with countries in the region on the role that everyone can play in making sure that the conflict doesn't expand, doesn't spread to other countries.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN (voice over): Israel continues to deny claims it's targeting civilians and says it reopened an evacuation route for people to get out of Northern Gaza. Israel says \uc2 Hamas\uc2  has been blocking the corridor, \uc2 Hamas\uc2  blames Israel.\par \par \uc2 OSAMA HAMDAN, SENIOR \uc2 HAMAS\uc2  OFFICIAL (through translator): Israel says we warned you to head down south and despite that, they conducted airstrikes on women and children on the street while they were heading down.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOBIN (on camera): Attacks on U.S. forces in the region are increasing. The Pentagon says there were eight attacks from Iranian proxies U.S. forces in Iraq and Syria. The injuries to U.S. personnel are said to be minor traumatic brain injuries, but there were 45, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: \uc2 Mike Tobin\uc2  in southern Israel. Mike, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Over the weekend, thousands of angry pro-Palestinian demonstrators marched here in Washington, some vandalizing the White House fence with red paint. This comes as new polling paints a grim picture for President Biden's reelection campaign.\par \par \uc2 White House correspondent Jacqui Heinrich has details tonight live from the North Lawn. Good evening, Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret. President Biden spoke with Israeli Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  again today. And although the White House readout of the call says that the two leaders discussed the possibility of pauses in fighting to get civilians out, aid in and hostages out. It appears that Israel is still not embracing this idea. Team Biden is telling reporters that they will continue to advocate for this.\par \par \uc2 It's a little bit unusual the administration is now acknowledging some distance between the U.S. and its ally on this particular issue, but it is apparently not enough to quiet Biden's critics.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): President Biden shrugging off 2024 anxieties growing within his party, touting investments in railway infrastructure in Delaware.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I can honestly say I've never been more optimistic about America's future. And, folks, in my view, the president after I'm -- long after I'm president is going to have more to do.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): But as Biden spoke, the \uc2 National Park Service\uc2  scrubbed fake bloody handprints off the White House gate left by pro- Palestinian protesters this weekend.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Biden genocide Joe.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): And progressive Democrats are getting louder.\par \par \uc2 REP. PRAMILA JAYAPAL (D-WA): The 2024 election is in great trouble for the president and for our democratic control.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 JAMAAL BOWMAN\uc2  (D-NY): People of color are disengaged. So, there's a large part of the Democratic base that is struggling to come around to continue to support President Biden.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): Congresswoman \uc2 Rashida Tlaib\uc2  accusing Biden of supporting genocide of the Palestinian people.\par \par \uc2 REP. RASHIDA TLAIB (D-MI): We will remember in 2024.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): So far, the White House holding firm on backing Israel.\par \par \uc2 JON FINER, DEPUTY NATIONAL SECURITY ADVISER: We strongly disagree with some of that messaging, and some of the terms used to describe this conflict.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): But resolve make it harder to maintain, a New York Times poll now has Biden losing to former President Trump in five critical swing states, and three quarters of Americans think the country is headed in the wrong direction according to a new ABC survey.\par \par \uc2 Former \uc2 Obama\uc2  adviser David Axelrod went so far as to say Biden should consider dropping out.\par \par \uc2 DAVID AXELROD, FORMER SENIOR ADVISER TO BARACK OBAMA: Or just has to ask himself is -- you know, is this the best path? This is probably the last moment for him to do that check. And it's probably good if he does.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (on camera): And the Biden campaign threw some cold water pretty quickly on those polls. They pointed to the predicted eight point loss that President \uc2 Obama\uc2  never suffered for one of his terms, and we have heard more progressive Democrats like Senator \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  now pointing to some of the issues that young voters are upset about in making the case that Trump would be far worse than Biden on those issues, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jacqui, on Americans inside Gaza, does the NSC, does the government have a number that they're using?\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Well, we know that about 300 Americans that were in Gaza and expressed a desire to leave got out over the course of this weekend. The initial number that we were given was that the State Department was tracking about 400 Americans, plus their families, a total of around a thousand people.\par \par \uc2 So, minus the 300 that were told got out, a little bit unclear exactly how many are left. And of course, that's an addition to the American hostages that are still unaccounted for, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That number we have at around 10, we think?\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: That's -- we haven't heard any change from that. And when we ask about the situation with hostages, we are told this is still something that they are actively engaging in. And that is a piece of the discussion about humanitarian pauses.\par \par \uc2 We know that there's been at least one humanitarian pause so far. That's why the administration keeps advocating for pauses in part to get these hostages out but Israel has said no pauses unless the hostages are released. So, that's sort of where we stand, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, Jacqui Heinrich live in the North Lawn. Jacqui, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Indianapolis police officers have arrested a woman after she drove her car into a building she thought was a Jewish school. They're calling her the suspect a terrorist. The building is actually used by an anti-Semitic hate group.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Garrett Tenney has the latest of this bizarre story from our Midwest bureau. Good evening, Garrett.\par \par \uc2 GARRETT TENNEY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Bret, good evening. Police and the \uc2 FBI\uc2  are now investigating this case as a potential hate crime committed by a woman with no apparent criminal record.\par \par \uc2 Late Friday night, after watching coverage of the Israel \uc2 Hamas\uc2  war, police say 34-year-old Ruba Almaghtheh, decided to plan an attack and chose this building because she thought it was a Jewish school. She drove her car into the building, breaking down one of the walls while several adults and children were inside. No one was injured.\par \par \uc2 When police arrested the woman, they say she made reference to her people back in Palestine, told officers, yes, I did it on purpose, and then admitted to committing a hate crime during a phone call with a family member while in jail.\par \par \uc2 The building she thought was a Jewish school is actually associated with an extremist sect of the Black Hebrew Israelites, which the \uc2 Anti-Defamation League\uc2  defines as anti-Semitic.\par \par \uc2 In response to the attack, the Indianapolis Jewish Community Relations Council said although a Jewish facility was not targeted, solely due to ironic misidentification, this is yet another reminder to maintain security protocols remain, vigilant of suspicious activity and to report the same prompted leaked to the appropriate authorities.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TENNEY (on camera): The suspect is currently facing a charge of criminal recklessness but could face additional charges including for hate crimes when she shows up in court for her initial hearing Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Garrett Tenney in Chicago. Garrett, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  took the stand today in his Manhattan civil fraud trial. Correspondent Nate Foy is outside the courthouse again tonight with the very latest. Good evening, Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE FOY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret. Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  left the courthouse tonight calling this trial a disgrace and a scam.\par \par \uc2 During his testimony, Justice \uc2 Arthur Engoron\uc2  instructed him to provide shorter answers. At one point saying, "I'm not here to hear what he has to say". And Trump talks about being treated unfairly while leaving the courthouse tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Because it's a scam, and this is a case that should have never been brought into a case that should be dismissed immediately. People are sick and tired of what's happening. This is a sad -- I think it's a very sad day for America.\par \par \uc2 FOY (voice over): Bret, earlier today, Trump called New York Attorney General Letitia James racist, something she responded to while leaving court tonight.\par \par \uc2 LETITIA JAMES, NEW YORK ATTORNEY GENERAL: He rambled, he hurled insults but we expected that. At the end of the day, the documentary evidence demonstrated that in fact, he falsely inflated his assets to basically enrich himself and his family.\par \par \uc2 FOY (voice over): Trump testified his net worth and properties are more valuable than what was listed on his financial statements. He also provided disclaimers urging banks to do their own research. Trump attorney Alina Habba says the former president improved their case today.\par \par \uc2 ALINA HABBA, LEGAL SPOKESPERSON FOR \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : This is a guy that changed the New York skyline. You think you're going to question him and know what you're talking about? You don't and he wiped the floor with them, frankly.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 FOY (on camera): Bret, Habba tells me after \uc2 Ivanka Trump\uc2  testifies on Wednesday when the state rest its case that they will move for a mistrial. If that doesn't happen, Trump's lawyers will present their defense and she confirms that former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  will testify again, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Nate Foy outside the courthouse. Nate, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the latest on the presidential race, the new dismal polls as we talked about for President Biden and the legal troubles for former President Trump. Brit has analysis of it all.\par \par \uc2 First, here's what some of our Fox affiliates around the country are covering tonight. Fox 32 in Chicago as the father of the man charged in a deadly Fourth of July parade shooting in Highland Park pleads guilty to seven misdemeanors in a case that centered on how his son obtained a gun license.\par \par \uc2 Robert Crimo Jr. was charged with seven counts of reckless conduct, one for each person whose son is accused of killing in Highland Park on Independence Day last year.\par \par \uc2 Fox 5 in Atlanta with the arraignment of dozens of people arrested over their protest against the city's new public safety training center. The state calls the group anarchists. The activists say they are exercising their First Amendment rights.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at New London Connecticut from Earth Cam, one of the big stories there tonight from Fox 61. The college basketball season tips off tonight defending national champion \uc2 UConn\uc2  host Northern Arizona to begin its season. \uc2 UConn\uc2  begins the year ranked sixth in the nation. Its women's team is ranked second.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look outside the Beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The people of the country understand it. They see it, and they don't like it. They don't like it, because it's political warfare. But we will go along and we will hopefully be very well in every regard, we'll win the election.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Former President Trump, not happy outside the courthouse today about what happened inside the courtroom, in his civil case in Manhattan, as you heard from Nate Foy. But he should be happy about the polling over the weekend from \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  and Siena College. He is in the lead in key battleground states across the country.\par \par \uc2 Five out of six as you take a look at it here. And there's some big leads in Nevada, for example, 52-41. And there you see the breakdown is only trailing President Biden according to this poll in Wisconsin. But inside the poll, there are even more positive numbers for the former president.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in Fox News chief political analyst Brit Hume. Brit, good evening.\par \par \uc2 You saw this poll. It tracks with some other polls that we've seen. What do you make of it?\par \par \uc2 BRIT HUME, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, I think it's striking that poll. This is you -- if one of you pointed out earlier, it's only one poll, but there are others that are similar.\par \par \uc2 And the other thing is you've got to have to worry the president is it means that his arguments about Bidenomics and how great the economy is clearly aren't selling.\par \par \uc2 And now you have a situation this weekend in Washington. Where these tens and tens of thousands of pro-Palestinian protesters outside the White House that -- look, the Muslim vote has become a part of the Democratic coalition, depended upon to win elections.\par \par \uc2 And if that's leaking away from him because of the war in the Middle East right now, Israel versus \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , that's got to worry him too. And what worries, I think, may worry other people about that is that he will be tempted, I'm afraid, to weaken his position in support of Israel, which he's been pretty steady on, as have his people.\par \par \uc2 But to now, you see another talk about ceasefires and pauses, and the rest of it, which suggested that he may be giving ground on that. So, I think this is -- these current political circumstances worrisome not only to him, but to others within his party, who think, oh, my God, we may have a loser on our hands.\par \par \uc2 David Axelrod, who is \uc2 Obama\uc2 's political adviser was suggesting gently over the weekend that it was time for Joe to go. Remarkable.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: This is remarkable. And democratic circles, I mean, are they getting to five alarm fire here? I mean, is this -- is this that time?\par \par \uc2 HUME: Well, but if this keeps up for much longer, and it looks like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , who -- I see -- I think, for the longest time, Democrats thought, well, the reason we can stick with Biden and be safe -- and safely do so is that, that if Trump is the nominee, as he looks to be, Biden will beat him because he beat him before, and very few people in history have ever motivated a party the way Trump has motivated not just Republicans, whom he -- whom he certainly motivated, but Democrats.\par \par \uc2 He brings them out. And maybe that will, you know, as we get closer to the reality of this, and it looks like he is the nominee, and so, when maybe that sentiment will revive and help Biden.\par \par \uc2 But at the moment, you know, you got Trump winning in what five out of those six battleground states you've shown, that is really quite striking.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. Brit, last thing, you know, as we look at these legal cases, some are different than others. Some are obviously more perilous for the former president than others, just based on the fact patterns.\par \par \uc2 The one in Manhattan, he seems to have turned the corner politically on the audience he's talking to. What about what's happening in that courtroom?\par \par \uc2 HUME: Well, look he is in -- he is likely to get convicted. This judge seems been on convicting him, you've got this prosecutor, not the one who's trying the case. But the state attorney general, who has behaved in the most extraordinary way for an officer of the Court.\par \par \uc2 You know,