l decide House seat up for grabs, expected to be a democratic hold.\par \par \uc2 Also, there are five states holding referendums, statewide referendums. Voters here in Colorado will consider changes to property taxes, also how to spend a revenue surplus on tobacco sales.\par \par \uc2 Maine is actually doing a lot of things. They have votes on eight measures, including whether to establish a public power company. You've got Texas and New York. Couple of big measures there. Several spending initiatives.\par \par \uc2 And Ohio here has two big initiatives. One of them would legalize marijuana, the other deals with language about individual reproductive rights, adding that to its state's constitution.\par \par \uc2 It will give legal protections to anyone who helps a woman get an abortion. It also prevent the state from interfering or penalizing abortions in most cases. That could set the table. It could be something that you see in other places, what you may see other states try to do in 2024, including here in Virginia where Governor Glenn Youngkin is seeking to implement a ban on abortions after 15 weeks. He flipped the governorship, as you may remember there in 2021, defeating Democrat \uc2 Terry McAuliffe\uc2 . His big job now is to get control of all of the branches. Republicans hold a narrow control of the House of Delegates, 48 to 46. They are looking to flip the State Senate there. That would pave the way for Youngkin's abortion bill and also a number of other bills.\par \par \uc2 High profile Democrats have campaigned heavily against the bill. And these other issues, including taxes they could take on if Republicans control all of the commonwealth of Virginia.\par \par \uc2 We have team FOX coverage on the election landscape. Mark Meredith here in Washington looks at how abortion could play a big role in 2024. We start with that Virginia battleground. Senior national correspondent Rich Edson is in Fredericksburg tonight. Good evening, Rich.\par \par \uc2 RICH EDSON, FOX NEWS STATE DEPARTMENT CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret.\par \par \uc2 Republicans say they want Governor Glenn Youngkin to have an effective final two years in office. For them that means ending divided government in Virginia and winning House and Senate races tomorrow in competitive districts like this one.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 GOV. GLENN YOUNGKIN, (R) VIRGINIA: Hold the House, flip the Senate. Hold the House and flip the Senate.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Governor Glenn Youngkin is campaigning across Virginia, targeting a handful of close Senate and House districts throughout the commonwealth. All 140 legislature seats are up this Election Day, a test of whether Youngkin and Republicans can hold the House of delegates, flip the Senate, and implement their agenda.\par \par \uc2 These local races have attracted national attention. President Biden and Vice President Harris have endorsed nearly two dozen candidates. Youngkin welcomed them to the race.\par \par \uc2 YOUNGKIN: I also think back to 2021 where he endorsed my opponent in 2021, and that didn't work very well then, either, because all they talk about are fear. They don't about how to create jobs.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Former President \uc2 Obama\uc2  has recorded a robocall to boost local Democrats. This weekend Maryland Governor Wes Moore campaigned for Democrats in Virginia, highlighting abortion and Republicans plans to limit them after 15 weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 GOV. WES MOORE, (D) MARYLAND: I can tell you from the people that I have been speaking to, not just in the commonwealth but also beyond, putting together a random as to when a governor should be involved in that conversation that should be between a woman and her doctor.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Democrats have also campaigned on stricter gun laws. Republicans say they will cut taxes and environmental regulations, pass stiff penalties for fentanyl dealers, and are pitching voters are their record of parental notification in schools.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 EDSON (on camera): These are expensive elections, too. Youngkin's PAC spent more than $3 million on these legislative races. National and state Democrats have announced committing more than $6 million. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Rich, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow's elections might also give us an indication with whether abortion will, again, be a major issue, this time in 2024. Here is correspondent Mark Meredith.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 MARK MEREDITH, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes or no, that's the question Ohio voters will answer tomorrow when deciding whether abortion should be protected by the state's constitution.\par \par \uc2 PAIGE, PRO-CHOICE ADVOCATE: Quite honestly, it's not up to you or I or a politician. It's up to that family and that doctor to determine what's best for them.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: In Cincinnati, pro-choice activists canvassed door to door, urging voters to approve the measure which would allow for abortions up until the point a fetus is viable outside the womb, roughly 23 to 24 weeks. Pro-life advocates including Republican Governor \uc2 Mike DeWine\uc2  says the measure could allow the procedure to take place even later.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 MIKE DEWINE\uc2 , (R) OHIO: The viability question will be determined by the person performing the abortion at the \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  or whoever that is being done. So that's the person who is going to determine. There is no review of that.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: Both sides are investing heavily on turnout efforts, spending efforts of $60 million to sway the outcome even as the abortion fight extends beyond Ohio. Efforts are already underway in six states to get similar measures on the ballot. Tomorrow, voters in Mississippi and Kentucky will choose their next governor. Kentucky's incumbent, Democrat \uc2 Andy Beshear\uc2  says he is eager to see his state ease its strict abortion laws.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 ANDY BESHEAR\uc2 , (D) KENTUCKY: We have the most draconian, restrictive abortion law in the country.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: While his opponent, Republican Attorney General \uc2 Daniel Cameron\uc2 , says voters in the traditionally red state support restrictions.\par \par \uc2 DANIEL CAMERON\uc2 , (R) KENTUCKY GUBERNATORIAL NOMINEE: At the end of the day, \uc2 Andy Beshear\uc2  wants to see no limits on abortion, and then he wants you, the taxpayers, to pay for it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (on camera): The Mississippi governor's race is not expected to be defined by abortion. That's because both the Democrat, Brandon Presley, and the Republican incumbent \uc2 Tate Reeves\uc2  identify as pro-life. But this race still appears pretty close. And Democrats, Bret, they are campaigning heavily on trying to expand Medicaid. That's become a big factor in this race.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We will follow it all tomorrow. Mark, thanks.\par \par \uc2 We talk with the president and CEO of the \uc2 Minneapolis Federal Reserve Bank\uc2  about the U.S. economy, inflation next. And later, the panel on the acrimony inside the Democratic Party over the Israel-\uc2 Hamas\uc2  war and the political landscape.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Optimism over the pausing interest rates edged stocks higher today. The Dow gained 35, the S&P 500 added eight, the Nasdaq finished ahead 40.5.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Kids put to bed, questions asked ordinary and profound. Will there be enough money left over at the end of the month after we pay all our bills, just have a little bit of breathing room? We are building an economy from the middle out and the bottom up where no one is left behind.\par \par \uc2 JEROME POWELL\uc2 , CHAIRMAN, FEDERAL RESERVE: Inflation remains well above our longer run goal of 2 percent. A few months of good data are only the beginning of what it will take to build confidence that inflation is moving down sustainably toward our goal.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: As we take a look at inflation since President Biden got in office, you can see the spike there in June of 22, and then the last data in September of 23, and where things stand.\par \par \uc2 Let's take a closer look at the U.S. economy and inflation. \uc2 Neel Kashkari\uc2  is the president and CEO of the \uc2 Minneapolis Federal Reserve Bank\uc2 . Sir, thank you for coming back on SPECIAL REPORT.\par \par \uc2 NEEL KASHKARI, MINNEAPOLIS FEDERAL RESERVE BANK: Thanks for having me, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Where do you see it right now, layman's terms? We try to read the tea leaves every time the chairman talks. But where do you see it for somebody sitting on the couch?\par \par \uc2 KASHKARI: The economy has proven to be resilient even though we've raised interest rates a lot over the past couple years. That's good news. The economy continues to grow. And inflation has come down, but just as the chairman said, it's not come back down all the way to where we need it, back down to two percent where the American people are used to having inflation. So we are making progress. The job market remains strong. The unemployment rate is still quite low at 3.9 percent. So overall, the fundamentals are healthy, but we haven't completely solved the inflation problem. We still have more work ahead of us to get it done.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You know what we see in polls is that despite some good data in different sectors, as you mentioned, people are just feeling a pinch at home when they're sitting around the kitchen table and they are writing on the back of an envelope how they are going to make ends meet. And one of those things is car loans, for example. If you look at the percentage from two years ago to now, 3.88 to 7.69. Mortgage rates, you go two years ago to 3.09 to 7.76 now. And the average comparison of a payment from two years ago at $1,600, $1,700 bucks to now $2,700, it is significant. People feel it. And that's what you are looking at.\par \par \uc2 KASHKARI: No. You are absolutely right. People actually do feel it. And at the same time, one of the surprises has been on average American consumers continue to spend. They continue to go out and they are going to restaurants. They are going on airplanes. They continue to spend money, and that's powering the economy. So, you are absolutely right that people's payments have gone up quite a bit. That's really tough sticker shock for a lot of folks. But this is part of the way monetary policy works. This is how we raise interest rates to try to gently tap the brakes on the economy. We are making progress. Once we get inflation back down, then we would be in a position for interest rates to go back down. But we need to finish the job.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But what about, is there a serious debate -- I know there is every month, but inside about the harm that raising rates would do again? "\uc2 Bloomberg\uc2 " had this quote whether the Fed was looking to do it again, "Hiking the cycle looks done after U.S. jobs report shows cooling. A cooling U.S. job market gives the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  room to keep interest rates on hold in December and reinforces market values that the U.S. central bank is done with the most aggressive hiking campaign in four decades." I saw a "Reuters" report that quoted you kind of urging a little bit more caution there, pumping the brakes. Is that fair?\par \par \uc2 KASHKARI: I'm a little nervous declaring victory too soon. It's just like the chairman said in the quote that you showed. We've had three really good months of inflation data showing a lot progress, which is great news. Three months is not enough. We need to let the data keep coming to us to see if we have got the inflation genie back in the bottle so to speak. And so before we declare that we are absolutely done, we have solved the problem, let's get more data and see how the economy evolves.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And around the world, the U.S. is still the place to put money, right?\par \par \uc2 KASHKARI: Oh, there is no question. We are so far ahead of all other major economies in the world in terms of competitiveness. We're making more progress on inflation. We have stronger growth, much more innovation. I would not trade places with any other economy in the world by a mile.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: \uc2 Neel Kashkari\uc2 , we appreciate your time.\par \par \uc2 KASHKARI: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Authorities in Nashville tonight say they are aware of a purported leak of the manifesto left by the woman who killed six people at a Christian school in March. That manifesto was found in the car of shooter Audrey Elizabeth Hale. She killed three nine-year-olds and three adults. She was fatally shot by police. Authorities say it is too early to confirm the authenticity of all those documents, but we're going to follow this story and have a report on it tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel on the war in Israel and former President Trump on the stand and in the lead. Plus, a throwback that truly shaped the United States.\par \par \uc2 And as we head to break, Veterans' Day is Saturday. If you would like to honor the veterans in your life and help fight veteran homeless this holiday, then make camo your cause. You can donate or make a purchase by scanning the QR code on your screen or going to Honor.USVets.org/FOXForward. There -- right there on your screen. FOX pledges to double match eligible donations.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MARIE HARF, FORMER STATE DEPARTMENT SPOKESWOMAN: The incumbent is always weighed down in polls against a challenger. We have seen this time and time again. I think there is precedent that once an incumbent starts making the case, getting on the campaign trail, their numbers will go up.\par \par \uc2 DAVID AXELROD, FORMER OBAMA STRATEGIST: We had lousy polling numbers at this time in our campaign in 2011 when I was working for \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 . But the two things that are different are that \uc2 Obama\uc2  was 50 and not 81. And we didn't have \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  on the other side.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, there you go. We showed a brief look at those states, the "New York Times"-Siena poll with Brit Hume, and you saw \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , the former president, leading President Biden in five of the six, barely being defeated in this poll in Wisconsin. "Wall Street Journal" says this, "A five-alarm Biden reelection fire. Public opinion polls are a snapshot in time and results can change quickly in politics as events intrude. But the polls have been sending Democrats and President Biden the same election warning for months. So, perhaps, they will eventually start listening."\par \par \uc2 With that, let's bring in our panel. We will start there, Charles Hurt, opinion editor for "The Washington Times," Ben Domenech, editor at large for "The Spectator" and host of "The Ben Domenech Podcast" on FOX News Radio, and Susan Page, Washington Bureau Chief at "USA Today."\par \par \uc2 Susan, I don't think David Axelrod was ever a Biden fan or acolyte, but his words still carry weight in Democratic circles, and he was a little harsh this weekend.\par \par \uc2 SUSAN PAGE, WASHINGTON BUREAU CHIEF, "\uc2 USA TODAY\uc2 ": So he has got a history of saying things about Biden that are negative. He called him Mr. Magoo the last time around, not really a favorable cartoon impression. That said, he is a serious Democrat who wants Democrats to win, and he is saying out loud what a lot of Democrats are saying privately, which is they are very concerned about President Biden's age and what is going to happen in this campaign.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Charlie, when you look at those polls, the headlines, the reaction is really something. "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 ," "Democrats express deep anxiety." "Axios," "Trump set to romp Biden," and "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 ," "An ominous poll for Democrats." The coverage of the poll. It's one poll, but I mean it's still tracking some other polls as well.\par \par \uc2 CHARLES HURT, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Yes, absolutely tracking a lot of other polls. And I think it's important that a guy like David Axelrod does have a lot of sway within the party. But also the timing also comes out at a time where you have \uc2 Obama\uc2  also second-guessing Biden, really does suggest that there is real consternation within the party. And, of course, they are trying to hide behind this whole idea that incumbents are often down, and, of course, it is true that incumbents are never 80 years old.\par \par \uc2 But the other thing is that when you actually look at the issues that are underpinning all of this and you compare Trump versus Biden or generic Republican versus Biden, oh my gosh, it gets even more devastating for Democrats. And so I think they are right to be very concerned.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Inside "The New York Times"-Siena poll, 77 percent said that Biden is too old, 54 percent of them were Democrats, in his own party, say he is too old. Only 19 percent of Trump supporters say is he too old even though he is 77. And the other one, Ben, was the -- if the election were held today, the breakdown, black voters would vote for Trump, in this poll, 22 percent. That's higher than any Republican has gotten in recent years.\par \par \uc2 BEN DOMENECH, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: I think that kind of number should terrify Democrats at this stage. But this is at this stage. I think this is a poll that basically is setting off the klaxon saying there is a lot of work being done. You made that point about the age issue. Underlying a lot of this is how poorly \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  performs among younger voters, younger voters who have, I believe, a set of priorities 