ific, and there is no justification for it. And what is also true is that the occupation and what is happening to Palestinians is unbearable. All of us are complicit to some degree.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: What do you think about that, Charlie?\par \par \uc2 HURT: Speak for yourself, pal. It's kind of extraordinary, the timing of this, especially here to have him second-guessing like this at a moment where you actually have Democrats protesting around the White House, shouting "Genocide Joe." It's astonishing. I can't remember a time we have seen something quite like this.\par \par \uc2 But -- and I take it sort of as an admission that he has blood on his hands from what is going on in the Middle East, which I accept that. That's great. But, seriously, speak for yourself. When you give billions of dollars to Iran, and you try to hand the entire Middle East over to the mullahs of Iran, this is the kind of thing that happens. So, yes, I'm glad you admit that you have blood on your hands, but don't drag everybody else into this.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So I'm trying to get what the next step is for the Democratic Party. If they are really concerned and going to break glass and say Joe, you've got to get out of the race, who is coming in?\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: Well, I think there is a lot of speculation about a certain other \uc2 Obama\uc2 . And look, the fact that the former president is willing to say something like that at this particular moment I found to be particularly inappropriate. This is not where you are supposed to be sitting back professor evaluating whose responsibility --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That's how he has always done it.\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: That's always how he has approached things. and to echo, Charlie, who is this "us" you are talking about? I don't know these us people. What I do see is a lot of the same people who worked for you in this current administration, and the question really remains how much he is still running things on the foreign policy side as opposed to having \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  be in charge of that, which I think is a factor that could play into any kind of parachuting in some kind of Aaron Sorkin like fantasy that we might see play out.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Susan, it does seem like this is a titanic shift, a little bit. Whether you look at Muslim American vote for Democrats, whether you look at a traditionally strong Jewish backing for Democrats, if they get wobbly on what was moral clarity at the beginning of this, and somehow it's taken a different way, could potentially effect the politics.\par \par \uc2 PAGE: The Democratic Party has been torn in two by what is happening now in Israel. And you see these -- and \uc2 Obama\uc2  is touching that wound, you know, which I thought was quite unhelpful to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . It's going to require enormous skill for President Biden to satisfy both those who are supportive of Israel in the Democratic Party and those voters -- and this is especially true of younger voters who feel a lot of sympathy for the Palestinians.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Finally, the former president takes the stand. Some fireworks there. What do you think of that, Charlie?\par \par \uc2 HURT: It's amazing. And it's a great example of where this poll reflects that you have a lot of Democrats and a lot of elected officials focusing on things that don't matter to voters, while on the issues they are going Trump's way or Republicans' way.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But there are other things that may be more dangerous down the road legally.\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: I absolutely think there is a lot of risks in here that Republicans have to be concerned about, they have to be aware of. And frankly, the number one takeaway I would have as a news network is I wish we had the audio. I wish we had the video to break it all down.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It's quite something, a lot to cover. Panel, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now for today's throwback, and it's a big throwback. On this day 163 years ago \uc2 Abraham Lincoln\uc2  of Springfield, Illinois, was elected president of the United States. Lincoln was the candidate of the newly created Republican Party, which officially wanted to limit the expansion of slavery. Lincoln, of course, went on to be one of the most celebrated American presidents of all time, famed for signing the Emancipation Proclamation in 1863.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT, our "Common Ground" segment features Democratic Congressman Ritchie Torres and Republican Congressman \uc2 Mike Gallagher\uc2  on social media and its impact on sentiment about the Israel-\uc2 Hamas\uc2  war as well as other things.\par \par \uc2 Remember, if you can't catch us live, set your DVR, 6:00 p.m. on the east, 3:00 p.m. in the west.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for this SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and still unafraid. Here's Laura.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020231107ejb600001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Israel's Ground Assault Into Gaza Continues; Interview with Speaker of the House Mike Johnson (R-LA); ; Secretary Blinken in Middle East as U.S. Attempts to Prevent a Broader War; \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  Leader Threatens to Expand Fight Against Israel; Senior \uc2 Hamas\uc2  Official Claims October 7 Attack on Israel Just the Beginning; Ohio Voters to Decide Whether Abortion Rights Should Be Added to State Constitution\b0\par\par\uc2 Shannon Bream, Trey Yingst, Alexandria Hoff, Rich Edson, Guy  Benson, Juan Williams \par \uc2 9,735 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 November 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Fox News Sunday\par \uc2 FOXN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Secretary of State Blinken makes a surprise trip to the West Bank today, as he tries to stop the conflict from widening through the region. Meanwhile, here in Washington, a major roadblock on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice-over): The future of the House-approved standalone aid package for Israel in limbo.\par \par \uc2 SEN. BEN CARDIN (D-MD): The House bill is dead on arrival in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: A big win for the new speaker of the House could be short-lived as critical funding for Israel's fight against \uc2 Hamas\uc2  and Ukraine against Russia gets caught up in political wrangling.\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE JOHNSON (R-LA), SPEAKER OF THE HOUSE: We want to pair border security with Ukraine because I think we can get bipartisan agreement on both of those matters.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Speaker of the House Mike Johnson will join us exclusively to talk about his first full week on the job and his plan to avoid a government shutdown just days from now.\par \par \uc2 And --\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN\uc2 , SECRETARY OF STATE: We provided Israel advice that only the best of friends can offer on how to minimize civilian deaths, while still achieving its objectives of finding and finish \uc2 Hamas\uc2  terrorists.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Secretary Blinken back in the Middle East, meeting with \uc2 Palestinian Authority\uc2  President \uc2 Mahmoud Abbas\uc2  in the West Bank.\par \par \uc2 While back home, thousands of pro-Palestinian demonstrators turned out on the streets of America, calling for a ceasefire.\par \par \uc2 Democratic Chairman of the \uc2 Senate Armed Services Committee\uc2 , \uc2 Jack Reed\uc2 , joins us, only on "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 Then, Election Day just two days away in key bellwether states for next year's presidential election, including battleground Ohio. Abortion is on the ballot and whether the right to have one should be added to the state's constitution.\par \par \uc2 Ohio Governor \uc2 Mike DeWine\uc2  joins us exclusively.\par \par \uc2 Plus, Republicans look to win a trifecta government in Virginia. We'll look at what a big win could mean for Governor Youngkin's political future and ask our Sunday panel about who needs to have the biggest night in this week's third GOP presidential debate.\par \par \uc2 All, right now, on "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM (on camera): And hello from FOX News in Washington.\par \par \uc2 Israel's ground assault into Gaza continues, as Israeli troops battle \uc2 Hamas\uc2  militants in the aftermath of the October 7th attacks on Israel.\par \par \uc2 Back Stateside, tens of thousands of pro-Palestinian protesters flooded the street of the nation's capital, demanding not only a ceasefire in the conflict, but also an end to any U.S. military aid to Israel.\par \par \uc2 It all comes as a tense time for Congress. There's a brand new speaker of the House looking to make his mark, decoupling Israel and Ukraine aid, a move that appears to face a certain death in the Senate. And yet another government shutdown is looming.\par \par \uc2 In a moment, we will speak exclusively with the speaker of the House, Mike Johnson.\par \par \uc2 But, first, we turn to FOX News foreign correspondent Trey Yingst on the ground at the Israel-Gaza border with the very latest.\par \par \uc2 And I understand, Trey, you may some incoming fire there.\par \par \uc2 TREY YINGST, FOX NEWS FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: Yeah, Shannon, you don't see me on camera right now, we just had sirens sounding and in this location, we've got about 10 seconds to get to cover. This area has taken a number of direct hits over the past several days.\par \par \uc2 But this comes as the fighting rages on inside Gaza nearly a month into the war between Israel and \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . We got a first hand look at the battle unfolding inside the Gaza Strip.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 YINGST (voice-over): Israeli bulldozers flanked by tanks and machine guns push deep into Gaza, nearly a month into the war and more than a week into the ground invasion. These troops must fight for each block of territory.\par \par \uc2 For the first time since the conflict erupted, the \uc2 Israel Defense Forces\uc2  are taking a small group of journalists into the fight.\par \par \uc2 Right now, we're with the Israeli military in an armored personnel carrier. The Israelis have cut the Gaza Strip in half. They fought their way to the Mediterranean Sea and encircled Gaza City. They're waiting now for the orders to go further in.\par \par \uc2 Seconds after arriving on outskirts of Gaza city, the crack of gunfire pierces the air.\par \par \uc2 IDF SOLDIER: So, right now, we are in --\par \par \uc2 YINGST: Israeli troops move carefully over mounds of dirt and debris. They understand \uc2 Hamas\uc2  can target from above and below ground.\par \par \uc2 IDF SOLDIER: We find two tunnels and now, we're going to destroy them.\par \par \uc2 YINGST: While Israeli troops focus efforts on destroying the tunnel network of Hamas Palestinian militants are still able to surprise and ambush them.\par \par \uc2 Right now, the Israelis are engaged in a gun battle with \uc2 Hamas\uc2  militants inside the Gaza Strip, they are working at this hour to go block by block and clear this area, trying to get to the Mediterranean Sea, effectively cutting the northern part of Gaza from the southern part.\par \par \uc2 As this is taking place, they've been ambushed. They've lost more than two dozen of their own soldiers and the battle continues.\par \par \uc2 Eight days ago the Israelis invaded with two missions, to hunt down \uc2 Hamas\uc2  leadership and bring hostages home. The latter is proving to be far more difficult.\par \par \uc2 LT. COL. GILAD PASTERNAK, \uc2 ISRAEL DEFENSE FORCES\uc2 : We know that the hostages, most of them, inside Gaza City, not inside the suburb area, but inside the tunnels underneath the city. We are doing everything we can in order to free them.\par \par \uc2 YINGST: Lieutenant Colonel Gilad Pasternak is fighting his third war. The 38-year-old is in charge of five battalions.\par \par \uc2 His soldiers include those like Jeremiah Wallace who's originally from Philadelphia. He moved to Israel to join the army. Now, he points to why the war began.\par \par \uc2 JEREMIAH WALLACE, ISRAELI SOLDIER: We are here. We're coming after terrorists. We're coming after people that came into Israel that killed civilians, killed men, women and children, kidnapped them, as well. And we're coming -- we're coming back for them and we won't leave until -- until that's taken care of.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 YINGST (on camera): Something that struck me was the level of destruction inside Gaza. Currently, there are more than a million Palestinian civilians internally displaced, many of them have no homes to return to -- Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Trey Yingst, thank you to you and your team for taking us places no one else is going. We so appreciate it. Thank you, Trey.\par \par \uc2 FOX News' Alexandria Hoff is traveling with the president. She joins us now from Rehoboth Beach, Delaware, where the president continues to be briefed.\par \par \uc2 Hello, Alex.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDRIA HOFF, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hello, Shannon, and good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 Yeah, aside from briefing from Secretary of State Blinken and National Security Advisor Jake Sullivan, all has been quiet here in Delaware. The president 100 miles from the White House where last night, pro-Palestinian protesters, they vandalized the fence and attempted to climb a wrought-iron gate. We are told no arrests were made.\par \par \uc2 The demonstrators are voicing anger for president's support of Israel and refusal to back a ceasefire. Instead, the administration has been pushing for a humanitarian pause in military operations ion Gaza to get aid in and hostages out. The president was asked about that yesterday.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Mr. President, any progress made on the humanitarian pause?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HOFF: The president gave a yes there, thumbs up. The idea was seemingly, though, rejected by Prime Minister Netanyahu when Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  visited Tel Aviv on Friday. Israeli officials want \uc2 Hamas\uc2  to release these some-240 hostages first and Blinken dismissed calls this weekend by Arab leaders for the U.S. to back an immediate ceasefire.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SAMEH SHOUKRY, EGYPT FOREIGN MINISTER (through translator): We are still asking for an immediate ceasefire and that Israel would stop hindering the delivery of humanitarian aid.\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN\uc2 , SECRETARY OF STATE: It's our view that ceasefire now would simply leave \uc2 Hamas\uc2  in place able to regroup and repeat what it did on October 7.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HOFF: A ceasefire was also likely urged by \uc2 Palestinian Authority\uc2  President \uc2 Mahmoud Abbas\uc2  when Blinken made an unannounced stop in the West Bank today.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the USS Eisenhower aircraft carrier strike group has now made it to the Red Sea, joining a massive naval power intended to send a message to \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  and Iran that the U.S. is prepared to strike if any U.S. interests are harmed.\par \par \uc2 And on Capitol Hill, another message for new House Speaker Mike Johnson after House approved more than $14 billion in aid for Israel in a standalone funded by cuts to the \uc2 IRS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, NSC STRATEGIC COMMUNICATIONS COORDINATOR: The president would veto an only-Israel bill. I think that we've made that clear.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HOFF: Now, the House did reject a more broad $106 billion aid package that was requested by the president that include aid for Ukraine, as well, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Alexandria Hoff with the president in Delaware -- Alex, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is the new speaker of the House, Mike Johnson.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE JOHNSON (R-LA), SPEAKER OF THE HOUSE: Hey, Shannon. Great to be with you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. I want to play a little bit more of what happened here in Washington yesterday. Estimates are this pro-Palestine rally was up to 100,000 people. We've got images of it and of what transpired last night when some of them took to the White House defacing one of the gates there, climbing on the gates.\par \par \uc2 Are you surprised at all by the amount that we've seen of this kind of response -- and I have -- to be fair, a lot of people I saw in that rally yesterday were very much worried about civilian lives that are being lost in Gaza. But there were others who were saying things that were clearly indicative of making sure that Israel no longer exists on the world map.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Yes, no. We -- it is surprising to see this level of antisemitism that has sprung up around the country. Apparently, it's been dormant for a while, but this has given rise to that. And we're deeply concerned about it.\par \par \uc2 The opening to the program today, Shannon, I think gave a great snapshot of the crisis that Israel faces. And that's why, right off the block, as soon as I became speaker, within the hour, we passed a resolution to make clear that Congress stands by our great friend in that region, we always will. And then we passed, as you saw, our first priority is an emergency immediate funding aid to Israel so they can defeat \uc2 Hamas\uc2  and protect and continue as a nation. That's what's at stake.\par \par \uc2 I spoke to Prime Minister Netanyahu on Saturday evening a week ago and he said this is a battle between good versus evil, light versus darkness. I could not agree and the world can see that.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, you passed a standalone measure, but the man who runs the Senate, Senate Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  has this to say about what you passed in the House.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY), MAJORITY LEADER: The proposal is simply not a serious one, and worse, it still wastes precious time at a moment when we need to help Israel, Ukraine and send humanitarian aid to Gaza ASAP.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And it's not just Democrats. This is the headline from "The Hill", "McConnell and Speaker Johnson sharply divided on the year-end strategy."\par \par \uc2 So, Democrat and Republican leaders over in the Senate say there's no way the stand-lone measure gets anywhere. The White House has said it would veto it anyway.\par \par \uc2 So, with time of the essence, the urgency here, why waste time on a measure that has almost zero chance of actually aiding the Israeli people?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Shannon, it's really surprising to hear Senator Schumer say that it's not a serious proposal. It's actually is what was requested, $14.5 billion.\par \par \uc2 What they don't like is that in the House, we're trying to be good stewards of the taxpayers' resources. We offset that spending. Instead of printing new dollars and/or borrowing it from another nation to send over to fulfill our obligations and help our ally, we want to pay for it. What a concept, and we're trying to change how Washington works.\par \par \uc2 And so, by taking that money from this giant fund, over $65 billion that's sitting there to build up the \uc2 IRS\uc2 , we weighed those priorities and said, you know what? It's more important to protect Israel right now than it is to hire more \uc2 IRS\uc2  agents.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well --\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Apparently, Senator Schumer disagrees with that. But I'll take that debate to the American people all day long.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, he's pointing, as others are, to the \uc2 Congressional Budget Office\uc2 , a nonpartisan group that scores these things. And they said actually, if you take that funding from the \uc2 IRS\uc2 , it's going to add billions to the deficit because you cut \uc2 IRS\uc2  personnel. They are not then collecting that revenue that they bring in.\par \par \uc2 One of your House colleagues, Democrat \uc2 Brendan Boyle\uc2 , put it this way. He says you are prioritizing, quote, deficit-busting tax giveaways for the wealthy over helping Israel.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Look, only in Washington can you cut funding, add a pay-for to a new spending measure, and they say it's terrible for the deficit.\par \par \uc2 Listen, we're taking care of our priorities and we will. We know that these other important measures that right there on the table and we're working through it hour by hour, day-by-day and we're going to meet those obligations. But we have to do things in the proper order and we are committed to changing how Washington works.\par \par \uc2 I think you see a united and energized House conference. All of our members are working together in good faith trying to solve these great challenges that we face, and I'm very optimistic that we're going to do that. But we are going to also change the trajectory we're on.\par \par \uc2 Shannon, right now, we have $33.6 trillion federal debt. Just last week, the Treasury Department and the Biden administration announced that we're going to have to borrow over $1.5 trillion over the next two quarters, six months, to continue our operation as a government.\par \par \uc2 This is not a sustainable track. We can take care of our obligations and we can do it in a responsible manner and that's what we're committed to.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. So, there's been talk that you want to quickly offer up Ukraine aid, of course, in a separate measure, potentially tying the U.S. border and security issues to that.\par \par \uc2 A group of Democrats say this and there's opposition from both sides on that plan, as well. But they say: Republicans can't move their extreme, cruel anti-immigrant agenda through the regular legislative process. So, they're trying to make an end-run around Congress and exploit two foreign wars to force it into law.\par \par \uc2 They say Republicans don't actually want to work on things that would change the problems at the border, beefing up port entry, personnel, things like more legal pathways to citizenship and getting at a root crosses -- causes of migration.\par \par \uc2 So, are there things that you can work on, and you can get bipartisan buy- in in attaching that to Ukraine aid, or is this going to be about holding your caucus together and getting all the Republican votes where you have very small majority?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: What this is about is advancing the agenda and first priority is the American people. I don't know which Democrat gave you that absurd quote, but they're clearly not listening to their constituents.\par \par \uc2 If you go into the country, people will say, look, we understand our role as a leader in the free world, we understand that we're the great superpower that needs to assist and ensure that freedom survives. But we have to take care of our own house first, and securing our border is an essential priority to American people.\par \par \uc2 So they're not listening to their constituents, I think that's a tone-deaf response. Again, we can do all of these things together. But when -- when you couple Ukraine and the border, that makes sense to people because they say, if we're going to protect Ukraine's border, and we have to do what is necessary there, we don't want \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  to prevail. We can't afford that.\par \par \uc2 The free world can't afford that. But we have to take care of our own border first. And that's what we're saying. This is policy changes that are necessary. There's a growing consensus in the Congress, certainly amongst Republicans, but also even some across the aisle who recognize we have to change what is happening over 6.3 million illegal crossings since \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  took office. It's more than the population of my state. We cannot continue this and everyone knows it and the fentanyl that's come over the border of human trafficking, the cartels making billions of dollars on our backs. We're going to stop that. And the House Republicans are committed to it. I think that people are with us, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So what as we talked about spending more dollars on those things, our border, Ukraine, more tax dollars flowing into those things. We're running out of money here again, in a couple of weeks. Are we going to have another continuing resolution that temporary financial band-aid, how long would it last? Would it be clean? Are you going to try to attach other things to it?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: The reason I look a little haggard this morning, Shannon, is because it was up late last night. We worked through the weekend. On a stopgap measure, we recognize that we may not get all the appropriations bills done by this deadline of November 17. But we're going to continue in good faith.\par \par \uc2 And the difference between what we call on Capitol Hill and continuing resolution now and what we've dealt with in years past is that this would allow us time and everybody understands, allow us time to continue this appropriations process. We're committed to bringing 12 bills to the floor as the law -- statutory law requires Congress to do that hasn't been done in many years. But again, we're changing the way Washington works because we believe it needs to be more accountable and more transparent for the people. And so we're going to fight that fight every single day. And we'll get that job done.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, I want to talk about some social issues. You got a lot of critics who say that you're wildly out of step with the American people. Let's talk abortion first. One of the groups opposing you says this. He wants a total abortion ban with no exceptions. He supported bans that would not only criminalize abortion, but ban IVF treatments, and common forms of birth control. And that you voted against access to contraception. Where are you on these issues? Is that an accurate assessment of where you are? Because that's not in step with the American people?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: No. Shannon, look, I'm pro-life. I've said very clearly, I'm a Bible-believing Christian. I believe in the sanctity of every single human life. So I come to Congress with deep personally held convictions. But guess what, sort of, my 434 other colleagues in the House, everyone comes to Congress with their deeply held convictions. But the process here is that you make law by consensus. And I've not brought forward any measure to address any of those issues.\par \par \uc2 Right now, our priorities are funding the government, handling these -- these massive national security priorities that we have in crises around the globe, and taking care of changing and reforming how Congress works. That's what we're going to do.\par \par \uc2 Listen, I -- prior to the modern time, I mean, until recently, actually, almost all of our nation's leaders openly acknowledged that they were also Bible-believing Christians. So I mean, this is not something that should cause great unrest. OK, it's just that Washington right now, what you're seeing, Washington and the Associated Press Corps are engaging with a leader who openly acknowledges faith and in the foundational principles of our country.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, but --\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: I think this is a healthy discussion, but it doesn't affect how we run Congress.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: To be clear, though, have you voted against fertility treatments and access to contraception? Would you?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: I don't -- I don't think so. I'm not sure what they're talking about. I really don't remember any of those measures.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But do you oppose anything --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: I am personally pro-life, yeah. No, no, of course not. No, that -- that's something that's blessed a lot of families who have problems with fertility, of course, that's a great thing. I would support that.\par \par \uc2 But, look, again, these are not issues that are on the front of the agenda, and we can come with our convictions and we can govern in an accountable transparent manner for the American people. And that's what -- that's what we're going to do.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. So you've talked a lot about your faith. You don't backtrack, you're not shy about that. You're bold about that. You said, if people want to know your worldview, go open up the Bible. You know, that concerns a lot of people, your critics are harkening back to things that you've said about gay marriage, homosexuality, conversion therapy. There's a lengthy guest essay in the \uc2 New York Times\uc2 , not surprisingly, not big fans of yours. It's titled this, "The Embodiment of White Christian Nationalism in a Tailored Suit."\par \par \uc2 They point to a definition in Christianity today of that saying it's a belief that the American nation is defined by Christianity, and that the government should take active steps to keep it that way. Is that an accurate description of your view of how the government should function?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: No, I'm not even sure what the term means. And look, there are entire industries built on taking down, tearing down people like me. I understand that comes with a territory and we're not fazed by it. But listen, what -- what I believe in, are the founding principles of the country, individual freedom, limited government, the rule of law, peace through strength, fiscal responsibility, free markets, human dignity. Those are essential American principles.\par \par \uc2 And so I've been labeled all kinds of stuff, but these people don't know me. Look, my family, it's no fun to be misquoted and maligned and -- and mocked, of course, but we know that comes with a job, and we're unfazed. We're going to continue to love all people. We're going to continue to bless, even those who persecute us. Because that's -- that's our worldview, and that's how we operate. And if people just -- everybody take a breath, give us a chance, and you'll see what principled governance looks like. That's what I'm committed to do.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. We got to go, but I have to ask you this, because there's been so much made about it. Vanity Fair says this, "What's up with Mike Johnson's very shady seeming financial disclosures?" They say, you've never reported a bank account or an assets on a financial disclosure form going back to 2016. Can you clear that up for us?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Yes, look, I'm a man of modest means. OK. I was a lawyer, but I did constitutional law. And most of my career has been in the nonprofit sector. We have four kids, five now that are very active. And I have kids in graduate school, law school, undergraduate. We have a lot of expenses. But I can relate to everybody else. My father was a firefighter, right? I didn't grow up with great means. But I think that helps us be a better leader because we can relate to every hard work in American family. That's who we are. And I think it governs and helps govern my decisions and how I lead.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, majority of Americans now say they're living paycheck to paycheck, so a lot of folks in the same boat as they try to figure out this economy. Speaker, we're wishing you the best in trying to keep the House running and the country functioning. Thank you, sir.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Thanks, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Congress clashes over how to fund urgent aid for Israel and Ukraine. You heard what the Speaker said, we're going to bring in the Chairman of the \uc2 Senate Armed Services Committee\uc2 , Democrat \uc2 Jack Reed\uc2 . He's just been in that region torn apart by war. Does he stand with Democrats insisting all of this aid be tied together? Or is he open to whatever gets aid to Israel most quickly? We'll ask him next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Following his surprise visit to the West Bank this morning, Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  is traveling to Turkey as part of his Middle East trip, hoping to keep the Israel-\uc2 Hamas\uc2  war from spreading. Blinken has also made stops in Israel and Jordan.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, someone who has also just been in the region, Rhode Island Democrat \uc2 Jack Reed\uc2 . He is the Chairman of the \uc2 Senate Armed Services Committee\uc2 . Chairman, welcome back to Fox News Sunday.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JACK REED (D-RI): Thank you very much, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, so Secretary Blinken has just been with \uc2 Mahmoud Abbas\uc2 . And we have this readout from the administration. It says, the secretary expressed the commitment of the U.S. towards working toward the realization of the Palestinians' legitimate aspirations for the establishment of a Palestinian state.\par \par \uc2 Do you think though, that that's something that \uc2 Hamas\uc2  would allow? Or even if that solution was worked out, would they continue? Do you believe to be about the extermination of Israel as a nation?\par \par \uc2 REED: Well, \uc2 Hamas\uc2  is committed to the extermination of Israel. That's why Israel is engaged in attacking, degrading, and ultimately defeating \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . What we have to do is not speak to \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , they are intolerant. We have to speak to the Palestinian people. We have to give them hope. We have to give them an aspiration so that they will break with \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . And they will begin to support, we hope, a stable and peaceful region in that whole area.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So Secretary Blinken now continues on to Turkey. We talked last week about the fact that they're a \uc2 NATO\uc2  ally, but Erdogan as they're talking about Israel as an occupier, as committing war crimes. What should the Secretary's message be as he visits Turkey?\par \par \uc2 REED: I think the secretary's message is, first, we cannot let this conflict expand to other areas, and Turkey can play a key role in containing this conflict. Second, I think he can explain how we are trying to advise, assist the Israeli forces so that they, one, are much more strategic and much more precise in their targeting.\par \par \uc2 And, two, that they recognize that helping the Palestinian people is not just a good thing to do, it is a smart tactical move, because what you want to do is separate \uc2 Hamas\uc2  and the Palestinian people. And you do that by making it appear, in fact, make it a reality that they can find some support, medical support, humanitarian support, within the Israel lines, and they can't find that within \uc2 Hamas\uc2 ' controlled territory.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So there's also \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  out there, their leader speaking out Friday saying that the U.S. is responsible in part for what's going on in Gaza, that we must be the ones to stop it, that they would overwhelm U.S. forces. They're not afraid of our warships, those kinds of things. How worried are you about a couple of things \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  opening up a separate front to the north, and about U.S. troops at any point being dragged into this conflict?\par \par \uc2 REED: Well, first, the speech by the leader of \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  was not unexpected. Second, we've moved in carrier task force groups to augment our deterrence efforts in the region. And I think they're very conscious of that, particularly our anti-missile capabilities, which are significant and can prevent an attack by \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  on -- on Israel and open up another front.\par \par \uc2 I think also too is that we are trying, as I said to help -- help Israel focus and refocus his efforts to be more precise in his targeting, and to give less of a rationale for some people who would attack Israel on political grounds.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So you are among those who are publicly saying we do want Israel to be more precise, Secretary Blinken says we've given them advice that a close friend would give them. But keep in mind, this is what we're hearing from a leader of \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , who talked about the fact that the October 7 attacks were just the beginning, he added this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GHAZI HAMAD, \uc2 HAMAS\uc2  OFFICIAL (through translator): We must teach Israel a lesson, and we will do this again and again. The Al-Aqsa Flood is just the first time, and there will be a second, a third, a fourth because we have the determination, the resolve, and the capabilities to fight.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: He went on to say that that means the annihilation of Israel. So should any country be lecturing Israel in any way about how they deal with a group that is truly an existential threat to them?\par \par \uc2 REED: \uc2 Hamas\uc2  is an existential threat to Israel. They make no bones about it, as that statement indicates very clearly. And what Israel is doing appropriately so is targeting \uc2 Hamas\uc2  to degrade it and then destroy it. And we are helping them in that effort. It is somewhat reminiscent of our efforts against ISIS in Iraq.\par \par \uc2 But what they have to do, not only for the complying with the rule of law, but also winning the battle of minds and hearts, is to do it in such a way as that they don't -- or they minimize the harm to civilians. And that's what we're trying to help them with. We want them to get better intelligence. We're helping them with intelligence. We want that intelligence to inform selected targets, precise targets, want them to use precision-guided weapons systems with smaller diameter bombs. So they really hit the people they're going after.\par \par \uc2 And then we want them also to be able to show how not just Israel but the entire world community wants to provide basic necessities to Palestinian people. This would deliver a blow, not just tactically, but also politically to \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . And the goal is to end that threat to Israel, which is existential, \uc2 Hamas\uc2  does wonders for the State of Israel.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Let's talk about aid because you heard the speaker at the beginning of the show talking about Israel, standalone aid was what he could get passed in the House. That was what the appetite was there. We know it's different over in the Senate. And there's talk of again, linking it together with Ukrainian aid.\par \par \uc2 But it was interesting, the Gallup Poll shows now that Americans are growing skeptical of providing unlimited continued aid to Ukraine. The number of Americans who say the U.S. is doing too much in that particular conflict, has jumped 12 points since just June. Do you worry that tagging those things together may slow down as Ukraine is less popular? The aid actually getting to Israel in a more timely fashion?\par \par \uc2 REED: No, I don't. I think on the Senate side, there's strong bipartisan support for aid not only to Israel, but Ukraine. And immediately, Ukraine is running down its resources. They're in the midst of a very serious conflict with the Russians. The Russians may have staged a major counterattack weeks ago. And they're tense casualties on both sides, particularly the Russian side.\par \par \uc2 So we can't just sit back and let time go by, and we have to support the Ukrainians. And it's in our interest. And frankly, and when I was talking to Secretary Austin at the hearing on Appropriations, he indicated, which I believe is, listen, if we don't provide these resources to Ukraine right now, it raises the probability that in the months or years ahead, we'll have to send American forces.\par \par \uc2 It's better in my view, to support the Ukrainian people now and their forces than to see a situation where we are pulled into a conflict with our young men and women. So this is a, not only the right thing to do, it's a, I think, a very, very smart thing to do.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, we will watch how that plays out on Capitol Hill. Senator, we thank you for your time in uniform --\par \par \uc2 REED: Thank you, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: -- defending and serving this country and for being with us today. Sir, thank you.\par \par \uc2 REED: Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Up next, abortion is on the ballot this week. Ohio voters go into the polls, Tuesday, to decide whether to amend the state's constitution to provide more abortion access in the wake of the Supreme Court decision to overturn Roe. Governor \uc2 Mike DeWine\uc2  joins us, live.\par \par \uc2 But first, Fox News' Rich Edson takes us to Virginia where Governor Glenn Youngkin is working overtime on state elections there this week. And President Obama getting involved too.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Election Day is two days away for parts of the country. All eyes are on four states, watching for clues about what this week could tell us about how voters are feeling about 2024. Key gubernatorial races are taking place in Kentucky and Mississippi. Kentucky's election featuring an opportunity for Republicans to try to flip the Governor's seat read, while Mississippi's GOP Governor \uc2 Tate Reeves\uc2  is hoping to win his second term in a race that is tighter than expected.\par \par \uc2 In Ohio, voters will decide on the state's abortion and marijuana laws. In a moment we will bring in Ohio Governor \uc2 Mike DeWine\uc2  to talk about the stakes there. But first, Virginia certainly one of the states to watch and could be a preview of what's to come next year. Fox News. Rich Edson is live in Richmond with the very latest. Hey Rich?\par \par \uc2 RICH EDSON, FOX NEWS SENIOR NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Shannon. Well, there's been persistent speculation surrounding Governor Glenn Youngkin's presidential ambitions. For now, he's focused on elections here in Virginia, and a test of whether he can deliver his party, the power to set the state agenda.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GLENN YOUNGKIN, (R) VIRGINIA GOVERNOR: What time is it? Time to win.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Governor Glenn Youngkin is campaigning across Virginia, trying to flip the Senate, hold on to the House of Delegates and deliver Republicans control of the state government.\par \par \uc2 YOUNGKIN: These races are incredibly tight, and we need to get everybody out to vote. And that's why we've been working so hard on early voting\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Every Senate and House seat is up this election. And there are only a handful of competitive races. Republicans are promising to build on their agenda. They say to give parents a greater say in their child's education. They also want to pass limits on abortions beyond 15 weeks. And this race is attracting attention from outside the state.\par \par \uc2 WES MOORE, (D) MARYLAND GOVERNOR: I know what it feels like when you're on the cusp of a big victory. And that's exactly what I'm feeling.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Halfway across the country, Kentucky Governor \uc2 Andy Beshear\uc2  is campaigning for his own election.\par \par \uc2 ANDY BESHEAR\uc2  (D), KENTUCKY GOVERNOR: In every corner of our Commonwealth, what I see is hope and optimism.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Beshear is one of the few Democratic governors in state former President Trump won by more than 25 percentage points in 2020.\par \par \uc2 DANIEL CAMERON\uc2 , (R) CANDIDATE FOR KENTUCKY GOVERNOR: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  endorsed this campaign for governor and he feels very strongly about my ability to lead this state.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Ohio is also voting on whether to legalize recreational marijuana. If so it'll become the 24th state to do so. Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. We're tracking it. Rich, reporting in Virginia for us. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, Ohio Governor \uc2 Mike DeWine\uc2 . Governor, welcome to Fox News Sunday.\par \par \uc2 MIKE DEWINE\uc2 , (R) OHIO GOVERNOR: Good morning. Good to be with you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, so let's talk about this abortion issue. Issue one, on the ballots there. Democrats say that it proves that the right is as extreme as they've warned everyone that they are. Here's one supporter saying this. What Republicans frankly have done in this environment is they've created a window for advocates on the left to date say this. See, look, this is what we've been talking about. Our greatest fears or nightmares, our coming true. And this is our time to stand up and fight back.\par \par \uc2 Abortion measures generally have not done well in the post office decision after the Supreme Court's decision to overturn Roe. Are you worried about the backlash on Republicans next week in your state?\par \par \uc2 DEWINE: No, if you look at issue one, it's a radical proposal and whether you're pro-choice or pro-life, it just goes much, much too far. It would enshrine in our Constitution the right to have an abortion up Until birth, so at any time during the pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 The second thing it would do is really threaten a law that we've had on the books for many years, requires parental consent, if we're dealing with a minor. So the lawyers who wrote this vote were very mindful for what they were doing. It is a radical proposal. It does not fit Ohio. And that's what's going to be voted on in two days.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK --\par \par \uc2 DEWINE: So -- yep, sure.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I was going to say, so let's read some of the language of this measure. It says that the patient's treating physician, the pregnant woman, they have the ability to determine if the fetus has a significant likelihood of survival outside the uterus, with reasonable measures.\par \par \uc2 The AP puts it this way, it says, Ohio lawmakers could still restrict abortion beyond the point where a fetus can survive outside the womb. And with modern medicine, referred to the point of as viability is typically about 23 or 24 weeks into pregnancy. There's wide support across the country for that. There is still the issue of viability. They -- you know, critics will argue that what you're saying is, it's not true that it's up until the moment of birth, your response?\par \par \uc2 DEWINE: Now, that's just absolutely not true. Constitutional scholars who have looked at that certainly have a very different opinion. First of all, the viability question will be determined by the person performing the abortion at the \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  or wherever that has been done. So that's the person who's going to determine it. And there's no review of that.\par \par \uc2 Second, there is a wide, wide exception written into this law, which talks about the health of the mother. The \uc2 Supreme Court of the United States\uc2  is defined this extremely broadly it can be health, can mean something having to do with her income, it can have something to do with, about children she has, and again, it is the person performing the abortion in that clinic, who's going to make that determination, and there's no review of it. So as a practical matter, they were very mindful, the lawyers very mindful what they were, language they were using. This allows abortion at any point in the pregnancy. And that is just much, much too far.\par \par \uc2 We've had on the books, for example, in Ohio, a law that deals with partial birth abortion and prohibits that. We had a doctor in Dayton, Ohio, who was performing these. We stopped that because of the law in Ohio. This constitutional amendment, because it is a constitutional amendment, not just a law, it would be a constitutional amendment would -- would trump that law as well as it would trump the parental consent law. So a very, a proposal that is just way too far and again, whether someone is pro-choice or pro-life. This is just not where the majority of Ohioans are.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So one of your fellow governors over in Illinois, \uc2 J. B. Pritzker\uc2 , he has been pouring money into a number of state efforts, including this one in Ohio. His new organization is called Think Big America. He says it's this, dedicated to safeguarding reproductive rights and standing up against the right-wing extremists who want to take us backwards. He says folks, like you want to ban books, you want to ban voting rights and civil rights that you want to send women back to a time where they didn't have choices about their bodies. How do you respond to your fellow governor?\par \par \uc2 DEWINE: Well, that's just absurd. And he knows that's absurd. He knows me. That's not what we're trying to do at all. It's interesting, the pro side in this has spent about $35 million to try to mislead the voters of the State of Ohio. It's interesting to me that a governor of Illinois would come in with a half a million dollars contribution. If you look at all the other people who are doing this, these are the same people who want to -- get outside their own state and want to control what's going on in other states. We've only -- we have about 10 million. So it's been about three and a half to one. But our message is really, really clear. This is just whether you're pro-choice or pro-life. This is just much, much too far.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, Governor DeWine, this is certainly a state. We will be watching on Tuesday. You've also got a measure on marijuana. We're out of time, but folks can research that as well. Governor, we appreciate your time.\par \par \uc2 DEWINE: Good to be with you. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, high stakes off your elections in key states like Ohio, give us a preview of how voters are feeling heading into the critical 2024 cycle. Our Sunday panel on what the results this week could tell us about next year's race for the White House, coming up.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: For a deeper dive on all the big issues this week, including Tuesday's elections, let's bring in our Sunday group. Olivia Beavers, congressional reporter for "Politico," Fox News senior political analyst Juan Williams, Guy Benson, host of "The Guy Benson Show," and former Bush national security official Michael Allen.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to all of you.\par \par \uc2 So, I want to pick up where we left off with the governor in Ohio. We do have important state elections this week. One of them involves Governor Glenn Youngkin, who is trying to prove his win was not a fluke in Virginia, which is very blue these days. This issue of abortion, new polling shows us it is something that Virginia voters are very dialed into.\par \par \uc2 Here is what Younkin says. He argues that his proposal, which is a 15-week abortion limit with exceptions for rape, incest and life of the mother, represents a politically acceptable compromise between those who want to ban all abortions and those who would see no legal limits. He's quoted as saying, people recognize that's a reasonable place.\par \par \uc2 Guy, that's just one of the issue on the ballot essentially, not - not, you know, legitimately, but in theory\par \par \uc2 GUY BENSON, "THE GUY BENSON SHOW" HOST, TOWNHALL.COM AND FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Yes. And it's a completely reasonable position. It's sort of a European-style proposal. The Democrats, the ads they're running on this, are suggesting that the Republicans would ban every abortion ever in Virginia, which isn't true.\par \par \uc2 What I'm struck by, watching this Virginia race develop, is, number one, Governor Younkin is very popular in the commonwealth. He's got plus 20, plus 25 approval rating. Can he parlay that into coattails down ballot? He's working very hard. He's very invested in this race.\par \par \uc2 The other dynamic that I'm seeing is, the Democrats are running their 2022 playbook, which was pretty successful for them. Trump, MAGA, abortion distortions, in my view on the later part. The Republicans in Virginia are running the 2021 successful playbook that Younkin won with on schools, education, crime, economy and that sort of thing. It will be fascinating on Tuesday to see which two - which of those two approaches prevails.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes. And if you live in Virginia or anywhere in this area you are seeing the ads nonstop.\par \par \uc2 BENSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And you're ready for that election to be over.\par \par \uc2 But let's look to Kentucky as well because there is a tight governor race there. The current governor, \uc2 Andy Beshear\uc2 , is a Democrat. He is up against - increasingly popular, the polling shows this has tightened with the AG there, \uc2 Daniel Cameron\uc2 . "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " says this, though, "Beshear is doing whatever he can to separate himself from Mr. Biden, whose approval ratings remain mired around 40 percent nationally and are much lower in Kentucky.\par \par \uc2 Juan, what about that race?\par \par \uc2 JUAN WILLIAMS, FOX NEWS SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, you've got -- Beshear is like one of the most popular governors in the country. I think like number 5. And the key point here is, with Beshear, a Democrat in a red state, is not to think so much party affiliation as to think good governance, like, you know, who's getting the roads paved, who's doing this or that.\par \par \uc2 And then you get into some really difficult issues, as we saw in the debate this week, where, you know, Cameron was asked about something like vouchers. And you would think for Republicans that's a strong point. But in rural areas of the state, they see that as a threat to their public schools, so he wouldn't say his position. Similarly on Medicaid, you know, he's - he's very - he has sort of a halting, stuttering way of dealing with this issue, but it's very popular in the state. So, I think Beshear is in a - in the - in the cat seat there.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, in Mississippi, you've got a governor's race there with an incumbent Republican. "Cook Political Report" says this, they've moved the race from "likely to lean Republican." So, that's a race that's been tighter than expected.\par \par \uc2 But some brand-new polling out this week takes us more broadly to the `24 race as we look to these states to see if we can gather anything about how voters feel about next year. "The New York Times"/Sienna College has a new poll out, six swing states, and President Trump is leading President Biden in five of those six states.\par \par \uc2 Olivia, that's tough news for the White House this morning. And some of these, the margins are not close at all.\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA BEAVERS, "POLITICO" CONGRESSIONAL REPORTER: No, there is -- in Nevada there is a 10-point lead for Trump. I would say this is pretty devastating. It was going over issues where they feel like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is prevailing, on economy, on national security, on border and immigration, which are some of the top issues of this upcoming election, where they felt like Biden, which we were talking about, is leading is abortion. He has time to make it up, but right now these are so many points that if he lost those states in the Electoral College, he would be in big trouble.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, and one of the issues, Michael, where he is upside down is on foreign policy. It was something that President Biden came in as Senator Biden saying this is, you know, my portfolio. I know what I'm doing there. The American public doesn't feel like he's delivering on this big, foreign policy issues, and now he's got a new one on his plate.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL ALLEN\uc2 , FORMER BUSH NATIONAL SECURITY OFFICIAL: He does. Even when President Biden seems to stand strong, like he has in his support of Israel, I think the voters in the United States just view the president very negatively. I think it's all seen through the framework of the economy, inflation, his ignoring of immigration all these years. So, I think he is in a deep-seeded free-fall. And I can already hear the panic from the Democrats as they try to figure out what do you do with a candidate when 71 percent of the United States thinks you're too old to be president.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: We know that -\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And it's - and it's not just about age. I mean it is the issues, Juan. I mean people are unhappy with how he's performing.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Right. But I think at this juncture, I think from the Democrats' point of view, what you have is polls that are a referendum on \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . And there are a lots of people who are upset about the wars that we're speaking about, Michael, Ukraine and obviously the events in the Middle East are quite upsetting. But also you have a broken Congress here on Capitol Hill. This looks dysfunctional. It looks like government's dysfunctional. So I think a lot of the angst, Shannon, gets directed to the guy who's in power, You're going to say, oh, you know, I'm sick of Washington. I'm sick of this.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: That's how it works. Yes.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Right. So, he gets it. But next year is not a matter of a referendum on \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , it becomes a choice election. And in a choice election people then start to say, oh, gee, the Congress was broken. Oh, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's an extremist. Oh, where are we going in the world?\par \par \uc2 ALLEN: But - but the poll put them up against each other. I hear you. Usually when you just ask about Biden, people say he -- I have a negative view. But here it's Trump versus Biden. And Biden is losing all over the place.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and - well -\par \par \uc2 BENSON: And people - people know Trump. People are very aware of who \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is and all of his issues.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Everything's baked in with him.\par \par \uc2 BENSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, but you mentioned Democrats. This is an interesting headline from "Axios." They said Thursday, "Democratic governors and senators are quietly boosting their national profiles to set up presidential campaigns in `28 or `24. With that in mind, here's something that Senator Fetterman said last night in Iowa.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (D-PA): There are two additional Democrats running for Pennsylvania - excuse me, running for president right now. One - one is a congressman from Minnesota, the other one is the governor of California. They're both running for president, but only one had the guts to announce it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Olivia, how nervous do you think the White House is about that because, listen, \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2 , he just got back from meeting President Xi in China. He's doing all the things you would be doing if you're running while saying I would never run. Not never but I'm not running this time.\par \par \uc2 BEAVERS: They're deferring but they're doing it just in case is sort of their approach. And he's not the only one. You have Booker, you have Pritzker doing the abortion campaigns. And, look, they should. This is politics. You should be building up your profile if you're interested in running. They're just saying we're not going to challenge Biden. We're doing it - you know, well, they're not saying it. It's implied in how they're operating. But they need to be building up their profile in case they need to step in, whether it's now or whether it's later because we know that the next presidential election cycle will be very competitive with Democrats looking to try to move up into the presidency.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Maybe it's `24 instead of `28.\par \par \uc2 But I want to make sure we get to these number on third-party threats because there's new polling out with Quinnipiac that should be giving a lot of pause to everybody who is thinking about running for president, or currently running.\par \par \uc2 When they look at independent, if you add RFK Junior into the mix, this is what they find, 36 percent among independents support Kennedy, 31 percent support Trump and 30 percent support Biden.\par \par \uc2 Guy, there is growing concern on the left, and the right, about some of these independent runs.\par \par \uc2 BENSON: Yes, they're not really sure gaming out who would benefit more of the two major parties. I suspect that that number that we just saw from Quinnipiac is a reflection of the American people and independent voters being deeply dissatisfied with their two likeliest options ahead of next year, not so much a full embrace of RFK Junior and all that he represents. I think those numbers would come down when people get a look at what he actually believes on a lot of issues. But it is useful because it again highlights how very unpopular both major party frontrunners are.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: A quick final word from you, Juan, because when you add Cornel West into these - the mix as well, it's also not good for the primary frontrunners.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Well, sure, because I think that right now lots of people, again, are looking and say, oh, the world is just falling apart. I'm tired. But, you know what, voters, typically on independents, they kick the tires and then those votes fade away. That's what we saw with, you know, -- John Anderson. That's what we saw with recently Jill Stein.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, we'll have to see because this could be an incredibly tight race. But based on the polling out of "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 " this morning, maybe not so much in some of those swing states.\par \par \uc2 All right, panel, we've got to leave it there for this week.\par \par \uc2 Up next, how you can help veterans reintegrate into civilian life with dignity after they've spent their life serving our country.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, here at Fox we believe all our veterans deserve dignity and independence. We want you to join us in Making Camo Your Cause in support of the U.S. Vets campaign which aims to eradicate veteran homelessness. You can visit honor.usvets.org/foxforward. It's there on your screen. You've got the QR code. You can donate and find some cool camo gear. You can also post your camo pics on social media with this hashtag, honor U.S. Vets, and share your message of thanks. If you donate between now and November 12th you can pick up some swag being modeled by our panel today. Ties, which I tried to tie in the break, mugs, shirts and hats. We love how you all look in this and we want to celebrate our vets.\par \par \uc2 Very nice. You even went with a shirt.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: I like this shirt.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I -- Guy did a better job tying the tie then I did during the commercial.\par \par \uc2 WILLAIMS: It's hard - it's hard to compete with Olivia and you. You guys look great. Really good.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Check it out.\par \par \uc2 That's it for today. Thanks for joining us. I'm Shannon Bream. Have a great week.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FOXN000020231105ejb500001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Manhunt Underway For Suspect In Maine Shooting; Interview With Speaker Of The House Mike Johnson (R-LA); U.S. Conducts Airstrikes In Syria\b0\par\par\uc2 Sean Hannity, Molly Line, Ted Williams \par \uc2 12,758 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 October 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Hannity\par \uc2 SHAN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SEAN HANNITY\uc2 , FOX NEWS HOST: And welcome to HANNITY.\par \par \uc2 And we are broadcasting live tonight from the heart of the Washington swamp right here on Capitol Hill, where in mere moments, we will be joined by the 56th speaker of the House of Representatives, Mike Johnson, for his very first TV interview since becoming speaker.\par \par \uc2 Now, the Louisiana native now is third in line to be president. He has vowed to secure our southern border, decentralize our federal government, reform the weaponized and politicized DOJ and \uc2 FBI\uc2 , and bring the Republican Caucus together with the thin majority.\par \par \uc2 And predictably, this was not hard to predict, the left has launched a full-scale attack on the new speaker, led of course by the DNC, calling him a MAGA extremist and much more, and advising him not to get comfortable in his new position.\par \par \uc2 Now, coming up, we're going to ask him how he plans to deal with his political adversaries, what are his top agenda items, how will he keep this caucus together?\par \par \uc2 Plus, we'll check in on Israel's looming ground invasion in Gaza. There was a short incursion last night. The first of what we expect to be a major ground war in the days and weeks ahead.\par \par \uc2 But, first, we start with breaking news out of Maine tonight, where we are tracking a developing situation near the home of Robert Card. He's the suspected mass shooter responsible for at least 18 murders, 13 injured.\par \par \uc2 Here now with at the very latest, she's on the ground. She's in Maine for us tonight. Molly Line has the latest on this.\par \par \uc2 Molly, thanks for being with us.\par \par \uc2 MOLLY LINE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Sean, thanks for having me tonight.\par \par \uc2 As you mentioned, the manhunt continues. There's a significant amount of activity in the suspect's hometown of Bowdoin. They've really focused in on a group of properties on Meadows Road, and the last couple hours, we've seen choppers overhead in that road and a search warrant being executed there at that property.\par \par \uc2 A law enforcement on a megaphone yelling for quite some time, calling for Robert or anyone in that home to come on out, to come out peacefully. Per Maine state police, those announcements that were heard over that loudspeaker megaphone are just standard search warrant announcements when they're executing a search warrant to ensure the safety of everyone involved. It's unknown whether Robert Card is in any of the homes that law enforcement is looking to search over there.\par \par \uc2 Law enforcement officials simply doing their due diligence, tracking down every lead and those properties are associated with this suspect. So they're doing uh everything they can to find out what sort of clues they might be able to draw from that area. They did also use a series of fast -- flashbangs earlier in the evening, once again another tactic, an intention to draw out this suspect if he is there, not necessarily any proof that he actually was. Things unfolded yesterday just a little bit before 7:00. Lewiston Police responding to those two mass shooting incidents, one in a bowling alley, another at a bar and billiard hall.\par \par \uc2 By 10:25 in the evening, Androscoggin County officials had called a countywide shelter in place order. Even now, authorities are still asking people to stay in their homes and Lewiston police originally calling Robert Card a person of interest. He is now clearly a suspect in this homicide mass murder case.\par \par \uc2 The Coast Guard, worth noting, also involved now. They were up in the sky earlier today over Boothbay Harbor. That's notably because the \uc2 Subaru\uc2 , the vehicle that is associated with this suspect, was found in a community called Lisbon, not so far from here, about a 10-minute drive from Lewiston. And there's a river there, and this suspect is known to have some familiarity with boats and also with weapons as well.\par \par \uc2 An internal law enforcement communication revealing that this individual is a firearms instructor, a member of the army reserves and that over the summer had been suffering an escalating mental health crisis essentially, ended up being committed for a series of weeks and getting treatment.\par \par \uc2 So that speaks to potentially how dangerous this individual really may be. Law enforcement officials are warning the public that he is armed and dangerous and right now, they're still running down every lead a lot of different agencies involved in this -- Sean.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. Molly Line, thank you for that report. Stay with us. We'll be back to you throughout the hour tonight, as news breaks.\par \par \uc2 And my exclusive interview with the House speaker, the new speaker, Mike Johnson, in just moments. First, let's get quick reaction to what is going on, on the ground, in Maine with national law enforcement and counterterrorism analyst, former Israeli ops vet \uc2 Aaron Cohen\uc2  is back with us, and former D.C. police detective, FOX news contributor Ted Williams.\par \par \uc2 Aaron, let's get your take. I mean, very predictably, people are already talking about here in the nation's capital about new gun laws. Maine does have some of the more lenient laws on the books for state in the country. No background check on the purchaser and they have what the -- they don't have their so-called yellow paper, red flag laws. They're under a lot of controversy tonight, conceal carry, without a permit is limited to people who are 21 or older.\par \par \uc2 However, when you look at the background, what do we see? Somebody recently fired, somebody that clearly had some mental issues, if in fact they have him surrounded tonight. Should this be a gun debate at this time and what is your take on what law enforcement is doing right now and why have they not entered that home that they have surrounded since this afternoon?\par \par \uc2 AARON COHEN\uc2 , COUUNTERTERRORISM EXPERT: I don't think it's the time right now, Sean, to turn inwards and start debating. I don't think this is polit -- this is -- we don't to be politicizing this right now. We're going have those conversations down the road, specifically when it comes to mental health. That to me is the big flag, when someone does get committed and someone has had a -- had a breakdown, that's definitely something I'd be looking at, but I don't have a problem with guns, Sean. You don't have a problem with guns as long as you're safe and you're trained with them.\par \par \uc2 I'm all for it. They're great for force multipliers and given the state of the world right now, it's not a bad thing to -- to be able to defend yourself.\par \par \uc2 Moving to -- moving to the situation right now, with that house that's being surrounded, law enforcement is going to exercise every lead right now. This is an intelligence hunt. They want to know where the suspect is, I'll take you back to 2012, Chris Dorner, went on a rampage, started killing -- started killing cops, ended up booting two and a half hours up to Big Bear, multiple SWAT teams were on him, took a long time to get that information.\par \par \uc2 But it's going to move a lot quicker now. We've got cell phones. We've got the photo of the suspect out. He is obviously the main person of interest at this point.\par \par \uc2 So intelligence number one, making sure that we get every lead. Number two, if in fact he is in that structure right now and now it's time to slow it down. And when I'm training SWAT teams, this is what we call slow and deliberate. There's -- if there's no hostages inside we go slow, we want to peel that can open nice and tender.\par \par \uc2 But the first thing is, if he's barricaded and this would be a barricaded sus -- subject, if there is no hostages, let's tire him out, let's start communicating with him, let's get a -- throw a phone in there and let's start getting some dialogue going and see if we can get him out alive. And we want to obviously take the suspect into custody alive.\par \par \uc2 Putting on my U.S. law enforcement hat here, we're not dealing with terrorism that we know, that we know, and you know me, Sean. I don't believe in coincidences when it comes to things happening u freakishly at the same time while we're in this monster conflict in Israel.\par \par \uc2 But let's -- let's try and talk him out. Let's tire him down, there's no hostages, let's try and end it peacefully and get them out so we can put some cuffs on them and then make an arrest without anybody else getting killed. There are hostages, becomes a different situation.\par \par \uc2 If there's federal laws that were violated here, FBI HR, national hostage rescue team would be on scene, but the good news is, is that a lot of these agencies, Sean, they've gotten a lot better over the years. Even these smaller agencies, I've been running around training them for close to 20 years, they've got the gear now. They've got the weaponry, they've got the tactics, their mindsets gotten better.\par \par \uc2 Everyone's in better shape now, it's just the tone has changed. So we just got to let these guys do what they're going to do, and they're going to go in there and they're going to make entry. If they have to, and if they do and there's no hostages. It'll be very slow, it'll be very deliberate, but let's -- I'm leaning towards they're going to try and get him to come out.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Aaron, good advice.\par \par \uc2 Ted Williams, let's get your take. I would assume law enforcement obviously would have night vision, something that would be able to aid and assist them, probably, they would be able to sense where a human body would be through heat sensing of some kind. Do you agree with Aaron? Anything else you want to add to that?\par \par \uc2 TED WILLIAMS, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, I agree with what you've just said about heat sensing equipment that would tell you perhaps if he's in that house. Now, the thing about it is they're only going to wait for so long. Normally, they would do an activity of this nature during the daytime because the daytime is the most friendliest time for law enforcement and to law enforcement. So, since they waited here at night, I think a SWAT team, I understand there's an armored vehicle on the scene there, and there's a good possibility that they've already gone in.\par \par \uc2 And one of the things that they would normally do with a SWAT team coming on a scene like that, Sean, would be to send the dogs in. And so, I don't know if they've done that, but I just got word that there were law enforcement agencies leaving that home.\par \par \uc2 And so, if that is true and can be corroborated, it's more likely than not that they have eliminated that property. And that is what law enforcement officers are trying to do. They've going through what we would define as a process of elimination, simply meaning --\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Right.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: -- they eliminate that house.\par \par \uc2 Now, what makes me believe that is more likely than not that he's not in that house is that after this carnage last night, law enforcement immediately was at those homes. So they had those homes under surveillance and so, it's a part, Sean, of a process of elimination but this guy could be anywhere. We know that he ditched his car in Lisbon, Maine.\par \par \uc2 We know that he had jet skis. We know that there was a waterway. We know that the Coast Guard is now looking down those waterways. He may not even be in the state of Maine at this immediate time.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. Ted Williams, we'll see. Law enforcement still surrounding the house, so there's still got to be some interest there. We'll have more on this throughout the hour tonight.\par \par \uc2 Thank you both. We appreciate it, Aaron and Ted.\par \par \uc2 All right, now to the big news out of Capitol Hill, where Louisiana Congressman Mike Johnson, he was just elected speaker of the House.\par \par \uc2 Now, notably, Johnson is not a creature of the swamp, a son of a fireman from Louisiana. He was first elected to Congress in 2017, but quickly earned a reputation of someone who could communicate well and get things done without making enemies, pretty hard in this town, unless you hire -- unless you're friends with dogs and cats. That's about the only friend you usually get in Washington.\par \par \uc2 As a prior member of the Judiciary Committee -- that's Jim Jordan's committee -- the Armed Services Committee, and a stint as the chairman of the Republican Study Committee, well, Johnson has been very busy. He has consistently supported lower taxes, law and order, and Second Amendment protections, and a secure border.\par \par \uc2 He has also fought against Biden's weaponization at our Department of Justice and the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and whether they have politicized. Also, he is against the woke far left propaganda now indoctrinating our kids.\par \par \uc2 As speaker, his first measure was a resolution in support of Israel's right to self-defense after the \uc2 Hamas\uc2  terrorist attacks. And his first major bill will, in fact, be saying bye-bye to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's Green New Deal radical socialism.\par \par \uc2 Johnson also has a plan to keep the government open, while getting our spending in check, without chaos, infighting or drama. That is the goal, no easy task, to say the least, especially when you have such a slim majority.\par \par \uc2 So, how will we get it done?\par \par \uc2 Joining us now with more, America's brand-new speaker of the House, his first interview.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Speaker, good to see you, sir.\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE JOHNSON (R-LA), SPEAKER OF THE HOUSE: Thanks, Sean. Welcome to Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right.\par \par \uc2 Two weeks ago, did you think -- have you gotten used to the word speaker yet?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: I haven't, Sean. I have been in the job about 48 hours now, and it's kind of surreal, to be honest.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: I just want to say at the outset here, God be with the law enforcement officers that are handling that situation in Maine. Our prayers have been with the families of that tragedy.\par \par \uc2 Everyone here in the House was dialed in on that all day, and it's really something. So, just to address that at the front end. It would be remiss if we didn't mention it.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Already, though, you're immersed in Democrats -- and this happens with almost every shooting incident. The media call by the left in this country, we need more gun laws, we need more legislation.\par \par \uc2 What's your answer to that?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: At the end of the day, it's -- the problem is the human heart. It's not guns. It's not the weapons.\par \par \uc2 At the end of the day, we have to protect the right of the citizens to protect themselves, and that's the Second Amendment. And that's why our party's stands so strongly for that. I agree with the comments of your guests there. This is not the time to be talking about legislation. We're in the middle of that crisis right now.\par \par \uc2 But I just want you to know and I want the American people to know that all the members of the House here are deeply concerned about the families involved and everyone. We pray for the law enforcement officers that are doing that hard job tonight that most people do not have the bravery to do.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: You talk about the human heart. If somebody really wants to kill innocent people, there's a lot of ways they can do it...\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: ... beyond using a gun.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I'm sure this will become a bigger issue throughout the days moving forward.\par \par \uc2 Is there any, any specific gun law that you would look at or any new legislation you would look at?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Well, I have been on the job for 48 hours. We will see.\par \par \uc2 I mean, there will be lots of discussion, as there are after these heartbreaking tragedies. But your point is well-taken. In Europe and in other places, they use vehicles to mow down crowds and parades, where they have done that here in the United States. It's not the weapon. It's the underlying problem.\par \par \uc2 I believe we have to address the root problems of these things. And mental health, obviously, as in this case, is a big issue. And we have got to seriously address that as a society and as a government. And there's lots of measures pending on that as well.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: You got a very warm welcome from the DNC. They said you were an anti-abortion MAGA extremist Mike Johnson. That was their first word.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Oh, yes, welcome to the job.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Welcome you -- welcome to your new job.\par \par \uc2 Then they went on to say that you were the co-sponsor to institute an extreme abortion ban -- we will get into detail -- nationwide. You want to cut Social Security and Medicare.\par \par \uc2 "And here's some free advice from Mike Johnson. And that is, don't get comfortable. We have been here before. This is the same exact MAGA extremism that the American people have already rejected, and they will do it again."\par \par \uc2 I thought that was the warmest welcome you could ever want from the Democratic Party.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: How about that? Yes, welcome to the job.\par \par \uc2 Look, they don't -- they don't know me. A lot of these people don't know me. And I think, if they would talk to some of my colleagues here, even on the other side of the aisle, that have worked with me for the seven years that I have been on Capitol Hill, they would tell them that those things are not true.\par \par \uc2 Give me a chance. Let me have a chance to lead here, and you will see what I'm really about.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Let me ask you, because a lot of people don't know you. And before I get to some really heavy-duty questions, issues of great national importance, what do you want people to know about you?\par \par \uc2 There were things about you that I learned in the lead-up to this interview. I didn't know that you actually didn't legally adopt a young 14- year-old, but you basically adopted him. It would have taken four years to finalize it. And he happened to be African-American. And he was your first son.\par \par \uc2 And now you have four other children. And one of them -- well, this son was named Michael. Your other 14-year-old is Jack, And you said some pretty deep, profound things that you think is harder for your African-American son than your biological son in life.\par \par \uc2 Are you saying that's institutional racism in the country? What were you referring to?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: No.\par \par \uc2 It's a reality, though, having raised two 14-year-old boys in America in the state of Louisiana. They had different experiences. And I'm not so sure it was all about skin color, but it is about culture and society.\par \par \uc2 Michael, the -- our first, came from a really troubled background and had a lot of challenges. Jack, on the other hand, we -- was raised in our household from the time he was born. And it just struck me, Sean, that it's a reality that there are -- there are different paths in life, and people have lots of things they have to overcome.\par \par \uc2 And so it gives you a lot of empathy. It allows you to see into the heart of people and really try to understand them better. And that comes in handy in this arena, because we're really expected to hate our colleagues on the other side of the aisle and beat them all the time as enemies, politically speaking.\par \par \uc2 But you have to recognize that this is a system that was built upon disagreement. I mean, what the founders intended here in this legislative context is that we're going to come to the table with very different philosophical ideas and core principles, and we have to arm-wrestle over that and reach consensus to move the ball forward for the American people.\par \par \uc2 That's what the system is built upon. And, right now, people are going to their corners and they're refusing to talk. We have got to get over that.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I don't know how we reconcile a lot of these issues.\par \par \uc2 You either believe in climate alarmism or energy independence. How do you reconcile that? How do you reconcile defund, dismantle versus funding law enforcement, and no-bail laws versus bail laws?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Right.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: How do you reconcile secure borders or sanctuary states or cities, very controversial issues?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: And I don't know where the middle ground is. Do you see it?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: There is less and less middle ground.\par \par \uc2 Over the decades, the parties have grown very far apart. There's a wide chasm now. If you rewound back 40 or 50 years, there wasn't really a lot of difference between the Republican and Democrat parties, if you compared their platforms. There were nuances about policy, but they believed in the same things. They were trying to do what's right for the country.\par \par \uc2 The allegation here about some of our colleagues is, that's not really their agenda. And some of them really would like to trade what we have for a European-style socialist form of government. We know that that is a terrible idea, so we stand for our foundational principles.\par \par \uc2 What you have to do is get down to those core principles and argue, debate with your colleagues, and use the facts, show them the reality. Socialism is a dead end. Communism and Marxism are terrible things. It's been responsible for the deaths of tens of millions of innocent people over the 20th century alone, right?\par \par \uc2 That is not the path we want to go down. America's different and we are exceptional because we stand on certain foundational principles. I said in my speech the other night that -- was that last night? All my hours are running together now.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I think it was yesterday afternoon.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Yesterday?\par \par \uc2 I talked about the core principles of American conservatism, and that's individual freedom, limited government, the rule of law, peace through strength, fiscal responsibility, free markets, and human dignity.\par \par \uc2 And under each of those, there would be subcategories. But that's what we stand for. I call them the core principles of American conservatism, but it's really the core principles of America itself. We're different. We're exceptional.\par \par \uc2 The reason we're the freest, most powerful, most successful nation in the history of the world is because that's what we stand for. And those values and those principles are under assault right now, and we have to defend them here every day with everything we have.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Let me ask you.\par \par \uc2 You had a bipartisan briefing with President Biden. \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2  was there. Other leaders were there. You got a phone call from President Biden.\par \par \uc2 Tell us about it. How'd it go?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Well, I actually had a visit with the president at the White House today for about 15 minutes or -- 15 or 20 minutes. The other parties were running late, so it was he and I sitting there together.\par \par \uc2 It was a meet-and-greet. I'd only met him once before. It was cordial and pleasant.\par \par \uc2 I have no problem with President Biden as an individual, right? You respect the office. It's a biblical admonition that you give honor where honor is. And that's the honor, the respect that we have for the office.\par \par \uc2 But he and I agree on almost no policy. So we didn't get into that too much today, Sean, but there will be time for that in the days ahead.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Is there any one thing you can think of, policy, that you would say he's done a really good job on this or that?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: I can't. I can't.\par \par \uc2 I think it's been a failed presidency. And all the problems, some of those that you have articulated already at the outset of the program here, were caused by policy choices. And that's the problem I have with him. It's philosophical.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Look, I'm sure, if you have ever seen my show -- and I don't know. I didn't ask you if you have ever watched it.\par \par \uc2 The president, in my view, has been struggling cognitively. It's a significant decline that I see. I'm not sure if you saw the tape, the interview that he gave a gaggle on Air Force One when he's flying back from Israel, after, by the way, a lot of allied nations did not want to meet with him. Leaders did not want to meet with him, which I found to be a pretty big snub.\par \par \uc2 I don't know if you saw that tape. Do you see in \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  a cognitive decline? And, if so, is that a danger to the country?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: I do. I think most of us do. That's reality. This is not a personal slight to him. It has to do with age and acumen. And everyone's different. Everyone ages differently.\par \par \uc2 Clearly, if you look at a tape of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  making an argument in the \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2  a few years ago, and you see a speech that he delivers now, there's a difference. Again, I mean, it's not a personal insult to him. It's just reality.\par \par \uc2 And this is -- this is what's concerning to us, is that we -- we cannot project weakness of any kind on the world stage right now. This is a dangerous time for all the things going on around the world. The world is a tinderbox. A strong America is good for the whole world. And we have to project strength.\par \par \uc2 I mean, Reagan used to remind us all the time, it's peace through strength. If America shows weakness, it invites aggression by our adversaries. And that's what you're seeing around the world. So I think that's a really important thing to remember.\par \par \uc2 And, also Sean, also remember, I have been watching your show since I can - - as long as I can remember, all 25 years almost?\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I love when people tell me, I have been watching you since I have been in first grade.\par \par \uc2 I mean, it makes me feel really good.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: I remember when -- I remember when your hair was dark, darker than mine.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: It was a lot darker.\par \par \uc2 By the way, as speaker, I think I'm going to catch up pretty quickly.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: It's already happening.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Especially with the smaller majority.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Already, the press, the left have come at you, and come at you hard.\par \par \uc2 Specifically, I will give you two big issues, one on the issue -- you once worked for the Alliance Defense Fund, a Christian advocacy group.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: And comments you had made both in writing and advocacy for this group about homosexuality, calling it sinful, destructive, and not supporting gay marriage -- quote -- "no clear right to sodomy in the Constitution."\par \par \uc2 You have been getting hammered on this.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: And I want to ask you about it. I want to know exactly where you stand. Some of these comments were 15 years ago.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: I don't even remember some of them.\par \par \uc2 I was a litigator that was called upon to defend the state marriage amendments. If you remember back, in the early 2000s, I think it was over 35 states, somewhere in that number, that the people went to the ballot in their respective states and they amended their state constitutions to say marriage is one man, one woman.\par \par \uc2 Well, I was a religious liberty defense lawyer and I was called to go in and defend those cases in the courts. Let me state this very clearly. And there's been questions about this. Let me say where I am. Anybody that knows me will tell you this is true. I am a rule of law guy.\par \par \uc2 I made a career defending the rule of law. I respect the rule of law. When the Supreme Court issued the Obergefell opinion, that became the law of the land, OK? I respect the rule of law, but I also genuinely love all people, regardless of their lifestyle choices.\par \par \uc2 This is not about the people themselves. I am a Bible-believing Christian. Someone asked me today in the media, they said, it's curious. People are curious, what does Mike Johnson think about any issue under the sun?\par \par \uc2 I said, well, go pick up a Bible off your shelf and read it. That's -- that's my world view. That's what I believe. And so I make no apologies.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: That's your personal world view.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: It's my personal world view.\par \par \uc2 But here's the thing. Everybody comes to the House of Representatives with deep personal convictions. But all of our personal convictions are not going to become law. That's -- this is a big body of people. There's 435 members in the House. You have to argue and find consensus and all of that.\par \par \uc2 So I have no agenda, other than what's best for the American people and to defend the rule of law. And that's what we're doing.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Well, then let me ask you on the issue of abortion, where you have been very pro-life.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Obviously, that comes from your issue as faith.\par \par \uc2 You had argued against Roe, bad law.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Dobbs happens.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: That issue now has been sent back to the states.\par \par \uc2 On either one of these issues, gay marriage, abortion, do you -- will -- in the next 14 months, as you're going to be the speaker of the House, assuming the motion to vacate never comes up, which I think is awful and shouldn't be there.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: I think we're going to change it.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Or have a -- have a higher bar, certainly, 25 people, instead of just any one member.\par \par \uc2 But on any -- on those two issues, where they have come at you so hard, and you're explaining your personal view, and you're kind of bifurcating it...\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Sure.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: ... or away from your political view, do you see any scenario where either one of those issues would be an agenda item for you and the Republicans for the next 14 months or at any point down the line?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Well, first, on the marriage issue, no one has discussed that for as long as I can remember. This has been settled by the Supreme Court in the Obergefell opinion in 2015.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Right.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: So, that's the decision. Now, they changed the definition of marriage that had been regarded by basically every human society for 5,000 years.\par \par \uc2 But when five justices on the Supreme Court changed it, that became the law of the land. I'm a constitutional law attorney. I respect that, and we move forward.\par \par \uc2 The abortion issue is different. The table was just reset, with Dobbs opinion overturned Roe, which I thought was one of the worst, probably the worst spring court opinion.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Roe v. Wade, yes, the most erroneous court opinion that had been issued.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: So, you like it back? You like the states to decide?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: We argued my entire career, for 25 years, that the states should have the right to do this. There's no national consensus among the people on what to do with that issue on a federal level, for certain.\par \par \uc2 This -- we have such big priorities in this moment right now, Sean. We have Israel being attacked. We have unrest. We have the Ukraine situation we have got to deal with. We have China being aggressive. We have Iran will all the meddling, and China, Russia and Iran working together. This is a dangerous time.\par \par \uc2 The economy is in the tank. The border is overrun. People are dying from fentanyl poisoning, all of these issues. These are the front-line matters that we -- that have our attention right now. And the rest of these things, they're just using for political attacks.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: So, basically, you're saying these issues will not be coming up, and you're going to focus on the agenda items that you have laid out already and the ones you just mentioned, which we will go into some detail with.\par \par \uc2 Let me ask you one other question on this. And this has come up as -- well, as controversial. You had authored legislation called the Stop the Sexualization of Children Act of 2022.\par \par \uc2 OK, now, I looked deeply into this act. Your critics have said -- or dubbed it, it's a federal don't say gay measure. By the way, the word gay was never in the Florida legislation, in fairness to Governor DeSantis. And yet this -- when you look at exactly what this is, it would prohibit the use of federal funds to develop, implement, facilitate, or fund any sexually oriented program, event, literature for children under the age of 10.\par \par \uc2 Now, a lot of our kids in a lot of school districts around the country, they can't read, write, do math, science. They're not learning history, and they certainly need to learn computers. So, to the age of 10 very specifically.\par \par \uc2 But you're not talking about anything beyond that?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: No.\par \par \uc2 And if you poll this anywhere in the country, almost any state, any community, they're going to say, moms and dads should be the ones responsible for talking about these sensitive issues with their children, not the public school system.\par \par \uc2 I mean, I will take that debate anywhere to Main Street and any town hall in any city in America. I would love for them to challenge on that, because that's what the American people believe, and that's what I believe.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Let me ask. I have a lot of questions.\par \par \uc2 As you point out, there is a lot going on in the world right now. Let me throw some numbers at you, because I think they're very important. We have got war in Europe, we have got Ukraine, and we got Russia. And it seems very precarious there.\par \par \uc2 Already, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has given $76.8 billion to Ukraine. He wants $61 billion more. He wants $14 billion to go to Israel. And I think he -- and then he wants to put money on the border, probably to satisfy conservatives in the House and get their support on Ukraine, for example, which seems to be his top priority, a big disparity in the amount of money.\par \par \uc2 I have some criticisms about the issue of Ukraine. Number one, if you're going to fight a war, you should fight it to win it. I thought the president made a horrible mistake by denying MiGs that were offered by Poland. I think the president made a horrible mistake trying to put handcuffs on the Ukrainian army, led by Zelenskyy, on the issue of how they will fight and what weapons they can and cannot use, like cluster bombs.\par \par \uc2 I don't like the fact that Europe doesn't pay their fair share. So they have got $78 billion. He wants another $61 billion. How will you deal with the issues that he -- he wants to tie all of these issues together, which now gets into the budgetary process.\par \par \uc2 Will you go issue by issue?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: I told the staff at the White House today that our consensus among House Republicans is that we need to bifurcate those issues.\par \par \uc2 I agree with your assessment in Ukraine, and that's why the American people are demanding some real accountability for the use of those dollars. Now, we can't allow \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  to prevail in Ukraine, because I don't believe it would stop there, and it would probably encourage and empower China to perhaps make a move on Taiwan. We have these concerns.\par \par \uc2 We're not going to abandon them. But we have a responsibility, a stewardship responsibility over the precious treasure of the American people. And we have to make sure that the White House is providing the people with some accountability for the dollars.\par \par \uc2 And we want to know what the objective there is. What is the endgame in Ukraine? The White House has not provided that. I was at the White House for a couple of hours today, and I told the staff there that this is where we are. This is where the House Republicans are.\par \par \uc2 So we're going to...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: ... Israel separately.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: With that kind of money, isn't America having a proxy war with Putin and Russia?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: It's a great concern.\par \par \uc2 We have got a group of our colleagues here in the House led by Mike Garcia out of California, a brilliant legislator and a Top Gun pilot. And some of our veterans have gotten together. Dan Crenshaw is involved in this and others. They came up with a document that presents 12 critical questions for the White House to answer as a condition of our supplying the additional support.\par \par \uc2 These are not hard questions, Sean. These are things the White House should be forthcoming about. I delivered that myself.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Oh, you did deliver it to the president?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: I delivered it myself today and to the national security adviser, Jake Sullivan.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Did he read it?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: And he says he's studying it.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Going to.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: He's going to study it tonight during the show.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 So, we want to be cooperative. We need to work together on this. But we owe it to the people to know what the plan is, where the money is going to be spent. And we need some auditing for the dollars that we have already sent over there. These are not tough questions, right?\par \par \uc2 Israel is a separate matter. We're going to bring forward a stand-alone Israel funding measure over $14 billion. But it's going to be...\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Is $14 billion enough?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Well, Israel has requested a little less than that, actually. The White House did, by way of Israel. And $14.5 billion specifically is what we're looking at.\par \par \uc2 It's a very specific number tied to very specific measures. But here's the important thing that distinguishes House Republicans from the other team. We're going to find pay-fors in the budget. We're not just printing money to send it overseas. We're going to find the cuts elsewhere to do that.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Let me ask you this.\par \par \uc2 In Israel, it's very complicated. I mean, Syria is now firing missiles into Israel. The Houthi rebels, which I don't know why \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  -- they were once designated a terrorist group. He's taken away that designation. Why, I don't know. They tried to fire missiles into Israel.\par \par \uc2 In the north, you have \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 , and you have Lebanon, in the south, where Gaza killed 1,400, including 30 Americans, and Americans are being held hostage. Beyond the money part, is there any involvement you see or any reason for any military involvement by the United States? Would Congress support that?\par \par \uc2 Because it seems like, with the maneuverings of our Navy and other indications, special forces, maybe to rescue American hostages, any movement that you would support that you can think of now?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: It's a very delicate situation. It changes by the hour.\par \par \uc2 We're watching it very closely. One thing that House Republicans are resolved on is that we must stand with our most important ally in the Middle East, and that's Israel. We will. We certainly hope that it doesn't come to boots on the ground.\par \par \uc2 If it comes to that -- and we communicated this to the White House staff as well today -- that we have the Article I power in the legislative branch of government, and they have Article II. They have very limited authority on what they can do to respond without coming to Congress to seek consent.\par \par \uc2 And even my Democratic colleagues, Sean, that are committees of jurisdiction understand this, in the Foreign Affairs Committee. They -- there's a purpose for that. The reason the framers put that into the Constitution is that they wanted a multitude of wise counsel to decide upon military intervention matters, on a declaration of war.\par \par \uc2 They wanted the duly elected representatives of the people to make that decision collectively, and not one commander in chief sitting in an office.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Here's my worst fear, worst-case scenario for the Middle East, OK, that Israel will be fighting a two-front war...\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Gaza, \uc2 Hamas\uc2  in the south.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: As soon as they -- the ground incursion, which could happen any minute, begins, I would expect more rockets to be fired from the north, from Lebanon, \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 They have two -- they both have something in common, that they're getting military strategy, weaponry, money, support from Iran.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: That's the head of the snake.\par \par \uc2 Now, we have another problem. Syria has involved itself in this conflict. Another problem, the Houthis seem to be wanting to involve themselves. And you have had comments made by the leaders of Iran that they themselves may get involved in a conflict.\par \par \uc2 If that happens -- correct me if you think I'm wrong -- I would say all bets are off in the Middle East. We could have a full-out war in the Middle East, Israel at the center of it.\par \par \uc2 And, at that point, if Israel's existence is put in jeopardy, I don't think Prime Minister Netanyahu, who I have known for almost 30 years, I don't think there's anything he won't do to preserve and protect his country from people that have committed their lives to destroy it.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: He has to do that.\par \par \uc2 And around here, people throw around the phrase existential threat. They have an existential threat every day. I mean, their neighbors want to eliminate them and wipe them off the map.\par \par \uc2 So, Prime Minister Netanyahu is resolved. I have spent time with him personally. I know him as well. I think he's a strong leader at this important time, and I think he's going to do what is necessary. And America will back him up.\par \par \uc2 I mean, they tell us when we're in Israel -- and you have been there. It...\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: They say, the reason that we are able to sustain ourselves and survive is because everyone knows that our big ally is America.\par \par \uc2 And that's an important role that we have in the world. We're not the world's policemen, but a strong America, as I said, is good for the whole world, because it's the perception that we would be there to defend freedom. And that's been our role. That's the Reagan doctrine.\par \par \uc2 A lot of our friends in our party are becoming more isolationist. And I understand the concerns. We can't be dropping money out of helicopters all around the world, because we also have a $33.5 trillion federal debt of our own.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I saw a sign on the way in. It said 33.6.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Did we go to 6? Great. See...\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Probably from this afternoon.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Exactly. It goes so fast, you can't track it.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: It's scary.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: That's the concern.\par \par \uc2 We deeply care and are concerned about all nations of free people around the world, but we have to take care of our own house first, and so that's also part of this delicate calculation and the decision.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I have been to Israel during a flare-up in, I believe it was 2014.\par \par \uc2 And, at that point, I got to see up front and go inside the terror tunnels that are built by \uc2 Hamas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: By the way, all of the money and resources, that was given to the Palestinian people for infrastructure, for schools and hospitals.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: But they have this massive network of terror tunnels.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: And when you go inside, it just is mind-numbing that this is how they're spending their time, because they want to kill Israelis or take them hostage.\par \par \uc2 And -- but it taught me a lot about -- and being in border cities, one border city, in particular, Sderot, you know, 10,000 rockets they have been hit with in 10 years, and these kids play indoors in bunker playgrounds.\par \par \uc2 My question to you is simple. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  wants to give $100 million to the Palestinians for humanitarian relief. Sounds great on paper. I have zero confidence one penny will make it to the Palestinian people, because \uc2 Hamas\uc2  has been forcing people to stay and pointing guns and shooting at the Palestinians that want to leave.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Right.\par \par \uc2 They use the Palestinians as shields. They don't even provide the people with clean drinking water. We're supposed to believe they're going to use U.S. aid for humanitarian purposes? Count me as a skeptic, OK? We have got to be very careful about what we're doing there. You do not want to further empower the terrorist groups.\par \par \uc2 Our heart goes out to the innocent Palestinian people, of course, as we do to anyone who's in a terrible situation like that. But we have to be very discerning in our policy and in our approach to this. And so that's why we're demanding, House Republicans are demanding more information from the White House, so that we can help and make these critical decisions.\par \par \uc2 Look, we're going to stand by Israel, but we have to be careful in how it's done.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: What about the $100 million for humanitarian relief for the Palestinians?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: I think it's a tough issue. I mean, we're going to have to see what that is tied to.\par \par \uc2 And I will tell you, the House Democrats, of course, are very resolute on that. And they try to portray us as being uncaring. Look, we care about that as well. But we have to be very careful that we're not further funding terrorist organizations that are trying to wipe Israel off the map.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Can you explain why then-President Obama, Vice President Biden did this billion-dollar deal with Iran? Can you explain why \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  made a deal with the Iranians for $6 billion to be freed up in money that was held that they had no access to?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: We're in -- yes, we're in fits over that. I don't understand it at all.\par \par \uc2 I don't understand most of their foreign policy, and I think it's gotten us in the shape that we're in. We have big disagreements with them. We're asking for and demanding accountability on that as well. And we have to -- the White House has to improve how they're messaging this and how they're explaining it to people, because we all have to understand that we have a right to.\par \par \uc2 This is the precious treasure of the American people. And if you're sending Iran money, we know that Iran is directly tied to all this. These are -- \uc2 Hamas\uc2  and \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  are proxies of Iran. And they're tied in now with Russia and China. I mean, it is a new axis of evil. That's how we see it.\par \par \uc2 And so it has to be addressed accordingly.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: You say, if Israel, with all the funding of terror and all these terror organizations, are they within their right to fight back and...\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Of course.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: ... hit directly at Iran?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Of course. That was the first -- as you noted, the first act of my speakership is that we passed that resolution to articulate that and make it very clear where we stand.\par \par \uc2 The House is back in business, and we're going to stand with Israel.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Let's ask -- I call it the new axis of evil, China, Russia, Iran.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: How big a threat?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: It's huge. It's the biggest threat.\par \par \uc2 Since World War II, we have been the big kid on the block, right? We're the last real superpower. And after the USSR fell, we have been unrivaled. Now, China is a near-peer-to-peer adversary to us now. And, of course, their goal is to rebuild the empire.\par \par \uc2 And so we're doing everything we can to ensure that we maintain our military superiority. We're the only nation that has the ability to project power and force like that around the globe right now. But China is doing its best to encroach upon that power. And you see they're even putting outposts around us. They're buying up farmland in states here in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 Now, all of these things have to be addressed at the federal level, at the state level, with the legislatures, and even in some of these localities. We have Chinese, CCP-owned companies that are coming in and open shop on Main Street in some towns, in our small towns.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: They're buying our farmlands.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: They're buying our ranchland, and they're buying land in military installations.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Would we be allowed to buy it there?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Of course not.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Of course not.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Of course not.\par \par \uc2 And they allowed a Chinese spy balloon to float unimpeded across the country for eight days. We have -- we scratch our heads a lot around here about these Biden decisions. And I don't know. It may be tied in some way to, I don't know, some of the money they received from some of these folks.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Well, you are on the House Judiciary Committee.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: We have been watching the House Oversight Committee with James Comer.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Let me ask you.\par \par \uc2 There's issues involving two congressmen in particular, a Democrat and a Republican, George Santos, the Republican. Many charges have been filed against him. And then the issue of Congresswoman Tlaib and others and this talk of censure and talk of removal.\par \par \uc2 Do you support either?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: We're working through those issues.\par \par \uc2 I will tell you, I talked to the authors of some of these resolutions today that want to deal with that. Here's the reality, Sean. We have a four-seat majority in the House. It is possible that that number may be reduced even more in the coming weeks and months, and so we will have what may be the most razor-thin majority in the history of the Congress.\par \par \uc2 We have no margin for error. And so George Santos is due, due process, right? He is -- my understanding is, I think he's appearing in a federal court tomorrow, and we have to allow due process to play itself out. That's what our system of justice is for.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: He's not convicted. He's charged.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: He's not convicted. He's charged.\par \par \uc2 And so if we're going to expel people from Congress just because they're charged with a crime, then -- or accused, that's a problem.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has allowed over 7.5 million people into this country illegally. It'll be probably over eight million, even people on terror watch lists, even people from countries like Iran and Syria.\par \par \uc2 How do you get the border secure? And what is your assessment of the secretary of homeland security, this -- Mayorkas? Should he be impeached?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Listen, you know that my duties here have included Judiciary and the Select Committee on the Weaponization of the Federal Government.\par \par \uc2 In my assessment, almost every federal agency under the Biden administration has been weaponized. The very agencies that were designed to protect and serve the people are now being used against them. And we have many examples of that, and you have talked about all of it on your show before.\par \par \uc2 Look, Mayorkas has been an abject failure in his position. I believe he has committed impeachable offenses, and I have been on a record saying that for a long, long time. In fact, he appeared in an oversight hearing in our House Judiciary Committee -- we have jurisdiction over his department -- less than four months into office.\par \par \uc2 And I saw where this was headed, and I told him in a clip that went viral, Mr. Mayorkas, you need to get your resume prepared, because I thought he was headed towards a dead end on this.\par \par \uc2 Look, the investigations are going forward. Jamie Comer and the Oversight Committee are doing an exceptional job. Jim Jordan, who is the chairman of Judiciary, has helped lead on that. We have gotten a lot of information on all these issues.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Well, you're on the Judiciary Committee.\par \par \uc2 Let me ask you.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Do you believe that Joe Biden's Department of Justice, do you believe our \uc2 FBI\uc2  has been politicized? Do you believe it's been weaponized? Do we have a dual justice system?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Absolutely, we have a two-tiered system of justice, and that erodes the people's faith in our system of justice itself.\par \par \uc2 And this is what I reminded \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 , when he was in front of us, the attorney general, \uc2 Christopher Wray\uc2  at the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . The greatest threat here is not these individual issues. It's that the people are losing their faith in our system of justice.\par \par \uc2 And, Sean, you cannot maintain a constitutional republic, a government of, by and for the people, if the people doubt that the system is fair. If they believe there's two tiers of justice, then the system doesn't work at all. And so I think Garland's been a great threat. I think he's the worst attorney general in the history of the United States.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  swore to the American people both as a candidate and as president not one time did he ever speak to his son, his brother, or anybody, for that matter, about their foreign business dealings.\par \par \uc2 We know that is a flat-out lie. We have learned from James Comer and his investigation that we are talking about tens of millions of dollars taken in from a lot of adversarial countries, some of our top geopolitical foes, like China...\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: ... Russia, Ukraine, Romania, Mexico, other countries, tens of millions.\par \par \uc2 They have discovered all of these shell corporations. They have discovered nine particular Biden family members have been paid. And then you have the issue of Joe on tape admitting that he used our money, taxpayer money, to leverage a billion dollars in loan guarantees, which was Obama administration policy, to fire a prosecutor investigating his son.\par \par \uc2 Well, one of the benefits of that, his son continued to get paid for a job he admits he had no experience in, in at a time where he was dealing with a drug problem. That sound -- what does that sound like to you?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: That's a -- that's a pretty good recitation of the facts.\par \par \uc2 And as John Adams said, facts are stubborn things. They're hard to ignore. I believe the documents are proving all that. As my good brother Jamie Comer often says, the bank records don't lie. So we have the receipts on so much of this now.\par \par \uc2 It's a real problem. That's the reason that we shifted into the impeachment inquiry stage on the president himself, because if, in fact, all the evidence leads to where we believe it will, that's very likely impeachable offenses. You know that's listed as a cause for impeachment in the Constitution, bribery and other high crimes and misdemeanors.\par \par \uc2 Bribery's listed there. And it looks and smells a lot like that. And I think the evidence -- we're going to follow the truth where it leads. We're going to engage in due process, because, again, we're the rule of law party. I know people are getting anxious, and they're getting restless, and they just want somebody to be impeached.\par \par \uc2 But that's not -- we don't do that like the other team. We have to base it upon the evidence. And the evidence is coming together. We will see where it leads.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Let me go back to the budget, if I may, for a second.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Sure.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: This whole issue of regular order.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: We have four of the 12 appropriations bills that have already been dealt with. My understanding is, that's about 70 percent of the budget.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: So you have eight more to go.\par \par \uc2 You have talked about a short-term C.R. that you would be willing to maybe have until January or maybe even to April. Would there be -- we talk about a clean C.R. Would you have conditions?\par \par \uc2 For example, Chip Roy and Byron Donalds had a plan, OK, well, we will give you a C.R., but it's not going to be clean. We're going to cut government spending by 8 percent, and we're going to secure the border and protect our military and veterans.\par \par \uc2 Would you have conditions to a C.R.?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: I think we have to if indeed we do that.\par \par \uc2 November 17, of course, is the deadline that was extended.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Yes, you have got about...\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: It's coming quickly.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: ... 21 days.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: And we're speeding it up as quickly as possible.\par \par \uc2 That was my commitment to my colleagues when they named me speaker of the House, and that work began today. In fact, we passed one of the approps bills just a couple of hours ago, before you arrived here. We got it off the House floor, the energy and water bill. That was a big box that we had to check. And we did that.\par \par \uc2 And so we're going forward. Chip and Byron are at the table with others. We're working through this with the ideas and trying to ensure that, if another stopgap measure is required, that we do it with certain conditions. And I think there will be conditions that the American people can live with and the consensus that we can build around here in the House.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Let me ask you this. We have two-thirds of this country now living paycheck to paycheck.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Many of those people can't afford bare necessities.\par \par \uc2 I know Biden has been crowing about Bidenomics. I know they got good numbers today. However, a lot of economists I spoke to earlier today, this doesn't look good at all. They think we're in a bubble that is about to burst, and it's going to be even worse, especially in the housing market. It's -- new home construction is stopping, sale of preexisting homes a disaster.\par \par \uc2 How bad is this economy? How long can we sustain another, what, $2 trillion in debt that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  took on last year? And how do we get inflation down once and for all? I have friends of mine, just three years ago, they were getting interest rates at 3 -- a 30-year fixed rate mortgage at 30 (sic) percent.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Now it averages 8 percent.\par \par \uc2 Nobody's going to sell their home and buy another home at that, because it's thousands of dollars more for the same type of home.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: It grinds the economy to a halt. It's a disaster.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Right.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: That's the word you used, and that's the appropriate word.\par \par \uc2 These are policy choices that got us in this situation. If you remember, if you rewind in your mind back to right before the pandemic began, in the Trump administration, we had the greatest economy in the history of the world. And it wasn't by happenstance.\par \par \uc2 It's because we cut taxes, cut regulations. We focused on American energy dominance. We were doing and following all the policies that led to economic success and growth for all Americans, everyone in every demographic. All boats were rising.\par \par \uc2 We can achieve that again, but you have to do almost exactly the opposite of what the Biden administration is pursuing and doing. This is not rocket science, but we're so far down the road now that we have to make these changes quickly. And we're trying to urge our Democrat colleagues to take a look, again, at the basic facts.\par \par \uc2 I mean, interest rates are so high now. They're going to continue to rise, apparently.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Double-digit, I can see it coming.\par \par \uc2 Let me ask maybe the question this way, because you do have such a small majority. And, obviously, that didn't help Kevin McCarthy. I think you should get rid of the motion to vacate and raise the bar dramatically, 20, 25 people, at least.\par \par \uc2 My question is specifically on the economy. If you pass your budgets in the House, you're going to send it over to the Senate, they're going to rip it up line by line. You will go into conference. But the only way you can succeed is if you, as speaker, are able to hold your slim majority.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: That's going to be a Herculean task.\par \par \uc2 How do you plan on doing that? I...\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: With truth and transparency and relationships.\par \par \uc2 This is a business that's based on that. And my colleagues trust me. I'm grateful for that. It's earned. And I'm going to do well by them. But we're going to govern well. We're going to show the American people we can.\par \par \uc2 When you get into the negotiations in the conference committee, the speaker of the House and the leader of our party has to have a strong hand. And so if we can keep the team together, keep rowing in the same direction, look, everybody's got to give up on some of their preferences. None of us are going to get everything we want.\par \par \uc2 We never -- I will never ask any of my colleagues to compromise core principles, because I share those principles with them, right? But \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  used to teach us, I'd rather get 80 percent of what I want than go over the cliff with the flag waving. I still believe in that idea.\par \par \uc2 And I'm very optimistic. I am bullish on the future of America. I am bullish on our chances to change the way Washington works, Sean. We have a once-in-a-lifetime opportunity here. That's the vision I have cast to my colleagues. I believe it to my core.\par \par \uc2 We're going to take this day by day, decision by decision, but keep everybody together and move the ball forward for the American people. I genuinely believe that we can get this done.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: One of the things that I have said over and over again to anybody that will listen -- although I can look in this camera and say, honestly, nobody ever listens to me.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Not true. Not true.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: But I try -- is, it's my strong belief that all Republicans, you're either going to win together or you're going to be -- in the eyes of the American people, you're going to win on your agenda or you're going to lose.\par \par \uc2 And I don't see any way of winning if you don't all stick together. I know you have this disparate group of egos, if you will. I hope everybody will maybe hear your admonition and work together as a team and go forward, fix our border. Can we become energy-dominant and maybe start paying down our debt? Can we come up with sound foreign policy to help out our allies at a time of -- a dire time for them?\par \par \uc2 I mean, these challenges are -- run deep. And then you have the issue of law and order, safety, security, open borders, pretty scary times. Do you think this country is in decline?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: I think, right now, we're in very desperate times. I think the hour is late and the crisis is great.\par \par \uc2 But here's why I'm encouraged. In previous generations of Americans, from the framers, the founders of this country all the way through, times the Revolutionary period, the Civil War, the Great Depression, the other World Wars, when the leaders here got in trouble that was beyond the scope of what they could solve themselves individually or collectively, you know what they did?\par \par \uc2 They sought divine guidance for it. We have "In God in trust" right above the rostrum there, and I pointed that out in my speech.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Is this the Ben Franklin moment?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: It is a Ben Franklin moment.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Constitutional Convention?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Exactly right. It's the same thing.\par \par \uc2 And we're calling on one another to humble ourselves and recognize that the challenges are so great. But that's where real opportunity is presented. I believe that God is not done with America yet, OK? I still believe in that motto. It's not just a quaint saying to me. It's what the nation was founded upon, this belief.\par \par \uc2 It's what distinguishes us from all these other communist, Marxist, socialist countries around the world. It's the key to our greatness. And we ought to appeal to that. And I believe, if we do, that God is going to give us his favor, and I believe we're going to solve these problems.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I did ask you before the show -- and I will make this the last question.\par \par \uc2 And I said, because I believe that, if you take this majority and you can somehow pull these disparate groups together, I do believe it would be good for the country, because, as -- I'm very clear about my conservatism. I believe those policies are best for our country. And I want the best for our country.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: I do too.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I look at this country. I have had a life, frankly, that's undeserved.\par \par \uc2 And I just really believe -- so, I asked you, I said would it be OK if we came back when you get settled -- you have had less than 48 hours, and we appreciate you doing the interview -- and bring the disparate groups in a room and do it for an hour and see what issues that we can find full agreement on?\par \par \uc2 I would like to know what those issues are and where the disagreements are. Will you be willing to have us back?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Of course. We're working on that. And that's a great admonition.\par \par \uc2 Look, this place is filled with people of good faith, of deep conviction, and they understand the necessity of working together.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: There's some big egos, Mr. Speaker.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Yes. Well, but Washington has that sometimes. We're going to work through it. I'm optimistic.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right, Mr. Speaker, we appreciate your time.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Thank you. Sure.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Thank you for being with us.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Thank you. Yes, sir.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. And by the way also breaking tonight, we have other breaking news and major updates to bring you from the Biden corruption probes. Now, according to Senator \uc2 Chuck Grassley\uc2  of Iowa, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  is now sitting on criminal allegations involving Joe, Hunter and James Biden from over separate confidential human sources.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, earlier this week, Scott Brady, the former U.S. attorney for the Western District of Pennsylvania, told the House Judiciary Committee in a closed door interview that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  literally hindered the Hunter Biden probe and that special counsel David Weiss actually skipped a briefing on the Biden bribery allegations.\par \par \uc2 Also breaking tonight, House Oversight Chairman James Comer is now demanding that the White House provide records proving that the $200,000 payment to Joe from his brother was, in fact, a loan. I guess that paperwork would be easy to produce. Here's when we lent the 200, here's the proof that it was lent, there you go.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, fellow House Judiciary Committee member Jim Jordan is with us. Well you just heard from your -- your new speaker.\par \par \uc2 REP. JIM JORDAN (R-OH): Yeah.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Are you glad it's not you?\par \par \uc2 JORDAN: Well, I think -- I think your audience heard, Sean, what a good man Mike Johnson is, and I'm proud to have him as our speaker of the House. He's worked on our committee, he's a man of just solid character, as you can see, a Christian man who cares about this great nation. And I think he's going to work his tail off.\par \par \uc2 Plus, he's just as you could see, just a -- just a sharp guy who's argued cases in front of the Supreme Court. He's the kind of guy we need at this particular point in American history. I'm proud to count him as a friend and proud that he's the speaker of the House.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Let me ask you, Jim, as the head of this powerful Judiciary. You're looking into the weaponization, whether our Department of Justice and our \uc2 FBI\uc2  have been weaponized. We have all this new information coming out every day. We have three committees really doing independent investigations -- though, they really do correlate and function as one big one.\par \par \uc2 JORDAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: On the issue of what we found out about these 40, you know, people that real -- real sources to the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and you read the story about how it was not brought to the attention, how the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and the DOJ seem to do nothing about it? This is where the first 1023 form came, isn't it?\par \par \uc2 JORDAN: Right. And all that -- that finding that Mr. Grassley talked about, Senator Grassley talked about was buttress by what we learned in the deposition with U.S. Attorney Scott Brady earlier this week, because Mr. Brady was tasked by the attorney general of the United States on January 3rd, 2020, Sean, to be the clearing house for all information related to \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 , the investigation and Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 And so, he goes to the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , Mr. Brady says, give me that information, I want to take a look at it. We'll get it to the proper U.S. attorney who's handling the investigative work, and the \uc2 FBI\uc2  didn't tell him about the confidential human source for six months, six months.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Hey, Congressman -- Congressman, I got to stop you. Apparently, air strikes have taken place.\par \par \uc2 Jennifer Griffin at the Pentagon right now.\par \par \uc2 Jennifer, this sounds like huge news.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRIFFIN, FOX NEWS CHIEF NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT: Sean, we can report based on senior military sources that U.S. war planes have carried out airstrikes tonight inside Syria against Iranian proxy forces. We don't know the number of strikes. We were told earlier today that to expect that perhaps F-15s and F-16s would be involved in the air strikes, but we don't have confirmation yet how -- about how many war planes. But those strikes have been carried out, I'm told. It is in response to the more than dozen strikes against U.S. bases.\par \par \uc2 The message is a clear message designed to Iran and its proxy forces to stop carrying out these drone and rocket attacks against U.S. bases. But I'm told that the planes have safely left the area and than the air strikes are complete at this time. They were targeting multiple locations inside Syria and Iranian proxy forces, Sean.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: So important, Jennifer. Thank you for that breaking news, especially in light of the fact that American forces have been attacked by both Iraq and by Syria. We appreciate that news.\par \par \uc2 We continue, though, with Judiciary Committee Chairman Jim Jordan.\par \par \uc2 I want you to respond to that in the less than two minutes we have left, tell us about this interview that you did in your committee and what you learned.\par \par \uc2 JORDAN: Well, with Scott Brady, we also learned that I asked Mr. Brady in that interview, Sean, when did you learn about the laptop? And he got a smile on his face and he said, when the story went public in "The New York Post" on October 14th. So they didn't even tell Scott Brady about the laptop and the pertinent information on that lop top about Ukraine and \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's dealings with Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 That shows you what Speaker Johnson talked about, how these agencies have been turned against the American people. When they won't share that information with the individual tasked by the attorney general of the United States to look into this and filter it out the way it's supposed to be, that tells you how bad it is. And then you have what Senator Grassley has added to that the last couple days, with these 40 individuals. That's how serious the situation is and why we're proceeding in our impeachment inquiry phase of our oversight work.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Real quick, Jim, you know, the fact that Syria and Iraq have been -- American soldiers have been attacked in both Syria and Iraq recently, I'm actually -- my initial instinct is to be glad that they fought back they fired back.\par \par \uc2 JORDAN: Yep, yep.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I'm happy at that news. Your reaction, quick.\par \par \uc2 JORDAN: No, same here. I want to get the details but, you know, when someone attacks the United States servicemen and women, we have to respond back. So I want to see what's happened here, but it shows you how serious this overall situation is and why we need strength projected from the Oval Office.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. Jim Jordan, thank you as always. That's all the time we have left this evening. Stand by though, continuing