this for him? He raises $43 million for this Anti- Racist Institute. Several years later now, the news is that it's been halved. Half of the people involved in this department have been laid off, their funds are drying up, and they've produced, as you point out, zero research.\par \par \uc2 Because what kind of research is there to produce on anti-racism? But it manages to loan 600 grand to a mortgage, for a mortgage, on that four and a half million-dollar townhome for an unnamed professor.\par \par \uc2 It's hard not to conclude that professor is Ibram X. Kendi, when the trust that controls it is his brother-in-law. Which I don't think should go unnoticed here that his brother-in-law, just for the record, is also the head of global content at \uc2 YouTube\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 If you need to see how incestuous the entire enterprise is, the system that controls censorship and pushes certain propaganda, just look at this one single story. It was a grift, Kayleigh. This entire thing, from BLM to anti-racism, was a grift that played upon white guilt in our society, to what is clear at this point, bilk millions from people from others own personal benefit.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: And Kendi has talked about the plight of student loans. So just think about that hypocrisy there, right? Does he actually believe it? Who knows?\par \par \uc2 Will, now to a different topic. We know you're a big country music fan. Oh, so am I. Good taste. Here's what singer Maren Morris said about the industry that helped her make millions.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: I think country music is such a like powerful arm of that because it is so steeped in, you know, weirdly, like, patriotism or quasi patriotism, lot's of like overt hyper masculinity, whiteness, I mean, that's just like how it's been from the jump.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Patriotism, I mean, I just think about \uc2 Toby Keith\uc2  brought to you, courtesy of the USA after 9-11. I mean this is, you know, this is what makes country music great. People love it.\par \par \uc2 CAIN: I welcome Maren Morris and her exit outside of country music. I don't think she ever really wanted to be in country music. She said that it's -- she considers it a positive step the country is getting more worldly, more like pop music.\par \par \uc2 I don't want the world to be in this monolithic, cultural, blase, nothingness where we all wear the same clothes and listen to the same music and are forced to celebrate Taylor Swift. If you like her, cool. But what I like about country is it's regional. It should speak authentically.\par \par \uc2 And by the way, if it was born out of Appalachia with influences from the blues or from Western swing, and that represented a largely white audience at one time, and it certainly is an exclusionary that loved America, what's wrong with that? We don't look at mariachi music and go, you know, it's really not diverse enough.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Right.\par \par \uc2 CAIN: We don't look at rap music and go, you know, it's weird. It's not enough white rappers out there. No one does that. But if it's country music, it's a problem that it represents, what? An America that loves America?\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 CAIN: I don't understand at all. Maybe it just wasn't the genre for her. And by the way, OK, she's realized that. Have fun in pop music.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Yeah, that's right. So quick question for you. My husband -- I love 90's country. My husband is so anti, this like pop music infiltrating country music. Is that -- does that grate you the way it does my husband?\par \par \uc2 CAIN: Absolutely. I, actually, like a more niche version of it, which is Texas country, red, red clay dirt. Country music is my genre.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: I love it. Good taste. Will Cain, thank you so much. We'll be watching you tomorrow on Fox and Friends. Watch every weekend. But first, watch Will tomorrow. And that's it for us tonight. I'm Kaylee McEnany in for Laura Ingraham. Don't forget to tune in weekdays at 12 P.M. Eastern time for when I co-host, Outnumbered. Jesse Watters is next.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020231007eja600001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Interview with Rep. Bryon Donalds (R-FL); Interview with Rep. French Hill (R-AR); Congress Now Has 45 Days to Reach Another Spending Agreement; Senator Manchin Clashes With White House Over Energy Policy; Ukraine Aid Not Included in Funding Bill; GOP Presidential Candidates Differ on Ukraine Aid Spending; Haley Slams Ramaswamy's Comments About TikTok\b0\par\par\uc2 Shannon Bream, Aishah Hasnie, Lucas Tomlinson, Juan Williams \par \uc2 10,548 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 October 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Fox News Sunday\par \uc2 FOXN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: I'm Shannon Bream.\par \par \uc2 Buying time, Congress gives the government funded for another 45 days but fails to resolve any of the major issues that led to the brink of a shutdown.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY): There will be no government shutdown.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice-over): Congress manages to put a Band-Aid over major differences for now.\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA), SPEAKER OF THE HOUSE: I'm sure every bet you had was government was going to shut down.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Leaving many to wonder if hardliners will try to remove McCarthy as speaker.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MATT GAETZ\uc2  (R-FL): So, all he had to do was comply with the agreement that he made with us in January.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Can Congress overcome a bitter split between House Republicans over spending or will the government face the risk of a short all over again in mid-November?\par \par \uc2 We'll ask two Republican Congressmen Byron Donalds and French Hill, with different takes on what needs to be done to bridge the divide.\par \par \uc2 Then --\par \par \uc2 SCHUMER: Her integrity shown like a beacon across the Senate and across the country for all to see.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Remembering a trailblazer -- Senator \uc2 Dianne Feinstein\uc2 , the longest- serving female senator in U.S. history.\par \par \uc2 West Virginia Senator Joe Manchin on Feinstein's legacy and the fight to fill her seat, and what the mounting legal problems for New Jersey Senator \uc2 Bob Menendez\uc2  mean for the very thin Democratic Senate majority.\par \par \uc2 Plus --\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY (R), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Now, Americans want to say, okay, what's the difference between all of you?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Just days after a very fiery second presidential debate, Nikki Haley joins us to make the case why she is the best person to challenge \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  for the GOP nomination.\par \par \uc2 Then, hard to bear. The National Zoo prepares to say goodbye to their three giant pandas after China recalls the fuzzy diplomats. We'll ask us our Sunday panel how all of this is playing out as relations between Beijing and Washington continued to deteriorate.\par \par \uc2 All, right now, on "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM (on camera): Hello from FOX News in Washington.\par \par \uc2 Congress narrowly avoiding a government shutdown. President Biden signing a 45-day short-term spending bill into law just before the midnight deadline. The Senate and House working late into Saturday to get that deal done.\par \par \uc2 The last-minute agreement comes as House Republicans tried and failed to pass a separate temporary funding bill that would have contained policy items like border security and spending cuts.\par \par \uc2 In a moment, we will bring in two Republican congressmen with some opposing views on all of these spending deals, Bryon Donalds and French Hill.\par \par \uc2 But first, Aishah Hasnie joins us from Capitol Hill -- Aishah.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Shannon, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 Congress once again kicking the can down the road on government funding, now to 45 days. It will keep the government funded at the fiscal year 2023 spending levels. It did add $16 billion into disaster relief.\par \par \uc2 Not in this solution is Ukraine aid and border security, two very important topics for these sparring parties. In fact, Democratic Senator Michael Bennet held up the Senate vote for a few hours last night over the lack of Ukraine funding, but eventually relented after Leaders \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  and \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  announced that they will fight for more aid in a separate measure.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SCHUMER: After trying to take our government hostage, MAGA Republicans won nothing.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: There was a lot more drama over in the House, Shannon. Democrats originally trying to stall the bill, angry at the Speaker Kevin McCarthy that he dropped the text last-minute.\par \par \uc2 And take a look at this. \uc2 Capitol police\uc2  now investigating New York Congressman \uc2 Jamaal Bowman\uc2  after he pulled the fire alarm during the proceedings. He claims that it was all an accident.\par \par \uc2 Eventually, though, Democrats helped McCarthy pass this bill. Ninety Republicans voted no. Hardliners who wanted the speaker to focus solely on passing a new slimmer budget.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. TIM BURCHETT (R-TN): Might tell us to pass a continuing resolution, so we won't have to pass another continued resolution. Well, that line of thinking is like telling a crackhead that I'm going to give you more crack to get you off of crack.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASHIE: Shannon, we start the new week here with questions about Speaker McCarthy's job. Congressman \uc2 Matt Gaetz\uc2  reportedly said that he will, in fact, file a motion to vacate the speaker.\par \par \uc2 Shannon, the speaker over the last couple of days has responded to those threats by saying, bring it on -- Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We'll watch and see if it happens.\par \par \uc2 Aishah Hasnie, reporting from the Hill. Thank you very much for the update.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, Republican Congressman Byron Donalds out of Florida.\par \par \uc2 Good to see you, sir.\par \par \uc2 REP. BYRON DONALDS (R-FL): Good morning. Good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. I'm going to ask you, first of all, why did you miss this one vote and not vote? Everybody knew it was coming, it's a big deal.\par \par \uc2 DONALDS: Right.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: You put something on X, formerly known as \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , but the replies are pretty brutal.\par \par \uc2 DONALDS: Look, here's what happened. I was coming up the elevator in the Capitol building to go vote. They closed the vote down because there were members on the House floor who were changing their votes from yes to no. I was told that there were senators on our floor making our leadership to close the votes so the measure would pass because it needed two thirds.\par \par \uc2 The leadership knew I was no. If it was a yes, they would have held the vote open for me. They didn't. It's that simple.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. Definite no.\par \par \uc2 Here's something for the president last night saying this: We should never been in this position the first place. Just a few months ago, Speaker McCarthy and I reached a budget agreement to avoid precisely this type of manufactured crisis. Extreme House Republicans tried to walk away from that deal by demanding drastic cuts and they failed.\par \par \uc2 So, a couple things. First of all --\par \par \uc2 DONALDS: Right.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: -- which deal was in place? Is it -- did the speaker keep his word with the White House? Did he keep it with you? Are those different things?\par \par \uc2 DONALDS: Well, what's really happened here is that speaker had an agreement with us in January about pre-COVID spending levels. The deal that was cutting in the -- in the debt ceiling deal, the speaker was told multiple times by myself in particular, and I told the entire leadership, you don't have the votes at that spending level from the debt ceiling deal. There are House Republicans who will not vote for that, and it's frankly more than House Freedom Caucus members. It was more than that.\par \par \uc2 So there was never an agreement on what the top line spending was. That's why we ended up at the situation.\par \par \uc2 What I will add to that also is, there was a measure that was produced by me and a couple other members. We would have cut at a minimum, 8 percent off the top of the federal agencies and secure the border with HR2, stopping the transition of migrants in our country which is being done by \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . There were some House conservatives that didn't agree with that plan. They wanted to go with 12 appropriation bills.\par \par \uc2 My concern was, what you were going to see was a clean CR, and unfortunately, I was correct, that's exactly what happened.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, that measure that you tried to offer in substitution was Friday.\par \par \uc2 DONALDS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And you didn't get pass those who were objecting on the individual bills, what they wanted to see.\par \par \uc2 DONALDS: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. So, I want to play something from Congressman \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2 . He's the House Democratic leader, the top Democrat.\par \par \uc2 Here's what he says.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. HAKEEM JEFFRIES (D-NY), HOUSE MINORITY LEADER: The American people have won. The extreme MAGA Republicans have lost. It was a victory for the American people and a complete and total surrender by right-wing extremists who throughout the year have tried to hijack the Congress.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So that language, extreme MAGA Republicans, right wing extremists, I'm assuming he's including you in that based on your votes and the things that you've said.\par \par \uc2 DONALDS: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: What do you say to that? He says you guys lost.\par \par \uc2 What did you extract from what we saw play out in the last few days?\par \par \uc2 DONALDS: Well, I'm going to be honest. We did not get anything out of this continued resolution. The border is still unsecured in our country. Why is that? That's because \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and the Democrats got their way.\par \par \uc2 The Democrat members are very happy with this deal. I think is a terrible deal for the American people because cutting this government spending down must happen.\par \par \uc2 Inflation is still running rampant. That's not MAGA Republicans. That's MAGA Republicans, that's common sense.\par \par \uc2 Having a secure border, that's MAGA Republicans. That's common sense.\par \par \uc2 The Democrats are extremely radical on this position. And so I would implore my colleagues that whatever is going to happen over the next 45 days, we got to stand together, because at the end of the day, it is the Democrats who don't want to secure our border, they do not want to get the fiscal health of this nation back on track.\par \par \uc2 They do not care about what's affecting the men and women in our country, regardless of their politics.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Do you think they will stand together? Do you think the speaker is in trouble?\par \par \uc2 DONALDS: I think he is in trouble. Part of that is because there has to be some level of strong leadership in our chamber. I'm just going to be totally blunt, there are a lot of trust issues in my chamber right now, where people feel on both sides of our conference that everybody is not going to hold hands and continue to do this work together.\par \par \uc2 That was indicative also of what happened yesterday on the floor, where it took more Democrats to pass this -- this deal to keep government open. The same thing happened on the debt deal.\par \par \uc2 But at the end of the day, we've got to find a way to come together, stay focused as a -- as a conference. That's what I want to do.\par \par \uc2 But border security has to be the number one thing that we find for going forward. We must secure our border.\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  simply won't do it. He wants this catastrophe which is affecting all of our cities, and the only people standing in his way are House Republicans.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We're going to talk about the record high numbers about that later in the show. But let me ask you, if there's a motion to vacate, would you vote against the speaker? And do you think Democrats would help you do that?\par \par \uc2 DONALDS: Frankly, I don't know right now. I've got to really think about that, because there's a lot of stuff going on in that building that's behind us.\par \par \uc2 What Democrats do, I can't even speak to it right now. They don't even want to take care of the key thing in this country -- securing our border and frankly getting our government to just do its job and no more.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Congressman Donalds, good to see you. Thanks for popping in.\par \par \uc2 DONALDS: All right. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 All right. Joining us now, fellow Republican Congressman French Hill, who's on \uc2 the House Financial Services Committee\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Congressman, thank you for being with us.\par \par \uc2 I want to play something from one of the folks who voted no yesterday. One of your fellow members who says you got this wrong. This is Congressman Tim Burchett.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. TIM BURCHETT (R-TN): We further abdicated our duties. It's a big word for me. But we did. We decided to kick the can down the road for 45 more days, and sweetened the pot, put some extra stuff and therefore some people would vote for it. And that's just not what leadership should do.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Says not what leadership should do and you heard from Congressman Donalds thinks that leadership got this wrong.\par \par \uc2 REP. FRENCH HILL (R-AR): Well, Shannon. It's good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 It's great to hear my good friend Byron Donalds' remarks.\par \par \uc2 I voted for Byron Donalds' continued resolution that secure the border and cut spending 8 percent. I thought it was an outstanding proposal for a stopgap spending measure, including, Shannon, something you haven't talked about, which is that two-thirds of spending is mandatory spending.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Uh-huh.\par \par \uc2 HILL: And Mr. Donald's' resolution and -- put together with his group also had a commission to reform mandatory spending programs, something that's a much more big fiscal problem for our country.\par \par \uc2 So, look, I supported that, it failed and failed because all the Democrats voted against it, plus 21 Republicans. And we had six Republicans tell us, Shannon, they just wouldn't vote for any continuing resolution, period, full stop. They prefer the government essentially to shut down while we continue to do (ph) the appropriations bills. I think that's the wrong approach.\par \par \uc2 We've passed 70 percent of spending across the House floor. The Senate has passed none. This four weeks will let us bring the rest of those individual appropriations bills to the floor that are substantially lower spending levels than \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and Kevin McCarthy's debt ceiling deal. And they have important conservative policy riders that turn back the Biden and \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  policies of the last two years.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, given that there is so much internal friction with the GOP, people who feel betrayed. You heard what Congressman Donalds said. He's not sure how he would vote right now if the speaker -- a motion came out to pull him out of there.\par \par \uc2 HILL: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: You understand their skepticism that people look at 12 appropriations bills in 45 days with that as a backdrop, and don't -- well, they think they may just be in the same place in 45 days, 44 days again.\par \par \uc2 HILL: What I would -- I would say we wouldn't be. I understand the trust point that Byron made, and I think that there are people inside our conference, freshmen, sophomores, and other members that have concerns about trust in the system. But we made a commitment to do 12 appropriations bills, and we've been trying to do that since July.\par \par \uc2 We got one passed in July. We got others passed this week. We've now approved in the House with full House Republican vote. No Democratic votes basically, 72 percent or so of all federal spending.\par \par \uc2 So, over the next four weeks, we have to prove the other 28 percent of federal spending. It's not all 12. It's --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Right, and --\par \par \uc2 HILL: It's a -- it's a subset. And the Senates -- and the Senate has done none, and this will hopefully get them into the game and get their work done as well.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yeah, and that 28 percent, very sticky. That's where the details become very difficult.\par \par \uc2 I want to ask you about Congressman Bowman. There is a still picture of him. He admits that he did pull a fire alarm yesterday during a sort of a chaotic time on the Hill that led to evacuation.\par \par \uc2 He says this though: I activated the fire alarm mistakenly thinking it would open the door, he was trying to get through those doors. I want to be very clear, this was not me, in any way going to delay any vote.\par \par \uc2 He said pulling that he thought would open those doors. Do you accept his investigation? Will there be an investigation?\par \par \uc2 HILL: Well, look, all my kids in elementary school went to Safety Town. They know how to pull a fire alarm and what happens when you do it in a public building.\par \par \uc2 I think it's just emblematic of the fact that Democrats can't figure out how to cut spending and have a right approach to government. They can't even run a fire alarm.\par \par \uc2 So I look forward to the investigation. We'll see the full videotape. We'll see what he's up to. Either way, it doesn't look good for him.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, Democrats are celebrating this morning, though.\par \par \uc2 You got Congressman \uc2 Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez\uc2  tweeting this late yesterday: Here's what went down: we just won a clean 45-day government extension, stripped the GOP's earlier 30 percent cut to \uc2 Social Security Administration\uc2 , et cetera, staved off last-minute anti-immigrant hijinks and averted shutdown for now. People will get paychecks and MTG threw a tantrum on the way out. Win-win.\par \par \uc2 If they are celebrating what happened yesterday, how is that a win for you?\par \par \uc2 HILL: Well, it's a win because we're finishing our commitment we made to America in 2022, that we would bring up individual spending bills. We would debate them on the floor with an open, long-standing rule process. We would get conservative spending levels that cut spending in 2024 over 2023. That we would get people back to work in this country by adding work requirements to the welfare programs, which President Biden caved on in the debt ceiling deal.\par \par \uc2 So, what we're doing is we want to lock in the conservative wins in the debt ceiling deal with lower spending more conservative policies, get some help from --\par \par \uc2 HILL: -- deal. So what we're doing is we want to lock in the conservative wins in the debt ceiling deal with lower spending, more conservative policies, get some help from that in the Senate. But the only way we do that, Shannon, is to pass those bills. And that's the debate. The debate in the House conference wasn't whether we should pass them or not at lower spending levels, the debate was, should we do it while the government shutdown?\par \par \uc2 And I don't think not paying border agents, not paying the men and women in uniform for four weeks, while we complete debating bills for the last 28% of spending is smart government.\par \par \uc2 Plus -- plus, \uc2 Moody's\uc2  this week announced that they may consider downgrading the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2 's credit rating. And we saw the 10-year Treasury rate go to four and a half percent. These are warning signs that we need to deal with mandatory spending programs. And we need to go back to pre-pandemic spending levels. That's the debate we need to have while we secure the border. All those are priorities, and those are shared by all Republicans in the House.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Very difficult decisions to come. Congressman French Hill, thanks for stopping in.\par \par \uc2 HILL: You bet, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Up next major events in the Senate this week as well as the House, Senator Joe Manchin joins us on the bribery allegations against New Jersey Senator \uc2 Bob Menendez\uc2  and mounting calls for his resignation. And we also remember the history making life and legacy of \uc2 Dianne Feinstein\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Senator \uc2 Dianne Feinstein\uc2  died at her home in Washington D.C. this week at the age of 90. She was the longest serving female senator in American history. Her final months were marked by failing health and by folks frankly jockeying for her seat.\par \par \uc2 Lucas Tomlinson is live outside the White House now with how the President and members on both sides of the aisle are remembering her legacy and talking about what comes next. Hello, Lucas.\par \par \uc2 LUCAS TOMLINSON, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Shannon, last night former House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  flew back to San Francisco with the casket. President Biden offered his condolences.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) U.S. PRESIDENT: She was a historic figure, trailblazer for women, and a great friend.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  and other senators mourn Feinstein at the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2 , (D-NY), SENATE MAJORITY LEADER: So today, we grieve. We look at that desk and we know what we have lost.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: Feinstein's death means the Democrats now have an even slimmer majority 50 to 49, giving potential leverage to embattled Democratic Senator \uc2 Bob Menendez\uc2 , charged along with his wife with bribery allegations to enrich three New Jersey businessmen and the Egyptian Government.\par \par \uc2 BOB MENENDEZ, (D) NEW JERSEY SENATOR: I will continue to cast votes on behalf of the people of New Jersey as I have for 18 years. I think Schumer may need those votes.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  was the first of 31 Democratic senators to call for Menendez to resign.\par \par \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (D), PENNSYLVANIA SENATOR: He's got to go. He's got to go. I can't imagine, you know, how he could possibly explain any of that kind of a thing.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: According to the indictment, Senator Menendez accepted hundreds of thousands of dollars in bribes, in cash and gold bars. Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, Lucas Tomlinson at the White House. Thank you, Lucas.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, West Virginia Democratic Senator Joe Manchin. Welcome back to Fox News Sunday.\par \par \uc2 JOE MANCHIN, (D) WEST VIRGINIA SENATOR: Great to be with you, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, let's start with those big stories there. First, your thoughts on the loss of Senator Feinstein?\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: Such a -- such a beautiful person, it really was. And I felt I was embarrassed by some of our colleagues asking her, you know, maybe a month or two or three ago, that you should step aside in this and that. There's another person to serve more honorably than \uc2 Dianne Feinstein\uc2 . There's not a person that basically tried to bring people together, than \uc2 Dianne Feinstein\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 I was there -- I was -- I had about 12, 13 years with her. And I could not have had a better role model, you know. We might not have agreed in our politics, might have been different. But she was so compassionate. She was understanding. She's -- I know, you can get there, Joe. And I said, Dianne, I can maybe meet you halfway, I can't go all the way. And we would find a way to move. And that's really what it's about. How do you find a way to serve the people of this great country, and also the people that you represent?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and that's consistently what we've heard from both sides of the aisle in the wake of her passing.\par \par \uc2 Now, let's talk about Senator Menendez, you're not among those who have called for him to resign. Can he serve effectively? I mean, is this -- the great group that have stepped for the Senate Democrats who have stepped forward, is this more about they're worried about what happens to his seat next year? Or are they outraged by what they believe about these allegations?\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: Bob, we all know Bob, I've served with Bob for quite some time. And he's very effective. He's very -- he's very strong person. And he spoke to the caucus the other day. And that was just unbelievable, the strength it took to go up there in front of your colleagues and say, let me tell you something. I've seen every one of you, every one of you speak eloquently about the rule of law, and how we're so different than any other place in the world. All I'm asking for is to be treated the same, nothing more, nothing less. Everyone is innocent until proven guilty. Let me defend myself. And that's all he asked for. And I can't disagree with it.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So you're going to let the legal process play out?\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: It should play out.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. So, I've asked you a few times about your 2024 plans. Many people have, are you going to run for your seat in the Senate, are you going to run third party? But let's start with this. You said this week that you hadn't thought about whether you would vote for President Biden this time, which a lot of us thought was a little bit difficult to believe. But how much may that have to do with the idea that you didn't get what you thought you were going to get in exchange for your vote on the Inflation Reduction Act?\par \par \uc2 You said this week, instead of embracing the all of the above energy bill that was signed into law, this administration has once again decided to put their radical political agenda over American energy security, and the American people will pay the price. You've said the administration has broken the law. You've said that they've lied to the American people. Did they lie to you?\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: Well, they're trying everything they can to basically implement a piece of legislation they never passed. And they've been taken to court twice. We got the \uc2 Mountain Valley Pipeline\uc2 . And now the leasing in the Gulf. And I will quote you what my Republican colleagues have said about the Inflation Reduction Act, in their brief supporting the coal or the oil companies who basically sued this administration for taking 6 million acres off the ability to lease which is not the way the bill.\par \par \uc2 They said this, the IRA is a balanced, diverse, complex and overlapping considerations, including growth in conservation, domestic needs, and global positioning and security and diplomacy. This is my Republican. These are the energy leaders of both the House and the Senate. Understand it's a balanced bill. And we're producing more energy today than we ever have, more oil 4.6 billion barrels this year, more gas, natural gas, 37 trillion cubic feet. We've never done that before. And we're producing more LNG to help our friends and allies overseas.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But are you getting what you thought you were going to get when it comes to electric vehicles and battery production and bringing manufacturing back to the U.S.?\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: Not as quickly as it is supposed to be. They're basically breaking every bond in that bill, that bill was very, very explicit than what we had to do in order to bring manufacturing back and not be reliant on foreign energy supplies, such as China. And they're trying to put more vehicles out, then basically what the market will, will digest right now or want. And it's wrong. It's a bad energy policy. We're trying to keep the -- the guardrails on.\par \par \uc2 Here's the thing, they cannot do what they want to do, because the bill prohibits. You can't just go with all renewables. If you're not producing the energy the country needs. We're producing a cleaner than anywhere in the world. Why can't we walk and chew gum at the same time?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, we'll leave it to you to sort out with the administration if they live up to what you thought you were getting in exchange there. That vote has probably hurt you. I think fair to say in West Virginia, should you choose to run again? There are conversations about how much that will impact things. There's one outlet that has said, it's driven up your disapproval numbers to the point that you're one of the most unpopular senators in America, not sure how that factors into you as well?\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: I don't think that's quite --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I think you're being likeable.\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: It's not that --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Whether -- whether -- whether people want to go vote for you in West Virginia, still we get out that conversation.\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: The people in West Virginia are seeing all that -- all of the rewards from that piece of legislation with the new investments coming. We have \uc2 Berkshire Hathaway\uc2  coming in. We have basically form energy. We're going to be building battery storage, or 1000 megawatts, things that we've never had a diversification.\par \par \uc2 Plus, we're producing more coal than we ever have, better and cleaner anywhere in the world. We're producing basically the MVP, 2 billion cubic feet more will go into the pipeline, helping the people in the northeast. Everything that we're doing, and basically this bill is a balanced bill has given us opportunities, we have never had before.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, we'll --\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: So people just have -- basically, people been demonizing it, but when my own Republican colleagues come out and say, this is a balanced -- balanced piece of legislation, that is good for America. They've said that in their brief, and they won, they won in courts.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. So we'll see whether you take this to the West Virginia voters, or whether you take it to a third party run. We've had these conversations. Here's what former \uc2 Obama\uc2  Campaign Manager, Jim Messina, said this week, this -- talking about these third party runs.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JIM MESSINA, FORMER OBAMA CAMPAIGN MANAGER: You and I remember in 2000, in Florida, where \uc2 Ralph Nader\uc2  gave the presidency to \uc2 George Bush\uc2 , so Democrats have that history, and they're going to understand that a vote for these third parties is a vote for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , I hope.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: You said you will not do this as a spoiler, that is not the goal that you have.\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: Jim -- I think Jim really forgot that basically, it was \uc2 Ross Perot\uc2  that elected \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 . So if he wants to look at it that way, the evaluation, why not have options? People are satisfied, right? And why not have option?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And people -- a lot of people like you as an option.\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And you've said once you make a decision, the target will be on your back. So you're waiting until the right time. But the fact is, the calendar is now going to start working against you. What is the timeline? Do you have any announcements to make this morning?\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: Not this morning.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK.\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: Let me just say this. I'll do it. I'm more concerned about the country right now. What you've seen, the theatrics have played out yesterday and up until yesterday is ridiculous.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But could have President Manchin fix that?\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: Well, the bottom line is you have to work in a bipartisan way. Democracy is still an experiment. It was never intended for an autocracy or one side to rule. You have to work together. Kevin McCarthy -- I praise Kevin McCarthy for what he did yesterday. He finally realized you can't reason with unreasonable people with the extremes. I hope that people were these people are represented. I hope their constituents finally say enough is enough. We want people that at least keep our government running and moving in the right direction. We can do that.\par \par \uc2 And I think yes, I can bring it together. That's all I've ever done. But there's other people, not just me. There's a lot of people that are basically in the center left, center right, that's how it's worked. And you saw that happen yesterday to move forward, keep the county -- you know, keep the government open.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I know you don't see President Trump in that place. Do you see President Biden in that place?\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: Well, he used to be.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So if you don't think either one of them are there, are you the alternative?\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: Well, we'll hoping. Maybe -- maybe they'll come back to rationale, thinking that hey, doesn't work, pushing everything to the extremes. This country does not run on the fringes, it never has and they can't start now. So they're either going to come back or we're going to bring it back.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: What's your timeline?\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: Well, I said before the end of the year, I will and we're still planning. I mean, if -- if something what you're saying would happen, there is no primaries, you just go right into the foray.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But West Virginia seems well underway, at least on the GOP side?\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: Yeah. It's exciting to watch. I mean, it's very entertaining as far as me watching in these people. I know everybody. So we'll see what happens there. The people don't look at me as, that's Joe Manchin, the Democrat, or that's so and so the Republican. They never, it's just Joe Manchin, he's going to be very independent. How's he's always voted for 47 years representing the people of West Virginia. And I've done everything I can in my power to make sure that they've got the best representation they can in a balanced way.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK.\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: I've said this, Shannon, if I can go home and explain it, I can vote for it. If I can't explain it to my friends and colleagues in West Virginia, I can't vote for it. You can put a gun to my head. I'm not going to vote for it.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We won't do that.\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: I know.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But we will pressure you to come back and give -- give us an answer before the end of the year.\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: Happy to do that.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Senator, thank you so much. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, fresh off the Fox Business GOP presidential debate, we're going to talk with Nikki Haley about a run for the White House accusations that she won't take a clear position on abortion and ask where we should draw the line on U.S. taxpayers financial commitment to Ukraine, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SCHUMER: We will not stop fighting for more economic and security assistance for Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 MITCH MCCONNELL\uc2 , (R-KY) MINORITY LEADER: I'm confident the Senate will pass further urgent assistance to Ukraine later this year.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So the Senate majority and minority leaders agree about their desire for more aid for Ukraine. Now, despite their efforts, no Ukraine money made it into this continuing resolution bill, that funds us now for 45 days. Resistance to billions more dollars of Ukraine aid has been growing among some Republicans primarily in the House. And one of the strongest supporters for keeping that aid flowing is former \uc2 U.N\uc2 . Ambassador Nikki Haley. She's out there defending it on the campaign trail. Well, I talked with the GOP presidential hopeful about that and much more this weekend.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So we are here in Washington against this backdrop of fights over funding, you know, keeping the government going but also how much the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2 , the taxpayer here is going to continue to spend on issues in Ukraine. It's one of the areas there is some daylight among the GOP candidates on the field of this issue of foreign policy. Here is what GOP Senator \uc2 Rand Paul\uc2  says about this. He says we have no extra money to send to Ukraine, our deficit this year will exceed $1.5 trillion borrowing money from China to send to Ukraine makes no sense. Do you think it makes sense?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY, (R) PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: You know, it's interesting that they give you that false choice. And this is why I think it's important for Americans to know the hard truth. Congress has only delivered a bill on time four times in 40 years. Think about that. That's one of their only jobs, four times in 40 years. And then you go and you look and we're having the shutdown of government or do we need to do it? Do we not do it? The reality is Republicans and Democrats all of them have been spending taxpayer dollars in a ridiculous way. They just take a budget from last year, add more to it and keep going. So the false narrative of we can either pay for certain things or Ukraine is wrong. What Ukraine is, is national security.\par \par \uc2 Now, I will put the blame on \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , and the President is always supposed to show leadership on why things are funded. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has done a terrific job of really explaining to the American people and making it transparent of where this money goes. The fact that this is actually going to prevent war, the fact that this is only three and a half percent of our national budget. The -- three and a half percent of our national defense budget. The fact that, you know, this goes back to 11 European countries are spending more than us. But what is this going to? It's going to weapons. It's going to replenish our stockpile. It's going to make sure Ukraine has what they need. And it's going to make sure that if they win, this is not a win for Russia or China but it's preventing war, so that it doesn't go into Poland and other \uc2 NATO\uc2  countries.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and -- but to be clear, we have a financial House that's out of order here in Washington, none of us at home could run our budgets this way. Governors can't get away with this at the state level. I mean, you can't print extra money or go into further debt on a number of issues. So the fact is, we are spending money, taxpayer dollars on Ukrainian, we are borrowing enormously from China from other places that do not have our best interest in heart. So that is part of the calculation the fact that we are taking on debt to another country in the service of what we can send to Ukraine?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: You know, it is. We're $33 trillion in debt, we're having to borrow money just to make our interest payments. China owns some of that debt. But, you know, if you're going to look at that, why don't we look at the fact that why -- you know, Republicans put in 7.4 billion of pet projects and earmarks in the budget and Democrats put in 2.8 billion. Why don't we look at all the things we don't need to spend on? It's the reason why I think we need to go back to a budget that starts at zero. Says, what do we have to have and then work our way up? It's also why I think we need to put more money in taxpayers' pockets.\par \par \uc2 I want to eliminate the federal gas and diesel tax. I want to reduce taxes on middle class Americans and make our tax structure safer. I want to make the small business tax cuts permanent. They only made them temporary. Let's make them permanent.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, I think a lot of people have vilified the Freedom Caucus in this shutdown conversation. But let's be clear what the Freedom Caucus is really trying to do. They're trying to cut spending. Let's go back and have spending caps so that we make sure that we never overspend. Cut up the credit cards, quit borrowing. 8% of our budget has interest. I will stop the spending, I'll stop the borrowing, will eliminate the earmarks. And I will veto any spending bill that doesn't take us back to pre-COVID levels. That's how we'll get our country back on track.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: There'd be a lot of work for President Haley to do on that Friday, watching how this is playing out D.C. now. OK, so we'll see how the financial House will shake out.\par \par \uc2 I want to talk about a piece in Politico. It's a very lengthy piece in their magazine, saying that you essentially talk around tough topics. They quote one analyst saying, she'll adapt to whatever benefits her. It follows on the heels of a podcast with a \uc2 New York Times\uc2  columnist who said this, "For someone who makes a big deal about being straight with voters, Haley is awfully cagey, uses kind of vague language, trying hard to wink at both pro-choice and anti-abortion voters at the same time."\par \par \uc2 You have said it's not fair to talk -- and we've talked about this, to voters about the possibility of a federal ban on abortion, because you say the numbers simply are not there. But Governor DeSantis, Senator Scott, they know the numbers aren't there either. But they're willing to have the conversation. Here's Senator Scott.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT (R-SC), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: I like Nikki, I wish she was the same kind of candidate as she was as a -- as a person. But now she's kind of flipped the tune a little bit. You really just can't trust her. She will not join myself and \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  joins me on the debate stage for 15- week limit on national limit on saving lives. She won't do that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: If you had the numbers, would you commit to that type of federal ban?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: Yes, And I've said that many times, Shannon. I mean, the goal is how do we save as many babies as possible and support as many moms as possible? You know, when the stories like Politico or \uc2 New York Times\uc2 , when they say that, it's because they think they can't -- they can't push me in a lane. I believe what I believe and I say what I say. I've been a realist. In order to pass that bill, you have to have 60 Senate votes. We might have 45, pro- life senators. I refuse to demonize the issue of abortion. I refuse to have people divided on it. What I'm saying is, let's do what we can get done.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But it is one of those issues that in this country, people are extremely divided on. I mean, there's no way to -- to have people sit around and sing Kumbaya over the issue of abortion, maybe about the other part of the conversation, funding women, helping them figure things out, but not on the issue itself of whether that procedure should be available and who should pay for it?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: Well, I mean, and Shannon, why do I have so many Republican, Democrat and Independent women, coming to my side on this? It's because I've said, yes I'm unapologetically pro-life but I'm not going to judge people for being pro-choice. In a situation of a federal law, I think it was right that it went to the hands of the people. I think it's right that states are deciding.\par \par \uc2 But if you're going to talk about a federal law, let's be realistic. That's what I am. I'm a realist. Let's be realistic. Don't go and divide people on something that doesn't look possible. I -- anything that we can agree on. And that's why I said, if we can ban late term abortions, if we can encourage adoptions, if we can make sure women don't go to jail or get the death penalty for an abortion, let's be realistic on it. And what however, we can find consensus on, that saves more babies, and that supports more mobs. That's my goal at the end of the day. But I'm not going to hate over it, I'm going to make sure that we're loving about it, and that we humanize the situation.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, something where there's been a little bit less love has been between you and \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2  on the debate stage, you all have clashed repeatedly that happened again this week, leading to one of the most talked about moments in the debate. It was this.\par \par \uc2 HALEY: I -- honestly every time I hear you, I feel a little bit dumber for what you say.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, but before he was running against you, you did write a blurb for his book, Woke Inc, in which you said he urges us to lift up all Americans, rather than to pit ourselves against one another. His combination of honesty, intellect, and foresight are exactly what we need to overcome our challenges in the years ahead. So were you right about him then, or you right about him now?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: Well, I -- look, I think I always praise people when they deserve to be praised. And I hit people when they deserve to be hit. The book that he wrote, I supported it. I helped him. He asked me for help with it. I was happy to do it. I'll help anybody that needs help. And it was a good book. But I think that things he's saying now as he's running for president is very different than that. The idea that he is saying, well, because 150 million Americans are on TikTok even though it's dangerous, I'm going to get on it anyway, is not leadership.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Let me stop you -- let me stop you there really quick.\par \par \uc2 HALEY: -- the crowd.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But let me stop you there very quickly, because I want to play something that he just said about this issue of TikTok and get you to respond.\par \par \uc2 VIVEK RAMASWAMY\uc2  (R), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: I do think it's a little hypocritical for the other candidates who were virtue signaling and criticizing me on stage, even as their own kids, adult children are on TikTok today. So I think we have to recognize the reality of where young voters are.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. He didn't name names, but is he referring to your kids?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: My son's not on TikTok, my daughter was on TikTok up until about 2020. And I think she posted something at a bachelorette party. But look, she's 25 years old. My job is to educate. My job is not to go and make my kids that are adults. My job is to educate and say why but him jumping on there. And making people think it's OK. Be very careful about how people talk about TikTok. This is the Chinese Communist Party, who is doing espionage. They don't just put things on your phone in an app, they get access to your contacts, they get access to your financial data, they get access to your emails, and they spread different dangerous things.\par \par \uc2 Right now they're spreading things about how to use steroids and things like that for -- for working out. These are dangerous apps. And what I'm going to do with keep telling every American, don't let your kids be on it, educate them, tell them why it's dangerous. And I'm going to keep saying that. I think TikTok is dangerous. And just because 150 million people are on it doesn't mean you do it because it's politically -- because it's politically popular, I think that's wrong.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. We have so much more to discuss. So please come back. But we got to leave it there for today. Ambassador Governor Haley, thanks so much for your time.\par \par \uc2 HALEY: Thanks so much, go to Nikkihaley.com and join us.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So breaking records, \uc2 U.S. Customs and Border Protection\uc2  sources confirmed the highest monthly number of migrant encounters at the border ever. We're going to get reaction from our Sunday panel, including on how that will play out in the 24 elections, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. GREG ABBOTT (R-TX): What's going on is unsustainable. It's a crisis. It's chaotic. And it must stop.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Texas Republican Governor Greg Abbott in New York City this week calling for tough action on the border. He says the migrant crisis facing New York is nothing compared to what's happening in the Lone Star State on a daily basis.\par \par \uc2 Time now for our Sunday group.\par \par \uc2 Julia Manchester, a national political reporter for "The Hill." Fox News senior political analyst Juan Williams. Jason Rantz, host of "The Jason Rantz Show" and author of the brand-new book "What's Killing America." And \uc2 The Heritage Foundation\uc2  president, Kevin Roberts.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to all of you.\par \par \uc2 All right, let's talk about this border.\par \par \uc2 Juan, this has blown up to be - it's been a continuous probable for a long time but we now have confirmation that in August we have hit, in September, 260,000 migrant encounters. That's the highest on record. Not a good look for the administration, especially when they're being called out by Democrats.\par \par \uc2 JUAN WILLIAMS, FOX NEWS SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST: I think it's not a good look for Congress or the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2 . Obviously, we have a problem and we should solve it. And we have the capacity to solve it.\par \par \uc2 But, you know, one of the troubling things to me is, we look at the pictures and we talk about the number of incursions, number of people crossing the border. But I hope you saw this week those pictures of children having to go through barbed wire.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: What is going on in America? That's troubling. I don't care where you are on the political spectrum, you should say, we -- we are better than this. We can solve this. And you don't focus on the idea that many of these families and children, they're coming through the jungle and the Darien Gap? They're putting their entire lives, children, women, moms at risk. Why? Because they're fleeing oppression. They're fleeing violence. They're fleeing economic harm. And they're saying they want to be in America. OK.\par \par \uc2 We have to order that progress.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: It's not It's very disorganized right now.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: And we have the capacity. But what we've seen in -- under \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2 , under \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 , under \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , and now under \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is the failure of the Congress and the administration to say, here's a deal. Let's compromise. Let's fix this problem.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, they haven't gotten it done. But, Jason, a lot of people think there are magnets here, there are sanctuary cities and promises that are pulling these people here as well.\par \par \uc2 JASON RANTZ, "THE JASON RANTZ SHOW" HOST AND "WHAT'S KILLING AMERICA" AUTHOR: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And these are human beings who are going through horrendous situations, some of them paying with their lives trying to get her.\par \par \uc2 RANTZ: Yes, they're coming here because we're signaling to them that if you make that journey, once you get into this country illegally, you're basically going to stay here.\par \par \uc2 And when you tell folks that, it doesn't matter the kind of journey that they're going through. To them they're saying it's worth it at the end of the day. And rather than make it pretty clear that, no, you're not going to get a free pass to come into this country, we're doing the exact opposite. So, all of those images, all of those journeys, that's the result of failed policy coming from the Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 What didn't happen under Trump and \uc2 Obama\uc2  was all of these people coming here and then staying here. There were record deportations, particularly under \uc2 Obama\uc2 . And I remember progressives were really upset with him for that.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: The were calling him deporter-in-chief.\par \par \uc2 RANTZ: Absolutely. And yet what happened? You have Democrats who have had control of the House, the Senate and the White House while constantly complaining about, well, we need to do something on immigration. OK, do something with immigration. They don't want to secure the border. And unless they're willing to do that, you're not going to get Republicans to buy-in.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Kevin, is that fair? I mean Republicans have also controlled House, Senate and White House and not gotten this fixed.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN ROBERTS, PRESIDENT, THE HERITAGE FOUNDATION: Well, it - it is fair. But let's -- let's be honest, Jason's totally right, that the person who initiated this is the president of the United States. And the - the most humane thing that we can do, my friend Juan, is to make sure that the president says, don't come to this country because the southern border is closed. If he does that, then we also know there have been 218 votes this year in the U.S. House to intimate that. Now we need some Republicans in the Senate and a few Democrats to do the same.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: How much of a headache is this for the White House when they've got so many other things, new revelations about Hunter, you know, impeachment moving forward potentially, the economy not looking good. Poll after poll says people are very unhappy with how that is playing out for them too. I mean this is just another visual -- and we've been looking at live pictures - of the reality of what's not working.\par \par \uc2 JULIA MANCHESTER, NATIONAL POLITICAL REPORTER, "THE HILL": Is certainly a headache. And you have had Republicans wanting to beat up on Democrats for this for quite some time. I would argue back in 2022 a lot of Republicans were trying to do this in the midterm elections. Obviously, the issue of abortion and the overturning of Roe v. Wade sort of trumped that in terms of voters' minds when they went to the ballot box to make their decisions.\par \par \uc2 But right now I think you're going to see a lot of Republican and conservative candidates putting this out on ads. You're going to hear the presential candidates, whoever the nominee is on the Republican side, talk about this. So, it puts Biden in a defensive position. And when you have someone like New York City Mayor Eric Adams, someone who has once called himself a Biden Democrat, criticizing the president on this, it just creates more of a headache for the administration.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Let me make sure we go back to our news at the top of the show. Narrowly averting this disaster of a shutdown. There were those on The Hill who thought it -- good could come from it. Conversations and difficult conversations could come from it. But - but now we're hearing this morning from Congressman \uc2 Matt Gaetz\uc2  who vowed if he thought that the speaker did not stick to the promises made to their group that he would file this motion to vacate. This morning it sounds like he's going to do it.\par \par \uc2 Kevin, does the speaker survived?\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: He may but he also may not. And the real tragedy from the policy and ideas standpoint is that for two weeks we were having a good conversation. .That, in fact, to - to this conversation threat about implementing one part of immigration reform, Republicans came together around that. So, while there are good members who apparently are not friends with Speaker McCarthy any longer, and while it looks like we're going to enter a phase that's a "Lord of the Flies" phase, from a policy standpoint, the American people just want them to get their work done. I would encourage them, on behalf of the entire Heritage enterprise to be focused on policies and ideas.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Who thinks that he does or doesn't survive? Does he - does he make it through this motion to vacate?\par \par \uc2 RANTZ: If he pulls a fire alarm right before the motion is made -\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Oh, boy.\par \par \uc2 RANTZ: I think he can probably save himself.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Oh, boy.\par \par \uc2 Speaking of "Lord of the Flies" and chaos, we had a little bit of that yesterday.\par \par \uc2 Let me - leT me get to this because I talked with Senator Manchin about this as we keep pushing him, are you going to do this third-party run? Some warnings from James Carville this week, who's really worried about all kinds of things that could hurt Democrats.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JAMES CARVILLE: Let's assume the election was November the third of this year.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Oh, Lord.\par \par \uc2 CARVILLE: And -- and they said the candidates of Joe - \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , the Democrat, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , the Republican, Joe Manchin and \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2  of No Labels, and Cornell West. Trump would be a betting favorite.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes. Totally.\par \par \uc2 CARVILLE: If I told you I would give you, even money, you would not take that bet, all right?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Um -\par \par \uc2 CARVILLE: And so somebody better wake the (EXPLETIVE DELETED) up.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Oh, boy.\par \par \uc2 Juan, how worried are you?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: You know, I think it - it definitely changes the dynamic. But the point is that just as you heard from Carville, I think there are a lot of Democrats who understand the threat, and understand what has taken place before with \uc2 Ralph Nader\uc2 , as you mentioned in your interview with Joe Manchin, he came back and mentioned \uc2 Ross Perot\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But the fact is here, in 2024, next year, it will really be something that weighs against \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , the incumbent. And I think lots of Democrats would be energized by that dynamic. I mean and also, you know, given what we saw this week, given what's going on with Kevin McCarthy, you've got to say, you know, there's like 12 Republicans, maybe two dozen Republicans, who delight in chaos and breaking apart the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  so it can't pay taxes and can't control the airports. I mean, can't pay our soldiers. People are going to say, no, this isn't - this is not just an ordinary election.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, and that's why Joe Manchin says that he is still apparently keeping his options open.\par \par \uc2 All right, we have a lot more to do -- to talk about with the panel, so don't go too far.\par \par \uc2 Julie, you're going to kick us off.\par \par \uc2 We're going to talk about this, the top attraction at the Smithsonian National Zoo soon leaving town and international relations may be to blame. Up next, why the giant pandas are being recalled to China.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: They've been here for a lot of years. Most definitely a huge part of the Smithsonian Zoo. So, it is really sad.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It's going to be devastating, especially for those that are the panda enthusiasts that come every week, every day. It's going to take some time.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: The Smithsonian National Zoo's star attractions, three giant pandas, are set to return to China in early December, disappointing millions of visitors. China's refusing to renew the deal to keep the panda's stateside. It appears to end five decades of cooperation. The move comes as relations between Beijing and Washington remain tense.\par \par \uc2 Panda lovers from across the country are now traveling to D.C. to get a final glimpse. And, by the way, if you want to see the pandas before you leave, it's free, but you do have to stop and get a pass.\par \par \uc2 OK, Julia, not getting a pass is the ongoing, you know, real minutia of looking into what - what our relationship is with China now and where it can possibly go. How would you grade it right now?\par \par \uc2 MANCHESTER: You know, I'd grade it like a D potentially. I wouldn't say it's failing necessarily, because there are efforts to get Biden and Xi, the Chinese president, in the same room talking. We do know that U.S. and Chinese diplomats met in Washington this week. But then again yesterday you heard the Chinese government calling the U.S. the manipulator of lies. So, clearly, a lot of tension.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Maybe frienemies (ph), Heavy on the enemy part.\par \par \uc2 OK, "The Wall Street Journal" has this headline, "U.S., China talks gain momentum paving way for Xi-Biden summit."\par \par \uc2 So, Kevin, to the point of these talks that are ongoing, do you see these two world leaders getting together, in person, anytime soon?\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Yes. And, unfortunately. And I - I say that because China is the greatest adversary the United States has ever had in its history. And so an A plus grade would be President Biden not kowtowing to Xi Jinping, who is evil, but instead to say, sir, you're not welcome on this soil until you meet these certain criteria. Until and unless that happens, and these pandas are an icon of that, the United States is going to be playing second fiddle to a very powerful China.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, ten seconds each before we get cut off.\par \par \uc2 RANTZ: I did not realize pandas were such a big deal.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: They are here.\par \par \uc2 RANTZ: Soon as I got here, everyone was smiling and talking - I'm going to put in the next printing of my book I'm putting on a chapter on just pandas.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: A quick, final word.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Well, I just think it's a sign that we're in a Cold War with China. But don't forget, China is struggling economically. Ukraine, take money away from Ukraine, you are making the Chinese happy. We've got to defeat the Chinese, defeat the Russians in the same blow.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. Panel, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 That's it for us today. Thank you for joining us. I'm Shannon Bream. Have a wonderful week. We'll see you next FOX NEWS SUNDAY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 Document FOXN000020231001eja100001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 Hannity Live From The Spin Room In California; Candidates Face Off In Second GOP Debate; FL Gov. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  Reacts To Second GOP Debate; \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2  Reacts To Second GOP Debate; 