ieve with all the fentanyl deaths and opioid, is this border secure?\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: I believe \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  was right in 1986 when he supported comprehensive immigration.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Yes. Well, he also said it was the worst decision he ever made.\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: Yes. But I believe that he did the right thing for the right reasons. Because he recognized it's not just a border issue.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: The entire system needs to be reformed. We haven't reformed since '86.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: You're not answering my question. I want to know -- you just said Joe successful.\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: I think \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has been unbelievably successful president.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Is this border secure?\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: The border, we all agree we have border challenge. We all agree --\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Challenge?\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: -- that we need to reform the asylum system. We agree in strong borders.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: So he's not successful in securing the border.\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: I think the Republican Party is complicit in this as well. You cannot do things. I mean, it's just factual.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: The border was secured with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: That's wildly overstated. But I get your fundamental point. Clearly, the challenges are more acute today. And by the way, I don't need to be educated on this. I have the largest land port in the Western Hemisphere here in the state of California.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: But you're a sanctuary state.\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: And?\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: And you want illegal immigration.\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: And it's the fifth largest economy in the world. We had 7.8 percent GDP growth and in 2021. What's -- and we dominate on innovation and --\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: A new pace for housing, food, clothing, education, health care, who pays for that?\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: Well, we're $177.7 billion operating surplus over two fiscal years. And I just did an $18.5 billion tax rebate, the largest in American history as we expanded universal health care, and we're providing more services including, by the way health insurance, which \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  apparently wasn't even aware of the question --\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: OK. You save that for the debate with Ron. Here's the next thing. So I come into your state.\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: Yes, sir.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: First thing I love to do --\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: What?\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: -- is bring money to your economy.\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I go straight to In and Out Burger. One of the greatest things of your state.\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: Legit.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Including the weather, including the terrain is phenomenal.\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: You can keep going. But yes.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: It's all phenomenal. However, then I'm passing gas station after gas station and gas station.\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: Oh, yes. Oh, you're right.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Excuse me.\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: Gouging us, oil companies are screwing us.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: $4 a gallon and every other state.\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: $7 I saw here. Why is your state nearly twice as mine?\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: As of today, I monitor this very close. I just did a special session. We became the first state in the country to price gouging legislation with a new monitor just went into effect. These oil companies are ripping us off $2.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: They only ripping off California though?\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: $2.06, this mystery surcharge that they've been advancing for years and years.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: But the average around the country is 4 and I saw anywhere between 6, 6.50 and $7 a gallon.\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: $2.6, you're absolutely right.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Your state is much more than every other state.\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: Exactly $2.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Why?\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: -- higher because they're screwing us. There's no justification.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: It's not regulation taxation?\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: Absolutely, yes, 85 cents, 85 cents low carbon fuel standard --\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: How much does California get per gallon for gasoline tax?\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: When you add the gas tax, you add cap and trade, and you add the low carbon fuel standards, and then periodically add, and I'm full -- I'm being as transparent as I can be, and you add our unique blend, you get a differential around 85 cents that you can justify not $2.06, that's where the oil companies are screwing.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: -- you should be $1 more a gallon,\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: And if want to trust the oil companies, I can assure you, that these guys don't have your back or the American --\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Let me tell you something when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was president, gasoline prices nationwide, never averaged $3 a gallon, never once averaged more than $3 a gallon.\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: -- getting back another million barrels. We didn't have \uc2 OPEC\uc2  plus screwing us. We didn't have a war in Ukraine that we didn't have -- we weren't going through -- no, we weren't also going through this COVID Supply Chain reality so there was a lot of competing factors that led to some of this. By the way --\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: But he also adopts -- wait a minute, he made the country energy independent for the first time in 75 years.\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: We are more energy dependent today look that up it's a fact and if -- no, no, no, it's just factually and true.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is unilaterally disarmed.\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: I want to encourage the people watching look this up, 5.94 quads, \uc2 Google\uc2  9.54 --\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Look at all the restrictions \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has put on oil and gas companies.\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: We have more energy independent today under Biden. Pence doesn't know that. Your audience doesn't know that. More domestic oil production than any time in history. We're on pace this year. That's a fact. You guys keep making that up.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: No. We don't make it up.\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: You're making it up.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: We're energy independent and now we're not.\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: We are energy independent.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: No we're not.\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: Net energy exporters.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: No, we are not.\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: Sean this is going to be so much fun when this gets PolitiFact tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Anwar, go there. Look at all the other restriction.\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: -- 5.94 quads, that's the margins, the highest margin and net energy export in America's history under the Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. I did have a little fun and for once you actually got back to it.\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: And by the way, I came a way for PolitiFact tomorrow. This will going to be some fun.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: PolitiFact, what are you going to --\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: I want you to show you audience.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: -- \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  fact checker, it needs to be fact checked.\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: All right, there's four Pinocchios that are coming your way.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: You get four Pinocchios your way. All right, so I come into your state.\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I love In and Out Burger. The high price of gasoline, of course not your fault. Although you admit 85 cents of it is. Here's the other thing. So I have a guy driving me loves California, hates your taxes. OK. And this is what we see. He points out to me, a bridge being built.\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: Yes, sir.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: For the people at home imagine this, they're building the bridge for mountain lions. It cost $100 million, some of it private money, some of it government money.\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: A lot of private money.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Some of it government money. So you're building bridges --\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: Yes, we are.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: -- for mountain lions and rattlesnakes?\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: Among other things, it's a wildlife crossing because we've seen so many accidents, we want to protect people's health and safety, impacting the economy and insurance and traffic in the region because of wildlife crossings. And we want to address a vision for this state as it relates to setting aside 30 percent of our land to be protected for future generations.\par \par \uc2 Vast majority of that comes from the Wallenberg Foundation, private sector, and it's inspired a generation of kids. And we want to inspire other like- minded wildlife crossings across the country.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: So you got this debate with Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Excited about it?\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: More so tonight, he lied about fracking. Nikki Haley laid him out, he lied about fracking. He lied to the American people tonight. He did an executive order.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: By the way, it's only going to be two of you. And --\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: But Sean --\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: You guys -- you don't like him. I know.\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: No. I just don't like -- well, I don't like people that demonize other people or go after vulnerable committees. But also I don't like liars. He, Nikki Haley laid him out tonight. He signed a 2019 executive order. I'm a governor. I know what executive orders. Why would you sign an executive order saying you will not do offshore oil rigs in your backyard then blame Biden for not doing more across the country.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I have an exit question.\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: -- hypocrisy is remarkable. And Haley laid him out.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Save that for the debate. Here's my exit question.\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: Happened tonight.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Under any circumstances at all --\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Yes or no, will you ever, ever accept the Democratic nomination to run for president in 2024 on under any circumstances at all? That's a yes or no.\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: I'm looking forward. I was just in Chicago with the --\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I don't need the long answer. I want the yes or no answer.\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: -- to President Biden's inauguration.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Will you accept this under any circumstance?\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: Of course not. It's a hypothetical --\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Yes or no?\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: No.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: No, under any circumstances?\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: It's ridiculous. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is our president.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: You called me ridiculous?\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is going to win this reelection.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I'm going to make you pay for In and Out Burger.\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  won the debate tonight. And you're right about In and Out Burger. And that's a great way to end.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: In and Out Burger. All right but the last thing is all those stupid interviews you did you made you prefer to do my show?\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: Well, I mean, I love talking about gas prices and wildlife crossings.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: That's all the time we have left. Thank you, Governor. We'll continue.\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document SHAN000020230929ej9r0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Officials Issue Warnings About Migrant Surge; \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  Joins \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  In Michigan; GOP Candidates Pitch To Voters Ahead Of Second Debate; Democrats Call On \uc2 Bob Menendez\uc2  To Resign Over Bribery Case; Sen. \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  (R-FL), Is Being Interviewed About Growing Chorus Of Democrats Calls For Resignation Of \uc2 Bob Menendez\uc2 ; Time Running Out For Spending Deal; Prospect Of Normalization Agreement; Academics And Researchers Tied To Iranian Effort At Improving Its International Image Examined; Oakland Business Owners Shut Down Stores To Protest High Levels Of Crime; China Returning Pandas Loaned To U.S. Zoos; President Biden Jones United Auto Workers Picket Line; Republican Presidential Candidates Excluding Former President Trump Prepare For Second Debate; New Report Lists President Biden's Address For Beneficiary Of Money Sent To \uc2 Hunter Biden\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Jacqui Heinrich, Chad Pergram, Bill Melugin, Mike  Emanuel, William La Jeunesse, Gillian Turner, Harold Ford Jr., Kellyanne  Conway \par \uc2 8,284 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 September 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JESSICA TARLOV, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Not much time so -- but you should know that 219 dogs went to a screening for the "PAW Patrol" sequel and broke the Guinness World Record for the most dogs attending a film screening. The paw-ty took place at Griffith Park in Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: How many were non-binary?\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Don't do that.\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL LEGAL ANALYST: That's it for us, have a great night, everybody. Thanks.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Hi, Judge, so, is Britney really on the show for Jesse?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes, in spirit.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: In spirit, OK. They're always good teases. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. I'm Bret Baier. We are coming to you tonight from Los Angeles not far from Simi Valley, the site of tomorrow's second Republican presidential debate. As you look there live, we'll talk more about that shortly.\par \par \uc2 But we begin tonight on the southern border. One Texas Congressman says America is out of time. Another says the Biden administration has tied the hands of the Border Patrol.\par \par \uc2 This evening, we hear from the chief of the U.S. Border Patrol in an exclusive interview in the midst of a massive record setting surge of illegal immigration.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Griff Jenkins starts us off tonight live again from Eagle Pass, Texas. Good evening, Griff.\par \par \uc2 GRIFF JENKINS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Hi, good evening, Bret. And U.S. Border Patrol chief Jason Owens got a firsthand look at this situation here in Eagle Pass today where agents are encountering more than 2,000 migrants every day straining resources to the point the more than 60 miles of the border is going unpatrolled.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: Is this sustainable?\par \par \uc2 JASON OWENS, U.S. BORDER PATROL CHIEF: No. This is -- we need this flow to stop. We need folks that want to immigrate to this country to use lawful pathways. This is dangerous for them. It's also dangerous for us because our law enforcement can't do their job while they're processing migrants.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: Do you feel that this -- the numbers, the large groups give the cartels an opportunity?\par \par \uc2 OWENS: Not just an opportunity. They're profiting off of this. They're profiting because they're charging these migrants to come across. Millions of dollars a week in revenue across each sector on the southwest border going directly into the hands of those cartels and smugglers.\par \par \uc2 Added to that, they're using this as a distraction to pull us off a task so they can cross other things like the narcotics.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS (voice over): Owens says nearly 70,000 pounds of narcotics have been seized between the ports of entry this fiscal year, including 2,700 pounds of fentanyl, enough to potentially kill the entire U.S. population. And he admits the continuing crisis is taking a toll on his agents.\par \par \uc2 OWENS: I see all of my men and women out here doing this, you know, impossible job and I'm overwhelmingly proud of them. At the same time, I need them out on the border, looking for the narcotics and the bad people that are coming across and right now, obviously because of this, they can't be there. That's what's keeping me up at night.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS (voice over): Thousands more migrants are on the way, some waving a Venezuelan flag atop a train bound for the U.S.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, Texas DPS removed a Colombian flag planted by migrants who tried to claim an island in the middle of the Rio Grande as their own.\par \par \uc2 LT. CHRIS OLIVAREZ, \uc2 TEXAS DEPARTMENT OF PUBLIC SAFETY\uc2 : It's disrespectful to the state of Texas into our country. The State of Texas will not stand for that and that's why we immediately took down that flag.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JENKINS (on camera): And Bret, the next VIP visitor may come as a bit of a surprise, tech billionaire \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  says after speaking with Congressman Tony Gonzalez who was here yesterday, Musk has decided he needs to see what's going on for himself. No date has been given for his arrival, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Griff Jenkins live along the border. Griff, thank you.\par \par \uc2 The leader who calls himself the most pro-union president in history walked the picket line today in Michigan with striking auto workers. Exactly what President \uc2 Obama\uc2  -- President Biden is doing to help those workers remains unclear even as former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  prepares a visit of his own.\par \par \uc2 White House Correspondent Jacqui Heinrich has the latest tonight from the North Lawn. Good evening, Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret. It is unclear what the president's visit to the picket line achieved or even which union demands he supports. The \uc2 UAW\uc2  wants a four day work week with five days of pay, something \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  supports, and also a 40 percent pay increase.\par \par \uc2 Today, Biden was asked if he supports that. And he said yes, with the nuance they should be able to bargain for that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): President Biden becomes the first sitting president to walk a picket line, picking up a bullhorn with striking auto workers in battleground Michigan.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: You guys, \uc2 UAW\uc2 , you saved the automobile industry back in 2008. And now they're doing incredibly well. And guess what, you should be doing incredibly well too.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): The union says negotiations progressed with Ford but not with \uc2 General Motors\uc2  where the president helped target distribution centers Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Despite concerns a prolonged strike could inflame economic pressures.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: You deserve the significant raise you need and other benefits.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): But the White House refuses to say which demands Biden is backing from 44 percent pay increases to reinstated pensions.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: We are not involved in negotiations. That is something for them to decide.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): Biden's visit comes one day before former President Trump makes his own, aiming to cut into blue collar support ahead of 2024.\par \par \uc2 Although the \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  typically backs Democrats, including Biden in 2020, it remains the only major union to withhold its endorsement so far.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: What will it take for the \uc2 UAW\uc2  to endorse you, sir?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I'm not worried about that.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): Prompting the Biden Harris campaign to tout another endorsement today from the \uc2 United Farm Workers\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 JULIE CHAVEZ RODRIGUEZ, JOE BIDEN CAMPAIGN MANAGER: Whether it is, you know, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  or any other opponent, you know, we believe that they're all putting forward the same extreme policies.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (on camera): The strike pits Biden's priorities against each other billing himself as the most pro-labor president in history while also pushing a transition to clean energy, which has proven costly for automakers.\par \par \uc2 Today, \uc2 General Motors\uc2  all but ignored the president's visit, saying its focus is not on politics, but on negotiating in good faith.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, some other news tonight, Fox has learned that Commander, the president's German Shepherd bit a Secret Service agent last night. Back in July, Secret Service reported that its officers had been bitten 10 times in just four months. So, we're still trying to get a tally of just how many bites this is. But that is the latest news here from North Lawn, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The Commander update. Jacqui Heinrich live on the North Lawn, thanks.\par \par \uc2 While President Biden focuses on auto workers, Republicans hoping to take his place are planning to criticize him heavily on the economy, the border and his fitness for office. Their second debate of the campaign season takes place tomorrow night right there, about an hour northwest of here in Simi Valley. Correspondent Bill Melugin is there tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY (R), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: I will veto any spending bill that doesn't take us back to pre-COVID level.\par \par \uc2 VIVEK RAMASWAMY\uc2  (R), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: If we can use military to secure somebody else's border. We can absolutely use our own military to secure our own border.\par \par \uc2 BILL MELUGIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Republican presidential candidates making the rounds with voters ahead of the second GOP primary debate. The field of seven candidates is now set. It's a smaller group with former Arkansas Governor Asa Hutchinson not qualifying due to tighter \uc2 RNC\uc2  standards. Florida Governor DeSantis will be standing center stage, former \uc2 U.N\uc2 . Ambassador Haley and entrepreneur Ramaswamy on his sides, Senator Scott and former New Jersey Governor Christie in the foreign five spots and on the wings, North Dakota Governor Burgum and former Vice President Pence.\par \par \uc2 Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is skipping the debate again, citing his commanding lead in the polls and saying the rest of the field is wasting their time.\par \par \uc2 Trump's former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  is firing back after Trump called Florida's ban on abortion after six weeks of pregnancy, a terrible thing. Posting on X, "I'm pro-life and I always will be a champion for life".\par \par \uc2 The candidates are now flocking to California for the debate with Senator Tim Scott posting a photo of him meeting California fishermen. And the states GOP Chairwoman says expect the deep blue states crime and homelessness problems to be highlighted on the debate stage.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA PATTERSON, CHAIRWOMAN, CALIFORNIA REPUBLICAN PARTY: I think what you're going to see from this debate this week is these candidates drawing a contrast between what California is and not bringing it to the rest of the country. And any one of those candidates could do a better job than \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN (on camera): And Bret, in a bit of a surprise, California Governor \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  is going to be here at the debate tomorrow night. That's because the President Biden reelection team is sending him here they say to push back against GOP talking points and the highlight any rhetoric they feel is extreme coming from these GOP candidates tomorrow night. We'll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Bill Melugin at the Reagan Library. Bill, see you tonight. Please join Dana Perino and \uc2 Stuart Varney\uc2  and Univision's Ilia Calderon as they moderate the second Republican presidential debate on Fox Business tomorrow, simulcast on Fox News from the Ronald Reagan Presidential Foundation and Institute in Simi Valley, California.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, a judge in New York has ruled that former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  committed fraud for years while building the real estate empire that catapulted him to fame and then the White House. The decision is seen as a win for New York Attorney General Letitia James. She is seeking $250 million in damages in the civil case. The judge says the former president and his company deceived banks, insurers and others by massively overvaluing his assets and exaggerating his net worth.\par \par \uc2 The judge ordered that some of Trumps business licenses be rescinded, making it difficult or impossible for them to do business in New York and said he would continue to have an independent monitor oversee the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2 's operations. The decision comes days before the start of the non-jury trial over the lawsuit.\par \par \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  is suing \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  and another attorney accusing them of wrongfully -- wrongly rather as accessing and sharing his personal data after obtaining it from the owner of a Delaware computer repair shop. The lawsuit accuses the man of spending years, "Hacking into, tampering with, manipulating, copying, disseminating, and generally obsessing over the data".\par \par \uc2 It says the information was taken or stolen from Biden's devices or storage, leading to the total annihilation of Biden's digital privacy. The president's son does not explicitly acknowledge that the laptop left at the computer shop was his, but he says at least some of the data was on his iPhone or backed up to iCloud.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  has again blocked Alabama's efforts to keep a Republican drawn congressional map in place. The justices are allowing a court appointed master, special master to continue creating a new map with greater black representation. Alabama has just won black majority congressional district despite having 27 percent of the state's population.\par \par \uc2 Also breaking tonight, increasing pressure on New Jersey Democratic Senator \uc2 Bob Menendez\uc2  to resign following last week's federal bribery charges. Yesterday, Menendez insisted he is innocent, and was critical of those calling on him to leave. Today, there are a lot more lawmakers who are making that call.\par \par \uc2 Senior Congressional Correspondent Chad Pergram is on Capitol Hill live tonight with the latest. Good evening, Chad.\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Bret, good evening. The dam broke today against New Jersey Democrat \uc2 Bob Menendez\uc2 . 21 Senate Democrats now demand he resigned. The senator returned to the Capitol for the first time since the indictment last week. He did not respond to questions.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Why aren't you resigning?\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): Fellow New Jersey Senator \uc2 Cory Booker\uc2  says Menendez should step down. Booker testified as a character witness for Menendez during his last trial in 2017. That case ended in a mistrial. But Booker says it's a, "mistake not to quit".\par \par \uc2 Vulnerable Democrats facing competitive reelections next year in swing states now insist Menendez resigned. That includes Pennsylvania's Bob Casey, Wisconsin's \uc2 Tammy Baldwin\uc2  and \uc2 Gary Peters\uc2  of Michigan. He is in charge of the Democrats reelection efforts next year. The top Republican on the ethics committee does not buy the senator's excuse that he kept cash at home because he worried the government could seize the assets.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JAMES LANKFORD\uc2  (R-OK): A member of the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  that says they don't trust their own government enough that they would have to be able to maintain that much cash at home, that is a challenging obviously issue to be able to try to explain.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): GOP Arkansas Senator Tom Cotton disagrees that Menendez should quit. He says the senator's fate is up to the jury, not Democrats who find Menendez, "inconvenient".\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (on camera): Menendez appears in court tomorrow, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Hi, Chad, on another topic, where do we stand with the possible government shutdown and government funding from Congress?\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Well, Senate leaders just announced agreement on a bipartisan plan to fund the government for 45 days.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY): I urge strongly reasonable members of both chambers to come together to pass this bridge C.R. and move forward. Freed from the hard right extremists who are hell bent on destroying trust in government.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY): The clearest path forward is a standard short term continuing resolution.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: It's unclear what can pass the House. It's only Tuesday and because it takes so long for Congress to act, it's possible a weekend shutdown could be locked in, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We'll follow it day by day. Chad, thank you.\par \par \uc2 The Dow had its worst day since March losing 388. The S&P 500 was down 64. The NASDAQ fell 208 today.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we'll talk live with Florida Senator \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  about the prospects for an agreement between Israel and Saudi Arabia and possible government shutdown.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): I waited a long time for them to do something on the debt ceiling every day. I waited a long time for them to do something on the border, they never did. They haven't passed anything. When they pass something, come back and ask me.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: And Republicans in the House don't start doing the job, we should stop electing.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MATT GAETZ\uc2  (R-FL): We are spending more than $7 trillion a year, bringing in around $5 trillion a year. We should have separate single subject spending bills.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What are our objectives in Ukraine and what's the plan? And until we get an objective and plan, they don't -- I don't -- I don't think that a taxpayers should pay additional money.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Schumer and McConnell are negotiating a 45-day short term spending bill. If that comes over without Ukraine funding or disaster aid, would you put it on the floor?\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): I'm not going to take up hypotheticals of some, daydreaming the Senate is going to do something.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, tonight, we discuss the looming government shutdown, what's been done about it, as well as the potential Saudi Arabia-Israeli deal with Florida Senator \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2 , a member of several key committees, including appropriations, foreign affairs and intelligence.\par \par \uc2 Senator, thanks for the time.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARCO RUBIO (R-FL): Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The Hill writes, "Senate Democrats Put McCarthy in A Shutdown Squeeze: The Senate Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  ramping up pressure on Speaker McCarthy to avoid a government shutdown by moving first on a stopgap funding bill that will pass the Senate this week. Some GOP senators are worried about 'sticking our necks out' if the stopgap is doomed to fail in the House." Are you worried about that?\par \par \uc2 RUBIO: Well, I think I'm realistic about the fact that whatever we pass here still has to be consulted with the House. And then, ultimately, the only way to pass something around here is to get both chambers to pass it. And I'm not going to get involved in the House's business, I acknowledge its -- right now, kind of difficult to decipher what's exactly going to happen over there. And the last thing they need or want is senators telling them what they need to be doing. Suffice it to say that, now what we pass here is probably not going to be what the House can pass, at least under the current outline.\par \par \uc2 So, I hate these shutdowns. I don't think we should have them. We had a bill last week, a bunch of Republicans supported it. We could vote for it tonight, if \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  would allow it. That would prevent these shutdowns in the future. But it's where we seem to find ourselves now on a sort of a three or four-year cycle.\par \par \uc2 And the right way to do this is to pass appropriations bills. And, you know, we had time to do more of that here in the House and Senate. It got caught up for some reasons, but at least we got them out of committee. The House is going to try to do that now. But obviously that can't happen by Saturday night and time to prevent a shutdown.\par \par \uc2 So, something is going to have to happen to keep the lights on.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. I mean, this is why people outside of Washington hate Washington. It's this constant game of chicken, of fiscal cliff almost every time.\par \par \uc2 You're saying you don't want to get involved with the House is doing. Well, what about a former president getting involved in making his feelings clear? The former President Trump, writing on Truth Social, Sunday night: "The Republicans lost big on the debt ceiling, got nothing, and now we're worried that they will be blamed for the budget shutdown. Wrong. Whoever is president will be blamed. In this case, crooked as hell, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 ! Our country is being systematically destroyed by the radical left Marxist, fascists and thugs, the Democrats. Unless you get everything, shut it down!"\par \par \uc2 What do you make of that?\par \par \uc2 RUBIO: Well, he certainly has a right to view that opinion, that's an opinion shared by a lot of people. Look, there's a lot of things that I would like to see done. There's a lot of things the House members have proposed that I would love to see part of our spending.\par \par \uc2 And unfortunately, Democrats control the Senate. That's why these elections matter so much. That's why I hope we're in the majority in the future, so we could drive more of what's in that policy.\par \par \uc2 I -- look at this happening on the border. It's a self-inflicted wound that we knew was going to happen. And these policies continue to encourage that, and this administration isn't really want to do anything meaningful about it.\par \par \uc2 I think there is legitimate issues to be raised. And I've been a supporter of Ukraine funding, because I think it would be devastating to our alliances, or now, reputation around the world. That would be like Afghanistan, you know, times 10, in terms of the hit we would take.\par \par \uc2 But it can't be -- there is got to be a strategy of -- on that. There's got to be a clear plan. There is got to be numbers around it that makes sense to people.\par \par \uc2 I mean, what the money they're asking for now, it's a lot of money, it's only for three months. So, we're going to be here a year from now, as $100 million -- $100 billion are saying the cost is. All these things have to be worked through, and they shouldn't be worked through as part of regular appropriations bills. But that's not an option at this point. Right now, between now and Saturday. And that's unfortunately, the situation you're right, we continue to find ourselves in, and no matter who is in charge, it appears.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Talking about alliances and around the world, I just returned from Saudi Arabia, and also talk to the Israeli prime minister. Just take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MOHAMMED BIN SALMAN\uc2  AL SAUD, CROWN PRINCE OF SAUDI ARABIA: For us, the Palestinian issue is very important. We need to solve that part. And we have good negotiations, continue.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: If you were to characterize it, are you close?\par \par \uc2 AL SAUD: Every day we get closer.\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2 , PRIME MINISTER, ISRAEL: I was delighted to hear what he had to say. And to borrow a phrase, I think we're getting closer to peace every day that passes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: In your committee assignments, you know how complex this is. Do you think it's possible to get this across the finish line?\par \par \uc2 RUBIO: I do, and I hope it happens. And I think it would be an important moment, and for the region, and ultimately, for the world.\par \par \uc2 And look, Israel is a close ally of the United States. Saudi Arabia is a key strategic ally of the United States. They both have deep and rightful concerns about Iran and worries about Iran.\par \par \uc2 I saw your interview last week where he said something we've been saying for years, and that is there is no way there is going to be a Shia bomb in Iran and there isn't going to be