d come to the rescue, taking bats to the getaway car.\par \par \uc2 TERRY WILEY, NAACP OAKLAND: This city is out of control. The crime is out of control.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: Business owners say it's not just being robbed, but because of crime, residents are afraid to leave their homes, which means shop and restaurant others can't make a living.\par \par \uc2 NIGEL JONES, OAKLAND RESTAURANT OWNER: Look at all these places around here. They are closed down.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: After 38 years in business, Le Cheval, an iconic Vietnamese restaurant, is closing next week.\par \par \uc2 SON TRAN, CLOSING OAKLAND RESTAURANT: This is my city. I love Oakland, and I cannot live anywhere. But I cannot do business in Oakland.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: Critics at city hall called today's protest an unproductive publicity stunt. But Democrat Mayor Sheng Thao says she understands.\par \par \uc2 SHENG THAO, (D) OAKLAND MAYOR: My home was broken into. And they saw my son inside the home. They just kept going it. Has traumatized my son. I get that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE (on camera): Now business owners want more police and financial help to hire private security. The city is broke, however. The state does have millions of dollars available, hundreds of millions, actually, for crime prevention, but Oakland missed out on the application, they said, because they had no one to fill out the paperwork.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That's amazing. And this isn't a left or right thing. You are talking to people on all sides that really have concerns about it.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: Yes, and that's why I was mentioning is that some of these progressive leaders admitted today say these policies are not working. In fact, 65 percent of the people up there identify as progressive, and they want more police.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Amazing. We will follow it. William, thanks. Good seeing you.\par \par \uc2 The political rift between the U.S. and China is 