W\uc2  president talking about how they endorse or don't endorse coming up. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHAWN FAIN, UNITED AUTO WORKERS PRESIDENT: Our endorsements are going to going be earned. We have been very clear about that, no matter what politician it is.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: How does he earn it?\par \par \uc2 FAIN: Who the president is now, who the former president was or the presidents before them isn't going to win this fight.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, let's talk about the strike and the politics around it. Let's bring in our panel, FOX NEWS AT NIGHT anchor Trace Gallagher, \uc2 Harold Ford Jr\uc2 ., former Tennessee Congressman, co-host of THE FIVE, and Kellyanne Conway, former senior counselor to President Trump. Kellyanne -- first of all, Trace, thanks for having us in your wonderful studio.\par \par \uc2 TRACE GALLAGHER, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Welcome to our home, Bret.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Kellyanne, what about the politics of this and seeing the president of the United States out on the picket line today?\par \par \uc2 KELLYANNE CONWAY, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, he responded to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  saying he was going to go there first. Trump is skipping the second debate and that he was going to go and be with the auto workers. So President Biden, unfortunately, was responding to that.\par \par \uc2 I think it's too little, too late. The unions are very skeptical of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  compared to other Democrats. And they know that the Democratic Party writ large is abandoning those core principles that used to galvanize union households toward the party, Bret. China, obviously, much more important, shipping jobs and wealth overseas. And, of course, this climate extremism. That is hurting these auto workers and other unions.\par \par \uc2 And by the way, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  spent one-and-a-half hours, 90 minutes on the ground with them. Let's not be fooled by a bunch of b-roll and soundbites. He spent 90 minutes on the ground, and then he's flying here to California for a fundraiser, probably to talk about electric vehicles.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, that is a big deal, Harold. You heard the speaker talking about what it means, the climate change policies of the Democrats and how that affects workers maybe more than this negotiation even does.\par \par \uc2 HAROLD FORD JR\uc2 ., FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Maybe. First of all, it is good to be in your house and good to be around the table.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 FORD: Two things. You are right, President Trump deciding to be on the side of workers there in Detroit, in Michigan and across the Midwest, certainly influenced maybe President Biden. But what is going to be interesting to me is tomorrow night, do Republican candidates on that stage say that they would have gone. It will be interesting to see if they get asked a question, is this a litmus test for you guys as well?\par \par \uc2 Two, I think the president, to your point, does have some issue when it comes to Bidenomics and the economy. You showed the numbers last night, Bret, some of the unfavorable ratings, or I should say lack of favorable ratings for the president on the economy and the border. These are things that they're going to have to tackle or at least show that they have a plan to do. Every time Republicans look like they may gain a little favor, they stumble a bit. You have got to keep the government open. If the government closes, history shows us that Republicans get blamed more than Democrats, particularly when they are in the majority. So the next few days are big for the government and big for the political parties as we go forward.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, and we have these fancy walkouts of the candidates for the debate tomorrow. And as they get to their position, it's got music, we are really going all out here. Oh, yes, there we go. There is number one. Let's hear the music in the background? Can we hear it? Anyway. Yes, that's the positioning. There we go.\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Tracy, it is a moment, but this race, to the former president's point, is separating even further. So there has to be something done with some of these people on the stage to change the dynamics.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes, we were talking, in fact, Harold and I were talking about strategy earlier, and the bottom line here is somebody has to come out. It's a FOX Business debate, right, so you know the economy is going to get talked about a lot. It's a weak point for Biden. You have "The Washington Post"-ABC poll coming out saying 70 percent of the population really not on board with the economy. It's not good for them. And so that's a big moment for these Republican candidates to kind of set themselves apart.\par \par \uc2 And I think \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  more than anybody really needs to kind of break out from the crowd. "The Washington Examiner" today was kind of comparing it to the rope-a-dope, saying he needs to get away from the rope-a-dope, the Muhammad Ali tactic against George Foreman in Manila many years ago, and get ahead. Because right now, Bret, he is just either running in place or running behind. Nikki Haley is kind of inching up, but \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  needs a moment, because if you are going to compete against the former president, you need your donors, you need your support. And that comes with being number one, number two.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I want to deal with this breaking news. This is from FOX Digital. \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  received $250,000 in wires originating in Beijing with beneficiary's address listed as \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's home. "The beneficiary for the wires is listed as Robert Hunter Biden with the address 1209 Barley Mill Rd. in Wilmington, Delaware. That address is the main residence for President Biden. Comer and the House Oversight Committee have obtained bank records as part of their investigation alleging that the Biden family and their business associates received millions of dollars from oligarchs in Russia, Ukraine, Romania, Kazakhstan, during the \uc2 Obama\uc2  administration as well as China."\par \par \uc2 Every time we talk about this, Democrats say it doesn't point to then Vice President Biden, now President Biden. But every time we talk about it, there is some other development.\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: Yes. Clearly, it all goes back to the big guy. And Bret, the 2024 presidential election will be the first time this is truly litigated by the voters in the court of public opinion. Why? Because in 2020 \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  lied to the country in the debate when he said I've never discussed business with my son. That's a lie. We have at least 20 incidents with the vice president on the phone helping his son. We have other emails.\par \par \uc2 And then he also told the country to not believe that this was really \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's laptop, that 51 intelligence officials have signed a letter clearly saying it was -- had the earmarks of, the markings of Russian disinformation. All of that was false. And we keep on learning more and more.\par \par \uc2 What I don't understand is why the White House and the family are not containing \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 . He's at state dinners. He's on the balcony. He is suing \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2 . He needs to go away. The biggest thing we know about him is he is denying paternity and support of his own daughter and last name to the daughter. Why would she want it? One wonders.\par \par \uc2 But this is a problem for the president because he has no good news in any polling. Border security is his worst issue, 23 percent approve of the job he's doing. And immigration is different than border security. People look at border security not about denying people entry. They look at border security as sovereignty, security, freedom from drugs. He doesn't have good news on any major issue or personal attribute. Hunter just exacerbates that.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So of the issues that the president is facing here, and you look at these polls, Harold, where do you see it? Is this going to keep creep up as being one key issues? Is his age going to take over, the border? What?\par \par \uc2 FORD: I put him in two categories. One, I think his age is on the minds of a lot of voters. It's interesting that Governor Newsom, obviously we are here in California. He has been assigned to be the surrogate for the campaign here at the debate. I think he is pretty good at it. He has also been cited as someone who might run in 28 or even in 24. So age is one thing.\par \par \uc2 I think the second thing, the border has always been an issue that I believe my party has got to deal with differently. Abortion is something that I think will favor Democrats. Not that people want abortions, but protecting the right for a woman to get an abortion. And the border and crime, alarming numbers. I saw those businesspeople in Oakland earlier in the show. That's happening everywhere. They are not protesting like that, but they certainly are concerned.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I mean, 40 carjackings a day, that's insane.\par \par \uc2 FORD: Crime and the border for Democrats, and abortion for Republicans are those social issues. And then the president has to deal with the age issue. So these are issues that could creep up.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: And they are protesting in Oakland. That's the whole thing is they're protesting today. Business owners walked out. They left their businesses behind. They know they are losing money, but they say it's worth it because they cannot do business in Oakland because of the laws. And California says we are going to crack down on retail crime. They will do everything except change the laws for retail crime to make crime legal. They refuse to do it, but they are pouring money to try and stop it. And it's just not working.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I just have 15 seconds. But the fact that \uc2 Cory Booker\uc2  comes out and calls for the resignation of \uc2 Bob Menendez\uc2 , significant?\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: Yes, this Jersey girl and Jersey voter finds it to be very significant. He was a character witness for \uc2 Bob Menendez\uc2  in the mistrial from before, 21 and counting senators, they don't want him there. He's got no friends in the Senate. He needs to resign.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It seems like it opened the flood gates today. Panel, thanks.\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Wait, it went back to "Tuesday Tweets"? Isn't it "X-tras?" It's called X. All right, we went to the other graphic.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, first up, \uc2 Merlin\uc2  says, "I love watching SPECIAL REPORT, and I'm always happy to see you hosting debates and interviewing foreign leaders. The question is a little off topic. When are we getting the SPECIAL REPORT mugs in the FOX News shop?" That is a good question, and we're going to get you an answer very soon.\par \par \uc2 Next, Anthony wants to know, "What is your biggest impression you took away from the Saudi crown prince?" A great question. I will say this, that he was very prepared. He knew broadly what he wanted to say. He does have this vision. And I will tell you this: he will be the leader there and probably in that region for 40, 50, maybe 60 years.\par \par \uc2 Last, Al asks, "What are some of the techniques you employ to keep your own inclinations, opinions, emotions, et cetera, from interfering with your effort to report and journalize objectively? How would you teach a young journalist?" I would say keep the emotion out of it. Just keep the emotion out of it.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT, we will be previewing the second Republican presidential debate. Fair, balanced, and unafraid, as always. We'll see you from Simi Valley. THE INGRAHAM ANGLE is next.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020230927ej9q00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Interview with Rep. \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2  (D-TX); Interview with Presidential Candidate, Sen. Tim Scott (R-SC); Second Republican Primary Debate; Lawmakers Face End of Month Deadline to Avoid Shutdown; Biden Plans to Join Picket Line in Michigan Tuesday; ; Leaders of Saudi Arabia and Israel Optimistic About Possible Peace Agreement; Ukrainian President Zelenskyy Pleads for More \uc2 U.S. Aid\uc2  During Trip to Washington; \uc2 RNC\uc2  Warns New Hampshire It Will Yank Debate If State Moves up Primary Date\b0\par\par\uc2 Bill Hemmer, Griff Jenkins, David Spunt, Guy Benson, Juan  Williams, Dana Perino, Katie Pavlich \par \uc2 9,804 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 September 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Fox News Sunday\par \uc2 FOXN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 BILL HEMMER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: I'm Bill Hemmer, in for Shannon Bream.\par \par \uc2 The chaos at the border grows by the day, as the pressure to take greater action builds yet again on the White House.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MAYOR ROLANDO SALINAS (D), EAGLE PASS, TEXAS: We need help. We need it now. We need people from the top. I'm talking from our president, our vice president.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER (voice-over): A border city mayor and Democrat declaring a state of emergency as thousands upon thousands of migrants flow into the country. The president pointing the finger across the aisle.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Republicans in Congress and my predecessor spent four years gutting the immigration system -- under my predecessor. They continue to undermine our border security today.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: We'll get reaction from border state Democrat, Texas Congressman \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Plus --\par \par \uc2 (CHANTING)\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: The nation's autoworkers expanding their strike into 20 states. President Biden says he'll join the picket line in Michigan on Tuesday, just a day before \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  will be there, too.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, another presidential hopeful pushes back.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT (R-SC), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: We need a president who says we are not going to subsidize unions, period.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: We'll discuss with a man whose eyes are on the White House, South Carolina Senator Tim Scott.\par \par \uc2 Then, the second Republican presidential debate only days away. We'll ask \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2  chairwoman \uc2 Ronna McDaniel\uc2  what voters can expect to see on stage Wednesday night.\par \par \uc2 And --\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JAMES LANKFORD\uc2  (R-OK): It's a symbol of respect for the country when you dress respectfully when you're doing this responsibility.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (D-PA): I think there are more important things we should be talking about rather if -- if I dressed like a slob.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: Our Sunday panel on the debate over the Senate ditching its own dress code.\par \par \uc2 All, right now, on "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HEMMER (on camera): And hello from FOX News in Washington.\par \par \uc2 The number of illegals crossing our border hit another new record. We want to show you our FOX News drone camera from Eagle Pass, Texas. We've been watching remarkable images today of a human flood that shows no sign of receding. And today, a new survey shows how displeased Americans are with the president's border policies.\par \par \uc2 In a moment, we'll speak with border state Democrat, Texas Congressman \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2 , on that.\par \par \uc2 But, first, to Griff Jenkins who has been in Eagle Pass for what seems like several years now.\par \par \uc2 Griff, hello to you. What's the news today?\par \par \uc2 GRIFF JENKINS, FOX NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Bill, good morning.\par \par \uc2 Well, there's a humanitarian crisis playing out along our southern border in places like here in Eagle Pass, Texas, where migrants have traveled thousands of miles in hopes of reaching the U.S. in numbers far greater than what border officials are able to handle.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JENKINS (voice-over): Homeland Security Secretary Alejandro Mayorkas visiting the border in McAllen, Texas, Saturday, saying the administration is taking more actions to stem the unprecedented flow of migrants, averaging more than 9,000 daily. Actions include sending active duty troops to the border, increasing deportations and granting temporary protective status to nearly half a million Venezuelans, making it easier for them to find work in cities like New York, where officials are struggling to find room for them.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Texas Governor Greg Abbott trying to deter the migrants from entering his state, with miles of dense razor wire, Humvees manning the riverbank and guardsmen in rafts attempting to turn them back.\par \par \uc2 But it's not working. CBP says a record number arrived at the southern border last month, more than 230,000, the highest August on record. And more are on the way. Trains in Mexico show hundreds of migrants heading toward the U.S. with the humanitarian crisis on full display. In Eagle Pass, Texas, the migrants, mostly from Venezuela, struggle to climb through sharp barbs after crossing a dangerous river that's claimed three lives in three days this week.\par \par \uc2 In temperatures so high, border agents cut through the wire, letting migrants escape scorching temperatures, some in need of medical attention.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: And, Bill, Eagle Pass isn't the only border community feeling the strain of this migrant surge. Over in El Paso, where there have been three confirmed cases of tuberculosis among the migrant population. The mayor there says due to overcapacity, they've reached, quote, a breaking point -- Bill.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: Wow. Griff Jenkins, thank you, Eagle Pass, Texas. Thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is Texas Democratic Congressman \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And, sir, thank you for your time on this Sunday.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 HENRY CUELLAR\uc2  (D-TX): Thank you, Bill.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: I want you to listen to a Democratic mayor in Eagle Pass, Texas -- Rolando Salinas is his name -- on President Biden's policies.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MAYOR ROLANDO SALINAS (D), EAGLE PASS, TEXAS: I'll be honest with you, I believe 100 percent he does bear some responsibility for this crisis. I haven't heard from anybody in the administration. The president hasn't put out a statement. The vice president, I haven't heard from anybody.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: Sir, you're a Democrat. Why is that Democratic mayor such a lonely voice?\par \par \uc2 CUELLAR: You know, some of us along the border have been talking about what's been happening on the border for many years.\par \par \uc2 Look, you know, what we need to do is do this -- one, we need to have repercussions at the border. What does that mean? You got to deport people and you got to show those images of people being deported. When is the last time we saw people going the other day instead of just seeing people flow in?\par \par \uc2 And the second thing is, keep in mind what happened in 2015, what happened in 2019 under President Trump, President \uc2 Obama\uc2  and what I think happened a couple days ago, but now we've got to see enforcement.\par \par \uc2 We've got to get Mexico to do more like they did in 2019, like they did in 2015, and hopefully, the agreement they reached a couple days ago with the administration, and that is get the people on the Mexican side, deport them to their countries, do more to stop people in their -- on their southern border with Guatemala, otherwise, we're just going to continue seeing this flow.\par \par \uc2 It worked in 2015 under \uc2 Obama\uc2 . It worked under President Trump, and now this agreement reached a couple days ago, if they enforce it correctly, then we should see --\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: Okay.\par \par \uc2 CUELLAR: -- a slowing of a flow of people coming in.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: All right. President Biden did set in motion the following things this week, a few of them. He granted work visa to 400,000 Venezuelans. He assigned 800 U.S. military members to work in border offices. Secretary Mayorkas met with the president of Honduras over the weekend at the border.\par \par \uc2 Does any of that, sir, change the current human tide?\par \par \uc2 CUELLAR: Well, again, it does provide some assistance. But if -- look, if you talk about those work visas without doing anything else, I mean, the work permits without doing something at the border, then it becomes a magnet.\par \par \uc2 There's -- there are several things you need to do. I still say this and I've been saying this for years, we as Democrats and Republicans can continue playing defense on the one-yard line or we can play defense on their 20-yard lines and that is getting Mexico to do more like they did in 2015, when the numbers started slowing down, 2019, where numbers slowed down.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: Uh-huh.\par \par \uc2 CUELLAR: And if we do it, then it's going to happen.\par \par \uc2 We need to stop playing defense. You can send more National Guard. You can seed more active members. You can add more, but think about what they do. Think about images you saw there.\par \par \uc2 What happens when they get to the border? Once they touch the riverbank, they can claim asylum and we know that 89 to 90 percent of them don't even qualify for asylum.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: On an \uc2 NFL\uc2  Sunday perhaps, that is a proper metaphor.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  tried to build a wall. President Biden said the root causes need to be addressed in Central America. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  was put in charged with that as you know.\par \par \uc2 Just this morning, we have this, ABC/"Washington Post" poll out shows only 23 percent of Americans approve of Biden's handling of immigration. A brutal 62 percent disapprove.\par \par \uc2 We are almost three years into this administration, why did it get this bad?\par \par \uc2 CUELLAR: You know, the policies that you have in place, it doesn't matter what administration, you've got to have policies that have repercussions. If you don't have repercussions, then people will see the border as a speed bump and continue coming in.\par \par \uc2 So, again, I think Trump went too much to the right by doing the separation of families and kids, and then I think President Biden, the administration is not doing enough. So, we've got to find the right balance. You've got to listen to the community leaders like the mayor of Eagle Pass, the mayor of El Paso and other places, they got to listen to communities at the border.\par \par \uc2 And again, one, you have repercussions at the border. That is you deport people that are not supposed to be here.\par \par \uc2 Number two, you got to play defense on the other 20-yard line and it's very important. You know, people give credit to President Trump. But if you look at it, it was Mexico doing a lot more just like they did in 2015. 2015, 2019 --\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: Okay.\par \par \uc2 CUELLAR: -- hopefully this year.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: Congressman Cuellar, appreciate your time. Thank you, sir, for joining us on this Sunday.\par \par \uc2 CUELLAR: Thank you, so much, and good morning.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: You bet.\par \par \uc2 Another crisis building for the White House is the striking autoworkers. President Biden is expected to join the picket line in Michigan on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 That will be a first for a U.S. president, one day before his top political rival is also in Michigan, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  will be in Detroit.\par \par \uc2 David Spunt with the latest on the walkout live from the White House where there was news this weekend.\par \par \uc2 And, David, hello to you.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hello to you, Bill.\par \par \uc2 The president who spent the weekend here at the White House touts himself as pro-union. In just two days, he will meet with those picketers on the picket line. The steady picket line, Bill, continues outside the GM and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  plants, also outside Ford, though, some progress has been made on the front with Ford.\par \par \uc2 The union president looks forward to seeing another president, that is the president of the United States.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHAWN FAIN, UAW PRESIDENT: My invitation to anyone today, including the White House, to come and join our workers on the picket line, that's open for anybody. We -- you know, we -- that is where you see support. Proof is in the action.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: The White House originally had negotiators from the president's planning to go to Detroit to try and hash things out, between the union and the auto giants, but recently pulled them back. Instead, the president will now go.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: The president has been really clear about this. He believes the union built the middle class, something he's said for years now and, of course, he is a union guy who will continue to fight for \uc2 UAW\uc2  and also union workers. So, that will not end.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: In addition to 36 percent pay increase, union wants a cost of living raise, pension benefits and four-day work week. One day after President Biden meets with picketers, former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  will do the same, instead of attending the FOX Business Republican debate at the Reagan Library in California.\par \par \uc2 And Anderson Economic Group, Bill, an independent economic agency based in Michigan, estimates the total loss since the beginning of the strike at $1.6 billion, a strike that is very much still alive -- Bill.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: David, thank you. David Spunt reports from the White House today. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 I asked Republican candidate for president, Senator Tim Scott out of South Carolina, about that strike, and much more this weekend.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: Senator Scott, thank you for your time.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT (R-SC), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Absolutely. Good to be with you, Bill.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: We just heard priorities from both sides, and you've been outspoken about union demands. How would you solve this?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Yes. Well, listen, one of the things you cannot do is what we're seeing \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  do with the first bill coming out of Congress when he became president. He provided $86 billion to shore up the pension plans of the unions.\par \par \uc2 That leaves a false impression on the minds of the union workers and encourages the union bosses to continue to sell something that could never be, and that's a major issue I have with what's happening with the current \uc2 UAW\uc2  struggle.\par \par \uc2 Their bosses continue to make promises that they cannot keep. I sat, Bill, in a finance hearing and heard a widow -- a union widow come before the committee because her monthly income cut from $4,000 down to $1,000. I want to make sure that we don't see that situation ever again.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: All right. And speaking of those workers, sir, in Iowa this week, you said the following, you said -- I think \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  gave us a great example when federal employees decided they were going to strike. He said, you strike, you're fired, simple concept to me. To the extent we can use that again, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 That's a quote from you. You've taken some heat for that comment.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: The problem, Senator, is that these are not federal employees --\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: -- and to win maybe ultimately, you might need some of their votes.\par \par \uc2 Was that a mistake?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: No, it was not a mistake and if you heard the whole con -- in context, you come to a different conclusion. I said without any question, the federal government does not fire private sector employees, without any question.\par \par \uc2 The question I was raising, which was a question, is, in fact, if we had a better president, one like \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2 , we would not have seen the $86 billion, and the first $1.9 trillion package. I mentioned that specifically after those comments were made.\par \par \uc2 I also mentioned the fact that any time you have a president who says he's willing to go down a picket with workers during the day time, but he cuts a deal at night with the environmentalists that literally destroys those jobs, the union bosses and this president are working against the very workers who are out there picketing.\par \par \uc2 We need a president who tells people the truth. The contracts they are signing for a four-day workweek with more pay, more benefits but fewer hours is one that doesn't work today. It's certainly will not work in the future.\par \par \uc2 But instead of having a president on the picket line, we need this president on the border, the southern border, helping us close the southern border.\par \par \uc2 So, we have two fundamental challenges that we see today. The first one is a president who overpromises and underdelivers, leaving in peril the workers, whether they're union or not.\par \par \uc2 And secondly, we have a president who doesn't solve the looming issues of the day like our southern border that's led to the deaths of 70,000 Americans because of fentanyl in the last 12 months.\par \par \uc2 As president of the United States, I will fix our southern border by closing it and finishing the wall, use $5 billion to use the military grid technology that's available today on our borders.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: OK, so --\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: This is simple for us to solve, but we need to have a president who gets the job.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: Let me see if I can fire through a couple more topics you brought it up, just there at the border.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Sure.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: This week, the President -- some New York Democrats are blaming people like you. They're blaming Republicans. Listen here.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) U.S. PRESIDENT: Unfortunately, the MAGA Republicans in Congress and my predecessor spent four years gutting the immigration system under my predecessor. They continue to undermine our border security today, blocking bipartisan reform.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 KATHY HOCHUL\uc2 , (D) NEW YORK: What's missing in this equation are Republicans who will get back to Washington, stop talking about shutting down the government, roll up their sleeves and work with the Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: So I know you disagree with that. You just ticked off a number of things that you would do to fix the border. Texas has tried to take action repeatedly and they're often sued by Washington. Why do you think that is?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Because Washington is out of touch with the rest of the nation. One of the reasons why we need to have change in the nation's capital is because Washington is so consistently broken that the American people don't trust the people in Washington.\par \par \uc2 I understand that. As a guy who spends some time in Washington, but a whole lot of time in South Carolina, I can just say to the leaders of this nation today, bless your heart, which is a southern way of saying, you guys just don't get it.\par \par \uc2 Here's what the Governor -- Governor Abbott is saying, sending the illegal immigrants to New York City where Mayor Adam says to the Biden administration, you guys don't get it. They sound like the Governor of Texas.\par \par \uc2 When you see the kind of spats that are happening within the Democrat Party, it's because President Biden refuses to fix a fixable problem on our southern border by not selling the construction material to finish the wall. That's what he's doing, using the construction material to complete the wall. We need a president who reinstates the remaining Mexico policy.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: Yeah, so we -- some of these topics may come up. The next debate is a few days away and you're already in California. I want to take you back to last month. This is the first debate in Milwaukee when you had this moment.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: The best thing to do is to bring our jobs home from China. If we create 10 million new jobs in my Made in America Plan, we will have a better economy and a better environment. Let me tell you why I say that, Bret. America --\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS HOST: Do it quickly.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: America has cut -- I'm a Southern boy, I talk slow. So, America --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: There is another one.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: That was quick.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: It might have been a funny moment there. Question though, serious question.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: Is that the Tim Scott we see on Wednesday night?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Yeah, I think what you just played is exactly what I hope to see. I hope you see on Wednesday night, is a guy being authentically who he is? I want to be myself. The goal is for us to be able to have a conversation about the most important issues facing this nation and frankly defend the next generation of kids who are losing hope that the American Dream will work for a kid in rural Iowa or inner-city Chicago.\par \par \uc2 I'm the defender for those who cannot find someone to stand up. I will be the voice for those who feel voiceless because I've been that kid. I felt like there was no hope for my future living in poverty and growing up in a single parent household but I will talk about the contrasts that exist on that stage because the American people deserve someone they can have confidence in because we've lived a similar journey that our obstacles became our opportunities and frankly, looking in the mirror, the problems revealed the promise because we are Americans.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: Well --\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: You're remarkable.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: Yeah, and --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: Yeah, here's your challenge too, though. Since that last debate, President Trump hasn't lost any ground. Here is our poll from Iowa this week. He's steady at 46%. You dropped a few points. And in your home state of South Carolina, Trump's also at 46%. Nikki Haley's at 18% and you trail there at 9%. I think the last question for you is, do you need a breakout moment in this debate?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: I don't think so. What I need to do is to continue to do what I have been doing, showing up the more time I spend in Iowa, the better off I will be, the more time I spend in New Hampshire, the better off I will be. The good news is polls don't vote, but people do. And so I need to make sure that I find myself in front of the most people as possible on a consistent basis. The more I do that, the better off we will be. The good news is this campaign is just getting ready to see the fourth quarter. I love football season. I love football. I like the fourth quarter.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: Well, it kicks off on Wednesday night. We're looking forward to it as well.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Yes, sir.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: Sir, thank you for your time. Senator Tim Scott, thanks for being with us.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Thank you, Bill.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: Also -- also a reminder, the second Republican primary debate is this week at the Reagan Library, Simi Valley, California. You can see it all on Fox Business, Simulcast on Fox News Channel come Wednesday night. It should be a good one.\par \par \uc2 Days after he was charged with accepting bribes in exchange for the use of his influence, Democratic Senator \uc2 Bob Menendez\uc2  steps down as Chairman of the Senate Foreign Relations Committee.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we'll ask our Sunday panel if he'll be able to ignore the calls from the own members of his party to resign altogether.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Do you think if you were to characterize it, are you close?\par \par \uc2 MOHAMMED BIN SALMAN\uc2 , SAUDI CROWN PRINCE: Every day we get closer. It seems it's for the first time real one, serious.\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2 , ISRAEL PRIME MINISTER: When push comes to shove and we have a historic agreement that changes the Middle East. By the way, I think it changes the world. Then I think -- I think everybody will be on board.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: Now, there's a Saudi Crown Prince, \uc2 Mohammed bin Salman\uc2 , Israeli Prime Minister, \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2 , striking an optimistic tone about the potential for a peace deal. Wow.\par \par \uc2 Time for our Sunday group, Townhall.com Editor, Katie Pavlich. Fox News Senior Political Analyst Juan Williams. Guy Benson, host of the Guy Benson Show, and Reuters White House Correspondent, Jeff Mason.\par \par \uc2 Hello to all of you on this rainy weekend in Washington, D.C. Jeff, to you. Both leaders admit there are big issues in front of them. \uc2 Mohammed bin Salman\uc2  talked about the Palestinians. Did Bret's interviews this week give you the sense that we could see a deal?\par \par \uc2 JEFF MASON, REUTERS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Well, it seems from both leaders, including from the interview with Bret that they're getting closer. And it would be a really big deal. It would be a big deal for the Middle East. It would be a big deal for those countries. And it would be a big deal for Israel's position in that region. Keep in mind Saudi Arabia and many other Arab states do not recognize Israel at all. So if they were to make this kind of a deal, it could have a ripple effect on its -- its relationships with other countries.\par \par \uc2 But the big question is what happens with the Palestinians? And what sort of concessions will be made for them in this kind of a pact?\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: We've been watching this story our entire lives, and that's not an exaggeration.\par \par \uc2 MASON: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: It has the potential here, Guy, to be huge. I don't think what Netanyahu said is an overstatement. It could change the world if they get this done.\par \par \uc2 GUY BENSON, TOWNHALL.COM: It would be seismic, no question about that. The Abraham Accords under the previous administration were a very significant achievement, but Saudi Arabia is different in terms of just the emphasis on that country and the role that it plays in the region if they normalize relations with Israel, it would be enormous.\par \par \uc2 It's hard to pick what the most newsworthy moment was in those interviews, from Bret this week. He did an incredible job over there. One of them probably had to be setting aside the Israel question was the nuclear question with Iran.\par \par \uc2 It is obviously understood that Saudi's position recently has been, if Iran keeps pursuing nukes we are going to do it too. To see MBS say it out loud, to me, it was really striking.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: OK Zelenskyy was here in Washington, just want to move to Ukraine on this, Katie, and keep it on foreign affairs. You are hearing more voices on the right that start to pull back and be more hesitant on the aid to Ukraine. Just give this a listen now.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BYRON DONALDS, (R) FLORIDA CONGRESSMAN: To be blunt, we're running a $2 trillion deficit. Any money we give to Ukraine we're borrowing from our future. That's the facts. Those are the truth.\par \par \uc2 MATT GAETZ\uc2 , (R) FLORIDA CONGRESSMAN: Republican voters do not want to see us continue to send more and more money to Ukraine. They want to focus on our country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: Katie, where do you see this debate heading?\par \par \uc2 KATIE PAVLICH, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, the Senate got a classified briefing on this Ukrainian situation and the request from the White House for more aid as President Zelenskyy was visiting the White House this week and a number of senators came out including Senator \uc2 Josh Hawley\uc2  from Missouri, Senator J.D. Vance from Ohio saying that they were not convinced that there is an end goal here and the White House is doing a very poor job in convincing them that there is some kind of goal setting when it comes to ending this war.\par \par \uc2 You have also Congressman Michael Waltz who was a former U.S. Army Green Beret who has been very supportive of sending aid to Ukraine who's bringing up new questions about accountability. You had the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  come out with a big story recently saying there's a lot of corruption questions now that were, you know, a year plus into the situation and Zelenskyy did not address those concerns when he met with a number of people in Washington this week.\par \par \uc2 I also think that the White House not making President Zelenskyy and President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  available for a press conference to answer some of these questions when the American people are war weary and they have not been given some kind of ending to not just the funding but worries about what this means for the rest of the world and this spinning out of control.\par \par \uc2 One final point, you had the Polish President this week saying Ukraine is acting like a drowning person who could bring down the rescuer with them. That is a very significant statement considering how supportive Poland has been and they are much closer to this issue than the United States physically.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: Yeah, to Katie's point on the visit here at the White House, they did have that moment in the Oval Office where they spoke but it seemed like the day after Zelenskyy left Washington they get these attacks on missiles that can, some believe, they can be a game changer. They can travel up to 190 miles. But the theme seems the same. If you want Ukraine to win, why didn't you do this some time ago?\par \par \uc2 JUAN WILLIAMS, FOX NEWS SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, first let me just say I had a different take on the visit. To me, it was disgraceful that the Republican House refused to allow the Ukrainian President to speak to the American people and speak to them to make his case. I mean, you wouldn't need a press conference.\par \par \uc2 Here's the man. He's standing with you. He's making his case for why it's important to continue this effort and why if you really care about standing up to autocrats, including the Chinese, you cannot afford to back off of what's going on in the Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 With regard to the missiles, the French, the British sent missiles of shorter range earlier this year. I think President Biden was reluctant to seem as if he was, you know, somehow about to make this a more -- you know, a more of a threat to the Russians directly.\par \par \uc2 The Russians could perceive this as somehow an escalation of the affair on the United States, the world's largest military power. But I think now the idea is, you know what, these are not going to have single warheads. It's going to be more cluster. We sent cluster bombs recently despite complaints from some human rights folks. And now this is a similar weapon.\par \par \uc2 But look at what has been going on. The Ukrainians have been able to go after the Russian military, especially the Navy and the Sea of Crimea. And you look at Russia right now, the economy showing real signs of stress. You look at, they're going to the North Koreans, they're going to bargain basement for weapons, make no mistake, the Russians are losing.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: And also an indication that the war is going to go on for some time. I want to get another story here in Washington, D.C. This was a stunner on Friday. The big news, the Chairman of the Senate Foreign Relations Committee, \uc2 Robert Menendez\uc2 , that is New Jersey. He and his wife were charged for taking bribes out of Egypt. This is \uc2 Damian Williams\uc2  for the Southern District of New York describing what they think they have.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DAMIAN WILLIAMS\uc2 , U.S. ATTORNEY FOR THE SOUTHERN DISTRICT OF NY: Special agents with the \uc2 FBI\uc2  executed search warrants on the residents and safe deposit box of Senator Menendez. They discovered approximately $500,000 of cash stuffed into envelopes and closets. Some of the cash was stuffed in the senator's jacket pockets. That's not all, agents also discovered a lot of gold.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: Wow. It sure sounds messy, and Menendez has been here before, Jeff.\par \par \uc2 MASON: He certainly has, and he's facing this again. The facts were just laid out roughly $500,000 in cash found in his house, along with the gold bars. Those facts don't look good, but he is innocent until proven guilty. Politically, it's not great for Democrats. In a Senate that has a 51 to 49 majority, although if he were not to run for reelection and somebody else were, New Jersey has not elected a Republican in decades. But even so, it's tricky for him, and it's tricky for his party.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: That last election, statewide election, in November of 2021 was close, a lot closer than a lot of people thought. Do you see him surviving this, Guy?\par \par \uc2 BENSON: Probably not. I'm from New Jersey, and I have a long memory, all the way back to Robert Torres-Selley (ph) in the early 2000s. New Jersey Democrats do what is required to win, and I think they now see him as more of a liability than an asset. There's a Democratic governor sitting there in place. My guess is immense pressure pushes him out.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: By the way, the Governor has said that he should resign.\par \par \uc2 BENSON: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: And so, as most of the congressional delegation from New Jersey, \uc2 Andy Kim\uc2 , the congressman, says he might even run against him. But I'd say --\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: There might be more, too, quickly.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Yeah, but they're not making excuses for the guy. Like some people make excuses for Trump and for Paxson. Oh, no. I don't see Democrats doing this.\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: Gold bars and DNA on the envelopes from both of them.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: It is a unique matter, that's right. Stand by, guys. Thanks, more to come with all you coming up in a moment here. But we want to turn, in a moment here, the Republican presidential hopefuls gear it up for debate number two. We're going to talk with the \uc2 RNC\uc2  Chairwoman, \uc2 Ronna McDaniel\uc2 , for a preview and ask her about the dire warning President Biden issued just last night in Washington about her voters.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE\uc2 , (R) PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: I've had enough already tonight of a guy who sounds like ChatGPT stand up here.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BILL HEMMER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Just one of the many moments during the First Republican debate last month in Milwaukee. So what fireworks can we expect to see this time?\par \par \uc2 With me now, the \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2  Chairwoman \uc2 Ronna McDaniel\uc2  and thank you for being here today on Fox News Sunday. Thank you, Ronna.\par \par \uc2 Round two is Wednesday night. Before I get to that, just last night, President Biden speaking with the Congressional Black Caucus here in Washington, D.C. He said this about the voters in your party.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Let there be no question. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his MAGA Republicans are determined to spread anger, hate, and division. They seek power at all costs to determine and destroy this democracy. I cannot watch that happen, nor can you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: So anger and hate and division and more in that. Meanwhile, we have this today from the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  and ABC in a hypothetical matchup. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  leads \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  in that poll by 9 points, 51, 42. What do you think of that? What do you think of the President's comments from last evening?\par \par \uc2 RONNA MCDANIEL\uc2 , REPUBLICAN NATIONAL COMMITTEE CHAIRWOMAN: Well, the President, you know, came in as this unifier. I'm going to work across the aisle, and he's been nothing like that at all. Our party, our country is more divided than it's ever been. But we're also failing as a country. And that \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  poll shows that three out of four Americans feel like they're doing poorly or not as well under \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . Bidenomics is failing, crime is rising, our border is out of control, people are dying of fentanyl. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is failing and his approval is at 37% with 63% of the country thinking we're on the wrong track. So that is the record of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and why Republicans are surging and have a good chance to take back the White House in 2024.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: OK, let's talk about Wednesday now. As of now, there are seven on stage. Is the field set? And what should viewers expect when they watch?\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: Yeah, the field will be set Monday night. Right now there are seven that have qualified. And I think you're going to see more of what you saw in Milwaukee and these candidates trying to make their case to the American people.\par \par \uc2 The first vote is going to be in January of 2024, January 15th in Iowa. And I think they're trying to get that momentum as they had and of course, President Trump's going to be in Michigan. So we'll be missing him from that debate.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: Right -- right and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  will be in Detroit. Those announced over the weekend. President Biden will be there on Tuesday. He'll be there Wednesday. Will he attend any of your primary debates?\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: Well, you know, I want him to attend a debate and he knows that, everybody knows. I think the other candidates also want him to attend a debate. I hope that before January he comes to a debate and participates in that process. But everybody is doing their own strategy.\par \par \uc2 So the \uc2 RNC\uc2 's role is to create a fair, transparent process and everyone can play their strategy as candidates as -- as how they think they're best going to garner that nomination.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: OK, so nothing -- nothing locked in right now with the former president?\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: No.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: OK. On the issue of abortion, you said that you wanted to see the candidates go after the issue head-on after this moment in Milwaukee in August.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY, (R) PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Can't we all agree that we should ban late-term abortions? Can't we all agree that we should encourage adoptions? Can't we all agree that doctors and nurses who don't believe in abortion shouldn't have to perform them? Can't we all agree that contraception should be available and can't we all agree that we are not going to put a woman in jail or give her the death penalty if she gets an abortion? Let's treat this like the -- like a respectful issue that it is and humanize the situation and stop demonizing the situation.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: So that was quite a moment. You've also said we're not going to win if we don't talk about it. How do you want your candidates to talk about it?\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: Well, I'll be very clear, Bill, the Democrats in 2022 spent $350 million lying to the American people about where Republicans stood on this issue. We have to put money behind our message and we have to put the Democrats on defense. So we can't just say it in an interview like this. Our candidates have to go on TV and say we are for prices -- pregnancy crisis centers. We are for easier adoption. We are for humane limitations. We believe that the rest of our country agrees that there should be limitations when a baby feels pain. That's where most of the world is. That's where Europe is. And that's where we should be as a civilized country.\par \par \uc2 But where -- where do the Democrats stand? What is an abortion there against? Nine-month, eight-month, seven-month, gender selection abortions where China is? So we have to define this issue and take it back for ourselves and push out the Democrats' extremeness on this issue. And if we don't do that, they're going to spend another $350 million lying to the American people.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: OK, this could come up again Wednesday night also. Now, the voting. This week, two major moves. Pennsylvania, I'm in a critical state, said it will automatically register voters when they get a driver's license or a state I.D. And then the state of New York late in the week said it will mail a ballot to every registered voter. I know you're challenging that ruling in New York, but some believe that Republicans don't change their approach on voting. You're going to lose again. What's your strategy, Ronna?\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: Well, I've always been for -- we've got to bank as many votes and vote early as much as possible. And the party did this in 2022. We turned out four million more Republicans, but we've got to continue to do that. Democrats go into the courts, they try and change the laws, they want ballot harvesting, they want to get rid of voter I.D. They're passing laws to allow non-citizens to vote. This is just a fact. They are trying to erode our elections.\par \par \uc2 But if those laws are on the field, that's what we have to play by. And that's why we've launched the Bank Your Vote initiative, encouraging voters to vote early, bank your vote that first day of voting so that we can go chase those other swing voters, or independent voters, text bank to 80810, commit to do that.\par \par \uc2 We can't -- and I say this, Bill, all the time, you're a football fan. We can't win a game if we only start playing in the fourth quarter.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: But you know, you've --\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: We can't allow Democrats to get a huge head start.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: Right. But you know --\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: We got to start voting early.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: -- your strategy, you don't know if it's going to be successful until the last month of next year?\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: Well, that's why we're pushing it now. It's an education, it's getting into different states. We've launched Bank Ohio, Bank Wisconsin, we just launched Bank California with Leader McCarthy. This is something we have to educate our voters on that they can't hold their ballot until Election Day and think we're going to just win elections. We have to do what the Democrats do and bank your vote as part of that.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: Just want to get to one last topic here. There's this ongoing dance between both parties on the primary calendar. The \uc2 RNC\uc2  just warned New Hampshire that if it moves up its primary, which is a possibility, depending on what Iowa does, it's all very convoluted and deep and complex. You are threatening to take away their debate if that happens. The Republican Governor of New Hampshire, \uc2 Chris Sununu\uc2 , did not hold back, he said this, "We will not be threatened, we will follow our law, and we WILL,' in all caps, 'go first. End of story." So how's this going to work out?\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: Well, there was no stride on going first. Listen, I think Chris and I are on the same page on this. And I think New Hampshire and Iowa should recognize the \uc2 RNC\uc2 , the Republican Party, actually through our members. We supported Iowa having the first in the nation caucus and New Hampshire having the first in the nation primary. It's the Democrats that just walked away from those two states, not us. So we're committed to that.\par \par \uc2 But the Democrats are trying to place shenanigans in Iowa. And I don't think New Hampshire should fall for that. And I hope they don't allow Democrats to mess with our primary process and our calendar, especially when they walked away from those two states. And so did \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , by the way.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: We'll see how it works out. \uc2 Ronna McDaniel\uc2 , thank you for your time. We'll see you on Wednesday night, in California. Thank you for being with us today.\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: The leaders of both houses of Congress under fire this week and for very different reasons. In a moment, we'll ask the panel about House Speaker Kevin McCarthy struggled to sell some conservative members of his party on a budget deal. That's sticky.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2 's Senate dress code shake up. That's in the news and for a very interesting reason.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: We are back now with a live look, Simi Valley, California. The day is breaking in a really beautiful way out there.\par \par \uc2 Home of this week's second Republican presidential primary debate, the stakes are high for these candidates as they trail \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in a big way. Trying to separate themselves from the rest of the pack, not easy.\par \par \uc2 Back now with the panel here in Washington. You all look great, by the way. I say that for a reason because it's our third topic coming up here.\par \par \uc2 Check this out, ABC/"Washington Post," earlier today, we woke up to this poll, Trump at 54, DeSantis at 15, Nikki Haley, 7, Tim Scott, 4. Trump's got a 39 point lead over DeSantis.\par \par \uc2 Let's - let's talk about the stakes for Wednesday night. Ladies first.\par \par \uc2 Katie.\par \par \uc2 KATIE PAVLICH, EDITOR, TOWNHALL.COM: Well, it's still a race for second place. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is far ahead. The \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  campaign, who I talked to this morning, says that they are confident they are gaining on him in places like Iowa. You did see \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's campaign backtrack a little bit on his comments about the pro-life issue in Iowa, coming out in favor of Tommy Tuberville's hold on nominees here in Washington, D.C., over the abortion issue.\par \par \uc2 But this week, you know, I'm expecting to see a number of Republicans drill down on policy. DeSantis issued an energy plan just this week. So, we'll see what happens, but it's certainly still a race for second place.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: OK.\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: And you're looking at Iowa. Of course, New Hampshire's, you were just discussing with the chairwoman. And then, of course, these candidates have to get to South Carolina, where Nikki Haley is going to have a better time than she's having in New Hampshire.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: Gotcha. Just want to go to the other Republican on the panel here. How do you see it? Does one candidate have more to win or lose on Wednesday night, do you believe?\par \par \uc2 BENSON: Well, the issue for everyone else in the field is that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  not only has a big lead in the primary, a huge lead in the primary, there are two polls out this morning, one from "\uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " that you put on the screen, big lead for Trump in a general over a \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  hypothetically. NBC has a poll showing the race exactly tied. So, the argument that Trump is unelectable, at least at the moment, isn't really gaining steam with Republican voters and their, you know, data that you can point to, to flesh that out.\par \par \uc2 So, if they want to make a splash for themselves, they need ultimately to consolidate the non-Trump people behind one person. I think for that reason \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  probably has the most at stake this week because he needs to remind people that he is sort of the second guy there. And if someone is going to brings a lot of the support behind him, it's going to be him, because he's been fading a little bit in some of the polls.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: OK. All right, let's talk of this shutdown. It's - it's the talk of this town. America may not necessarily be paying attention to it, but everybody in D.C. does.\par \par \uc2 Here is the House speaker this week on the possibility and what the White House has to say about it. Check it out.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): There are still a few members that won't vote for funding the government while we finish all the 12 bills.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: The best plan is for there to not be a shutdown. This is something that can be avoided here. This is something that House Republicans know very well that they have to do their jobs. They're the ones - they're the ones to fix this problem.\par \par \uc2 TIM BURCHETT(R-TN): Dadgum it, pass the dadgum budget.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: Dadgum twice there.\par \par \uc2 Jeff, if there is no deal at this hour next Sunday the government would shut down. Can they avert this?\par \par \uc2 JEFF MASON, WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT, "REUTERS": Well, first I think it's important to talk about what happens if the government shutdown -- shuts down. The military doesn't get paid. Programs stop. Some things continue, but the non-essential quote/unquote things do not. And the political ramifications of that will almost certainly be blaming the Republicans. The White House will have a credible argument that this is a Republican shutdown and I can guarantee you that will be something that will be part of a \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  campaign ad.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: OK. In the past, Juan, we have seen the World War II Memorial being shut down and people thought, what's the significance of that? I mean it's unfortunate to see, but sometimes the - the hyperbolic nature of a shutdown is bigger than it actually is. How would you see it?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: I think that you have to understand there are people who don't get paid, people who don't go to work. And if this is a significant portion of the American economy, at a time when we're trying to keep the economy functional.\par \par \uc2 You know, the point here, politically, is that you see a lot of in-fighting on the Republican side in the House. And Kevin McCarthy, the speaker, is trying to hold on to his job. You know, he can be challenged by just one member of the Congress in terms of holding the speaker's job. And he's having a difficult time. He is in the storm and I think it's all about that Republican fight in the House over power.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: OK. Next week, is it open or closed? Care to wager?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: I'd wager it's going to close because I don't see a solution.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: We'll see if Juan's right. OK, replay the tape.\par \par \uc2 The other big story here is how the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  will be required to dress. \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  is suspending the jacket and tie rule, in large part to Pennsylvania Senator \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 . Peggy Noonan writes this, this week in "The Wall Street Journal." She says the senator's shorts and American decline. We want to be respected, but no longer think we need to be respectable."\par \par \uc2 There has been pushback already on this, Katie, from Republicans and Democrats. Do you think this rule survives in a week?\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: This - this is a reflection on \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2 's sloppy leadership in the Senate as majority leader, whether it's not being able to tie votes properly or whether it's not talking to the entire Senate about whether they would approve of this rule. You have a number of Democratic senators coming out and saying, we wish we have just had a discussion about this and we could, you know, deny this - this rule for being changed for one man because it's not reflective of respect for the job that we're doing for the American people. So, I think it will probably be repealed and does not reflect well on his leadership.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: Jeff, what do you think? What are you hearing?\par \par \uc2 MASON: I think that norms have changed. People are dressing more casually, for sure. Whether that means it should be more casual in the Senate is another --\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: Come on.\par \par \uc2 MASON: Well, it's true. But I think - but the point -\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: Just - you can - you can look nice and dignified without wearing a suit.\par \par \uc2 MASON: Hold on, the point - the --\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: And he - \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  looks like a slob.\par \par \uc2 MASON: The point that I was going to take is, you know, what you look like does impact your work. It's important to - to dress - I - I mean, I wear a tie when I go to the White House. I think - I think a lot of people dress conservatively. But I think the American people, at the end of the day, probably care more about what their senators are doing than what they're wearing.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: To this side of the table then, Guy and Juan, I've got a minute left. What happens?\par \par \uc2 BENSON: They say this is because it - it's his condition. It's easier for him to dress this way. \uc2 John McCain\uc2  was tortured and had painful war wounds. He managed to abide by the code. Mark Kirk had a massive stroke. He managed to abide by the code. Tammy Duckworth lost limbs in war. She manages to abide by the code. I think that excuse is pretty pitiful for Senator Fetterman.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: Juan, wrap it up.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Well, I think it's - I think most Democrats don't want to change the rules. We don't have to go back to powdered wigs and spats (ph). You know, not that far.\par \par \uc2 But I think if you're on the floor or here in the gallery, you do expect some degree of decorum.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: Thank you. As I said, you all look great on this Sunday.\par \par \uc2 MASON: Back at you, Bill.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: Thanks for being here. Great to see you, panel.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: You look marvelous.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Oh, man.\par \par \uc2 Taking another live look now, Simi Valley, home of Wednesday's second Republican presidential debate. In a moment here I'll speak with one of the co-moderators. And my "AMERICA'S NEWSROOM" co-host Dana Perino will join me live with a preview.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: Here is a live look at the \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  Presidential Library, Simi Valley, California. A stunning place to be. Republican presidential hopefuls take the debate stage on Wednesday night.\par \par \uc2 And Dana Perino, my co-anchor on "AMERICA'S NEWSROOM," also co-anchor of "THE FIVE," with me now.\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, GOP PRIMARY DEBATE CO-MODERATOR: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: One of the three moderators for this debate.\par \par \uc2 Great to see you.\par \par \uc2 As we like to say on "AMERICA`S NEWSROOM," before we go.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: And before we go, I want - I want to get your sense. You told me over the weekend, you said, this is where the rubber meets the road in this debate. What do you mean by that?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: So, I believe in the first debate all of the candidates in some ways were introducing themselves to GOP primary voters and the public more widely. A month later, this is, to me, where the rubber meets the road because the standards in order to get in were a little bit tougher, but still you have seven people on the stage. And you have President Trump with a commanding lead in this race. And so right now this is the moment where all of them realize, if I don't have some sort of break-out moment to show that I could be the rival, the alternative to President Trump, their campaigns probably start to lose even more steam.\par \par \uc2 Now, you do see some uptick for DeSantis and Haley, a little for \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2 , but not in that NBC poll or the ABC one that you showed this morning. So, you have all of these people saying they think they can be better than either President Trump or \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , and now they're going to have to really show if that's true.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: Yes, as I mentioned earlier on our program here, Fox Business is the host, but you're going to run the gamut on issues. And I - I just want to get a sense of that from you. And also, you know, this is - this is round two now. It - do you feel it's better to go second? Would you have rather gone first in Milwaukee a month ago?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: This is my first time moderating, so I'm happy to go second. Frankly, I'm just happy to be here. I love this area. I think that the Reagans chose the most perfect place to have their library. The legacy lives on. It's such a gorgeous place and I think that everyone will be just amazed by what Fox News Channel and Fox Business have been able to do with this set.\par \par \uc2 And so, yes, I'm really honored to be here.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: So, with your co-moderators, one of the challenge in Milwaukee was keeping people reigned in. I just need kind of a quick answer, do you have a strategy for that?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Well, I think that they should have a strategy. If they're all talking over one another, no one is going to be -- hear anything that they have to say. The audience, it will be made clear what the rules are and people like people following rules and standards, even dress codes. I liked your last panel.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Great to see you, partner. We'll talk to you during the week. And best of luck out there in California.\par \par \uc2 Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Dana Perino, thanks.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Thank you. Miss you.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: Miss you, too. See you soon.\par \par \uc2 Stuart Varney\uc2  and Univision's Ilia Calderon will also co-moderate with Dana. So we'll see that on Wednesday night.\par \par \uc2 We'll see you tomorrow morning on "AMERICA'S NEWSROOM" on the Fox News Channel. So, check that out.\par \par \uc2 That is it for today here in Washington, D.C..\par \par \uc2 Thank you for being here. Shannon is back next week and she will see you on the next FOX NEWS SUNDAY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FOXN000020230924ej9o00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 Border Crisis Reaches Historic Breaking Point; Thousands Of Migrants Surge Into Eagle Pass, Texas; Biden Deliberately Exacerbates Border Crisis; Senator Menendez Indicted On Bribery Charges\b0\par\par\uc2 Will Cain, Griff Jenkins, Chad Pergram, Jonathan Hunt, Laura  Ingraham, Jimmy Failla \par \uc2 7,503 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 September 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: The Ingraham Angle\par \uc2 INGANG\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Teens charged in fatal hit-and-run appear in court; What's going on with our kids; Pregnant mom with cancer defied doctors' abortion guidance> BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS HOST: Miles for the interview with the Prime Minister and all worked well. Thanks to a lot of people. Thank you for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for this "SPECIAL REPORT". Fair, balanced, and still unafraid. THE INGRAHAM ANGLE is up next.\par \par \uc2 WILL CAIN, FOX NEWS HOST: I'm Will Cain in for Laura Ingraham on this special edition of THE INGRAHAM ANGLE from New York City tonight. I want to wish a very big congratulation to the Biden Administration because they have hit a brand-new record.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (D), VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We did it, we did it, Joe. You're going to be the next President of the United States.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAIN: They did it. We just learned that last month they had the highest number of illegal immigrant apprehensions at the border ever, for the month of August. And so far in this fiscal year, there has been nearly 233,000 apprehensions already beating out last year, that's basically importing a Boise, Idaho, a Spokane, Washington, a Scottsdale, Arizona, every month. So that's \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's closed border. It's so locked down that we needed to go now live to Eagle Pass, Texas where Fox News National Correspondent Griff Jenkins, is there to show us exactly how tight this closed border is by the Biden Administration. Griff.\par \par \uc2 GRIFF JENKINS, FOX NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Will, good evening, Happy Friday to you and it just doesn't stop, the numbers keep coming and coming here. In Eagle Pass, you walk along the river bank, Will and you see that this is a crisis. Texas, putting the miles of this dense, dangerous barbed wire, razor wire, which isn't stopping the migrants from coming through.\par \par \uc2 In fact, let's go to our drone in the sky right now. And you can see it's getting hotter in the afternoon. The groups have been smaller last few hours. But we see still just a couple of migrants, the back part of a larger group that came earlier, they're still struggling to negotiate that razor wire trying to get through.\par \par \uc2 Why? Because they know that this administration favors mass catch and release and they are likely to be guaranteed the ability to stay, get a notice to appear at a court date years from now that they won't show up to and now, in the case of Venezuelans, many will be able to get a work authorization and get work which is the reason why they left their countries to begin with.\par \par \uc2 But economic insecurity isn't enough to qualify for asylum under our laws. Yet this administration has created an incentive to get them here. And you talk about the 232,000 in month of August. It's just a long stream of this year fiscal year. We now know CBP sources confirming to us, Will, that there's nearly 2.4 million migrant encounters, the most ever on record, breaking last year's all-time high record of 2.378 million. And it's just not stopping with 2400 in the last 24 hours here.\par \par \uc2 Now, let me show you some of the videos that we've been shooting all day today and yesterday of the migrants going through that razor wire, but not all of them make it, Will. In fact, just a few hours ago, they pulled yet another body from the river just feet from where we're standing. It's the third drowning in this many days, three drownings in three days. That is why these agents are having to see this and they're reaching a breaking point,\par \par \uc2 Will, they're overworked. They're overwhelmed. They don't see support from an administration. They don't see policy changes coming. And yet the numbers keep rising and rising and now they're having to pull bodies out of this river or treat wounded migrants coming through the razor wire.\par \par \uc2 Now here in Maverick County, the Sheriff Tom Schmerber, that is Maverick County where Eagle Pass is a part of, asked him, can you sustain this? Can this continue? Here's what he had to say. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: Can Maverick County sustain these numbers?\par \par \uc2 TOM SCHMERBER, MAVERICK COUNTY SHERIFF: No, no way. No way. As you can see these people here walking and they're not running away from anybody because they know they're going to be stopped by anybody.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: And here in Eagle Pass, that's the situation but other sectors are getting hammered as well, Will. In El Paso, they're seeing the same surge in numbers in three cases they've had in the last 24 hours confirmed cases of tuberculosis, highly contagious disease and it is hard to overstate how difficult it is for Border Patrol agents to mitigate having overwhelmed numbers in your shelters or over capacity so you have nowhere to put them and now you have to contact trace among 2000, 3000, 4000 people a day with tuberculosis running through it. It's absolutely a crisis in every sense of it. And no sign of it slowing down. Will.\par \par \uc2 CAIN: Live images right there, Griff, you're giving us, people trying to make their way through that razor wire into the United States. Thank you so much, Griff. Authorities from Maverick County, Texas to New York City telling you something that actually we should not make light of, that we should not joke, that this country is changing right before our very eyes and we cannot handle the influx of illegal immigrants.\par \par \uc2 And all the while the Biden Administration not only refuses to take responsibility for this crisis or provide a strategy or resources to stop it, but they provide incentives to make these numbers a mere drop in the bucket to ensure that we will explode the number of illegal immigrants coming to this country in the future. The Democrat mayor of Eagle Pass where Griff has been says Biden is 100 percent responsible, by the way for what is happening to his city.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROLANDO SALINAS (D) EAGLE PASS MAYOR: I haven't heard from anybody in the administration. Nobody has bothered to call me, anyone in the city staff saying, hey, this is the federal government. We know what you're going through. We're worried about you. This is our plan of action. Nothing. We're here abandoned. We're on the border. We're asking for help. This is unacceptable, please, we have thousands of people coming in without one single consequences, just not fair.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAIN: A Democrat, just like the mayor of New York City Eric Adams, a Democrat. Joining me now is Brandon Jodd, President of the National Border Patrol Council. Brandon, great to have you -- on the program this evening. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , Brandon has said that the real fault in this situation does not lie with his administration, but actually lies with a man who hasn't been in office for two and a half years, he still blames it on President Trump, Brandon. He blames it on MAGA Republicans. What do you say?\par \par \uc2 BRANDON JUDD, NATIONAL BORDER PATROL COUNCIL PRESIDENT: Well, let's fact check him in real-time. That's absolutely false. All we have to do is look at what was took place. under the Trump Administration, we had the most orderly immigration system in my history. In 25 years of doing this job, I'd never seen anything that was more orderly than under President Trump.\par \par \uc2 Then you look at the numbers that we apprehended under President Trump. We dropped to 45-year lows, something that this administration completely ignores. Look at the number of -- look at the amount of fentanyl that was on our streets. It was at all-time lows under President Trump because we were able to go after the criminal cartels in real-time. That is fact checking right now, when you look at what's going on under this administration, we are seeing more fentanyl on our streets than we've ever seen before.\par \par \uc2 We're seeing more deaths of United States citizens than we've ever seen before. We're seeing more crime than we've ever seen before by illegal immigrants in the United States. We're also seeing more deaths of those people that are crossing our borders illegally. We're seeing more drownings than ever before. We're seeing more deaths in the deserts than we've ever seen before.\par \par \uc2 Everything is at record highs under this administration. And it's all based upon their policies, they change policies that were working that President Trump implemented. And because of those changes, we're seeing a complete chaos on our borders, and everything is allowing our -- is allowing the cartels to generate more profits than in any time in my 25-year career, which again, is killing more United States citizens, we have to care, we hope that this administration would care more about the American citizens than their political aspirations. But unfortunately, that's what we're seeing, we're seeing them --\par \par \uc2 CAIN: More on those policies --\par \par \uc2 JUDD: Their political aspirations than the American people\par \par \uc2 CAIN: More on those policies in just a moment. Brandon, take a look at these numbers. You're in the Tucson sector, I believe you can see the numbers what's happening just in that sector alone. 11,000 apprehensions this week, as you mentioned many other angles of this entire tragedy, you can see the number of narcotics events and human smuggling involved as well.\par \par \uc2 But I want to move to a potential solution, Brandon and I think every American respects and appreciates the job that border patrol does even with at times its hands politically tied. But many Americans have asked for more, not necessarily from border patrol, but from any politician. They've asked for state governors to declare an invasion, giving them powers to invoke their own national guard to address the problem.\par \par \uc2 Governor Greg Abbott of Texas today did so, Brandon, he put up a declaration he invoked Article 1 Section 10 clause three of the United States Constitution, which he says gives him the power to declare an invasion and do the following things on your screen, which is most importantly, probably that bottom one, repel migrants. He said in that declaration, by the way to the border.\par \par \uc2 Now Brandon, that's normally a job of border patrol. But I think at this point, Americans are saying anyone, everyone, stop triage, stop the bleeding. Is this something you believe will help?\par \par \uc2 JUDD: It will as long as we remove people immediately, if we end catch and release, if we end the reward that releases people into United States, people will stop coming. That's what's so amazing about what this -- what Biden is doing. If he would simply enact policy that would allow us to remove people immediately from the United States, we would see an immediate decrease in illegal immigration. And when we see that immediate decrease, then we're able to go after the cartels. Yes, it will help what Governor Abbott is going to do. Let's just hope that he does it immediately.\par \par \uc2 CAIN: That's right. Let's watch and make sure it's executed in Texas. Brandon Judd, thank you so much for being with us tonight. Alright, Joining me now Monica Crowley, former Assistant Secretary of Treasury for Public Affairs and \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2 , former New York. Congressman and gubernatorial candidate. Great to have you both on the program this morning.\par \par \uc2 I said Monica just a moment ago that I was going to return to those policies of the Biden Administration that serve as incentive. I want you to watch this clip if you would of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  at the \uc2 Congressional Hispanic Caucus\uc2  last night, saying exactly what he plans to do that in my estimation, Monica, will only continue to serve as a magnet for this problem. Listen to this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (D), PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We're significantly expanding legal pathways for entry so businesses can get the workers they need. I've also directed my team to make historic increase in the number of refugees admitted from Latin America, we've launched outreach campaigns, helping over a million eligible migrants apply for work permits. And we're accelerating the process for work permit application. Right now --\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAIN: Monica, he's asking more illegal immigrants to claim asylum and get jobs in America.\par \par \uc2 MONICA CROWLEY, FORMER ASSISTANT SECRETARY OF TREASURY: Yes, he's slurred his way through that statement. But the incentives are very obvious to the American people, but also to people around the world who want to come into the United States and do it illegally. He's made that very clear.\par \par \uc2 Look for all the people, Will, who talk about, well, what doesn't \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  have a plan to deal with the out-of-control border and these massive waves of illegal immigrants coming into the country. This is the plan, any normal politician when they're seeing the negative fallout, the negative economic, humanitarian and national security implications of this wide-open border for nearly three years now, any normal president would have corrected the course, change the policy, we have not seen one second of that.\par \par \uc2 He has not changed the policy at all. So this is the plan. The plan is to flood the country. You know, the old communist radicals covered in Pivon (ph) talked about the most effective way to destroy the United States was to overwhelm her systems. And what better way to do it than to have a wide- open border where you have millions of illegal immigrants coming into the country, bringing tsunamis of crime, drugs, disease, the massive strain on all of our resources, from medical care to education. This is the deliberate destruction of the country.\par \par \uc2 CAIN: Yes, and Lee, every politician just about who is affected by this problem, including very prominent Democrats, like right here in New York City, Mayor Eric Adams are saying the system is overwhelmed to Monica's point. And New York, by the way, I mean, I have a hard time extending sympathy to New York as someone from Texas or anywhere along the southern border, but New York is now absorbing hundreds of thousands of illegal immigrants.\par \par \uc2 LEE ZELDIN\uc2  (R) FORMER NEW YORK CONGRESSMAN: And the governor of New York was calling for this a couple of years ago, you had democratic elected officials cheering on President Biden, when he was instituting this, plan is, as you and Monica have been pointing out, this is on purpose. No border, no country, more Democrats are realizing that no border, no reelection. You can't lecture the American public about Bidenomics, national security and homeland security, health care, child trafficking, combating fentanyl, and the list goes on. If you're going to be deliberately implementing this policy at the border.\par \par \uc2 I think that this is an existential threat, not just for many challenges for our country on the substance and the policy, but politically, it's a nightmare. So it doesn't make much sense, especially as 2024 gets closer.\par \par \uc2 CAIN: You both have said something so intelligent tonight that I just want to underline it from both of you. Monica, you're absolutely right. This is not a policy failure. This is a policy success for the Biden Administration. This is the execution of a policy. And Lee, there's no nation, there's no nation state without a border. We're watching the destruction on purpose successfully of the nation state of the United States. Thank you, both, Monica and Lee for being with us tonight.\par \par \uc2 Alright, coming up, why are we better protecting Ukraine's border than our own? Plus cash, gold bars, bribery. pockets full of money. I'm not talking about our latest gift to Ukraine. I'm talking about now twice indicted New Jersey Democrat \uc2 Bob Menendez\uc2 . The details are next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAIN: Gold bars, hidden cash, fancy cars, sounds like a rap video. It's not. It's the indictment against Democrat Senator \uc2 Bob Menendez\uc2 . Fox News Senior Congressional Correspondent Chad Pergram is here with all the details. Chad.\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS SENIOR CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Good evening. There are now calls for Menendez to resign his seat, Democratic New Jersey Representative \uc2 Frank Pallone\uc2  wants the senator out. So does the Democratic Governor \uc2 Phil Murphy\uc2 , but it's unclear if that will happen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE\uc2  (R) FORMER GOVERNOR OF NEW JERSEY: If \uc2 Bob Menendez\uc2  were to resign, or be removed from office after a conviction, \