ow, Bob, there's a lot of different kind of crime. There's a lot of different type of youth crime. We often see when it comes to violence as something like gang criminal activity. But what we're watching here in all of these instances is just general mayhem. Why?\par \par \uc2 BOB WOODSON, WOODSON CENTER PRESIDENT AND FOUNDER: Well, for one thing, we're sending the lethal message, particularly to black kids, that you are exempt from any personal responsibility. In fact, it's even racist to talk about personal responsibility. You have the hypocrisy of some of these leaders, like the Minnesota vice chair of the Republican -- I mean, the Democratic labor party -- the leader in defunding the police after George Floyd.\par \par \uc2 And what happened last week is that she was violently assaulted by four young black men with guns, breaking her legs and giving severe lacerations to her face, but it was the police that saved her. And now she is a law- and-order advocate.\par \par \uc2 The question is, why do we have these leaders in these cities continuing to a priest exemption if you're black. The crisis that we have is moral and spiritual and it can only be solved by going into the community suffering the problem and recruiting moral mentors and character coaches who serve as antibodies from within that community. And I've seen it work to witness to these young people.\par \par \uc2 CAIN: Can you outsource that, Bob? You know, I think about the things that might be contributing to this. I do think, by the way, that generally human beings have been dehumanized through this device, through constantly being on technology. Always somebody far away. I think religion's role in this and the decline of religion is part of this. As you pointed out, lack of accountability.\par \par \uc2 But ultimately, Bob, the foundational structure of society is the family. And when we see young people go wrong, I can't help but think you can't fix it unless you have that foundational element, the family.\par \par \uc2 WOODSON: But there are surrogates that have been effective in redirecting young people. I can give you the names of Terrence Daly, Curtis Monroe, other young people, many of them ex-offenders who were themselves very troubled, who became transformed and redeemed.\par \par \uc2 And I've seen just four of these young leaders reach out to 120 middle schoolers and coach them in football, and have these young men take directions from people like the Alliance of Concerned Men.\par \par \uc2 So, there are surrogate parents that are available in these communities, but we don't invest in that. We invest in shop detectors. We invest in social workers. We do not invest where the solutions exist.\par \par \uc2 And you will never hear entities indigenous leaders who are positively redirecting these young people whine about racism or injustice. They preach resilience, personal responsibility, but the qualities that make them effective make them invisible. We've got to change that.\par \par \uc2 CAIN: Right. I appreciate that answer twofold, Bob, that note of optimism, because one, we need solutions. We need optimism. And number two, because I know that you're a man who's been on the ground and gives me that answer from a place of experience, and someone who has helped find those solutions. Thank you for sharing that with us tonight, Bob Woodson.\par \par \uc2 All right. Now it's time for our political palate cleanser. I used to, you know, do a little \uc2 ESPN\uc2  first take. My sparring partner, my friend, Stephen A. Smith, he didn't have a shining moment last night at Yankee Stadium.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Order. Oh. Oh. Oh, well, if you're playing stickball, that one worked out.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAIN: It's supposed to go over the plate, Stephen A. You don't hit the grass before the plate. It's supposed to -- over the plate, into the mitt. He's taking it well. He's laughing along. So, bravo for Stephen A. I haven't been on the mound. So there before the grace of God, go I.\par \par \uc2 Up next, Laura's powerful interview with a pregnant woman whose doctors told her she needed an abortion to survive a rare brain cancer. She tells us the decision she made next. Stay there.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LAURA INGRAHAM, FOX NEWS HOST: Your health or your baby's life? Which would you choose? Well, that's a question that no mother would ever want to face, but my next guest had to. Last year, when she was 20 weeks pregnant with her second child, Tasha Khan was diagnosed with a rare and aggressive form of brain cancer. She needed chemotherapy and radiation, her doctor said, and she needed it right away.\par \par \uc2 They urged her to have an abortion so she could start her treatments. She said no. They told her she'd only have 12 months left to live. Yet, she still said no. She was determined not to abort her baby, regardless of what it did to her own health or her own life.\par \par \uc2 Tasha Kann joins me now, along with her husband, Taylor, and daughter, Gracie. I hear her there, Tasha. What a beautiful family, first of all. Most people hearing about stories like this, and you and your husband have had to go through so much, Tasha.\par \par \uc2 And every cancer is different. I had to go through chemotherapy and radiation like 17 years ago, so it's no walk in the park. But your cancer is extremely aggressive. Brain cancers are very tough to treat. Why did you decide to choose your baby's health and life and put yours on hold?\par \par \uc2 TASHA KANN, REFUSED ABORTION: Just for that exact reason, because I knew how aggressive the cancer was and how hard it would be to treat. I didn't think it would be possible to treat it. So, putting my baby before me was more important. And that's what I decided, you know, to do.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Taylor, did you, for any moment, think that you could dissuade your wife, or anyone, I should say, could dissuade your wife from making this decision, knowing her as you do?\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR KANN, TASHA'S HUSBAND: No, I knew as soon as they mentioned it, it was not a great idea because it was never an option for any of us, but especially her. And I feel like she made the best decision that we all could and it's been wonderful.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Tasha, tell us about you, your health, and I want to get back into kind of your faith and your motivation for this and what's keeping you going, because I know this has been a very tough year.\par \par \uc2 KANN: My family keeps me going. God keeps me going. You know, the doctors telling me I Wouldn't survive pregnancy, and I did. And then telling me I had to have chemo and radiation and it would be my only options for survival, and I refused that and I Still had stable scans over and over again.\par \par \uc2 They kept telling me, you know, we don't know -- really know what's going on, why the cancer isn't spreading, and just kept pushing the chemo and radiation, which made no sense, the cancer wasn't spreading, why would I do a treatment that could, you know, kill me?\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: When you think about this past year, and you know there are women all across the country who are going through similar things. I mean, you have to take this, and if you don't take this, you could die, or you'll be irreparably damaged health-wise if you don't get an abortion.\par \par \uc2 Given your own experience, what might you say to those women facing that same choice?\par \par \uc2 KANN: Abortion is never an option. What does a baby have to do with your health? It just, to me, you have to look at all the options. Advocate for yourself. You know, doctors look at you as a patient, and not your baby as a patient. You know, the baby is in their way. So, the first thing they want to do is get that baby out of their way.\par \par \uc2 So, you have to consider that and think of, you know, well, w