ight 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Or if you're a mother, and you think your kids in the car, mother can do it.\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Right.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Or if you're an angel dust.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes, yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes, or --\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Highly underrated, by the way.\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: More than Tyrus.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes. Tyrus can lift anything. All right, including our hearts.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Oh.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: That's it for us. Have a great night.\par \par \uc2 MIKE EMANUEL, \uc2 FOX\uc2  NEWS CHANNEL CHIEF WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT: Good evening. Welcome to Washington. I'm Mike Emanuel, in for Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 "BREAKING TONIGHT", a pivotal point in the border crisis. The southern border town of Eagle Pass, Texas is under a state of emergency tonight the Democratic mayor of Eagle Pass made the declaration as 1000s of illegal immigrants are crossing into the country in virtually nonstop fashion.\par \par \uc2 We have "FOX TEAM COVERAGE". Peter Doocy at the White House shows us what the President's press secretary did when he asked about the crisis. We begin with correspondent Bill Melugin who has the latest from Eagle Pass. Hello, Bill.\par \par \uc2 BILL MELUGIN, \uc2 FOX\uc2  NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Mike, illegal crossings here at the southern border are back at record high levels. Border Patrol agents tell us their morale has never been lowered. And after 2-1/2 years already, it appears this border crisis is showing no signs of stopping.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN (voice over): Illegal crossings into Eagle Pass, Texas, surged once again today, with Fox News cameras catching a nonstop flow of migrants, beginning at sunrise and continuing almost nonstop all day long.\par \par \uc2 Neither Texas razor wire nor federal Border Patrol have been able to dissuade migrants from crossing into Eagle Pass, where a mass illegal crossing of 3,000 migrants also took place on Wednesday. Most of them, adult men from Venezuela.\par \par \uc2 CBP sources tell Fox, there were over 10,000 migrants encountered at the southern border on Wednesday, an all-time record high in number only seen once before, in May, before the end of Title 42.\par \par \uc2 Eagle Pass's mayor, a Democrat, has declared an emergency, and is calling out the White House.\par \par \uc2 ROLANDO SALINAS, MAYOR OF EAGLE PASS, TEXAS: Eagle Pass is a city of 30,000 people. We cannot sustain this flow. We need help. We need it now. We need people from the top. I'm talking from our president, our vice president. We're scared to even go outside. We can't walk anymore, because we have random people walking the streets.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN (voice over): Meanwhile, the Biden administration announced late last night, it will offer nearly half a million Venezuelan migrants in the U.S. legal status and work permits through an expansion of the Temporary Protected Status program, as Biden bows to demands from sanctuary states and cities, who say they are full.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 KATHY HOCHUL\uc2  (D-NY): We have to let the word out that when you come to New York, you're not going to have more hotel rooms, we don't have capacity. So, we have to also message properly that we're at our limit. If you're going to leave your country, go somewhere else.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN (on camera): And Mike, on top of the TPS for Venezuelans, DHS just announced this afternoon, they are also re designating Temporary Protected Status for Afghan nationals here in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 Listen to this, they say any Afghan nationals who crossed into the United States between May of 2022 and yesterday will now be offered work permits and protections from deportation, even if they crossed the U.S. border illegally.\par \par \uc2 We'll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Bill Melugin, starting us off live in Eagle Pass, Texas. Bill, many thanks.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration is refusing to answer questions about the border crisis. White House correspondent Peter Doocy has that part of the story tonight. Good evening, Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, \uc2 FOX\uc2  NEWS CHANNEL WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Mike, officials around here are very effective with branding. When they want to take credit for economic policy, they call it Bidenomics. So, what about immigration policy?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: What do you call it here at the White House, when 10,000 people illegally cross the border in a single day?\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: So, what do you call it, Peter, when GOP put forth a -- wait, no. No, no, no, no, no, no. No, you can't.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: I'm asking, Karine.\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: I'm answering, OK, we're going to move on.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: You're answering question of the president. OK.\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: No, no, no, no. No, no, no. No, no, no. We're moving on.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): Responding to a separate inquiry, the press secretary insisted, the Biden border strategy is an improvement.\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: The president have worked very hard to implement a strategy when it comes to the border. That is humane, safe, and has orderly enforcement.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): A different border was top of the agenda in the East Room today, the one between Russia and Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I began the process of formalizing our long-term commitment to Ukraine security alongside the \uc2 G7\uc2 , and with other partners.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: The visiting President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2 , is seen in the West Wing as being politically savvy enough to ignore a congressional fight over funding for Ukraine's defense.\par \par \uc2 JAKE SULLIVAN, NATIONAL SECURITY ADVISER: President Zelenskyy is not coming here like a babe in the woods.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Officials did catch SPECIAL REPORT last night, where the Saudi Crown Prince was asked about Iran's nuclear weapons ambitions.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, \uc2 FOX\uc2  NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR: Are you concerned though, Iran gets a nuclear weapon, about them, getting a nuclear weapon?\par \par \uc2 MOHAMMED BIN SALMAN\uc2  AL SAUD, CROWN PRINCE OF SAUDI ARABIA: When we are concerned of any country getting a nuclear weapon.\par \par \uc2 If they get one, we have to get one. For security reason, for balancing power and --\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): The White House sees that as another reason to stop Iran from building a nuke in the first place.\par \par \uc2 SULLIVAN: One of the major reasons that we are working overtime with partners and allies, to ensure that Iran never gets a nuclear weapon is that if they did, not only would they be a direct threat to the region and beyond, but it likely would trigger a regional arms race.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (on camera): The Ukraine meeting at the White House tonight is part of the White House strategy to answer questions for people concerned about President Biden's age.\par \par \uc2 We have learned that he's starting to tell campaign donors, he knows that people are talking about his age and he gets it. But he thinks that just gives him an argument to talk about his experience with two things, vaccinating, the nation, and rallying the world against Russian aggression.\par \par \uc2 Mike?\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: We'll follow it. Peter Doocy, live in the North Lawn. Peter, thanks a lot.\par \par \uc2 There are new questions tonight about the seeming inevitability of former President Trump's campaign to regain the Republican nomination. He is apparently been hurt by recent comments on abortion. Now, one rival in particular appears to be benefiting.\par \par \uc2 Here is correspondent Mark Meredith.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY (R), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Thank you so much. It's great to be here.\par \par \uc2 MARK MEREDITH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Stomping today in New Hampshire, former \uc2 U.N\uc2 . Ambassador Nikki Haley is seeing a spike in support ahead of next week second GOP debate.\par \par \uc2 A poll of New Hampshire Republicans finds Haley now essentially tied with businessman \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2 . Their gains come at Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 's expense. He is now polling fifth.\par \par \uc2 In Iowa, DeSantis maintains a second-place lead, but the latest Fox Business survey, shows him still trailing former President Trump by 31 points.\par \par \uc2 Trump held multiple events in Iowa, Wednesday, telling supporters his victory is all but guaranteed.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We are going to win the Iowa caucuses in a historic landslide.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (voice over): But GOP strategist Karl Rove believes Trump's lead could still collapse. Rove writes, "The higher he raises expectations, the easier will be for his performance to disappoint."\par \par \uc2 Voters tell Fox, they are convinced Trump will prevail.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: He did what he said he was going to do and he put our country first.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (voice over): Still, Trump is facing skepticism from some evangelical voters about his views on abortion, after he criticized Florida's recently imposed six-week limit.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER, WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT, NBC NEWS: That goes too far.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I think what he did is a terrible thing and a terrible mistake.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (voice over): DeSantis says Trump's comments insulted many conservatives.\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: For him to then attack people like Iowa, South Carolina, Florida, all these other states. I thought it was a big mistake.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: South Carolina Senator Tim Scott says abortion should also drive Republican turnout.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT (R-SC), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: What the American people and Republican primaries want to know is where are we different?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (on camera): The former president's abortion comments are also sparking criticism from GOP leaders who are not even running for the White House, including Georgia's Governor Brian Kemp and the Governor of Iowa \uc2 Kim Reynolds\uc2 , both of whom are defending efforts to limit abortion in their states.\par \par \uc2 Mike, they were pretty upset by these comments.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Very interesting. Mark Meredith, live in the studio. Mark, thanks a lot.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: You bet.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Please join Dana Perino and \uc2 Stuart Varney\uc2  and Univision's Ilia Calderon, as they moderate the second Republican presidential debate on Fox Business, September 27th. That's next Wednesday from the Ronald Reagan Presidential Foundation and Institute in Simi Valley, California.\par \par \uc2 There appears 0to be a connection tonight between the bankrupted cryptocurrency giant \uc2 FTX\uc2  and one of the largest left wing dark money networks in the country.\par \par \uc2 Fox Business correspondent Kelly O'Grady has details tonight from Los Angeles. Hello, Kelly.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O'GRADY, \uc2 FOX\uc2  BUSINESS NETWORK CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Mike.\par \par \uc2 Yes, less than two weeks until \uc2 Sam Bankman-Fried\uc2 's front trial begins, a new suit now targets the alleged crypto (INAUDIBLE) parents. The \uc2 FTX\uc2  management team is suing the Stanford law professors and the filling reveals a surprising political link.\par \par \uc2 Now, the suit highlights that SBF's father, Joseph Bankman, served on the board for Arabella Advisors, a major left wing dark money group. Arabella, however, is denying any connection with SBF's father, instead, arguing the link is with a subsidiary. "Mr. Bankman has never had any role of Arabella advisors. Arabella Advisors is a vendor to the New Venture Fund. It briefly worked with the \uc2 FTX Foundation\uc2  to provide administrative services."\par \par \uc2 More broadly, the suit looks to claw back over 26 million in gifts in luxury real estate, alleging SBF's parents exploited their access within \uc2 FTX\uc2  to enrich themselves.\par \par \uc2 For example, SBF's mother, Barbara Fried, allegedly advised her son to violate campaign finance laws by using a straw man to secretly donate to campaigns.\par \par \uc2 His father Joseph Bankman, is accused of expensing many perks, including privately charter jets and Formula One Grand Prix tickets, as well as giving lucrative donations to Stanford which the school is now returning.\par \par \uc2 And when Bankman was unhappy with his \uc2 FTX\uc2  salary, he pressured his son to increase it to $1 million.\par \par \uc2 The lawsuit notes this e-mail where he calls on SBF's mother for support.\par \par \uc2 "Gee, Sam, I don't know what to say here. This is the first I have heard of the 2,000 a year salary! Putting Barbara on this."\par \par \uc2 Lawyers for the couple argue the allegations are not true, telling Fox Business "This is a dangerous attempt to intimidate Joe and Barbara and undermine the jury process just days before their child's trial begins. These claims are completely false."\par \par \uc2 Now, \uc2 FTX\uc2  new management shares they have recovered 7 billion of 8.7 billion in customer funds with suits like this, seeking to make up that difference. Mike.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Kelly O'Grady, thanks very much.\par \par \uc2 The Dow lost 370. The S&P 500 was off 72. NASDAQ dropped 245.\par \par \uc2 The search for a convicted murderer let out of jail by mistake is next. And later, Bret Baier takes us inside the rapidly growing sports and entertainment industry in Saudi Arabia.\par \par \uc2 But before we go to break, we have some personal news to share with you. \uc2 Rupert Murdoch\uc2  is transitioning from chair of our parent company, \uc2 Fox Corporation\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In a note to employees today, he says he's decided that in November, he will move to the role of chairman emeritus at both Fox and at \uc2 News Corp\uc2 . He says going forward, his son Lachlan will be the sole chair of both companies.\par \par \uc2 In his note, \uc2 Rupert Murdoch\uc2  says he will still be an active member of the Fox community, reaching out with thoughts, ideas, and advice. In a news release -- released today, Lachlan congratulated his father on his 70-year career in media as someone who once worked at the Fox own station in Austin, Texas, and has been here at Fox News for more than 26 years.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Mr. Murdoch. You are a legend into the industry, and I'm grateful for the opportunities you have provided for all of us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: There is pushback tonight from one Democratic senator on the Majority Leader suspension of the dress code in the chamber. A spokesperson for \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  of West Virginia says the senator plans to find a -- file a bipartisan resolution to ensure the Senate dress code remains consistent with previous expectations.\par \par \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2 's decision to allow casual clothing was seen as a concession to Pennsylvania's \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , who prefers hoodies, t-shirts and shorts.\par \par \uc2 Convicted murderer Alex Murdaugh has pleaded guilty to financial crimes. Today's federal court appearance was the first time that disbarred attorney admitted responsibility for a crime before a judge. Murdaugh is serving life in prison without parole for killing his wife and son but he adamantly denied shooting them at his double murder trial.\par \par \uc2 Today in court, Murdaugh pleaded guilty to 22 counts of financial fraud and money laundering. He will be sentenced at a later date.\par \par \uc2 For the second time this month, we have a major search for a person considered a dangerous criminal. This time it is not an escaped prisoner, but someone who was simply let go. Senior Correspondent \uc2 Mike Tobin\uc2  has the latest from Chicago.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 COL. JAMES MARTIN, MARION COUNTY, INDIANA SHERIFF'S OFFICE: The U.S. Marshal Service has offered up to a $10,000 reward.\par \par \uc2 MIKE TOBIN\uc2 , FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR CORRESPONDENT (voice over): A manhunt is currently underway after accused murderer Kevin Mason walked out of the Marion County Indiana jail. Authorities did not inform the public for nearly a week.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: We have used this time as a tactical advantage for us.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN (voice over): No dramatic escape but human error appears to be to blame. Clerks thought they were eliminating redundant paperwork and remove the order to hold Mason. Two days after his arrest, he walked out of the door of the jail.\par \par \uc2 KERRY FORESTAL, MARION COUNTY, INDIANA SHERIFF: They made it pretty well known that we're short staffed, they're underpaid. That's not an excuse, but it's a fact.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN (voice over): The sheriff says Mason got a ride from a girlfriend Desiree Oliver who subsequently purchased men's underwear, a travel kit, slippers and a burner cell phone. Oliver's been arrested but Mason is still on the run. He's accused of killing Dontevius Catchings in June of 2021. The two men allegedly got into a fight. When it ended, investigators say Mason shot and killed Catchings.\par \par \uc2 The sheriff believes with two years experience as a fugitive, Mason is good at life on the run, and that someone is still helping him.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: We're going to be very aggressive in pursuing you. We will find you. We will criminally charge you just as we did Deseree Oliver.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN (voice over): The clerical blunder in Indiana is a contrast from the manhunt that ended about a week ago in Pennsylvania after convicted murderer Danelo Cavalcante crab walked his way up and out of the Chester County Prison. Amid concerns of poor leadership during a town hall, the interim warden of the prison said razor wire and mesh have been added.\par \par \uc2 HOWARD HOLLAND, CHESTER COUNTY PRISON ACTING WARDEN: I'm confident that we have mitigated the factors in which Cavalcante bold escape.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOBIN (on camera): Now back in Indiana, the Marion County Sheriff's Department is standing behind the decision not to inform the public that an accused killer was on the loose, even though the so called tactical advantage did not result in an apprehension. Mike, back to you.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: \uc2 Mike Tobin\uc2  reporting live from Chicago. Mike, thanks a lot.\par \par \uc2 Up next, Bret Baier on the rapidly growing sports and entertainment industry in Saudi Arabia.\par \par \uc2 First, Beyond our Borders tonight, India's visa processing center in Canada suspends services as a rift widens between the countries. This comes after Canadian Prime Minister \uc2 Justin Trudeau\uc2  said India may have been involved in the killing of a Canadian citizen. Canada expelled an Indian diplomat and India responded in kind and calls Trudeau's allegation absurd.\par \par \uc2 Syrian President Bashar as-Assad arrives in China on his first visit to the country since the start of Syria's 12 year conflict. Beijing has been one of his main backers in that fight.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Paris, one of the big stories there tonight, \uc2 King Charles III\uc2  and Queen Camilla see the fire damaged Notre-Dame cathedral during the second day of their state visit to France. The Royals were not able to enter the sanctuary for safety reasons. They will end their trip Friday with a stop in Bordeaux which has a large British community, not to mention great wine.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories Beyond our Borders tonight, we'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Republican David McCormick has just announced he will enter Pennsylvania's \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  race. he will try again for the GOP nomination to face Democratic incumbent Bob Casey. McCormick lost in last year's crowded Republican primary to Dr. Oz who then lost to \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  in the November general election.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, we have one last report from Bret Baier shot during this week's visit to Saudi Arabia. The Kingdom is trying to raise its profile and diversify its economy, making investments beyond oil in industries like sports and entertainment. Here's a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ABDUL AZIZ BIN SALMAN, SAUDI ARABIA ENERGY MINISTER: The fact of the matter is that we are siding with Saudi Arabia. We're not siding with anybody.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST (voice over): Saudi Arabia's Energy Minister Prince Abdul Aziz bin Salman defending recent production cuts affecting the global oil market and likely raising gas prices in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 He spoke with Fox from the World Petroleum Congress in Calgary, Canada. His country and its membership in OPEC Plus, is a major presence.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The influence they have on oil markets, pretty significant.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN BOOK, CLEARVIEW ENERGY PARTNERS: Hard to overstate. It's the world's biggest energy exporter, one of the three big oil producers in the market along with Russia and the United States. Saudi Arabia is the -- is the big dog of \uc2 OPEC\uc2  as well.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice over): Some analysts say the recent Saudi and Russian production cuts could send oil prices to more than $100 a barrel by the end of the year.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I spoke to your energy minister who was fantastic, Prince Abdul Aziz, about your decision for oil production cuts.\par \par \uc2 A. BIN SALMAN: The best thing we could do deliver for us and for the rest of the world is stable oil markets.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: There are some supporters of Ukraine who say by doing that, and prices spiking, that you have essentially boosted Russia's war effort. So, how do you deal with that?\par \par \uc2 MOHAMMED BIN SALMAN\uc2 , SAUDI CROWN PRINCE: So, we just watch supply, demand. If there is shortage of supply, our role in OPEC Plus is to fill that shortage. If there is over supply, our role in \uc2 OPEC\uc2  plus to major that for the stability of the market.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So, it doesn't have anything other than that?\par \par \uc2 M. BIN SALMAN: No, it's purely about demand, supply.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: What about that defense and their actions so far?\par \par \uc2 BOOK: This is essentially a change in stance we first saw in October. They said, look, we want to get in front of the market, because it looks like demand is going to weaken. And history have indicated that, it turns out the demand was weaker than expected. The cut looked well advised.\par \par \uc2 Now, typically, \uc2 OPEC\uc2  has not cut when inventories have been lean. They've been cutting when inventories have been long. So, this is a change.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice over): Another change for Saudi Arabia, a big shift away from oil and toward additional investments elsewhere.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: When Americans think about Saudi Arabia, I think the thing that first comes to mind is oil. That is how you've raised a lot of revenue.\par \par \uc2 But interestingly 2030 and the vision is to try to increase non-oil businesses and also green energy.\par \par \uc2 A. BIN SALMAN: We are big in oil, yes. We have been using oil, yes. We will continue using oil, of course. We would be marketing oil, yes. But how about we would like to demonstrate to the whole world that we are equally transitioning. We are actually going to get better and better with it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Saudi Arabia is an oil-rich country, but it is trying to diversify its economy to be non-oil or a bigger part of non-oil. This image behind me is a perfect example of what we're seeing in the country. The history, which are the ruins at Diriyah, the birthplace of the Saudi Kingdom. And in the distance, a gaggle to cranes. There are more cranes in this country than in any other around the world.\par \par \uc2 The net oil export revenue last year, $311 billion. The cost of the projects around the country $1.1 trillion. Besides real estate, they are spending big bucks on big names and events Americans know well.\par \par \uc2 From Cristiano Ronaldo and Neymar and to Dustin Johnson and Phil Mickelson, Saudi Arabia is investing heavily in professional sports.\par \par \uc2 What do you say to the people who charge that's part of sports washing, that you are trying to use all of that to somehow improve or somehow affect your image?\par \par \uc2 M. BIN SALMAN: Is sports washing going to increase my GDP by one percent? Then I will continue doing sports washing.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You have got Cristiano Ronaldo, who is a huge draw. Did that change the dynamic here?\par \par \uc2 ABDULAZIZ BIN TURKI AL FAISAL, SAUDI ARABIA SPORTS MINISTER: That changed everything. This investment in the players and the clubs and the and the events that we host reflect directly to the youth and reflect directly to the people in the kingdom.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: According to "Forbes," Ronaldo will be the highest paid professional athlete this year.\par \par \uc2 Sport is important for the kingdom, for the crown prince?\par \par \uc2 AL FAISAL: Well, sports is important, I think, for everyone, especially for the kingdom and for the crown prince. Seventy percent of the population is below the age of 40. And this population wants to compete in different sports.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And the kingdom is investing in sports at the youngest level to compete on the global stage.\par \par \uc2 So it's a bit of a farm club from around the country?\par \par \uc2 AL FAISAL: Exactly. So we do scouting and we do tryouts from all around the kingdom, and from different regions. And we choose these kids, and then move them. They have special coaches for each sport and nutrition and so on.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And you have had some successes, some real stars?\par \par \uc2 AL FAISAL: Yes. We have had some real outcome from this in the past two years, that this year we had a couple of our athletes who participate on Asian level and on regional level who have achieved some medals.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: As a millennial himself, it's all part of the young crown prince's vision.\par \par \uc2 You are a big gamer.\par \par \uc2 BIN SALMAN: Since I'm a kid I love it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Really?\par \par \uc2 BIN SALMAN: It disconnects me from reality.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And there is a big video game part of your ministry, right? There is out of sports?\par \par \uc2 BIN SALMAN: E-Sports is one of the most important things happening globally. It's one of the most growing industry in the planet.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: He's familiar with what draws a younger population to travel and visit his country.\par \par \uc2 One thing that has changed specifically is the entertainment, the music festivals, the concerts, the football matches with thousands and thousands of fans. That's a big change in a short time.\par \par \uc2 AHMED AL KHATEEB, SAUDI ARABIA TOURISM MINISTER: Yes, Bret. When people travel, they travel to enjoy the culture. Whenever they come, they can dine in the best restaurants, they can enjoy the amazing entertainment, and they can go to sport activities. We can fill their programs whether they stay for a week or two or three.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: What were once underground events, concerts in the kingdom, have become giant music festivals. Headliners have included Bruno Mars, Post Malone, and David Guetta.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I would have never expected that I would come to Saudi Arabia for a Comicon.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: There is also a renewed interest in the big screen. The government reopened movie theaters in 2018 and announced a goal to open more than 300 by 2030. In just a few years, Saudi Arabia has become the biggest box office in the Middle East. It recorded a total revenue of $242 million in 2022.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Of course, I'm very happy to be here with the team representing our movie, our countries.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It now hosts an international film festival. Saudi Arabia, however, has been criticized for holding the Red Sea Film Festival over accusations the kingdom suppresses free speech.\par \par \uc2 Under the crown prince's vision, for someone out of the country that looks at Saudi Arabia and says they are a little skittish about coming here. They have read the stories about concerns over human rights or other things. And maybe they're not ready, or they are not sold yet, what do you tell them?\par \par \uc2 AL KHATEEB: I tell them come and experience this. People are free to go where they want to go. Saudi cities very safe and among the safest cities in the world. I'm very, very sure they would enjoy it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: There are funds set aside for new films as well. The Red Sea Fund will invest $14 million in film projects.\par \par \uc2 AL KHATEEB: We have accomplished a lot. We are very hungry. We want to accomplish more. We have an amazing chance to do that.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And do you think that the perception of Saudi is changing? Do you sense that?\par \par \uc2 BIN SALMAN: Well, I hope that's happening. But for us in Saudi Arabia, we don't look at that. For us in Saudi Arabia, when we do change, we look to our needs, to our people needs, to our economy needs. If it changes perception positively, great.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER (on camera): So it's a battle for the hearts and minds of tourists around the group. Human rights groups on one side, \uc2 Freedom House\uc2  says the kingdom restricts almost all political rights and civil liberties. However, even that group acknowledges rights for women have improved significant in recent years. The Saudis say come visit, give us a shot. We'll show you the hospitality.\par \par \uc2 Mike, we'll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 MIKE EMANUEL, \uc2 FOX\uc2  NEWS CHIEF WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT: Bret, great stuff. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT, Israeli Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  responds to Saudi Arabian Crown Prince \uc2 Mohammed bin Salman\uc2 's statement to Bret Baier that the two nations are getting closer to normalizing relations.\par \par \uc2 Up next, Vice President Harris says climate change is keeping young people from doing one very basic thing.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , (D) VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I have heard young leaders talk with me about a term they have coined called climate anxiety.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I don't believe climate change really is the reason. I don't really think many people care about climate change.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Entertainment industry writers and producers are said to be near an agreement to end their strike. They were scheduled to meet with studio executives again today after what was called a very encouraging session Wednesday. It was the first meeting since mid-August.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  and Detroit automakers have one more full day to make significant progress in their new contract before the union plans to announce an expansion of its U.S. strikes. The standoff is fueling worries about a prolonged industrial shutdown that could disrupt production and ripple through the supply chain. Approximately 12,700 workers are currently on strike.\par \par \uc2 Vice President Harris has an unusual explanation for the fact that young Americans are not buying homes or starting families during the Biden economy. It seeks to advance one of her boss's key agenda items. Here is White House correspondent Jacqui Heinrich.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, \uc2 FOX\uc2  NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  says climate anxiety is making younger Americans reconsider major life choices like buying a home.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , (D) VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The fear of the future and the unknown, of whether it makes sense for you to even think about having children, whether it makes sense for you to think about aspiring to buy a home.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Their solution, launching the American Climate Corps, a federal workforce training and service program focusing on conservation, clean energy, and environmental justice with the goal of leading to jobs. Team Biden openly framing it as a way to court young voters.\par \par \uc2 VARSHINI PRAKASH, SUNRISE MOVEMENT: Over the last three years, thousands of young people stepped up and made the civilian climate corps a generational rallying cry. President Biden acknowledges that this is just the beginning.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: But Biden's critics and 2024 Republican White House hopefuls say the economy is really driving decisions from would be home buyers.\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE, (R) FORMER U.S. VICE PRESIDENT: People are focused on the rising costs of living, runaway inflation.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY, (R) PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: The first thing we have got to do is get inflation down. The second thing, that will give builders confidence that they can build. But right now, even banks don't want to lend.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: New \uc2 FOX\uc2  polls show 79 percent of voters extremely or very concerned about housing costs, second to inflation at 91 percent. The \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  skipped another interest rate hike this week, but left the door open to one more this year, reiterating cuts aren't coming any time soon, meaning higher mortgage rates are here to stay for now.\par \par \uc2 JEROME POWELL\uc2 , CHAIR, FEDERAL RESERVE: Activity in the housing sector has picked up somewhat, though it remains well below levels of a year ago, largely reflecting higher mortgage rates.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Market experts say the standoff will persist because while home prices are up, would be sellers don't want to lose locked in lower mortgage rates, and would be buyers can't afford it. And the Fed chairman says that is not reason enough to bring rates down. Mike?\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Jacqui Heinrich in the briefing room, thanks a lot.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel on the reaction to Bret Baier's exclusive interview with Saudi Arabian Crown Prince \uc2 Mohammed bin Salman\uc2 , plus the massive migrant influx at out southern border.\par \par \uc2 First, here's what some of our \uc2 FOX\uc2  affiliates around the country are covering tonight. \uc2 FOX\uc2  5 in New York as at least one person is killed and dozens injured in a bus crash on Interstate 84 in Orange County. It involves students and parents from Farmingdale High School on Long Island.\par \par \uc2 FOX\uc2  2 in San Francisco as the city supervisor sponsors a hearing on strategies to address car break-ins. The district attorney's office says there have been 127 felony auto burglary or theft cases presented in the first seven months of the year.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Atlanta from \uc2 FOX\uc2  5. One of the big stories there tonight, Elton John's condo goes on the market for about $5 million bucks. The 36 floor, 13,000 square-foot residents has six units, a fitness studio, spa massage room, and a two-story dining room. The realtor says it is fit for royalty. Sounds pretty sweet.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look outside the beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: What do you call it here at the White House when 10,000 people illegally crossed the border in a single day?\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: So what do you call it, Peter, when the GOP puts forth a -- wait, no. No, no, no --\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Answer me. Karine?\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: You can't -- I'm answering. OK, we're going to move on.\par \par \uc2 REP. JOEY ARRINGTON, (R-TX): They need to secure the border, they need to enforce the immigration laws, and they need to protect the citizens in Texas and throughout the country.\par \par \uc2 REP. GREG CASAR, (D-TX): Unfortunately, Republicans refuse to actually fix the system to come up with solutions.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Let's take a look at the border numbers of the southwest border under the Biden administration. More than 6 million encounters, 200,000 plus average encounters per month. That is up 348 percent compared to the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 With that, let's bring in our panel, Katie Pavlich, news editor at Townhall.com, Olivia Beavers, congressional reporter for "Politico," and Guy Benson, political editor at Townhall.com, host of "The Guy Benson Show" on FOX News Radio. Welcome to all of you.\par \par \uc2 KATIE PAVLICH, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Great to see you.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: So, Olivia, on Capitol Hill, they don't love it when the White House says it's their fault, right?\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA BEAVERS, "POLITICO": No. No, they don't. And, you know, this is an issue where you have finger pointing. If you talk to members like Chip Roy and Tony Gonzales, they have very different views of how to address the border, but they will say that is one of their top issues. They are in a border state. And the border numbers that came out recently are bad. If you look at what they were compared to July, coming in, there is a 60 percent increase. And so this is going to be an issue. You hear talking presidential, talking about down ballot.\par \par \uc2 And that's also why Republicans are trying to attach a policy, HR-2, their border bill, to the government spending. They think that they could have a win on that, but they can't seem to really take off on actually getting anything passed right now at the moment in the House.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Guy, being a multimedia star, what are you hearing from people on the radio?\par \par \uc2 GUY BENSON, POLITICAL EDITOR, TOWNHALL.COM: I think there is a huge amount of frustration just among American voters in general because the images, to the extent that they are being shown by much of the media, but the images speak for themselves. It's absolute chaos -- 10,000 encounters a day at this point, plus all the got-aways. And just this unbelievably lame, insulting talking point that it's the Republicans' fault. Republicans passed a border bill back in May. The Democrats don't like it. They're not interested in enforcement, but the Republicans have taken action.\par \par \uc2 The president has plenty of tools at his disposal to make this better on the enforcement side. He chooses not to exercise those options because he is not interested in it. And so that exchange between \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2  and Peter Doocy earlier to me was just very almost infuriating to watch, because Peter asked a specific question, and the response from the press secretary was a quick deflection to something he didn't ask, and then she just moved on to someone else hoping for a different question.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Katie, you are from one of those border states. What about it?\par \par \uc2 KATIE PAVLICH, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Talking to folks back in Arizona, they are seeing the same situation play out as in Texas with thousands of people coming over every day, most of them single adult males. And for the press secretary today to try and pivot to Republicans, her argument is that Republicans don't want to give the administration more money, and that's not to enforce the border or to deter illegal immigration. It's to process illegal immigrants. They have no intention, as she said today, of putting a cap on the number of illegal immigrants who cross, essentially saying the number is going to be infinite.\par \par \uc2 And when it comes to the Border Patrol's mission, they have completely violated that. The mission of Border Patrol is to protect the United States of America. And they have turned them into a processing organization. Morale is low. You know, that's been -- morale has been low for a long time on the border. And it's not Republicans who are sounding the alarm on this. They are, obviously. But Democrats -- the mayor of Eagle Pass, a Democrat, the governor of New York, Democrat, the mayor of New York City, a Democrat. This is a bipartisan problem. The president has Title Eight at his disposal through the executive branch to stop this, and they have no intention of stopping the flow, only managing it by letting as many people in as want to come.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: OK, Bret, made a lot of news with his interview with the Saudi crown prince. One issue was relations with Israel. Let's play it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MOHAMMED BIN SALMAN\uc2 , SAUDI CROWN PRINCE: Now we don't have a relation with Israel, but if the Biden administration succeeded to make, I believe, the biggest historical since the end of the cold war, then we've got to start the relationship. And that relationship ahs got to be continuous.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Bret is due to talk to \uc2 Bibi Netanyahu\uc2  tomorrow. Look forward to that interview. Guy, your thoughts on what this could mean if there are relations between the Saudis and the Israelis?\par \par \uc2 BENSON: It would be huge. It would be part of a realignment in the region that we've seen, although there has been some rapprochement between the Saudis and the Arab states and the Iranians. Clearly, there's still concerned about Iran and a nuclear weapon, which also came up during the course of that interview. If Saudi Arabia joins with some of these other countries in the Abraham Accords to recognize the state of Israel and have open relations with them, what a sea change and a hopeful one. Let's pray for that outcome.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Katie, your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: At the beginning of the Biden administration, the State Department didn't even want to recognize the Abraham peace accords that were struck under President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Now they have, luckily, and good for the world and good for the United States of America, endorsed that concept, and Saudi Arabia certainly on the list to try and join those pacts.\par \par \uc2 But the most interesting part of the interview just in terms of the consequence was the Saudi Arabian prince saying that if Iran gets a nuclear weapon, we have to get one, too. The \uc2 IAEA\uc2  just came out a week ago saying that they are no longer allowing inspectors in. This comes on the heels of statements in May from the \uc2 IAEA\uc2  saying that Iran was enriching uranium to nuclear grade levels, which comes -- the warning from the Israelis that it's for a nuclear weapon, and they, of course, went in and stole a bunch of information from Tehran in 2018 showing that the Iranians have been disingenuous and lying about their nuclear intentions, that they're not for peaceful purposes. So that certainly plays into the negotiations with normalizing relations on a national security front.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: All right, I've got a special topic for Olivia, my Hill correspondent friend, about the dress code in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 , dialing it back, going casual for Senator Fetterman. No more jacket and tie. Well, that's created quite a stir. It caught my ear when Senator Mark Kelly responded to this question from Jake Tapper.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JAKE TAPPER, CNN ANCHOR: I wondered what you thought of Schumer changing the Senate dress code. Do you like these more casual looks? Should they be allowed on the Senate floor?\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARK KELLY (D-AZ): I don't like it, and I think it would have been better if we had a discussion about it ahead of time.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BEAVERS: Mike, we have a bipartisan issue happening in the Senate. Basically Majority Leader Schumer changed the rule for Fetterman, and Manchin is now trying to codify it. You have Senator \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  basically getting almost all the Senate Republicans to sign a letter saying that they have an issue with this. And I would also say this is an attempt by the leader to change for one man's brand, and you are seeing pushback in the clip there.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: All right, I will ask you guys about the dress code after the show. Thank you all, panelists.\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: Thanks, Mike.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: And finally tonight, today's throwback. Fifteen years ago today, the final game was played at the old Yankee stadium. The Bronx Bombers defeated the \uc2 Baltimore Orioles\uc2  seven to three as Mariano Rivera closed the contest with a perfect inning. The historic stadium opened 100 years ago in 1923 and came to be known as the house that Babe Ruth built. The Bambino's daughter, Julia Ruth Stevens, threw out the ceremonial first pitch of that final game. And as a lifelong Yankee fan, that was the good old days in the Bronx. Hopefully they will come back again.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT, we will have continuing coverage of the crisis at our southern border. Please join Dana Perino and \uc2 Stuart Varney\uc2  and Univision Ilia Calderon as they moderate the second Republican presidential debate on FOX Business September 27th from the Ronald Reagan Presidential Foundation and Institute in Simi Valley, California.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for watching SPECIAL REPORT. I'm Mike Emanuel in Washington.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020230922ej9l00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Dave Portnoy\uc2  Slams The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  Reporter For Deceptive Tactics; 10,000 Migrants Flood \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's Open Border; Pornography For Kids: Parents Slam Gender Queer Author And Schools; Dems Want To Boost Women Unless They're Republican; Wild Card: Poker Player Admits Lyng About Cancer To Get Donations\b0\par\par\uc2 Greg Gutfeld, Jeanine Pirro, Jessica Tarlov, Jesse Watters, Dana  Perino \par \uc2 8,321 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 September 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: The Five\par \uc2 FOXFIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 DAN MERICA, NATIONAL POLITICAL REPORTER, THE MESSENGER: And that's why frankly you've seen him not show up to these debates. Is he doesn't feel the need to compete and to elevate these other contenders because of the position he finds himself in.\par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Yes, worked for him last time, that might work for him again. Dan Merica, thank you, National Politics Reporter. That'll do it for us. Here comes THE FIVE.\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Hi, I'm Greg Gutfeld along with Judge Jeanine Pirro, Jessica Tarlov, Jesse Watters and she can moderate a debate and fit in a puppy crate, Dana Perino, THE FIVE.\par \par \uc2 Dishonest reporter at \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2  just got a glorious well-deserved roast. Thanks to the owner of \uc2 Barstool Sports\uc2 , \uc2 Dave Portnoy\uc2 , catching wind of a hit piece they were writing about him.\par \par \uc2 Apparently, the democracy dies in the darkness doofuses had been reaching out to the sponsors of a pizza event that he hosts and asking if they felt comfortable being associated with him. WaPo didn't bother giving Portnoy a call beforehand for comment. So, he called them.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DAVE PORTNOY\uc2 , FOUNDER, \uc2 BARSTOOL SPORTS\uc2 : You're basically sending an e-mail that says to the effect, Dave's a misogynic racist.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I haven't said anything like that.\par \par \uc2 PORTNOY: If you want, I can read what you actually sent. I have it. We are planning to write about the festival and how -- and how some of the sponsors and participants have drawn criticism by seemingly to associate themselves with \uc2 Dave Portnoy\uc2 , who has a history of misogynic comments and other problematic behavior.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Oh, that's the one I said, which was definitely the most pointed of them.\par \par \uc2 PORTNOY: That is pretty pointed. You said you didn't do it, then I have the exact evidence of you doing it, so --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I didn't say I didn't do that. I said I did. That was the one that was the most --\par \par \uc2 PORTNOY: Well, no, you know what, before I -- before I provided proof, you said you didn't really remember doing that. And then I read it to you. And you're like, oh, yes, I did it that one time. So, you did do it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Wow. She backpedaled like \uc2 Lance Armstrong\uc2  in a windstorm. But by the way, Dave, it's misogynistic. I didn't know what you were talking about.\par \par \uc2 Those smear tactics seem pretty shocking. But apparently, that is how the liberal media operates.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: This is kind of standard journalistic stuff.\par \par \uc2 PORTNOY: Unfortunately, yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I was hoping for a dialogue with them. You know, sometimes you'd have to say something like, this is like, you know, it's sort of a reporting tactic. When you want someone to respond, you kind of have to indicate that there might be something negative, and then you get them to engage. That's all I was trying to do. I really wanted them to engage with me.\par \par \uc2 PORTNOY: That is a bad state of journalism. If that's a tactic that you have to -- what I would say is make up something about somebody.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Amen. We reached out for comment from WaPo multiple times, but we haven't heard back. If you want to hear more from Portnoy, he will be on Jesse's show tonight helping with the ratings.\par \par \uc2 So Jesse, this was not about a story. It was a strategy to damage a business by targeting and scaring advertisers. We all know how that feels. I mean, why is the food editor trying to get a pizza festival canceled?\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Right. This is corporate terrorism. She's supposed to be doing reviews for food. It's not like he made her some osso buco (PH) that wasn't well presented. This is a guy who's not even a chef. He doesn't even own a restaurant. And this food critic is trying to take down a charitable event.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: It's a pizza party to help small business owners and she's trying to destroy it. So, you have Media Matters that run ad boycotts to take down popular companies and people. And then you have journalists that run ad boycotts to take down popular people. They're the same thing. This is an agenda.\par \par \uc2 So, why do they have this agenda? Why is \uc2 Dave Portnoy\uc2  such a threat? That's the question I'm going to ask him tonight. Because for the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 , they don't compete against \uc2 Barstool Sports\uc2 . Is it because he's politically incorrect? Is it because they're not in the same tribe? Is it because hate sells or division sells? I don't know.\par \par \uc2 But this woman and we get this all the time. You've gotten it, you've gotten it. This is what they do. They go in, the editor tells them what to write. The story is 95 percent written, they call all your sponsors, or all your advertisers and everybody that hates you, and then they write about all of your controversies.\par \par \uc2 And then five minutes before the deadline, they say, hey, look, we'd like you to comment.\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL LEGAL ANALYST: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: And then you just check the box, boom, and then it's going to kill you. And it's the most disgraceful and dishonest thing.\par \par \uc2 The fact that she got caught and then lie. And says this is a journalistic tactic. Now, tell me if this is journalism. You lie to some sponsor and say, your house is on fire, you got to call me back. And then they call you back and see that your house isn't actually on fire, but it might be if you don't do what we say, that's not journalism. That's a threat. She's taking hostages.\par \par \uc2 And this is disgraceful. I'm so glad he recor