on camera): Weiss and a court filing announced that Hunter could face tax charges, perhaps in California where he currently lives or Washington D.C. where he once lived before. We'll follow it, John.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Every day something new. David Spunt for us from the DOJ. David, thank you.\par \par \uc2 The White House is getting involved in the strike against Detroit automakers, even if the parties involved do not want the interference. This comes as union leaders and company negotiators continue to talk.\par \par \uc2 Fox Business Correspondent Grady Trimble is in Wayne, Michigan tonight. He's got the update for us. Hi, Grady.\par \par \uc2 GRADY TRIMBLE, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK CORRESPONDENT: Hi, John. The big three automakers and the \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  were back at the bargaining table today. By all accounts though, progress has been slow.\par \par \uc2 At this Ford plant, picketing workers have been blocking semi-trucks from pulling in. At one point, police came to tell striking union members they have to let the trucks through. Workers held some trucks up for a few minutes but eventually let them pass.\par \par \uc2 The picketers have welcomed with open arms several Democratic lawmakers who want to walk with them in solidarity. President Biden says he's sending two administration officials to Detroit to help with the negotiations.\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 UAW\uc2  President Shawn Fain and the workers we've talked to tell us they don't want it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I say we don't need his help.\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE: Why not? Because we are united through for people. We don't need the president's help.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It's our fight. Let us fight our fight. That's all we want. We don't want to hurt the economy. We don't want to be here not getting paid peanuts. But you know, it's our fight. It's fight for our life.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I think we can negotiate our contract 