t.\par \par \uc2 What to do about Ukraine is shaping up to be a big issue in the Republican presidential campaign. Another source of contention for the candidates, abortion rights. Here is senior national correspondent Rich Edson.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 RICH EDSON, FOX NEWS CHANNEL WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Former President Trump is condemning Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , for signing a bill prohibiting most abortions after six weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: What he did is a terrible thing and a terrible mistake. But we'll come up with a number.\par \par \uc2 EDSON (voice over): Trump has refused to embrace a number, a specific timeline on when abortion should be legal. The Republican frontrunner says he'd negotiate a compromise.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : For the first time in 52 years, you'll have an issue that we can put behind us.\par \par \uc2 EDSON (voice over): DeSantis' campaign says, "We've already seen the disastrous results of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  compromising with Democrats: over $7 trillion in new debt."\par \par \uc2 DeSantis has said states should decide. He says he'd appoint conservative judges and prevent taxpayer money from enabling abortions. Conservative groups have pushed the Republican field further to embrace nationwide restrictions at 15 weeks.\par \par \uc2 Some candidates are already there.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT (R-SC): President Trump, Nikki Haley, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , and Vivek as well, Ramaswamy, they all are the same place, they have not committed to a 15-week limit. We need a president who fights for life from day one.\par \par \uc2 EDSON (voice over): Former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  is also urging the field to back restrictions after 15 weeks. "There's no negotiating when it comes to the life of the unborn."\par \par \uc2 Former \uc2 U.N\uc2 . Ambassador Nikki Haley has criticized any 15-week ban is impossible to get through a divided Senate.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY (R), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Let's focus on what we do agree on. Let's ban late term abortion. Let's encourage adoptions.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: The GOP field is also at odds over funding for Ukraine, with Congress debating an additional $24 billion in assistance.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 EDSON (on camera): And Trump has been critical of U.S. funding and is demanding the Europeans spend more. Just giving a speech here in Washington. Pence said that his former running mate and the other Republicans are abandoning American leadership on the world stage.\par \par \uc2 Pence says failing to help Ukraine is a giant flashing green light for China to move against Taiwan. John.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: More coming up with the panel a little bit later on in the program. Rich, Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Please join Dana Perino, \uc2 Stuart Varney\uc2 , and Univision's Ilia Calderon, as they moderate the second Republican presidential debate. That will be on Fox Business, September the 27th, from the Ronald Reagan Presidential Foundation and Institute in Simi Valley, California. Don't --\par \par \uc2 Well, SPECIAL REPORT will be coming to you from Saudi Arabia this week. Bret Baier is at the capital city of Riyadh tonight with some background on the rapidly changing kingdom.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Hey, John, we're on the outskirts of Riyadh, in a place called Diriyah. It's the birthplace of the first Saudi state, one of six sites on \uc2 UNESCO\uc2 's World Heritage list inside Saudi Arabia.\par \par \uc2 It is also a $63 billion project initiated by \uc2 King Salman\uc2  and his son, Crown Prince Mohammed bin Salman, known around the world by his initials, MBS. The de facto ruler has a nonstop leadership style and his ministers follow his goal called Vision 2030. That has initiated sweeping changes in the kingdom in a very short period of time.\par \par \uc2 For women, to drive to participate in sports and entertainment, to allow them to wear what they choose to being able to start businesses just to name a few.\par \par \uc2 Here is the minister of economy and planning on his boss's drive.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 FAISAL ALIBRAHIM, MINISTER OF ECONOMY AND PLANNING, SAUDI ARABIA: His leadership style is coming from his ownership of the cause. He wants Saudi Arabia to be the best version of itself. He wants Saudi Arabia to be a global player that helps improve of the world, a global change agent.\par \par \uc2 He wants Saudi people and people residing in Saudi to live the best lives and actually to be able to contribute the most to this economy, but also, to the global economy at large.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: When you hear critics of the kingdom from the outside, it doesn't seem like you have time to listen to that criticism. You're too busy.\par \par \uc2 ALIBRAHIM: I think we're used to criticism, but we're more focused on getting the job done. It's -- the proof is in the numbers. The proof is in the progress.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice over): While Saudi is making progress in a goal to make a big green energy transition, oil still drives revenues here. And there are a lot more revenues now after a recent production cut in oil.\par \par \uc2 The energy minister joined me from Calgary and the World Petroleum Congress.\par \par \uc2 PRINCE ABDULAZIZ BIN SALMAN AL SAUD, MINISTER OF ENERGY, SAUDI ARABIA: What we are doing is -- has nothing to do with prices. What we are doing is making sure that we reduce volatility of the market.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: As you all know, Your Highness, pump prices affect presidential approval ratings, they go back all the way to Reagan that the higher the prices, the lower the presidential approval rating.\par \par \uc2 So, there are some people inside the U.S. who look skeptically. They understand what you're saying about volatility, but the fact that you and Russia, if you get together on a decision on production cuts, can affect how America works.\par \par \uc2 AL SAUD: I understand but you've got to take -- look at the components. Because a good chunk of what is happening in your gasoline market now or diesel market even more is lack of investment and lack of refining capacity, and lack of infrastructure.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: When asked about the oil production cut and how it may affect Russia's revenues when it's in the middle of a war in Ukraine, \uc2 Prince Abdulaziz\uc2  said there is a such thing as Saudi first, making decisions based on the good of the kingdom.\par \par \uc2 SPECIAL REPORT We'll be live in Saudi Arabia tomorrow night. We'll give you a behind the scenes tour of this kingdom and talking to a lot of different ministers, all setting the table for the big one-hour interview with Crown Prince \uc2 Mohammed bin Salman\uc2 , MBs, Wednesday on SPECIAL REPORT.\par \par \uc2 John, we'll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: All right. Looking forward to that, Bret.\par \par \uc2 Again, Bret is going to be hosting SPECIAL REPORT from Saudi Arabia about tomorrow, and again on Wednesday with that first major American television news channel interview with the Saudi Crown \uc2 Prince Mohammed bin Salman\uc2 , coming to you on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Do not miss that.\par \par \uc2 Up next, It's casual Friday every day for senators. Why the majority leader is suspending the dress code for members?\par \par \uc2 First, beyond our borders tonight. China's military says 103 war planes toward Taiwan in a 24-hour period in what the islands defense ministry calls a recent new high. As is customary, the planes turn back before reaching Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 Chinese warplanes fly toward the self-governing island on a near daily basis, but typically in much smaller numbers than today.\par \par \uc2 Climate activists staged another day of protest in Berlin, blocking key roads during rush hour before being removed by police. Members of the Last Generation group glued themselves to the road, causing a traffic jam for commuters driving into the city.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Rome. One of the big stories there tonight. Russia's new ambassador to the Vatican meets with \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2  for a protocol visit. This comes as signs emerge that the Vatican's Ukraine peace envoy could soon be taking a second visit to Moscow to try to find peace there.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories BEYOND OUR BORDERS tonight. We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Senate Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  has instructed the sergeant at arms to cease enforcement of the Chamber's dress code. This is seen as a concession to Pennsylvania Democrat \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , who prefers extremely casual attire.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Mark Meredith has got to look for us tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The Senate will come to order.\par \par \uc2 MARK MEREDITH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): In a place where tradition is celebrated, tonight, a change. Senate Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  says instead of a formal dress code, now, "Senators are able to choose what they wear on the Senate floor. I will continue to wear a suit."\par \par \uc2 Schumer has yet to say what sparked the change, but critics blame one man.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Welcome back, Senator.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: Pennsylvania's \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  is well known for sporting hoodies and shorts instead of suit and ties, except on the Senate floor as was required. The 54-year-old even prides himself on standing out.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (D-PA): I feel it's, you know, a little more freedom, which should be bipartisanship, you know what I'm saying, so -- but I don't know why the right side seems to be losing their minds over it, like, dogs and cats are living together, and you know, like the world spinning off its axis. But, you know, I think it's a good thing.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: But Fetterman's fellow senators are outrage over efforts to ease Senate standards.\par \par \uc2 SEN. BILL HAGERTY, (R-TN): To see it completely downgraded, no dress code now, I just think it's another step in the wrong direction.\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: Don't do it.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: Fetterman continues to defend his clothing choices, even last week responding to one Republican lawmaker who criticized him, posting "Instead of crying about how I dress, how about you get your S- together and do your job, bud."\par \par \uc2 In recent years, reporters have spotted lawmakers from both parties sporting a more casual look, but some fear it's a slippery slope.\par \par \uc2 JAMES WALLNER\uc2 , R STREET INSTITUTE: I think the big issue is there is a lack of respect and continuing decline in respect that the members themselves and the people who work there and the people who watch the Senate from afar have for this institution.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (on camera): The Senate dress code changes do not apply to staffers and any others who may be on the Senate floor, meaning they will still have to dress up. And we don't know if this casual code is going to stick around for senators forever or if another majority leader comes to power and changes it, John. But \uc2 C-Span\uc2  will be more fun to watch.\par \par \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: I think he is going to be an army of one when it comes to that extremely casual wear, but we will see. Mark, good to see you tonight, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Colorado Republican Congresswoman \uc2 Lauren Boebert\uc2  apologized on Friday after getting kicked out of a musical play for disruptive behavior and then falsely denying that she had been vaping in the theater. Surveillance video emerged showing Boebert vaping in her seat at a Denver theater. Her campaign earlier denied the theater's contention. Boebert says her actions were not meant to be malicious or cause harm, quote, "but the reality is that they did."\par \par \uc2 Former South Carolina attorney and convicted murder Alec Murdaugh has agreed to plead guilty to several crimes relating to his business practices. Those charges include wire fraud, bank fraud, money laundering, and conspiracy to commit wire fraud. He's expected to be in federal court Thursday in Charleston.\par \par \uc2 Some very disturbing video tonight. A retired California police chief is hit and killed by a teenager driving a car, and police say it was no accident. Correspondent Alexis McAdams shows us tonight. And we warn you, some of the video may be very distressing to you. Good evening, Alexis.\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS MCADAMS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: John, good evening. That's right, one teen faces murder charges after that video surfaced, and police are still searching for the passenger who was filming as the duo drove right into this innocent biker. This dangerous joy ride left an innocent dad, husband, and former police chief dead. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Ready?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: We stopped that video right before impact there, but you can see the 64-year-old fly up into the air after that, shattering the car's windshield on the way down before he landed lifeless in the roadway. This happened back in mid-August when Andreas Probst was out riding his bike. Probst was only a few miles from his house and thought he would be back within an hour, but he was killed. Probst spent 35 years in law enforcement according to community members who I talked to, serving as a police chief out in California before he moved to Vegas for a remote job.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PAT TREICHEL, GHOST BIKES: From talking to his daughter and his wife, as he was just a great, down-to-earth guy who was doing the right things, and maybe the wrong place at the wrong time.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: Moments before that fatal crash, investigators tell me the teen driver slammed into another car. The duo then posted that now viral clip on social media after driving off, leaving Probst on the road to die. More than 100 community members came to vigil showing support for the Probst family. The group Ghost Bikes Las Vegas left a white bike near the crash site to honor Probst and raise awareness for cyclists safety.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TREICHEL: He was doing -- it was textbook. He was doing everything perfect. I think this particular situation probably is riding on most of us more as to what is going on in society.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: And that's what the community members are also trying to figure out. Police tell me it was that social media clip, John, that changed the case to a homicide investigation after about two weeks when it surfaced, and the community and the nation are outraged. John?\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: We will see what happens when that goes to court. Alexis McAdams for us. Alexis, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Authorities in Los Angeles say they have arrested a person accused in the ambush murder of a sheriff's deputy over the weekend. Eight-year veteran Ryan Clinkunbroomer was gunned down while stopped at a traffic like. A dark gray Toyota Carolla pulled alongside him, stopped, and then you see it speed away. Twenty-nine-year-old Kevin Salazar was taken into custody after an hours long standoff. Deputy Clinkunbroomer was 30 years old.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT LUNA, LOS ANGELES COUNTY SHERIFF: People absolutely loved and adored him. See, he wasn't just one of our deputy sheriffs. He comes -- is he third generation.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: The deputy had become engaged to be married just four days ago.\par \par \uc2 The 