ldren have, indeed, tested positive for fentanyl. While New York City's Mayor Eric Adams says inspectors never noticed any sort of drug lab here inside this daycare, the city's chief health commissioner admits his inspectors aren't trained to test for this sort of deadly substance. The two suspects in this case have pleaded not guilty and are currently being held without bail. A grand jury is expected to convene sometime this week. John?\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Wow, what a tragedy. CB Cotton for us tonight up in the Bronx. CB, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel on the Iran prisoner swap, the presidential race, and \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  suing the \uc2 IRS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 First, here's what some of our FOX affiliates around the country are covering tonight. FOX 29 in Philadelphia as nine juveniles are back in custody within 12 hours after police say they broke out of a detention center in Berks County. The escape was sparked by a riot that broke out at that facility on Sunday night.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Dayton, Ohio. One of the big stories there tonight from FOX 19, commercial air taxis are coming to Dayton. They will be built at a half-a-billion dollar manufacturing facility. Governor \uc2 Mike DeWine\uc2  says the effort will great 2,000 jobs. The all-electric vertical takeoff and landing vehicles will be used mostly for commercial passenger operations.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look outside the beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We will be right back with the panel.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN\uc2 , U.S. SECRETARY OF STATE: From day one, our sanctions have clearly and, indeed, always exempt the use of resources for humanitarian purposes. We have absolute confidence in the process and the system that's been set up.\par \par \uc2 REP. MICHAEL MCCAUL, (R-TX) HOUSE FOREIGN AFFAIRS COMMITTEE CHAIRMAN: We all know money is fungible. And then the president of Iran just came out and said I'm going to spend it however I want to. And of course he is. And guess where it's going to go? It's going to go into terror proxy operations.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Five American hostages that were held in Iran are on their way back home. They'll be landing soon. But lots of debate over how it all came together.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in our panel, FOX News chief political analyst Brit Hume, FOX News senior political analyst Juan Williams, and Ben Domenech, editor at large for "The Spectator" and host of "The Ben Domenech Podcast" on FOX News Radio.\par \par \uc2 So, a lot of debate over whether or not freeing up the $6 billion was a ransom or not. Morgan Ortagus was on with us earlier. She said either way the deal gave Iran access to money they wouldn't have access to. Listen here.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MORGAN ORTAGUS, FORMER STATE DEPARTMENT SPOKESPERSON: They didn't have that money because it was frozen because of sanctions. So you can say, you can argue that I'm not lifting sanctions. And maybe you're not technically lifting them. But if you are not enforcing them and you're giving Iran, the world's leading state sponsor of terrorism, $6 billion, then we're just arguing semantics.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Brit, why don't you start us off there from your undisclosed location on whether or not it was American money, they still got cash for the hostages.\par \par \uc2 BRIT HUME, FOX NEWS SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST: That's exactly right. A hostage swap is defensible and easily so. This is the kind of thing you have to do to get your people back, which is a matter of some urgency. But throwing in the $6 billion freed up that was frozen which the Iranians have made clear they are going to do with whatever they want, and because, as has been repeatedly pointed out, money is fungible, it's effectively the same thing as giving them $6 billion they didn't have before that they are free to spend on the malign activities in which they are continuously engaged.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Juan, it's great to have five hostages coming back home, but to Brit's point, is this going to encourage more hostage taking? And was there a different way to do this? This administration seemed to be negotiating from a point of weakness.\par \par \uc2 JUAN WILLIAMS, FOX NEWS POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, two points to be made. One is the United States, in terms of our values, we care about hostages. So that's always going to put us at disadvantage, not only vis-a-vis the Iranians, but you think "The Wall Street Journal" reporter being held by the Soviet Union. Think about our behavior, the deal we made for Brittney Griner.\par \par \uc2 BEN DOMENECH, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: The Russians, not the Soviet Union, I will just say, though I agree.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Give it time.\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: Give it a minute.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Give Putin a little more time. He will be back.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: My point is, we are always at a disadvantage because we care about it, so we are always going to come out on the wrong end of that deal.\par \par \uc2 But, to Brit's point, I don't like the deal if it's in isolation. But my thought is maybe this is part of a larger going on. As you know, the Biden administration is trying to put something together between the Saudis and Israelis. The Saudis and the Iranians are always rivals, if not purely enemies. We have not heard a lot today from Israelis on this deal, which is a little bit of a surprise. Normally they would be quite upset. And so it may be that there is something larger taking place, and maybe we will put it in that context.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Do you want to comment on this bit, or do you want to go right to Republicans and abortion?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: I just would say, I think you are correct, Juan, in your analysis. But I just think that we don't have a lot of faith in this administration, in this White House, and in this president when it comes to his dealings in the past with the Iranian regime and on this point I think as well. I would agree with Morgan that this is something that looks in the aggregate like a very bad move.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Republicans, campaign 2024, and the abortion issue. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  on "Meet the Press" yesterday called \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  signing the six-week abortion ban a, quote, "terrible mistake." Brit, the candidates are all over the place on this while the Democrats have a pretty coherent message.\par \par \uc2 HUME: Yes, the Republicans having won the big win that they had waited for for years when the Supreme Court overturned Roe versus Wade have now had a terrible time figuring out where to land as a party on the issue of abortion. And Democrats have clearly gained ground on it. Mr. Trump's statement over the weekend seems to me that he is about as purely political as anything you could imagine. I think his stances on this issue all along have been that way, and he's calculated that the issue is hurting the Republicans and would hurt him if he adhered to it as his party's nominee. So he flipped on it, and he is now clearly pro-abortion rights. At least he is against banning abortion at any stage of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 It's going to be interesting to see how people who have stuck with him, people on the religious right, evangelicals and so on, will feel about this, if it's going to hurt him.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: So far nothing he has said hurt his standing with Republicans.\par \par \uc2 Juan, one of the reasons why Democrats limited their losses in the House in the 2022 midterms was because of the abortion message. Is this going to be a way for them to potentially get the House back?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: I think it is. Look, we have been going over all the last few days that don't look good for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . And one thing we don't factor in is not only \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  as a as the opponent in the general election, but the impact of the abortion decision, the Dobbs decision by the Supreme Court, that continue roil and be a defining magnet for Democrats. It energizes them and brings out Democratic voters, especially those famous suburban females.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Does this spell trouble for Republicans?\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: Every single governor who signed a heartbeat bill, the same kind of bill that the former president criticized, got reelected. All of them. Republicans have passed these bills in states that were even purple states, and they have done so successfully, and they have done so with people who would advocate for them.\par \par \uc2 Him undermining them at this moment, at a moment when the pro-life cause believes that they are very much indebted to the former president for achieving this historical aspect of reversing Roe is something I think that is essentially going to test how much those voters believe that they can get out of him in a second term. If they believe that this term is going to be about building his own legacy or in some ways trying to find some peace with "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 " readers that he very much wants to be held in approval by, then I think that there is an actual opening there for conservatives who are willing to stand up on the abortion issue, willing to really take it to the former president on this issue, to basically say you can't trust him another four years the way you invested in him last time around.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Only four months and a little bit left to go before the first contest. We'll see if anybody can make inroads on this issue.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us, folks, because coming up next, the spending, the Senate dress code, and jitters about a government shutdown.\par \par \uc2 Later on, shark bites man, we've heard that before, but man saves shark? That you need to see.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. BILL HAGERTY, (R-TN): The \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  is the world's most important deliberative body. And to see it completely downgraded, no dress code now, I just think it's a step in the wrong direction.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 MARKWAYNE MULLIN\uc2 , (R-OK): There's a side of me that's super excited about it because I hate wearing a tie and I would rather be in blue jeans and a pair of boots and a white t-shirt, but the fact is that you do dress for the job.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: And we're back with the panel now as across the street from us they are changing the dress code in the Senate. No longer will you have to wear a coat and tie or suit pants when you are on the floor of the Senate. Brit, there are more than a few people who are saying this is a nod to the casually attired \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  as a man who is always decked out in sartorial splendor. Why don't you start us off here?\par \par \uc2 Fetterman is a man had difficulty because of the stroke that he had, the severe stroke that he suffered, in handling his duties as senator. And you remember he was out and hospitalized for a long period of time with continuing after-effects from the stroke. You would think that he would want people to think he is truly fit for the job. And I don't know why he thinks it's such a great idea for him to show up on the Senate floor dressed like the man, looking like the man who came to fix the air conditioning. These are gym clothes he wears.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 HUME: And I don't think it adds to his credibility and I don't think it adds to the Senate's credibility. There is such a thing as decorum. Decorum means fitness, but it doesn't mean fitness in the way that he apparently thinks.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Juan, I want to put up on the screen here pictures of four members of Congress in the Senate and the House, \uc2 Ben Ray Lujan\uc2 , \uc2 Chris Van Hollen\uc2 , Mark Kirk, and Tim Johnson. These are all members of Congress who had strokes. Tim Johnson had an aneurysm that led to a massive stroke. And they came back from their strokes, and they continued to wear a coat and tie. Why should \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  be able to go in his gym clothes to the floor of the Senate?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: I don't make the connection between the fact that he had a stroke and what --\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Because people are saying this is what is he comfortable in. This highlights his recovery.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: I don't buy that argument. I don't buy that argument.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: That's what his colleagues are saying.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: No. I think that they're saying, listen, this is the way he likes to dress. But I don't think it's because of his stroke.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Why should he get a bye?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: I don't think he should get a bye. In fact, Ben and I were talking about this in the green room, and I said, you know, I don't think all traditions need to be protected, but if you are in the gallery or you've been on the Senate floor, it's a workplace. You expect people to dress in a decent, respectable fashion. So do I sound like I'm an old geezer? OK. But I'm telling you, that's the way I feel about it, John. Sincerely. What's next? Bare feet, you can just walk in there? No, don't do that.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Flip-flops or slides are the next best thing.\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: I don't want to see senator in Crocs. Come on, this is ridiculous.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Crocs would be a step up for some people.\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: But look, this is completely unacceptable. And look, let's have a higher standard at some point that expects our senators to adopt a certain level of decorum. And I think that this is absurd. The idea that it's being done in order to further the way that he feels about himself, if that's the rationalization, is absolutely ridiculous. The guy could --\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: I do think we can do away with powdered wigs.\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: That's fine. That's fine.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: I don't know. I think, Brit, you still have a couple of those in your closet, don't you?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 HUME: I do. I do.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: There was time when they looked really good on people.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, gentlemen.\par \par \uc2 HUME: They still wear them in the courtrooms in England.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: That's true, they do. Thankfully they don't wear them across the street anymore. Thank you, all. We'll see you again very soon.\par \par \uc2 And finally tonight, a SPECIAL REPORT salute.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Oh, my. Babe, it's too dangerous.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: A massive mako shark washed ashore at a Pensacola, Florida beach. She said hey, that's do dangerous. She was half right. Several men came together to drag the shark back into the water. It thrashed and threw rescuers aside as they tried to help, tried their best to stay away from the business end of the shark. Onlookers watched as the shark flailed at the air, showing his teeth several times during the struggle. It did swim away, though, after a few minutes of being back in the water. And the men received a standing ovation from the crowd. A thing like that could win you a Darwin Award, but thankfully for them it turned out better than that.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT, Bret Baier anchors the show from Riyadh, Saudi Arabia, with the inside look at the kingdom. On Wednesday, Bret is going to head the first major American television news channel interview with the Saudi Crown Prince \uc2 Mohammed bin Salman\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Thanks for watching SPECIAL REPORT.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020230919ej9i00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Special Edition of HANNITY\b0\par\par\uc2 Sean Hannity, Mark Levin \par \uc2 7,323 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 September 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Hannity\par \uc2 SHAN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SEAN HANNITY\uc2 , FOX NEWS HOST: And welcome to the special edition of HANNITY.\par \par \uc2 And tonight, an important warning about the Democrat Party. Traditional liberals, moderates they no longer have a place in the party that they once called their home. Far left authoritarians have now taken over and now they're using the Democratic Party as a vessel to destroy our great American way of life.\par \par \uc2 They don't believe in freedom or individualism as much as they claim, or on inalienable rights. Instead, they prefer government, mandated collectivism. For them, the Bill of Rights is just standing in the way in the name of, quote, common good, they want to control where you live, how you travel, what you eat, what you wear, what you drive, and, of course, what you're allowed to think and what you're allowed to say, and God forbid you say the wrong thing.\par \par \uc2 And they want to force you or shame you to follow their health care demands. They want to eliminate self-defense, self-reliance and force everyone to comply with mother government.\par \par \uc2 And according to these Democrats -- well, they are going to do all of this for your own good. Thank goodness they're in your life, in order to achieve this left-wing utopia. They are using even our Department of Justice to enforce their radical vision, first by squashing any and all dissent. God forbid you speak out at a school board meeting.\par \par \uc2 You know, another example, the political persecution of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Far beyond anything this country has ever seen. It's never happened in history before. Now, first, they attempted to destroy his presidency over a real conspiracy theory hoax. Now he's facing hundreds of years behind bars because of a variety of farfetched, overreaching indictments, all timed for maximum political impact and a lead-up to a presidential election.\par \par \uc2 At the very same time, the DOJ is seemingly ignoring, you know, very real evidence of corruption, possible bribery, money laundering against President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . In other words, Biden's weaponized DOJ, trying to imprison his chief political rival, all the while giving Joe a free pass.\par \par \uc2 That is what you see in a far left banana republic, or in the old Soviet Union. Political enemies, they get locked up. They get silenced. Those in charge, they live privileged lifestyles. No rules at all.\par \par \uc2 Ever wonder how \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  affords two lavish residences in Delaware, despite his life in public service? The far left Department of Justice doesn't seem to care. They're not curious at all that's because his last name is Biden and not Trump or Clinton.\par \par \uc2 In the case of Clinton or Biden or Trump, you know the difference. And the Bidens are always willing to toe the party line.\par \par \uc2 Well, our friend, the great one, Mark Levin, has a brand new book entitled the Democrat -- "The Democrat Party Hates America". And tonight, he will join us for the full hour, explain the deep history of how we have gotten to this point and just how bad this has become.\par \par \uc2 We also know him as a nationally syndicated radio host, host of "Life, \uc2 Liberty\uc2  and Levin" right here on the FOX News Channel, every Saturday, Sunday night, 8:00 p.m.\par \par \uc2 Great one, Mark Levin, great to have you, sir.\par \par \uc2 MARK LEVIN, FOX NEWS HOST, "LIFE, \uc2 LIBERTY\uc2  AND LEVIN": Well, thanks for having me. I think this is a very, very important topic and I appreciate you spending time on it, Sean. I really do.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Let me put up the chapter titles if I can here and let people take a look at it. You know, I've read this now cover to cover. It is an extremely comprehensive book, exhaustive -- exhaustively researched. And we talk about the real, and there's a distinction -- you call it "The Democrat Party Hates America" and we'll get to that in a second.\par \par \uc2 Few Americans likely know about the deep history that you go into and then a lot of depth here. Some of the history I knew, some I did not know, as I was reading this. And more importantly, the danger that they represent to the country today to fundamentally change our country.\par \par \uc2 So in many ways, the history is relevant to where we are today because you know most Americans I meet, they're extremely troubled about the direction of the country. I'll let you take it from there.\par \par \uc2 LEVIN: Well, Sean, I really want the American people to understand what we're dealing with here for over 150 years, the Democrat Party has done everything it can to undermine this country. The party of the Confederacy was the Democrat Party, the party of slavery was the Democrat Party, the party of the Klan, the party of lynching, the party of segregation right up into the `60s was the Democrat Party.\par \par \uc2 The party that supported the internment of Japanese Americans was the Democrat Party. The party that was all hyped up in support of eugenics -- which was also used against Blacks, by the way, was the Democrat Party. I look at \uc2 Woodrow Wilson\uc2 , a Klansman, a racist, a segregationist, Franklin Roosevelt. I spent a lot of time on him because the lies on Franklin Roosevelt are immense, despite all the documentaries and books.\par \par \uc2 Why? Because \uc2 Franklin Roosevelt\uc2  was the first big socialist, anti- constitutionalist elected to the presidency. He was a racist. He was a segregationist and an anti-Semite.\par \par \uc2 Lyndon Johnson who they credit with the 1964 and '65 Civil Rights Acts. Lyndon Johnson was a racist and segregations to the day he died, and I explained that in the book.\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  -- \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  came into the Senate, immediately sought mentors who were racist and segregationists, mostly from Mississippi and Alabama. He's been able to conceal that because he's also quite the chameleon.\par \par \uc2 This is the history of the Democrat Party. We can go into Joseph Kennedy, the head of the Kennedy clan, a horrendous anti-Semite, so bad that he was quite supportive of the early Third Reich that Franklin Roosevelt had to yank him back as ambassador of Britain.\par \par \uc2 Margaret Sanger, \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  got award from \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 . She founds \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 . \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  supported birth control, eugenics and so forth, mostly because he wanted to control the population, particularly Blacks. She was a racist and so forth.\par \par \uc2 So when you look at these issues and not just the history, you look today, the Democrat Party cannot survive without racism, segregation. It's now busy re-segregating society. Ask the Asian community, ask anybody who goes to colleges or universities today, read critical race theory. That's what it's all about. So they've moved