elementary school, libraries. You don't see the Republican Party trying to destroy American sovereignty or other aspects of Americanism.\par \par \uc2 As bad and weak as the Republican Party is, the Republican Party doesn't hate America. It loves America. It just has very weak leaders in many respects right now who don't even have the foggiest idea what's swirling around. So they give aid and comfort, many of them unwittingly, some of them just because they're lazy, to the very people who are trying to destroy this country.\par \par \uc2 And let us make this very clear, when you're trying to destroy parental rights, religious rights, free speech rights, Second Amendment rights, due process rights, federalism, when you're trying to use the 14th Amendment to keep a presidential candidate off the ballot or the 14th amendment to empower \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  to have complete control over what congress's core powers are, that is borrowing money, spending money and so forth and so on -- when these things are happening, if you don't believe the Democrat Party hates America, then my question is: this how do they love it?\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Mark Levin, thank you for being with us. We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 We'll have more of the special edition of HANNITY right after the break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: And thank you for joining us for this special edition of HANNITY. Unfortunately, that's all the time we have left this evening. Please set your DVR so you never ever, ever miss an episode of HANNITY. And don't forget, "Life, \uc2 Liberty\uc2  and Levin" with Mark, Saturday and Sunday nights, 8:00 p.m. Eastern. Don't forget, "The Democrat Party Hates America", amazon.com, hannity.com, bookstores everywhere.\par \par \uc2 Programming note, live audience shows Wednesday and Thursday, tickets are free. Just go to hannity.com.\par \par \uc2 And in the meantime, let not your heart be troubled, why? Greg Gutfeld standing by to put a smile on your face. Have a great weekend.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document SHAN000020230916ej9f00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Interview With Rep. Nancy Mace (R-SC); Hurricane Lee Targets New England; Hunter Biden Under Fire; Interview With Rep. Jason Smith (R-MO); President Biden Voices Support For UAW Workers; Autoworkers Strike\b0\par\par\uc2 Alexis McAdams, Nicole Valdes, Jacqui Heinrich, Grady Trimble,  David Spunt, Neil Cavuto \par \uc2 7,959 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 September 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 STRIKERS: We are the union, mighty, mighty union!\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: All right, as you can hear, the strike is on; 13,000 U.S. autoworkers are now off the job. That number could get a lot higher. A 40-day strike against \uc2 General Motors\uc2  back in 2019, for example, costs nearly $4 billion.\par \par \uc2 So anyone is trying to guess on how long this could go. We're told the talks are going to resume tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 For now, we have got FOX team coverage with Grady Trimble in Wayne, Michigan on those picket lines, and Jacqui Heinrich at the White House on what's on the line.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everybody. Glad to have you. I'm Neil Cavuto.\par \par \uc2 Let's go right to my buddy Grady Trimble following all of this in Michigan, as we now get close to concluding the first full day of this strike.\par \par \uc2 How are things looking there, Grady?\par \par \uc2 GRADY TRIMBLE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, Neil, I can tell you that the automakers were disappointed that the \uc2 UAW\uc2  president, Shawn Fain, was not at the main negotiating table today.\par \par \uc2 I spoke with \uc2 Jim Farley\uc2 , the CEO of Ford, yesterday, and he told me: We have put a generous offer forward. We're working on a historic deal, while the \uc2 UAW\uc2 , he says, is focused on a historic strike.\par \par \uc2 And now that strike has happened. Here's what \uc2 GM\uc2  CEO \uc2 Mary Barra\uc2  told me.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MARY BARRA\uc2 , CEO, \uc2 GENERAL MOTORS\uc2 : The strike was completely unnecessary. We're ready to get to the table. Again, we have a historic offer on the table. We want to get to those last final issues. I'm extremely frustrated and disappointed, and I think the \uc2 UAW\uc2  leadership needs to get to the table, because we need to get this resolved.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE: I'm with Shantel Johnson, one of these striking workers.\par \par \uc2 So they say the offer has been generous, your employer, Ford, as well as \uc2 General Motors\uc2 , 20 percent wage increases, cost of living pay bumps. Why is that not enough?\par \par \uc2 SHANTEL JOHNSON, UAW MEMBER: It's just we do a lot of hard work in here and we feel like we just deserve a little bit more.\par \par \uc2 A lot of people were OK with maybe the last offer that they put up on the table. But, like I said, we only could stand behind our big bosses down at -- up at the big house. They're -- if that's what they say they want to do, we only can stand behind them so far.\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE: This is your first time striking. You have been with Ford for 12 years. Let me ask you about the strategy, 13,000 workers to start, several thousand of them at this plant, as well as a \uc2 General Motors\uc2  plant in Missouri and a \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  plant in Ohio.\par \par \uc2 But your union says they could ramp this up and strike different plants, depending on how negotiations are going. What do you think of this approach?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Honestly, I think this approach is going well, for this to be my first strike. I think, like I said, we do a lot of stamping over here at our department for a lot of other \uc2 Ford Motor Company\uc2  businesses, buildings.\par \par \uc2 So I think that, eventually, all of the plants will be shut down, depending on how long they take to come up with an agreement.\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE: In other words, what you're saying is shutting down one or three plants could shut down an entire network?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Correct. Correct. Yes, we do a lot of stamping over here for Dearborn and sometimes Lavonia. So, as far as our stamping being down, it's going to really affect a lot of those businesses also.\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE: All right, thanks for your time.\par \par \uc2 Neil, you might have been hearing the honking horns behind me. That's been going on throughout the day. We know that the \uc2 UAW\uc2  president is going to be joined in just about 45 minutes at a rally with Senator Bernie Sanders, a lot of other Democratic lawmakers voicing their support for these striking workers -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You had a lot of great questions you were asking that striking worker, but one thing stands out that I'm just curious what you're hearing.\par \par \uc2 Already, one of the big automakers had already proposed a 20 percent pay raise, and they sort of sloughed that one off. I mean, obviously they're hoping for far more generous numbers.\par \par \uc2 How likely are they to get it, from what you're hearing?\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE: It's possible, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Some of the workers I have talked to said we'd have been happy with a 20 percent pay raise if it came with other improvements to benefits like health and pension. Shantel, who you just heard from, matter of fact, joined after Ford dissolved its pension program for workers. So she has a 401(k), she told me. She said she'd really like to see a pension.\par \par \uc2 And so for some of these workers, it's not just about increasing pay. They'd also like to see improvements to benefits as well. And that is what they say they're picketing for.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much for that, Grady Trimble.\par \par \uc2 How the White House is handling that and all of this and trying to prepare for getting involved in this.\par \par \uc2 Jacqui Heinrich there -- Jacqui\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Yes, this strike is bad news for the president, who said just a little over 10 days ago, I believe, that he didn't think a strike was going to happen and he was not concerned about it. The president, who calls himself the most pro-labor president in history, has still not gotten the endorsement of the \uc2 UAW\uc2 , which traditionally backs Democrats and backed Biden in 2020.\par \par \uc2 So, today, he made clear that he's siding with the workers.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Auto companies have seen record profits, including in the last few years, because of the extraordinary skill and sacrifices of the \uc2 UAW\uc2  workers.\par \par \uc2 Those record profits have not been shared fairly, in my view, with those workers. The bottom line is that autoworkers help create America's middle class. They deserve a contract that sustains them in the middle class.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: The strike puts nearly one in 10 of America's unionized autoworkers on strike to pressure \uc2 General Motors\uc2 , Ford and Chrysler-owned \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  to deliver huge wage increases, 36 percent over four years, among other demands.\par \par \uc2 It could push up already high prices and drive buyers toward foreign automakers and also add stress to inflation.\par \par \uc2 The Chamber of Commerce president writes: "The \uc2 UAW\uc2  strike and indeed the summer of strikes is the natural result of the Biden administration's whole-of-government approach to promoting unionization at all costs. For the 94 percent of the American private sector workers not in a union, the costs are starting to stack up, from increasing consumer costs now for new cars to sudden loss in business for those in related industries like auto suppliers, restaurants and caterers, whose customers are now on strike."\par \par \uc2 This was the White House response to that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: The president believes that collective bargaining works. That's what he believes. And we have seen that work. And it is important that workers are able to have benefits and wages, so that they can raise their family.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: So the president spoke to \uc2 UAW\uc2  President Shawn Fain yesterday. He talked to the Big Three auto CEOs when he was on his foreign trip in India and Vietnam.\par \par \uc2 But, for now, he has dispatched his senior adviser Gene Sperling and the acting labor secretary Julie Su, to Detroit to lead these negotiations from here on -- Bret.\par \par \uc2 Excuse me, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: That's OK. That's quite an honor.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right. Jacqui, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Jacqui Heinrich on that.\par \par \uc2 Well, so, how do car dealers about this? They're kind of between a rock and a hard place?\par \par \uc2 Tom Maoli is one of them. He owns six car dealerships, including a Four dealership in Toms River, New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 So, Tom, if folks are looking for an automobile right now, is there any problem with supply?\par \par \uc2 TOM MAOLI, FORD DEALERSHIP OWNER: No.\par \par \uc2 Listen, the -- we have had supply issues all along. We haven't even recovered from the chip issue. And the chip issue really shows that if you're missing one piece of a part for a car, you can't finish the manufacturing. But what the \uc2 UAW\uc2  is asking for here, Neil, is unsustainable, a 32-hour workweek, 40 percent pay raise.\par \par \uc2 And President Biden came out this morning and he said workers should share in the profits. And you know what? I agree. They should share in the profit. But what happens when the market turns and it goes the opposite way and inflation comes back down, car prices come back down, and the profits start eroding?\par \par \uc2 Do they want to share in that? You can't give a 32-hour workweek and a 40 percent pay raise and -- in a contract when you're dealing with a marketplace that fluctuates year to year. Ford had $1.9 billion in profits, but the \uc2 UAW\uc2  or no one's looking at the fact that they lost 4-point -- or they're projecting to lose $4.5 billion this E.V. insanity that the president and the White House is pushing.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, they turn that around, Tom, and say they're still making money even with all those crummy E.V.s. So they use that in their favor and say, if the industry is so tight with the dough and in such disarray, one of them would not have offered us a 20 percent pay raise on the spot.\par \par \uc2 That is the one they ignored, and they're hoping for more. So where do you see this going?\par \par \uc2 MAOLI: Well, listen, ultimately, it has to get settled because, at the end of the day, what's going to happen is, you're shutting down not only the supply chain, you're shutting down the transportation system. And at the end of the day, the consumer's the one that takes it on the head.\par \par \uc2 And if this is going to -- if this goes on for any more than two, several weeks, what you're going to do is, it's not just the supply of the vehicles, it's supply of parts to start repairing those vehicles. People have to go to work, they have to get their children to school.\par \par \uc2 So it has to be resolved. And, ultimately, it will get resolved. But I think that the \uc2 UAW\uc2  has got to come back and be much more reasonable. I think that if the White House has to step in, they should step in. And they should understand that, listen, yes, the automakers have record profits right now, but if you look back pre-COVID, Ford was losing its shirt.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Oh, absolutely. Those record profits are just in the last few years. I'm not an apologist for the industry, but we got to look at things through a five-to-seven, eight-year snapshot. And it's a very different snapshot at that.\par \par \uc2 But we will see. Hopefully, cooler heads and maybe calmer heads resolve all of this.\par \par \uc2 Tom, good seeing you again. Have a good weekend.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Go ahead. Finish your thought.\par \par \uc2 MAOLI: Good seeing you.\par \par \uc2 No, I was just saying, I think it will get resolved.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, thank you, my friend.\par \par \uc2 All right, in the meantime here, more charges could be coming \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's way, and all of this as Republicans are moving pretty fast on an impeachment inquiry.\par \par \uc2 Jason Smith, the guy who runs the House Ways and Means Committee and will be a key player in that -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: CAVUTO: All right, taking a look at three counts we already know on gun possession and worse for \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 , but conditions that could get worse for him in the weeks and months ahead, potential extra charges here.\par \par \uc2 Let's get the latest from David Spunt following all of this -- David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Neil, as we look forward into the future, we see what \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  may be charged with.\par \par \uc2 It's possible he may be charged with tax crime. And that's not just us speculating. It's because special counsel David Weiss said so in a court filing from last month in August, that the tax charges that were part of the original plea deal that fell apart are still on the table, and Hunter could be charged in either California, where he lives, or Washington, D.C., where he used to live.\par \par \uc2 But this indictment, as of yesterday, is about a gun. \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  faces those three felony counts. Two are false statements. The third is possession of a gun. According to the special counsel's office, back in 2018, Hunter lied on a federal forum to purchase a gun when he said he was not addicted to any stimulant, narcotic drug or controlled substance, when in fact he knew that statement was false.\par \par \uc2 The second charge is a false statement made to a person who worked at the gun shop in Delaware, and the third, Neil, is actually owning the gun when unlawfully using illegal substances. Now, last night, Hunter's attorney, Abbe Lowell, criticized special counsel David Weiss and the overall charges, and he said Weiss is someone who caved to pressure by Republicans.\par \par \uc2 Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ABBE LOWELL, ATTORNEY FOR \uc2 HUNTER BIDEN\uc2 : Number two point, this office has never brought a charge like this against anybody. When they are bringing this charge, it's either because somebody has had the gun in the commission of a crime, they have bought multiple guns, they are a straw purchaser for somebody else, or they're a felon in possession.\par \par \uc2 That -- none of that is true about \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: Now, the Justice Department put out this release yesterday that added up the amount of time for each charge. It's up to 25 years in prison and a significant amount of money, $250,000, for every charge.\par \par \uc2 But since Hunter's not a prior felon, unlikely he would see anywhere near that. And also to note, there's still no first appearance scheduled in Delaware for this case. We're waiting to have a judge assigned and waiting for his initial appearance in Delaware. That has yet to be scheduled. That's why you haven't heard anything on that front about him in court yet, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Understood, David. Thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: You're welcome.\par \par \uc2 Now to the guy who runs the House Ways and Means Committee. Very happy to have Jason Smith with us.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, very good to have you.\par \par \uc2 Do you think that additional charges will be filed against Hunter?\par \par \uc2 REP. JASON SMITH (R-MO): I sure hope so.\par \par \uc2 If you go on based what the \uc2 IRS\uc2  whistle-blowers told our committee, the Ways and Means Committee, and also what additional \uc2 IRS\uc2  employees have said in our most recent testimony in the last week, they all believe that \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  should be charged with several felony tax crimes that ranged from 2014 to 2019.\par \par \uc2 And that was more than 19 months ago that the \uc2 IRS\uc2  tax investigative team set before Department of Justice and recommended it.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Now, you're a crucial player, one of three tasked with leading this impeachment probe, inquiry, whatever you want to call it. What will you be probing? What will you be looking at to take it to the next level?\par \par \uc2 SMITH: The next step that we're looking at is we -- for one, we have asked for numerous individuals to come forward. We have sent over 16 letters since June 22, both myself, Jim Jordan and Jamie Comer combined, ask for documents, but also asking for answers to select questions.\par \par \uc2 Right now, I'm focused on the Department of Justice Tax Division. I think it's very crucial that they come in, answer numerous questions based on what the road map that the \uc2 IRS\uc2  whistle-blowers laid out.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So is it your sense -- because I know there was some skittishness among some of your colleagues to initiate a probe or whatever you want to call it, because it would look like you're too zealous and all of that.\par \par \uc2 Did \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's indictment on these gun charges give you cover? I mean, I know there are a little apples and oranges, I grant you, but that it seemed to take the heat off you or some of you in this impeachment effort.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: I just look at Hunter Biden's gun charges as being some of the weakest crimes that he could be charged with.\par \par \uc2 I -- and they're actually the only crimes that it appears that he could be charged with that aren't affiliated with anyone else in his family. And so that's the lowest-hanging fruit that the Justice Department was able to lay out there to try to get the attention off of them, I believe.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But many have been concerned that David Weiss, looking into all of this, wasn't even going to go that far, and now indications that he might be looking at some of these very issues.\par \par \uc2 What are your opinions now of David Weiss?\par \par \uc2 SMITH: I will tell you, I have great concerns about David Weiss because our two \uc2 IRS\uc2  whistle-blowers highlighted a meeting on October 7, 2022, that they were in where Mr. Weiss told them that he wanted to bring charges in Washington, in California, and was denied by people above him.\par \par \uc2 But then after we released this information with the whistle-blowers on June 22, you saw letters that came to Jim Jordan and to Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  that said the other way around. However, just in the last week, Neil, we have had four different witnesses, two \uc2 FBI\uc2 , two \uc2 IRS\uc2 , that came in, and they confirmed what the \uc2 IRS\uc2  whistle-blower said.\par \par \uc2 That, in fact, prosecutor Weiss said that he had to go higher up in the food chain to get permission before he could bring any charges against \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 . And that is completely counter what the attorney general of the United States, \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 , has testified before Congress, saying that the prosecutor has complete and total authority.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Chairman, there were reports that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  had called a number of key Republicans to pursue this impeachment effort. Did he ever call you?\par \par \uc2 SMITH: He hasn't. And he has my number. And he's called me. I have not talked to the president in several months.\par \par \uc2 This is about getting the right information to the American public. The American public deserves answers.\par \par \uc2 The fact that we have seen now that the president of the United States has lied, we have seen that the president of the United States was part of selling his own brand, according to what Devon Archer referred to as his own brand, to help \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's business, the fact that his family has received more than $20 million that we have tracked to 20-plus different shell corporations for their foreign business dealings, and the fact that his family members have been treated differently.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, you don't have -- you don't have unequivocal proof that the president is part of the -- saving all of those millions of dollars. Are you saying that you have clear proof that he has and he did and he does?\par \par \uc2 SMITH: What we have proof is that clearly he was setting down with \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  and his business associates.\par \par \uc2 We have testimony showing that the president did phone calls.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, I understand that's \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  and his business associates, sir. I apologize. I wasn't clear.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But you seem to link the president unequivocally to that, and I didn't think we had pro of that.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: I think that there's a lot of information that we're looking at that is connected to him, but...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But that sounds like smoke, but not fire.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: This is the very beginning.\par \par \uc2 The reason why we're doing the impeachment inquiry now is the fact that the Biden administration has been obstructing our investigation from day one.\par \par \uc2 This is the first time in the history of Congress that a special counsel was ordered just to obstruct a congressional investigation. That should alarm all Americans.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But, again, I just want to be clear, because when you were mentioning the millions of dollars and this Hunter Biden situation, you seemed to indicate that the president himself was a beneficiary of that.\par \par \uc2 You're not so sure?\par \par \uc2 SMITH: What I said is, is, there's been $20 million tracked by our \uc2 IRS\uc2  whistle-blowers of Hunter Biden and his associates and his family members.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, and the family members are and do include \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  or not, that you know of now?\par \par \uc2 SMITH: I'm not going to get the cart before the horse. We will get to that at some point. But, Neil, I will be happy to share that with you in time.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK, because a lot of people, as you know, a lot of your colleagues have already linked that. And it sounds like we're not quite at that stage yet, right?\par \par \uc2 SMITH: I'm not going to get the cart before the horse, but I'm telling you right now that what we have been seeing is extremely alarming, that the president of the United States' son has received more than -- his son and his son's affiliates have received more than $20 million from China, Russia, Romania, Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 It doesn't smell right, and it's not making people happy.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, we will follow it very closely.\par \par \uc2 Chairman, thank you for taking the time. Have a good weekend.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: You too, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, in the meantime here, we have got some nasty weather to deal with in a hurricane that's been, well, sticking around forever, but right now getting very close to hitting some very populated areas.\par \par \uc2 The very latest on that with Nicole Valdes -- Nicole?\par \par \uc2 NICOLE VALDES, FOX WEATHER CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Neil, winds are already starting to pick up all across New England, as Hurricane Lee slowly moves north, taking aim at Nova Scotia, but the impacts here are going to be plenty.\par \par \uc2 We will talk more about that here coming up.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Just call Hurricane Lee the storm that will not go away. It's massive, a slow-mover, and headed to Maine right now, already declaring a state of emergency just in case.\par \par \uc2 We're on it right after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, it might not be hitting land, but it will have an impact on land around the north, Northern New England, well up to Nova Scotia. It's a massive storm. I'm talking about Hurricane Lee.\par \par \uc2 Nicole Valdes following it all in Scituate, Massachusetts -- Nicole.\par \par \uc2 VALDES: Neil, the winds you see right now are truly just the beginning, as Hurricane Lee is still approaching the New England area.\par \par \uc2 But I will tell you, the peak of this storm is really going to be felt here tomorrow morning as that hurricane inches closer here. Now, I want to point out that the state has really tried to take preemptive action, not only getting all their resources in place hours ahead of that storm, but really trying to focus in on preparing for the worst, hoping for the best, especially after we have seen a lot of video come from areas that have already started to feel what Hurricane Lee can really produce.\par \par \uc2 Take a look at this video showing rough surf in Bermuda. That is absolutely a major threat to places like Scituate, Massachusetts, where I'm standing here today. That rough surf could produce some coastal flooding.\par \par \uc2 So, Governor \uc2 Maura Healey\uc2  now says she's got about 50 National Guardsmen on stand by, with high-water vehicles ready to navigate the potential floodwaters that may come into play with this storm, hopefully not needed, but ready if so, as well as the Red Cross and utility workers spread out throughout across the state.\par \par \uc2 She says she's gone ahead and taken action to try to be in the best position possible ahead of the anticipated impacts.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. MAURA HEALEY (D-MA): I declared a state of emergency in Massachusetts. I do this because it's necessary to get assets in place before anything lands here in Massachusetts. It puts us in the best possible position to be able to respond in the ways that we need.\par \par \uc2 This includes both positioning equipment and personnel. We also have sent to the federal government the formal request for an emergency declaration under the Stafford Act.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 VALDES: Now, of course, this is not the first time this state has been needing to prepare for a flood event, and actually not the first time this month.\par \par \uc2 Just a few days ago, 11 inches of rain flooded neighborhoods across the state of Massachusetts. The video you saw earlier showed impacts from a nor'easter back in 2014, a storm that will produce -- really, Hurricane Lee, I should say, will produce some similar really impacts that this area is used to seeing during those nor'easter, Neil.\par \par \uc2 So, quite an interesting next couple hours we're going to be seeing here.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, I would imagine, Nicole. Thank you for your great coverage, my friend, and be safe.\par \par \uc2 Nicole Valdes on that.\par \par \uc2 By the way, FOX Weather is indeed your hurricane headquarters for coverage of all of this and any of this. I always say that we might forget about this storm. Of course, they have been all over it and reminding us, we're not out of the woods here. You can stream it on your favorite connected device, always on, always free. It is remarkable.\par \par \uc2 When I first heard about that and got it myself, now it's glued to me. It's like I know what barbecue conditions are going to be three years out. I exaggerate, but you got my point. They're very good at what they do.\par \par \uc2 All right, in the meantime, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  is very good at what he does. It's what made him the world's richest man. But \uc2 Walter Isaacson\uc2 's book on him right now shows him warts and all, even some of the crazy things he does. Is it about getting attention or just because he's crazy?\par \par \uc2 After this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: We had a lot of buzz on my conversation with \uc2 Walter Isaacson\uc2 , who has just written a great book on \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But one of the issues that came up from that FOX Business chat, which we're going to play a little bit for you here, is just the oddity of \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , very guy -- rich guy, very smart guy, but kind of weird. And that kind is part and parcel of the DNA of the guy. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WALTER ISAACSON\uc2 , AUTHOR, "ELON MUSK": He really has all these personalities. And, at times, he is totally crazy. And he will admit it. He will almost cackle about his craziness.\par \par \uc2 And then he might go into a dark, brooding period. But, there, you see that picture. He takes risks. Even at his birthday party, he gets a blindfolded knife thrower.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, that was a little weird.\par \par \uc2 ISAACSON: And he's put down balloons between his legs, or the sumo wrestler.\par \par \uc2 But we used to be a nation of risk-takers. We used to -- and no matter where you came from, whether it was on the Mayflower or you crossed the Rio Grande. People would take risks. And now we have become a nation filled with more referees than risk-takers, more people telling you, no, you can't do it, it's not safe, or regulators, than people who are willing to innovate.\par \par \uc2 And so I think that crazed risk-taking gene, page after page in my book helps explain why he's getting rockets into orbit and us into the electric vehicle age.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: No, I take nothing away from that. As you write yourself, as Shakespeare writes, the best are molded out of their faults. So he's got a lot of faults. To what extent did that mold him?\par \par \uc2 But those faults or whatever you want to say seem to go back to a rocky relationship with his dad. And his father was -- seems like a real SOB, but I'm just wondering, why does he react the way he does?\par \par \uc2 ISAACSON: He would turn dark whenever we talked about it, sort of a sad look coming over his face.\par \par \uc2 And he was beaten up as a scrawny kid, Elon was. Not any social graces then or now. And his -- the scars, though, were worse that came from his father. He had to stand in front of his father once after a really bad -- being beaten up on the playground. And his father berated him for an hour-and-a- half and said, you're stupid, you're at fault, took the side of the people who beat him up.\par \par \uc2 And so that produced a lot of demons in his head. And all successful people, I think, have some demons. The question is, which demons do you travel -- do you channel into drives?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: He can be pretty abusive. You had written that he lacks the empathy gene. And others have said of that.\par \par \uc2 I'm just wondering, in the two years, I believe it was that long, that you were tracing and following him and seeing this play out, whether you saw it enough for yourself. They say, you know, this is not a nice guy.\par \par \uc2 ISAACSON: Definitely.\par \par \uc2 And a lot of times, he -- that's an understatement to say he's not nice to the people around him at times, especially when he's mission-driven. And in the book, you will see dozens of anecdotes, dozens of tales where he will be walking at the -- say, the base in South Texas where they're launching Starship, and suddenly he will get angry and order up a surge and really ream people out.\par \par \uc2 But there are times he's inspiring. There's times when he's funny, times when he's really caring. And he would say and does say in the book that, yes, he sometimes lacks what we would call empathy, but it's partly because, if you care too much about pleasing everybody around you, you're never going to fulfill your mission.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 Did he ever reveal to you someone he really liked for president? There was a time when it was DeSantis.\par \par \uc2 ISAACSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But I'm not sure whether that flips too.\par \par \uc2 ISAACSON: I don't think he's settled on a candidate.\par \par \uc2 He spends a lot of time making political pronouncements.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 ISAACSON: But in politics and everything else, he's rather mercurial.\par \par \uc2 There will be times in the middle of the day, he's in a good mood, he tells me that we need to have a center in this nation, we need to help support moderates, people on Republican and Democratic side that can come together.\par \par \uc2 You're showing him with Obama there. He was a big fund-raiser for Obama.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: That's right.\par \par \uc2 ISAACSON: So he will talk about the need for bringing center together.\par \par \uc2 Then there will be times late at night, when he's particularly angry at somebody for dissing him, and it may be a Democratic politician, it may be whatever, and he gets dark. So I don't think his politics are easy to pigeonhole.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: He was a rock star to a lot of the people on the left with his very energy-efficient, environmentally safe and beautiful cars.\par \par \uc2 And then he started espousing some of these political views, and the same people who said great things about and turned on him. How did he feel about that?\par \par \uc2 ISAACSON: Well, he got very -- once when he was at a wilderness camp as a little kid, he used to get beaten up. They took all his food. Next time he went, he was a little bit bigger. And he said, I just learned to punch people in the nose as hard as I can. And even though they beat me up, they'd remember that I had punched them in the nose.\par \par \uc2 So he's pugnacious, pugnacious, so much so that it can be a bit shocking. So he has done that, especially when Elizabeth Warren says, you're dodging taxes. He ends up not only punching back, but he ends up exercising options and paying, I think, more tax than any human has ever paid to any person, any government in history.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ISAACSON: But that gets them all set up and riled up.\par \par \uc2 So he will get pugnacious politically, but I'm sure he will do it to people on the right, as well as the left.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: The other stuff that's come out and all this personal stuff, and the demon mode and all of this, and how he lacks the empathy gene, and his rocky relationship with his father, and the fights back and forth, and all this baggage, he didn't get back to you on this and say, this isn't at all what I thought?\par \par \uc2 ISAACSON: Oh, no, he knew what I was going to write. I mean, we talked about all of these things.\par \par \uc2 He's not very happy talking about his childhood. It makes him choke up.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ISAACSON: But there's -- we talked about all these things in the book.\par \par \uc2 And I think he wanted something that was almost brutally honest, and he's not objected. I have seen the things he's posted in the past week, and he will comment on things but he has not -- knock on wood -- decided that -- to go ballistic on anything.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So he's OK with you revealing that the world's richest man cuts his own hair?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 ISAACSON: You should look at his hair. Sometimes, it looks like it's cut by a Korean barber who's about to be executed.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Is he really concerned that humankind has a shelf life on it?\par \par \uc2 ISAACSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 And when you ask about, is he empathetic, does he care about -- is he nice to the people around him, he's more empathetic to humanity, if you know what I mean, than maybe the five or six people who might be in front of him on a factory floor.\par \par \uc2 And he has three great missions that he's had since he was in college. One is to get us into an era of sustainable energy, including electric vehicles. The other is something could happen to this planet, to our civilization. We're the only consciousness we know of in the universe. We got to become a spacefaring civilization. And that's the adventurous part of us.\par \par \uc2 And, thirdly, he read Isaac Asimov as a kid. He worries about artificial intelligence and robots being dangerous. I used to think that some of those, these are my missions, were kind of the pep talks you give your team or the pontificating you do on podcasts. But over and over, I heard him talk about it and relate things back there.\par \par \uc2 I really think that this is part of this deep motivation of a man-child who thinks of himself on the stage.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: An impressive stage, as \uc2 Walter Isaacson\uc2  put out. Whatever you think of the guy, he is the world's richest human being, and he has overcome many difficulties to become that success, warts and all, like a lot of successful people.\par \par \uc2 All right, in the meantime, the controversial exchange right now in New York just a few blocks from here earlier today concerning a migrant crisis that got loud and, yes, nasty.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (SHOUTING)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, very hard to hear everything that was going on there, but Alexandria Cortez was there with people like \uc2 Jerry Nadler\uc2 , liberal Democrats, who were trying to say they want to look after the migrants who were here, take care of them.\par \par \uc2 But a lot of people in that crowd were not hearing it, as both sides went at each other.\par \par \uc2 Alexis McAdams was in the middle of that, kept her calm and cool through that.\par \par \uc2 But now things are a little bit better, but I don't think that they were expecting this, were they?\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS MCADAMS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Neil, good evening.\par \par \uc2 Yes, I don't think the congressmembers were expecting it either, right? We're in a Democratic city, and they probably were expecting more of a warm welcome. But we heard from lots of frustrated New Yorkers who came with their yelling, their screaming chaos, and blowhorns.\par \par \uc2 Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: Close the border! Close the border!\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: Their message there, close the Southern border. That was a scene outside of the Roosevelt Hotel in New York, protesters interrupting those members of the \uc2 Congressional Hispanic Caucus\uc2 , Neil.\par \par \uc2 More than a dozen Democratic congressmembers, including AOC, did tour the hotel, which is the main processing center for migrants. So, as we know, it is one of the busiest areas that people see walking by with lines of migrants outside on a daily basis. New Yorkers came to the hotel today to demand answers, saying they're for legal immigration.\par \par \uc2 Tensions boiled over, though, as the surge continues to show really no sign of slowing down, at least 1,000 migrants showing up in the Big \uc2 Apple\uc2  each and every month. Congressmembers tried to talk about their plans to help, but you really couldn't hear much.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. ADRIANO ESPAILLAT (D-NY): The American dream will not be bullied into submission today. We will continue to fight for the American dream. We will not be bullied.\par \par \uc2 We will not be pushed into a corner. We will fight for these commonsense solutions.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: Yes, and I think they thought it was going to be a quick press conference, talk about what they saw inside, talk about what they plan to do.\par \par \uc2 But New Yorkers say they're sick of the finger-pointing from the mayor looking at the White House, the White House pointing back to the city, and they just want to know what's going on and what the plan is, because you can check it out over my shoulder here in Midtown Manhattan again. There's migrants just lined up outside.\par \par \uc2 So a lot of questions and a lot of concern -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, we could see all of this again. Alexis, great coverage.\par \par \uc2 Alexis McAdams on that.\par \par \uc2 Well, don't say that South Carolina Congresswoman Nancy Mace hadn't warned you about the possibility this kind of thing was spreading deep into blue states and that within those blue states there would be rage. She's next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: Close the border! Close the border!\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, in the bluest of cities, in the bluest of states, a reminder that this migrant issue that has now gone way beyond Texas into a number of sanctuary cities and states, it's alive and well.\par \par \uc2 And, for Democrats, it could prove a major headache.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman Nancy Mace joins us right now in South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman, I just wondered, with these developments here, whether we have exposed a clear weakness among Democrats who up to now have been kind of in lockstep with each other on operation ignore this if they can.\par \par \uc2 REP. NANCY MACE (R-SC): Right.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Now you have New York Mayor Adams, of course, among those saying, Mr. President, you got to do something, the Democratic governor in New York, Mr. President, you got to do something, Governor Newsom out in California, you got to do something.\par \par \uc2 What's going on?\par \par \uc2 MACE: Yes. No, it's interesting.\par \par \uc2 And it's -- surprisingly, it's all these sanctuary cities that would say that they welcome illegal immigrants or the ones that do not want them in their cities. And I would -- I commend every single border governor who is taking these illegals crossing the border and taking them to other cities, especially sanctuary cities, especially blue cities, so they can understand that the president's administration, the president's policies are what are causing this problem.\par \par \uc2 And it's not just the people coming across the border. We all know the fentanyl crisis is at sky high. I have known multiple people in South Carolina who have died from fentanyl overdoses just this year. I didn't even know what fentanyl was 10 years ago. It's also about terrorists that are just freely walking across the border.\par \par \uc2 The numbers we're seeing this year are historic and it's damaging our country. We need to have laws that we follow.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Do you think, Congresswoman -- I know you have been trying to address this and this impeachment inquiry against \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , that you're open to that.\par \par \uc2 You and some of your colleagues have said that we can walk and chew gum at the same time. I get that. But this is a potent issue, this migrant issue, the economy.\par \par \uc2 MACE: It is.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And now with a worker strike going on, potent issues.\par \par \uc2 Do you think, even with the best of intentions, that focus on that impeachment at this time is distracting from these bigger concerns?\par \par \uc2 MACE: I think that's always a risk, because the broader public is going to see a tit for tat. They're going to see this as a wash on either side, because there are investigations on either side.\par \par \uc2 And it takes from the focus of issues that matter. We have inflation. I mean, we just had our district work period. I went to the grocery store with my kids. It cost almost $500 for a week's worth of groceries. No junk food, by the way, Neil, for a week. And that -- just families can't afford...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: That's unfortunate right there, yes.\par \par \uc2 MACE: I know. Well, it's $2,000 for a family of four for a month of groceries without even going out. That's unaffordable.\par \par \uc2 It's women's issues. It's abortion. It's immigration. These are top issues facing America.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But they don't come up, Congresswoman, in this.\par \par \uc2 MACE: No. No.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: When you were on CNN the other night, you said, I -- "You don't do that by making Republicans and moderate districts walk the plank on abortion..."\par \par \uc2 MACE: Right.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: "... walk the plank on women's issues, walk the plank on birth control, walk the plank on impeachment. Well, like, those are all reasons why we will lose next year if we go down that path."\par \par \uc2 Are you afraid of the party getting MAGA blindsided?\par \par \uc2 MACE: I am very concerned, Neil.\par \par \uc2 And I will tell you today I'm about as frustrated and jaded and angry as I have ever been. I have tried to show some -- a framework and a road map on how to handle women's issues, particularly in the era post-Roe, being a victim of rape, working on rape kit legislation, working on birth control legislation, working on commonsense issues that bring everybody together.\par \par \uc2 And absolutely nothing has been done on these issues. And, instead, it's just all these floor fights, and we have not done anything for hardworking blue-collar Americans who can't afford to put food on the table, who can barely make ends meet, put gas in the tank. It's very difficult for them.\par \par \uc2 And I'm -- we're going to have to ask ourselves at the end of the year, Republicans and Democrats alike, what have we done for our country and our countrymen.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So by going after those who are more moderate on this issue and don't want to necessarily punish moderate members who might not down the road, Congresswoman, vote for impeachment -- and I'm getting ahead of myself here.\par \par \uc2 MACE: Yes. We're still early in the process.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: They would be -- they would be -- they would be targeted, though, wouldn't they?\par \par \uc2 MACE: Well, they will be targeted on all the issues, whether it's the impeachment inquiry or women's issues.\par \par \uc2 I will tell you, abortion is going -- people are going to vote on abortion next year. And -- yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, no, within your party, there is a possibility that becomes the single issue, this impeachment thing, you're with impeachment or you're not.\par \par \uc2 MACE: Right, it could be.\par \par \uc2 But there are a lot of other issues out there that we haven't done anything on. And as a suburban woman...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So they shouldn't be punished if they go that route, you're saying? I'm not...\par \par \uc2 MACE: No, they shouldn't be punished.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK.\par \par \uc2 MACE: I mean, nobody should be punished for the vote that they take for their own districts.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it.\par \par \uc2 Nikki Haley, who right now is doing very well in the polls, still trails \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  by a lot. His lead in your state is incredible. Is he the nominee?\par \par \uc2 MACE: Well, it's still very early in the process, but it does look that way.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 MACE: Unless other candidates start dropping out, he -- I believe he's going to be the nominee.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman, thank you. Sorry, I wanted to jump on some of these issues.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman Nancy Mace.\par \par \uc2 Now, I mentioned Nikki Haley. She will be my special guest tomorrow on my weekend show, "Cavuto Live," get her views on this race and some of these issues that we just raised with the congresswoman, all of that coming up at 10:00 a.m. Eastern time.\par \par \uc2 For now, here's "The Five."\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020230916ej9f00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 China A Big Beneficiary Of Pro-Pot Movement; Legalizing Marijuana Was A Predicted Failure; More States Considering Disastrous Pot Policies; China's Tentacles Are All Over The U.S.; Mom Who Lost Custody Of Daughter Due To Gender-Affirming Care Dispute Speaks To The Angle; Cult Of Gender Ideology Targets Parental Rights; Mexican Congress Displays Non-Human Corpses; \uc2 Apple\uc2  Releases Cringe Worthy Climate Sketch; Sen. Tuberville blocking DOD's Left-Wing Agenda\b0\par\par\uc2 Laura Ingraham, Mike Pompeo, Raymond Arroyo \par \uc2 7,436 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 September 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: The Ingraham Angle\par \uc2 INGANG\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS HOST: Economic relationship with the U.S. and her meeting with Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2 . Please remember, if you can't catch us live, set your DVR, 6 PM in the East, 3 PM on the West. Thanks for inviting us in your home again tonight. That's it for this Special Report. Fair, balanced, and still unafraid. Here's Laura.\par \par \uc2 LAURA INGRAHAM, FOX NEWS HOST: Hi, everyone. I'm Laura Ingraham. This is The Ingraham Angle from Washington tonight. Weed wackos, that's the focus of tonight's Angle.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: For me, marijuana legalization is a social justice issue. It's a racial justice issue. It's an economic justice issue. And I think the war on drugs has been an abject failure.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It was supposed to be a model for the rest of the nation. Big venture capital firms raised $25 million to help pass California's Prop 64 back in 2016. And they argued that marijuana legalization would not only keep people out of jail for using small amounts of weed, but it would also reduce crime by squeezing out the illegal drug market. Well, that didn't work out.\par \par \uc2 Far from reducing illegal weed, those efforts instead allowed the black market to flourish after legalization, with the help of organized crime operations that run massive unlicensed farms and storefront dispensaries in plain view, bringing crime and terrorizing nearby residents. And those raided by police are often up and running again within weeks, or even days.\par \par \uc2 Now you know what kills the LA Times to publish these stories, but the broken promises of legalization with the violence and the illegal grows, they're just impossible to ignore. And what's happening and all the other states that legalized it? Well, in Maine where adult use of cannabis is legal, and sales are regulated, the Chinese have stepped in to undercut prices by starting their own illegal operations. And it's so bad that now state lawmakers and Maine's two senators are asking the Feds for help.\par \par \uc2 Now, of course, most of these politicians still aren't against cannabis legalization. They just want to protect the legal market from being again, undercut by the criminals. But again, that's what they promised legalization would do. And walk around Manhattan lately as I have well, that horrid pot smell, it's everywhere. Little kids have to breathe it in. It's like we're all walking around in a big pit of burning manure mixed with skunk smell. No one I know likes it.\par \par \uc2 And when the Big \uc2 Apple\uc2  reeks of dirty diapers, people say well, people aren't going to take out the trash or the trash collection isn't happening. No, no, no, it's not the garbage in the street, it's the legalization that has destigmatized the use of marijuana even in front of children.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ERIC ADAMS (D) NEW YORK CITY MAYOR: The number one thing I smell right now is pot. Everybody is smoking a joint now. You know, everybody has a joint.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: What an idiot. Oh, yes, it's hilarious mayor. Just like what actually happens to school aged kids when the THC infused gummies or snacks or vapes