ay drug. Well, it's true it's not a gateway drug for everyone, or maybe even most people, but it is for most of those sad souls that you see staring into space, muttering to themselves in the streets.\par \par \uc2 And for all the liberals who love to talk about the science, the science on what regular THC use does to the developing brain. Well, it's devastating. From Psychology Today, highly concentrated marijuana products are causing cannabis induced psychosis, paranoia, and confusion. Increased marketing on social media may send a message to teens that cannabis is safe and harmless. And of course it does.\par \par \uc2 Now, parents who've lost children to marijuana have had about enough of the glorification of the drug, and the relentless lies told about its claimed benefits.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Well, he very quickly became addicted. Five years later, he died by suicide, saying that the mob was after him. We don't have a mob, that UNC was an \uc2 FBI\uc2  base that everybody knew everything about him, he became completely delusional.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Paranoid.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It's heartbreaking and infuriating, and all too common. No matter how much money that state governments get from cannabis sales tax is a lot. It's not worth it. It's time for all 50 states to see the cruel lessons of drug legalization, especially for our kids. And that's the Angle.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Heather Bacchus whose son tragically committed suicide after she says he suffered, yes, a marijuana induced psychosis. And Frank Rodriguez, former drug addict and former drug dealer. Heather, if you can believe this, Biden's \uc2 HHS\uc2 , their priority now is to move to reclassify marijuana in a lower risk category, get it out of the -- I think the schedule one of drug classification. But after your experience with your son, and everything we've learned about the science and young Americans, should that happen?\par \par \uc2 HEATHER BACCHUS, MOTHER OF CANNABIS PSYCHOSIS VICTIM: Absolutely not, it's hard to believe that they would take something that is much stronger than when it used to be and that they would OK a substance that would say it's safer. It will give an idea that it's a safe drug, and it isn't, it's much more potent. And it does cause a lot of issues with the developing brain, anxiety, depression, psychosis, suicidal ideation, and unfortunately, we're going to lose a lot of beautiful souls.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And it's not that -- it's not that everyone who does marijuana as a young person is going to be psychotic. That's not what anyone's saying. So before people start texting me and saying that, but what we are saying and I believe the big university study that was recently published confirmed is that a significant percentage of young people who regularly use high THC marijuana products are much more susceptible to psychotic disorders, schizophrenia, suicidal ideation, and obviously violent outbursts. That is the science that we already know. And yet people act like this is all just fine and dandy.\par \par \uc2 Now, Frank, I know you were recently in Philadelphia and saw what the Trank epidemic we've seen what fentanyl does, but now this drug Tranq that is everywhere. What can you tell us?\par \par \uc2 FRANK RODRIGUEZ, FORMER DRUG DEALER AND ADDICT: Yes, so just like you said, it's everywhere. It's literally littering the streets of Philadelphia. I go to New York; I see the same thing. I go to Baltimore; I see the same thing. It's everywhere. It's eating people's skin. the xylazine is actually a horse tranquilizer, and it eats people's skin. Literally, you'll walk down the block, you'll smell rotting flesh. It's 100 percent chemical warfare.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And Frank, tell us your own background. You are dealing drugs and you ultimately turned your life around. But you were in that world. And it was all readily available both for purchase from a dealer point of view and then for sales on the street.\par \par \uc2 RODRIGUEZ: Yes, absolutely. Like you said for so long, that was my world. I spent 17 years in prison. I went through a heroin addiction. And everything was you know, and that was just when heroin was around, not the fentanyl, not the carfentanil, not the xylazine, even the marijuana. The hard drugs. The opiates have graduated and evolved and so has the marijuana. The marijuana isn't the same, you know, joints that kids were smoking back in the day where you had to wake up, maybe you get a headache or something like that.\par \par \uc2 They're finding fentanyl and marijuana. There's little kids that are smoking marijuana and dying. This is a huge problem and it's not just in the inner cities, it's everywhere in America now\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And Frank, I interviewed a number of fentanyl addicts and people who are in between addictions in San Francisco to a -- to -- the last person I interviewed. Every single person started on marijuana. Was that how you started on marijuana or something else?\par \par \uc2 RODRIGUEZ: No, absolutely. Marijuana was the first drug that I ever tried. It's -- you know, being young, it's a social thing. It's sort of being cool, the peer pressure. And everybody that I interview, I interview people, every single person that I've interviewed, addict wise, they all have that in common, where they started with marijuana. And it doesn't say that -- it's -- we're not saying that everybody that smokes marijuana is going to turn into a hardcore drug addict. It's not it. Those of us that are predisposed to addiction, we smoked marijuana, we're going to graduate into other things. It's just a matter of time.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And Heather, how did your son become addicted to marijuana? Again, a lot of parents watching this tonight. They don't think that's an addiction drug. That's an occasional recreational use drug. But he became addicted. And how did it lead to his death?\par \par \uc2 BACCHUS: Well, he started using it the age of 15, for acceptance, and he had older friends that were using it, and he liked it pretty instantly. And it wasn't like he started using it constantly. But by the time he was 16, he had a marijuana use disorder. And we sought treatment for him when he was 17. It was just, it had him hooked. He was adamant that it was good for you, because the industry led him to believe that it was good for him. And he would tell me all the benefits, health benefits. At the time, I didn't have the knowledge of the science and the studies that I have now today.\par \par \uc2 And I so wish that he would have known how it affects the adolescent brain in the development. But essentially, it changed the function of his brain. And he became anxious, he became depressed. When he wasn't using, he would get really, really low. And in fact, when he took his life, he had low levels of THC in his system. He was trying to quit, but he just couldn't. He couldn't.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Frank, the story and -- we're blessed to have these stories as horrific as they are to tell. Because you might say, both of you might save a life tonight. And I know that maybe sounds dramatic, but I don't care if it sounds dramatic. It's true. Frank, what can we do to reach out more to kids and parents about this trajectory? And where this is going, especially with more states legalizing even harder drug use?\par \par \uc2 RODRIGUEZ: Yes, so I think the most important thing is we have to educate our children, we have to let them know that marijuana although you know, it's put out there to be so harmless and this calming drug, it's not -- it's a gateway, it's going to lead to other things, you know, when -- when people they'll be having a bag of weed and if they drop it on the floor, they're going to be searching on their hands and knees just like somebody who dropped some heroin, or some cocaine, it's the same exact thing.\par \par \uc2 We have to educate our children, we have to let them know what's going on, the dangers out there. Because the fact of the matter is, if we don't do it, somebody else will. So we have to educate them, the youth and anybody that are listening, we have to start in our own home and speak to our children. Because if not, the sad reality is, they might not wake up, they might, might not wake up with a hangover, they might be in the cemetery.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Frank, Heather, we're going to keep talking about this. A lot of people kind of don't like to talk about it, but I'm going to keep talking about it. And I thank both of you. Frank, we're going to go out with you, I think at some point and see more of this ourselves. And Heather, thank you. The administration's favorite fantasy fiction claims that Americans are actually benefiting from Bidenomics. So we decided to go out and ask folks. Plus, Newt Gingrich reacts next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: All right, this is a Fox News Alert. Late today a federal judge temporarily blocked the New Mexico governor's emergency ban on open and concealed carry in Albuquerque and its surrounding area. Now we're going to discuss more in moments. But first,\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (D), PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Bidenomics just another way of saying restoring the American dream,\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: Our economy and labor market remains strong.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Not only has inflation come down, it's now growing more slowly than the pay of low and middle wage workers, meaning their buying power is increased\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Whoa, whoa, their buying power has increased and inflation is down. A little fact check here. Inflation actually rose this month. The news just came out this morning. And its biggest monthly jump since January. Now, the White House can try to hit the spin cycle and over and over again. But is it any of this going to convince the voters?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Inflation has been really high in the last several years. A lot of the wages are not going up. People just can't afford the things that we used to enjoy every day.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I guess I'm kind of insulated from a lot of stuff, but I don't know. inflation's not great. But I don't know. Expect\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Prices have gone up, but that's to me normal.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You don't have too many choices out here right now; you've got to get to it. I'm actually about to pick up a tow (ph) vehicle right now. So you got to find another source of income aside of your daily source for sure.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Have you noticed like utilities going up recently?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes, I have. I noticed the price of gasoline fuels going up too.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Everything is definitely more expensive these days.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Do you feel like you're better off today than you were two years ago?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: No.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What's hitting you the hardest?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Groceries and gasoline. It's hard. I take the train to work because of it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Joining me now Newt Gingrich, former Speaker of the House and Fox News, Contributor. Newt, groceries, gas prices increasing again. They're the two things, everyone seems to go back to, the two things that everyone needs. And here we are Bidenomics is working, Newt.\par \par \uc2 NEWT GINGRICH (R) FORMER SPEAKER OF THE HOUSE: Well, this might be one of the great strategic mistakes for reelection that anybody has made in modern times. Bidenomics is going to become an absurdity, the average everyday American knows that the price of gasoline is going up. And by the way, every indication is that the Biden Administration policies are going to make gasoline even more expensive. So gasoline is a major factor in the consumer price index. And as it keeps going up, you're going to see the inflation go up.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, everybody who goes grocery shopping, in Clifton (ph), I did this over the weekend, you're startled by some of the prices. And you know, the \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , he lives -- has a great chef at the White House, he gets to fly Air Force One, he gets to go to Wilmington, and be surrounded by people. I don't think they have a clue how bad inflation is, high prices are for the average American and Bidenomics may be one of the reasons that one of the most important deep state columnist, David Ignatius, wrote a column this morning saying Biden should drop out. I think this begins to sink in to Democrats. Biden getting reelected is going to be very, very hard.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Newt, I think what's been amazing about this, from the very beginning in this administration, is that they think that every problem is just a PR problem. And we have to communicate it better -- people -- people don't see it the way we're seeing it, but in reality, it's a substance problem, I mean, they're shutting off oil supplies, exploration, cordoning off land that can't be drilled. And so now, well, it turns out, our utilities are going to be sky high, this winter, new report just came out about that, and all the other things we're seeing.\par \par \uc2 So it's not a PR or messaging problem. This is a policy problem, and it has been across the board.\par \par \uc2 GINGRICH: Look, if you are a hardline Left-winger, you believe in a world that's a fantasy. Now, if you really believe in that world, you can't face any facts, because the facts would destroy your belief system. So you make things up, and you get used to lying to each other. And that makes you out of touch with reality.\par \par \uc2 I thought the other day when the Secretary of Energy couldn't actually take the drive and the electric vehicle, because for example, I think they're only 37 charging stations in all of West Virginia. And here she is out in a PR stunt. And it turns out they staffed it so stupidly; they didn't realize she couldn't possibly do what they were promising. This kind of stuff goes on every day in this administration.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Newt, really quickly on that New Mexico concealed carry and open carry ban that was put into place by the New Mexico governor, because of a gun violence emergency was slapped down temporarily. Your quick reaction to that.\par \par \uc2 GINGRICH: The governor is crazy. It's another example of the rule of power, replacing the rule of law. There is no constitutional provision that allows you to do that. And by the way, she has to be personally unilaterally suspending New Mexico law to do it. It is an example of the total power that the Left believes they have to tell us what to do.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And Biden got slapped down with a student loan forgiveness. I mean, they left and right they're getting hit by all these unconstitutional moves, Newt, great to see you tonight as always.\par \par \uc2 Alright, China has its red tentacles wrapped around everything here in the United States, our schools, our universities, research facilities at major colleges and universities. The real estate market gobbling up commercial and residential real estate everywhere. But a new shocking report out of the UK shows us how things could get even worse.\par \par \uc2 The Times is reporting that MI5, Britain's Intel agency has warned leaders of the Conservative Party there that two of their potential parliamentary candidates could be Chinese spies. Now the party blocked them in 2021, 2022 from actually becoming candidates due to the security concerns. But has the damage already been done and given the money on the line with corporate elites here in the United States and the CCP doing business together makes a lot of dough. Could we be vulnerable as well?\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2 , former Trump, Secretary of State and Fox News Contributor, Mr. Secretary, is it only a matter of time before China gets its talons into \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  agencies in a really profound way?\par \par \uc2 MIKE POMPEO\uc2 , FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Laura, thanks for continuing to stay at this problem of China being here at home. Inside the gates we often think about China as Taiwan or far away, but the story out of the United Kingdom where their equivalent of our \uc2 FBI\uc2  found that there were government officials that they suspected of spying for China is something that can happen here.\par \par \uc2 Look, we saw it with Representative Swalwell. I can tell you they have been trying to spy on a city councilman county commissioners This is a deep effort by what they call their united front. It's their propaganda campaign here inside the United States. These are real efforts. You talked about them being in our universities, they will certainly come after elected officials, those who are around them, donors in the like attempting to steer America towards the ideas of the Chinese Communist Party in a way from the central understandings of America.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, one thing that we have seen is a relentless push and promotion by corporate elites to continue U.S. corporate engagement with China, Mr. Secretary, you and I've talked about this for years. But is there not more evidence day-after-day that decoupling divesting from China and every critical industry is what we should be doing right now?\par \par \uc2 POMPEO: Laura. there is no doubt that the Chinese Communist Party's effort is deep and broad. It's like nothing that the United States has ever seen, Laura. You and I remember, we grew up in the Cold War, where Russian spies were working -- this makes that effort pale in comparison. And so yes, their efforts is stealing our intellectual property, destroying our jobs. We saw what they did with the virus, lied to the world about it for years and still haven't been held accountable.\par \par \uc2 No, these efforts are broad, they are diplomatic, they're economic. It's the espionage, which you spoke about. They're inside the universities stealing our research. We need to wake up. We can't be naive. If the Chinese Communist Party is funding research at your college or university. It is not because they like your alma mater, it is because they have an intense interest in undermining the United States of America. And so yes, Corporate America has got to pick this up too, they've got to do their part.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now, were you -- well, I should say, what was your reaction to the report of the CIA whistleblower, who has told members of Congress that we were pressured, we were pressuring people paid off not to actually look more into China's real, the origin of the virus, namely, not in a wet market, but in a lab, and that this actually was happening? I mean, your reaction there, Mr. Secretary,\par \par \uc2 POMPEO: It seems unimaginable that they would compensate analysts at the CIA for committing fraud, for undermining their very analytic efforts. So I hope it's not true. I've seen the reporting. But make no mistake about it, the pressure, the pressure that Dr. Fauci and others put on the \uc2 USG\uc2  to avoid what we know now to be the almost certain truth. This virus came from the Wuhan Institute of Virology. We spoke about it --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: We were funding Wuhan --\par \par \uc2 POMPEO: Back in March.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes, Mr. Secretary, we were funding, gain-of-function type research or gain-of-function research at Wuhan through the \uc2 EcoHealth Alliance\uc2 . So there's a really disturbing tale here involving --\par \par \uc2 POMPEO: And hard at lying to America as well.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Mr. Secretary, it's been too long. Great to see. Thank you so much. Now a bill in California could charge you with child abuse and take away your kids, if you don't affirm your child's gender transition. My next guest lost custody after questioning her own daughter's gender decision. Jeannette Cooper speaks out, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Did you know that if you have family --LGBTQ -- you can just go home, not see them. If you need permission, this is it.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Parents don't have rights, not parents' rights. Kids have rights.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: A few months ago, we told you about a new law being proposed in California that would allow the state to remove a child from his or her own parents if the parents refuse to affirm the child's new gender identity. And worse, the parents could even be charged with a crime, child abuse.\par \par \uc2 Now, it's this close to becoming law. It passed in the state senate, the assembly, and now it's just waiting for Governor \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2 's signature. What are you going do, Gav?\par \par \uc2 If you think for a second that this is never going happen in your state or where you live, well maybe if you're lucky enough to live in a solidly blue state, but will it be -- I mean, a red state, but it will be solidly red forever.\par \par \uc2 Well, there's a threat out there, and you need to hear from our next guest. Joining me now is Jeanette Cooper. She lost custody of her 12-year-old in 2019 because she questioned her daughter's new gender identity. She's also one of the co-founders of Partners for Ethical Care.\par \par \uc2 Now, Jeanette, that bill wasn't a law. It still wasn't a law back then. So, how did you lose your parental rights? How was that allowed?\par \par \uc2 JEANNETTE COOPER, LOST CUSTODY OF DAUGHTER: Well -- yeah, after two and a half years of them coercing you into consenting to your own destruction, that's how it works. I mean, when a custody evaluator determines that you are a serious endangerment to a child because you don't believe in such a concept of -- as gender identity, you have nowhere to go. The state has a lot of control.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Right. So, these arbiters, these -- whether they're court- appointed arbiters, can just come in and say, well, the child feels, you know, not supported by you. And it's a hostile environment for the child for you not to agree with this new identity. Is that essentially how it went down?\par \par \uc2 COOPER: Essentially. I mean, now the concept of parenting is to say yes to your child for just about everything. And so, if you say no, all of a sudden, you're unsafe. And if you don't agree with a child's self- identification as something other than their biological sex, then somehow, you're contributing to their suicide.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, I think if a child says she doesn't want to have an abortion, that would -- and the parent decides, no, I'm not going to -- I'm going to force you -- then the state would be fine with that, right? That's one of the things they would be fine with. Here's the California bills Democrat author last week. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Parents affirm their children. They have since the dawn of time. Because that is our duty as parents to affirm our children.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: She seems like she left off the operative clause where this law is concerned, proposed law, or else. You either, quote, "affirm this transition or else." What do parents across the country need to understand about how serious this is?\par \par \uc2 COOPER: Well, I run a parent group of over 3600 parents that are across the world. And what we know is that you can affirm a child's feelings without telling them that they're true. So, somebody might feel uncomfortable, but what is the source of that discomfort? And are they capable of coping with the discomfort? They are.\par \par \uc2 And if we push everything, if we snowplow everything out of their way, then they'll never be able to cope with anything. We basically disable children when we continuously say yes to everything that they say.\par \par \uc2 And we don't push back and say, I can understand you feel that way, but I'm not going to say that that's true. Because it's not true that my daughter is a boy. It's not true that my daughter is anything but a girl.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yeah, I don't think -- I don't like the whole affirming language. I think you love your child and you guide your child according to your own family's values. You put guardrails in front of your child, so your child doesn't, you know, go off the edge.\par \par \uc2 Jeannette, before we let you go, what's the latest with you and your daughter? When's the last time you saw her?\par \par \uc2 COOPER: Yeah, I saw her for an hour in January of 2022, and before that two-years previous. So, I'm planning a petition that should be filed shortly in family court here in Cook County, Illinois, to request Sundays, 9 to 5, which I used to have six days a week custody time. And so, I'm requesting that to come out of reserve and for me, actually, to see my daughter before she turns 18.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Jeannette, where is she now?\par \par \uc2 COOPER: She lives about a mile and a half up the street from me.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Jeannette, thank you for sharing the story tonight. It's really disturbing. All right. Real alien corpses found, what? Scarcity hits the VMA's. Raymond Arroyo has it all. Seen and Unseen and a lot more, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It's time for our Seen and Unseen segment where we explore the cultural stories of the day. And for that we turn to the author of the soon to be published, The Magnificent Mischiefs of Tad Lincoln, Raymond Arroyo. All right, Ray, there was a hearing today about -- this can't be, alleged aliens in Mexico, illegal aliens? Or what are we talking about?\par \par \uc2 RAYMOND ARROYO, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: No, not illegal ones, the ones from the skies, from other planets. This researcher brought in what he claimed were non-human life forms. But to my eye, they looked more like E.T. garden statues. This researcher alleges that these things are 1,800 years old, but we should qualify this. He claimed to have found aliens in Peru back in 2015. Those were debunked as frauds.\par \par \uc2 My question is, why are we suddenly so worried about aliens from other planets, Laura, when we have plenty of aliens right here that we can't keep track of. We just had a big manhunt that discovered that guy.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, we also lost track of, what is it, 177,000 illegals who came into the country, and then 120,000 children, we don't know where they are, the sponsors, the address was fake. I mean, so we can't keep track of much.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Right. And we're worried about, you know, aliens. But look, I also think everybody's hoping to find an extraterrestrial, while alien life forms are actually all around us, Laura, if you look closely enough.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: My gosh, you know, oh, it's devastating.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Like, so happens I got a pedicure.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: I know where I got these from. This is my dad's trick.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Laura, that's a close encounter of the worst kind right there. You heard of Barbarella? That's Smell-o-rella. That's a little fright.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: No. Matthew McConaughey is really -- he'll go above and beyond to be a good guest on a show, which is --\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Yeah, that's a real man. But look, I have to tell you about the latest \uc2 Apple\uc2  ad to hawk their iPhone 15. It looks like it could have been made by aliens. \uc2 Tim Cook\uc2  and the staff in this skit try to placate their climate critics by having a sit down with Mother Nature. Naturally, she is played by Oscar winner, Octavia Spencer.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Mother Nature, welcome to \uc2 Apple\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: In 2020, you promised to bring \uc2 Apple\uc2 's entire carbon footprint to zero by 2030.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: After your \uc2 Apple\uc2  office, store and data center runs on clean electricity thanks to you and your powerful wind and sun.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Seriously, explaining carbon neutrality to Mother Nature?\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Right, no, I'm sorry.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: You want to tell me how photosynthesis works, too?\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Our aim is to permanently remove carbon from the atmosphere.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: As you can see, we've innovated and retooled almost every part of our process to reduce our impact on the planet. But there's still a lot more work to do.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: This is the biggest act of virtue signaling, Laura. And what they don't tell you in that ad, or anywhere else is, 95 percent of every iPhone, iPod, everything that they create is made in China. Ninety-five percent of it.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well --\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: If they were really worried about the environment, they'd get out of China.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: First of all, \uc2 Tim Cook\uc2  could not be a worse actor. OK, he is horrifically bad, and we've seen bad acting. But Raymond, the rare earth materials that are necessary for the iPhone, and what China does to get those rare earth materials.\par \par \uc2 But we're supposed to be, what, really impressed that they have biodegradable toilet paper in the \uc2 Apple\uc2  bathrooms? I mean, are they kidding me? I mean, ooh. And what is Tim's jet run on, sunshine? I mean, come on.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: They're bragging on, you know, they have paper wrappings around their products. Now, you're sending children into cobalt mines in Africa to dig this stuff up with their hands. Spare me your virtue signaling. And they're using ships instead of planes to deliver the stuff, like that doesn't pollute the ocean.\par \par \uc2 Finally, Laura, some big cultural fails before we go. We watched, both of us, the \uc2 MTV\uc2  Video Music Awards last night, and you were struck by the costuming.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Raymond, inflation must be hitting the music scene pretty hard because the outfits on these ladies, if that's what we can call them. I hope you can actually see this well on the screen. Doja Cat looked like she was wearing an actual spider's web. That's the spider who stole the costume on the set. Look at that. The spider who stole wardrobe.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: The only thing missing there is the fly.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: The fly is not there. And Nicki Minaj, Laura, she went for the Demure look. She even wore a veil, even over her underwear. That's pretty good.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: She also stripped up. Yeah. Well, I think one thing I can't figure out, though, is why -- was it B.B. Rez's outfit? Had it, Rexha -- I don't know how to say it, had the backside cut out in a horse tail? What was equine -- an equine move.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: I can't quite understand what's happening there, and I won't even try, but it is bizarre to have a tail hanging off the back of your dress above the cutouts. I don't quite get that. All the audience needs to know about --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Hold on, Ray. What we have to say to everyone is, look, everyone's trying to outdo one another. I get it. This is what they do. But at some point, when you're in a bra and panties on stage as the emcee of the VMAs, what's next?\par \par \uc2 I mean, just show up in an adult diaper and just nothing? I mean, I don't know what you show up in after that. I mean, you're basically not wearing clothes.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: They have to keep topping each other. And the bottom line is, Taylor Swift won everything, and Shakira stole the night. Not only do her hips not lie, they don't quit either. She was pretty good, I have to say. At 46 years old, she was working it. That was quite a routine. She stole the evening.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yeah. Taylor Swift took it all, and I agree. The oldies, even the old hip hop artists, they did the best job. Ray, great to see you.\par \par \uc2 All right, McConnell is now criticizing, of course, Tommy Tuberville for blocking military promotions over the Pentagon's abortion policies. But Senator \uc2 Ron Johnson\uc2  says there's a simple solution to all of it. He'll explain it next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Senator Tommy Tuberville has not budged and is blocking unanimous consent vote for hundreds of Pentagon staff promotions. They'd like to do it all at one fell swoop. Now, he wants the Pentagon to drop its new policy supporting abortion. And now, of course, the guy who seems always ready to help the Biden administration in a pinch, Senate Minority Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , he's weighing in.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: I think holding these non-policy-making career military who can't be involved in politics at all is a mistake.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: A mistake? Well, it seems like Senator McConnell doesn't know really what he's talking about. The fact is, these nominations could easily be voted on one by one. If this is really a threat to military readiness, then why hasn't anyone suggested actually holding these votes?\par \par \uc2 They could quickly get through the new joint chiefs of staff confirmed and 4 million retires. That's not a problem. So, what's the real reason this is being held up?\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Wisconsin Senator \uc2 Ron Johnson\uc2 . Senator, are Democrats worried about being held accountable for certain individual nominees' votes?\par \par \uc2 SEN. RON JOHNSON (R-WI): Of course not. I know this will shock you and your audience, but Democrats are being complete hypocrites on this. If they really wanted a comment for the Marines, they would just call up the nomination for a vote, first to close your vote, and then two hours later, vote to confirm the nomination. It takes literally no more than four hours, but generally two if you do it as a comeback vote.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, I think Leader McConnell is completely out of step with our conference. I just feel a great deal of support for Senator Tuberville within our conference, as well as among our base. I mean, we are the party of life. We're also the party that upholds the rule of law.\par \par \uc2 And the simplest solution here is just have the administration follow the law, have the Department of Defense not facilitate or, you know, spend taxpayer money facilitating abortions. It's just that simple.\par \par \uc2 But if they won't do that, all they have to do, if they want a general, they want one of these promotions, just bring the nomination up for a vote. Four hours later, that individual is going to be confirmed.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: All right. Well, you guys had 5 weeks off, 4 weeks off, whatever, as is customary in August, he had a lot of time off. So presumably, again, if this was a national security or military imperative, McConnell and Schumer, they would have gotten together and said, OK, we're going to knock out the top 12 people. We're going make sure these votes are held and we're going do it in the course of a day and a half, or less.\par \par \uc2 But I think they don't want to do it, number one, because some of these nominees are pretty bad, like the guy who's tapped to lead the Pacific Air Command, you know, Pacific Air Forces, watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Ultimately, that's really what DEI is getting after. You know, not identifying the barriers that currently exist, getting messaging out to the public as to what opportunities do exist, and then just, you know, drawing the talent that we need to continue to excel as a force.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now, are senators worried about being, again, held accountable or going on the record for this guy's promotion?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Well, first of all, this has been a hallmark of the Biden administration. They have been nominating and confirming, Democrats have been confirming, the most radical leftists you could ever imagine. It is just sad what is happening throughout all these agencies in terms of the people that President Biden is populating in those agencies.\par \par \uc2 But again, Democrats have no problem confirming these votes. And quite honestly, the mainstream media won't hold them accountable for it. So again, this is obviously a political issue. Democrats are being complete hypocrites. And it's just a simple matter of holding votes. And by the way, you could hold votes over Friday, Saturday, Sunday.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Right.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: You know, hold the Senate in. We can do these things. So, again, it's just grossly hypocritical and --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Senator, senator, look, I don't think Tester wants to take on any of some of these nominees, like the Air Force nominee who's a very controversial. Jim Slife said George Floyd's death was an Air Force issue, or Colonel Ben Johnson said that white colonels are largely blind to institutional racism.\par \par \uc2 So, I think Tester doesn't want to take that vote. I don't think, you know, maybe Sherrod Brown and some, you know, maybe Manchin. I mean, I don't want to -- I don't think those guys want to vote for it. I think the Democrats want that abortion issue for the election next year.\par \par \uc2 But thank you for speaking truth to power on this, Senator \uc2 Ron Johnson\uc2 . Remember, it is America now and forever. Jesse takes it from here.\par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020230914ej9d00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Interview with Nikki Haley (R) Presidential Candidate\b0\par\par\uc2 Brian Kilmeade \par \uc2 3,414 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 September 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Live Event\par \uc2 FXLV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 BRIAN KILMEADE, FOX RADIO HOST: Well, if you talk to anyone on the right or left, they would say the candidacy of Nikki Haley, well, she is surging. She is a 2024 GOP hopeful.\par \par \uc2 We know we're a few months away from the Iowa caucus, and then up comes New Hampshire, South Carolina, and then we'll have a Super Tuesday before you know it. And this is an important time. No longer can people say, well, wait until Labor Day, then it gets serious. Well, it's after Labor Day. And Nikki Haley has always been serious. She was the first major candidate in.\par \par \uc2 Ambassador, welcome back to "The Brian Kilmeade Show."\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY (R), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Thanks so much, Brian. It's great to be with you.\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: So, 9/11 is here, and we see the first lady and we see the secretary of defense. We don't see the president. He is going to be in Alaska at 4 p.m. today Eastern Time. And one of the comments from his traveling -- his traveling committee was, well, you know, 22 years after Pearl Harbor, we weren't still celebrating -- we still weren't marking it like we're doing now with 9/11.\par \par \uc2 Do you think it's time for us to stop marking it?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: You know, it's not only that, Brian, I mean, I -- you know -- every American that remembers that day gets that pit in their stomach. I mean, I was watching this morning, and that pain never goes away. I had just come home with my son. I had been home maybe a day. And I remember lying in bed with him as my husband, Michael, took our little one to preschool, and looking on the TV and watching that play out thinking, what world did I just bring him into. And the pain that followed.\par \par \uc2 But, more importantly, it was such a reminder that true evil really does exist in this world, and we're never immune from it. And it also reminded us about the greatness of the American spirit and our resilience.\par \par \uc2 That's why Biden and Kamala need to be out there, out front. That's why they need to be reminding every high schooler and every college student to watch 30 minutes of what that day was like, and understand that we can never be so flippant, and we can never be so absent to think that that can't happen again.\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: So, his decision was to spend another day in Vietnam and put it on the tail end of the G-20. So, the G-20 did deconflict from 9/11. But he thought the Vietnam relationship was more important. Was he right?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: No. I think the American spirit always needs to take priority, period. I mean, I think you're looking at -- you're in Vietnam. That's not a democracy. You need to be in America. That is a democracy.\par \par \uc2 You need to acknowledge something that happened that was terrific, not just for -- as a reminder to Americans, but as a tribute to those American families who lost loved ones, as a tribute to the heroes that went and risked their lives and lost their lives in the days following because of the aftereffects of it. That's why you're here. That's what an American president does, is just remind people about the spirit, the resilience, what it means.\par \par \uc2 I mean, I don't get it. I don't get a lot of what he and Kamala do. But what I do get is there has never been a day where we need more of a reminder of what's happening in the world and who we are and what we need to believe in, and the fact that division is not where we need to be, but unity and the American spirit is where we need to be.\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: So, people tell you, well, if you're in diplomacy, you don't want to go directly at something. Your actions really tell your intent. So, that's really what the spirit, I think, that President Biden was giving with Hanoi, Vietnam.\par \par \uc2 She - he decided to upgrade their relationship because of their natural resources, and they seem to view China as a threat. Ease up our relationship with Japan and Korea -- South Korea, and their relationship with each other.\par \par \uc2 He has done the same thing with Australia, who see a huge threat on a daily basis. Nobody has to explain to India, there is still border skirmishes, especially to you, with your heritage. And now, you have a situation where India and Saudi Arabia have set up a corridor to try to counter the Belt and Road program.\par \par \uc2 So, the actions show that President Biden and his administration know that China is on the move and the march, and they see us as an enemy. But we get this, cut 20.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT, UNITED STATES OF AMERICA: Really what this trip is about, it was less about containing China. I don't want to contain China. I just want to make sure that we have a relationship with China that is on the up and up, squared away, everybody knows what it's all about. We're not looking to hurt China, sincerely.\par \par \uc2 (END AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: Your thoughts.\par \par \uc2 HALEY: OK. He is not looking to hurt China, but China is looking to hurt us. They've been preparing for war with us for years.\par \par \uc2 And the amazing part is, he sent four cabinet members there, Brian, already. Four Cabinet members after a Chinese spy balloon flew over our country, four cabinet members after we -- it came out that a Chinese spy base was being developed on Cuba, four cabinet members after one of those cabinet members was hacked, four cabinet members after seeing the intrusion into our country that they bought up 400,000 acres of U.S. soil near our military bases, the fact that they bought the largest exporter of -- the largest pork producer in the country here in America, the fact they steal $600 billion worth of intellectual property, the fact that they've killed more Americans from fentanyl than the Iraq, Afghanistan and Vietnam Wars combined, the fact that they are building up their military.\par \par \uc2 It's unthinkable to me that he continues to want to appease an enemy that has so openly tried to show that they are at war with us. And that's the problem, because when you start to be that weak in the knee on a country like China, who is so involved in actually being on the ground in our country now and causing problems, then what message are you sending to Iran that says death to America? What message are you sending to Russia, that continues to hold hands with China? What message are you sending to North Korea?\par \par \uc2 Because now, what you're seeing is, you're seeing a unification of China, Russia, Iran, and North Korea --\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HALEY: -- and what are they unifying about? They're unifying over their hatred over America.\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: So, we have a San Francisco summit coming up that we really want China to go to. They say, well, you better invite the leader of Hong Kong. We don't want to do that. This guy, John Lee, he had a huge hand in crushing Hong Kong at the behest of Beijing. They also say we have to show more sincerity if we want to show -- want him to show up to the APEC summit. What's your reaction to this stance?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: You don't beg China for anything. You let kind of know what we expect of them. It's their choice to make.\par \par \uc2 What we need to be doing is being strong. We should go back and say no more buying U.S. oil, and we're going to take back the land you've already purchased. We should go and say, you're not going to put any more money in our universities until our universities either take Chinese money or American money.\par \par \uc2 We should go and say that we're going to create a blacklist of all technology that China shouldn't have, because they build up their military. We should tell China we're going to end all normal trade relations with them until they stop killing Americans with fentanyl. And we should start strengthening our military.\par \par \uc2 Forget if they show up at a summit. Let's start letting them know that we're on to them. Let's start letting them know that we're going to lead (ph), not react to them.\par \par \uc2 And every time that he reacts and every time that he does things like this, China and Russia get closer, and it just defies logic. I mean, I've dealt literally, Brian, dealt with China and Russia every day for two years, every day.\par \par \uc2 And Russia would always just create chaos. That's what they did. Every day, they'd create chaos and lie. And China always trying to find ways to be more aggressive, and they would literally just condemn everyone that wasn't them.\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: right.\par \par \uc2 HALEY: They acted like they were the best country in the world, and they constantly were trying to make us feel bad.\par \par \uc2 And the thing is, I always made sure that they knew that we were strong. We were proud. And if they wanted to be working with us, then they had to come to us. We were never going to go to them. And that's the difference between me and what Biden is doing right now.\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: Well, foreign policy, China is connected to Russia, is connected to the war in Ukraine. I know you see that. I see that. The Republican Party necessarily is getting impatient, the ones that aren't supportive of it, on the pacing of which were supplying Ukraine, giving them enough to survive, not win.\par \par \uc2 But I do want to talk about domestically, you were the first to say you're not electing -- re-electing \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . You're electing \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . And one of the key things that Democrats had in 2022, and I think you'll agree with this, was messaging as it came with Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 It gets overturned, and it became Republicans want to take over women's healthcare. They had no message. Listen to \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  when asked what week is the week, when it comes to abortion. Cut 12.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I mean, what week of pregnancy should abortion access be cut off?\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT, UNITED STATES OF AMERICA: We need to put back in place the protections of Roe v. Wade. We need to put back the protections that are in Roe v. Wade into law. We need to restore the protections of Roe v. Wade. The point is, we --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Do you mind (ph) being more precise?\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: I am being precise. We need to put into law the protections of Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 (END AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: And she would not agree to any week. What's your message, if that's the debate you're having with her?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: That means she is for abortion anytime, anywhere, for any reason. I mean, that's what she doesn't want to say.\par \par \uc2 That's why I've said constantly, we need to ask her and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , are you for 38 weeks? Are you for 39 weeks? Are you for 40 weeks? Because they will tell you they're the ones that pushed that law into Congress, trying to get that passed. They are for abortion anytime, anywhere, for any reason.\par \par \uc2 What we know is, we need to make sure that we are always focused on saving as many babies as we can, and supporting as many moms as we can. And whatever it takes to do that, we should be willing to come together and make that happen, because I think the majority of Americans do want to ban late-term abortions. I think they do want to encourage adoptions. I think they do want to make sure that doctors and nurses who don't believe in abortion shouldn't have to perform them. I think they do think that contraception should be accessible.\par \par \uc2 And I think all Americans can agree that any woman that gets an abortion should not have to go to jail or get the death penalty. That's where we should come together.\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: Are you for \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 's 15 weeks?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: I'm for anything that will save babies and support as many moms -- if we can get 60 Senate votes -- and that's the key, Brian, is they have demonized this issue for years. And I refuse to be a part of it. What we need to remember is, in order to pass any federal law, it takes 60 Senate votes and a majority of the House and a signature of a president.\par \par \uc2 We haven't had 60 pro-life senators or 60 Republican --\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: Ever.\par \par \uc2 HALEY: -- senators in over 100 years. So, we might have 45 pro-life senators. So, the reality is, a Republican president can't ban abortions any more than a Democrat president can ban these state laws.\par \par \uc2 So, we have to come together on where those 60 Senate votes are. If 60 Senate votes falls at 15 weeks, absolutely I'll sign it. But let's talk about what it takes to get the 60 votes.\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: Right.\par \par \uc2 HALEY: Because without that, you're not saving more babies. You're not supporting more moms.\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: Why do you think she doesn't answer the question?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: Because she knows that she is for 38, 39 and 40 weeks. She knows that Roe v. Wade being overturned, that that's what she is talking about, is abortion anytime for any reason. And the thing is, she doesn't want it turned back on her.\par \par \uc2 Like, they loved it -- the problem with the fellows on the stage is they would not acknowledge the issue, Brian.\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: Right.\par \par \uc2 HALEY: We need to lean into this. Our ways are the right ways. The difference is how you talk about it. You know, I'm not going to condemn anyone for being pro-choice any more than I want them to condemn me for being pro-life. So, let's talk about it and deal with it in the humanizing way that it is.\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: Right.\par \par \uc2 HALEY: This is personal for every woman and man, and we have to treat it like that. And they want to go demonize men and women who don't think like them. And that's not leadership.\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: So, at the border, obviously, the biggest failure of this administration and in my lifetime, it's infecting every major city, if -- they said it, if the mayor of New York City said that New York will change forever unless we find a way to stop the 60 - or the 3,000 a week and the 66,000 that are here right now. But they got what they wanted - they got what they wanted, because sanctuary city is here, and a right to shelter is mandatory in the city.\par \par \uc2 So, here is \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , who is in charge of the border. Cut 38 -- 35.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: When the border crossings went down earlier in the summer, the administration said it was due to your policies working.\par \par \uc2 Now, they're going back up, as they did in the month of August. Does that show the strategy is no longer working?\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Absolutely not. What it means is that we have to stay focused on a number of issues related to the irregular migration, that, again, we're seeing around the world, and America is not immune. You know, my --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Do you think the strategy is working, despite the numbers being up?\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Overall, we are seeing progress. But there is -- we're not going to have a constant -- there are going to be fluctuations. That is normal, just like the weather fluctuates and circumstances fluctuate.\par \par \uc2 (END AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: Are you happy with that answer?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: Ladies and gentlemen, that is who we are running against for president. That is who will be your president if you vote for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . And that's what everybody needs to remember. That is your border czar. That's the person that was given the responsibility to fix the border, and that is her answer.\par \par \uc2 We will be in a world of hurt if \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  becomes president. That's why I'm fighting so hard, Brian. That's why it matters.\par \par \uc2 And I'll say this about the border, because I think it's really important, I don't think that Greg Abbott gets enough credit for what he has done, because, first of all, when all of this was happening in Texas -- and I went 400 miles down that border, when I talked to the ranchers, when I talked to the sheriffs, when I talked to the border security there, they were so underwater, but they were just taking it.\par \par \uc2 They were taking it because they always had to take it. No one was helping them. And Greg Abbott had the courage to say, you know what? I'm not doing this to my people anymore. And he started sending it to the sanctuary cities around the country who were lecturing Republicans saying that we were bad people because we didn't want to let illegal immigrants in.\par \par \uc2 And all of a sudden, what happens? Are they bad people now? Are they bad people because they see it infiltrating their schools and their hospitals and taxpayers having to pay for it? No. Nobody is -- none of none of us are bad for saying that we want law and order. None of us are bad for saying that we need to make sure that Americans are secure and that national security is a top priority.\par \par \uc2 And now, all of these Democrat-run sanctuary cities are suddenly saying, oh, wait a minute, don't do this to us. Well, you know what? If you're a sanctuary city, they're going to keep coming. And I think Greg Abbott needs to keep sending them, because that's the only way they're going to wake up.\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: And Ambassador, we know that - there's a lot (ph) turning up there on the \uc2 FBI\uc2  most wanted list. So, this is - this is not a good mix of what's happening at the border. Something is going to come to a head. I -- hard to imagine this is not being the top issue, barring any sudden change of events come Election Day.\par \par \uc2 Ambassador, what is your --\par \par \uc2 HALEY: And remember, too, that Iran says that the easiest way to come into America is through the southern border.\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: And we'll see (ph). What's your - right - what has changed since the debate, for your campaign?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: I mean, we've seen an incredible amount of support, and I'm extremely grateful. You know, I think people see that we need a new generational conservative leader. I think they need someone who is going to tell hard truths and let the chips fall where they may. I think we need someone who has been an executive and also negotiated --\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: Right.\par \par \uc2 HALEY: -- across the table from Russia, China, and dealt with Iran and North Korea.\par \par \uc2 And I think they're ready to leave the past and move forward. We've got big problems. We need new solutions.\par \par \uc2 And so, we're incredibly grateful for the support that we've had, but we're going to keep our head down. We're going to keep working hard. This is about earning the support on every single American, and we're not going to stop until we get America back strong and proud again.\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: And she'll keep up with the pledge here in Iowa, and in New Hampshire, and then she'll meet you in South Carolina. Nikki Haley, thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 HALEY: Go to NikkiHaley.com.\par \par \uc2 Document FXLV000020230911ej9b00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Biden Violates White House Mask Procedure; COVID Season Renews Mandate Debate; Adams Slams Texas Governor For Busing Immigrants; Pentagon Accusing Tuberville Of Impacting Readiness; Officials Focus On Location Of Last Known Sightings; Retired General Frank McKenzie Reflects On Afghanistan Withdrawal; New Polls Shows Republican Presidential Candidate Nikki Haley Leading President Biden In Head-To-Head Matchup By Six Points; Policy For Notifying Parents If Their Children Seek To Change Gender Identities On Hold In California School District; President Biden's Unmasking During Medal Of Honor Ceremony Draws Criticism Of CDC Masking Recommendation Due To Recent COVID Surge\b0\par\par\uc2 Shannon Bream, Jacqui Heinrich, Bryan Llenas, Aishah Hasnie, Nate  Foy, Jennifer Griffin, Bret Baier, Mike Emanuel, William La Jeunesse \par \uc2 8,418 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 September 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 HAROLD FORD J.R., FOX NEWS CHANNEL POLITICAL CONTRIBUTOR: They were unable to exchange names in all the chaos, but Mr. Brown is wanting this -- has offered to take -- he and his mom are going to take her to dinner when she comes to Dallas next.\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Beautiful.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: I love stories like this.\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: What a great story.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Jesse, what's on tonight?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: That is fantastic.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: We have a new Biden plagiarism situation. It's an exclusive, so check us out at 8:00.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: I will check it out at 8:00. That's it for us. Goodbye.\par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR: Good evening, welcome to Washington. I'm Shannon Bream, in for Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 President Biden is on his way to India for the G20 Economic Summit. He tested negative, again, today for COVID. First Lady Jill Biden who had tested positive also received a negative result today.\par \par \uc2 The president leaves behind the country, growing more concerned about a possible resurgence of COVID and of COVID-era rules. He disregarded his own policy yesterday when he went unmasked at a public event.\par \par \uc2 Many in the nation are weary of new mask mandates. White House correspondent Jacqui Heinrich starts us off tonight. Good evening, Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 We are seeing a groundswell of opposition to something that hasn't yet happened. The return of any federal mandates in some schools and localities are trying to tamp down on this recent rise in cases.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, the governors of South Carolina and Florida are announcing that their states will not bring back any restrictions as President Biden's CDC is warning people to remain vigilant.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We know that masks don't work. We knew that from the very beginning.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): President Biden flouting CDC mass guidance this week after being exposed.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: 10 days are something. I got to keep wearing it. But don't tell them I didn't have it on when I walked in.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): Spurring support for a Senate Republican bill that would ban the return of federal mask mandates. Democrats unwilling to go that far.\par \par \uc2 SEN. SHELDON WHITEHOUSE (D-RI): I think that would depend on the lethality of COVID. If hundreds of 1000s of people are dying, the government response is a little bit different.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JEFF MERKLEY\uc2  (D-OR): I think it's appropriate to have the local officials were most familiar with the circumstances. So, they decide what's best.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): The CDC which still recommends universal masking for kids in school, addressing this question without a clear yes or no.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: -- people think that you're going to bring masks mandates back.\par \par \uc2 DR. MANDY COHEN, DIRECTOR, CENTERS FOR DISEASE CONTROL AND PREVENTION: Yes. What we're seeing here at the end of summer, we have seen an uptick in COVID.\par \par \uc2 That doesn't -- that doesn't mean that there's mandates, but please know what tools are available to protect yourself.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): President Biden testing negative today, now heads to the G20 in India, as his campaign promotes Biden's leadership on the world stage in a new ad.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: In the middle of a war zone, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  showed the world what America is made of. That's the quiet strength of a true leader who doesn't back down to a dictator.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): Critics say, Chinese President Xi's absence shows Biden's failure.\par \par \uc2 REP. MICHAEL WALTZ (R-FL): There is a reason he didn't attend the G20. But he is attending BRICS. Xi plans to replace the United States, and part of that is sidelining some of these organizations that the U.S. has traditionally led.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (on camera): President Biden did not speak to reporters as he took off for India, and we won't see him again until he lands in Germany for a refueling stop a little bit after midnight. Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Jacqui Heinrich at the White House. Thank you very much, Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: President Biden's problems over his border crisis continued tonight within his own party. New York's mayor says the raging migrant crisis he says will "destroy" his city.\par \par \uc2 Eric Adams is blasting the president, saying, the White House has given no support for the 1000s of asylum seekers arriving each month. Not everyone agrees.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Bryan Llenas is in New York tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DAVID BANKS, NEW YORK CITY SCHOOLS CHANCELLOR: Don't believe the hike. When you hear the messages as notice, somehow, chaos, and anarchy.\par \par \uc2 BRYAN LLENAS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Tonight, the chancellor of New York City schools insists the migrant crisis is under control, as teachers welcome 21,000 non-English speaking migrant children on the first day of school.\par \par \uc2 But just last night, his boss, Mayor Eric Adams, painted a much more dire picture of the migrant crisis.\par \par \uc2 ERIC ADAMS (D), MAYOR OF NEW YORK CITY: Never in my life, have I had a problem that I did not see an ending to. I don't see an end into this. I don't see an end into this. This issue will destroy New York City.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS (voice over): Mayor Adams, says this is a national crisis, attacking the Biden administration for not providing the city any support. Yet, Adam says the ultimate blame lies with Republicans, Former President Trump, and Texas Governor Greg Abbott.\par \par \uc2 ADAMS: Started with a madman down in Texas.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS (voice over): More than 110,000 illegal immigrants have arrived in New York City since April of last year. But according to Governor Abbott, about 13,000 or just 12 percent were bused into the city by his administration.\par \par \uc2 Republicans blame President Biden.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TED CRUZ (R-TX): We've had 7 million illegal immigrants under \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . And so, seeing Eric Adams complain about 100,000 is cute.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS (voice over): Meantime, Chicago is also struggling to house 1000s of migrants. Some 1,500 are temporarily sheltered at city police stations where for migrants were arrested for threatening and assaulting police officers.\par \par \uc2 BEATRIZ PONCE DE LEON, DEPUTY MAYOR, CHICAGO: I cannot guarantee that everybody that comes into any of our shelters has had a background check that we can look at reliably.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 LLENAS (on camera): Mayor Adams says New York City will continue to take in these migrants or asylum seekers. He says it's the right and compassionate thing to do as a sanctuary city, even though he admits it could destroy the city. Shannon?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Bryan Llenas, thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 Alabama Republican senator Tommy Tuberville is under increasing criticism tonight over his hold on military promotions and appointments in protest of the Pentagon's abortion policy.\par \par \uc2 But he's getting support from some of his GOP colleagues. Congressional correspondent Aishah Hasnie has the latest from Capitol Hill. Good evening, Aishah.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Shannon, good evening to you. Ever since the Senate returned to Capitol Hill, Senate Republicans have been banding together around Senator Tuberville and really accusing Democrats and the Pentagon of playing politics here.\par \par \uc2 So, take a look at this. Right Now, these are the 301 promotions and appointments on hold by Senator Tuberville, who wants the military to stop paying for service members to travel across state lines to get an abortion.\par \par \uc2 Now, the Pentagon has been ramping up its media blitz, you could call it against Tuberville, claiming that these holes are impacting military readiness.\par \par \uc2 The navy secretary even accusing the senator of aiding and abetting communists.\par \par \uc2 Today, the Pentagon spokeswoman double down on those threats.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SABRINA SINGH, DEPUTY PRESS SECRETARY, PENTAGON: Four adversaries are watching this right now. And at a time when we have a war raging in Ukraine, at a time when we have the rising challenge of China growing in the East, we need our Senate confirmed military leaders in place.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice over): But Tuberville and Senate Republicans disagree. They say if the situation is so dire then, Senate Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  should use his authority as leader to go around these holds, and bring each individual nomination to the floor for a vote.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TOMMY TUBERVILLE (R-AL): It just goes to show you this is all propaganda that's been carried on by the secretary of defense. And the president of the United States, saying you all got to start putting pressure on this -- on this senator. They don't know what pressure is.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (on camera): So, Shannon, we took that to Democrats and ask them what's going on?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Why do you think leader Schumer isn't trying harder to get around these military holes that Senator Tuberville --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 SEN. DICK DURBIN (D-IL): Oh, he has tried everything. And we do --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Why did he keep you guys here a little bit longer?\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: Well, we didn't think it would change things.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: And Shannon, just moments ago, Senator Tuberville fired back at those service secretaries, who wrote an op-ed about him this week, attacking him. And he's demanding to know from the military, the rank of the service members who were -- who were discharged over that COVID vaccine mandate of 2021. And he wants to know how those discharges affected military readiness. Shannon?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, a lot more to come on that story. Aishah, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Got it.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Officials tracking an escaped murderer in Pennsylvania say he's becoming more desperate. That search is now in its second week.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Nate Foy is in Kennett Square, Pennsylvania tonight. Hello, Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE FOY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 So, authorities are really focusing their search tonight in Longwood Gardens. The botanical garden here in Chester County. You can see some authorities are making their way in behind me right now. There was another reported sighting of Danelo Cavalcante earlier today, just before noon here.\par \par \uc2 This is also the last known location where authorities have proof of his whereabouts.\par \par \uc2 Take a look at these pictures. This was Monday night. He was seen twice at this botanical garden, an hour apart on the trail camera. \uc2 Pennsylvania State Police\uc2  say their current search zone is between eight and 10 square miles. They are using dogs, horses, drones, helicopters and hundreds of personnel on the ground to capture Cavalcante. But troopers say the terrain is too difficult to simply march through.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 LT. COL. GEORGE BIVENS, PENNSYLVANIA STATE POLICE: The number of people that it would take to contain that area and be able to walk shoulder to shoulder through that area and squeeze, and the amount of time that it would take, it's just simply not realistic.\par \par \uc2 It is very important we keep the pressure on him as we continue this hunt. Cavalcante is considered extremely dangerous.\par \par \uc2 FOY (voice over): Lieutenant Colonel Bivens, also said today, he has every reason to believe that Cavalcante is still within their search perimeter. He is again, urging residents to lock their homes, garages, and cars. So, Cavalcante can't obtain any more supplies than he already has.\par \par \uc2 Chester County District Attorney Deb Ryan admitted today that authorities are frustrated.\par \par \uc2 DEB RYAN, DISTRICT ATTORNEY, CHESTER COUNTY: This is an outrage. This should have never happened. We're all upset. We're all concerned and there will be a thorough investigation.\par \par \uc2 FOY (voice over): Meanwhile, the tower officer during Cavalcante's escape at Chester County Prison remains on administrative leave, pending an investigation.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 FOY (on camera): Now, back out here live, this is now the eighth reported sighting of Cavalcante in the eight days since his escape. And authorities are offering $20,000 for information leading to his arrest.\par \par \uc2 They're again asking for the public's help for tips, so they can bring this manhunt to a close that's now entering its second week. Shannon?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Nate Foy, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well, officials here in Washington are looking for a homicide suspect who escaped custody at George Washington Hospital.\par \par \uc2 Police say 30-year-old Christopher Haynes was last seen Wednesday afternoon. The \uc2 FBI\uc2  is offering a reward of $10,000 for information that leads to Haines arrest.\par \par \uc2 Stocks were mixed today. The Dow gained 58. The S&P 500 lost 14. NASDAQ fell 124.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we talked with the former head of U.S. Central Command about the Afghanistan withdrawal two years later.\par \par \uc2 First, here is what some of our Fox affiliates around the country are covering tonight.\par \par \uc2 FOX 11 in Los Angeles, as actor Danny Masterson is sentenced to 30 years to life in prison. He was one of the stars of that 70 show.\par \par \uc2 Masterson was convicted of two counts of rape by force or fear in late May.\par \par \uc2 FOX 29 in San Antonio, as a couple kidnapped in front of their children at gunpoint is found safe.\par \par \uc2 The two were taken from their southwest side home yesterday morning. Police officials confirmed they've made two arrests in connection with that kidnapping and are looking for a third suspect.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Kansas City from EarthCam. One of the big stories there tonight. The \uc2 National Football League\uc2  season kicks off tonight as the Super Bowl champion Chiefs, host the \uc2 Detroit Lions\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Chiefs began their campaign to become the first repeat champs since the Patriots back in 2004.\par \par \uc2 That is tonight's live look "OUTSIDE THE BELTWAY" from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We are a little more than two years out since the U.S. military withdrawal from Afghanistan. President Biden has taken extraordinary criticism for the move that left 13 U.S. service members dead and many others gravely injured following that 2021 suicide bombing attack at the Kabul airport.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, we hear from the general who was in charge of U.S. Central Command at the time. Here's Chief National Security Correspondent Jennifer Griffin.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRIFFIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT: General McKenzie, it's been two years since the Afghan withdrawal was complete. You've had a lot of time to think about what could have been done differently. Do you have any regrets?\par \par \uc2 GEN. \uc2 FRANK MCKENZIE\uc2  (RET.), FORMER CENTRAL COMMANDER: I have a lot of regrets about how it ended in Afghanistan. I have a regret with a basic decision, which I think was the wrong decision. And I have -- and I particularly regret that we did not choose to begin to evacuate our people, our embassy personnel, our American citizens and our at risk Afghans at the time we made the decision to bring on our combat forces.\par \par \uc2 I think that was a serious mistake. And I think that led to the events of August 2021 directly.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: In March, we heard testimony from Sergeant Tyler Vargas-Andrews, a marine sniper at Abbey Gate. He's now a double amputee. He testified under oath that he believed he saw a person who matched the description of the Abbey Gate suicide bomber. The Intelligence said the bomber was clean shaven, wearing a black backpack with three yellow arrows on it and traveling with an older companion.\par \par \uc2 SGT. TYLER VARGAS-ANDREWS (RET.), U.S. MARINES: I requested engagement authority while my team leader was ready on the M110 Semi-Automatic Sniper System. The response, leadership did not have engagement authority for us, do not engage.\par \par \uc2 Eventually, the individual disappeared to this day, we believe he was a suicide bomber, we were ignored.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Is that true?\par \par \uc2 MCKENZIE: First of all, I honor his sacrifice and everybody else who got injured or killed as a result of the Abbey Gate attack. But I can tell you there was no be on the lookout for a person meeting that description on that day or prior to that day in Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Is he misremembering? What's happening?\par \par \uc2 MCKENZIE: Well, I don't know.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: What was the intelligence before the attack? How specific was it? Did you know that a bomber was staging somewhere?\par \par \uc2 MCKENZIE: So, the day of the attack, and the day prior, we were dealing with four significant threats. We were dealing with a threat of a vehicle borne IED, a car with a bomb in it that we thought was being worked to attack us. We were dealing with the possibility of a suicide vest attack, but without specific description of the person. We were dealing with the possibility of an indirect fire attack, either rockets or mortars.\par \par \uc2 But I do know that there was no intelligence to support the assertion that we knew what the bomber looked like.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: How do you explain that he thinks he remembers it so clearly?\par \par \uc2 MCKENZIE: So, I can't explain that. All I can tell you is the intelligence that was in play at that time, and I'm pretty confident of that.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: A new book by author Jerry Dunleavy and James Hasson says there was also a request for a drone strike to be carried out on a hotel where the bomber was believed to be. The request was allegedly turned down, is that true?\par \par \uc2 MCKENZIE: That is not true.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: We've also heard a lot lately from the families of the servicemen and women who were killed at Abbey Gate. They were invited to Capitol Hill by Republican lawmakers, they feel military leaders, including you have not been transparent with them.\par \par \uc2 MARK SCHMITZ, LCPL JARED SCHMITZ FATHER: How could it just be acceptable that our snipers cannot get an immediate and straight answer from their commanding officer?\par \par \uc2 STEVE NIKOUI, LCPL KAREEM NIKOUI FATHER: When they asked McKenzie, did you guys have any opportunity to kill the suicide bomber before August 21st -- 26th? He stated no. We now know that they were tracking them for up to 30 days before the incident and even asked the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  to go where he was staying at and raid it, which they didn't.\par \par \uc2 MCKENZIE: I can tell you that we just didn't have that fidelity of information.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Did you have intelligence that the suicide bomber or members of ISIS-K were gathering in a hotel near Kabul airport?\par \par \uc2 MCKENZIE: So, there were a variety of targets that we passed to the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  to take a look at, more than 10. Some they did, some they didn't action. We have nothing specific about a hotel that we asked them to take a look at.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Do you think the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  let the suicide bomber through intentionally?\par \par \uc2 MCKENZIE: I don't believe so.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Do you regret cutting that deal with the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  to have them provide security outside the perimeter?\par \par \uc2 MCKENZIE: I do not regret cutting that deal when I feel had we not done so, our casualties would have been significantly higher.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Do you believe these families are being used as political pawns?\par \par \uc2 MCKENZIE: Really not for me to say, I feel their pain. I understand the nature of the loss that they have suffered. I have tremendous respect for the suffering that they've gone through.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Is there anything you could have done differently?\par \par \uc2 MCKENZIE: You always look back anytime you lose people and you wonder if you could have done things differently. And I am haunted by that. I think about it quite a bit. It's one of the many regrets that I have.\par \par \uc2 I examine everything we did, I think about it, particularly in the month of August of every year for the rest of my life. I'm going to -- I'm going to think about this very hard.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Do you blame the State Department for delays that led to the chaos we saw at the airport?\par \par \uc2 MCKENZIE: -- want to stay on until the very end, and often push that decision to your internet and extremists situation. I think it's a case of American exceptionalism or American arrogance, depending on your perspective, to believe that you can actually withdraw beaten from the field of battle and still make maintain a large political platform in the country you're actually retreating from.\par \par \uc2 I go back to the basic decision to wait so very late to begin to bring people out after we had already given away Bagram Airfield, after we had already drawn down to a very low footprint in the country. I think those were the decisions that led to the scenes at the -- at the airport in Kabul.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: In your opinion, should the U.S. still have troops in Afghanistan today, from a national security perspective?\par \par \uc2 MCKENZIE: That was my recommendation then, I see no reason to change that recommendation now.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: How do you think history will treat the withdrawal and President Biden's decision to bring all U.S. troops home from Afghanistan?\par \par \uc2 MCKENZIE: I believe history is going to view the decision to come out of Afghanistan in the way that we did, and the manner that we were directed to come out as a fatal flaw. And I think history is going to be very hard on that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: That is Jennifer Griffin reporting.\par \par \uc2 Well, the gap between parent's right to know and what some schools are telling them is hitting a flashpoint around the country and presidential candidates are taking notice, we're going to take a look.\par \par \uc2 But first, Beyond our Borders tonight, Hurricane Lee strengthens into a Category 4 storm as it turns through the open waters of the Atlantic, it is on a path that will take it near the north eastern Caribbean. Current predictions indicate it will not make landfall but past just northeast of the British Virgin Islands.\par \par \uc2 Rescue experts from across Europe converge on a cave in Turkey. They are trying to save an American researcher who became trapped about 3,000 feet below the surface after suffering gastrointestinal bleeding.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at London, one of the big stories there tonight. The baby grand piano belonging to the late Queen frontman Freddie Mercury sells at auction for nearly $2.2 million. \uc2 Mercury\uc2  used the piano to compose some of the group's most popular songs including Bohemian Rhapsody, that piano was part of an auction of hundreds of \uc2 Mercury\uc2 's personal possessions.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories Beyond our Borders tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Former President Trump may seek to move his election interference case from state court in Georgia to federal court. That's according to a new filing.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump and 18 others face charges of illegally trying to overturn the results of the 2020 voting in that state. The former president has denied wrongdoing. He and the other defendants have pleaded not guilty.\par \par \uc2 Fulton County District Attorney \uc2 Fani Willis\uc2  says House Judiciary Committee Chairman Jim Jordan is improperly interfering with the case and trying to punish her personally. Jordan has demanded records of Willis's communication with Justice Department officials who have also indicted Mr. Trump on election interference charges at the federal level.\par \par \uc2 Former Trump White House official \uc2 Peter Navarro\uc2  has been convicted of contempt of Congress and says he will appeal. The charges were filed after he was accused of refusing to cooperate with the Congressional investigation into the Capitol riot. Navarro is the second Trump aide convicted of those charges. Former White House adviser \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  is also appealing his conviction.\par \par \uc2 Congressional district maps previously deemed unconstitutional by the Ohio Supreme Court will be used in 2024 after the high court dismissed legal challenges against the Republican drawn districts today. The voting rights groups that brought forward the challenges say the turmoil is not in the best interest of Ohio voters. The maps were found to be unconstitutional by the court several times for unfairly favoring Ohio's GOP. They will be redrawn after next year's general election.\par \par \uc2 Republican presidential candidate Nikki Haley is unveiling her education agenda, and she says it's all about parents regaining control of their children. Here is chief Washington correspondent Mike Emanuel.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 MIKE EMANUEL, FOX NEWS CHIEF WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT: New polling suggests Nikki Haley would be the strongest in the matchup against \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . Haley is pushing her vision on education in battleground early states, including her home state of South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY, (R) PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: We need to make sure that parents decide which schools their kids go to. Every child deserves a good education, not based on where they are born and raised. Let parents make those decisions where they want their kids to go to school.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Haley has signed onto the Moms for \uc2 Liberty\uc2  pledge. The group's mission, advance policies that strengthen parental involvement and decision-making. A new CNN poll finds Haley is the only GOP candidate to hold a lead over Biden, 49 percent to 43 percent, above the poll's margin of error. That may be why a Biden campaign spokesperson attacked Haley on her education vision as, quote, "no real plan to improve our nation's public schools, address youth mental health, or keep guns out of our schools."\par \par \uc2 While Haley appears to have found a growing following, former New Jersey governor \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  is trying to secure a lane of his own.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE\uc2 , (R) PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: I'm appealing to those people who are in that middle, that alternative folks -- the folks who are looking for an alternative, and also for the folks who would never vote for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: One Republican governor not in the race sounded like a candidate and raised some eyebrows today when he returned to the theme that he ran on -- parental involvement in education.\par \par \uc2 GOV. GLENN YOUNGKIN, (R) VIRGINIA: Parents have a fundamental right to make the decisions in their children's lives, not just in education but in their care and upbringing.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL (on camera): Youngkin added, leaders must jolt the educational system, needing to do more and do it faster for the sake of today's students. And Shannon, just want to add that FOX News has confirmed South Dakota Governor \uc2 Kristi Noem\uc2  is expected to endorse former President Trump tomorrow at a rally in her home state. A second source saying that would really be the only reason for Mr. Trump to go to South Dakota, a conservative state, at this point in the race.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Thanks for the tip. We will watch for that. Thanks, Mike.\par \par \uc2 By the way, Governor Youngkin will be my guest on FOX NEWS SUNDAY exclusively this weekend. We are doing a deep dive on education.\par \par \uc2 And please join \uc2 Stuart Varney\uc2 , Dana Perino, and Univision's Ilia Calderon as they moderate the second Republican presidential debate on FOX Business, September 27th, from the Ronald Reagan Presidential Foundation and Institute in Simi Valley, California.\par \par \uc2 A policy notifying parents if their children seek to change gender identities is on hold tonight in one southern California school district. Officials in Chino Valley say parents have a right to know what is going on with their students, but California's attorney general disagrees, at least on that issue. National correspondent William La Jeunesse reports from Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL MALDONADO, PARENT: It's my child. It's not the state's. It's not the city. It's not the school district. It's my child. I'm the one that has ultimate power over those decisions that that child has. I'm the one that raises them, not the government.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM LA JEUNESSE, FOX NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Another showdown in California between a school board and the state over a parent's right to know if a child is changing their gender identity at school.\par \par \uc2 TRAVIS MOUGEOTTE, ROCKLIN TEACHERS PROFESSIONAL ASSOCIATION: We're here to stand up against a board policy, or a proposed amendment to a board policy that will violate state law, that will risk student safety and essentially erode any trust we have with students in the classroom.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: This district near Sacramento becomes the fourth statewide to approve a policy requiring schools to inform parents within three days if their child identifies as transgender, changes pronouns, uses a bathroom, or joins a sports program that does not align with their biological sex.\par \par \uc2 State Attorney General \uc2 Rob Bonta\uc2  argues the policy presents a clear and present danger to some students from their own parents.\par \par \uc2 ROB BONTA, (D) CALIFORNIA ATTORNEY GENERAL: It is children who would be the loser from this policy, and that is why I am moving in to protect children from being hurt, from being harmed.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: Wednesday, a judge temporarily blocked the policy in a district near Los Angeles, angering the board that approved it.\par \par \uc2 SONJA SHAW, CHINO VALLEY SCHOOL BOARD PRESIDENT: Bonta said we don't want you as the parents involved. I think is the inflection point. Parents need to wake up and realize the state wants control of their children.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: Nationwide, five states require parental notification, six promote but do not require it. The remainder do not mandate staff inform parents.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE (on camera): Tonight, a district in Orange County will try to reach a middle ground -- a school must inform parents unless students 12 or older object or a school principal believes that child is in danger by telling a parent. Shannon?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: William La Jeunesse, thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 A push to increase domestic production of items like electric cars, semiconductor chips, and other new technology has created a rising demand for manufacturing workers. Tonight, my colleague Bret Baier takes a look at what is being done to address those job gaps.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Thanks, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 In our last piece on the need for more manufacturing employees, we looked at some of the factors causing the increase in demand. Tonight, we'll explain efforts to fill some of those gaps with a younger generation of workers.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 RICHARD PETTY, FORMER NASCAR DRIVER: You can make a decent living out of it, and you will always have a job.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: NASCAR Hall of Fame driver Richard Petty is partnering with motorcycle builder Billy Lane to get more kids interested in their expertise.\par \par \uc2 BILLY LANE, CUSTOM MOTORCYCLE BUILDER: They can step outside themselves, tackle something they have never done before, challenge themselves and succeed at it. So I think that's one of the lessons we will teach them along with this skill is the fact that this is the path to success.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Petty has won more NASCAR cup series than any other driver. He says he could not have done it without the people who built his cars. Now he and Lane are working with students at Minneapolis public schools and Lakeville North to help them build go carts as part of their school curriculum.\par \par \uc2 PETTY: There wouldn't have been a Richard Petty race car driver if it hadn't been for the kids who we are trying to get to.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The build off is part of Northern Tool and Equipment's Tools for the Trades program. The company donates the engines that will be used in the go carts. \uc2 Northern Tool\uc2  hopes the effort gets young people to fill in some of the shortages across the manufacturing industry.\par \par \uc2 WADE KOTULA, NORTHERN TOOL: We work with people in the trades every single day and we see it playing out firsthand. And it is a concern for us. So that's why we are trying to do our part to take a step in the right direction to reverse this trend.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The number of manufacturing workers between the ages of 45 to 54 has fallen slightly in recent years. Younger workers have begun to help fill that gap, with those between the ages of 20 and 34 increasing slightly in the last decade.\par \par \uc2 Are you seeing more schools going to a trade school? Are you seeing more states saying we need this option?\par \par \uc2 JAY TIMMONS\uc2 , \uc2 NATIONAL ASSOCIATION OF MANUFACTURERS\uc2  CEO: You are starting to see more of that. This is shop class supercharged. This is really saying, hey, if you want to so he what manufacturing is like, come take this class and be a part of this potential opportunity.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Teachers such as Zachery (ph) Humphrey (ph) say having specialized classes is important for schools, while having the resources and mentorship could be life-changing for students.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: That's a key aspect to a young person's life. This is going to be something that they are going to remember forever.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: About 220 students at Minneapolis public schools take automotive classes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We're getting credits, we're building credits while we are in these classes that are going to transfer on to college. So it's preparing us for our future. Having the teachers and the connections that we do.\par \par \uc2 ASH RASCHE, STUDENT: Being in the program, it sparked an interest in me as well, which has made me want to do college as well.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: How do we as a country get more people to go into trade work, schools, and make that attractive? Is it rising salaries? What is it?\par \par \uc2 TIMMONS: So, right now in manufacturing, we pay more than any other sector of the economy. And in fact, the average salary is six digits. So that is a pretty substantial salary, and getting that message out.\par \par \uc2 If you are thinking about curing that next disease or you're thinking about that next probe into space that's going to teach us things that we need to know about our universe, or if you are trying to solve the climate crisis, all of those things can only be done through manufacturing, because we are the innovators and we're the ones that come up with the solution.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER (on camera): The students have their first day of school this week and will soon begin work on their go-cart build. They will race the finished cars in June.\par \par \uc2 Shannon, we will send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I love that. All right, thank you, Bret.\par \par \uc2 Republican presidential candidates are taking aim at each other and President Biden. The panel is up next.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY, (R) PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  thinks that he is going to run and win this election and then he is going to hand it off to a President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , which should scare all of us.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE\uc2 , (R) PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: This is what the Republican party is facing -- drip, drip, drip on the guy who is the frontrunner, which is why he cannot be the nominee. And if he is the nominee, he will not beat \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 VIVEK RAMASWAMY\uc2 , (R) PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: I think this is a fundamental ideological divide in the GOP, and we should have an open debate about it. The Karl Rove, John Bolton, Nikki Haley, \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  wing of the party does have one set of views.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY, (R) PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: If \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  thinks I'm being a MAGA extremist for being a Mom for Liberty, then count me as one of them, because we have to be extreme when it comes to our kids.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Let's bring in our panel, Byron York, chief political correspondent of "The Washington Examiner," Marie Harf, former State Department spokeswoman under President \uc2 Obama\uc2 , and Josh Kraushaar, FOX News Radio political analyst. All right, welcome to all of you.\par \par \uc2 You heard everybody's take going after each other and going after President Biden. But I want to put up this new polling that was out today that shows head-to-head Nikki Haley has the strongest chance, at least according to this poll, against President Biden. As Mike Emanuel reported, she was the only one outside the margin of error with a six-point lead.\par \par \uc2 Marie, she came off of that debate and got a lot of attention. The snap poll showed that people were very impressed with her. She likes to dig into policy.\par \par \uc2 MARIE HARF, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: She does. And she was very comfortable in that debate. I think she had the best night of anyone on that stage, and the polls are bearing that out. There are a lot of Democrats who would tell you that she is probably the candidate they're most scared of. They will also tell you she is probably not going to be the nominee.\par \par \uc2 I think that Nikki Haley has a long political career ahead of her. She may be running for vice president right now. And look, national polls in any presidential election, particularly at this point, I don't put a lot of stock in them. But she is doing well right now. She feels comfortable with where her campaign is, and she has really -- her stock has really risen in these past few weeks.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And the interesting thing about this poll, too, Josh, is it shows Haley, Pence, Scott, Ramaswamy, \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2 , DeSantis, almost all of them are leading President Biden. We have got President Trump and we have got them head-to-head as well, up by one point. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  showing at a tie. I think \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2  was the only one at a negative in these head- to-head matchups. But what does that tell you? Like Marie said, you've got to be comfortable with these numbers this early and done this way.\par \par \uc2 JOSH KRAUSHAAR, FOX NEWS RADIO POLITICAL ANALYST: The big news, Shannon, is that Biden is tied or is losing to every Republican running in the field.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Just about.\par \par \uc2 KRAUSHAAR: It's a real worrisome sign if you are a Democrat. And look, Biden has a strong -- he is pitching Bidenomics. He's trying to run on his economic record, but the American public isn't feeling it. And almost three quarters of Americans are worried about his age, and that's the elephant in the room that's not going to be going away. If anything, it's going to be getting worse as we get closer to 2024.\par \par \uc2 So look, Nikki Haley is the most electable conservative to run against \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . She leads him by six points, almost at 50 percent. If Republicans prized electability, if they wanted someone who is a good political performer, she had the best performance at the debate. The problem is that there is a lot of Republicans that still are beholden to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . They like \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2 's message of populism. And there's as much infighting, Shannon, within the party than there is between the Republicans and the Democrats right now.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So Byron, do any of these numbers move? Because former President Trump has a commanding lead in the state polls and the national polls. But in this head-to-head, it does show him squeaking ahead of President Biden by one point.\par \par \uc2 BYRON YORK, CHIEF POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT, "THE WASHINGTON EXAMINER": Well, we have definitely seen the numbers move around a little bit since the first debate. Nikki Haley is doing better now than they did before. I do think a lot of that is word of mouth. Debates take a while to sink in with the public. You have these quickie polls right afterwards. Most people actually didn't see the debate. They don't know quite what to think about it. And Nikki Haley making a really good impression has just spread by word of mouth.\par \par \uc2 In terms of the big picture, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  being a mile ahead of everybody else, it's hard to see that changing in a big way with any of the milestones that we have coming up, with the debates coming for the rest of the year, not really until January when the candidates make their first contact with the voters on January 15th in the Iowa caucuses.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And there continues to be this whisper campaign, would a late person get in under the radar. Governor Glenn Youngkin, say, out of the commonwealth of Virginia. He is getting a lot of attention. Newt Gingrich has written an op-ed saying look what he is doing on these issues and how he is getting out there. He's raising money, he's trying to get people to bank their votes early. And obviously one of the biggest issues for him has been education. Here's what he said today about his trying to give, you know, authority back to parents.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. GLENN YOUNGKIN, (R) VIRGINIA: We signed a bill on a bipartisan basis that empowers parents to make decisions in the commonwealth of Virginia as to whether their child wears a mask or not in school. Parents make this decision, not school boards, not governors. Parents.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Josh, a lot of Democrats, they see abortion as their mobilization issue. A lot of Republicans think education is the one for them.\par \par \uc2 KRAUSHAAR: Look, Glenn Youngkin is in office because of a really fierce backlash to the mask mandates and school closures during the pandemic. And look, when \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 , when Marie's boss was president, Democrats had like a 20-point advantage on the issue of education. It's now tied in some polls, and Republicans feel like it's a real vulnerability,, especially when you look at the policies that Democratic governors pursued during the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Look, I think it's an issue, I think, for Republicans that can appeal to these swing voters, the suburban voters that, like Nikki Haley talks about it. That's exactly the type of voter she is trying to appeal to. So it's an issue that really resonates with a silent majority of Americans.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We are going to leave it there, but after this quick break, are COVID restrictions making a comeback? We're going to talk about that with the panel.\par \par \uc2 And later, a special day that hearkens back to biblical times.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS, (R) FLORIDA: We've enacted permanent protections against these policies now and in the future for Floridians. And that is really, really important. And so you as a parent can send your kid to school, and the school can't force them to wear a muzzle all day.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, we are back now with our panel. And Byron, that is a man who is part of our 2024 discussion but also a current governor, and he says Florida got it right. He is standing by it. There's a lot on heat him today, though.\par \par \uc2 YORK: Yes. He comes out basically today and says, look, some people are talking about new mandates with COVID, in the state of Florida we are not going to have them. And he also said if you live in another state and they try to impose them on you, don't comply with them.\par \par \uc2 And then we saw this kind of comedy of errors at the White House at the Medal of Honor ceremony where the president takes off his mask and then he says, well, don't tell anybody I did that. And then the next day the White House press secretary is making excuses for the president by saying it was a really important event and she wanted to honor -- the president wanted to honor Captain Taylor who won the Medal of Honor, and so he just took it off for a little bit. It was OK. And the White House looked kind of ridiculous in the whole situation.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. Well, let's talk about what's going on on the Hill, because you had Senator J.D. Vance try to offer up this ban on public transit masks and school masks for a limited period of time. I think it would have been through December of 2024. It did not go well for him on the Senate. It went nowhere. But he said in advance of that, "Democrats insist that they have no plans to mandate masking again. But if Senate Democrats block my legislation, they must be planning to reinstate mask mandates once again. The bill is very simple. It prohibits government from forcing to you wear mask on planes, public transit, or public schools for the next 15 months." So, Marie, is that what Democrats are planning?\par \par \uc2 HARF: No. And God bless the senator from my home state, J.D. Vance. His argument there just doesn't hold up. Nobody is talking about mask mandates. But what they are talking about is right now we are in a wave. We are in a COVID wave in this country. We are not going to force people to wear masks, but I wish that this issue had not become so politicized, where if somebody said you maybe should wear a mask if you are immunocompromised, if your family is. We are in a wave right now. We need to take precautions, extra precautions, maybe when you are on public transportation.\par \par \uc2 But for \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  to basically say none of those even matter puts people at risk. And it did in Florida. We should take common sense steps when we are in COVID waves, we are going to keep seeing these ups and downs with COVID throughout the next number of years that we are going to live through together. And I wish that government officials, particularly on the Republican side, said no to mandates but yet to protecting ourselves and didn't make the argument that every way we do that is somehow taking away our liberty. It's actually quite a selfish argument.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Josh, you know, and we've talked about this. People are not left with great taste in their mouth over the CDC. And what \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2  is saying from the podium at the White House is these aren't mandates, these are guidelines from the CDC. We heard the same thing from Dr. Fauci this weekend. Nobody can tell you what to do. They are CDC guidelines. But the minute the CDC made those recommendations, a number of states and school district systems and other places said we've got to do it. They