butor, as well as David Schoen, former Trump impeachment lawyer. Sol, explain to us what is going on here. I think this came as a surprise to some people today that indeed the walls may be closing in and not on Trump this time, but on Hunter.\par \par \uc2 SOL WISENBERG, FORMER DEPUTY INDEPENDENT COUNSEL: Well, Laura, it's not a - - it shouldn't have been a surprise, because he's under -- there's a criminal information filed against Hunter on the gun charge. And so speedy trial act comes into play. And after a certain amount of days, the government has to indict him. And so that's what the judge pointed out on August 31st. She said to the parties hey, what's going on and today, the government said we're going to indict him by September 29th. Because we have to under the speedy trial act.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And David, for Hunter's council to be saying no, no, no, no, no, we're abiding by this other agreement that the judge seemed blindsided by when it was first presented to the court. How does that hold up?\par \par \uc2 DAVID SCHOEN, FORMER TRUMP IMPEACHMENT LAWYER: You know, Abbe Lowell is one of the most experienced criminal defense lawyers in the country. And you can be sure that he recognized what you call the sweetheart deal for exactly what it was. And he'll fight tooth and nail to try to enforce it. There's the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2  case from 1971. Sani Bello versus New York, that allows the lawyer -- defendant to move for specific performance, make the plea deal happen, based on representations made by the prosecution.\par \par \uc2 But it's going to be a tough way to go in this case, I think, because remember, it fell apart, because there clearly was not a meeting of the minds between the defense team and the government in that case, that required one of his lawyers, Mr. Clark to withdraw, because he's going to be a witness, but they're going to certainly have a battle over whether to enforce that plea agreement or not. And I think Abbe Lowell is right to try to force that.\par \par \uc2 But remember also, the judge never had to accept it. The judge could have rejected that plea, as she didn't want to do additional consideration of it. So I think that's going to be a tough battle. I'm skeptical of the whole process. I'm skeptical of anything that happens with Mr. Weiss now. I think that was a sham, appointing him as special counsel. I think Abbe Lowell if push comes to shove, he's going to challenge that appointment. It violates 600.3, the Special Counsel regulations, because Mr. Weiss is a part of the Justice Department. I think you'll see all kinds of battles if they don't allow him to enforce the agreement.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, Sol, what's your take on that? Because I mean, my gut is that it's so ridiculous that even in this court, it wouldn't be accepted, and they wouldn't be able to go with it. Am I seeing this incorrectly?\par \par \uc2 WISENBERG: Well, first of all, I think David meant to say, he referred to the plea agreement, I think he meant the diversion agreement, because both sides agree the plea agreement is off. I think, I think there might be -- I might be a little in a different position to David on this. I think they've got a real shot at enforcing the diversion agreement, Abbe Lowell in the Hunter Biden defense team. And if they do, that's the end of the story for the gun charges. But not only that, if that broad immunity provision in the diversion agreement is valid, they're not going to be able to prosecute Hunter for any tax crimes because it clearly covers other tax offenses.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, David, think about that. We have this trail albeit it's kind of difficult sometimes to follow, but through all these shell corporations and all these accounts that ultimately end up getting funneled back to the Biden family. Now, once all that income declared we had a lot of fake email addresses used in this time period as well, was the income declared I don't think we know the answer to that, do we?\par \par \uc2 SCHOEN: We do not and I don't even think we've scratched the surface on all that was going on in the machinations with the Biden family. I don't know that we ever will. The other reason I think, by the way, this gun charge is a sham, they will know about the Rahimi decision in the Fifth Circuit, that's a decision that you know, throughout a bar to gun possession based on the domestic violence problem.\par \par \uc2 So here, it's not a big reach. In fact, one might say, it's less onerous to say that we're not going to be able to uphold a conviction for saying no to being a drug user, an unlawful drug user on this application, that the right to carry a firearm or possess a firearm, Trump's that under brewing, and so on. So, I think the whole thing is for show, personally.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now even CNN, though, Sol, hold on one second is finally seems to be catching on maybe and admitting Hunter's legal woes may go a little deeper. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SARA MURRAY, CNN POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT: There is the question about whether this investigation would broaden further or whether there are other potential crimes that are now back on the table. They brought up their potential foreign agent dealing. So look, \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  now has a host of problems that he didn't have or didn't think he had a couple of weeks ago.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Sol, they want this to go away. I mean, they don't want this flying around next year during the election. Because it taints Biden, I don't care what anyone says, the Biden family got rich during this time period. He's in public service for 50 plus years. Yet, obviously, if the son is doing well, he's very happy and looks like influence peddling to most people who have any common sense.\par \par \uc2 WISENBERG: Here's another reason they don't want it to happen. Laura. Let's say that the courts hold that the diversion agreement is off, and the government can prosecute, Weiss can prosecute Hunter for either being on drugs when he purchased the gun or lying about it, because under the diversion agreement, they didn't charge him with lying and buying, but they could still charge him with that, if they do it by October 12th.\par \par \uc2 So let's say they do that, what's Hunter going to do? He's going to go into court, and he's going to say, under the Bruen decision of the Supreme Court, this is unconstitutional. You can't charge me. But Laura, that's the same decision that President Biden said was an affront to common sense and an affront to the constitution --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Oh, that was yesterday --\par \par \uc2 WISENBERG: And his own son, his own son is going to be arguing in different position. So it, isn't going to be pretty.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes. So David, they rely on precedent that Biden previously slammed, I mean, that's just kind of par for the course here and both sides do that. But in the end, I think this -- does it pass the straight face test the smell test for most Americans watching this? I mean, you guys are incredibly experienced, white-collar litigators, you understand how this process works.\par \par \uc2 For most Americans watching this, it can be confusing, they kind of get what's going on. Will this be something that ultimately as a political matter, regardless of what happens on the legal side, as a political matter, does damage \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 SCHOEN: I think there's that very real possibility, it depends on how long it drags on also. I think the American people have seen enough of this, if it's going to tarnish it then it already has. I think Hunter is a pitiful case, unfortunately. I mean I don't know that anybody has an interest in seeing this prosecution. The question is, how much of the underlying facts is going to be investigated and exposed? I don't think again, that we've even scratched the surface. And that's the real political risk, I think.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now, I think the Left has just decided it's perfectly fine for the Biden's to become multimillionaires while he's in office. I mean, they just have no problem with that. Meanwhile, Sol, something you and I have talked about before, but it's gaining traction as you and I predicted it would. This group called Citizens for Responsibility and Ethics in Washington. I love it, called CREW filed this lawsuit to bar former President Trump from the primary ballot in Colorado, arguing that he's ineligible to run for the White House citing the 14th Amendment.\par \par \uc2 Sol, this is going to be replicated elsewhere. The Attorney General in Colorado has said she's waiting for further clarification, it's ultimately going to go to the Supreme Court. What of this now?\par \par \uc2 WISENBERG: Well, I think these private groups and private citizens might have a standing problem. But I think one of these state attorney generals are even under their theory, a county official is going to make the decision to leave Trump off the ballot. It's going to go to the courts. I think it's going to go to the Supreme Court fairly rapidly. And I think they are going to rule that you cannot take former President Trump off the ballot without some kind of enabling statute and a judicial finding.\par \par \uc2 As I pointed out before, there is a statute on the books, a criminal statute prohibiting insurrection or rebellion and saying that if you're guilty, you can't run, you can no longer hold office. To me that's the application of that 14th Amendment provision Section 3 Congress passed that almost a century ago, maybe more than a century ago. And I think that's the way the court is going to come down. You can't just have an official deciding Trump should be off the ballot. And a court deciding that without any kind of factual basis,\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes, that says -- that -- the great defenders of democracy, David quickly,\par \par \uc2 SCHOEN: Right, frankly, Justice Chase said that in Griffin's case in 1869, he said there has to be -- Congress has to act. It's not self-executing. These two law professors who wrote the article, University of Pennsylvania Law Review have said otherwise, but they recognize Griffin's case is a problem for them. They just say it was wrongly decided. They've concluded. There are many, many reasons why I don't think this applies.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Sol, David, thank you so much. Now up next, Congress is ramping up its investigations into Hunter and Joe's tie. So what have they just discovered? House Oversight Committee Chair \uc2 James Comer\uc2  is here, all the details, you don't want to miss it next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Alright. Tonight, we have brand new details on how Joe, I never talked business with my son. Biden did talk about Hunter's business dealings and as vice president had his office involved.\par \par \uc2 House Oversight Committee Chairman \uc2 James Comer\uc2  today demanding that the National Archives provide unredacted emails and documents related to the vice president's office colluding with the Biden family and their business associates. Here's one example, December 4, 2015, Hunter's longtime business partner, Eric Schwerin wrote to then VP Comms Director \uc2 Kate Bedingfield\uc2 , giving her quote she could use from Hunter regarding his work with \uc2 Burisma\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 She responded, VP signed off on this, and that she would provide a statement from Joe's office on \uc2 Burisma\uc2  as well. So, how can \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  who claims he's never spoken to his son about his business dealings which we never believed, sign off on quotes about those dealings? Now even the White House press corps thought something is fishy here today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: How do you respond to criticism that that shows there was no wall between then Vice President Biden's work and his family's business dealings?\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: I understand the question. I appreciate the question. I get the question. I'm just going to let the White House counsel team answer that question.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I bet she is. Joining me now House Oversight Committee Chair \uc2 James Comer\uc2 . Congressman, you and I have suspected this all along. Something else so happened the same day the White House received that email, what was it?\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 JAMES COMER\uc2  (R-KY): Well, they had the meeting, and we've had testimony and there's emails, there's more evidence of more meetings where Hunter was told that they needed to call Washington for help. We've had Devon Archer testify and say that. There's evidence that points that the \uc2 Burisma\uc2  executives were squeezing \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  to call Washington for help.\par \par \uc2 So we know that \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  was communicating with the White House, this email that you just posted was one of many, and we believe there are more that the Archives are sitting on, where the Biden, the \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 , legal team, the Hunter Biden PR people, that \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's shady characters who were paying him, were communicating through him to his father, back and forth, there was no wall between \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and his son \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  and his shady business dealings.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: So the meeting among the \uc2 Burisma\uc2  executives happening, clearly, they needed a lifeline of some sort or some type of reassurance. And it's your contention or supposition at this point, that at that point, Hunter kicked in, got word through Schwerin to the comms director, comms staffer at the White House. And then she sought to reassure saying, yes, the vice president has signed off on these quotes. That's what you're seeing so far.\par \par \uc2 COMER\uc2 : Yes. The \uc2