any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Don't worry, we'll do it together.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Can I do the show if I'm not Jewish?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Well, good question.\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL LEGAL ANALYST: But if you are Roman --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: OK, we got to stop.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: All right, we're going to go away now. OK, that's it for us. Have a great night.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Good evening, welcome to Washington, I'm Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, the Special Counsel in the Hunter Biden investigation has told a judge today, he plans to indict \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  before September 29th. It's the latest outgrowth of the collapse of a plea deal that would have allowed the president's son to escape punishment for a gun charge.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent David Spunt has details tonight. Good evening, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Bret, good evening. You know, this was tucked into the third page as kind of a surprise nugget in this court filing, this routine court filing filed just a few hours ago, Special Counsel David Weiss plans to indict the first son before the 29th of September. The exact charge is unclear. This update appeared on the docket for the pending felony gun charge, Hunter is accused of misdemeanor lying on a federal gun form when checking no to a question about substance abuse.\par \par \uc2 One of Weiss's top prosecutors Leo Wise wrote the following to judge Maryellen Noreika, thus, the Speedy Trial Act requires that the government obtain the return of an indictment by a grand jury by Friday, September 29th, at the earliest. The government intends to seek the return of an indictment in this case before that date.\par \par \uc2 As part of the plea agreement that fell apart on July 26th in Delaware, Hunter would have entered into a diversion agreement that would have helped him avoid jail time on this gun charge in return for Biden's promise not to buy another gun and to consent to entry into a background check system.\par \par \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's attorney say the diversion agreement was signed. And they will act like it's still in effect, Bret, writing late this afternoon, Biden has and will continue to follow the agreement which the U.S. attorney's office agreed and signed and informed the court on July 20th that the probation office had agreed to and had recommended to be put into effect.\par \par \uc2 Bret, also unclear where this indictment would be filed. And remember the separate tax violations are also on the table according to Special Counsel David Weiss. Technically he dismissed those last month but that means likely he's going to refile them. He indicated he could refile those in California where Hunter Biden lives or here in Washington, D.C.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. So, there's two tracks, what's happening there legally and then the investigation continues on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK. David, thank you.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: You bet, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We're getting mixed messages from President Biden in the White House concerning COVID. The first lady as we told you has tested positive. President Biden continues to test negative. The White House says he is masking now indoors.\par \par \uc2 But today, he entered an event without a face covering. This comes as many opponents of masking are getting riled up about the possibility of new mandates across the country.\par \par \uc2 White House Correspondent Jacqui Heinrich has that story tonight live from the North Lawn. Good evening, Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret. The president will test again before departing for the G20 in India tomorrow, but the White House won't talk about contingency plans if he pops a positive and in the meantime, president is taking CDC guidance lightly.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: They keep telling me because it has to be 10 days or something, I got to keep wearing it. But don't tell him I didn't have it on when I walked in.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): President Biden again flouting CDC mask guidance and joking about it after First Lady Jill Biden tested positive Sunday, a far cry from January of last year.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Please wear a mask because I think it's part of your patriotic duties.\par \par \uc2 I've been tested again today. I'm clear across the board.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): Yesterday too, Biden removed his mask to honor an 81-year-old Vietnam veteran.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: And we wanted to honor the captain.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): Abruptly leaving the room before 15 minutes elapsed. The rough amount of time approved by the CDC for casual contact.\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: It was planned. He left when there was a pause in the program because again, he wanted to minimize certainly his impact on folks who were there.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): Biden picking and choosing which CDC guidance he follows mirroring the patchwork of mask protocols across the country. As students return to school, the CDC is still recommending universal school masking even though the Atlantic reports the CDC's own studies and others they cite as supportive evidence don't justify it.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Should we keep funding these studies if the CDC is not making guidance that follows the results of those studies?\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: I mean, we know what works. We are in a different place than we were two three years ago.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: CDC does not seem to be responding to the data.\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: CDC, they're the experts.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): Barring a positive test, Biden will head to the G20 tomorrow, no plans for the V.P. to sub in. A job Harris says she's ready for under any circumstance.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Every vice president understands that when they take the oath that they must be very clear about the responsibility they may have to take over the job of being president. I am not difference.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (on camera): If Biden does make it to India, there will be no meeting with Chinese President Xi who is skipping this summit despite Biden sending three cabinet officials to Beijing in recent months to thaw relations. China is now continuing the cold shoulder saying today that plans for a meeting this November will hinge on whether the U.S. shows enough sincerity, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jacqui, other news today, the administration canceling all existing leases in the Arctic National Wildlife Refuge just as we reported, the Saudis announced they're extending their oil production cuts, what's the White House saying about the impact possibly on gas prices?\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Well, they're punting a lot of questions, Bret. The Biden administration didn't just cancel those existing leases that were issued under Trump after Congress passed a law designating it back in 2017. But they also barred drilling on more than half of the national petroleum reserves.\par \par \uc2 With the Saudi cuts, the White House is punting any questions about how they plan to refill the strategic reserves that Biden previously tapped and how that might be impacted.\par \par \uc2 But this decision is seen as a response to climate activists who are outraged that Trump undid 60 years of environmental protections in the Alaskan wilderness. And also who were mad at President Biden when he allowed the Willow Project to move forward. The State of Alaska owns these leases and does plan to sue, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, Jacqui, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Right now, law enforcement is swarming an area of Pennsylvania where a convicted murderer who escaped from prison last week is believed to be hiding. Correspondent Nate Foy has our update tonight from Chadds Ford, Pennsylvania. Good evening, Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE FOY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret. Tonight, \uc2 Pennsylvania State Police\uc2  still believe they have Danelo Cavalcante surrounded but now on day seven of the manhunt, we're seeing for the first time exactly how he escaped Chester County Prison last Thursday in this new video. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 This is inside an exercise yard at the prison. You see Cavalcante crab walk up between two buildings. After this, Bret, he pushes through razor wire and scales a fence. This is the same way another inmate escaped this same prison back in May. But that inmate was caught almost immediately and the acting ward in today explained a key difference that may have contributed to Cavalcante getting away.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HOWARD HOLLAND, CHESTER COUNTY, PENNSYLVANIA PRISON BOARD: The tower officer did not observe nor report the escape. The escape was discovered as part of the inmate counts that occurred when inmates come in from the exercise yard. We'll be taking appropriate action against personnel based on the results of that investigation.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FOY: Today, troopers say a resident saw Cavalcante last night within their current search area. Authorities moved their perimeter south after the convicted killer was seen here at this botanical garden twice Monday night. Cavalcante is convicted of murdering his girlfriend in 2021 and he's accused of another murder in Brazil in 2017. He's an illegal immigrant set to serve life in prison. The reward for information leading to his arrest is now up to $20,000, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, Nate, we'll head back for any breaking details. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, authorities here in Washington D.C. are looking for a homicide suspect who escaped police custody this afternoon while at George Washington Hospital. Students at nearby \uc2 George Washington University\uc2  were told to shelter in place today. That school's independent student newspaper described this individual as dangerous. The paper also reporting a large police presence now on campus and saying a helicopter was searching for the escapee over the Foggy Bottom neighborhood.\par \par \uc2 Republican presidential candidate former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  says there is a fight going on for the soul of the Republican Party. He says his presidential rivals are betraying conservatism, including and specifically his former boss former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Senior National Correspondent Rich Edson shows us tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE (R), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , along with his imitators often sound like an echo of the progressives they seek to replace.\par \par \uc2 RICH EDSON, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  chiding his presidential rivals in his speech warning of the rise of populism in the Republican Party. The Trump campaign contends the GOP has changed for the better and, "President Trump's victory in 2016 expose the massive divide between voters around the country and the establishment beltway insiders who made terrible trade deals allowed our southern border to become overrun and never missed an opportunity to play World Cup".\par \par \uc2 Pence criticized Republican candidates for ignoring debt and entitlements, advocating for a U.S. withdrawal from international affairs and leading campaigns against Americans businesses.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: The governor of Florida still justifies using the power of the state to punish a corporation for taking a political stand that he disagreed with. And one of the president's populist protegees actually advocated a 59 percent inheritance tax.\par \par \uc2 EDSON (voice over): That a reference to challenger \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2 . DeSantis's press secretary says, "No matter if he has to fight the Biden administration, deep-pocketed advocacy groups or woke corporations, Governor DeSantis will always stand up for families and children in the face of the less gender ideology onslaught".\par \par \uc2 Nikki Haley's campaign manager piled on DeSantis and Ramaswamy. Saying in a memo, "Some run as Trump-lite, while others throw rocks at Trump to win points in the media". Ramaswamy accused Haley and Pence of comprising a misguided wing of the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 VIVEK RAMASWAMY\uc2  (R), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: This foreign policy and military establishment. We have failed when it comes to Homeland defenses.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 EDSON (on camera): Several Colorado voters are also fighting the Republican front runner. They filed a federal lawsuit today to block Trump from the state's election ballots, claiming the former president violated the 14th Amendment of the Constitution in supporting an insurrection against the United States, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, Rich. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well, the district attorney in Fulton County, Georgia wants to try former President Trump and his 18 co-defendants at the same time. The D.A. wants the election interference trial to begin next month.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Bryan Llenas reports some big decisions have to be made before that. good evening, Bryan.\par \par \uc2 BRYAN LLENAS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Bret, good evening. In the first televised hearing in the Georgia 2020 election fraud racketeering case, Fulton County prosecutors proposed trying all 19 defendants, including former President Trump at the same time beginning on October 23rd. Prosecutors say the trial will last at least four months, not including jury selection. And 150 witnesses will be called to testify.\par \par \uc2 Judge Scott McAfee has not ruled on the issue yet but says he was, "very skeptical trial could start 48 days from now".\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JUDGE SCOTT MCAFEE, FULTON COUNTY, GEORGIA SUPERIOR COURT: It just seems a bit unrealistic to think that we can handle all 19 in 40 something days. That's my initial reaction.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS (voice over): Today, codefendant former White House Chief of Staff \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  filed a motion asking Judge McAfee to stop all state proceedings against him until the district courts decides whether he can move his case to federal court which optimistically the judge says could take six months.\par \par \uc2 Judge McAfee says in October trial date means defendants could find themselves being tried twice for the same crime.\par \par \uc2 MCAFEE: Where does that leave us in the middle of a jury trial? Is double jeopardy attached. Have you now risked your entire prosecution because this case has now been removed to federal court? And we've sworn in a jury and it's been presenting evidence against all these other codefendants.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS (voice over): Judge McAfee did grant attorneys and codefendants \uc2 Sidney Powell\uc2  and Kenneth Chesebro a speedy trial date of October 23rd as is their right under Georgia law. The pair will be tried together. The judge denied the request for their cases to be tried separately from one another.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 LLENAS (on camera): Six other codefendants, Bret, including former President Trump have asked for their cases to be severed too. Prosecutors say if they try all 19 defendants separately, it will take four months per trial. And 150 witnesses would have to testify 19 times, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: 150 witnesses 19 times. All right, Bryan, thank you.\par \par \uc2 A federal judge has ruled former President Trump is liable for damages in another lawsuit from writer E. Jean Carroll. She successfully sued the former president for defamation earlier this year. A jury awarded her then $5 million.\par \par \uc2 Today's ruling means a second civil trial will concern only how much more the former president will have to pay Carroll.\par \par \uc2 Fears of another \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  interest rate hikes sent stocks down today, the Dow lost 199, the S&P 500 fell 31, the NASDAQ was off 148.\par \par \uc2 As we approach the 9/11 anniversary our Common Ground segment focuses on bipartisan efforts to support victims of terrorism.\par \par \uc2 Plus, reaction to Senate Minority Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  answering questions today about his recent health issues.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We'd like to hear from you.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY): I know you are hearing from me. I think Dr. Monahan covered the subject fully. You've had a chance to read it. Don't have anything to add to it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Senators weigh in in a minute.\par \par \uc2 First, here's what some of our Fox affiliates around the country are covering tonight. Fox 9 in Minneapolis as three people are found dead at a townhouse development in Breezy Point. Authorities have not seen details about the situation but say there is not believed to be a threat to the public.\par \par \uc2 Fox 2 in Detroit as Michigan State Police look for a suspect who shot at and lit multiple patrol vehicles on fire early today. The units were empty at the time. Police said they are seeking a white man wearing camouflage driving a silver Honda CRV.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Orlando from Fox 35, our affiliate down there. One of the big stories there tonight.\par \par \uc2 A Florida man is facing federal criminal charges after he attempted to cross the Atlantic Ocean. In a human-sized hamster wheel contraption. Reza Baluchi was stopped by coast guard officers last month, about 70 miles east of Tybee Island, Georgia.\par \par \uc2 He told officials he was headed for London. They say he threatened to kill himself if officers removed him from the wheel. It took four days to get him off that machine.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look "OUTSIDE THE BELTWAY" from SPECIAL REPORT. Keep rolling on, after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Welcome back to SPECIAL REPORT. We're just a few days away from the anniversary of the 9/11 terror attacks. This evening, we're going to talk about bipartisan efforts in Congress to support the victims' families.\par \par \uc2 Joining us tonight for our "COMMON GROUND" segment, Vermont Democratic senator Peter Welch and Texas Republican senator, \uc2 John Cornyn\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Senators, thanks for being here.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN CORNYN\uc2  (R-TX): Good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I want to talk about your bill, the bipartisan effort. But I want to deal with something that's come up in recent days. And that is the health of your colleague, Senator McConnell, who talked today at the stakeout.\par \par \uc2 Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY): I'm going to finish my term as leader and I'm going to finish my Senate term.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So, Senator McConnell didn't talk about it much about the freezing specifics. He pointed to the physician's letter. How is he, number one. And are you concerned about leadership going down the road?\par \par \uc2 CORNYN: Well, he seems fine now, and I'm glad that we were. Dr. Monahan, the attending physician released a letter yesterday saying that all the tests that had been conducted, neurological tests and others ended up negative.\par \par \uc2 So, in his view, this was maybe just a little bit of a rocky recovery --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: From that concussion.\par \par \uc2 CORNYN: From a concussion. And you probably know this, but the older you get, the harder it is to come back. None of us are 25 years old anymore.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You know, this is a bipartisan thing, Senator Welch. There's -- obviously, Senator Feinstein from California has gotten some criticism, even from Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Dean Phillips\uc2 , over in the house from Minnesota tweeted out, "For goodness' sake, the family, friends, and staff of senators Feinstein and McConnell are doing them and our country a tremendous disservice. It's time for term limits for Congress and the Supreme Court, and some basic human decency."\par \par \uc2 What do you say to people who say, you know, it is time for some of these folks to move on?\par \par \uc2 SEN. PETER WELCH (D-VT): You know, Senator McConnell, and speaking on behalf of him, he is totally committed in the institution, and he'll always put that first. My observation is similar to yours, John. He was great today.\par \par \uc2 Senator Feinstein, obviously, she's having a much more challenging time. And when you talk about her situation, friends, and family, obviously have prevailed on her. You know, she was president pro tempore, and she gave that up.\par \par \uc2 She was the chair of the \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2 . She gave that up. And she's determined from everything we here to try to finish out her term.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I want to move to the topic you're here to discuss. And that is this bipartisan effort, really, to change the existing law. Explain. It's a little technical, but it basically as to help victims of terrorist attacks.\par \par \uc2 CORNYN: Well, in 2016, we passed a law that provided an opportunity for the families who lost loved ones in the terrible tragedy. The terrorist attack of 9/11, to be able to go to court, and to be able to prove that who financed that and who made that possible during the \uc2 Obama\uc2  administration, we were able to pass this ultimately over a veto.\par \par \uc2 There have been some concerns expressed, the diplomatic concerns by the administration.\par \par \uc2 But now, the argument is being made that somehow this is limited only to persons and not businesses and property damage that is also result of the same incident, the 9/11 attack.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Senator Welch, I mean, it's important for these (INAUDIBLE)?\par \par \uc2 WELCH: You know, it is important. Just to step back for a minute. The doctrine of sovereign immunity has been used, essentially, to allow states that are involved in things that injure our citizens, immunity from being held accountable.\par \par \uc2 And this legislation was a breakthrough on that. And it would give people that access to justice.\par \par \uc2 These corrections that we're making now are going to mean that businesses who have been injured also have a right of action, as John said. It means that it's going to be more possible to collect on your judgment, getting a verdict, but not getting the money. Doesn't it all help.\par \par \uc2 So, I'm glad we're standing up for the 9/11 victims. They can feel forgotten and the injuries are brutal. And, you know, in Vermont, good ways from New York, we had 35 families that were affected by this.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We're looking at the victims of state sponsored terrorism. 15,500 claimants, $6 billion. But it's being said that, essentially, it's running out of money, this is fund.\par \par \uc2 "Special Masters determined that there will be insufficient funds to authorize an additional round of payments by January 1st, 2024". You're coming up to another fiscal cliff. Senator, where you're dealing with the House. Is this all going to get rolled up together?\par \par \uc2 CORNYN: It will all get worked out in the end. We always figure it out after we exhaust every other possibility. But we'll figure it out.\par \par \uc2 But some of this is by design, because here we are, with 15 legislative days between now and the end of September. We should have done this work a long time ago. But unfortunately, that's not the case.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You know, the House has said that they don't want to operate this way. They don't want to do behind closed doors. They don't want to do omnibus last-minute deals. And that's something they ran on.\par \par \uc2 So, is this going to be a tough, bumpy road to getting to this figured out?\par \par \uc2 WELCH: Well, no, it's definitely a tough and bumpy road, I think much more so in the House than it is in the Senate. I mean, we've gotten really good bipartisan cooperation with the chair and the vice chair, Democrat and Republican in the Senate Appropriations Committee.\par \par \uc2 So, I think we've got a shot at doing what's called regular order. But this is a residue of really, many years in Congress of not having the committees be the ones that are the primary presenters.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Of both parties.\par \par \uc2 WELCH: And that's been in both parties. That's right. It's a breakdown of the institution. It's very, very bad for the country. But this is a new situation we've got in the House where there's a lot of folks who are really arguing that shutting the government down is a way to get to a good outcome.\par \par \uc2 CORNYN: We'd like to fight, occasionally. And sometimes that's the right answer. But sometimes when you're actually interested in passing legislation, the only way you can do it is for Democrats and Republicans to find that overlap, what I call the 80-20 rule. The 80 percent, we could agree on and leave the other 20 percent to fight about on another day.\par \par \uc2 So, sometimes it's important to stand and fight and to disagree. But sometimes it's important to be able to make some progress. And the only way you can do that is by trying to build consensus.\par \par \uc2 WELCH: It takes some humility to be in Congress, you come with the benefit of having been voted in by the people you represent. But it's a big country.\par \par \uc2 People that elected me, and I'm not sure what elect John -- people elected John would never elect me. And the fact is, it's a big, complicated country.\par \par \uc2 So, there's got to be some mutual respect here that each of us is doing the best we can for our citizens and focusing on what they all need, I think is the way forward.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK. Senators, thanks for the time.\par \par \uc2 CORNYN: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You can see all the "COMMON GROUND" segments on my podcast, "COMMON GROUND". Find that in all the all-star panel podcasts under Bret Baier podcast at Fox News, podcast.com, \uc2 Spotify\uc2 , or wherever you download podcasts.\par \par \uc2 You can also see the extended discussion on the Fox News \uc2 YouTube\uc2  page.\par \par \uc2 Up next, Democrats and others growing more impatient with President Biden's border policies.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The head of the \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  is warning the union plans to go on strike against any Detroit automaker that has not reached a new agreement by the time contracts expire next week. Union President Shawn Fain is reporting some progress in the negotiations. He says the union will meet Thursday with \uc2 General Motors\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 A federal judge is ordering Texas to move a large floating barrier in the Rio Grande River intended to deter illegal border crossings. That decision is likely to be appealed. Dozens of bright orange wrecking ball size buoys created a water barrier longer than a soccer field on a stretch of river where migrants often try crossing from Mexico. We brought that you story before. Their deployment brought a swift legal challenge from the Justice Department that accused Texas of putting a barrier on the international boundary without permission.\par \par \uc2 The disparities in federal funding to deal with the fallout from the border crisis were the subjects of debate today in the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 . Arizona Senator \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2  is not happy that New York state is getting more money than Arizona. It's part of a backlash on the part of some Democrats and independents against the president's border policy. Congressional correspondent Aishah Hasnie reports tonight from Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 SEN. KYRSTEN SINEMA, (D-AZ): Tucson sector is currently one of the busiest sectors in the country for migrant encounters.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Arizona Senator \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2  digging into the border crisis during a hearing on Capitol Hill, fresh off the heels of a White House snub. The Biden administration sending $134 million in border relief to New York, twice as much in what was handed to Arizona. The Democrat turned independent calling the move deeply wrong. But the money not enough for Governor \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2 , who is publicly blaming President Biden for the migrant surge as protests break out across Democratic states and cities.\par \par \uc2 The White House taking incoming from all sides as "Axios" writes, "Blue state migrant crisis sparks political disaster for Biden."\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Will this be a political problem in 2024 for Democrats?\par \par \uc2 SEN. DICK DURBIN, (D-IL) SENATE MAJORITY WHIP: Well, it's a political challenge.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: The number two Democrat in the Senate now trying to shield Biden from any blame.\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: It's 30 years since we have discussed immigration, and we're going to blame this president for the problems? For God's sake, how about ourselves?\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: While the White House tries to shift the blame on to Republicans who refuse to vote for a government funding bill unless it includes border security.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: Breaking this promise would inflict painful costs on the country, making troops and Border Patrol agents work without paychecks.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: As Congress heads towards a funding showdown, the border crisis is now one of the top issues that could grind the government to a halt.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2 , (R-SC): Any supplemental needs to address border security.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Will you vote no if it doesn't?\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Yes, totally, 100 percent.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (on camera): And Bret, tonight Congressman Chip Roy of Texas is digging in. He is, of course, from the House Freedom Caucus, and he tells me that while he does not want to see the government shut down, he is not afraid of it either as long as it means the border will be secure. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Aishah Hasnie live on Capitol Hill. Aishah, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Mexico's supreme court has decriminalized abortion nationwide two years after ruling that abortion was not a crime in one northern state. That earlier ruling had set after a grinding process of decriminalizing abortion state by state in Mexico. Today's decision comes amid a trend in Latin America of loosening restrictions on abortion.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel on mixed messages about COVID from President Biden and the White House.\par \par \uc2 First, Beyond our Borders tonight. The death toll from severe rainstorms that lashed parts of Greece, Turkey, and Bulgaria now increases to 14. Rescue teams in the three neighboring countries recovered seven additional bodies today.\par \par \uc2 Three sailors from Russia and France are rescued after the inflatable catamaran they were trying to navigate to Australia came under attack from sharks. The men are due to arrive in the Australian city of Brisbane tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at London from Earth Cam. One of the big stories there tonight, The Rolling Stones announce their first album of new songs in 18 years. It's called "Hackney Diamonds." It features appearances from Lady Gaga, \uc2 Paul McCartney\uc2 , and Stevie Wonder.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories Beyond our Borders tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. JIM COMER, (R-KY) HOUSE OVERSIGHT COMMITTEE CHAIRMAN: This is an opportunity for David Weiss to clear his good name. This is an opportunity for him to do the right thing. I mean, it's obvious. I have said from day one that investigating the Bidens is like tracking a bleeding bear through a snowstorm. There's evidence everywhere. So, this is the obvious thing that a special counsel should do.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Bleeding bear through a snowstorm. Well, there is an indictment coming, according to the filing today. "The Wall Street Journal" writes it this way, "Prosecutors will seek to indict \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  this month. Weiss's statement," U.S. attorney special counsel, "issued an update to the federal court in Delaware, provided confirmation that prosecutors are moving ahead with the criminal case against the younger Biden. Government have traded blame in recent weeks over the implosion of two previously negotiated agreements that would have resolved in a long running investigation into \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's tax and business dealings."\par \par \uc2 We'll start there with our panel, Guy Benson, political editor at Townhall.com, host of "The Guy Benson Show" on FOX News Radio, Charles Hurt, opinion editor for "The Washington Times," and Amy Walter, publisher and editor in chief of the "Cook Political Report."\par \par \uc2 OK, Guy, it seems like it's moving forward. The question is whether there are other charges, maybe FARA that may be coming down the road. This was significant. It was kind of buried in this filing today.\par \par \uc2 GUY BENSON, POLITICAL EDITOR, TOWNHALL.COM: It is significant. I have to say that my analysis of this is pretty cynical. I think it's cynicism that has been well earned by David Weiss over the course of this investigation, the M.O. of which has been, up to this point, to my eyes at least, an attempt to protect \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  to the greatest extent possible from consequences or accountability and to fully insulate his father.\par \par \uc2 This gun charge could and probably should have been filed years ago. It's an open and shut case according to many legal experts I have spoken with. They could have indicted in 10 minutes. But here we are years later, finally getting it once David Weiss has become a special counsel. He has been embarrassed twice before, once by the \uc2 IRS\uc2  whistleblowers and then with the plea deal following apart in court. After each of those he came forward with a new sort of box checking enterprise, or exercise, saying look, no, we are going to be serious about this. There will be accountability. I suspect this might be the latest element of that same pattern, but I'm open to changing my mind.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Amy?\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER, NATIONAL EDITOR, "COOK POLITICAL REPORT": You are open to changing your mind that it's --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Evolution.\par \par \uc2 WALTER: Yes, that it could be something more or that it's you're not cynical about --\par \par \uc2 BENSON: I'm open to changing my mind about David Weiss and his approach to this investigation if some of those other elements, the financial misdeeds --\par \par \uc2 WALTER: Are open.\par \par \uc2 BENSON: FARA and other things are aggressively pursued. He has shown no appetite for any of that so far.\par \par \uc2 WALTER: Yes. I guess the real question, and you are starting to hear this from Democrats more and more is what the team Biden does, the campaign and the president himself, and whether there's more pushback on this, whether they talk more about this in a way that we haven't seen before. There are some allusions being made right now to the ways in which the Kerry campaign in 2004 allowed the Swift Boat adds to go on for a long time without responding, and that essentially shaped the narrative around that issue.\par \par \uc2 So, I think beyond the fact that this has legal repercussions for the political piece, I will be very curious to see if Democrats start to push more on seeing Biden go back at Republicans and try to redefine --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, there's two tracks. The investigation, obviously, and what is happening on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 I want to turn to COVID. We talked yesterday, Charlie, about this video of the Medal of Honor award and the president coming out, and he was wearing a mask at first. And then he presents the award to the Medal of Honor recipient, and he doesn't have a mask on, and then does the whole thing, the presentation, and then walks out before the end of the presentation before the convocation and the whole thing. Here's how the White House press secretary described that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: The president took off his mask, as I said he would, to deliver incredibly powerful remarks about this captain, Captain Taylor, and what he did in service to our nation, and he wanted to honor the captain. You all reported that, noticed that he left when there was a pause in the program because, again, he wanted to minimize, certainly, his impact on folks who were there.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Minimize his impact on the COVID front?\par \par \uc2 CHARLES HURT, OPINION EDITOR, "THE WASHINGTON TIMES": Yes. No, it defies everything here. Captain Taylor is 81 years old. He is right there in the sweet spot of people who are vulnerable to COVID.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: By the way, the first lady, obviously, has tested positive.\par \par \uc2 HURT: Exactly. But it could be very serious for a guy like that. She sat there yesterday and told us that he was going to wear a mask indoors and outdoors until, and only when appropriately distanced, would he take his mask off. And then he proceeds to take his mask off, walk up to an 81-year- old unmasked person, and put the Medal of Honor, pin it around his neck in very, very close proximity, and then walks off, which is the strangest part of all of it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: They said it was because of the 15-minute timeline or something.\par \par \uc2 HURT: Right. And then they come back today and try claim oh, no, he was actually looking out for the well-being of Captain Taylor and other people at the ceremony.\par \par \uc2 The problem here is that it's all -- they may try to pretend like they are taking it seriously. They are not taking it seriously. And either that or they are lying about it. And I don't know which is worse.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Amy, how big a deal is COVID going to be as an election issue. Have you --\par \par \uc2 WALTER: This is starting to feel like we are seeing it bubble up again. Though in many of the states where this was a significant issue in 2020, some of those states now have Republican governors. So we may not see that same level of lockdowns. I will be very curious to see in some of these bluish leaning states if they are spread in the schools, if we start to see over the course of the fall a higher incidence of this, whether those same governors will be thinking maybe we will not have the same level of mandates and school closures that we had in 2020.\par \par \uc2 BENSON: Due to science, of course. It's going to be the science.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We're going to do it differently. It just, it depends state to state.\par \par \uc2 WALTER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: One issue that will come up is border security. You look at the latest poll about the job approval for the president, 61 percent disapprove. This is coming not just from Republicans. Here are some local Democrats calling out the president's policy.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. MAURA HEALEY, (D) MASSACHUSETTS: I am delivering an urgent and formal appeal to the federal government for intervention and action.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 JAMAAL BOWMAN\uc2 , (D-NY): We need leadership from President Biden, period. We need that leadership right here in New York state, because, you know, New York state is struggling.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR ERIC ADAMS, (D) NEW YORK CITY: We need the national government to stand up. This is not a New York City issue. This is a national issue.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR BRANDON JOHNSON, (D) CHICAGO: The city of Chicago cannot go on welcoming new arrivals safely and capably without significant support and immigration policy changes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I mean, that is a change, all of that in recent months.\par \par \uc2 BENSON: I think this is a moment to reflect on the wildly successful political stunt, yes, but an effective one by Governor Abbott in Texas to force a lot of the politicians that you just saw, not just to talk the talk about sanctuary policies, but to actually confront what being a sanctuary looks like in the middle of a giant crisis caused by their party. And I think absent those bus trips and those relatively small handful of migrants, we wouldn't be seeing dissension in the ranks among the Democrats. It would be Texas's problem to deal with. It's been a master stroke politically by Abbott, and it's actually affecting policy, too.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And I guess DeSantis as well.\par \par \uc2 BENSON: DeSantis absolutely was part of the playbook.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, stand by, panel. Up next, former vice president \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  comes out swinging at his old boss, saying the former president and other candidates are abandoning conservatism.\par \par \uc2 And, later, an amazing rescue.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE, (R) FORMER U.S. VICE PRESIDENT: When \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  ran for president in 2016, he promised to govern as a conservative. And together we did. But it's important for Republicans to know that he and his imitators in this Republican primary make no such promise today. The truth is the Republican Party did not begin on a golden escalator in 2015.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Taking some swings at his old boss, former vice president \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  today. And as you can imagine swiftly after that speech the former president went to truth social, responded, "For seven years \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  only spoke well of me. Now he has decided to go to the dark side. Why didn't he do this years before? Just like why didn't DOJ and deranged Jack Smith bring these fake indictments three years ago? Why did they wait until the middle of my campaign where I am beating DeSanctimonious and all others badly and beating Biden in almost every poll?"\par \par \uc2 So we're back with our panel. Charlie, this is interesting. I mean, obviously the former vice president is trying to make a mark.\par \par \uc2 HURT: We knew this was going to be exciting, and it is. No, it's kind of interesting to listen to \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  suddenly wake up and be all upset about populism. I mean, he hitched himself to the most populist campaign since Andrew Jackson and rode it all the way into the White House very happily.\par \par \uc2 As he himself noted, Trump governed as a conservative. And so I don't really understand how he is now going to say but he is not going to govern as a conservative next time, or he is going to distance himself from the fact that that sort of populist agenda got him into the White House -- got him closer to the White House than anything else would have.\par \par \uc2 The only issue that they really diverge on when I think about this sort of greater populism, is Ukraine. And \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is far more in line with the American people on something like Ukraine in both the Democrat Party and Republican Party than \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  is on that issue. So I don't really see exactly what his argument here is.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: He's trying to change the dynamic, because the dynamic is not changing. "Wall Street Journal" poll has the former president expanding his lead exponentially. You see \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  going down. Nikki Haley and \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2  picked up some points from previous polls. But, Amy, the dynamic in this race really has not changed.\par \par \uc2 WALTER: It has not. And \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  hasn't gotten any more popular, either. In fact, he is much less popular today with Republican voters than he was, let's say, at the end of 2022. I think that speech was also notable for being a pretty serious swipe at the other person he has a beef with, and that's \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2 , who he at the debate, as you very well know --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I remember it well.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 WALTER: I'm sure you do, is very much against seeing him get any traction. So, if he can't take out a \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , he maybe at least can make a dent in Ramaswamy's now ascendence in the polling.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: What does he call him. He says the other imitators, the imitators.\par \par \uc2 WALTER: The wannabe Trump.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And you know it is interesting, and it may be the dynamic in the second debate that \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2  has become the target more so than \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . He really didn't take any incoming.\par \par \uc2 BENSON: Yes, he had a few digs that he ignored. But you put the numbers on the screen. This is Trump and everyone else. And if you are going to try to make a move, you have to train some fire at Trump and see if it works. So far it hasn't for basically anyone. And if that's going to change any time soon, it's going to be a dynamic that will be brand new to us. So this race is just frozen in ice right now to Trump's advantage.\par \par \uc2 And to Charlie's point Ukraine, is not a winner as far as a political issue, is it?\par \par \uc2 I don't think it's necessarily a game changer, let's put it that way.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, panel, thank you.\par \par \uc2 All right, finally tonight a Special Report salute.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: He was just telling me to don't let him go, you know. And I grab him, I won't let you go.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: A good Samaritan saving an elderly man from being swept away in rushing floodwaters in Las Vegas. Arturo Castrejon was driving through a neighborhood with a friend when he noticed a man in distress. He sprang into action, take a look at this, jumping out of the truck to rescue the man, lifting him up, carrying him across the street to safety. That good Samaritan said did he not even know how to swim, but he knew it was the right thing to do. And for that we salute you, sir.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT, we look at efforts to address worker gaps in the manufacturing industry.\par \par \uc2 Please remember, if you can't catch us live, set your DVR, 6:00 p.m. in the east, 3:00 p.m. on the west coast. Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for this SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and still unafraid.\par \par \uc2 "THE INGRAHAM ANGLE is now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020230907ej9600001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Interview With Sen. Tim Scott (R-SC)\b0\par\par\uc2 Neil Cavuto \par \uc2 1,877 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 August 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT (R-SC), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: If I were president of the United States, I would literally sign the most conservative pro-life legislation that they can get through Congress.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: All right, that`s popping up in an ad for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And you might be resonating with your opponents if your opponents are featuring you in their ads.\par \par \uc2 The aforementioned Senator Tim Scott kind enough to join us now.\par \par \uc2 Senator, what do you think of that? You`re the star in their latest ad.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Well, Neil, obviously, my candidacy scares the dickens out of the radical left, without any question.\par \par \uc2 It`s one of the reasons why they have me featured in their ad. When you bring out the former President Obama, and now you have the Biden/Harris ticket featuring me in their ads, it tells you what the polls have shown for several weeks now, that I`m the most likely Republican candidate to beat \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 I`m looking forward to being that candidate. I`m looking forward to being the next president of the United States. I`m also looking forward to making sure that we have a 15-week federal limit, stopping abortions after 15 weeks. That is in all of our best interests; 72 percent of Americans agree with that position.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And you don`t go as far as some in your party, including those up for the debate this past week, Senator, who want to push that to six weeks.\par \par \uc2 But, nevertheless, the administration seems to you -- be sort of lumping you in with them. And they sense that this is an issue that will poll well for Democrats, as it did in the last election. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Well, Neil, there`s no doubt that they`re looking for some genie in a bottle, some rabbit in the hat to try to find a way to bring \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  across the finish line first. It will not happen in 2024.\par \par \uc2 Here`s what the American people are so frustrated with. They`re frustrated with this culture of grievance that brings with it the drug of victimhood, the narcotic of despair.\par \par \uc2 They want to get away from this victimhood mentality and get back to talking about basic issues affecting the average kitchen table in America today, Bidenomics leading the way, thousands of dollars of lost spending power because \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  drove inflation over the last two years nearly 16 percent, leading, as you know, Neil, to 12 consecutive Federal Reserve increases, meaning they have destroyed the ability for so many Americans to buy their first home.\par \par \uc2 It means that the average family is paying 40 percent more for gas, 20 percent more for food, 20 percent more for electricity. That is devastating for households like I was raised in.\par \par \uc2 But the border, our Southern border is still wide open, unsafe, insecure, because \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  refuses to use the resources available to finish the wall.\par \par \uc2 No, he`s actually selling parts of the wall. It`s ridiculous what he`s doing to our Southern border and to our...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, could I go back on the spending -- I`m sorry, Senator.\par \par \uc2 Back on the spending thing, of course, Republicans are no stranger to spending a lot of money, right...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: ... and debt that piled up under the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 How do you explain then to the American people that this time, Republicans take charge, they will be different?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Well, Neil, one of the things that you can look back to when we were in the majority is the 2017 Tax Cut and Jobs Act that I was one of the three primary authors in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 What we did there was, we created an economy that led to low inflation, high employment, and $4,000 back to the average family. What we also need to do in my administration is work on and then pass a balanced budget amendment that restrains the growth of our government.\par \par \uc2 That is one of the most important things that we can do. But, Neil, you know this because you`re an expert and an guru in finance. So let`s just say it the way that it is. We have to also grow our economy somewhere near 5 percent consistently. That would help us create 10 million jobs in pursuit of that 5 percent growth.\par \par \uc2 My Made in America Plan that is based on my Opportunity Zones legislation would help us create millions of energy jobs, two million jobs in the innovation economy, and millions of high-tech manufacturing jobs being created or brought home from around the world.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: No, there are a lot of very promising things you have offered.\par \par \uc2 And even in the debate I noticed you were -- you might have been quieter than most, but you -- you didn`t raise your voice. You didn`t shout. You didn`t interrupt people. But you did say afterwards, Senator, we can`t have a food fight on the stage.\par \par \uc2 This was part of a fund-raising e-mail, I guess. Do you think that degenerated into a food fight? Do you think that that is what -- something that hurt your audience, the general audience for the Republicans who want to be the next president?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Well, think about what this country wants in the next leader.\par \par \uc2 They want someone who is stable, consistent, and dependable. What we saw on the stage was chaos. A food fight helps two groups, number one, the national media. They love it when Republicans fight. The road to socialism runs right through a divided Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 And, number two, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and everybody else who`s working on his reelection, those were the two biggest winners during the debate.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Who contributed the most to that, do you think, Senator? A lot of people mentioned \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2 . And then it went back and forth, back and forth.\par \par \uc2 Who`s to blame?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Well, obviously, Vivek was probably the leading candidate who provided the chaos.\par \par \uc2 Listen, the bottom line is that we have to find a way to talk about substantive issues if we hope to attract independents to the GOP, the great opportunity party, and have the kind of movement that leads to a red wave, meaning we win the House with a larger majority, we win back the control of the Senate, and I become the president of the United States.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I thought your closing remarks were the most memorable and penetrating, where you just sort of rephrase what you`re about and what you think the Republican Party should be about.\par \par \uc2 You don`t need this reminder, but for those who missed it at home, I think they should hear this. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: If you`re able-bodied in America, you work. If you take out a loan, you pay it back. You commit a violent crime, you go to jail. And if God made you a man, you play sports against men.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And you all -- did that without getting beat or buzzed or any of that.\par \par \uc2 Do you think the party has to do more of that, you and your colleagues have to do more of that?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Well, if we want to be the majority party in 2024 at the end of the election, we better go where we`re not invited. We have got to talk to voters that have not traditionally voted for us in the past, and we also have to win back voters who have left the party in the last few years.\par \par \uc2 We can do all that by talking about the goodness of America. America is not in decline. We are in a Biden-led retreat. There`s a big difference. The good American people are strong. They are healthy. They lean into the next future, the best future for their kids.\par \par \uc2 The government is the problem.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: That`s where the failure is being -- is happening.\par \par \uc2 If we get that right, we have another American century just about to start. If we get that wrong, it may take us a century to recover from the challenges that will be created.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You were not among your colleagues who felt this way, but a couple said they would have a problem supporting \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  if he were the nominee.\par \par \uc2 Assuming you`re comfortable with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in that role, is there anyone else among those who were with you on that debate stage with whom you might have a problem being the nominee?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Well, the good news is, I will be the nominee of our party.\par \par \uc2 And, therefore, I`m excited about signing the pledge that I will support the 2024 Republican nominee. I think that`s really what I`m trying to do...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: ... is to remind America that America can do for anyone what she`s done for me.\par \par \uc2 If we refocus on restoring hope and creating opportunities and protecting the America we love, we will have another American century. If we focus on the rearview mirror, and not the windshield of America`s future, we won`t get there.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: The rearview mirror means relitigating the last election, is what you`re saying?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: The last election and, frankly, we also have too many people who spend so much time in the past...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: ... that they forget that the next generation is more concerned about the future.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But if any one of those others, including Nikki Haley, your fellow South Carolinian, were to get the nomination, and you did not, you would be OK with that, even if that included \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: I can`t imagine that happening. So, of course, I would be OK with that theoretically.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: But the bottom line is, simply, I look forward to being the nominee.\par \par \uc2 And, Neil, I`m going to ask you for your vote too. God bless you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, very good seeing.\par \par \uc2 And I didn`t ask you any questions about your spot-on Mr. T impression. That will be for the next interview.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Well, and you didn`t ask about UFOs. I want to thank you for that as well.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, I just ran out of time. It was on that -- my next one here.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But, Senator, really, very good seeing you again. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Take care, Neil, have a good one.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Senator Tim Scott.\par \par \uc2 He does an outstanding Mr. T impression, if you wanted to know.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, Inc.  Copyright CQ-2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020230826ej8p0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Donald Trump Mug Shot Would Be First For U.S. President; Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  To Surrender In Georgia Election Case; Republican Presidential Debate Recap And Look Ahead; Biden Team Reaction To Republican Presidential Debate; Putin Says Prigozhin Made "Serious Mistakes"; Mourners Gather Outside Wagner Group Headquarters; Assessing \uc2 Wagner Group\uc2  Future In Africa Deployments; \uc2 FDA\uc2  Warning Parents Some Vaping Paraphernalia Designed To Resemble School Supplies; Maryland Judge Rules Against Group Of Parents Suing Over LGBTQ Material In Children's School Curriculum; Republican Presidential Candidates Argue U.S. Policy On Ukraine In First Primary Debate; Former President Trump To Turn Himself In At Fulton County Jail For Processing In Election Interference Case\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Peter Doocy, Mike Emanuel, Jonathan Turley, Bryan  Llenas, Jennifer Griffin, CB Cotton, Griff Jenkins, Aishah Hasnie \par \uc2 7,528 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 August 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Greg is on the ball crew.\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: That is true and at tennis too.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: We got "PRIMETIME" tonight. \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  on Air Force Two. There is more, so tune in at 8:00. That's it for tonight. Have a good one.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Good evening. I'm Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, we're following two major stories. We have reaction to last night's first debate among Republican presidential candidates.\par \par \uc2 But first, we begin with former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  expected to surrender tonight to officials at Georgia's Fulton County Jail later this evening. The president facing charges over what the prosecution says was his attempt to overturn the 2020 election results in that state.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Bryan Llenas is waiting for the former president in Atlanta. Good evening, Bryan.\par \par \uc2 BRYAN LLENAS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Bret, good evening. In about an hour, former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  will land in Atlanta then his motorcade will make its way to the Fulton County Jail behind me where inside we're told -- well, outside, it has actually a hard security lockdown outside here.\par \par \uc2 Inside, the sheriff says that he will be treated just like any other criminal defendant. He'll be fingerprinted. He'll surrender and yes, it's anticipated that he will take the first presidential mug shot in history.\par \par \uc2 Outside the jail today, over a hundred or about a hundred Trump supporters rallied outside the jail behind barricades in front of dozens of sheriffs, deputies.\par \par \uc2 Today, Trump also shook up his legal team hiring Steve Sadow as his new lead attorney, a veteran Atlanta based criminal defense attorney tonight.\par \par \uc2 Sadow's first legal move was to oppose District Attorney \uc2 Fani Willis\uc2 's proposed new trial date of October 23rd. The D.A. wants to try all 19 defendants nearly five months sooner than she originally proposed in response to a request for a speedy trial by co-defendant Kenneth Chesebro. Tonight, the judge agreed to the October speedy trial date but only for Chesebro, effectively severing his case from the other 18.\par \par \uc2 Back in Washington, Congress is now investigating D.A. Willis. Today, House Judiciary Chairman Jim Jordan launched a probe into her Trump investigation. Jordan is demanding documents as he looks into whether the D.A. spent any federal dollars and whether she coordinated with special counsel Jack Smith's investigation.\par \par \uc2 In a letter to the D.A., Jordan justified the probe this way, "Given the weighty federal interests at stake, the committee is conducting oversight of this matter to determine whether any legislative reforms are appropriate or necessary".\par \par \uc2 11 of Trumps 18 co-defendants have now surrendered, including former White House Chief of Staff \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2 . The deadline to surrender is tomorrow at noon. Tonight, the former president will be released on $200,000 bond.\par \par \uc2 And Bret, the former president says that he will surrender at 7:30 tonight at this jail, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Bryan Llenas in Atlanta. Bryan, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in \uc2 George Washington University Law School\uc2  Professor Jonathan Turley. Good evening, Jonathan.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN TURLEY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Good evening.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You know, this is a little different. This is a processing that is handled different. And it looks like a mug shot is going to happen here for the former president of the United States.\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: It does, and this is going to be particularly demeaning process for the president. Because of that, there's going to be a mug shot, there's going to be bond, it's going to have all the elements of the criminal process, some of which he was spared in the earlier indictments.\par \par \uc2 And in the end, it's going to, I think, reaffirm what people already believe about the case on both sides.\par \par \uc2 You know, many on the left have gotten to a point of absolute ecstasy in anticipation of this mug shot. There's a type of thrill kill environment waiting for that mug shot to come out.\par \par \uc2 On the right, this is a frustrating moment. They view this as the weaponization of the criminal process by a democratic prosecutor.\par \par \uc2 And that's why I think this is ultimately pretty damaging to our country right now. We're already very divided and what's about to happen I think is going to push people further to the extremes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, you're right. I mean, listen, this image will be remembered through history. And both sides will look at it differently, but it will be an indelible mark, a historic mark, whether you think it's really horrible what he's going through and shouldn't happen, or you think it's really what he should face for Justice.\par \par \uc2 I want to listen to \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2 , former lawyer for the President, former New York City Mayor.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUDY GIULIANI\uc2 , FORMER NEW YORK CITY MAYOR: \uc2 Fani Willis\uc2  will go down in American history as having conducted one of the worst attacks on the American Constitution ever when this case is dismissed.\par \par \uc2 She has violated people's First Amendment right to advocate the government to petition the government for grievances like an election they believe was poorly conducted or falsely conducted. People have a right to believe that in America\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Now, there's this push to have a speedy trial and October 23rd is supposed to be the possible date for this tribe to begin. They've got these 19 people, former President Trump and 18 others. How is this going to happen? How?\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: Right, you had your hands full with eight people in Wisconsin trying to make their case.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: True.\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: You can double that, add three and put their liberty at stake. And you can imagine what that courtroom is going to be like.\par \par \uc2 I can't honestly see how that day is even possible. I think it undermines the credibility of the prosecution to set such a date.\par \par \uc2 What's the interesting factor here is the request for a speedy trial by one of the defendants, that defendant has a right to a speedy trial. Most defendants waive that right because time works to the advantage of the defense in terms of getting more evidence and trying to figure out what the best defense may be.\par \par \uc2 So, for those defendants that are seeking a speedy trial, they may have to be severed in order to fulfill their constitutional rights.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And the last thing quickly, I mean, won't they have to do batches of them? I mean, I don't think we've ever seen 19 people in a courtroom, have we?\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: I think that it would be a circus. And frankly, I think it would do an injustice. There are bunches of defendants here that is defendants who are lean to critical acts. Things like accessing voting machines or putting together alternative elector lists, things of that type.\par \par \uc2 You can bunch them together, I just don't really see the value of having this mass trial. I think it's going to just add to the really uncomfortable feeling that many people have about this, that it has -- looks like a political trial.\par \par \uc2 There are some legitimate -- you know, there's some strong charges here. Some of these defendants are looking at a really tough going in court when you deal with some of these insular crimes.\par \par \uc2 But I don't think that they're all need to be tried together. In fact, I think it would -- it would really undermine all of the trial to try to get it all done at one time.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jonathan Turley as always, thank you.\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, no shortage of emotion, aggressiveness, confrontation during last night's first Republican debate of the 2024 election cycle, the candidates trying to make the most of the fact that the clear front runner in this race, former President Trump was not on that stage.\par \par \uc2 Chief Washington correspondent Mike Emanuel joins us now with highlights. Good evening, Mike.\par \par \uc2 MIKE EMANUEL, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CHIEF CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Bret, good evening. A feisty debate as eight Republican candidates tried to stand out in a crowd.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 VIVEK RAMASWAMY\uc2  (R), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: As Republicans, I'm the only person on the stage who isn't bought and paid for.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE\uc2  (R), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: I've had enough already tonight of a guy who sounds like ChatGPT.\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE (R), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: We don't need a president who's too old. And we don't need a president who's too young.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL (voice over): Some of them are now trying to maintain the momentum while others are clarifying their remarks.\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL): There was a kind of a lot of cross chatter that was going on. And you know, my thing is, is like, look, we're auditioning to be the President of the United States.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL (voice over): The morning after the first GOP primary debate, candidates were out putting an exclamation point on their main themes from the debate stage. The only woman candidate in the debate called for being honest and realistic on abortion.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY (R), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: All Americans do agree that we shouldn't have late term abortions. I think they do agree that we should encourage more adoptions and good quality adoptions.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL (voice over): South Carolina Senator Tim Scott said there must be federal action on abortion to avoid liberal states offering abortion on demand.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT (R-SC): We have to create a culture of life in this country. And that starts with a 15 week limit. Seven out of 10 Americans agree with that position.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL (voice over): Today, former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  emphasized his resume.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: What we need is leadership that has the experience and proven conservative credentials.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL (voice over): Last night, Pence launched this attack on political newcomer \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 PENCE: Now is not the time for on the job training. We don't need to bring in a rookie.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL (voice over): Ramaswamy was clearly the lightning rod of the debate.\par \par \uc2 HALEY: The problem that Vivek doesn't understand is he wants to hand Ukraine to Russia. He wants to let China eat Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL (voice over): Ramaswamy fire back at Haley.\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: Good luck in your future career on the boards of \uc2 Lockheed\uc2  and \uc2 Raytheon\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL (voice over): When asked if the candidates would support the former president if he's the GOP nominee, Christie and former Arkansas Governor Asa Hutchinson did not raise their hands facing a chorus of boos from the audience.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIE: Here's the bottom line, someone's got to stop normalizing this conduct.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL (on camera): DeSantis is still pulling second in most polls, the gap between him and former President Trump has been growing.\par \par \uc2 But the candidate who took the most heat was Ramaswamy. His campaign has been surging in recent polling, suggesting candidates may no longer see DeSantis as their most immediate threat, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, Mike, thank you, more on this with the panel a bit later.\par \par \uc2 President Biden's campaign has had plenty of time and plenty of things to say about the debate. All of it as you can imagine negative toward the candidates and their policies.\par \par \uc2 White House Correspondent Peter Doocy has that part of the story tonight live from the North Lawn. Good evening, Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Bret, President Biden's long dormant reelection campaign is coming alive. Even though the Bidens were mentioned less than expected. Two mentions for \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 , 29 mentions for the president.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: We will send \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  back to his basement.\par \par \uc2 DOUG BURGUM\uc2  (R), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Biden's inflation is choking us.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has weakened America at home and abroad.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): The Biden campaign is pushing back against GOP candidate claims on climate.\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: More people are dying of bad climate change policies than they are of actual climate change.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): Saying, despite another record breaking summer, MAGA Republicans have railed against efforts to take action on climate and some continue to deny its very existence. The Biden campaign also caught this on crime.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIE: We have plenty of room in the federal prisons to lock up these violent criminals and clean up what's going on.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): Responding, "MAGA Republicans shout crime instead of coming to the table with real solutions because they're more concerned with protecting the NRA than they are with protecting our kids and communities". There was a common ground on the debt.\par \par \uc2 HALEY: Biden didn't do this to us. Our Republicans did this to us too.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): Biden spokesman says Nikki Haley is right, Republicans are responsible for some of our country's worst economic decisions.\par \par \uc2 Vice President Harris put out a statement before the president, her assessment, "No one on stage won tonight's debate. Instead, the American people heard how much they stand to lose from an extremist agenda".\par \par \uc2 But there was a winner according to the former White House Chief of Staff.\par \par \uc2 RON KLAIN, FORMER CHIEF OF STAFF, WHITE HOUSE: The person with the best night was \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . Most of them blew their big chance to seem presidential.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (on camera): President Biden already knows how Trump debates, they've done it twice. We don't have his scouting report on these other Republicans he may have to debate next year if he shows up to the debates at all. Because President Biden has not been seen or heard from on a huge political day. He remains on vacation, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Peter Doocy live on the North Lawn. Peter, thank you.\par \par \uc2 A big sell off today on Wall Street. The Dow lost 374. The S&P 500 was down 60. The NASDAQ fell 257.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the latest on the apparent death of one of \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 's political enemies in Russia, what we know.\par \par \uc2 First, here's what some of our Fox affiliates around this country are covering tonight. Fox 11 in Los Angeles says federal and local authorities investigate a shooting at an Orange County bar that left four people dead, six others injured. Official said gunshots erupted when the suspect walked in with an estimated 40 people inside the Cook's Corner bar in Trabuco Canyon.\par \par \uc2 Fox 13 in Seattle as police body camera video shows authorities storming the home of Shanna Gardner-Fernandez last Thursday to arrest her for allegedly orchestrating the fatal shooting of her former husband, the \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  executive Jared Bridegan. Two men have already been charged in that case.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Newton Massachusetts. One of the stories there tonight from WFXT, a marine mammal team from the \uc2 International Fund for Animal Welfare\uc2  is opening a first of its kind, short term dolphin hospital on Cape Cod. The group is hoping the facility will not only improve survivability rates but also enhance the research it has developed over 25 years.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look outside the Beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: North Korea says its second attempt to launch a spy satellite has failed. The government vowing now to make another launch in October. Japan issued a brief alert, ordering some residents to evacuate to safe places as the North Korean rocket flew over its southern most islands of Okinawa to the Pacific Ocean.\par \par \uc2 A court in Moscow rules Wall Street Journal reporter \uc2 Evan Gershkovich\uc2  must stay in jail on espionage charges until the end of November.\par \par \uc2 Gershkovich has been detained since being taken into custody in late March. He and his employer, and the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  have denied he is a spy.\par \par \uc2 U.S. and Western officials say a preliminary intelligence assessment has found that the plane crash presumed to have killed Russian mercenary leader, \uc2 Yevgeny Prigozhin\uc2  was intentionally caused by an explosion.\par \par \uc2 Many people are pointing fingers directly at Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Here is chief national security correspondent Jennifer Griffin.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRIFFIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT (voice over): The plane carrying \uc2 Yevgeny Prigozhin\uc2 , the man who embarrassed \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  by attempting a coup against Russia's defense chiefs, dropped like a stone, twisting as it fell, missing a wing.\par \par \uc2 BRIG. GEN. PATRICK RYDER, PENTAGON PRESS SECRETARY: Our initial assessment is that it's likely, Prigozhin was killed. The press reporting -- stating that there was some type of surface to air missile that took down the plane that -- we assess that information to be inaccurate.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN (voice over): Which suggests, a mid-air explosion, perhaps, the result of a bomb. It's not unusual for Russia to put out conflicting scenarios to add to the mystery, surrounding the attacks against high profile Russians.\par \par \uc2 The attempted murder of \uc2 Alexei Navalny\uc2 , the poisoning of ex-spy \uc2 Sergei Skripal\uc2 , and Alexander Litvinenko.\par \par \uc2 In his first remarks since the crash, Putin spoke of Prigozhin in the past tense.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR PUTIN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF RUSSIA (through translator): I know \uc2 Yevgeny Prigozhin\uc2  for a very long time, since the early 1990s. He was a man with a complex destiny and he made the serious mistakes in life. He achieved the results he needed, both for himself, and when I asked him, for the common cause, as in these last months. He was a talented person, a talented businessman.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN (voice over): Six of Prigozhin's deputies were also killed. Dmitry Utkin, a retired Special Forces officer and founder of the \uc2 Wagner Group\uc2 , and the head of Wagner is logistics in charge of securing weapons.\par \par \uc2 Outside the group's headquarters in St. Petersburg, signs of mourning and shock, and a sledgehammer, like the one, Prigozhin used to kill a Russian deserter in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE (voice over): I came here to pay tribute to the symbol of our resistance. He was indeed a symbol of truth. This man, in our situation, the situation of the special military operation.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN (voice over): The question now is what impact will the death of Prigozhin and his top lieutenants have on Wagner's paramilitary forces, who've been Putin's proxies helping armed the juntas, behind several military crews in Africa, including the latest in Niger, which threatens U.S. and French military efforts to go after al-Qaeda and ISIS? Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jennifer Griffin, live at the Pentagon. Jennifer, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, how students may be trying to sneak vapes into school this fall? We'll bringing that story.\par \par \uc2 First, "BEYOND OUR BORDERS" tonight.\par \par \uc2 Firefighters in Greece, struggle against strong winds and hot dry conditions to tame multiple outbreaks in the country.\par \par \uc2 Officials say one wildfire in the country's northeast is the largest ever recorded in the \uc2 European Union\uc2 , with the 20 people have been killed so far.\par \par \uc2 Japanese demonstrators protested a beach near the tsunami wrecked Fukushima nuclear power plant. The facility has begun releasing its first batch of treated radioactive water into the Pacific Ocean.\par \par \uc2 China responded by banning seafood from Japan.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at London. One of the big stories there tonight.\par \par \uc2 Buckingham Palace announced his \uc2 King Charles III\uc2  and Queen Camilla will visit France in September. Their plan to state visit in March was postponed amid widespread demonstrations against President Emanuel Macron's retirement age reforms.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories, "BEYOND OUR BORDERS" tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Maui County is suing \uc2 Hawaiian Electric Company\uc2  over the fires that devastated Lahaina. It says the utility negligently failed to shut off power, despite exceptionally high winds and dry conditions there.\par \par \uc2 Witness accounts and video indicated sparks from power lines, ignited those fires as utility poles snapped in the winds.\par \par \uc2 The fire has killed at least 115 people and left an unknown number, possibly more than 1,000 others missing.\par \par \uc2 Researchers in California will leverage artificial intelligence to study body cam video recorded by Los Angeles police to analyze whether officers escalating interactions with the public through their language or tone.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Los Angeles Times\uc2 , writes the top \uc2 LAPD\uc2  official says the study will be used to help train future officers on how to best interact with the public, while also promoting accountability.\par \par \uc2 The justice department is suing \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 -owned rocket and satellite company \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  for allegedly discriminating against asylum seekers and refugees in hiring.\par \par \uc2 It says the firm wrongly claimed that under federal regulations, he could only hire us citizens and lawful permanent residents. No comment yet from the company.\par \par \uc2 This comes as \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  and \uc2 NASA\uc2  are scheduled to send astronauts from four different space agencies to the International Space Station tomorrow. The launch window opens 3:49 a.m. eastern time.\par \par \uc2 As schools return -- as students return, rather, to schools, the government is advising parents tonight about vapes being disguised as classroom supplies. Apparently, it's a problem. Correspondent CB Cotton shows us tonight from New York.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 CASEY MCKINLEY, SMOKIN' VAPES SHOP: These are items that would be easily concealed in a school bag.\par \par \uc2 CB COTTON, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: The \uc2 FDA\uc2  is warning parents and teachers about highlighter e-cigarettes with bright neon colors and a familiar shape. They could easily pass as school supplies.\par \par \uc2 KAYLA MCKINLEY, SMOKIN' VAPES SHOP: The tip comes out and is for the smoking vessel, and the cap that hides the bottom is actually the charging port.\par \par \uc2 COTTON: This dad and daughter duo own a vape store in north Texas and are joining other business owners who refuse to sell them.\par \par \uc2 MCKINLEY: We want to keep it out of the children's hands.\par \par \uc2 COTTON: It's not just highlighter vapes to look out for.\par \par \uc2 KAYLA MCKINLEY: There is other ones out, like this, for example, that kind of look like a Slurpee cup.\par \par \uc2 COTTON: The \uc2 FDA\uc2  is now telling 15 online retailers to stop selling unauthorized e-cigarettes with designs that look like a Gameboy, Super Mario, or those school supplies.\par \par \uc2 MCKINLEY: SpongeBob, Slurpee cups, there is obviously a very attractive target there for younger audience.\par \par \uc2 STEFAN BJES, LAW ENFORCEMENT EXPERT: To highlight, there is other devices shaped like a USB flash drive. The most recent item I have seen is a hooded sweatshirt, but the strings of that hooded sweatshirt are a vape device.\par \par \uc2 COTTON: The CDC says e-cigarettes are highly addictive for kids and teens and can harm brain development. And some vapes designed to appeal for children have not been federally regulated and can be especially dangerous.\par \par \uc2 BJES: There is no regulation of what chemicals or the amounts of nicotine that are being placed in these items.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 COTTON (on camera): The \uc2 FDA\uc2  warns several online retailers this week to stop selling these products in the U.S. until they meet the agency standards. If they don't, there could be fines and legal action. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: CB, thank you.\par \par \uc2 A judge has ruled in Maryland against a group of parents upset over LGBTQ material in their children's school curriculum. Correspondent Griff Jenkins is live tonight in Montgomery County, Maryland, with the latest. Good evening, Griff.\par \par \uc2 GRIFF JENKINS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Bret. Good evening. And when those Montgomery County public school students return to class on Monday, parents won't be able to opt them out of lessons in the curriculum they believe are contrary to their religious beliefs. After months of appealing to a school board and a lawsuit seeking temporary relief, a federal judge just a few hours ago denied a request for an injunction to give parents an opt-out while the case continued, Bret. At the center of the controversial curriculum, books that focus on LGBTQ and transgender issues for students as young as kindergarten. The director of the parental rights group Family Rights for Religious Freedom has this reaction.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WAEL ELKOSHAIRI, FAMILY RIGHTS FOR RELIGIOUS FREEDOM: We are very resilient, and we are very determined, because our religious beliefs are on the line. We are requesting our First Amendment rights to free exercise, and we will not stop until we get that here.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: In the ruling against the parents, the judge writes this, quote, "Public schools are not obliged to shield individual students from ideas which potentially are religiously offensive," continuing, "particularly when the school imposes no requirement that the student violate his or her faith during classroom instruction."\par \par \uc2 Earlier today several residents on both sides of the issue spoke to the school board. We caught up to them. Here's what they had to say.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TARA GRIER, MONTGOMERY COUNTY, MARYLAND, MOTHER: This is about the children. They are children. They should not be exposed to things that they don't even understand. It's that simple.\par \par \uc2 PHILLIP ALEXANDER DOWNIE, COALITION INCLUSIVE SCHOOLS AND COMMUNITIES: I don't think individuals in our public education system should be able to opt out of any lessons about any of our amazing diverse personalities and our amazing diverse identities.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: So, a setback for the parents, but attorneys representing them in the lawsuit say that they will, indeed, appeal this. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Griff, thank you.\par \par \uc2 New York Governor \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  is urging the White House to provide expedited work authorization for asylum seekers. She also wants financial assistance for the city and state, the use of federal lands and facilities, and reimbursement for nearly 2,000 New York National Guard members. New York City Mayor Eric Adams responded by saying that he is disappointed the state appears to be minimizing its role in the crisis. He says the state must fulfill its duty to more than 8 million residents who call New York City home.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine is marking its second independence day since Russia's full scale invasion. Officials are vowing to keep up their fight to try to drive out the Kremlin's forces. Norway's prime minister says his country will donate F-16 fighter jets to Ukraine. Norway would be the third European country to make such a commitment following the Netherlands and Denmark.\par \par \uc2 Funding for Ukraine is one of the most divisive issues facing Republicans in congress. Only one candidate came out against it during last night's presidential candidate debate. Congressional correspondent Aishah Hasnie has details from Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY, (R) PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: You have no foreign policy experience and it shows.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: One of the biggest digs of Wednesday night's debate came as GOP hopefuls clashed over Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 VIVEK RAMASWAMY\uc2 , (R) PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: You are protecting against an invasion across somebody else's border when we should use those same military sources to prevent across the invasion of our own southern border.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2 , the self-proclaimed outsider, says he will end Russia's war by handing \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  the disputed Donbas region and ban Ukraine from entering \uc2 NATO\uc2 . The plan horrifying some of his challengers.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE\uc2 , (R) PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: They have gouged out people's eyes, cut off their ears.\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE, (R) FORMER U.S. VICE PRESIDENT: Vivek, if we do the giveaway, it's not going to be too long before he rolls across a \uc2 NATO\uc2  border.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  wants European allies to pull their weight.\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS, (R) FLORIDA: Our support should be contingent on them doing it.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Nikki Haley pushing back.\par \par \uc2 HALEY: If you look at the percentages per GDP, 11 of the European countries have given more than the U.S.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: The friction reflects a deep divide within the Republican Party over the war.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2 , (R-SC): To the media reports saying this is a stalemate, you are wrong.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Hours before the debate, Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 , a staunch Ukraine supporter, visited Kyiv alongside two Democrats as the White House tells Ukraine Congress will approve an additional $24 billion in aid. But some conservatives are already lashing out over plans to tie the money to FEMA assistance in a short-term government funding bill.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MATT ROSENDALE\uc2 , (R-MT): That is all nothing more than an exercise to try and block Republicans into this support.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: And Bret, today the Pentagon announced that the U.S. will begin training Ukrainian pilots on F-16s right here at home in Tucson, Arizona, as early as October. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Aishah Hasnie on Capitol Hill. Aishah, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel on former President Trump's imminent surrender in Georgia. It should be happening at the end of this hour, landing there. And last night's Republican presidential debate, a review of that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY, (R) PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Now it's been put in the hands of the people. That's great. When it comes to a federal ban, it will take a majority of the House. So in order to do that, let's find consensus.\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE, (R) FORMER U.S. VICE PRESIDENT: And Nikki, you are my friend, but consensus is the opposite of leadership.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT, (R-SC): Bidenomics has led to the loss of $10,000 of spending power for the average family. We can stop that by turning the spigot off in Washington.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE\uc2 , (R) PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: We cannot sit by any longer and allow the kind of spending that's going on in Washington. It's robbing our country, and it's wrong.\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS, (R) FLORIDA: We must reverse Bidenomics so that middle class families have a chance to succeed again. We cannot succeed as a country if you are working hard and you can't afford groceries.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Starting the debate last night, we talked about the economy and what people feel about inflation. More than 50 percent of them feel that it's a major problem going to the grocery store. If you look at the president's approval on inflation, it is really upside down, 31 percent, 67 percent.\par \par \uc2 What about the debate and also the former president heading to Georgia tonight? Let's bring in our panel, FOX News senior political analyst Juan Williams, Katie Pavlich, news editor at Townhall.com, and Jason Riley, "Wall Street Journal" columnist, senior fellow at the Manhattan Institute.\par \par \uc2 A lot of policy to talk about. We spent the first hour, really, on a number of different policy issue, and the top of the second hour mentioned the former president for the first time. Jason, your overall assessment of what you heard?\par \par \uc2 JASON RILEY, COLUMNIST, "WALL STREET JOURNAL": I thought it was a very useful debate, Bret, and I'm glad you did spend that first hour talking about policies. Trump wasn't there. He'll come under criticism for not being there. I understand that criticism, and probably didn't himself any favors by choosing alternative forum where he was not going to be challenged on his views or record and so forth. But the voters will decide on that.\par \par \uc2 But I think he did the party a favor, in some sense, by not showing up because we got a chance to hear the views of other people running for the nomination, how they would take the country forward. And I thought you and Martha did a great job of not letting Trump dominate both hours of the debate, and people got a chance to hear what other people who are running for the nomination would do. And so I thought, over all, it was very, very useful, useful two hours.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, when they got to hear. Obviously, the candidates took control at some point. What you didn't see is they have these lights that face them. That's a green light, a red light -- green light, yellow light, red light. And their time is heading towards being finished, and then you heard the bell. Well, Vice President Pence definitely rolled through the bell a number of times, and there was a lot of interchanges where it was tough to herd the cats, Katie. But your thoughts overall on that?\par \par \uc2 KATIE PAVLICH, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, my favorite part of the debate was actually you and Martha opening it up by mentioning the song "Rich Men North of Richmond," because it's such a cultural moment that song, which has gone global. It's the number one song on the billboard charts, took over all the charts here in the United States, and every chart it's number one. And it really is a reflection of how Washington, D.C., and Democrats and some Republicans, but mostly Democrats over the past two years with all the extra spending they have done have really inflicted serious pain on American families and made it much more difficult for them to get ahead.\par \par \uc2 And it's on everybody. Inflation is a permanent tax. The Biden administration keeps trying to claim that inflation has come down. It hasn't come down it. It hasn't come down. It just means that prices are not going up as quickly as they were before. In terms of the debate and the results of it, I thought that \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  looked a little bit desperate. He was punching up in terms of polling at \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2 , but he really looked like he was punching down because he continued to go back and forth with the interruptions.\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  kind of was right in the middle. President Trump was not there, and according to the polling that's come out post-debate he has actually made some headway when it comes to consideration from voters, and according to the polls, 50 percent of GOP primary voters could be undecided. So a little bit of movement for him and for Nikki Haley as well.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, Nikki Haley as well. I think I heard a number of pundits talking about her having some big moments on abortion, on foreign policy, and kind of hitting her marks.\par \par \uc2 Juan, take a listener to this, GOP candidates, a couple other moments clashing in Milwaukee.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY, (R) PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: If you want something said, ask a man. If you want something done, ask a woman.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS)\par \par \uc2 VIVEK RAMASWAMY\uc2 , (R) PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: I'm the only person on this stage who isn't bought and paid for.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE\uc2 , (R) PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: You have never done anything to try to advance the interests of this government except to put yourself forward as a candidate tonight.\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE, (R) FORMER U.S. VICE PRESIDENT: I think the American people deserve to know whether everyone on this stage agrees that I kept my oath to the Constitution that day.\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS, (R) FLORIDA: Mike did his duty. I got no beef with him. Is this what we're going to be focusing on?\par \par \uc2 PENCE: I'm relieved.\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: Democrats would love that, and they will win if we let them get away with it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And obviously, at that point, we had spent the whole hour before that talking about policy. "Politico" writes, "DeSantis was ignored by GOP rivals. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  expected to take the brunt of his opponent's fire. Instead he faded into the crowd, largely ignored for two hours by his lower polling rivals on Wednesday. The Florida governor watched as the first debate of the GOP primary turned into a pile on \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2 ." Juan, what do you think?\par \par \uc2 JUAN WILLIAMS, FOX NEWS POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, I think that's about right. \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , he gave you a lot of trouble, Bret, but he got the most of the time, the camera time as a result. And he had some good points to make. On abortion, for example, I think he was very resolute in his evangelical point of view.\par \par \uc2 But the real focus of the evening turned out to be Ramaswamy. And it's clear that the other candidates see him a threat. Otherwise, they would not have been so loaded for bear when it came to going after him. I thought Nikki Haley, though, when it came to Ukraine, she made mincemeat of his argument. And I think Pence went at him again. The entire stage seemed to be saying he was amateur. He was called ChatGPT candidate. I think a lot of that fire indicates where people see movement in the polls in the GOP primary right now.\par \par \uc2 Other than that, I would think that the key point is DeSantis, who has been the number two to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in all the polls. And again, he came in losing support in terms of the polls. He has been sliding, losing donors, and even losing staff. And yesterday, he -- again, it did look like he was an afterthought on the stage. He was standing there. But I can't tell you that he made an impression.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Here's \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  reacting to what's happening to Former President Trump and some of the fallout from that section of the debate last night. This is on Cavuto today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE\uc2 , (R) PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: The question was, if he is a convicted felon, would I want to support him? I don't believe it's right for a convicted felon to be the president of the United States.\par \par \uc2 I don't think any Republican should want our standard-bearer, let alone our president of the United States, to be a convicted felon.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. Jason, that was the hand raise, and it kind of was like one side of the row raised their hands and everybody looked at everybody else and kind of made it down to Christie and Asa Hutchison who didn't raise their hand.\par \par \uc2 What about this? We're going to talk about the former president on the other side of the break.\par \par \uc2 RILEY: It wasn't just everyone looking down the row. I think this is probably \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 ' weakest point of the evening, because he looked to his left and his right before he decided whether to raise his hand and answer that question. And that did not come off well, I think. And he should have anticipated that question coming. All of them should have anticipated it coming.\par \par \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2  did steal a lot of the thunder of the evening. He got most of the attention. He's who we're talking about today. But I didn't find his argument particularly substantive. He came off, to me, as a little cocksure for someone who is only polling at seven, seven-and-a-half percent nationally. And so I did think that compared to him, the Nikki Haleys and the Mike Pences had a pretty good night. And I think they were able to answer a lot of his sort of glib responses -- I'm the only one not bought and so forth -- with some substance. And I think people were looking at that, and that will resonate.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: A lot of people tuned in. It was a big, big night for viewership, about 12.8 million combined, the viewership. So, thanks for tuning in.\par \par \uc2 And when we come back, in just a few minutes, the former president is going to land in Georgia, and is he going to make his way to the Fulton County jail. There you see the airport. And he is going to be processed, including a mugshot there. We'll talk about that and what comes next in all of this.\par \par \uc2 And, later, a special first day of school.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: If former president Trump is convicted in a court of law, would you still support him as your party's choice? Please raise your hand if you would.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, there's the moment we were referring to earlier in the show. As you look live at Hartsfield International Airport, we are waiting for Trump Air to land there, the former president's plane coming from New Jersey, as he lands to be processed at the Fulton County jail.\par \par \uc2 And we're back with the panel. Katie, this is different. There's going to be a mugshot. There is a full-on processing in Fulton County. And we talk a lot about moments and images. No matter what you -- how you look at this, it is an indelible image.\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: Yes, and these are state charges, so the president cannot seek a federal pardon either from himself or a Republican or presidential candidate.\par \par \uc2 But I would just point out that \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  ran for governor in the same state of Georgia. She lost to \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2 . She filed a lawsuit contesting that election. She lost. She also pushed money into a group that funded false information about voting machines. And yet, she is not in the same position of indictment as President Trump and his election attorneys. So that's really the crux of the issue here, not to mention the D.A. who has brought these charges, \uc2 Fani Willis\uc2 , campaigned on going after President Trump and is fundraising off of what she has filed.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We are looking live just outside the jail there. There's a lot of security already. Juan, your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: I think this is the real world end to the question that you posed last night at the debate that we saw with the candidates raising their hands. And only two didn't raise their hands, as you pointed out, Christie and Asa Hutchison. But there was also the roar from the crowd. The crowd apparently, most Republicans, are still backing Trump even after four, now, indictments. This is historic, unprecedented, and real world. This is where it's playing out.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, but, Juan, obviously they have a chance to make their case in all of these courtrooms. And a lot of those GOP voters, Jason, are saying he is wrongly being accused and charged here. And every indictment his poll numbers have gone up, his fundraising has gone up.\par \par \uc2 RILEY: Sure, but we saw a bunch of people on the stage last night that I think could beats a very vulnerable \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  in terms of his popularity in the polling and job performance and so forth. And the question is, will Republicans go with someone like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  who will have all this baggage with him while is he running to replace \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  versus someone who does not have this baggage? And I think it's something we'll see. But to me, I think that Republicans might rethink, if there is actually a conviction, of whether to go with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  as the nominee, baggage and all.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We'll see. He is obviously up 30, 40, 50 points depending on the poll you look at, as I mentioned to Governor DeSantis last night in the debate. Panel, thank you, as we continue to look like at the airport and all these transport things. We take them because it's a moment. It's the 45th president of the United States that's going to all these courtrooms.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT, our "Whatever Happened To?" segment, we'll have that.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight. Please remember, if you can't catch us, set your DVR, 6:00 p.m. in the east, 3:00 p.m. on the west coast. That's it for this SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and still unafraid. We'll continue coverage of the former president. "THE INGRAHAM ANGLE" is next right here on FOX News Channel.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020230825ej8o00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 GOP Candidates Come Out Swinging At First Debate; Republican Candidates Look For Post-Debate Bump; Candidates Slam "Weaponization" Of DOJ At Debate; Pentagon: No Indication Missile Took Down Plane; Reports: Wagner Chief Killed Assassinated In Plane Crash; Department Of Defense: United States Will Train Ukrainian Pilots On F-16s; Former Trump Chief Of Staff Meadows Surrenders In Georgia\b0\par\par\uc2 Sandra Smith, John President, Martha MacCallum, Kayleigh McEnany,  Peter Doocy, Trey Yingst, Brett Velicovich, Nate Foy, Jonathan Turley \par \uc2 8,007 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 August 2023\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Wonderful friend who has a pretty good-sized farm up in New Hampshire, so she gets to run around, stretch her legs not get into any trouble, so --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: No more passport eating.\par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST (on camera): It'll be a vacay for Chicky too, that's great. Congratulations you two and enjoy your trip.\par \par \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Yes, have fun.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: All right.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: All right. Set your DVR.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Set your DVR and never miss AMERICA REPORTS. Thanks for joining us I'm \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 SMITH: I'm Sandra Smith and here's Martha.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: all right thank you very much you guys. Good afternoon, everybody. I'm Martha MacCallum back at Fox News headquarters here in New York after last night's barn burner debate in Wisconsin. A lot to dig into as we get a closer look at some of these GOP hopefuls from last night. Take a look at this and some of the breakout moments that they had. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VIVEK RAMASWAMY\uc2  (R), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: I'm the only person on the stage who isn't bought and paid for so I can say this. The climate change agenda is a hoax. The climate change agenda is a hoax.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE\uc2  (R), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: I've had enough already tonight of a guy who sounds like ChatGPT standing up here.\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE (R), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Now is not the time for on-the-job training. We don't need to bring in a rookie. We don't need to bring in people without experience.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY (R), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: This is exactly why \uc2 Margaret Thatcher\uc2  said, if you want something said, ask a man. If you want something done, ask a woman.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: I think the American people deserve to know whether everyone on this stage agrees that I kept my oath to the Constitution that day.\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Mike did his duty, I got no beef with him, but here's the thing, is this what we're going to be focusing on?\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: Ukraine is not a priority for the United States of America.\par \par \uc2 HALEY: You are choosing a murder over a pro-American country. You have no foreign policy experience and it shows.\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: And you know what?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: It shows.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Whoa, so you get the idea. Let's kick things off with \uc2 Kayleigh McEnany\uc2 , former White House Press Secretary and Co-Host of "OUTNUMBERED." Kayleigh, great to see you.\par \par \uc2 KAYLEIGH MCENANY\uc2 , FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Thank you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I'm happy you're here. Having been sitting there, it's good for me to actually look back at some of what was said last night and kind of slow it down and take a look at what made impressions on people. This focus group this morning that I know you saw on America's Newsroom was very interesting. I want to play a little bit of this and get your thoughts out of that. Let's watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I think Vivek's on a little bit of thin ice, even with me. At the onset, I'm looking for someone that can win the general. And I think he might have alienated a few people last night, just his abrasiveness.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I was all about Vivek, but you know what, after watching the debate, I do have to agree with some other members on the panel. I think the strongest performer in terms of who will do well in the general election, who will do well with the most Republicans, is actually \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Very interesting. What was your take on who stood out and who might be able to pick up some more support?\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: First, I just want to say what a phenomenal debate. You and Bret did a wonderful job.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you, Kayleigh.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: As a voter, it was just refreshing to be able to watch a debate about the issues, and that is what we got last night. Who was most behooved by the debate? I think \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . Just reaching out to some conservatives I know across the nation, many of whom are Trump supporters and remain Trump supporters. But I said, who was on the stage? Who do you like? \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  was the takeaway. They loved the fact, as one 22-year- old in that focus group said, that the work was done for \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  before he walked into the room.\par \par \uc2 He has perhaps the best conservative record of any governor in the nation, although I would say Glenn Youngkin is also in that category. He showed that. He didn't get involved in the frays; I think the frays helped him because it might have knocked some people off the edges of the stage. It might be a smaller stage, but he wasn't in the frays. And he was able to level punches against, not Trump, but the ultimate opponent, which is \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . He also showed a more personal touch which is one of the critiques of \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  which I think will help him in the long run.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, I want to ask you about that, but just to on the Vivek issue because he was getting a lot of attention going in his numbers were going up he was you know front and center with, with \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  based on the polling last night, but I think it's interesting that some of those voters said that they thought that he went too far, that he was abrasive.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: And that's exactly what I was hearing. You know, the Wall Street Journal said that in some ways he sounded like a young man in too much of a hurry, and I think an example of that is by saying everyone was bought and paid for. I think it's hard to make the argument that someone who took on \uc2 Disney\uc2 , someone say to his own peril \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is bought and paid for. Also, he seems to be trying to mimic the style of President Trump. And if there's one thing I know, you can never out Trump, Trump. There is one \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . He is beloved. And if his ultimate goal is to win the GOP nomination, you've got to get beyond President Trump. But I don't see how you do that if you're Vivek.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, obviously, one of the things that \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  was hoping to do last night, based on observing and what he said, was to try to connect with people. Because the criticism against him is that he doesn't connect with people, that he doesn't seem relaxed and emotional. Here's some efforts that he made at doing that last night. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: One of the most impactful moments of my life was when I heard the heartbeat of my oldest daughter in my wife's womb and then saw the sonograms of all three of my kids. I know a lady in Florida named Penny. She survived multiple abortion attempts. She was left discarded in a pan. Fortunately, her grandmother saved her and brought her to a different hospital. We're better than what the Democrats are selling.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: How do you think you did?\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: I think that was blunting the biggest criticism against \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , which is you're not connecting with voters. Elections are about people; they're not about politicians. And to actually hear the names of people in a succinct way, to see their stories, to hear their stories from a politician was powerful. It's one thing that former President Trump really excels at. When he does these off-the-record stops, I know he went to a Dairy Queen and you know he says, what the heck's a blizzard and has this viral moment. He's very good at this retail politics. And to succeed in a GOP primary, you've got to be good at that. And to make this about people was a huge win for \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, but I think there's still a question about whether or not he is really making that personal connection, where people feel like they know him and they like him. I know he is very well liked in Florida. But crossing, you know, it's one thing to sort of say, I'm going to tell this story and I'm going to tell that story. But do you think, based on what you're hearing from people, that he had a breakthrough in terms of oh, you know, that's who he is. I really saw his heart last night, do you think?\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: That's the million-dollar question. I mean to have a breakthrough you have to stop and the hemorrhaging in the polls where there is a 40 right percent gap between you and President Trump. I don't think this reversed the numbers in any dramatic way. Did he make some gains? Absolutely, he did. But I think it's about winnowing the field. He's going to get more time as these debates progress, because the field will get smaller and smaller. So, the longer he can stay, the longer he can rack up points. Perhaps, he can creep up on Trump, but I would just note, Trump's lead, no one in the history of modern politics has ever lost with a lead that big. So, in order to win, you've got to defy history.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, or something very big has to change in the whole dynamic in the world and the election. Let's watch this from Nikki Haley, because I'm also hearing a lot of people say that this resonated with them. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HALEY: Can't we all agree that we should ban late-term abortions? Can't we all agree that we should encourage adoptions? Can't we all agree that doctors and nurses who don't believe in abortion shouldn't have to perform them? Can't we all agree that contraception should be available? And can't we all agree that we are not going to put a woman in jail or give her the death penalty if she gets an abortion? Let's treat this like a respectful issue that it is and humanize the situation and stop demonizing the situation.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, obviously suburban women voters, who I should point out, have different views on abortion. They're not a monolith by any means, but they're a very important part of the vote. Do you think she made ground with them last night?\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Look, in a general election, that argument may go somewhere. I don't know of a single person on that stage that wants the death penalty for a woman, a vulnerable woman who decides to have an abortion. No one in the party believes that. I think she went too far, especially when the Des Moines Register poll says 60 percent of Iowa caucus goers agree with the fetal heartbeat legislation, like what \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  signed and other governors like Glenn Youngkin -- yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK, Kayleigh, thank you. I could go on with you for the rest of the hour, but it's great to have you here. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: \uc2 Kayleigh McEnany\uc2  joining us on THE STORY. And the weaponization of the Justice Department. Very hot topic in this election. \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's, "sweetheart plea deal" that unraveled in that case. And Senator Tim Scott saying that if elected, he would get politics out of the DOJ on day one by doing that. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT (R-SC), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: When I'm president the first thing I'll do is fire \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 . Secondly, I'll do fire \uc2 Christopher Wray\uc2  because we need Lady Justice to wear a blindfold.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: White House Correspondent Peter Doocy reporting live from the North Lawn. Hi Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: And Martha, the Republican candidates just are not buying President Biden's repeated assurances that he does not direct his Justice Department who to prosecute and who to leave alone.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: We cannot set a precedent where the party in power uses police force to indict its political opponents. It is wrong. We have to end the weaponization of injustice in this country.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice-over): The Biden campaign is responding to part of this. They say the president's first major bill signed into law has provided $10 billion in additional funding for law enforcement, and every Republican in Congress voted against that bill. While MAGA Republicans are blocking legislation to ban assault weapons and strengthen background checks, a record number of kids and teens died from gun violence in 2021. We've heard a ton lately from Republicans about the Justice Department cutting what they call a sweetheart plea deal for \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 , but \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  only came up two times in two hours.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIE: When \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  fills out a fake application, a false application for a, for a gun permit and then is facing a 10-year mandatory mission minimum. We're telling people that the law doesn't apply to everybody.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (on camera): President Biden remains on vacation in Lake Tahoe today, and White House officials have given us a lid for his activities until 6:00 Eastern. Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK, Peter, thank you very much. So, two stories that are breaking right now. The Pentagon addressing the likely sabotage that killed Wagner boss, Prigozhin, and former President Trump preparing to head to Georgia for his surrender in the Fulton County Jail. And that will be happening a little bit later today. Jonathan Turley, standing by.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BRIG. GEN. PATRICK RYDER, PENTAGON PRESS SECRETARY: First of all, our initial assessment is that it's likely Prigozhin was killed. The press reporting stating that there was some type of surface to air missile that took down the plane that -- we assessed that information to be inaccurate.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, the Pentagon now confirming, the chief mercenary, who led a brief revolt against Russia's military was likely intentionally killed potentially by an onboard explosion.\par \par \uc2 Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , also addressing this incident for the first time today, calling \uc2 Yevgeny Prigozhin\uc2  of "talented businessman" who made serious mistakes. They were quite close at one time.\par \par \uc2 Trey Yingst, reporting on this live from Jerusalem. Hi, Trey.\par \par \uc2 TREY YINGST, FOX NEWS CHANNEL FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: Martha, Good afternoon.\par \par \uc2 That's right. We are getting the first reaction today from Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  to the death of Wagner leader \uc2 Yevgeny Prigozhin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Putin sending his condolences to Prigozhin's family, adding that he's known him since the early 1990s.\par \par \uc2 The Russian president said that the former mercenary leader made serious mistakes in his life, but got results when asked. Putin did not directly address Prigozhin's coup attempt in late June.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR PUTIN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF RUSSIA (through translator): He achieved the results he needed both for himself and, when I asked him for the common cause, as in these last months. He was a talented person, a talented businessman.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 YINGST: The search for answers continues today about what exactly caused the plane to crash outside of Moscow.\par \par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal reports that U.S. officials believe a bomb or a similar act of sabotage took down the business jet with 10 people on board.\par \par \uc2 Other American officials say there is no indication that a missile brought down the plane.\par \par \uc2 Overnight, supporters of Prigozhin, laid flowers in St Petersburg in his honor. While some \uc2 Wagner Group\uc2  fighters vowed revenge for their leader's death. Wagner link telegram channels vowed to take over the Kremlin, if the news was confirmed, though they likely lacked the capacity and organization for any serious response.\par \par \uc2 Now, despite the developments out of Moscow over the past 24 hours, still, the Russian military continued the relentless bombardments against Ukraine, as the country celebrated its Independence Day. Martha?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Trey, thank you very much. Trey Yingst in Jerusalem.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in Brett Velicovich, former U.S. Army Special Operations intelligence officer and a Fox News contributor.\par \par \uc2 Brett, good to have you with us. Thank you for being here.\par \par \uc2 I'm curious about your thoughts on this. And I find it interesting in Trey's report to see those people laying roses for \uc2 Yevgeny Prigozhin\uc2 . That there was affection for him in Russia, even though he tried to mount a coup against his former friend, the leader \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BRETT VELICOVICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Well, Martha, I mean, he was seen as a hero in some of these Russian circles. But the fact is, he made a lot of enemies. And a lot of people saw this coming. I think it's becoming incredibly clear. This was a deliberate act. And I'm no aviation investigator. But just from the public footage alone, you can see how the plane rapidly descended vertically with a destroyed wing.\par \par \uc2 Plus, you have local eyewitnesses reporting, hearing some sort of explosion in the air. So, you know, there was likely some sabotage on board, you know, this particular aircraft. It was a well-known method for his travel inside Russia, so it would have been nothing for insiders to conduct a deliberate act of sabotage.\par \par \uc2 But this was likely some sort of payback for Prigozhin and his Wagner troops seizing key Russian military sites, and storming his mercenaries toward Moscow, a couple of months back.\par \par \uc2 When he did this, he became a threat to Putin's rule, he put a target on his back. And I think the phrase don't bite the hand that feeds you is applicable here.\par \par \uc2 The one thing that seemed to be protecting him for these last couple years of internal defiance was that he maintained close ties to Putin. And that seemed to be enough, even when he would go on social media from the frontlines of Ukraine and call out and challenge Russian authority, repeatedly criticized the Russian defense minister, the head of their army.\par \par \uc2 But it was when he stormed those troops towards Moscow in an act of mutiny against Putin, that was the mistake that I believe here cost him his life.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, because he did have some support within Russia, if the people there believe that Putin was involved in taking him out, which seems to be what a lot of people are, obviously suggesting here, does this destabilize Russia at all during an already unstable period for them as people in Russia feel the brunt of this war more and more?\par \par \uc2 VELICOVICH: Well, I think it could potentially cause, you know, in the immediate future, some acts of clashes, especially from members of \uc2 Wagner Group\uc2  who have already called out and said, if this was, in fact, a deliberate act of sabotage, they plan to seek revenge.\par \par \uc2 I think the larger question though, is what happens to Wagner as a whole. I mean, this was a mercenary group, equal in size to some country's entire army. They've been operating across Ukraine, Russia, they've expanded the Belarus, they're recruiting now in Europe.\par \par \uc2 I was just in Poland recently, and you're seeing sticker start to appear at random places calling on people to join \uc2 Wagner Group\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And so, this group right now is threatening revenge. But personally, I believe that while Prigozhin did start to serve as a threat to the Russian government, which was in our best interest, he really served as a greater threat to instability around the world when it came to the power he was coalescing.\par \par \uc2 You know, their group was operating in Africa, they were conducting a number of destabilization activities in there. They're stealing natural resources from around the world. So, I think the world is better off that now he's gone.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Quick soundbite here from Brigadier General Pat Ryder, talking about training Ukraine pilots on F-16s. Quick on this, and then I want to get your thought on that on the other side, please.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RYDER: So, you know, we're talking months, not weeks, obviously. And as we said from the very beginning, in May, this is about the long-term support to Ukraine. This is not about the counter offensive that they are conducting right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: What do you think about that, Brett?\par \par \uc2 VELICOVICH: We've been talking about this for months. They need F-16s here. We need to get them out here and stop kicking the can down the road with this.\par \par \uc2 They need F-16s, and we just got to get it out here as soon as possible.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Well, I mean, what about his qualification that this is really just about the long term, you know, military support for Ukraine. It's not about the counter offensive.\par \par \uc2 VELICOVICH: The war is not going away anytime soon. That's vividly clear from what I'm seeing on the ground. And so, I think we have to continue to support Ukraine all -- in any way we can.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VELICOVICH: And these F-16s are definitely going to support that.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Quick thought on last night. I'm not going to play the soundbite, but, you know, people being questioned on the -- on the Republican debate stage about their support. And there were a couple of people on that stage who said no more money for Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 VELICOVICH: It's going to be a big thing for the political season here when people start talking about Ukraine. And I hope that this would be, you know, something in the best interests of both Democrats and Republicans. But it's definitely going to be up for debate for the next few months here on the political stage.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Contentious issue, to be sure.\par \par \uc2 Brett Velicovich, thank you very much. Lots of experience in Ukraine and in our military. Thank you very much. Good to see you. We'll see you soon.\par \par \uc2 All right. So, a lot of movement today surrounding the Trump indictment in Georgia, as the former president prepares to head there to surrender at the Fulton County Jail.\par \par \uc2 House Republicans launched an investigation into the Fulton County D.A. who brought these charges.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan Turley after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. Former President Trump set to turn himself in today on charges of election interference in Georgia. Now, House Republicans investigating the prosecutor who brought that indictment giving D.A. Fani Willis over about two weeks to hand over these documents, including any communication with Special Counsel Jack Smith, to see if they were coordinating their investigations.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan Turley is standing by. First to Nate Foy. Correspondent Nate Foy, live at Trump National in Bedminster, New Jersey right now, where the former president is departing. Hi, Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE FOY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Martha. Good afternoon.\par \par \uc2 So, yes, the goals of this investigation for House Judiciary Committee Chairman Jim Jordan are twofold. First, as you mentioned, to establish if there was any coordination between Fulton County D.A. Fani Willis and the special counsel here, Jack Smith.\par \par \uc2 The other is to learn if Willis spent any federal money while investigating former President Trump. This is very similar to the House Republicans investigation of Manhattan D.A. \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2  in the New York hush money case involving President Trump.\par \par \uc2 We have learned that investigation into Bragg is now settled. This all comes as Trump is also switching up his legal team today, just hours before being booked at the Fulton County Jail.\par \par \uc2 Steve Sadow, one of the top criminal defense attorneys in Atlanta will now lead Trump's defense. Sadow's client list includes top musical artists, including T.I. and Rick Ross. He also notably represented the rapper Gunna in a racketeering case that carried the same RICO violation that President Trump is facing.\par \par \uc2 Now, Sadow tells Fox News today that Trump is innocent. Adding, "Prosecutions intended to advance or serve the ambitions and careers of political opponents of the president have no place in our justice system."\par \par \uc2 A Trump's previous lawyer, Drew Findling is no longer with him. He helped negotiate Trump's $200,000 bond. Trump is expected to be booked and fingerprinted tonight, which would make him the first president ever to be featured in a mug shot.\par \par \uc2 Now, also today, Martha, former President Trump's -- former Chief of Staff \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  was booked and released on a $100,000 bond.\par \par \uc2 Trump is set to leave Bedminster for Atlanta soon, and he's expected to turn himself in at 7:30 tonight. We'll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. Nate, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 With that, we bring in Jonathan Turley, constitutional attorney, G.W. law professor and a Fox News contributor.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan, welcome. Several things we want to get to here. But it is stunning to see mug shots of \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2 , the former chief of staff and also \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2 , as we saw yesterday, which does make it seem as if we're going to see one of the former president as well. Do you think?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN TURLEY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: I think so. And first of all, great job last night.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thanks, Jon.\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: You have a future as a cat herder if you -- if this media thing doesn't work out. It's quite impressive. But yes, it does make it very likely that we will see this rather ignoble moment of a mug shot.\par \par \uc2 It appears to most of us being just clearly gratuitous. Why you would need a mug shot of the most recognizable face in the United States, if not, one of the most in the world.\par \par \uc2 But that is going to be part of this process, as is the bond that he will have to pay. And then, it's going to get as they say, rather sporting because there's going to be an effort to try to get some of these issues reviewed before trial.\par \par \uc2 That could be difficult with a racketeering case. Prosecutors tend to say, look, you might not like my evidence, but I've got enough of it that I should be able to get to a jury. A lot of appellate judges agree with that. The problem is that, this is an indictment, Martha, where there are strong claims against some of these defendants when you look at insular crimes like access to voting machines.\par \par \uc2 It's not that strong in my view with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . In fact, I think it collapses if you believe that Trump believed that he could flip the state, that he did in fact win the state. And he took the advice of his legal team. That's not the type of case I think that's good for democracy. I don't think it's good to have a Democratic prosecutor pulling in the leading Republican candidate on that type of thin connection.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. One of the defendants, Ken Chesebro, asked for a speedy trial through his attorneys. And speedy it is, October 23rd which is just a few months away. Everything I have heard about this case, and I believe from you as well, Jonathan, to get 19 defendants lined up in a Rico case and ready to go in really what's a matter of weeks, is that even possible?\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: Right. I mean, you and your hands full with eight cats trying 19 in a courtroom.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: Yes. And how you would get all of these defendants in a case in that amount of time, that is a NASCAR pace, and I don't think that it would serve justice. This is a complex case. This prosecutor made it that way. In some ways, she created her own problem. She basically indicted everyone and said, let the jury and God sort them out.\par \par \uc2 And then she included virtually everything she could lay her hands on, from tweets to phone calls to speeches. And said, oh, they're all part of this conspiracy. Well, that's a massive amount of evidence that you're going to be trying to convict 19 people on. I don't think that this is a reasonable date.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So Jim Jordan and the chairman of the Judiciary Committee wrote a letter. One of the questions that they want to pursue in investigating this case, which we seem to see a lot of these days, investigations of investigations. But in this case, they're asking if she was coordinating, the DA in Georgia was coordinating with Jack Smith, the special counsel, on the January 6 case. What are they trying to get at there? What would that mean? And is that something that should not have happened?\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: I don't think it should have happened. I don't like the optics. I also don't like the substance of Congress investigating prosecutors before trial. And more importantly, I think that courts are going to be repelled by this move. This is not a good time, if there is a good time, for such an investigation to occur. They're trying to get ready for trial.\par \par \uc2 I have the same reaction as I did to the indictment in Atlanta. I don't like the looks of a Democrat pulling in the leading Republican without a stronger nexus in the evidence to show a crime. And I have the same reaction to Republicans investigating Democratic prosecutors before trial.\par \par \uc2 We have a court system, the greatest court system in the world. Let's let it work.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. Jonathan Turley, as always, thank you very much. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Still ahead here on THE STORY today, Kat Timpf and Carley Shimkus join us as dozens of Democrats band together to push President Biden further on a key issue heading into 2024.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Boy, here we go again. Look at this. A number of videos like this that we have easily all the time coming our way is just extraordinary. Just a bunch of people walk into a store, rip off everything that they can carry and walk out. This is a Nordstrom Rack store in California, this one in Riverside outside of LA.\par \par \uc2 Police say that groups of robbers hit the same store twice in the past two months. Can you imagine owning and running this place, and stacking it, and this is what you have to deal with. They got away with thousands of dollars of designer bags and other items. Yes, she's got five or six on her arm. Isn't that great?\par \par \uc2 So, police are asking if you can help track these people down. In many of these videos, you can see these people's faces closely. They're not even ashamed enough to cover up their face. They're just, you know, it's OK to just walk in and steal everything that you want. So they will be tracking those people down, we hope, and arresting them for robbing the store.\par \par \uc2 So, President Biden getting pressure from his own party to forgive more student debt. He launched a new program this week after the Supreme Court, as you may remember, shot down his original plan to forgive $400 billion in loans that people took out to go to college. But dozens of House and Senate Democrats are calling on him to find a way around that, to do more and to do it as soon as possible. Fox Business correspondent Lydia Hu is here. Hi, Lydia.\par \par \uc2 LYDIA HU, FOX BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT: Hey there, Martha. The pressure is coming from 90 Democratic lawmakers who signed on to a letter to the President. Among them, Senators Elizabeth Warren and Bernie Sanders, Representatives \uc2 Ayanna Pressley\uc2  and \uc2 Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez\uc2 . They write this, "Although the Supreme Court has chosen to stand in the way of your initial student debt relief plan, you have additional tools to provide relief. We urge you to continually find ways to use your authority to bring down student debt."\par \par \uc2 Well, Martha, the administration has pressed ahead with debt cancellation. As you mentioned just two days ago, the White House shared details on a new income driven repayment plan. It slashes monthly payments. The plan will cost taxpayers an estimated $$475 billion over ten years. That's according to the Penn Wharton business model. And the administration also announced plans to forgive $39 billion in student loan debt last month that prompted a lawsuit.\par \par \uc2 And I think here's, Martha, it's safe to expect more pushback ahead. In response to this recent letter from the 90 Democrats, Republican Representative Lisa McClain, from Michigan, who serves on the Committee for Education and Workforce, she tells Fox Business this. "They, the Democrats, want to cancel loans and make American taxpayers foot the bill. It's hypocrisy and abuse of power at the highest level and I will fight them to the bitter end."\par \par \uc2 But what we know for now, Martha, the Democrats, they are pushing Biden for more debt forgiveness. The timeline, they want it by early 2024. It's a campaign issue. They need it before the election.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, no kidding. We had numbers that we worked on for the debate. I think it's 62% of those polled said they like this plan. So people do like it when you put money back in their pocket and that's why they want to try to get this done before the election. It has been a popular program, although there's a lot of opposition to it as well from people who, as you point out, believe that they're going to pay for it one way or another. And they already paid off their student loans.\par \par \uc2 HU: Right, exactly. Concerned about the economy.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you very much, Lydia. So racially tinged dog whistles, that's how some in the media are characterizing Oliver Anthony's smash hit number one song "Rich Men North of Richmond," which we started the debate with last night. Carley Shimkus up next.\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: "Rich Men North of Richmond" skyrocketing to the top of the charts. It is the number one Billboard song. It's the most downloaded song on \uc2 Apple\uc2  in the country, and globally it's also in first place. It's astonishing. So it has obviously struck a chord with a lot of people across the country. And so, there's going to be some people that think that is a negative thing. So listen to what an ABC News contributor said about this hit song.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Fans hear an authentic from the heart hit that speaks on behalf of an overlooked America. Critics hear racially tinged dog whistles.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: "Fox and Friends First" co-host Carley Shimkus is here. Hi, Carley.\par \par \uc2 CARLEY SHIMKUS, FOX NEWS HOST: Hi.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Great to see you in the afternoon.\par \par \uc2 SHIMKUS: You too. Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I watch you all the time in the morning. It's great to have you with us.\par \par \uc2 SHIMKUS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So tell me what you think about the criticism that's emerging of this.\par \par \uc2 SHIMKUS: I think that the reaction to that should just be sort of an eye roll in a laugh, like so typical, right, that race is getting injected into something that has absolutely nothing to do with race. And if you really think about the lyrics of the song "Rich Men North of Richmond," he's criticizing Congress, which is a body that is comprised over 75% white. And he's standing up for working people, which encompasses all races.\par \par \uc2 So I think that if anybody sees a racist dog whistle in this song, it's because they don't see this song as a working class anthem. They see it as a conservative anthem. And then they make the personal leap. Oh, conservative -- well, it must be racist. And I think that says more about prejudices that they might have than anything else.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. He's a young white man, obviously, singing this song. And I also think that just as you listen to rap music, you listen to all different cultures and all different experiences and people can express what their own experience is, right? He's allowed to do that. He doesn't have to worry that he's going to have to check every box and try not to offend. There's no direct reference to race in this song.\par \par \uc2 I just went and looked back at the words again. He is critical of government programs that put people on welfare. And he talks about people being overweight and on welfare. He doesn't mention any --\par \par \uc2 SHIMKUS: No. That could be just about anybody under the sun. And there is this reaction video that I loved watching, and I hope you thought, too, if you haven't out there watch it because it'll put a smile on your face. It shows all of these people -- listening to the song for the first time, and their genuine reaction. And it's men, women, black, white.\par \par \uc2 And it's just like everybody has that same mind blown reaction, like, oh my, this is exactly how I've been feeling. I've been working so hard and I'm still drowning. And you know when you are going through something and you're so stressed out and you feel somebody articulating the exact same thing that you feel, it's just an immediate release because you just feel heard, you feel seen. And I think that's why this song is doing so well.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. I think you're so right about that video because it shows people from all over the world, actually.\par \par \uc2 SHIMKUS: Yes. And some people are --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Listening to the song, people are crying because they say -- and isn't that what music is all about? It's all about authentic --\par \par \uc2 SHIMKUS: Totally.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: -- feeling. And I think that some music has just become all about the show, all about the pyrotechnics, and this is --\par \par \uc2 SHIMKUS: There's a place for that totally.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: -- really old -fashioned kind of protest song, right?\par \par \uc2 SHIMKUS: There's also a place for this. And as you were introducing this segment and you talked about the charts, I went on the iTunes chart and this is unbelievable. He is number 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 on the iTunes charts. And then Jason Aldean's "Try That In A Small Town" is number six right now. And then "Fast Car" by Luke Holmes is number seven.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SHIMKUS: And I think that there's just got to be a reason for that right now, and it just is maybe a bit of a possible counter protest to the way people are feeling right now.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I think you're absolutely right. And that's why we opened the debate with it last night. We're going to talk a little bit more about that tomorrow on the show with an interesting guest on that, too. So that'll be coming up.\par \par \uc2 SHIMKUS: Great.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you, Carley.\par \par \uc2 SHIMKUS: Definitely we'll be tuning into that.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you. You're welcome.\par \par \uc2 So, do you remember this disastrous Fyre Festival that was promoted by luxurious looking influencers and then it turned out to be a total flop? Well, now he's going to try again after spending time in prison, after he did it once. And people are buying up the tickets. Kat Timpf has bought her tickets and she joins me next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Are not. The promo for the Fyre Festival six years ago, go promising a luxurious adventure on a private island with all sorts of beautiful people. That is not what people got who paid their hard earned money to attend this. And it turned into a documentary about how this whole thing blew up.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: There's mattresses all over the place getting soaked.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The save yourself mode kicked in, right? It's a free- for-all.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Powerful models built this festival, and then one picture of cheese on toast ripped down the festival.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, now a new Fyre Festival is in the works. They're going to do it again. And apparently people are buying tickets to do this again. Kat Timpf is here, "Gutfeld!" co-host and author of "You Can't Joke About That," best selling author of that. Great book, Kat, good to see you.\par \par \uc2 KAT TIMPF, FOX NEWS HOST: Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I want to start actually by playing this sound bite. This is a couple who paid $549 each for a ticket to Fyre Festival 2 which promised glamorous vacation and turned out to be those little cheese sandwiches that you saw in the box and the wet mattresses on the beach. So they're going to go for it. Here they are this morning yesterday.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Anything, it'll just be a really cool vacation.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Our main concern is that we don't know if big headliners will be willing to take that risk to be in something so controversial. I think we're going to stay optimistic but we're going to expect that there may be some cheese sandwiches.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: What do you think?\par \par \uc2 TIMPF: Yes. Look, I mean, this guy obviously people still buying tickets is crazy to me. Even if he wasn't this person who went to prison for being a fraudster because not only did they mention you don't know who the headliners are going to be performing, you don't even know what day it's going to be. I would not buy tickets for something if I didn't even know what day that the thing was going to be. These people clearly live very different lives than the one that I am living.\par \par \uc2 But also my thought was what does selling out mean? Because we don't know how many tickets there were to start with, right? I mean, call me crazy but I don't totally trust this guy.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. I'm glad you pointed out. I should have said that in the beginning. Billy McFarland, who ran the first one, went away to prison and spent time in solitary confinement. This is the video that he put out when he said, you know, basically it's good for everybody. Show that, yes -- this is him saying I'm out. I'm starting my life again. And this is -- everybody should want me to make money because I owe money to people.\par \par \uc2 It sounds to me, I don't know, could be totally legit, but that is the kind of routine that ponzi schemes look like, right? Where you owe money and then you get more people to give you money. And we know that people who do these con things, they tend to do them over and over, and over again, right?\par \par \uc2 TIMPF: You're obviously a different sort of person if you're able to do this sort of thing, right? And he hasn't even said this could be anything. He just said it's Fyre Festival 2. I can't tell you when it is. I can't tell you who's going to be there. And people still are buying tickets. I mean, they must just have a lot of money that they don't know what to do with, and a lot of time that they don't know what to do with.\par \par \uc2 Because I don't understand, he does -- he's not even -- we don't even know what it is he's marketing this time.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I know. So they paid about $540 each. So they're over $1,000 for the two of them to go. There were a lot of other people who were part of this whole thing. I wonder if he's going to be able to find partners to work with him on this. And like they said, are these music acts going to show up for this thing when the people who were involved last time got really embarrassed?\par \par \uc2 TIMPF: Absolutely. And some people who have bought tickets I saw interviews were saying, well, I don't think he's going to want to go to prison again, OK? But he also hasn't promised you anything. He hasn't promised -- he's promised it's going to be massive headliners worth that kind of money. He just knows there's going to be a thing called Fyre Festival 2.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Right. I mean, maybe call a travel agent and say, I'd like to go to an island and have a vacation and maybe hang out with some beautiful people down there.\par \par \uc2 Kat, thank you. It's always good hanging out with you.\par \par \uc2 TIMPF: You too.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Beautiful person.\par \par \uc2 TIMPF: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I'm really happy that you stopped by. We'll see you soon.\par \par \uc2 So then there's this, OK? So there's a live look in Bedminster, New Jersey. Here we go for another round. The former president is now on his way to Georgia. This is the fourth state where he will surrender in an election interference case. We talked to Jonathan Turley earlier. He said that he thinks that the case against some of those who were indicted in the 19 are stronger in this situation than the case against the former president. So we will see what happens.\par \par \uc2 We're also going to see if they do a mug shot. They did mug shots of \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  and \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2 . Pretty stunning to see the White House Chief of Staff in a mug shot, and there is more to come on this, so we'll stay tuned. We'll cover it for you.\par \par \uc2 Great to be with all of you this afternoon. Thanks for joining us. That is THE STORY for this Thursday. THE STORY goes on. I will see you back here tomorrow. It's the day they call Friday. "YOUR WORLD" is coming up next.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020230825ej8o00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 2024 Republican Presidential Primary Debate\b0\par\par\uc2 Martha MacCallum, Bret Baier, Brit Hume \par \uc2 9,511 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 August 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Live Event\par \uc2 FXLV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BRIT HUME, FOX NEWS SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST (voice-over): Some call it the heartland. Others, flyover country. But for the 2024 presidential hopefuls, the Midwest is the place to land. Here, there is one battleground that may be harder to win than ever, Wisconsin. A state known for its beer and cheese, gridiron and grit, and also its razor thin elections.\par \par \uc2 In 2016 Republicans narrowly flipped the Badger State red for the first time since \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2 . But it was back to blue by 2020. That election came during an unprecedented year. The country was rife with tension. In the suburbs of Milwaukee, the division seemed deeper than ever. But that was just the beginning.\par \par \uc2 Two years later the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  struck down Roe vs. Wade, leaving the legality of abortion up to the states. In Wisconsin, that meant a nearly total ban. The power of that issue drove Democrats to the polls this year. Now that new thin liberal majority on the court could have that abortion issue before it again just as the race for the White House accelerates.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, the Republican candidates will face off at the home of the Bucks and Marquette Golden Eagles, hoping to return next July to this city on the shore of Lake Michigan to accept the Republican nomination at their party's national convention.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX ANCHOR: Tonight, the race for the White House takes flight. Welcome to the first debate of the 2024 presidential campaign live at Fiserv Forum in Milwaukee.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX ANCHOR: Eight Republican candidates have qualified and have chosen to be here on our debate stage tonight. They are here to lay out their vision for America, the battle -- as they battle for the GOP nomination.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, everybody. I'm Martha MacCallum.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And I'm Bret Baier. This is the very same stage on which the Republican choice for president will accept the party's nomination next summer. The eight contenders are positioned by the order they sit in the polls with the highest polling candidates in the middle, standing center stage. Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Next, (inaudible) \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Former South Carolina Governor Nikki Haley.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Former New Jersey Governor \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE AND BOOING)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: South Carolina Senator Tim Scott.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Former Arkansas Governor Asa Hutchinson.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And North Dakota Governor \uc2 Doug Burgum\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Some ground rules for tonight. We will ask the questions and then candidates get one minute to answer. If someone is singled out, you get 30 seconds to respond. And when the time runs out, we all will hear this.\par \par \uc2 (DOUBLE BELL SOUND)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Very pleasant.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So tonight, these candidates have a big opportunity to break out of the pack and to explain why they are best prepared to be the Republican choice for president at a time when the likely Democrat nominee, President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , is working to convince the country that with Bidenomics, things are looking up.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: They call my plan Bidenomics. I'm not totally sure they meant it in a totally complimentary way at the time.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: But guess what? It's working.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (BOOING)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But, is it? More than 65 percent of Americans say the country is headed in the wrong direction. And here is the reality for some voters we talked to here in Wisconsin this week.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED: We have noticed a dramatic increase in prices at the grocery store just across the board.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED: Gas is high and food is high. A lot of people out here are homeless because they can't buy food.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED: It's tough when you've got mortgage rates at 7-8 percent versus 2 to 3. It's just, you can't afford a house anymore.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED: Inflation is -- is ridiculous. It's killing us out here.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: As we sit here tonight, the number one song on the Billboard chart is called "Rich Men North of Richmond."\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It is by a singer from Farmville, Virginia, named Oliver Anthony. His lyrics speak of alienation, of deep frustration with the state of government and of this country. Washington, D.C., is about 100 miles north of Richmond.\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC PLAYING, "RICH MEN NORTH OF RICHMOND")\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, Governor DeSantis, why is this song striking such a nerve in this country right now? What do you think it means?\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDAITE: Our country is in decline. This decline is not inevitable. It's a choice. We need to send \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  back to his basement and reverse American decline.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: And it starts with understanding we must reverse Bidenomics so that middle class families have a chance to succeed again. We cannot succeed as a country if you are working hard and can't afford groceries, a car, or a new home while Hunter Biden can make hundreds of thousands of dollars on lousy paintings. That is wrong.\par \par \uc2 We -- we also cannot succeed when the Congress spends trillions and trillions of dollars. Those "Rich Men North of Richmond" have put us in this situation.\par \par \uc2 And, finally, we need to lower your gas prices. We're going to open up all energy production. We will be energy dominant again in this country.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: I showed it could be done in the state of Florida. I pledge to you as your president we will get the job done and I will not let you down.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, Governor DeSantis, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Governor Christie, do you agree with Governor DeSantis just said there? And why would you be better on the economy than him?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE\uc2  (R), FORMER NEW JERSEY GOVERNOR, PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Well, look, I do agree predominantly with what Governor DeSantis just laid out. I think that if you asked every one of us up here that we would agree predominantly with what he just laid out.\par \par \uc2 Here is the difference. The difference is that we're going to have to work and make sure that we sell these ideas. And we able -- be able to put ourselves in a position where we get a majority of the vote, not only by winning the Congress and the Senate in '24, but also by having someone who has had the experience of doing it.\par \par \uc2 Now I was elected as a conservative Republican in a blue state with 61 percent of the vote, with a Democratic legislature against me the entire time. And we still, through hard, strong decision-making, brought them around to our point of view.\par \par \uc2 We cut taxes in New Jersey. We cut debt in New Jersey. We made sure that each and every time we were confronted with bad Democratic ideas we stood and stopped them. And when there were good ideas, we brought people together to make progress going forward. Truth and accountability are the things we need to do to fight waste.\par \par \uc2 And I'd say the last thing is this, Bret. We cannot sit by any longer and allow the kind of spending that is going on in Washington. Because every dollar they spend is a dollar that these people are not allowed to spend on their children and their grandchildren. It's robbing our country and it's wrong.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, Governor, let me just follow up very quickly.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: New Jersey, when you were governor, had the second-lowest credit rating in the nation after Illinois, and it was downgraded 11 times.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIE: Yes, yes. And -- and that's what happens when you inherit a blue state that has done that. But when you look at what we did on debt, Bret, in that state, we cut debt in that state, debt that had been left to us by three Democratic gubernatorial predecessors of mine who ran up that debt tremendously.\par \par \uc2 And what you also saw us to do was to cut the unemployment rate in half. It was over 10 percent when I became governor in 2010. What we also did was cut pension payments to public employees to make sure that taxpayers were not being soaked by a public employee...\par \par \uc2 (DOUBLE BELL SOUND)\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIE: ... union system that was killing the taxpayer.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Thank you, Governor.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Governor Scott -- I mean, Tim Scott, Senator Scott, excuse me, the song also goes after welfare programs. As a senator, now President Biden argued for freezing federal spending back, this was back the '80s, and dealing with sacred cows. He does not talk about that anymore. You have been a senator though for 10 years. So what have you done to rein in the increasing size of government?\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT (R-SC), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Well, thank you for the question. Over the last several years I've had an opportunity to vote against spending package after spending package after spending package.\par \par \uc2 What we also need to understand is that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's Bidenomics has led to the loss of $10,000 of spending power for the average family. When you see 16 percent inflation, your gas is up 40 percent. Your food is up 20 percent. Your electricity is up 20 percent. We can stop that by turning the spigot off at Washington, sending the money back to the states, and allowing the decisions to be made at their own houses.\par \par \uc2 I helped write the Tax Cuts and Jobs Act in 2017. We cut taxes for single moms like the one that raised me by 70 percent, for dual households by 60 percent, returning to the average family $4,000. If you don't send it to Washington, we can't spend it. That's good news for the American people.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. But, just to follow-up, you did -- during the Trump administration, you approved $4.4 trillion, $4.1 trillion, $1.7 trillion, over the course of that administration. That's a lot of money.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: There is no doubt that during the Trump administration we were dealing with the COVID virus. We spent more money. But, here is what happened at the end of our time in the majority. We had low unemployment. Record low unemployment is 3.5 percent for the majority of the population, 70 percent -- 70-year-low for women. African Americans, Hispanics and Asians had an all-time low. But, our inflation was at two percent. Under \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , we've seen the exact opposite. We've seen inflation explode, which led to 12 Federal Reserve increases. That's devastating homebuyers today.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Mr. Ramaswamy, you're listening to these answers.\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE (R), 2024 PRESDIENTIAL CANDIDATE: If I may --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Now, hold on for a second.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 PENCE: So, I don't know if I get a chance to respond.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: He didn't mention you specifically. But, we'll be with you in a second, Mr. Vice President. Mr. Ramaswamy, listening to all of this, why should voters choose you over more experienced politicians on this stage? You're basically a blank slate for people. You're 38-years-old. You've said that you only voted in two presidential elections before this moment, this political race.\par \par \uc2 VIVEK RAMASWAMY\uc2  (R), 2024 PRESDIENTIAL CANDIDATE: So, first, let me just address the question that is on everybody's mind at home tonight. Who the heck is this skinny guy with a funny last name, and what the heck is he doing in the middle of this debate stage? I'll tell you, I'm not a politician, Bret. You're right about that. I'm an entrepreneur.\par \par \uc2 My parents came to this country with no money 40 years ago. I have gone on to found multibillion dollar companies. I did it while I married my wife Apoorva, raising our two sons, following our faith in God. That is the American dream. And I am genuinely worried that that American dream will not exist for our two sons and their generation unless we do something about it.\par \par \uc2 And I do think, but it's going to take an outsider, because for a long time, we have professional politicians in the Republican Party who have been running from something. Now is our moment to start running to something, to our vision of what it means to be an American today. If you have a broken car, you don't turn over the keys to the people who broke it again. You hand it over to a new generation to actually fix the problem. That's why I'm in this race, and we're just getting warmed up.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. So, you (inaudible) Haley. Why are you better positioned to turn around this economy that we've heard all of these voters talking about tonight? Then, Mr. Ramaswamy, who is a successful entrepreneur, nationally right now, he is beating you in the polls.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY (R), 2024 PRESDIENTIAL CANDIDATE: Well, I don't care about polls. What I care about the fact is that no one is telling the American people the truth. The truth is that Biden didn't do this to us. Our Republicans did this to us too. When they passed that $2.2 trillion COVID stimulus bill, they left us with 90 million people on Medicaid, 42 million people on food stamps.\par \par \uc2 No one has told you how to fix it. I'll tell you how to fix it. They need to stop the spending. They need to stop the borrowing. They need to eliminate the earmarks that Republicans brought back in, and they need to make sure they understand these are taxpayer dollars. It's not their dollars.\par \par \uc2 And while they're all saying this, you have \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . You've got Tim Scott. You've got \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 . They all voted to raise the debt. And \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  added $8 trillion to our debt. And our kids are never going to forgive us for this. And so, at the end of the day, you look at the 2024 budget. Republicans asked for $7.4 billion in earmarks. Democrats asked for $2.8 billion. So, you tell me who are the big spenders? I think it's time for an accountant in the White House.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Vice President Pence, you had mentioned that 54 percent of voters say the cost of groceries is a "major problem for them".\par \par \uc2 PENCE: Right.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You blamed the Biden administration spending for that increase. But, as Vice President, your administration spent more than, any prior, $7.8 trillion added to the national debt, $3.5 trillion of that before COVID. So, does that mean that you're part of the spending problem?\par \par \uc2 PENCE: Well, first of all, thanks for the question. Thanks for letting me respond, reference to our administration's record. I'm incredibly proud of the record of the Trump-Pence administration. In four short years, we rebuilt our military. We revived our economy. We unleashed American energy. And we appointed three conservatives to the Supreme Court that gave the American people a new beginning for the right to life.\par \par \uc2 Now, Martha, you asked earlier who is the most best prepared for this job? And I must say, with all due respect to all of my friends on the stage, and even to one that's probably looking on, I think, unquestionably, I'm the best prepared, the most tested, the most qualified and proven conservative in this race.\par \par \uc2 I was a leader in the \uc2 Congress of the United States\uc2 . I led Indiana where we balanced budgets and had a AAA bond rating, when I was governor. And as Vice President of the United States, we spent funding to backfill on the military cuts of the Obama administration, and then we were there in the worst pandemic in 100 years. All that being said, I was the first person in this race to say that we've got to deal with the long-term national debt issues. You got people on this stage that won't even talk about issues like Social Security and Medicare.\par \par \uc2 Vivek, you recently said a President can't do everything. Well, I got news for you, Vivek. I've been in the hallway. I've been in the West Wing. A President in the United States has to confront every crisis facing America. I will put our nation back on the path to growth and prosperity and restore fiscal responsibility, just as I did in Congress and as governor --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Mr. Vice President --\par \par \uc2 PENCE: -- and when I was Vice President.\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: But --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Hold on, Vivek.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Hold on.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You were named earlier. I am going to give Vivek first.\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: Yes. This isn't that complicated, guys, unlock American energy, drill, frack, burn coal and embrace nuclear, put people back to work by no longer paying them more to stay at home, reform the U.S. Fed, stabilize the U.S. dollar, and go to war.\par \par \uc2 The only war that I will declare as U.S. President will be the war on the federal administrative state that is the source of those toxic regulations acting like a wet blanket on the economy. So, I'm not sure exactly understood \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 's comment. But, I'll let you all parse that out. For me, it's pretty simple. That's something the U.S. President can do with focus, and I'll deliver on that.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: Let me explain it to you. Let me explain it to you, Vivek, if I can. I'll go slower this time.\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: You know, I sometimes struggle with the reading comprehension.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: Look, I was a House conservative leader before it was cool. I actually pushed the Deficit Reduction Act. That was the last time we actually reduced the national debt in the United States when I was the leader of House conservatives. I balanced budgets and cut taxes when I was Governor. I mean, look, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has weakened this country at home and abroad. Now is not the time for on-the-job training. We don't need to bring in a rookie. We don't need to bring in people without experience. We need to bring --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Wait a second.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Hold on. Hold on. Hold on. In 30 seconds when you have a rebuttal. OK? And you are up Governor DeSantis.\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: So, here is the thing. Why are we in this mess? Part of it, and a major reason is because how this federal government handed COVID-19 by locking down this economy. It was a mistake. It should have never happened. And in Florida, we led the country out of lockdown. We kept our state free and open. And I can tell you this. As your President, I will never let the deep state bureaucrats lock you down. You don't take somebody like Fauci and coddle him. You bring Fauci in. You sit him down. And you say, Anthony, you are fired.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We're going to bring in --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: I just want to respond to Mike for one second, because he invoked me back. Listen, now that everybody has gotten their memorized, pre-prepared slogans out of the way, we can actually have a real discussion now. The reality and the fact of the matter is --\par \par \uc2 PENCE: Is that one of yours?\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: Not really, Mike, actually. We're just going to have some fun tonight. And the reality is, you have a bunch of people, professional politicians, super PAC puppets, following slogans handed over to them by their 400-page super PACs last week. The real choice we face in this primary is this. Do you want a super PAC puppet, or do you want a patriot who speaks the truth? Do you want incremental reform, which is what you're hearing about, or do you want revolution?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK.\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: And I stand on the side of the American Revolution rather than - -\par \par \uc2 PENCE: I got to address --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We are going to take control back here.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We need everyone to have a moment on the economy.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I think that's fair.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: Can I address the COVID issues the Governor brought up?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: There are two people who have not -- we're going to get back to that. Yes. So, there are two people who we have not heard from yet. So, let's hear from Governor Burgum and then from Governor Hutchinson on the economy, sir.\par \par \uc2 DOUG BURGUM\uc2  (R), 2024 PRESDIENTIAL CANDIDATE: Well, great. Thank you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 And, of course, I'm from a town of 300 people. It's a big deal to make it on the stage with all these folks.\par \par \uc2 But --\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS)\par \par \uc2 BURGUM: But when they were -- they were all wishing me well and I think I took them a little too literally when they said go to Milwaukee and break a leg.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BURGUM: But I do want to say on this, we're missing something. We can't just talk about the Biden economy because the economy, energy and national security are all tied together.\par \par \uc2 We -- of course, we're paying too much for energy in our state right -- in country right now. But part of the reason why is because the Biden policies on energy. We've got a plan right now, the $1.2 trillion of Green New Deal spending buried in the inflation creation act is something that is just subsidizing China.\par \par \uc2 We're -- if we're going to stop buying oil from the Middle East and start buying batteries from China, we're just trading \uc2 OPEC\uc2  for \uc2 Sinopec\uc2 . And then belatedly, belatedly, the Biden administration says, no, we're going to put sanctions on Russian oil.\par \par \uc2 Well, we put sanctions on Russian oil. Well, then, it's 20 percent off. Who's buying it? China.\par \par \uc2 So, if you buy a battery in this country, you buy a solar panel, it's being produced in a plant in China powered by coal where it's being powered by oil and gas at 20 percent off. And every farmer in this county like to buy diesel at 20 percent off, just like they're buying it in China.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Governor Hutchinson, jump in.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 ASA HUTCHINSON (R), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Thank you, Bret. Delighted to be here tonight.\par \par \uc2 And let me just tell you that I'm a pro-life governor from a conservative state that have a conservative record in which I lowered taxes in Arkansas as governor. I created a $2 billion surplus that I passed over to my successor. And I made sure that we shrunk the size of government.\par \par \uc2 We have 14 percent fewer state employees in Arkansas after I left the government than when I took over as governor eight years ago. I tell that because that's what we need in Washington, D.C. We need somebody who can actually constrain the growth of federal government, that can actually reduce the size.\par \par \uc2 And I pledge to reduce it by 10 percent our federal nondefense workforce. That's a specific pledge to make that attacks the administrative state. And let me applaud some of the business partners that are here that have had success in business.\par \par \uc2 But let me tell you, I've been a federal prosecutor. I've served our country in terms of being head of the DEA and Homeland Security in times of crisis. And while I think that that's experience that is important for the future of our country to be the president of the United States that can lead with positive solutions to be held accountable.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 We have a lot of topics to get to and I promise we're going to get to everyone if we play within the rules.\par \par \uc2 Okay, next topic. More than a thousand people are still unaccounted for in Maui after the deadliest U.S. wildfire in more than a century. Hawaii's governor and White House officials said that climate change amplified the cost of human error.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And a tropical storm hit California for the first time in 84 years. The ocean hit 101 degrees off the coast of Florida. And in the last month, the heat wave in the Southwest broke records nearly 50 years old.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So, Alexander Diaz from Young America's Foundation has a question for you all.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER DIAZ, \uc2 CATHOLIC UNIVERSITY OF AMERICA\uc2 : Polls consistently show that young people's number one issue is climate change. How would you as both president of the United States and leader of the Republican Party calm their fears that the Republican Party doesn't care about climate change?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, we want to start on this with a show of hands. Do you believe in human behavior is causing climate change? Raise your hand if you do.\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: Look, we're not schoolchildren. Let's have the debate. I mean, I'm happy to take it to start. Alexander --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So do you want to raise your hand --\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS)\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: I don't think that's the way to do it.\par \par \uc2 So, let me just say to Alexander this -- first of all, one of the reasons our country has declined is because of the way the corporate media treats Republicans versus Democrats. Biden was on the beach while those people were suffering. He was asked about it and he said no comment. Are you kidding me?\par \par \uc2 As somebody that's handled disasters in Florida, you've got to be activated. You've got to be there. You've got to be present. You've got to be helping people who are doing this.\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: Can we start for us (ph) to answer the question?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: And here's the deal.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: Let's just answer the question.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Is that a yes? Is that a yes? Is that a hand raise?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You do not --\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: I think it was a hand raise for him. And it's -- my hands are in my pockets because --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: No, I didn't -- I didn't raise my hand.\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: Let us be honest as Republicans. I'm the only person on the stage who isn't bought and paid for, so I can say this -- the climate change agenda is a hoax.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Oh, whoa, whoa, whoa.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: That is ridiculous.\par \par \uc2 (BOOS)\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: The climate change agenda is a hoax. And we have (INAUDIBLE) independent school.\par \par \uc2 And the reality is the anti-carbon agenda is the wet blanket on our economy. And so, the reality is more people are dying of bad climate change policies than they are of actual climate change.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Governor -- Governor Haley, are you bought and paid for?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: The temperature (ph) is down by 98 percent.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Listen, hold on. Hold on. Listen, listen, listen --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE\uc2  (R), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Look, I've had it. No, no --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Wait, hold on, hold on.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIE: I've had enough -- I've had enough already tonight of a guy who sounds like ChatGPT standing up here.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIE: And the last person in one of these debates, Bret, who stood in the middle of the stage and said, "what's a skinny guy with an odd last name doing up here" was \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 . And I'm afraid we're dealing with the same type of amateur standing on the stage tonight.\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: Give me a hug -- give me a hug just like you did to Obama.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIE: The same type of amateur.\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: And you will help elect me just like you did Obama, too. Give me that big (ph) hug, brother.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIE: The same type of amateur.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Hold on, hold on.\par \par \uc2 Governor Haley, would you like to respond? Are you bought and paid for?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: So, Bret, what I would like to say is the fact that I think this is exactly why Margaret Thatcher said, if you want something said, ask a man. If you want something done, ask a woman.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS)\par \par \uc2 HALEY: First of all, we do care about clean air, clean water. We want to see that taken care of. But there's a right way to do it.\par \par \uc2 And the right way is first of all, yes, is climate change real? Yes, it is. But if you go want to go and really change the environment, then we need to start telling China and India that they have to lower their emissions.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 HALEY: That's where our problem is. And these green subsidies that Biden has put in, all he's done is help China because he doesn't understand all these electric vehicles that he's done, what that does that, half of the batteries for electric vehicles are made in China. And so, that's not helping the environment. You're putting money in China's pocket. And Biden did that.\par \par \uc2 So, first of all, I think we need to acknowledge the truth, which is these subsidies are not working. We also need to take on the international world and say, okay, India and China, you've got to stop polluting. And that's when we'll start to deal with the planet -- the planet (ph).\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Senator Scott, are you bought and paid for?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Are you bought and paid for?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: I'm sorry?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Are you bought and paid for?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Absolutely not.\par \par \uc2 I mean, here's what the American people deserve. It's a debate about the issues that affect their lives. Going back and forth and being childish is not helpful to the American people to decide on the next leader of our country.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Number one. Wait a second.\par \par \uc2 Number two, as a kid who grew up with a single parent household mired in poverty, I wondered was the American dream real for kids who are devastated by poverty, devastated by the challenges of life?\par \par \uc2 I came to the conclusion that America can do for anyone what she's done for me if we focus on restoring hope, creating opportunities and protecting America.\par \par \uc2 If we want the environment to be better and we all do, the best thing to do is to bring our jobs home from China. If we create 10 million new jobs in my Made in America Plan, we will have a better economy and a better environment.\par \par \uc2 Let me tell you why I say that, Bret.\par \par \uc2 America --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Do it quickly.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: America has cut -- I'm a Southern boy. I talk slow.\par \par \uc2 So, America --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: There's another one.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: That was quick.\par \par \uc2 America has cut our carbon footprint in half in the last 25 years. The places where they are continuing to increase -- Africa, 950 million people; India, over a billion; China, over a billion.\par \par \uc2 Why do we put ourselves at a disadvantage, devastating our own economy? Let's bring our jobs home.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We have a lot of different topics to get to.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yeah. We do. We thank you all. Thank you, Senator Scott.\par \par \uc2 So, coming up next, the candidates will weigh in on what could be a defining issue in the 2024 campaign.\par \par \uc2 The first Republican debate continues, moments away.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Abortion has been a losing issue for Republicans since the Dobbs decision. In six state referendums, all have upheld abortion rights in this country. And even in red states, there are more swing state referendums that are coming up as we head into the elections, as well on this.\par \par \uc2 So, Governor Haley, what do you say to your party and to your state, which today confirmed a six-week abortion law as well, especially the impact on women suburban voters across this country?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: Thank you, Martha. I am unapologetically pro-life, not because the Republican Party tells me to be, but because my husband was adopted, and I had trouble having both of my children. So I'm surrounded by blessings.\par \par \uc2 Having said that, we need to stop demonizing this issue. This is talking about the fact that unelected justices didn't need to decide something this personal, because it's personal for every woman and man. Now, it's been put in the hands of the people. That's great.\par \par \uc2 When it comes to a federal ban, let's be honest with the American people and say it will take 60 Senate votes. It will take a majority of the House. So in order to do that, let's find consensus. Can't we all agree that we should ban late term abortions? Can't we all agree that we should encourage adoptions?\par \par \uc2 Can't we all agree that doctors and nurses who don't believe in abortion shouldn't have to perform them? Can't we all agree that contraception should be available? And can't we all agree that we are not going to put a woman in jail or give her the death penalty if she gets an abortion?\par \par \uc2 Let's treat this like the -- like a respectful issue that it is and humanize the situation and stop demonizing the situation.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Hold on. You're not mentioned, Vice President Pence.\par \par \uc2 Governor DeSantis, you signed a six-week abortion ban in Florida. One of your biggest financial backers said that you need to, quote, "shift to get moderates or you will lose." What do you say to him and others who say, politically, that is a tough thing to sell nationally?\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: Well, I would say we sold the biggest election landslide victory in the history of the Republican Party in the state of Florida in 2022. That's what I did. We can win.\par \par \uc2 But second of all, look, you got to do what you think is right. I believe in a culture of life. I was proud to sign the Heartbeat Bill. I remember the most impactful moments of my life was when I heard the heartbeat of my oldest daughter in my wife's womb, and then saw the sonograms of all three of my kids.\par \par \uc2 What the Democrats are trying to do on this issue is wrong, to allow abortion all the way up to the moment of birth. I know a lady in Florida named Penny, she survived multiple abortion attempts. She was left discarded in a pan. Fortunately, her grandmother saved her and brought her to a different hospital.\par \par \uc2 We're better than what the Democrats are selling. We are not going to allow abortion all the way up till birth, and we will hold them accountable for their extremism.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But just to be clear, Governor, would you sign a six-week ban federally?\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: I'm going to stand on the side of life. Look, I understand, Wisconsin is going to do it different than Texas. I understand Iowa and New Hampshire are going to do different. But I will support the cause of life as governor and as president.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Vice President Pence, you're shaking your head.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Hold on, hold on, hold on, Senator. Vice President Pence, you're shaking your head.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: Well, look, I'm not new to this cause. After I gave my life to Jesus Christ as my Lord and Savior, I open up the book and I read, before I formed you in the womb, I knew you. And see I set before you life and death, blessings and curses now choose life.\par \par \uc2 And I knew from that moment on the cause of life had to be my cause. And I've been a champion for life in the Congress, a champion for life as governor, and as vice president.\par \par \uc2 And to be honest with you, Nikki, you're my friend. But consensus is the opposite of leadership. When the Supreme Court returned this question to the American people, they didn't just send it to the states only. It's not a state's only issue. It's a moral issue.\par \par \uc2 And I promise you, as President of the United States, the American people will have a champion for life in the Oval Office. Can't we have a minimum standard in every state in the nation that says when a baby is capable of feeling pain, an abortion cannot be allowed?\par \par \uc2 A 15-week ban is an idea whose time has come. It's supported by 70 percent of the American people. But it's going to take unapologetic leadership, leadership that stands on principle and express compassion for women --\par \par \uc2 HALEY: I want to --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. Hold on.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: -- and crisis, pregnancies, I'll do that as President of the United States.\par \par \uc2 HALEY: He called my name, so I want to respond to that.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thirty seconds.\par \par \uc2 HALEY: So first of all, I will say it is in the hands of the people, and that's where it should be. But when you're talking about a federal ban, be honest with the American people.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: I am being honest.\par \par \uc2 HALEY: We have had 45 pro-life senators in over 100 years. So no Republican president can ban abortions, any more than a Democrat president could ban all those state laws. Don't make women feel like they have to decide on this issue, when you know we don't have 60 Senate votes in the House.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: Seventy-percent of the American people support legislation to ban abortion --\par \par \uc2 HALEY: But 70 percent of the Senate does not.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: -- after a baby is capable experiencing pain. It's called leadership.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Hold on. I'm going to (inaudible)\par \par \uc2 HALEY: Seventy percent of the Senate does not. You have to be honest with the American people.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We have to have a 15-week limit.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. Let's get Governor Burgum in for one minute here. So -- but the Supreme Court did overturn Roe v. Wade, and the result of that decision was that it went back to the States. So that's where it is right now.\par \par \uc2 So as I understand it, you are not in favor of a federal ban. What do you say about the states? There's about five of them, including New Jersey, I think a few others, that allow abortion up until the time of birth.\par \par \uc2 Now, if you were president, would you be able to abide that?\par \par \uc2 BURGUM: Well, first of all, I'm a pro-life governor of a very pro-life state. And this issue is, of course, very important. But I am on the record and I stand behind that we should not have a federal abortion ban. We should not.\par \par \uc2 And the reason why we shouldn't is very simple, it's the 10th Amendment in the Constitution. In the Constitution, which the states created the federal government, not the other way around, it says that there were certain duties allowed to the federal government delegated to them by the states. The rest are left to the states comma, or importantly or to the people.\par \par \uc2 We need to get back to freedom and liberty for the people in this country. And we can have -- we can have Republicans who fight for 50 years for this great cause and we return it back to the states. And then the next day, they turn around and go, no, the Fed should do that. Because the Feds are stepping into people's lives. They're stepping into people's businesses over and over.\par \par \uc2 If we say that the Fed should be in on this one, where do we stop? I say that we follow the Constitution and this is returned to the states. This is where it should be.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But, Governor Burgum, you signed a six-week ban...\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Time out. You signed a six-week ban. Governor Burgum, you signed a six-week ban. So you're saying federally it's all going to go to the states?\par \par \uc2 BURGUM: Yes. And what -- what is going to work in New York will never work in North Dakota...\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK.\par \par \uc2 BURGUM: ... and vice-versa. That's why...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Governor...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BURGUM: ... 50 states.\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON: This -- this is too important of an issue that I have to address. First of all, the Supreme Court gave it back to the elected representatives, whether it's the state's or whether it's the \uc2 United States Congress\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That's right.\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON: So there is authority. And that's why President Biden is pushing for a Democrat proposal which is, in essence, abortion-on-demand through the term. So they have their extreme position at a national level. We -- it's most likely going to be addressed in the states, but it's certainly fine to be addressed at the national level as well.\par \par \uc2 Arkansas has the record of being the most pro-life state in the nation. I've signed 30 pro-life pieces of legislation while I was governor. And every state can determine a different outcome here. And it is the most important issue for women and for the unborn child and for our country that we get this right. It's going to be a continued debate.\par \par \uc2 Let's talk about it in terms of compassion, in terms of protecting the life and also understanding how we have to enhance abortion -- excuse me, adoption services. How we have to enhance maternal care. Those things we've done in Arkansas and are important for our nation's future.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: There are a lot of issues that are very important.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Bret...\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Senator, I'll let you...\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: ... weigh in.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: We cannot let states like California, New York, and Illinois have abortions on-demand up until the day of birth. That is immoral. It is unethical. It is wrong.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: We must have a president of the United States who will advocate and fight for at the minimum a 15-week limit. I am 100 percent pro-life conservative. I have 100 percent pro-life record. I've got to tell you, though, we must fight for life.\par \par \uc2 Our Declaration of Independence says our creator gave us inalienable rights that include life. That is (inaudible) -- that is an issue we must solve. We can't leave it to Illinois. We can't leave it to Minnesota. We can't leave it to Illinois. We must solve that issue...\par \par \uc2 (DOUBLE BELL SOUND)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: ... with a 15-week limit at a minimum.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Thank you, Senator.\par \par \uc2 HALEY: Bret, I think we're all pro-life.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HALEY: But what I would love is for someone to ask Biden and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , are they for 38 weeks or are they for 39 weeks, are they for 40 weeks? Because that's what the media needs to be asking.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right. Another issue is America and the crime crisis, the homelessness crisis. American cities are in decline. People are moving out as homelessness, drugs, crime move in. There are problems accelerated -- they did accelerate during the pandemic and are still rising, actually.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Murders in Los Angeles, New York, Chicago, all up 30 percent between 2019 and 2022. Homelessness is up 11 percent, the largest jump in recorded history.\par \par \uc2 Vice President Pence, a lot of this began in the COVID era. How much of what we are seeing happening around this country is a result of those COVID lockdowns? And is your administration in part to blame for how we got here?\par \par \uc2 PENCE: Well, I think what's in part to blame is the Democrats been talking about defunding the police for the last five years. And we ought to be funding law enforcement, particularly in our major cities at -- at unprecedented levels. I mean, it's extraordinary to think about the violence that's claiming innocent lives literally every week in every major city in this country.\par \par \uc2 And yet Democrats and liberal prosecutors in major metropolitan areas continue to -- to work out their fanciful agendas, to -- to do bail reform and -- and go easy. What we need is -- is strong commitment to law enforcement. We need leadership in Washington, D.C., that will marshal the resources of the states, marshal the resources of the American people.\par \par \uc2 But let me also say it's about opportunity. I mean, a lot of people don't know that those Trump-Pence tax cuts that we got signed into law go away at the end of 2025 if we don't have a Republican president and a Republican House and a Republican Senate.\par \par \uc2 But when I'm president of the United States, we're actually going to cut taxes further. We're going to extend those tax cuts. And we're going to close the federal Department of Education, block grant all that...\par \par \uc2 (DOUBLE BELL SOUND)\par \par \uc2 PENCE: ... funding back to the states with a growing economy and educational choice and law enforcement. We will bring our cities back.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Governor Christie, another issue. This weekend here in Milwaukee reports say there were 30 shootings. And a number of them including kids. Add that to the big increase in school shootings around the country.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Democrats blame this crisis on easy access to guns. They also blame Republicans for blocking gun control legislation. What would President Christie do?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIE: You know, I'm proud of the fact, Bret, that I'm the only person along with Governor Hutchinson up on this stage who has actually run a United States Attorney's Office. I ran the fifth largest office in America, in a -- in a state where there is significant urban crime. And the problem is not going to be solved by more money. The problem is that these prosecutors in these localities, in the states, are refusing to do their job, and to arrest violent criminals.\par \par \uc2 So, what a President Christie would do is appoint an Attorney General who would instruct each of the 93 U.S. attorneys that they are to take over the prosecution of violent crime in every one of those cities that are failing to do so. We have plenty of room in the federal prisons to lock up these violent criminals and clean up what's going on all across this country in these individual cities.\par \par \uc2 Secondly, what we need to make sure that each and every one of these criminals understand is that the laws apply to everybody, and when \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  fills out a fake application, a false application, for a gun permit, and then is facing a 10-year mandatory minimum, which was mandated by legislation sponsored by his father, and then you have a Justice Department that walks away from those charges. We're telling people that the law doesn't apply to everybody. In a Christie administration, he would go to jail for 10 years.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: What about a President Ramaswamy? What does a President Ramaswamy do about guns?\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: So, the reality is, we have a crime wave in this country, and we know how to fix it. The question is, do we actually have the spine to do it? More cops in the streets, who are on the streets able to do their jobs without looking over their shoulder for getting sued. And we also have a mental health epidemic in this country. Just over the same period that we have closed mental health institutions, we have seen a spike in violent crime. Do we have the spine to bring them back? I think we should. As President, I will.\par \par \uc2 But, it's not just drugging up people in those psychiatric institutions with Zoloft and Seroquel. It's a deeper issue. I think faith-based approaches can play a role here, too. We're in the middle of a national identity crisis. And I say this as a member of my generation. The problem in our country right now, the reason we have that mental health epidemic is that people are so hungry for purpose and meaning, at a time when family, faith, patriotism, hard work have all disappeared. What we really need is a tonal reset from the top saying that this is what it means to be an American.\par \par \uc2 Yes, we will stand for the rule of law. Yes, we will close the southern border where criminals are coming in every day. And yes, we will back law enforcement, because we remember who we really are. And that's also how we address that mental health epidemic in the next generation that is directly leading to violent crime.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: Can I speak --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Governor DeSantis, really quickly, Governor DeSantis.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: We don't have an identity crisis, Vivek. We're not looking for a new national identity. The American people are the most faith filled, freedom loving, idealistic, hard-working people the world has ever known. We just need government as good as our people.\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: Mike, I think the difference is, you might have -- some others like you may have on the stage, it is Morning in America speech. It is not Morning in America. We live in a dark moment, and we have to confront the fact that we're in an internal sort of cold cultural civil war and we have to recognize that --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 PENCE: You are (inaudible) the American people with a failed government in Washington, D.C. We just need government as good as our people again.\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: So --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Governor DeSantis -- Governor DeSantis, I want you to weigh in here.\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: Let me just finish addressing that slogan, because I don't know what that slogan means. We need to shut down the administrative state, and that's actually how we translate.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Governor DeSantis, I want you to weigh in here.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I want you to weigh in, Governor DeSantis. Crime has been on the rise Florida, Governor DeSantis. How do you stop crime?\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: Well, actually, crime is at a 50-year-low in Florida.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Not in Miami.\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: We're happy with that. Well, the statewide, it's a 50-year-low. And so, here is the thing. These hollowed out cities, this is a symptom of America's decline. And one of the biggest reasons is because you have \uc2 George Soros\uc2  funding these radical left-wing district attorneys. They get into office and they say they're not going to prosecute crimes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: They disagree with -- the inmates start running the asylum. There is one guy in this entire country that's ever done anything about that, me. When we had two of these district attorneys in Florida elected with Soros funding who said they wouldn't do their job, I removed them from their posts.\par \par \uc2 They are gone. And as President, we're going to go after all of these people because they are hurting the quality of life, and they are victimizing innocent people in every corner of this country, and it will stop when I get into office.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. One word here before the break -- Governor Burgum?\par \par \uc2 BURGUM: Bret and Martha, I just thought it's interesting, you asked your question about the problems we're having in big cities. No one ever asked the question of, what -- what about the crime wave in small towns?\par \par \uc2 Because in a small town, neighbors help neighbors. People understand each other. If a farmer gets sick, everybody comes together and helps him get the crop up. There's accountability. There's transparency.\par \par \uc2 One thing I think this country could use is somebody in the White House that understands small town values because that's our road back to get this country on track again.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Governor Hutchinson?\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON: As former head of the DEA, I understand the drug crisis in America.\par \par \uc2 And right now, whenever you look at the challenges in our inner -- inner- city, there's three simple words that would be helpful. One, enforce the law when it comes to crime. Secondly, let's deal with the challenge of fentanyl. And it's both about stopping the fentanyl coming from Mexico, but it's also about education of our young people, making sure that we have the tools that are needed for addiction counseling. That's what we expanded in Arkansas as well.\par \par \uc2 Whenever you look at the underlying challenge of America, though, no one likes to see an America with smash-and-grab in our inner -- inner cities. As president of the United States, that will stop. It starts at the top with respect for our justice system, that a former president who's under indictment has undermined by attacking judges, by attacking prosecutors, by attacking the system and saying he's aggrieved.\par \par \uc2 And so, we have to have respect for our justice system and the rule of law. And it starts at the top with the president of the United States.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Bret -- Bret, hold on.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We need to take a break.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Bret --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, speaking of that, right now, you are looking live at Fulton County jail where former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  will be processed tomorrow. So, next, the candidates will have an opportunity to talk about the coming trials of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We have a lot to get to in this second hour of this GOP primary debate policy discussions the Americans want to hear you all on, China, Ukraine, immigration, education. But we are going to take a brief moment and talk about the elephant not in the room.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Former President Trump has been indicted in four different states on 91 counts. He will be processed tomorrow in Georgia at the Fulton County Jail for charges relating to the 2020 election loss.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You all signed a pledge to support the eventual Republican nominee. If former president Trump is convicted in a court of law, would you still support him as your party's choice? Please raise your hand if you would.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Just -- hold on. So just to be clear, Governor Christie, you were kind of late to the game there, but you raised your hand?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIE: No, no. I'm doing this. Look. Look, I'm doing this.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And I know you didn't.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIE: Here's the -- here's the bottom line. Someone's got to stop normalizing this conduct. OK? Now -- and now, whether or not -- whether or not you believe that the criminal charges are right or wrong, the conduct is beneath the office of President of the United States.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS)\par \par \uc2 And, you know, this is the great thing about this country. Booing is allowed, but it doesn't change the truth. It doesn't change the truth.\par \par \uc2 (BOOING)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Mr. Ramaswamy, you raised your hands supporting.\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: I'd like to get in and respond. Let's just speak the truth. OK? President Trump, I believe was the best president of the 21st century.\par \par \uc2 It's -- and \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2 , honest to God, your claim that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is motivated by vengeance and grievance would be a lot more credible if your entire campaign were not based on vengeance and grievance against one man.\par \par \uc2 And if people at home want to see a bunch of people blindly bashing \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  without an iota of vision for this country, they could just change the channel to MSNBC right now. But I'm not running for president of MSNBC. I am running for President of the United States.\par \par \uc2 We're skating on thin ice and we cannot set a precedent where the party in power uses police force to indict its political opponents. It is wrong. We have to end the weaponization of justice in this country.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Thirty seconds, Governor DeSantis.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIE: Well, you know, let me tell you something.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And I'm sorry, 30 seconds, Governor Christie.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIE: You make me laugh because --\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: (inaudible) always, brother.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIE: -- you sit -- you sit -- you sit here in an answer -- you sit here in an answer right --\par \par \uc2 (CROWD BOOING)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Hold on one second.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FXLV000020230824ej8n0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Hannity Live From The Spin Room On Milwaukee; 2024 GOP Hopefuls Clash In First Debate; FL Gov. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  Reacts To Tonight's Debate; Sen. Tim Scott Reacts To First GOP Debate; Former V.P. \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  Reacts To First GOP Debate\b0\par\par\uc2 Sean Hannity, Kellyanne Conway, Laura Ingraham, Ari Fleischer \par \uc2 10,582 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 August 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Hannity\par \uc2 SHAN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE (R) PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: The disastrous withdrawal from Afghanistan, their war on energy, runaway spending that launched the worst inflation in 40 years, a crisis at our southern border and assault on our values and liberties. And the American people have had enough. And I know we can bring it back. But different times called for different leadership.\par \par \uc2 The Republican Party owes the American people the choice, proven leadership at the national level that knows how to move a conservative agenda forward. We proved in the Trump-Pence years you can turn this country around faster than you can imagine. And I have faith we will again, because I have faith in the American people. Good, decent, hardworking, faith-filled idealistic people, this country.\par \par \uc2 And I have faith that God has not done with America yet. And if we will renew our faith in one another and renew our faith in him who has ever guided this nation since we arrived on these wilderness shores. I know the best days for the greatest nation on Earth are yet to come.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Thank you, Mr. Ramaswamy.\par \par \uc2 VIVEK RAMASWAMY\uc2  (R) PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: I was born in 1985. And I grew up into a generation where we were taught to celebrate our diversity and our differences so much, that we forgot all of the ways that we are really just the same as Americans bound by a common set of ideals that set this nation into motion in 1776. And this is our moment to revive those common ideals. God is real. There are two genders.\par \par \uc2 Fossil fuels are a requirement for human prosperity. Reverse racism is racism. An open border is not a border. Parents determine the education of their children. The nuclear family is the greatest form of governance known to man. Capitalism lifts us up from poverty. There are three branches of government, not four and the U.S. Constitution. It is the strongest guarantor of freedom in human history.\par \par \uc2 That is what won us the American Revolution. That is what will win us the revolution of 2024. Thanks for letting me introduce myself tonight. Thank you,\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Governor DeSantis. Governor?\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: This is our time for choosing, we will send \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  back to his basement and we will reverse the decline of this country. I'm a blue-collar kid. I work minimum wage jobs to be able to make ends meet, I understand the importance of the American dream. And I know how that slipped away from so many millions of Americans will restore it.\par \par \uc2 I'm a veteran who served in Iraq. I know what it means to put service above self. Most have a dad and a husband to six, five and three-year-old. I understand the importance of protecting parents' rights and the wellbeing of our children. In Florida, we showed it could be done. I made promises and I delivered on all of those promises. 2024 is make or break. We're not getting a mulligan, no excuses. I will get the job done. And as your president, I will not let you down. God bless you all.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We want to say thank you. Thank you to all the candidates on the stage tonight. And thank you to Milwaukee.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you, everybody. We will see you on the campaign trail from debates to primaries to the general election and the conventions before that. Thanks for joining us. \uc2 Sean Hannity\uc2  from the Spin Room starts right now.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Take it away, Sean.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Take it away, Sean.\par \par \uc2 SEAN HANNITY\uc2 , FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: All right. Well, thank you both, by the way. Great job, Martha and Bret. Welcome to HANNITY. The very first debate of the 2024 campaign is now in the books. The election season is officially up and running and tonight we're broadcasting live from the spin room. We are in beautiful Milwaukee, Wisconsin, where in just a moment, we're going to hear directly from the candidates themselves.\par \par \uc2 Florida Governor DeSantis. He will be joining us. \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2  will join us. Tim Scott, Nikki Haley. We'll get reaction from Trump supporters. But without a doubt every single Republican running. Guess what, they are far more tech articulate and ethical than your current president. You know, the compromised cognitive mass that is \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . And tonight, that was more than very obvious and transparent.\par \par \uc2 In case you missed it. Here's how it all went down. Take a quick look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: We cannot succeed as a country. If you are working hard and you can't afford groceries, a car or a new home while Hunter Biden can make hundreds of thousands of dollars on lousy paintings.\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: Now is our moment to start running to something.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE\uc2  (R) PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: I've had enough already tonight of a guy who sounds like ChatGPT standing up here. And --\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: Give me a hug just like you did to Obama.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIE: The same time --\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY (R) PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: This is exactly why \uc2 Margaret Thatcher\uc2  said if you want something said, ask a man. If you want something done, ask a woman.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: The American people are the most faith-filled, freedom loving, idealistic hard-working people the world has ever known. We just need government as good as our people.\par \par \uc2 TIM SCOTT (R) PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: It is time for a change in America.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: So, who are tonight's winners and losers? Here with reaction. Fox News Contributor Kellyanne Conway. Former Trump White House Chief of Staff, former \uc2 RNC\uc2  chairman Reince Priebus. All right. Not our first rodeo. The thing that stood out without getting into the specifics, who won who lost, we'll let everybody at home make those decisions. What really stood out to me is what a contrast?\par \par \uc2 This party is to this guy in the White House. I don't care if it's borders, I don't care if it's Ukraine, I don't care if it's energy, I don't care if it's law and order, foreign policy on every single issue. America is going to have a choice election here. That -- and I think one thing they agreed on America is in decline under \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 KELLYANNE CONWAY, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: They all made the case and they haven't been making the case as strongly, Sean. They've been going after \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  more than \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and to make yourself truly electable, you have to transport yourself in a split screen against \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . And to do that you take him on all these issues. There's no major public policy issue right now, the 12 that are tested routinely where \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's approval rating is above water. He's underwater.\par \par \uc2 He can't point to anyone issue and say, that's my signature accomplished. That's where people see me as strong, to say nothing of his personal attributes. In addition to the substantive policy issues, every single Republican on the stage tonight and President Trump who wasn't there have higher personal attributes in \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . Meaning cares about people like me, has a plan for the country, has the mental acuity and physical capacity.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Wait a minute. Joe has shown real empathy towards the people of Maui.\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: Maui. No kidding.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: When he showed up two weeks later after saying any comment? No comment. I don't want to be a distraction on the ground. Meanwhile, there's nothing on the ground to distract from.\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: By the way could the Democrats, Reince and Sean, could they even find eight people to be on the stage tonight? They don't have to have a bench at all. I mean, these are -- this is all the undercard right now. Trump's beating every single one of them, every single poll in every single state and nationwide. And they're running for number two. Look, I think Nikki is going to get a second look from some people based on some stuff she said tonight on abortion.\par \par \uc2 I'm very pro-life. But I like what she said that you don't demonize or punish women. That's important. We have to say babies and serve women. The people who love Vivek are MAGA people, they're rooting for Trump and that people have a problem with him tonight are not going to vote for him. I think he's inexperienced. \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  is aggressive. \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  did his I'm a seasoned debater thing.\par \par \uc2 I think DeSantis held his own. He didn't have enough time really to differentiate himself. But he delivered in a different way than he usually does.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: So, I think he had a very strong open. The interesting thing when I do my audience show is I'll pull them every night. And those that like President Trump, they also like DeSantis.\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: Correct.\par \par \uc2 REINCE PRIEBUS, FORMER RNC CHAIRMAN: Right. And I think that every one of these folks on stage tonight they're going to -- where they're going to win or lose, this debate 's going to be in this room right behind us. And every one of them had a moment every one of them can clip something that they've said, a one liner, their position on something that they're going to use to attract voters, attract money.\par \par \uc2 And look, as you were saying before, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is not just doing a horrible job as president, but the proof is in the pudding. He's 20 points worse today in his approval rating than he was in 2020. So, when people debate, well, who can beat \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 ? Can trump be \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 ? Can DeSantis beat Joe? \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is 20 points worse in this election comes down to 100,000 votes in five states. That's it. You're talking razor thin margins.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: And your state of Wisconsin will be critical.\par \par \uc2 PRIEBUS: And we're here --\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: If there's any Republican that's going to have a chance at winning the presidency, they always have to run the table. Wisconsin is a big part of that equation.\par \par \uc2 PRIEBUS: The -- and Wisconsin was won by less than point-seven percent of the vote in 2016 and 2020. It comes down to about 20,000 votes. And, you know, I think that, you know, this debate tonight was a great debate. Every one of these folks are better than \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . Unfortunately, I also think and it's a little harsh to say and I didn't want it this way. I wanted \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to be here.\par \par \uc2 But I think he probably made the right decision after seeing tonight because tonight --\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Why do you say that?\par \par \uc2 PRIEBUS: Because I think it looked a little bit as an undercard debate tonight. And I --and I think that that having the price fighter, I think not having \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  on that stage, took down the temperature a little bit, made it a little less, I think attractive. And I think that -- I think that he probably made the right decision --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: From the other candidates, I mean, let's be honest, this guy is sucking out 95 percent of the oxygen in the entire campaign.\par \par \uc2 PRIEBUS: Right.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: They should have been happy that this was a moment for them to get their opinions across and introduce themselves. Like in the case of the \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2  who, you know, who am I? Why am I here?\par \par \uc2 PRIEBUS: Right. But who is going to take a percentage of the vote from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  after tonight? And maybe they can do it. Maybe they can spin it out their way but the question is and this is dishonest, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is at 55 percent. What percentage of that 55 percent are one of these other people is going to -- people going to take from him?\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: That's really the question here, Sean. It's that he's got these four different indictments. He's got -- he wasn't here at the debate. If polls don't move appreciably after this, it's hard to know what any of them can do to effectuate it. Now, I was like you. I wanted President Trump to come to the debate. I believe in direct democracy. This is his sweet spot, his natural habitat. We want to hear from him.\par \par \uc2 But if the point of this debate was to introduce yourself to voters who don't know you, he's the most popular person on the -- political person on the planet best known. And number two, he's got a whole record of accomplishments. Tonight, \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  tried to say these are the Trump-Pence accomplishments. So, he didn't need to be here for that. I think it was smart in the end for him not to come because it -- he -- they proved his point that without him -- without him, it's not as exciting. It's not as animated.\par \par \uc2 And you know what, gentlemen, they all talked a good game against him. And they hardly laid a glove on him tonight. They're going to pretend they wanted to ignore him. But they did that because they know that in attacking Trump, you're alienating his voters. They can't do that.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: And that's a big part of the base. We're at a -- we're at an inflection point I think, though, for the country. How does America reconcile? And I think this is really -- how do you reconcile securing the borders or Joe's policies of what? In three years, he'll have led in maybe eight million illegal immigrants. How do you reconcile climate alarmism with the belief that we should be energy independent and energy dominant?\par \par \uc2 How do you reconcile law and order versus defund, dismantle the police? How do you reconcile, you know, a school choice versus, you know, catering to the teacher unions or foreign policy and Ukraine? I don't see middle ground. One side is going to win in November 2024. One side is going to lose.\par \par \uc2 PRIEBUS: Right. And, you know, we were living in zero sum game and politics. We're moving 40, 50,000 votes in five states is a difference between winning or losing the entire Electoral College. We're going -- we're -- we've become a country playing Ping Pong between Republicans and Democrats in complete Republican control, complete Democratic control, back and forth. And it's like night and day.\par \par \uc2 The other thing is that division is very profitable in America. As long as you're dividing, you're making money and when you're -- and unity is a loser, unfortunately. And that's where we are. It's a very divided country and I don't know how to fix it.\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: We have to bank those votes early too. I'm glad the \uc2 RNC\uc2  has to bank --\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I'm glad the \uc2 RNC\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: I'm glad President Trump has endorsed it. I've told the --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: The next thing you have to do is get in the legal ballot harvesting game.\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: Correct.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Great to see you both.\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 PRIEBUS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: You bet. Love the people in your state. Really do. Wonderful. All right. By the way, we will be talking to a lot of the candidates in just a moment. But first for more reaction, we bring in the host of "THE INGRAHAM ANGLE" while the band is back together, the one and only Laura Ingraham. You know, you know, you -- I was watching you with Bill hammer and Dana Perino and you said something, I've always believed that debates are often about moments.\par \par \uc2 And I thought at different times, most of the candidates had a moment tonight. What did you think?\par \par \uc2 LAURA INGRAHAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: I agree with you, Sean. I think it's starting sort of backwards. I think Nikki Haley was the best establishment candidate. You know, she had some zingers but it wasn't good enough to kind of justify for the, you know, most of the electorate. A lot more money to Ukraine. Probably not. I think in the end. I mean, Christie is always the best talker. He's probably the best debater up on stage.\par \par \uc2 But it just -- actually connecting with the Republican electorate, it's DeSantis and \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2 . But after the end of this whole debate, did any of them really make the case to the American people that they're better, they would be better than Trump to win the nomination? I think that's a hard one. You know, having moments. That's one thing. But actually, having a record to run on and say I did this when I was president, the only one that comes close is DeSantis.\par \par \uc2 And I think he got better as the night went on. He started off maybe a little too stern. But as the night got -- night went on, I think he got better and better. So, I think DeSantis and Vivek and the pylon on \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2  with Nikki Haley and \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2 , I thought that --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Let me ask you this. You say he got better as time went on. I thought his opening answer was very powerful. And that is, this is a nation in decline. And now that to me kind of set the tone of the night and that is OK. All -- most of the people with the exception like \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  or Asa.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Most of the guys on this stage would point this country in a totally different direction with I believe conservative policies that would benefit every American.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Right. You're right, Sean. Obviously, this is a Republican primary though, right? So, there are differences in the Republican Party. The old establishment more represented by someone like \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 . Obviously, he's a very smart senator. He's done a lot of great things. But is he the future of the party? Probably not. So, I -- again, Vivek and -- as a young guy and \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  as still young with an incredible record as governor, those two men to me stood out as -- really the potential future of the party.\par \par \uc2 Is it enough to overcome Trump? I'm kind of with, you know, your previous panel. I think that's going to be pretty - -that's going to be pretty tough. Maybe things will change. But I again, I think Vivek coming out and just taking on everyone on that stage. That's pretty gutsy. I mean, maybe some people were annoyed by it, but I thought it was pretty gutsy. I enjoyed it. I thought it was -- I thought it was a fantastic event tonight --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I thought it added to the flavor of it, for sure.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: You bet. I enjoyed it and --\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Laura, great to have you. I miss our handoffs.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And I like just talking to you again.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I miss you attacking me every night. It's great to see you.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I know. I don't have -- thanks for putting on the tie for me, Sean. Good to see.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: You're very welcome. Thanks for pointing it out, as usual. All right. Joining us right here in the spin room, the man in the middle of the stage tonight, Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . Governor, how are you? Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: Hey, good to see you.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: You know, I thought we -- she was saying that -- Laura was saying that she thought you got stronger throughout the night. But I thought your open was particularly strong. And it's a message that most Americans believe. This is a nation in decline, then you went into American first policies, then you talked about Ukraine, you know, we're not securing our borders. Why are we -- why are we spending all of that money when Europe won't even step up?\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: You know, look, I think that the nation is in decline. But this decline is a choice. And we're going to make this choice over the next 18 months. We can reverse the decline. We can restore America. I think it's similar to the late 1970s when \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2  was president. A lot of people said it was an age of limits. America wasn't going to be able to be great again, and \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  had a different idea.\par \par \uc2 So, that's just I think what the average American is seeing in their own daily lives. And so, in some respects, we can point out all the bad things, and there's a lot of bad things, but I am optimistic that it can be done. You know why? Because we did it in Florida. Everything I laid out in Florida, we accomplished and the state has boomed. We can do the same thing for the U.S.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: OK. So that was a big part of your theme tonight. This is Florida. 2018. You had a pretty close race. I was down in Florida covering that race as you know. And then you come back and four years later, you win by a record million and a half votes. That would be -- I would think validation for your governing skills for the state. And you kept talking about, I will not let you down. Explain.\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: Well, look, I think that there's a lot of people that get elected they over promise under deliver, when times get really tough there's a lot of people that aren't going to stay in the fire. And what I did in Florida, particularly during COVID, making sure the kids can be in school protecting everyone's business as a job.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Your kids were back in school in August of 2020.\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: Right. And I got pilloried for that. But when I stand my ground for you, you can take that to the bank. I am not going to back down. I've taken on the left and the Democrats in the media. And we've beaten all those people. And I think that when -- in Florida, part of the reason I did so well is because people -- they knew where I stood and they knew look, if the governor says he's going to do something, he's going to do it.\par \par \uc2 He doesn't just make idle promises, when he says it will do it. So, I think that's the same thing as president. I'm campaigning on a bold agenda. But I am not saying anything that I don't intend to follow through with. And so, when I talk about using the military to take on the drug cartels, because they're killing tens of thousands of our citizens. We have every right to do it. I'm going to do it. I'm not just going to get an office and say forget about it.\par \par \uc2 So, people should know that. Now's the time to get it done. I think our country, if we get another four years of this, I don't know if we could bring it back.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I don't know if we recover. I really don't. So, I'd be negligent if I didn't ask you. I asked this question to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and it was about I said, you and Governor DeSantis. I said, because you guys were friends. He did support you. And --\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: And I supported him. I mean, I -- we -- I support him in '16, obviously. And then in 2020 they really relied on me, you know, to do a lot in Florida. I raised more money than him than any elected official in the country. I mean, I raised, you know, 10 $15 million. And we worked really hard to make sure the Florida election ran really well. You know, we have the best election integrity in the country and sorted out --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: By the way, there were two points when it wasn't the best, right?\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: Yes. And we got it done. And so that's really important. And so - - and, you know, the thing is I mean, I know he attacks me a lot now but that's just, you know, when I started to get steam for reelection, you know, he came out and started dinging me. That's fine. I don't think he should do that. But it is what it is. But I do --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: When I did ask him what happened. He did say I supported him, he felt it was disloyal. What -- did you ever respond to that?\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: No. Here's the thing. Sean, I'm loyal to my family, the Constitution and the Lord our God. I work with other politicians to advance a common agenda. But nobody's entitled to be endorsed or supported. You've got to earn that. And I would mind you, you know, he started attacking me before I even got in -- before I even got reelected, he started to do it. So that's fine. But at the end of the day, we're going to -- we're going to do what we need to do.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Let's ask about the controversial issue of abortion. And you signed a six-week bill, the heartbeat bill. Now, look, I don't think anybody really anticipated Roe v. Wade would be overturned, right? It was been in -- been in matter of laws?\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: Most Republican justices have led us down over the years.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Right,\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: So even when Dobbs was pending, a lot of people said Kavanaugh, are they going to do it? And so, they did do it, though, and they deserve credit.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: But it's an issue that went back to the states. Do you support this more as a state issue or do you think it should be federal legislation?\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: So, what I've said is one our heartbeat bill, I'm proud of. It was the right thing to do. The reality in our country right now is advancing protections for life is bottom up. That's just the reality based on kind of the demographics and the public opinion and whatnot. And so, you're not going to have Wisconsin do what Texas is done, just like Iowa is going to be different than New Hampshire. That's just where we find ourselves. And so, you know, I'm going to be it.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Should it stay a state issue or should federally there be a standard?\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: Well, look, if there is something to prevent, like late term abortions, that's been done before. I mean, obviously, you know, as a pro- life president, I'm going to support that.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: You pointed that out too. And I think this is key. There is an extreme radical left-hand position. Oh, the government, it should not get in the room with a patient and a woman. And -- OK. So that that position basically is you can have an abortion up until the moment of birth.\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: And they will not -- a Democrat politician, if you ask them, when does the child get protection? Nine months, they will not give an answer.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: No, they don't.\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: When we point this out, the corporate media, the liberal media, they run interference for the Democrats. So, they attack us for being pro- life. And then they go to the Democrats and act like the Democrats aren't taking very, very extreme positions.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I was glad you did fire those two prosecutors that refused to do their job. I thought that went over really well.\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: We need that nationally. I mean, these cities are war zones in some of the places. And it's all because of leftist ideology. Any place this ideology has been put in, those cities are failing. I'm going to clean it up as president.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: You're looking forward to \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: Yes. It's going to be fun. We got to get -- we got to get the date and the venue and everything.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: We got the date. We're working on the venue part. That's really -- we have one little piece of the puzzle left and I think we'll get it done. I didn't want to bother him this week with all the bad weather and issues.\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: Yes, yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Governor, great to see you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: Thank you. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Appreciate you for being on.\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: OK. Now, one of the candidates who took a lot of heat tonight was a relative newcomer to the Republican Party. Self-described outsider. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: So first, let me just address a question that is on everybody's mind at home tonight. Who the heck is this skinny guy with a funny last name? And what the heck is he doing in the middle of this debate stage? I'll tell you, I'm not a politician, Bret. You're right about that. I'm an entrepreneur. My parents came to this country with no money 40 years ago. I have gone on to found multibillion dollar companies.\par \par \uc2 I did it while married my wife Apoorva. Raising our two sons, following our faith in God, that is the American dream. And I am genuinely worried that that American dream will not exist for our two sons and their generation unless we do something about it. And I do think, Bret, it's going to take an outsider because for a long time, we have professional politicians in the Republican Party who have been running from something.\par \par \uc2 Now is our moment to start running to something. To our vision of what it means to be an American today. If you have a broken car, you don't turn over the keys to the people who broke it again, you hand it over to a new generation to actually fix the problem. That's why I'm in this race and we're just getting warmed up.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. Vivek. Ramaswamy. Vivek, I've been calling you Vivek. Why didn't you correct me?\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: Well, you know, Vivek --\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I didn't know that.\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: -- that's OK. It's like cake.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: OK. You should have told me.\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: I appreciate best efforts. I appreciate best efforts.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. So, it was interesting tonight. I thought you were an interesting position. You brought a lot of energy and a smile and you seem to be having fun up there. I'm the only one on stage not bought and paid for. Ouch. I thought they were -- they were coming after you pretty good. Christie came back at you and said, oh, OK, you know, compared you to \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 . A guy with a long last name and liberal, you know, obviously.\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: Yes. And I encouraged \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  to give me a hug because he did that to \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 . And that actually -- unfortunately --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: It's a good point.\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: -- charm for \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 . But the reality is, everybody on that stage had been on a debate stage before. This is my first time in a political debate and I am thrilled with how it came out. We over exceeded my expectation. Frankly, even Trump and as an outsider last time around, I think his second debate was a lot better than his first.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Right.\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: Frankly, this went way better than I expected.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I mean, I think -- I think obviously, one of the reasons is you're very media savvy, you have a -- you have a warm and enticing personality, big smile.\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Politicians don't smile enough. They need to smile more.\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: I'm not a politician and honestly it was fun.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: OK. I hate to tell you. You're now officially a politician.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: -- into being a politician.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Yes. You're in.\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: OK. Officially, I'm going to have to cringe but wear that label. But I do think, Sean, it's going to take an outsider. I do not think whether it's ending the war in Ukraine, whether it's reviving this economy. And the war that I really do want to fight is the war on the administrative state and the shadow government here at home. That is not going to happen from somebody who comes from the super PAC class.\par \par \uc2 It's not going to come from somebody who comes from the historical Republican Party. It is going to take an outsider. And the other thing, Sean, that I think is really important in this election, is that we're skating on thin ice, this has to be a moral mandate. This cannot be another 50.1 election. And I'm in this race to build a multi-ethnic working-class majority like Reagan did.\par \par \uc2 This has to be a landslide, young, old, black, white, Hispanic, man, woman doesn't matter. I'm running this campaign that way. We're going to the Southside of Chicago to Kensington, in the inner city of Philadelphia where Republican candidates are taught not to go in a primary. But the reality is, that's why I don't mention Republicans and Democrats on the stage. And I'm a little different than the other candidates here because everybody else was focused on \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and I think \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's a disastrous president.\par \par \uc2 But I think the way we win in a landslide is we have to offer an independent vision of our own. And the more we do that, the more likely we are to win.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: OK. So, there were a few shots, not a time for on the job training.\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: You don't have that background, that experience. You have an impressive personal story talking about your parents, my parents, my mom was a prison guard. My dad waited tables on weekend and was a family court probation officer.\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: Look where you are now.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: You know, by the way, they don't believe it and they're in heaven. So, they're looking down saying, God, how did that happen?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: My mom thought I'd be on the other side of the prison. That's a joke.\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: Well, I think -- I think it's a remarkable --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: But the point is --\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: But it's a remarkable story. That -- the fact that your story --\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: That's the American story. Right.\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: The extraordinary thing about our stories, Sean, is that they're not out of the norm. There's normal in the United States of America.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Are you confident -- are you confident that you can convince the American people that, you know what, I've got enough experience that can -- that can take care of this country's needs?\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: Yes. And that is my job in this race. This was the first chance that I really had to introduce myself directly to the people. And I believe I will be the next nominee. I believe I will win the general election in a landslide that reunites this country. And I do think it's going to take --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: You answer the question. You would pardon \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , would you pardon the Bidens?\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: No. I mean, it depends on -- I don't want to be them. Right? The real heart of the problem with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  is that I believe they're selling off our foreign policy to make money. And if that's proven in a court of law, they should be in prison, probably for the rest of their life.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Oh, I think that's --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: But I don't want to be them. So, I will let the -- I want to see impeachment inquiries proceed. And if they're convicted in a -- in a court of law, absolutely. They deserve full punishment for that. And the facts look pretty ugly to me as they stand.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Vivek, anytime I mispronounced them, you have every right to correct me and I wish you would have earlier because -- but I