ity, when I look at the challenges facing America, and I look at the experiences we've had, I'm simply the most experienced, proven, tested conservative in this field. And it's that kind of experienced conservative leadership that's going to be required to turn this country around.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: If you look on the issue of borders, if you look on the issue of energy independence, if you look at the issue of American foreign policy and law and order and on so many of these issues, then you guys got hit with a pandemic. You know, the worst one in a hundred years. And I think that threw everybody for a loop. And nobody in the beginning knew anything, let's be honest.\par \par \uc2 And I think everyone was just flailing around a little bit, trying your best to understand the magnitude of what we were dealing with here. That certainly, up to that point, you had set record low unemployment for every single demographic in the country and achieved energy independence, and beat the caliphate, and Soleimani, and Baghdadi and associates. A lot of success there.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: Well, it's one of the reasons that --\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: And trade deals and you know --\par \par \uc2 PENCE: I tried to get back to a little bit on COVID after Governor DeSantis, you know, lines about that.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: And he's entitled his opinion, but he's not entitled his own facts. I mean look when I was tapped two months into that task force to lead the White House Coronavirus Task Force by President Trump, our number one focus was to save lives, to make sure health care workers had the testing and the resources the supplies and ultimately the vaccines that would see our nation through this.\par \par \uc2 We spun up support for families and businesses in paycheck protection and there was an enormous investment in that. But I believe that's what government is for. You know, when the roof falls in, when hard times strike a nation, that's why you have to balance your budget. That's why you have to be on a solid fiscal foundation. One of the things we never talked about tonight was we have a national debt the size of our nation's economy today.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Scary.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: For the first time since World War II. And I was the first one to be willing to step up and say, look, as President of the United States, I'm going to say to everybody over the age of 40, no changes in Social Security or Medicare. But for Americans under the age of 40, I want to have a conversation.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: It's headed towards insolvency.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  --\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: That's all there is to it.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: Yes, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's policy is insolvency and candidly my former Rennie Mays policy is identical to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's, but even you know I had to call out Vivek on that. He literally said that he wasn't going to do anything about the national debt, Social Security, and Medicare reform because he said --\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: A president can't do everything.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: A president can't do everything. And I just said to him, you know, I was in the building, I was in the hallway, I was in the Oval Office for four years almost every day. Presidents don't get to choose. You have to look those crises in the eye, you have to provide leadership and solve them, and I will.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Vice President Pence, great to see you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: Thank you, Sean.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right, more now. Straight ahead.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right, welcome back to HANNITY. We're live. We're in the spin room. We're in beautiful Milwaukee, Wisconsin, following the first GOP presidential primary debate. Here with more on tonight's event, DeSantis, campaign surrogate, and Texas Congressman Chip Roy, Trump campaign surrogate, Florida Congressman Byron Donalds, and Fox News Contributor \uc2 Ari Fleischer\uc2 , who I have no idea who he's supporting. So, I'm not going to -- that actually helps. You're going to be the neutral voice. So, you guys, the funny thing is, I know we're all best friends and you guys agree on a lot of stuff, but you're taking a different position in the primary.\par \par \uc2 REP. CHIP ROY (R-TX): Yes, I mean, I'm happy to be here with my good friend Byron. We can show to America.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: No, no, no, we'd rather have a brawl.\par \par \uc2 ROY: Well, we'll get there. We'll get there. Like, we can disagree on the primary and then still lock arms to go fight the establishment in Washington. We do it every day. Happy to do it. But look, I'm proud to endorse \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . And I think tonight he showed why he's the clear leader. I think he was a clear winner of the debate. He was speaking directly to the American people. He showed how his track record in Florida is something that the American people can get behind because they're frankly sick of the noise.\par \par \uc2 There was a lot of noise on that stage. There was a lot of bickering and talking to each other. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  was the guy who was standing up there as a guy who served in the military, who demonstrated how he would use that to be commander in chief and showed that he believes that this is a country in decline and that the Florida blueprint will provide a path for us to get this country back.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Byron.\par \par \uc2 REP. BYRON DONALDS (R-FL): Look, I love my friendship but I don't know what he's talking about. I thought that Governor DeSantis was fine but fine was not good enough in this moment. He had to be dynamic. He had to take presence of the entire debate, all the bickering back and forth. And frankly, a lot of the candidates, some of them had really good points, the governor was good, but he wasn't dynamic. And he has to be dynamic because the elephant, the true elephant in the room is \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . He's massively ahead. And all the policies, Sean, that everybody's talking about, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  did it. He did it once, he could do it again. That is still the leader in this entire race.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: How, I mean, he's now going to be arraigned tomorrow. This is the fourth time. I believe our DOJ has been weaponized and politicized, all that's true. I mean, we have a president now, we have the \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  bribery and money laundering scandal allegations that I believe are the walls closing in on the entire family enterprise. My question is, you know, how much does that impact this race in your view?\par \par \uc2 DONALDS: Look, I think in our primary process what it's really doing is it is going to continue to galvanize support around \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , that all goes the facts.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: It defies all -- listen I've been in this 34 years, it defies all conventional, you know, political --\par \par \uc2 DONALDS: It defies conventional political wisdom and political wisdom in our country for a long time has been flat wrong. What it's done is that people are realizing we have a two-tier system. This is not working. And if they're going to go after \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  like this, they'll do it to anybody.\par \par \uc2 ROY: I 100 percent agree with that, Byron, 100 percent. But the elephant in the room that we're talking about, as Bret Baier pointed out, wasn't here. And the elephant in the room isn't going to be there at the Reagan Library. And the American people expect you to show up. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  shows up. Byron talked about the track record of success. The governor's track record of success in Florida is something that these candidates didn't want to run against because they're so proud of what he did in Florida and how that's the model for America.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Let me bring Ari, the neutral voice in here. What do you say, Ari?\par \par \uc2 ARI FLEISCHER\uc2 , FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Yes. And I am neutral. My job here is to tell you what I see and just call it as I see it. And I thought \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  had the strongest night of any of the candidates on stage. He did it with his answer on lockdowns, reminding people that Florida opened early when it was controversial to do so. He did it with his answer about use of force involving Mexico and drugs, and he did it about focusing on the future instead of the past.\par \par \uc2 So, I thought DeSantis was real good. Second place to me was \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2 . I mean, that guy is a, he's a blunt force instrument. And he's articulate, he's fast on his feet, and he's not a politician. And that's one of the reasons he's resonating right now. He just says what he thinks, and he says it in a forceful, blunt way. And people are sick of politicians, they're sick of Washington, and that's why Vivek did well.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. Thanks, Ari. Appreciate it. Byron, good to see you. Chip, good to see you. Unfortunately, that is all the time we have left this evening. As always, thank you for being with us. As always, thank you for being with us.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document SHAN000020230824ej8n00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 GOP Voters On What They Want To Hear Tonight; \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  Booked In G.A. Election Case; Report: Russian Mercenary Leader Dies In Plane Crash; Biden Camp Not Committing To Debating GOP Nominee; Walker: DeSantis Should Learn From My Mistakes; Hours Away From First High-Stakes GOP Debate\b0\par\par\uc2 Martha MacCallum, Bill Hemmer, Dana Perino, Karl Rove, Trey Gowdy,  Trey Yingst, Daniel Hoffman, Kellyanne Conway \par \uc2 8,140 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 August 2023\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS, FOX NEWS HOST (on camera): Thank you for the update, appreciate it. Well, Sandra, that was a news-filled two hours, and we've got a lot more coming up tonight.\par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS HOST: All right. And for continuing coverage, watch right here on the Fox News Channel, set your DVR, and never miss AMERICA REPORTS. Thanks for joining us. I'm Sandra Smith.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: And I'm \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 . "THE STORY" starts right now with one of our co-moderators of the debate. Here's Martha.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: We are in downtown Milwaukee, and it's getting exciting around here. You can see the press tents going up behind me as all the reporters come into town to cover this event. Usually, when you think of Milwaukee, you think of beer, and cheese, and the Packers, and of course the \uc2 Milwaukee Bucks\uc2 . But right now, in this town, it is all about politics and it is all about tonight, where Bret Baier and I will co- moderate the very first Republican primary debate. It's going to be blockbuster.\par \par \uc2 Indeed, it is, and here we are in the final few hours before candidates take the stage right behind us inside Fiserv Forum. And before we get to that, we've got breaking news out of Russia. A lot going on this afternoon. We've also got new polling out of the crucial state of Iowa that suggests that Republicans watching this debate tonight say that they can be persuaded, they can be won over by any of these candidates. So, we've been talking to those voters all week about what matters most to them tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I'm really hoping to see the candidate that actually talks about the issues in the country and not about each other. We got to be focused on train on or fire on \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Their plan for the economy.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: My concern is that they all address the issues around personal safety.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What are you looking to do to help the average ordinary person?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I want to hear them tell me why I should vote for them. I don't want them to tell me why I shouldn't vote for the other person.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK, so let's bring in our fantastic panel today. Dana Perino and Bill Hemmer, Co-Hosts of "AMERICA'S NEWSROOM," of course. Karl Rove, former Deputy Chief of Staff to President \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  and a Fox News Contributor, and \uc2 Trey Gowdy\uc2 , the Host of "SUNDAY NIGHT IN AMERICA." Great to have all of you with us.\par \par \uc2 BILL HEMMER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Good afternoon.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Wow, what a fantastic panel. So, I, you know, Dana, let me start with you. You listen to those voters and you hear what's on their mind. If you were helping one of these candidates prepare tonight, what would you be telling them at this point?\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: I think part of it is that you have to shed any sort of pre-planned, pre-canned, artificial sound bite that you want to try to land, OK? I think that what these people are looking for, and we heard it over and over again, they want authenticity and they want to know that how life might be different under your presidency. What would that look like? And these voters are thoughtful. And the economy, the border, the crime issues in the country are all super important, but really, I think they've got to figure out a way to say how would the economy actually be better if they were to take over the presidency?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, and you know, Bill, you look across the country and you know all of these different counties, you know what Wisconsin looks like on the ground here. It's always a razor-tight state, it has trended a little bit more blue than red in the past election, but what do you think about being in Wisconsin tonight, what do you think people at home should understand about that dynamic?\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: You know, to \uc2 Dana\uc2 's point, we had a great focus group this morning on our program. We had nine folks that live in Southeastern Wisconsin, a few from the Milwaukee area, a few from the counties outside. And I would rate immigration number one. I would rate economy number two. And what I heard from their answers from a lot of them was leadership and they wanted direction from whomever is on stage tonight. But I think Scott Walker, the former governor here in Wisconsin, was very popular, he did -- and, and very successful too.\par \par \uc2 He relayed a story that I have not heard of before and I don't know if the folks here in Wisconsin are aware of it or not but he was on our program. You have to remember in the summer of two thousand fifteen in Cleveland, right, where there they have the big debates and he's flying high and he was in and out of New York raising a lot of money and he but he was well up there in terms of the running and he said he went into that debate any listen to his consultants and his super pack guide him on his message, and he, and he said the guy next to me is \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and he's not relying on any of that stuff, and Walker fell flat in his campaign really imploded that night. I think that's, like that's the drama of tonight. Can you rise like a volcano or is your campaign about to implode on that stage behind us?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, so Karl what do you think about that? What do you think about people telling their candidates don't listen to the donors, don't listen to the Super PACs, don't listen to what everybody's telling you, you should be saying up there, do your own thing?\par \par \uc2 KARL ROVE, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Yes, well the purpose of a debate prep is to help the candidate be his or her best self. Dana's absolutely right. Most important thing is authenticity. People are going to be watching these people tonight on the stage and they're going to be saying, is that really who they are? And they develop a sense for it. So, the most important thing is, help them understand the facts, help them understand the weaknesses, help them understand the strengths of their arguments, but most important of all, help them hone the sense of who they are, themselves on their best day, in their best way.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, Trey, I'm struck by the fact that so far, a lot of these candidates have been, you know, in campaign events, they've been at the Iowa State Fair, they've been talking to people who show up to see them. This is the first time that they're going to have, hopefully, questions that hold them to the things that they've said against what they've actually done in their political life, straightforward. But and then, they're going to have other people coming at them on either side of them as well. What you know, do you think they're ready for that?\par \par \uc2 TREY GOWDY\uc2 , FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: I think some of them are. I mean, this is like the first date for 47 of the 50 states. I mean, South Carolina, New Hampshire, Iowa, they're familiar with these candidates. But here's what I'd tell them. Look, you know, there's I don't mean to be like overly cynical, but you're 40 points down. You are 40 points down to somebody who was facing criminal trial in four different jurisdictions.\par \par \uc2 So, being more of yourself, I would argue right now, it's important to be authentic. And once you learn a fake authenticity, there's nothing you can't do in the world. But something's going to have to change, because you're down like five touchdowns right now. He's forgotten more about politics than I'll ever know. But I think a three-pronged plan for the economy is probably not going to cut it. You're going to have to say why you're electable and the guy that's not here is not.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, well said. So, this is Jamie Harrison, the DNC chair, talking about his take on what's happening tonight. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JAMIE HARRISON, DNC CHAIR: Tonight, in primetime, Americans will have an opportunity to see in action the most chaotic slate of presidential candidates in history. When these MAGA 2024 Republicans take the debate stage here in Milwaukee.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Dana?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Well, the thing is that, of course, he's a \uc2 Democratic National Committee\uc2  chair, he's going to have a little bit of hyperbole. This country is still a center-right nation and what a lot of people are going to hear from these candidates, and we know a lot of them, we don't know how they're going to perform tonight, but we know a lot of them, and they're not extreme. And so, the caricatures that the Democrats and many in the media have used to try to paint all of them, that is actually off the table tonight, and these candidates have a chance to introduce themselves to America.\par \par \uc2 I was surprised this morning looking at this \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  report about name ID, and still after all these months, we're very focused on it. Most of America is not. Some of them still have the lowest name ID that they could imagine. So, tonight, begins this opportunity. And they're not just talking to Republican-based voters. We won't just have Republicans watching this debate tonight. There'll be a lot of Democrats, even though they probably won't admit it, they're going to watch. But who also is going to watch? The independents, especially the suburban women that help really determine who will be the next president of the United States.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, I think it's so true and we've seen an independent polling in Iowa for example that DeSantis and Trump are a lot closer, they're very tight among independent voters there. Carl, just a quick thought is the president, the former president overconfident with these numbers which obviously, to Trey's point, are extremely substantial in this moment.\par \par \uc2 ROVE: Oh, yes, yes, I mean the it would be better if he were here because, otherwise, he looks like he taking everybody for granted. And the way that his team is approaching this thing belittling everybody if it was important to belittle everybody then you should have done it in person.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK, guys. Thank you so much. Great to see all of you. And we'll be all together throughout this whole process. Thank you very much guys. Great to see you all this afternoon.\par \par \uc2 So, breaking right now, a live look at the Fulton County Jail in Atlanta. This is where former President Trump attorney, where his attorney, I should say, \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2 , has officially been booked now on charges relating to the 2020 election interference in Georgia. The former New York City mayor defiant before boarding a plane from New York to Georgia just earlier.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUDY GIULIANI\uc2 , FORMER ATTORNEY TO \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : The wall wrote there must be something to this. Where there's smoke there's fire. Well, there was fire. It was their fire. They're dishonest. They're crooked. They want to destroy the Constitution of the United States, and they're doing it. And if we don't change it, they're coming for you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, the former mayor there of New York City, he will be released on a $150,000 bond. Former President Trump says that he will surrender to authorities in Georgia sometime tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 And we are following breaking reports in a very big story developing out of Russia this afternoon that a plane carrying the man who led a revolt against Putin and his military, has crashed from the skies, killing all, we believe, 10 people who were on board that plane.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Breaking right now, the leader of the mercenary group who led the brief revolt against Russia's military reportedly killed in a plane crash just a few hours ago. That is according to Russian media and some estimates are that it may have happened as much as 48 hours ago. We're getting more information on this as we speak. \uc2 Yevgeny Prigozhin\uc2  released his first video since the uprising that he led against Putin's military and the generals that he had major disagreements with him, saying his organization, the \uc2 Wagner Group\uc2 , is, "making Russia even greater on all continents."\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Trey Yingst, reporting live from Jerusalem. Hi, Trey.\par \par \uc2 TREY YINGST, FOX NEWS CHANNEL FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: Martha, good afternoon.\par \par \uc2 The leader of the \uc2 Wagner Group\uc2 , \uc2 Yevgeny Prigozhin\uc2  was killed in a plane crash outside of Moscow, according to Russian state media. Reports indicate the private jet had 10 people on board, three of them crew members when it was flying to St. Petersburg, and it was shot out of the sky, according to reports, by air defense surrounding the Russian capital.\par \par \uc2 The images we're seeing from the ground indicate a ball of fire falling out of the sky. The juxtaposition though, of interest, Russian President 80th anniversary of the Battle of Kursk at a concert in southern Russia, celebrating the 80th anniversary of the Battle of Kursk. He didn't comment on the plane crash directly, but his body language appeared to be taking a lack of victory.\par \par \uc2 He was smiling and interacting as Russian state media was showing these images of the plane crash.\par \par \uc2 Now, remember the timing of this is significant. Back in June, \uc2 Yevgeny Prigozhin\uc2  led a march on the Russian capital. He was trying to implement a coup and pull Russian President Putin from power. He was unsuccessful, but the moment was embarrassing for the Russian leader and could have led to what we are seeing today those reports in Russian state media.\par \par \uc2 Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Trey Yingst, thank you very much, reporting live from Jerusalem.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in Dan Hoffman, former CIA station chief and served in the former Soviet Union in that capacities. Also, spending five years in Moscow. He is also a Fox News contributor.\par \par \uc2 Dan, good to have you here today. What's your -- what's your reaction to all of this very dramatic events playing out in Russia today?\par \par \uc2 DANIEL HOFFMAN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Yes, I would say it's not entirely unexpected, and it's kind of the way that \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  does things.\par \par \uc2 Look, I think he allowed Prigozhin to roam free. It gave Prigozhin a false sense of security, all while Russia FSB, that's their internal security service, the one that \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  led in the late 1990s, was tracking Prigozhin, as a sniper would.\par \par \uc2 And when Prigozhin's plane took off, they had to file a manifest before they flew from Moscow to St. Petersburg, I think that \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  saw that is the best opportunity to eliminate a man he had called a traitor during the mutiny.\par \par \uc2 Putin could not allow Prigozhin to live after Prigozhin took -- lead that mutiny against the state.\par \par \uc2 First and foremost, \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  is messaging his own intelligence and security services, all of them need to know, especially the higher ups that if they betray \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , they are going to pay the ultimate price.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Indeed, this is \uc2 William Burns\uc2 , the CIA director back in July on this at the Aspen Security Forum. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM BURNS\uc2  DIRECTOR, \uc2 CENTRAL INTELLIGENCE AGENCY\uc2 : Putin is someone who generally thinks that revenge is a dish best served cold. So, he is going to try to settle the situation to the extent he can. But again, in my experience, Putin is the ultimate apostle of payback.\par \par \uc2 So, I wouldn't be surprised if Prigozhin escapes further retribution for this.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, it seems as if, you know, everybody is lining up on expecting that this could clearly happen to him. I would imagine he felt that this could happen to him at any moment as well.\par \par \uc2 What's the impact on Ukraine and Russia, and Russia's military operations right now? The \uc2 Wagner Group\uc2  has been very instrumental in, you know, keeping them as strong as possible on that front and others.\par \par \uc2 HOFFMAN: Yes, I think \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  may have assessed that to some extent, Wagner was going to be a spent force for the time being, because he had to vet any of those Wagner mercenaries who are going to be carrying on the fighting.\par \par \uc2 He also has to vet his own military, especially, in the Rostov military district on the border of Ukraine, which the Wagner forces led by Prigozhin walked in and took over during the initial stages of the mutiny.\par \par \uc2 I don't expect there's going to be a significant impact on the war. The Russians are dug in with their trenches, and the tank ditches are making it very slow going for Ukraine's counter offensive, which as we all know, lacks the F-16s, and the long-range attack artillery that would have given Ukraine the advantage that they need.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. Dan Hoffman, former Moscow station chief. Dan, always good to have you with us. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 HOFFMAN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, coming up, Kellyanne Conway, predicts the Democrat Party won't allow President Biden to debate his eventual GOP challenger.\par \par \uc2 And today, we got an answer to this question from Biden campaign co-chair Cedric Richmond. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Will President Biden commit to debating in the general?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We will show you what the answer was and the reaction from Kellyanne Conway, coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Busy afternoon here in Milwaukee. The Republican candidates are all buzzing around getting ready for their first big night, their first face off with each other.\par \par \uc2 And it's just hours away now here in Milwaukee. And the Biden campaign is now saying that they will not promise that there will be a debate with the GOP nominee.\par \par \uc2 Listen to this carefully. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Will President Biden commit to debating in general?\par \par \uc2 CEDRIC RICHMOND, CO-CHAIR, BIDEN CAMPAIGN: We have not had a conversation about that at all. I'm sure one day we will. But it's not today and I don't have any news on that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Let's bring in Kellyanne Conway, former senior counselor to President Trump, now president of KAConsulting. She has advised many candidates over the course of her career. And she is a Fox News contributor.\par \par \uc2 And we're delighted to have her with us, Kellyanne, great to have you.\par \par \uc2 KELLYANNE CONWAY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Thank you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, what did you make of that? I know you have said that you don't think that Biden will debate, and now, it looks like Trump isn't too interested in debating either. So --\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: I can understand why the frontrunner beating every one of these candidates in every poll at this moment would skip the first debate or two. But the fear I've had all along and have articulated is that it's going to open the door for Biden and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  to not debate. And that would be disastrous for the GOP. And I think unfair to the electorate. They have a right to see the candidate shoulder to shoulder live, confronting each other, being confronted by the debate moderators, who are well prepared for this.\par \par \uc2 There is, of course, there will be no rationale for the \uc2 Democratic National Committee\uc2  to want \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  to stand somewhere for 90 minutes and need to speak without a teleprompter and a break. Let alone \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , who battles a teleprompt every day and loses.\par \par \uc2 So, that's a concern I had. That's a long way from now. But I don't think the voters would let \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  get away with that. They are going to -- they already see according to everyone's polls, Martha, they think \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  lacks the mental acuity and the physical agility to be president just to get the job done. So, that could actually backfire against him.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. And we know that there are a couple of other candidates in the mix on the Democrat side. They would love to have the kind of debate that we're going to see tonight.\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And they would love to have an opportunity to tell Democrat voters what they would offer as an alternative to the current president.\par \par \uc2 Scott Walker this morning, speaking about \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , because there have been some comparisons made. There were high hopes for Scott Walker as he got into the Republican race back then. And, of course, President Trump ended up beating him.\par \par \uc2 So, let's listen to this from Scott Walker, former governor of Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. SCOTT WALKER (R), WISCONSIN: I think, Governor DeSantis, in particular, should learn from the mistakes I made. Just because you have a bold record, doesn't mean you're going to win. It gets you on the stage. But then, you got to be as bold or bolder in terms of your pronouncements what you're going to do.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Good advice from Governor Walker?\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: It's excellent advice. And Governor Walker deserves a ton of credit for how we won Wisconsin in 2016 in the Trump-Pence campaign, because he softened the ground here.\par \par \uc2 He had won, I think, three elections, including the special election, they tried to recall him in about 4-1/2 years.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Great point.\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: And show that it could be done.\par \par \uc2 Martha, it's a good advice. And it's actually advice that these candidates tonight, the eight of them can take in a different way than Walker himself could have leveraged, because Trump won't be on the stage.\par \par \uc2 So impossible to get around or over under Trump when he's on the stage. He has got a quip for everything, he looks resilient, he had center stage, he is not here.\par \par \uc2 And as I wrote on foxnews.com this morning, how these eight candidates deal with the absent president? He is not here, but he is hovering over, he is high in the polls, is important.\par \par \uc2 I think they should get in and out of it. They should explain which policies in the America First agenda they would continue, they support, which ones they disagree with? Would they pardon him? Perhaps, they'll have to answer that question.\par \par \uc2 But -- and then, I think there -- they need to show that they are visionary, unifiers. They've got something for independent, suburban women, young people, seniors.\par \par \uc2 I'd like to hear some of them talk about healthcare. 27 million Americans, 8.3 percent of our population, have no health insurance. 13 years after Barack Obama lied everyone would have it, keep the pan (PH), keep your doctor, education reform, crime, all these local issues, people want to hear presidents talk about it.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. And we've heard them telling us that ever since we got here. Kellyanne, thank you.\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We're going to talk to you a little bit later with some voters when they join us as well.\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: OK.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, we're looking forward to that about the issues that are driving the big debate tonight as we head in, and we'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN, FLORIDA VOTER: For me, I'm all in on \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . Yes, 100 percent. Well, I think he's got a proven track record. Yes, and what he's done in Florida -- I think his military service is impressive, and that stands out in the field. So, yes, excited.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Where are you from? (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 JOHN: We're actually from Florida. Yes, yes, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: No. No, just up visiting. So, we're actually here for the debate.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Interesting, right?\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I know.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We are back here live in Milwaukee with Kellyanne Conway, former senior counselor to President Trump, now President of KA Consulting and a Fox News contributor. We also have three Republican voters who are here to watch tonight, and we're going to talk to them about what they'll be listening for, looking for. Juliana Purcell is a member of the Young Americas Foundation, Fernando Riveron is a registered Florida voter, and Callie Harrell (ph) is a member of Moms for Liberty who previously worked for Wisconsin Senator Ron Johnson and currently volunteers with the GOP. Great to have all of you with us tonight.\par \par \uc2 So, Callie (ph), let me start with you. Are you open? Are you a persuadable voter at this point? Are you sticking with President Trump? Where are you?\par \par \uc2 CALLIE HARRELL (ph), MEMBER, MOMS FOR LIBERTY: So I will always back him and anybody that goes into the primary that is actually a part of the GOP, I will vote for him back. But I actually am swaying a little bit more towards DeSantis but I'm open to learning.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Why is that?\par \par \uc2 HARRELL (ph): Well, I back him a little bit more because of faith and family and love, and so just like what he stands on in those policies so.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. Is there anybody else that you're keeping a close eye on tonight?\par \par \uc2 HARRELL (ph): I'm looking to learn about every single one of them, so I'm keeping an open mind.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Good. That's what we're here for, absolutely. Fernando, tell me your thoughts. Did you vote for President Trump in the last round?\par \par \uc2 FERNANDO RIVERON, REGISTERED FLORIDA VOTER: I did.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: What about now?\par \par \uc2 RIVERON: I love President Trump's policy. I have been concerned that there's such turmoil surrounding him, and much of it is created, of course, by the media that wants to destroy him. But nonetheless, it distracts so much from the imperative, which is that we absolutely need to win this election.\par \par \uc2 What's happening with the Biden administration is so, not just dangerous, but destructive to our society that I think above all, regardless of any personality buddy, we need to make sure that what we stand for as Republicans wins in this election.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You're a heart surgeon, still working as a heart surgeon. And the economy comes up on every poll that we have, whether it's Republicans or voters across the board, as the number one issue. What's your number one issue?\par \par \uc2 RIVERON: Oh, I'm also a Cuban American. And so, came to this country and have benefited from the unbelievable fruits of freedom in this country. And it's terrifying to see how easily we've given up many of our freedoms. The last three years were just an amazing example of this. Our freedoms were abrogated. We lost our First Amendment rights, our rights to go to church so easily. So that is a terrifying thing to me.\par \par \uc2 So loss of -- the disinformation, that would be kind of Orwellian-Newspeak that we, you know, everything that you say that's true is really misinformation. Or there are fact checkers that are telling you that you're wrong. The loss of our civil liberties, those are frightening to me, and those are things that I think we need to just step up and fight against.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Interesting. Juliana, how do you look at the stage tonight? You say that you are very interested in DeSantis, from what you've told us. What issues are really the most important to you? You're a young woman involved at YAF, what do you really care about tonight?\par \par \uc2 JULIANNA PURCELL, MEMBER, YOUNG AMERICA'S FOUNDATION: Yes. What I really care about tonight, my first and foremost issue is pro life. So I'm not only looking for a candidate who says they're pro-life. It's easy to say you are pro-life, but I'm looking for a candidate who will walk the walk, who has pro-life policy in the past. And I think \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , he has that track record.\par \par \uc2 He has passed a six week abortion ban in Florida. He is fiercely and proudly pro-life, and that's what I'm looking for in the president. As well as great foreign policy, looking and standing up to China. And Ukraine, I want to hear foreign policy on that, as well as immigration. How is he going to secure the southern border? These are things I'm looking forward to hearing in the debate. And that's why I'm supporting \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  at this point but I'm really excited to see what --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: The former President has said that he thinks that abortion is kind of a loser issue for Republicans. What do you think about him saying that?\par \par \uc2 PURCELL: I disagree with him. Human life here is at stake. Science tells us that the pre-born are human lives. I think it should be the top issue that says -- the Constitution says that we have the right to life, liberty, and the pursuit of happiness. And I think, as a candidate, that's my top issue and I think I stand for a lot of students when I say that's what's front --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, Kellyanne, obviously a lot of educated, thoughtful voters here, and this is what we're hearing from everybody that we're speaking to, which I think is something that the candidates really need to stand up and pay attention to, right?\par \par \uc2 KELLYANNE CONWAY, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, you just saw one, two, three, why the candidates need to bring it on policy tonight. Not just personalities and politics, and even sound bites and trying to go viral, Martha, but to dig into actual solutions. We all know what the problems are, and there are many. Crisis and chaos everywhere.\par \par \uc2 Look, I want to say something about abortion since \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was the most pro-life president in history. It is true that these candidates, as I like to say, should be peacocks not ostriches on abortion. They should be proud of the gains for life. But a year after Dobbs, they've been losing on these state ballot initiatives, but it's supposed to go back to we the people, the states. I think to lose to a party, the Democratic Party, whose party platform is abortion anyone, anytime, anywhere, anyhow, the most extreme you can imagine would be pathetic.\par \par \uc2 The Democrats have become science deniers. They knew enough about science to mask up a five-year-old child during COVID. They look at a five-month sonogram and they can't tell you the limbs, the heartbeat. So we should be winning on that issue, not hiding from it. I agree with you. I will be watching for who tonight has a really great answer on that.\par \par \uc2 Last thing, everybody here was talking about the economy one way or the other. It's not just prices, it's not just everyday affordability, it's also financial security. You take away someone's financial freedom, you take away their very freedom. But it's also this feeling that the government is taking too much of what we earn, that everyday life is just difficult to meet the expenses. I'd like to hear specific plans tonight as to how they'll tackle that.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Callie (ph), do you feel that one?\par \par \uc2 HARRELL(ph): I do, I do. I actually do, because everything's just getting out of control, and so we just actually have to focus and work together and lead with peace enough, so.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Do you -- of the three of you, obviously, the former president is well ahead in the polls right now. Ultimately, if you had to say your best guess here today, who do you think the GOP candidate will be the nominee in the end? Callie (ph)?\par \par \uc2 HARRELL (ph): I would say Trump, honestly.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Fernando?\par \par \uc2 RIVERON: I'd like to say Trump, but this legal issue that's going on is so unpredictable that none of us can really understand how that's going to play out. So I don't know.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: What do you think, Juliana?\par \par \uc2 PURCELL: I agree. I think it will be \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK, thank you very much. Great to have all of you with us. Kellyanne, great to have you with us as well.\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you very much. All right, everybody. Also breaking today, watch what has been unfolding in Pittsburgh.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Boy, listen to that. Scary situation. All afternoon in Pittsburgh, it's been going on for hours now. Our senior national correspondent Rich Edson is following this story for us from our headquarters in New York. Hi, Rich.\par \par \uc2 RICH EDSON, FOX NEWS SENIOR NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: You know, Martha, in that video that you played there with the sound of gunshots that's been on and off for several hours, if we've been watching this. Police are moving in an armored vehicle as this standoff shuts down a significant part of Pittsburgh here, a significant part of this neighborhood in Pittsburgh here.\par \par \uc2 Neighbors say they have heard hundreds of shots over the past several hours. The Allegheny County Sheriff's Office tells Fox News this started when deputies went to this address to serve an eviction notice. One neighbor says deputies tried getting in the house for about 45 minutes when they announced they were moving in, and that's when the shooting started.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Oh, my Lord. At the urgent of my director, she just kept saying, get to the basement, get to the basement. So that was my goal, to get to the basement. Get to the basement. However, on my way to the basement, there was a barrage of bullets just coming from everywhere.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: This is in a residential neighborhood across the street from a large cemetery and about a half mile from a children's hospital. There are no reports of shooting victims here. Police cleared the neighborhood and are asking others to shelter in place. They say they also put up some drones that the shooter targeted.\par \par \uc2 ROCCO GAGLIARDI, \uc2 PENNSYLVANIA STATE POLICE\uc2 : We just sent up a couple more drones. Not the first time. The suspect did shoot at earlier, previous drones, right? So right now, it's an active gunfire situation right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EDSON: And there are local reports saying at one point, deputies ran out of bullets and the SWAT team had to come and escort them out. The \uc2 FBI\uc2  says it has agents on scene as well. Martha?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. Thank you, Rich Edson, reporting on what's unfolding today right now in Pittsburgh, Pennsylvania. And President Biden vacationing in Lake Tahoe right now as investigations into his son Hunter loom large over the president's reelection bid and has just revealed whether he plans to watch the big debate tonight. Brit Hume joins me live from Milwaukee coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Top priority is himself and his family. He don't care about the American people.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Himself.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Children, families, and education.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Breathing.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I don't know what his priority is. And I guess that speaks volumes, doesn't it?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Probably be the economy.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Keeping his kid out of jail.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I don't even know his priority. So I can get his name is Sleepy Joe because he doesn't do anything. That's how I know, because I don't know what his priorities are. Like from what I've known, he's tried to do the Ukraine thing.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I think his number one priority was trying to get rid of student loans, and he honestly failed at that because the Supreme Court definitely, like, booted him down.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. Former Trump attorney \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  speaking now after being booked at the Fulton County Jail, released on $150,000. Let's watch.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Do you believe the election was stolen?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN LIVE COVERAGE)\par \par \uc2 RUDY GIULIANI\uc2 , FORMER TRUMP: You know too well what this is all about. The \uc2 FBI\uc2  stole my iCloud account. You know when they went and stole it, the day that I began representing \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  four years ago. You know when they gave it back? The day after I represented \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Mr. Mayor, you effectively --\par \par \uc2 GIULIANI: All that time (inaudible) government was fine on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his lawyer. I am being indicted because I'm a lawyer.\par \par \uc2 (INAUDIBLE)\par \par \uc2 GIULIANI: By the way, you're wrong. I can do that. I entered into a stipulation for the purposes of that case to move on. I specifically says I do not --\par \par \uc2 (END LIVE COVERAGE)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. Obviously, the shot has frozen there -- is it back?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN LIVE COVERAGE)\par \par \uc2 GIULIANI: If you read it says it was only for the purposes of that case. It was not an admission.\par \par \uc2 (INAUDIBLE)\par \par \uc2 GIULIANI: It's lies, please. That is a total lie. Why don't you go but --\par \par \uc2 (INAUDIBLE)\par \par \uc2 (END LIVE COVERAGE)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Okay. A lot of chaos outside of Fulton County Jail right now, that is quite a scene. There's been 19 people who have to turn themselves in there as part of this Georgia case. That questions -- the actions that were taken by the part of the president and his lawyers after the 2020 election. That was \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  answering some questions, getting bumped around in the crowd there as a lot of people were tussling. He said the \uc2 FBI\uc2  had stolen his data from day one. So we'll keep an eye on that situation.\par \par \uc2 Obviously, the president is also going to turn himself over, the former president, tomorrow, so we'll keep an eye on what's going on at the Fulton County Jail.\par \par \uc2 So in the meantime, something going on here as well. The final hours to the first Republican presidential debate are before us, five hours and ten minutes to go on the clock. So President Biden is on vacation in Lake Tahoe right now for the second time this month, and he was asked by one of his representatives if he's going to be watching the big debate tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Will you watch the debates later?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF UNITED STATES: I'll try to get as much as I can.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What's your expectation for the GOP debate?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I have none.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I have none, he says. Brit Hume standing by but first to chief Washington correspondent Mike Emanuel. Hi, Mike.\par \par \uc2 MIKE EMANUEL, FOX NEWS CHIEF WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Martha. The first family's vacation comes as there is, of course, a special counsel probe into \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 . White House aides say President Biden is renting the $18 million home of environmental activist \uc2 Tom Steyer\uc2  at "fair market value."\par \par \uc2 We don't know much about who else is joining the Bidens. A White House spokesperson tells Fox Digital there have never been visitor logs during Biden family travel and they intend to follow that precedent. \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 , his wife and their son are joining the President and First Lady on this trip. Hunter is facing legal troubles after a proposed plea agreement for misdemeanor tax charges fell apart as House Republicans are digging into Biden family business dealings.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA), SPEAKER OF THE HOUSE: Provide us the documents we're asking. The whole determination here is how the Bidens handle this. If they withhold the documents and fight like they have now to not provide to the American public what they deserve to know, we will move forward with impeachment inquiry when we come back into session.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: David Weiss is the special counsel overseeing the Hunter Biden probe. Weiss once worked with the president's late son, Bo Biden, raising some eyebrows with Republicans. A prominent Senate Republican says the special counsel must act.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. RON JOHNSON (R-WI): This is obvious tax evasion, this is obvious criminal activity and David Weiss was willing to just look the other way for over five years and he's the one appointed special counsel. This is sick joke.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: There is clearly some GOP frustration feeling like the Biden administration has stonewalled Congress as lawmakers try to follow the money trail. Martha?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Mike, thank you very much, Mike Emmanuel reporting. Let's bring in Brit Hume, Fox News senior political analyst. Great to be with you here in Milwaukee.\par \par \uc2 BRIT HUME, FOX NEWS SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST: Thanks, Martha. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I saw the open for tonight's big debate which you voiced and I encourage everybody to tune in for that at the very beginning because it's a stunning piece that was put together --\par \par \uc2 HUME: And the best thing about it is it's just my voice, my face isn't on camera.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And your voice is fantastic. So everybody do not miss Brit's narrative at the very top of the show.\par \par \uc2 The Biden's are on vacation. People are allowed to take a vacation. He's with his family. A lot's been happening though over the course of that time and they have a lot of financial pressures on them. What's your assessment of the President's posture as we head into tonight?\par \par \uc2 HUME: Well, I think, I'm sure he looks at it and thinks if he can stave off this investigation into his son and his activities that he's in pretty good shape, because he and I think all the Democrats continue to believe that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is in the commanding position and will almost certainly be nominated. And having beaten \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  before, I think the President believes he can beat him again.\par \par \uc2 In fact, my sense of it is that if it weren't \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in the ascendancy in the Republican Party, that the Democrats wouldn't dare nominate \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  because he's too old. He's had all kinds of issues in his presidency, inflation, chief among them, the abandonment of Afghanistan that have gone down badly with people and they've lost confidence in him. But he still believes, and I guess they all believe that he can still beat \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So all the more reason in some ways for them to pay attention tonight because if something changes in this dynamic and we know that has happened in many prior presidential elections, granted it's one of the most substantial leads we've seen at this stage of the game, but anything can happen, Brit. So one of these folks on that stage could be the person running against.\par \par \uc2 HUME: Yes. Well, think about Biden himself back at the end of New Hampshire --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 HUME: -- in 2020. He finished fifth. Did anybody believe he was going to be the next president or even the next nominee? I don't think so. But things can change in a hurry. You're right.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So you said if he's able to stave off the troubles that plague his son --\par \par \uc2 HUME: Right.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: -- that's a very open question at this point, because now there's ongoing investigation. They thought this was going to go away with this plea deal, but now it hasn't. And our polls show that there's an increasing number of people who think that \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  did something illegal.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, I think if you ask the majority of people, they would certainly suspect that. And it does burden the president. Whether it'll burden him enough when the guy -- they think is going to be running against you is under four indictments. Biden isn't under any indictments,, at least not yet. And given the way this case has been handled, the \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  case has been handled, one does not sense that he's in any imminent danger, although more and more keeps coming out.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: What about President Trump? You saw those moments with \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2 , and there's a lot going on there. There's a lot of those moments still to come through the course of this election cycle. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 HUME: Think of a guy like \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2 . He was America's mayor.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HUME: And stood tall after his response to 911. And now look at him under indictment, I've heard, out of money. This is a consequence of his association and support of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , and it's brought him down. You think of the number of people that have ended up in real trouble after becoming part of the Trump team, and it hasn't been a good experience for a great many people.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I think that's true. And I'm sure the 19 people on that list would feel that way. When you look towards tonight, looking at the candidates that are going to be on that stage, what's at stake for them? What kind of dynamic are you watching for?\par \par \uc2 HUME: Well, look, everybody's running to be the alternative to Trump, right? And in a scattered field, you got eight candidates on the debate stage and several more who wish they were. Everybody's trying to do the same thing. It's hard to do in a large field like that, to stand out and emerge. So I think it's a big night for everybody, particularly for \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  who's lost ground so much in the polling.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. We'll be watching along with you. Brit, thank you very much. It's great to have you with us.\par \par \uc2 HUME: Thank you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. So our live coverage continues from Milwaukee right after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: There's a live look at the stage, very exciting. Everybody's buzzing around. The candidates are coming in and out, stand behind the podium, get a feel for everything with their staff. So that's what's going on over there right now.\par \par \uc2 And Fox can also confirm that North Dakota governor and Republican presidential candidate \uc2 Doug Burgum\uc2  will indeed participate in tonight's debate after tearing his Achilles while he was playing basketball earlier today. So, \uc2 Doug Burgum\uc2  was a little bit on the fence. They have come. They have done the walk around. There's only one flight of stairs, which he was glad to see, and he will indeed make this stage tonight.\par \par \uc2 So we look forward to a very big night tonight. I will see you then with Bret Baier coming up at 9:00 tonight, 9:00 to 11:00 tonight, Eastern Time for the first Republican debate. We'll see you there.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020230824ej8n00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Democrats Blast Republican Candidates Ahead of Debate; \uc2 Yevgeny Prigozhin\uc2  Reportedly Killed in Plane Crash; Interview With Fmr. Sen. Joe Lieberman (I-CT); Republican Presidential Candidates Set to Debate in Milwaukee; Interview With Rep. Nancy Mace (R-SC)\b0\par\par\uc2 Grady Trimble, Jacqui Heinrich, Jennifer Griffin, Bill Melugin,  Neil Cavuto \par \uc2 7,976 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 August 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 ("THE FINAL COUNTDOWN" PLAYS)\par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: What other music do you think we would be playing?\par \par \uc2 It is indeed the final countdown to the first Republican presidential showdown right here on FOX. Less than five hours to go.\par \par \uc2 And away we go with Bill Melugin in Milwaukee, where the anticipation is building, South Carolina Republican Congresswoman Nancy Mace on who she will be watching, 2000 vice presidential nominee Joe Lieberman on why he wants to see a third party in the running, and \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  donor \uc2 Dan Eberhart\uc2  on how his guy is preparing.\par \par \uc2 All that and these reports we're getting on \uc2 Yevgeny Prigozhin\uc2 . He was the Wagner leader behind that attempted coup in Russia a few months ago. He's apparently been killed in a plane crash, no doubt an issue that comes up tonight.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everybody. I'm Neil Cavuto.\par \par \uc2 A busy news day. Let's get right to it, going to Bill Melugin now in Milwaukee with the very latest.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Bill.\par \par \uc2 BILL MELUGIN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Neil, good afternoon to you.\par \par \uc2 For a moment, it looked like we were maybe going to have seven candidates on stage behind us tonight, instead of eight, because North Dakota Governor \uc2 Doug Burgum\uc2  injured his Achilles' tendon while playing basketball. He had to go to the hospital. He was here on crutches, but FOX News can confirm he is going to participate in tonight's debate.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, the other candidates have been preparing. Take a look at this video of \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2  last night hosting a pre-debate party that was themed 1776 Revolution Style. He says he's looking for revolution, not just change. He's compared himself to Thomas Jefferson in recent days because of his young age, Thomas Jefferson 33 years old when he helped write the Declaration of Independence.\par \par \uc2 And Ramaswamy was asked last night to respond to Nikki Haley's attacks against him regarding his foreign policy ideas. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Nikki Haley has a lot of experience in foreign policy. And she recently said that yours are making America less safe. How are you going to handle that if she brings it up on the debate stage?\par \par \uc2 VIVEK RAMASWAMY\uc2  (R), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: My general view is that, if somebody breaks your car for 25 years, you don't hand over the key to the same person.\par \par \uc2 And so I view my absence of so-called foreign policy experience as a badge of honor.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: Now, Nikki Haley is actually going to be standing right next to Ramaswamy on stage tonight, and she is doubling down on her attacks against him, saying that his proposals on how to handle China show he really has no understanding of the threat China poses to the United States.\par \par \uc2 Here's what she told \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2  in part -- quote -- "That's the problem with people who don't understand national security. And he proved that. We can't have a novice going into the White House" -- end quote.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, take a look at these images. The DNC has placed banners all outside the arena here in Milwaukee touting what they say are the successes of President Biden's agenda in his administration and also taking shots at the Republicans who are going to be debating tonight, calling them MAGA Republicans with a MAGA agenda and saying they're going to be looking for tax breaks for the rich.\par \par \uc2 Here is what the DNC had to say earlier today with some harsh words for the Republican field.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JAIME HARRISON\uc2 , DEMOCRATIC NATIONAL COMMITTEE CHAIRMAN: No matter who you pick, this group is as extreme as it gets, a bag full of MAGA apples, and they are all rotten.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: And center stage tonight is going to be Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  right next to \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2 . They will be right next to each other.\par \par \uc2 I talked to Governor DeSantis' campaign earlier this morning. They told me he is ready to go. They say while a lot of these other candidates are going to be talking about what they would like to do, Governor DeSantis is going to talk about what he already did in the state of Florida. They say he's got a serious conservative record to run on.\par \par \uc2 We will send it back to you, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right. He could be a target tonight. We will watch it closely.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Bill Melugin, on that.\par \par \uc2 With us now is South Carolina Congresswoman Nancy Mace.\par \par \uc2 Of course, there are two prominent South Carolinians in this race and debate tonight, of course, Nikki Haley and Tim Scott.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman, do you have a horse in this race?\par \par \uc2 REP. NANCY MACE (R-SC): We do not have a horse yet, Neil. As you just mentioned, both Tim and Nikki are constituents of mine.\par \par \uc2 I'm very much looking forward to watching the debate tonight, and I definitely would not undercut either of them. Nikki, obviously, a former governor and former ambassador, she shouldn't be underestimated tonight. And I'm looking for her to make some moves. She's a great orator, a great debater. She's seasoned at debate.\par \par \uc2 And I hope that both of them have a strong night and they can break out from the crowd.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: It's tough to break out right now in this environment where \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  seems to be running away with it, Congresswoman.\par \par \uc2 Now, it's still early and a lot could still happen, but, given the fact that he won't be there, it might not get the attention it normally would, a debate like this. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 MACE: Well, I'm going to be watching tonight. I hope every American will watch tonight. I do wish that everybody would debate.\par \par \uc2 I don't want us to open the door for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  to not debate the Republican nominee next year. I don't think he can string sentences -- words together to make a sentence, let alone debate for more than 10 minutes. And so I want to make sure that, at some point, every single candidate, we see them on the stage, so that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  can't say no when we get our nominee next year.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: As we're speaking, or we're learning right now that there's a move afoot to really get a Biden impeachment inquiry going led in the House, Kevin McCarthy hinting of that distinct possibility maybe as soon as next month.\par \par \uc2 What do you think about that?\par \par \uc2 MACE: Well, it's certainly a tool in the toolbox.\par \par \uc2 And I will tell you, Neil, right before I came over here to do this interview with you this afternoon, I ran into a constituent and voter here in the Lowcountry, very independent-minded, not really Republican or Democrat alike, not a supporter of the former president, who was lamenting about the double standard in the way that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's been treated versus the way that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and his family have been treated, given the circumstances involved.\par \par \uc2 And I just -- I hope that -- the Americans -- Congress hasn't given America a reason to trust the Congress. But I hope that as evidence comes out, no matter what tool we use, that all the evidence is shown to the American people, and the American people can decide what's right and what's wrong at the end of the day when all these investigations nations are done.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, is an inquiry the answer? I know, last time we chatted, Congresswoman, you had your concerns that it might look like Republicans -- I'm paraphrasing here -- are overreaching or being a little too zealous.\par \par \uc2 MACE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: What do you think?\par \par \uc2 MACE: It's always, always a concern, especially for Republicans who won Biden districts. We want to make sure we follow the facts, but we don't expedite it.\par \par \uc2 We have got to be very judicious. We have to be very deliberative. We have to be very thoughtful. We have to be very factual and not rush any sort of impeachment process. That's what \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  does, and we should be better than.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You have also raised your concerns about the party potentially locking itself into a corner on abortion.\par \par \uc2 There's apparently a billboard up not too far from the debate cite, Congresswoman, saying that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  will bring back Roe, referring to Roe v. Wade. And I'm just curious what you think of the position of the candidates and how they play that tonight?\par \par \uc2 MACE: Well, it is an important issue. And, in 2024, our nominee will have to answer for the issue of abortion.\par \par \uc2 It is the number two issue in swing states and in swing districts. We see it down here. It's inflation, the economy, it's abortion. And I have tried to show a road map, show a pathway on how you can be pro-life and pro- woman. And our nominee is going to have to do that, or we're going to lose, and not lose by a little bit. We're going to lose big.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: There are reports right now that the man who led the coup in Russia, the \uc2 Wagner Group\uc2 's Prigozhin, was killed in a plane crash today, along with nine others.\par \par \uc2 I'm just wondering what you think the candidates' response should be. What do you think this takes and where it takes relations right now with \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 MACE: Well, I don't think anybody is surprised by this news. I think it was just a matter of not if, but a matter of when. And it happened sooner than I could have imagined.\par \par \uc2 But we're dealing with someone who is willing to break the law, who's willing to murder allies and adversaries to get what he wants. It shows the stakes are very high in Russia, and especially with the invasion of Ukraine. And we have got to figure out a path forward. And that path forward does not include a nation that's divided.\par \par \uc2 I cannot tell you. I just got back from an international delegation trip overseas with House Armed Services. And when we are divided as a country, it really hurts our standing with nations, our allies, our partners and friends around the world. And we have got to be united moving forward, no matter the outcome of this election.\par \par \uc2 And I cannot emphasize that enough, especially when we're taking on an adversary like a guy like Putin.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Congresswoman, we will see how it all sorts out.\par \par \uc2 I know you will be watching the debate, as will we.\par \par \uc2 MACE: Yes, sir.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Congresswoman Nancy Mace of the beautiful state of South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 MACE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, in the meantime here, we're focusing on the money and donors.\par \par \uc2 A lot of them are holding their wallets tight right now, wanting to see how their candidates perform this evening. A lot's on the line for them and the money they hope to get as a result.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: He doesn't want to make this about criticizing other candidates, those there or not there. He wants to make this about looking at the future of America, very uplifting and all that, maybe very Reaganesque.\par \par \uc2 But can that survive? Can that type of guy survive in this environment?\par \par \uc2 ANDY SABIN\uc2 , TIM SCOTT DONOR: Absolutely. I think people are tired of the divisiveness that we're seeing and the fighting. They're looking for a guy like Tim Scott. He's got a great message. Why not have a nice guy that also could be a sensational president?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: \uc2 Andy Sabin\uc2  is a big backer, financial and otherwise, donor, as they like to call them, to the Tim Scott campaign, because he thinks America is hungering for someone different. A nice guy would fit the bill.\par \par \uc2 Dan Eberhart\uc2  says the exact same thing about \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , the Canary CEO, major funder, joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Dan, the search for a nice guy or an alternative guy, I guess the message is, to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , what do you make of that?\par \par \uc2 DAN EBERHART\uc2 , RON DESANTIS DONOR: Well, Neil, I don't think people mind that, but what people want to see is, they want to see an energetic executive who's got a proven track record of getting things done, and they want to see somebody that's going to move America forward that's a consistent conservative.\par \par \uc2 And, to me, that person is clearly \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , and it's a two-person race, and he's the person to take on Trump, then ultimately take on Biden and beat him. And Tim Scott is just not that guy.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Dan, many people argue, say what you will of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , but he has a proven record, and that record was a good one before COVID hit and some of these other things hit, so, the fact of the matter is, that's your guy, he's your guy.\par \par \uc2 And for many Republicans polled on the matter, overwhelmingly, he's their guy. What do you say?\par \par \uc2 EBERHART\uc2 : Well, look, the polls are what they are, but I think it's early.\par \par \uc2 Barack -- \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  was not in first place by a long shot at this point in his cycle. I think this is a point where, look, voters are familiar with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . They're supporting \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  at the moment, but the support is weak. The Des Moines Register poll with Ann Selzer showed a lot of weakness for Trump in that his voters being persuadable.\par \par \uc2 And the thing is, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  -- DeSantis is enemy number one for the left and for the media because they know that he's going to accomplish things if he gets in, in the way that Trump was good for the moment and good for the time, but didn't accomplish everything he said he was going to do.\par \par \uc2 And Governor DeSantis has done exactly that in Florida. That's why he was reelected by 19 points and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  lost in 2020. So, to me, when the proof is in the pudding, when primary voters are given a choice, Neil -- they haven't had a choice in seven years. And when they have a choice, they're going to vote for a consistent conservative that can move the country forward.\par \par \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is the future of conservatism. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is the past and a little bit of the present.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Do you worry, though, Dan -- let's say you're right on that -- it doesn't matter? The governor seems to be dropping like a stone. How do you convince people that that isn't justified?\par \par \uc2 What does he have to do tonight to sort of win people who are wandering away?\par \par \uc2 EBERHART\uc2 : No, I -- look, that's a good question.\par \par \uc2 Look, Governor DeSantis has made adjustments in his campaign. I think that's what a leader does. And that's why you saw the campaigns that folks like Jeb Bush and Scott Walker failed and why Governor DeSantis is going to ultimately win.\par \par \uc2 Look, he's going to be the comeback kid, and he's going to be like \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  and become the next president. And what you're going to see tonight is Governor DeSantis solidify his position in second place. What he's got to do tonight is, he's got to show he can take a punch and he's got to show American people what he's done in Florida, what he's going to do for the country, and what he's going to do for their pocketbooks, and that he's an energetic executive that's ready to take the fight to Biden.\par \par \uc2 And Governor DeSantis is the person to do that. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is a growth stock, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, and that might -- that might -- that might pan out, to your point, Dan. It hasn't yet. He's done a lot of shuffling of people and getting rid of a lot of people. And he sinks more and more in the polls as a result, even with all that.\par \par \uc2 So that could change. You're quite right. Plenty of time for things to change. But he is going to be a target tonight. He's the number one polling guy tonight, if you think about it. So they're all going to be gunning for him. Word is that \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2 , particularly, will try to \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  him.\par \par \uc2 What do you think of that?\par \par \uc2 EBERHART\uc2 : Look, the entire rest of the field -- \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  has had more negative ad money spent against him than the entire rest of the field combined, including the seven dwarfs on stage tonight, including \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , including \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And that's because the left and the establishment know that he will actually do what he says if he's elected. And \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  knows that he's the one person stage tonight that can beat him.\par \par \uc2 Look, what you're going to see come out of tonight is, Governor DeSantis is the clear two -- the clear person to beat \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and it's a two- person race. The other seven dwarfs on stage can't beat \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and they can't beat \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Dan, we will watch it closely. Thanks for keeping your patience with drumming behind you and all of that.\par \par \uc2 Dan Eberhart\uc2 , the Canary CEO, a major backer and donor to one \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In the meantime here, Joe Lieberman has had it not only with the candidates as they stand now, but he's craving an alternative, a third-party alternative. Why that is -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, the leader of the \uc2 Wagner group\uc2  in Russia is dead.\par \par \uc2 We're learning that \uc2 Yevgeny Prigozhin\uc2  was among 10 killed in a plane crash that got little official reaction from \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The latest -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FMR. SEN. JOE LIEBERMAN (I-CT): I cannot resist telling a quick story about my mother.\par \par \uc2 In 2000, when I was on the ticket, Larry King interviewed her and said: "Mrs. Lieberman, what do you say to your son when he makes a mistake?"\par \par \uc2 And my mom said: "Mistake?"\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You need those moments, right, in a big debate. Maybe someone will deliver lines like that tonight. They can be spontaneous. They can be planned, but, oftentimes, they just sort of pop up.\par \par \uc2 The aforementioned Joe Lieberman is with us right now. The former vice presidential candidate was a presidential candidate as well.\par \par \uc2 Senator, very good to see you in person. This is great.\par \par \uc2 LIEBERMAN: You too, Neil. Really great. Thank you. You're looking good.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You are too.\par \par \uc2 LIEBERMAN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I'm curious too what you make of the pressure on candidates to have a moment like you had there.\par \par \uc2 LIEBERMAN: Yes, those just happen.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LIEBERMAN: I mean, there's a way in which I bet every one of the candidates and their teams have prescripted seemingly hilarious lines or killer lines.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 LIEBERMAN: But they got to make sure they don't deliver them, if I may use the term of the year, like robots. I mean, it's got to fit into what's happening.\par \par \uc2 And the best way for it to happen is to be a spontaneous reaction to something one of the other candidates said.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 One of the things that you learned from watching \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2 , who is very good at this...\par \par \uc2 LIEBERMAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: He's an excellent debater. I don't know whether that will improve his likability or not as a candidate, but he's ready to go after \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  on his scripted campaign approach, that he's overadvised by aides and all.\par \par \uc2 What do think of that?\par \par \uc2 LIEBERMAN: Well, Christie is going to be -- first of all, I think Christie's going to have a good time tonight, and I think it's going to be fun to watch him.\par \par \uc2 But he's obviously got to be more than the prosecutor who prosecutes both Trump and DeSantis. He's got to talk a little bit, hopefully, about what he...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And, sometimes, a scorched-earth strategy scorches your earth as well.\par \par \uc2 LIEBERMAN: It does. It does.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LIEBERMAN: Because, in the end, it seems to me that the American people are yearning for a leader who will bring the country together and solve some of our problems.\par \par \uc2 And they don't see that now in President Trump or President Biden.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Now, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  obviously is not there for this big debate tonight.\par \par \uc2 LIEBERMAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: He might have eschew debates for a while.\par \par \uc2 I'm just wondering what your thoughts are on that. He can afford to, if you look at the polls. But as you have reminded me many times, Senator, polls can be fleeting.\par \par \uc2 LIEBERMAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: What do you think?\par \par \uc2 LIEBERMAN: No, I think he's right not to. Or let's put it this way. He can avoid tonight debate, but he can't do it for long. And there is peril.\par \par \uc2 I will just tell you a quick story. When I first ran for the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  against an incumbent senator, I was way behind in the polls. He totally ignored me, until I suddenly, in, I guess, early October broke even in the polls. Then he came out and he attacked me.\par \par \uc2 And I think the public felt it was forced. And where were you before? So, at some point...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, it can boomerang. It can boomerang.\par \par \uc2 LIEBERMAN: It can boomerang.\par \par \uc2 At some point, President Trump has to get in the ring with these people and prove that he is what apparently most Republicans want him to be, which is the leader of the party.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You were saying -- and I don't want to give away conversations during the break -- but this might be a battle for -- to see who could be the Trump alternative...\par \par \uc2 LIEBERMAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: ... all behind \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  by a country mile in the polls.\par \par \uc2 Who do you think that will eventually be?\par \par \uc2 LIEBERMAN: Well, I don't know.\par \par \uc2 I mean, right now, President Trump is so strong in the polling among Republicans that, barring some real big surprise, you have to say he's the probable nominee.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LIEBERMAN: But maybe not if this boils down to Trump against one other.\par \par \uc2 So, I think tonight, without Trump on the stage, this is really a competition among the others who are there to see who's going to make it to the finals with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But you don't like the way the finals are looking. You're not pleased with the idea that the choice might be exactly what we had the last presidential election.\par \par \uc2 LIEBERMAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You have been pushing this idea of an alternative candidate, and are you still looking at that, a third party?\par \par \uc2 LIEBERMAN: Yes. This is not just me, although I'm very supportive of it. It's a group called No Labels, which I helped to co-found about 13 years ago.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Absolutely. It seemed like you guys were sort of rallying around a \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  type.\par \par \uc2 LIEBERMAN: Maybe.\par \par \uc2 We spent a lot of time in the last dozen years trying to elect centrist, independent bipartisan...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But is he your favorite of that potential bunch?\par \par \uc2 LIEBERMAN: He deserves real consideration.\par \par \uc2 But here's the point. Right now, No Labels is focused on getting on the ballot with a third line in all 50 states, and we're making progress. We're over...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: The argument is that they would just siphon votes from...\par \par \uc2 LIEBERMAN: Yes, but here's the argument for doing this, and this is why we're doing it, because our members all around the country really asked us to.\par \par \uc2 People are sick and fed up with the two major parties, because all they do is fight each other, and they don't work together to get anything done for the country. And most voters think that, if it ends up in Trump and Biden again, it's going to simply be more of the same attack/counterattack, not only during the campaign, but the country will be divided by party.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But a third-party bid is always uphill, right?\par \par \uc2 LIEBERMAN: It's always uphill, but I have never -- I think the country has never seen the anger and disappointment with the two major parties as strong as it is.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LIEBERMAN: I mean, there was a poll, I think it was a Gallup poll a while ago, 49 percent of the American people said that they identify themselves as independents, which was more than the total of Republicans and Democrats.\par \par \uc2 And you ask them, do you want the race to beat Trump and Biden again, 75 percent say they don't want Biden to run again, and 69 percent say they don't want Trump again.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LIEBERMAN: So I understand that, historically, it's hard to win as a third-party candidate. We're talking about a bipartisan unity ticket.\par \par \uc2 And it may be, when we get into next year, hopefully, we're on the ballot in the 50 states, that we will say, you know what, this is the time. This is what America wants now. And, at a minimum, they deserve -- the American people deserve a third choice between Trump and Biden and more of the same.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: We just might see that.\par \par \uc2 Senator, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 LIEBERMAN: Thank you, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: We ran out of time, but an issue I want to pursue, that we will pursue right after this, is the death of the \uc2 Wagner Group\uc2  leader in Russia.\par \par \uc2 The guy who had stormed Moscow a little more than two months ago is dead, along with nine others, in a plane crash that the Russian authorities are saying was just that, a plane crash, nothing ill -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, two months ago, he had stormed Moscow.\par \par \uc2 Today, reports that he is dead, the \uc2 Wagner Group\uc2 's \uc2 Yevgeny Prigozhin\uc2  dying today in a plane crash that claimed nine other lives. We don't know a lot more than that, outside of the fact it has reverberations worldwide.\par \par \uc2 Jennifer Griffin at the Pentagon with more.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Jennifer.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRIFFIN, FOX NEWS NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Neil.\par \par \uc2 The Pentagon is not commenting on the news of \uc2 Yevgeny Prigozhin\uc2 's apparent death announced by Russian state media in a fiery plane crash north of Moscow. The news comes just one day after the exiled Russian mercenary leader appeared in this recruiting video for new Russian recruits to help him fight in Africa.\par \par \uc2 News of the plane crash came exactly two months after the \uc2 Wagner Group\uc2  head attempted a coup against \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  and his military chiefs on June 24, after expressing frustration that he and his mercenaries were not receiving enough weapons and support as they fought in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Putin spared Prigozhin, the 62-year-old whose cyber hackers interfered on Putin's behalf in the 2016 U.S. election and later recruited Russian prisoners to fight in Ukraine and Syria by allowing the president of Belarus to negotiate an end to the short-lived coup as Prigozhin's armed forces drove toward Moscow in a convoy that threatened the Kremlin leader, who has -- who was not seen or heard from in public for hours during the crisis.\par \par \uc2 Putin surprised onlookers by offering Prigozhin clemency in neighboring Belarus at the time. Prigozhin later said he was not trying to challenge Putin's authority. He then met with Putin at the Kremlin. Prigozhin was allowed to return to his home in St. Petersburg, Russia.\par \par \uc2 But few thought that Putin, who had faced the greatest challenge to his iron rule, would forgive or forget. Prigozhin was listed on the manifest of the plane which fell out of the sky north of Moscow near Tver.\par \par \uc2 Video at the time of the crash appeared on Telegram appearing to show a Russian air defense missile which is alleged to have shot down the plane. Prigozhin, who was once Putin's closest ally, challenged the Kremlin leader, making this video standing in a field of Russian corpses, blaming Putin's defense chiefs for the slaughter that Russian forces are facing in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 In another video, Prigozhin was seen killing a Russian deserter with a sledgehammer. Yesterday, the Kremlin announced that another Prigozhin ally during the coup, one of Russia's top air force generals, \uc2 Sergey Surovikin\uc2 , had been fired. A day later, Prigozhin and nine others appear to have died in a mysterious plane crash -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Jennifer Griffin, thank you.\par \par \uc2 To Lieutenant Colonel Bob Maginnis.\par \par \uc2 Bob, what do you think?\par \par \uc2 LT. COL. BOB MAGINNIS (RET.), \uc2 U.S. ARMY\uc2 : Yes, there aren't any coincidences in that world.\par \par \uc2 Certainly, Putin doesn't forgive, and he certainly doesn't forget. I'm reminded of a saying that revenge is a dish best served cold. And in this case, he waited until the second anniversary, monthly anniversary, of the mutiny by Ferguson and, of course, the firing of the general that she just mentioned really to send a very clear message to those in Moscow and elsewhere that Putin's in power, and he's not going to be humiliated by the likes of Prigozhin.\par \par \uc2 And so this is something that will certainly send chills up the backs of oligarchs and the general staff and others. So he's clearly in charge and has every intention of doing what he has in mind.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: He's been going around and looking like -- up until this tragedy, like, hands off this guy.\par \par \uc2 He came close to toppling \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , if you think about it. Putin at the time was running to St. Petersburg when this was going on, and he looked like a man on the run, now just the opposite. So, for \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , what does this mean?\par \par \uc2 MAGINNIS: Yes, well, Putin's a very savvy guy, KGB. He understands how to deal with those that stab him in the back. He has elections coming next month. He has a presidential election next year.\par \par \uc2 He wants to keep the social media in check. He wants his people to understand that, yes, things aren't going all that well, perhaps, in Ukraine, but he's in charge and he's going to continue doing what he thinks is important for the nation and best for his own regime.\par \par \uc2 So, the messages, I think, will be loud and clear. You will have announcements from virtually across that country recognizing that the thug is in charge. They better salute. And they're going to be taking the orders, whatever he puts out in the future.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, he was on shaky ground not too long ago, Colonel. He does not appear on I don't know if the term is as much shaky ground, or how would you describe it?\par \par \uc2 MAGINNIS: Yes, I think that, because some of the nationalists were helping really using social media to fan the flames against Putin, that this was eliminate.\par \par \uc2 And I think that probably the message is very clear, that you have got to support this guy. He's deadly serious. He was weakened to a certain degree, obviously, by the mutiny back on the 24th of June. And the troubles that are permeating from the Ukrainian fiasco are not looking good for him.\par \par \uc2 But this shows, just like in a jailhouse, the biggest, meanest guy gets his way. Putin's stepping up. He's making it very clear that he's in charge, and you're going to do what he wants done. And so, yes, I think he was weakened, but I think he's back in charge now.\par \par \uc2 I'm surprised he didn't take a guillotine to the Red Square and chop off Prigozhin's head, which would have been a clear sign, but that's something short of perhaps even what Putin would do.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes a bit graphic, I guess.\par \par \uc2 Colonel, thank you very much, Bob Maginnis, \uc2 U.S. Army\uc2 , "Kings of the East," multibestselling author.\par \par \uc2 Thank you again, Colonel.\par \par \uc2 MAGINNIS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Some big news to share with you today that, any other day, maybe, given all this other news, would have led broadcasts pretty much around the world.\par \par \uc2 India has landed on the moon, a safe landing on the old south pole of the moon. This is that area where we're told there is a good deal of water and ice that could be used in future international space missions. In case you're counting, India becomes the fourth country to get to the moon, after the United States and China and the old Soviet Union.\par \par \uc2 Russia, by the way, did have a capsule that was attempting to do the same thing in kind of the same neck of the woods earlier this week, but it crashed on the lunar surface, very analogous to what happened to the then- Soviet Union. The same week we landed \uc2 Apollo\uc2  11 on the moon, Luna craft, their unmanned craft at the time, also crashed. Missing history then, missing history now.\par \par \uc2 More after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Will President Biden commit to debating in a general?\par \par \uc2 CEDRIC RICHMOND, BIDEN/HARRIS CAMPAIGN CO-CHAIR: We have not had a conversation about that at all. I'm sure, one day, we will, but it's not today. And I don't have any news on that. But what we're going to continue to do is continue to govern, because Vice President Harris and President Biden have a job to do.\par \par \uc2 And they take it serious. So when we have news on that, we will let you know.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, that kind of came out nowhere. It's generally a given that a president up for reelection will participate in debates, apparently, no guarantee, at least we're hearing from the DNC.\par \par \uc2 Let's get the latest on all of this from Jacqui Heinrich with the president on vacation right now in Lake Tahoe, California.\par \par \uc2 This came, again, out of nowhere.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Neil, it was a very interesting answer.\par \par \uc2 You would think that they might commit to that right now, but they declined to do so. The president just, though, stepped out from his vacation. He went to do some shopping in South Lake Tahoe with some relatives, including \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's son. Hunter reportedly joined in on the family vacation, but arrived separately on a commercial flight.\par \par \uc2 And the president just told reporters that he has been briefed on the plane crash in Russia that reportedly killed the head of the Wagner mercenary group, Biden adding that he wouldn't be surprised if Putin was behind it.\par \par \uc2 But in talking to reporters, he also revealed that his plans for tonight include tuning in to the GOP primary debate here on FOX.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I'm going to try to see as much as I can, yes.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: What's your expectation from the GOP debate tonight?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I have none.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Biden's campaign has blanketed Milwaukee with billboards, even a plane, contrasting his administration's policies to what they call the extreme MAGA agenda.\par \par \uc2 They're also running a $25 million ad campaign. And they issued a prebuttal in partnership with the DNC earlier today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JAIME HARRISON\uc2 , DEMOCRATIC NATIONAL COMMITTEE CHAIRMAN: Tonight, in prime time, Americans will have an opportunity to see in action the most extreme, the most divisive, the most chaotic slate of presidential candidates in history, when these MAGA 2024 Republicans take the debate stage here in Milwaukee.\par \par \uc2 And I don't know if it's going to be a debate, but more like a circus.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Well, team Biden's not addressing how it will handle Republican criticism of the president's son or congressional investigations into the first family, even as House Speaker Kevin McCarthy is warning an impeachment inquiry could begin as soon as next month when Congress returns.\par \par \uc2 Cedric Richmond said the president will continue to govern, and there's no time to focus on what McCarthy may or may not do under pressure from the right flank of his conference.\par \par \uc2 But the Biden campaign does appear to be playing into the reported warning from the \uc2 RNC\uc2  chairwoman to Trump and his team that his absence tonight could potentially give cover to President Biden to opt out of a face-off with the eventual Republican nominee, declining today, as you just played in the beginning of this segment, to commit to any general election debates, at least for now, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Thank you for that, Jacqui Heinrich.\par \par \uc2 Take a quick break here, let you know that \uc2 Nvidia\uc2  numbers are out. This is the so-called artificial intelligence play extraordinaire, making money hand over fist, earning a lot more than analysts thought, and $3 billion revenue better than most people had thought. The stock is climbing 9 percent in after-hours trading. It has effectively doubled this year. It has quadrupled over the past year.\par \par \uc2 This is considered, again, one of the more meaningful artificial intelligence play. Nothing artificial about those numbers or how much money it's making for shareholders and for those who work at the company as well.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, what, we're not even a little over four hours away from the big debate, Grady Trimble there in Milwaukee with more, where the economy, I understand, could become quite the issue.\par \par \uc2 GRADY TRIMBLE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Voters we have talked to here are eager to hear the Republicans -- Republican candidates' plans to tamp down inflation, unleash American energy, and rein in spending at the federal government level.\par \par \uc2 President Biden lately has been touting the economy as strong, but these eight presidential hopefuls who will be on stage tonight say Americans are not feeling that. And voters themselves have told us that they're worried about the economy.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The job market is not great right now for young professionals, and what are they going to do about that? And how are they going to support their people when the economy is the way it is right now?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You're spending more on everything now, because the inflation is real high now, and we need somebody in office that can bring the inflation down.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE: We have heard over and over again from folks here in the Milwaukee area that the economy is their number one issue, and if it's not number one, it's a close second.\par \par \uc2 So, tonight, the GOP candidates will have a chance to contrast their vision for the economy with Bidenomics.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MADISON GESIOTTO GILBERT, CONSERVATIVE COMMENTATOR: I talked to some Democrats this morning. They're not voting in that Republican primary, but they're upset with paying more for everything. The wages are down. They're making less. They're paying more. They're getting taxed more.\par \par \uc2 And they want to hear, what is the alternative? What can I expect from a potential Republican nominee or potential Republican president post November next year?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE: So whoever makes the most compelling case on the debate stage tonight on those pocketbook issues could stand out among the crowded field, not only on that stage, but also, Neil, against President Biden -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Thank you, Grady, for that.\par \par \uc2 Of course, a lot depends if they don't tear each other apart first.\par \par \uc2 Let's get into that aspect of the story and the possibility for tonight's big debate with Doug Schoen, the former Clinton adviser, Julia Manchester, The Hill national politics correspondent.\par \par \uc2 Julia, we already know that these guys have, some of them at least, a plan to sort of knock the other guy down a peg or two, to emerge as the alternative guy to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  themselves, or woman, in the case of Nikki Haley. So they might not spend enough time on doing just what Grady outlined on the economy.\par \par \uc2 What do you think?\par \par \uc2 JULIA MANCHESTER, NATIONAL POLITICAL REPORTER, THE HILL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Look, I think \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is certainly want to -- going to want to outline that in order to contrast himself with President Biden and make himself seem like the younger alternative to a President Biden or even a President Trump.\par \par \uc2 We know that \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  has certainly been trying to make that contrast. But, for the others, look, I think there's definitely going to be some shots taken at President Biden. But we could see a lot of infighting, particularly when it comes to these other candidates really looking to knock down \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And the DeSantis team is very much expecting that at this point. We know that DeSantis is number two. He's technically number one on this debate stage with the absence of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . So, everyone else, particularly \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2 , is looking to get into that second-place perch that DeSantis is in.\par \par \uc2 So they're going to be knocking him.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: It's interesting, Doug Schoen. I was talking to Scott Walker, the former Wisconsin governor, himself for a brief period a rising front-runner star for president, who had a great record to run on.\par \par \uc2 And he pounded that record, I guess you could say very similar to \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  today. But they weren't looking at records back then. They were looking for bigger-than-life individuals. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  fit that bill and then some. So what would be \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 ' strategy? If he wants to be that alternative to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , how does he pull that off?\par \par \uc2 DOUG SCHOEN, FORMER BILL CLINTON POLLSTER: Well, look, if I were advising \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , I would tell him, do basically what Grady Trimble said, talk about inflation, talk about the cost of living.\par \par \uc2 Outline a plan that clearly differentiates yourself from the Biden administration and offers people the one thing that's lacking nowadays, Neil, which is hope for a better future. If all the candidates do is elbow each other for position as the alternative to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , I can tell you who the winner will be, and that will beat \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 They need a rationale, a vision, an idea of what our future is going to look like.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Julie, the one thing interesting for the Biden team is the way they are playing the economy. And despite how grim Americans view things right now, they say the trend is their friend.\par \par \uc2 I have mentioned this on the show and with you guys as well a number of times, that they're looking at the steady jobs improvement, the improvement on inflation numbers, still up, but not going up as much, and they're clinging to that and say time is on their side for it to resonate in time for the general election. What do you think of that?\par \par \uc2 MANCHESTER: Yes, Neil, we saw that happen last cycle as well in 2022, where the Biden campaign -- or, I should say, Democratic campaigns supported by President Biden that were down-ballot were talking about unemployment going down under the Biden administration and under Democratic leadership in Congress.\par \par \uc2 So they're going to be talking about that quite a bit. And with inflation, I think that they will certainly say, look, it's better than it was last year. And in terms of whether it could go down in the future, they're going to be talking about that, talking about the Inflation Reduction Act.\par \par \uc2 And I don't really think they have a choice on that matter because we know that the economy is always or normally the number one issue voters vote on when they go to the polls. So they need to address it somehow, and you're going to see them address it going forward.\par \par \uc2 We hear a lot about how Democrats have used abortion to really galvanize their base, and it did help them in 2022.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 MANCHESTER: But the Democrats I talked to say they can't rely solely on abortion. They need to tackle this issue of the economy in their messaging as well.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But that's a delicate walk, isn't it, Doug, if you think about it.\par \par \uc2 If you keep telling Americans who overwhelmingly say they're concerned about inflation in the economy that there are no need for such worries, you're kind of insulting them. I don't think kind of. You are.\par \par \uc2 So how do you play that if you're \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and you're hearing these candidates tonight and how they're portraying them?\par \par \uc2 SCHOEN: Yes, I think what \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has to do to answer that part of the question is to acknowledge the pain that people are feeling and suggest that you're going to do everything you can to rein in inflation, reduce spending and offer people a better future.\par \par \uc2 To just say things are getting better, we saw with the interviews that you just played, people don't feel that way. The poll numbers show the same thing. There's got to be a frank acknowledgment of what they're facing.\par \par \uc2 And for the Republicans, they all have to have something more than just criticism of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , because, right now, as bad as the Biden numbers are, he's even with or ahead of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in the polling.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Good point.\par \par \uc2 SCHOEN: So the Republicans need a credible alternative, and Biden needs to wake up to reality of the real pain people are facing.\par \par \uc2 Right now, neither party has addressed those objectives.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well put. Final word on the subject.\par \par \uc2 Doug. I want to thank you. Julia, thank you.\par \par \uc2 SCHOEN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, that's what's really up for grabs tonight, just how you make a connection.\par \par \uc2 And I think Bret and Martha are up to the task of trying to elicit from these guys where they go from here and who captures the evening.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow, we will be getting a reaction from \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2 , what he makes of this, \uc2 Robert F. Kennedy Jr\uc2 ., two of my special guests tomorrow, as we handicap where the race goes from here after the big night tonight.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, here's "The Five."\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020230823ej8n0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Interview With Rep. Nancy Mace (R-SC)\b0\par\par\uc2 Neil Cavuto \par \uc2 1,144 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 August 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: With us now is South Carolina Congresswoman Nancy Mace.\par \par \uc2 Of course, there are two prominent South Carolinians in this race and debate tonight, of course, Nikki Haley and Tim Scott.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman, do you have a horse in this race?\par \par \uc2 REP. NANCY MACE (R-SC): We do not have a horse yet, Neil. As you just mentioned, both Tim and Nikki are constituents of mine.\par \par \uc2 I`m very much looking forward to watching the debate tonight, and I definitely would not undercut either of them. Nikki, obviously, a former governor and former ambassador, she shouldn`t be underestimated tonight. And I`m looking for her to make some moves. She`s a great orator, a great debater. She`s seasoned at debate.\par \par \uc2 And I hope that both of them have a strong night and they can break out from the crowd.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: It`s tough to break out right now in this environment where \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  seems to be running away with it, Congresswoman.\par \par \uc2 Now, it`s still early and a lot could still happen, but, given the fact that he won`t be there, it might not get the attention it normally would, a debate like this. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 MACE: Well, I`m going to be watching tonight. I hope every American will watch tonight. I do wish that everybody would debate.\par \par \uc2 I don`t want us to open the door for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  to not debate the Republican nominee next year. I don`t think he can string sentences -- words together to make a sentence, let alone debate for more than 10 minutes. And so I want to make sure that, at some point, every single candidate, we see them on the stage, so that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  can`t say no when we get our nominee next year.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: As we`re speaking, or we`re learning right now that there`s a move afoot to really get a Biden impeachment inquiry going led in the House, Kevin McCarthy hinting of that distinct possibility maybe as soon as next month.\par \par \uc2 What do you think about that?\par \par \uc2 MACE: Well, it`s certainly a tool in the toolbox.\par \par \uc2 And I will tell you, Neil, right before I came over here to do this interview with you this afternoon, I ran into a constituent and voter here in the Lowcountry, very independent-minded, not really Republican or Democrat alike, not a supporter of the former president, who was lamenting about the double standard in the way that Donald Trump`s been treated versus the way that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and his family have been treated, given the circumstances involved.\par \par \uc2 And I just -- I hope that -- the Americans -- Congress hasn`t given America a reason to trust the Congress. But I hope that as evidence comes out, no matter what tool we use, that all the evidence is shown to the American people, and the American people can decide what`s right and what`s wrong at the end of the day when all these investigations nations are done.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, is an inquiry the answer? I know, last time we chatted, Congresswoman, you had your concerns that it might look like Republicans -- I`m paraphrasing here -- are overreaching or being a little too zealous.\par \par \uc2 MACE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: What do you think?\par \par \uc2 MACE: It`s always, always a concern, especially for Republicans who won Biden districts. We want to make sure we follow the facts, but we don`t expedite it.\par \par \uc2 We have got to be very judicious. We have to be very deliberative. We have to be very thoughtful. We have to be very factual and not rush any sort of impeachment process. That`s what \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  does, and we should be better than.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You have also raised your concerns about the party potentially locking itself into a corner on abortion.\par \par \uc2 There`s apparently a billboard up not too far from the debate cite, Congresswoman, saying that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  will bring back Roe, referring to Roe v. Wade. And I`m just curious what you think of the position of the candidates and how they play that tonight?\par \par \uc2 MACE: Well, it is an important issue. And, in 2024, our nominee will have to answer for the issue of abortion.\par \par \uc2 It is the number two issue in swing states and in swing districts. We see it down here. It`s inflation, the economy, it`s abortion. And I have tried to show a road map, show a pathway on how you can be pro-life and pro- woman. And our nominee is going to have to do that, or we`re going to lose, and not lose by a little bit. We`re going to lose big.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: There are reports right now that the man who led the coup in Russia, the Wagner Group`s Prigozhin, was killed in a plane crash today, along with nine others.\par \par \uc2 I`m just wondering what you think the candidates` response should be. What do you think this takes and where it takes relations right now with \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 MACE: Well, I don`t think anybody is surprised by this news. I think it was just a matter of not if, but a matter of when. And it happened sooner than I could have imagined.\par \par \uc2 But we`re dealing with someone who is willing to break the law, who`s willing to murder allies and adversaries to get what he wants. It shows the stakes are very high in Russia, and especially with the invasion of Ukraine. And we have got to figure out a path forward. And that path forward does not include a nation that`s divided.\par \par \uc2 I cannot tell you. I just got back from an international delegation trip overseas with House Armed Services. And when we are divided as a country, it really hurts our standing with nations, our allies, our partners and friends around the world. And we have got to be united moving forward, no matter the outcome of this election.\par \par \uc2 And I cannot emphasize that enough, especially when we`re taking on an adversary like a guy like Putin.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Congresswoman, we will see how it all sorts out.\par \par \uc2 I know you will be watching the debate, as will we.\par \par \uc2 MACE: Yes, sir.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Congresswoman Nancy Mace of the beautiful state of South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 MACE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, Inc.  Copyright CQ-2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020230823ej8n00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Politics and the Iowa State Fair; Iowa State Fair Foods; State of Emergency Declared for Much of Southern California as Hilary Approaches; Interview with GOP President Candidate Nikki Haley. Haley on How She Would Handle U.S. Policy Toward China; First Republican Primary Debate Wednesday Night on Fox News; Legal Problems Plague Both Frontrunners in '24 Election; Governor Reynolds Stays Neutral in GOP Race; Governor Reynolds Signed Bills on School Vouchers & Abortion Ban\b0\par\par\uc2 Shannon Bream, Lucas Tomlinson, Juan Williams, Craig Herrera,  Alexis McAdams \par \uc2 10,284 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 August 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Fox News Sunday\par \uc2 FOXN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: I'm Shannon Bream. "FOX News Sunday" is coming to you from the Iowa state fair, three days ahead of the first Republican presidential primary debate.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: I think Iowans want to see us.\par \par \uc2 VIVEK RAMASWAMY\uc2  (R), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: It's good to see you, guys.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice-over): They've made the rounds.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT: I love them and they love me.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: They've seen the sights.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 RICK SCOTT\uc2  (R-SC), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: No state is doing it better than the state of Iowa.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: They've made their cases.\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE, PRESIDENTIAL CANDIADATE: We need new leadership in the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 ASA HUTCHINSON (R), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: America needs a president that sets the example for character and integrity.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 DOUG BURGUM\uc2  (R-ND), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Presidential campaigns need to be about the future.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY (R), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: It is time for a new generational leader.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Now, they're preparing for their biggest test yet, this week in Milwaukee, as the front-runner prepares to surrender yet again.\par \par \uc2 FANI WILLIS\uc2 , FULTON COUNTY DISTRICT ATTORNEY: The indictment brings felony charges against \uc2 Donald John Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BREAM: This hour, we're joined by Nikki Haley, who will be the only woman on Wednesday night's debate stage.\par \par \uc2 And --\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 KIM REYNOLDS\uc2  (R), IOWA: Thank you so much. We really appreciate you being here.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: -- by Iowa Governor \uc2 Kim Reynolds\uc2  whose fair-side chats have been a draw here for the candidates.\par \par \uc2 Then, we'll get straight talk from some political insider about the state of play for 2024 in the first in the nation caucus state.\par \par \uc2 Plus --\par \par \uc2 Here it is and here we go.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JONI ERNST (R-IA): Right here.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Senator \uc2 Joni Ernst\uc2  takes us on a tour of all the must-see stops.\par \par \uc2 Cheers!\par \par \uc2 ERNST: Cheers!\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All, right now, on "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM (on camera): Hello, today from Des Moines, on site of the Iowa state fair.\par \par \uc2 This is one of the most famous stops for presidential candidates as they try to win over savvy Iowans, and they've been here in force over the past few days, along with thousands of other fairgoers here for fun, and also looking to question the contenders in person. Just three days from now, millions more will get to see the top candidates together for the first time on one stage.\par \par \uc2 This is a live look now at Fiserv Forum in Milwaukee, where Wednesday night, the candidates will face off in the first Republican presidential primary debate on FOX News, facing questions from Martha MacCallum and Bret Baier and probably each other.\par \par \uc2 Much more to come from the fair, but, first, we want to touch on some breaking news. Hurricane Hilary barreling towards southern California. This is a rare occurrence. Forecasters say it is bound to make the history books as the first tropical storm to hit California's coast in 84 years.\par \par \uc2 We turn now to FOX Weather meteorologist Craig Herrera live in San Diego where storm reps have been taking place over the last couple of days.\par \par \uc2 Hello, Craig.\par \par \uc2 CRAIG HERRERA, FOX WEATHER METEOROLOGIST: Hello, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 Yes, already seeing a wash out here in southern California, San Diego included. This is the day that students should be preparing to go back to \uc2 San Diego State University\uc2  or \uc2 San Diego Unified\uc2 , the second largest school district in the state of California. But instead, we've got a lot of rain behind us.\par \par \uc2 And as we go into the weekend, some places across southern California may get more than a year's worth of rain in just 24 hours.\par \par \uc2 So, this has been a major concern watching Hillary over the past couple days. In fact, out in San Diego, the sun was out for a moment at times on Saturday, giving folks here a chance to fill their sand bags, prepare for some possible flooding as well, and the emergency response teams out here, which is a group comprised of volunteers. They are ready to go and any type of natural disasters, they are on standby and ready to go right through Monday as well.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, we do have a flash flood threat. And when you look at this, you are talking about a level four out of four to see some flash flooding, goes all the way from the U.S. Mexico border, clear up into portions of Death Valley, huge amount of rain could fall and some of these spots across the desert, 8 to 12 inches of rain could lead to some flash flooding.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, Southern California not used to this much rain in the month of August.\par \par \uc2 One state that is (AUDIO GAP) a bit of rain is Washington. Meantime, they are dry. Portions of eastern Washington are seeing some dry conditions and a fire burns there, scorching nearly 10,000 acres near the town of Spokane.\par \par \uc2 It's dubbed the Great Fire. It has been reducing visibility over portions of eastern Washington, also bringing the air quality down, as well.\par \par \uc2 Shannon, we'll continue to follow the stories in Washington, as well as Hilary as it moves to southern California. Heavy rain expected not only for San Diego, Los Angeles, \uc2 Riverside County\uc2 , but also the Coachella Valley near Palm Springs.\par \par \uc2 And already, the city of San Diego closing all beaches, city parks and public buildings today through tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Craig Herrera and the team over at FOX Weather got you covered.\par \par \uc2 Craig, thank you so much, reporting from San Diego.\par \par \uc2 Back now here in Iowa, where the Republican hopefuls have been making rounds at the fair.\par \par \uc2 FOX News correspondent Alexis McAdams catches you up.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Bacon pecan pie, sounds good.\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS MCADAMS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): The 2023 Iowa state fair -- always full of fried food and fun.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: On the stick, one spicy, one original.\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: But the main course is politics.\par \par \uc2 How is it going in Iowa?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Great, beautiful place. We love it. We love Iowa.\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: The fair grounds turned into the main stage for the Trump show, with the former president handing out pork chop sandwiches to the crowd.\par \par \uc2 You feel you're pretty popular in Iowa?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I guess so. This is the biggest crowd, they've ever had, they say. So, I love them and they love me. That's a good combination.\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: Just steps away, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  hit the bumper cars with his kids.\par \par \uc2 And in the sky above, a small plane carrying the banner with a message "Be likeable Ron", ongoing dig at the candidate.\par \par \uc2 Do you think you are a likeable candidate?\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: I think that's a -- that's a narrative. But, you know, when you have Trump doing that -- he's got so many people that would never vote for him, and I've seen that in Florida.\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: Unlike Trump, DeSantis sat down for the fair-side chat with Iowa Governor \uc2 Kim Reynolds\uc2 . Though, he was briefly interrupted by two hecklers.\par \par \uc2 REYNOLDS: You know what? You know what? We're in Iowa and in Iowa, we are Iowa nice. So, let's give everybody the opportunity to hear our candidates.\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY (rapping): He opens his mouth --\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2  had a mic-drop moment of his own.\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: I think they can tell a difference between who's real and who's not.\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: Former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  worked the crowd.\par \par \uc2 Do you think Americans are ready to see a fresh face in the White House?\par \par \uc2 PENCE: I think Americans are ready for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  to leave the White House.\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: And Senator Tim Scott let his personality shine.\par \par \uc2 You're a popular guy out here at Iowa state fair.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Thanks God. Yeah, I mean, I tell you, I love people.\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: Miami Mayor \uc2 Francis Suarez\uc2  says Iowa is all about meeting voters face-to-face.\par \par \uc2 FRANCIS SUAREZ\uc2  (R), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: I think, for me, as a candidate that's not as well known as some of the other established candidates, it's incredibly important for me to personally connect with people.\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: As Nikki Haley walked around wearing cowboy boots and a T-shirt that said, underestimate me, that'll be fun.\par \par \uc2 HALEY: Because strong girls become strong women. Strong women become strong leaders.\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: Big crowds, big names and a big year for these candidates.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: That was Alexis McAdams reporting right here in Des Moines.\par \par \uc2 And joining us now from Philadelphia, someone you just saw on that piece, Republican presidential hopeful, Nikki Haley.\par \par \uc2 Governor, welcome back to "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY (R), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Thanks so much, Shannon. It's great to be with you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Let's start with polls. We have brand new FOX News polling looking overall at the field.\par \par \uc2 You are in fifth place at 4 percent, and we also have polling that looks head-to-head how each of these contenders would do, including you, with President Biden. It gives him a six-point advantage.\par \par \uc2 "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " says this: her attempts to gain national traction, talking openly about her positions on abortion, taking a hard stance against transgender girls playing in girls sports, attacking Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  appear to be falling flat with the Republican base at large.\par \par \uc2 So, how important is Wednesday night for you? Is it make-or-break in getting that traction you're looking for?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: Shannon, I think the media gets it wrong. They don't -- they don't see the fact that families have been on vacation. Kids have been out of school. Americans have not been paying attention.\par \par \uc2 Once this debate happens this week, it's off to the races. That's when you're going to start to see people really focusing on different candidates, look at their options are. We feel really good going into the debate, I love debates, and I'm looking forward to it. I think it's a great opportunity for Americans to start focusing on who's going to lead us after 2024.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: There are some we talked to about coming to join us this weekend, they didn't want to because they are deep into debate prep. What does that look like for you?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: Debate prep for me was 80 town halls that I did in Iowa and New Hampshire where we let anybody ask whatever question they wanted. There was -- you know, you get hard questions. You have to give true answers, and that's the best debate prep I could hope for.\par \par \uc2 So, we are looking forward to going and being on that debate stage, letting everybody see the options. But we're going to talk about our vision, our vision for America, our vision of making sure that we stop the spending and lower inflation, our vision of making sure there's transparency in the classroom, our vision of securing our borders and our vision of a national security that protects all Americans.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I want to ask about somebody, \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2 , who has been doing well, rising in the polls. He was asked by our Neil Cavuto yesterday, would he take a VP slot and he said essentially, no, he's got a very specific vision of how he would operate as president and he's the only one who can execute it, kind of all or nothing proposition.\par \par \uc2 Is that where you are? Or if you don't win the nomination and one of these other contenders asks you join the ticket, would you consider it, or are you hard no, like Vivek, unless you're top of the ticket?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: Shannon, I've said from the beginning, I don't play for second. I've never played for second. I'm not going to start now.\par \par \uc2 We have a country to save and I'm determined to save her. It is time for a new generational leader. It's time for someone who has executive experience and has actually managed government like I was when I was a two-term governor. And it's time for someone who has some strong foreign policy experience that can focus on getting the world safe again and that's what I'm determined to do.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, let's start there with foreign policy, a big part of your resume.\par \par \uc2 You said China is beating us every single day. This weekend, over last few days, the president has gathered the leaders of Japan and South Korea to get together.\par \par \uc2 Here's what he said about their discussions.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: This summit was not about China. That was not the purpose of the meeting, but China obviously came up. Not to say we don't share concerns about the economic coercion or heighten tensions caused by China, but this summit was really about our relationship with each other.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So a lot of folks thought it was interesting that he started it off by saying this wasn't about China, these meetings. But U.S. Ambassador Rahn Emmanuel to Japan has said this actually -- this meeting is sort of China's worst nightmare.\par \par \uc2 Do you give the president some credit for bringing together Japan, South Korea, to tighten those relationships as the Chi -- as the threats from China increase?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: The problem with what Biden says is he takes the playbook from Obama. He starts apologizing before he tries to show strength.\par \par \uc2 This absolutely is about China. The idea we can get Japan, South Korea and U.S. together scares China. That's exactly what we want.\par \par \uc2 The idea that we are going to work together more with them on tech, on military, on exercises -- those are things that China doesn't want. The last thing China wants is a \uc2 NATO\uc2  in the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 We want to make sure that we are focus on anything we need to get with our allies. It should not just be South Korea and Japan. It should be Australia, it should be India, it should be Israel, and it should be the Arab countries.\par \par \uc2 So, yes, I hope China is nervous, but I doubt that they are because he apologized before he ever got started with his speech.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We are deeply intertwined with China economically and in many ways on trade issues. We're dependent on them for key critical medication, critical minerals, all those kinds of things. You've got talked about the fact that you want us to decouple from them as much as we can.\par \par \uc2 But how in reality could we do that?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: That's the same question that Europeans used to say about Russia before Russia invaded Ukraine. They thought they were too intertwined to separate.\par \par \uc2 America needs to understand, China has been infiltrating America. They have started and been planning war with us for years.\par \par \uc2 We have to be smart. They should not be buying U.S. soil near our military installations. They should not be killing 75,000 Americans a year by sending fentanyl over. They should not be stealing our intellectual property. They should not be building up their military because they're getting ready for war.\par \par \uc2 America needs to be smart. No more talking about how economically dependent we are. Let's talk about the fact that we shouldn't be independent on our medicines from China. We should not be selling them land and we should be taking back the land they've purchased.\par \par \uc2 We should make sure we get that infiltration out of our universities. We should make sure that we stop selling them technology that's actually building up their military that they're using to threaten America, and we should create a strong military, not one that focuses on gender pronoun classes, but a strong military doesn't start wars, a strong military prevents wars.\par \par \uc2 This is not the time for the U.S. to be scared of China. This is time for the U.S. to be strategically smart and say we are not going to let them intimidate us. We are going to be come strong and independent on our own and do whatever it takes to keep Americans safe.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But those thousands of products and materials that we are dependent on them for, how do we possibly on-shore those? What about the costs? It's clearly going to be passed on to U.S. consumers.\par \par \uc2 Is it even practical or feasible in any way to suggest that we can actually do that?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: The focus would be on national security first. So, you know, we don't care as much if China buys T-shirts and light bulbs from us any more than we have problems with them buying our agricultural products.\par \par \uc2 But when it comes to medicines down the aisle in our \uc2 Walgreens\uc2 , when it comes to having them get technology that actually hurts our military, those are things we want to focus on.\par \par \uc2 I mean, look at the fact that they just hacked our commerce secretary and the way she's responding is she's going to China to visit them. That's the wrong approach. We need to go and say we're going to end all normal trade relations until 