e to be moved off. But it, you know, it's just a little bit of a reflection of where we're at today. But that's, you know, you don't see a lot of it. I haven't seen a lot of it at the events that I've been doing. So I think it's still more rare than it's not.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So candidates have been here for months. And there are no questions about whether there could be some late entrants into the race, people who haven't spent the time here. Axios reporting this. Big GOP donors are pushing for a Trump alternative. They say Virginia Governor Glenn Youngkin, and Governor -- Georgia Governor Brian Kemp are getting secret overtures from establishment Republicans and have not ruled out running. So would it be too late? Would Iowans be open to hearing from somebody who's missed the fair, who's missed a lot of the big events?\par \par \uc2 REYNOLDS: I think we have a great field of candidates right now. And I think we don't need more candidates in the field. We probably need less. And that's kind of what we do with the Iowa caucus. Our role is not really to protect the winner, but to really start to narrow the field. So, you know, I think, you know, these candidates have put in a lot of time. We have a great field of candidates. And I think probably we don't need more at this point.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, you had a commanding reelection when much like Governor DeSantis 19-point margin, you're not without your critics here. So I want to talk about some of the issues that are going on here in Iowa. On the issue of books that are being taken out of school libraries, those kinds of things, the intercept rights this, the law signed by Iowa Governor \uc2 Kim Reynolds\uc2  in May is yet another piece of astroturfed right-wing legislation aimed at eliminating gender nonconformity, anti-racism and basic reproductive education from schools, while solidifying the power of the conservative family unit." Are you banning books that have information about basic reproductive education?\par \par \uc2 REYNOLDS: No, we're banning books that are pornography, that actually have pornography in them. And I am telling you parents do not want their young kids exposed to that, as I talk to parents and I am all over the fairgrounds, I am all over the state, I go to all 99 counties, if you look at the Iowa the register poll, by overwhelming majority, probably over 60% want their kids to have a quality education. They don't want the -- the teachers and the school boards providing that kind of information to their -- to their kids.\par \par \uc2 And so let's focus on reading and writing and civics and math. And make sure that we're preparing these kids to be successful. Let the parents decide what they're going to teach their kids when it comes to social issues. But in addition to that, we can't teach our kids to hate this country and to hate each other based on the color of their skin.\par \par \uc2 Again, let's just get back to the basics. We're not where we should be. We kept our kids in school, we were one of the first states that got our kids back in the classroom. So we didn't see the dramatic learning loss that a lot of the states that kept the kids out of the classroom for two years, they kept those kids out of the classroom, not only the mental health, but the decline in the learning loss that they're experiencing. So let's really come together and focus on what we should be doing as educators and that's -- and we've got great educators in the state of Iowa that are doing that. But let's really focus on getting these kids where they need to be.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I've met some of them here at the fair.\par \par \uc2 REYNOLDS: Yeah, yeah.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. Today's last day, you're heading to Texas tomorrow. Is that correct?\par \par \uc2 REYNOLDS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So with the number of GOP governors out there, you've got National Guard down at the border.\par \par \uc2 REYNOLDS: Right.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I want to read something from a letter that an Iowan wrote to the Ames -- Ames Tribune. He said it's un-Christian, what's happening at the border, he said that life is sacred to you. But you're going to work with another governor who's says life is sacred, but the policy is to put razor wire in rivers, maiming innocent people in brutal and defenseless situations, ordered not to provide water in extreme temperatures to people in need, and even to take it away. What do you say about those policies? And how do you answer --\par \par \uc2 REYNOLDS: Well, first of all, they're not accurate. So they've been provided misinformation, because that is actually not true. And I believe Governor Abbott is going to -- as we were going to go down there, we're going to do a press conference and be able to demonstrate to the media, what these actually do. But what is un-humanitarian is President Biden's unwillingness to address the open border and the crisis that we're seeing at the southern border. It is unconscionable the number of young girls and women that are being exposed to different set sex -- use as sex traffickers and what they're doing to these children. That's what's unconscionable. And the number of drugs that are coming across the border and infiltrating our state.\par \par \uc2 Every state is a border state right now. It's not just Texas, we're seeing tremendous increases in the number of drugs, fentanyl, meth and cocaine, 500 percent increase in fentanyl in just -- since 2020. So that's what's unconscionable. We're going down to help Governor Abbott secure this border. That is the constitutional duty of the president and he is not fulfilling his constitutional duty to protect this country and its citizens.\par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: We will track your visit down there. And, in the meantime, thank you very much for hosting us at the fair here in Iowa.\par \par \uc2 REYNOLDS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: If you haven't been, folks, it's a once-in-a-lifetime thing. You've got to do it.\par \par \uc2 REYNOLDS: It is an amazing fair. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Governor, thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 All right, coming up, insight from two Iowa journalists who have been here on the ground covering the candidates, seeing the reaction from people in real time. We'll find out about that when FOX NEWS SUNDAY returns from the Iowa State Fair in Des Moines ahead of the first Republican presidential primary debate just three days from now in Milwaukee on Fox News Channel.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: What is different or interesting about Iowa, the people here, how you campaign here? How important this state is?\par \par \uc2 WILL HURD, 2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: It was surreal being up on the Des Moines soap box at the Iowa State Fair, husking to being, you know, president of the United States. You see this in movies. You hear about it in - in stories. I never would have imagined that some kid from south Texas would one day be in Iowa campaigning for president.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Former Texas Congressman \uc2 Will Hurd\uc2  talking with me at the fair about the importance of Iowa as he attempts to gain ground in the race for the Republican nomination.\par \par \uc2 We're back now in Des Moines with two journalists who know Iowa inside and out, Kathie Obradovich, editor-in-chief of "The Iowa Capital Dispatch," and Galen Bacharier, a politics reporter for "The Des Moines Register."\par \par \uc2 Thank you both for being here this morning.\par \par \uc2 KATHIE OBRADOVICH, EDITOR-IN-CHIEF, "IOWA CAPITAL DISPATCH": Thanks for having us.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, so you heard Governor Reynolds being coy on whether there will be an endorsement. She's got an 86 percent approval rating among GOP here.\par \par \uc2 So, Kathie, how important would an endorsement from her be?\par \par \uc2 OBRADOVICH: So, it would be huge for the candidate. But the reason why Iowa governors and top senators, et cetera, don't usually endorse before the caucus is about preservation of the caucus. So, say she endorses somebody who doesn't win the caucus or doesn't win the nomination, but goes on to win the nomination, that person is head of the party now and doesn't really like how they were treated in Iowa. So, you have to be very, very careful. And everything she does - and I think it helps her, you know, continue to be a focus with the candidates if she's being coy.\par \par \uc2 But if I had to put money on it, I'd say she's not going to endorse.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. All right.\par \par \uc2 OBRADOVICH: Not before the caucuses.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK.\par \par \uc2 So, Galen, who has surprised you the way that they've either connected well with people here - we've seen certain people rise in the polls, and others who have flounder a little bit in this kind of really hands-on retail politicking.\par \par \uc2 GALEN BACHARIER, POLITICS REPORTER, "DES MOINES REGISTER": I think the state fair has really demonstrated that a lot of these candidates sort of know where they're at in Iowa and they're tailoring their fair experience to reflect that. We've seen Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  really hit retail politics hard. He was doing the classic fair circuit, hitting a lot of different locations, trying to connect with voters, shake a lot of hands. And similar thing with Senator Tim Scott, who I think sees a possible path for himself through here.\par \par \uc2 And then some of the folks here, some of the folks trying to not become also rans, right? Trying to get a last-minute push for donors. Trying to get up on that debate stage. Making a pitch over at the soap box. I think we really see that - those different priorities reflected on how they're taking on the fair.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, somebody, when he first got in, who had a lot of hopes here in Iowa, was former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 . The evangelical vote is huge here within the GOP. But "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " says this, he's "been emphasizing his loyalty to the Constitution and invoking his faith as he tries to win the support of the evangelical voters he is counting on to propel him in the 2024 primary."\par \par \uc2 Kathie, why isn't he connecting better with evangelicals, and more broadly Iowans?\par \par \uc2 OBRADOVICH: Evangelicals are like a lot of other Republican voters. What they care about more than, you know, a candidate's issue, positions or values is electability. And \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  is struggling. He's not popular with the pro-Trump crowd, which is, obviously, the biggest part of the Iowa caucus, you know, universe right now. But he -- he also, you know, is - they -- people who might also like him are worried about his electability. So, he's not connecting really with either side, the pro-Trump or the anti- Trump side. He's in a bad place for the caucuses.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: It's a strange thing to try to straddle.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, Tim Scott has had a nice surged here in Iowa. He spent a lot of money. He's got the ad dollars to pour into Iowa. It's a very nice, optimistic message.\par \par \uc2 Galen, does it go long haul with Iowans? Are they flirting with him? What's going on there?\par \par \uc2 BACHARIER: I think it will depend on who you talk to. I've spoken with a lot of Iowa voters who really like what they hear from Tim Scott. That optimistic message for a lot of them is a change of pace. It's something that they can see themselves really appreciating.\par \par \uc2 I think for a lot of them maybe they enjoy it but it's not necessarily what they see coming to the forefront, right? I like Tim Scott. I appreciate Tim Scott. But right now maybe necessarily hasn't soared to the top for them. But he's certainly something that I hear a lot of potential caucus goers keep in mind.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, he'll be on the stage there, as will many of these others who have been here at the fair this week.\par \par \uc2 Kathie and Galen, thank you for some inside scoop. Good to see you both.\par \par \uc2 OBRADOVICH: Thanks again (ph)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, up next, my first time at the fair with a verified insider. Senator \uc2 Joni Ernst\uc2  and I throw all caution to the wind. We're sampling the most interesting things being offered here at the fair. You have been warned.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We're back live in Des Moines. The state fair here is about more than just politics. More than a million visitors come each year to check out the contests, the competitions and, of course, the food.\par \par \uc2 Senator \uc2 Joni Ernst\uc2  took me around in this week's "Sunday Special."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, so at the fair one of the things you've got to do is, we have to try all of the weird foods.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JONI ERNST (R-IA): Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: What do you think is the most unusual or what's been one of your favorites?\par \par \uc2 ERNST: Oh, my gosh. Well, favorites, of course, way behind us here is the Iowa pork tent.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Right. You have to go there.\par \par \uc2 ERNST: Pork chop on a stick. It is magnificent.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 ERNST: Love the corn dog. But then we've got all of these new fair foods this year, as well. And I think we're going to have a (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We are going to try something called the deep fried pickle dog. And it actually comes in different version. Pastrami and ham, OK. All right.\par \par \uc2 ERNST: OK. So, that one looks like the ham. This looks like the pastrami.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Oh, that actually looks good.\par \par \uc2 ERNST: It's very good.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, I'm going to be brave and take a bite here.\par \par \uc2 ERNST: Deep fried.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: This deep fried pickle dog.\par \par \uc2 ERNST: Oh.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: That's really good.\par \par \uc2 ERNST: That was really good.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I don't know what all is in here, but it's all bad for me and tastes delicious.\par \par \uc2 ERNST: Cream cheese, pickle, ham, and then battered and deep fried.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Do you also subscribe to the philosophy that, if you fry anything it could taste good?\par \par \uc2 ERNST: Pretty much anything deep fried or on a stick at the Iowa State Fair.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Right. That's right. All right.\par \par \uc2 ERNST: It's a winner. Cheers.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Cheers.\par \par \uc2 ERNST: OK.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, so this is a classic. I feel like the fried Oreo, the fried Twinkie -\par \par \uc2 ERNST: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Is something that's on the must-do list.\par \par \uc2 ERNST: Yes. This is really good.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I've had a deep fried Oreo before and I think it is worth every bite.\par \par \uc2 ERNST: Oh, it's so good. So good.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Oh, these are hot.\par \par \uc2 Mmm. These are good for us, right?\par \par \uc2 Welcome.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Boom, done.\par \par \uc2 We are going to do something a little bit different and we are going to have corn dogs that are made out of alligator and rattlesnake.\par \par \uc2 ERNST: OK.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: This is your beautiful home state that has given us this.\par \par \uc2 ERNST: OK.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, we're going to be brave. We - will you try it?\par \par \uc2 ERNST: Hello, sir.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Hi.\par \par \uc2 ERNST: How are you?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Fantastic.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. We would like a rattlesnake dog and an alligator dog.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You know what, we can help you out with that.\par \par \uc2 ERNST: OK.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So excited.\par \par \uc2 OK, which is which, does it matter?\par \par \uc2 ERNST: OK.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Well, I'm not allowed to tell you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Oh, we just have to guess?\par \par \uc2 ERNST: We have to guess?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: No. It's a green tip is for gator. Green for gator.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK.\par \par \uc2 ERNST: Green for gator.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: This one's rattlesnake. And I went ahead and put a little bit of our venom sauce in the bottom. It's an -\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Venom sauce.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Avocado, green chili sauce. So, if you'd like to dip that.\par \par \uc2 ERNST: OK.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: All right.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Now we're going to break -\par \par \uc2 ERNST: Green for gator.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, and that's -\par \par \uc2 ERNST: And this is rattlesnake.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Rattlesnake. All right.\par \par \uc2 I'm going to break a piece off, because I feel like that's the only way we can really eat a dog.\par \par \uc2 ERNST: Yes. Yes. Yes. That's the only way we can do it here.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Would you ladies (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 ERNST: OK.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, here we go. I'm trying the alligator corn dog.\par \par \uc2 ERNST: And I have got a rattlesnake corndog. And I'm going to put a little venom sauce.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Dipping in the venom sauce.\par \par \uc2 ERNST: OK.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: A little bit of a kick.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Just a slight kick.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Uh-huh. What do we think?\par \par \uc2 ERNST: I'm getting the venom sauce right now.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Heavy on the venom. It is a rattlesnake.\par \par \uc2 ERNST: This is a little kick.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: It's a rattlesnake dog, so, the venom makes sense.\par \par \uc2 ERNST: It's actually not -- not bad. I'm surprised.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes. I think this is good. The - the - the - well, pleasantly surprised.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Actually pretty good.\par \par \uc2 All right, that's it for today. Thank you for joining us from the state fair in Iowa.\par \par \uc2 Final reminder, do not miss the first Republican primary debate this Wednesday on Fox News Channel. The candidates face off on one stage for the first time at 9:00 Eastern. Until then, I'm Shannon Bream in Des Moines. Have a fantastic week. We'll see you next Fox News Sunday back in D.C.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FOXN000020230820ej8k00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  Leads Rankings But Iowa Voters Weigh Options; President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  Touts Inflation Reduction Act; Hawaiian Electric Facing Multiple Lawsuits; Abortion Drug Access Restricted Ahead Of Supreme Court Review; Elder Yet To Qualify One Week Out; Motivation For Presidential Run; Top Voter Concern Heading Into Election; Former Santos Fundraiser Indicted On Federal Charges; Surge of Enrollment Of Migrant Children In New York City Schools May Effect Education Programs; Large Amounts Of Materials Purchased By Government For Building Of Southern Border Wall Remain Unused; California Democratic Congressman Scott Peters And Michigan Republican Congressman Bill Huizenga Interviewed On Bipartisan Efforts To Reduce U.S. National Debt; New Polls Show Former President Trump Continues Leading Republican Presidential Primary Candidates\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Jacqui Heinrich, Alexis McAdams, William La Jeunesse,  Shannon Bream, Eric Shawn, Casey Stegall, Guy Benson, Katie Pavlich \par \uc2 8,160 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 August 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 CHARLIE HURT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Check out this video between father and son. It's really awesome.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What we call it, Cash?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Trespassing?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You were supposed to say pond hopping.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Smart kid.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA TARLOV, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Super cute.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: That's it for us tonight. Have a great night.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: There's the music. Good evening. Welcome to Washington. I'm Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 We begin tonight with our first head to head Fox Power Rankings of the campaign cycle. Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  continues to dominate, Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  and Senator Tim Scott listed as contenders, five others in the conversation, and the final four declared candidates considered outsiders at this point.\par \par \uc2 We'll talk with presidential candidate \uc2 Larry Elder\uc2  live here in a few minutes.\par \par \uc2 New Fox polling out tonight backs up those rankings, the Power Rankings, former President Trump checks in with 53 percent support. Governor DeSantis is next at 16. No one else has more than 11 percent after that.\par \par \uc2 If the former president is not in the equation for some reason, Governor DeSantis leads the way over \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Alexis McAdams starts us off tonight live from Des Moines, Iowa with reaction. Good evening, Alexis.\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS MCADAMS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Bret, good evening from Des Moines where we talked with lots of voters here at the state fairgrounds about those new Fox Power Rankings, which we'll get to in a minute but amongst all the fried food and all the fun here in Iowa, the main course still politics.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ALLEN MILLER, IOWA VOTER: Tim Scott, I think he's got a very, very positive message.\par \par \uc2 RON BINGHAM, IOWA VOTER: I like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . I voted for him. This time around, it's iffy.\par \par \uc2 RICHARD KUNTZ, IOWA VOTER: I think Trump's the only one that can get us out of this mess we've gotten into.\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS (voice over): Iowa voters not surprised that former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  came out on top in the newest Fox News Power Rankings.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Maybe DeSantis. But I'm still standing with Trump.\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS (voice over): Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  remains in second place though recent polls show that gap between him and Trump is widening.\par \par \uc2 And in most national polls, South Carolina Senator Tim Scott is a distant third.\par \par \uc2 NANETTE FORD, IOWA VOTER: I just don't think he's as well-known, unless you've really been following the Senate.\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS (voice over): Right now, Scott, the only other candidate to pull double digit support in more than one state according to Fox polls.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT (R), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: If people are excited about an optimistic positive message anchored in conservatism, as long as you have a backbone, you do not have to be angry to win people over to a conservative proposition.\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS (voice over): Right now, \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2 , Nikki Haley and \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  listed as in the conversation.\par \par \uc2 VICKY BINGHAM, IOWA VOTER: I liked Mr. Pence, but I know he's running behind quite a ways.\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE (R), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: The thing that I've come to realize though, traveling around the country is -- I'm well known, but I don't think I'm known well.\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS (voice over): As a new Fox News national poll survey shows that nearly half a dozen Republican candidates are within striking distance of beating President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BRAD FRANZWA, IOWA VOTER: I'd like to get a candidate that can succeed in beating Biden at any costs, like we just need to get it done this year.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS (on camera): And Bret, every potential candidate and candidate we talked to here on the ground continues to point out that it's still early in this race except for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  because he's sitting at the top according to those new rankings, but it won't be too early for long, right? We're going to have Thanksgiving, Christmas. Before you know it, it'll be caucus time here in Iowa, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You got Alexis in Des Moine, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Our Fox poll indicates President Biden has a three point edge over foreign President Trump in a head-to-head matchup, numbers 44 to 41.\par \par \uc2 President Biden is celebrating the anniversary today of his Inflation Reduction Act, which by the way, he recently told supporters he should not have called it that. But Republicans say the American people have been feeling the effects of Bidenomics and they don't like it. The president's approval ratings in our latest poll tend to backup that GOP position.\par \par \uc2 White House Correspondent Jacqui Heinrich joins us live from the North Lawn with the latest. Good evening, Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret. One notable absence at this bill's first birthday party was the senator who helped write it. Joe Manchin today was railing against the Biden administration for implementing this law as a radical climate agenda. As new estimates show this climate spending that was passed will end up costing nearly three times what they planned but the White House is glossing over it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Thank you, Mr. President.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): President Biden celebrating the one-year anniversary of the Inflation Reduction Act as a key part of his economic agenda.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Guess what? It's working.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): Even though economists say price cooling over the past year had more to do with gas prices dropping than it did with the IRA. And today, one top White House official acknowledged what it really amounts to.\par \par \uc2 JOHN PODESTA\uc2 , \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  SENIOR CLEAN ENERGY ADVISER: The largest investment in clean energy and climate action ever in the United States in the history of the world.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): Biden admitted last week the Inflation Reduction Act's name isn't really right. Telling a crowd in a Utah fundraiser, "I wish I hadn't called it that because it has less to do with reducing inflation than it does to do with dealing with providing for alternatives that generate economic growth". But no new branding is in the works ahead of 2024.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: You said yourself that voters don't understand everything the president has done. Polls keep showing that people are broadly unhappy with the president's handling of inflation in particular. And the president has said he wished he didn't call this the Inflation Reduction Act. What should you call it?\par \par \uc2 PODESTA: Well, he said, following that sentiment, because I think it's a complicated bill, that it also has reduced cost.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): Biden still marketing it as a mixed bag.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Lower costs for families, especially health care costs, increase America's energy security, restore fairness to a tax code, create good paying jobs here in America, and to address the existential threat of climate crisis.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (on camera): Well, it is not just attitudes about the economy that the president might have to turn around ahead of 2024. This new Fox polls show that the criminal investigation into his son might just end up hanging over him with now more than twice as many Democrats thinking that \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  did something illegal as compared with six months ago.\par \par \uc2 And among independents, that number jumped by 20 points, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jacqui Heinrich live on the North Lawn. Jacqui, thank you.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: President Biden will travel to Maui Monday to see the devastation from last week's wildfire in Hawaii. The confirmed death toll there now has risen to 106. But 1,300 people are still unaccounted for. The search is also continuing for a cause and that is leading to more questions than answers so far.\par \par \uc2 Senior National Correspondent William La Jeunesse has the latest tonight from Maui.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Right across the street from my home. Our line just went down.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM LA JEUNESSE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Early moments of the Lahaina fire captured on camera appear to show fallen power lines may have sparked the fire that already took more than a hundred lives.\par \par \uc2 SHANE TREU, LAHAINA WILDFIRE WITNESS: I hear a pop coming from across the street. Soon as I hear the pop, all I heard is like electrical like zzz, saw sparks and then there was a fire.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE (voice over): Security video from a bird sanctuary last Monday appears to show a similar cause of another Upcountry Maui fire, a bright flash in the woods followed by flames. \uc2 Hawaiian Electric\uc2  faces numerous lawsuits that had ignored weather warnings and kept its power lines electrified. The utility says it's cooperating with an ongoing investigation.\par \par \uc2 SHELEE KIMURA\uc2 , CEO, \uc2 HAWAIIAN ELECTRIC\uc2 : It's important to understand what happened and it's important to make sure it doesn't happen again.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE (voice over): Famous largest ever team of cadaver dogs searches for human remains.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: This is the absolute worst disaster I've ever seen.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE (voice over): The painstaking process includes not only finding bones and teeth, but identifying charred remains.\par \par \uc2 So far, only a few of the more than 100 confirmed dead have been positively I.D.ed as families are asked to provide DNA swabs.\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY HALL, UC DAVIS FORENSIC SCIENCE CENTER: If it's from a relative. It's pretty straightforward. We know the level of the relationship.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE (voice over): Those who lost everything tried to see the next chapter in their life as the fire erased so much of what they already wrote.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: All my life is all gone in a minute.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Not just me lost everything. Everybody, everything is gone.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: William La Jeunesse reporting from Maui.\par \par \uc2 You can help the victims of the wildfires just go to redcross.org/foxforward. You can make a donation there or text Hawaii to 90999 and you can make a $10.00 donation there that need the help.\par \par \uc2 Stocks were off after -- off after the release of the Federal Reserve minutes concern over rising inflation, fed action possibly. The Dow lost 181 today, the S&P 500 was off 34, the NASDA