ers cost that were not prepared for that are going to be in place now because they decided to kick the can down the road and use these individuals as pawns on the chess board.\par \par \uc2 SHAWN: Three hours north of New York City, near Albany, the Mohonasen school district is beefing up by adding teacher assistants who speak Spanish and specialists who can teach English as a second language. The district has under 3,000 students and has enrolled 70 migrant children who were bussed to the area. Superintendent Shannon Shine says current parents should be reassured.\par \par \uc2 SHINE: Their children are safe and well and will be well-provided for. And we won't diminish services to them. We won't see spikes in class sizes, and that kind of all is well.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 SHAWN: Public schools in New York City start September 7th. And now three weeks out, officials still do not know just how many migrant students will be in the classrooms. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Eric Shawn in Manhattan. Eric, thanks.\par \par \uc2 In tonight's "Whatever Happened To" segment we look at the much-discussed wall along the southern border. It apparently presents a large and expensive problem for many people. Senior correspondent Casey Stegall explains from El Paso, Texas.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 CASEY STEGALL, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: On a blazing hot summer day, piles of steel beams sit unused on a New Mexico ranch along the southern border.\par \par \uc2 RUSSELL JOHNSON, CATTLE RANCHER: There is a huge amount of taxpayer waste here.\par \par \uc2 STEGALL: Russell Johnson says this material has been on his cattle ranch for more than two-and-a-half years after President Biden took office and halted construction of the border wall, and there is nothing he can legally do about it.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: If I ran my business like the federal government has run this project, I'd be broke.\par \par \uc2 STEGALL: Under President Trump the feds