1 trillion now. You all are working across the aisle on financial matters. Tell me how Congress gets their head around this from both sides of the aisle?\par \par \uc2 REP. BILL HUIZENGA, (R-MI): Frankly, starting the conversation or maybe restarting the conversation has been a big challenge. Obviously, we have been spending a lot of money over the last number of years coming into COVID, coming out of COVID. But we have got to find some common ground here. And I have got some constituents, to be honest, that don't think we ought to have been having that conversation. I get told on occasion you shouldn't even be talking to these people, et cetera, et cetera.\par \par \uc2 And I always say to them, look, if we are not having the conversation, how are we supposed to figure out whether we agree on five percent, 15 percent, or 50 percent. And there are some things that we know that are looming here. For example, in 2033 there is going to be a cut to Social Security it. Is a conversation we have to have.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Congressman Peters what about that? We are facing another fiscal cliff come September. How can you guys work together to avoid that?\par \par \uc2 REP. SCOTT PETERS, (D-CA): Well, I think we have to. I mean, the thing about Bill and I realize that this has been an issue that has been developed over time. It's not just a Democrat or Republican issue. Both parties have been voting for funding our ongoing expenses with debt. And President Trump bears his share as well. The deficit -- he set the record for the most debt added in a single four-year presidential term before this. So we are for getting past that blame and looking at each other and figuring out how do we get this country on a sustainable fiscal path.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The house will be in order.\par \par \uc2 PETERS: The fact that we are going to be spending four years more money on interest than defense, we already spend more money on interest than our children.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Listen, I have been covering Washington for a long time. There hasn't been a real hunger for dealing with the debt. We -- both parties, to your point, Congressman Peters, have been spending a ton. Now we dealt with the pandemic, and both administrations dealt with that. But the last time the debt, the national debt ever decreased was 1957. Most times cuts in Washington deal with slowing increases, Congressman Huizenga. So there's got to be a different mindset up there completely.\par \par \uc2 HUIZENGA: Unfortunately, we have some folks that don't see it as a problem. They think as long as we can keep ahead and keep up on those interest payments, it doesn't really matter what we spend. And, to me, that's just wrongheaded. I'm a small businessperson. Three generations my family has been involved in construction here in Michigan. And I'm no captain of industry, but I know how to read a P and L sheet. And it's math, Bret. At the end of the day, this is simply math, and the math isn't adding up.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Bipartisan fiscal forum, and I applaud you for trying to find common ground, but Congress has been at this for a long time. There are structures and committees. But you have to create something else to try to get around what has been years and years of spending. I'm wondering, we went through Simpson-Bowles, Congressman Peters, that was an idea that everybody could get their head around, but it never got across the finish line. Do you guys see some big thing that's going to change the dynamic?\par \par \uc2 PETERS: The big thing is the debt is way bigger. And both parties have been complicit in not really paying attention to the long-term effect of borrowing to pay our ongoing expenses.\par \par \uc2 HUIZENGA: I think not only is it elected officials and the structure that surrounds our government operations that need to realize this, but citizens. Citizens have to understand what this means, and they have to have be heard, whether it's at the state level or at the federal level. They have got to make sure that those of us that do end up on the ballot have an understanding that they aren't happy with the situation. That will also motivate some folks.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So both of you, really quickly, to wrap up. When you hear somebody say it's just all partisanship on Capitol Hill and you can't really get anything done, what do you say? Congressman Peters and then Congressman Huizenga.\par \par \uc2 PETERS: Bill and I actually -- I think we see that we are problem solvers. You can't solve any big problem in Congress without Democrats and Republicans. You basically need 60 votes in the Senate for anything. So you've got two people here leading a group, a significant group to really deal with this problem in a bipartisan way. That's the way it should be done. And I don't think it will be easy, but I think it's the only way.\par \par \uc2 HUIZENGA: Well-said. And at the end of the day, we have to have people who have the courage of their convictions to speak the truth and explain what is going on, no matter what they might be from constituents. We've got to be realistic and we've got to be honest with them about our future, and the sad reality is we are in trouble.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, we are in trouble in the big picture on this finance front. But there are a lot of things happening on the positive side. Common Ground wise, we like to focus on that. We appreciate you both coming on to talk to us.\par \par \uc2 You can see all of the "Common Ground" segments on my podcast "Common Ground." Find that and the "All-Star Panel" podcast under the Bret Baier podcast at FOXNewspodcast.com, \uc2 Spotify\uc2 , or wherever you download podcasts. You can also see the extended discussion on the FOX News \uc2 YouTube\uc2  page.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel on the new FOX power rankings, the latest polls, what is happening ahead of the debate next week. And, later, a special anniversary for a very special weapon of war.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I'm really looking for a strong leader that will turn this country from bad to good. Maybe DeSantis, but I'm still standing with Trump.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I voted for Biden the first time. I'll vote for him again if that's the option. I don't think that he's the best option, either.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I think Trump is the only one that can get us out of this mess we have gotten into. So I'm going to support him all the way.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I would like to get a candidate that can succeed in beating Biden at any cost. Like, we just need to get it done this year.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Some voters there at the Iowa State Fair. As our new FOX polls came out today, you can take a look at them with the former president now versus June. It drops three points, but Governor DeSantis dropped six. Can you see a move by \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2  up to 11 percent in this poll. The vice president, former vice president \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  at vice percent.\par \par \uc2 Another poll came out today, the Associated Press poll asking voters who they would not support, would definitely or probably would not support as the party's nominee. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  54 percent, 63 percent would not support, probably or definitely, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So, with those two polls, and everybody says you focus too much on polls, it's a snapshot in time. It gives you a little bit of a view of the different polls. Let's bring in our panel, Guy Benson, political editor at Townhall.com, host of "The Guy Benson Show" on FOX News Radio, New Hampshire talk radio host Chris Ryan, and Katie Pavlich, news editor at Townhall.com. Chris, it is great to have you out of the state of New Hampshire and here on set. But what is happening on the ground there? There is a lot of candidates now spending time there.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS RYAN, TALK RADIO HOST: Yes, there is no question. And \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is dominance in this field in New Hampshire. And the question is, are any of the other candidates going to start to emerge and resonate with voters in the state? And what I'm hearing from both people in New Hampshire and in meetings here in Washington, D.C., today is that your debate coming up on the 23rd may actually not winnow the field but may actually increase it. I'm hearing about \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2 . I'm hearing about Glenn Youngkin.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Governor of Georgia, Governor of Virginia.\par \par \uc2 RYAN: Yes. And the potential for those two to get into the race -- I thought Kemp's tweet in response to President Trump's claims about Georgia was interesting as was Youngkin's ad earlier which seemed like more of a national ad a month ago as opposed to an ad that was focused specifically on the state election.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Of those conversations, Chris, just to interrupt you.\par \par \uc2 RYAN: Sure.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Where do they see the vacuum? Where do they see the road that they take?\par \par \uc2 RYAN: They see the vacuum that no one else is resonating with voters. And if someone is going to defeat \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , they feel that it has to be someone that comes into the race and has the ability to move upwards. DeSantis has been steadily dropping. You look at your poll, even more so the polls within Iowa and New Hampshire show DeSantis coming back to the pack, Tim Scott moving up, \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2  resonating with voters. And a lot of folks felt that DeSantis was going to be the person, and he has not resonated so far.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Speaking of DeSantis, a certain talk show had him on today, Guy Benson. And we were talking about the Trump Truth Social talking about press conference he is having Monday where he says he is going to lay out all of this election evidence and he's going to provide it, very complete and detailed, in his Truth Social post. And here is \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  on your show talking about that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS, (R) FLORIDA: If we are fighting about what happened in 20 or January 6th, 2021, if that is what the election ends up about, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is going to be hanging out in his basement in Delaware again, not a care in the world, and Republicans are going to lose.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: What did you think about that?\par \par \uc2 GUY BENSON, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: It's hard to argue with it. I'm not going to say definitively that Trump couldn't win a general election. I think he could, especially if the economy is in the toilet, which it might be next year. But overall, as you look at especially the numbers from the Associated Press poll that you highlighted at the beginning ever the segment, Bret, the vast majority of independents in this country and swing voters, poll after poll has shown and election results have shown they are over all the re-litigation, stop the steal, 2020, January 6th, looking back. They don't want any part of that. And for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to win a general election, it's going to be a huge challenge if the election cycle is dominated by the Democrats, by the media, and in some respects by Trump with these backward-looking discussions of something that is absolutely radioactive to most voters.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But it seems impossible not to go back to discussions because these legal cases are in your face. And if all goes as planned, January 2nd could be the first trial on the January 6th issue 13 days before the Iowa caucuses.\par \par \uc2 KATIE PAVLICH, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: It's certainly going to be a topic of conversation, and the president has made clear that he has no plans to move on from that discussion while he has offered other issues to talk about the economy, for example, and things, of course, could change with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's legal troubles as well given the Hunter Biden plea deal fell apart, and they wanted that to be behind them so they could move forward thinking they have a clean slate.\par \par \uc2 But on the issue of another Republican governor possibly jumping into this, I think there is this deep misunderstanding from the donor class with the Trump base and who can actually pull that off. They have to be careful about bloodying up Republican candidates as they have done with \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  who was convinced to get into this race. He has not done well. He was offered as alternative to Trump. He has not been able to do it, especially given the Trump campaign has been very successful at showing that they think that DeSantis owes him for his position as governor in Florida.\par \par \uc2 The person who has actually benefited from that is \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2  as we have seen in this polling. He is moving up. He has been able to poll the three percent off of Trump, pulling from the DeSantis folks. And he actually does understand what the base wants and argues he is a good alternative. You might not bridge the gap of Trump being ahead by 30, 40 points, but he is an option that people are looking at.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: He says yes to every interview request, it seems. He does a lot of posting on social media and online.\par \par \uc2 Here is former Vice President Pence on the upcoming debate in Milwaukee.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE, (R) FORMER U.S. VICE PRESIDENT: I have debated \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  a thousand times.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 PENCE: Just not with the cameras on. Look, I do hope he comes. I hope we have a fulsome debate. And I hope it's the beginning of a vigorous debate about the future of the country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And he has been popping into Iowa more but New Hampshire as well.\par \par \uc2 RYAN: I think that Katie makes a really great point about late entrance into this race. We saw it with Rick Perry, we've seen it on the Democratic side with \uc2 Mike Bloomberg\uc2 , Fred Thompson, on down the road. The fact that they are talking about it doesn't mean they are going to be successful. But there is a thirst and desire for something else. And whether the debate creates an environment in which candidates rise up or other candidates come in, it's a pivotal moment in this campaign.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And quickly, the A.P. poll, this is number eight, guys, would you like to see Biden run again in 24 -- 24 percent yes, 75 percent no.\par \par \uc2 BENSON: It's pathetic. The numbers are awful. The number we put up before, a majority of the American people don't want either of these guys to run.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, we'll see you in Milwaukee, on that note. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 And finally tonight, a special day.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Today is a special day because it's the 80th birthday. But there are over 200 crew members here today.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: A special ceremony in New York to mark 80 years since the commissioning of the USS Intrepid. More than 180 former crew members returned to the ship at Pier 86, including some who were part of the original crew in 1943. The Intrepid's first mission was in the Pacific during World War II. The celebration including a special presentation for those who served in the war. The ship was decommissioned in 1974, opened as a museum in 1982. Very cool. You should see it.\par \par \uc2 All right, tomorrow, more politics with the pollsters, fair, balanced and unafraid. We'll cover it all here on SPECIAL REPORT.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020230817ej8g00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2  Slams The Left's Culture Of Oppression; Entire Police Force In Minnesota Town Resigns; Tone-Deaf Democrats: Party Pushes Biden's Economic Plan As Folks Struggle; Cowboy Mentality: Media Blames Masculinity For Men's Poor Mental Health; Eiffel Asleep: Drunk Tourist Spend Night In French Landmark\b0\par\par\uc2 Jeanine Pirro, Jessica Tarlov, Charlie Hurt, Dana Perino, Greg  Gutfeld \par \uc2 8,134 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 August 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: The Five\par \uc2 FOXFIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Karl Rove, good seeing you.\par \par \uc2 I'm going to be talking to Bill Barr on what's at stake legally for the former president in all these battles and indictments. 91 different charges across four criminal cases, how that sorts out for the former president, that's tomorrow. THE FIVE is now.\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Hello everyone, I'm Judge Jeanine Pirro, along with Jessica Tarlov, Charlie Hurt, Dana Perino and Greg Gutfeld. It's 5:00 in New York City and this is THE FIVE.\par \par \uc2 Here's how to deal with the woke mob Republican presidential candidate \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2  just schooling a self-described pansexual activist who decided to confront him over LGBTQ issues.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VIVEK RAMASWAMY\uc2  (R), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: I don't have a negative view of same-sex couples, but I do have a negative view of a tyranny of the minority. We have now in the name of protecting against tyranny of the majority created a new tyranny of the minority. And I think that that's wrong.\par \par \uc2 I don't think that somebody who's religious should be forced to officiate a wedding that they disagree with. I don't think somebody who is a woman, who's worked really hard for her achievements should be forced to compete against a biological man in a swim competition. I don't think that somebody who's a woman that respects her bodily autonomy and dignity should be forced to change clothes in a locker room with a man. That's not freedom, that's oppression.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: And here's exactly the type of woke insanity. Vivek is standing up to, CNN publishing a detailed guide to the use of neopronouns. It's a new kind of gender-neutral or non-binary pronoun that allows people to characterize themselves using genderless descriptions like leaf, sun, and star.\par \par \uc2 And as we tried to figure that one out, there is growing outrage after a biological male who identifies as a woman smashed a women's world powerlifting record in Canada.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 APRIL HUTCHINSON, FEMALE POWERLIFTER: It's so important to keep women and girls sports fair and safe. We need to use our voices, men and women, to let everyone know that fairness and safety has to be top priority of every federation.\par \par \uc2 Any woman, men or federation that supports men lifting or competing with women is part of the problem. And they should be ashamed. And they're literally helping erase women's sports.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: OK, so let's start with you, Charlie. I mean, a recent poll showed that 65 percent of Americans believe that there are only two gender identities.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE HURT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Shocking.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: And 34 percent disagree. Is Vivek right that the minority at this point is now speaking for the majority?\par \par \uc2 HURT: I think without a doubt, especially if you consider how much conversation gets used up talking about this issue.\par \par \uc2 And you know, and I think it's -- by the way, I think it's sort of tactical, I think a lot of leftists want to do this, because sane people just throw their hands up and say, this is nuts, I'm walking out, I'm not having a conversation. And it drives people out of the political discussion, which is very bad for the country. But it's good for sort of leftist ideology.\par \par \uc2 But I love the way Vivek handled that. And it's -- and I think it's why Vivek is so valuable to the -- to the discussion today. And why I think he's going to be part of the discussion for a long time, no matter what happens.\par \par \uc2 He starts off by chiding her for doing the LGBTQ thing, saying I don't see those as one thing. I see people as individuals. And then those full Federalist Papers on her, starts talking about the tyranny of the minority, which is just as bad as the tyranny of the majority. And that whole discussion, while we shouldn't really have to be having it is something that like the founders envisioned, and they explained it all. And they argued about it. And we came up with the process that we came up with.\par \par \uc2 But then, at the end of all of it, he then says, and he thanks her, she's got this look on her face, like, I just got my lunch eaten. I don't know what to say. And she's kind of freaking out. And then he says, thank you for your civility. It was perfect.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Well, yes. And that's why it's gone viral. I mean, Jessica, so many people are stunned by the fact that they had a very civil conversation about something that is divisive in this country.\par \par \uc2 But more to the point, they have now identified more than 100 genders. Are we supposed to or are we compelled to call people the gender that they want, like these neopronouns, xyr, aer, zir. I mean, we insulting people if we can keep it straight?\par \par \uc2 JESSICA TARLOV, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: No. And I don't know anybody that's adhering to that. I do think that these are things that D.I. units at companies and we've seen that there's been a big backlash against that and people are losing their jobs because they're finding that these things are incredibly divisive, that it's not actually helping company culture, etcetera.\par \par \uc2 I think that these are just talking points and kind of clickbait items. I do think actually that the point was that the civil conversation occurred. And you guys know that I'm a big supporter of the idea of talking to people who you don't ideologically agree with. And you can find a lot more common ground than you expected to, walking into a conversation like that.\par \par \uc2 And he knows how to be a politician, and so far, he's treating every potential voter with respect, and that's what you absolutely have to do.\par \par \uc2 I do think, though, on the merits of his point, that he's wrong. So, the tyranny of the minority party is the GOP today, if you look at on the trans issue. For instance, our own Fox polling shows that 57 percent of Americans think that trans kids and their families being the target of political attacks, and these anti-trans bills is a major problem.\par \par \uc2 When you look at the actual numbers of kids that are for instance, they have gender dysphoria, and they're getting top surgery. How many kids do you think got top surgery last year? Under 300. Then, think about an issue that's really important to Democrats.\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Children?\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Minors.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: That's an atrocity but continue.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: OK. But over 5800 kids were victims of gun violence, for instance. I don't hear Republicans talking about that.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: What a false comparison.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: It absolutely isn't. It's a priority on the right to talk about trans issues, a priority on the left to talk about gun violence, which affects more people. Abortion is another instance of the tyranny of the minority.\par \par \uc2 I get it, federalism, you send it back to the States, where a group of elected officials have decided, for instance, in like Texas, Louisiana, that women should not be able to get abortions after six weeks, we have women that are bleeding out going into sepsis.\par \par \uc2 In Louisiana, a woman was forced to carry a baby that had no skull (PH).\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: What does that have to do with this topic?\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: What are you talking about, this is -- he says tyranny of the minority. That is --\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: But it had to do with LGBTQ not with all of your favorite issues.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: He's running to be president of the United States of America.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: But that's not what we're talking about, I asked you specifically about the pronouns.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: And I answered and I said why he was wrong to act as if the GOP is the party of the majority opinion. They are not. They're taking minority positions and running with that.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Pro-life is in a minority position.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: It is actually.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Are you going to talk about the fact that it's like 48 percent. But many people --\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: No, I'm going to talk about the 69 percent is the highest it's ever been recorded than now support abortion, at least through the first trimester.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: First trimester?\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Yes. And what's happening then in Ohio and Kansas and Kentucky, if it's not --\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: What's a Democrat stance on abortion?\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: That it's between a woman and her doctor to make those decision.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: What's the -- what's the cut off? Is that what you call that tyranny of minority as well? I think the logic here if you're slicing these issues.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Oh, you're trying to pretend that we want to kill live babies?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: No, I didn't say that -- I didn't say that. I'm just pointing out that this isn't actually the right argument but go ahead.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: OK.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: We finally got to the right argument?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: No. I'd like to get back to the issue and --\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: The issue is not calling someone sun or leaf.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: The issue is not abortion today. The issue was pronouns and LGBTQ. And the tyranny of the minority that Vivek was talking about was LGBTQ.\par \par \uc2 Now, you know what, Greg, I'll let you answer that. But a Maryland court ruled on Monday, the parents now cannot challenge school district's decision to keep gender transitions from families. How do you think teachers feel about this? And no one is asking for their moral perspective.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes, first of all, you know, I've got nothing against it. I am a pansexual. I mean, who doesn't love a woman who can cook? So, I did that for you, Jessica.\par \par \uc2 Look, OK, this is a perfect example. See, the -- if you believe in two sexes, and there are only two sexes, we have these chromosomes that tell us this, you are putting trans lives in danger.\par \par \uc2 So, the activist class creates an illusion of risk, which puts everybody in fear. That's what this story is really about. Like people don't get scared when they're talking about abortion issues, or whether they're talking about race. Sometimes they do, they'll be called racist.\par \par \uc2 But in this case, you have an aggressive activist class that will come after you. So, if you are against removing healthy genitals from children, top surgery.\par \par \uc2 First of all, top surgery, remember when they changed the language, they're screwing you over, OK? So, this is -- that gets that back to the pronouns.\par \par \uc2 But the fact is, if you are against removing healthy genitals or breasts from children, then you know, you're putting people at risk, well, you ask how many people are you putting at risk? They don't know. They don't know because it doesn't exist, or we would have seen those numbers already.\par \par \uc2 So, we're in this disturbing reality where this attention that is given to children is being disguised as therapeutic, right? But it only happened to 300 people, right?\par \par \uc2 But once that phase is over, those 300 kids are destroyed. So, the people that are faking victim status in the -- in the trans activist world are actually creating more victim comes out of children, that is the tyranny of the minority.\par \par \uc2 He is right. Radical fascists dawn this cloak of victimhood, for minority status to claim protection, which enables them to exact insane demands in sports and in school.\par \par \uc2 To your question, it's like they can now say, like, you know what, you can't talk to your kids, because it's a privacy matter that matter. It's between the activist teacher and the child, not the parents. That is insane. That is absolutely insane. How long before Vivek is going to get destroyed for saying that?\par \par \uc2 So, I think that there is this big lie. This is the biggest lie of all that you are punching down when you criticize trans activism. No, you're punching down when you go after parents and their children. Right? That's - - that is the attack because they don't have the media on their side.\par \par \uc2 So, as long as you have the trans community changing the language, top surgery, bottom surgery, non-binary, I'm a leaf, you can get away with anything. But now, it's gone. I think that people are finally fighting back.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: All right, Dana, on a sim