ime.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Splash. And they were in the splash zone.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: OK. Charlie, hit it.\par \par \uc2 HURT: So, check out this video between father and son. It's really awesome.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What we call it, Cash.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Trespassing?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You're supposed to say pond hopping.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Smart kid.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: You go, boy.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: That's it for us tonight. Have a great night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FOXFIV0020230817ej8g00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 One Week Until 1st GOP Presidential Debate On Fox; Biden Tells Debunked Stories; Trump Remains Clear Frontrunner In GOP Primary; GOP Race Takes Shape One Week From First Debate; Ramaswamy Exchange With LGBT Activist Goes Viral; GOP Voters On Importance Of Trump Debating Rivals; Appeals Court Restricts Abortion Pill Access Decision On Hold Until Supreme Court Review; New Report Refutes Baldwin Claim About "Rust" Gun; California Gripped By String Of Brazen Flash Mobs; Big Wall Street Investors Signal Economic Uncertainty; Boy Finds Out He's Mullet Champ On "Fox & Friend\b0\par\par\uc2 Martha MacCallum, Kellyanne Conway, Leo Terrell, Liz MacDonald,  Brian Brenberg \par \uc2 8,111 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 August 2023\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 TODD LAMPHERE, VICE PRESIDENT OF GOVERNMENT RELATIONS, CITYSERVE: This is going to be a long-term process.\par \par \uc2 GILLIAN TURNER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST (on camera): Todd, we've got to leave it there. Thank you so much for taking time with us today. We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 LAMPHERE: Thank you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS\uc2 , FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: A long-term process is an understatement. It's going to need a lot of help from everybody. Set your DVR and never miss AMERICA REPORTS. Thanks for joining us. I'm \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 TURNER: I'm Gillian Turner. The "STORY STARTS" right now.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: All right. Thank you very much guys. Good afternoon, everybody. I'm Martha MacCallum. And exactly one week from the big debate here on Fox and we are starting to see some movement in some areas of these polls and also some moments that have been happening on the campaign trail for the candidates.\par \par \uc2 Kellyanne Conway is going to join me on the new Fox power rankings that the former president is well ahead on, but as I said, some movement in New Hampshire and a couple of places, so we'll see what happens. Kellyanne and I are also going to speak with some voters who tell us what they would like to see and hear from these candidates on the stage and the message that they have ahead of the debate for former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  as well.\par \par \uc2 But first, President Biden is about to make some remarks on the U.S. economy. They've been pushing Biden-omics very hard over the past couple of days. Clearly, this is central to the president's re-election push. Yesterday's speech, though, after it kind of got fact-checked by many, it ran into some serious headwinds. It's a head-scratcher as to why he brings up some of these things that are so easily unwound on the truth meter.\par \par \uc2 There were about seven or eight of them in this speech that got picked apart one by one. Obviously, we're not going to go through them all. The question is just why he keeps raising the same things over that get debunked. But here's just one random one. The president said, oh, I saw this bridge collapse in Pittsburgh when I was on the scene last year. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I watched that bridge collapse. I got there and saw it collapse with over 200 feet off the ground going over a valley. It collapsed.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But Fox News.com points out that, "Hours before Biden's arrival in Pittsburgh for a pre-planned speech, the 477-foot Forbes Avenue bridge collapsed, injuring multiple people. He later visited the site of the damage, but he did not witness the collapse firsthand." So, there's a difference there.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, let's bring in Fox Business host because there's an economic message coming up: Liz MacDonald and Brian Brenberg, and OutKick Founder, Clay Travis. Great to have all of you with us. We're still waiting for the President to speak about the economy. Obviously, it's been a big push over the last several days and I want to start with you on that to our economic experts.\par \par \uc2 There's a new Quinnipiac poll out, Liz MacDonald. 58 percent disapprove of the handling of President Biden of the economy. 71 percent say the economy is good or poor. And 51 percent say that they believe, in their experience, that the economy is getting worse. What do you think of the push that he's making and how effective it is?\par \par \uc2 LIZ MACDONALD, FOX NEWS BUSINESS HOST: Well, you know, it's that Quinnipiac poll follows what Gallup, what Pew, and CNN, ABC, Reuters polls are showing too. And what you're seeing now, Martha, is you don't see Democrats barnstorming the country in their districts talking about the Inflation Reduction Act. You see now the White House changing their rhetoric and their narrative on the Inflation Reduction Act after the President said, I wish I hadn't called it that.\par \par \uc2 And today, you know, you see \uc2 John Podesta\uc2 , you see White House officials calling it basically the Climate Spending Act. And so, it's a different narrative now coming out of the White House. And what is what's really happening behind the scenes is it's mistaking, mistaking spending for achievement. When you have the \uc2 Chicago Fed\uc2 , the \uc2 San Francisco Fed\uc2 , now you have CBO Director Philip Swagel saying, you know, inflation has been triggered by old government overspending.\par \par \uc2 It's helped ignite it and fuel it. And you'll hear the White House say, well, the IRA has Medicare drug pricing negotiations. Yes, that's a good thing. But senior citizens are seeing now compounded 16 percent plus inflation since the president took office. And that's a lot of sticker shock. And that's, I think, hitting his poll ratings.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Brian, your thoughts on this. And let's put up some of the prices that are compounded, as Liz says, since the beginning of the Biden presidency, eggs up 23 percent, milk is 14 percent higher, poultry 23 percent higher, gas 39 percent higher, and in some places people are seeing the gas prices starting to creep up again. Now, we all hope these things are getting better, but what's the reality, Brian?\par \par \uc2 BRIAN BRENBERG, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Well, those numbers are the real numbers that people feel. You know, they're reporting your year-over- year inflation at 3-4 percent. That is meaningless to a family that's paying 20 percent more for groceries than they did two years ago. And the reality is, to make do, to make good on that, they're borrowing at record levels.\par \par \uc2 And worse than that, Martha, and this is the part, the President doesn't lie about this, he just omits it. People are becoming delinquent on their borrowing, their credit card borrowing, their auto loan borrowing. We're hitting levels that are higher than pre-pandemic.\par \par \uc2 So, put that together, people don't feel like inflation's abating, they're putting it on a card, they're increasingly not able to pay that, and then the president tells them, hey, everything's getting better, and they're like, friends, seriously? Have you looked at how we're living lately? That's just not true.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Real experience is the best barometer for Americans across the country. They know what they're going through on a personal level. Clay, you know, I only point out this thing about the bridge because you know that these things keep happening and there are a lot of questions about why the president keeps sort of repeating some of these things that are, are not true.\par \par \uc2 He talked about his father not being able to go to college, turned out he went for a time to \uc2 Johns Hopkins University\uc2 . You know what difference does it make, I guess, if this older gentleman, you know, likes to tell stories that aren't exactly, exactly true?\par \par \uc2 CLAY TRAVIS, FOUNDER, OUTKICK: Well, Martha, I think it matters because it plays into the larger story here about \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , which is, in my opinion, he's not mentally or physically capable of being president of the United States. And I think increasingly that is the opinion of a huge majority of Americans, including, by the way, a lot of Democrats who voted for him.\par \par \uc2 And when he continues to have these errors and these things that are easily refuted falsehoods. These are not factual disputes or even sometimes defensible positions that you could have in the political arena. These are things that are immediately able to be refuted as untrue. For instance, how many times has he said that \uc2 Beau Biden\uc2  died in Iraq, his son?\par \par \uc2 It's awful that his son died, but his son did not die while serving in Iraq. He says it all the time. And it starts to, in my opinion, reflect upon the larger issue for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , which is his mental and physical lack of strength in terms of being able to do this job. And again, this is not just a Republican argument. This is not just an independent argument.\par \par \uc2 Democrats, if they had the choice, would prefer that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  didn't have this job. And I'll just say this, there's not a single job. I'm at Outkick. We've got a lot of employees, Martha. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  couldn't do a job at any of the places that we have people employed at Outkick.\par \par \uc2 And let me just put it this way. If you owned a gas station, could \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  run a gas station? Instead, he's running the country. I don't think he could run an average gas station on a corner anywhere in America.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, it's a 60-plus percent say that they don't want President Biden to run again, uh, somewhere in that neighborhood north of that for the former President Trump as well. So, Americans are looking for alternatives, it would appear, although within the Republican Party, the vice -- President Trump, I should say, is still extremely popular. And we're going to get into that in just a moment.\par \par \uc2 Thank you all, great to have you here. We're going to keep an eye on the President's statements on the economy from the White House. We'll monitor that for you, get it when it breaks. And as I said, brand new Fox Power rankings today. This is the first round of these for the 2024 election.\par \par \uc2 So, as I said, 74 percent of registered Republican voters say that they would support former President Trump if he becomes the GOP nominee. And another new poll is showing a shift in the other candidates that are running in New Hampshire, and it's significant. Kellyanne Conway is up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Breaking news, just moments ago a proposed trial date has now been announced for former President Trump in Georgia in that election interference case. Fulton District Attorney Bonnie Willis proposing March 4, 2024. That is smack in the middle of primaries and Super Tuesday right around there as well. That's coming from Reuters.\par \par \uc2 The GOP frontrunner was indicted along with 18 others on Monday night. He has until August 25th to voluntarily surrender to Georgia authorities for arraignment and processing in that particular case. Tough to keep track of all of these, but that one is she's pushing for March 4th. We'll see what happens.\par \par \uc2 All right. So, we have a lot of new numbers to chew over this afternoon in the Fox power rankings, showing us the Republican candidates stack up one week before the first debate here on Fox. Here's a closer look. Former President Trump, the clear frontrunner, no big surprise in that regard. He has been ahead all along. So, in numbers two and three, down about nearly 30 points from the former president. You've got the contenders there, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  and Tim Scott.\par \par \uc2 And then we have candidates who have moved themselves into the, in the conversation category. You've got \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2 , who is really always in the conversation, always saying something out there that is getting him in the conversation. We're going to look at something he said that was very interesting over the past day.\par \par \uc2 So, \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  also in that category, \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  and Doug Burgum, several former governors in that group. And finally, the outsiders at this point: Asa Hutchinson, \uc2 Will Hurd\uc2 , \uc2 Francis Suarez\uc2 , \uc2 Larry Elder\uc2 , none of these individuals have made the cutoff for the debate stage come Wednesday night.\par \par \uc2 And look at this, the former president has more endorsements from Republican lawmakers and governors than all of his rivals combined. As for the voters, a brand-new Quinnipiac poll shows the former president leading by almost 40 points ahead of \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  who's at 18. That is his lowest number in this survey all year.\par \par \uc2 So, let's chew on these with Kellyanne Conway, former senior counselor to President Trump, now, president of K.A. Consulting and a Fox News contributor.\par \par \uc2 Kellyanne has tons of experience looking at presidential races and how all these candidates are stacking up over the course of the last several years.\par \par \uc2 Kellyanne, great to see you.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 KELLYANNE CONWAY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Thank you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you for being here today. What stands out to you in those numbers at this point?\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: A few things that if you are a Republican primary voter nationwide or in almost any of these early key states, Martha, all these legal woes for President Trump have given you an excuse of not a reason to shop around, and very few people are.\par \par \uc2 And I think next week with Brett and you moderating the debate, the challenge to each of those candidates on the stage who aren't named \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is twofold.\par \par \uc2 The first, you've got to attack \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , take them on hammer and tong. But the second, you almost at the same time as a companion need to attract his voters.\par \par \uc2 It's a nearly impossible task. I predict anybody not named Ron De Santis in that debate stage is going to go after him. I think we're going to try to rough up DeSantis. He's shown a certain resilience -- he recently his also shown as sort of inability to get outside of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  on, you know, the Trump plane flying above in Iowa, him, being heckled by some Trump supporters when he shows up.\par \par \uc2 But he has got a very good ground game in Iowa, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , you're putting it all there. And \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  edging out now, getting some separation from the rest of the field in New Hampshire, shows strength there.\par \par \uc2 The curiosity for me, is everything that another Republican primary candidate, not named \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  would want to say doesn't seem to be evidenced in these polls.\par \par \uc2 Number one, you'd want to say on the one who can beat \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . They all like to say that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  isn't not electable. Well, according to Republican primary, but it was the New York Times Sienna poll, if not, Fox News's poll, 58 to 28 say --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You put that up, guys.\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: Yes, it's -- this is huge, actually, because the electability argument was the one that was supposed to favor all the other candidates, particularly, DeSantis.\par \par \uc2 So, it says 58 percent Trump, 28 percent DeSantis can beat Biden. So, that's two to one.\par \par \uc2 The second thing you'd want to say is -- on there it is, I'm attracting small donors. I'm the grassroots favorite candidate. It turns out \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's contributions, 82 percent of them are $200 or less. 17 percent for \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But DeSantis is playing big in Iowa. Planting a big flag there, a big ground game going into all 99 counties has a ton of canvassers and a good ground game. You got people like \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2 , with help of his governor friend, \uc2 Chris Sununu\uc2  in the state, trying to win New Hampshire.\par \par \uc2 South Carolina is fascinating to me, because you have two South Carolinians in the race: Nikki Haley, the former governor, Tim Scott, the current senator. They are both at about 10 or 11 percent in the polls, Trump's beating them there too.\par \par \uc2 But the key to South Carolina is this. Are you really going to try? If you're one of these candidates, how much you're going to try? How much money are you really going to spend there, when it's winner take all? You can come in a strong second the way Cruz did or third.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: right.\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: The way \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  did last time and still lose every single delegate to the winner.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. I'm so glad you pointed that out. Because this is a game of accumulating delegates.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, when you look at these national polls, when you look at some of these numbers, they don't matter. It's a state-by-state accumulation of delegates. There is some evidence that \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is gathering some delegates in Iowa. So, we'll see what happens with that.\par \par \uc2 You know, in terms of what happens and how they separate themselves, and I think you say it perfectly in terms of the rock and a hard place that they're in, because they want those so-called MAGA voters, and then, they also want to gather some independent votes as well.\par \par \uc2 But take a look at this from \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2 . Because there's a lot of buzz around this young candidate. And it's moments like these that I think people see and it makes them at least interested. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I was just wondering what your views on same-sex couples are?\par \par \uc2 VIVEK RAMASWAMY\uc2  (R), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: I don't have a negative view of same sex couples. I believe that we live in a country where free adults should be free to dress how they want, behave how they want. That's fine.\par \par \uc2 But you don't oppress -- you don't become oppressive by hoisting that on others. And that especially includes kids, because kids aren't the same as adults.\par \par \uc2 I respect that you may have a different opinion. And that's OK. It's part of what makes our country great is that you and I can be civil and have this conversation.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: What do you think?\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: It's a great way of answering the question, because it's the new way of answering that question. So, I worked for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , the first president in U.S. history, Martha, to come to the Oval Office already in favor of marriage equality, same sex marriage.\par \par \uc2 Not \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 , not \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 . If you go back, and I've made my four teenage kids do it.\par \par \uc2 Go back and watch the clips of Barack Obama and \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  in 2008 in those primaries. They are practically yelling at each other. As for whom is more in defense of traditional marriage.\par \par \uc2 So, this is relatively new. But the new part of the conversation that disturbs many people, regardless of their sexual orientation is that you see people at the Pride Parade saying we're coming for your kids. People don't like that.\par \par \uc2 And I think, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  did a marvelous thing in Florida when he said kindergarten through third grade, basically, 8-year-olds should not be exposed to sexually explicit content. It's when he went from 8 years old to 18-year-olds that he lost a lot of people. But when you talk about kids, I think that's a different kind of the conversation.\par \par \uc2 But I personally agree with \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2 . We don't oppose people based on their differentiate characteristics. That's what makes America so great. And I think she was waiting for a different kind of answer, because the stereotypes have taken hold.\par \par \uc2 Tell me exactly what Biden and Harris are doing for her and her community right now, as opposed to this very diverse group of candidates. You know, what I love best for Bret and you in Fox News next week in that debate? Is that the diversity of the group of people that we already know is going to be before.\par \par \uc2 An Indian American woman, Indian American man, African American man, perhaps, a Hispanic man, blue state governor. It's just really incredible to say that --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And that's an organic diversity.\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: That diversity comes from who's getting attention? Who's attracting donors? Who people want to see on that stage? It's not a we need one of these and one of these, right? It's an organic diversity, which I think people -- that's what people really want to see. It's an honest, diverse group.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Kellyanne. We're going to stick around.\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: OK.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We're not going to let you go just yet. Because we're going to speak to some voters who voted for President Trump in the past about next week's debate. What are they going to be watching? Who are they going to be watching? Coming up?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Do you want the former president to come and debate?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: That is a good idea.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes. That's a good idea.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: But he said he may or may not want to. So, do you kind of have a feeling like what do you want to hear from him if he were to go out and debate like all the other candidates?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: To make America great again.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And to make America stronger.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Do you think that he needs to be on that debate stage?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: No. I think he just needs to do like Biden did in the last election. Just stay quiet. Not sure, OK.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I agree.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: A week away from the big debate. Bret and I will be in Milwaukee to moderate for the Republican candidates. President Trump, keeping everybody guessing at this point about whether or not he will be there. He hasn't said that he --\par \par \uc2 He has said though that he does not want to sign a pledge to support the GOP nominee if he loses one of the \uc2 RNC\uc2 's requirements for that debate, because there are -- he said two or three up there that he says he can't support.\par \par \uc2 So, we'll see where that goes. Kellyanne Conway back with us, former senior counselor to President Trump, now, president of K.A. Consulting and Fox News contributor, along with two Republican voters, Deb Ludwig from Ohio, and Noreen Johnson from Pennsylvania, both very important states.\par \par \uc2 Great to have both of you with us, Deb and Noreen.\par \par \uc2 Let's take a look at this first poll. Most Americans -- this was released today from the A.P.\par \par \uc2 Most Americans don't want Trump or Biden to run. Trump, no, 69 percent -- don't run. Biden, no, 75 percent for the current president, don't run. Deb, how do you feel? Do you want the former president to run or not?\par \par \uc2 DEB LUDWIG, REPUBLICAN VOTER, OHIO: I would prefer he did not run. I'd like to make the election about the people and about the country. And I think we're going to get all hung up on Trump, which is unfortunate.\par \par \uc2 But I think he did a pretty good job that so many people can't get past it today. Do you have a favorite of the other candidates out there, Deb?\par \par \uc2 LUDWIG: It's DeSantis.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And what do you think about the fact that he hasn't had the lift off in his campaign that most people expected?\par \par \uc2 LUDWIG: I'm sad about that. I would prefer that we have somebody who is of action and can actually make things happen, which is what I think he did in Florida.\par \par \uc2 But I don't know how you get elected if you'd get can't get that push to actually get people to follow you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LUDWIG: I want people to listen to what he is saying, and then -- and let go of all this other baggage.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. Let's take a look at this one, when we go to Noreen on this, Kellyanne.\par \par \uc2 The Quinnipiac poll shows a majority of GOP voters feel it's important for all qualified Republicans to be at that first debate. 57 percent say it's very important. You agree with them, Kellyanne? And let's bring in Noreen.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: I'm with them. I'm one of them. And it's 84 percent, you know, or actually 91 percent all told at some level of importance, and I think it's because that's direct democracy. This is the opportunity for we the people, free of charge, to access those candidates, and watch them face the tough questions about each other, about the front runner \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , and certainly about past statements they've made or policy positions that they not let -- not yet surfaced.\par \par \uc2 I want to say something about your earlier point, which is the poll numbers showing people don't want Trump or Biden. Do you know, from 1980 to 2008, when Hillary was denied being on the ballot by \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  being the primary, we had had a Bush or Clinton on the ticket for 24 years.\par \par \uc2 So, Americans love to say I don't want Trump or Biden, but they do go to the -- they go to \uc2 Chick-fil-A\uc2  every night in the minivan, and order number three. We love to talk that we're revolutionaries, we want new things, they have all these choices. And yet, Biden is probably going to be the nominee with Harris as his running mate, and Trump is the frontrunner.\par \par \uc2 I think the more important polls are the ones that you are pointing out, which is who is responding to the voters? Who showing up in these states? And then also, what percentage of the vote does Biden have among Democrats right now, and just Trump having among Republicans?\par \par \uc2 Just a little bit of softness there for each of them? They all tie it up if they are the nominees, but people are shopping around, they're just not yet convinced with what they see.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK, Noreen, this is another poll out there that says, Quinnipiac, a majority of GOP voters think of felony conviction should make them ineligible to be president. 58 percent say that.\par \par \uc2 Do you agree, Noreen? I remember we talked before. I know you're a solid Trump voter. Right? What do you say about that?\par \par \uc2 NOREEN JOHNSON, REPUBLICAN VOTER, PENNSYLVANIA: I am, Martha. And yes, nice to see you again. And you as well, Kellyanne.\par \par \uc2 Obviously, you know, Trump has a base, and Trump's base is the most loyal base on planet earth. He has the strongest people that are behind him, honestly, regardless, because -- and we have to remember, these attacks, and these accusations, and these -- even these indictments and the 400 charges, it's incredible.\par \par \uc2 But the truth is, the actual being attacked here and the target is not \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . It is us. it is Americans. He's just in the way. So, we can look at all these polls. I know you love, you know, the percentages that are out there.\par \par \uc2 I think until we get down to election day, they are all just polls, as we remember with Hillary's fireworks show that was canceled.\par \par \uc2 So, I think until we get there, we have to remember that these indictments and these charges are a joke, and that the truth will come out. The truth will be revealed. Right now, we're just going through those motions.\par \par \uc2 I think the debate would be a fantastic idea because the other thing that Trump is, is the best debater in the world. So, I think, him, coming out and being honest and open about everything he is going through, only shows his strength.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. We'll see if he's listening to you, Noreen. Thank you very much. Good to see you both. Deb, thank you. Great to see you. We'll check in with you as we move through the election. And thanks to Kellyanne, always great to have you with us.\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you so much. So still ahead, Leo Terrell is here on these flash robs that are burdening American retailers. It is absolutely disgusting what is going on in these stores. And they're not arresting anybody. Also new, forensic evidence that disputes Alec Baldwin's claim that he didn't pull the trigger in the deadly movie set shooting, a new development here that could change the scene for him.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALEC BALDWIN, ACTOR: The trigger wasn't pulled. I didn't pull the trigger.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Breaking just a short time ago, there could soon be new limits on a medication that is used in most, I believe around 60%, of all abortions in the United States are by pill. And now there is a decision by this appeals court that is ordering restrictions on it, but that ruling is on hold. So that means nothing changes for right now, but it may change based on what the court is saying until the Supreme Court can weigh in on this. The justices previously took action to keep the drug, the abortion drug, on the market while this legal battle plays out. So a lot of fast moving parts in that debate, but we'll keep you posted.\par \par \uc2 So, how about this? Charges for Alec Baldwin could be back on the table in the wake of a new report claiming that he did pull the trigger on the gun that killed cinematographer Halyna Hutchins on the "Rust" movie set almost two years ago. The actor has long insisted the opposite.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS, ABC ANCHOR: It wasn't in the script for the trigger to be pulled.\par \par \uc2 BALDWIN: Well, the trigger wasn't pulled. I didn't pull the trigger.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANOPOULOS: So you never pulled the trigger.\par \par \uc2 BALDWIN: No, no, no, no. I would never point a gun at anyone and pull a trigger at them, never, never.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So involuntary manslaughter charges against Baldwin were dropped back in April, reportedly because new evidence revealed that the gun had been modified before he handled it, which would line up with what he said. But now, a new expert analysis has been done for Santa Fe special prosecutors who say, "the trigger had to be pulled or depressed sufficiently to release the fully cocked or retracted hammer of the evidence revolver."\par \par \uc2 Steve Wolf is a weapons safety expert for films. He's been involved investigations of accidental movie set shootings, including the 1993 shooting of Bruce Lee's son, Brandon Lee. He's serving as an expert witness in the civil suit against Baldwin brought by relatives of Halyna Hutchins, and also a suit brought by the "Rust" script supervisor. Steve, good to have you back on the program. Thank you for being here.\par \par \uc2 Can you explain this to everybody at home in simple terms about what they thought the gun had been modified, but now they say it hasn't. What are they talking about?\par \par \uc2 STEVE WOLF, WEAPONS SAFETY EXPERT FOR FILMS: They're talking about how much effort has to be applied to the trigger to make the gun fire. So if you pull this back and you press here, the gun fires. If you find that this is just too difficult for you, people have modified this to make it easier to fire the gun. And it's irrelevant because there's no part of gun safety that relies on any part of the gun to be working properly.\par \par \uc2 When we say all guns are always loaded, OK, well, you either loaded it or you check that it was empty. That doesn't depend on the gun, right? We say, don't put your finger on the trigger. Well, you do or you don't. I mean, I saw photographs that he did. If they say, don't point the gun somebody, and he did, then he's breaking the rules. So gun safety doesn't rely on mechanical parts because mechanical parts are always subject to fail.\par \par \uc2 And so, the idea that the gun being modified or not would have any relevance to the case is moot.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But if he was arguing that it had been modified and that it released without him doing anything, without him pulling the trigger, and now this analysis is saying that is not possible, that he had to have, is that correct?\par \par \uc2 WOLF: That is correct. That is correct. So let me turn this around so you can see exactly what we're talking about here. If I'm holding the action revolver, and I have my finger on the trigger, I'm not, to my knowledge, pressing it right, I'm just holding it. But I'm actually applying sufficient pressure that if I then cock the gun and release it, it fires. So it'll fire without me pressing the trigger because I'm already pressing the trigger.\par \par \uc2 And so, in that instance, it's possible for both people to believe that they're right. He says, I didn't press the trigger, meaning he didn't deliberately press the trigger. And it's also not possible to have caused the gun to fire unless there had been pressure on it at the same time.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So there was a prior prosecutor in this case who's not there anymore, but they spoke with Judge Jeanine back in January and here's what she said. This is Andrea Reeb and Mary Carmack-Altwies. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MARY CARMACK-ALTWIES, DISTRICT ATTORNEY: We definitely believe he pulled the trigger. The \uc2 FBI\uc2  lab report confirms that.\par \par \uc2 ANDREA REEB, THEN BALDWIN SPECIAL PROSECUTOR: Mr. Baldwin had a duty at the base level to never hold a gun and point it at a person while pulling the trigger.\par \par \uc2 CARMACK-ALTWIES: We believe Baldwin, as a producer, knows everything that goes on this set. And so, yes, there was a lot of problems, there are a couple accidental discharges, there was a lot of safety concerns that were brought to the attention of management and he did nothing about it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You're nodding, Steve, real quick reaction.\par \par \uc2 WOLF: Yes. Well, as soon as you find out that one is dangerous, you get them off your set. And this particular armorer had safety claims against her from her prior movie, which was her first movie, and then again on her second movie, and they did nothing to remove her or replace her with somebody. So they allowed a dangerous situation to persist. With regard to the \uc2 FBI\uc2  lab, who are you going to believe, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  or Alec Baldwin's lawyers?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. We'll be watching. We'll see if he faces new legal jeopardy in this situation and how that would work. Steve Wolf, thank you, always good to see you. Thanks a lot.\par \par \uc2 So, still ahead, \uc2 Kevin O'Leary\uc2  joins us and Leo Terrell after a string of brazen flash robs. I mean, it is just unbelievable what is going on in these stores across the country, that one in the Golden State, when we come back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Groups of thieves brazenly smashing into stores across the country, stealing thousands of dollars of merchandise within just minutes. It's frightening. And the blatant law breaking in front of cameras again and again is really, I just think, shocking people across this nation. Just watch this for a second.\par \par \uc2 I mean, they're dragging the display things across as they're grabbing suitcases and bags, and running down the street to steal all of this stuff, very well-orchestrated. It happens within just a few minutes. You can imagine how terrified the people are who work in this store. Look at this guy. Just sort of like, what's going on?\par \par \uc2 So in Los Angeles, that happened, 30 people ransacked it, stealing $300,000 worth of stuff. One of them sprayed a security guard with bear spray in his face. Glendale, last week, 30 suspects, middle of the day, people out there eating lunch, as you can see under the umbrellas, are just watching what's going on (inaudible). They stole $300,000 worth of items from there.\par \par \uc2 And in Irvine, California, three men, broad daylight, stealing close to $900,000 worth of jewelry. You wonder, why doesn't someone stop them? Aren't there cameras? What is going on? Where are the police? Aren't they afraid they're going to go to jail? No.\par \par \uc2 Leo Terrell has lived in California all his life. He's a civil rights attorney and a Fox News contributor. Leo, great to have you here.\par \par \uc2 LEO TERRELL, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Thank you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: What is going on? I could have shown ten more videos that are of similar scenes, and it's frightening.\par \par \uc2 TERRELL: It's very frightening. And Fox is the only network that's broadcasting. Martha, the bottom line is this, no one's afraid to go to jail when they steal. There's no arrest at all. Crime is encouraged. And you know what's so amazing, Martha? These crimes are not happening at night where these guys have to break in. It's happening in broad daylight.\par \par \uc2 They can walk in, steal in broad daylight, walk out, no arrest whatsoever. Why? Because if they even get arrested, they're going to be released because of the no-bail policy. This is what's happening in progressive Democratic cities, and all we've gotten from Karen Bass, the city mayor, is we should stop this. It is \uc2 George Soros\uc2 's money that is forcing Democrats to stand down and allow crime to happen.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You do hear, though, from more and more people, celebrities and other folks out there who are saying, you know, we can't live like this, folks. Here's an \uc2 HHS\uc2  memo about the \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  Federal Building in San Francisco where they're telling workers basically, don't come in. It's not safe to come here. In light of conditions, we recommend you maximize the use of telework for the foreseeable future.\par \par \uc2 I mean, why are they letting these people win and shut down this building, Leo?\par \par \uc2 TERRELL: Well, I'll tell you simply why, Martha. This is the problem. For the last four or five years, it is -- see, the system, the criminal justice system, is systemically racist. It's racist to arrest someone. It's racist to stop people from committing crime. The Democratic Party had bought into the left wing, progressive 1619 critical race theory, (inaudible). And this is why this is happening in these cities.\par \par \uc2 These individuals who wake up in the morning, walk into a store knowing right off the top they're not going to get arrested --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But you know what, their lives would be so much better if they were afraid to get arrested because then they would have to find another course of life and get a job and stay in school. Brandon Johnson, the Chicago Mayor, says you really must be careful how you refer to these individuals. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You're not aware of some of these large gatherings?\par \par \uc2 BRANDON JOHNSON (D), CHICAGO MAYOR: No, that's not appropriate. We're not talking about mob actions. We have to be very careful when we use language to describe certain behavior. There's history in this city. I mean, to refer to children as like baby Al Capones is not appropriate.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: What is he talking about? Does he want to have a safe city of Chicago? I mean, who does he think he's helping, Leo?\par \par \uc2 TERRELL: He's an apologist for crime. And basically, let me say it right now, mob, mob, mob, because that's what you see. Flash mob terrorizing. Notice this, Martha, San Francisco, Los Angeles, Chicago, New York, all Democratic cities, all run by people of color, all claiming racism. It's ridiculous. It's insulting.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Leo Terrell, thank you very much. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TERRELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Always good to see you, Leo. Thanks. So the investor who got rich predicting a housing market collapse back in the early two thousand s is now placing a very large bet against the market, the S&P and the Dow. \uc2 Kevin O'Leary\uc2  helps us break it down next.\par \par \uc2 (CLIP FROM THE MOVIE " THE BIG SHORT")\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So the guy who predicted the 2008 mortgage meltdown seems to betting against some major stock markets right now. \uc2 Michael Burry\uc2 , made famous by the book in the movie "The Big Short," has apparently been shorting again, this time the NASDAQ and the S&P. So what is he seeing and is he right?\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in \uc2 Kevin O'Leary\uc2 , chairman of O'Leary Ventures and "Shark Tank" star and investor. Great to have you here today, Kevin.\par \par \uc2 We reminded everybody in the tease of that moment from the movie where \uc2 Michael Burry\uc2  is telling them that the housing market is about to fail, and he's got bare feet and his t-shirt on, and they're asking if they should trust him. What do you think about what he's doing right now? Can you explain it to us?\par \par \uc2 Telling them that the housing market is about to fail. And he's got bare feet and his T shirt on, and they're asking if they should trust him. What do you think about what he's doing right now? Can you explain it to us?\par \par \uc2 KEVIN O'LEARY\uc2 , SHARK TANK STAR: Well, the difference between "The Big Short" story, which was the housing market, was very specific to one sector of the economy, real estate, but more specifically to the mortgage backed market. And the syndication of debt was very complicated then, very opaque. He figured it out. That was a house of cards on that one specific metric, and that was one area of the market. And he was 100% right, and he deserves to win that one because no one else saw it the way he did.\par \par \uc2 What he's doing now is completely different. The S&P 500 has 500 mega cap companies in it in 11 sectors of the economy, real estate only being one of them. And so, you would need every sector to falter, or at least the valuations of every company in the S&P to significantly go down at the same time to win on that bet.\par \par \uc2 Now, NASDAQ, the NASDAQ 100 is primarily technology, but not all tech, but there are seven stocks in there right now that are trading at a very high price to earnings ratio, and that's a different kind of bet. But they, too, are in multiple sectors of technology.\par \par \uc2 So I don't think it's going to be that easy. People don't understand about shorting. Many investors learn it the hard way. I was one of them long time ago. When you short a stock, you have unlimited liability. If you buy a stock at $10, it goes to zero. You lose $10. But if that stock, if you're short it and it goes up to $200, $300, $400, $500, you don't have enough money to cover the margin calls you're getting, and very few people do.\par \par \uc2 Now, when you bet the whole market short, you have real cajunas. And that's what he's doing here.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. He's got 1.6 billion potential exposure here. We don't know if he has changed that investment since he originally placed those bets. But \uc2 Warren Buffett\uc2  also selling $8 billion worth of stock, that also has people concerned. Are you concerned as you look at the larger market right now? Is there something going on that people are not seeing or that they're choosing not to see?\par \par \uc2 O'LEARY: No. I'm not concerned about one individual short making that decision. When you short the market, you're essentially saying, I'm timing the market. I know something others don't know, because people that try and live off market timing have a very hard time. You may get lucky once, as he did in housing, syndication of mortgage debt, but this is a whole different kettle of fish that he's playing with.\par \par \uc2 Warren Buffett\uc2  selling $8 billion worth of stock, that's actually a rounding error in his portfolio. It's almost meaningless. It doesn't matter. And he's just trying to stay diversified. And my bet is that sale is actually one of his new managers just trimming a portfolio, and that's why the market doesn't care that much about it.\par \par \uc2 But to actually say that I know the market's going to go down, he will be right one day. When is unknown, and how much pain will he have to take along the way, and does he have enough dry powder, as they like to say, at the margin desk? Because every time that market goes up another 1% ne or 2%, that phone is ringing and they're saying, send in some more cash.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You got to cover this, yes.\par \par \uc2 O'LEARY: So you really have to know what you're doing. It's very risky.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Interesting. Thank you so much, Kevin. Great to see you. Thanks for coming on today. We'll see you soon.\par \par \uc2 O'LEARY: Take care.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. So take a look at these kids. The three finalists in the 2023 USA Mullet Children's Championship, a contest to raise money for wounded warriors. And the winner was announced live on "Fox and Friends." Steve and Ainsley looking a little bit different today. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN AINSLEY, FOX NEWS HOST: Drum roll. Rory Ehrlich and his cheddar wiz mullet. And Rory joins us now with his mother, Erin.\par \par \uc2 STEVE DOOCY, FOX NEWS HOST: That's right.\par \par \uc2 AINSLEY: Hello.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: They did not know until just now that they are the winner.\par \par \uc2 AINSLEY: They're so happy.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: They're cutie's. The happiest little mullet wearing kid in America, and his mom was so excited at finding out 