ks she's going to be able to stop right at those cones. Let's make sure that she can't.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: No, no, no, no. I can't operate the brakes at all. That is frightening.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It's not just about physically taking over your car, but loads of personal information can be stolen as well, like your biometric data and all the info from your phone. And what makes this entire thing a little scarier is who can actually access the data.\par \par \uc2 According to the House Select Committee on China, CCP-aligned firms now control 45 percent of the world's cellular connectivity module market. Joining me now is Andrea Amico, founder and CEO of Privacy 4 Cars. Andrea, can China or any other bad actors hack into our cars?\par \par \uc2 ANDREA AMICO, PRIVACY4CARS FOUNDER AND CEO: Well, yes. We are given increasing capabilities to bad actors to do bad things, whether it's hostile government like China, or gangs of hackers, or frankly, the local stalker and identity thief. Unfortunately, technology in cars is making all of this much easier.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, I think people love the convenience of connectivity. It feels, you know, really good to be able to, oh, I got everything right there. It's a big screen. But back in 2016, this Chinese company hacked a \uc2 Tesla\uc2  car remotely. And the team advised \uc2 Tesla\uc2  owners, please do update the firmware on your \uc2 Tesla\uc2  car, the latest version, to ensure that the issues are fixed, and then to avoid potential driving safety risks.\par \par \uc2 OK. So, one thing, Andrea, to have your information taken, that's horrible, but also, it's the vision of a car being able to be turned around or driven off a road. Is this just a science fiction fantasy or a potential real problem?\par \par \uc2 AMICO: No, there's been a number of well-known exploits that have been done by researchers. And unfortunately, you know, what researchers do and what we see is, you k