.\par \par \uc2 But breaking tonight, a Utah man is dead following \uc2 FBI\uc2  raid in Provo, sparked by alleged threats against President Biden and other officials. Authorities say they had reason to believe the man was serious about his intentions, with President Biden scheduled to arrive in Salt Lake City in just a few moments.\par \par \uc2 Senior Correspondent Claudia Cowan is in Salt Lake City tonight with the latest. Good evening, Claudia.\par \par \uc2 CLAUDIA COWAN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret. As you mentioned, this happened in Provo, Utah. That's about 45 minutes away from Salt Lake City at the home of a man who has been on the \uc2 FBI\uc2 's radar for many months.\par \par \uc2 According to a federal criminal complaint. Craig Robertson claimed online to be a 74-year-old former Air Force vet and the owner of many rifles, shotguns and handguns, and a so called ghillie suit. That's a type of camouflage. The \uc2 FBI\uc2  says Robertson posted many credible threatening messages on social media and was planning to take action.\par \par \uc2 The complaint quotes Robertson is saying, I hear Biden is coming to Utah digging out my old ghillie suit and cleaning the dust off the M24 sniper rifle. Welcome buffoon-in-chief.\par \par \uc2 Documents show Robertson also made threats against former President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  and back in March against New York County District Attorney \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2  saying he planned to go to New York with a gun to, "put a nice hole in his forehead".\par \par \uc2 Court documents show agents tried contacting Robertson about that particular post. He reportedly told them it was all a dream and not to come back for him without a warrant.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FBI\uc2  has not said what precipitated this morning's shooting only that the incident is still under investigation.\par \par \uc2 So far, no changes to the president's schedule here tomorrow where he will talk about health benefits for veterans and attend a fundraiser, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Claudia, thank you.\par \par \uc2 New developments tonight in the Biden family business scandal, the House Oversight Committee has released bank records detailing alleged payments from Russia, Ukraine, Kazakhstan, to the Biden family and its business associates.\par \par \uc2 The White House is calling the memo old news innuendo and misdirection without any connection to President Biden. Republicans say they are continuing to follow the money and the communication.\par \par \uc2 Senior Congressional Correspondent Chad Pergram is on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): The memo appears to reveal millions coming from Ukrainian energy firm \uc2 Burisma\uc2  where \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  served on the board. Records released by House Republicans indicate President Biden attended dinners in Washington with ex Soviet empire oligarchs.\par \par \uc2 REP. BYRON DONALDS (R-FL): This is pay back for the millions of dollars that the Biden family received because of their access to the brand, to the big guy being \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . There were no services being delivered, except for access.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): Russian oligarch Yelena Baturina is one of the richest women in Russia. She allegedly scored a dinner with then Vice President Biden at D.C. swank Cafe Milano, but only after sending millions to \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's business partners.\par \par \uc2 The White House docked but asked about how Yelena dodge sanctions earlier this year.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: I'm not speaking to individual persons from Russia.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): Republicans believe this makes the connection between the son and the father.\par \par \uc2 REP. JIM JORDAN (R-OH): 30 plus meetings, 20 some phone calls, dinners with the wealthiest woman in Russia and all they talked about was the weather we're supposed to believe?\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): Also, a transaction of $142,000 which allegedly landed in the account of a Hunter Biden related firm. Hunter later received a high end sports car supposedly costing the same amount.\par \par \uc2 The top Democrat on the House Oversight Committee Jamie Raskin says the transactions do not involve the president. Raskin characterize this as an effort to justify calls for an impeachment inquiry. Other Democrats believe it's about something else.\par \par \uc2 REP. JIM MCGOVERN (D-MA): This is about distraction. This is about revenge. And this is about doing what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  wants them to do.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): Some Republicans say the records point toward impeachment.\par \par \uc2 REP. SCOTT FITZGERALD (R-WI): There's no doubt that there's going to be not only an inquiry, but I think an impeachment trial that will move forward sometime this fall.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (on camera): The GOP says this was not only pay to play but pay to dine, and with the car pay to drive, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Chad Pergram on Capitol Hill. Chad, thank you.\par \par \uc2 There is news tonight regarding a possible new plea deal for \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 . This comes as you heard President Biden continues his western swing with a focus on climate change.\par \par \uc2 White House Correspondent Peter Doocy traveling with the president in New Mexico tonight. Good evening, Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Bret, good evening. There's been a lot of news on this front while President Biden has been out of pocket but he did about 90 minutes ago give us first of its kind reaction when he waved me over at the end of an event.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: There's this testimony now, where one of your son's former business associates is claiming that you were on speakerphone a lot with them, talking business, is that --\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I never talked business with anybody, and I knew you'd have a lousy question.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Well, what do you -- why is that a lousy question?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Because it's not true.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (on camera): No surprise. I've heard from a White House official who tells us that in their reading of that exchange, the question is wrong. And his answer is right, because they insist President Biden never did talk about business on those calls, which is what he said in response to my question, so he didn't really seem like he wanted to talk about that today. He did want to talk about climate change.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I'm not here to declare victory on the economy. I'm here to say we have a plan.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): President Biden says his Climate Action is historic and Republicans agree.\par \par \uc2 RICK PERRY, FORMER ENERGY SECRETARY: This is the largest fight against fossil fuel in the history of the world.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): That criticism really isn't that different than official White House talking points.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I wanted to stop all drilling in east coast and the west coast and in the Gulf. But I got lost in court.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): As a different court awaits details of an updated plea deal for \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 , White House officials are responding to bank records just released today by House Republicans. "Comer and his fellow extreme Republicans in Congress are now admitting they haven't uncovered any proof of involvement or wrongdoing by President Biden".\par \par \uc2 The just released bank records detailing transactions from 2014 and 2015 allegedly linked \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  to payments from Kazakhstan, one of the countries that stands to benefit from a new U.S. ban on uranium drilling, critics question if that's a coincidence.\par \par \uc2 DONALDS: \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  just took an allotment of uranium off the table for what? So we could be more reliant on foreign countries? I mean, I get it, his son gets a bunch of money from these countries overseas.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): President Biden is hoping to keep people talking instead about investments he's making to address climate change.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE ABRAMS\uc2 , TWC METEOROLOGIST: Are you prepared to declare a national emergency with respect to climate change?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: We already done that.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): He hasn't, which has the Sunrise Movement a climate focused youth group saying hmm, so just do it? Still no emergency but lots more taxpayer funding for green energy.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: That's Bidenomics.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (on camera): President Biden wants to talk about the transition to greener energy and he picked a good place to do it. The factory where they set up a microphone for him now makes wind towers, but they used to make something that is familiar to anyone who has ever been to a tailgate, solo cups, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We didn't think your question was lousy, we hope you get more. Peter Doocy on the trail with the president. Peter, thanks.\par \par \uc2 In Ohio, ballot initiative to make it harder to amend that state's constitution has failed. We told you about it last night. Voters overwhelmingly rejected Ohio's issue one Tuesday. That means the threshold for future ballot measures to pass will remain at 50 percent. Conservatives had pushed for a new 60 percent requirement. Ohio voters will decide on an amendment in November ballot -- in the November ballot that if passed would cement abortion rights in Ohio's constitution.\par \par \uc2 One key House Republican is urging his GOP colleagues to make border security a requirement before continued funding of the \uc2 Homeland Security Department\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Texas Republican Congressman Chip Roy also wants DHS Secretary \uc2 Alejandro Mayorkas\uc2  fired. Here's Congressional Correspondent Aishah Hasnie.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE WALTZ (R-FL): We have to use the power of the purse.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): A funding showdown is brewing again on Capitol Hill, this time over the southern border crisis. Texas Congressman Chip Roy ginning up support to block funding for the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  unless President Biden meets his demands.\par \par \uc2 In this letter obtained by Fox News and circulating amongst House Republicans, Roy says he wants Biden to sign the House GOP border bill into law. Give law enforcement tools to target cartels, reimburse Texas $10 billion for protecting the border and --\par \par \uc2 ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS\uc2 , SECRETARY, HOMELAND SECURITY: The border is secure.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (VOICE OVER): Fire DHS Secretary \uc2 Alejandro Mayorkas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 REP. CHIP ROY (R-TX): Republican should have one simple message. No border security, no funding.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (VOICE OVER): But the president is on a different page when asked if the U.S. should protect migrants fleeing extreme weather.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I think the United States should do everything it can to help people who are in desperate means and have no other means of help.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (VOICE OVER): Roy's ultimatum comes as the GOP conference struggles to find the votes to impeach Mayorkas, moderates are stalling in fear of the political ramifications.\par \par \uc2 REP. ANDY BIGGS (R-AZ): According to his own definition, he's doing all right. I think that's impeachable.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice over): Arizona Congressman Andy Biggs held a field hearing Tuesday night about how the migrant surge is affecting border communities. And folks like John Ladd, a fourth generation rancher are fed up.\par \par \uc2 JOHN LADD, RANCHER: Your school systems are ruined, your hospital systems are ruined, and your welfare is ruined. America is nowhere near what it was when I grew up.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (on camera): And Bret, tonight, Fox did reach out to the White House to get a response to Congressman Chip Roy's list of demands. We haven't heard back just yet, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Aishah, the State Department has finally responded to that heart wrenching testimony from Gold Star families this week.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Yes, exactly. Those -- many of those families feel betrayed by this administration over this withdrawal, especially because the administration continues to call it a success. And I got to ask the State Department about that today, watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: They have a real hard time when the administration calls this withdrawal a success. Do you still believe it was a success?\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW MILLER, STATE DEPARTMENT SPOKESPERSON: We believe it was the correct policy choice, but again -- it was the correct policy choice but again, I would never want to quibble with a family member who has suffered such an enormous tragedy.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: But I think it's -- I think that word is --\par \par \uc2 MILLER: I'm going to move -- I'm going to move on to someone else.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: So, that's Matt Miller. He did move on, but he came back to me for a follow up and I asked him if the State Department can admit that it made mistakes. And Matt would only say that an after action report concluded that there were things that the State Department could have done differently, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Aishah Hasnie live on Capitol Hill. Aishah, thanks.\par \par \uc2 California Democratic Senator \uc2 Dianne Feinstein\uc2  was briefly hospitalized after suffering a fall in her San Francisco home yesterday. Her office says all of the senator's scans were clear and she returned home. That 90-year- old has suffered from extensive health issues for more than a year, leading some in her own party to question her fitness for office.\par \par \uc2 Stocks were down today ahead of new inflation numbers. The Dow lost 101, the S&P 500 was off 32, the NASDAQ fell 162.\par \par \uc2 China is making some of its boldest statements yet regarding its threat to Taiwan, and who it thinks is to blame. This comes amid a significant display of military force in the region.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Alex Hogan has details tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ALEX HOGAN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): New tensions off the coast of Taiwan today as the island claims 10 Chinese Air Force aircraft entered its air defense zone and five Chinese warships ran combat readiness patrols. It's the second such incursion this week.\par \par \uc2 MILLER: We have always urged China not to take actions that would escalate cross strait tensions.\par \par \uc2 HOGAN (voice over): But China points blame at the U.S. calling Washington the hidden force provoking South China Sea tension.\par \par \uc2 Now, China's airing a documentary on the country state broadcaster which shows Chinese troops claiming they're ready to attack Taiwan, one of them even saying if war broke out, and the conditions were too difficult to safely remove the naval minds in actual combat, we will use our own bodies to clear a safe pathway for our forces.\par \par \uc2 Tensions also flaring this week as China urged the Philippines to tow away a grounded warship used as a military outpost in contested waters.\par \par \uc2 FERDINAND MARCOS JUNIOR\uc2 , PHILIPPINE PRESIDENT: We continue to assert our territorial rights in the face of all of these challenges.\par \par \uc2 HOGAN (voice over): Neighboring nations are warning of the danger in the region. Yesterday, Taiwan and Japan promised to deepen relations.\par \par \uc2 TARO ASO, FORMER JAPANESE PRIME MINISTER (through translator): The most important thing for us currently is that there should be no war in the region, including the Taiwan Strait.\par \par \uc2 HOGAN (voice over): President \uc2 Tsai Ing\uc2 -wen of Taiwan says it will continue to bolster defense capabilities.\par \par \uc2 TSAI ING-WEN, TAIWANESE PRESIDENT: Taiwan is always ready to defend our democracy and way of life.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOGAN (on camera): New tonight, President Biden has signed an executive order that would ban American investments on technology industries in China that Beijing could then use to fortify its military. That includes things like micro electronics, computer chips, or even artificial intelligence, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Alex Hogan in London. Alex, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, why officials in Hawaii are declaring a state of emergency? We'll take you there.\par \par \uc2 And later, a new space race is emerging with eyes on returning to the moon.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Newly released court documents reveal Special Counsel Jack Smith obtained a search warrant for former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's \uc2 Twitter\uc2  account in January.\par \par \uc2 A federal judge held \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , now known as X, in contempt, for delays in compliance with that warrant, and fine the company $350,000. The judge's decision was upheld by a federal appeals court in Washington last month.\par \par \uc2 In a sealed opinion, a redacted version of that opinion was released by the court today, bringing the previously unreported legal battle to light.\par \par \uc2 Hawaiian officials have declared a state of emergency as multiple wildfires burn across the islands. Authorities say at least six people have died so far. There are reports residents in one Maui Island community jumping into the ocean to try to escape the flames.\par \par \uc2 Senior national correspondent William La Jeunesse has the story tonight from our West Coast bureau.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: This was a home. Here is their car.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM LA JEUNESSE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Paradise lost on Maui, in what's likely Hawaii's worst natural disaster since Hurricane Iniki 30 years ago, and the Kilauea eruption in 2018.\par \par \uc2 Governor Josh Green, calling the fire catastrophic.\par \par \uc2 LT. GOV. SYLVIA LUKE (D), HAWAII: Wildfires that wiped out communities, wildfires that wiped up businesses, wildfires that destroyed homes.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE (voice over): Firefighters are battling three fires, including one near Lahaina, Hawaii's first capital and a historic whaling town filled with shops, galleries, and restaurants.\par \par \uc2 A pilot who flew over says the town and some 200 homes were completely destroyed, along with many boats who were tied up in their slips as owners and captains could not get into Lahaina in time to save them.\par \par \uc2 The governor also reporting 14 people who jumped into the water to save themselves were rescued by the Coast Guard.\par \par \uc2 Fires knocked out power, cell service, and landlines, and forced two evacuation centers to evacuate, as 60-mile-an-hour winds from Hurricane Dora push the flames.\par \par \uc2 The governor called up the National Guard to help with some 1,500 tourists stranded at the Kahului Airport, and hundreds of others already evacuated to Oahu.\par \par \uc2 Maui Memorial treated 20 burn victims, three were airlifted for emergency treatment in Honolulu.\par \par \uc2 LUKE: This is not a safe place to be. On certain parts of Maui, we have shelters that are overrun, we have resources that are being taxed.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE (on camera): Now, because of the fires are still active, officials cannot give us an exact count on injuries or property damage. Though the governor puts it into billions.\par \par \uc2 Aerials I saw show the Lahaina harbor and the charter fishing boats inside, Bret, almost entirely destroyed, leaving a huge hole in Maui's economy. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: William La Jeunesse, in our West Coast bureau. William, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we talk with Republican presidential candidate, former New Jersey Governor \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 First, here is what some of our Fox affiliates around the country are covering tonight. Fox 11 in Los Angeles says thieves are seen fleeing a luxury clothing store in Glendale, carrying arm loads of merchandise reportedly worth hundreds of thousands of dollars.\par \par \uc2 It happened Tuesday in broad daylight as you see here. One store worker says the entire theft took less than a minute.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Jacksonville, Florida. One of the big stories there tonight from Fox 30. Mega Millions says its largest jackpot ever has been won by someone who bought a ticket at a \uc2 Publix\uc2  in Neptune Beach.\par \par \uc2 If you were in that \uc2 Publix\uc2 , check your ticket. That ticket is worth $1.58 billion with a B. Sorry to my staff.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look "OUTSIDE THE BELTWAY" from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Welcome back to SPECIAL REPORT.\par \par \uc2 The first debate of the 2024 presidential campaign is two weeks from tonight. One of the candidates who will be on that stage in Milwaukee is Republican \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2 . He joins us tonight.\par \par \uc2 Governor, thanks for being here.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE\uc2 , (R), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Thanks for having me, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You're in Manchester, New Hampshire and the RealClearPolitics average of the New Hampshire polls, recently, has you tied for third with the former president at 41.7; \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  at 17.7. You're at 6.7 along with Tim Scott.\par \par \uc2 What's happening on the ground there, and how do you see this race?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIE: Well, you know, Tim, just -- rather, in just the last two days, have been two more polls that have come out that now have me tied for second with Governor DeSantis; one at 11 percent and one at nine percent.\par \par \uc2 So, we feel like it's really moving in our direction. Our momentum is going the right way, Governor DeSantis is going in the wrong direction. And so, first job is to get pass Governor DeSantis here.\par \par \uc2 In New Hampshire, we have now caught him. Now, we need to pass him. And then, we're going to take on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  one-on-one.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But when you say take on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , you look at all these polls, whether it's the national polls or in New Hampshire or Iowa, he is up 20, 30, 40 points.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIE: Yes. Look, and the campaign hasn't begun yet, Bret. The campaign is going to begin when you and Martha get on that stage with the rest of us two weeks from tonight. The idea that the American people are really focused on this race right now in the middle of vacation season in August around America is kind of silly.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I have some video of you in New Hampshire talking to folks. But speaking of the debate, also in New Hampshire, just over the past couple of days, the former president asked the crowd what he thought -- what they thought about whether he should go to the debate. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: If you are leading by 50 and 60 and 70 points, do you do that or not? I don't know. Should I? OK, are you ready? Poll. We take a free poll. Should I do the debate?\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We couldn't discern from that answer what that means, but he has been doing that on the stump now for a couple times. What difference does it make one way or another for you if he is on that stage or he is not?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIE: It doesn't make any difference for me. The difference it makes is for the Republican voters, because it shows his complete lack of respect for Republican voters. He thinks because he won the nomination twice that it's his. And he thinks that these numbers actually mean they approve of what he did, when in essence it's just that he is the best known person in the race. And so if he doesn't show up two weeks from tonight, what it will show, once again, is what he showed throughout his presidency, was complete disrespect for the people. The only good thing for me will be I will have more time to speak because he won't be droning on about his three indictments with a fourth to come.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You just returned from Ukraine, a trip, and you met with the president there, \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2 . What's your takeaway from that trip? And the politics of that is tricky within the Republican Party, at least in the primary.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIE: Bret, it's tricky if you try to thread a needle. If what you try to do is tell the truth, it's not tricky. I went to the city of Bucha, Breg, where they told, the mayor of Bucha told me of the Russian attack there where men were dragged out of the homes, their eyes were gouged out while they were alive. Their hands were tied behind their back and they were shot execution style in the back of the head. And then when they were done with that, the Russian soldiers went into the homes and raped the women. Nearly 20,000 children, Bret, have been kidnapped from Ukraine, ripped from the arms of their parents by the Russian army and taken back to Russia for them to be reprogrammed to be anti-Ukraine and pro-Russian federation.\par \par \uc2 This conduct is barbaric and is incomprehensible. And it is being funded by the Communist Chinese and executed on by the barbaric authoritarian dictator in Russia \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , who \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has called brilliant and a great leader. I'm telling you, Bret, if we don't stand up for this, American men and women will wind up fighting in Taiwan because we have been unwilling to arm the Ukrainians to the extent to allow them to win the war. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has given them just enough to lose this war, not enough to win it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You share this position with Vice President Pence, Ambassador Haley that have come from similar points of view. But you've heard, I'm sure, on the trail concerns about the money going there, the focus there, the problems on the border and the problems in the U.S. That is popping up in the GOP primary, is it not?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIE: It is. And it's popping up because people are being ill-informed by people like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  who calls this a territorial dispute when you have 160 civilians murdered behind the cathedral in Bucha and buried in a shallow grave, and a 10-year-old girl shot dead while she was walking her dog. That's not a territorial dispute, Bret. That is a full out authoritarian war against freedom and liberty.\par \par \uc2 And it's only defeatists who think that we can't support the Ukrainians and secure the border at the same time. We will fight this proxy war against China. We will make sure they understand that any move against Taiwan will be one that will make the juice not worth the squeeze. And we will not have to send American men and women over there to defend Taiwan, who manufactures two thirds of the semiconductors, Bret, in the entire world. Do we want the Communist Chinese to be in charge of doling that out? I don't think so.\par \par \uc2 And Ukraine is where this battle starts, and American can win it there without having to spill any American blood anywhere else in the world.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Last thing. We talked about this when you launched your campaign, but your feelings about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  as a person, as a candidate, as a president have evolved quite significantly over time.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIE: Sure. He has left me. And you know, this is not the guy that I have known all those years. In fact, when he decided on election night 2020, Bret, to say the election was stolen when we now know, you know, I know, and any objective person knows the election was not stolen, when he stood behind the seal of the president of the United States in the East Room of the White House and said that, that was is it for me. You cannot undermine the American people's faith in democracy because your ego was hurt, because you are the first person to ever lose to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  outside of the state of Delaware. I understand that must hurt to lose to somebody as feeble and incompetent as \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , but he did. And if we nominate him again, he will again.\par \par \uc2 He left me, Bret. And that's why I need to stand up and make sure the truth is told about this. And quite frankly, I don't understand why my other colleagues in the race are not saying it, except that either they're unwilling or too scared to do it. I'm neither, and I will be there two weeks from tonight with you and Martha to make this case and the case for why a Christie presidency is the right one for America.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Like Ukraine, though, in the GOP primary, a two-tiered system of justice as people look at what is happening with the former president, how it's being adjudicated, and they look at what is happening with \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 , how that's being handled. This say this is not fair, and there is a sympathetic figure, you acknowledge, in the former president inside the GOP primary.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIE: I agree that it's not fair. That doesn't change \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's conduct, whether it's criminal or not. Is that what we want behind the desk in the Oval Office?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Governor Christie, as always, we appreciate the time, and we will see you in Milwaukee.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIE: Looking forward to seeing you, Bret. Thanks for having me on tonight.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Please join Martha MacCallum and me as we host the first primary debate of the Republican primary season. It will happen right here on FOX News Channel on August 23rd, 9:00 p.m. eastern, live from Milwaukee, Wisconsin. The second debate, by the way, can be seen on FOX Business September 27th from the Ronald Reagan Presidential Foundation and Institute in Simi Valley, California.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the race to return to the lunar surface. And, later, the panel on the new revelations in the Biden family business scandal and the politics of the moment.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Florida Governor and Republican presidential candidate \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  has suspended a state attorney for dereliction of duty on crime. The suspension of Ninth Judicial Circuit State Attorney Monique Worrell came in an executive order from DeSantis. The governor's office says Worrell has routinely allowed murders and other violent offenders to escape the full consequences of their criminal conduct.\par \par \uc2 Vermont Senator Bernie Sanders quietly funneled $200,000 from his campaign's coffers to his wife's nonprofit institute. FOX News Digital reports that organization appears to do very little on paper and pays six figures worth of compensation to her son. \uc2 Federal Election Commission\uc2  records show the independent senator's committee cut two $100,000 checks to the Sanders Institute for Reported Charitable Contributions in January and March. The expenditures are the largest from the Sanders campaign to any entity this election cycle. Senator Sanders campaign and the Sanders Institute did not respond to FOX News Digital's inquiries by press time.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, we examine how the U.S. is stacking up against the competition in the space race. \uc2 NASA\uc2  is working to send humans back to the moon while other nations are making major efforts of their own. Here is correspondent Jonathan Serrie.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN SERRIE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Artemis 2 astronauts toured the crew module that will carry them on a 10-day mission around the moon and back.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And liftoff of Artemis 1.\par \par \uc2 SERRIE: Concerns about the capsule's heat shield raised during an uncrewed test flight last year may push next year's astronaut mission into early 2025, and putting boots on the lunar surface may take even longer as \uc2 NASA\uc2  contractors \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  continues work on the perspective landing craft.\par \par \uc2 More than half a century after America beat Russia to the moon, Russia and India are in a race to land uncrewed spacecraft near the lunar south pole this month, and China plans to send astronauts by 2030. Ice discovered in that region could be used for life support or converted into rocket fuel for future missions to Mars. That has spurred demand for robot manufacturers to test how machines and, ultimately, humans can work for extended periods in this unexplored, super cold environment.\par \par \uc2 TOM MCCARTHY, MOTIV SPACE SYSTEMS: We are testing systems to minus 180 degrees. Those are liquid nitrogen region temperatures. Those are significantly cold. There are some areas that look like they might be even colder than that on the moon.\par \par \uc2 SERRIE: Legal experts say the race for resources presents new challenges to a 56-year-old treaty that sets general guidelines for international space exploration.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE HANLON, FOR ALL MOONKIND CEO: A state like China or the United States cannot claim property on the moon, cannot claim territory on the moon. But what does that mean for commercial enterprises? And what does it mean when you want to go to the moon for a specific reason like getting access to the water ice?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 SERRIE (on camera): Experts warn Chinas ambitions could lead to potential conflict on the moon. But they also point out cooperation between the U.S. and Russia led to the construction of the International Space Station. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jonathan, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel on the latest news in the Biden family business scandal and some more politics.\par \par \uc2 First, Beyond our Borders tonight. An American nurse and her daughter who were kidnapped in Haiti's capital have been released. Alix Dorsainvil works for a Christian aid organization. No word whether a ransom was paid. The \uc2 U.S. State Department\uc2  thanked its Haitian and U.S. partners for helping facilitate that release.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Japan. One of the big stories there tonight, \uc2 Prince Harry\uc2  is extolling the virtues of sport during his first leg of his Asia tour. The Duke of Sussex said while in Tokyo, sport not only helps build physical strength but can also heal the body and mind.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories Beyond our Borders tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. BYRON DONALDS, (R-FL): The White House says \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  never got a dime. I don't believe it. And at the end of the day, if \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  was spending money for his dad on behalf of his dad, his dad doesn't have to take a dime.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT FITZGERALD, (R-WI) HOUSE JUDICIARY COMMITTEE: Everyone is kind of waiting to see did they take the money, did \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  take the money, is the money in the accounts. We are at that level of scrutiny at this point.\par \par \uc2 REP. PAT FALLON, (R-TX) HOUSE OVERSIGHT COMMITTEE: It's clear to me that \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  was Joe's bagman. And they were smart enough not to be that stupid to where direct payments would go to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, the House GOP oversight committee releasing images of bank statements they say shows direct payments from various folks in Russia, Ukraine, Kazakhstan, to the company that \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  worked with Devon Archer on. The president really hasn't answered questions about this. Peter Doocy traveling with the president tried to ask a question on this topic.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: There's this testimony now where one of your son's former business associates is claiming that you were on speakerphone a lot with them talking business. Is that --\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I never talked business with anybody. And I know you would have a lousy question.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Well, why is that a lousy question?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Because it's not true.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We didn't think it was a lousy question. He said he never talked business with them. White house counsel office memo from Ian Sams, "House Republicans can't prove President Biden did anything wrong, but they are proving every day they have no vision and no agenda to actually help the American people."\par \par \uc2 OK, with that, let's bring in our panel, Katie Pavlich, news editor at Townhall.com, "USA Today" White House correspondent Francesca Chambers, and Josh Kraushaar, FOX News Radio political analyst.\par \par \uc2 Josh, listen, we know that this comes out in pieces. We are following each step, and there are some people who get frustrated because it doesn't end where they think it's going to end, yet. But you can't say there's nothing there.\par \par \uc2 JOSH KRAUSHAAR, FOX NEWS RADIO POLITICAL ANALYST: Yes. And when President Biden said he never talked business with these executives, with these \uc2 Burisma\uc2  executives, we know he sat down for a dinner that presumably lasted quite a bit of time with \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 , with some of these oligarchs. I don't think these denials are passing the credibility test, Bret.\par \par \uc2 And look, when there is smoke, there is a fire. And there is a lot of smoke here. And Republicans are trying to do a lot of digging connecting President Biden, at least payments, trying to figure out whether there were actual payments or transfers to President Biden himself. I think it's going to be tough to prove that and it's going to be tough to actually have a sort of a quid pro quo there. But it's a big political headache for the White House. I think the next big question is whether the Republicans are going to have the political unity to pursue any kind of impeachment inquiry, whether they're going to take the next step to try to really put the political pressure on the White House.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It's interesting, the White House -- first of all, the payments by the Russian oligarch, we have got a graphic kind of showing where it's going. But Francesca, the White House is pretty -- they have to walk back some of the statements, even today's, the way the president talks about it. He is not asked about it except from us. And it wasn't really a lot to be said, other than he didn't talk business.\par \par \uc2 FRANCESCA CHAMBERS, "\uc2 USA TODAY\uc2 ": Well, this week there hasn't been too many opportunities to ask him the questions about it because there haven't been White House press briefings. He has been traveling this week. Last week he was on vacation. And so then he did get the question about it today. The White House has been arguing, as you were pointing out, that this is a wild goose chase, and Democrats are saying that this is a fishing expedition. And allies of the White House are essentially saying that they have not proven that President Biden took money, took bribes, that when these speaker phone conversations took place that Devon Archer says that business wasn't discussed. It was more like how's the weather, that business wasn't discussed at these dinners. And Republicans haven't been age to show proof connecting President Biden directly to \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's business dealings.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. It's just tough, the dinners, the phone calls, it's not all about the weather, number one. Number two, the firing of that prosecutor in Ukraine, you go back to that tape and it has a different look through that prism, Katie. But big picture, what do you think?\par \par \uc2 KATIE PAVLICH, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, you don't need to have a bank account with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's name on it to connect the dots on what may have been occurring here. At the very least it brings up big questions, again, about why \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  hasn't been charged with not registering as a foreign agent. These new documents today show $20 million in foreign money going to \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  and his business partners, $6.5 million from \uc2 Burisma\uc2  alone. That, of course, was happening when \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was the vice president.\par \par \uc2 And on a policy level, that's a problem when it comes to the two standards of justice, but also, when it comes to what President Biden was doing with that portfolio under President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 . He was in charge of the Ukraine portfolio. \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  went to China with his father on Air Force 2, of course. And the question about him having dinner. He says that he didn't have discussions about business. But he is having dinner with Russian oligarchs at Cafe Milano in 2014 and 2015 after Russia invaded Crimea. So how does that affect U.S. foreign policy, and how does it you know go into his thinking today when it comes to how he is negotiating and discussing these issues on the Ukraine war with Russia?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We'll see where this goes politically for Republicans and how they take it forward. One thing that politically for Republicans does not look too good at this moment happened in Ohio last night, and that is this special election just on this issue to amend the state's constitution. No on Issue 1, 57 percent to 43 percent. This essentially will mean that abortion will be on that ballot. Here is Democratic Congressman \uc2 Jim McGovern\uc2  in Ohio.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. JIM MCGOVERN, (D-MA): Their extremism on abortion rights is scaring people. And I think that is to the benefit of Democrats. But here's the deal. The Republican Party stands for nothing anymore. We know what they're against, but they are for nothing.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Ann Coulter put up, what do you say now, is it an "X" post? What is it, an X-ing?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: "By the time Republicans noticed states keep voting in favor of abortion, there will be no elected Republicans left. Ohio makes it seven. A no vote is pro-abortion."\par \par \uc2 She is right to point out that these states, even conservative states, are taking these referendums and voting that way, Josh. And it is a canary in the coalmine on that issue.\par \par \uc2 KRAUSHAAR: Look, abortion is a tough issue for Republicans, and frankly, a lot of Republicans haven't figured out how to message the issue. There are many Americans who support restrictions on abortion, but when you kind of have an all or nothing choice like you did in Ohio, it was sort of a proxy for that, that debate, you're going to have a hard time getting the pro- life side represented.\par \par \uc2 Also, this is -- voters generally want to keep the status quo no matter what the issue is. And I think it's hard to win a referendum when you are actually changing the constitutional laws of Ohio.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But seeing this result, Francesca, seeing Kansas's result, I bet you see Vice President Harris and President Biden on abortion a lot more.\par \par \uc2 CHAMBERS: Well, and you have seen Vice President Harris out there on this in the 2022 midterms, and her team, Democrats, are looking at and this saying this is an issue that they think she should be out there more on in this election, and you should expect to see her continue to campaign on that.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Will Republicans have an answer though, Katie?\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: Republicans so far have had terrible answers on this issue. The one person who has had a good answer is Virginia Governor Glenn Youngkin, Republican, says look, I'm pro-life, but the majority of Americans, including Democrats, want limits at 15 weeks. So we can talk about that 15 weeks.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: There is a couple other candidates who are at 15 weeks.\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: For the most part, though, in the last election, for example. They ran away from the issue thinking it was settled because it had been returned to the states, and now we are seeing it voted on and that's the way it was supposed to be. But Republicans certainly have to come up with better answers about the majority, what the majority of people think.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Panel, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Finally tonight, a SPECIAL REPORT salute.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: On the boat when this happened, I said you will never have a fishing trip like this in your life.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: New Jersey businessman being hailed as a hero after rescuing a group clinging on for dear life in the Atlantic Ocean. Joey Cabasso and a fishing crew held their lines in the water for only a few minutes. They saw the group screaming for help. They rushed in, pulled one man on deck. They saved the rest. That's great story.\par \par \uc2 Fair, balanced, and unafraid. THE INGRAHAM ANGLE is up now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020230810ej8900001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Suspected Cartel Gunmen Cross Illegally Into Texas; Abbott Policy Criticized By Democrats; House Republicans Meet In Arizona; Recreational Space Being Converted To Migrant Shelter; Governor Shapiro Floated As Potential Democratic Rival; Abortion And Gun Control Influence Ohio Referendum Vote; Guardianship Fight Over Child Found After U.S. Raid; Eastern Town Of Pokrovsk, Ukraine Targeted Overnight; Authorities Investigating Fight Which Broke Out Around Boating Incident; Electric Vehicle Company Touted By Biden Administration Files For Bankruptcy; Car Companies May Focus Again On Hydrogen Powered Cars; Massachusetts Governor Declares State Of Emergency Related To Accommodating Immigrants; President Biden Goes On Multistate Tour Discussing Biden Administration Initiatives To Combat Climate Change\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Bill Melugin, Madison Alworth, Mike Emanuel, Mike  Tobin, Greg Palkot, Jennifer Griffin, Jeff Paul, Peter Doocy, Grady  Trimble, Byron York \par \uc2 7,725 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 August 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 KATIE PAVLICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR (on camera): It is amazing to see all of them, being there in D.C. on a sunny day, in front of the Lincoln Memorial, and the World War II Memorial is not far from there.\par \par \uc2 So, Honor Flight does great work, and amazing to see all these Purple Heart recipients in one place. Thank you all for your service.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: It would be -- it was an honor flight, and they just fought each in the plane.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: It's an honor -- they did the fighting.\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: That would be the only way that they could get on T.V.\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: All right. That's it for us. Have a great night, everybody.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Good evening. Welcome to Washington, I'm Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 Another big shake up in the \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  presidential campaign.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, many Democrats are looking for an alternative to the incumbent. We'll explain.\par \par \uc2 A melee in Alabama over the weekend, what caused it and what police are doing about it?\par \par \uc2 Plus, our "WHATEVER HAPPENED TO" segment on hydrogen powered vehicles.\par \par \uc2 But we begin tonight with the crisis along the southern border. We have pictures you will only see here on Fox News, showing heavily armed operatives from drug cartels, illegally crossing into this country.\par \par \uc2 This comes as a delegation of Democratic lawmakers visits the region today.\par \par \uc2 We have "FOX TEAM COVERAGE". Madison Alworth on Randalls Island, New York, where locals are beginning to tear up athletic fields to construct migrant shelters.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Bill Melugin, though, starts us off tonight, live from Eagle Pass, Texas. Good evening, Bill.\par \par \uc2 BILL MELUGIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Bret, good evening to you.\par \par \uc2 Illegal crossings are starting to surge once again here at our southern border. This is after a lull during the month of June. Now, Fox News has obtained some brand-new startling photographs showing it is not just asylum seekers crossing into the U.S.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN (voice over): Startling exclusive images from the southern border, as a group of suspected cartel gunmen are seen crossing illegally into Fronton, Texas in the Rio Grande Valley.\par \par \uc2 A law enforcement source tells Fox, it happened Saturday night. The men were armed with rifles and body armor, and a lead Border Patrol BORTAC agents responded, but couldn't find the gunman.\par \par \uc2 Republican presidential candidate \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  reacted on X, the platform formerly known as \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , saying "Another example of why I will authorize use of deadly force at the border."\par \par \uc2 The incident happened just days after an armed human smuggler was seen by a Texas DPS drone, as he guided a group of illegal immigrants through the Rio Grande in Eagle Pass.\par \par \uc2 He escaped back to Mexico.\par \par \uc2 A delegation of House Democrats visited Eagle Pass today, where they criticize Texas Governor Greg Abbott, and his deployment of razor wire and water buoys to the Rio Grande.\par \par \uc2 REP. JOAQUIN CASTRO (D-TX): Texas Governor Greg Abbott has launched a barbaric campaign of cruelty against migrants. This operation isn't about border security, it's a political stunt.\par \par \uc2 LT. CHRISTOPHER OLIVAREZ, SPOKESPERSON, \uc2 TEXAS DEPARTMENT OF PUBLIC SAFETY\uc2 : There is never been any mistreatment of migrants. No one else is doing more right now than Governor Abbott and what we've been doing as far as the state of Texas.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: In the meantime, illegal crossings at the southern border are beginning to spike again; up 30 percent in July over June, and the surge is continuing into August.\par \par \uc2 Sources tell Fox, Border Patrol's Tucson, Arizona sector has seen daily crossings hit almost 2,000 in recent days.\par \par \uc2 One border patrol station in the Rio Grande Valley sector reported 1,100 illegal crossings in just 24 hours.\par \par \uc2 And the Fox drone team continues to witness illegal crossings here in Eagle Pass.\par \par \uc2 North Dakota governor and Republican presidential candidate \uc2 Doug Burgum\uc2 , also sounded off in Eagle Pass.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 DOUG BURGUM\uc2  (R-ND), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: So far, right here in this sector, 98 different countries people crossing, including Syria, Iran, China.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN (on camera): And Bret, in Arizona tonight, House Republicans from both the oversight and judiciary committees are going to be coming together in Sierra Blanca. That's where they're going to be holding field hearing on the Biden administration's handling of the border crisis. That's set to start any minute now.\par \par \uc2 They'll also be continuing their investigation of DHS Secretary Alejandro Mayorkas, as they continue to investigate him for a possible impeachment inquiry.\par \par \uc2 We'll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Bill Melugin, along the border. Bill, thank you. Parents and neighbors in one New York City area neighborhood are upset tonight that a popular play area for children and adults is being converted to shelters for migrants.\par \par \uc2 Fox Business correspondent Madison Alworth is on Randalls Island tonight. Good evening, Madison.\par \par \uc2 MADISON ALWORTH, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret.\par \par \uc2 New York City has wasted no time, converting these soccer fields into a large migrant shelter. The plan was announced just yesterday. Crews here all day today, working on these fields, converting them into what will be a large migrant shelter. Hammering and pile driving to create a home and a floor that we'll use to house 2,000 single adult migrants.\par \par \uc2 A soccer coach who showed up this afternoon expecting to play on these fields was shocked at how quickly the city moved. He said this will impact 1,000s of students.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL LEON, COACH, ASPHALT GREEN SOCCER CLUB: I heard about this two days ago. I thought this was month -- two-month that I had -- I haven't even spoken to my bosses. I don't know what the school is going to do.\par \par \uc2 I don't think a 10-year-old girl, a 10-year-old boy is going to understand that fields not available anymore, for whatever reason. They're just going to think, well, why is an open field closed? What's the reason?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALWORTH: During a press conference today, Mayor Eric Adams defended the decision to use Randalls Island by saying, it's a choice that Zachary Iscol, his commissioner of Emergency Management made.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ERIC ADAMS (D), MAYOR OF NEW YORK CITY: if he determines the space that he's using must be used. That's the space we're going to use, because I trust him, and I trust the team that they put together.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALWORTH: The move also sparking concerns over safety. With unvetted migrants set to live next to fields and parks utilized by families and kids.\par \par \uc2 This, as news that a migrant in Erie County, New York 26-year-old Jesus Guzman-Bermudez has been arraigned on first-degree rape charges for allegedly assaulting a woman in the presence of a 3-year-old. Guzman- Bermudez is being held without bail.\par \par \uc2 Back out here live, construction will continue on the soccer field to convert it into that large scale migrant shelter.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, in Massachusetts, the governor there, \uc2 Maura Healey\uc2 , declaring a state of emergency today over the amount of migrants crossing into her state. Saying they do not have adequate shelter. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Madison, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well, there is a new push tonight among some Democrats to find an alternative to President Biden for next year's election. As of right now, the president has only two declared primary opponents, and neither has much traction inside the Democratic Party.\par \par \uc2 On the Republican side, there is another shake up in the campaign, a former President Trump's most significant challenger for the nomination.\par \par \uc2 And we have breaking news tonight about the former president's legal issues. Chief Washington Correspondent Mike Emanuel takes a look tonight. Good evening, Mike.\par \par \uc2 MIKE EMANUEL, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CHIEF WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT: Bret, good evening.\par \par \uc2 The federal judge overseeing former President Trump's case here in Washington has ordered a hearing this Friday morning. It's about the back and forth over so-called protective order regarding what Mr. Trump's able to speak about sensitive information in this case; a major campaign issue for the former president.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL (voice over): In battleground New Hampshire, GOP frontrunner former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  took aim at rival candidate, Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: That son of a bitch is running. Can you believe it? I just got him elected. So, that's why I've been particularly hard at him. Unfortunately, his work because he's crushing.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL (voice over): A distant second in most polls, DeSantis announced today, he is replacing his campaign manager. A DeSantis campaign spokesman saying, "We are excited about these additions as we continue to spread the governor's message across the country."\par \par \uc2 It is DeSantis's third staff shakeup in less than a month, and comes just two weeks after his campaign laid off one-third of its staff. Former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , announced he's qualified for the upcoming debate, the eighth candidate so far.\par \par \uc2 A Pence campaign spokesman saying, "\uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  made quick and easy work of the donor threshold and he's looking forward to a substantive debate about the issues important to the American people. Hopefully, former President Trump has the courage to show up."\par \par \uc2 On the democratic side with \uc2 New York Times\uc2  polling suggesting 50 percent of Democrats believe the party should nominate someone other than President Biden, moderate Minnesota Congressman \uc2 Dean Phillips\uc2  is urging big name Democrats to challenge him.\par \par \uc2 Phillips argues Democrats want a competition among well-qualified candidates and not a coronation, and admits he is considering a run.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 DEAN PHILLIPS\uc2  (D-MN): People who are should jump in, because we need to meet the moment. The moment is now. That is what the country is asking.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL (on camera): The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  floated a trial balloon in recent days for Pennsylvania Governor Josh Shapiro, who is only been in office since January.\par \par \uc2 The publication noting, Shapiro won the critical battleground state by a wider margin than President Biden did in 2020, and praise Shapiro for quickly repairing Interstate 95 after a partial collapse in 12 days. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right. More on this with the panel. Mike, thanks.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: You bet.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Please join Martha MacCallum and me, as we host the "FIRST REPUBLICAN DEBATE" of the primary season. It will happen right here on Fox News Channel August 23rd, 9:00 p.m. Eastern, live from Milwaukee, Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 Voters in Ohio are deciding today whether to make it harder to amend the state's constitution. The practical effect of this could determine the fate of some very controversial issues, drawing national attention from some big names in politics and entertainment.\par \par \uc2 Senior Correspondent \uc2 Mike Tobin\uc2  tells us how that plays out.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Mike.\par \par \uc2 MIKE TOBIN\uc2 , FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR CORRESPONDENT: And Bret, polls are getting ready to close in Ohio's unusually hot summertime referendum.\par \par \uc2 The item on the ballot is called Issue 1. It's about making it more difficult to change the state constitution. But issues driving the effort or abortion and gun control. That's why this referendum is attracting national attention, big money and big names from outside of the state.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN LEGEND, SINGER: I need you to vote no on Issue 1. Some people are trying to alter the rules of our democracy, to stop us from having a say. Our vote is our voice. It's our power to shape the future we believe in.\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE (R), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Now, for years, left-wing interests have abused the constitutional amendment process in Ohio to get their extreme agenda into law. But the Ohio constitution protection amendment will put an end to that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TOBIN: Currently, an amendment to the Ohio Constitution can be passed by a simple majority vote. If I to 1 passes today, that threshold raises to 60 percent of the voters.\par \par \uc2 The timing is significant. In the fallout of the Dobbs decision that overturn Roe v. Wade, Ohio Democrats got an amendment on the November ballot that if passed, would submit abortion rights in the state constitution.\par \par \uc2 So, Republicans want to make that more difficult. The impact will reach past abortion. Ohio Democrats have said they want gun control in the Constitution. Ohio Republicans want Issue 1 to put that out of reach for them.\par \par \uc2 Republicans and Democrats both say they're trying to prevent outside forces from influencing Ohio politics, but both sides are accepting significant contributions from out of state donors.\par \par \uc2 And polling leading up to today showed us statistical dead heat for and against Issue 1. Bret, back to you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: \uc2 Mike Tobin\uc2  in our Midwest bureau. Mike, thanks.\par \par \uc2 A downgrade of credit ratings for small and mid-sized banks sent stocks into the red today. The Dow lost 159. The S&P 500 was off 19. The NASDAQ fell 110.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the unusual custody battle involving a victim of the Afghan War and the Americans who want to adopt the child. First, here is what some of our Fox affiliates around the country are covering tonight.\par \par \uc2 Fox 11 in Los Angeles, as more than 11,000 city workers participated a one- day walkout to protest what their union calls, bad faith contract talks. Residents are facing an array of service disruptions from trash pickup delays to swimming pool closers. L.A. mayor, Karen Bass insists the city will not shut down.\par \par \uc2 Fox 13 in Tampa, as authorities arrest the former leader of St. Petersburg church for allegedly operating a multimillion-dollar theft ring that spanned several Florida counties.\par \par \uc2 Robert Dell was pastor of The Rock Church and operator of a halfway house for people recovering from drug addiction.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Chicago. One of the big stories there tonight from our affiliate Fox 32. \uc2 NASA\uc2  scientist resume flights over Chicago to perform research on air quality.\par \par \uc2 Their DC-8 was scheduled to fly over Chicago airspace from mid-morning to midafternoon. It's the third such flight in the past week.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look "OUTSIDE OF THE BELTWAY" from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: A custody battle is developing involving a child whose parents were killed during a U.S. operation in Afghanistan. Now, her extended Afghan family is fighting a U.S. Marine and his wife.\par \par \uc2 Chief National Security Correspondent Jennifer Griffin has that story from the Pentagon.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRIFFIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Court documents called her Baby Doe. Her parents and five siblings were killed in a U.S. military raid in September 2019. The baby survived in the rubble and was rescued by U.S. Army Rangers treated at a U.S. military hospital in Bagram and reunited with her next of kin and the State Department who found a cousin and his wife, both educated and in their 20s who agreed to raise the child.\par \par \uc2 But U.S. Marine Major Joshua Mast and his wife say they saw it as their Christian duty to save the child, beginning a complicated legal battle to adopt the child, which court documents allege involves deception and the abduction of Baby Doe, whose Guardian say Major Mast lured them to the U.S. during the chaotic Afghan withdrawal and then took the baby from the Afghan couple when they arrived at Fort Pickett.\par \par \uc2 "After they took her, our tears never stop", the young Afghan woman told \uc2 The Associated Press\uc2 . Right now, we are just dead bodies. Our hearts are broken.\par \par \uc2 The Mast deny this characterization. All parties are now under a judge's gag order. A new judge reversed the Mast adoption order in March. But Baby Doe now 4 years old remains with major Mast, his wife and their children. The Mast say they want a DNA test for the Afghan family now living in Texas.\par \par \uc2 But cousins often do not have exactly the same DNA, Major Mast brother Richard who served as his lawyer promised a judge in 2020 his brother was not trying to adopt the Afghan child, which proved to be untrue. What are you asking for ultimately, the judge asked, your client is not asking to adopt the child. No, sir. He wants to get her medical treatment in the United States because we dispute that this is a family member.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN (on camera): In a strongly worded statement to Fox News, the State Department reiterated its original decision to reunite Baby Doe with her next of kin as required by international law. The \uc2 FBI\uc2  and NCIS are currently investigating.\par \par \uc2 A senior U.S. defense official said Major Mast has been reassigned to administrative desk duty at Camp Lejeune, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jennifer Griffin at the Pentagon. Jennifer, thank you.\par \par \uc2 At least nine people were killed, dozens injured during Russian shelling in eastern Ukraine overnight -- Ukraine overnight. Moscow has launched more than 100 missiles, rockets and other weapons into Ukraine in the last two days.\par \par \uc2 Senior Foreign Affairs Correspondent Greg Palkot is in Kyiv tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GREG PALKOT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR FOREIGN AFFAIRS CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Horror again in Ukraine at the hands of Russia. Apartments, a hotel and a restaurant in the eastern town of Pokrovsk targeted overnight by one cruise missile, then, a short time later and another. Nine killed, more than 80 injured. First responders, police, civilians, young and old.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE (through translator): First impact came, we were not hit. Then suddenly, the window fell on me.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT (voice over): Russia claimed it was hitting a military target, injured local say otherwise.\par \par \uc2 VOLODYMYR NIKULIN, INJURED UKRAINIAN POLICE OFFICER: The Russian are criminals committed another awful crime.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT (voice over): This as Ukraine's summer counter offensive against Russia grinds on, Kyiv is getting more creative in its attacks on Russia, including recent naval drone strikes on Russian ships.\par \par \uc2 VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY, UKRAINIAN PRESIDENT (through translator): If Russia continues to dominate the Black Sea, Ukraine will do the same.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT (voice over): The U.S. continues to back Ukraine. Another $200 million of military aid expected to be announced, delivery of M1 Abrams tanks to start at the end of the summer.\par \par \uc2 SABRINA SINGH, DEPUTY PENTAGON PRESS SECRETARY: We feel confident that we can continue to supply Ukraine with what it needs on the battlefield.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT (voice over): And Ukraine is already thinking about holding Russia responsible for civilian attacks like this week's. Assembling the wreckage of Russian hypersonic rockets, long range cruise missiles, short range but big danger models taking each missile apart to find out where it's from right down to the last detail and serial number. Collecting evidence to use against Moscow and hope for future criminal trials.\par \par \uc2 ANDRII KULCHYTSKYI, KYIV FORENSIC INSTITUTE: (through translator): The damage is impossible to estimate. So we do everything to investigate.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PALKOT (on camera): And back to that Russian striker in the east. In his video message this night, President Zelenskyy said Russia will bear maximum responsibility for this. There must be verdicts against terrorists. Strong words, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Greg Palkot early Wednesday morning in Kyiv. Greg, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, a massive brawl in Alabama, what caused it and what authorities are doing about it?\par \par \uc2 First, Beyond our Borders tonight, former Pakistani Prime Minister Imran Khan appeals his conviction and three year prison sentence. The Islamabad High Court will hear the appeal Wednesday. Khan was ousted in a no confidence vote in April of last year. He remains the country's leading opposition figure and was convicted and jailed Saturday on charges of concealing assets after selling steak gifts he received while in power.\par \par \uc2 Tens of thousands of people move inland as South Korea evacuates the World Scout Jamboree ahead of a tropical storm. The scouts are arriving at university dormitories, government and corporate training centers and hotels. Thousands of British and American scouts left the country over the weekend.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Rio de Janeiro from Earth Cam. One of the big stories there tonight, Presidents of South American Amazon Rainforest Nations converge to chart a common course for protection of the bio region and address organized crime there. That summit in Brazil is the first of its kind in 14 years.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories Beyond our Borders tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: At least two people were killed and more than a million customers were left without power as severe storms impacted the Midwest and the East yesterday. A South Carolina teenager was killed after being hit by a tree and then, an Alabama man was fatally struck by lightning in a parking lot.\par \par \uc2 That system -- the storm system brought flooding rain, hurricane, force wind gusts, large hail and reports of tornadoes.\par \par \uc2 A member of the Washington D.C. Council -- City Council is suggesting the National Guard may be needed to address crime in the area. Trayon White says police in the district are outgunned. He says he has reached out to D.C. Mayor Muriel Bowser about this issue. The D.C. National Guard says it's not aware of any request for assistance.\par \par \uc2 Authorities in Montgomery, Alabama are investigating a melee that broke out over the weekend. There are suggestions that race may have played a part in that fight. Authorities are investigating, Correspondent Jeff Paul reports tonight from Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The confrontation with the owner of the boat.\par \par \uc2 JEFF PAUL, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: What started as a pontoon boat docked in the wrong place quickly turned into an all-out brawl after a group of white boaters attacked a heavily outnumbered black co-captain of a riverboat who was trying to make space to dock.\par \par \uc2 Jim Kittrell, the captain of the Harriot II, says well before fists flew, he tried several times over the intercom to get the boaters to move from his designated space. He told a local radio station he was ignored.\par \par \uc2 JIM KITTRELL, HARRIOT II RIVERBOAT CAPTAIN; They're going to see the alcohol that you have on your boat. I said I don't think you want that. So, move your boat before I call the police. And they started shooting birds at us.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Took matters into his own hands.\par \par \uc2 PAUL: When the boaters left the dock, Kittrell says his co-captain disembarked to push the pontoon boat down to make room. But the boaters returned, words were exchanged, and then so were punches. The fight broke up for a moment, but as soon as the riverboat docked, passengers angered by the fight, wanted to get in their shots.\par \par \uc2 KITTRELL: It was difficult for me to sit there in the wheelhouse watching him being attacked. The whole time I'm on the P.A., "Stop! Stop!"\par \par \uc2 PAUL: Montgomery police issued four arrest warrants, one for a black man who appeared to use a chair to hit people, the other three relating to the white boaters who started the fight and ganged up on the black boat captain.\par \par \uc2 DARRYL ALBERT, MONTGOMERY, ALABAMA, POLICE CHIEF: There was a lot of obscene finger gestures and comments and innuendos made at a distance. Of course, when the co-captain approached, that continued. Working with the local \uc2 FBI\uc2 , we did examine if there was enough to file hate crimes charges on this case.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 PAUL (on camera): The police chief says this remains an open investigation and more charges could be filed. Montgomery's mayor says the co-captain who was attacked was just trying to do his job and justice will be served. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jeff Paul in Atlanta. Jeff, thanks.\par \par \uc2 President Biden is on a west coast swing this week. He is talking about climate change and energy agendas. But a lot of other people in his party are talking about his continued problems in the opinion polls. White House correspondent Peter Doocy is in Albuquerque tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: As President Biden looks out at the Grand Canyon, all the way down there, his approval on climate change, just 40 percent according to a "Washington Post"-University of Maryland survey.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We have taken an unprecedented action to combat climate crisis.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: A big part of that, promoting companies like \uc2 Proterra\uc2 , the heavy- duty electric battery company.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: We are going to change the way in which the air people breathe is a consequence of what you all do in the battery technology overall.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Today more than two years after that presidential plug, \uc2 Proterra\uc2  filed for chapter 11 bankruptcy.\par \par \uc2 DOUGLAS HOLTZ-EAKIN, AMERICAN ACTION FORUM: We're going to see more failures because the technologies and the business models aren't ready.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: That's one less alternative to gas powered vehicles. And today in Arizona, no talk about rising gas prices.\par \par \uc2 DOUGLAS SCHOEN, FORMER CLINTON ADVISER: If I was advising them, which I am not, I would tell them to change their approach, change their rhetoric, acknowledge the problems that exist, and make it clear that they see the pain that consumers have been facing.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Today a brief mistake while describing the Grand Canyon.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: One of the earth's nine wonders.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Corrected minutes later.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I said nine. It's one of the seven wonders of the world.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: White House officials told reporters on Air Force One President Biden remains their best messenger on climate issues.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: No one ever doubts I mean what I say. Sometimes I saw all that I mean, though.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: All the money President Biden is out west investing in climate friendly companies did not come from a congressional climate friendly act. It's coming from the Inflation Reduction Act, which we were told last year was supposed to