c2  and Hyundai only sell their fuel cell vehicles in California. This, \uc2 Kelley Blue Book\uc2  executive editor \uc2 Brian Moody\uc2  says, illustrates one of the biggest roadblocks for these cars.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN MOODY\uc2 , KELLEY BLUE BOOK: If we feel the need to give electric cars all of these outs by saying, oh, one day it's going to be great, one day it's going to fuel up fast, then let's give hydrogen the same benefit of the doubt. Let's say that it's going to catch up with everything else.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE (on camera): Some automakers envision a future where hybrids, plug- in hybrids, and batteries EVs all share the road with fuel cell vehicles. Several are investing a lot of money into research and development of semitrucks that run on hydrogen. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, Grady, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel on the emergence of abortion, the border, the economy, key issues for 2024.\par \par \uc2 Today's throwback, on August 8th, 1968, \uc2 Richard Nixon\uc2  was nominated for president by the Republican Party. Six years to the day after that during his second term as president, on August 8th, 1974, facing growing evidence in the Watergate investigation, President Nixon announced that he had would resign from the presidency the following day. At noon on August 9th, 1974, President Nixon officially resigned. Vice President \uc2 Gerald Ford\uc2  ascended to the office of president. One month later, on September 9th, in an effort to move the country past Watergate, President \uc2 Ford\uc2  issued a full pardon to former President Nixon, protecting him from any prosecution for his role in the scandal.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 JERRY NADLER\uc2 , (D-NY): Is the border open, Mr. Secretary?\par \par \uc2 ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS, HOMELAND SECURITY SECRETARY: No, it is not.\par \par \uc2 NADLER: Thank you, Mr. Secretary. The border is not open.\par \par \uc2 GOV. MAURA HEALEY, (D) MASSACHUSETTS: The increased level of demand is not slowing down. Today, I am declaring a state of emergency in Massachusetts.\par \par \uc2 ART DEL CUETO, NATIONAL BORDER PATROL COUNCIL: They are asking for asylum, and they are getting released in the country. That is what is horrible. You have \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  -- President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  now in Arizona, and he isn't even addressing the border.\par \par \uc2 REP. CHIP ROY, (R-TX) HOUSE JUDICIARY COMMITTEE: The president and this administration can go straight to hell. I'm tired of Republicans who are giving lip service to it and for years have been supporting it because they want cheap labor.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: If you thought the illegal immigration issue is going away or border problems somehow subsiding, they aren't. In fact, FOX News obtained these images of cartel members coming across the border armed. There are real concerns about security along the border. And now, as you heard, other states and cities saying that they have a state of emergency because of the migrants they are dealing with.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in our panel, Byron York, chief political correspondent of "The Washington Examiner," Josh Holmes, co-host of "The Ruthless" podcast and former chief of staff to Senator \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , and "Axios" political reporter Stef Kight.\par \par \uc2 Byron, you heard in that montage the Massachusetts governor, Healey, saying that she is declaring a state of emergency. It wasn't too long ago that the same Massachusetts governor was saying "If cities want to take on sanctuary city designation, I think that's great." And there's a big turn now.\par \par \uc2 BYRON YORK, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: This is what the border crisis has done, which is exposed the hypocrisy of the sanctuary cities and the sanctuary jurisdictions all around the country. I think the governor of Texas, Greg Abbott deserves a lot of credit for that. A lot of people thought when he started sending voluntary busloads to New York City and Washington D.C., that it was a stunt. But in fact, it really has made a very, very serious point what is going on.\par \par \uc2 Politically, I think we are going to have an interesting situation where whoever the Republican candidate is, is going to make a really big deal of the border, and the Democratic candidate is going to try to argue that it's not even there.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. And now up on the Hill you have Republicans still considering this impeachment of the Homeland Security secretary.\par \par \uc2 STEF KIGHT, POLITICAL REPORTER, "AXIOS": Yes, we are continuing to see some Republicans say that they think that is Secretary Mayorkas should be held accountable for his role in executing border policy at the border. We are going to continue to see this issue become front and center.\par \par \uc2 And the White House, the Biden administration has really struggled with both the logistics and practicality of dealing with the border, the changing demographics of the border, issuing border policy. They also haven't really found an effective way to politically message around this issue. Not only are they getting attacks from the right, but they're also getting attacks from the left as well for their policies that they have implemented trying to slow the numbers at the border. And as we are seeing now, we are seeing increasingly more and more local communities kind of raising their hand and saying, hey, we need help to deal with this.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And this isn't just any local communities. This is Massachusetts, this is New York City pushing back on Democratic principles, saying this is not working for us.\par \par \uc2 JOSH HOLMES, "RUTHLESS" PODCAST CO-HOST: Yes. Well, look, a lot of eastern and west coast folks who didn't have this problem on their own border had great NIMBY politics when it comes to denying what was happening at the border. And now that we have seen migrants having been bused, flown, and everything else to all these cities across America, lo and behold there is actually an immigration problem to recognize here.\par \par \uc2 We are not far removed from a Democratic primary debate in 2020 where you had every candidate raising their hands, basically saying oh, come on in, free healthcare and everything else. We saw the dismantling of all of the administration -- previous administration's policies when it came to remain in Mexico and everything else. They built what is happening right now. It's not just about messaging and trying to figure out sort of how you politically handle it. This is the result of just absolute incompetence all the way through.\par \par \uc2 And you saw \uc2 Jerry Nadler\uc2  with that extremely rigorous oversight asking Mayorkas if the border is secure. He says yes, and he is like, well, there you have it. And that's basically what we saw for the better part of two years.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: In the GOP primary, though, Stef, it's interesting watching Governor DeSantis trying to attack former President Trump on the issue of immigration, saying he didn't finish the wall. It wasn't a finished product.\par \par \uc2 KIGHT: Yes. DeSantis has kind of tried to take that issue away from Trump who, obviously, immigration was such a big part of his campaign in 2015 and 2016. But DeSantis is trying to point to some of the ideas that the Trump administration considered but maybe never followed through on, things like birthright citizenship, ending that for undocumented immigrants in the U.S., finishing the wall, some other harsh policies. Of course, DeSantis has taken his own very harsh approach to immigration in the state of Florida, and he is very proud of that. And that's going to be a number one issue for Trump, DeSantis.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Although he has his own issues, replacing his campaign manager, and the shakeup continues on the DeSantis campaign, Byron.\par \par \uc2 YORK: They