red, of good character and competency -- they know who they are - - to jump in, because Democrats and the country need competition. We need alternatives for the massive majority of the middle in America.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Josh, what about that? We hear more and more stories, more and more soundbites that fit that narrative. Of course, the Biden campaign is not saying it.\par \par \uc2 HOLMES: Yes. I think they are acknowledging the obvious in some ways is that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is a very diminished candidate in 2024, regardless of where you are at within the Democratic Party. And I think \uc2 Dean Phillips\uc2  is acknowledging the obvious there. I think he is also doing the V.P., Cheney search party where he is like, well, we need somebody that is credibly well qualified and handsome, sort of like myself.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Lives in my house.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, panel, stand by. Up next, the president goes out west to talk climate and energy. And later, "Tuesday Tweets."\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I signed the largest climate bill in the history not only of the United States but literally in the history of the world. It will save working families thousands of dollars a year if they install rooftop solar, weatherize their home, and also conserve energy.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , (D) VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We are fighting to build a nation where every person, not just the wealthy or well-connected, have the opportunity to thrive, and that, all my friends here, is called Bidenomics.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Defending Bidenomics, specifically on climate. This as "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " has a new poll out suggesting that Americans disapprove of the Biden climate agenda, 40 percent to 57 percent. And we have a green energy company that the president hosted at the White House, actually filed for bankruptcy today. "\uc2 Proterra\uc2 , the electric bus company repeatedly celebrated by President Biden filed for bankruptcy Monday, blaming various market headwinds for its financial struggles. Earlier this year, even appointed Joyce Proterra's CEO to serve on the White House Export Council, the principal national advisory committee on international trade."\par \par \uc2 Back with the panel. Stef, the left, progressives really are pushing climate change as an issue. But when it comes to delivering on the economics of it, it really hasn't panned out for the Biden White House.\par \par \uc2 KIGHT: Clearly those poll results are not great for the Biden administration. I think what we are seeing today is the beginning of what they are going to be focusing on in this lead up to 2024, which is trying to convince the American public that the things that they have achieved over the past two years or so actually are helpful and we are going to see them continue to tout both Bidenomics and also things like their climate agenda, which some polling suggests people might not fully understand some of the work they have done there, and they are trying to explain that to the American public in these key states.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, Bidenomics, White House back and forth defending the green energy push, take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The first quarter of this year, \uc2 Ford\uc2  lost $700 million on their EV program, lost $600 million last year. So, is that what we can expect with the Bidenomics plan?\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA DALTON, WHITE HOUSE DEPUTY PRESS SECRETARY: I would just say looking across the economy broadly, we are seeing signs of progress. Jobs that are coming back by the hundreds of thousands overseas as a result of private investment in clean energy manufacturing in our country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Byron?\par \par \uc2 YORK: Well, I think one thing the president did was admit that the Inflation Reduction Act was a climate bill. It really wasn't the Inflation Reduction Act. Listen, the president has a huge environmentalist constituency that he has to keep fairly happy here. And the other thing, I think, is what we are seeing in this three-state tour that he is making now is a real return to very traditional Democratic campaigning, which is we gave you all this stuff. You need to reelect us.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I want to turn to issues, Josh, to abortion. In Ohio there is this statewide ballot initiative that essentially says how they can put things on the ballot. The big issue is abortion. It's a proxy war over abortion according to "Politico." "More than 500,000 voters have already voted on Issue 1, an amendment that would make it significantly more difficult to alter the state's constitution, bring citizen-initiated ballot measure to voters. Campaigns are working to remind the public that Issue 1 could have dramatic implication for a number of issues that come before voters in the state." Talking abortion, every single one of those referendums, even in conservative states, has passed the abortion rights way so far.\par \par \uc2 HOLMES: Right. And in this particular one, what they're doing is raising the threshold from a simple majority to amend the state's constitution up to a 60 --.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: To prevent that vote.\par \par \uc2 HOLMES: -- a 60 percent threshold. And as a practical matter, I think that's probably a -- the only reason that it works well to do a simple majority is it's like a pocketbook of a political consultant because it's a terrific line of work. But as a policy matter, you are right, this has become a proxy war for the abortion issue. Any time you have a very well- defined issue like abortion, well-defined sides, the intensity is always behind where the policy is moving away, in this case why Democrats have sought to use it as their advantage.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We will look at it. It could be interesting to watch for the election. Panel, thanks.\par \par \uc2 OK, it's time for "Tuesday Tweets." Holly asks, "Are you planning on doing any town halls with presidential candidates? You did for the Democrats in 2020. I thought they were well-done." We want to. We plan to. So, yes. I will say yes.\par \par \uc2 Next, Carl tweets, "Loved your interview with Governor DeSantis. Two questions -- how do you prepare? Does the panel hear the full interview or the edited version?" Preparing, I have a great staff. A lot of research, trying to figure out which way candidates go. The panel hears what you hear on the air. But if you want to hear the full interview, you can you go to my podcast, the Bret Baier Podcast. All of that interview with Governor DeSantis unedited is on there now, and you can take a look at it.\par \par \uc2 Lastly, Kathryn wants to know, "Any book tour dates "To Rescue the Constitution?" We are planning a trip to California in October." Yes, I'll be at the Reagan Library and the Nixon library, just don't have dates yet. But I will.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT, we look at how progressives are pushing President Biden to prioritize his climate change agenda, as we talked about a little bit ahead of the 2024 campaign.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for this SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and still unafraid. THE INGRAHAM ANGLE is now.\par \par \uc2 END \par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020230809ej8800001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Former Business Partner Interviewed By House Panel; Democratic Lawmakers Downplays Statements; DeSantis Meets With Voters In Key States; Criticism Over New Florida Curriculum; Presidential Candidate Gov. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  Is Being Interviewed About Roe V. Wade, Abortion Stance And The War In Ukraine; Florida Governor And Republican Presidential Candidate \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  Interviewed On GOP Presidential Primary Rival Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's Criticisms Of Him; Congress Debating Renewal Of Section Of Foreign Intelligence Surveillance Act.\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Chad Pergram, Mark Meredith, Alexandria Hoff \par \uc2 9,629 \uc2 words\par \uc2 31 July 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 KATIE PAVLICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Up all day, to get mostly everybody's power restored in 24 hours or less. It was such a relief to student.\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: That was a big storm.\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: Yes. Thank you very much to all the hard workers out there.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: Amen.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: OK. Well, we have 10 seconds.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: And women.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: So, I will say this very slowly. That's it for us. Have a great night.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR: Welcome to Rochester, New Hampshire. I'm Bret Baier. We're at the historic Gonic Mill.\par \par \uc2 In just a moment, I'll sit down exclusively, extensively, with Florida Governor, GOP presidential candidate \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But first, some of the other headlines and the top story of the day.\par \par \uc2 State department officials say they are working with Haitian authorities to locate a New Hampshire woman and her child kidnapped while doing faith- based charity work in Haiti. Alix Dorsainvil and her child were abducted Thursday morning near Port-au-Prince.\par \par \uc2 Russian ballistic missiles slammed into an apartment complex in a university building in Ukrainian president \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2 's hometown today. Officials say six people were killed; 75 others wounded.\par \par \uc2 Back in the U.S., the judge presiding over a Georgia grand jury investigation into former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has rejected his bid to disqualify the prosecutor leading the inquiry, and block any indictments stemming from the probe.\par \par \uc2 The judge also denied his request to quash a special grand jury report that included recommendations on whom to charge in connection with a campaign to overturn Georgia's 2020 election results.\par \par \uc2 And stocks were up on positive earnings reports. The Dow gained 100, The S&P 500 finished ahead seven. The NASDAQ rose 29.\par \par \uc2 This is a big day in the Biden family business scandal and the investigation of it. A former associate of \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  sat for a closed- door transcribed interview before the House Oversight Committee.\par \par \uc2 Both sides say Devon Archer statements confirm their position.\par \par \uc2 Senior congressional correspondent Chad Pergram has details tonight live from Capitol Hill. Good evening, Chad.\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Bret, good evening.\par \par \uc2 A source tells Fox that former \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  business partner, Devon Archer told house investigators, the president's son put him on the speakerphone 20 times to talk with associates.\par \par \uc2 Republicans believe this is the smoking gun which connects Hunter to his father and in effort to peddle access.\par \par \uc2 GOP members contend the president is compromised?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 REP. ANDY BIGGS (R-AZ): I think so. I think so. And we'll continue to do our due diligence in our research.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): The GOP says business associates wanted \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  to sell the brand of Ukrainian energy firm \uc2 Burisma\uc2 . The GOP called the information "damning". Democrats are not so sure.\par \par \uc2 REP. DAN GOLDMAN (D-NY): It was -- and sound as like most of the time. Now, President Biden didn't even know who the people he was at dinner.\par \par \uc2 None of those conversations ever had to do with any business dealings or transactions. They were purely what he called, casual conversation.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): A democratic source says Archer told investigators that \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  was selling the "illusion" of access. And the brand was not President Biden, it was Washington, D.C.\par \par \uc2 The White House said Archer undercut the GOP's allegations. The president maintains that he is stayed away from family affairs.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I've never discussed with my son or my brother or anyone else anything having to do with their businesses. Period.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): Republicans portrayed Archer as the linchpin to their probe. Archer, finally spoke to house investigators after three tries, but would not speak to Fox.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Mr. Archer, what do you intend to tell the committee today?\par \par \uc2 Do you have anything to say? Did you have meetings with the Biden's and can you elaborate on this (INAUDIBLE)?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (on camera): Archer's attorney says both sides are claiming victory. The attorney says, Archer shared the truth. It's up to others to decide what to do with the truth. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Chad Pergram on Capitol Hill. Chad, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Now, joining us, Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . Governor, thanks for the time.\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Yes, sure.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Focusing on \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 . Breaking news today is that his business partner, Devon Archer said allegedly that some 20 times, then-Vice President Biden was on a speakerphone talking about the businesses.\par \par \uc2 Your reaction to that and the focus on this investigation.\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: Well, this is why we say there is two standards of justice.\par \par \uc2 If Hunter were a Republican, he'd be in jail by now. You look at all this smoke and yet the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , where is the search warrants? Where is the grand jury? Where's the aggressiveness that they've shown going after some Republicans? You just don't see it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Including former President Trump.\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: Including former President Trump. And they went through everything they could to get any piece of information. They are not doing that with Hunter. Of course, they didn't do with Hillary back in 2016. So, we look at that.\par \par \uc2 But look at all the possible corruption. I mean, Hunter, he's selling paintings for over a million dollars? You know, my 6-year-old daughter does better paintings than him. Maybe we'll put ours up and see what kind of thing she confessed.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Is it tough though --\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: I don't think you're going to get a million dollars on it.\par \par \uc2 We will go in there. And we will make all of this stuff better very, very quickly.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, look, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was president for four years, this problem got worse under him. He did not succeed in draining the swamp. And my worst critics and opponents in Florida will acknowledge, the governor says he's going to do something, you can take that to the bank, he's going to do it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And clarify, you don't believe the foreign president Trump could win a general election against \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: I don't think so. Because I think that there's too many voters, who just aren't going to vote for him going forward. I saw it in Florida in my reelection, you know, I won the first time by less than a percent, second time by 20.\par \par \uc2 Why did people vote against me in '18 but voted for me in '22? The number one reason they gave was because of Trump. Because in 18, they said that they were voting against Trump. Voting against me to vote against Trump, even though they didn't know much about me. By '22, that was there.\par \par \uc2 So, I think in states like Georgia and Arizona -- and there is -- and look, I don't put a stock in the polls. But, you know, you guys talk about them.\par \par \uc2 The polls that come out that put, I beat Biden in Georgia, Trump doesn't. I beat Biden soundly and Arizona, Trump doesn't. Those are just the realities. And I also think in terms of the base, you need to have a cadre of personnel.\par \par \uc2 If you want to slay this administrative state, you got to be disciplined, you got to be focused, and you got to have people surrounding you that are going to go and support the mission.\par \par \uc2 I think the former president would have a very difficult time getting the type of personnel to join the administration that you would need to actually bring this stuff to fruition.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: What do you think the most important issue facing our country is?\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: Our country is in decline. I don't think it's inevitable. I think it's a choice. But I'm running for president to reverse that decline. And that's, of course, our economic decline, the decline of our military power, the decline of our culture. And so, we have it within our power over the next 18 months -- 18 months to choose a better course. And I'm running for president to lead that effort.\par \par \uc2 What kind of a country do you want to have? And we're a country with an economy, not the other way around. We're citizens of Republic, we're not just cogs in the wheel of a global economic empire.\par \par \uc2 And so, I think, to have a good economy means, if you work hard, you play by the rules, you get the most of your God given talent, you should be able to succeed in this country, you should be able to raise a family, afford a home, a car, all these different things. And we've lost a lot of that in recent years. We need to get it back.\par \par \uc2 I want to talk about substance and policy. But this week, there is a lot of focus on politics and your campaign. Is this the start of a campaign that you envisioned?\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: Well, look, at the end of the day, I'm a leader, you put out a commander's intent, and you delegate for people to do it. Then, you evaluate, and if it's not up to what you want, then you just make adjustments.\par \par \uc2 But at the end of the day, you know, that happens all the time. It happens in governing. And the question is, are you going to be able to do identify whatever may be getting a poor ROI, change it. So, we've done that.\par \par \uc2 And I think at the end of the day, no one's going to care about what happened in early July 6th, seven months before the primaries.\par \par \uc2 What they want to know, the voters here in New Hampshire is, OK, why you?\par \par \uc2 You know, I can talk about, you know, my biography, for example, being the only war veteran running. I can talk about my accomplishments in Florida. We are the number one state in the country in terms of people wanting to come, and then, the vision for the future, and that goes with the economy, and it goes with strengthening our military.\par \par \uc2 All these things that are so important. At the end of the day, it's going to be about substance.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Your campaign says it's resetting. Says that you're going to be sort of more insurgent, leaner, and meaner. That has happened, you've recently laid off a third of your campaign staff. It's happened before on campaigns historically.\par \par \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  did it. \uc2 John McCain\uc2  did it. They went on to win the nomination. Obviously, Reagan went on to the White House. But what happened to you and your campaign?\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: Everything you do, needs to have a return on the investment. And so, we have a little bit of a different structure. We don't pay consultants; we hire people to work in house. So, some of the reasons we may have some more is because we're just bearing those costs.\par \par \uc2 Ultimately, we'll make money off doing it that way. It's just a different model. You know, but some of the other stuff, if there isn't value in it, then, I get changes. And then, that's just the reality of what you have to do.\par \par \uc2 So. I'm not a political operative. I'm not a campaign professional. You know, you set out the vision and you execute on it. And if it's not being executed, you just make the core scripture --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: A political operative who has been around the block, Ed Rollins, longtime Republican political consultant, supported former President Trump, jumped over to you and your pack, Ready for Ron PAC, told the \uc2 Rolling Stone\uc2  this week this, "I don't think it's the campaign's fault at all; it's his. I think he's a very flawed candidate. He's not a particularly articulate candidate. He does not come off as warm and fuzzy, when you get into those culture wars, the way that he has, the vast majority of people don't understand what they are. That may work in parts of Florida, but not these other places he needs to win. That's not what sells."\par \par \uc2 It's harsh. But what do you say to that? This is from a guy who's obviously been around the block and who's been a central figure in your political action committee?\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: Well, it's obviously nonsense. I mean, I came into a state that had been decided by one point for a generation. You've covered some of those races.\par \par \uc2 I governed boldly, I governed unapologetically, I delivered big results. And we won by 20 points. You don't win a state like Florida, that big if you're not doing things that are resonating.\par \par \uc2 And I would push back. When I hear about the culture war, standing up for the rights of parents, standing up for the well-being of children, that's not some culture war. That is central to the lives of tens of millions of people throughout this country. It is the right thing to do to stand with our kids. It is right thing to impose indoctrination in the schools. And I totally reject being in Iowa, New Hampshire, that people don't think that those are important. They do think there's important these families with children are thanking me for taking stands in Florida and bringing that type of vibrancy to the White House.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: How do you turn it around is my question.\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: Being on the ground. Heck, in New Hampshire, we did the economic --\par \par \uc2 All right. I had three guys come up to me separately. They said, you know, I was with Trump, but now I'm with you. You won me over.\par \par \uc2 But that's what it's going to take. People got to see you. I think, most of these Republican voters, you know, they know I've done good in Florida. They know Florida has done well, and they -- and they liked me. But they want to know you know why me is the leader and what am I going to be able to do as president?\par \par \uc2 And once we're able to fill that in, we gain support.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You don't like to call it culture wars or culture war issues. But three quick questions on this. You've said repeatedly, Florida is where woke goes to die.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: We've made the state of Florida the place where woke goes to die.\par \par \uc2 We have fought woke in corporate America, we have fought in our education system, we have fought woke in the halls of the legislature.\par \par \uc2 So, in Florida, we said we're the state we're woke goes to die, and we have made that a reality.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: This week, there has been some pushback to Florida's new slavery history curriculum.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT (R-SC), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Slavery was really about separating families, about mutilating humans, and even their wives. Because it was devastating. So, I would hope that every person in our country, and certainly running for president would appreciate that.\par \par \uc2 REP. BYRON DONALDS (R-FL): My issue is with one sentence of the entire thing. One sentence of 200 pages, and the DeSantis team wants to make a big issue out of it. The fact that they made this a story is dumb, in my view.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Now, you've doubled down and you said anyone saying that is essentially siding with Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  of the Democrats. Is this a fight worth having?\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: We didn't pick the fight, Bret. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  got on a jet at taxpayer expense, and flew to Florida to lie about the African American history standards that were developed.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Understood.\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: And why -- and why is this important, though?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But you're asked about it a lot --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: But you can't -- No, no, I'm saying it is important, why? You can't bend the knee to the left's lies. When the left lies and creates these phony narratives, you've got to push back. They've been doing this to Republicans for years and years, Republicans bend the knee. And it's just one thing after another.\par \par \uc2 Second, we -- this goes into the whole fight on indoctrination in schools. We eliminated critical race theory a couple years ago. When we did that, the media lied, and the left lied, saying, you don't want to teach about African American history, equating political activism and CRT with black history.\par \par \uc2 I said, no, not only do in the bill, it says you've got to do all these things. Teach about injustice, teach about discrimination.\par \par \uc2 So, these standards were born out of the fight against CRT, because this is true history. So, to take something and demagogue it like that, that's bad faith.\par \par \uc2 And here is the thing. This was a public process. These guys were going through it. That people could have raised objections, no one said anything about this, they were being lauded for the job that they did by people across the political spectrum.\par \par \uc2 Now, Harris comes in and parachutes. What Republicans should have done is pushed back against her, say you are operating in bad faith. These guys down in Florida.\par \par \uc2 They didn't have an agenda. They were just trying to shoot straight. We know what the left does. Republicans, you cannot take that bait. You've got to fight back against these people.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And that's a very detailed answer. And we've heard from the head of that curriculum, who's very articulate about what he was thinking. But these are two very successful black Republican politicians who are telling people how they feel.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: Do you honestly think that --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I think, Byron Donald refers to one sentence.\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: OK. Well, look, I think you -- to take something out of context and mangle it and accept bad interpretation, I would just challenge people to read them.\par \par \uc2 And here's the thing, like, I feel a need to defend my state. If I wasn't running for president or I wasn't who I was, no one would have cared about this.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It's not all about these culture issues. However, your campaign has focused on them a little bit. Here's another one.\par \par \uc2 Another culture issue, your campaign ran an ad that focused on former President Trump's support for LGBTQ citizens. USA Today called it bizarre and staggeringly homophobic. Log Cabin Republicans called it extreme, naive, dangerous, politically stupid.\par \par \uc2 Why did you do that ad? And what do you say to people that you're subtracting, not adding to potential supporters?\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: What they hit him on was injecting men into women's competitions, which he did with his beauty pageants. And then he's expressed support for allowing men to use women's locker rooms and bathrooms.\par \par \uc2 So, those are the two issues. I think those are totally legitimate. I don't believe in demeaning anybody. And we have not done that since I've been governor.\par \par \uc2 You know, these things get shared or whatever, and I'm responsible for it. Don't get me wrong. But the idea that like I was sitting there, like, oh, share this video, that's a rapid response thing.\par \par \uc2 But on those issues about injecting men into women's competitions, that's wrong. We've taken a strong stand with respect to women's athletics, protecting women's sports, we've also protected women's bathrooms and locker rooms.\par \par \uc2 And at the end of the day, you know, we can't go down this road where there's 37 different genders. There's not true, there's two. And I do think we have a difference of opinion on that.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: After Roe v. Wade was overturned, the issue went to the states, abortion became a bigger issue in the 2022 midterms than really anybody thought it was. They thought it'd be big, but not as big as it was and now beyond.\par \par \uc2 Some of your top supporters had issues with you signing a six week abortion ban in Florida, suggesting that 15 weeks maybe is more defensible, more politically viable in a general election. Why did you do that? Were you trying to get to the right of former President Trump on that issue?\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: I am pro-life. I've always been pro-life. I ran as being pro- life. I've always said I supported the heartbeat bill, the 15 that we did was prior to Dobbs, this was after Dobbs. And this is still all being litigated under Florida's constitution. We've got a case going on now. So, it'll take some time to play out.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But you support it?\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: But once I sign the bill, at the end of the day, you know, when there's a detectable heartbeat, is that a life or not? And does that deserve protection.\par \par \uc2 And in Florida, you know, there -- the legislature put protections in for rape, incest, life of mother, all that stuff, which I know is a bit difficult issues for a lot of people.\par \par \uc2 But at the end of the day, you know, when there's a detectable heartbeat, the right thing to do is to protect that life, and welcome that child into the world. I think we're better off when everybody counts, when everybody has an opportunity.\par \par \uc2 Now, I also acknowledge and understand that in my region of the country and down in the southeast, it's a little bit different there than where we are up here in New England. I don't think New England states would probably, you know, adopt something along the lines of a heartbeat.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So, they want to know what you're going to do as president?\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: What I'm going to do as president is couple things. One, you know, we're going to lead as a pro-life, leading a culture of life using the bully pulpit. We're not going to fund it with tax dollars, the military's abortion tourism policy, we're going to eliminate. We are going to assure that we have conservative justices appointed at the Supreme Court who are going to respect the Dobbs' decision. And we will absolutely be able to block if there is a Democratic Congress, their attempt, they want to impose abortion on demand up until birth nationwide. And that is a horrific practice. And so, we would not let that happen.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You mentioned the Pentagon's policy. Do you support Senator Tommy Tuberville from Alabama blocking the military promotions for Pentagon policy -- abortion policy?\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: I do because I think the Pentagon is violating the law. They're basically being told by the Biden administration to do this. This is not consistent with long standing U.S. law. And I think it plays into a larger problem that we've seen in the military, you have a lot of civilians, forcing them to engage in political and culture issues that are detracting from mission. This is one of them, but the pronouns and the drag queens and all those other things.\par \par \uc2 So, we need to get the military back on focusing on mission first. And I think if we can do that, you can solve the recruiting crisis that you see.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I mean, you were in the military, you know how it works. But there's 250 stalled military promotions because of this, and the Pentagon saying it's really doing damage.\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: Well, at the end of the day, though, the Pentagon largely because of civilians telling them to do this. They're the ones that pick this by violating the law.\par \par \uc2 And so, I think at some point, you know, we talked about the executive branch and the administrative state, part of the ways that you make sure they're within their bounds. And look, the military hasn't been as bad as DOJ, \uc2 FBI\uc2 , \uc2 EPA\uc2 , all those others, but Congress is the one that does oversight, they have to ensure that they're following the law. And so, I think that standing up for that, that's the right thing to do.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Governor DeSantis, standby if you would. More with the governor of Florida, GOP presidential candidate right after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Welcome back to New Hampshire. Back now with Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 All right, foreign policy. Here's a tweet. John asked, how does he the governor plan to support Ukraine if he's elected? And I guess I add or do you?\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: We need a sustainable peace in Europe without rewarding Putin's aggression. We don't want this to be an issue for the next 10, 20, 30, 40 years.\par \par \uc2 Well, how do you get there? The Europeans need to do their fair share, I think the fact that we have drawn down our ammunition to crisis levels, some of our weapons stocks, we have other contingencies we have to be worried about.\par \par \uc2 So, as president, I would prioritize the China threat first, I would have more resources and power in the Indo Pacific. I would also deal with issues in our own hemisphere, starting with our border and some of the threats there. And then I'm willing to work with the Europeans.\par \par \uc2 But I think the goal needs to be, you know, let's bring it to a sustainable conclusion. You know, I think the Europeans really need to take the lead will help to try to bring this in for a landing.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Early in your campaign when you were asked about Ukraine, you used two words. You said territorial dispute, your opponent's jumped on that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY (R), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: And for them to sit there and say that this is a territorial dispute. That's just not the case.\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE (R), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: This is not a territorial dispute. \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  is not a genius.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE\uc2  (R), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Rolling tanks and artillery into a free country, mercilessly killing their people and taking their land by force, Governor DeSantis, is not a territorial dispute.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Do you stick by that characterization?\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: The initial invasion was for Putin to decapitate the Ukrainian government and install a Belarus style puppet regime. He failed at that. He's not going to succeed at that. And that's a good thing.\par \par \uc2 So, the fight is really on that far eastern flank of the border region between Ukraine and Russia. They read that to me that somehow I said Putin didn't invade or have a right, of course, he invaded. I said that at the time. He doesn't have a right to do it. But the reality is, that's where the fighting has been.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Do you think a weakened Russia helps the United States?\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: Yes, I do. I mean, I think one of the things we're going to do is when we do our energy policy, so one of the problems I have with Biden, he invited this with his weakness, the Afghanistan debacle, saying, oh, you can do a small invasion, it's not going to be a big deal. But his energy policy has helped sustain Putin's war machine, we open up domestic energy that's going to lower the price of oil, that's going to hurt Putin.\par \par \uc2 The other thing that Biden's done, he's given relief to the Iranians. They're like the number one benefactor for Putin. I'm going to turn the screws on the Iranians that's going to dry up support for him.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Would Ukraine be allowed to join \uc2 NATO\uc2  under a President DeSantis?\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: To just bring in a country, all that it does is add obligations to us. I would say -- I would say, what are the benefits? And right now, none of the countries -- I mean, some are, but not all the countries are meeting their obligations.\par \par \uc2 So, I think we got to figure out what do we want out of \uc2 NATO\uc2 , and then everyone needs to do their fair share.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So, if Russia moved on Finland, when President DeSantis stand up for Article 5 and move --\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: Look, we are obligated under that to meet those obligations. And that's why I think, you know, we got to be careful about adding new obligations to what we would have to respond to.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Would you be willing to pressure Ukraine to cede some territory to Russia if it meant stopping this war?\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: So look, at the end of the day, it's not for us to say give or not give. I think the reality is, is we want a sustainable peace in Europe, you're not going to have a sustainable peace if Russia is making out like a bandit, because that will tell Putin that he can do more in the future.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The former president says he can end it in 24 hours.\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: Well, he also said that Mexico would pay for a border wall and all these other things. So clearly, that's not going to happen.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So, you've called Putin a war criminal. Does that mean you think he should be jailed or executed?\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: Well, I was asked in the context of the \uc2 ICC\uc2 . We're not parties to the \uc2 International Criminal Court\uc2 , we will not be, it's not something that I support.\par \par \uc2 However, if you invade another country, that is a violation of the laws of war. It just is.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Do you think you can negotiate with a war criminal?\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: You have to be willing to negotiate with unsavory people. This world is not all peaches and cream, you got to be very mindful of what you're walking into.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: China invades Taiwan. The real question about what the U.S. does, what does President DeSantis do?\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: So, my policy is going to deter -- is going to be to deter that from happening. And we're going to have a strategy of denial so that China is not going to be able to pull the trigger on that, given the amount of hard power that's in the region, giving the levers we can pull on the economy, and our goal should be to stop -- China respects hard power. If they don't think that -- if they think the costs are going to exceed any potential benefits, they are not going to do it.\par \par \uc2 I do think under Biden, we're on a course where in the next five or 10 years, they probably would be able to take it. If we change course, I think we can prevent it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Former President Trump has been critical of you for a number of different things on a number of different fronts. But interestingly and specifically about your handling of COVID.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: I told all governors you do what you want, you can shut it down or not. Florida by the way, he shut it down tight, no highways, no beaches, no this.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But then he pushed back pretty hard, he opened up. But is it fair to criticize him on that? He was credited for how Florida dealt with COVID.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Sure because he has better P.R. than other governors but other governors did a better job than Ron DeSanctimonious.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: He used to say the opposite. Let's just be clear, you got back a year or two he would say what a great job I've done in Florida, how I'm one of the country's great governors, how Florida is such great state. He always said that for years and years, until about three days before the midterm election, then he started to attack me. And I think it's because he saw that we were going to win a big victory, and I think he viewed me as a political threat.\par \par \uc2 So now he has flipflopped on all of that on is trying to say we did a bad job. I fought his administration to keep our state open, to have kids in school. Fauci, the task force, all those people, they were sending me missives. Even January of 2021, right before he left office, his task force was telling us in Florida to close. And so he didn't have control over his own government. He didn't have control over Fauci. Fauci ran that government his last year in office. Trump should have fired him. He did not do that. He elevated Fauci, and he made Fauci an international celebrity.\par \par \uc2 And here's the thing, I fought back against it, but the reality is, Fauci's policies were adopted in a lot of the blue states. Kids were locked out of school for year. You had businesses shuttered for good, small businesses.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Should the U.S. have locked down at all?\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: No. No. It was a catastrophic mistake to do that. Back at that time there was a lot of uncertainty, I get it. But knowing what we know now, that was a catastrophic mistake, and we can never let that happen to our country again.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Did the vaccine work?\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: It didn't stop the spread. We were told if you take the vax, you will not get COVID. That is not true. And now for CDC to say they should be doing annual boosters like a flu shot, there is not sufficient data to support that. And one of the things I'm going to do, we are going to bring a reckoning to the medical swamp, NDC, CDC, \uc2 NIH\uc2 , \uc2 FDA\uc2 . They failed the American people. You are going to see big changes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You raised eyebrows when you said Robert F. Kennedy could be part of some task force or head up the CDC.\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: I didn't say that, head up.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You said, or be a part of --\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: I was asked about, should he be V.P. And I pointed out, look, conservatives agree whim on Fauci and against the lockdowns, and we need more Democrats to stand up and admit that that was wrong. But I said he is liberal on a lot of issues. Of course, I'm not going to be V.P. I was like, let him go after these organizations. That didn't necessarily mean from an appointed position.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Do you ever get sick of hearing Ron De-sanctimonious?\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: Not really. At the end of the day, it is what it is. I don't think a lot of that stuff really tracks with the voters.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: He says it because you weren't loyal to him, and he stood up for you when you needed him in an election and you wouldn't have won because of it.\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: So here's the thing. I'm loyal to my family, the Constitution, and the good Lord. Politicians don't deserve fealty. They have to earn support.\par \par \uc2 I fought for him on the Russia collusion. I was one of only a handful of people very early on to publicly go out and defend him. And I did that for years. And, yes, he supported me for governor. But then I supported him for re-election. I raised him more money than any elected official. We helped deliver Florida. Republican Party, his people were saying if we don't win Florida, it's all the governor, we're relying on the governor. Well, we won it. And so we were there for him.\par \par \uc2 Then, three days before my reelection, I have never done anything to the guy, he starts attacking me and does this.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You've said you are going to show up in Milwaukee for the debate --\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: That's right.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: -- August 23rd. And there are a number of people who qualified. You are among them. So the is former president. He said he hasn't decided whether he is going or not. In fact, he moved today on his Truth Social and said maybe all of the other candidates should debate and I will determine who might be a vice presidential candidate.\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: You've got to earn this nomination. Nobody is entitled to it. You've got to go work. And I think the debates are part of that process. I think being on the ground in Iowa and New Hampshire is a big part of that process. And so you will see me showing up in all these places. We look forward to being age to speak directly to the American people, and I think it will be a very worthwhile endeavor. If the election ends up about all these other side issues and it becomes a referendum on Trump, Biden is going to be in the basement again, hiding out, no scrutiny. And I think he's going to get away with it again.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Two more quick things. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is 80. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is 77. Senator \uc2 Dianne Feinstein\uc2  who is 90 and Senator \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , 81, both had issues last week that raised some questions about their fitness for office given their advanced ages. Nikki Haley is calling for mental competency tests for politicians over the age 75. Do you agree with that?\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: Look, I think voters can sort it out. But I do think we need an energetic executive as president. We need somebody that is going to get in there on day one and spit nails and not look back. You only have a finite period of time to get things done, but two terms, eight years. As you know, the way the political cycle works, there are times when the iron is hot when you first get in. Maybe you get reelected. You have got to be able to capitalize on all of this.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Should there be term limits? There is obviously two terms for president.\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: There should definitely be term list limits for members of Congress. We have it in Florida with our legislature. It works so much better. People say, oh, if you have term limits the lobbyists are going to run the show of the staff. They already run the show. It would not get worse in that regard. So yes, do term limits. If you did the age limits, you'd need to do it so you are not targeting candidates running right now.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Why do you want it? What do you want to be president?\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: Because this country is in decline. I think it can be reversed, but it's not going to be reversed if we content with just managing the decline. We have got give this country a new trajectory and a new birth of freedom. I think I'm the only guy that's running that is able to get that job done. Some people say, well, you could have run later in life. I'm not running to be president. I'm running to do things as president, and now is the time the country needs the help.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Governor DeSantis, we really appreciate the time. We will follow you on the campaign trail and we will see you in Milwaukee.\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: When we come back, the debate over a national security measure that some Republicans say has facilitated the weaponization of the \uc2 FBI\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Welcome back to New Hampshire. President Biden spending the week at his vacation home in Delaware. Tonight, we look at a big issue for the president, the renewal of a contentious national security authority that critics say has enabled the weaponization of the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . Correspondent Mark Meredith is in Rehoboth Beach tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 MARK MEREDITH, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: President Biden gave reporters a brief wave during his morning bike ride while in Washington his aides urgently called on Congress to reauthorize a controversial government surveillance program. The White House today publishing a 42-page report strongly supporting the renewal of Section 702 of FISA, or the Foreign Intelligence Surveillance Act, which allows the government to conduct targeted surveillance of people outside the U.S. A presidential advisory board writing the data collected has already saved countless lives and, quote, "forms the corner stone of the intelligence community's ability to uncover and track threats to America." But lawmakers from both parties argue the act has also been abused, with some Americans' communications, including politicians, collected without proper oversight.\par \par \uc2 REP. TROY NEHLS, (R-TX) HOUSE JUDICIARY COMMITTEE: I will not support the idea of reauthorizing 702 for the \uc2 FBI\uc2  until they get their house in order.\par \par \uc2 REP. MADELEINE DEAN, (D-PA) HOUSE JUDICIARY COMMITTEE: It feels like we are in one of those places now with a clearcut understanding that the abuses of the FISA applications and queries are unacceptable.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: The advisory suggests various additional checks and balances be created to prevent abuse, and the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , which admits to mistakes in the past, says it's open to discussions on adapting. It tells FOX, quote, "We agree that section 702 should be reauthorized in a manner that does not diminish effectiveness as well as reassures the public of its importance and our ability to adhere rigorously to all relevant rules."\par \par \uc2 Congress has until the end of the year to decide its next steps.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (on camera): And while the president is keeping a fairly low profile at the beach this week, he did make some news in a podcast interview in which the second time now he has publicly acknowledged that he does indeed have seven grandchildren. In past critics have basically accused the president of cruelty for failing to acknowledge that \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  has a daughter in Arkansas, and he was not part of the Biden family. The president, though, saying he does now have seven grandchildren. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Mark Meredith live in Rehoboth Beach. Mark, thanks.\par \par \uc2 President Biden has decided to keep U.S. Space Command headquarters in Colorado, that's according to senior officials. It overturns a decision by the Trump administration to move to Alabama. Space Command chief General James Dickinson argued that moving his headquarters now would jeopardize military readiness. Air Force leadership had determined relocating to Huntsville, Alabama was the right move. Alabama Republican Senator Tommy Tuberville, who we mentioned earlier in the show is holding up promotions as a block Pentagon abortion policy, calls this a disastrous mistake and says he will keep fighting for the move.\par \par \uc2 Former President Trump says he expects a new federal indictment in the next few days. The prediction comes as the head of maintenance at his Florida estate makes his first court appearance relating to the classified documents investigation. Correspondent Alexandria Hoff is in Miami tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDRIA HOFF, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Carlos De Oliveira did not have a Florida attorney with him as he made his first court appearance in Miami. He will need counsel to proceed with his arraignment now scheduled for August 10th where he is expected to enter a not guilty plea. But the 56- year-old's D.C. based attorney was present.\par \par \uc2 JOHN IRVING, DE OLIVEIRA ATTORNEY: Now, it's time for them to put their money where their mouth is.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Back up, back up.\par \par \uc2 IRVING: I'm looking forward to seeing what discovery is.\par \par \uc2 HOFF: The longtime Mar-a-Lago maintenance employee is the third defendant charged in the classified documents case alongside former President Trump and Trump valet Walt Nauta. Prosecutors allege that De Oliveira lied about helping to move boxes and accused him of conspiring to delete security camera footage at the direction of former President Trump. The special counsel has not yet presented evidence that security footage was, in fact wiped.\par \par \uc2 ALINA HABBA, TRUMP SPOKESWOMAN: No tapes were deleted. He turned them over, he cooperated, as he always does.\par \par \uc2 HOFF: De Oliveira's charges were contained within the same superseding indictment that revealed three new felony charges against Trump. An additional count of willful retention of national defense and two new obstruction counts now puts the total count for Trump at 40. The legal jeopardy for the leading GOP presidential contender is mounting. A federal grand jury looking into 2020 election interference is expected to meet again this week and may be prepared to hand up indictments against Trump. A separate state probe in Georgia could do the same next month against multiple defendants.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOFF (on camera): In a Truth Social post today, Trump said that he suspects an additional federal indictment, quote, "Will be coming out any day now." Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Alexandria Hoff in Miami. Alexandria, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel with reaction to my interview with Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , plus that key witness in the Biden family business scandal appears on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 First, here's what some of our FOX affiliates around the country are covering tonight. FOX 11 in Los Angeles with the death of the comedian who played Pee-wee Herman. Paul Reubens died Sunday following a battle with cancer. Reubens' character, with his too tight gray suit, white chunky loafers, and red bowtie was best known for the film "Pee-wee's Big Adventure" and the television series "Pee-wee's Playhouse." Paul Reubens was 70.\par \par \uc2 FOX 13 in Seattle as an Idaho woman who killed her two youngest children and a romantic rival is sentenced to life in prison. Lori Vallow Daybell had claimed her son and daughter were zombies and that she was a goddess sent to usher in the biblical apocalypse.\par \par \uc2 Oregon Governor \uc2 Tina Kotek\uc2  declines to veto a bill allowing self-service gasoline. The legislation eliminates a 72-year-old restriction on drivers pumping their own gas. Stations must still staff at least half their pumps for customers who are unwilling or unable to service themselves.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look outside the beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: De-sanctus is gone. De-sanctus is gone. He's just about over. I think if he ever made a comeback, it would be the greatest comeback in political history.\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: Some of the candidates that are doing Trump, it's more personal for them. They are attacking him on personal stuff. That's just not my cup of tea. I'm a leader. I'm running for you. I'm not running for me.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  talking about the attacks, or lack thereof, the criticism of candidates not attacking the former president who is, by far, the leading candidate in the GOP primary so far.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in our panel, Morgan Ortagus, former State Department spokesperson under President Trump, Byron York, chief political correspondent of "The Washington Examiner," and Leslie Marshall, Democratic strategist. Morgan, let me start with you, your thoughts on the interview, what you took away with it.\par \par \uc2 MORGAN ORTAGUS, FORMER STATE DEPARTMENT SPOKESPERSON: First of all, Bret, fantastic interview. You are always a very tough person to ask the questions, and you certainly gave it to the governor. And I think that's important because you threw every sort of critique of his campaign at him. He did have solid answers. But I think what he will have to continue to work on is the message that he talked about, which is one in his argument, the reason why he is running he doesn't think Trump can win. I thought it was powerful when he made the argument and said that he is running to be president not just for the title, but he felt like now was time.\par \par \uc2 The issue that he is going to continue to have, and I have seen this campaign after campaign after campaign, he put out very detailed policy proposals to you. He put out a 10-point economic plan today, which as a policy wonk, I read it, I loved it. I was going through the policy stuff. But that still has to translate to voters, and they still have to feel that emotion coming from him.\par \par \uc2 And you're going to have to -- here's the tall order. You're going to have to convince 37, 35, almost 40 percent of the Republican party that you can beat \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and that you are a better general election candidate than he is. That's going to be tough whenever so many polls are out showing that Trump can beat Biden. But that's the key argument he still has to make.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Leslie, we talked a little bit about culture war. He doesn't like that phrase. He doesn't think it's the focus. However, his campaign has focused on some of those issues. Here is some of that pushback from him.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: You don't win a state like Florida that big if you're not doing things that are resonating. I would push back. What I hear about culture war, standing up for the rights of parents, standing up for the well-being of children, that's not some, quote, culture war. That is central to the lives of tens of millions of people throughout this country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Leslie, that said, he said today he spent talking, as Morgan mentioned, about economic pillars and talking about New Hampshire economy.\par \par \uc2 LESLIE MARSHALL, DEMOCRATIC STRATEGIST: Well, voters care more about the economy, whether you are a Democrat or Republican, an independent, whether you love Trump or you're going to give DeSantis a try. I would echo some of what Morgan said, including that you give great interview, Bret. You do. And I like the fact that you ask questions that other people don't ask.\par \par \uc2 And, regarding culture, he talks about Florida. The problem is this is not a one state race. It's a 50-state race. And not all 50 states, and I would argue most of the states are not like Florida. And when you talk about rights, well, then some people would argue what about the rights of LGBTQ community, specifically children in that community, parents of those children. What about rights of parents like me, even though they are not living in Florida, who would like their children to have access to a lot of these books and read all of this. And of course, there are African- Americans that don't like changes not just with books, but in the educational system.\par \par \uc2 So I think that's going to be a problem with him, not just with the Republican Party, but for those that are looking for somebody other than \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to vote for.\par \par \uc2 And, lastly, I would just say on this, when you talk about -- I think it's admirable and if I ever ran for office I don't want to get down in the mud with that person. If that person is \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , I don't think you can avoid it. I'm going to predict that \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  comes up some points because is he playing the same game. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is not. And I think that's actually going to hurt him when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is on stage with him in those debates.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We should point out that the governor gave us a lot of time today. We obviously had a show we had to get it all into. We are going to post the entire back and forth on my podcast. Byron, what was your takeaway?\par \par \uc2 BYRON YORK, CHIEF POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT, "THE WASHINGTON EXAMINER": I thought one of the more striking things was something we really did not hear, which is any sort of openness or transparency about what's been going wrong about his campaign. In the new "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " poll today, is he 37 points behind \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  nationally with likely Republican voters. In the Real Clear Politics average of polls, he is below 20 percent, has dipped below 20 percent. Things are not really working very well right now. And he didn't really seem to give any idea or any impression he knows that, he has done this wrong or that wrong. He talked to you vaguely about return on investment.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, Bryon, let me push back just a second, because he wanted to say reset. He did fire a third of his campaign staff. He said they needed a course correction. He admitted they were spending too much money. They needed better ROI. And then I pressed him on that Ed Rollins quote that was pretty harsh, if you think about Ed Rollins running his political action committee saying what he said about him.\par \par \uc2 YORK: Well, that was more of a personal criticism of Rollins. And the sense that you got is that everything he has been saying is fine. And I really didn't get a sense of any change in that. I remember a long time ago, 2007, when \uc2 John McCain\uc2 's campaign imploded, I asked him what went wrong? And he said immigration. They really don't like what I say about immigration. This very frank admission that he was doing some things wrong and needed to fix them. He ultimately, actually went on to win the nomination, which is the comeback that's being used as kind of a model in discussing what DeSantis might be able to do.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. Morgan, you look at "The New York Times"-Siena poll out today, and Byron mentions it. It's a massive lead, and it tracks the other polls that the former president is up. Inside that poll there are four percent, roughly, who believe that the -- among Republicans that the former president really committed serious felonies, and of those 22 percent of those are still voting for him, they say. And, in that environment, the governor has to find traction. And talking about that, saying got to campaign on not being chaos, you wonder if it's going to work.\par \par \uc2 ORTAGUS: Listen, this applies to whether it's a presidential run, a congressional run, or if you are running for dog catcher. You have to connect with people emotionally. They have to know where you are. Not just that you have the best policies and that you are the smartest person in the room. They have to know and feel that you are there for them and that you are going to fight for them. That's what is so much of the Republican base has with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . That's why I think it's the emotional connection is why every time he gets an indictment that his numbers go up, because people in the base feel defensive of him.\par \par \uc2 I think the best thing that the governor could do is talk about his children. That's when he seems the most passionate, the most warm, when he is talking about fighting for his children and other children. That's a way to connect.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, panel, thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 Finally tonight, a Special Report salute.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Take a look at this video. A sentinel never broke stride while guarding the Tomb of the Unknown Soldier. A violent storm ripping through the Washington Metro area, wind gusts reaching 60 miles an hour, even north of that Saturday, damage widespread across the region. But that guard continued marching on, leaning into the gusting wind, the sideways rain. The tomb serves as symbolic grave for all war dead whose remains have not been found or identified. Soldiers from the third U.S. infantry regiment known as the old guard have stood watch over that tomb every minute of every day since 1937 in any weather. Thank you for your service.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT, we'll talk with Miami Mayor, Republican presidential candidate, \uc2 Francis Suarez\uc2 . Fair, balanced, and unafraid. Here's THE INGRAHAM ANGLE.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020230801ej7v00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 Hunter Biden Plea Deal Rejected by Judge; Mixed Reactions to Talk of Possible Impeachment Inquiry; Rep. Mace urges GOP to Find Middle Ground on Abortion; Burgum Meets Fundraising & Polling Thresholds for First GOP Debate; Burgum Signed 6-Week Abortion Ban Into Law; Young Voters are Expected to be Pivotal Demographic in Next Election; Interview with Trump Attorney Alina Habba; Hunter Biden Plea Deal Rejected by Judge\b0\par\par\uc2 Shannon Bream, Alexis McAdams, Jonathan Turley, Marie Harf, Karl  Rove \par \uc2 10,562 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 July 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Fox News Sunday\par \uc2 FOXN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: I'm Shannon Bream.\par \par \uc2 The son of the sitting president in legal limbo as a proposed plea deal falls apart. And a former business associate of his heads to the Hill to tell lawmakers what he knows. While President Trump faces new federal charges in one case and braces for another possible indictment in another.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT: If I weren't running, I would not have anybody coming for me.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice-over): Former President Trump now facing additional criminal charges in a classified documents case, as the nation awaits news on two more potential indictments over January 6th and alleged election interference in Georgia. We'll break it all down for you with the legal spokesperson from the former president, Attorney Alina Habba.\par \par \uc2 Then --\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  is a private citizen and this was a personal matter for him.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's plea deal hits a roadblock as the unraveling of his agreement with the government gives more fuel to Republicans pursuing their own investigations.\par \par \uc2 REP. MICHAEL WALTZ (R-FL): You can't hide it from bank records. You can't hide from suspicious activity reports.\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA), SPEAKER OF THE HOUSE: This is going to rise to an impeachment inquiry.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Republican leaders make moves towards an impeachment of President Biden. We'll give reaction from South Carolina Republican Nancy Mace. It is a "FOX News Sunday" exclusive.\par \par \uc2 And Republican candidates descend on Iowa, months ahead of the first in the nation caucus as the DeSantis campaign undergoes a reset.\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: I'm all about substance at the end of the day.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: While more candidates meet some of the key qualifications for the debate stage, we'll sit down with one of those candidates, North Dakota Governor \uc2 Doug Burgum\uc2 , who's taking heat from getting creative -- only on "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 Then --\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: What are your top three issues that are important to you?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: "FOX News Sunday" talks to young voters about what will drive them to the polls. The Sunday panel weighs in on all the latest 2024 developments.\par \par \uc2 Plus, we go one-on-one with Congressman \uc2 Adam Smith\uc2  on the painful secrets he hid and why he's now going public.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 ADAM SMITH\uc2  (D-WA): It stressed me out a lot. You know, I was really worried about, you know, my ability to keep doing the job.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All, right now, on "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM (on camera): Hello from FOX News in Washington.\par \par \uc2 Former President Trump, who remains the odds on front runner for the Republican nomination for the White House, facing intense legal pressure -- indictments in two cases and under investigation in at least two others. New charges this week in a case that accuses him of mishandling classified documents, and potential indictments in the case alleging attempts to overturn the 2020 election, including January 6th, and alleged interference in Georgia, where this week, barricades were placed outside the Fulton County courthouse ahead of possible charges there.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, Alina Habba, legal spokesperson for former President Trump and general counsel for the Save America Leadership PAC.\par \par \uc2 Alina, welcome back to "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 ALINA HABBA, LEGAL SPOKESPERSON FOR FORMER PRESIDENT TRUMP: Thank you. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. So, let's start here. The president said on Thursday, he felt his legal team -- attorneys had had good meetings with the Justice Department. But by that afternoon, he had been indictment on new federal criminal charges.\par \par \uc2 Was that a shock to you? Are you bracing for the next indictment?\par \par \uc2 HABBA: Nothing is a shock to me anymore coming out from this Department of Justice, this Biden administration. As President Trump mentioned merit-less Garland group of people. Let's be clear this is election interference.\par \par \uc2 So, no, the charges are never a surprise to us because they can't stopped him. He's got 18 percent turn of independents towards Trump. And this is what happens when you're winning in the polls and the Democrats can't win. They have nothing left to do. So --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, let's talk another poll. This one comes out this week showing a majority of Americans, 51 percent, now do believe that President Trump did something illegal, another 27 percent think he did something at least unethical.\par \par \uc2 So, how do you overcome that perception with jurors, that's the first step, and also with voters?\par \par \uc2 HABBA: Yeah. I think with the facts. So, let's talk about the facts which the American people need to know. In that indictment, the new superseding indictment that came out, which they tried to get another headline for President Trump, was facts that said that President Trump, what, did what? What was the obstruction of justice? Because no tapes were deleted. He turned them over. He cooperated, as he always does.\par \par \uc2 But they would like the American public to believe in these bogus indictments that there are some facts that say that President Trump was obstructing justice.\par \par \uc2 When he has his turn in court and when we get to file our papers, you will see that every single video, every single surveillance tape that was requested was turned over. If President Trump didn't want something turned over, I assure you, that is something that could have been done but he would never act like that.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. Well --\par \par \uc2 HABBA: He's the most ethical American I know.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Let's talk about the indictment, because you bring this up, and there are allegations now that he, with a couple of employees at Mar-a- Lago, had conversations. There are allegations about trying to get rid of surveillance videos once it was under subpoenaed.\par \par \uc2 So, let me read from the indictment, this is the new superseding indictment. De Oliveira, the property manager there at Mar-a-Lago, told Trump employee four that, quote, the boss wanted the server deleted. This is where the surveillance video was. Trump employee four responded that he would not know how to do that and that he did not believe he had the rights to do that. Trump employee four told de Oliveira that de Oliveira would have to reach out to another employee who was a supervisor of security for Trump's business organization.\par \par \uc2 De Oliveira then insisted to the Trump employee four that, quote, the boss wanted the server deleted and asked, what are we going to do?\par \par \uc2 You rightly say that the tapes were turned over. I mean, they're in custody of the Justice Department. But the question is, was there an attempt to try to make sure that didn't happen?\par \par \uc2 HABBA: Well, let's be clear. If there was an attempt by somebody who lives in a home, take out President Trump who owns a company. He owns it. He owns the I.T. He pays for all these employees.\par \par \uc2 If there was an attempt for him to not turn over documents or he wanted something deleted, do you not think that that's something he could not have gotten done?\par \par \uc2 Let's use common sense.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Does it sound like that there was an effort.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HABBA: I think the American public forgets to use common sense.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I mean, that's the allegation, is that he did try to talk to these employees --\par \par \uc2 HABBA: I know the facts.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: -- and these employees said, eh, we may have to talk to someone else. I don't know that I can even do that.\par \par \uc2 HABBA: Shannon, I know the facts. And the facts are, when President Trump gets the subpoena, it goes to the organization, the organization turns it over. That is what actually happened. I was -- I've been an attorney for the president during this whole process, and I can assure you that that -- that is not the case.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. Well, innocent until proven guilty, he will have his day in court and we will watch as that plays out.\par \par \uc2 Alina, thank you for joining us again.\par \par \uc2 HABBA: Yes, thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. Now to our legal panel, \uc2 George Washington University\uc2  law professor Jonathan Turley, and Thomas Dupree, former deputy -- principal deputy assistant attorney general.\par \par \uc2 Great to have you back and have you guys in person as well.\par \par \uc2 THOMAS DUPREE, FORMER PRINCIPAL DEPUTY ASSISTANT ATTORNEY GENERAL: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. So, Professor, your reaction of what you just heard from Alina there.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN TURLEY, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, that dog won't hunt, if that's their position in court. You know, they can't just simply the tapes are turned over because the government is not alleging that the tapes were destroyed. They're alleging, as you noted, an effort to get rid of the tapes.\par \par \uc2 Now, that doesn't mean at all that the indictment is true. What's interesting about the indictment is it seems to be an effort to broaden the evidentiary bases, because the one -- one of the weaker links for this indictment is a lot of it is based on Trump's own lawyers. That could pose a serious problem later. And these new allegations are not as dependent on those lawyers.\par \par \uc2 So, this may be a sort of effort to get cushion in case things go bad.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: There is always a presumption of innocence, one of the beautiful things about our legal system.\par \par \uc2 But now, we have a couple of employees, Tom, who are drawn into this. How much do you think this is pressure on them to flip on the former president?\par \par \uc2 DUPREE: I think those employees are under substantial pressure.\par \par \uc2 Look, if you are someone who works at the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2 , and kind of managerial capacity, or, you know, work at Mar-a-Lago and you got a call from the Justice Department and the special counsel summons you in, and says, look, we suspect, you may even have evidence that you committed some criminal activities. We're thinking about prosecuting you, but we'd like to hear what you know, what can you tell us? What can you tell us of President Trump?\par \par \uc2 You are under immense pressure to cooperate, to come clean effectively, to turn against President Trump and say, here's what I have, here's what I know.\par \par \uc2 So, I think the special counsel is playing from a position of strength here. It sounds like he's already gotten some information from people close to President Trump and he may well have more that we'll see as this case unfolds.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yeah. And we still wait to see if there will be a separate indictment from the special prosecutor on the January 6th, the post-2020 election, things that happen. We'll see what the allegations are if we get one of those.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, I want to talk about \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  plea deal falling apart this week, because we were talking before the show. It was just highly unusual, all of these different portions of this, and how this came over. The judge was probing on this.\par \par \uc2 Our buddy over at "National Review", Andy McCarthy, writes this: The point of the agreement is to outline and detail the fuel extent of the immunity the defendant is getting in exchange for his plea, because of the Hunter Biden defense and the Biden Justice Department are on the same side, he argues, the collective objective was to give Hunter immunity as possible with as little said as possible about why he needs it.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan, do you agree with that assessment?\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: I do. I've never seen a hearing like this. I've never seen a plea agreement like this. You know, first of all, they put the immunity grant in with the gun charge which is a lot like putting your luggage tag inside your luggage. It's not what you officially look for it.\par \par \uc2 And the court was not pleased when it found it there, and then said, well, what does this mean?\par \par \uc2 And it looked like to her, a sort of a wink and a nod, and the best the prosecutor did was sort of shrug, and I'd say, I've -- honestly, I have never seen anything quite like this agreement, but it is what it is. Well, that's not enough for a federal judge.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well -- and there seem to be some misunderstanding or miscommunication between the parties about whether or not this deal, which was about tax and gun charges would extend to other potential investigations. And we're keeping told, Tom, that there is an ongoing investigation related to Hunter.\par \par \uc2 So, do you plea deal a part of it while it's still continuing?\par \par \uc2 DUPREE: Well, if you're in \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's position, you probably would because you in theory could put these particular charges to rest, even if it doesn't insulate you from charges coming down the road.\par \par \uc2 Look, I agree, what happened last week in Delaware was extraordinary, shocking, unprecedented. But in one sense, the system worked, the federal judge did her job. She was presented with this deal, a half baked deal to be sure, but a deal from the prosecutors and from the Biden team.\par \par \uc2 She looked at it. She kicked the tires on it and she said, there's no way I'm signing off on this. She did her job.\par \par \uc2 She sent the parties back for homework assignments, to clarify what they need mean, see if they can work out the differences and maybe return to court, and possibly, she'll approve it down the road.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. So, at this point, again running over "National Review", McCarthy says time for special counsel. A lot of people have called for this. One of the whistleblowers in the \uc2 IRS\uc2  case has called for this again and again, just let's make sure that there's no political or appearance of any political bias. So, let's do that.\par \par \uc2 Andy notes this -- he says that Garland has to appoint a special counsel because, A, there's a substantial basis for a criminal investigation and, B, that the Justice Department is beset by a profound conflict of interest.\par \par \uc2 Do you think that there will be a special counsel? Any chance that the attorney general is considering that?\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: I don't know if he's considering it because for two years, he has refused to admit that there was an overwhelming case for a special counsel. It didn't happen just recently. This has been the situation for two years. Many of us have been writing for two years saying, what are you thinking?\par \par \uc2 It's not clear that he's going to change that. The question is timing. Right now, there is a move towards an impeachment inquiry. That is likely to move things more quickly than a special counsel.\par \par \uc2 Once you go to a special counsel, which I think would be justified, there's no question about that. But it would tend to slow the process down.\par \par \uc2 The -- all these committees are closing in on some answers and what we know so far is deeply disturbing. I don't think Congress really has a choice but to get these answers. I think Speaker McCarthy is right about that. The Constitution doesn't just give you powers, it gives you obligations, and this is one of them.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and some of those answers may come from Devon Archer, who is expected to testify behind closed doors, an interview with the House Oversight Committee.\par \par \uc2 Miranda Devine writing about this over at "The New York Post" says this: Devon Archer is a former best friend and business associate of \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  in Ukraine, is expected to testify under oath to Congress this week that President Biden met with dozens of Hunter's business associates while he was serving as vice president between 2009 and 2017.\par \par \uc2 Okay. So if it's some speaker phone, you know, call, phone calls, if it's stopping in to say "hi" to kind of lend a little bit of glamour to the proceedings, you know, what difference does it make?\par \par \uc2 DUPREE: Well, look, if the question is did President Biden interfere with these business dealings? Was he present? Was he on the phone? And was there money exchanged?\par \par \uc2 These are all important questions. And, look, I think Devon Archer could very well be an absolutely critical witness here. He was someone closely involved. He's someone who apparently has a lot of information.\par \par \uc2 And I think the congressional committee has been doing a fantastic job in listening this information. I suspect that if this has been left to the Justice Department, you know, they're not going to really pursue this the way they have referred to this as political interference.\par \par \uc2 And so, I think the congressional committees have been doing a masterful job in identifying what this has been bringing them and giving them the testimony that is allowing us to figure out what actually happened with President Biden and his son.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: In the meantime, our Mark Meredith over at the White House already asked the question about, will the president pardon Hunter if he needs to? Here's that exchange.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MARK MEREDITH, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: From a presidential perspective, is there any possibility that the president will end up pardoning his son?\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: No.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: Well, is there --\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: Well, I just said no. I just answered.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Were either of you surprise that they -- that door was so firmly closed?\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: Yeah. It would be a little more compelling if the same spokesperson had just two days before changed the president's last denial over having any contact or knowledge of these business deals. Nobody is taking that one to the bank.\par \par \uc2 He can obviously follow what I think is a rather painful history, a president is using pardons for friends and family. \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  being an example of that.\par \par \uc2 We have to wait to see how this unfolds. Obviously, while he's running for president, he cannot say that he's going to pardon his own son for federal crimes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, there are many steps between here and there in both of these cases. We'll track them with your help. Gentlemen, thank you.\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 DUPREE: Thanks, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Much more coming up on these legal issues throughout the show. And Congress takes on claim of the cover-up over whether or not we're alone in the universe.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we'll talk with one of the lawmakers pushing the government to tell us whether it actually has down UFOs or what it found inside.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. NANCY MACE (R-SC): Do we have the bodies of the pilot who piloted this craft?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: More now on the legal issues facing the Biden family as new details emerge about that failed plea deal for the President's son and as a former key associate of \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  prepares for closed door testimony tomorrow before the House Oversight Committee.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, South Carolina Congresswoman Nancy Mace, who's a member of that committee. Welcome back to Fox News Sunday.\par \par \uc2 REP. NANCY MACE (R-SC), HOUSE OVERSIGHT COMMITTEE: Thank you, Shannon. Thanks for having us on this morning.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, so that happens tomorrow behind closed doors. The Wall Street Journal says maybe don't get too excited for fireworks, reporting this, "A person close to Archer expected him to testify that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  wasn't substantially involved in any of \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's business dealings." Are you worried that Republicans are overhyping what you might get from Mr. Archer?\par \par \uc2 MACE: No, I don't think so. This was a person who was in business with \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  and Biden family members. He was the BFF of the President's son. He has a lot of information, I believe, that we'll be able to fact check and corroborate with other witnesses and other whistleblowers. And we just had two \uc2 IRS\uc2  whistleblowers admitting that their investigation has been blocked by federal agencies.\par \par \uc2 And is he going to tell us that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was in the room via conference call or in person and to what extent he was involved in this mess? And the American people have the right to know whether he was or was not.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: In the meantime, the Ranking Member of that Committee, Democrat Congressman \uc2 Jamie Raskin\uc2 , says Republicans are kind of playing fast and loose with the truth. He says there's an \uc2 FBI\uc2  agent and he wants that transcript of that interview release because he said it actually wipes away the notion that there was any political bias in the investigation into \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 He sent a letter to the Chair, James Comer, saying this, not releasing that transcript is the latest in your troubling pattern of concealing key evidence in order to advance a false and distorted narrative about your investigation of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  that has not only failed to develop any evidence of wrongdoing by President Biden, but has, in fact, uncovered substantial evidence to the contrary. He says, Republicans are now doing what they accused Democrats of doing.\par \par \uc2 He says there's no there, there. The accusations that Democrats were going down, rabbit trails with regard to President Trump and Russia. That he says that's what Republicans are actually doing now.\par \par \uc2 MACE: Well, I mean, I believe -- I do believe in transparency and all the evidence that the Oversight Committee, either for or against should absolutely be released to the American people. They don't trust Congress because often it's a bunch of political games. So trust the evidence that comes out.\par \par \uc2 But I will tell you, the evidence that we've seen is overwhelmingly piling up. Some of it we can't share because it's -- it's confidential. For example, the 170 plus suspicious activity reports pointing to money laundering, bribery, racketeering and corruption, et cetera. So we have to go find the bank records. We have to go find witnesses and other whistleblowers to prove it in other ways since we can't release that information. So it's really important, in my estimation, no matter what side of the aisle you are on, release everything at the point that you can, release it all of the American people.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and this is sparking conversations about impeachment. And Politico is one of the many places here in D.C. that's reporting on this schism sort of within the Republican Party. There are Republicans who are publicly expressing concerns, Congressman \uc2 Ken Buck\uc2 , one of them, about going down this trail.\par \par \uc2 Here's what Politico says, McCarthy must placate his hard right critics whose support he'll need in this week's high stakes spending votes without alienating his most skittish members who face re-election on turf that Biden won. So what about this issue of impeachment, is the party divided?\par \par \uc2 MACE: Well, I do believe we are, at this point, an inquiry is different from an impeachment vote and is another tool in the toolbox. But I will tell you, every time we walk the plank, we are putting moderate members, members that won Biden, won districts. We are putting those seats at risk for 2024. We are putting the majority at risk. And it's not just impeachment that does that. Other issues, like abortion, et cetera, also put those members on the plank.\par \par \uc2 But the one thing I'll say is this, is that whatever the evidence shows us, we ought to follow the facts, and we have to be better than \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 . Pelosi really politicized the impeachment process. We do not want to do that here. We have to show overwhelming, undeniable evidence in order to move this thing forward. And if we can't, then we should not. But if we do, then we ought to use every tool in the toolbox to make sure the American people see it for what it is, and we can hold everyone accountable.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, about that, what do you say to Americans who increasingly believe, no one is going to be held accountable when things happen, when there are all these congressional investigations. They say at the end of the day, nobody actually gets whether it's a slap on the wrist, time in jail, whatever it would be for the corresponding allegations. What do you say to them?\par \par \uc2 MACE: And it's the truth. I mean, I sit there. I sat in the chair of a committee that I chair on Oversight on Wednesday. I cursed out the witness that day because they didn't come with information. They refused to answer any of our questions. And then they laughed at us as we were probing about $400 billion that was wasted by the federal government. And so we have these bureaucrats. We have people that come before us and testify, and nothing ever happens. Nobody ever gets fired. No one is ever held accountable. And the government goes on being in some ways very corrupt or at least not transparent, with the American people wasting billions of taxpayer dollars. And it's wrong. And I'm one person. I'm doing what I can to show the truth and show what's happening at every level, at every stage. And I call out both sides of the aisle. I call it straight down the middle, but it's not easy.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and this week, one of the things you were calling for is more transparency with regard to what we used to call UFOs. I know they have a fancy new name. I want to play a little bit of back and forth from the hearing. You have witnesses who say the government has remains of crashes. And that's not all. Here's a bit of that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACE: Were they -- I guess, human or non-human biologics?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Non-human, and that was the assessment of people with direct knowledge on the program I talked to that are currently still on the program.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. Some serious allegations that raised a lot of eyebrows. Do you believe them? And do you think the government has an obligation to be transparent with all of this information, whatever it may be?\par \par \uc2 MACE: Well, the Oversight Committee on Wednesday, we had three very credible witnesses, a former intelligence officer, a retired military, retired pilots who'd seen things they could not explain. And what we learned on Wednesday, because this really isn't about little green men. This is about the federal government laundering your tax dollars, paying for programs that none of us in Congress are aware of, let alone you, the taxpayer. And you have the right to know what's really going on. And we left with more questions than answers, because a lot of the information that was shared, can't be shared in a public setting. So a lot of it might have to, at the next hearing, be in a private setting and a skiff, which is frustrating. But I want to be transparent with the American people, with everything that we learn from these hearings.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, please do. OK, you mentioned the issue of abortion. You've -- we've talked about this. You don't think the GOP is handling this well. You describe yourself as pro-life, but you were clearly frustrated over a recent measure on the Hill --\par \par \uc2 MACE: I was.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: -- in which it disallowed funding for paid time off or travel expenses for women in the military who travel to get abortions. You did ultimately vote for. But here's how one pro-life leader reacted, said this, "Nancy Mace is a disgrace to Republican women. Our military exists to protect American lives -- we shouldn't be giving them taxpayer funding for abortions to take them."\par \par \uc2 So you voted for the provision, but do you think the government should be backing those expenses?\par \par \uc2 MACE: Well, I am pro-life. My voting record will reflect that. But I also don't believe it's OK at this time, this recently post Roe to target women in uniform. That's wrong. It looks bad.\par \par \uc2 And the other thing is, every time we do an abortion vote, and I'm pro- life, we should also be having a vote on bills and legislation and amendments that this -- that showcase that we care about women. What are we doing to help women who've been raped or girls who are victims of incest? What are we doing to improve the foster care system for to children who are unwanted when they're born? How are we ensuring that women have the option of adoption versus abortion when making really tough decisions? Are we giving counseling to women who've had abortions? Because a lot of women have very difficult times recovering mentally, making that decision afterwards, they regret it. And so what are we doing to show that we care about women and finding that middle ground?\par \par \uc2 You know, we can take those votes, but we haven't done anything in seven months in this legislative session to show that we care about women. And that's where my frustration is. And that's where comments like that really don't read the room about what suburban moms and women really care about in this country today. It's the number two issue in swing districts and swing seats. And if you want to win the White House, if you want to keep the majority in the House, and you want to flip the Senate, then we've got to be a lot smarter in how we talk about women and how we show them that we care.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, Congresswoman, we're out of time. I know you're disappointed. You don't get to talk about the prayer breakfast again but maybe next time. We thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACE: I'll talk about anything. But I'm on my way to church. We're on our way to church right now, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Have a great Sunday. Thanks for stopping in here first.\par \par \uc2 MACE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, up next --\par \par \uc2 MACE: Thank you, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: -- Republican presidential candidates try out new tactics to make the debate stage. We're going to talk to North Dakota Governor \uc2 Doug Burgum\uc2  about that. Where he stands in the polls, and why he says people got to start, stop calling him a billionaire.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: The first Republican debate is quickly approaching. This week, another candidate met the \uc2 RNC\uc2 's polling and fundraising thresholds. North Dakota Governor \uc2 Doug Burgum\uc2 . He sparked a heated ethical debate when he offered $20 gift cards to anyone who donated at least a dollar to his campaign in order to meet the party's requirement, the candidates get $40,000 unique donors.\par \par \uc2 Governor Burgum joins us now from Iowa. Great to see you, Governor.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 DOUG BURGUM\uc2  (R-ND), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Great to be with you, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, so there's been this ethical debate. Most people think, it's fine. I know that you've said instead of paying a political consultant, you decided this was a good way to invest the money. But the Independent has this report, says Biden's supporters exploit Republicans $1 donation cashback campaign pledge. One on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  wrote, I gave \uc2 Doug Burgum\uc2  $1 so he would send me $20. Then I gave the $20 to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . And he attached the photos of his donation. Did you foresee that might happen?\par \par \uc2 BURGUM: I just think the whole thing is humorous. These are clubhouse rules designed to keep fresh faces, fresh ideas, entrepreneurs and innovators off the stage because you set a limit like this. And, of course, it favors people who've helped national office, it favors people who've been, you know, pundits on TV. It favors people that have been career politicians and their name recognition. And I'm an innovator, an entrepreneur. I grew up in a small town in North Dakota, literally bet the farm to start a software company.\par \par \uc2 And if you're starting an online retail company today, if you're a young person, the first thing you do when you're going online is you offer a promotional thing. And of course, we did this. The $20 gift card, these consulting firms wanted $100 per customer acquisition. This is one fifth the cost. And this is what we've done in North Dakota, is use creativity and innovation, solve a problem, do it for one fifth the price that a political party or the government might do it. And we're thrilled to have an opportunity to bring our message about the economy, energy, national security to the national debate stage.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And I want to try to get to as many of those topics as we can. But let me start with this. You endorsed President Trump in 2016 and 2020, so why the need to run against him this time?\par \par \uc2 BURGUM: Well, I believe that we need to focus on the things that matter the most to most Americans, and that is the economy. This is running -- about running against \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . I mean, we just need -- don't just need course corrections on the economy, energy, national security, we need 180-degree turn, because on the economy, whether it's inflation, taxes, spending, deficit, trade, all in the wrong direction.\par \par \uc2 On energy policy, we should be selling energy to our friends and allies instead of buying it from our adversaries. And on national security, that starts with border security. And right now, we've got an open border. I've been down there with the National Guard troops from North Dakota, and it's completely unreported what's going on down there. And all of these things are affecting every American.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yeah. And part of that, it ties together with Fentanyl. So I want to talk about in the context of China. You've said that we are in a Cold War essentially with them. But a lot of American business leaders are willing, apparently, to put up with a lot, look the other way. They've gone over there for very cozy meetings with Chinese leaders, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , Tim Cook, \uc2 Jamie Dimon\uc2 , among those. We know that our economies are intertwined. Secretary Yellen has said you can't decouple. So how do you address the issues?\par \par \uc2 BURGUM: Well, we are in a Cold War with China by all definition, but is very different. When \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  was leading this country and we were in a Cold War with Russia, Russia had a small economy. They weren't very connected, and we were able to just outperform them with our -- with our economy and with our strong economy, we were able to have a strong military. We need to do the same thing now in our Cold War with China. We've got to get our economy sprinting. It's crawling right now. And part of the reason it's crawling is this incredible red tape. I've seen the red tape as an entrepreneur, as a small business person. I've seen it running large global businesses. And now I've definitely seen it as a governor where the federal government is always outside of its lane. It's always bigfooting the states and businesses with regulation.\par \par \uc2 And the way America has always won is with innovation. And we drive that forward, we get this economy going. But we -- China is the number two economy in the world. They're completely interconnected. We've got to be much smarter about how we manage this.\par \par \uc2 And I'll give you one horrible example. But, you know, Putin likely doesn't even invade Ukraine. If not for having all of Western Europe dependent on his energy. Well, then, belatedly, the Biden administration says, we're going to put sanctions on Russian oil. And that takes some Russian oil out of our markets. But Russia is selling oil 20% to 30% off, to who? To China. China the world's largest importer of oil and gas in the world. And we're over there with Blinken, Yellen, Kerry and nobody's even talking about how we can use energy policy as a lever in our relationship in the Cold War with China.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: You -- and you mentioned Russia, and obviously all of those interconnected, very complex foreign policy problems. What do you consider defeat of Russia victory tree for Ukraine? Where do you see the line starting and stopping for U.S. aid continuing to flow there, to Ukraine?\par \par \uc2 BURGUM: Well, we have to have dollars, have to have accountability across government. It doesn't matter whether it's domestic or military or wherever the spending is. We've got to have more transparency and accountability. And that's what we've done in North Dakota. I mean, we've cut budgets in North Dakota. We've balanced the budget. We've got our economy rolling there. We're on track to have the highest GDP in the nation.\par \par \uc2 But when we're talking about Russia, we can't have a win for Putin. Putin - - we can't even think about it like a country the way we think about ourselves. It's a series, basically, of mob bosses. They have a fancy name oligarchs. But, you know, what's Putin worth? $70 billion? I mean, we can't -- you know, they've amassed all this worth in Prigozhin and what the \uc2 Wagner Group\uc2  was doing, we now know.\par \par \uc2 These guys have got 64 shell companies. They're controlling gold mines in the Central African Republic. When they helped, quote, "help Syria," they took over 25% of Syria's oil production. These are large international criminal organizations. And a win for Putin, as I said, is again, Putin and China have had 40 meetings over the last 10 years. We're just pushing these folks together. And now with Putin becoming China's gas station, it's just exacerbating our biggest challenge, which is the Cold War we have with China and our competition with China.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, I want to make sure we hit on domestic issues as well. You signed one of the toughest abortion laws in the country, but you've said you wouldn't sign a federal ban. You think that presidents need to stay out of cultural issues.\par \par \uc2 Susan B. Anthony\uc2 , big pro-life group says this about. GOP candidates who won't commit to some kind of federal ban, says, "Saying that the issue should only be decided at the states is an endorsement of abortion up until the moment of birth, even brutal late-term abortions in states like California, Illinois, New York and New Jersey. We will oppose any presidential candidate who refuses to embrace at a minimum a 15-week national standard."\par \par \uc2 Is it a cop out to now say with the really interesting and some people would say aggressive of culture war issues that you've had in North Dakota that you've signed off on, then, now that you're in a national race, you shouldn't have to talk about that?\par \par \uc2 BURGUM: No, what I've -- it's just -- it's amazing. We've spent 50 years, the pro-life movement, fighting to have Roe v. Wade overturn so that the decision could return to the states, and about 24 hours later, the same people say, no, now it's been turned over to the states. Now we got to have the Feds back involved again.\par \par \uc2 I'm a 10th Amendment guy. Read the 10th Amendment. There's a very specific set of things that the federal government is supposed to do, and the rest is reserved to the states, comma, or to the people, and importantly to the people, because so much of this culture wars, it's one group trying to stamp their set of rules on another group. And we have to -- we have to just say, hey, look, what's going to fly in New York is not going to fly in North Dakota. We know that. And so as president, we've got to focus on the things that matter and things like national security, which includes border security. And certainly as president, we got to -- I will be focusing on the things that are actually part of the job description of running the federal government, return the power to the states, shrink the size of government, and -- and then some of these other issues are going to have to be debated at the state and local level.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yeah. And I would note that there are people who do read the Supreme Court decision to include the federal legislature could be involved. But we know your position, and we will see you on the debate stage, Governor. We thank you for your time.\par \par \uc2 BURGUM: Great, Shannon. Thanks to be on -- great to be on with you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And don't forget, Fox News Channel is your home for the first Republican primary debate, August 23 in Milwaukee. Bret Baier and Martha MacCallum will moderate candidates take the stage, 09:00 p.m. Eastern.\par \par \uc2 All right, up next, we've got to look at what young conservative voters think about the 2024 GOP presidential field.\par \par \uc2 Plus, reaction from our Sunday group on talk of resets and wrangling less than a month from that first GOP debate.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Young people showed up in droves during the '22 midterms, nearing record turnouts and voting nearly two to one for Democrats. Now, Republicans are trying new ways to reach out to this pivotal demographic ahead of the next presidential election.\par \par \uc2 We sent Alexis McAdams to speak with college students attending the National Conservative Student Conference here in D.C. this week.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS MCADAMS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Who do you see as being the next President of the United States?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I think \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: As Republican presidential candidates hit the campaign trail, young voters are listening.\par \par \uc2 IRENGE NKERE, BELMONT ABBEY COLLEGE SOPHOMORE: I'm still undecided. I'm most likely leaning towards Trump, just from what he's done in the past. And I really don't want another Democrat in office.\par \par \uc2 PATRICK WILKINSON, COLLEGE SENIOR: I want to see more policy answers because a lot of it right now is talk on social issues, and I think policy is a lot more important.\par \par \uc2 JUSTIN HILL, UNIVERSITY OF BUFFALO SENIOR: The economy is a big one. It's not doing too well right now. I feel like -- I think everybody feels.\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: According to data from \uc2 Tufts University\uc2 , the number of college students who voted went up 14 points between 2016 and 2021. When expert says that growing electorate is up for grabs.\par \par \uc2 DR. KEI KAWASHIMA-GINSBERG, \uc2 TUFTS UNIVERSITY\uc2 : When you look at how they vote, I know that majority vote for Democratic candidates in federal elections, but when you ask, which party do you belong to? A lot of young people really have no affiliation.\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: A third-party candidate could be a concern for the GOP.\par \par \uc2 JULIETTE COLUNGA, THE MASTER'S UNIVERSITY JUNIOR: If I felt that they aligned with the issues that I think are important and we're pretty conservative, yes.\par \par \uc2 RYANNI GOLDBERG, DALLAS BAPTIST UNIVERSITY SENIOR: I would not, personally. I just don't see that there's a chance for that.\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: What do you think the conservative movement can do to get more young people to vote?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Market. They need to do better in marketing.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: Young people I talk to say age does play a factor in who they vote for because they're looking for a fresh face to lead the Republican Party. Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, Alexis, thank you very much. That conference sponsored by Young America's Foundation, one of the partners in the first Republican debate.\par \par \uc2 Time now for our Sunday group. USA Today Washington Bureau Chief Susan Page, Marie Harf, former State Department Spokesperson, former Bush White House Adviser Karl Rove and Julia Manchester, National Political Reporter with The Hill.\par \par \uc2 All right, welcome to all of you. Politico has this headline, young voters are getting less likely to identify as Dems, and that spells trouble for Biden. They look at this analyst who has looked at youth voter data for more than 20 years and says voters under 30 today are less likely to identify as Democrats compared to the spring of 2019. Julia, more of them consider themselves independent.\par \par \uc2 JULIA MANCHESTER, THE HILL NATIONAL POLITICAL REPORTER: Yeah, you're seeing that a lot from Generation Z because you see a lot of crossover in terms of the issues that a lot of these voters like. So you see young conservatives actually very interested in the environment, for example, and maybe younger Democrats who may not be as fiscally liberal as other Democrats.\par \par \uc2 So I think what you're seeing right now is that these voters don't want to be labeled. They don't like the labels, particularly in Gen Z. They don't want to be put into a box when it comes to these issues, and they want to be able to have that choice.\par \par \uc2 That being said, though, we did see in 2022 that young voters overwhelmingly really turned the tide for Democrats.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So this same analyst went on a deeper dive in his \uc2 Substack\uc2 . He says, ring the alarm about this group. He says, in the last four years, the number of young black men and women who identify as Democrats has declined a jaw-dropping 15 percentage points from 64 percent to 49 percent, Marie.\par \par \uc2 MARIE HARF, FORMER STATE DEPARTMENT SPOKESPERSON AND FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: I mean, I think Julia's right, people -- young voters are less likely to identify with a political party. But we do know that they care a lot about climate change, they care a lot about gun control and gun regulation. They care about issues that increasingly Republicans are just not in line with many young voters on. When it comes to women's health, for example, these are voters who, for the first time now, are thinking about a world in which women may not have the same health care options and rights that - that our generation has taken for granted.\par \par \uc2 So, I think that these are definitely independent voters, but Republicans are really struggling with them. We've seen that in election after election because their policies, not these cultural issues. It was fascinating that that young voter said he didn't want to hear as much about cultural issues. The policies on all the issues, many of them, that these young voters care about, Republicans are moving so far away from them because they're going so far to the right. And that spells trouble for Republicans.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I see, Karl - I feel you shaking your head over there, Karl.\par \par \uc2 HARF: I can feel it, too. I can feel it\par \par \uc2 KARL ROVE, FORMER BUSH WHITE HOUSE ADVISER AND FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: I didn't shake my head, but I - but inside I was.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Right, we could feel the vibes.\par \par \uc2 ROVE: Look, between 2018 and 2022, the youngest cohorts of voters, 18 and 29, move more Republicans. They're still overwhelmingly Democrat, but they moved towards the Republicans. And if I were a Democrat, id' be as worried as the Biden White House is about them. If you take a look at all the polls, this is the group that is most - is most uncomfortable with the idea of an 82-year-old running for re-election as president. The lack of enthusiasm among younger Democrats or among younger voters towards the Democratic frontrunner, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , is palpable. That's exactly why they have continued to try, for example, on the student loan forgiveness. They're trying to bribe them, hoping that - that Biden - you know, it's that time of bread (ph) and circuses, we're back in the Roman era, and they can buy their votes. And they - it ain't working. But the polling data, these - there are two groups that looked like the lack of enthusiasm could be deadly to the Democrats in 2024 if the Republicans nominating a fresh face. And those are young voters and blacks, whose enthusiasm levels are (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 SUSAN PAGE, WASHINGTON BUREAU CHIEF, "\uc2 USA TODAY\uc2 ": Let me shake my head, Karl, because -\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Susan (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 PAGE: The Biden White House - the Biden team is not concerned that young voters are going to vote for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  or for the Republican ticket. They're concerned they will either not vote at all - that's a real possibility - or that they'll vote for like Cornell West, who has a lot of appeal for young voters as an independent candidate. And so their task is really to not persuade them to vote Democratic, but to persuade them to bother to go to the polls.\par \par \uc2 And the big problem there is that younger voters do not see politics as an effective way to address the issues that matter in their life. So you need to get Washington to be more functional if you're going to energize younger voters to go vote.\par \par \uc2 KARL: I agree with you that the likelihood is - the decline in turnout is what they're most concerned about. Six percent fewer, 18 to 29 year-old voters in Arizona fail to turn out, he loses Arizona. One percent fewer black voters in Georgia turn out to vote in 2024 than voted in 2020, he loses Georgia. So, even on the margins, these groups that are not enthusiastic or may be willing to go to Cornell West or maybe to contemplate staying home, that's the problem for the Democrats.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and "Politico" has this. In addition to Cornell West, they talk about, is this going to be a four-way race because they say U.S. Representative \uc2 Dean Phillips\uc2 , a Minnesota moderate, has been receiving inquiries about his willingness to challenge Biden and is going to New York City next week to meet with Democratic donors.\par \par \uc2 I mean, Marie, that's another headache this White House does not need.\par \par \uc2 HARF: I - I -- \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is going to be the nominee here. I'm trying to figure out how - how aggressive to be about - about these stories because Joe -\par \par \uc2 BREAM: What about third party issue?\par \par \uc2 HARF: Well, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  will be the nominee. And the fact that in quarter two he outraised \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  by more than two to one, people - you know, all these Democrats who were bed wetting about whether there's enthusiasm about \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , he's raising a lot of money. And that's one way to judge that.\par \par \uc2 I think Democrats are, though, very concerned, not about \uc2 Dean Phillips\uc2 , but about this sort of No Label movement, the Joe Manchin. You know, is there going to be some, quote, moderate Democratic or Republican who think they can inject the race with some enthusiasm, you know, try and get issues talked about in a way they might not be. That almost always, you know, ends up hurting people - you know, candidates like \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . I think Democrats are very concerned that if Joe Manchin or some other third-party candidate runs it will hurt \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , it will not hurt \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, an issue that hasn't been talked about, but late Friday, in a statement in "People" magazine, is now being talked about by the president after some mainstream articles and others really started raising this issue about, you have seven grandkids, not six. This is what the president said and the first lady to "People" magazine. This is not a political issue, it's a family matter. Jill and I only want what is best for all of our grandchildren, including Navy.\par \par \uc2 SO, Julia, why now?\par \par \uc2 MANCHESTER: Yes, that - it was certainly a choice to give a statement to "People" magazine on a Friday night. Certainly, a way to bury the lead with maybe a friendlier publication.\par \par \uc2 Look, I think, why now? \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  is not going away. It's going to be an issue for the president. He's going to get asked these questions, whether it's about Hunter's legal issues or his child. So, I think the Biden campaign, the Biden White House, want to essentially put this to rest, want to acknowledge this and move on. We see that campaigns lately have becoming a lot more personal. So, this is something I think that could play. They wanted to get ahead of that.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and, Susan, it comes ahead of - listen, behind a very tough break for \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 , a tough week for him but now this break of the president taking a breather.\par \par \uc2 PAGE: Yes, and Hunter -- when the word \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  appears in the news, it is bad for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . You know, there are no - there are - there are no substantiated allegation against \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  about corruption involving \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 , but there's certainly a lot of credible allegations against \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 . His - his four year old daughter just being one of the messier personal ones that the White Houst just had to deal with. They were in an unsustainable position to deny that she existed.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and another trick for -- a tricky place for Hunter maybe this week, Karl, that Devon Archer is heading to The Hill tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 ROVE: Absolutely. Devon Archer, I'm not certain he's going to involve \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  in it, but we're going to learn more about the ugly, sleezy things that \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  did because Devon Archer was involved in them as well.\par \par \uc2 Both men were on the board of \uc2 Burisma\uc2 , the corrupted Ukrainian company, at the same time that the close friend of \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  - or, excuse me, of Devon Archer was secretary of state and Hunter's dad was vice president of the United States, in charge of the Ukrainian issue.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, we'll see if that transcript gets released.\par \par \uc2 Things are very leaky on Capitol Hill, unlike other places in Washington.\par \par \uc2 So, panel, thank you very much. We'll see you next Sunday.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, a long serving congressman is opening up about his year's long journey battling the depths of anxiety and chronic pain. Up next, I spent some time with \uc2 Adam Smith\uc2 , who shares the story of how he finally found the help he needed and he's hoping others will, too.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Lawmakers on Capitol Hill are not immune to life's struggles. Washington state Democrat \uc2 Adam Smith\uc2  spent years silently dealing with chronic physical pain coupled with anxiety and depression, fighting to get the help he needed. He's got a new memoir "Lost and Broken: My Journey Back from Chronic Pain and Crippling Anxiety," hoping to help others in similar situations. I sat down with the congressman this week to talk about his journey. It's our Sunday's special.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: This book is very vulnerable and transparent. I mean you're so detailed about all of the different ways that you were struggling, mentally, physically, down to details about the medications and the dosages you were taking.\par \par \uc2 Why so much transparency in this book?\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 ADAM SMITH\uc2  (D-WA): The journey that I went through is one that a lot of people go through. You know, people in this country have some combination of chronic pain, anxiety or depression. Millions of people. And I thought that if I would tell the story it would be helpful to people trying to figure out how to navigate on their own journey through this. So, I really felt it could help advance the conversation. And the more honest, the more likely you are to get to the right answer.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Everybody, as you said, out there is struggling with something. But to do it against the demands and the backdrop and the divisiveness often on The Hill, what was that part of the journey like?\par \par \uc2 SMITH: I had a great support system. I mean the - the staff on the Armed Services Committee, my staff in my office, my family, you know, I was very lucky in that I had a lot of people who were supporting me through that. But it was still extraordinary difficult.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And as a House member, it feels like you're always running. I mean you're always fundraising. You have the two years. I mean how much did that weigh on trying to manage all the physical and mental things that you are going through as well.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Yes, it stressed me out a lot. You know, I was really worried about, you know, my ability to keep - keep doing the job and keep getting re- elected. It certainly added to the anxiety. My life as, you know, a good father, good husband, good congressman. Those are my responsibilities. That's what I want to do. And so it really beat down on me. Am I meeting those responsibilities. So I had to work really hard to do that. It certainly added to the difficulty without questions.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And through this journey you talked to hundreds of medical providers on both the mental and physical. And you found that there aren't easy answers a lot of times. We can't just look to one person and say, they got the education, they'll be able to fix me and figure this out. How frustrating was that part of the process?\par \par \uc2 SMITH: First of all, on the mental health side, the stigma that surrounds mental health is a problem. You know, I had a bout of depression when I was 25, a bout of anxiety in 2005. And I didn't tell anyone. And then when it came back in 2013, it wouldn't go away and I had to try to get help.\par \par \uc2 And I would tell a lot of people, if you're having a mental health problem, it's OK to talk about it, it's OK to seek help. We need to be more supportive of that and to try to figure out your way through it.\par \par \uc2 The second thing I would say is, you do have to take a little bit of responsibility for your own healthcare. I don't know anything about this stuff, particularly on the mental health side. And I assumed, I go to a professional, this person, they're educated, they don't - they're going to tell me and I'm just going to do it.\par \par \uc2 Your mind and body are yours. That person you're talking to, they deal with hundred -- thousands of people that are all a little bit different. So you're going to have to work with your provider to get to the right answer. You're going to have to be involved and engaged.\par \par \uc2 And the third big message on both mental health and chronic pain, help exists. Believe me. You can find help for both mental and physical problems. So - so be persistent and you will be able to find it.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: What's the bottom line message for the book, of opening your life?\par \par \uc2 SMITH: I think in public policy, as well as in health care, we need a more problem solving approach to things. I think we tend to resist that. We tend to resist, you know, doing the hard work of, OK, what's really going on here? And it's part of the reasons why I think we have so many prescription drugs being passed out in this country. It's the easy answer. You know, you go see a doctor and give me a pill I'll be better. As opposed to, the mental health side - I mean, I went through three and a half years' worth of psychotherapy to really figure out, OK, why do I have clinical anxiety.\par \par \uc2 That was difficult on the physical side. We talked a little bit about muscles activation techniques, which was the thing that ultimately saved me was the idea that your muscles don't just need to be strong and flexible, they need to be activated. But that took time. It was - it was hard work both to figure out my mind and figure out my body. So, you've got to be willing to do that work, willing to have a problem-solving approach. And I think you can - your life can get better if you do that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Congressman Smith's book is available wherever you like to get your books. It's a great read.\par \par \uc2 And a quick reminder, my podcast, "Livin the Bream" drops this morning. This week country music star Granger Smith talks about how the death of his son shook him to the core and why the hopes he found actually led him to announce his retirement and some very new plans. It's all in his new book, "Like a River: Finding the Faith and Strength to Move Forward After Loss and Heartache." It's out this week.\par \par \uc2 Plus, you can hear all of today's program on the FOX NEWS SUNDAY podcast. Download and subscribe at foxnewspodcast.com, or wherever you get your podcasts.\par \par \uc2 And don't miss the best moments of FOX NEWS SUNDAY. Set your DVR to record the show every week here on your local Fox station or on Sunday's, 2:00 p.m. Eastern, on Fox News Channel.\par \par \uc2 That's it for today. Thanks for joining us. I'm Shannon Bream. Have a wonderful week. We'll see you next FOX NEWS SUNDAY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FOXN000020230730ej7u00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Interview with NSC Communications Coordinator John Kirby; Secret Service Closes Case Without Any Suspects; Democrats Say House Defense Bill Won't Make It To The President Desk; Senator Cotton Introduces Bill to Prevent Schools from Hiding Gender Transitions from Parents; Reports: Some Top Dems Privately Reaching Out to Potential Biden Replacements; Biden, DNC Announce $72 Million Combined Fundraising Haul; White House Says It Will Cancel $39 Billion in Student Loan Debt\b0\par\par\uc2 Shannon Bream, Grady Trimble \par \uc2 10,718 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 July 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Fox News Sunday\par \uc2 FOXN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: I'm Shannon Bream.\par \par \uc2 The House narrowly passes a sweeping defense bill chockfull of Republican add-ons, setting up a showdown in the Senate over social issues.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 JAMAAL BOWMAN\uc2  (D-NY): They're bringing the cultural wars into our national security.\par \par \uc2 REP. CHIP ROY (R-TX): The American people I talk to back home don't want a weak military. They don't want a woke military.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice-over): House Speaker Kevin McCarthy advancing a deeply partisan defense bill, including passage of amendments on abortion access, medical care for transgender troops and diversity programs.\par \par \uc2 The future of the military taking center stage as President Biden returns from the NATO Summit and signs an order that could send thousands of U.S. reservists to Europe.\par \par \uc2 While lawmakers on both sides of the aisle sound the alarm over the U.S. running out of ammo and weapons, saying our supply cannot meet the demand.\par \par \uc2 SEN. CHRIS COONS (D-DE): We have to in the long run have a stronger industrial base.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We'll sit down with NSC communications coordinator John Kirby to discuss our military's readiness and leadership on the world stage. And we'll get reaction from Senator Tom Cotton, a member of the Armed Services Committee.\par \par \uc2 Then, how worried should Democrats be about a third-party threat? We'll ask our Sunday panel about fresh speculation of a potential presidential spoiler, along with efforts to stop it.\par \par \uc2 All, right now, on "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM (on camera): Hello from FOX News in Washington.\par \par \uc2 An annual defense bill that sets the nation's military and national security priorities has landed itself in the middle of a partisan battle field (ph). The must-pass legislation now through the House, chockfull of conservative amendments, measures that will have a nearly impossible time getting through the Senate. At stake, an $886 billion plan that grants pay raises to service members and programs that counter threats by China and Russia.\par \par \uc2 It comes as President Biden is authorizing a mobilization of 3,000 reserve troops to Europe, following meetings with allies at the NATO Summit, and also amid growing concerns about ammunition and weapon shortages, and what that could mean for our military readiness for current and future conflicts.\par \par \uc2 We'll have more on that now in this report from FOX Business correspondent Grady Trimble.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GRADY TRIMBLE, FOX NEWS BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): As the war in Ukraine rages and tensions grow between China and Taiwan, lawmakers are raising questions about the United States' readiness to supply weapons and ammunitions to our partners and to protect ourselves.\par \par \uc2 SEN. DEB FISCHER (R-NE): We don't want to lose our capacity to defend our country. We want to make sure we have production lines up and running.\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE: But those lines might not be running quickly enough. A January report from the think tank \uc2 Center for Strategic and International Studies\uc2  says replacing inventories of the most commonly used ammunition, 155 millimeter shells, could take four to seven years. Javelins up to eight years, and Stingers as many as 18 years.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TOM COTTON (R-AR): Has the Ukraine war caused the challenge to our munitions and defense industrial base, or has it exposed some of the fragility and cracks in the defense industrial base?\par \par \uc2 GEN. CHARLES BROWN JR., AIR FORCE CHIEF OF STAFF: Senator, I would say it would -- it has exposed.\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE: In Taiwan, officials are frustrated. The backlog of arms the U.S. has approved for the island is reportedly close to $19 billion. Some war game simulations of a conflict with China revealed the U.S. would likely run out of certain munitions in less than a week.\par \par \uc2 A State Department spokesperson tells FOX increased demand and supply chain issues are delaying production in deliveries to defense partners around the world. Some reports also suggest defense manufacturers want assurance that if they spend money to ramp up production, it will be worth it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE (on camera): The Pentagon says it's looking for ways to stream line and speedup foreign armed sales. Last month, a bipartisan group of lawmakers launched a task force with same goal -- Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Grady Trimble reporting for us, thanks, Grady.\par \par \uc2 Joining us is John Kirby, the National Security Council coordinator for strategic communications.\par \par \uc2 Welcome back. Good to have you.\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, NATIONAL SECURITY COUNCIL COORDINATOR FOR STRATEGIC COMMUNICATIONS: Great to be with you, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Let's start there with Grady's report. You saw the analysis that several key munitions could take years for us to handle the backlog. The president says this is a problem.\par \par \uc2 So, where are we? Have we acted in a Defense Production Act? How are we solving this problem?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: We're working very closely with the defense industry to try to ramp up production, particularly for artillery shells. You saw that we gave some cluster bomb munitions to Ukraine as a bridging solution here while we ramp up production.\par \par \uc2 We're having very, very strong conversations with the defense industry and believe that we'll be able to get there. But there is going to have to be some production rate increases. No question about it.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Are they worried about our commitments -- the federal government's commitment to orders? Is the funding there? What's happened?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Well, it's not a matter of funding, and we'll obviously continue to work closely with Congress on that. But the defense industry obviously wants to make sure that if they're going to increase production, that, you know, that that production rate is going to stay elevated for a period of time, because that means hiring more workers, it means retooling and adding -- adding capacity in their -- in their factories and manufacturing capabilities.\par \par \uc2 And we understand that, and that's sort of a central thesis here of the -- of the discussions that we're having them, is to get them to increase production and let them to know that, you know, we're serious about doing that for -- for some period of time here.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: What's the message to folks in Taiwan who've been waiting three years on deliverables they made, with China breathing down their neck constantly?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Well, you know, we just approved the recent arms sales. We're going to continue to do that, obviously, working in close concert with Capitol Hill because this is --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But approving the sales thing is one thing, getting them the hard goods a different thing.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: We -- we are -- we have provided, and we will continue to provide self-defense capabilities to Taiwan. We take that responsibility very seriously.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay. As we continue to send munitions and military aid to Ukraine, there are growing questions about \uc2 NATO\uc2  and having everybody else step up. The president fresh off those meetings.\par \par \uc2 NATO\uc2  itself says only seven countries made that goal, the 2 percent of GDP in 2022.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Others like Canada not even close. A "Wall Street Journal" editor board says Canada is military free-rider when it comes to \uc2 NATO\uc2 . They say, somewhere along the way, it began to think of its membership as largely a place to rub shoulders with global powers and a platform for making moral pronouncements.\par \par \uc2 So, what do you say to American taxpayers about their dollars going to somewhere like \uc2 NATO\uc2  when allies like Canada are not even close to meeting that obligation?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Well, they ought -- the taxpayers ought to know that that was a message that the president carried with him to Vilnius, was that this 2 percent GDP spending needs to be a floor, not a ceiling. And he was very clear about that at the \uc2 NATO\uc2  summit, that more countries need to step up to meet that 2 percent goal and hopefully, they will.\par \par \uc2 We're going to keep pressing. We're going to do everything we can to make sure that they know it's important to us. Obviously, it's important to the American taxpayer.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, you know, the United States is still -- you know, has a leadership role inside the alliance, and we're proud of that and we're going to keep that going.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. So, let's talk about some hot spots. Russia, we had reports on Friday that a spy plane flew over a U.S. base in Syria --\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: -- at extended period of them. There was one senior administration official that said it appeared to be mapping the area.\par \par \uc2 How worried are you -- listen, Russia, Iran, Syria, they all want us out of that area -- how worried are you about these coalitions of anti-U.S. actors coming together, you think of Russia and China as well?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Yeah, look, this burgeoning defense relationship, whether it's Iran and Russia, whether it's Russia and China, clearly of concern to us. There's no question about, particularly the one between Iran and Russia right now because that's actually providing real capability in terms of Mr. Putin's ability to kill Ukrainians.\par \par \uc2 The Iranians continue to provide, several hundred now, drones that Mr. Putin is using to attack Ukrainian cities and kill people. And what Iran wants is reciprocity. So, they want capabilities that -- from Russia that they can introduce into Middle East and further destabilize it. So, that's clearly of concern.\par \par \uc2 Now, with this -- with this Russian drone, I mean, we've made our concerns known clearly to the Russians. It violates -- they violated the deconfliction channel that we've been in Syria that has worked up until recently. It's only been in recent weeks now that they started to do this kind of activity, but there wasn't -- it wasn't deemed a specific threat to our people on the ground.\par \par \uc2 So, we're going to keep watching this, we're making sure that we can continue to defend ourselves. I would note that two of the drones, two of the drones that they harassed a week or so ago we're the same two that took out ISIS leader in Syria.\par \par \uc2 So, it's important for Americans to understand that we're not letting off the gas when it comes to going after ISIS in Syria. We still have troops on the ground. They still have vital, viable mission, and we're going to continue to make sure that they can execute it.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And you talk about this interchange between Russia and Iran. Obviously, that's very concerning on a number of fronts. A few weeks ago, "The New York Post" -- excuse me, "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 " reported, and they cited Iranian officials, Israelis officials, U.S. officials that there are some secrets or informal talks going on with Iran about a new nuclear deal. Critics have blasted the idea, say it's unenforceable.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: There have been pushback from the administration about whether those talks are actually happening or not.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But read something from "The Spectator". They say, at a time when the Islamic Republic is in the grip of economic collapse and widespread popular unrest against its theocrats in Tehran, taking pressure off is the last thing Washington should be contemplating, easing pressure on Iran will leave it one step closer to the bomb which would endanger millions.\par \par \uc2 So, are the talks happening? Are these sources uninformed, are they're lying? And why we would take off the pressure now?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Nobody is taking any pressure off Iran. And I think if you look at how we're approaching the challenges in the Middle East, the military presence that we have there, the work -- the strength of the work we're doing with allies and partners to push back on Iran destabilize -- their destabilizing behavior, you can see that we're not -- we're not taking the pressure off.\par \par \uc2 Now, look, if they start to take steps to ease nuclear tensions, that's a good thing. That's a good thing for the region. It's a good thing for us and for our national security interest.\par \par \uc2 We are having discussions obviously with Iranian officials about trying to get wrongfully detained Americans home, that's true. But there's no mini deal on the works right now.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But is it -- is that tied to --\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: The return to the Iran deal is not a major focus of the agenda right now.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: We've got to make sure that we're dealing with their destabilizing behavior, and we are.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And we do have to have, obviously, conversations of American citizens who are held there.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Absolutely, yeah.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Is that tied in any way though to the behavior, their measurements with regard to their nuclear program?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: We are focusing on trying to get wrongfully detained Americans home. And that's -- that's what we're doing.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay, let's talk China. We understand that Climate envoy, former secretary, former Senator \uc2 John Kerry\uc2  is heading over there for conversations on the climate issue. Our next guest who is going to up, GOP Senator Tom Cotton obviously disagrees with a lot of foreign policy decisions that this administration is making. But he says this constant sending over of secretaries, of leaders to interact with China is not helping us.\par \par \uc2 Here's what he said a few weeks ago.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. TOM COTTON (R-AR): Biden administration officials should stop chasing after their China communist counterparts like lovestruck teenagers. It's embarrassing and it's pathetic. In fact, it projects weakness to China.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, it seems like the administration is having a tough time articulating what we're getting out of these meetings. They wouldn't even agree to military to military communication, which we all agree is very important thing and it's happened --\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Absolutely, yeah.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: -- to avoid things escalating out of control.\par \par \uc2 Now, with former Secretary Kerry going there. I mean, they're one of the -- they are the foremost polluter in the world.\par \par \uc2 Why are we going to their home turf? Why are we having these conversations when it looks like we don't have anything measurable coming back out of it?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Well, I think it would disagree with that, and I certainly would take issue with the senator's view of this bilateral relationship and how we are trying to manage it. I mean, these are two of the most powerful economies, two of the most powerful militaries in the world, this is the most consequential relationship in the world and the president believes strongly that we need to manage that responsibly.\par \par \uc2 And it's a competition. There's no reason for it to devolve into conflict. And one of the ways you do that, one of the ways that you get hopefully military to military communications open is to establish other lines of communication.\par \par \uc2 Dialogue is not weakness. Diplomacy is not weakness. It's strength. And that's what we're pursuing right now.\par \par \uc2 Now, Secretary Blinken had a good set of discussions. Did he come home with military lines of communications open, no? But we didn't expect him to.\par \par \uc2 Secretary \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  had some good conversations about our trade and economic relationship, which is the most consequential in the world right now and needs to be on better and stable footing. That's good for American jobs. That's good for the American people.\par \par \uc2 And now, secretary -- special envoy Kerry is going over to talk about climate. Between the United States and China, we emit 40 percent of greenhouse gases in the world. We're the two biggest emitters.\par \par \uc2 I think we can all understand that there's value in us talking about ways we can cooperate to get that -- those emission levels down. And we'll see where these conversations go, but dialogue and diplomacy is strength. It's not weakness.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Our latest FOX poll says 57 percent of Americans, this is across party lines, disapprove of the job the president is doing on foreign policy. What you say to them, these millions of Americans who think on a world stage, this administration is not getting it right?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: I would -- I encourage them to take a look at the trip last week. People said there's no way Sweden is going to get into \uc2 NATO\uc2 . You're not going to get the 32 allies and you certainly going to do it by Vilnius. President Biden got it done.\par \par \uc2 They said that there's no way that any kind of assurances can be given to Ukraine and make them feel comfortable about their \uc2 NATO\uc2  membership. Now, they feel comfortable that they're going to eventually get in and that that war -- you know, that we weren't going to be able to continue to support Ukraine and we are. The American people understand that.\par \par \uc2 The president got an awful lot done just on this last week of trips. And again, I would go back to the way we revitalized partnerships and alliances in the Indo-Pacific. Getting nuclear power submarine capability to the Australians, getting the South Koreans and the Japanese to work more closely together bilaterally. Some of this stuff is historic.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. I want to talk about a national security issue that is domestic here in nature. This is the cocaine found at White House. Secret Service says they didn't have enough evidence to look at any particular person and the case is closed.\par \par \uc2 Speaker Kevin McCarthy says that is a national security issue. Here's what he says:\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA), SPEAKER OF THE HOUSE: If they can't tell us who brought it, what else is happening in the White House they can't tell us about? What else is coming into the White House they can't tell me about? That even concerns me more now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Certainly, you can understand that Americans, many of them, first of all, are aghast that there was ever cocaine in the White House. We heard that there are reports of marijuana a couple of times found there last years as well.\par \par \uc2 But importantly, that it's case closed, in less than two weeks. We know nothing about who brought this in, inability to track people, no surveillance cameras.\par \par \uc2 What if it was something much more dangerous?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Well, again, I can't really speak to the investigation that was done by the Secret Service. They did the best they could to track down how it gets there and who might it belong to, and they just were not able to come up with any forensic evidence that proves it.\par \par \uc2 But, of course, look, we take this seriously. It's not the kind of thing we want to see happen. Now, it did happen at a visitors' lobby area out -- just outside the main West Wing, so it was highly trafficked area.\par \par \uc2 We're going to take a look at how that happened. And obviously we'll -- if there's things we can do to prevent that in the future, certainly we'll do that. Nobody's happy about this.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, please get us updated if you track anything else down.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Yeah, you bet.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: John Kirby, always great to have you with us.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: My pleasure.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Come back soon.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Up next, reaction from a Republican Member of the \uc2 Senate Armed Services Committee\uc2 , Tom Cotton joins us next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: The annual defense bill now on its way to the Senate where many of the amendments passed by Republicans in the House will likely render their version dead on arrival.\par \par \uc2 Joining us here in Washington, Arkansas Republican Senator Tom Cotton. Welcome back.\par \par \uc2 SE. TOM COTTON (R-AR): Thank you Shannon. It's good to be back.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: You've already had an appearance on the show. We'll get back to that in just a minute. But I want to talk about this, what passed through the House and now heads to the Senate. Democrats say this will never see the light of day. It'll never get to the President's desk. Here's a little bit of their reaction.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MARK WARNER, (D) VIRGINIA SENATOR: I am bitterly disappointed that a bipartisan defense authorization bill is being caught up by political grandstanding amendments.\par \par \uc2 HAKEEM JEFFRIES, (D-NY) HOUSE MINORITY LEADER: They are so obsessed with jamming their extreme right-wing ideology down the throats of the American people. That extreme mega Republicans are willing to even detonate the ability of our military to do what it needs to do, to keep us safe.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: One of your fellow vets over in Congress \uc2 Seth Moulton\uc2  says essentially Republicans are using troops as political pawns. How do you respond?\par \par \uc2 COTTON: Well, first off, it is rich to hear these Democrats complaining about a partisan defense bill because this happened when \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  was the Speaker as well. We worked it out. We worked it out every year for over 60 years to pass the defense bill. But it's \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and \uc2 Lloyd Austin\uc2 , the Democrats who are politicizing the military.\par \par \uc2 What the House has done is taken reasonable steps supported by a large majority of Americans to ensure that we're funding our troops and the weapons they need to keep our country safe. Not paying for abortion tourism or sex change operation or diversity commerce RS. Thanks that should not be happening in our military.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: You know how this is going to land though in the Senate. So what happens? You all pass your version of goes to conference. Do any of these amendments survive? Do they go away?\par \par \uc2 COTTON: We'll see. The Senate may take up the defense bill this year or this week and next week. And if so, then we'll move on to, what you said, is a conference where the two chambers reconcile the differences. You never quite know how that was going to work out. I hope all these measures stay in there. Because, again, the military should not be paying for abortion tourism or for diversity consultants or sex change operations. They should be buying the weapons that we need to defend our nation and to support our troops.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So let's talk about that. Because Democrats and the left will say, women who are serving in uniform if they're stationed in an area where they cannot get an abortion that the Pentagon should facilitate a way for them to get that abortion. Senator Tommy Tuberville is taking a lot of heat over this. Because he said, listen, I told the Pentagon I was going to do this, if they made this move. He is now blocking hundreds of military promotions. Here's what the Secretary of Defense says about that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LLOYD AUSTIN\uc2 , U.S. DEFENSE SECRETARY: This is a national security issue. It's a readiness issue. And we shouldn't kid ourselves. I think any member of the \uc2 Senate Armed Services Committee\uc2  knows that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And Senate Minority Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , when he was asked about this said, "I don't support putting a hold on military nominations." Do you?\par \par \uc2 COTTON: Well, Shannon, the Secretary of Defense and the President thinks that this is a readiness issue, and they want to find the responsible party, they should look in the mirror. They're the ones who are violating the law and using taxpayer funds to pay for abortion tourism. Just let me give an example of how extreme this policy is. A young airman at Little Rock Air Force Base could walk in and demand three weeks of uncharged paid leave, and get travel and meals and lodging paid for to go get an elective abortion. Another airman could walk in and say my mother and my father just unexpectedly died. Can I have leave to go to their funeral. That would be charged leave that comes out of their annual allotment and they will get no expenses paid. I think most Americans agree that that is an insane policy. Again, the arm -- the military should not be paying for abortion tourism.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So what should women who are in uniform in a place where they cannot access abortion? Because they are there on orders. What should they do?\par \par \uc2 COTTON: Well, if they want to take that step, they have 30 days of annual leave. It shouldn't be taxpayer funds, giving them three weeks of paid uncharged leave and then also paying for travel and lodging and meal. Something that we don't even give our troops when they have a parent die or a sibling die, or a beloved grandparent die.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: How does this end?\par \par \uc2 COTTON: Well, should end with \uc2 Lloyd Austin\uc2  and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  agreeing to follow the law and rescinding their policy.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: They don't sound like they're inching towards that at all. I mean, does Senator Tuberville have enough pressure on him that he rethinks where he's at?\par \par \uc2 COTTON: Well, again, if \uc2 Lloyd Austin\uc2  and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  thinks this is a threat to our national security, the simplest thing to do would be to revert to the same practices we had had for decades. The same practice that we had last year after the Supreme Court reversed Roe v. Wade, and resend this unlawful policy on abortion tourism.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, let's talk about cocaine at the White House. You heard what John Kirby had to say. They take this very seriously. He's not happy that they haven't found answers either. But they said, you know, the Secret Service says, we sent this stuff to the \uc2 FBI\uc2  lab. We got no latent fingerprints. We did not get enough DNA. They said insufficient DNA. They also say there were no surveillance cameras in the area that would have caught this. They end with this, without physical evidence, the investigation will not be able to single out a person of interest from the hundreds of individuals who pass through the vestibule, where the cocaine was discovered, the Secret Service investigation is closed due to a lack of physical evidence. I know that you reached out directly to the Secret Service. You demanded answers by Friday afternoon, what have you gotten?\par \par \uc2 COTTON: We've got no answers. That's not surprising. The Secret Service is a troubled agencies, has long had challenges. It probably needs new leadership. I take this very seriously. I mean, what if this was anthrax? This is supposed to be the most secure building in the world. If the Secret Service closed down this investigation after just a few days, it's my understanding, they barely conducted any interviews.\par \par \uc2 I don't think they interviewed the president's son who's a known cocaine addict. This is like if Hamburglar lived in the White House and all the hamburgers disappeared. And they said they didn't have any suspects or no one they could question. They know who went through those doors so they can interview them as well. If the President were really serious about it, he could demand everybody that goes through those doors, submit to a drug test. You can use hair testing samples and identify anyone who's used cocaine in the last few months. Those are the steps you would take if you took this seriously. This just seems like another Biden cover up.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well. And to be clear, the White House has said that \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  was not there at the time that this was discovered. The days around that apparently were searched as well. So we'll wait and see.\par \par \uc2 Is there any other body, I mean, are there members of Congress who have said they may have some oversight to launch some kind of investigation, would that go anywhere?\par \par \uc2 COTTON: Well, this is part of the oversight that we conduct on the Judiciary Committee also on the Homeland Security Committee. I suspect Republican -- the Republican majority in the House will take this seriously. I don't think the Senate Democrats will and add this to one of the growing numbers of investigation into cover ups related to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and his family.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, let us know if you get anything from Secret Service on your request.\par \par \uc2 OK. Also \uc2 FBI\uc2  Director \uc2 Christopher Wray\uc2 , testified this week on the Hill and he was pressed on a number of things and admitted that a number of businesses do regularly turn over information on U.S. customers without warrants, "all the time." Here's part of his interaction with Representative \uc2 Thomas Massie\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 THOMAS MASSIE\uc2 , (R) KENTUCKY CONGRESSMAN: \uc2 George Hill\uc2 , former FBI Supervisory Intelligence Analyst in the Boston Field Office told us that the \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  with no legal process was -- gave to the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , gun purchase records with -- with no geographical boundaries for anybody that was a \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  customer. Is that true?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER WRAY\uc2 , FBI DIRECTOR: A number of business community partners all the time, including financial institutions share information with us about possible criminal activity. And my understanding is that that's fully lawful.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, we're both lawyers. Do you think it's fully lawful? And more importantly, should it be?\par \par \uc2 COTTON: Well, I think the second question is a key point whether it is or is not. I don't think most Americans expect their banks to be handing over records of their purchases without any kind of probable cause or even reasonable suspicion. I think the conversation that happened there on that tape and in the hearing to, contrast what we're just talking about the Secret Service investigation in the White House.\par \par \uc2 The government will dedicate thousands and thousands of agents to try to find every grandmother with a red MAGA hat on who was in Washington, D.C. on January 6, that they'll shut down the investigation of cocaine at the White House, and just a few days. Look, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  had problems under \uc2 Jim Comey\uc2 . That's why he was rightly fired. \uc2 Christopher Wray\uc2  was hired to help clean it up, especially to reform its culture at the top. Unfortunately, it hasn't happened. You see -- you still see \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents, threatening that traditional Catholics can be domestic extremists or conducting raids at the home of pro-life activist, for the grave crime of singing hymns in front of abortion clinics. There's a lot of reform that needs to happen at the \uc2 FBI\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BREAM: To be fair, the Director did say that that memo on Catholic churches was pulled. I know that the House is pushing to talk to the people who are behind it, because they want to know how it ever got there. I don't know if we'll ever get testimony from them.\par \par \uc2 COTTON: Well, I think what worries most Americans, at least most Republicans and conservatives is it seems like every incidents of misconduct or abuse or mistake for which the \uc2 FBI\uc2  is to apologize is always targeting people in the center right. It's never targeting people in the center left.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Let's talk about another hot button issue. You've introduced a bill that would give parents more rights, more information, if say their child at school is considering going through gender transitioning, what the school has to disclose to parents, those kinds of things. But a GOP appointed federal judge just days ago, ruled against a parent in California who had this situation with an elementary aged child. The school did not tell them what was going on. The judge said this, "The issue before this court is not whether it is a good idea for school districts to notify parents of a minor's gender identity and receive consent before using alternative names and pronouns, but whether the U.S. Constitution mandates such parental authority. This Court holds that it does not."\par \par \uc2 I don't imagine you're going to get a lot of Democrats support on your bill. But if you do, will it survive legal scrutiny? This judge says if it's based on the U.S. Constitution, no.\par \par \uc2 COTTON: Well, so I think that's in conflict with a state law in which parents are trying to say that they have an inherent right under the Constitution to control the upbringing of their child. The Supreme Court has recognized such right in some cases. But my law would simply say that state law cannot keep from parents the fact that their kid may be trying to transition or maybe have teachers and counselors influence them. I mean, schools can't even give kids a Tylenol without getting permission from their parents. They shouldn't be able to change their gender without notifying their parents. I think that's basic common stance that every parent would agree with.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Do you think you could get some Democrat votes?\par \par \uc2 COTTON: I would hope so. I would hope that most Democrats that most sane Americans would think that parents should have a say over what sex their kid is. But, you know, in these days the Democrats are kind of very focused on the extremist elements of their base, so I'm not sure.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We'll follow the legislation. Senator, thank you.\par \par \uc2 COTTON: Thank you, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Is the bite administration making an end run around Republicans and the Supreme Court to wipe out billions of dollars in student loan debt? Is it legal, we'll bring in the Senate panel to discuss whether that move could galvanize younger voters, again.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MARJORIE TAYLOR GREENE\uc2 , (R) GEORGIA CONGRESSWOMAN: Have they drug tested this list of 500 potential suspects that brought an illegal substance of drug, cocaine, into the White House? Their answer was no and that they're unwilling to do so.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE RASKIN\uc2 , (D) MARYLAND CONGRESSMAN: And if there were a small amount of marijuana or cocaine found somewhere in the Capitol complex, we would not want to drug test everybody who works here.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Mix reaction from lawmakers after the Secret Service announced its investigation into who brought cocaine into the White House is over without naming a suspect.\par \par \uc2 It is time now for our Sunday group. Politico Congressional Reporter Olivia Beavers. \uc2 Kevin Walling\uc2 , a former 2020 Biden Campaign Surrogate. \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  Columnist, Marc Thiessen. And Mary Katharine Ham, Columnist for OutKick.\par \par \uc2 All right, welcome, everybody. You've heard our conversation about what went down at the White House and the lack of any name or conclusion to it. Well, the conclusion has happened. I found this interesting. \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2  reporter Jason Leopold, did a Freedom of Information Act request trying to get information from Secret Service. They sent him a letter saying this. We regret to inform you that we cannot comply. The file is being exempted since disclosure could reasonably be expected to interfere with enforcement proceedings. But, Mary Katharine, I don't know that they're going to be any enforcement proceedings, I'm not sure.\par \par \uc2 MARY KATHARINE HAM, OUTKICK COLUMNIST: I'm going to use a phrase borrowed from my liberal colleagues from the Bush -- from the Trump administration, which is this is not normal. This is not normal. And that phrase was used rightly, I think sometimes to say, you should be more appalled by this. And I think the Biden administration should be a bit more appalled by this. I think if they wanted to know, they would know who the perpetrator was here. I think it is a big deal despite my libertarian streak, to have hard drugs inside the White House. I think in all likelihood, it is a staff member or a family member that is to be protected.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and Kevin, I mean, it just leaves speculation rampant if we don't get a name, better for them if they actually got to someone?\par \par \uc2 KEVIN WALLING\uc2 , FORMER 2020 BIDEN SURROGATE: Yes, and certainly, I mean, we've been talking about it nonstop for about two weeks or so. But, you know, Admiral Kirby said, you know, the Secret Service is the lead investigative agency on this. You know, there was just little evidence in terms of the physical evidence with that baggie. We -- we've all been through that White House entrance. There are not cameras inside for security reasons, and maybe the Secret Service will readjust some of their security procedures.\par \par \uc2 But I'm a Democrat, you know, as a surrogate for President Biden. This is certainly concerning. And certainly we have more questions than answers at this point. I think we'll likely move on as a country to bigger and more interesting and important issues, but certainly troubling.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and we already talked about last week about how the President has made clear to his staff. He doesn't want to hear anything that is about Hunter. He doesn't want Hunter to be dragged in and anything. He also doesn't want to be told, don't have Hunter front and center at big events, his family. This is the way things are going to be.\par \par \uc2 Well, the Axios now follows up on that by talking about this with the headline Old Yeller saying that the President is cranky, and has privately a very bad temper. It says Biden has such a quick trigger temper that some aides try to avoid meeting alone with him, "No one is safe,' said one administration official." My question mark is like, why are we now getting these reports from inside? Why are we now starting to get these negative leaks about the President?\par \par \uc2 MARC THIESSEN, WASHINGTON POST COLUMNIST: Well, apparently Old Yeller isn't too upset about the cocaine in the White House. I think that there's a -- there's a concern about the President's campaign. He's -- he's -- this is day 907 of the Biden presidency. And he is, according to the 538 average, the most unpopular president in the history of the country going back to \uc2 Harry Truman\uc2 , except for \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2 , at this point in his presidency. And he's -- 7 in 10 people think that he's too old to do the job. And his -- apparently his successor, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is less popular than he is. So this is a problem. If -- if they weren't convinced that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was turning onto the nomination, I think there'd be panic in the White House right now and in the Democratic Party.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, CNN is reporting on some of that saying that there are people that are reaching out to other potential alternatives. They say conversations keep happening, quiet whispers on the sidelines of events, texts, emails, furtive phone calls, as top Democrats and donors reach out to those seen as possible replacement presidential candidates, because they feel like time is already running out, and that the lack of the more robust campaign activity they want to see is a sign that his heart,' meaning the President's, 'isn't really in it." Olivia?\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA BEAVERS, POLITICO CONGRESSIONAL REPORTER: I mean, so I think that the underlying fear there, the Biden supporters are dismissing that the Biden's not going to be running. But I think the underlying fear is that Biden's not putting all of his in, all of his energy into this reelection campaign. That report talked about how they do not have key staff in early election states. And he doesn't really -- he hasn't been building out his infrastructure. He just got his first fundraising numbers since he -- since he's -- he basically launched his campaign, reelection campaign, and they are not as strong compared to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  when he did his and President Obama years back. So I think this -- this worry them attempting to say, OK, we're going to be looking for alternatives. If you do not get going, but you need to start going.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, who are we talking about, \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2 ? Governor Pritzker, Governor Whitmer?\par \par \uc2 HAM: We have two advanced aged men who are likely to be the nominees, who are running on inevitability. And that is inherently, to borrow another phrase, a low energy way of running. And it does not inspire a bunch of confidence. I think Biden, some low energy problems, just in general, it's creating -- it's a market failure. And that's why these sorts of third- party buzzes popping up. It's why this discussion of maybe an alternative to Biden is popping up because people's -- people's desires are not being served here, and certainly not energetically.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: They were happy, though, to tout their fundraising numbers. This is a bright spot for the campaign, $72 million raised in Q2. The Biden-Harris team, they say dramatically outraised the announced totals from every GOP candidate running for president, including \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  by more than 2:1 and \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  by more than 3:1. So Kevin, that gives you some smiles this morning?\par \par \uc2 WALLING: Those are incredibly strong numbers. And one of the more interesting things I think coming out of that report is 30% of those donors are new since 2020 to the President's campaign, nearly 400,000 Americans contributed over 600,000 times to this campaign in -- in Q2. Those are great numbers from every single state in this country. And again, as you just said, 3:1 DeSantis, 2:1 \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And to Olivia's point that gives a lot of cash now to build out this campaign effort in Wilmington.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Marc seems a little skeptical.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Yeah, it's --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: It's a lot of money?\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: It's a lot of money. It's propping up. It's like weekend at Bernie's, it's propping up a guy who is, you know, days is declining.\par \par \uc2 WALLING: It was a great movie.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Money is important. But, you know, look if you just look at how much \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has declined in the last 18 months, you know, I remember when I worked in the Bush administration, you look at the pictures of Bush on the day, he took office in the day he left office and how old he got into the job really, you know, wears you down.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: You pay dollar and all of that.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: If you can get pictures of the day, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  took his -- took the oath of office compared to today. It's like eight years of -- of fresher. How's it going to be an 18 months from now? I mean, he literally, you know, we could get to the Election Day and he could look so and feeble that he literally Americans are going to conclude he can't do the job.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, he says he's getting the job done when it comes to student loan debt forgiveness after the Supreme Court struck down $430 billion plan. They've come up with a new one. Wall Street Journal says this, Biden administration to wipe out $39 billion in student loans for 800,000 borrowers. So student loan borrowers on income-driven repayment plans who've made 20 or 25 years of payments, we'll get their remaining balances wiped out in coming weeks. The Education Department said that inaccurate payment counts in the past resulted in borrowers losing progress to a loan forgiveness.\par \par \uc2 Olivia, I keep trying to press for more details on exactly how this is going to work. But they say it's an administrative fix to the past. Just conveniently, though, benefits 800,000 people who might be voting next time?\par \par \uc2 BEAVERS: Yes, and it comes after this -- excuse me, the Supreme Court ruled against their previous attempts. They're doing this under the Higher Education Act of 1965. And they're claiming, as you said, trying to fix some issues in the past, and deficiencies, issues with the loan servicers that the administration worked with. But Republicans are out there saying you're buying votes, this is your attempt to pay off people and Democrats are saying no, we're just keeping our promises. So we need to be looking at what more information the administration can provide, in terms of why they are giving this -- where they're actually issues. And I think that that's something we're going to be seeing unfold going forward.\par \par \uc2 HAM: I like that these adjustments are based on the Federal Department of Education saying I'm sorry, we can't count so we're going count that.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 WALLING: This is a bipartisan problem.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Student loan borrowers are demanding a recount. But let me -- let me just really quickly, Congresswoman Lisa McClain, who is a Republican, went to the floor to blast this on Friday and says this in a tweet. Biden's unconstitutional loan forgiveness scheme is a slap in the face to hardworking taxpayers that have dutifully paid off their student loans. Rest assured come hell or high water. I will fight this blatant overreach until the end. Marc, I'm assuming this thing ends up in court?\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Absolutely, it does. And it's issues 100% right. But also, here's the thing, this is only 39 billion compared to the 500 billion to a trillion dollars of the first plan. So it's peanuts financially, but it's good. We can give it to people who have paid off their loans from 20 to 25 years. So you're talking to people who are 40, 45 years old. The reason they were doing this in the first place was to energize young voters, right? They're not going to get a penny out of this. This is -- this is for older voters. This is a giveaway to Gen X and older millennials when their -- when their problem is with -- with Gen Z.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, let's not call people in their 40s older.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 WALLING: Be careful, Marc.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I'm (inaudible).\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, thank you panel. Take a quick break here. But when we come back, increasing anxiety on the left, the third-party candidate could spell trouble for President Biden. That possibility inching closer to reality and new legal headaches for former President Trump as the Justice Department presses to try him on those charges related to classified documents before the election. They're also talking to his inner circle about what he really thought if he had won or lost in the days after the 2020 election, that's next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HARRY HOGAN (ph): Well, I think No Labels has no interest in being a spoiler. They're not going to start a third party. They're not going to run a race unless they believe they can win.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Former Maryland Governor \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2 , who is national co-chair of the bipartisan group No Labels, on its effort to set up a third party unity ticket to take on the two major party nominees.\par \par \uc2 We are back with the panel.\par \par \uc2 OK, so they had their brand-new "Common Sense" booklet they put out this morning. This is what they say from the - No Labels. American politics no longer feels like a battle of ideas. It feels more like a war of two tribes bent on each other's destruction. The way out is for America's growing common sense majority to come together in advance of the '24 presidential election to demand something better.\par \par \uc2 Kevin, the left worries that that's going to hurt the president, it's going to help, if he's the nominee, President Trump, and put him right back in the White House.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN WALLING\uc2 , FORMER 2020 BIDEN SURROGATE AND DEMOCRATIC STRATEGIST: Certainly I think they're worried about this spoiler issue with No Labels.\par \par \uc2 Interestingly enough, one of my first jobs in politics was the national field director for No Labels. I helped launch the organization back in 2010, 2011. And I think, to your point with the "Common Sense" agenda, I hope they return to their roots, which is more policy focused.\par \par \uc2 You know, we were founded early on -- No Labels was founded early on to beat back against the Tea Party, right, and to support moderates, especially in tough Republican races. I hope they return to that mentality, supporting bipartisan solutions. You've got a president you can work with who's one of the most bipartisan presidents we've seen in a generation. And I think, going down this route, in terms of the presidency aspect of it, this nominating convention, all the dark money, you know, racing $70 million, we don't know who's funding this effort. I think there's way more questions than answers. But there is a real fear among Democrats that they will be a spoiler.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " is reporting on this group that may include former House Democratic Leader Dick Gephardt trying to stop No Label, saying this, the new group has already commissioned private polling showing that a generic, moderate, independent, third party candidate would pull more votes away from President Biden than former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Olivia, they are worried.\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA BEAVERS, CONGRESSIONAL REPORTER, "POLITICO": Yes. And I think you're seeing this kind of across both the Democratic and the Republican campaign in different ways. And let me explain.\par \par \uc2 With No Labels, their fear is that if they pick someone who's a centrist, a moderate, an independent, that that will hurt Biden if he's up against Trump or DeSantis. DeSantis, meanwhile, his campaign is fearing that Tim Scott is pulling away power from him when he's running against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And so both sort of sides are trying to figure out, how do we get rid of this - this threat against up. And that's why you're seeing Gephardt and his campaign swinging at them and No Labels saying, well, you're denying voters options.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, Marc, you wrote about this recently in "The Post."\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: I did. I think - I think No Labels could win.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: You do? Well, I mean, there's polling that -\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: I - I think - I think - I mean it's a long shot but I think it's possible.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But there's polling that shows, I mean, 40 plus percent of Americans are willing to consider a credible third party option.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: So here - so here's why I think it could win. \uc2 Ross Perot\uc2 , who was the most successful candidate - third party candidate yet had a -- never polled above 38 percent. Right now, according to the Harris X (ph) poll, 59 percent of Americans say they would consider a third party ticket, including 59 percent of Democrats, 53 percent of Republicans, and 70 percent of independents.\par \par \uc2 Why is that? It's because the vast majority of Americans have said very clearly, we do not want a Trump/Biden rematch. But they're - it looks like we're heading towards that. What do you do if you're in that position. If you're a Democrat, you're not going to vote for Trump because you don't want Biden. If you're a Republican you're sure - you know, you're not going to vote for Biden. So you - you have no safe harbor.\par \par \uc2 There's a desire for a safe harbor. And so if they put forward a serious ticket with like say Joe Manchin and a serious Republican on it, I think a lot of people will run to that safe harbor, more so than- it's still - you know, it's still a longshot, but I think this would be - this would actually be a very serious, serious presidential campaign.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, and they've got an event tomorrow with Senator Manchin and with former governor and ambassador John Huntsman in New Hampshire, a key early state tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 HAM: Yes, there was a nonprofit effort in 2012 to do something similar. I believe it was called America Elects. And it came four years too early because in 2016 a bunch of people wanted a safe harbor, right?\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HAM: So it's interesting that this may be sort of coinciding at a correct time this time around.\par \par \uc2 And I think, look, people's needs are not being met. They are crying out for something different. I like that the Democrats have raised fresh-faced Dick Gephardt to front this effort, to give people a little spice - a little spice in this race.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I feel young again.\par \par \uc2 HAM: But, no, people are saying, look, we want something different. And I can't be mad at expanded ballot access in these states, which is the tricky part of this and that they did in 2012.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, OK. It is. It takes a lot of work. But here's somebody else who also wants to run a third-party potential bid. He is running in the Green Party, Cornel West.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CORNEL WEST (G), 2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: So I'm just trying to speak the truth and pursue justice. I wish they would spend as much time focusing on the plight of poor and working people as they do focusing on the spoiler.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, DNC Chairman Jamie Harrison said, quote, this is not the time to play around on the margin, is what he told "The Hill." He's sounding the alarm, Kevin.\par \par \uc2 WALLING: Certainly. And I think, you know, Cornel West is also a fear among Democrats that he will be a spoiler as well.\par \par \uc2 We're facing, you know, an existential threat to this country if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is the likely nominee six months out from Iowa caucuses where he's leading the field. That is problematic. And anything that would disrupt President Biden's chance of beating \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  on that November ballot is something that Democrats, and I think the vast majority of Americans, are not interested in.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, we still have a very, very crowded GOP primary to get through. And NBC News has this memo that went out. It's confidential but it went to family and friends, a DeSantis memo, kind of saying, this is our way forward when he's not taking off in the polls the way that they had hoped.\par \par \uc2 Says, the memo comes at a time when DeSantis' political orbit for the first time has to respond to him being seriously punched in the mouth. Trump continues to pummel him, leaving his political team playing defense to prevent a donor exodus.\par \par \uc2 Marc.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Yes, I mean he's - his -- this is a guy who ran a perfect campaign for governor, like, you know, picture perfect and - and has come out of the box stumbling. He - look, he's still the most likely Republican to - bot beat \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . It's still early. The debates are a month away. The first vote is six months away. We haven't even started the game. So, this is the early stages.\par \par \uc2 But if a - but this is - you know, this Republican field, this is why, to some extent, why No Labels is a serious possibility because if Trump becomes the nominee there's a significant -- in 2016, the point you were making was, a lot of people who didn't like Trump still voted for Trump because they cared about the Supreme Court. The Supreme Court was on the ballot. Dobbs -- you know, what became Dobbs was on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 Ironically, Trump's success in transforming the Supreme Court frees a lot of Republicans to go and vote for a third party candidate because the Supreme Court isn't on the ballot right now and Dobbs is done and the issue has been put back to the states. So something -- the glue that was holding a lot of Trump skeptical Republicans to the Republican - to - to him doesn't exist anymore. And as long as they're abortion neutral on - on No Labels, they might get some Republican votes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and, Olivia, the DeSantis campaign is now having to deal with these reports that they've laid off some staffers -\par \par \uc2 BEAVERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But they say they're building a huge digital out - you know, output. That they've got tons of people on the ground, they're fleshing out in these early states. They feel very confident. They've got a lot of money on hand.\par \par \uc2 BEAVERS: They sounding very confident. I - but I think that that's not really matching with the reality of the memo that we were just talking about. They had a hard launch and they've had a shaky start. They've already had a fast burn, right, through staff as they realized that they were - I think there was about 90 staffers that they had and they realized that that was costing the campaign way too much money on the front end and now their focus is shifting to Iowa. That's their primary focus. They're not even worrying about super Tuesday states. They want to make sure that they can have a strong resurgence in the polls with the first few states.\par \par \uc2 And I also think he's - you know, there was an interview with him not too long ago where he blamed the media saying, oh, they just don't want me to be elected. Well, if that's how you want to do it, I think you should be looking at \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  going after your campaign and figuring out how to break more into his base. And that's where he can probably be making a stronger pitch to voters.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, and some of the reporting says too that he's preparing, DeSantis, to do a lot more mainstream media interviews and to kind of go into the arena and get attention there.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the president, the former president who wants to be president again, is dealing with all kinds of legal issues, which only seem to be helping his campaign, but they are very real legal issues.\par \par \uc2 A couple of things. Prosecutors are talking to witnesses about whether he acknowledged that he had won or lost in the 2020, the days after the election. "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " says prosecutors don't need hard evidence of a defendant saying, I know that I'm breaking the law, but their cases are made stronger when they can produce evidence that the defendant knows there is no legal or factual basis for a claim but goes ahead with making it anyway.\par \par \uc2 Mary Katherine, they were talking to people like his son-in-law, \uc2 Jared Kushner\uc2 , \uc2 Hope Hicks\uc2  and others who would have been close to him and could say, did he really think he'd lost or not.\par \par \uc2 HAM: Well, and this is yet another one of those issues that, unfortunately for the rest of the GOP field in the primary perhaps cuts in Trump's favor because they feel like they're in opposition to Trump and, you know, we're going to join up behind him.\par \par \uc2 Look, I think, for DeSantis, he actually performs well in a debate sort of argument style. So I think sitting down with mainstream media that will give that to him is good. In opposition to Trump and his attack is where you need to be. That's the - that's the argument you need to be mounting but he has to grow momentum somewhere like Iowa, I think it helps, Trump giving himself more trouble, maybe going after the very popular governor of Iowa, \uc2 Kim Reynolds\uc2  -\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Very popular, yes.\par \par \uc2 HAM: Not the best step in that direction. So, early in the game.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, I mean, he's - he's up multiple of, you know, points there, 20 or 30 points in most - of the polling out of Iowa. But he also has to deal with this. His legal team is trying to push the document case, the Mar-a- Lago document case, this federal case, until after the election. But special counsel Jack Smith, and the DOJ, this week said, no, we know you're busy running for president, but this is their motion against delaying the trial. They say, many indicted defendants have demanding jobs that require a considerable amount of their time and energy or a significant amount of travel. The speedy trial (INAUDIBLE) contemplates no such factor as a basis for a continuance and the court should not indulge it here.\par \par \uc2 I mean, Marc, there's a lot of debate about whether it's better for him to get this over with before the trial, which, to me, as a lawyer, knowing everything involved, it seems almost impossible for that to happen, or is it better to have it behind him?\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: You know, it's - it's hard to say but this is why it's unprecedented to be -- for the administration of the president - the former president's political opponent to be indicting him and trying him in the midst of an election when he's the leading candidate to run against the president. This is - this is -- these are unprecedented legal questions. But this - it's unquestionably helping him politically. I mean before the Bragg indictment Trump was in the mid-40s, DeSantis was at 30. It was within striking distance. And as soon as that - as soon as that - as that first indictment happened, it tightened up. He's now in the 50s and he's stayed there and more indictments, more - more rally effect.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, and great for his fundraising, too.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: That's for sure.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, panel, we've got to leave it there. Please come back next Sunday. We'll see you then.\par \par \uc2 A reminder, Fox News Channel hosts the first 2024 Republican primary debate just five weeks from now in Milwaukee. Bret Baier and Martha MacCallum will moderate. Candidates take the stage 9:00 p.m. Eastern. Who makes it? We'll see.\par \par \uc2 Up next, a preview of my interview with a familiar face about persevering when things get really tough.\par \par \uc2 We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: A quick note. My podcast drops this morning. If you've ever felt like giving up on a dream, do not miss my conversation with Raymond Arroyo on this weeks "Livin' the Bream." He's got a new book on the amazing life story of Thomas Edison and what drove him past many failures and toward inventions that changed the world.\par \par \uc2 Plus, you can here all of today's program on the FOX NEWS SUNDAY podcast. Download and subscribe at foxnewspodcast.com, or wherever you like to get your podcasts.\par \par \uc2 And do not miss a single moment of FOX NEWS SUNDAY. Set your DVR to record the show each week on your local Fox station or Sundays at 2:00 p.m. Eastern on Fox News Channel.\par \par \uc2 That's it for today. Thank you for joining us. I'm Shannon Bream. Have a great week. We'll see you next FOX NEWS SUNDAY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 Document FOXN000020230716ej7g00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 59-Year-Old Rex Heuermann Faces Six Murder Counts; House Narrowly Passes Divisive Defense Bill; \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  Opponents Criticize Economic Performance; Historic Heat Wave Scorches Southwest; Cooling Shelters Opening Across Las Vegas; Transgender Females Barred From Women's International Events; State Laws On Dealing With Transgender Students; Political Influence \uc2 Chicago Teachers Union\uc2  On Recent Mayor Election Examined; Wait Times For Patients To See Health Care Professional Increasing In U.S.; Analysts Explain How Gas Prices Fluctuate Around U.S. Election Time And Their Influence On Election Outcomes; House Republican Pass National Defense Spending Bill; Republican Strategists Suggest Candidates Focus On School And Education Related Issues In Upcoming Election\b0\par\par\uc2 Shannon Bream, Aishah Hasnie, Lauren Green, Chad Pergram, Max  Gorden, William La Jeunesse, Bret Baier, Mike Tobin, Alicia Acuna \par \uc2 8,176 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 July 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 EMILY COMPAGNO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: I would love for you guys to listen. It's really awesome.\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: You look so like detective in that.\par \par \uc2 COMPAGNO: I know. And I'm doing the arms crossed.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: I can't believe (INAUDIBLE) slip by. That's it for us. We'll see you Monday, have a nice weekend.\par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Good evening, welcome to Washington, I'm Shannon Bream in for Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, a suspect is in custody in connection to a series of murders along a Long Island Beach more than a decade ago. He's a Manhattan architect who is allegedly implicated in potentially up to 10 killings.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Lauren Green starts us off tonight from New York. Hello, Lauren.\par \par \uc2 LAUREN GREEN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CHIEF RELIGION CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Shannon. Well, you know, the case has been an unsolved mystery for years, a string of deaths known as the Gilgo Beach murders, named for the sandy shores of Long Island where several bodies were found. Today, police finally identified a suspect.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 RODNEY HARRISON, SUFFOLK COUNTY POLICE COMMISSIONER: Rex Heuermann is a demon that walks among us. A predator that ruin families. If not for the members of this task force, he will still be on the streets today.\par \par \uc2 GREEN (voice over): 59-year-old Rex Heuermann is married and has two children. He was cuffed and surrounded by police as he headed to court where he pleaded not guilty to six counts of murder.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL BROWN\uc2 , DEFENSE ATTORNEY: The only thing I can tell you that he did say, as he was in tears was I didn't do this.\par \par \uc2 GREEN (voice over): He was arrested Thursday night not far from his architectural design office on Manhattan's 5th Avenue and early this morning, a swarm of police vehicles surrounded his home in Massapequa Park on Long Island not far from where the bodies were found, law enforcement searching for evidence and towing away cars.\par \par \uc2 The charges are connected to the killings of three women and Heuermann is the prime suspect in a fourth murder, but he could be linked to as many as 10 killings.\par \par \uc2 The investigation began in 2010 with the search for missing 23-year-old Shannan Gilbert. Police discovered the bodies of four other women hidden along a remote beach highway. 24-year-old Melissa Barthelemy, 25-year-old Maureen Brainard-Barnes, 22-year-old Megan Waterman and 27-year-old Amber Lynn Costello, now known as the Gilgo four, all reportedly working as \uc2 Craigslist\uc2  escorts.\par \par \uc2 Court documents show Heuermann has been on investigator's radar for months. In January they found DNA on pizza crust that connected him to the murders.\par \par \uc2 Documents also show he used burner phones to contact sex partners and conducted online searches related to sex and prostitution, as well as "violent sadistic child pornography".\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GREEN (on camera): Heuermann is in custody held without bail. The bail hearing is scheduled for August 1st. If convicted, you could face multiple life sentences. And investigators say more charges could be filed, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, Lauren Green, thank you very much for getting us up to date.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, Suffolk County District Attorney Raymond Tierney. Great to have you with us, Mr. Tierney.\par \par \uc2 Your press conference today was so detailed in explaining how all of this came together, you decided to put together a massive task force, it was early last year, February March of 2022, that this suspect's name came to light.\par \par \uc2 So, you've been building the case quietly with hundreds of subpoenas and warrants. How does it feel to get to today?\par \par \uc2 RAYMOND TIERNEY, SUFFOLK COUNTY DISTRICT ATTORNEY: I think it's satisfying. I think all of our task force partners realize that there's still a lot of work to be done. But considering you know, when we identified him, it's a great -- it's a good day.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, you also gave us a lot of detail about things that he would do that he was searching for family members of the victims that he would taunt them with phone calls that he was trying to locate them. Have you ever seen a case like this? And do you think there was something about his mindset, and these are all allegations but that he didn't think he would eventually be caught?\par \par \uc2 TIERNEY: I think that you can tell there were you know, at various times during the investigation, and this was, you know, way before, you know, I got involved in the investigation, you can see he was very confident. And that would -- that confident -- confidence would manifest itself with taunting phone calls or similar type behavior.\par \par \uc2 I think that as the case got more and more notoriety, I think he became less and less comfortable. I think that given the amount of searches he had on our task force, I think he was concerned.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, so you use a grand jury, obviously, to get to this point, you talked about how important secrecy was so he wouldn't be tipped off to exactly what was going on. Go on about his daily life.\par \par \uc2 But you talk about a couple of things. First of all, cell phone evidence how important that was in -- I mean, essentially finding a needle in a haystack.\par \par \uc2 TIERNEY: Yes, I think that, you know, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and other phone analysts. They're really skilled in what they can do. And so they can really analyze phone records and really isolate areas or location areas where, you know, suspicious activity occurs. And that really allows you to capture or identify suspects.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, and I remember you were talking about there were sort of a box location, one that was made around the area where he lived. And when that was -- that he wound up being in an area where he worked as well. And that helped you guys lead your investigation to him.\par \par \uc2 And there's also the issue of DNA evidence because these bodies were found more than a decade ago, you did have material and evidence. But it wasn't until some advances in DNA testing and technology that gave you the extra evidence that you were looking for, explain for us.\par \par \uc2 TIERNEY: So, with regard to the four victims, we were able to obtain five hairs of significance. But because the bodies had been exposed to the elements for so long, that DNA was degraded.\par \par \uc2 So, when people think of DNA, it's new killer DNA. That's the traditional DNA analysis that is done. But as the science advanced, and although the hairs were degraded for new killer DNA, we were able to use them for mitochondrial DNA.\par \par \uc2 So, we were able to use that technology, but we still had to develop a suspect. And that's where the phone evidence and the evidence with regard to the car and some of his other activities came in.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: How broad do you think his scope of victims could be? And do you anticipate more charges?\par \par \uc2 TIERNEY: Well, you know, as I said at the press conference, you know, what we did was we identify -- you know, I took office January of 2022. In March of 2022, he was identified as a suspect in these -- in these murders for the first time.\par \par \uc2 During that time, we downloaded an incredible amount of information. Today was a watershed day because not only did we arrest him, we also executed a number of search warrants.\par \par \uc2 So, what we're going to do is we're going to download that information from the search warrants. And we're going to analyze that evidence and you know, follow it wherever it goes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: In many cases -- in many ways, this case really just getting started. Suffolk County District Attorney Raymond Tierney, thank you. We know this is a day of mixed emotions for all the investigators, the family especially. We hope that this will bring them some closure. Thank you for the hard work, sir.\par \par \uc2 TIERNEY: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, the House has passed a controversial defense bill that includes several amendments pushed by conservatives to eliminate what they call wokeness from the military, and now moves on to the Democratic controlled Senate where it faces a whole lot of scrutiny. Senior Congressional Correspondent Chad Pergram is on Capitol Hill tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): There wasn't much debate about missiles and troops but there was an earful about abortion, and so called woke policy.\par \par \uc2 REP. STEVE SCALISE (R-LA): It was a massive rebuke of that far left ideology in the push that we've seen under the Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): The House narrowly approved the annual defense policy bill 219 to 210. Only four Republicans voted nay and only four Democrats voted yes. More Democrats would have been yeas had the bill not veered from the center.\par \par \uc2 REP. PAT RYAN (D-NY): The far right hijacked this, hijacked our national security.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): Republicans uprooted the Pentagon's policy allowing service members to travel across state lines for abortions and block transgender surgeries.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 JAMAAL BOWMAN\uc2  (D-NY): It is a culture war document, it's more focused on attacking trans people and black people and women and DEI in so called wokism.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): House Speaker Kevin McCarthy embraced the bill for gutting, "rRadical Pentagon policy".\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): We don't want Disneyland to train our military.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): The House rejected an effort by Georgia Republican \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  to curb money for Ukraine. But Greene still voted yes, because McCarthy tapped her to negotiate the bill with the Senate.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MARJORIE TAYLOR GREENE\uc2  (R-GA): That allows me to go and be a voice in the room to talk about removing the money going to Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): Democrats note that the House bill isn't the final say.\par \par \uc2 REP. HAKEEM JEFFRIES (D-NY): We are going to cut out the cancer that the extreme MAGA Republicans have put in the National Defense Authorization Act.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): Democrats will target the abortion language.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 ADAM SMITH\uc2  (D-WA): If the Republicans insist that it has to be in it, then we will not have an NDA this year.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): But House conservatives warn the bill better not change too much.\par \par \uc2 REP. CHIP ROY (R-TX): Striking that balance ought to be a whole lot closer our position than just defaulting to whatever the Senate says.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (on camera): Conservatives are watching McCarthy closely, especially after he cut a deal with Democrats on the debt ceiling, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, Chad Pergram, thank you very much, always got us covered on the Hill.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: President Biden appears to be getting significant financial support for his reelection bid. New figures indicate the incumbent has more cash on hand at this point than any other Democrats ever.\par \par \uc2 Congressional Correspondent Aishah Hasnie is at the White House to look at the numbers tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Mr. President, when are you returning to the campaign trail?\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): President Biden ignoring Fox's questions as he starts his weekend at Camp David, fresh off the heels of a meeting with NATO the White House is touting as a success.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We need a bigger room.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice over): And team Biden-Harris boasting a whopping $72 million raised in the campaign's first quarter with 77 million in cash on hand.\par \par \uc2 JIM MESSINA, FORMER OBAMA CAMPAIGN MANAGER: There's a whole bunch of Democrats this morning taking a big sigh of relief.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice over): Relief after a CNN report reveals some donors were getting anxious about Biden's slow campaign start, even looking for a replacement.\par \par \uc2 KARL ROVE, FORMER GEORGE W. BUSH DEPUTY CHIEF OF STAFF: He's weak. He's old, he's unpopular. Significant number of Americans do not want him to run again.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice over): Biden's Achilles heel continues to be the economy despite cooling inflation recent polling shows the president is still struggling to convince Americans Bidenomics is working.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT F. KENNEDY JR. (D), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: I'm hearing not only inflation but interest rates, the Feds way of dealing with inflation is actually worse for a lot of Americans.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice over): GOP front runner former President Trump telling Fox's \uc2 Maria Bartiromo\uc2 , Americans wouldn't have to worry about a recession if he wins.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: If there's even a thought that Trump is getting elected, you will never have a recession because people will start investing money.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice over): Meanwhile, the administration still pursuing a campaign promise for giving 39 billion more in student loan debt for more than 800,000 borrowers. Two weeks after the Supreme Court struck down their massive bailout program.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE PARKER, CHIEF COMMUNICATIONS OFFICER, JOB CREATORS NETWORK: He's committed to more government overreach in forgiving $39 billion on the backs of taxpayers.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (on camera): And Shannon, the White House did not offer a press briefing today or yesterday when the Secret Service announced it was closing its investigation into that bag of cocaine.\par \par \uc2 One Republican calling that probe a clown show. Fox did try to ask the president about this today when he departed the White House. He did not answer, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. So, Aishah, also we got some developments in that court battle over government contacts with social media companies. A judge had put something on hold there, now what?\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: OK, so this is a temporary legal win for the Biden administration here over it's communication with big tech to moderate their contacts.\par \par \uc2 So, here's what's happening, a federal judge last week limited that contact after states like Missouri and Louisiana, basically said that efforts to police any online contact for disinformation amounted to censorship.\par \par \uc2 But a federal appeals court has now reversed this Shannon, and allowing those communications to continue, at least for now while the litigation proceeds, it really is turning into a free speech issue that we expect to possibly go all the way to the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, we'll be watching for that. A bright spot for the administration on a very rainy stormy day there at the White House. Aishah, thank you.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Very stormy.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, do so safely.\par \par \uc2 All right, residents in North Dakota may get a chance to vote on an age limit for state congressional candidates. North Dakota Secretary of State is reviewing a proposed petition for a measure on next year's ballot. It would set the age limit at 80 years old. Petitioners need to gather more than 31,000 signatures in order to put that item on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 Stocks were mixed, the Dow gained to 114, the S&P 500 lost five, NASDAQ finished off 25.\par \par \uc2 For the week, the Dow was up 2-1/3 percentage points, the S&P 500 gained almost 2-1/2, NASDAQ rose 3-1/3.\par \par \uc2 Up next, a historic heatwave is gripping the Southwest, we'll have a live report from Las Vegas.\par \par \uc2 And later, how teachers unions may be favoring social activism over education.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: The \uc2 Disney Corporation\uc2  has asked a Florida judge to dismiss a lawsuit against the company's efforts to neutralize a takeover of \uc2 Disney\uc2 's world -- Disney World's governing district by Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  and his appointees.\par \par \uc2 Disney\uc2  has accused the Republican presidential candidate of violating its free speech rights.\par \par \uc2 Triple digit temperatures are baking much of the Southwest today. Daily record highs are being threatened throughout the region over the weekend.\par \par \uc2 It could reach 129 degrees in Death Valley. Las Vegas is forecast for 117 on Sunday. Fox Weather correspondent Max Gorden is there right now. How you holding up, Max?\par \par \uc2 MAX GORDEN, FOX WEATHER FIELD CORRESPONDENT: Hey Shannon. Well, it is pretty darn hot here in Sin City. Feels like an oven just opened up right next to me. 110 degrees right now. But that are not nothing compared to what we're going to see later on this weekend. 117, potentially, 118. If we hit 118, that's going to be a new record for the city of Las Vegas.\par \par \uc2 Now, of course, all this heat means that power consumption is going to be spiking with all the air conditioning units going on blast. Now, \uc2 NV Energy\uc2 , that's the power provider for the State of Nevada says that power demand could hit near all-time highs.\par \par \uc2 But they also say that no blackouts are expected and they're not even asking folks to cut back on electricity use. They're bringing on extra staff and anticipate that they'll be able to get out to any potential outages fast.\par \par \uc2 While \uc2 NV Energy\uc2  says they have increased generation in recent years between 25 to 30 percent of the energy that will be needed during this surge in demand will have to be purchased from the open market, from other states.\par \par \uc2 And even with the heat waves spanning across much of American Southern Tier, officials are confident that there will be enough power to go around.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RYAN ATKINS, VICE PRESIDENT, RESOURCE OPTIMIZATION, NV ENERGY: We are fully prepared and we're ready to handle this heat event. We maintain our generating fleet year-round to ensure that it's ready to handle these exact types of extreme situations.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GORDEN: More than 93 million Americans across the southern and western United States are baking, under dangerously hot temperatures.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, marked 14 consecutive days of temperatures above 110 degrees in Phoenix, Arizona. Death Valley forecasted by the National Weather Service to reach a new daily record of 129 degrees on Sunday.\par \par \uc2 Now, back here in Las Vegas, this heat can be deadly. according to local health officials. Since April, there have been seven deaths due to the heat here in Southern Nevada.\par \par \uc2 Now, \uc2 NV Energy\uc2 , the state's power provider says that if there are any blackouts, they're urging people to go to air-conditioned spaces. There are cooling centers that have been set up all across the Las Vegas Metro. Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Very serious situation and the heavens have opened here, Max, in Washington. So, all kinds of weather issues out there. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 GORDEN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Up next, parents rights. A judge in California makes a major ruling on what moms and dads are allowed to know about their own children.\par \par \uc2 First, here is what some of our Fox affiliates around the country are covering tonight.\par \par \uc2 Fox 32 in Chicago as Reverend \uc2 Jesse Jackson\uc2  says he is stepping down as president of the Rainbow PUSH Coalition. The 81-year-old says he will announce a successor within the next few weeks. Jackson was diagnosed with Parkinson's disease in 2017.\par \par \uc2 Fox 11 in Los Angeles says a woman is arrested in connection to the suspected fentanyl overdose death of Robert De Niro's grandson.\par \par \uc2 20-year-old Sophia Haley Marks was taken into custody Thursday in New York. Police say she sold drugs to 19-year-old Leandro De Niro-Rodriguez before he was found dead July 2nd in his Manhattan apartments.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Honolulu. One of the big stories there tonight from KHON. Preparation for the 46th Annual Prince Lot Hula Festival takes place tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 That event has been virtual for the past three years because of the pandemic, but it's back.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look, "OUTSIDE THE BELTWAY" from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Heavy rains and high winds at the White House tonight.\par \par \uc2 OK, the international governing body of cycling is banning transgender women, who've undergone male puberty from competing in the female category of competitive events.\par \par \uc2 The group is also renaming the male division as men slash open. The new rules go into effect July 17th.\par \par \uc2 Federal judge in California has ruled a law banning schools from notifying parents about their children's transgender or gender identity status is constitutional.\par \par \uc2 National correspondent William La Jeunesse, tells us what it means tonight from Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SHAWN HYLAND, DIRECTOR OF ADVOCACY, NEW JERSEY FAMILY POLICY CENTER: To keep parents in the dark, to lie to parents, and to view parents as a problem, is grossly irresponsible --\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM LA JEUNESSE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: From New Jersey to California.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: My daughter, the last two years has been asked for her pronouns on the first day of school.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: A simple question, do parents have a right to know if their child is changing his or her gender identity.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: To having teachers to confide in before my parents made me feel amazing.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: This week, a federal judge appointed by \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2 , upheld California's rule prohibiting teachers and counselors from telling parents when a child adopts a new gender identity.\par \par \uc2 AURORA REGINO, PARENT: I just want them to stop, stop keeping parents in the dark.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: Aurora Regino claimed the district interfered with her right to make informed decisions about her fifth grader, who at the time, lived as a girl at home, while using a boy's name at school.\par \par \uc2 REGINO: They were talking to my daughter about different support groups in town to help her with her transition, and then discuss breast binding with my daughter that I had no knowledge of.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: 17,000 schools in 37 states protect student privacy and stop officials from outing transitioning students to their parents.\par \par \uc2 BILL ESSAYLI, ASSEMBLYMAN, CALIFORNIA STATE: Never in the history of the United States have we said that kids have a right to privacy from their own parents.\par \par \uc2 DANIEL MORA, CALIFORNIA HIGH SCHOOL GRADUATE: This has nothing to do with a school issue, a transgender issue.\par \par \uc2 It is a parenting issue that don't create a say, space for their children to be who they are.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: Conservatives who brought the case are appealing to the Ninth Circuit.\par \par \uc2 ERIC SELL, INAUGURAL SHILLMAN LEGAL FELLOW, CENTER FOR AMERICAN LIBERTY: These schools are performing the psychological intervention on kids without their parents knowing about it. It's a clear constitutional violation.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE (on camera): Federal courts across the country are grappling with this question as red and blue state lawmakers go in different directions.\par \par \uc2 So, no surprise if this issue soon lands at the Supreme Court. Shannon?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We'll be watching. William, thank you.\par \par \uc2 So this evening we are going to look at and see what some see as the evolution of the Chicago teachers' union from a labor group to a political organization. Area Republicans say the union is more concerned with social activism than education. Senior correspondent \uc2 Mike Tobin\uc2  reports from Chicago.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 MIKE TOBIN\uc2 , FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Clear evidence of the clout of the Chicago teachers' union, the election of union organizer Brandon Johnson to the mayor's office.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Our brother, teacher, organizer.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN: When Johnson makes his public appearances, \uc2 CTU\uc2  President \uc2 Stacy Davis Gates\uc2  is often nearby.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN BOULTON, CHICAGO REPUBLICAN PARTY CHAIRMAN: She is the power behind the throne now. He is a creature, virtually, of the Chicago teachers' union.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN: The \uc2 CTU\uc2  directly funded more than $2.2 million or 20 percent of Johnson's campaigned, and in the Illinois Federation of Teachers, the \uc2 American Federation of Teachers\uc2 , and educator unions funded about half of Johnson's war chest. Ted with the conservative think tank Wirepoints.\par \par \uc2 TED DABROWSKI, WIREPOINTS PRESIDENT: This is no longer a teachers' union. This is a political group. And their goals are to do social justice. They don't really care about education anymore.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN: Last year, test scores showed only six out of every 100 black K through 12 students in Chicago public schools could do math at grade level, and only 11 out of 100 could read at grade level. Chicago teachers have walked out 14 times since 1969. Demands from recent strikes have ranged from COVID safety to affordable housing and defunding the police.\par \par \uc2 DABROWSKI: The reality is that they ignore what is happening in the schools and they tend to push more for their social justice agenda.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN: Now at the helm of the nation's third largest city with the support of a liberal governor and a Democrat state legislature, their agenda is on the march.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR BRANDON JOHNSON, (D) CHICAGO: In my view, the state of Illinois has become the vanguard for progressive policy all over this country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOBIN (on camera): Hispanic students also fell behind. About half of white students Chicago public schools were up to grade level. The Illinois state report card rated 84 percent of the teachers excellent or proficient. The teachers' union did not respond to our request for comment. Shannon, back to you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: \uc2 Mike Tobin\uc2  in Chicago. Thank you, Mike.\par \par \uc2 Tonight in our series focusing on challenges Americans face in accessing quality healthcare, if it seems to you that it is taking longer these days to get in to see your doctor, you may be right. A new report says wait times are getting worse. Here is senior correspondent Alicia Acuna.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 HEATHER GRAGG, PATIENT: It's terrifying because you know, OK, I have to get this done today and this done tomorrow, and I don't have time for this. And I don't want to be in pain anymore.\par \par \uc2 ALICIA ACUNA, FOX NEWS SENIOR CORRESPONDENT: Heather Gragg has suffered from debilitating migraines for more than two decades. Lately they've gotten worse. Some hit so hard they interrupt the Denver mother's work and time with her young daughter.\par \par \uc2 GRAGG: There's a lot of guilt associated with that.\par \par \uc2 ACUNA: When she was referred to a neurologist in June, the first available in person appointment was November, a five month wait.\par \par \uc2 GRAGG: I was shocked.\par \par \uc2 ACUNA: Accord to a survey by \uc2 AMN Healthcare\uc2  in Merritt Hawkins, in 2022 the average wait time for a new patient appointment in 15 major U.S. cities was 26 days, up five days from 2004. The numbers get worse for certain specialties. For an OBGYN, the average wait is 31.4 days, a 19 percent increase from 2017. Cardiology wait times are up 26 percent, and orthopedic surgery 48 percent.\par \par \uc2 TOM FLORENCE, \uc2 AMN HEALTHCARE\uc2 : We have arrived at a point in time where demand for physicians is rapidly exceeding supply.\par \par \uc2 ACUNA: The reasons for that vary, including an aging population, delayed appointments during the pandemic, population growth, and physician burnout is now adding to pre-COVID staffing shortages as more doctors retire.\par \par \uc2 FLORENCE: Another factor is the rate of poor health within this country, as six in 10 adults in the U.S. have at least one chronic condition.\par \par \uc2 ACUNA: So what to do?\par \par \uc2 FLORENCE: You're just going to have to really get in line.\par \par \uc2 ACUNA: Urgent care centers are absorbing many primary care patients. But there is no guarantee you will see an M.D. at one, but rather a nurse practitioner or a physician's assistant.\par \par \uc2 FLORENCE: Because a lot of times in those retail clinic settings and those urgent care settings, those allied professionals are usually the ones that are going to be on the front lines seeing patients.\par \par \uc2 ACUNA: Another option is telehealth, which Gragg opted for, but that only shortened her wait time from five months to three.\par \par \uc2 GRAGG: I was like, oh, OK, so I just get to suffer in pain for this much longer.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 ACUNA (on camera): The survey was done in areas with higher concentration of doctors, which means the situation is even worse in more rural locations. Shannon?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Alicia, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, Bret Baier will be here with a look at how gasoline prices seemed to stabilize during election season.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Inflation is expected to remain a hot topic as the 2024 campaign season ramps up. The price of gasoline is something that comes up nearly every cycle. My colleague Bret Baier looks at why costs tend to trend lower as Election Day draws nearer.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Thanks, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 Lawmakers and voters have historically kept a close eye on gas prices in election years. When prices are low, the party in power often takes credit for cheap gasoline. When costs are high, opponents blame the incumbents. Tonight, we take a look at that trend and how much power presidents and lawmakers on Capitol Hill have over the price at the pump.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 GEORGE W. BUSH\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: As long as cars and trucks run on gasoline, we will need oil, and we should produce more of it at home.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: During President \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2 's term in office the average weekly price per gallon of gasoline was $1.59. That's the lowest among recent presidents.\par \par \uc2 And it almost tracks with success if prices are low.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN BOOK, CLEARVIEW ENERGY PARTNERS: There is a really strong inverse relationship between pump prices and approval ratings at a presidential level, and it hasn't changed in four decades.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: In 2002 Republicans gained seats in both the House and the Senate. In 2004, the GOP picked up even more seats in Congress, and President Bush was reelected.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: During his second term gas prices are slightly higher and more volatile.\par \par \uc2 BUSH: You are predicting $4.00 a gallon --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: A number of analysts are predicting $4.00 a gallon gasoline this spring when they reformulate.\par \par \uc2 BUSH: That's interesting. I hadn't heard that.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: In July of 2008 prices spiked to a high during his presidency of $4.17 a gallon. By Election Day in November, prices had fallen by around $1.70. The lower cost did little to help the Republican Party. Democrats made gains in both the House and the Senate, and Barack Obama was elected president.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA, FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: We import more oil today than ever before.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The \uc2 American Petroleum Institute\uc2  reports the amount of oil produced on federal lands plummeted while President Obama was in office.\par \par \uc2 PHIL FLYNN, ENERGY MARKET ANALYST: The president can make policies that can make prices cheaper than they would have been.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: In 2010 costs were on the rise when Republicans picked up more than 60 seats in the House. 2012 marked the highest average price per gallon for any year during the Obama presidency.\par \par \uc2 OBAMA: Do you think the president of the United States going into re- election wants gas prices to go up higher?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Prices eventually fell nearly 40 cents in the two months leading up to Election Day. President Obama defeated Republican \uc2 Mitt Romney\uc2 . Republicans lost eight seats in the House, two seats in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 FLYNN: The voters feel the impact of rising gasoline prices perhaps more than any policy that a politician puts in place.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: When President Trump came into office, he vowed to increase domestic oil production. During his single term, the U.S. recorded the lowest gas prices since President \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2 's first term.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: You know what that's like? That's like a tax cut. That's bigger than a tax cut.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: President Biden's partial term has recorded the highest cost in recent presidencies.\par \par \uc2 FLYNN: Presidents know that their popularity ratings can go up and down with gasoline prices. And they have done some major things to try to cool off prices before the election.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Most recently President Biden faced criticism over releasing oil from the Strategic Reserve ahead of the 2022 election season. Prices did fall from more than $5 for the monthly average in June to around $3.80 in November.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We have a crisis. A price at the pump.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Democrats argue the release was necessary as the world recovered from the global pandemic and as oil supply was limited due to the war in Ukraine. But some energy experts say the release from the reserve was, in fact, strategic.\par \par \uc2 ANNE BRADBURY, EXPLORATION AND PRODUCTION COUNCIL: They are meant for short-term supply disruptions to address short-term price spikes, not as a long-term market manipulation tool in the runup to the election.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: While policy can influence gas prices, analysts acknowledge the November drop has more to do with timing than strategy.\par \par \uc2 FLYNN: Usually, gasoline prices go up until Labor Day and then crash after Labor Day because everybody is back in school, nobody is traveling. So we usually see those prices go down. It's a normal seasonal pattern.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: One thing is certain -- as the 2024 campaign season ramps up, patterns likely will not stop politicians from blaming each other for the rise and fall of gas prices.\par \par \uc2 We're getting ready to get into another election season. That always is an interesting factor.\par \par \uc2 BOOK: It is. People care an awful lot about the price at the pump. And they don't care about a lot of the details. It's very hard to explain a price going up when you are going to the polls.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER (on camera): The Energy Information Association is already forecasting lower global oil inventories in 2024. That could extend to higher gas prices as well. It is sure to be a hot topic as we get ready for next year's election.\par \par \uc2 We'll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Absolutely, Bret. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel on the defense bill, parents versus School, plus Winners and Losers, and later, "Notable Quotables."\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY, (R-CA) HOUSE SPEAKER: A military cannot defend themselves if you train them in woke.\par \par \uc2 REP. HAKEEM JEFFRIES, (D-NY) HOUSE MINORITY LEADER: These extreme MAGA Republicans have decided that we want to attack women who serve in the military. We want to attack communities of color.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 CHRISSY HOULAHAN\uc2 , (D-PA) HOUSE ARMED SERVICES COMMITTEE: Targeting LGBTQ families like my own, targeting libraries, targeting the reproductive freedoms of service women in military families.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 BRIAN MAST\uc2 , (R-FL): Do you believe that units should be made up of something that you can't see and that you can't ask about? Idiotic policy, dangerous for our service members.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, let's bring in our panel to talk about this new bill just passed through the House, Mollie Hemingway, editor in chief of "The Federalist," Marie Harf, former State Department spokeswoman under President Obama, and Real Clear Politics White House correspondent Philip Wegmann. All right, everybody. Philip, I get the feeling on Capitol Hill that everybody is just talking past each other.\par \par \uc2 PHILIP WEGMANN, REAL CLEAR POLITICS: And I think the reason why is Republicans, they don't feel like they dragged this defense bill to the right. Instead, they see this as a court correction back to the center because, in their mind, they believe that President Biden is the one who pushed the Pentagon to the left. And certainly, they would like to have a bipartisan NDAA, but they don't want to give this administration a free hand to set policy on abortion, trans issues, or diversity, equity, and inclusion. And I think the takeaway, frankly, from this is we thought that Kevin McCarthy would perhaps be an anemic speaker, but, instead, he has again got his entire caucus behind him.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: He is herding the cats effectively at this moment. OK, so this has passed the house, but now it goes to the Senate, where the outlook is much different. Here is a little back and forth from a couple of House members about what comes next.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY, (R-CA) HOUSE SPEAKER: I hope the bills that come out of the House are different than the bills that come out of that Schumer is in charge of. And you know what? We designed our government to be this way, because then we go to conference, and we find where we can unite.\par \par \uc2 REP. CHIP ROY, (R-TX): What we want out of Senate Republicans and out of House leadership fighting is to not do what we did on Memorial Day limit said row, and then essentially capitulate.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So a little tension, I think, between those two. But Mollie, the Republicans, unlike the House side, they do not have the numbers in the Senate, and I think the fight is going to feel very different.\par \par \uc2 MOLLIE HEMINGWAY, EDITOR IN CHIEF, "THE FEDERALIST": Right, but you look at what is happening in the House, Republicans only have the majority of four, and to Phil's point, they managed to pass this NDAA, which it isn't just that Republicans think this move is more to the center. The majority of Americans say that they do not want to be doing taxpayer funding of abortions. The vast majority of Americans are opposed to some of the radical gender ideology that President Biden and Democrats were putting into this bill.\par \par \uc2 And so then you see on the Senate side, and yes, it's true, Republicans don't have the majority there, there has been just more weakness from Republican leadership there. And so I think Kevin McCarthy and Chip Roy and others people are saying now it's time for you Senate Republicans to do a little bit