e. There is no evidence to support that.\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: A lot of Americans are very concerned about what is happening with the radical gender ideology. They think that America's military is supposed to be for fighting wars and winning wars. And instead a lot of Democrats seem to have been saying that it should be to push some radical experiments on the American people.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and to this issue about cultural issues, we had the story earlier in the show about parents, this ruling by a judge appointed by President Bush, I believe, \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2 , saying that this California law that prevents schools from sharing information about transgender and gender issues that students having with their parents is constitutional. He is going to allow it to stand. Here is a little bit of sound from that story.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BILL ESSAYLI, (R) CALIFORNIA STATE ASSEMBLY: Parents have a lot of power here. I introduced this bill. It's not going to go far in a Democrat legislature, but it can go far at a school district level. And what we're doing now is working with school districts to implement a mandatory notification policy at the district level. And that is consistent with that judge's ruling. The judge said it's not a constitutional issue, it's a political one. So we need to have that political debate and battle at the local school district level. Parents can win there.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So Philip, this is another thing that Republicans then on the other side of the aisle can go and lobby on this kind of thing and campaign on this, saying, hey, we think parents should, with minor children, be informed about big decisions in their lives.\par \par \uc2 WEGMANN: That's absolutely right, and I think a lot of Republican candidates for president are probably going to echo these types of arguments. In fact, they already are saying that things have gotten out of hand. They are arguing