ht 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 HAROLD FORD JR\uc2 ., FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: So, they called another flight attendant who was in Detroit. He came to the airport with her passport. Things -- everything was saved, so they had a great honeymoon and bless their hearts and blessed Delta flight attendant.\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Wow, Delta with some good P.R.\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL LEGAL ANALYST: Do you think they sleep together?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: For now.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: My wife sent me things in our path forward might be us.\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: That's it for us. SPECIAL REPORT is up next. Hi, Gillian.\par \par \uc2 GILLIAN TURNER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Greg, everybody knows that \uc2 Shake Shack\uc2  has the best fries, OK? Thanks. Got to go. Bye.\par \par \uc2 Good evening from Washington. I'm Gillian Turner in for Bret Baier tonight.\par \par \uc2 Well, Secret Service has now concluded its investigation into the cocaine that was found inside the West Wing. We're going to tell you what they found.\par \par \uc2 And Hollywood actors are now going to join T.V. and movie writers out on the picket line. What this will mean for your family's summer entertainment options?\par \par \uc2 Plus, our Whatever Happened To segment on the infamous Chinese spy balloon.\par \par \uc2 Well, Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  has made a significant decision now about the investigation into President Biden's son. House Republicans are pressuring Garland to make multiple people involved in the investigation available for transcribed interviews. So far, Garland is not giving in.\par \par \uc2 David Spunt joins us from the Justice Department tonight. Hi, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Gillian. Good evening. We are waiting to hear what that decision is from the Attorney General. I'm told a decision has been made. There are still some discussions going on. And a response is being worked on. Perhaps, we're waiting for that to get over to Capitol Hill before we can actually see a copy of that written response to a request for the Attorney General to make roughly a dozen people connected to the Hunter Biden probe available for transcribed interviews.\par \par \uc2 House Judiciary Chairman Jim Jordan, Oversight Chair James Comer, and Ways and Means Chair Jason Smith wrote to Garland and the head of the \uc2 IRS\uc2  and Secret Service two weeks ago, asking them to make 11 people available, including David Weiss, the Trump appointed U.S. Attorney in Delaware, who's gone on the record in three different letters to Congress to say that he has been in charge of the Hunter Biden probe, despite claims from an \uc2 IRS\uc2  whistleblower who insists Weiss said the opposite.\par \par \uc2 But it was just three weeks ago, Gillian, when the Attorney General said this about David Weiss.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MERRICK GARLAND\uc2 , U.S. ATTORNEY GENERAL: I would support Mr. Weiss, explaining or testifying on these matters when he deems it appropriate.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (voice over): And Weiss did say in a letter to Congress, he will talk at the appropriate time.\par \par \uc2 Other two big names Weiss's Assistant U.S. Attorney Lesley Wolf and the U.S. Attorney Matthew Graves in Washington D.C.\par \par \uc2 According to the whistleblower, Weiss complained that graves office prevented him from charging \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  in Washington D.C. Weiss said, simply not true, writing in a letter earlier this week, "I was assured that I would be granted this authority if it proved necessary".\par \par \uc2 Hours ago, House Speaker Kevin McCarthy said the Attorney General must cooperate with Congress.\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): He doesn't have really the ability to tell us whether Congress could find the answer or not. The American people deserve the answer of the truth.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (voice over): And a few weeks ago, the House Speaker floated the word impeachment but today when asked about impeaching the Attorney General perhaps, several Republicans backed off and lowered the temperature.\par \par \uc2 REP. DARRELL ISSA (R-CA): The need to impeach a cabinet officer depends on two things. One, a full due process investigation of the wrongdoing and two, a refusal by the president to dismiss somebody who serves at the pleasure of that president. So, we're a long way from that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (on camera): I mentioned the \uc2 IRS\uc2  whistleblower, there was a second \uc2 IRS\uc2  whistleblower who wants to remain anonymous. His attorney calls him Mr. X. He will testify before Congress next week. The week after that on July 26th Hunter Biden will enter his plea at federal court in Delaware, Gillian.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: David, we're also hearing there are some updates in former President Trump's criminal case. What can you tell us about that?\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: That's exactly right, Gillian, two updates. The first is Fox News can confirm the former president's son in law \uc2 Jared Kushner\uc2 , testified before a grand jury here in Washington D.C. This is the special counsel Grand Jury looking into efforts to overturn the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 We also know in the second investigation involving Special Counsel Jack Smith, this is the documents probe. We are told specifically that DOJ, the team doubled down on keeping the trial on December 11th.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his team wants it after the 2024 presidential election but Special Counsel Jack Smith's team submitted a motion just a few hours ago saying we want this to be in December, Gillian.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: All right, David Spunt at the DOJ for us this hour. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: You're welcome.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: We're also getting new details now about the investigation into the discovery of cocaine at the White House inside the West Wing. The Secret Service's testing has been inconclusive. They have not been able to figure out where the drug came from or who brought it to 1600 Pennsylvania Avenue.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Mark Meredith has the latest tonight from the White House.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. NANCY MACE (R-SC): It is deeply frustrating.\par \par \uc2 MARK MEREDITH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): South Carolina Congresswoman Nancy Mace says she's dumbfounded the Secret Service was unable to determine who brought cocaine into the White House.\par \par \uc2 MACE: This is one of the most secure locations in the world with some of the best law enforcement officers in the world and they don't have any answers.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (voice over): For the past 11 days, investigators say they reviewed security footage, access logs and physical evidence trying to trace how narcotics got into the West Wing. But they say after both fingerprint and DNA testing came up short. The investigation is now closed.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, the Secret Service says, "Without physical evidence, the investigation will not be able to single out a person of interest from the hundreds of individuals who pass through the vestibules where the cocaine was discovered".\par \par \uc2 Some lawmakers say investigators never went far enough.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MARJORIE TAYLOR GREENE\uc2  (R-GA): My question to them was have they drug tested this list of 500 potential suspects that brought an illegal substance of drug cocaine into the White House? Their answer was no and that they're unwilling to do so.\par 