ay and Tuesday, but it will be dangerous in the areas that experience flash flooding.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FOY: Now, earlier today, take a look at this. Crews pulled the car out of a river in Barre, Vermont. Thankfully, Gillian, no one was inside.\par \par \uc2 State officials confirmed again today, as of this morning, nobody has been killed in Vermont as a result of this flooding. But there are a lot of homes with severe damage.\par \par \uc2 Take a look at our Fox News drone in the town of Barre as well. This is a live look tonight. Nearly every home in this community has a flooded basement. Many have first floor water damage as well. Some homeowners told us, they don't have flood insurance and we'll be relying on FEMA.\par \par \uc2 One person told us, this is five times worse than Tropical Storm Irene a dozen years ago.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We never had mud like this and our cellar is turned upside down. It's going to be a long recovery.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FOY: Yes, and that recovery, Gillian, will be delayed with more severe weather expected again tonight and through the weekend.\par \par \uc2 There are no active rescue operations happening right now. But state officials are preparing for more and they are ready to respond as the weather gets worse in the coming hours and days. Gillian?\par \par \uc2 TURNER: All right. Nate Foy in Montpelier for us tonight. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 The National Weather Service is surveying storm damage in the Chicago area in northeast Illinois.\par \par \uc2 Fierce winds there, ripped roofs from buildings down trees, and sent residents fleeing yesterday.\par \par \uc2 Confirmed tornado was spotted on the ground near Chicago's O'Hare International Airport. Weather team suspect there may very well have been others.\par \par \uc2 And this record setting temperatures are hitting out west. Las Vegas is forecast to reach 118 degrees over the weekend.\par \par \uc2 Fox weather correspondent Max Gorden is there right now. Max, what can you tell us?\par \par \uc2 MAX GORDEN, FOX WEATHER FIELD CORRESPONDENT: Gillian, I can tell you, it is sweltering here in Sin City. 107 degrees. And right now, as we're on our way to 109, it really feels like a furnace out here. But this is going to be nothing compared to what we are expecting later on in the weekend.\par \par \uc2 On Sunday, we're expecting to go to a height of at least 117, potentially 118 degrees. If we hit 118, that would be a new record high for Las Vegas.\par \par \uc2 Now Clark County, Nevada has been opening up cooling shelters. Those will be open through Monday. Safe places for folks to go.\par \par \uc2 Officials advising people to stay inside if they can. But still, lots of action out here on the Las Vegas Strip. Take a listen to what some folks are saying about the heat.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We're struggling. We're not used to this type of weather in Ireland, are we?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We're sweating loads, yes!\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It's like the heat is been turned on and someone needs to turn it off.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes!\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Very hot. It is too hot.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You get out in it early, and stay out in it, and then you kind of accumulate to it, and you don't -- you don't realize it's getting hot.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GORDEN: 84 million Americans under some sort of heat warning from the Mississippi Valley, all the way to the West Coast. This is, of course, part of a larger heatwave that is sweeping across the United States.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, back here in Las Vegas, officials telling people that if they have to be outside right now, make sure to hydrate and wear light loose clothing. Gillian?\par \par \uc2 TURNER: All right. Stay safe out there. Max Gorden, thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 Coming up next, T.V. and film actors now have voted to join union writers on the Hollywood picket line. We'll take you live in Hollywood.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Well, federal officials have approved the first over the counter birth control pill. The \uc2 FDA\uc2  has cleared what is called Perrigo's once-a-day Opill to be sold without a prescription.\par \par \uc2 For the first time now, women will not have to get the medication from behind the pharmacy counter.\par \par \uc2 The company is slated to start shipping the pill out early next year.\par \par \uc2 The company that created ChatGPT is now under investigation by the \uc2 Federal Trade Commission\uc2 . The agency is looking into whether \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  tools have actually harmed the consumers they were built to help.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FDA\uc2  -- FTC has informed the company it's probing deceptive practices that risk user's data security.\par \par \uc2 Also "BREAKING TONIGHT", they say the show must go on, but this time it's not going to, at least for a while.\par \par \uc2 Actors in L.A. have voted to walk off the job after negotiations with industry executives have broken down. Writers have been on strike since May, meaning the few productions that have continued are now likely going to come to a complete standstill. Chief correspondent Jonathan Hunt has those breaking details.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: \uc2 SAG\uc2  is on strike, baby, first time we've struck together since 1960.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN HUNT, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hollywood is in crisis. Tonight, every film and TV studio effectively shut down now that the actors' union, \uc2 SAG-AFTRA\uc2 , has joined writers in striking.\par \par \uc2 FRAN DRESCHER, SAG-AFTRA PRESIDENT: We had no choice. We are the victims here.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: The main issues for the actors are the same as they are for writers. They want a better and guaranteed share of income from the streaming of movies and shows. And they want guarantees over how A.I. might be used in future productions.\par \par \uc2 ADAM CONOVER, COMEDIAN: Most of those actors are making a middle class living. They are just trying to make their mortgage payment that month.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We just want a fair contract and a living wage.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: The studios say the demands, particularly on revenue from streaming, are unrealistic given that many streamers are currently struggling to make a profit. Through their umbrella organization, the \uc2 Alliance of Motion Picture and Television Producers\uc2 , studio executives accuse the actors' union of intransigence, saying, quote, "Rather than continuing to negotiate, \uc2 SAG-AFTRA\uc2  has put us on a course that will deepen the financial hardship for thousands who depend on the industry for their livelihoods."\par \par \uc2 The joint strike actions mean the production of movies and shows and the promotion by actors of anything already made has now ended. And depending how long this goes on, it could cost the entertainment industry billions of dollars.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 HUNT: And as production shuts down at studios like the one behind me, it's worth remembering that the last times the actors' and writers' unions went out on strike at the same time was back in 1960. Leading the Screen Actors Guild then, \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2 . Gillian?\par \par \uc2 TURNER: It sounds like a great summer to pull out a great new book. Jonathan Hunt, thank you.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: For sure.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Producers of the new "Barbie" movie are taking considerable criticism tonight over a map featured in the film. The movie has already been banned in one country over a propaganda map that gives China control over the South China Sea. Congressional correspondent Aishah Hasnie is here to tell us what all the fuss is about.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Wow, this is the real world.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Trouble in Barbie land. As the world awaits the release of the most highly anticipated movie, China hawks on Capitol Hill are bursting Barbie's bubble.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: They are willing to turn their eye to make a buck.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Good gracious, stand up for freedom.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: The outcry is over this scene of Barbie standing next to a world map which appears to show the nine-dash line representing Beijing's illegitimate claim to almost all of the South China Sea. Vietnam banned the film in response, and the Philippines wants the map blurred before allowing it in theaters.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Get that Barbie.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Republicans on Capitol Hill say this is the latest example of Hollywood kowtowing to Beijing to reach its 1.4 billion consumers.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MIKE GALLAGHER\uc2 , (R-WI) HOUSE INTELLIGENCE COMMITTEE: We have seen all these instances of Hollywood self-censuring or censuring in order to get access to the Chinese market.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: \uc2 Warner Brothers\uc2  is defending the map, saying it depicts Barbie's make-believe journey from Barbie Land to the real world. China is dismissing the controversy while some on the left think Republicans are making much ado about nothing.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I guess Barbie might be a little too woke for them.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Whatever you believe, entertainment report Erich Schwartzel says China's influence on Hollywood isn't fantasy, and that's something movie goers can't escape.\par \par \uc2 ERICH SCHWARTZEL, "RED CARPET" AUTHOR: There is so much economic leverage that China has not just at the box office with these companies but in their broader business plans that I don't see going away any time soon.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: -- an update from the Justice Department relating to our top story tonight. FOX News has obtained a letter in which the Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  has agreed to negotiate with House Republicans about testimony from officials working the Hunter Biden probe. Tomorrow morning, Justice personnel will reach out to House Judiciary Committee Chairman Jim Jordan to talk about a timeline for U.S. Attorney David Weiss to sit down for a transcribed interview.\par \par \uc2 And coming up next, Bret Baier with our "Whatever Happened To" segment on the infamous Chinese spy balloon.\par \par \uc2 But, first, let's take a look Beyond our Borders tonight. Tourists in central Athens huddled under mist machines and zoo animals in Madrid are fed fruit popsicles and chunks of frozen food as southern Europe is now bracing for a historic heat wave. The high pressure system has crossed the Mediterranean from North Africa.\par \par \uc2 Britain's state funded healthcare service is facing its longest ever strike. Tens of thousands of doctors across England have launched into a five-day walkout over pay beginning today.\par \par \uc2 And this, South Korea and the U.S. military are holding joint air drills. They have deployed one B-52H strategic bomber along with South Korea's F- 15K and American F-16s. Those drills were staged just a day after North Korea launched a missile test of its own.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories Beyond our Borders we are tracking tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: It's been over five months now since the Chinese spy balloon was shot down off the coast of South Carolina, and now U.S. officials are beginning to learn more about that balloon's actual capabilities. In tonight's "Whatever Happened To" segment, my colleague Bret Baier takes a look at the ongoing investigation and why so little is still known about three other objects shot down by the U.S. and Canada in the wake of that incident.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Thanks, Gillian. SPECIAL REPORT viewer Jack James asked, "Whatever happened to the efforts to recover the electronics from that Chinese spy balloon?" There are two known efforts to investigate. The \uc2 FBI\uc2  is the main government entity looking into the balloon with the expertise of other government agencies, specifically focusing on that electronic payload. Congress has also pledged a bipartisan probe. It focuses on why the objects were not detected sooner. We'll dig into those investigations tonight and explain where they stand.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I think it was more embarrassing than it was intentional.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: President Biden recently said China was not fully aware of its spy balloon's whereabouts.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I don't think the leadership knew where it was or knew what was in it and knew what was going on.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: U.S. officials recently determined, while the balloon had intelligence collection capabilities, it did not transmit information back to Beijing.\par \par \uc2 BRIG. GEN. PATRICK RYDER, PENTAGON PRESS SECRETARY: We also took steps to mitigate the potential collection efforts of that balloon.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: A senior Pentagon source confirmed to FOX News the device held specialized Chinese sensors. It also contained American made equipment, some of which is available for sale online. The U.S. has export controls in place to prevent China from accessing its more sensitive technology.\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW MILLER, STATE DEPARTMENT SPOKESMAN: We're not going to allow you to take U.S. technology and use it against us.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The \uc2 Defense Intelligence Agency\uc2  has been examining the military- related equipment within the device. The DIA told FOX its analysis is still ongoing and pointed us to its report which details China's investment in reconnaissance equipment. The agency often makes certain reports public that explain how adversaries spy on the U.S. But administration officials have pushed back on any public release concerning the spy balloon's technology.\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, NATIONAL SECURITY COUNCIL COORDINATOR FOR STRATEGIC COMMUNICATIONS: I wouldn't expect -- and I have said this before -- that we are going to lay all that out for the public. This was an espionage piece of equipment, and we want to get a better handle on it, understand it for our own national security purposes.\par \par \uc2 MILLER: I would say it's a matter for the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . It's not for the state department.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We asked the \uc2 FBI\uc2  for an update on its investigation. It pointed us to our February interview with \uc2 FBI\uc2  Director \uc2 Christopher Wray\uc2  and said it has nothing further to add at this time.\par \par \uc2 What have you learned? Can you talk about that?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER WRAY\uc2 , FBI DIRECTOR: Our technical folks, our lab folks, our counterintelligence folks who specialize in Chinese spying, are working hand in hand with our military and other government partners to analyze all the debris, and that work is ongoing. This balloon thing is just one piece of a much broader threat.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The \uc2 FBI\uc2  also said it has no updates on the three smaller objects shot down over Alaska, Lake Huron, and Canada's Yukon territory.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Why do you think we don't we know what we shot down?\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARK KELLY (D-AZ): I'm pretty sure we haven't been able to find, because they are rather small, we haven't been able to find everything.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Officials suspected they were likely research or recreational balloons. Search efforts for those ended soon after they began, with U.S. and Canadian authorities saying no debris was located.\par \par \uc2 We shot down, as a country shot down other things that we never really found out what those were. Were they balloons, too?\par \par \uc2 SEN. ROGER WICKER, (R-MS): Yes. And, again, what looks embarrassing is that we shot down some toys, it seems.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Arizona Democratic Senator Mark Kelly and North Carolina Republican Senator \uc2 Ted Budd\uc2  have legislation that would mandate tracking systems on high altitude weather and research balloons.\par \par \uc2 That Chinese move, how did you see it?\par \par \uc2 KELLY: It was clearly a system that they thought they could fly over the United States and we wouldn't notice. We're going to have to have a way where we can identify it and track it, because there's enough of these things that we can't be scrambling an F-22 to potentially shoot it down on a weekly basis.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Amid calls to hold China accountable for its actions, Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  met with Chinese officials in Beijing. His initial trip had been canceled over the incident. He told NBC News relations with China have since stabilized so long as its spy balloons stay grounded.\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN\uc2 , U.S. SECRETARY OF STATE: We said what we needed to say and made clear what we needed to make clear in terms of this not happening again. And so as long as it doesn't, that chapter should be closed.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Some lawmakers want to keep that book open, and have grown frustrated with the lack of information provided by the administration.\par \par \uc2 There's a lot of people wondering, for all of the focus that we had on this thing, whatever happened to the fallout from it and what we learned from it? Where are those answers?\par \par \uc2 WICKER: There are a lot of questions left unanswered. What does it say about preparedness? What does it say about how seriously we take threats from China? What does it say about the way we responded and our capability of responding?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER (on camera): President Biden has said he hopes to be meeting with President Xi again in the next several months. He plans to discuss the differences between the two nations and ways the U.S. and China can get along. We'll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: All right, thank you, Bret.\par \par \uc2 If there's a story that has left you curious and you want to know whatever happened to it, you can let us know on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , \uc2 Instagram\uc2 , or \uc2 Facebook\uc2 . We may just look into it and then get back to you by using your question right here on SPECIAL REPORT.\par \par \uc2 Coming up next, the panel and the White House investigation into cocaine, plus China's growing influence in Hollywood, and the new "Barbie" movie. Plus, later, a very emotional reunion.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MARJORIE TAYLOR GREENE\uc2 , (R-GA): It makes no sense to me whatsoever why they would not follow through on one simple task, and that is to drug test a list of 500 people that they have that are potential suspects for this.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 JAMIE RASKIN\uc2 , (D-MD): Some people are saying everybody should be drug tested on the way into the White House. And these are the same people who are opposed to COVID-19 tests.\par \par \uc2 REP. NANCY MACE, (R-SC): It's just interesting that every time there is something strange going on with the president, Biden or his family, or anything regarding his administration or the White House, no one can ever seem to find an answer.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: All right, welcome back. Time to bring in the panel. We have FOX News senior political analyst Juan Williams, Ben Domenech, editor at large for "The Spectator" and host of "The Ben Domenech Podcast" right here on FOX News Radio, and Olivia Beavers, congressional reporter for "Politico." Thanks, everybody, for being here.\par \par \uc2 Olivia, Nancy Mace is right, this is a very frustrating end to this whole debacle. But it is also a fallacy to think that the Secret Service has the authority to drug test 500 people, many of whom probably don't even work for the federal government.\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA BEAVERS, "POLITICO": Yes. House Republicans weren't blown away, pun intended, by how the Secret Service has handled this. They walked in hoping to get answers. They came out and they were saying that they were very disappointed.\par \par \uc2 The White House does do drug testing. I think the Biden administration has talked about it in terms of legalizing marijuana. They are for that, but they still test some of their staffers. As to whether they are going to be able to find it, they are not getting those answers. But it's just bad optics for the White House, right? There's someone who is talking about being serious, and yet, here you have a bag found in a locker and they can't figure out who did it.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: It is bad optics. But Juan, is it fair for Republican lawmakers to blame the inconclusive findings on the Biden White House? Is it true that they could have found the culprit if they were just tougher and more security oriented like Republicans are saying?\par \par \uc2 JUAN WILLIAMS, FOX NEWS POLITICAL ANALYST: I don't think so. I think that this is on the Secret Service. If you want to say that they are somehow corrupt or that they are flawed that they said they did a thorough investigation, could not find DNA evidence. They looked at the tapes and were unable to specify any one person, given that there were 500 people just before the Fourth of July.\par \par \uc2 But I must say, it's the most observed building in the world, I think, the White House, and with all the sensors and the cameras it's a little odd. The investigation took like 10 days, so people are going to have conspiracy theories. You can imagine that.\par \par \uc2 The one last thing I would say on this is I think a lot of Republicans tie it to \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  and the fact that he is a drug addict, or was a drug addict. But the Biden family is not acting like they are guilty. I mean, if they wanted to, they could have said hey, OK, if it's his, he has got to go back to rehab. And this is a guy that people criticize because he's at White House dinners and he's on White House trips. If they wanted to get him out of the picture, this was the chance.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Ben, another big story we are covering this hour is coming out of Hollywood. We learned today that actors, TV and movie actors are going to join writers on this strike. Everybody is predicting this is basically going to bring movie production to a complete standstill now. President Biden, as you know, has long touted himself as the most pro-union president in history. Take a listen to this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I intend to be the most pro-union president leading the most pro-union administration.\par \par \uc2 I'm proud to be the most pro-union president in American history.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I promised you I would be.\par \par \uc2 I meant what I said when I said I'm going to be the pro -- most pro-union president in American history, and I make no apologies for it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Is it a coincidence, do you think, that this massive strike, historic strike, is happening on the president's watch?\par \par \uc2 BEN DOMENECH, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: I don't think it's a coincidence, but I do think it's a huge mistake, and it's rare that I would come on here and agree with someone like \uc2 Bob Iger\uc2 , but he said this is one of the worst timings of any kind of strike along these lines -- obviously, the head of \uc2 Disney\uc2  -- that he has seen, one that runs the risk of really setting back the industry in a lot of different ways.\par \par \uc2 Look, American pop culture is still one of our greatest exports. We are dominant in that area in a way that we are not in others. And so I think that that's something that's certainly an item of concern. It's going to set back production. And what did we get out of the last time that we had a significant long writers' strike and then talent strike associated with it? What we got was an enormous, ebullient rise of reality television, which I don't know about the rest of you but I think is one of the worst things that can possibly happen.\par \par \uc2 So we'll see what comes of this, but I do think that it's a very poorly timed matter. And one more thing on this. The idea that this is going to be able to in any way adjust the rise of A.I. as being a creative source for Hollywood is, I think, very dubious. Whatever they come out with at the end of this, I think it's going to be a band-aid, and I think the studios and others are going to turn to A.I. simply because it is a source for, frankly, enormous creativity if you use it right.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: So related to that, Olivia, I'm going to go to you since you are wearing pink. I want to talk about the "Barbie" movie. Critics are blasting early release editions of the film because it uses a map -- let's pull it up on the screen so folks at home can see it. It essentially is a piece of Chinese propaganda that makes it look like China has full control over the South China Sea, which is disputed territory. And this is according to "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 ," "An international tribunal at the Hague ruled in 2016 China had no legal basis for its claims over that territory." Beijing has yet to concede the ruling. So this is an instance, critics say, of Hollywood caving to the Communist Party's demands.\par \par \uc2 BEAVERS: Yes, Vietnam has blocked it from even playing in their country just because of that one short moment in the film. And House Republicans are coming out and they are pointing out that this is a sign that the U.S. needs to be much more aware how propaganda being played in the U.S. There's about $4.6 billion in China's film industry. And so you have seen a whole shift over probably the last two decades of Hollywood trying to work with and cater.\par \par \uc2 Btu we are entering a new sort of phase of self-censorship. And that's what these Republicans that you are hearing from, from \uc2 Mike Gallagher\uc2  who has been warning that this is appeasing the CCP to other Republicans, that this is what this is. It's broader than the Barbie movie. We saw it with "Top Gun Maverick." We have seen it with other big blockbusters. And so that's what they're warning about here.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Also, caving in this way gives American movie studios access to billions of watchers in China. So there is financial interests at stake as well.\par \par \uc2 Panel, thanks so much for joining us. We have got to leave it there. Learned a lot from talking to you, as always.\par \par \uc2 Finally tonight, it's a very special day. Two California orphaned black bear cubs siblings were rescued from the San Bernardino national forest. They have now been reunited with one another. They were found originally nearby their mother who was deceased. This after officials tracked their locations. The brothers, once they were reunited, immediately started vocalizing, re-bonding, and connecting with one another. Kind of an amazing story. The wildlife center team says they are now planning to move the bears to a larger outdoor space as they grow, and they anticipate returning those cubs to their natural habitat early in the new year as soon as they can. So we'll bring you that story once they move outside, keep you updated on their progress.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT, Bret Baier is going to take a look at how gas prices are factoring into the 2024 presidential race already. Remember, if you can't catch us live, to set your DVR to 6:00 p.m. eastern time, 3:00 p.m. out on the west coast.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for watching SPECIAL REPORT. I'm Gillian Turner in Washington. "JESSE WATTERS PRIMETIME" begins right now. Hey, Jesse.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020230713ej7d00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Interview With Rep. \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2  (D-TX); Vermont Bracing For More Flooding; Interview With \uc2 White House Council of Economic Advisers\uc2  Chairman Jared Bernstein; Interview With Fmr. Rep. \uc2 Will Hurd\uc2  (R-TX); Interview With Pentagon Deputy Press Secretary Sabrina Singh; Interview With Sen. Tim Scott (R-SC)\b0\par\par\uc2 Max Gorden, Katie Byrne, Rich Edson, Neil Cavuto \par \uc2 8,198 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 July 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Iowa, what now? So many candidates, but we really can't say so little time, because the fact is, the Iowa caucuses still aren't for another six months. But man, oh, man, you would not know it, looking at all the candidate traffic and now the candidate controversies there.\par \par \uc2 And we're not talking about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  picking a fight with the state's governor, but that governor, \uc2 Kim Reynolds\uc2 , picking a big issue that is putting candidates on the spot.\par \par \uc2 Now, we won't tell you the issue just yet. We will leave you to guess on that one. But we will tell you the presidential candidates ready to take it on, and we just can't wait for that one, because South Carolina Senator Tim Scott is here, and so is former Texas Congressman \uc2 Will Hurd\uc2 , both here. One is a lock for that big FOX debate next month. The other says he's getting very close.\par \par \uc2 And guess who else is stopping by? None other than the chairman of the president's Council of Economic Advisers, because Republicans might be pounding inflation these days, but Jared Bernstein has a question for them: What inflation?\par \par \uc2 What a show.\par \par \uc2 And it begins right now.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everybody. I'm Neil Cavuto. So glad to have you.\par \par \uc2 Well, get ready for, well, a lot of this, yes, even in the case of Iowa, where we're still a half-a-year away from all of this, all these boxes, all so little time to get to that.\par \par \uc2 To Rich Edson on why you would never know this thing is still half-a-year away -- Rich.\par \par \uc2 RICH EDSON, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, Neil, it feels like caucus time, only, it's about 50 degrees warmer now.\par \par \uc2 Several of the Republican presidential candidates are in Iowa over the next few days. Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is scheduled to campaign across seven Iowa counties. The DeSantis campaign contends the GOP presidential nomination is a two-candidate race between the governor and former President Trump.\par \par \uc2 Trump has held a steady, substantial lead over DeSantis and the rest of the field. The former president was in Iowa last week, won't be there this week, though he's been criticizing the state's governor, \uc2 Kim Reynolds\uc2 . Reynolds refuses to endorse a candidate in the GOP primary, and Trump takes exception to that, since he says he endorsed her for governor.\par \par \uc2 Then, DeSantis just happened to tweet -- quote -- "\uc2 Kim Reynolds\uc2  is a strong leader who knows how to ignore the chirping and get it done."\par \par \uc2 South Carolina Senator Tim Scott's already running ads in Iowa. His campaign says he will be in Davenport tomorrow, will return later in the month for an event with Governor Reynolds. Scott and former New Jersey Governor \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  now say they both have 40,000 individual donors. It's a key threshold to qualify for that first GOP presidential debate next month.\par \par \uc2 And with its strong religious Republican base, former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , an Indiana native, chose Iowa to launch his campaign. Iowa's legislature just passed a six-week abortion ban, a policy Pence has previously said he supports.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow morning, Nikki Haley and \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2  are scheduled to hold campaign breakfast events in Iowa. In the evening, \uc2 Asa Hutchinson\uc2  has a \uc2 VFW\uc2  fund-raiser in Des Moines -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Rich Edson, thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 Now one of those presidential candidates crisscrossing not only Iowa, but pretty much everywhere right now.\par \par \uc2 Will Hurd\uc2 , the latest entrant, he joins us right now, the former Texas congressman.\par \par \uc2 Sir, very good to have you.\par \par \uc2 FMR. REP. WILL HURD (R-TX), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Hey, Neil, it's always a pleasure to be on with you. Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: No, thank you.\par \par \uc2 So let me first get to what Governor Reynolds wants to do, ready to sign some legislation that really limits the ability to have an abortion beyond six weeks. Some say that's sticking very close to the right-to-life intentions that are near and dear to your fellow Republicans, but goes a long way toward alienating some of those same voters who look at that as too restrictive.\par \par \uc2 What do you think?\par \par \uc2 HURD: Well, I have said that if Congress put a 15-week ban on my desk, I would sign that.\par \par \uc2 I also think that the Dobbs decision was the right decision and states should be making their own decisions. I also think that the GOP should be talking about maternal health, neonatal health to make sure that women that are having these kids have the support and the help that they need.\par \par \uc2 It's unfortunate that -- and especially with black women -- black women having children are dying at a rate that's equivalent in some developing countries. That's not acceptable. And this is an issue that we could win and help us in November.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Do you think the six-week policy that the governor in Iowa is pushing is so restrictive, it's going to turn off some folks?\par \par \uc2 HURD: Look, I think \uc2 Kim Reynolds\uc2  knows what she's doing and is reflective of the needs of her state.\par \par \uc2 I think, though, this is going to be something that is going to be campaign fodder in November that Democrats are going to be using against Republicans.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: The reason why I mention it, Congressman, in 2015 and 2017, you had supported bills to ban abortion, I believe, after 20 weeks. Obviously, this is six weeks.\par \par \uc2 But you know what I was getting at in some of the prior questioning here...\par \par \uc2 HURD: Sure.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: ... that could this be an issue that hurts Republicans, depending on your point of view, of course, to your point...\par \par \uc2 HURD: Sure.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: ... in the '24 election? Could it have the same effect some felt that it had on the limited gains Republicans had in the midterms?\par \par \uc2 HURD: Look, we know that this issue was an issue in the 2022 election at a time that we thought there was going to be a red wave and people were predicting Kevin McCarthy was going to have 35- to a 40-seat majority in the House. It didn't happen.\par \par \uc2 This will always be an issue. As I have said, when I was in Congress, I was pro-life. My record reflects that. I think 15 weeks is something that a majority of Americans could be on. But this was also -- this was -- yes, this was an issue in 2022. It's going to be an issue in 2024.\par \par \uc2 But, more importantly, I think one of the issues that we saw in 2022 was that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  failed to grow the electorate. He was -- he failed to grow the Republican brand amongst women with college degrees, against -- with folks in the black and brown communities and with people under the age of 35.\par \par \uc2 And so he's proven to be a loser. He hasn't won an election since 2016. And this is another thing that was a weight around Republicans' necks. And that's why I think this feuding that he's doing with \uc2 Kim Reynolds\uc2  is absolutely unnecessary.\par \par \uc2 She's someone that has helped the state of Iowa a red state. She's a good governor, has respect around the country. And instead of fighting with some of our successful governors, I think \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  should be putting a -- his ideas out on what his vision for the future is, not having an axe to grind and trying to get everybody to kiss his butt.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You're referring, obviously, to Governor Reynolds, who has declared she is neutral in this race. That's typically the role a governor has to have in this state for the caucuses.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has been saying she's really siding with \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , and that it's causing a dust-up. And some say it's going to jeopardize his standing in the state. So, you think he's making a mistake attacking her, even if she does lean to Governor DeSantis?\par \par \uc2 HURD: Look, he's absolutely making a mistake.\par \par \uc2 She's the most popular politician in the state. And this is a history that we have seen with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . He's attacked Governor Kemp in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 HURD: He's attacked -- attacked the governor, Sununu, in New Hampshire. He's attacked Governor DeWine in Ohio.\par \par \uc2 These are all incredibly successful governors who have been able to grow the GOP and grow the party. And trying to -- being focused on all these axes to grind is not a successful thing. And the American public is trying -- is thinking, why is -- while \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is fighting with all these successful Republican governors, who is he actually trying to fight for, for the future?\par \par \uc2 So I think it's a bad strategy. But guess what? He's probably not going to listen to me, but I think the governor has shown her willingness to call balls and strikes and be a leader for the party, and has been open to all the campaigns. And that's what I have seen and witnessed in my short time in this race.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, you talk about it. You are the latest entrant, the shortest in the race thus far.\par \par \uc2 You mentioned \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  not probably listening to you. He's not talking about you. I notice as well he's not saying that much about Tim Scott, with whom I will be chatting later in the show, sir. And, normally, he reserves that not talking about you for candidates he's not worried about.\par \par \uc2 And I don't want to frame it that way, but what do you make of that?\par \par \uc2 HURD: Look, I really don't care what he decides.\par \par \uc2 This is about -- the Republican Party is the party of ideas. We shouldn't be focused on groupthink. We should embrace having multiple candidates in this race and have a competition of ideas. And, as you said in the run-up of the show, the first election is not until over five months away.\par \par \uc2 A lot can happen in this time. But here's what I have seen as I have crisscrossed the country in the last three weeks, is that Americans not -- want someone who -- and GOP voters want someone who's not afraid of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , but is also putting a vision forward for the future. They want someone who is not afraid of talking about the issues.\par \par \uc2 They want someone who's going to articulate, how are we going to have an unprecedented peace in this period and time, in complicated times, at a time when the Chinese government is trying to surpass us as a global superpower? They want to learn at a time when we have persistent inflation -- I know you're going to have a conversation later in the show.\par \par \uc2 But if you have seen over the last 21 years -- 23 years -- excuse me -- in this century, cost of goods and services has increased three times more than the average salary. Americans want to know what people are going to do about that.\par \par \uc2 And this is what these campaigns are about. This is what voters in Iowa and New Hampshire and South Carolina want to hear from their candidates.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And you're quite right. We have a ways to go.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, thank you very much. Very good seeing you.\par \par \uc2 HURD: Appreciate you, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, the congressman was talking about the cost of a lot of things that are exceeding what we're earning. That is a very big issue these days, although, to hear the White House tell it, we're actually seeing things improve on that front.\par \par \uc2 So, you can have all these Republican candidates crisscrossing Iowa, but the fact of the matter is, the \uc2 chairman of the Council of Economic Advisers\uc2  for the president of the United States is saying inflation isn't what it was. It's getting a lot better.\par \par \uc2 Jared Bernstein is next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, we had another inflation report today that shows, on the wholesale level, that is, the level before it gets to you and me, on the retail price level, that continues a trend of declining prices.\par \par \uc2 What we have seen currently on the retail front is 12 consecutive months of the inflation rate and the increase in the rate going down, in fact, in the latest numbers, the lowest it's been in two years.\par \par \uc2 Jared Bernstein joins us right now. Jared is now the newly minted chairman of the president's Council of Economic Advisers. Kind enough to join us.\par \par \uc2 I should probably call you Mr. Chairman now, Jared, but thank you. Very good to have you.\par \par \uc2 JARED BERNSTEIN, CHAIR, WHITE HOUSE COUNCIL OF ECONOMIC ADVISERS: That's fine. You can call me Jared.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: No, I will go with Chairman. It sounds august.\par \par \uc2 But let me ask you about that, because, as you know, Chairman, you have the Republican candidates running around saying inflation is still a problem. Things are costing more than people are making. So you can put all the spin on this you want. Inflation is still a problem. What do you say?\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: Our work is not done when it comes to helping households with this challenge.\par \par \uc2 But the amount of progress we have made is pretty remarkable. And you just took us through it, 12 months in a row of slower inflation, down two- thirds. If you and I were talking a year ago on this very date, we'd be talking about inflation that was two-thirds higher than it is now.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, when you have that kind of a trend, you're talking about relief from a set of price pressures that were much higher before than they are now. One of the other outcomes of these recent dynamics is that real wages are up. The buying power of the hourly wage of working people, say, middle-wage workers, that's up 1.6 percent over the past year.\par \par \uc2 So that kind of progress is giving housing some breathing room, the pricing -- the price of gas down $1.50 over the past year. That's real breathing -- you. But -- breathing room. But our work is not done. We're going to keep pushing.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it.\par \par \uc2 And, of course, if I wanted to compare that to food prices, those real wage gains evaporate.\par \par \uc2 But I wonder whether you're taking a bow for something the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  is doing. As you know, they have been aggressively raising rates,. And, at the rate they're going, they're going to do it again at the end of this month. That's what's causing this to go down, nothing you're doing. What do you say?\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: Well, I think, if you and I were out here talking about rates that were 9 and 8 and 7 percent, as opposed to 3 percent, you would probably be suggesting that we had something to do with that. So I think, when it goes the other way, we got to be kind of symmetrical there.\par \par \uc2 Look, I think when you look at the...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I'm sorry. I don't understand that. I mean, the fact is that the rates are going up. It's having the corresponding effect of pushing some prices down because demand is hit by that rate rise.\par \par \uc2 Are you -- is that not so?\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: Yes, so, there's no question that the Fed is in the mix.\par \par \uc2 But I also was going to tell you, if you look at one of the important aspects of where we have seen some real relief for households on the inflation side, it's in the area of goods, OK? So core goods inflation came in, I believe it was a negative sign, slightly declined last month. And...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You're talking about without volatile food and energy price swings, right?\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: Well, I'm actually talking about goods here...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: ... so specifically the things that come through ports.\par \par \uc2 Well, if you go back to 2021. President Biden stood up the Supply Side Disruption Task Force. He recognized right out of the gate that, if we were going to help people deal with some of the global inflationary pressures from the pandemic, we were going to have to unsnarl our supply chains.\par \par \uc2 And we got to work on that. And our fingerprints are on some of those improvements. Obviously, we worked closely with the private sector work force in the ports, particularly on the West Coast. So, now that we're seeing supply chain unsnarl-ups, that's showing up in lower goods prices and think our fingerprints are legitimately on that.\par \par \uc2 I talked about the gas price down $1.50 over the past year. Of course, when the president released all those barrels of oil from the Strategic Reserve, that made a difference. And now we can talk about the Inflation Reduction Act and the fact that lower costs for prescription drugs, for insulin, for the drugs themselves, for prescription drugs themselves, those prices are going to be knocked down by the Inflation Reduction Act.\par \par \uc2 So, I do think our fingerprints are on those issues.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: That part isn't happening yet. I mean, that's a hope.\par \par \uc2 But you do have your fingerprints on trillions of dollars worth of added spending. And, in retrospect, looking back at all of that spending, do you think that created the monster that the Fed is dealing with now?\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: No, I think that, when it comes to the fiscal outlook, I think you really need to look at the deficit reduction that this president has presided over, I mean, $1.7 trillion of deficit reduction in his first two years.\par \par \uc2 He just signed legislation to take that deficit down another trillion. And, of course, there's $2.5. trillion...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But our total debt -- but, Jared, our total debt is trillions higher than it was when the president took office. So, is that really something to brag about?\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: So, I think you have to look at -- well, I think you have to look at, what direction is the president's proposals going in when it talks about fiscal rectitude?\par \par \uc2 And I think you should probably compare that to...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But is it the president's proposals, Chairman?\par \par \uc2 That's why I'm curious, because a lot of it could just be the effect of coming out of COVID and all the spending from COVID, and a lot less spending for COVID, because we're out of COVID.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: So, I will speak to that.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And that's what's doing it, nothing -- nothing you're doing.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: No, I will speak to that. Let me...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Sure.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: You're raising a couple of different issues. Give me a sec.\par \par \uc2 So, in terms of the president's proposals, he's proposing $2.5 trillion in deficit reduction in his budget, which, by the way, is not just tax increases on the richest of the rich, injecting some fairness back into the tax code. It's also cutting spending in some wasteful areas.\par \par \uc2 And, meanwhile, you have House Republicans going the other way, proposing $3 trillion in deficit cuts for the richest folks. Now, when you talk about the spending coming down out of COVID, that's definitely part of the story.\par \par \uc2 But another big part of the story is that revenues, particularly in 2022, came in way above projections. And one of the reasons for that was because of the very strong economic recovery. The unemployment rate below 4 percent now for about a year-and-a-half, actually a little bit longer than that, that tells you a lot about the strength of the economy. And that shows up in higher revenues.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, we will see how that goes.\par \par \uc2 I think this is a side question. I would be curious. Apparently, they have been investigating this cocaine that was found in the White House, and they can't find the source of it, who brought it in or whatever. What did you think of that whole thing? Any idea that you might have as to that how that got there?\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: That sure is a side question.\par \par \uc2 I mean, look, I'm here to talk about the economy. I think we have a lot of really important things to report, 12 months of inflation coming down, month after month.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: No doubt. And I don't mean any disrespect, Chairman, but that's still cocaine. It's in the White House. It's kind of an important building.\par \par \uc2 Did it worry you when you found out about it?\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: Yes, I'm not going to comment on any of that, because I'm really out here to talk about our economic progress, which, by the way, has to be the thing that Americans need to hear us talking about, in terms of the progress we have made and our plans going forward.\par \par \uc2 And when you get to that part, in terms of our plans going forward, we want to build on this progress. And if you look at the investments, key component of Bidenomics...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: ... if you look at the extent to which our public investments are pulling in private investments, standing up a domestic semiconductor industry, standing up an E.V. battery industry, electric cars, clean energy products, we're not just making Bidenomics -- we're not just talking about Bidenomics working today.\par \par \uc2 We're talking about Bidenomics working in the future. And that is a powerful combination.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Who came up with Bidenomics, by the way, Chairman? I'm just curious.\par \par \uc2 So, was it the president? Was it you?\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: I have heard -- I heard the president talk about that.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: And he said -- I think he said he saw it in a couple of newspapers. I think he might have said \uc2 Financial Times\uc2  and The Journal.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: But I feel like I have been saying it for a while. So, you know, I will claim some ownership.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I actually think it was who you started it, because I remember, some time ago...\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: I don't know. I'm sure it wasn't just me.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: But I may have been in the mix.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Jared Bernstein, very good chatting with you again, the \uc2 White House Council of Economic Advisers\uc2 , the chairman.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: You too, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: He's in charge of all of that at the White House.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, we are focusing on this heat wave and all of this flooding going on, Mother Nature still being brutal, and we're told even more so -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, our climate czar on Capitol Hill today.\par \par \uc2 John Kerry\uc2  says, look around. Look at the flooding. Look at the record high temperatures. He warned us. Now he's saying, time to pay up for all of us - - after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN KERRY\uc2 , U.S. SPECIAL ENVOY FOR CLIMATE CHANGE: Torrential downpours that we see now that come because more moisture arises because of the heating of the ocean; 90 percent of the warming of the Earth goes into the ocean. And now we're seeing exactly what happens with these floods as a result of that increase.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, our climate czar on Capitol Hill today saying, well, I told you, I warned you.\par \par \uc2 Now, a lot of people saying he was seizing on weather phenomenon that might not have much to do with climate change. Be that as it may, the climate right now has been changing for the worse for a lot of folks in this country, and it's not getting any better, particularly with the flood conditions going on in Vermont.\par \par \uc2 Katie Byrne is there with more -- Katie.\par \par \uc2 KATIE BYRNE, FOX MULTIMEDIA JOURNALIST: Neil, crews are racing to clear debris here in the town of Barre before more rain starts.\par \par \uc2 You can see trucks pushing all of this mud into one place. There is so much mud in this town, and some of it is actually caked onto storm drains. So, a big priority today was to clean them out, so this next round of rain drains and we don't get even more flooding.\par \par \uc2 I have seen people using snow shovels to move this really heavy mud. Some have been wearing hazmat suits and gas masks to clean out their homes. And you will see a lot of muddied furniture on the of the roads that can't be saved.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JON SOLOMON, RESIDENT OF BARRE, VERMONT: It's just beyond words. I live up on a hill, and my basement got flooded. So I can just -- these people all around here -- this guy, he couldn't leave his shop.\par \par \uc2 He had to climb up in the attic and stay here. The water was up to his waist.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BYRNE: This afternoon, State Police geared up and pulled a van from the Stevens Branch River in town just to our right. They say no one was inside.\par \par \uc2 Search-and-rescue crews stage across the state ahead of this incoming rain, which right now is in Upstate New York, making its way here to us in Vermont in the next couple of hours, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, thank you very much for that, Katie.\par \par \uc2 Want to go right now to the other side of the country, Max Gorden, FOX weather correspondent, on how hot it's getting out there.\par \par \uc2 What do you have for us, my friend?\par \par \uc2 MAX GORDEN, FOX WEATHER CORRESPONDENT: Neil, it is sizzling here in Sin City, 103 degrees right now.\par \par \uc2 We're on our way to 109 today. We already had one of our cameras die on us because it overheated. And this is nothing compared to what we're going to see later on this weekend, 117, potentially 118 degrees by Sunday. That might be a new high record for Las Vegas.\par \par \uc2 Clark County, Nevada, where Las Vegas is located, is opening up cooling shelters, telling people to stay inside. But that hasn't stopped people from getting out on the Strip. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Oh, we're struggling. We're not used to this type of weather in Ireland, are we?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We're sweating loads, yes.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The heat is on and someone needs to turn it off.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Very hot. It is too hot.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You get out in it early and stay out in it, and then you kind of accumulate to it, and you don't realize it's getting hot.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GORDEN: This is part of a broader heat wave across the country, 84 million Americans under some type of heat warning from the Mississippi Valley all the way to the West Coast.\par \par \uc2 Back here in Las Vegas, officials telling people once again, stay inside if you can. And if you got to be outside, wear light, loose clothing and be sure to hydrate -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Just incredible. Max, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Max Gorden.\par \par \uc2 Well, they say it's a dry heat, right? But I think when you get up to 110, 120 degrees, it's hot.\par \par \uc2 All right, in the meantime here, it's a hack attack, the Chinese at it again, but, this time, they can willy-nilly control and dip into the e-mail of Cabinet officials and many more -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: It's one thing when we get hacked, quite another deal when a top Cabinet secretary, the commerce secretary of the United States, for example, Gina Raimondo, gets hacked as well.\par \par \uc2 The State Department officials, others are routinely seeing this happening, we're told, at the mercy of the Chinese.\par \par \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2  joins us right now. He's been watching us very closely because he's been also following how busy the Chinese have been in our neighborhood, by the way.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, always great to have you. What do you think of this?\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 HENRY CUELLAR\uc2  (D-TX): This shows us that the cloud servers of companies have to work with the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  to make sure that we're providing the best security to the end users, or, in this case, we're talking about the different federal agencies.\par \par \uc2 We got to keep in mind that Russia and China are constant threats. It's not -- they don't take any days off. They don't take any vacation time. They're constant. And we have to make sure that we're constant doing all the security protocols, because they don't stop.\par \par \uc2 As you know, as we set up more export control measures to make sure that we hold off sensitive technology to go to the Chinese, they're going to get this information one way or the other. If they can't buy it, they're going to go ahead and try to steal whatever they can.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: They seem to have relative ease getting through whatever defenses we have in this area, but they seem to have free rein throughout South America now.\par \par \uc2 That's another concern of yours. What's going on there?\par \par \uc2 CUELLAR: If you look at from Mexico all the way down to Argentina to the very, very end, Chile, they're almost in every country, except maybe Guatemala, Uruguay.\par \par \uc2 But they're up there in so many ways. They're getting port rights. They're setting up infrastructure. They got their 5G in so many countries. They're doing a lot of things. And one of the things that worries me is that, while we're looking at the Indo-Pacific and the Eastern Europe and other places, which are important, we cannot forget about our own backyard, because they're there.\par \par \uc2 And they're very, very aggressive. I mean, think about this. They got about 12 space stations where they're trying to track certain satellites. You know they're going to use that to track our own satellites. So, they're doing so many things that a lot of people are not aware of.\par \par \uc2 And the last thing we want to do, Neil, is to wake up and say, hey, they're right across the Rio Grande, which they are already.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Speaking of right across the Rio Grande, I know that the administration has been talking about how better things look at the border.\par \par \uc2 But when I look at the breakdown of countries and those apprehended at the border, they're from all over the world. It used to be largely Mexico, Guatemala, Uruguay, as you said, but many from China, and that blew me away. Now, they're not by overwhelming numbers, but we never used to see that.\par \par \uc2 What's going on there?\par \par \uc2 CUELLAR: Yes, you're absolutely right.\par \par \uc2 In the old days, it was young Mexican males coming in from Mexico to come work. Then it changed to Central Americans in the 2000s. And now we got over 50, 60 countries are represented.\par \par \uc2 And what's interesting is places like we saw them in Matamoros, you had large numbers of Chinese individuals that were trying to come in. The bottom line is, if they feel that there's a way that they can come in, they're going to come in.\par \par \uc2 And when you look at the asylum cases percentage-wise, you know who gets the largest grants of asylum cases granted? Chinese individuals. It's not Central Americans. So it's not South Americans. It's folks from -- Chinese. I have looked at those numbers.\par \par \uc2 So, again, it's a threat. And I'm talking about what's happening in Latin America. And this is why we're pushing to make sure that the State Department, the -- our own Department of Defense, that we do a lot more to watch our own backyard, because, again, I keep repeating this. The last thing we want to do is wake up and realize that the Chinese are right across the border.\par \par \uc2 And, again, I will say it. They are right across the border right now.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Incredible. You were the first to remind folks about that.\par \par \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2 , very good seeing you again. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CUELLAR: Thank you so much, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, in the meantime, Tim Scott, he has made the debate stage, and rather easily.\par \par \uc2 He's next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, you are looking at fund-raising numbers for one South Carolina Senator Tim Scott, 21 million bucks of cash on hand, and the latest quarter haul, more than $6 million. Not bad for a candidate who many say is a distant third, fourth, however you want to put it, to the prominent candidate. That is remaining \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So, obviously, some people putting money on the fact that will not be the case.\par \par \uc2 The senator taking some time out to join us.\par \par \uc2 Senator, very good to have you.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT (R-SC), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Thank you, Neil. It's always good to be back on your show.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Same here.\par \par \uc2 So, let me ask you a little bit about the money you're raising and also securing that position on the debate stage next month for the debate and the 40,000-plus individual donors, sometimes just a few bucks at a time, who coughed up the money in all 50 states for you.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: That's a pretty good haul.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Yes, I'm really thankful for it.\par \par \uc2 What it says is, is that our message has traction. We believe that America can do for anyone what she has done for me. We're focusing on restoring hope, creating opportunities, and protecting the America that we all love.\par \par \uc2 For me, that starts with our Southern border. Without any question, Neil, 70,000 Americans who've lost their lives because of fentanyl is 70,000 too many. We have to target the Mexican cartels. Their actions and activities must cease to exist.\par \par \uc2 As president of the United States, I would sign the legislation I sponsored as a senator, which cuts off the money supply by sanctioning the cartels. We have over 60 current co-sponsors on that legislation. There's a way for us to be strong on our border, strong on behalf of life, and strong for America's future.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You mentioned being strong on behalf of life.\par \par \uc2 As you know, \uc2 Kim Reynolds\uc2 , the Iowa governor, pushing the state law that would ban abortions after six weeks. What do you think of that?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Well, Neil, we should celebrate the fact that our states are creating a culture of life.\par \par \uc2 As president of the United States, I believe my job would be to stop the radical left from having abortion on demand up until the day of birth. I say that because the Democrats in the Senate today have voted for late-term abortions. I would start with a limit of 15 weeks on abortions, federal limit of 15 weeks.\par \par \uc2 We have to create the culture of life, so that I can sign the most conservative pro-life legislation that can get to my desk. Neil, here's the truth, though. A 15-week limit today would not pass through this Congress. The 20-week bill that we worked on, the Pain-Capable bill, never saw the light of day.\par \par \uc2 As a matter of fact, \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  and the Democrats won't even allow any pro-life legislation. Thirty weeks could not pass this Congress today. That means we have work to do to create the culture.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Do you worry then, Senator, that six weeks is too much? Do you worry that six weeks is too much, and that that might hurt Republicans' chances?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Well, on the federal level, here's what we know.\par \par \uc2 We want states to do the very best they can to create that culture I just discussed earlier. I'm 100 percent pro-life.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: I have got a 100 percent pro-life voting record.\par \par \uc2 For us to get it done on the federal level, we're going to have to convince people. And, today, we haven't convinced enough Americans even for a 15- week limit. So, every other conversation outside of getting the number from 39 or 40 weeks down to 15 is inconsequential if we can't win it in the Senate or the House right now.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, when you look at where this whole abortion thing stands, \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , the former vice president, has said on this Iowa move to make it six weeks: "I'm pro-life. I don't apologize for it."\par \par \uc2 He says: "I just have heard so many stories over the years of courageous women and families who were told that their unborn child would not go to term or would not survive, and they had a healthy pregnancy and a healthy delivery."\par \par \uc2 He is going so far as to say, as you're probably familiar, that women should carry even difficult pregnancies to term. What do you think of that?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Well, I think the answer to your question is a question of viability.\par \par \uc2 And for those who -- that are not viable, that's the question that I think Mike was answering. He and I have very different opinions on that topic. I think the one thing I would consistently say is that, if we can consistently protect the culture of life, focus our attention as a nation and as a party on making sure that we have a limit that stops the radical left from pushing abortion on demand for any reason whatsoever...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: ... we find ourselves where three out of four Americans actually agree with us.\par \par \uc2 We need to take that as momentum to be successful.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, I'm sorry, Senator. I just -- I apologize.\par \par \uc2 I just want to be super clear on this.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: As I see it so far, Senator, the vice president, the former vice president, is the only major candidate who supports a federal ban on abortion at six weeks.\par \par \uc2 That is what Iowa Governor \uc2 Kim Reynolds\uc2  is recommending. Are you saying that that's too extreme for you?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Well, what I said, and I will continue to say, is that the 15-week limit is where we start the conversation. Until we win the hearts and minds of the American people, we cannot get that through Congress.\par \par \uc2 I'm looking from the perspective, as president of the United States, I would sign the most conservative pro-life legislation we can get through Congress. And, today, that's not even a 30-week. So having the hypotheticals that we're talking about is something that I know we can't get done, because, today, we can't get the 20-week done, we can't get the 15-week done.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: And so I'm going to continue to work as president of the United States to create the culture where at least the 15-week limit is something we are able to accomplish.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I mentioned the governor, \uc2 Kim Reynolds\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And she's in a dust-up not of her own making, but \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and. Her she says she has to be impartial in this race. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was offended by that, kind of picked a fight with her that she's ungrateful because he had supported her and helped make her governor.\par \par \uc2 I just want your thoughts on that.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Well, Governor \uc2 Kim Reynolds\uc2  is one of the most remarkable individuals in the country, and she is one of the strongest governors her state and any state has ever seen.\par \par \uc2 This is the woman who passed pro-school choice legislation for the first time in the history of her state. She has provided a $7,500 scholarship for kids within 300 percent of poverty. What that means, Neil, as the guy who's championed the cause of pro -- being school choice, pro-school choice, what that means is that we now have the poorest kids in her state having the same opportunities as the richest kids in her state.\par \par \uc2 She's also worked on starting -- jump-starting one of the strongest economies during the most difficult times in the state. I am thankful that, today, she's agnostic about which candidate should be president of the United States. I look forward to winning her support.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: It's very early in the race, and we -- you have been reminding me of that in past interviews, Senator.\par \par \uc2 But one poll that I found interesting on you is that you're rapidly rising to be everyone's favorite number two. In fact, you have doubled in that sentiment in polls that have been taken around the country.\par \par \uc2 Are you interested or would you be interested, should you fail to get the nomination, to be the runner-up, to be the guy running with the person who wins?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Yes, well, Neil, the one thing -- I never correct you on TV because you're so good at what you do, but I don't think everybody wants me to be the number two.\par \par \uc2 There are some who want me to be the number one. And so I'm going to continue to run this race for one objective. It's to be the president of the United States. I did not enter this race to come in second place. Second place is the first loser.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: From my perspective, the only way to run for president is to do it 100 percent of the time.\par \par \uc2 And all we're focused on is making sure that the next generation of stories of the American dream make mine pale in comparison. We are the city on the hill, Neil, and we must remain the beacon in the midst of the storm.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Would you support any one of your colleagues if you don't get that nomination for president, all the way up to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Here's what I can tell you. I will be the nominee for the great opportunity party. And I will stop there.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it.\par \par \uc2 Is it really true as well -- I looked in your background -- that you really love the movie "Rocky III"? Tell me that is not true.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Well, listen, I'm going to bust you up, sucker.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: That's what Mr. T said to Rocky.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: So, here's what we know.\par \par \uc2 And, of course, the response to that, of course, was Neil: "Go for it."\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: And so I would just say, let's go. I love "Rocky III."\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, there we go. I pity the fool who thinks otherwise.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But, Senator, thank you.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: I pity the fool!\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Oh, that's pretty good. That's very good.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: God bless you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Senator Tim Scott, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, we have a lot more coming up, including why stocks continued running up today, maybe having to do with, well, prices not -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: He's jeopardizing U.S. security by what he's doing.\par \par \uc2 The idea that we're injecting into fundamental foreign policy decisions what, in fact, is a domestic social debate on social issues is bizarre. I don't ever recall that happening, ever. And it's just totally irresponsible.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, President Biden not too keen on what Alabama Republican Senator Tommy Tuberville is doing to block U.S. military promotions, protesting the Pentagon's abortion policies.\par \par \uc2 Sabrina Singh joins us right now, of course, the Pentagon deputy press secretary.\par \par \uc2 Sabrina, the president is saying this is unfair and not the way you go about trying to force your way.\par \par \uc2 What do you say?\par \par \uc2 SABRINA SINGH, PENTAGON DEPUTY PRESS SECRETARY: Well, thanks, Neil, for having me.\par \par \uc2 The president is absolutely right. We're talking about a hold on our general and flag officers, our nonpolitical and in a nonpolitical -- in a fight that should not be political.\par \par \uc2 Again, these are our folks that are stationed all around the world who are selected at a time when we need them most, at a time when the acute threat of Russia is rising in Europe and the growing pacing challenge of China. And so we need our best team on the field. And that means that these Senate-confirmed nominees who are up for nomination being held currently by Senator Tuberville need to be confirmed by the Senate.\par \par \uc2 And just earlier this week, on Monday, the secretary participated in a change of responsibility of office of the Marine Corps of the commandant. And so, right now, in over a century, we have an acting commandant of the Marine Corps, who is, by the way, doing two jobs, both the commandant job and the assistant commandant job.\par \par \uc2 And we're going to have many more of these positions come up where the person in those roles are going to have to be dual-headed. And that definitely impacts our national security and it impacts our readiness all around the world and here at home.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: We still have the commander in chief. So we have been in positions where we go through this interim. You're saying this is different from those?\par \par \uc2 SINGH: Well, this is unprecedented.\par \par \uc2 This is a hold on every single general and flag officer who has been nominated and has currently been held by one single senator. So this is unprecedented. We have certainly seen this with our political nominees. But, again, these are our nominees who have incredibly important jobs all around the world, who are working with our partners and allies.\par \par \uc2 And it sends a message to our adversaries. If you do not have your equivalent three-star in place communicating with your ally or partners' three-star in command, that certainly sends a message to our allies and our adversaries. And so we urge Senator Tuberville to lift these holds.\par \par \uc2 And these holds not only have an impact on our service members. They have an impact on their families. Families are being forced to make moves on their own, to decide where to enroll their children in school, if that means at the duty station where the service member might be positioned in the future, or do they stay back?\par \par \uc2 Spouses also have to find new jobs. So this is not just an impact on the military. This is an impact on the families as well.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Real quickly -- I apologize for the truncated time, Sabrina -- but the president also made a reference to \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  today leaving Finland that he might want to watch what he eats. I'm paraphrasing here, that he might want to look over his shoulder.\par \par \uc2 Do you think that was appropriate?\par \par \uc2 SINGH: Again, I will let the president's comment speak for itself.\par \par \uc2 I'm here representing the Department of Defense. And I will say that, again, we are here in support of Ukraine, and we will be in the fight for as long as it takes with Ukraine. I think you saw the president reiterate that at the \uc2 NATO\uc2  summit, and, again, an incredible showing of support, with the 31st country entering the \uc2 NATO\uc2  alliance, and soon to be the 32nd with Sweden.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, he was not trying to encourage a coup in Russia?\par \par \uc2 SINGH: Again, I will let the president's comments stand for themselves.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK.\par \par \uc2 SINGH: I'm here, as you know...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 SINGH: ... representing the department, and proudly doing so.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it.\par \par \uc2 All right, Sabrina Singh, thank you very much, the Pentagon deputy press secretary.\par \par \uc2 Here now "The Five."\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020230714ej7d00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Interview With Sen. Tim Scott (R-SC)\b0\par\par\uc2 Neil Cavuto \par \uc2 1,859 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 July 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: All right, you are looking at fund-raising numbers for one South Carolina Senator Tim Scott, 21 million bucks of cash on hand, and the latest quarter haul, more than $6 million. Not bad for a candidate who many say is a distant third, fourth, however you want to put it, to the prominent candidate. That is remaining \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So, obviously, some people putting money on the fact that will not be the case.\par \par \uc2 The senator taking some time out to join us.\par \par \uc2 Senator, very good to have you.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT (R-SC), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Thank you, Neil. It`s always good to be back on your show.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Same here.\par \par \uc2 So, let me ask you a little bit about the money you`re raising and also securing that positi